1520	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024865	Training and outreach for advanced computing and research	Table of contents\nOverview\nWorkshops, trainings, and seminars\nAdvanced cyberinfrastructure tours\nOutreach\nEducation and workforce development\nScience, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics (STEM) pipeline\nOverview\nUITS\nResearch Technologies\nprovides targeted training, seminars, workshops, and tours that connect you with the services you need to reach discovery.\nWorkshops, trainings, and seminars\nSupercomputing for Everyone Series (SC4ES)\nThe\nResearch Technologies\nSupercomputing for Everyone Series (SC4ES) offers in-person trainings and online courses that teach you how to use the research computing, storage, and software resources available at IU. Some SC4ES courses are available online via the\nIU Expand\nportal. For more, see the\nSupercomputing for Everyone Series\non the IU IT Training website.\nCompute, storage, research software\nResearch Programming with GPUs\nIntro to High Performance Computing (HPC)\nResearch Desktop at IU (online via IU Expand)\nJetstream Overview\nStorage for High Performance Computing\nStatistical and Numerical Software for HPC\nParallel Programming for Data Science (Matlab, R, Python)\nParallel Programming for Modeling and Simulation (C, C++, Fortran)\nUnix: The Basics\nBasic UNIX/LINUX Skills (online via IU Expand)\nDatabases and surveys\nIU\nREDCap\nSeminars/Webinars and Office Hours\nWeekly office hours via Zoom (Wednesdays, 3-4:30pm)\nGetting Started with IU\nREDCap\nSurveys in IU\nREDCap\nData Quality in IU\nREDCap\nExtensive library of\nREDCap\ntraining videos\nVisualization and data services\nServices for Digital Humanities and Creative Activities Seminar Series\nPlaylist of archived presentations on YouTube\nImpact of Virtual Reality Seminar Series\nPlaylist of archived presentations on YouTube\nAugmented, Virtual, and Extended Reality Seminar Series\nPlaylist of archived presentations on YouTube\nFor biologists and genomicists\nHPC Onboarding for Biologists\nHPC Onboarding for Biologists (online via IU Expand)\nIntro to R for Biologists\nIntro to R for Biologists (online via IU Expand)\nTranscriptome Assembly, Annotation, and Analysis (course materials)\nMetagenomics Analysis Workshop (course materials)\nDe Novo Assembly of Transcriptomes Using HPC Resources (course materials)\nIU students, faculty, staff also may request that a SC4ES trainings be taught at time and/or locations that are convenient for their schools, departments, or programs;\ncontact IT Training\n.\nPeebles Memorial Lectures in Information Technology\nThe\nPeebles Memorial Lectures in Information Technology\nseries sponsors one lecture per academic year in areas of information technology relevant to research, teaching, or creative activity in academia.\nAdvanced cyberinfrastructure tours\nIU's advanced cyberinfrastructure includes high performance supercomputers, advanced visualization facilities, and high-throughput networking technologies. Tours are available for individuals, classes, or departments. To schedule a tour,\ncontact UITS\nResearch Technologies\n.\nOutreach\nPublications\nImpact highlights from\nResearch Technologies\nIU\nResearch Technologies\nYouTube channel\nResearch Technologies\nnews\nResearch Technologies\nevents\nBibliography of publications by UITS\nResearch Technologies\npersonnel\nNational impact\nEach year, UITS Research Technologies collaborates with other IU entities (for example, SICE and GlobalNOC) to display IU computer science, informatics, and cyberinformatics innovations at the annual\nInternational Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage, and Analysis\n.\nAdditionally, the PTI hosts a number of national and international conferences at IU, providing the opportunity for IU students, faculty, and staff to attend conferences they might not otherwise by saving them travel costs and time. Attendees can present their research and learn more about particular topics of interest. Hosting conferences also helps strengthen IU's reputation as a national research institution, and introduces researchers from around the world to Bloomington and the state of Indiana.\nRecent conferences hosted by PTI and Research Technologies include:\nHPSS User Forum 2019 (HUF19)\nThe Campus Alliance for Advanced Visualization Fourth Annual Conference (THE CAAV 2019)\nLustre User Group 2017 (LUG 2017)\nGalaxy Community Conference 2016 (GCC2016)\nEducation and workforce development\nCourses\nUITS Research Technologies staff teach courses at IU and travel the world to teach topics including scientific visualization, virtual reality, and digital humanities.\nInternships\nInternships with UITS Research Technologies and other research centers affiliated with the PTI; for currently available internships, check the\nJobs @ IU\nwebsite.\nJetstream2 REU program\nThe\nJetstream2\nResearch Experience for Undergraduates (REU) program\nprovides students the opportunity to work with IU researchers and staff on mentored research projects using\nJetstream2\n.\nScience, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics (STEM) pipeline\nReady, Set, Robots!\nEach year, UITS Research Technologies hosts the Ready, Set, Robots! camp, where junior high and high school students learn computer programming, problem-solving skills, critical thinking, and teamwork. Students also gain public speaking experience, respect for their equipment and facilities, and insight into working "green" (in IU Bloomington's LEED-certified\nCyberinfrastructure Building\n).\nK-12 and university education and training resources\nResearch Technologies events and curricula resources help entice the next generation of Hoosiers to pursue exciting careers in STEM fields.\nView all education resources\n.	KB0024865	KB0024865 v1.0	 Computing (<strong>HPC</strong>) Research Desktop at IU (online via IU Expand) Jetstream Overview Storage for High Performance Computing Statistical and Numerical Software for <strong>HPC</strong> Parallel Programming for Data Science of archived presentations on YouTube For biologists and genomicists <strong>HPC</strong> Onboarding for Biologists <strong>HPC</strong>	112.8523		2026-01-31 14:33:03.11173	2026-01-31 16:39:16.457619	0	a300b07f476456583d373803846d43de
11701	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=654078b347a456583d373803846d4313	Use the local security settings to force NTLMv2	Table of contents\nOverview\nUse the Local Security Policy console\nEdit the registry (advanced method)\nOverview\nIf the NTLM authentication setting on your Windows computer is not set to NTLMv2, your computer may repeatedly prompt you for your\nIU username and passphrase\nwhen you attempt to access your IU\nExchange\naccount via Outlook (or any other desktop email client).\nWindows 8.x\nand later and\nWindows Server\nuse NTLMv2 authentication by default, but in rare instances, this setting may become incorrect, even if the NTLM setting was previously correct.\nYou only need to use one of the following methods. Using the Local Security Policy console is easier, but not all versions of Windows include the\nsecpol.msc\napplication necessary to use this method.  It is usually found on business-class versions of Windows (for example, Enterprise and Ultimate).  The\nregistry option\nwill work on all versions of Windows.\nUse the Local Security Policy console\nTo use the local security settings to force Windows to use NTLMv2:\nOpen the Local Security Policy console, using one of the following methods:\nFrom the Control Panel:\nNavigate to the Control Panel. Double-click\nAdministrative Tools\n, and then\nLocal\nSecurity Policy\n.\nVia search:\nSearch for the\nsecpol.msc\napplication and launch it.  To do so:\nIn\nWindows 11\n,\nWindows 10\nor\nWindows Server 2016\n, use the search function from the Taskbar.\nIn\nWindows 8.x\nor\nWindows Server 2012\n, swipe down from the upper right corner, select\nSearch\n, enter\nsecpol.msc\n, and press\nEnter\n.\nThe Local Security Policy console will appear.\nFind "Network Security: LAN Manager authentication level", which is located in Security Settings, Local Policies, Security Options.\nSet the LAN Manager authentication level to\nNTLMv2 response\nonly/refuse LM and NTLM\n.\nEdit the registry (advanced method)\nIf the\nsecpol.msc\ncontrol described in the instructions\nabove\nis missing, you can make this change directly in the registry.\nWarning:\nThis contains instructions for editing the\nregistry\n. If you make any error while editing the registry, you can potentially cause Windows to fail or be unable to boot, requiring you to reinstall Windows. Edit the registry at your own risk. Always back up the registry before making any changes. If you do not feel comfortable editing the registry, do not attempt these instructions. Instead, seek the help of a computing support provider.\nIn\nWindows 8.x\nand later, initiate a search.\nEnter\nregedt32\n. Select\nOK\nor\nEnter\n.\nDouble-click\nHKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\n, then\nSYSTEM\n,\nCurrentControlSet\n,\nControl\n, and finally\nLSA\n.\nIn the right pane, double-click the\nLMCompatibilityLevel\nvalue.\nIn the "Data" field of the DWORD Editor window, enter\n5\n. Select\nOK\n.\nIn the\nRegistry\nmenu, select\nExit\n.\nRestart your system for the registry changes to take effect.	KB0026071	KB0026071 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Use the Local Security Policy console Edit the registry (advanced method) Overview If the NTLM authentication setting on your Windows computer is not set to NTLMv2, your computer may repeatedly prompt you for your IU username and passphrase when you attempt to access your IU Exchange account via Outlook (or any other d	4.4727		2026-01-31 17:15:47.687281	2026-01-31 17:15:47.687281	0	654078b347a456583d373803846d4313
11008	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c9ea4210477eb6d0d500b87b716d43cb	Docker container best practices	Table of contents\nBest practices\nResource limits\nDo not run as root\nTag all images\nExpose only the ports needed for your container\nRoutinely audit and update Dockerfiles\nBest practices\nKeep in mind that Docker containers are intended for running a single process, and are not intended to replace an operating system.\nEach container should run a single process and be linked to other containers running dependent processes (for example, database container, memcached container, etc.)\nA container should contain the minimum number of dependencies and configurations needed to run the application.\nThis recommendation is important since everything installed will need regularly applied security patches.\nYou don't need text editors in the container.\nAs the only thing running in the container is a single process, installing a text editor is a waste of disk space since you will not be inside the container to use it.\nYou don't need SSH daemons in the container.\nAs you are running a single process in the container, you won't need to SSH into the container while it's running. Allowing SSH connections opens up the possibility of security issues and other concerns. Things like log files should be shared with the host running the container, so you should be able to SSH there to find the any information you may need.\nYou likely don't need version control or build tools in the container.\nInstalling packages to build the application adds to the complexity of keeping things up to date inside it.\nConsider packaging your application and adding that to the container instead of trying to replicate the entire build environment in a single container.\nIf you feel like you need build tools and/or version control inside the container, consider creating different containers for building and running your application as part of your CD process.\nAny directories where data changes (for example, log files) should be mounted as a volume from the host system into a storage container so you can access them on the host machine. This is specifically for running containers via the\ndocker\ncommand, as container orchestrators like Kubernetes have different best practices.\nSee\nManaging data in containers\n.\nThe\nDockerfile Best Practices\npage contains a lot of good information on how to make your Dockerfiles more reusable.\nRemember to apply the\nSingle Responsibility Principle\nto your Docker images and layer them where needed.\nSeparate out different layers for different portions of the runtime if there are reusable pieces.\nFor example, create a Java layer that installs the JDK and any needed customization, and then create another image that is built from the Java image to add Tomcat on top of it.\nUse\nMulti-Stage Builds\n.\nEnsure your container\ndoes not run as the root user\n. It is best if your containers run as a user with the least privilege needed to run (even though it is an isolated environment).\nUse specific versions of dependencies\n. Docker images are published with the tag "latest" by default, and it can be tempting to use it so you do not need to update your images very often. Using something like the "latest" tag can cause your builds to fail, since the image may or may not be updated before it is used, and you could end up with an older or newer version than expected.\nVersion your Docker images along with your application code. When you tag or release your code, consider also releasing a tagged version of a Docker image for that version.\nResource limits\nEnsure you add CPU and memory limits to your containers or services so they don't monopolize the resources on the host machines. This will be enforced, and containers or services missing these constraints may be removed without notice.\nWhen using\ndocker container run\n, the options are as follows:\nFlag\nDescription\n--cpu-quota\nLimit CPU CFS (Completely Fair Scheduler) quota\n--memory\n,\n-m\nMemory limit\nFor more, see\ndocker container run\n.\nFor details on how to set resource constraints when deploying an application using Kubernetes, see:\nAssign CPU Resources to Containers and Pods\nCreate a LimitRange and a Pod\nDo not run as root\nDo not run as the root user inside a container. Creating a user with limited privileges is a general best practice for Docker containers, but it is especially important for enterprise applications. For example:\nRUN adduser -S\nuser\n&& \\\nchown -R\nuser\n/usr/src/app && \\\nWORKDIR /usr/src/app\nUSER\nuser\nTag all images\nAll images pushed to Harbor should be tagged to provide an easier rollback if an updated version of the application malfunctions (without specifying a label, Docker will always tag it as "latest").\n# Tagging an image when building\n$ docker build -t registry.docker.iu.edu/esimw/app:\ntag\n.\n# Tagging an existing image\n$ docker tag app registry.docker.iu.edu/esimw/app:\ntag\nExpose only the ports needed for your container\nExpose only the ports that are absolutely necessary for your container. Opening additional ports beyond what is strictly necessary expands the attack surface for malicious users.\nRoutinely audit and update Dockerfiles\nRoutinely audit your Dockerfiles to ensure that dependencies, such as base images, are using the most up-to-date versions.	KB0027157	KB0027157 v4.0	 running dependent processes (for example, <strong>database</strong> container, memcached container, etc.) A container	3.5587	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 16:58:32.43374	2026-01-31 16:58:32.43374	0	c9ea4210477eb6d0d500b87b716d43cb
2439	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7800703f476456583d373803846d4374	Eligibility for IU computing accounts at the School of Medicine	Anyone who is affiliated with Indiana University is eligible for\nIU computing accounts\n. Also eligible are people at institutions such as IU Health who are collaborating with researchers at the IU School of Medicine. To get a sponsored account for a collaborating researcher, contact\nHealth Technology Services (HTS) Phone Support\nat 317-274-5336 or\nhts@iu.edu\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:35:58.978185	2026-01-31 15:05:50.635441	1	7800703f476456583d373803846d4374
10051	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=95cf2cb7476456583d373803846d43ec	Access your MySQL database at IU RDC	Table of contents\nOverview\nMySQL clients\nConnect to MySQL using the command-line client\nConnect to MySQL using MySQL Workbench\nGet help\nOverview\nThe Indiana University\nResearch Database Complex\n(\nRDC\n) supports research-related MySQL and PostgreSQL databases and data-intensive applications that require databases. The\nRDC\nis strictly devoted to supporting research, and is not an instructional, classroom environment.\nWhen you requested the creation of your RDC MySQL database, you received information that you need to connect to your database:\nYour MySQL database login ("_root" username)\nInformation about your initial database password\nYour database host and port number\nIf you no longer have this information, email the RDC Administration group (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n) for help.\nIf you are off campus, make sure you're using\nIU VPN\n.\nTo request an account on an Indiana University research system, see\nGet additional IU computing accounts\n. Account availability depends on your\neligibility\n.\nMySQL clients\nTo connect to your account on the RDC, use one of the following:\nMySQL command-line client:\nThe MySQL command-line client is available on\nQuartz\n.\nMySQL Workbench:\nThe MySQL Workbench graphical client is available at IU via\nResearch Desktop (RED)\n. Also, a community edition is available for free download from MySQL; see\nDownload MySQL Workbench\n.\nConnect to MySQL using the command-line client\nIf you have an account on\nQuartz\n, you can use the command-line MySQL client to access your MySQL database:\nLoad the\nmysql\nmodule; on the command line, enter:\nmodule load mysql\nOn the command line, enter (replace\nusername_root\nwith your MySQL database login):\nmysql --host=sasrdsmp01.uits.iu.edu --port=3306 -u\nusername_root\n-p\nNote:\nFor security reasons, do not enter your password on the MySQL command line.\nWhen prompted, enter your MySQL database password.\nWhen you log in successfully, you will be placed at the MySQL prompt (\nmysql>\n).\nConnect to MySQL using MySQL Workbench\nOpen MySQL Workbench. On the home screen, select the\n+\n(plus sign) next to the MySQL connections.\nIn the "Setup New Connection" window:\nEnter your database credentials.\nFor "Connection Name", enter a descriptive name, such as the account name used for the database account.\nFor "Connection Method", leave the default\nStandard (TCP/IP)\nunless you need another one.\nFor "MySQL Hostname", enter the RDC MySQL hostname\nsasrdsmp01.uits.iu.edu\n; for the port, enter\n3306\n.\nIn the "Username" field, enter the account name sent to you (a "_root" username).\nFor "Password", UITS does\nnot\nrecommend storing the password in the vault.\nTo test your credentials, select\nTest Connection\n. If you didn't save the password in the vault, you will be prompted for it. Use the password sent with your account name. If the connection is successful, select\nOK\n.\nWhile testing your credentials, you may get the following error: "You must reset your password using ALTER USER statement before executing this statement". If this happens, close the error window, select\nOK\nto create the connection, and then select the newly created connection. A "Password Expired" window should appear for you to change the password.\nGet help\nFor help connecting to your RDC MySQL database, email the RDC Administration team (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n).	KB0023277	KB0023277 v1.0	 client: The MySQL command-line client is available on <strong>Quartz</strong>. MySQL Workbench: The MySQL Workbench-line client If you have an account on <strong>Quartz</strong>, you can use the command-line MySQL client to access your	6.2802		2026-01-31 15:56:20.874521	2026-01-31 15:56:20.874521	0	95cf2cb7476456583d373803846d43ec
10265	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a6efe07b476456583d373803846d432a	About Slack for IU HPC Users	Table of contents\nOverview\nCreate an account\nLog in\nChange your email address\nChannels\nResearch Technologies\nsupport in Slack\nOverview\nResearch Technologies\nhas created a\nSlack\norganization called "IU HPC Users" for users of Indiana University\nresearch supercomputers\n. If you have an\nIU email address\n, you can join IU HPC Users Slack.\nCreate an account\nTo join IU HPC Users Slack and use it to connect with other researchers and\nResearch Technologies\nstaff:\nGo to\nhttps://iu-hpc-users.slack.com\n.\nAt the upper right, under "Don't have an account yet?", select\nCreate an account\n.\nEnter your\nIU username\nin the text box and then choose\nContinue\n.\nYou'll receive an email message at the address you entered. To confirm you own that email account, select the button in the message, and you'll be taken to the\niu-hpc-users\nworkspace to create a new Slack account.\nEnter your full name (not your IU username) and create a password.\nNote:\nDo not enter your\nIU passphrase\n. This password should be unique to the IU HPC Users Slack space.\nFill in the required information and choose\nCreate Account\nto log into the IU HPC Users Slack space.\nOnce you've created your account, there are a variety of\nchannels\nyou can join depending upon your interests.\nLog in\nIf you've already created your IU HPC Users Slack account and want to log in:\nGo to\nhttps://iu-hpc-users.slack.com\n.\nEnter your IU email address as your login name, and enter the password you used when you created your account (not your IU passphrase).\nWhen you join the organization, consider putting your department/discipline information into the last name field, to give more context to other users in the Slack organization.\nChange your email address\nIf you previously signed up with a campus-specific email address, you can update your email address for this Slack workspace to your\niu.edu\nemail address following the instructions at\nChange your email address\n.\nImportant:\nIndiana University will be transitioning to a single email domain,\niu.edu\n, between now and the end of 2025. For more about the transition, see\nOne Mail\n.  For actions you may need to take, see\nPrepare for One Mail at IU\n.\nChannels\nThe IU HPC Users Slack workspace has channels for each of the\nresearch supercomputers\n, as well as channels for storage systems and other topic-oriented channels.\nResearch Technologies\nsupport in Slack\nStaff from\nResearch Technologies\nare often available in Slack; however, although this space is intended for user support,\nResearch Technologies\ncannot guarantee response times or the availability of staff in Slack.\nResearch Technologies\nprovides a full suite of support offerings via email and in-person/virtual meetings.	KB0024086	KB0024086 v2.0		0	Public	2026-01-31 16:39:07.975828	2026-01-31 16:39:40.460114	1	a6efe07b476456583d373803846d432a
10268	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=072ac6163322ea5004e605570e5c7b48	Install R packages in your home directory on the IU research supercomputers	Table of contents\nOverview\nSet up your user environment\nInstall R packages\nSearch installed R packages\nGet help\nOverview\nR packages are extensions to the\nR\nlanguage. Developers commonly contribute packages to R package repositories, such as the\nComprehensive R Archive Network (CRAN)\nrepository.\nOn Indiana University's\nresearch supercomputers\n, many third-party R packages are installed to supplement commonly used R builds. If you have a unique need for a third-party R package that is not installed, you can install the package in your\nhome directory\n. If you know several researchers are interested in using an R package that is not already installed, you can\nsend a request to the\nResearch Applications and Deep Learning\n(\nRADL\n) team\nto have it installed as a system-wide site package.\nSet up your user environment\nThe IU\nresearch supercomputers\nuse module-based environment management systems that provide a convenient method for dynamically customizing your software environment.\nTo install R packages on an IU\nresearch supercomputer\n, you must have R added to your user environment.\nTo add R to your user environment, load the\nr\nmodule; on the command line, enter:\nmodule load r\nFor more, see\nUse modules to manage your software environment on IU research supercomputers\n.\nInstall R packages\nCreate a subdirectory for the R packages you're installing (for example,\n~/r-packages\n); on the command line, enter:\nmkdir ~/r-packages\nCreate an R setting file (for example,\n.Renviron\n) in your\nhome directory\n; on the command line, enter:\ntouch ~/.Renviron\nUse your\npreferred editor\nto add this line to your R setting file (replace\n~/r-packages\nwith the path to the subdirectory you created for R packages):\nR_LIBS_USER="~/r-packages"\nDownload the distribution archive from the source; for example, to download\nfoo-1.0.3.tar.gz\nfrom the CRAN repository, on the command line, enter:\nwget https://cran.r-project.org/src/contrib/foo-1.0.3.tar.gz\nInstall the package; on the command line, enter (replace\nfoo-1.0.3.tar.gz\nwith the distribution archive you downloaded):\nR CMD INSTALL foo-1.0.3.tar.gz\nSearch installed R packages\nUse the following commands in the R terminal to search installed package directories and installed packages:\n.libPaths()\nlibrary()\nlibrary(lib.loc='~/r-packages')\nGet help\nIf you have questions about using research software at Indiana University,\ncontact the UITS\nResearch Applications and Deep Learning\nteam\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 16:39:20.263163	2026-01-31 16:39:47.549748	1	072ac6163322ea5004e605570e5c7b48
10271	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=27eeb1be4712de589286dfbd436d43d3	Deal with external dependencies when receiving errors installing R packages on IU's research supercomputers	Installing R packages on Indiana University's\nresearch supercomputers\ncan be challenging when external dependencies are involved. These dependencies may include system libraries or applications that are not pre-installed in IU's research HPC environment. Following are some tips for ensuring a smooth installation process.\nUnderstand the source of the error:\nWhen installation fails, the resulting error messages typically point to missing or incompatible dependencies. Common causes include:\nMissing system libraries (for example,\nlibgdal.so\nor\nlibproj.so\n)\nLack of permissions in the HPC environment\nVersion conflicts with installed software or libraries\nReview CRAN documentation:\nThe Comprehensive R Archive Network (CRAN) provides comprehensive documentation to help address issues with package dependencies; see the\nPackage Dependencies\nsection in the\nWriting R Extensions\nmanual for help specifying and resolving package dependencies.\nUse the R CMD check command:\nThe R CMD check command is a good tool for diagnosing issues. Running this command on your package can identify missing dependencies and provide actionable insights.\nIf you need help or have questions about using R packages on IU's research supercomputers,\ncontact the UITS Research Applications and Deep Learning team\n.	KB0026504	KB0026504 v5.0		0	Public	2026-01-31 16:39:20.263163	2026-01-31 16:39:57.742231	1	27eeb1be4712de589286dfbd436d43d3
11703	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=baaf6c37476456583d373803846d43b3	Prevent programs from starting automatically in Windows	Table of contents\nBefore you begin\nRemove a shortcut\nDisable items not in the Startup folder\nStartup apps or tasks\nTask Manager\nRemove a registry entry\nBefore you begin\nOften, a program starts automatically because of a\nshortcut\nin the\nStartup\nfolder in the\nStart\nmenu. The program may also run automatically from a\nregistry\nentry. To remove programs from automatic startup, follow these instructions in order. After completing each set of steps, reboot your device to see if the problem is fixed. If necessary, you can then continue to the next set of instructions.\nRemove a shortcut\nNote:\nThe shortcut will remain in the Recycle Bin until you empty it.\nTo remove a shortcut from the\nStartup\nfolder:\nPress\nWin\n+\nr\n. In the "Open:" field, type:\nC:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\StartUp\nPress\nEnter\n.\nRight-click the program you don't want to open at startup and select\nDelete\n.\nIf a shortcut you want in the\nStartup\nfolder is pointing to the wrong program, correct it as follows:\nIn the\nStartup\nfolder, right-click the appropriate icon and select\nProperties\n.\nSelect the\nShortcut\ntab. In the "Target:" field, verify that the path statement points to the filename of the correct program. If it does not, enter the accurate path to the program.\nDisable items not in the Startup folder\nSome startup items have no shortcut in the\nStartup\nfolder. You can disable these with either the Startup apps/tasks tool in Settings or the Task Manager. Otherwise, they are only accessible through the registry. If you are unfamiliar or uncomfortable with registry editing, use these utilities.\nStartup tasks\nIn the Windows search box, type\nstartup apps\n(\nWindows 11\n) or\nstartup tasks\n(\nWindows 10\n), and press\nEnter\n.\nThe window that opens will contain a list of applications that may start when your device boots. To disable an app, toggle the switch to\nOff\n.\nTask Manager\nNavigate to the Task Manager.\nIf necessary, select\nMore details\nto see all of the tabs; select the\nStartup\ntab.\nSelect the item not to launch at startup, and select\nDisable\n.\nRemove a registry entry\nWarning:\nThis contains instructions for editing the\nregistry\n. If you make any error while editing the registry, you can potentially cause Windows to fail or be unable to boot, requiring you to reinstall Windows. Edit the registry at your own risk. Always back up the registry before making any changes. If you do not feel comfortable editing the registry, do not attempt these instructions. Instead, seek the help of a computing support provider.\nTo remove a startup entry from the registry:\nPress\nWin\n+\nr\n. In the "Open:" field, type\nregedit\nand press\nEnter\n.\nTo save a backup copy of the registry, from the\nFile\nmenu, select\nExport...\n.\nMake sure to note the file's destination; by default, it will be saved on the desktop.\nGive the file an obvious name, such as\nregistry\nor\nregback\n, so that you'll remember this file is your registry backup.\nIn the "Export Range" field, make sure\nAll\nis selected, and then select\nSave\n. This will make a backup of a working registry.\nTo expand\nHKEY LOCAL MACHINE\n, select the\n+\n(plus sign) to the left.\nExpand the\nSOFTWARE\ntree. Then expand\nMicrosoft\n, then\nWindows\n, and then\nCurrentVersion\n.\nHighlight the\nRun\nfolder. On the right side of the screen, you will see programs that are set to run on startup that might not be included in the\nStartup\nfolder. Some of these may be important programs, such as antivirus programs or firewalls. There also may be undesirable or unnecessary programs, such as spyware and advertisement software. You'll need to ascertain what is needed and what is not.\nHighlight any program(s) you want to prevent from starting automatically, and then press the\nDel\nkey. Remember that deleting programs here does not remove them from your computer; it only stops them from running automatically.\nChanges to the registry are implemented immediately, so you do not need to save your changes. At this point, you may simply close the Registry Editor.	KB0022699	KB0022699 v1.0	Table of contents Before you begin Remove a shortcut Disable items not in the Startup folder Startup apps or tasks Task Manager Remove a registry entry Before you begin Often, a program starts automatically because of a shortcut in the Startup folder in the Start menu. The program may also run automatically from a registry entry. To remove progr	4.4727		2026-01-31 17:15:51.143261	2026-01-31 17:15:51.143261	0	baaf6c37476456583d373803846d43b3
10273	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b1e30fec47e0a250d500b87b716d43ec	Common installation solutions for research software from IUware	Table of contents\nOverview\nGeneral download issues\nUser cannot download files from IUware\nJMP\nUser cannot find the license file location\nNVivo\nUser cannot write or execute programs\nSAS\nUser cannot write or execute programs\nOther configuration issues prevent SAS installation\nSPSS\nUser has selected a concurrent license during installation and is now prompted for a server address\nUser cannot write or execute programs\nStata\nUser cannot find the license file location\nOverview\nUse the information below to assist users with research software issues.\nGeneral download issues\nUser cannot download files from IUware\nPossible solutions:\nIf there are network issues, the user can try downloading from a faster network. The IU network on campus generally suffices.\nThe browser may have cache issues. Try restarting the browser or using a different browser.\nIf the user cannot log into IUware, double-check their IU status. The licenses generally cover IU students, faculty, and staff. They do not cover former employees, affiliates, or IU Foundation employees. Those who believe they should have student, faculty, or staff status should\ncontact RADL\n.\nJMP\nUser cannot find the license file location\nThe ZIP file downloaded from IUware contains a\n.txt\nfile. This is the license file.\nFor help, see the\nvendor's installation page\n.\nNVivo\nUser cannot write or execute programs\nThis generally happens when someone does not have administrative rights to their machine. The user should talk to their departmental support.\nFor help, see the\nvendor's installation page\n.\nSAS\nUser cannot write or execute programs\nThis generally happens when someone does not have administrative rights to their machine. The user should talk to their departmental support.\nOther configuration issues prevent SAS installation\nFor other configuration issues:\nStudents should seek assistance from the\nSupport Center\n.\nFaculty and staff should seek assistance from departmental IT support.\nFor help, see the\nvendor's installation page\n.\nSPSS\nUser has selected a concurrent license during installation and is now prompted for a server address\nThe user should go back and select the standalone license option not the concurrent option.\nUser cannot write or execute programs\nThis generally happens when someone does not have administrative rights to their machine. The user should talk to their departmental support.\nFor help, see the\nvendor's installation page\n.\nStata\nUser cannot find the license file location\nThe ZIP file downloaded from IUware contains a\n.txt\nfile. This is the license file.\nFor help, see the\nvendor's installation page\n.	KB0026574	KB0026574 v7.0		0	Public	2026-01-31 16:39:29.013738	2026-01-31 16:40:06.02459	1	b1e30fec47e0a250d500b87b716d43ec
11764	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6d7848e047dcb254d500b87b716d4346	Migrate Denodo scheduler jobs at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nMigrate resources\nGet help\nOverview\nIn\nDenodo\nat Indiana University, developers can migrate their own Scheduler jobs from DEV to STG to PRD.\nSchedulers for all environments are accessed in the\nSolution Manager\n(for off-campus access, an\nIU VPN\nconnection is required).\nUse the "Environment" drop-down to select the appropriate environment, and then, on the "Scheduler" tile, select\nOpen\n.\nNote:\nThis document describes the process that migrates jobs from a Denodo Scheduler project, which is distinct from the process for Denodo objects (such as views, associations, services, and folders). There are two other migration processes for such virtual database (VDB) objects. For a description of the process that migrates a full VDB, see\nMigrate resources in Denodo at IU\n; for a description of the process that migrates selected views, folders, associations, and services in a VDB, see\nMigrate selected objects in Denodo at IU\n.\nMigrate resources\nPrepare to migrate:\nBefore migrating your jobs, prepare the selected jobs and data sources in the Scheduler project associated with your VDB. Only the selected jobs in this project will be migrated; the process will not migrate Scheduler data sources.\nTo migrate data sources, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\n. Under "Application", select\nDenodo\n, and under "Question Type", select\nRequest datasource migration\n. Include proof of\ndata manager approval\nfor all data sources that contain any enterprise data. Migrate any new or modified data sources before proceeding further.\nData sources that already exist in Staging and Production Scheduler projects and are unchanged in the Development Scheduler project do not need to be migrated again. If any required data sources are missing in Staging and Production Scheduler projects, any jobs that rely on those data sources will fail due to a missing dependency and be created in draft mode.\nTo migrate a job, you must create a materialized view in the Development environment in the VDB that corresponds to the Scheduler project. This view will list the jobs to be migrated. Optionally, you can schedule the migration to occur after a specific date and time and/or migrate to only the Staging Scheduler project.\nIf you need to delay the migration, first create a materialized view named\nscheduler_migration_timestamp\nin the source VDB (as described below). If you want the migration to occur at the next scheduled time, skip this step. Migrations are scheduled for the top of every hour. Your migration will occur, potentially along with others, at the first scheduled migration after the timestamp you have created. If you don't create a timestamp, the migration will occur at the next scheduled time.\nTo create the materialized view, execute the following commands in a VQL shell in the Development environment of the VDB that corresponds to the Scheduler project, substituting an appropriate value for the timestamp:\nCREATE OR REPLACE MATERIALIZED TABLE scheduler_migration_timestamp (migration_time:timestamp);\ninsert into scheduler_migration_timestamp (migration_time) values ('\n2025-10-19 12:30:00.000\n');\nIf you want to migrate to the Staging environment only, create a materialized view named\nscheduler_migration_options\nin Development environment of the VDB that corresponds to the Scheduler project (see the following VQL); if you want to migrate to both environments, skip this step.\nCREATE OR REPLACE MATERIALIZED TABLE scheduler_migration_options (migrate_to_production:boolean);\ninsert into scheduler_migration_options (migrate_to_production) values ('false');\nFor each object to be migrated, you need only to list the name of the job.\nIn the Development environment of the VDB that corresponds to the Scheduler project, use the example VQL below to create a materialized view. Name it\nscheduler_migration_candidates\n, and list the job(s) to be migrated.\nSubstitute the name of a valid job in your Scheduler project. Multiple rows can be inserted into the materialized table.\nCREATE OR REPLACE MATERIALIZED TABLE scheduler_migration_candidates (name:text);\nINSERT INTO scheduler_migration_candidates (name) values ('Denodo query log');\nA Scheduler job on the Production environment Scheduler will process the jobs to be migrated in the timeframe described above. If the Staging import fails, or the\nmigrate_to_production\ncolumn on the optional\nscheduler_migration_options\nmaterialized table is set to\nfalse\n, then the Production import is not performed.\nEmail reporting the status of the migration process and relevant errors, if any, is sent to all members of the Developer security group for the source VDB in the Development environment, except group accounts. If only group accounts are members of the Developer security group, the email is sent to the EDSD Denodo support queue.\nGet help\nIf you have questions, or if after reviewing the results of the migration a view needs to be recovered from backup, use the\nEDSD Support Form\nto create a support request.	KB0026810	KB0026810 v4.0	 of a valid <strong>job</strong> in your <strong>Scheduler</strong> project. Multiple rows can be inserted into the materialized table INTO scheduler_migration_candidates (name) values ('Denodo query log'); A <strong>Scheduler</strong> <strong>job</strong> on the Production, developers can migrate their own <strong>Scheduler</strong> jobs from DEV to STG to PRD. Schedulers for all environments	270.5861	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:17:22.848463	2026-01-31 17:17:22.848463	0	6d7848e047dcb254d500b87b716d4346
2458	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?sys_kb_id=1282538d475a9ed09286dfbd436d430c&id=kb_article_view&sysparm_rank=52&sysparm_tsqueryId=4bdaee914776f610d500b87b716d4332	Deprecated data connectors on Tableau Server	Table of contents\nOverview\nDeprecated data connectors\nDenodo\nGoogle Sheets\nData Connector 2.0\nCheck for deprecated data sources\nOverview\nData connectors are sometimes deprecated on Tableau Server. Deprecated connectors will not receive updates or maintenance, and eventually will be removed entirely. When a connector is deprecated, UITS recommends using the proposed replacement to avoid potential issues.\nDeprecated data connectors\nBelow is a list of data connections slated for retirement as of Tableau version 2024.2.\nDenodo\nThe native Denodo connector was deprecated in Tableau version 2023.1 and has been replaced by the\nDenodo JDBC driver\n.\nGoogle Sheets\nThe Google Sheets connector was deprecated in 2023 and has been replaced by\nGoogle Drive\n.\nData Connector 2.0\nData Connector 2.0 was deprecated in Tableau 2023.1. You will need to republish your Tableau reports with Web Data Connector 3.0, which was released in version 2022.3. For help, see:\nConnectors Built with the Web Data Connector 3.0 SDK\nTableau Web Data Connector\nCheck for deprecated data sources\nTo check if you're using a deprecated data source in Tableau:\nLog into Tableau Server and go to the\nExplore\npage.\nFrom the "Explore:" drop-down, select\nAll Data Sources\n.\nFrom the "Show As:" drop-down, select\nConnections\n.\nIn the "Connection type" column, look for any connections that have "(deprecated)" in the name.	KB0026499	KB0026499 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Deprecated data connectors Denodo Google Sheets Data Connector 2.0 Check for deprecated data sources Overview Data connectors are sometimes deprecated on Tableau Server. Deprecated connectors will not receive updates or maintenance, and eventually will be removed entirely. When a connector is deprecated, UITS recommend	0.9483		2026-01-31 14:36:02.341385	2026-01-31 14:36:02.341385	0	1282538d475a9ed09286dfbd436d430c
2452	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a3a433d6334baa9804e605570e5c7b67	Access to IU resources for official IU retirees	Table of contents\nOverview\nInformation for spouses of deceased retirees\nAccess to specific services\nAdobe Creative Cloud\nAT&T discount\nCanvas\nCrimsonPerks\nEmail\nEquipment\nGoogle at IU\nIT Training\nIUware and IUanyWare\nLibrary services\nMicrosoft 365\nSecure Share\nVPN\nZoom\nOverview\nTo be eligible for these services, you must be retired according to IU's criteria. For a list of the age and service requirements necessary to qualify as an official retiree, see\nRetiree Status and Benefits\n. To verify your status, contact your campus\nHuman Resources (HR)\nor\nAcademic Affairs\noffice.\nNote:\nIU retirees are now required to complete a short\nannual attestation form\nto continue using available IU resources. New IU retirees will need to complete the form one year after their separation date.  You'll receive email communication prior to the attestation due date; be sure to respond to maintain access.\nFor more, see\nIU account types and eligibility\n.\nInformation for spouses of deceased retirees\nNote:\nUpon learning of a retiree's death from Human Resources, UITS will deactivate the deceased's computer account, typically within seven days.\nThe spouse of a deceased faculty or staff member may request computing accounts if the faculty or staff member achieved official retiree status before death. To proceed, contact your spouse's department chair or human resources administrator. That person needs to\nrequest the creation of an Affiliate account\nfor you.\nWith appropriate authorization, UITS may be able to provide a deceased retiree's email messages to a spouse upon request. In accordance with university policy, UITS only downloads the email messages and will not sort or read them.\nAccess to specific services\nList of services and links\nAdobe Creative Cloud\nRetired emeriti faculty are eligible to use\nAdobe Creative Cloud\n. Retirees without emeriti status are ineligible.\nAT&T discount\nAbout AT&T discounts\nCanvas\nAbout Canvas at IU\nCrimsonPerks\nCrimsonPerks\nmay be available at each vendor's discretion.\nEmail\nYour email account will remain active after you retire. If you need to request your first email account after you retire, see\nCreate your first IU computing accounts (admitted students, faculty, staff, affiliates, and those returning to IU)\n.\nYou can access your email directly at\nOutlook on the web\n.\nFor more details about your IU email and other accounts, see\nIU account types and eligibility\n.\nEquipment\nSign in via\nIU Login\nto purchase equipment at a discount with Dell and Apple through each vendor's IU purchasing site; see\nComputerGuide: Deals by vendor, recommendations, and common questions\n. You can also receive any educational discounts that may be available on Apple products at the\ncampus bookstores\n; check with your local bookstore for details.\nGoogle at IU\nYou keep full access to your\nGoogle at IU account\n.\nIT Training\nYou can take advantage of many\nIT Training\nresources and services without fee, or at significant discounts compared to market prices.\nIUware and IUanyWare\nRetired faculty have access to IUware and IUanyWare, but retired staff do not.\nLibrary services\nIf you have questions about library services not addressed below, contact your local IU library.\nUse online databases, electronic journals, and electronic books:\nYou retain access to the resources provided by their local IU library. Use your\nUniversity account\nto log into library-owned computers, connect to electronic resources from off campus, and access many library services. The easiest way to access electronic subscription resources is via your campus library website; alternatively, to access them through the library catalog, log into\nIUCAT\n.\nSome professional schools provide access to special databases for faculty who have retired from that school; for details, contact your local library.\nCheck out books and other materials:\nLibrary accounts:\nLibrary accounts expire annually and must be renewed. If you receive a notice that your library account has expired, contact the circulation desk at your local library to confirm your contact information and renew your account.\nNote:\nAll library notices will be sent to your IU email address. You\nmust\ncheck this address regularly if you borrow library materials.\nRetired faculty and professional staff:\nYou may keep your library account and use your IU ID card to check out library materials. Renew materials through IUCAT or in person; all libraries except IU Kokomo also allow renewal of materials by phone or by mail.\nRetired clerical and service-maintenance staff:\nYou may keep your IU ID card and barcode number. On most campuses, when you retire, your library account will transfer to an Indiana resident account with a 30-day loan period. Renew materials through IUCAT or in person; all libraries except IU Kokomo also allow renewal of materials by phone or by mail. Alternatively, at IU Bloomington, Indiana resident account users can renew materials via the\nRenew Items Online form\n; at IU Indianapolis University Library, see\nRenewing Materials\n.\nALF requests and Request Delivery service:\nRetired faculty and staff may request materials from the Auxiliary Library Facility (\nALF\n) via IUCAT, and may use the Request Delivery service in IUCAT to request materials from libraries at other IU campuses.\nInterlibrary loan and document delivery:\nYou may request materials via interlibrary loan and document delivery services. Loan periods for interlibrary loan materials vary, and are usually shorter than those for materials borrowed from the IU Libraries. Renewals may or may not be available depending on the lending library's policies.\nFaculty studies at IU Bloomington:\nRetired IU Bloomington faculty members may request or renew a faculty study by filling out the form at\nWells Library Faculty Study Rooms\n. For more, see\nWells Library Faculty Study Guidelines\n.\nMicrosoft 365\nRetired emeriti faculty:\nEligible for\nMicrosoft 365\nA5 licenses\n, which include full use of\nMicrosoft 365\n, including installation of desktop versions of\nMicrosoft 365\nfor installation on personal computers.\nRetired faculty and staff:\nEligible for\nMicrosoft 365\nA1 licenses\n, which include a smaller\nOneDrive\nstorage allotment\nand use of online apps in\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n. This does not include access to desktop versions of\nMicrosoft 365\nfor installation on personal computers.\nSecure Share\nAbout Secure Share\nVPN\nAbout the IU VPN\nZoom\nGet started with Zoom				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:35:58.978185	2026-01-31 15:06:13.774423	1	a3a433d6334baa9804e605570e5c7b67
7743	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e5af6837476456583d373803846d432d	2017-18 Peebles Memorial Lecture in Information Technology	The 2017-18\nPeebles Memorial Lecture in Information Technology\nwas presented by Dr. Craig Stewart on April 5, 2018, in conjunction with the Research Services Expo in the Herman B Wells Library at Indiana University. Dr. Stewart, Executive Director of the IU\nPervasive Technology Institute (\nPTI\n)\npresented "Computing and Cyberinfrastructure in support of research, scholarship, and creative activity: forward in challenging times".\nAbstract:\nComputing resources provided and used in support of research, scholarship, and creative activities have been viewed as a common good since the early 1950s. At this time, Marshall Wrubel, astronomer and first director of the Research Computing Center at IU, established as a principle that IU's electronic computer was a resource available to the entire IU research community. Since that time, the use of computing resources - now termed "cyberinfrastructure" - has expanded to benefit all fields of scholarship, particularly humanities research, as well as the fine and performing arts. Since the late 1990s, IU has been a national leader in the use of cyberinfrastructure for accelerating innovation and expanding the capabilities of researchers.\nWe stand now at the beginning of what may be years of challenging times for research and higher education generally. Many current predictions hold that hundreds of small- to mid-size colleges and universities will become insolvent in coming years. In years to come, federal funding for research likely will remain constant or decrease - and certainly decrease relative to the perceived needs of the research community. At the same time, we face more diversity in the availability of computing architectures and uncertainty in processor roadmaps than we, as a national community, have faced in years. Locally sourced supercomputers, federally funded cyberinfrastructure resources, commercial cloud computing, uncertainty in processor roadmaps, the end of Moore's Law scaling, and competition from China all constitute a broad set of opportunities for the IU community and challenges to the global position of the US.\nBio:\nDr. Craig Stewart leads the\nPervasive Technology Institute (\nPTI\n)\n, IU's flagship initiative for advanced IT research, development, and delivery in support of research, scholarship, and artistic performance. Dr. Stewart has extensive experience leading and managing cyberinfrastructure services that support IU researchers. Dr. Stewart has served as director of the IU Stat/Math Center and the Indiana Genomics Initiative IT Core, and as the Associate Dean and Assistant Vice President of the Research Technologies division of UITS.\nWatch Dr. Stewart's lecture (via YouTube)\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:57:56.08646	2026-01-31 16:40:20.367008	1	e5af6837476456583d373803846d432d
10274	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=45cf2cb7476456583d373803846d4323	2012-13 Peebles Memorial Lecture in Information Technology	The 2012-13\nPeebles Memorial Lecture in Information Technology\nwas presented by Dr. Paul Messina on April 25, 2013, in Presidents Hall (inside Franklin Hall) at Indiana University. Dr. Messina, Director of Science at the Argonne Leadership Computing Facility (ALCF), presented "Scientific computing - will the lessons learned apply to the future?"\nAbstract:\nScientific computing has advanced dramatically during the last four decades, despite several upheavals in computer architectures. The evolution of high-end computers in the next decade will again pose challenges as well as opportunities. This talk examined the factors that enabled the advances and speculated about how the lessons learned might be used in grappling with the coming changes.\nBio:\nDr. Messina has been Director of Science at the ALCF since 2008. From 2002 to 2004, Dr. Messina served as Distinguished Senior Computer Scientist at Argonne National Laboratory (ANL) and as Advisor to the Director General at the European Organization for Nuclear Research CERN (European Organization for Nuclear Research). Previously, Dr. Messina served as founding Director of California Institute of Technology's Center for Advanced Computing Research (CACR), and as Assistant Vice President for Scientific Computing and Faculty Associate for Scientific Computing at Caltech. He led the Computational and Computer Science component of Caltech's research project funded by the Academic Strategic Alliances Program of the Accelerated Strategic Computing Initiative. While at Caltech, he also served as co-principal investigator for the National Virtual Observatory and TeraGrid, as chief architect for the National Partnership for Advanced Computational Infrastructure (NPACI), as principal investigator for the Scalable I/O Initiative, as principal investigator for the CASA gigabit network testbed, and as director of the Concurrent Supercomputing Consortium. During a leave from Caltech in 1999 and 2000, he led the DOE-NNSA Accelerated Strategic Computing Initiative. In his first association with Argonne from 1973 to 1987, he held a number of positions in the Applied Mathematics Division and was the founding director of the Mathematics and Computer Science Division.\nWatch Dr. Messina's lecture (via YouTube)\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 16:39:32.998672	2026-01-31 16:40:23.824775	1	45cf2cb7476456583d373803846d4323
10280	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=92af2c37476456583d373803846d4350	Modify your shell script to avoid errors with interactive commands when running a batch job	Batch queuing systems, such as\nSlurm\n, use certain shell scripts (for example,\n.profile\n,\n.cshrc\n, or\n.login\n) to initialize\nbatch\njobs\n. When a job is set to run in batch mode, any interactive commands (or commands that require a terminal, such as\nstty\n) in those shell scripts will fail and produce error messages.\nTo avoid such errors, you can modify your shell script with an\nif\nstatement that skips specific commands whenever a batch queuing system is in use. The script you modify and the syntax of the particular\nif\nstatement you create will depend on the\nshell\nand computer you are using.\nFor example, if you are using the\ncsh\nshell on a system that uses Slurm for job submission, insert the following\nif\nstatement into your\n.login\nfile:\nif ( ! "$SLURM_JOB_ID" ) then <stty or other interactive commands> endif\nThe above\nif\nstatement checks for the $SLURM_JOB_ID environment variable; if the variable isn't defined, then\nstty\n(and any other commands you specify) will execute.	KB0022664	KB0022664 v1.0	Batch queuing systems, such as <strong>Slurm</strong>, use certain shell scripts (for example, .profile, .cshrc, or .login) to initialize batch jobs. When a job is set to run in batch mode, any interactive commands. For example, if you are using the csh shell on a system that uses <strong>Slurm</strong> for job submission, insert	265.2459		2026-01-31 16:40:27.246624	2026-01-31 16:40:27.246624	0	92af2c37476456583d373803846d4350
11704	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f905478c4798fed02fc6a55b416d43a8	Known issues with Exchange Online	Important:\nIU's\nExchange Online\nenvironment does not support\nIMAP\n. For email configuration instructions, including details for those using Linux and Thunderbird, see\nConfigure your email client for Exchange Online\n.\nThe following is continually updated information about the status of known issues with\nExchange Online\nat Indiana University.\nNote:\nIf you are not prompted to authenticate with\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n, and you see an error that says "We're sorry, access is not allowed because you are not enrolled. Please contact your organization's IT help desk for assistance", you'll need to enroll a device in Duo before you can proceed. For more, see\nGet started with Two-Step Login (Duo) at IU\n.\nMajor issues\nIssue\nDescription\nSolution\nStatus\nLast updated\nMeeting invites may be re-sent without the sender's or organizer's knowledge.\nThe Calendar app for iOS or iPadOS may rarely send updates to meetings on additional calendars that the client has access to.\nUse the Microsoft Outlook app for iOS or iPadOS, which are not affected by this issue.\nKnown issue\nSeptember 25, 2025\nSome messages from a FASA-accessed mailbox may fail to send.\nReplies and forwards from a FASA-accessed mailbox may generate an error: "This message could not be sent. You do not have the permission to send the message on behalf of the specified user".\nEnsure that the\nfrom\nfield of the outgoing reply/forward is set to the primary email address of the mailbox you're sending from, regardless of the email address that was initially used to contact the mailbox.\nKnown Issue\nSeptember 10, 2024\nDelegates may be unable to update calendar appointments created in the native Calendar app for iOS or iPadOS.\nDelegates may find they are unable to make changes to appointments that were originally created in the Calendar app for iOS or iPadOS. No errors are displayed, but any changes made are not actually saved.\nCreate appointments in the Microsoft\nOutlook\napp for iOS or iPadOS, which are not affected by this issue.\nKnown issue\nDecember 20, 2022\nExchange Online\ncalendars may become out of sync after initial Modern Authentication connection attempt.\nSome clients are unable to connect to\nExchange Online\nin the current device profile. In email, there will be a blocked account notification, but the calendar application does not give warning independent of the mail application.\nRemove the email account profile and re-add the account.\nKnown issue\nJune 24, 2022\nDifficulties connecting to\nExchange Online\nvia Modern Authentication on initial attempt\nSome clients may receive the message "Your email access has been blocked" on initial connection to\nExchange Online\nvia Modern Authentication.\nRemove the email account profile and re-add the account.\nKnown issue\nJune 3, 2022\nSome Windows\nOutlook\nclients cannot connect to\nExchange Online.\nOutlook\n2007, 2010, and newer versions last updated in 2017 or earlier will be unable to connect to\nExchange Online\n.\nUpdate to a supported version of\nOutlook\n.\nKnown issue\nNovember 3, 2021\nAfter migration, forwarding rules from IU Health may not work.\nAfter being migrated to\nExchange Online\n, IU Health users may have difficulties with rules that forward their mail to IU.\nConnect to your IU Health email account, re-create the rule, and re-select the contact record for your IU account.\nKnown issue\nFebruary 23, 2021\nOn-premises shared resources not accessible\nOn-premises shared resources, such as mailboxes, calendars, and public folders, are not accessible once an account has been migrated to\nExchange Online\n.\nAn on-premises account is required to access on-premises resources. A new on-premises account (for example, a group account) will need to be created if an existing on-premises account with permissions to access the needed resource is not available. Also, UITS recommends replacing public folders with resource rooms; for more, see\nAbout public folders and Exchange Online\n.\nKnown issue\nFebruary 23, 2021\nGeneral slowness when sending, receiving, or searching emails\nSending, receiving, or searching emails 24-48 hours after a migration is likely to be slow due to the reindexing of mailboxes.\nThis issue should resolve itself 24-48 hours after a migration. If it has been more than two days and the issue persists, contact your local UITS support person or the Support Center.\nKnown issue\nFebruary 5, 2021\nAfter migrated, shared calendars in\nOutlook\nmay stop updating\nAfter migration, shared calendars that have been added to\nOutlook\nmay still appear, but they will stop updating due to the authentication to them no longer being valid.\nRemove and re-add shared calendars to update authentication to them.\nKnown issue\nFebruary 4, 2021\nInability to successfully authenticate using mail clients (such as\nOutlook\n)\nThere are repeated prompts for credentials, and you are unable to connect using mail clients, but you are able to log into\nOutlook on the web\n.\nEnsure that you passphrase does not end with a space or other escape type characters (for example,\n/\n,\n?\n,\n%\n, and others)\nKnown issue\nFebruary 4, 2021\nTo Do items, as well as pinned and recent items, may not be preserved.\nContent in Microsoft To Do and pinned/recent items may not transfer when you are migrated to\nExchange Online\n.\nUnfortunately, you'll need to recreate these.\nKnown issue\nNovember 25, 2020\nLicense errors when accessing group accounts\nWhen accessing a group account, a license error can occur if the mailbox type was not updated after migration.\nContact the\nSupport Center\nso they can work with the\nExchange\nadministrators to resolve the issue.\nCan be resolved with an account update\nNovember 9, 2020\nExchange\ncall trees no longer work in\nExchange Online\n(feature has been removed)\nAdvanced call forwarding and call routing rules available in\nExchange\non prem do not currently exist in\nExchange Online\n.\nCreate a new on-prem\nExchange\naccount\nto use until a solution can be determined.\nKnown issue\nOctober 19, 2020\nExchange Online\nhas lower limit for quotas on group accounts\nGroup accounts that exceed 50 GB of storage will not be able to receive or send email.\nArchive email (to secondary account,\n.pst\n, etc.) to stay under the 50 GB limit.\nKnown issue\nOctober 19, 2020\nMeeting touch panels using resources migrated to EXO in the ICTC and CIB are generating an error\nTouch panels located in the CIB and ICTC area cannot be used for scheduling walk-up meetings.\nBeing investigated by administrators. Will need to update the touch panels to point to\nExchange Online\nenvironment.\nKnown issue\nOctober 19, 2020\nFASA rights do not work with some mailboxes in\nExchange Online\nFull Access/Send As (FASA) rights do not work with\nExchange Online\nunless the AD group with FASA rights was mail-enabled before the mailbox migrated from on-premises\nExchange\nto\nExchange Online\n.\nMail-enable the AD group, remove FASA permissions from the group, and then re-add FASA permissions. You may have to wait for this change to propagate to\nExchange Online\n.\nKnown issue\nOctober 7, 2020\nSome email clients cannot authenticate to\nExchange Online\nSome email clients (for example, Thunderbird) do not support the authentication method used in\nExchange Online\n.\nUse an email client that supports modern authentication protocols.\nKnown issue\nJuly 17, 2020\nUnable to send encrypted messages from on-premises to\nExchange Online\nand vice versa\nExchange Online\nuses a different Global Address List (GAL) from the on-premises environment, so digital certificates that are used to encrypt messages are unable to communicate with the other environment's GAL.\nThis issue will be resolved when on-premises users are migrated to\nExchange Online\n.\nKnown issue\nJuly 6, 2020\nLimited calendar sharing permissions between\nExchange Online\nand\nExchange\non-premises users\nAfter someone is migrated to\nExchange Online\n, the highest permission level that an on-premises\nExchange\nuser can grant the\nExchange Online\nuser is the ability to view free/busy status. This limitation does not exist in the other direction (an\nExchange Online\nuser should be able to grant any permissions desired to an on-premises user).\nThe issue will be resolved when on-premises users are migrated to\nExchange Online\n.\nKnown issue\nMay 28, 2020\nMac Outlook clients show limited resource room details\nOutlook for Mac cannot access full details about resource rooms on premises (for example, free/busy information).\nUse a Windows version of Outlook, or request that the resource room be migrated to\nExchange Online\n.\nKnown issue\nMarch 18, 2020\nOutlook cached contacts\nAfter someone is migrated to\nExchange Online\n, other people that have emailed them previously may have issues sending messages to the new mailbox because of a cached contact in their Outlook profile.\nTo clear a cached contact, start typing the name in the "To:" line of an email message. You'll see an\nx\nto the right of the contact name when it pops up. Click the\nx\n, and then enter the contact name into the "To:" line again.\nKnown issue\nDecember 20, 2019\nShared calendar security prompt\nAfter someone is migrated to\nExchange Online\n, people who have not yet migrated may receive a password prompt when attempting to view the migrated user's shared calendar in Outlook.\nWhen trying to view a shared calendar, authenticate as\nusername\n@iu.edu\n(not your campus-specific email address). If the prompt continues to appear after a successful authentication, follow\nthese Microsoft instructions\nto force a Modern Authentication session.\nWarning:\nThis contains instructions for editing the\nregistry\n. If you make any error while editing the registry, you can potentially cause Windows to fail or be unable to boot, requiring you to reinstall Windows. Edit the registry at your own risk. Always back up the registry before making any changes. If you do not feel comfortable editing the registry, do not attempt these instructions. Instead, seek the help of a computing support provider.\nKnown issue\nDecember 20, 2019\nMinor issues\nIssue\nDescription\nSolution\nStatus\nLast updated\nDue to security reasons, some domains may be blocked from sending to or receiving mail from IU constituents\nDomains currently blocked:\nshaw.ca\nExternal collaborators will need to provide an alternative email address for communication.\nKnown issue\nAugust 9, 2024\nUnable to make changes to distribution groups (including mail-enabled security groups) using\nOutlook on the web\n(outlook.com) or the\nOutlook\ndesktop application\nIn Outlook on the web, you will receive an error which reads in part "The action <\naction name\n> can't be performed on the object <\ngroup name\n> because the object is being synchronized from your on-premises organization" will occur. You will receive a similar but less specific error in the desktop application.\nLocal UITS support people can use Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC) to make the change.\nKnown issue\nMarch 11, 2021\nAfter migration, some email messages bounce\nAfter being migrated, some email messages bounce with the following error message:\nRemote Server returned '550 5.6.2 SMTPSEND.BareLinefeedsAreIllegal; message contains bare linefeeds, which cannot be sent via DATA'"\nThis occurs when a bare line feed character is present in an email message and the destination server does not support SMTP chunking/SMTP BDAT, as defined in\nRFC 3030\n. Some mail clients, such as default mail clients for Android, may implement bare line feeds.\nSwitch to a mail client that does not use bare line feeds, such as\nOutlook\n. If an email chain includes bare line feeds, create a new email chain from a client that does not implement bare line feeds.\nKnown issue\nFebruary 9, 2021\nOutlook\nmay show a "We can't show MailTips right now" message with certain email addresses\nThis message can appear if an address that does not correspond to an\nExchange Online\nmailbox enabled user is entered. For more details, see\nWe can't show MailTips right now warning in Outlook in Microsoft 365\nThis behavior is by design.\nKnown issue\nFebruary 4, 2021\nAfter migration, a "Something went wrong" message may appear when trying to access\nOutlook on the web\nA "Something went wrong" message can appear after migration due to cache issues in the web browser.\nClear the cache and cookies in your web browser (see\nClear your web browser's cache, cookies, and history\n).\nKnown issue\nFebruary 4, 2021\nUnable to access voice mail from a physical phone\nAt this time there isn't a way to access voice mail from physical phones after you're moved to\nExchange Online\n.\nYou can access your voice mail via\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n.\nKnown issue\nJanuary 7, 2021	KB0023780	KB0023780 v3.0	The following is continually updated information about the status of known issues with Exchange Online at Indiana University. Major issues Issue Description Solution Status Last updated Meeting invites may be re-sent without the sender's or organizer's knowledge. The Calendar app for iOS or iPadOS may rarely send updates to meetings on additiona	4.4727	km-aud-iu	2026-01-31 17:15:54.756269	2026-01-31 17:15:54.756269	0	f905478c4798fed02fc6a55b416d43a8
10281	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=8d40f4b347a456583d373803846d4371	About batch jobs	A batch job is a computer program or set of programs processed in batch mode. This means that a sequence of commands to be executed by the operating system is listed in a file (often called a batch file, command file, job script, or\nshell\nscript) and submitted for execution as a single unit. The opposite of a batch job is interactive processing, in which a user enters individual commands to be processed immediately.\nIn many cases, batch jobs accumulate during working hours, and are then executed during the evening or another time the computer is idle. This is often the best way to run programs that place heavy demands on the computer.\nOn high performance compute clusters, users typically submit batch jobs to pre-defined groups of compute nodes (called queues or partitions) that are managed by a resource management application. Some clusters employ a separate job scheduler to dispatch batch jobs based on the availability of compute resources, job requirements specified by users, and usage policies set by cluster administrators.\nThe Indiana University\nresearch supercomputers\nuse\nSlurm\nto coordinate resource management and job scheduling.	KB0026023	KB0026023 v1.0	, and usage policies set by cluster administrators. The Indiana University research supercomputers use <strong>Slurm</strong>	6.4406		2026-01-31 16:40:27.246624	2026-01-31 16:40:33.289792	1	8d40f4b347a456583d373803846d4371
11769	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=04edc2184776f6d0d500b87b716d437d	One-time and recurring batch jobs in Kubernetes	Table of contents\nOverview\nBatch/short-lived jobs in Kubernetes\nMaintain batch processes\nJobs\nCronjobs\nSuspend Cronjobs\nHelm-k8s-boot values\nMonitoring\nCustomize jobs/Cronjobs\nRetention\nOverview\nFollowing is information on scheduling, executing, and cleaning up short-lived batch processes in Kubernetes at Indiana University.\nBatch/short-lived jobs in Kubernetes\nIn Kubernetes, short-lived tasks can be executed with\nJobs\n. A Job will create pod(s) until a specified number exit with a\n0\nexit code. By default, it will succeed after the first pod exits successfully. It will then cease creating pods. If the pod exits with a non-zero exit code, the\nJob will try again\nin a manner specified by the resource.\nJobs can be scheduled on a repeating basis by using\nCronjobs\n. As the name implies, a Cronjob creates Jobs on a cron-based schedule.\nMaintain batch processes\nJobs\nAs with other Kubernetes resources, a Job is created from a\nJob spec\n. In general, a Job\n.spec.template\nis going to be similar to any other Pod template you may have already written. If no additional fields beyond the pod specification are supplied, the job will start one pod and will be marked as complete as soon as its pod terminates successfully. Jobs can be created by applying the Job spec:\nkubectl apply -f <job spec YAML file>\nBy default, when a job is completed, the Pod(s) created will not be deleted so you can review the logs (you can also view logs in\nLogInsight\nafter the retention for the pod's logs has expired). Deleting a job will clean up the associated Pod(s). You can delete both complete or incomplete Jobs with the command\nkubectl delete -f <job spec YAML file>\nor\nkubectl delete jobs/<job name>\n.\nCronjobs\nA Kubernetes\nCronjob\nresource creates Jobs on a specified cron schedule. One caveat to scheduling Cronjobs in the Enterprise Kubernetes Environment is that the control plane uses the GMT\ntime zone\n, so all Cronjob resources should be scheduled with that offset in mind. Future versions of Kubernetes may let you specify time zones for a Cronjob, but this feature is not currently enabled. Cronjobs can be manually triggered by creating a Job from the Cronjob on the command line with the following command:\nkubectl create job --from=cronjob/<name of cronjob> <name of job>\nSuspend Cronjobs\nYou can easily\nsuspend Cronjobs\nso they stop running on the specified schedule but remain installed. To suspend a Cronjob without applying a new file, use the following command:\nkubectl patch cronjobs <job-name> -p '{"spec" : {"suspend" : true }}'\nHelm-k8s-boot values\nIf you are developing a Spring Boot project using Launchpad or the\nhelm-k8s-boot chart\ndirectly, you can easily create Jobs and Cronjobs from your existing codebase and deployment values as\ndocumented on the helm-k8s-boot project\n.\nMonitoring\nThe UITS Enterprise Platform team currently provides monitoring services for Jobs at a namespace level. If any jobs are incomplete for more than one hour, your organization will be alerted. To enable job monitoring on your namespace, fill out the\nEP support form\n; indicate that your area of interest is Kubernetes and provide a brief summary of your monitoring needs.\nCustomize jobs/Cronjobs\nRetention\nSince a Kubernetes Job does not remove itself when completed (\nas previously described\n), you may want to set a\nTTL\nfor finished jobs.\nFor Cronjobs, you can set\nJobs history limits\n. By default, a Cronjob will keep three successful Jobs and one failed Job.	KB0027149	KB0027149 v4.0	 can be executed with Jobs. A <strong>Job</strong> will create pod(s) until a specified number exit with a 0 exit code. If the pod exits with a non-zero exit code, the <strong>Job</strong> will try again in a manner specified resources, a <strong>Job</strong> is created from a <strong>Job</strong> spec. In general, a <strong>Job</strong> .spec.template is going to be similar	172.5082	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:17:27.269216	2026-01-31 17:17:27.269216	0	04edc2184776f6d0d500b87b716d437d
9921	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=4ccfa4b7476456583d373803846d4378	Research Desktop (RED) usage policies and interface features	Table of contents\nUsage policies\nFair share policy\nConnection policies\nMonthly maintenance window\nFeatures\nDesktop icons\nPop-up menu\nMenu bar\nAccess your\nSDA\nor\nSlate\nstorage\nAppearance customization\nAnalyze memory and CPU consumption\nSupported languages\nGet help\nUsage policies\nThe instructions here are for using\nResearch Desktop\n(\nRED\n) once you are already connected.\nFair share policy\nRED\nnodes are login nodes. You should run parallel or compute-intensive applications on\nQuartz\ncompute nodes.\nEveryone who logs into\nRED\nshares dedicated nodes that are part of the\nQuartz\nresearch supercomputer\n. Because high loads on shared nodes can negatively impact system usability, be aware of how your processes use the system; see\nAnalyzing memory and CPU consumption\nbelow.\nIU uses a fair share policy that throttles processes and slows down applications for users who are consuming a high percentage of CPU resources; running highly parallel applications can lead to high CPU consumption. Although running such an application for a few minutes for testing purposes is fine, running it for hours can negatively impact the usability of\nRED\nfor everyone on the node.\nIf you are going to use highly parallel applications on\nRED\n, do one of the following:\nLimit the parallelism the applications use to 5 or fewer.\nRun the applications via a normal batch job. You can do this in various ways, including an interactive batch job that allows for graphical output:\nsrun -p interactive -A slurm-account-name --x11 ...\nOnly run such applications for short periods of time.\nUsers who don't follow these policies and fail to respond to requests to change how they use the system may have their jobs halted and lose their login privileges.\nIn addition, each user is limited to 100 GB of memory usage within the entire session. If user sessions reach 100 GB of memory consumption, one or more of their processes will be terminated to reduce usage.\nConnection policies\nThinLinc Client lets you disconnect your\nRED\nsession without terminating it; this keeps your session and any open applications running, allowing work to proceed while you are disconnected.\nNote:\nDisconnecting is not the same as logging out, which closes open applications, terminates your session on the server, and closes the remote desktop window.\nDisconnected sessions are terminated after seven days of inactivity.\nOnly one\nRED\nsession is allowed per user. When you connect, if you have no disconnected sessions running, ThinLinc Client automatically creates a new session. If you do have an existing session, you are automatically reconnected to that session. However, if you want a new session rather than your existing one, select\nEnd existing session\nbefore connecting; the existing session will be terminated and a new one created.\nMonthly maintenance window\nThe scheduled monthly maintenance window for IU's high performance computing systems is the second Sunday of each month, 7am-7pm.\nIU recommends saving your work and logging out before 7am on maintenance days. All sessions still open at 7am on maintenance day will be terminated.\nFeatures\nDesktop icons\nWhen you connect to\nRED\n, the following icons appear by default on the remote desktop:\nDisconnect ThinLinc Session\n: Double-click to close\nRED\nwithout terminating your remote session. This allows you to reconnect to the same session at a later time.\nLog Out\n: Double-click to close\nRED\nand\nterminate your remote session. (For more, see the\nLogging out versus disconnecting your session\nsection of\nDownload, install, and configure ThinLinc Client to use Research Desktop (RED) at IU\n.)\nTrash\n: Delete items by dragging and dropping them to\nTrash\n.\nNote:\nItems placed in\nTrash\nwill continue to count toward your research computing storage quota until you permanently delete them. To view items in\nTrash\n, double-click\nTrash\n. To permanently delete all items, select\nEmpty Trash\n, and then select\nEmpty Trash\nagain to confirm; to delete an individual item, right-click (or\nCtrl\n-click) the item, select\nDelete Permanently\n, and then select\nDelete\nto confirm.\nHome\n: Double-click to access your\nQuartz\nhome directory. (When you're logged in, the\nHome\nicon's label will include your\nIU username\n.)\nBR200 Login\n: Double-click to access options for logging into your\nBig Red 200\naccount.\nInteractive Job\n: Double-click to open an interactive session on one of the compute nodes on\nQuartz\n. This opens a Terminal window and submits an interactive job request for four hours of wall time using eight cores and 32 GB of a single compute node. These jobs normally start within two or three minutes. When your job is ready, you will be placed at the compute node's command prompt.\nNote:\nThe\nInteractive Job\nshortcut is intended as a convenient way to log into a\nQuartz\ncompute node. If you need more time, cores, or memory, use\nsrun\nin a Terminal window to request the resources that you need. For more about submitting interactive jobs, see\nUse Slurm to submit and manage jobs on IU's research computing systems\n.\nIf your job does not start after two or three minutes, or if you need help using this tool,\nuse this form to contact the\nRED\ndevelopment team directly\n.\nSlate Scratch\n: Double-click to access files in your\nSlate\n-Scratch\nspace.\nthindrives\n: Double-click to access drives and directories on (or connected to) your personal computer.\nNote:\nTo enable\nthindrives\naccess to drives and directories on your personal computer, before connecting to a new session you must\nconfigure the ThinLinc Client's "Export Local Resources" option\n.\nRED Documentation\n: Double-click to access\nRED\nsupport documentation.\nFeedback and Questions\n: Double-click to open a window that will let you send feedback and questions to the\nResearch Desktop\nteam.\nTerminal\n: Double-click to open a Terminal window that logs you in automatically on the same node that is serving your\nRED\nsession.\nPop-up menu\nThe\nRED\npop-up menu lets you change your screen size settings, toggle full-screen view on and off, and access other connection settings during your\nRED\nsession:\nTo access the pop-up menu, press\nF8\n. (Depending on your local system, you may need to press\nFn\n+\nF8\nor\nWin-F8\n.)\nTo map the pop-up menu to another key:\nBefore opening a session:\nIn the ThinLinc Client login window, select\nOptions\nto open the "ThinLinc Client Options" window. On the\nOptions\ntab, under "Session Options", make sure\nSend system keys\nis selected, use the\nPopup menu key\nselector to assign another key, and then select\nOK\nto return to the login window.\nDuring your session:\nOpen the pop-up menu, select\nOptions\n, and then, on the\nOptions\ntab, make sure\nSend system keys\nis selected. Use the\nPopup menu key\nselector to assign another key, and then select\nOK\n.\nTo disable the pop-up menu, do the same as above, but set the\nPopup menu key\nselector to\nNone\n.\nMenu bar\nAt the top of the screen, you can use the\nRED\nmenu bar to launch installed applications, access local and remote resources, and set personal preferences.\nSelect\nApplications\nto browse and run installed software in several categories:\nAnalytics:\nJupyter Notebook, Mathematica, MATLAB, RStudio, SAS, SAS Setup, SPSS, Stata\nCoding and Editing:\nArm Forge, Emacs, Geany, gedit, Pluma, Spyder, VS Code\nCompute jobs:\nInteractive job,\nQuartz\nJob Manager, Slurm Info\nStorage:\nGlobus,\nScholarly Data Archive\n(FileZilla),\nSDA\non Globus\nUtilities:\nFile Browser, FileZilla, Firefox, MATE System Monitor, MATE Terminal, Spreadsheets, Take Screenshot, VLC Media Player\nVisualization:\nChimera, DSI Studio, Fiji, FSLeyes, Integrative Genomics Viewer, Metashape, ParaView, QGIS, VMD\nIU HPC Slack Channel:\nOpens a Firefox browser tab to the IU HPC Slack Channel where the IU HPC user community can chat and communicate with one another and Research Technologies staff.\nSelect\nPlaces\nto access local and remote folders, browse bookmarked and local network locations, search for files, or connect to remote computers or shared disks.\nSelect\nSystem\nto access the\nPreferences\nmenu, which includes several options for changing the appearance and behavior of your desktop.\nSelect the\nCaja\nicon (\n) to access your\nQuartz\nhome directory and browse the file system.\nSelect the\nTerminal\nicon (\n) to open a Terminal window that logs you in automatically on the same node that is serving your\nRED\nsession.\nSelect the\nFirefox\nicon (\n) to open the Firefox web browser.\nTo customize your desktop, right-click (Windows) or\nCtrl\n-click (Mac) on any part of the desktop or menu bar.\nNote:\nSome keyboard shortcuts used in the\nRED\ninterface may differ slightly from the ones on your local computer and may vary from application to application. For example, to copy text, some applications use\nShift\n+\nCtrl\n+\nc\n, while others use\nCtrl\n+\nc\n. Check the application's\nEdit\nmenu to see which shortcuts it uses.\nYou can also change your\nRED\nsession preferences, such as the keyboard layout or keyboard shortcuts. For example, to change the keyboard shortcuts, inside your\nRED\nsession:\nFrom the\nRED\nmenu bar, select\nSystem\n>\nPreferences\n>\nHardware\n>\nKeyboard Shortcuts\n.\nIn the "Keyboard Shortcuts" window, change your customized keyboard shortcuts.\nAccess your\nSDA\nor\nSlate\nstorage\nIf you have an account on the\nScholarly Data Archive\n(\nSDA\n) or on\nSlate\n, you can access it from\nRED\n:\nSDA\n:\nFrom the\nRED\nmenu bar, select\nApplications\n>\nStorage\n, and then choose how you want to connect:\nSelect\nScholarly Data Archive (Filezilla)\nto open a virtual FileZilla window; when prompted, enter your IU passphrase to connect to your\nSDA\naccount.\nNote:\nFor SFTP connections to the\nSDA\n,\nRED\nuses a managed version of FileZilla that is safe to use; later versions of FileZilla have been identified as malware. If the FileZilla "Check for updates" window opens, select\nClose\nto close it and continue with your\nSDA\nsession.\nSelect\nSDA on Globus\nto log into your\nSDA\naccount via the\nIU Globus Web App\n.\nSlate\n:\nOn the\nRED\ndesktop, double-click your\nHome\nicon, and then:\nIn the file browser window, above the right pane, to the left of "Location", select the\nToggle between button and text-based location bar\nicon to switch to the text-based location bar. The "Location" field should display your current location in the file system as\n/N/u/username/Quartz\n(where\nusername\nis your\nIU username\n).\nIn the "Location" text field, select and delete the displayed path, and then enter the path to your\nSlate\naccount; for example (replace\nusername\nwith your\nIU username\n):\n/N/slate/username\nPress\nEnter\nor\nReturn\n.\nOptionally, you can bookmark your\nSlate\naccount. In the file browser menu, select\nBookmarks\n, and then select\nAdd Bookmark\n. A persistent link to your\nSlate\naccount will be added to the\nPlaces\nlist (under "Bookmarks") on the left side of the file browser.\nAppearance customization\nTo customize the appearance of the\nRED\ninterface, use the\nAppearance Preferences\nutility:\nFrom the\nRED\nmenu bar, select\nSystem\n>\nPreferences\n>\nLook and Feel\n>\nAppearance\n.\nIn the "Appearance Preferences" window, use the\nTheme\n,\nBackground\n,\nFonts\n, and\nInterface\ntabs to configure a personalized look and feel for the\nRED\ninterface.\nNote:\nThe available pre-set window and folder themes can be modified by selecting\nCustomize\n, allowing you to specify your own color scheme, window appearance, icon appearance, icon style, and more.\nAnalyze memory and CPU consumption\nTo view current system status data, use the System Monitor utility:\nFrom the\nRED\nmenu bar, select\nApplications\n>\nUtilities\n>\nMATE System Monitor\n.\nTo view memory and CPU consumption for your processes and applications, select the\nProcesses\ntab. By default, only your processes are displayed; to see processes besides your own, in the top right, select the menu icon (next to the search icon), and then select\nActive Processes\nor\nAll Processes\n:\nTo add or remove information fields, in System Monitor menu, select\nEdit\n>\nPreferences\n, and then use the checkboxes to select the fields to display.\nSupported languages\nThinLinc Client supports the following languages:\nBrazilian Portuguese (\npt_BR\n)\nEnglish (\nen_US\n)\nDutch (\nnl_NL\n)\nFrench (\nfr_FR\n)\nGerman (\nde_DE\n)\nItalian (\nit_IT\n)\nRussian (\nru_RU\n)\nSpanish (\nes_ES\n)\nSwedish (\nsv_SE\n)\nTurkish (\ntr_TR\n)\nNote:\nIf your computer's operating system is set to an unsupported language, ThinLinc Client may not function properly.\nTo change your keyboard layout, inside your\nRED\nsession:\nFrom the\nRED\nmenu bar, select\nSystem\n>\nPreferences\n>\nHardware\n>\nKeyboard\n.\nIn the "Keyboard Preferences" window, on the\nLayouts\ntab, select\nAdd\n.\nIn the "Choose a Layout" window, on the\nBy country\nor\nBy language\ntab, use the drop-down lists to select a keyboard variant by country or language, and then select\nAdd\n.\nGet help\nIf you are using\nResearch Desktop\n(\nRED\n) and need help or have questions,\nuse this form to contact the\nResearch Desktop\ndevelopment team directly\n.	KB0023170	KB0023170 v4.0		0	Public	2026-01-31 15:54:10.766619	2026-01-31 16:28:25.000996	1	4ccfa4b7476456583d373803846d4378
1528	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0025370	About Posit Connect at IU	Posit Connect\nis a publishing platform for deploying interactive applications written in R or Python over the web.\nPosit Connect\noffers several advantages over more commercial offerings such as shinyapps.io. These include integration with IU's identity management, allowing you to publish to specific IU users or groups, and more generous data storage and computation limits. Additionally, many of the premium features of commercial offerings (for example, content URLs) are provided with this service.\nIU's\nPosit Connect\nservice is hosted at\nconnect.posit.iu.edu\n.\nCurrently, the service is open for use cases in support of IU's research and teaching mission. To request access, go to\nRT Projects\nand create a project with a\nPosit Connect\nservice allocation. For more on\nRT Projects\n, see\nAbout RT Projects at IU\n.\nThe limit on application size is 100 MB per application.\nAn additional 100 GB of storage space is allocated per department for data supporting applications. This storage is available via SFTP at\nsftp.rstudio.iu.edu\nand is managed by each individual department.\nIU's\nPosit Connect\nservice does not support data classified by the university as\nCritical or Restricted data\n.\nThe system is not backed up. Users are responsible for backing up their applications and data.\nYou can publish from any device that can connect to\nconnect.posit.iu.edu\n.\nResearch Technologies\nstaff provide official support and troubleshooting for publishing from\nResearch Desktop\n(\nRED\n).\nIf you are using\nResearch Desktop\n(\nRED\n) and need help or have questions,\nuse this form to contact the Research Desktop development team directly\n.	KB0025370	KB0025370 v2.0	 from any device that can connect to connect.posit.iu.edu . <strong>Research</strong> Technologies staff provide official support and troubleshooting for publishing from <strong>Research</strong> <strong>Desktop</strong> (RED). for use cases in support of IU's <strong>research</strong> and teaching mission. To request access, go to RT Projects	137.3715		2026-01-31 14:33:03.11173	2026-01-31 16:40:39.136188	0	dc8f129047b39a54d500b87b716d4333
10282	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5ccf68b7476456583d373803846d4381	Research Technologies chargeback services policy	Note:\nChanges for the rates and chargeback policies are periodically reviewed and updated. Check back frequently to make sure you have the latest information.\nWhenever a\nResearch Technologies\nsubunit is engaged in work for which a customer is charged (chargeback work), the following policies and guidelines should be followed. Additional factors may come into play depending on the nature of the work being done and the\nResearch Technologies\nsubunit involved.\nDefinition\nResearch Technologies\n(RT) and the IU\nPervasive Technology Institute (\nPTI\n)\nare available to do chargeback work on behalf of customers within the IU community. This work is routinely done to supplement the human resources available to a particular group at IU when RT and PTI consulting services are not sufficient to meet the needs of an academic or administrative unit. The differences between consulting and chargeback work for hire are as follows:\nConsulting is when we offer a member of the IU community advice about how to do something and/or work with a member of the IU community on a project.\nChargeback work is when an organizational unit at IU becomes a paying customer and pays RT/PTI to do work.\nConsulting is offered at no direct charge to the client (paid for by IU general funds) but limited in duration (typically up to one person-month of effort per year on a particular project).\nChargeback work is limited only by the availability of staff with skills needed to do the work and is paid for on a basis allowing the Office of Vice President for Information Technology (OVPIT) to recover the cost of doing the work.\nRT/PTI partners with NSF-funded organizations that provide both consulting and support. For example, in the past, XSEDE-related consulting and support was provided without fee but was awarded via an allocation model. Such support typically entails 25% of a full-time employee's time (.25 FTE) for 1-12 months. Consultation work exceeding these limits (>.25 FTE) is subject to chargeback rules.\nPurpose\nChargeback work is offered as a service to departments needing to extend their capabilities, particularly in circumstances when IU subunits have external funding available for specific functions but do not have the personnel to perform those functions. RT services done on a chargeback basis include:\nProgramming of applications, including clinical and translational medicine research projects, and custom programming of parallel applications\nBuilding scientific gateways (web portals to scientific computing applications)\nLinux cluster management\nInstallation and management of research software\nIT-28 alignment activities for Linux workstations\nDevelopment of secure applications for work involving protected health information (\nPHI\n)\nUA Technology Support\nalso offers chargeback services to the IU community. RT and PTI do not duplicate UA Technology Support services.\nServices include:\nClient support, desktop system management, and management of UITS Intelligent Infrastructure (\nII\nvirtual machines\nDepartmental inventory\nGeneral IT-28 alignment\nSecurity monitoring, including real-time automated log analysis\nDevelopment of secure applications for work involving Critical institutional data other than\nPHI\nCustomers\nRT/PTI chargeback services are available to any IU subunit and (through a special memorandum of understanding) the Regenstreif Institute.\nRates\nTo find the current cost of RT/PTI chargeback services:\nGo to\nRates for direct-bill services\n.\nClick to access the current fiscal year's rate sheet for IU Bloomington or IU Indianapolis.\nNear the top, use the "Table of Contents" drop-down to select\nIU Internal Research Technologies Services\n.\nScroll down to find the current rates listed under "\nResearch Technologies\nConsulting Rates".	KB0023189	KB0023189 v1.0	 back frequently to make sure you have the latest information. Whenever a <strong>Research</strong> Technologies subunit of the work being done and the <strong>Research</strong> Technologies subunit involved. Definition <strong>Research</strong> Technologies (RT include: Programming of applications, including clinical and translational medicine <strong>research</strong> projects	126.2686		2026-01-31 16:40:45.267057	2026-01-31 16:40:45.267057	0	5ccf68b7476456583d373803846d4381
1461	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024406	About the Scholarly Data Archive (SDA) at Indiana University	Table of contents\nSystem overview\nSystem information\nWork with data containing PHI\nRequest an account\nAccess the\nSDA\nand transfer files\nAcknowledge grant support\nSupport\nSystem overview\nThe Indiana University\nScholarly Data Archive\n(\nSDA\n) provides extensive capacity (approximately\n79 PB\nof tape overall) for storing and accessing research data. The\nSDA\nis a distributed storage service co-located at IU data centers in Bloomington and Indianapolis, providing IU researchers with large-scale archival or near-line data storage, arranged in large files, with two copies of data made by default (for disaster recovery).\nThe\nSDA\nis based on the High Performance Storage System (\nHPSS\n), a consortium-developed hierarchical storage management (HSM) package that makes the\nSDA\n's hierarchy of storage media transparent to its users. The\nSDA\n's system architecture comprises fast, efficient disk cache front-end components that move infrequently accessed data to two high-end tape libraries. Using the\nI-Light\nhigh performance network between IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis, the\nSDA\ncreates two tape copies of user data simultaneously (one at each data center), adding a degree of disaster tolerance to both sites.\nThe\nScholarly Data Archive\nis well suited for storing large volumes of data (that is, tens of gigabytes to several terabytes per project), and data that are accessed relatively infrequently (archival or near-line storage). The\nSDA\n's tape-based backend is not designed for storing a large number of small files. The SDA performs best with larger files; storing and retrieving many small files can negatively impact the SDA's performance. Individual files should be at least 1 MB. If you need to store many small files on the\nSDA\n, use a file compression utility (for example, gzip, tar, or 7-Zip) to bundle your files into a single, large archive file.\nThe\nSDA\nsupports high performance access methods, such as the Hierarchical Storage Interface (HSI); an HPSS API is available for programmers, as well.\nImportant:\nBefore storing data on any of Indiana University's research computing\nor storage systems, make sure you understand the information in\nTypes of sensitive institutional data appropriate for UITS Research Technologies services\n.\nMake sure you do not include sensitive institutional data as part of a file's filename or pathname.\nSystem information\nMachine type:\nDistributed HPSS data archive\nOperating system:\nRed Hat Enterprise Linux 7\nPotential tape library capacity:\n79 PB\nTotal disk capacity (cache):\n1,800 TB\nAggregate I/O:\n80 Gbps\nBackup and purge policies:\nData are not backed up; the system is never purged as long as the account owner has a valid\nIU account\n.\nDue to the mammoth volume of data stored on the\nScholarly Data Archive\n(\nSDA\n), back-ups are neither practical nor economical. The\nSDA\ndoesn't have an offline, traditional backup system, so any deletion you perform will cause an irreversible loss of data.\nHowever, to protect against random tape errors, two tape copies of the data are created by default. If one tape fails, data can be retrieved from the other tape. The two copies of data reside at two geographically distant sites (at IU Bloomington and at IU Indianapolis) in two separate tape libraries. Also, two separate\nmetadata\nbackups are performed, at IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis. As a result, even in the event of a catastrophic disaster affecting either the IU Bloomington or IU Indianapolis site (such as a fire or a tornado), all dual-copy data on the\nSDA\nwould still be safe.\nQuotas:\n50 TB\n(default) per user or project; when in support of research activities, extensions beyond\n50 TB\nmay be granted in additional 50 TB increments (requires a submitted data management plan and annual renewal) up to 200 TB before a nominal charge is assessed. If you need more than\n50 TB\n, submit your request using the\nSDA Quota Increase Request form\n.\nOn October 6, 2019, UITS\nResearch Technologies\nimplemented a file quota to limit the number of files users can store in their\nScholarly Data Archive\naccounts. The file quota for new accounts is\n25,000 files\n.\nDirectory path and file name limits:\nOn the SDA, HPSS limits the length of directory paths and file names as follows:\nDirectory paths:\nThe directory path of any file may not exceed 1,024 characters in length.\nFile names:\nFile names may not exceed 256 characters in length.\nNote:\nFile name and directory path limits in HPSS are separate from (and less restrictive than) similar limits imposed when using HTAR; for more, see\nHTAR limitations\nin\nUse HTAR with your SDA account\n.\nFile and directory names within the\nSDA\nmay only contain ASCII characters in the range 0x20 to 0x7e.\nSDA\ndoes not support Unicode and similar encodings; rarely, you may need to rename external files before transferring them into the\nSDA\n.\nWork with data containing PHI\nThe Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (\nHIPAA\n) established rules protecting the privacy and security of individually identifiable health information. The HIPAA\nPrivacy Rule\nand\nSecurity Rule\nset national standards requiring organizations and individuals to implement certain\nadministrative\n,\nphysical\n, and\ntechnical\nsafeguards to maintain the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of protected health information (\nPHI\n).\nThis system or service meets certain requirements established in the\nHIPAA Security Rule\nthereby enabling its use for work involving data that contain protected health information (\nPHI\n). However, using this system or service does not fulfill your legal responsibilities for protecting the privacy and security of data that contain PHI. You may use this system or service for work involving data that contain PHI only if you institute additional\nadministrative\n,\nphysical\n, and\ntechnical\nsafeguards that complement those UITS already has in place.\nFor more, see\nYour legal responsibilities for protecting data containing protected health information (PHI) when using UITS Research Technologies systems and services\n.\nNote:\nAlthough\nPHI\nis classified as\nCritical data\n, other types of institutional data classified as Critical are not permitted on\nResearch Technologies\nsystems. For help determining which institutional data elements classified as Critical are considered PHI, see\nAbout protected health information (PHI) data elements in the classifications of institutional data\n.\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.\nRequest an account\nFor eligibility requirements, see the\n"Research system accounts (all campuses)"\nsection in\nComputing accounts at IU\n.\nTo request an individual (personal) account on the Indiana University\nScholarly Data Archive\n(\nSDA\n), follow the instructions in\nGet additional IU computing accounts\n.\nIf you are eligible to request an\nSDA\naccount, but the\nSDA\nis not listed among the accounts available for you to request, contact your campus\nSupport Center\nfor help.\nTo request an\nSDA\naccount for your IU\ngroup account\n, use the\nSDA Group Account Request\nform.\nAfter submitting your account request, UITS will notify you via email when your account is ready for use.\nNote:\nIn accordance with standards for access control mandated by the\nHIPAA\nSecurity\nRule\n, you are not permitted to access data containing protected health information (\nPHI\n) using a group (or departmental) account. To ensure accountability and maintain appropriate levels of access control, all users must use an individual login for all work involving PHI.\nAccess the\nSDA\nand transfer files\nOnce you have an\nSDA\naccount, you can access it from any networked host. The method you use depends on your operating system and level of comfort with the command-line interface.\nNote:\nThe\nSDA\nis offline for regularly scheduled maintenance every Sunday 7am-10am.\nTo access the\nSDA\nfrom off campus, UITS recommends protocols such as Globus and SFTP, which provide\nIU Login\nand\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\nauthentication, as well as encryption in transit. Because the HSI/HTAR protocols don't provide the same protections, to remotely access the\nSDA\nwith a local HSI/HTAR client you must first email\nstore-admin@iu.edu\nto request an off-campus exemption.\nMethods available for transferring data to and from the Indiana University\nScholarly Data Archive\n(\nSDA\n) include\nsecure FTP\n(SFTP), secure copy (\nSCP\n), GridFTP (via the IU Globus Web App), and Hierarchical Storage Interface (HSI). For instructions, see:\nUse SFTP or SCP to access your SDA account at IU\nUse the IU Globus Web App to transfer data to and from your accounts on IU's research computing and storage systems\nUse HSI to access your SDA account at IU\nImportant:\nFiles containing\nPHI\nmust be encrypted when they are stored (at rest) and when they are transferred between networked systems (in transit). To ensure that files containing PHI are encrypted when they are stored, encrypt them before transferring them to storage. To ensure that files containing PHI remain encrypted during transit, use\nSFTP/SCP\nor the\nIU Globus Web App\n. For more, see\nRecommended tools for encrypting data containing HIPAA-regulated PHI\n.\nHSI, the highest performing non-grid method, provides shell-like facilities for recursive operations, and can take input data from standard input. HSI also can perform file migration to tape, stage files from tape to disk, and purge files from the disk cache. HSI is available on UITS\nresearch supercomputers\nwhen you load the\nhpss\nmodule. For more about HSI, see the\nHSI Reference Manual\n.\nFor more, see\nUse modules to manage your software environment on IU research supercomputers\n.\nFor use on personal workstations, IU\nSDA\nusers can download and install HSI version 10.3 (bundled with its companion program, HTAR) from the UITS\nResearch Technologies\nHSI folder\nin\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n. (You must be signed into your\nGoogle at IU\naccount to access this folder; see\nAccess Google at IU\n.) Precompiled packages supplied by the vendor are available for 64-bit Linux distributions only.\nNote:\nTo connect to the\nSDA\nwith a local HSI/HTAR client, make sure you have version 10.3 installed.\nTo connect to the\nSDA\nwith a local HSI/HTAR client from an off-campus network location, you must first email\nstore-admin@iu.edu\nto request an off-campus exemption.\nFor Windows or macOS users who prefer a graphical interface,\nUITS\nrecommends using a graphical\nSFTP\nclient. For macOS users, particularly those needing to transfer large amounts of data, UITS recommends\nFetch\n.\nAccess the\nSDA\nin Research Desktop (RED)\nYou can access the\nSDA\nin Research Desktop (RED) from the ThinLinc Client by clicking\nApplications\n>\nStorage\n>\nScholarly Data Archive\n.\nAcknowledge grant support\nThe Indiana University cyberinfrastructure, managed by the\nResearch Technologies\ndivision of\nUITS\n, is supported by funding from several grants, each of which requires you to acknowledge its support in all presentations and published works stemming from research it has helped to fund. Conscientious acknowledgment of support from past grants also enhances the chances of IU's research community securing funding from grants in the future. For the acknowledgment statement(s) required for scholarly printed works, web pages, talks, online publications, and other presentations that make use of this and/or other grant-funded systems at IU, see\nSources of funding to acknowledge in published work if you use IU's research cyberinfrastructure\n.\nSupport\nThe\nSDA\nis maintained by the UITS Research Storage team. If you have questions or need help, contact UITS Research Storage (\nstore-admin@iu.edu\n).	KB0024406	KB0024406 v2.0	 <strong>Desktop</strong> (RED) You can access the SDA in <strong>Research</strong> <strong>Desktop</strong> (RED) from the ThinLinc Client by clicking or project; when in support of <strong>research</strong> activities, extensions beyond 50 TB may be granted <strong>Research</strong> Technologies systems and services. Note: Request an account Access the SDA and transfer files	90.4551		2026-01-31 14:32:41.739544	2026-01-31 16:40:48.699304	0	44f6d5af4728aa182fc6a55b416d430d
10286	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c9df6cf7476456583d373803846d4390	Guidelines for submitting support requests to Tier 2 Support: Desktop, Server, and Mobility (DSM)	Note:\nFor hours, see\nAbout Tier 2 Support\n.\nTo submit support requests or questions regarding the following services to\nTier 2 Desktop, Server, and Mobility (DSM)\n, go to\nForms\n, and then select and submit the appropriate form:\nDepartmental OU request\nDFS root request\nExchange public folder request\nExchange resource room request\nKerberos keytab request\nSIS problem report\nTo escalate a support issue to Microsoft (create a Premier Support ticket), local UITS support people must provide the following:\nGeneral information:\nProduct name\nProduct version and service packs\nOperating system version and service pack\nWhat is the issue?\nWhat symptoms did you notice that led you to submit this support request?\nWhat is the exact text of any error messages that you received associated with this problem?\nWhat is occurring, and what do you expect to be occurring?\nWhat system and version are you using when the problem occurs?\nAre there any online articles that match the problem, even if the solution is not working? (Include links to the articles.)\nContact information:\nFirst name\nLast name\nEmail address\nPhone number\nContact preference: email or phone (determines how Microsoft will contact you with questions and or answers related to this issue)\nNote:\nTier 2 DSM\nwill submit a ticket to Microsoft when: the above information is provided, the issue can be replicated via a remote session, the issue can be replicated on a\nTier 2 DSM\nsystem, staff are unable to resolve the issue, and research suggests that the reported behavior is not a known issue.\nTier 2 DSM\ntypically has the local UITS support person or service owner then communicate directly with Microsoft to resolve the issue.\nOnce\nTier 2 Support\nhas the correct information, staff will complete the fields using the information provided by the local UITS support person, and will then email the confirmation number to the local UITS support person.\nSend\nTier 2 Support\nrequests to\nsct2@iu.edu\n.\nFor more about the various teams of\nTier 2 Support\nand how to contact them, see\nAbout Tier 2 Support\n.	KB0023610	KB0023610 v1.0	Note: For hours, see About Tier 2 Support. To submit support requests or questions regarding the following services to Tier 2 <strong>Desktop</strong>, Server, and Mobility (DSM), go to Forms, and then select can be replicated on a Tier 2 DSM system, staff are unable to resolve the issue, and <strong>research</strong> suggests	41.3958	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 16:40:52.852658	2026-01-31 16:40:52.852658	0	c9df6cf7476456583d373803846d4390
10290	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=22eb14e0479c3654d500b87b716d43e4	Support contacts, IT account resources, and other UITS services for those providing local UITS support at IU	Table of contents\nSupport basics and contacts\nHow IT support typically works at IU\nSpecific technical assistance\nStrategic planning, collaborative efforts, and connecting with the IT community at IU\nSensitive and/or HIPAA-regulated data\nSupporting IT accounts at IU\nTypes of accounts\nAccount-related tools\nOther UITS services and resources\nInformation policy and security offices\nEdCert\nSoftware vendor agreements\nSoftware distribution\nIT Notices\nChange Management\nIT Training\nSupport basics and contacts\nHow IT support typically works at IU\nIU provides a number of support options:\nSelf-help\nvia this\nIU Knowledge Base (KB)\nCampus Support Centers\nresolve many issues directly.\nIn the case of IT questions related to department-specific resources or admin rights, Support Centers refer customers to departmental local UITS support staff for help. The Support Center makes referrals based on information in\nIT People\n.\nAll local UITS support staff have access to the support resources listed above. Registered and authorized local UITS support staff at IU have access to additional support tools:\nAccess to restricted KB content after\nIU Login\nauthentication (see\nAbout logging into the IU Knowledge Base\n). To find some commonly referenced articles and services, see\nReference guide for those providing local UITS support\n.\nMay consult with Support Center Frontline and\nTier 2 Support\n, who may escalate issues to other UITS units, IU-contracted vendors, or manufacturers on behalf of departmental local UITS support staff.\nMay get support from these additional UITS teams:\nThe\nUniversity Information Security Office (UISO)\nand the\nUniversity Information Policy Office (UIPO)\n; see\nReport a suspected information or information technology (IT) security issue at IU\nIU\nNetwork Operations Center (NOC)\nfor issues involving voice and data service delivery at IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis, including switch and traffic at the building level; see\nContact the IU Network Operations Center\nDNS administration to\nRequest a static IP address at IU\nDHCP administration (for DHCP lease information or DHCP problems in your area):\ndhcp-admin@iu.edu\nSpecific technical assistance\nThe UITS\nSupport Center\noffers general help and assistance from professionals with specialized knowledge. Support Center staff may escalate on your behalf to a specialist within the Support Center, to colleagues in other UITS units, or to contracted vendors or manufacturers (when available).\nSupport Center Frontline\noffers help for general IT questions and routine inquiries about IU systems from 7am to 11pm daily. SC Frontline also collects information about IU system outages, alerts system administrators, and publishes IT Notices. Anyone at IU may contact the SC Frontline team during their available hours.\nPhone: 812-855-6789 (IU Bloomington); 317-274-4357 (IU Indianapolis)\nEmail:\nithelp@iu.edu\nChat:\nhttps://ithelplive.iu.edu\nTier 2 Support\nis dedicated primarily to serving those providing local UITS support, providing technical expertise in these core areas:\nAccounts Administration\nDesktop/Server/Mobility (DSM)\nStudent Information Systems (SIS)\nUITS Web team\nFor contact information and more, see\nAbout Tier 2 Support\n.\nStrategic planning, collaborative efforts, and connecting with the IT community at IU\nIT Community Partnerships (ITCP)\nserves as an information brokerage, working to strengthen relationships between UITS and university schools, departments, and campuses and to cultivate a community in which everyone works or has an interest in IT at IU. Email\nitcp@iu.edu\n, and ITCP will put you in contact with the best individual or unit (within UITS or elsewhere) to address your concerns.\nWhen you're not sure whom to contact, start with ITCP:\nEmail:\nitcp@iu.edu\nITCP delivers support in several ways:\nITCP sends notices (via a registered distribution list) to address relevant projects, security issues, new software releases, and other subjects to keep IU's IT community informed of changes and developments in the university's information technology environment.\nITCP owns and moderates the\nIT-Announcements-L\nmailing list, which is open to anyone at IU who's interested in IT. Posts include news, announcements, and information about events of interest to local UITS support staff throughout the university.\nTo subscribe, email\nit-announcements-l-subscribe@iu.edu\n. Relevant messages are welcome from all subscribers.\nThe\nITCP website\nprovides a collection of tools, information, and resources, and a platform for communicating with each other.\nITCP hosts IT Water Cooler meetings, providing opportunities to meet and exchange ideas with others interested in IU IT, and\ntech talks and infoshares\n(under the\nMedia\ntab) for sharing information and bolstering peer-to-peer communication among university departments. If you have a topic or vendor product to suggest for an upcoming presentation, email\nitcp@iu.edu\n.\nITCP visits each IU IT department at least once a year to find out what's happening in each area, provide information, and identify opportunities for partnership and collaboration.\nSensitive and/or HIPAA-regulated data\nOfficial classifications for institutional data at IU are defined in\nManagement of Institutional Data (DM-01)\n. If you have questions about the classifications of institutional data at IU, see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n, use the\nData Sharing and Handling (DSH) tool\n, or contact the appropriate\nData Stewards\n. To determine the most sensitive classification of institutional data you can store on any given UITS service, see\nChoose an appropriate storage solution\n.\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.\nAlthough\nPHI\nis classified as\nCritical data\n, other types of institutional data classified as Critical are not permitted on\nResearch Technologies\nsystems. For help determining which institutional data elements classified as Critical are considered PHI, see\nAbout protected health information (PHI) data elements in the classifications of institutional data\n.\nIT accounts at IU\nUse of Indiana University technology resources (such as computers, networks, phones) is restricted to purposes related to the university's mission of education, research, and public service. Access to IU computing resources is granted to all admitted IU students and current or\nretired\nuniversity faculty and staff, in support of their studies, instruction, duties as employees, official business with the university, and other university-sanctioned activities. Under certain circumstances, guests and affiliates of IU are also eligible.\nTypes of accounts\nUniversity account:\nFor faculty, staff, students, affiliates, and others, the\nUniversity account\nis based on\nADS\n, and consists of a unique username and passphrase for\nsingle sign-on\nauthentication on most university resources.\nAffiliate:\nSponsored by faculty or staff, Affiliate accounts are for longer-term use by non-IU contractors and vendors. For details, see\nSponsor a computing account for an IU affiliate\n.\nGroup:\nSponsored by faculty or staff, group accounts are for departmental or shared use of certain IT resources. For details, see:\nAbout group and departmental accounts\nComputing accounts for IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis student organizations\nConference:\nSponsored by faculty or staff for conference attendees needing temporary use of Student Technology Center (STC) computers and printers; fees may apply; IU account number required; for details, see\nAbout sponsored accounts for conference participants at IU\n.\nIU retirees:\nIU retirees\nretain access to some accounts and resources. For details, see\nAccess to IU resources for official IU retirees\n.\nIU Guest:\nFor those with no formal relationship with the university, the IU Guest account provides limited access to certain online applications and services needed for a specific purpose; the amount of access is determined by the particular application. The IU Guest account is separate from any other type of IU account; for details, see\nAbout IU Guest accounts\n.\nEmail accounts:\nIU email addresses and the types of accounts available vary based on campus and IU status. Each campus allocates email accounts slightly differently. For more, see:\nYour IU email address\nEmail at IU\nOnline storage:\nCurrent students, faculty, and staff have multiple options for storing files online. For more, see\nOptions for storing files at IU\nand\nStorage at IU\n.\nResearch systems:\nEligible students, faculty, staff, and sponsored research affiliates can request access to the compute, storage, visualization, and bioinformatics systems and services that make up IU's world-class scholarly and artistic cyberinfrastructure. Research systems and services are managed by the Research Technologies division of UITS. For details, see\nResearch computing services at IU\n.\nFor more about basic accounts, additional accounts, accounts for groups, departments, and student organizations, research system accounts (all campuses), specialized system accounts, and accounts available for those who are not students, faculty, or staff, see:\nComputing accounts at IU\nIU account types and eligibility\nAccount-related tools\nAll IU students, faculty, staff, and affiliates can use the following tools:\nOne.IU:\nOne.IU\nallows you to search for direct links to various tasks (managing accounts and email, for example).\nPassphrase Reset:\nPassphrase Reset\nis a self-service tool for those needing to change or reset their IU passphrase; see\nChange or reset your IU passphrase\n.\nFor more, see:\nYour IU passphrase\nPrecautions when changing your passphrase\nSelf-Service Unblock Tool:\nThe\nunblock.iu.edu\nsite provides a self-service IT incident resolution tool that lets users re-enable access to the IU network after having resolved policy and/or security issues identified in email from the UIPO and/or UISO.\nAdditionally,\nunblock.iu.edu\nallows users to:\nMonitor their login activity across various IU services, and sign up to receive login notices via email\nSign up for email notifications alerting them whenever peer-to-peer file sharing software or vulnerable applications are detected on their computers\nNote:\nUsers who are blocked because of copyright infringement incidents must comply with specific UIPO and UISO disciplinary procedures before regaining access to the IU network; see\nCopyright infringement incident resolution\n.\nLocal UITS support staff at IU can access the following tools, unless otherwise noted:\nAMS Admin support tool:\nThe\nAMS Admin support tool\nlets you look up user accounts by username, first and last name, or 10-digit IU ID for troubleshooting account creation and certain email issues. If you're off campus, you'll need a VPN connection.\nView a video about recent changes to AMS Admin\n.\nTISA:\nUse\nTISA\n(Telecommunications Infrastructure System Application) for:\nNetwork troubleshooting:\nData jack lookups\nSubnet allocations\nSwitch lookups\nDHCP troubleshooting:\nView registered MAC addresses\nView active MAC addresses\nClearPass:\nUse\nClearPass\nto register MAC addresses, look up registered MAC addresses, and manage MAC address registrations.\nCCI Tools:\nUse\nCCI Tools\nto troubleshoot and resolve\nADS\n,\nExchange\n, and\nMicrosoft Teams\naccount issues:\nActive Directory tools:\nQuery account\nUnlock account\nAdd/modify/remove UIPO/UISO VPN block\nVerify identity\nAccount lockout logs\nExchange tools:\nQuery account\nIncrease quota\nActiveSync policy\nIMAP settings\nDeleted items retention\nRename mailbox\nTeams tools:\nQuery account\nManage common phones\nSet Outlook voice mail\nSet personal operator extension\nSet directory phone number\nPhone number lookup\nSuperpass:\nSupport Center staff, local UITS support staff, and regional help desk personnel can use\nSuperpass\nto reset unknown or forgotten IU passphrases.\nNote:\nLocal UITS support staff can reset passphrases only for users within the department(s) with which they are associated in IT People.\nOther UITS services and resources\nInformation policy and security offices\nThe\nUniversity Information Policy Office (UIPO)\n, part of the Office of the Vice\nPresident for Information Technology (OVPIT), is charged to develop,\ndisseminate, review, interpret, and provide education regarding\npolicies\nabout information and information technology.\nThe\nUniversity Information Security Office (UISO)\n, part of the\nOffice of the Vice President for Information Technology (OVPIT),\nprovides security analysis, development, education, and guidance\nrelated to Indiana University's information assets and information\ntechnology environment. The objective is to establish and maintain a\nresilient and secure infrastructure in which to conduct university\nbusiness.\nUIPO and UISO staff respond to and investigate incidents related to misuse or abuse of IU information and information technology resources, computer and network security breaches, and unauthorized disclosure or modification of institutional or personal data:\nIf you have a "phishy" email from one of your users, or you're not sure what's the best way to isolate an infected server, or you suspect unlawful or unethical activities, email\nit-incident@iu.edu\n.\nIf you suspect a security incident concerning a computer hosting sensitive institutional or personal data, follow the instructions at\nReport an IT incident\n.\nFor links to cybersecurity resources, including IT and data management policies, security bulletins, and information about securing computers and mobile devices, protecting data, and staying safe online and in the cloud, see\nInformation Security and Policy\n.\nFor other key UIPO/UISO resources, see:\nResources\nInformation and IT policies\nPrivacy Matters\nSecurity Bulletins\nVulnerability Scanners\nSSL/TLS certificates\nRequired & Recommended Data Training\nEdCert\nEdCert\n, or Education Certification, is a UITS initiative aimed at elevating the level of expertise in departmental computing support and building a solid technical infrastructure at Indiana University. The EdCert Program offers advanced technical training to\nlocal UITS support people\n, currently enrolled IU students, and faculty who teach advanced technical skills in their courses. Topics include Microsoft Windows, Unix system administration, and Windows and Unix Security, among others.\nSoftware vendor agreements\nAffordable and up-to-date software for students, faculty, staff, and\ndepartments is readily available at Indiana University thanks to\nlicense agreements with companies such as Microsoft, Adobe, SPSS, and\nThomson Reuters. Offerings include operating systems, antivirus\nprograms, and software for word processing and spreadsheets, web\nauthoring and development, graphic design, citation management,\nstatistical analysis, and more.\nKey vendor agreements include:\nMicrosoft:\nNotable applications available to all IU students, faculty, and staff via IU's software agreement with Microsoft include the latest 32- and 64-bit versions of the Windows operating system, and the latest versions of\nMicrosoft 365\nfor Windows and macOS. Notable applications for departments include\nWindows Server\n, SQL Server, and SharePoint Online. For details, see\nSoftware available through the IU/Microsoft Campus Agreement with Core Server Site License\n.\nThe IU/Microsoft Campus Agreement also includes Microsoft Premier Support Program membership for IU, providing UITS with expanded access to Microsoft incident packs, and allowing the university to open problem incidents with Microsoft on a case-by-case basis. For guidelines on submitting a Premier incident with Microsoft,\ncontact Tier 2\n.\nAdobe:\nSoftware covered by IU's software agreement with Adobe is available to currently enrolled students, current and retired faculty, current staff, and some affiliates without fee through various means; see\nAbout software at IU\n. For details, see\nIndividual Adobe software titles available at IU\n.\nOther vendor agreements at IU include:\nIU's software agreement with Red Hat\nAbout IU's educational site license with Esri\nIU/Clark Labs site license\nNote:\nFor more about the availability of scientific and mathematical software at IU, see\nResearch software available at IU\n.\nSoftware distribution\nCurrent students, faculty, staff, affiliates, and retired faculty members have several options for accessing university-distributed software:\nIUware:\nDownload and install software free of charge from\nIUware\n. Log in with your\nUniversity account\nto download applications for\nWindows\n,\nmacOS\n, and\nLinux\nsystems from categories including Analysis and Modeling, Databases, Design and Production, Development Tools, Multimedia, Network and Printing, Office Tools, Operating Systems, Security, Utilities, and Web and Email.\nFor more, see\nDownload and install software from IUware\n.\nNote:\nIUware includes a section accessible by IU local UITS support staff only; you must be logged into IUware to see it. Several support tools, volume-licensed software installers, and volume-licensed operating system images are available in this section.\nIUanyWare:\nIU's client virtualization service for personal computers, tablets, and smartphones,\nIUanyWare\nlets you use a web browser or mobile app to run selected IU-licensed software applications without installing them. You also can connect your IUanyWare login with selected online storage options via\nIU Cloud Storage\n.\nThe software available via IUanyWare changes periodically; see\nSoftware Published to IUanyWare\n.\nFor more, see:\nSet up and use IUanyWare\nWork with files when using an IUanyWare application\nPrint from an IUanyWare application\nRequest software for the STCs or IUanyWare\nNote:\nAll user IUanyWare profiles are reset at the end of each academic year; see\nAbout the IUanyWare user profile reset\n.\nComputer labs:\nStudents can use the computers in campus\nStudent Technology Centers (STCs)\n. Campus housing residents can use the computers in the\nResidential Technology Centers (RTCs)\n. For details, see:\nAbout software at IU\nRequest software for the STCs or IUanyWare\nIU\nresearch supercomputers\n:\nThe\nResearch Applications and Deep Learning\n(\nRADL\n) unit in\nResearch Technologies\nmanages the software available on IU's\nresearch supercomputers\n; see:\nResearch software available at IU\nIT Notices\nIT Notices, posted on\nStatus.IU\nand updated by the UITS\nSupport Center\n, provide timely announcements about the Indiana University computing systems, services, and applications that students, faculty, and staff use daily to achieve academic progress and conduct university business. IT Notices include general announcements, listings of scheduled changes and planned maintenance windows, and notices about outages, problems, and interruptions.\nDevelopers and service owners may pull status information into their applications using the\nStatus.IU API\n.\nUITS recommends that all local UITS support staff at IU subscribe to IT Notices in some fashion:\nStatus.IU\nAbout IT Notices and Status.IU\nAccess IT Notices at IU\nChange Management\nUITS Change Management ensures that changes to UITS resources are appropriately communicated, coordinated, scheduled, and monitored to protect the university from changes that are potentially disruptive, in conflict, or of unacceptable risk.\nChange Management convenes every Wednesday at 2pm; local UITS support staff are welcome to attend. For agendas and other resources, see\nUITS Change Management\n.\nIT Training\nUITS IT Training\nserves all IU campuses, providing critical training and educational support for the technology education needs of students, faculty, and staff.	KB0023548	KB0023548 v11.0	 technical expertise in these core areas: Accounts Administration <strong>Desktop</strong>/Server/Mobility (DSM files online. For more, see Options for storing files at IU and Storage at IU. <strong>Research</strong> systems: Eligible students, faculty, staff, and sponsored <strong>research</strong> affiliates can request access to the compute	37.659	km-aud-uits, km-aud-local-support	2026-01-31 16:41:00.144847	2026-01-31 16:41:00.144847	0	22eb14e0479c3654d500b87b716d43e4
2128	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023203	RADaRS at IU	On this page:\nOverview\nSystem information\nServer security\nServer backups\nAccess RADaRS\nGet help\nOverview\nThe Restricted Access Data Remote Server (RADaRS) at Indiana University is a secure research hub that allows IU researchers to access and analyze datasets from a variety of providers that require that their data be used only within highly secure environments. RADARS is intended for use by researchers in all areas of social sciences (and beyond).\nRADaRS data custodians upload secure datasets to project folders and manage access control permissions. Individual researchers and collaborative project teams can access RADaRS remotely from their personal workstations to work with datasets. Statistical packages and productivity software, including SAS, SPSS, Stata, R, and\nMicrosoft 365\n, are installed on the server.\nNote:\nOnly data custodians can import or export data. Researchers are prohibited from getting data on or off the RADaRS server without the cooperation of a data custodian. Research results are exported from RADaRS only after review by data custodians, who must ensure compliance with terms set forth by data providers in their data use agreements.\nRADaRS is maintained by UITS\nResearch Technologies\nand the IU Social Science Research Commons (\nSSRC\n.) For more, see\nSSRC RADaRS\n.\nSystem information\nServer security\nRADaRS runs on a virtual server in the UITS Intelligent Infrastructure (II) environment, which is securely housed in a hardened UITS Data Center. The RADaRS virtual server runs Microsoft Windows Server 2019 Datacenter edition (64-bit), with Microsoft Forefront Client Security providing antivirus, antispyware, and antimalware protection. A local software firewall and the IU Data Center firewall provide additional security. RADaRS server software and client applications are updated from secure vendor sources on a regular monthly basis and whenever critical updates are released by vendors or required by the University Information Security Office (\nUISO\n).\nResearchers can connect to the RADaRS server via Microsoft Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) only. To do this, they first must establish a connection to IU's SSL VPN. While connected to RADaRS, researchers cannot copy and paste from the server, map network drives to other servers, print to any printers, or access the internet via HTTP or any other protocol.\nOnly data custodians are permitted to transfer data on or off the RADaRS server. Only data custodians have the permissions needed to upload secure datasets received from data providers and release research output (for example, tables for research papers) to researchers.\nData custodians use access control lists (ACLs) to secure project folder access to authorized project members only. Researchers can request personal folders to which only they have access for storing temporary files or additional research materials.\nWhen research is complete, data custodians will export research output and, upon ensuring that it contains no sensitive data, deliver the output to the researcher. Data custodians also securely purge files and sensitive data from project folders at the end of each project.\nServer backups\nFor disaster recovery purposes, the RADaRS server is backed up daily (this portion of the backup contains no sensitive data). Project and individual researcher folders also are backed up daily; these folders are stored off site in an encrypted tape archive. Folders may be excluded from backups on a per-project basis, as needed; when applicable, this information should be included in the project documentation, and in the RADaRS User Agreement and RADaRS Service Owner Agreement for the project.\nAccess RADaRS\nBefore obtaining restricted-use data and beginning research on RADaRS, you also will need to obtain documentation from the IU Institutional Review Board (IRB) and, if required, institutional signatures for your data use agreement. You will need to provide this documentation to the data custodian before beginning your RADaRS project. Additional clearance (for example, human subjects approval) and signed data use agreements also may be required.\nTo request an account on the RADaRS server, see\nSSRC RADaRS\n.\nOnce your account is set up, to access RADaRS:\nUse a remote desktop application to connect to\newa-tsxap1.ads.iu.edu\n.\nOn a Windows computer, use the native Remote Desktop Connection application (usually available in the\nWindows Accessories\nfolder).\nOn a macOS computer, you will need to download and install the latest version of\nWindows App for Mac\nfrom the Mac App Store.\nYou will be prompted for credentials when you connect; use your\nIU email address\n(for example,\nusername\n@iu.edu\n) and\nIU passphrase\n.\nThe gateway is the IU RDP gateway:\nadmin-rdgw.eas.iu.edu\n.\nGet help\nFor more on RADaRS, see\nSSRC RADaRS\n.\nIf you have a general question about the RADaRS service,\ncontact the SSRC\n.\nIf you have questions or need help using the RADaRS service,\ncontact Research Technologies\n.\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.	KB0023203	KB0023203 v5.0	 Overview The Restricted Access Data Remote Server (RADaRS) at Indiana University is a secure <strong>research</strong>. <strong>Research</strong> results are exported from RADaRS only after review by data custodians, who must ensure <strong>Research</strong> Technologies and the IU Social Science <strong>Research</strong> Commons (SSRC.) For more, see SSRC RADaRS	10.5535	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 14:35:07.091651	2026-01-31 16:41:11.169344	0	68b5bf6d478dfa9c2fc6a55b416d4364
10314	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=12403cb347a456583d373803846d438a	Backup a MySQL database at IU RDC	Table of contents\nOverview\nUse mysqldump to back up data\nAccess mysqldump\nBack up your database\nBack up multiple databases\nStore connection information in a file\nPerformance considerations\nRestore a database\nStore and protect your exported data\nStorage options\nProtect your files\nGet help\nOverview\nThe Indiana University\nResearch Database Complex\n(\nRDC\n) supports research-related MySQL and PostgreSQL databases and data-intensive applications that require databases. The\nRDC\nis strictly devoted to supporting research, and is not an instructional, classroom environment.\nIndividual database backups are not provided in the Research Database Complex (RDC).  UITS maintains system-level backups for disaster recovery, but these don't allow point-in-time data recovery for an individual database or schema. For this reason, UITS strongly encourages individual database owners to back up their data regularly. You can access backup utilities via\nResearch Desktop (RED)\n.\nUse mysqldump to back up data\nFor MySQL data, UITS recommends the\nmysqldump\nbackup utility.\nAccess mysqldump\nOpen a Terminal window in\nRED\n, and then enter:\nmodule load mysql\nThis loads the MySQL client and related utilities, including\nmysqldump\n.\nBack up your database\nThe following example assumes you have an RDC account called\nmyaccount\nand are using the\nmyaccount_root\nlogin to manage the account's databases.\nTo back up the\nmyaccount_db1\ndatabase, enter:\nmysqldump --host=sasrdsmp01.uits.iu.edu --port=3306 -u myaccount_root -p --no-tablespaces --routines myaccount_db1 > myaccount_db1_dump.sql\nEnter your password when prompted.\nNote:\nIf you store connection information in a\n.my.cnf file\n, you can simplify the above\nmysql\ncommand to:\nmysqldump --no-tablespaces --routines myaccount_db1 > myaccount_db1_dump.sql\nThe\nmysqldump\nutility will begin exporting your database to the specified file. Note that the\nroutines\noption includes any stored procedures or functions in the export, and that the\nno-tablespaces\noption is needed to avoid a permission error introduced on MySQL 8.0.21.\nThe process creates a readable text file that contains both data definition language (DDL) and data insert statements. Be sure to protect the file; for help, see\nStore and protect your exported data\nbelow.\nBack up multiple databases\nTo back up multiple databases with\nmysqldump\n, enter the\n--databases\noption along with a list of database names separated by spaces. For example:\nmysqldump --no-tablespaces --routines --databases myaccount_db1 myaccount_db2 > myaccount_backup.dump\nIf you don't have a\n.my.cnf file\n, you will need to add connection information to the\nmysql\ncommand above.\nStore connection information in a file\nFor convenience, you can store connection information in a file called\n.my.cnf\nin your home directory. Here is an example file:\n[client]\nhost=sasrdsmp01.uits.iu.edu\nport=3306\nuser=myaccount_root\npassword="<mypassword>"\nNote:\nIf you include your password in the\n.my.cnf\nconfiguration file, be sure to change permissions so that the file isn't world-readable. For help, see\nManage file permissions on Unix-like systems\n.\nPerformance considerations\nIf your database is large, the export could take time. In this scenario, consider running\nmysqldump\nin the background. UITS strongly recommends running\nmysqldump\non RED. For help, see\nRun a Unix process in the background\n.\nRestore a database\nThe dump file contains drop statements. Databases, tables, and routines are dropped and recreated, and any exported data is reloaded. Your database objects will be restored to the state they were in when the dump file was created. Consider the timing of backups and restores, and use caution when executing these processes.\nTo restore your database from a\nmysqldump\nbackup, enter:\nmysql < myaccount_backup.dump\nIf you don't have a\n.my.cnf file\n, you will need to add connection information to the\nmysql\ncommand above.\nStore and protect your exported data\nConsider the following options for storing and protecting your export files.\nStorage options\nBecause they contain both DDL and your data,\nmysqldump\nfiles can become large. Be sure to choose a location that can accommodate the expected size of your export file. If your database doesn't exceed the size of your home directory, it's simplest to keep your exports there.\nIf you need additional space, see\nAvailable access to allocated and short-term storage capacity on IU's research systems\n.\nProtect your files\nBecause export files contain raw data, you should take appropriate steps to protect them. Ensure that your files have the\nappropriate permissions\n, and consider encrypting any that contain sensitive data. This is especially important if you choose to move or copy them to the\nSDA\nor other storage locations.\nGet help\nIf you have questions, or need help connecting to or administering your RDC MySQL database, email the RDC Administration team (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n).	KB0026125	KB0026125 v1.0	 to back up their data regularly. You can access backup utilities via <strong>Research</strong> <strong>Desktop</strong> (RED). Use Individual database backups are not provided in the <strong>Research</strong> Database Complex (RDC). UITS maintains capacity on IU's <strong>research</strong> systems. Protect your files Because export files contain raw data	9.8147		2026-01-31 16:41:14.588263	2026-01-31 16:41:14.588263	0	12403cb347a456583d373803846d438a
10316	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=856bcfb547e4f698d500b87b716d4345	Join a Zoom meeting	Table of contents\nOverview\nInstall Zoom on a laptop, desktop, or mobile device\nJoin a meeting with your Zoom at IU account\nAbout joining multiple Zoom meetings\nOverview\nImportant:\nZoom requires you to update your software if it's more than nine months behind the current version. If your version of Zoom software is older than nine months, you will be blocked from joining meetings until you upgrade. For help, see\nUpgrading Zoom to the latest version\n. Upgrading your Zoom software lets you take advantage of all available features and avoid potential issues. For more, see\nZoom Software Quarterly Lifecycle Policy\n.\nZoom released their final update for 32-bit versions of Zoom for Windows on November 17, 2025; 32-bit Windows will not be supported beyond this version.\nCheck if your Windows computer is on Windows 32-bit\n.\nTo join a\nZoom\nmeeting, you must first have Zoom installed on your device, and then provide the meeting's unique 10- or 11-digit ID.\nThese instructions provide information on installation options, and step-by-step instructions for joining a Zoom meeting with your Zoom at IU account.\nImportant:\nDue to federal law enforcement concerns regarding foreign government interference and surveillance risks associated with Zoom, it is strongly recommended that individuals involved in research activities discontinue using Zoom. Instead, all virtual meetings should be conducted through\nMicrosoft Teams\n, which meets government requirements for secure and private communications.\nIU Research has chosen to prohibit the use of Zoom by its employees. Other IU divisions may enact similar prohibitions.\nTo learn more about using Microsoft Teams for virtual meetings, see\nUITS IT Training Microsoft resources\n.\nFor questions or further guidance,\ncontact your local UITS Support Center\n.\nInstall Zoom on a laptop, desktop, or mobile device\nYou have the following options for connecting to Zoom:\nZoom client for meetings (recommended):\nDownload the Zoom client\nfor your Windows, macOS, or Linux computer.\nView a video about installing and updating the Zoom desktop client\n.\nNote:\nIf you're outside the US, see\nAccommodate international students who can't access Zoom\n.\nZoom mobile app:\nYou can download the Zoom mobile app for your iOS or Android device from the\nApple App Store\nor the\nGoogle Play Store\n.\nZoom Launcher plug-in:\nTo connect to a Zoom meeting from Chrome, Firefox, or Safari without downloading the Zoom client, see\nJoin a meeting with the invite link\n. The Zoom Launcher plug-in will install the Zoom client on first use.\nNote:\nThe\nOutlook\nplug-in requires the Zoom client.\nOutlook\nplug-in/add-in:\nTo schedule and start Zoom meetings directly from\nOutlook\n, download the\nOutlook\nplug-in from the\nZoom Download Center\n. For more, see\nMicrosoft Outlook plugin (desktop)\n. If you're on macOS, use the\nZoom for Outlook add-in\ninstead of the plug-in.\nBe aware that calendar sharing in Zoom won't have the same permissions as in\nOutlook\n; you'll likely want to test editing and revising meetings using the plug-in.\nJoin a meeting with your Zoom at IU account\nNote:\nBefore hosting or joining a meeting,\njoin a test meeting\nto test your internet connection, audio, and video.  Be sure to test your Zoom meeting with the device you intend to connect or host from (such as videoconferencing equipment). If you want to test connecting to a meeting from videoconferencing equipment, you will need to\ncreate and host a meeting\nto join first, as meetings cannot be hosted from videoconferencing equipment.\nSign into your Zoom account.\nWhile it is possible to\njoin a Zoom meeting as a guest\n, UITS recommends that you sign into Zoom\nwith your IU account\nbefore joining a meeting, for a more secure meeting experience.\nProvide Zoom with the 10- or 11-digit ID associated with the meeting you want to join (if you don't know your meeting ID, contact the meeting host). Follow the appropriate instructions below, depending on how you're using Zoom:\nThrough a browser (such as Google Chrome):\nGo to\nhttps://iu.zoom.us/j/\nmeetingID\n.\nOnce you launch the application, you will be joined with the meeting. If the host has passcode-protected the meeting, you will be prompted to enter it. Then, if you're placed in a waiting room, the host will need to admit you before you can participate in the meeting.\nThrough the Zoom desktop application:\nOpen Zoom, and select\nJoin\n.\nType in the meeting ID, and set your join options. Select\nJoin\n. If the host has password-protected the meeting, you will be prompted to enter it. Then, if the host has enabled the waiting room, you'll be placed in it before you are admitted into the meeting.\nThrough the Zoom mobile app for iOS or Android:\nOpen the Zoom app, and tap\nJoin\n. If you see the option to "Join a Meeting", you need to sign into your Zoom at IU account.\nEnter the meeting ID and set your join options. Tap\nJoin Meeting\n. You will be joined with the meeting. If the host has passcode-protected the meeting, you will be prompted to enter it. Then, if you're placed in a waiting room, the host will need to admit you before you can participate in the meeting.\nAbout joining multiple Zoom meetings\nIf you need to join multiple meetings simultaneously, see\nJoining different meetings simultaneously\n.	KB0025213	KB0025213 v5.0	Table of contents Overview Install Zoom on a laptop, <strong>desktop</strong>, or mobile device Join a meeting for joining a Zoom meeting with your Zoom at IU account. Install Zoom on a laptop, <strong>desktop</strong>, or mobile and updating the Zoom <strong>desktop</strong> client. Note: If you're outside the US, see Accommodate international	7.8601		2026-01-31 16:41:18.589275	2026-01-31 16:41:18.589275	0	856bcfb547e4f698d500b87b716d4345
10326	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b06778724795629cd500b87b716d4331	About IU's educational site license with Esri	Indiana University's educational site license with Environmental Systems Research Institute (\nEsri\n), developers of GIS and geodatabase management applications, provides desktop and server software and extensions, data- and web-based training, and an ArcGIS Online academic organization for IU. Many of the products are available for teaching, research, and administrative uses; others are available only for teaching, research, and student learning purposes. None of the products can be used for any profit-making or commercial purpose.\nIU's educational site license with Esri stipulates the following rules:\nStaff can install and use Esri software from UITS only on IU-owned computers (not on personally owned computers).\nStudents can install and use ArcGIS Pro (available from\nIUware\n) and other Esri software on their personally owned computers.\nFaculty can install and use Esri software on both IU-owned and personally owned computers.\nStaff or faculty who are no longer associated with IU must immediately discontinue use of any Esri software from UITS.\nAll technical support requests to Esri must originate from designated Esri technical support contacts. Esri software users at IU cannot start a technical support incident with Esri directly. To contact IU's Esri administrator to request support from Esri, complete the\nGIS help form\n.\nFor an overview of the educational site license, including a list of the software provided, see the\nEsri Educational Site License Program\nand\nEsri's Rubric for Permitted Uses\n(both in PDF format).	KB0022556	KB0022556 v2.0	Indiana University's educational site license with Environmental Systems <strong>Research</strong> Institute (Esri), developers of GIS and geodatabase management applications, provides <strong>desktop</strong> and server software of the products are available for teaching, <strong>research</strong>, and administrative uses; others are available	6.7527		2026-01-31 16:41:25.396308	2026-01-31 16:41:25.396308	0	b06778724795629cd500b87b716d4331
10329	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=582030ff476456583d373803846d43f4	Use Microsoft Teams to host meetings that contain protected health information (PHI)	Table of contents\nOverview\nRequest a Microsoft Team for use with PHI\nSchedule a meeting in a secure Team\nOverview\nImportant:\nThis system or service meets certain requirements established in the\nHIPAA Security Rule\nthereby enabling its use for work involving data that contain protected health information (\nPHI\n). However, using this system or service does not fulfill your legal responsibilities for protecting the privacy and security of data that contain PHI. You may use this system or service for work involving data that contain PHI only if you institute additional\nadministrative\n,\nphysical\n, and\ntechnical\nsafeguards that complement those UITS already has in place.\nIndiana University and Microsoft have a signed Business Associates Agreement (BAA) to facilitate private, secure online collaborations for research, teaching, or administration involving the transmission of protected health information (PHI).\nFollowing are some examples of virtual collaborations that involve PHI and should be conducted using a\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\naccount:\nDistributed research team working sessions with patient data\nClinical researchers interviewing participants or participant teams as part of research projects\nDistributed administrative or technical teams working directly with patient data\nClinical faculty and healthcare providers conducting telehealth services for students and patients in an IU clinical care area\nNote:\nThe lobby function in\nMicrosoft Teams\nis turned off by default for future and recurring meetings. If you want to modify that setting, see\nAbout the lobby in Microsoft Teams meetings at IU\n.\nRequest a Microsoft Team for use with PHI\nImportant:\nIf you wish to host virtual meetings with\nMicrosoft Teams\nthat will contain PHI, you cannot use the standard version of\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n.  Instead, you will need to request a secure Team via the\nInstitutional storage request form\n.  Using the\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\noption will ensure all PHI-related meetings in that Team meet the additional security requirements for hosting meetings with PHI at IU.\nBefore deciding to host a meeting via a secure Team, be aware of the following:\nIndividuals must schedule each meeting in the secure Team if the meeting will contain PHI data.  As long as the meeting was created via the secure Team, the meeting has appropriate safeguards in place for PHI-related data.\nParticipants can be members of multiple secure Teams and create multiple meetings inside of each secure Team they are a member of.\nPersonal vs. department secure Team: Once your secure Team is created, any member of the Team can use the Team to host meetings that may contain PHI.  When creating your Team, it is important to keep this in mind.  Naming your Team for personal use vs. naming it for use by an entire department likely facilitates a different naming convention for each.\nMeeting recordings containing PHI must be stored within your\nsecure Microsoft Teams channels\n.\nImportant:\nAt Indiana University,\nnever\nstore files containing sensitive\ninstitutional data\n, especially protected health information (\nPHI\n) regulated by the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (\nHIPAA\n), on your desktop workstation, laptop, USB flash drive, tablet, smartphone, or other mobile device unless the files are properly encrypted on the device,\nand\nyour senior executive officer or the IU Institutional Review Board (IRB) has given prior written approval. Because PHI must remain encrypted at rest, make sure you are using full-disk encryption on any device that has research data containing PHI on it. If you are not sure, ask your department or school\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help.\nBreakout rooms are available in a secure Team; you can enable them in your\nMicrosoft Teams\nsettings and use them normally.\nSchedule a meeting in a secure Team\nWhen scheduling a meeting in your secure Team, be sure to include a channel from your secure Team in the meeting invite. Follow the instructions to "Make it a channel meeting", or create the meeting directly inside the secure Team channel by selecting\nSchedule a meeting\nvia the\nMeet\nbutton inside the channel.\nAdditionally, if participants need to start a meeting immediately, they can select the dropdown option to\nMeet Now\ninside the secure Team channel\nMeet\nbutton.\nFor full instructions, see\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n.	KB0025281	KB0025281 v1.0	 Agreement (BAA) to facilitate private, secure online collaborations for <strong>research</strong>, teaching Storage account: Distributed <strong>research</strong> team working sessions with patient data Clinical researchers interviewing participants or participant teams as part of <strong>research</strong> projects Distributed administrative	5.9455		2026-01-31 16:41:40.423793	2026-01-31 16:41:40.423793	0	582030ff476456583d373803846d43f4
10334	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f7ed3ba2471c3ad0d500b87b716d4358	About Microsoft Teams at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nEligibility\nSoftware\nNew Teams\nLog into Microsoft Teams\nCreate a team\nLearn the basics\nRecordings\nTroubleshooting, help, and feedback\nOverview\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nis a collaboration tool that gives Indiana University students, faculty, staff, and external users the ability to work together and share information in a common space. Included among its features are real-time collaboration, one-on-one chat, and file sharing.\nTeams Calls\nprovides office telephone service for faculty and staff at Indiana University.\nMicrosoft Teams\nMeetings\nis the recommended way to host and schedule live videoconferencing and collaboration at IU.\nFor the security of data stored within Microsoft Teams at IU and SharePoint Online, IU may employ automated tools to detect sensitive information and potential misconfigurations. These tools are crucial for maintaining a secure environment. In cases where a detection occurs, designated IT staff may evaluate the findings to assist people using the service in resolving the issue, consistent with IU’s IT privacy obligations outlined in\nPrivacy of Electronic Information and Information Technology Resources (IT-07)\n.\nEligibility\nIf you are eligible to use\nMicrosoft 365\nat IU, you are eligible to use this application and its related suite of products.\nNote:\nEveryone who is eligible to use\nMicrosoft 365\nat IU can use the\nTeams Calls\nfeature for PC-to-PC calling with other\nMicrosoft Teams\nusers both within and outside of IU. However, you cannot use\nTeams Calls\nto dial by phone number unless you have been assigned an IU office telephone number.\nSoftware\nThis application is included in\nMicrosoft 365\n, and you can access it from\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nNote:\nYou can use the\nMicrosoft Teams\nweb app in a\nfully supported browser\n(Chrome or Edge) for most Teams functions, including PC-to-PC calls (campus calling only), but the dial pad will not appear in the web app for IU employees with office phone numbers. If you want to dial off campus or by phone number, you'll need to install the app.\nDownload Microsoft Teams Desktop and Mobile Apps\n.\nFor mobile clients,\nMicrosoft Teams\napplications are also available via the Google Play Store and the Apple App Store.\nNew Teams\nMicrosoft's new Teams client is now the default for Microsoft Teams at IU, and the classic Teams app is deprecated. If you encounter issues with the new app, select\nSettings and more\n(\n…\n) in the upper right of your Teams client to see if you have the option to toggle\nNew Teams\noff. If you don't see a version toggle on your managed computer, ask your local UITS support person to contact the Endpoint Management team to manage this policy for you.\nLog into\nMicrosoft Teams\nBy default,\nMicrosoft Teams\nlaunches automatically when you log into Windows, although you can change this setting if you wish. You can also set up automatic launch in macOS.\nBy default, all clients will log you in with your primary user account; if you want to log into an alternate account, sign out and log in manually.\nCreate a team\nAny\nMicrosoft\nteam at IU (except for a Canvas course) must be created using IU's\nInstitutional storage request form\n. To access the form in the Teams client, in the left navigation pane, select\nNew Team\n. This process ensures the team is created as private, where specific members are assigned access, and it provides additional safeguards, naming conventions, and tools that will be valuable for storage owners and the IU community.\nTo create a team for your Canvas course, see\nAbout the Microsoft Education app for Canvas\n.\nIf you need to create a team with an alternative template,\ncontact your campus IT Support Center\n.\nManually created teams (created without using the form above) need to be registered on the\nInstitutional storage request form\nwithin seven days, or they will be deleted.  Team owners will be notified via email for three days after creation with this requirement.\nLearn the basics\nTo learn how to perform common tasks in\nMicrosoft Teams\n, see\nMicrosoft Teams help & learning\n.\nRecordings\nAt Indiana University, recordings made in Microsoft Teams on or after October 1, 2025 have a retention policy applied.  This means that by default, the recording will be deleted after 120 days.  No existing recordings from before that date will have a policy applied, nor will this policy affect manually uploaded video content.\nYou can change how long a recording you own is kept.  To do so, select the recording in Microsoft Teams; this will open the video file in Microsoft SharePoint’s Stream interface.  Directly under the player and the recording name, use the drop-down menu to change the deletion date or prevent the file from being deleted at all.\nA bar on the recording in the chat or channel will also warn you when the recording is close to being deleted, offering the option to extend the retention.\nTroubleshooting, help, and feedback\nIf you have trouble with any application, sign out of\nMicrosoft 365\nand completely close your browser or mobile app, and then restart it. You can also switch to a different browser to see if that resolves the issue.\nFor help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0026269	KB0026269 v5.0	Table of contents Overview Eligibility Software New Teams Log into Microsoft Teams Create a team Learn the basics Recordings Troubleshooting, help, and feedback Overview Eligibility Note: Everyone who is eligible to use Microsoft 365 at IU can use the Teams Calls feature for PC-to-PC calling with other Microsoft Teams users both within and outsi	5.325		2026-01-31 16:41:40.423793	2026-01-31 17:14:29.53073	0	f7ed3ba2471c3ad0d500b87b716d4358
10335	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6ac90f754724f698d500b87b716d43fe	Create and store recordings of Zoom meetings	Table of contents\nOverview\nWho can record meetings\nCreate a recording\nFrom a desktop computer\nFrom a videoconferencing room system\nFrom a mobile device\nAccess your recordings\nLocal recordings\nRecording file types\nBack up local recordings\nCloud recordings\nChoose recording layouts\nOverview\nAt Indiana University, you can record your\nZoom\nmeetings and either save the recordings to a local storage device, or save them to the cloud using\nKaltura\n. See below for information about creating and storing recordings of Zoom meetings.\nIf you want to record a Zoom meeting that uses breakout rooms, see\nRecord a Zoom breakout room\n.\nImportant:\nZoom accounts with healthcare permissions do\nnot\nallow cloud recordings. Hosts with these accounts can record Zoom meetings locally to their workstations. Attendees can only record with host permission.\nOnce a recording is complete, follow HIPAA best practices for storing any recording that contains\nprotected health information\n(PHI).\nTo request a new institutional storage account, fill out the\nInstitutional storage request form\n.\nThis system or service meets certain requirements established in the\nHIPAA Security Rule\nthereby enabling its use for work involving data that contain protected health information (\nPHI\n). However, using this system or service does not fulfill your legal responsibilities for protecting the privacy and security of data that contain PHI. You may use this system or service for work involving data that contain PHI only if you institute additional\nadministrative\n,\nphysical\n, and\ntechnical\nsafeguards that complement those UITS already has in place.\nAt Indiana University,\nnever\nstore files containing sensitive\ninstitutional data\n, especially protected health information (\nPHI\n) regulated by the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (\nHIPAA\n), on your desktop workstation, laptop, USB flash drive, tablet, smartphone, or other mobile device unless the files are properly encrypted on the device,\nand\nyour senior executive officer or the IU Institutional Review Board (IRB) has given prior written approval. Because PHI must remain encrypted at rest, make sure you are using full-disk encryption on any device that has research data containing PHI on it. If you are not sure, ask your department or school\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help.\nFor guidance on division of responsibility when using a UITS system approved for PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.\nWho can record meetings\nBy default, only the meeting host can record a Zoom meeting. However, the host can allow specific users to record the meeting through the\nManage Participants\npanel.\nCreate a recording\nFrom a desktop computer\nTo create a recording of a Zoom meeting:\nEnter the Zoom meeting room.\nSelect\nRecord\nand select either\nRecord on this Computer\nor\nRecord to the Cloud\n.\nControls to pause or stop the recording will appear in the menu bar at the bottom of the meeting room:\nWhen you are finished recording, select\nStop Recording\n. If you forget to do this, the recording will end when you exit the meeting room.\nNotes on cloud recordings:\nOnce your meeting has ended, it will need to be processed in Zoom before it can be transferred to Kaltura. Depending on the length of the meeting, this may take some time. To check the status of your recording, log into\nzoom.iu.edu\nand select\nRecordings\nin the navigation menu.\nAt IU, Zoom cloud recordings are stored in Zoom for 120 days. Permanent copies are delivered to the university's media management system,\nKaltura\n.\nDepending on your personal settings, cloud recordings use either "Gallery" or "Active Speaker" views. To review or change these settings, see the steps for\nrecording layouts\n.\nNotes on local recordings:\nBecause files are stored on your local hard drive, you must have enough space available to store the recording.\nRecording file sizes may be in Gigabytes.\nFrom a videoconferencing room system\nVideoconferencing room systems (such as Cisco or Tandberg) are unable to initiate local recordings. To make a local recording, the host must join separately from a Windows or Mac computer running the Zoom client software.\nHowever, you can initiate a cloud (Kaltura) recording from a room system. To get started:\nLog into\nzoom.iu.edu\n.\nAt the top left, select\nProfile\n.\nFind the "Host Key" line and select\nShow\n. Record or memorize this number.\nAt the top left, select\nMeeting Settings\n.\nFind the "Recording" section and select\nEdit\n.\nCheck the box for\nCloud recording\n.\nSelect\nSave Changes\n.\nOnce these settings are enabled, join your Zoom meeting normally from the room system. After joining, use the room system's handheld remote or touch panel to enter the commands below. On some room systems, you may need to press the pound key (#) before entering the commands.\nPress 1 (to open the command menu).\nPress 5 (to initiate the recording).\nEnter your host key from step 3 above.\nPress the pound key (#).\nYou should now see the red recording indicator at the top left of your Zoom meeting. To end the recording, hang up or press 1 and 5 again.\nFrom a mobile device\nYou cannot use a mobile device to initiate a local recording of a Zoom meeting. However, you can initiate a cloud (Kaltura) recording from a mobile device; for instructions, see\nRecording on iOS/Android\n.\nAccess your recordings\nLocal recordings\nWhen you record Zoom meetings locally, each meeting recording is saved on your desktop device to its own folder labeled with the date, time, and meeting name. By default, these folders are inside the\nZoom\nfolder, located inside the\nDocuments\nfolder on Windows, macOS, and Linux. For more about accessing your local recordings and changing the recording location, see\nLocal Recording\n.\nRecording file types\nMeeting recordings consist of raw Zoom files, converted video files, and plain text chat logs; the chat recording and main meeting recording are separate. The easiest way to ensure that all parts of the meeting are backed up is to back up the recording folder itself.\nMeeting recordings are converted from raw Zoom files to MP4 video files automatically. If your recording fails to convert, follow\nZoom's instructions\nto retry conversion or open a ticket.\nBack up local recordings\nUITS recommends backing up recordings stored on your local computer to one of IU's file storage systems. You can also upload the converted MP4 video file to Kaltura. This is very convenient for sharing, but will not preserve the chat transcript.\nCloud recordings\nTo access cloud recordings, see\nAccess and share Zoom recordings in Kaltura\n.\nChoose recording layouts\nYou can choose a recording layout. Before recording a meeting or webinar, review your recording settings:\nLog into\nzoom.iu.edu\n.\nSelect\nSettings\n, and then select the\nRecording\ntab.\nMake sure\nCloud recording\nis toggled on, and then check one or both of the following:\nRecord active speaker with shared screen\nRecord gallery view with shared screen\nYou can browse\nexamples of Zoom recording layouts\n. Note that local recordings always show a thumbnail gallery view with the shared screen.\nTo record an ASL interpreter, the host should spotlight the interpreter's video to ensure it is visible at all times. If viewing or recording in speaker view, the host may also wish to spotlight the presenter so that they are both visible.	KB0025063	KB0025063 v3.0	Table of contents Overview Who can record meetings Create a recording From a <strong>desktop</strong> computer From a <strong>desktop</strong> computer To create a recording of a Zoom meeting: Enter the Zoom meeting room. Select meeting recording is saved on your <strong>desktop</strong> device to its own folder labeled with the date, time	5.9455		2026-01-31 16:41:43.897309	2026-01-31 16:41:43.897309	0	6ac90f754724f698d500b87b716d43fe
10333	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=db85c5fe47c4ee982fc6a55b416d43b1	Zoom for healthcare uses	Table of contents\nOverview\nAlternatives\nEligibility at IU\nDifferences between Zoom with healthcare permissions and standard Zoom accounts\nAccount requests\nOverview\nImportant:\nThis system or service meets certain requirements established in the\nHIPAA Security Rule\nthereby enabling its use for work involving data that contain protected health information (\nPHI\n). However, using this system or service does not fulfill your legal responsibilities for protecting the privacy and security of data that contain PHI. You may use this system or service for work involving data that contain PHI only if you institute additional\nadministrative\n,\nphysical\n, and\ntechnical\nsafeguards that complement those UITS already has in place.\nFor guidance on division of responsibility when using a UITS system approved for PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.\nImportant:\nDue to federal law enforcement concerns regarding foreign government interference and surveillance risks associated with Zoom, it is strongly recommended that individuals involved in research activities discontinue using Zoom. Instead, all virtual meetings should be conducted through\nMicrosoft Teams\n, which meets government requirements for secure and private communications.\nIU Research has chosen to prohibit the use of Zoom by its employees. Other IU divisions may enact similar prohibitions.\nTo learn more about using Microsoft Teams for virtual meetings, see\nUITS IT Training Microsoft resources\n.\nFor questions or further guidance,\ncontact your local UITS Support Center\n.\nIndiana University and Zoom have signed a\nBusiness Associates Agreement (BAA)\nto facilitate private, secure online collaborations for research, teaching, or administration involving the transmission of\nprotected health information\n(PHI).\nFollowing are some examples of Zoom collaborations that involve PHI and should be conducted using a Zoom account with healthcare permissions:\nDistributed research team working sessions with patient data\nClinical researchers interviewing participants or participant teams as part of research projects\nDistributed administrative or technical teams working directly with patient data\nNotes:\nIf the meeting host uses an IU Zoom account with healthcare permissions, the meeting satisfies HIPAA requirements (even if some participants do not have IU Zoom accounts approved for healthcare uses).\nBreakout Rooms are available for Zoom accounts with healthcare permissions; you can enable them in your Zoom settings and use them normally.\nAlternatives\nThe fastest way to gain the ability to host and schedule meetings approved for PHI is by switching to a secure\nMicrosoft Teams\nmeeting; see\nUse Microsoft Teams to host meetings that contain protected health information (PHI)\n.\nEligibility at IU\nAll active IU students, faculty, staff, and affiliates who will be working with PHI in Zoom should request and use a Zoom account with healthcare permissions.\nUsers who are approved for Zoom accounts with healthcare permissions will be placed into a subgroup within IU's primary Zoom instance.\nNotes:\nZoom meetings inherit the settings of the user who creates them, even if that user does not begin or attend the meeting.\nIf you schedule Zoom meetings for others in which PHI is present or discussed, you should use a Zoom account with healthcare permissions.\nAnyone using Zoom accounts with healthcare permissions must be up to date with IU's annual\nHIPAA compliance training\n.\nIU Health employees who do not also have an active IU status are\nnot\neligible for Zoom accounts with healthcare permissions.\nPer HIPAA guidelines, IU Group accounts are\nnot\neligible to host meetings that involve PHI.\nDifferences between Zoom with healthcare permissions and standard Zoom accounts\nWhile standard IU Zoom accounts provide encryption for all data transferred between the Zoom cloud, client, and room, Zoom accounts with healthcare permissions offer:\nEncryption for third-party endpoints (SIP/H.323)\nAnonymized usage reports\nIncreased privacy through limited access to features that are not appropriate when working with PHI\nImportant:\nWaiting rooms are enabled by default for all attendees. Hosts must admit each attendee. If an unexpected attendee attempts to join the meeting, the host can message them while they remain in the waiting room.\nUsers with Zoom accounts with healthcare permissions do not have access to third-party apps offered in the Zoom App Marketplace.\nHosts with Zoom for healthcare permissions can only save chats from health-related sessions to their local workstations. Chats cannot be saved to Zoom cloud storage. Attendees cannot save chats from these sessions.\nZoom accounts with healthcare permissions do\nnot\nallow cloud recordings. Hosts with these accounts can record Zoom meetings locally to their workstations. Attendees can only record with host permission.\nOnce a recording is complete, follow HIPAA best practices for storing any recording that contains\nprotected health information\n(PHI).\nTo request a new institutional storage account, fill out the\nInstitutional storage request form\n.\nAt Indiana University,\nnever\nstore files containing sensitive\ninstitutional data\n, especially protected health information (\nPHI\n) regulated by the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (\nHIPAA\n), on your desktop workstation, laptop, USB flash drive, tablet, smartphone, or other mobile device unless the files are properly encrypted on the device,\nand\nyour senior executive officer or the IU Institutional Review Board (IRB) has given prior written approval. Because PHI must remain encrypted at rest, make sure you are using full-disk encryption on any device that has research data containing PHI on it. If you are not sure, ask your department or school\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help.\nAccount requests\nTo request the appropriate permissions to host PHI-related meetings via Zoom, fill out the\nrequest form\n.\nTo request the removal of healthcare permissions from your account, fill out the\nrequest form to remove Zoom healthcare permissions\n.	KB0026094	KB0026094 v4.0	 (BAA) to facilitate private, secure online collaborations for <strong>research</strong>, teaching, or administration permissions: Distributed <strong>research</strong> team working sessions with patient data Clinical researchers interviewing participants or participant teams as part of <strong>research</strong> projects Distributed administrative	5.9455		2026-01-31 16:41:40.423793	2026-01-31 16:41:51.27993	1	db85c5fe47c4ee982fc6a55b416d43b1
2408	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0026776	Changes to Microsoft 365 storage	Table of contents\nOverview\nStorage quotas\nTimeline\nIf you're over quota\nRecommended actions to reduce storage\nIdentify unneeded data\nMove institutional data to appropriate storage locations\nFind your largest files\nMove data if needed\nOverview\nOver the 2025-2026 academic year, Indiana University will change storage allotments in the Microsoft\nOneDrive\nenvironment to align with new\nMicrosoft storage policies\n.\nThose whose accounts are using at least 90% of the new allotment will be contacted directly.  Local UITS support people will also be provided with an impact list so they can directly support people in their departments.\nStorage quotas\nNew storage allotments are as follows:\nStudents (A5s license):\n50 GB\nFaculty and staff (A5e license):\n100 GB\nAffiliates, group accounts, and retirees (A1 license):\n50 GB\nTimeline\nSeptember 10, 2025:\nAccounts with data storage under 75% of the new allotment were moved to their new quota.\nFebruary 1, 2026:\nAccounts for students and A1 license holders that are near or above the new allotment will have the new quota applied. Faculty and staff (A5e license) accounts will not be affected at this time; more information coming soon.\nMarch 1, 2026:\nAccounts that remain over the assigned quota above will be contacted regularly up until this date.  If an account remains over the quota, a final notice will be issued to the account that its content will be purged, starting from the oldest documents and files until the account is under the quota allocation.\nIf you're over quota\nIf you're over your OneDrive quota, your desktop application and web storage pages will alert you to the fact that OneDrive is full (see\nMy OneDrive says it's full\n).  Until you delete files or empty your recycle bin, you'll be unable to create new files or modify existing files.  At Indiana University, if your account remains over quota after March 1, 2026, some content in your drive will be deleted to bring your account into quota.\nRecommended actions to reduce storage\nIdentify unneeded data\nTo address the underlying storage problem at IU, first determine whether data is still needed and remove it if not, rather than moving unneeded data.  The General Counsel publishes a\nRecords Retention Guideline\n.  If data is retained beyond retention guidelines, this exposes the unit and university to increased risk from data leaks, attacks, and FOIA and other judicial findings.\nMove institutional data to appropriate storage locations\nAs a good rule of thumb, if a coworker would need access to the data if you left your role or the university, it doesn't belong in OneDrive or Google Drive.  Also, be sure not to store Restricted or Critical files in OneDrive. For details, see\nTypes of institutional data appropriate for Microsoft 365 at IU and Google at IU\n.\nWhile UITS generally recommends\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nand\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nfor institutional data, in some cases,\nResearch Technologies platforms\nmay be appropriate storage locations for certain types of institutional data.  Departments and divisions may also choose to run their own file servers or departmental shared drives.\nFind your largest files\nIn the\nOneDrive web interface\n, scroll down to the bottom left corner. Under "Storage", you'll see the amount of storage you're currently using.  Select the linked amount to see the largest files in your OneDrive, in descending order.  Often, Teams recordings are a large addition to a drive; you may be able to delete these if they're no longer needed.\nMove data if needed\nIf you're still using too much storage space after removing data you don't need and transferring institutional data as appropriate, move data to other appropriate locations.\nPersonal data:\nIf personal data is causing your OneDrive to remain over quota, move it to a personal storage account or personal device.  (Remember, IU institutional data should not be moved off of a managed system.)\nResearch data:\nUITS Research Technologies provide multiple\nresearch storage options\n, many of which offer significant storage allotments at no charge.  The\nGlobus\nweb and software platform have direct links into OneDrive and Google Drive and can directly move data to those locations.	KB0026776	KB0026776 v5.0	. If you're over quota If you're over your OneDrive quota, your <strong>desktop</strong> application and web storage cases, <strong>Research</strong> Technologies platforms may be appropriate storage locations for certain types system.) <strong>Research</strong> data: UITS <strong>Research</strong> Technologies provide multiple <strong>research</strong> storage options, many	5.8889		2026-01-31 14:35:52.330803	2026-01-31 16:41:54.738929	0	139bd1ac47e67ad0d500b87b716d436b
10347	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9866a8073b22ea58476d941864e45a78	Keep working from remote locations	At Indiana University, if you need to work from home or another remote location, consider the following technology issues in advance:\nComputer equipment:\nRemote employees should use only IU-owned IT devices with an installed Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) tool (such as CrowdStrike or Windows Defender for Endpoints, depending upon availability). If IU-owned IT devices are not available, you must exclusively use remote access tools such as Virtual Desktop Infrastructure (\nIUanyWare\n) or\nRemote Desktop\n. Ensure that any device used for IU activities has been reviewed by departmental IT support staff for compliance with\nSecurity of Information Technology Resources (IT-12)\n.\nVPN connection:\nUse of\nVPN\nis required when accessing any system that handles\nCritical or Restricted data\n.\nConnectivity:\nDo you have a high-speed internet connection at home? Many high-speed solutions are offered in the area of most IU campuses, including cable, DSL, and fiber. Contact your local provider of choice for information about services, pricing, and estimated installation dates.\nSecurity:\nTake\nproper security measures on your home computer\nbefore connecting to IU resources. Be sure to install all the updates for your operating system, and have an updated antivirus application installed and a\nfirewall\nenabled. If you don't, your computer is at risk for being infected with a\nvirus\n, worm, or other malware, which might prevent you from finishing your tasks.\nAccess:\nYou will need your\nIU username and passphrase\nto access many IU resources (such as\nOne.IU\n). Also, be sure you have what you need for\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n.\nEmail:\nYou have multiple options for checking your work email, including using your default web browser or configuring a stand-alone email client. Select the best way for you to\nread your IU Exchange mail\n.\nApplications:\nIU offers many university-licensed software packages for day-to-day work requirements. These software options are available virtually though\nIUanyWare\n, or you can download and install them directly from\nIUware\n.\nStore files:\nDo you need to store and share files, particularly files containing\nCritical or Restricted data\n?\nIf you need to access and store institutional data that contains personally identifiable information (PII), do\nnot\nuse your desktop or mobile device to store this data locally. Institutional data classified as Critical or Restricted (including student data,\nprotected health information\n(PHI), research data, and employee information) may already be stored in\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nand\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\n. If you need to request secure storage, see\nRequest institutional storage in Microsoft shared storage\n.\nImportant:\nShared storage in Google is available\nonly as an option for departments that want to\npay for continued storage in Google\n.\nDue to\nlimits to IU's storage footprint in the Google platform\n, all individual\nGoogle My Drive\ns now have a\n5 GB\nquota.\nAlso, see:\nProtect sensitive data in Microsoft at IU Secure Storage\nPaid storage in Google at IU Shared Drives\nFor data that is not Restricted or Critical, UITS offers multiple options through the\nCloud Storage Portal\n. For more about available services, see\nStore and share your individual files at IU\n.\nTo ensure the availability and security of institutional data, where practicable, use cloud storage solutions appropriate for the relevant data classification. When hard-copy material cannot be kept on campus, you must store those materials in a secure location that only you can access (for example, a locked storage cabinet within your home or remote location). Do not store sensitive data in cars or in public spaces where unauthorized individuals can view or access the information.\nVideoconferencing:\nUse\nZoom\nto connect remotely with an individual or a group. Zoom provides audio, video, and collaboration tools either via the web or through a standalone application on your computer or mobile phone.\nNote:\nZoom at IU offers several features and options that can help you maintain the integrity of your Zoom meeting or webinar. For tips to help secure your Zoom meeting and also help prevent\nZoombombing\n(where uninvited users enter your Zoom meeting and use the screen share feature to display inappropriate content), see\nPrevent Zoombombing using Zoom privacy and security features\n.\nImportant:\nYou shouldn't record meetings that may involve critical data or FERPA-protected information (for example, advising sessions or individual discussions with students regarding their education records, including grades). Meetings involving FERPA-protected student information and Protected Health Information (PHI) should not be stored on the Zoom cloud service or\non Kaltura\n. If you have a requirement to record a meeting that will involve FERPA or critical institutional information, consult with the appropriate\nData Steward\non storage and retention requirements.\nIf you plan to use Zoom to host meetings that involve any type\nHIPAA\n-regulated\npersonal health information\n(PHI), whether or not the meetings are recorded, you must use a\nZoom account approved for healthcare uses\n.\nCollaboration:\nAll students, faculty, and staff can use\nMicrosoft Teams\nto collaborate in a common space; see\nAbout Microsoft Teams at IU\n.\nUITS staff:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nUITS staff:\nSome departments have also opted to use\nSlack\nfor office communications, which requires an authorized administrator. To request the creation of a shared channel in Slack for UITS, post in the #slack-requests channel. Also, see the\ndetails about channels shared with non-UITS staff\n.\nAdditional considerations for faculty or staff:\nIf you need to use remote desktop, departmental servers or resources, or other services used by your department, you may need to speak to your\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help replicating your workplace setup at home. If you don't know who your local UITS support person is, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0024943	KB0024943 v2.0	, you must exclusively use remote access tools such as Virtual <strong>Desktop</strong> Infrastructure (IUanyWare) or Remote <strong>Desktop</strong>. Ensure that any device used for IU activities has been reviewed by departmental institutional data that contains personally identifiable information (PII), do not use your <strong>desktop</strong>	5.6252		2026-01-31 16:41:58.075479	2026-01-31 16:41:58.075479	0	9866a8073b22ea58476d941864e45a78
10352	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b52cd132476efe58d500b87b716d4359	Generative AI tools at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nTools available at IU\nOther publicly available tools\nAI features in other IU applications\nFeatures, availability, and approved uses\nOverview\nNote:\nFor questions on how to use a specific AI tool, it is recommended you ask the tool for help (for example, to learn how to use Gemini to create an image, go to\nGoogle Gemini\nand ask the tool for instructions).\nGenerative AI tools are becoming increasingly important in various fields, including education, research, and administration. At Indiana University, several generative AI tools are available to faculty, staff, and students to enhance productivity, creativity, and efficiency. This document provides an overview of the primary generative AI tools approved for use at IU, their core features, data classification approvals, and usage guidelines.\nTools available at IU\nImportant:\nAlways use your IU account for Indiana University business. Personal (free) non-IU accounts are not approved for institutional data. When working in these apps, check to see which account you're signed in with before sharing any institutional data with these AI tools.\nResearch data for many projects may not have constraints, but the lead investigator for a project is responsible for accurately determining whether such constraints exist. If you have questions, contact the Data Steward for Research Data (\ndatard@iu.edu\n).\nAt IU, the following AI tools have been reviewed and are available for enterprise-wide use.\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot Chat:\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot Chat is built into the Microsoft Edge browser and is available on the web. Available at no cost for all IU students, faculty, and staff, this version of Microsoft 365 Copilot offers a basic generative AI chatbot interface. Microsoft 365 Copilot Chat is built on the same AI models and data as ChatGPT-5o, so you can use it to write and summarize content, create code, answer complex questions, and more. It also allows public web searches and image generation with DALL-E. When used with your\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\naccount, Microsoft 365 Copilot Chat is approved for\nPublic\n,\nUniversity-internal\n,\nRestricted\n, and\nCritical\ndata, with the exception of research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints, and Payment Card Industry Data Security Standards (PCI DSS) data.\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot app:\nThis version of Microsoft 365 Copilot offers advanced features and integration with Microsoft 365 applications. It is available for faculty, staff, and group accounts at IU. It is a paid service; the cost is $30/user/month, billed annually. The Microsoft 365 Copilot app accesses all data you can access within IU's Microsoft 365 ecosystem (SharePoint, OneDrive, Outlook, Teams, etc.), it is critical to apply\nsensitivity labels\nto all documents containing\nCritical data\nthat are not required for use by Copilot. When used with your\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\naccount, the Microsoft 365 Copilot app is approved for\nPublic\n,\nUniversity-internal\n,\nRestricted\n, and\nCritical\ndata, with the exception of research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints, and Payment Card Industry Data Security Standards (PCI DSS) data.\nGoogle Gemini:\nGoogle Gemini is a basic generative AI chatbot interface available on the web and through mobile apps for iOS and Android. It is available at no cost to all IU students, faculty, and staff. It is built on Google's Gemini AI models and includes unique features, such as a response verification feature powered by Google Search and the ability to create custom AI agents (called "Gems") for specific tasks. When accessed with your\nGoogle at IU\naccount, Google Gemini is approved for\nPublic\n,\nUniversity-internal\n, and\nRestricted\ndata, with the exception of research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints.\nGoogle NotebookLM:\nGoogle NotebookLM is a generative AI research assistant. It is available on the web at no cost to all IU students, faculty, and staff. Built on Google's Gemini AI models, NotebookLM allows users to upload their own materials. such as PDFs and Google Docs. and interact with them by creating summaries, study guides, audio overviews, and more. It is especially useful for synthesizing large volumes of content across multiple documents. When accessed with your\nGoogle at IU\naccount, Google Notebook LM is approved for\nPublic\n,\nUniversity-internal\n, and\nRestricted\ndata, with the exception of research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints.\nChatGPT Edu:\nChatGPT Edu is a secure, privacy-focused version of OpenAI's generative AI platform, designed for responsible use with university data. Information entered in IU's workspace is not used to train AI models. IU ChatGPT Edu supports advanced writing, editing, coding, math, and data analysis; document summarization, web browsing, and building custom GPTs; and offers higher message limits along with faster performance in more than 50 languages. When accessed through your IU account, the ChatGPT Edu app is approved for\nPublic\n,\nUniversity-internal\n, and\nRestricted\ndata, with the exception of research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints.\nAdobe Firefly:\nThis tool is used for generating and editing multimedia content, such as images, within Adobe applications. It is approved for\nPublic\ndata and is part of IU's Adobe contract. It is available to all users with access to Adobe Creative Cloud. Adobe Firefly is available on the web and is integrated into other Adobe web, mobile, and desktop applications, including Adobe Express, InDesign, Photoshop, and Illustrator.\nAI at IU Toolkit:\nThe\nAI at IU developer toolkits\nallow developers build their own AI tools using various underlying technologies and AI models.  These include:\nAzure hosted LLM service:\nAzure OpenAI Service\nis a cloud-based software development platform that provides REST API access to powerful language models, including the GPT-4, GPT-35-Turbo, and Embeddings model series. When used with your IU account, it is approved for\nPublic\n,\nUniversity-internal\n,\nRestricted\n, and\nCritical\ndata, with the exception of research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints, and Payment Card Industry Data Security Standards (PCI DSS) data.\nREALLMS:\nThe\nResearch and Academic LLM Services (REALLMS)\nis an on-premise service, hosted in the IU Data Center, offered without fee to the IU community by UITS Research Technologies. At IU, REALLMS is approved for use with\nRestricted\nand\nCritical\ndata, including protected health information (\nPHI\n), with the exception of research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints.\nOther publicly available tools\nIn addition to the tools listed above, several other generative AI tools are available online (for example, the public versions of ChatGPT, Claude, and Perplexity, among others), some with a cost associated with them. These tools are\nnot approved for any institutional data at IU\n, even when data are anonymized. See\nAcceptable uses of generative AI at IU\n.\nAI features in other IU applications\nThe university is continuously monitoring the generative AI environment, including generative AI features being developed into its existing suite of tools. For example, Ally, a tool that helps instructors improve the accessibility of their Canvas sites, includes an\nAI assistant\nfor creating alternative text descriptions for images. At IU, the purchase of any new technology tool or service, including generative AI tools/services, must be approved through the Software and Services Selection Process (SSSP).\nIU community:\nFor more about the SSSP and how to submit a request, see\nAbout the Software and Services Selection Process (SSSP)\n.\nFeatures, availability, and approved uses\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot Chat:\nAlternative names:\nCopilot for Web, Bing Chat Enterprise\nAvailability:\nIU students, faculty, and staff\nCore features:\nGenerate text-based responses to prompts, summarize information, assist with content creation, perform public web searches, generate images with DALL-E\nData classification approval:\nPublic, University-internal, Restricted, Critical; not approved for PCI DSS data or research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints\nUnderlying technology/AI models:\nGPT-5o, DALL-E\nData protections:\nChat history is only available to the logged-in user and the University Information Security Office (in the case of an IT incident). Microsoft does not use your data to train the underlying AI models.\nCost:\nNo cost\nWhere to access:\nBuilt into Edge browser,\nhttps://M365Copilot.com\nGet started:\nAbout Microsoft 365 Copilot at IU\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot app:\nAlternative names:\nCopilot Pro, M365 Copilot, Copilot for Microsoft 365\nAvailability:\nIU faculty, staff, and group accounts\nCore features:\nAbility to summarize and work with documents in OneDrive and Teams storage spaces to which you have access; integration with Microsoft 365 apps, such as Word, Outlook, and PowerPoint; meeting summaries in Teams\nData classification approval:\nPublic, University-internal, Restricted, Critical; not approved for PCI DSS data or research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints;\nsensitivity labels\nmust be applied to all documents containing\nCritical data\nUnderlying technology/AI models:\nGPT-5o, DALL-E\nData protections:\nChat history is available only to the logged-in user and the University Information Security Office (in the case of an IT incident). Microsoft does not use your data to train the underlying AI models.\nCost:\n$30/user/month, billed annually\nWhere to access:\nMicrosoft 365 applications;\nhttps://M365Copilot.com\nGet started:\nPurchase a Microsoft 365 Copilot app license\nGoogle Gemini:\nAlternative names:\nGemini (formerly Bard)\nAvailability:\nAll IU students, faculty, and staff\nCore features:\nGenerate text and images, answer questions, summarize or draft content, assist with research and brainstorming, verify responses using Google Search ("Double-check" feature), create custom AI agents called "Gems"\nData classification approval:\nPublic, University-internal, Restricted; not approved for research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints\nUnderlying technology/AI models:\nGoogle Gemini model family\nData protections:\nData is protected when accessed with your Google at IU account. Chats and uploaded files are not reviewed by humans or used to train generative AI models. Chat history is saved for up to 72 hours and remains within IU's domain.\nCost:\nNo cost\nWhere to access:\nhttps://gemini.google.com\n, Gemini mobile apps for iOS and Android\nGet started:\nAbout Google Gemini at IU\nGoogle NotebookLM:\nAlternative names:\nNotebookLM\nAvailability:\nAll IU students, faculty, and staff\nCore features:\nUpload your own documents (for example, PDFs, Google Docs), ask questions about uploaded content, get summaries, generate study guides and audio overviews, synthesize information across sources, share notebooks with others\nData classification approval:\nPublic, University-internal, Restricted; not approved for research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints\nUnderlying technology/AI models:\nGoogle Gemini model family\nData protections:\nData is protected when accessed with your Google at IU account. Files and interactions are not used to train generative AI models.\nCost:\nNo cost\nWhere to access:\nhttps://notebooklm.google.com\nGet started:\nAbout Google NotebookLM at IU\nChatGPT Edu:\nAlternative names:\nChatGPT\nAvailability:\nAll IU faculty and staff\nCore features:\nSupports advanced writing, editing, coding, math, and data analysis; document summarization, web browsing, and building custom GPTs; and offers higher message limits along with faster performance in more than 50 languages\nData classification approval:\nPublic, University-internal, Restricted; not approved for research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints\nUnderlying technology/AI models:\nChatGPT models\nData protections:\nChat history is available only to the logged-in user. OpenAI does not use data to train the underlying AI models.\nCost:\nNo cost\nWhere to access:\nhttps://chatgpt.com\n, mobile apps for iOS and Android\nGet started:\nAbout ChatGPT Edu at IU\nAdobe Firefly:\nAlternative names:\nFirefly\nAvailability:\nAll users\nCore features:\nGenerating and editing multimedia content, such as images, in Adobe applications\nData classification approval:\nPublic\nUnderlying technology/AI models:\nProprietary Adobe model trained on Adobe Stock, licensed content, and content in the public domain\nData protections:\nAdobe does not use customer content to train its generative AI models.\nCost:\nNo cost (limited by\nmonthly generative credits\nfor each user)\nWhere to access:\nFirefly on the web, Adobe Express, InDesign, Photoshop, Adobe Stock, Illustrator\nGet started:\nAdobe Firefly Overview\nAI at IU Toolkit:\nTwo services provide toolkits for developers, namely:\nAzure hosted LLM service:\nAvailability:\nIU faculty, staff, and group accounts\nCore features:\nBuild your own AI tool for developers\nData classification approval:\nPublic, University-internal, Restricted, Critical; not approved for PCI DSS data or research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints\nUnderlying technology/AI models:\nOpenAI\nData protections:\nSame security capabilities as Microsoft Azure; your data will not be used to train the underlying model\nCost:\nContact\newahelp@iu.edu\nfor quote.\nWhere to access:\nContact\newahelp@iu.edu\nfor more information.\nGet started:\nAbout Azure OpenAI at IU\n(requires IU Knowledge Base login)\nResearch and Academic LLM Services\nAlternative names:\nREALLMS\nAvailability:\nAnyone with an IU account\nCore features:\nLLM-powered chat; API connection under development\nData classification approval:\nPublic, University-internal, Restricted, Critical (including PHI); not approved for research data with contractual, regulatory, or legal constraints\nUnderlying technology/AI models:\nOpen WebUI/various models available to choose from\nData protections:\nAll hardware is on premise in the IU Data Center.\nCost:\nNo cost\nWhere to access:\nhttps://reallms.rescloud.iu.edu/\nGet started:\nAbout the Research and Academic LLM Services (REALLMS) at IU	KB0026507	KB0026507 v23.0	 tools are becoming increasingly important in various fields, including education, <strong>research</strong> institutional data with these AI tools. <strong>Research</strong> data for many projects may not have constraints, but the lead. If you have questions, contact the Data Steward for <strong>Research</strong> Data (datard@iu.edu). At IU	5.5989		2026-01-31 16:42:01.464924	2026-01-31 16:42:01.464924	0	b52cd132476efe58d500b87b716d4359
10383	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9d04e09147f9ba1c2fc6a55b416d43fd	UITS Support Center job opportunities at IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis	Table of contents\nSupport Center consultant position\nJob description\nWorking conditions and equipment requirements\nScheduling\nCompensation\nApply online\nPolicy notice\nSupport Center consultant position\nThe\nUITS Support Center\nis renowned for providing excellent IT support to Indiana University and IU-supported communities. Consultants in this position can gain gain marketable customer service and technical skills. Our operational structure requires staff who are motivated by growth and opportunity. Many former consultants have used their experience to obtain positions with reputable firms and organizations worldwide.\nApplicants will be considered based on:\nCustomer service skills and work experience\nTechnical skills and abilities\nApplicant availability\nPart-time consultants are scheduled to work between 20-29 hours per week and are required to be available to work evening and/or weekend shifts. We require a commitment to work at least two full semesters (eight months).\nMake sure your application is complete, accurate, and representative of your skills, abilities, and work history. Incomplete applications will be rejected. Once submitted, your application will be reviewed, and you will be notified of your status via email. Please be patient, as the hiring process often takes 30 to 60 days.\nJob description\nSupport Center consultants provide tier 1 technical assistance to students, faculty, and staff of the IU-supported communities via phone, email, and chat. Job duties include, but are not limited to, the following:\nProvides technical advising and troubleshooting support to clients in order to resolve problems reported for multiple service areas, including but not limited to:\nDesktop and mobile computing device platforms (including Windows, macOS, iOS, Android and others)\nCentral systems (email, learning management systems, storage platforms, etc.)\nCampus networking (wired and wireless connectivity)\nDocuments all client interactions for research, review, and future reference.\nFollows up with clients until a satisfactory resolution is reached. As necessary, escalates unresolved issues to the appropriate supervisors, tier 2 staff, and administrators.\nUpdates technical documentation in the IU Knowledge Base for both internal and public consumption.\nThis position requires the ability to communicate effectively and to operate computers and other related technical equipment. The person in this role must be able to perform the essential functions with or without accommodation.\nWorking conditions and equipment requirements\nConsultants are eligible for a hybrid schedule (mix between remote and in-office work), subject to change based on university policy and business needs.\nWe ask that our hybrid remote work employees have access to a reliable internet connection and a dedicated, fixed location within the United States that is free of distractions and dependencies. A laptop and docking station are provided to consultants. Consultants must provide their own peripherals. A headset with a microphone in good working order is required for this position.\nNote:\nConsultants must be present in the office for their initial orientation to pick up equipment and complete I-9 forms.\nScheduling\nConsultants are assigned shifts at the beginning of each semester, based on consultant availability. Shifts are assigned based on a combination of staffing needs, consultant availability, seniority, and attendance history.\nSupport Center consultants are required to work a minimum of 20 hours per week and may work a maximum of 29 hours per week. Consultants must be able to work some evenings and weekend shifts.\nCompensation\nStarting wage is $15 per hour for consultants at both IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis.\nPositions include work-study opportunities.  For more, see the Student Central Work-Study pages for\nIU Bloomington\nor\nIU Indianapolis\n.\nApply online\nIf you're interested in becoming a Support Center consultant, complete the\nApplication for Consultant Position\n.\nApplicants not affiliated with Indiana University should select\nContinue as Guest\n. Use the dropdown to select the appropriate application for your campus, IU Bloomington or IU Indianapolis.\nFor questions or more information about Support Center job opportunities, email\nschiring@iu.edu\n.\nPolicy notice\nNotice:\nIndiana University is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access/Affirmative Action institution. IU intends to maintain an alcohol- and drug-free workplace and to comply with the Drug Free Workplace Act of 1988 and its amendments. To that end, all employees must comply with the university's Substance Free Workplace policy. Policy statements and crime and fire statistics for Indiana University campuses are available at\nAnnual Security Reports\n.	KB0023728	KB0023728 v5.0	 to: <strong>Desktop</strong> and mobile computing device platforms (including Windows, macOS, iOS, Android and others (wired and wireless connectivity) Documents all client interactions for <strong>research</strong>, review, and future	4.3727		2026-01-31 16:42:18.644436	2026-01-31 16:42:18.644436	0	9d04e09147f9ba1c2fc6a55b416d43fd
10033	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=563cf49b1bb0d25416c02fc4bd4bcbd9	IU account types and eligibility	Table of contents\nOverview\nAccount types\nAccount eligibility\nCurrent IU students, faculty, and staff\nAll others\nNotes\nOverview\nNote:\nFor accounts and services available to non-enrolled students, see\nIU account access chart for non-enrolled students\n.\nThe following describes the different types of accounts available to enrolled students, faculty, staff, and others associated with Indiana University, as well as the systems they can access.\nAccount types\nAttributes of various account types\nAttributes\nPersonal account\nGroup account\nAffiliate account\nConference\nIU Guest account\nGeneral purpose\nIU account for faculty, staff, students, ACNP employees,\naffiliates\n, and Purdue students and employees in Indianapolis; allows provisioning of other accounts\nSponsored account by faculty and staff to be used for departmental or shared use\nContractors and vendors, sponsored by faculty and staff, for longer-term use\nTemporary account for computer use at IU, sponsored by faculty or staff.\nLight credential used to provide guest authentication through the\nIU Login\napplication code GUEST or ANY. An example would be for guest authentication to\nCanvas\n.\nDefault accounts\nADS\n(also see\nYour University account (formerly Network ID)\nIU Login\nGuest Account\nHas an IU email account\nExchange\nExchange\nExchange\nNo\nNo\nAdditional accounts\nComputing accounts at IU\nComputing accounts at IU\nComputing accounts at IU\nNo\nNo\nBulk generation\nNo\nNo\nNo\nYes\nNo\nWireless and internet access\nIU users: Wireless access to IU Secure or eduroam; wired access with registered MAC address\nPurdue students and employees: Network access via Purdue credentials only\nNo wireless access\nto IU Secure or eduroam; wired access with registered MAC address\nWireless access to IU Secure or eduroam; wired access with registered MAC address\nLimited internet access (web browsing) to IU Secure or eduroam;\nIU Guest wireless\nis also available. Access to wired network in campus housing requires manual registration by the\nSupport Center\n.\nNo wireless or wired internet access\nTime limit\nMust be active in SIS, HRMS, or Affiliate data feeds\nNone\nSponsor can request up to one year\nYes, established at time of request\nNone\nProvisioning site\nCreate My First IU Account\nYour Accounts\nManage your affiliates\nManage your conference accounts\nCreate\nIU Guest account\nMain Knowledge Base document\nYour University account (formerly Network ID)\nand\nComputing accounts at IU\nAbout group and departmental accounts\nSponsor a computing account for an IU affiliate\nAbout sponsored accounts for conference participants at IU\nAbout IU Guest accounts\nRecommendations\nAll IU faculty, staff, students, affiliates, Purdue students and employees, and others need one of these accounts.\nSee\nAbout group and departmental accounts\n.\nLong-term contractor or vendor use; see\nEligibility to Use Information Technology Resources (IT-03)\n.\nSee\nAbout sponsored accounts for conference participants at IU\n.\nFor those needing access to Canvas and One.IU\nAccount eligibility\nCurrent IU students, faculty, and staff\nSystem\nEnrolled\nunder-graduate\nstudent\nEnrolled\ngraduate\nstudent\nFaculty\nStaff\nPart-time\nemployee\nAcademic,\nnon-paid (ACNP)\nGroup/\ndepartment\nAccess Management System (AMS)\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nAdobe Creative Cloud\nYes\nSee notes\nYes\nSee notes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nNo\nADS\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nBig Red 200\nFaculty sponsorship required\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nBUY.IU\nApproval required\nApproval required\nYes\nYes\nYes\nApproval required\nNo\nCanvas\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nActivation required\nChrome River\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nApproval required\nNo\neduroam\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nNo\nEmployee Center\nNo\nNo\nYes\nYes\nYes\nNo\nNo\nePTO\nNo\nNo\nYes