10439	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7ddf203b476456583d373803846d4367	Apply for an account on the SDA	For eligibility requirements, see the\n"Research system accounts (all campuses)"\nsection in\nComputing accounts at IU\n.\nTo request an individual (personal) account on the Indiana University\nScholarly Data Archive\n(\nSDA\n), follow the instructions in\nGet additional IU computing accounts\n.\nIf you are eligible to request an\nSDA\naccount, but the\nSDA\nis not listed among the accounts available for you to request, contact your campus\nSupport Center\nfor help.\nTo request an\nSDA\naccount for your IU\ngroup account\n, use the\nSDA Group Account Request\nform.\nAfter submitting your account request, UITS will notify you via email when your account is ready for use.\nNote:\nIn accordance with standards for access control mandated by the\nHIPAA\nSecurity\nRule\n, you are not permitted to access data containing protected health information (\nPHI\n) using a group (or departmental) account. To ensure accountability and maintain appropriate levels of access control, all users must use an individual login for all work involving PHI.\nTo get started on the\nSDA\n(once your account is created), see\nAccess the SDA at IU\n.\nThe\nSDA\nis maintained by UITS Research Storage. If you have questions about the\nSDA\n, or need help, contact the UITS Research Storage team (\nstore-admin@iu.edu\n).	KB0023685	KB0023685 v1.0	To get started on the SDA (once your account is created), see Access the SDA at IU. The SDA is maintained by UITS Research Storage. If you have questions about the SDA, or need help, contact the UITS Research Storage team (store-admin@iu.edu).	21.6175		2026-01-31 16:44:53.222797	2026-01-31 16:46:15.69623	0	7ddf203b476456583d373803846d4367
10487	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3b20b43347a456583d373803846d4344	Questions you'll need to answer when requesting research computing accounts	When requesting accounts on the following Indiana University systems administered by\nUITS\nResearch Technologies\n, you will be prompted for additional information:\nIU\nresearch supercomputers\n:\nFor accounts on IU\nresearch supercomputers\n, you will be asked to provide:\nCountry of citizenship\nPrimary discipline\nPrimary sub-discipline\nSecondary discipline\nSecondary sub-discipline\nTertiary discipline\nTertiary sub-discipline\nYou will also be asked to:\nSend a list of citations to any intellectual property (for example, journal articles, reports, presentations) developed as a result of your use of the system to\nresearchtechnologies@iu.edu\nonce a year;\nCite your use of the system and other university resources in your publications;\nIndicate whether you will be storing\nHIPAA\ndata (\nPHI\n) on the system, and, if so, acknowledge that you have read and understand your responsibilities with respect to HIPAA data (PHI).\nResearch Database Complex (RDC):\nFor\nRDC\naccounts, you will be asked to agree to the following:\nAlthough these systems are appropriate for use in research projects involving ePHI, you and your collaborators are responsible for maintaining the privacy and security of any sensitive data in compliance with applicable federal and state regulations and university policies. Any software (including operating systems) and services you deploy or administer on these systems are not automatically HIPAA-aligned. IU researchers must acquire the appropriate Institutional Review Board (IRB) approvals and submit a data management plan before storing ePHI on IU research systems, and are responsible for maintaining administrative and technical controls that restrict access to sensitive research data. For more information, please see\nhttps://kb.iu.edu/d/biaa\n.\nBy checking the box below, you are confirming the following:\nYou understand your responsibilities with respect to sensitive data and will act to uphold those responsibilities.\nYou agree to store no data classified as Critical other than ePHI data on RDC resources.\nYou will use your database and/or web-services for research purposes.\nYou agree to acknowledge the use of Indiana University Research Technologies in publications that result from your research, and you will provide periodic listings of their citations upon request.\nYou agree to annually renew the account.\nScholarly Data Archive\n(\nSDA\n):\nFor\nSDA\naccounts, you will be prompted as follows:\nProvide additional information by filling out the following form (note that all fields are required). If you have questions, email\nstore-admin@iu.edu\n.\nDepartment:\nPhone Number:\nStorage space needed\nImmediately:\nWithin 6 months:\nWithin 12 months:\nEstimated number of files:\nDuration of storage:\nAverage file size:\nComments:\n(a brief description of the project that will use the\nSDA\nand the manner in which\nSDA\nwill be used)	KB0025574	KB0025574 v1.0	. You agree to annually renew the account. <strong>Scholarly</strong> <strong>Data</strong> <strong>Archive</strong> (SDA): For SDA accounts resources in your publications; Indicate whether you will be storing HIPAA <strong>data</strong> (PHI) on the system <strong>data</strong> (PHI). Research Database Complex (RDC): For RDC accounts, you will be asked to agree	19.2616		2026-01-31 16:46:24.095052	2026-01-31 16:46:24.095052	0	3b20b43347a456583d373803846d4344
11729	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3240bcb347a456583d373803846d435e	Sign into and out of Adobe Creative Cloud or Adobe Acrobat in IUanyWare	Table of contents\nSign into Adobe\nSign out of Adobe\nSign into Adobe\nWhen you launch an Adobe product in\nIUanyWare\n, you'll be prompted with a "Sign in" window. To sign in and use Adobe via IUanyWare:\nIn the email address field, enter your\nIU username\nfollowed by\n@iu.edu\n(in other words,\nusername@iu.edu\n), even if your primary IU email address is different, and wait for redirection.\nLog in with your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nYou should now have access to Adobe software. If you receive a "Limit Reached" notice, select\nSign me out of the other computers so I can sign in here\n, and then click\nContinue\n.\nNote:\nThe\nManage Activated Devices\nlink will not work if you use an Enterprise login.\nSign out of Adobe\nIU's Adobe Creative Cloud license allows two concurrent active instances of Creative Cloud. You can install Creative Cloud on as many computers as you want, but you can only be logged in on two of them at any given time. If you attempt to log into a third instance, you will receive a warning and an opportunity to deactivate (in other words, log out of) the other instances. For this reason, if you have Creative Cloud installed on more than one computer,\nUITS\nrecommends that you deactivate Creative Cloud any time you are not actively using it.\nUITS also recommends that you sign out of your Adobe account just before closing your application in IUanyWare, even if you only use Adobe via IUanyWare. To do so, in the Adobe application, click\nHelp\nand then select\nSign Out\n.	KB0026171	KB0026171 v1.0	Table of contents Sign into Adobe Sign out of Adobe Sign into Adobe When you launch an Adobe product in IUanyWare, you'll be prompted with a "Sign in" window. To sign in and use Adobe via IUanyWare: In the email address field, enter your IU username followed by @iu.edu (in other words, username@iu.edu), even if your primary IU email address is d	2.1869		2026-01-31 17:16:29.588617	2026-01-31 17:16:29.588617	0	3240bcb347a456583d373803846d435e
10341	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b01cc3754728f698d500b87b716d4337	About Zoom at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nUse Zoom\nRun a meeting\nGet help\nHelp documentation and training\nPedagogical or technical assistance\nOverview\nImportant:\nZoom requires you to update your software if it's more than nine months behind the current version. If your version of Zoom software is older than nine months, you will be blocked from joining meetings until you upgrade. For help, see\nUpgrading Zoom to the latest version\n. Upgrading your Zoom software lets you take advantage of all available features and avoid potential issues. For more, see\nZoom Software Quarterly Lifecycle Policy\n.\nZoom released their final update for 32-bit versions of Zoom for Windows on November 17, 2025; 32-bit Windows will not be supported beyond this version.\nCheck if your Windows computer is on Windows 32-bit\n.\nZoom is a web collaboration tool available to all Indiana University students, faculty, and staff. Zoom provides high-quality audio and video, breakout rooms, whiteboarding tools, the ability to easily add content to meetings "on the fly", and the option to download meeting recordings as MP4 files.\nStandard Zoom meetings support up to\n300\nsimultaneous participants. Licenses for large meetings (up to 500 participants) and webinars in two sizes (up to 1,000 or 3,000 participants) are available to faculty and staff; to request a license,\nemail UITS Videoconferencing Support\n.\nZoom integrates into Canvas as an external tool, and has apps for all platforms, including fully featured iOS and Android apps. Zoom's high-quality video and recording features also allow for recording simple videos with a webcam.\nAnyone who has the meeting URL can attend a Zoom meeting even if they aren't affiliated with IU; however, only those in the IU community can create Zoom accounts and schedule meetings. Non-IU attendees count toward the 300-person maximum capacity.\nImportant:\nYou shouldn't record meetings that may involve critical data or FERPA-protected information (for example, advising sessions or individual discussions with students regarding their education records, including grades). Meetings involving FERPA-protected student information and Protected Health Information (PHI) should not be stored on the Zoom cloud service or\non Kaltura\n. If you have a requirement to record a meeting that will involve FERPA or critical institutional information, consult with the appropriate\nData Steward\non storage and retention requirements.\nIf you plan to use Zoom to host meetings that involve any type\nHIPAA\n-regulated\npersonal health information\n(PHI), whether or not the meetings are recorded, you must use a\nZoom account approved for healthcare uses\n.\nImportant:\nDue to federal law enforcement concerns regarding foreign government interference and surveillance risks associated with Zoom, it is strongly recommended that individuals involved in research activities discontinue using Zoom. Instead, all virtual meetings should be conducted through\nMicrosoft Teams\n, which meets government requirements for secure and private communications.\nIU Research has chosen to prohibit the use of Zoom by its employees. Other IU divisions may enact similar prohibitions.\nTo learn more about using Microsoft Teams for virtual meetings, see\nUITS IT Training Microsoft resources\n.\nFor questions or further guidance,\ncontact your local UITS Support Center\n.\nUse Zoom\nGet started with Zoom\nJoin a Zoom meeting\nAccommodate international students who can't access Zoom\nAbout Zoom roles and permissions\nUse Zoom on a desktop or mobile device\nUse Zoom in Canvas\nUse Zoom for large video meetings or webinars at IU\nManage departmental meetings in Zoom\nUse Zoom breakout rooms\nAccessibility features in Zoom\nAbout closed captioning in Zoom\nSave Zoom meeting chats\nFor important information about Zoom privacy settings for macOS, see\nGranting macOS permissions for the Zoom client\n.\nRun a meeting\nAdjust Zoom settings for standard meetings\nCreate and store recordings of Zoom meetings\nUse screen layouts with Zoom\nPrevent Zoombombing using Zoom privacy and security features\nSecure publicly advertised Zoom meetings\nGet help\nHelp documentation and training\nImprove and troubleshoot your Zoom experience\nTroubleshoot audio and video issues on your computer or mobile device\nView available training and request a training session by UITS IT Training\nZoom Help Center\nTo register for free Zoom training, visit the\nZoom Learning Center\n.\nPedagogical or technical assistance\nInstructors can get help using technology in their teaching at the\nteaching and learning centers\non each campus.\nFor help or to learn more,\ncontact UITS Learning Spaces Support\n.	KB0024473	KB0024473 v4.0	 students who can't access Zoom About Zoom roles and permissions Use Zoom on a <strong>desktop</strong> or mobile device Use	4.5641		2026-01-31 16:41:43.897309	2026-01-31 16:42:15.142607	1	b01cc3754728f698d500b87b716d4337
11730	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=eacfa0f7476456583d373803846d4372	Deactivate Adobe software	Table of contents\nOverview\nFrom the Creative Cloud desktop application\nFrom any Creative Cloud application\nGet help\nOverview\nIU's Adobe Creative Cloud license allows two concurrent active instances of Creative Cloud. You can install Creative Cloud on as many computers as you want, but you can only be logged in on two of them at any given time. If you attempt to log into a third instance, you will receive a warning and an opportunity to deactivate (in other words, log out of) the other instances. For this reason, if you have Creative Cloud installed on more than one computer,\nUITS\nrecommends that you deactivate Creative Cloud any time you are not actively using it.\nYou may choose to deactivate Adobe Creative Cloud through the Creative Cloud desktop application or through one of the Creative Cloud applications you have installed on your device (such as Photoshop, Dreamweaver, etc.). In either case, ensure that your computer is connected to the internet before deactivating.\nFrom the Creative Cloud desktop application\nTo deactivate Adobe software from the Creative Cloud desktop application:\nOpen the Adobe Creative Cloud desktop application from the icon on the taskbar or\nStart\nmenu (Windows), or from the dock or Finder (Mac).\nIf you are prompted to sign in, enter your IU email address (in the format\nusername\n@iu.edu\n) into the username field and then press\nTab\nor\nEnter\n. If you are asked to choose between\nAdobe ID\nand\nEnterprise ID\n, select\nEnterprise ID\n.\nNote:\nWhenever you log into Adobe Creative Cloud, always use the format\nusername\n@iu.edu\nas your username, even if it doesn't match your primary IU email address.\nLog in with your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nRefresh your account information and restart the app (\nCtrl-Alt-r\nin Windows, or\ncommand-option-r\non a Mac).\nThe Creative Cloud desktop application should restart automatically. If it doesn't after a few minutes, launch it manually as you did in step 1 above.\nTo open the profile menu, at the upper right, select your profile picture (or the head icon).\nSelect\nSign out\n.\nConfirm that you want to sign out. This will sign you out of all Creative Cloud applications so that you may log in on another device.\nFrom any Creative Cloud application\nTo deactivate Adobe software from any Creative Cloud application:\nOpen the Adobe Creative Cloud application of your choice (for instance, Photoshop).\nFrom the menu at the top of the page, select\nHelp\n, and then choose\nSign Out\n.\nConfirm that you want to sign out. This will sign you out of all Creative Cloud applications so that you may log in on another device.\nGet help\nAdobe provides online\nSupport\nand\nContact\ninformation.\nTroubleshoot Creative Cloud download and installation issues\n.\nContact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0023399	KB0023399 v1.0	Table of contents Overview From the Creative Cloud desktop application From any Creative Cloud application Get help Overview You may choose to deactivate Adobe Creative Cloud through the Creative Cloud desktop application or through one of the Creative Cloud applications you have installed on your device (such as Photoshop, Dreamweaver, etc.). I	2.1869		2026-01-31 17:16:34.492012	2026-01-31 17:16:34.492012	0	eacfa0f7476456583d373803846d4372
10517	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b784f0171b7c925416c02fc4bd4bcb3e	Screen reader considerations when choosing between Google at IU My Drive and Microsoft OneDrive at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nSummary table\nConsideration 1: Screen reader compatibility details\nConsideration 2: Learning curve details\nConsideration 3: File access and sharing permissions details\nConsideration 4: Commenting feature details\nConsideration 5: Offline collaboration details\nConsideration 6: Local documents details\nAccessibility documentation for\nOneDrive\nand\nGoogle My Drive\nOverview\nThe choice between\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\nand\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nisn't simple when considering screen reader accessibility. There are pros and cons to both, which can differ depending on the screen reader and browser combination used.\nAlso, keep in mind that no matter what your own personal choice is for data storage at IU, you will have to interact with documents that use the other system.\nFor gathering these considerations, we looked at four simple use cases:\nLocating files\nSharing files, including determining and configuring access\nPerforming collaborative word processing\nUsing the comments feature to read, add, and respond to comments\nNote:\nFor those who need to use spreadsheets or presentation slide decks, download the file to a local machine and use the appropriate Microsoft desktop application.\nSummary table\nOverview of pros and cons for both platforms\nConsideration\nOneDrive\nand Word Online\nGoogle My Drive\nand\nGoogle Docs\nCompatibility with Windows screen readers\nMostly works. See below for details.\nMostly works. See below for details.\nCompatibility with macOS VoiceOver\nRequires setup. Some conflicting keyboard shortcuts; alternate methods may be needed for some tasks.\nRequires setup. Some conflicting keyboard shortcuts, but these can be invoked by first using the "passthrough" VoiceOver key command. Works best with Chrome.\nLearning curve\nSome keyboard shortcuts may be familiar from\nMicrosoft 365\n. Documentation is written for Windows, requiring translation for macOS if using VoiceOver.\nRequires learning specialized keyboard shortcuts, but fewer are needed than in\nOneDrive\nand Word Online.\nFile access and sharing\nLocating and sharing files does not require special keyboard navigation -- possible with usual website navigation methods. Sharing permissions allow either editing or viewing.\nLocating and sharing files is easy with the custom keyboard commands. Sharing permissions allow editing, viewing, or commenting.\nCommenting: Comment context\nThe presence of a comment is announced in-line with the body text of the document if the text cursor lands on the selected text. Selected text is not announced when reading comments in the comments pane.\nThe presence of a comment is announced in-line with the body text of the document, including start and end of the selected text. The selected text is also announced when reading comments in the comments pane.\nCommenting: Reading comment contents\nComment contents are available when using NVDA with Firefox, VoiceOver with Safari or the advanced "Jaws" cursor in JAWS with either Chrome or Firefox.\nComment contents are not available when using either NVDA or VoiceOver with Chrome.\nComment contents are available when using VoiceOver with either Chrome or Safari and when using NVDA in browse mode with either Firefox or Chrome.\nComment contents are not available with JAWS.\nOffline collaboration\nA document can be opened in either Word Online or the desktop Work application. Changes made offline will update to the cloud-based document after saving.\nNo true desktop experience, but documents can be edited offline if offline editing has been enabled. Changes will be saved to the cloud-based document when the offline document syncs with the online document.\nLocal documents\nDownloaded documents are compatible with\nMicrosoft 365\n's desktop applications and should retain most formatting.\nDocuments downloaded from\nGoogle My Drive\nmay require edits to formatting when opened in a desktop application. Most formatting edits should be cosmetic.\nConsideration 1: Screen reader compatibility details\nFunctionality is highly dependent on the screen reader used, and\nGoogle Docs\nrequires enabling screen reader support in your account settings. To activate full functionality with refreshable Braille displays in Google Docs, you must enable Braille support by using the keyboard shortcut\nCtrl-Alt-h\n(Windows) or\nCommand-Option-h\non macOS.\nWindows screen readers\nWindows-based screen readers (JAWS, NVDA, Narrator) work similarly well with either cloud storage system.\nVoiceOver\nBoth products require some setup in order to effectively navigate with VoiceOver. Both require turning off quick nav by pressing the left and right arrow keys at the same time.\nMicrosoft Word Online has a number of keyboard interactions that may conflict with the keyboard commands used with VoiceOver. Alternate methods of doing those tasks may need to be used, such as navigating through the ribbon rather than using the keyboard shortcut.\nGoogle My Drive\nand associated applications also have keyboard interactions that conflict with the keyboard commands used with VoiceOver. These can be used by ignoring the next key press by using\nCtrl\n-\nOpt\n-\nTab\nfirst, allowing the keyboard command to be activated without VoiceOver interference.\nConsideration 2: Learning curve details\nAll of the products require specialized keyboard shortcuts to effectively use them with any screen reader. It will take some time to become familiar with when to use those specialized keyboard shortcuts versus standard screen reader navigation methods.\nFor Word Online, keyboard shortcuts may be familiar to users of the desktop versions of Word. Other commands, such as those used to add and access comments, may be less familiar.\nGoogle My Drive\nand\nGoogle Docs\nhave their own custom keyboard shortcuts, which may be less familiar to some people. However, fewer shortcuts are needed overall, when compared to\nOneDrive\nand Word Online.\nVoiceOver\nDocumentation of the Word Online keyboard shortcuts is written for Windows, so anyone using VoiceOver will need to translate them for OSX.\nConsideration 3: File access and sharing permissions details\nLocating and sharing files while using a screen reader is fairly straightforward in both\nOneDrive\nand\nGoogle My Drive\n. Files can be shared and document permission settings can be identified and changed from the cloud drive (\nOneDrive\nor\nGoogle My Drive\n) and from within a document (Word Online or\nGoogle Docs\n).\nDocument sharing and permissions\nOne difference between the Microsoft and Google options is the type of sharing permissions. Microsoft offers only editing or viewing permissions while Google allows editing, viewing, or commenting options.\nConsideration 4: Commenting feature details\nThe commenting features in Word Online and\nGoogle Docs\nbehave differently in the two platforms when using a screen reader.\nWindows screen readers\nWord Online commenting\nComments are handled similarly in JAWS and NVDA on Windows, in that the commenter's name and the date and time of commenting are announced.\nWith NVDA and FireFox, the comment contents are announced from within the comments pane. However, the contents of comments are not readable with NVDA and Chrome. With JAWS using either browser, comment content can be accessed in the comment pane using the advanced feature "Jaws" cursor.\nGoogle Docs\ncommenting\nNVDA allows the user to read the contents of comments when in browse mode (toggle with\nNVDA\n-Spacebar).\nJAWS does not currently allow the user to read the contents of comments from within the comments pane.\nBoth JAWS and NVDA Windows screen readers announce the presence of comments in-line with the body text of the document, including at the beginning of list items and paragraphs.\nWhile JAWS announces the number and order of comments in the comments pane (for example, "1 of 2 comments"), NVDA does not. Both screen readers announce the number of replies to a particular comment. As stated, both screen readers do not allow the user to read the comment contents.\nVoiceOver\nWord Online commenting\nThe presence of a comment is announced while reading a document, but only if the text cursor lands directly in the selected text. A comment attached to a phrase within a paragraph won't be indicated while reading the paragraph.\nThere is direct access to a comment from the selected text. This allows the comment to be read with the context.\nComments may also be accessed in the comment pane. The selected body text is not announced when using the comment pane, removing the comment contents from their context.\nThe name of the commenter and date and time of the comment are announced. Comment contents are read by VoiceOver when using Safari but not when using Chrome.\nGoogle Docs\ncommenting\nThe presence of a comment is announced while reading the document text. Comment start and comment end are both indicated, defining the start and end of the selected text.\nThere is direct access to a comment from the selected text, or the comment pane can be opened, and then keyboard shortcuts used to navigate through the comments.\nWhile navigating through the comment pane, the name of the commenter, number of replies and the selected body text are announced. This allows the screen reader user to find the desired selected body text, then choose to read the related comment(s).\nConsideration 5: Offline collaboration details\nBoth services allow some form of offline editing.\nWord Online\nOneDrive\nallows documents to be opened in the desktop version of the software, which may be the preferred method for some screen reader users. For example, a Word document can be opened either with Word Online or the desktop Word application.\nIf multiple people are collaborating on the same document, changes made in the desktop application are updated in the cloud-based document after saving. The cloud-based document will need to be refreshed to show the changes.\nSimilarly, changes made using Word Online will update to the local desktop document after the desktop document has been saved.\nIf multiple people are working in the same document with one or more using the desktop application, all changes will be merged as the document in the desktop application is saved. Note that any conflicting changes will result in an error when saving the desktop version. The conflicting changes will need to be removed or approved before the changes are merged.\nGoogle Docs\nGoogle My Drive\ndoesn't offer a true desktop experience; however, you can work on documents offline if they have previously been set up for offline editing. This is useful if you may be working somewhere with limited or no internet access. Once you are reconnected to the internet, your changes will be saved to the online version of the document.\nIf multiple people have been working on the same document while at least one person was working offline, all the changes will be merged when the offline document syncs with the online version of the document.\nChanges are merged in the order they were made.\nConsideration 6: Local documents details\nWhile not strictly a screen reader issue, the transferability of documents from a cloud storage service to a local copy may also be important if you frequently download documents from cloud storage to your local machine. Transferability in this case specifically refers to downloading a local copy to your preferred word processing or other application.\nDocuments downloaded with\nOneDrive\nare compatible with\nMicrosoft 365\napplications, and should retain most formatting. Documents downloaded from\nGoogle My Drive\nmay require edits to the formatting once opened in a desktop application. Most of the formatting edits will likely be cosmetic.\nAccessibility documentation for\nOneDrive\nand\nGoogle My Drive\nOneDrive\nKeyboard shortcuts for OneDrive\nUse a screen reader to explore and navigate OneDrive\nBasic tasks using a screen reader with OneDrive\nMicrosoft Word Online\nKeyboard shortcuts in Word\nUse a screen reader to explore and navigate Word\nBasic tasks using a screen reader with Word\nUse a screen reader to add, read, and delete comments in Word\nGoogle My Drive\nKeyboard shortcuts for\nGoogle Drive\non the web\nScreen readers that work with\nGoogle Drive\nUse a screen reader with\nGoogle Drive\nNavigate in\nGoogle Drive\nwith a screen reader\nManage files in\nGoogle Drive\nwith a screen reader\nGoogle Docs\nAccessibility settings for Docs editors\nKeyboard shortcuts for\nGoogle Docs\nEdit documents with a screen reader\nCollaborate and comment with a screen reader	KB0025501	KB0025501 v2.0	 6: Local documents details Accessibility documentation for <strong>OneDrive</strong> and Google My Drive Overview The choice between Google at IU My Drive and Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU isn't simple when considering desktop application. Summary table Overview of pros and cons for both platforms Consideration <strong>OneDrive</strong>	28.2638		2026-01-31 16:50:45.830801	2026-01-31 16:50:45.830801	0	b784f0171b7c925416c02fc4bd4bcb3e
11732	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ec053d6447c0ee942fc6a55b416d4359	Adobe Creative Cloud at IU	Overview\nLicensing and installation\nEligibility\nLimit on concurrent activations\nInstall Creative Cloud software\nAccess Creative Cloud without installation\nDepartmental use\nGet help\nGet started\nTroubleshoot\nOverview\nIndiana University offers the Creative Cloud suite to\neligible users\n.\nFor Adobe software available at IU outside of Creative Cloud, such as Adobe Stock, see\nIndividual Adobe software titles available at IU\n.\nLicensing and installation\nEligibility\nUnder IU's contract with Adobe, Creative Cloud eligibility is limited to currently enrolled students, current faculty, emeriti faculty, current staff, and certain affiliates.\nNote:\nTo be eligible to use Creative Cloud, students must be admitted to IU and enrolled in or registered for a credit-bearing course. This means that students who are enrolled for a future semester are eligible.\nTo continue using Adobe products that require a license after you leave IU, you must purchase your own retail license. You will receive an academic discount if you purchase your license before leaving the university.\nShould you lose your eligibility and need information about preserving your Adobe data, see\nStudent computing accounts, data, and software when you leave IU\nor\nEmployee computing accounts when you leave IU\n.\nLimit on concurrent activations\nIU's Adobe Creative Cloud license allows two concurrent active instances of Creative Cloud. You can install Creative Cloud on as many computers as you want, but you can only be logged in on two of them at any given time. If you attempt to log into a third instance, you will receive a warning and an opportunity to deactivate (in other words, log out of) the other instances. For this reason, if you have Creative Cloud installed on more than one computer,\nUITS\nrecommends that you deactivate Creative Cloud any time you are not actively using it.\nInstall Creative Cloud software\nTo access the programs that are part of the Creative Cloud suite, such as Photoshop or Illustrator, you will first need to install the Creative Cloud desktop application:\nGo to\nAdobe at IU\nand select\nDownload Creative Cloud\n.\nLog in with your\n@iu.edu\nemail address and IU passphrase.\nOn your Adobe Home page, in the top right corner, select\nAccount\n(your avatar), and then select\nInstall Creative Cloud Desktop\n.\nThe file you'll use to install Creative Cloud will begin downloading. If prompted to save the installation file, choose\nSave File\n.\nOnce the file is downloaded, locate and launch the install file. Wait for the installer to download the entire package and install the application.\nNote:\nWhen installation is complete, the Creative Cloud desktop application should open automatically. If it doesn't, manually open the Creative Cloud desktop application from your desktop.\nIf prompted to log in, enter your IU email address (\nusername\n@iu.edu\n). Then, wait for redirection to enter your IU username and passphrase.\nOnce you have downloaded the Creative Cloud desktop application, you can start installing software that is part of the Creative Cloud suite. To do this:\nLaunch the Creative Cloud desktop application (if it is not open already).\nSelect the\nApps\ntab to preview the list of products available to you.\nFor each desired product, choose\nInstall\n. If you choose more than one, they will install in the order that you selected them.\nWait for the applications to install completely. If you are prompted to update any of your applications, select\nYes\nto ensure you have the latest version of the product license.\nAccess Creative Cloud without installation\nTo access the Creative Cloud suite without installing any programs on your computer, see\nSign into and out of Adobe Creative Cloud or Adobe Acrobat in IUanyWare\n.\nDepartmental use\nAll single-user machines require a named-user installation for all Creative Cloud applications, including Acrobat Pro. On multi-user machines, you can deploy a shared device license, but to remain compliant with the terms of IU's contract, all users accessing the software on those machines must meet the eligibility criteria for Adobe usage described\nabove\n. For details, see\nWhat is Shared Device Licensing\n.\nUsing UDM/CPE:\nAll single-user machines must use a named-user license installation for Creative Cloud. The named-user license installer for the Creative Cloud Desktop Application plus Acrobat Pro is available via the Windows Device Management (UDM) and Apple Device Management (CPE) services. Users will then be able to choose additional applications to install. Serialized installers for multi-user machines, including labs, classroom instruction stations, infostations, and so on, are also available via the Global ConfigMgr (UDM) service. Departments may also package Creative Cloud applications for their own UDM site using the Adobe Admin Console; to obtain the necessary permissions,\ncontact Tier 2\n.\nNot using UDM/CPE:\nInstallers for managed systems not using UDM or CPE services can be built using the Adobe Admin Console; to obtain the necessary permissions,\ncontact Tier 2\n. Although the Creative Cloud Packager (CCP) was previously used to build these installers, the CCP cannot be used to create packages that include Creative Cloud 2019 or later applications.\nGet help\nGet started\nUser Guide\n: Get quick answers and instructions for downloading and accessing Creative Cloud.\nTutorials\n: Find tutorials to help you expand your skills.\nIT Training\nprovides various training options for core Adobe products.\nTroubleshoot\nAdobe provides online\nSupport\nand\nContact\ninformation.\nTroubleshoot Creative Cloud download and installation issues\n.\nContact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0023586	KB0023586 v3.0	 eligibility and need information about preserving your Adobe data, see Student <strong>computing</strong> accounts, data, and software when you leave IU or Employee <strong>computing</strong> accounts when you leave IU. Limit	2.1869		2026-01-31 17:16:34.492012	2026-01-31 17:16:38.948955	0	ec053d6447c0ee942fc6a55b416d4359
10527	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9732cdc34770525c3d373803846d432d	Protect sensitive data in Google at IU Secure Storage	Important:\nShared storage in Google is available\nonly as an option for departments that want to\npay for continued storage in Google\n.\nDue to\nlimits to IU's storage footprint in the Google platform\n, all individual\nGoogle My Drive\ns now have a\n5 GB\nquota.\nTable of contents\nBefore you begin\nUnderstand folder ownership\nConfigure folders to protect data\n(co-owners)\nVisual indicators\nFolder icons\nFolder naming convention\nDescriptions\nShared links\nCollaborator permission levels\nUse\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nwith sensitive data\nLaptop and mobile device security\nBefore you begin\nAt Indiana University, to store\nRestricted data\nand some\nCritical data\n, such as approved protected health information (\nPHI\n), in\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\n(\nGoogle Shared Drives\napproved for this use):\nVerify that your data are allowed in\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\n; see\nTypes of institutional data appropriate for Microsoft 365 at IU and Google at IU\n.\nUnderstand and implement the security measures listed below.\nNote:\nThe following procedures and practices are necessary with\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\n, but many of them can be applied anytime you are collaborating in\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\n.\nImportant:\nThis system or service meets certain requirements established in the\nHIPAA Security Rule\nthereby enabling its use for work involving data that contain protected health information (\nPHI\n). However, using this system or service does not fulfill your legal responsibilities for protecting the privacy and security of data that contain PHI. You may use this system or service for work involving data that contain PHI only if you institute additional\nadministrative\n,\nphysical\n, and\ntechnical\nsafeguards that complement those UITS already has in place.\nUnderstand folder ownership\nAlthough Indiana University has secure platforms to store data, individual choices determine how secure a given piece of data is. Storage ownership and settings are key to the security of data in cloud storage services. When you log into file collaboration spaces for everyday work, you will interact with a variety of shared and private folders, each with its own level of security. At Indiana University, institutional data must be stored in a non-individual storage space, such as\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n, rather than in individually owned storage spaces, such as\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\nand\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\n, to ensure that data will not move or be lost if an employee moves departments or leaves IU.\nTo maintain security, approved PHI and Restricted data stored via Google services may only be stored in\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\n, although you will interact with this data from within your own\nGoogle at IU\naccount.\nConfigure folders to protect data\nVisual indicators\nThere is no Google folder icon that will indicate the sensitivity of the data it contains. A folder with Restricted data or approved PHI will appear with the same icon as other\nGoogle Shared Drives\nfolders. Therefore, the owner or co-owner needs to give visual cues to collaborators indicating the nature of the contents. IU has established folder naming conventions for folders in\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nto reinforce collaborators' awareness of the folders they are working in; descriptions and tags are additional options. You should also know the difference between the different folder icons in\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n. None of these visual cues will protect files or folders by themselves, but they can help you prevent inappropriate access by making it clear which information you and your collaborators need to take care with and where sensitive data should be stored.\nFolder icons\nFolder icons for\nGoogle Shared Drives\nwill appear under the "Shared Drives" section in\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n. Icons in this section can be for either standard institutional\nGoogle Shared Drives\nor for\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\n.\nFolder naming conventions\nThe most visible indication of a folder's contents is its name. To clearly delineate folders containing sensitive data, you must use the appropriate naming conventions. These are similar to IU's\nrecommended Windows naming conventions\n.\nNote:\nThese conventions do not pertain to individual storage in\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\nor in\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\n.\nFor\nMicrosoft Secure Storage\nand\nGoogle Secure Storage\n, names must start with the prefixes\n[Sec]\n. If collaborators external to IU are permitted, then the prefix must instead be\n[Sec-E]\n.\nMicrosoft Teams\nand\nGoogle Shared Drives\nsites not meant for sensitive data must not use either of these prefixes.\nThe rest of the name should use the format\nCampus-Department-Name\n:\nCampus:\nCan either be an individual campus, or\nIU\nfor cross-campus activities.\nDepartment:\nFour or fewer letters; normally should be set to a departmental code. Student organizations, multi-departmental committees, ad hoc groups, and others that aren't affiliated with a specific department may set this as they prefer.\nName:\nA brief description of the site itself (entered as the "Short Name" in the institutional storage request form). It may be up to 24 characters long. Spaces and hyphens are permitted.\nThe name must be unique. These characters are not permitted:\n~ " # % & * : < > ? / \\ { | } .\nExample names\nSecure storage sites for internal collaborators\n[Sec] IU-UIPO-UDMC\nSecure storage sites that allow external collaborators\n[Sec-E] IU-ORA-Research Standards\nMicrosoft Teams\nor\nGoogle Shared Drives\nBL-SPEA-projectZ\nDescriptions\nAny file or folder in\nGoogle Storage\ncan have a description. To view the description, right-click the file or folder, and then select\nView details\n. UITS recommends using the description field to indicate the purpose or nature of an item to collaborators. To add a description to a file or folder that has been uploaded to\nGoogle Storage\n:\nRight-click the folder, and select\nView details\n.\nSelect the pencil icon beside "Add a description", type the description, and click out of the box to save it.\nShared links\nShared links are used primarily for distributing content; inviting others as collaborators is appropriate when others will be working with the content. However, for\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\n, you may only use shared links only to share content with those who are already collaborators in the folder. For more, see\nAbout Google at IU My Drive\n.\nCollaborator permission levels\nTo share data, add collaborators to the folders stored in Shared Drives. To protect sensitive data,\nalways\nmake an intentional choice about the permission level of each collaborator in each folder, giving each person the lowest level necessary to accomplish his or her tasks.\nNever use single-file collaboration with restricted and critical data. Collaboration must occur on the folder level only, as this is the level where the naming convention will tell collaborators that they are working with sensitive data. If you feel that your use case absolutely necessitates using single-file collaboration, you must consult with\nHTS\n(for HIPAA-regulated data) or your departmental\nlocal UITS support person\n.\nAccess roles and permissions\nAvailable access roles and associated permissions are outlined in the chart below.\nThis information is drawn from\nShared drives access levels\n.\nNote:\nRequests for access to shared drive files go only to the file creator. If the file creator is no longer a member of a shared drive, the request will be sent to the shared drive Managers.\nTask and access\nTask\nManager\nContent manager\n1\nContributor\n2\nCommenter\nViewer\nView shared drives and files\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nComment on files in shared drives\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nMake, approve, and reject edits in files\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nCreate and upload files and create folders in shared drives\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nAdd people to specific files in shared drives\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nMove files and folders within a shared drive\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nNo\nMove files from one shared drive to another shared drive\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nNo\nNo\nMove shared drive files into the trash\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nNo\nPermanently delete files in the trash\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nNo\nNo\nRestore files from trash (up to 30 days)\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nAdd or remove people to or from shared drives\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nNo\nNo\nModify shared drive settings\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nNo\nNo\nDelete a shared drive\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nNo\nNo\nNotes:\n1\nDefault role for new members\n2\nContributor access provides read-only access to files in\nDrive for desktop\nor files in the Chrome OS Files app. Assign the Content manager access level for users who need to edit files in\nDrive for desktop\nor on Chrome OS. See\nAbout Google Drive for desktop\n.\nUse\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nwith sensitive data\nEveryone who interacts with sensitive data in\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nmust help keep it secure. If you put sensitive data in\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\n, you are responsible not only to abide by the following policies and guidelines, but also to make sure that anyone with whom you share the data is aware of them.\nLaptop and mobile device security\nIU's\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\napplies to all faculty, staff, affiliates, and student employees who use a mobile computing device to access, store, or manipulate institutional data, regardless of who owns the device. It outlines the requirements for any mobile device, including laptop computers, that will access or store university data. Full compliance with this policy is a requirement for using\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nwith sensitive data.	KB0025314	KB0025314 v2.0	Table of contents Before you begin Understand folder ownership Configure folders to protect data (co-owners) Visual indicators Folder icons Folder naming convention Descriptions Shared links Collaborator permission levels Use Google at IU Secure Storage with sensitive data Laptop and mobile device security Before you begin At Indiana University,	9.8293		2026-01-31 16:51:42.416476	2026-01-31 16:51:42.416476	0	9732cdc34770525c3d373803846d432d
10530	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=09307c3347a456583d373803846d43df	About the IT Equipment Order form for the IU School of Medicine Dean's Office	Table of contents\nOverview\nLifecycle funding\nThe IU School of Medicine lifecycle policy\nSubmit an order\nTransfer data from an old computer to a new one\nOverview\nThe IT Equipment Order form is used by the IU School of Medicine (IUSM) Dean's office to order new IT equipment and submit lifecycle requests.\nLifecycle funding\nThe IUSM Dean's office lifecycle fund will pay for the following for all staff and faculty:\nThe Dell Latitude 7440 laptop, with a WD22TB4 docking station, up to two Dell P2422H monitors, a Logitech MK540 wireless keyboard and mouse combo, and a Dell Slim Soundbar SB521A speaker bar\nThe Dell OptiPlex 7010 SFF Plus desktop, with up to two Dell P2422H monitors, a Dell Slim Soundbar SB521A speaker bar, and a Logitech C920s webcam\nCertain positions such as Deans, Associate Deans, graphic designers, librarians, data analysts, and data managers can select a more powerful computer configuration based on their role and use case. The additional standard power configurations and MacBook Pro laptops are as follows:\nDell Latitude 7440 Laptop (i7 Processor, 32GB RAM, 1TB SSD) with a WD22TB4 docking station, up to two Dell P2422H monitors, a Logitech MK540 wireless keyboard and mouse combo, and a Dell Slim Soundbar SB521A speaker bar\nDell OptiPlex 7010 Plus SFF Desktop (i7 Processor, 32GB RAM, 1TB SSD) with up to two Dell P2422H monitors, a Dell Slim Soundbar SB521A speaker bar, and a Logitech C920s webcam\nDell XPS 15 Laptop (i7 Processor, 32GB RAM, 1TB SSD) with a WD22TB4 docking station, up to two Dell P2422H monitors, a Logitech MK540 wireless keyboard and mouse combo, and a Dell Slim Soundbar SB521A speaker bar\nMacBook Pro M3 Pro 14” Laptop (8C-8C Processor, 16GB RAM, 512GB SSD) with a WD22TB4 docking station, up to two Dell P2422H monitors, a Logitech MK540 wireless keyboard and mouse combo, and a Dell Slim Soundbar SB521A speaker bar\nApple MacBook Pro M3 Pro 14" Laptop (8C-10C Processor, 32GB RAM, 1TB SSD) with a WD22TB4 docking station, up to two Dell P2422H monitors, a Logitech MK540 wireless keyboard and mouse combo, and a Dell Slim Soundbar SB521A speaker bar\nIf none of the standard configurations are appropriate for your needs, you can use the IT Equipment Order form to create and submit a custom quote.\nThe IU School of Medicine lifecycle policy\nFor questions or to learn more about the IUSM Dean's office lifecycle policy, see the\nIU School of Medicine Policies\n.\nSubmit an order\nTo submit an order:\nGo to the\nIT Equipment Order\nform. (You also can access the form in the ServiceNow Service portal by selecting\nIT Equipment Ordering\n.)\nIf prompted, log in with your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nYour name, IU username, and IU email address will be automatically entered into the "Submitter Information" section.\nYou will be asked whether you are placing this order for someone else:\nIf you are placing this order for someone else, select\nYes\n, and then enter the name or IU username of the person for whom you are ordering the equipment.\nIf you are placing this order for yourself, select\nNo\n.\nSelect whether the order needs to be shipped to a home address:\nIf you select\nYes\n, enter the appropriate street address, city, stage, ZIP code, and phone number.\nNote:\nIf you enter the home address information before you submit the order, the Endpoint Lifecycle team will ship the equipment without any additional charge. If you submit the order and later ask the Endpoint Lifecycle team to ship the equipment, you will need to provide an account number to pay for shipping.\nIf you select\nNo\n, select the appropriate campus code, and then enter the building name, phone number, and room/cube number for the person receiving this equipment.\nIn the "Computers" section, select the computer you would like. If you are placing an order for computer accessories only, check\nComputer Accessories only, no computer needed\n, and select the accessories you would like from the list under "No computer, Accessory only order (dock, monitors, keyboard/mouse, speaker, webcam)".\nNote:\nThe standard configuration for Health Technology Services (HTS) and\nUA Technology Support\nclient\ns is an i5 processor, 16 GB of RAM, a 512 GB solid state drive (SSD) hard drive, and a four-year warranty.\nIf the list of computers provided does not meet your needs, and you need a more powerful computer configuration, choose\nThe computers listed do not meet my needs\n, and a list of additional computer configurations will appear. If you choose a computer in this list, an automated approval request  will go to your direct supervisor and director equivalent, who must approve the order.\nChoose whether you want a full setup:\nFor desktop systems, this adds two monitors, a speaker bar, and a webcam to the order.\nFor laptop systems, this adds a docking station, two monitors, a speaker bar, and a wireless keyboard and mouse to the order.\nIn the "Computer Accessories" section, select any items you want to add to your order.\nNote:\nAs you add or remove components and peripherals, the price total (located just above the\nSubmit\nbutton) will change to reflect the cost of the order.\nIn the list of additional computer configurations, if none of the computers and configurations meet your needs, select\nCustom request\n. If you choose this option, you will need to go to the Dell or CDW B2B catalog, and create a quote for the computer you need; see\nCreate a custom IT equipment quote from the B2B catalogs in Buy.IU\n.\nFor "IT support provided by", use the drop-down to select\nHealth Technology Support\n.\nIf your order needs to be paid for by an account besides the School of Medicine's lifecycle account, under "Accounts for Purchasing" you can add up to four departmental account numbers:\nSelect\nAdd\n, and then enter an account number.\nIf applicable, add the sub-account suffix.\nEnter the dollar amount the account should be charged.\nIf you need to include supporting documentation with your order, select\nUpload\n, and then select the files that should be submitted with your order.\nUnder "Notes", indicate any additional equipment specifications pertaining to the order, enter additional notes about the order, and/or ask questions about the ordering process.\nWhen you're ready to submit your order, on the right, select\nSubmit\n. This creates a ticket that the Endpoint Lifecycle team will use to place your order.\nTransfer data from an old computer to a new one\nUITS highly recommends moving all data on your current computer to\nOneDrive\n. When you get your new computer, all of your data will be available on\nOneDrive\n. This is the recommended way to store your data, in general.\nYour support technician will want to collect your old computer upon deploying your new one. If you need time to confirm that all of your data is in\nOneDrive\n, you may keep your old computer for a short period of time (one or two weeks). Your support technician will ask for a date to retrieve your old computer. When they reach out to you on that date, if you are unable to provide the computer, its recovery will be escalated to your departmental administrator or financial officer.	KB0025685	KB0025685 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Lifecycle funding The IU School of Medicine lifecycle policy Submit an order Transfer data from an old computer to a new one Overview The IT Equipment Order form is used by the IU School of Medicine (IUSM) Dean's office to order new IT equipment and submit lifecycle requests. Lifecycle funding The IUSM Dean's office li	6.8009		2026-01-31 16:52:01.05567	2026-01-31 16:52:01.05567	0	09307c3347a456583d373803846d43df
10532	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f14b171547f4aa1cd500b87b716d4388	About the IT Equipment Order form	Table of contents\nOverview\nSubmit an order\nTransfer data from an old computer to a new one\nOverview\nThe IT Equipment Order form is used to order new IT equipment and submit lifecycle requests for all groups except the University Administrative (UA) department. UA members should use the UA Lifecycle Request form instead; see\nAbout the University Administrative (UA) Lifecycle form\n.\nSubmit an order\nTo submit an order:\nGo to the\nIT Equipment Order\nform. (You also can access the form in the ServiceNow Service portal by selecting\nIT Equipment Ordering\n.)\nIf prompted, log in with your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nYour name, IU username, and IU email address will be automatically entered into the "Submitter Information" section.\nYou will be asked whether you are placing this order for someone else:\nIf you are placing this order for someone else, select\nYes\n, and then enter the name or IU username of the person for whom you are ordering the equipment.\nIf you are placing this order for yourself, select\nNo\n.\nIf you are placing this order for a vacant position, select\nNo\n, and indicate that the equipment is for a vacant position in the "Notes" section of the form.\nNote:\nSomeone from the requesting department will need to take delivery of the equipment, even if the position has not yet been filled. The Endpoint Lifecycle team has limited storage space and cannot hold equipment until the new employee is hired.\nSelect whether the order needs to be shipped to a home address:\nIf you select\nYes\n, enter the appropriate street address, city, stage, ZIP code, and phone number.\nNote:\nIf you enter the home address information before you submit the order, the Endpoint Lifecycle team will ship the equipment without any additional charge. If you submit the order and later ask the Endpoint Lifecycle team to ship the equipment, you will need to provide an account number to pay for shipping. Any computer equipment for a client who works fully remote will be shipped to the Endpoint Lifecycle team first and then later shipped to the client's home address, so we may image and prepare the computer.\nIf you select\nNo\n, select the appropriate campus code, and then enter the building name, phone number, and room/cube number for the person receiving this equipment.\nIn the "Computers" section, select the computer you would like. If you are placing an order for computer accessories only, check\nComputer Accessories only, no computer needed\n, and then select the accessories you would like from the list under "Peripherals".\nNote:\nThe standard configuration for Health Technology Services (HTS) and\nUA Technology Support\nclient\ns is an Intel Ultra 5 processor, 16 GB of RAM, a 512 GB solid state drive (SSD) hard drive, and a four-year warranty.\nChoose whether you want a full setup:\nFor desktop systems, this adds two monitors, a speaker bar, and a webcam to the order.\nFor laptop systems, this adds a docking station, two monitors, a speaker bar, and a wireless keyboard and mouse to the order.\nIn the "Computer Accessories" section, select any items you want to add to your order.\nNote:\nAs you add or remove components and peripherals, the price total (located just above the\nSubmit\nbutton) will change to reflect the cost of the order.\nIn the list of additional computer configurations, if none of the computers and configurations meets your needs, select\nCustom request\n. If you choose this option, you will need to go to the Dell or CDW B2B catalog, and create a quote for the computer you need; see\nCreate a custom IT equipment quote from the B2B catalogs in Buy.IU\n.\nFor "IT support provided by", use the drop-down to select your IT support provider, or select\nDon't Know\nif you do not know who provides your IT support.\nUnder "Accounts for Purchasing", add up to four departmental account numbers:\nSelect\nAdd\n, and then enter an account number.\nIf applicable, add the sub-account suffix.\nEnter the dollar amount the account should be charged or the percentage of the total cost covered by the account number.\nIf you need to include supporting documentation with your order, select\nUpload\n, and then select the files that should be submitted with your order.\nUnder "Notes", indicate any additional equipment specifications pertaining to the order, enter additional notes about the order, and/or ask questions about the ordering process.\nWhen you're ready to submit your order, on the right, select\nSubmit\n. This creates a ticket that the Endpoint Lifecycle team will use to place your order.\nPrinters\nTo order a printer:\nUnder the "Computers" section, check\nNo Computer, accessories order only\n.\nGet the CDW number for the printer you need:\nGo to\nBUY.IU\nand open the CDW catalog.\nOn the home page, in the left navigation section, hover over the word "Printers" to see a selection of printers.\nSelect the type of printer you want (for example,\nB&W Laser Printer\n,\nColor Laser Printer\n, etc.).\nSelect the printer that fits your need and note the CDW number (for example, CDW# 6661002 for "Xerox C235/DNI - multifunction printer - color"). Alternatively, create a quote using the instructions in\nCreate a custom IT equipment quote from the B2B catalogs in Buy.IU\n.\nReturn to the form and enter the CDW number for the printer that meets your needs in the "Notes" section at the bottom of the form, or upload the quote.\nIf the printer you need is not listed in the steps above, in the notes section, describe the type of printer you need (for example;\nlabel printer\n).\niPad\nTo order an iPad:\nUnder the "Computers" section, check\nNo Computer, accessories order only.\nGet the CDW number for the iPad/iPad Air/iPad Pro you need:\nGo to\nBUY.IU\nand open the CDW catalog.\nOn the home page, in the left navigation section, hover over the sentence "Apple iPad-iPad Pro-iPad Mini" and select\niPad\n.\nSelect the type of iPad you want, and note the CDW number. Alternatively, create a quote using the instructions in\nCreate a custom IT equipment quote from the B2B catalogs in Buy.IU\nReturn to the form and enter the CDW number for the iPad that meets your needs in the "Notes" section at the bottom of the form, or upload the quote.\nIf the iPad you need is not listed in the steps above, in the notes section, enter a description of the iPad you need (for example,\niPad Air, Wi-Fi only, 128 GB\n).\nSoftware\nTo order software:\nUnder the "Computers" section, check\nNo Computer, accessories order only\n.\nGet a quote for the software you need and attach it to the form:\nSelect\nUpload\nat the bottom of the form.\nNavigate to where the form is stored on your computer and select the quote to upload.\nIn the notes section of the form, add the IU usernames or group account usernames of all persons receiving a license.\nTransfer data from an old computer to a new one\nUITS highly recommends moving all data on your current computer to\nOneDrive\n. When you get your new computer, all of your data will be available on\nOneDrive\n. This is the recommended way to store your data, in general.\nYour support technician will want to collect your old computer upon deploying your new one. If you need time to confirm that all of your data is in\nOneDrive\n, you may keep your old computer for a short period of time (one or two weeks). Your support technician will ask for a date to retrieve your old computer. When they reach out to you on that date, if you are unable to provide the computer, its recovery will be escalated to your departmental administrator or financial officer.	KB0025900	KB0025900 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Submit an order Transfer data from an old computer to a new one Overview The IT Equipment Order form is used to order new IT equipment and submit lifecycle requests for all groups except the University Administrative (UA) department. UA members should use the UA Lifecycle Request form instead; see About the University 	6.8009		2026-01-31 16:52:08.767774	2026-01-31 16:52:08.767774	0	f14b171547f4aa1cd500b87b716d4388
10941	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d7af6077476456583d373803846d4304	Set up an ODBC source in Windows to access your MySQL database at IU RDC	Table of contents\nOverview\nDownload and install the MySQL Connector/ODBC driver\nConfigure the MySQL ODBC data source\nGet help\nOverview\nAny program that uses Open Database Connectivity (ODBC) to connect to a remote database needs a way to identify the type of database and the computer to which it is connecting. This is accomplished through the use of an ODBC data source, which associates a name with database connection information.\nFollowing are instructions for setting up an ODBC data source to connect to your MySQL database on the Research Database Complex (\nRDC\n) at Indiana University.\nDownload and install the MySQL Connector/ODBC driver\nTo configure an ODBC data source for MySQL, you must have the MySQL Connector/ODBC driver installed on your workstation. The MySQL Connector/ODBC driver allows ODBC applications to communicate with MySQL servers.\nFollowing are instructions for downloading and installing the MySQL Connector/ODBC driver (to install the MySQL connector, you must be logged into your workstation as an\nadministrator\n):\nGo to Oracle's\nDownload Connector/ODBC page\n, and under "Generally Available (GA) Releases", make sure\nMicrosoft Windows\nis the platform selected.\nFrom the list of downloads, find the installer file (MSI or ZIP) you prefer for your version of Windows (32- or 64-bit), and then, next to the preferred installer type, select\nDownload\n.\nNote:\nThe MSI files are stand-alone installers that launch setup wizards to step you through the installation process. To use one of the ZIP files, you must extract its contents, and then launch the included batch file (\nInstall.bat\n) from your workstation's command prompt. In either case, the same software is installed.\nOn the "Begin Your Download" page:\nTo start the download immediately, select\nNo thanks, just start my download\n.\nTo log in with an existing Oracle Web account before starting the download, select\nLogin using my Oracle Web account\n.\nTo create an Oracle Web account before starting the download, select\nSign up for an Oracle Web account\n.\nLocate and double-click the file you downloaded. If your system asks whether you want to install the program, select\nYes\n.\nThe MySQL Connector/ODBC Setup Wizard will launch; to begin the installation process, select\nNext\n.\nRead and accept the License Agreement, and then select\nNext\n.\nChoose the installation type, and then select\nNext\n.\nNote:\nOracle recommends choosing either the\nTypical\nor\nComplete\ninstallation option. The\nTypical\noption provides the standard files needed to connect to a MySQL database using ODBC. The\nComplete\noption installs all the available files, including debug and utility components.\nOn the "Ready to Install" page, select\nInstall\n.\nIf your system asks whether you want to install the program, select\nYes\n. (If you are not already logged in as an\nadministrator\non your workstation, you will be prompted to log in with a local administrator account.)\nWhen the files are copied to their final locations, the installation is complete; to exit the installer, select\nFinish\n.\nConfigure and locate an ODBC data source\nNote:\nTo follow these instructions, you will need information contained in your RDC MySQL database welcome letter (in email). If you do not have your welcome letter, email the RDC Administration team (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n) for help.\nTo create an ODBC data source for your MySQL database on the RDC:\nFrom the Windows\nControl Panel\n, select\nAdministrative Tools\n, and then\nData Sources (ODBC)\n.\nIn the "ODBC Data Source Administrator" window, select the\nSystem DSN\ntab, and then select\nAdd\n.\nThe "Create Data Source" window will list two MySQL ODBC drivers (one for ANSI and one for Unicode); select the Unicode driver unless you know you need the ANSI driver, and then select\nFinish\n. (The Unicode driver handles data from any character set and,\naccording to the MySQL documentation\n, is recommended for most users.)\nIn the "MySQL Connector/ODBC" dialog box, next to "Data Source Name", enter a name for this server connection (for example,\nRDC-MySQL\n).\nOptionally, enter a description of the source.\nMake sure\nTCP/IP Server\nis selected.\nEnter the hostname of the RDC host:\nsasrdsmp01.uits.iu.edu\n.\nFor "Port", enter the TCP/IP port:\n3306\n.\nNext to "User" and "Password", enter the MySQL username and password.\nFrom the\nDatabase\ndrop-down list, select the database you want to access.\nTo confirm the connection, select\nTest\n. If the connection is valid, select\nOK\n.\nThe ODBC data source you added will now appear in your System DSN list. To finish your ODBC configuration, select\nOK\n.\nGet help\nFor help with MySQL Connector/ODBC Driver, see the\nMySQL Connector/ODBC Developer Guide\n.\nIf you need help connecting to your MySQL database on the RDC, email the RDC Administration team (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n).\nFor information about RDC database accounts, see the\nDatabases\nsection of\nAbout the Research Database Complex (RDC) at Indiana University\n.\nNote:\nUITS does not support MySQL Connector/ODBC Driver beyond providing installation instructions and helping you connect to your database.	KB0022754	KB0022754 v1.0	 to connect to your MySQL <strong>database</strong> on the Research <strong>Database</strong> <strong>Complex</strong> (RDC) at Indiana University the Databases section of About the Research <strong>Database</strong> <strong>Complex</strong> (RDC) at Indiana University. Note: UITS ODBC data source Get help Overview Any program that uses Open <strong>Database</strong> Connectivity (ODBC	43.6023		2026-01-31 16:56:47.445368	2026-01-31 16:56:47.445368	0	d7af6077476456583d373803846d4304
11719	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3bafda8447103ed02fc6a55b416d43a7	About rogue DHCP servers on the IU network	Overview\nImportant:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nThis information assumes you have some knowledge of networking.\nAt Indiana University, a rogue\nDHCP\nserver is any active DHCP server on the IU network that\nUITS\nhas not approved. These appear from time to time, particularly in campus housing. Often such servers are set up unintentionally.\nWhen a rogue DHCP server exists, many computers on a particular subnet (often a floor, a section of a building, or an entire building) suddenly have non-IU IP addresses and are unable to browse the web or access other network resources.\nIU community:\nStandard and non-standard IP addresses\nStandard IU IP addresses should look similar to these:\n156.56.75.1\n134.68.1.12\nThe problem occurs because the rogue DHCP server changes your computer's standard IU IP address into one of the following:\n10.x.x.x (except 10.10.x.x or 10.56.x.x)\nbetween 172.16.0.0 and 172.31.255.255\nbetween 192.168.0.0 and 192.168.255.255\nTroubleshoot internet connection problems from rogue DHCP servers\nIf your computer is configured for DHCP but has lost connectivity and does not have a standard IU IP address, first try renewing the IP address. If it still has a non-IU IP address, and instead has one in the ranges given above, a rogue DHCP server is probably on that computer's subnet. The above IP addresses are set aside for private networks, and are used by\nInternet Connection Sharing\n(ICS) and other similar network programs that have DHCP service built in, so with the exception of the 10.10.x.x and 10.56.x.x range, you should not be getting them while connected to the IU network.\nNote:\nThe 10.10.x.x and 10.56.x.x ranges are legitimately used at IU; in campus housing, unregistered wired computers will have those addresses. In such cases, go to\nIU Network Registration\nand register the computer.\nA non-IU IP address could also be statically assigned. Check the\nTCP/IP\nsettings to be sure that your computer is configured to get an IP address from the server, and does not have an IP address entered manually. Some new computers come with a static IP address the manufacturer used to configure the software that came with the computer. You can remove that IP address and change the TCP/IP settings to get an IP from the DHCP server.\nIf the computer appears to be getting a non-IU IP address from a DHCP server, you can use the\narp\ncommand to determine the hardware address of the rogue server. At a\ncommand prompt\n, type the following:\narp -a\nMake note of the IP and MAC addresses (the MAC address will be called the "Physical Address"), and report this information to your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0023492	KB0023492 v2.0	Overview Important: This information assumes you have some knowledge of networking. At Indiana University, a rogue DHCP server is any active DHCP server on the IU network that UITS has not approved. These appear from time to time, particularly in campus housing. Often such servers are set up unintentionally. When a rogue DHCP server exists, many	2.4578		2026-01-31 17:16:10.989156	2026-01-31 17:16:10.989156	0	3bafda8447103ed02fc6a55b416d43a7
10944	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5f356f5247ee7618d500b87b716d4350	Get started with Denodo at IU	Table of contents\nRequest a new virtual database\nAccess a virtual database\nDevelop in Denodo\nCreate Denodo data sources\nAccess Denodo views\nUse of enterprise data\nLearn more\nGet help\nRequest a new virtual database\nDenodo\nis a\ndata virtualization\nand data integration platform in use at Indiana University. Denodo can connect to many different kinds of data sources and then combine, aggregate, or join and filter these data sources and present the results in multiple formats.\nNote:\nDenodo is a highly available enterprise service that is only interrupted for maintenance on the third Sunday of each month, 5-6am. If Denodo requires a longer maintenance window, it will be advertised in advance.\nAll your department's Denodo activity takes place in a virtual database (VDB). To request a new VDB for your department, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\n; for "Application", select\nDenodo\n, and for "Question Type", select\nNew VDB\n.\nNote:\nBecause Denodo can combine data from a variety of sources, all Denodo developers are required to complete:\nAcceptable Use Agreement\nFERPA tutorial\nHRMS Data Use tutorial\nAccess a virtual database\nFor you to obtain developer access to an existing VDB, someone who already has developer access to that VDB must fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\nand authorize your access. To request access to consume Denodo data, contact the appropriate\nData Manager\nwho oversees that particular area's data. If you need assistance with either type of access, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\n; for "Application", select\nDenodo\n, and for "Question Type", select\nRequest access\n.\nDevelop in Denodo\nBefore you begin developing in Denodo, read\nDenodo - Best Practices\n.\nYou have two options for developing in Denodo:\nUse the web-based tool Design Studio:\nSelect the appropriate Denodo Solution Manager link below. Once you are logged in, select an environment from the drop-down menu, and then choose\nOpen\non the "Design Studio" tile. (For more, see the Denodo documentation for\nDesign Studio\n.)\nDevelopment and Stage: Log in at\nhttps://denodo-dev.dvt.iu.edu/solution-manager-web-tool/\n.\nProduction: Log in at\nhttps://denodo.dvt.iu.edu/solution-manager-web-tool/\n.\nSandbox: Log in at\nhttps://denodo-snd.dvt.iu.edu/solution-manager-web-tool/\n.\nUse the Virtual DataPort Administration Tool client:\nInstall the Denodo VDB client software\non your computer, available for download from\nIUware\n.\nStart the client on your computer, and then launch the Virtual DataPort administration tool in the client. In the "Login" and "Password" fields, enter your\nIU username and passphrase\n, respectively. The "Server URI" value depends on which environment you need to access.\nImportant:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nIU community:\nDevelopment occurs in the IU Denodo Dev environments at the following URI:\nDEV:\n//ebidvt-dev.uits.iu.edu:9999/<your_VDB_name>\nAfter migration, you can access Denodo views in Stage and Production at the following URIs:\nSTG:\n//ebidvt-stg.uits.iu.edu:9999/<your_VDB_name>\nPRD:\n//ebidvt.uits.iu.edu:9999/<your_VDB_name>\nFor details about migration, see\nMigrate resources in Denodo at IU\n.\nCreate Denodo data sources\nImportant:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nFor help creating data sources in Denodo, see the following:\nIU community:\nCreate Denodo data sources\nConnect Denodo to departmental databases\nConnect to IU Active Directory as a Denodo data source\nConnect to DSS as a Denodo data source\nAccess Denodo views\nImportant:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nOnce you create resources in Denodo, you can share views with others. To request access for others, see\nUse Data Delivery Apply Security to assign security to Denodo views\n.\nYou can access Denodo views from many different platforms, including:\nTableau:\nSee\nDenodo/Tableau Connection Setup\n.\nVisual Studio:\nSee\nConnect to Denodo from visual studio for SQL server reporting\n.\nWeb services:\nSee\nDenodo and REST APIs\n.\nODBC:\nSee\nConnecting from your application: ODBC Clients\n.\nExcel:\nSee\nAccessing Denodo Views in Excel\n.\nPower BI:\nSee\nConnect to Denodo from Power BI\n. (Use port 9995 for IU Denodo connections.)\nIU community:\nJDBC:\nSee\nConnect to Denodo through JDBC\n.\nUse of enterprise data\nData manager approval is required whenever you make use of any data that has been sourced from enterprise data. Audits are in place for data sources in the development environment. Data sources will not be moved from the DEV environment to the STG environment until there is proof of data manager approval.\nImportant:\nIf you are exposing\nCritical or Restricted data\nprogrammatically, make certain that the appropriate Data Manager is aware of this use case and that access to your program requires strong credentials.\nFor more, see\nData Manager approval for Denodo and Tableau\n.\nLearn more\nTo learn more about Denodo, consult the following resources:\nTo get started setting up views, see\nFirst steps: Organizing the Database\n.\nLearn about\nlinked data\nto examine existing views.\nTo improve the performance of a view, see\nOptimize Denodo views\n.\nFor more, see the\nDenodo Platform Documentation\n.\nGet help\nEnterprise Decision Support Services manages access to Denodo resources and can assist with data connection and design or help to improve the performance of your views. To contact EDSS, use the\nEDSD Support Form\n.	KB0022990	KB0022990 v7.0	Table of contents Request a new virtual <strong>database</strong> Access a virtual <strong>database</strong> Develop in Denodo Create virtual <strong>database</strong> All your department's Denodo activity takes place in a virtual <strong>database</strong> (VDB of sources, all Denodo developers are required to complete: Access a virtual <strong>database</strong> For you to obtain	2.0722		2026-01-31 16:57:11.095279	2026-01-31 16:58:39.097382	0	5f356f5247ee7618d500b87b716d4350
10535	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f845867747f21e90e06785e8536d431a	About whiteboard tools at IU	Interactive online whiteboard tools can facilitate engaged learning and help teams brainstorm creative ideas by providing a virtual space for visualization. Indiana University has enterprise licenses for the collaborative whiteboard tools\nMicrosoft Whiteboard\nand\nZoom Whiteboard\n.\nList of online whiteboard tools\nMicrosoft Whiteboard\nZoom Whiteboard\nAccess\nVia\nWhiteboard app\nVia\nZoom desktop client\n,\nweb portal\n, or mobile app (mobile app whiteboards are view-only)\nAsynchronous usage (outside a meeting)\nYes\nYes\nSticky note text length\nAutomatic scrollbar if text exceeds space; can use larger notes for longer text\nSticky note text resizes with amount of text, larger sticky note for larger text to keep at adequate reading size\nSticky note colors\n12\n8\nUpload existing document\nYes\nCan insert single pages from PDFs and PowerPoint presentations\nNo\nAdd images and gifs\nYes\nAllows static images and moving GIFs\nYes\nOnly static images\nEmojis/stickers/reactions\nYes\nNot in whiteboard; can use in Zoom Meeting\nTemplates\nYes\nYes\nBackgrounds\nNo\nNo\nFile integration\nCan insert single pages from PDFs and PowerPoints\nNo\nFrames/pages/sheets\n1\nUp to 12\nToolbar placement\nLeft, fixed\nLeft, movable\nAlt text for images\nYes\nNo\nCommenting\nNo\nYes\nShapes\nYes\nPreformatted shapes; autoformats drawn shapes\nYes\nPreformatted shapes\nLaser pointer\nYes\nIn Zoom meeting\nLock for editing\nYes\nCan also lock individual objects\nYes\nExportable\nYes\nPNG\nYes\nPNG or PDF\nStorage location\nSeveral possibilities: Whiteboard app, tab in a Microsoft Team,\nOneDrive\nFor more about Whiteboard storage on\nOneDrive\n, see\nWhiteboard Moves Its Storage to OneDrive for Business\nZoom storage	KB0024069	KB0024069 v4.0	 in a Microsoft Team, <strong>OneDrive</strong> For more about Whiteboard storage on <strong>OneDrive</strong>, see Whiteboard Moves Its Storage to <strong>OneDrive</strong> for Business Zoom storage	6.7001		2026-01-31 16:52:12.703762	2026-01-31 16:52:12.703762	0	f845867747f21e90e06785e8536d431a
10539	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3d0f8f7947cd72dc2fc6a55b416d4399	Build information for Apple computers under the IU Common Build	Table of contents\nOverview\nPrepare a device\nBuild a device\nClient checkout process\nClient data migration\nOverview\nFollow these instructions to build an Apple device under the IU Common Build. You may wish to update to the latest version of macOS prior to rebuild or perform an online rebuild as outlined below.\nNote:\nDevices must have the latest version of macOS to migrate to the IU Common Build.\nIf the device is not compatible with the latest version of macOS, then it is not compatible with the IU Common Build and will need to be replaced.\nDo not use Wi-Fi for initial device setup. Departments must have a Belkin USB-C to Gigabit Ethernet adapter on hand for setup of any MacBook. All iMacs must have Gigabit Ethernet added at the time of order.\nIf Wi-Fi was used during initial setup, the Wi-Fi configuration must be removed before the client's first log into the machine. Clients will need to use their own credentials for Wi-Fi configuration.\nPrepare a device\nFill out the\nIU Endpoint Management Services Ticket Submission form\n, selecting\nmacOS/iOS\nand then\nApple School Manager Request\n. Specify your unit designation and whether the device is single- or multi-user. Wait until assignment has been confirmed.\nBoot the device into Recovery Mode.\nIntel devices:\nPower off the device, and then tap the Power button while holding\nOption-Command-r\nuntil you see the Apple logo appear. This will perform an online build. For full instructions on boot options, see\nHow to reinstall macOS\n.\nARM-based devices:\nFollow the instructions in\nRebuild an Apple Silicon Mac\n, and skip the next steps. Alternatively, power off the device and hold the Power button on startup until you see Recovery Mode options.\nSelect\nDisk Utility\n, and then choose the\nMacintosh HD\ngroup.\nSelect\nErase\n. A confirmation message will display once the erase is complete.\nClose Disk Utility, and then select\nInstall MacOS\n.\nBuild a device\nProceed through the initial setup slides. For "Region", select\nUnited States\n. Check the box to enable "Location Services". After connecting to Wi-Fi, the "Remote Management" slide will verify the computer has been properly enrolled.\nWhen you get to the first account creation, the username will be locked to\niusetup\n; you can then enter a passphrase that meets IU passphrase requirements and proceed to the next step.\nNote:\nThe\niusetup\naccount is intended only for initial setup. After the primary user account has been created and FileVault enabled,\niusetup\nshould be removed. It will be automatically removed 30 days after enrollment.\nFuture administration can be performed with the\nclient\nvia their account, via the administrator account created during the prestage (\nemsadmin\n), or via any authorized administrator account set on an AD-bound device.\nFileVault is now enabled on laptops and desktops during onboarding.\nThe following applications will install automatically for any device under the IU Common Build:\nmacOS Security Policies\nFileVault Disk Encryption (triggers on next login)\nOpen System Settings and select\nPrivacy & Security\n. In the "FileVault" section, turn FileVault on. Verify that the recovery key has been escrowed:\nLog into the Jamf console.\nLocate the inventory entry for the device.\nSelect the\nDisk Encryption\ntab under "Inventory".\nConfirm that the section is subtitled "Enabled" and that "Personal Recovery Key Validation" is set to\nValid\n.\nLaunch IU Self Service from the\niusetup\naccount to initiate the onboarding process. The following will be installed during onboarding:\nAdobe Acrobat\nCisco AnyConnect\nDockUtil\nDefault Dock Items (adding IU Self Service to the Dock)\nFirefox\nGoogle Chrome\nMicrosoft Defender\nMicrosoft Office 365\nRosetta (if device is not running Apple Silicon)\nSlack\nZoom\nBeyondTrust Jump Client\nUniversal Print Queue\nNote:\nIf there are applications your department requires that are not in the above list, fill out the\nJamf Application Package Request\n. A ticket will be sent to\nEndpoint Management Services\n, requesting that the application be made available.\nFor devices bound to Active Directory, open IU Self Service and select the\nAD Binding\npolicy. This will bind the device to Active Directory while logged into the\niusetup\naccount.\nNote:\nActive Directory accounts do not get automatically enabled on FileVault. The client will have to log into the device on campus during the checkout process, and may need manually added to FileVault.\nTo set up the AD binding option, contact EMS via the\nIU Endpoint Management Services Ticket Submission form\n. Provide an AD admins group and specify which systems will be bound. See\nAD binding macOS on the IU Common Build\n.\nBinding is supported only for devices that will be on campus full time.\nClient checkout process\nLog into IU Self Service with your administrative group account.\nNavigate to the\nUtilities\ntab and select\nMake Me Admin\n. This makes the\niusetup\naccount an administrator for 15 minutes.\nOpen System Settings and select\nUsers and Groups\n>\nAdd User\n.\nTo unlock, authenticate with the\niusetup\ncredentials.\nHave the\nclient\nenter their IU username and password into the "New User" setup window. Make sure that this is a standard user account.\nNote:\nFor printing functionality to work correctly, the username and password must match the\nclient\n's IU username and passphrase in Active Directory.\nSelect\ncreate user\n. This adds the\nclient\n's local account to the computer with a secure token.\nLog out of the\niusetup\naccount. Have the\nclient\nlog in with their username and password and enable Touch ID with their finger print.\nCompletely reboot the device, and then ensure the\nclient\ncan authenticate through a login.\nClient data migration\nA client may wish to migrate data from an old managed device to a new one. In this case, EMS recommends that all data migration is done manually. EMS recommends\nagainst\nusing the macOS Migration Assistant; this includes data migration using Time Machine. If a client uses Time Machine for backups of an old macOS device, they may move files or folders individually from that backup to the new device. However, they should not use Migration Assistant to migrate the Time Machine backup onto the new computer.\nFor most clients, manual backups/migrations can be accomplished by copying the contents of the\nDocuments\n,\nDesktop\n, and\nDownloads\ndirectories. Some clients may also have files in their\nPictures\n,\nMovies\n, and\nMusic\nfolders. Manual migrations can be done using supported\nIU cloud storage\nservices (for example, OneDrive).\nNote:\nDo not copy over the client's\nLibrary\nfolder, cloud storage folders (for example, their synced\nOneDrive\nfolder), or any applications. The\nLibrary\nfolder contains many device-specific configuration files, and copying it may create conflicts. Applications and cloud storage services should simply be reinstalled fresh on the new device.\nClients may encounter permissions issues if they attempt to open a copied iCloud Photo Library on a new device. UITS does not support clients' personal photo libraries.\nManual migration can be done quickly and efficiently using the steps below. Moreover, OneDrive and macOS use different file systems, and there are certain file-naming restrictions that cause errors when clients attempt to upload files with names that OneDrive does not support. Therefore, the steps below are recommended when using OneDrive for data migration.\nOpen\nFinder\n.\nIn the menu bar, select\nGo\n>\nHome\n.\nSelect\nDocuments\n.\nRight-click (or\nCommand\n-click) and select\nCompress "Documents"\n.\nWait for compression to finish. This may take some time depending on the contents of the\nDocuments\nfolder.\nA single\n.zip\nfile that contains the entirety of\nDocuments\nwill be created, which can then be uploaded to OneDrive with no naming issues.\nRepeat steps 3-6 for other relevant folders (\nDesktop\n,\nDownloads\n,\nMovies\n, etc.).\nImportant:\nRestricted\nand\nCritical\ninstitutional data may neither be stored locally on IU devices nor in Microsoft OneDrive. Always follow and recommend\nproper data handling procedures\nwhen working with clients to migrate their files.	KB0025726	KB0025726 v13.0	 using supported IU cloud storage services (for example, <strong>OneDrive</strong>). Note: Do not copy over the client's Library folder, cloud storage folders (for example, their synced <strong>OneDrive</strong> folder), or any can be done quickly and efficiently using the steps below. Moreover, <strong>OneDrive</strong> and macOS use different file	6.4699	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits, km-aud-ems-admins	2026-01-31 16:52:16.559992	2026-01-31 16:52:16.559992	0	3d0f8f7947cd72dc2fc6a55b416d4399
10554	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=0bb9514147beba94d500b87b716d4388	Move files into Microsoft or Google institutional storage from an individual storage location	Table of contents\nOverview\nBefore you begin\nMap individual storage to the\nIU Cloud Storage\nservice and access the IUanyWare Desktop\nMove files to institutional storage\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\nIf your data is in\nGoogle My Drive\nbut contains Google documents (such as\nGoogle Docs\nor\nGoogle Sheets\n) that haven't been converted to\nMicrosoft 365\ndocuments\nOverview\nIf you have\ninstitutional files\nin your individual\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nor\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\naccount, follow the steps below to move that data to an institutional storage location, such as\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nusing\nIU Cloud Storage\nand the\nIUanyWare desktop\n.\nImportant:\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\nare available only as a paid option for departments.\nStorage costs for paid\nGoogle Shared Drives\nare $48 per year, in 100 GB increments.\nUITS strongly recommends that you\nchoose an option other than\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\n; see\nOptions for storing files at IU\n.\nBefore you begin\nImportant:\nIn both the Google and Microsoft environments, there is no way to move large amounts of data from individual storage to institutional storage while retaining collaborators and metadata. This is a limitation of the vended platforms themselves.\n(Microsoft) Attempting to move a folder containing a large amount of data (~100GB or more) from\nMicrosoft OneDrive\nto a\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nlocation using the web or mobile app interface will fail.\n(Google) There is no way to move a folder from\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\nto\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\nusing the web or mobile app interface.\nMoving files or folders from individual to institutional storage using any method other than the web or mobile app interface (such as those outlined below, which rely on synchronizing content to a local computer) will result in the uploaded data being treated as new content, rather than a move or copy.\nIf you need help assessing whether you have institutional files, see\nDetermine whether your files are individual (personal) or institutional\n.\nMap individual storage to the\nIU Cloud Storage\nservice and access the IUanyWare Desktop\nDetermine which files are institutional and choose the institutional storage location that will best serve your department's needs. If needed, request storage at that location. For more information, see\nOptions for storing files at IU\nand\nStore, share, and collaborate on institutional files at IU\n. If many other people at IU will access the files, you may wish to consult with your\nlocal UITS support person\n.\nAuthorize your individual storage location for\nIU Cloud Storage\n; see\nAbout the IU Cloud Storage Portal\n.\nOrganize your institutional files into folders. If you will upload all of the files to the same institutional storage location, you can move all of them into one folder. If you will move them to different locations, you can move files into specific folders for each location.\nLog into an IUanyWare Desktop and open a web browser (Chrome, Firefox, etc.) within the Desktop. For detailed instructions, see\nAbout the IUanyWare Desktop\n.\nMove files to institutional storage\nNote:\nThe following steps will maintain copies of your files in your individual\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nor\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\naccount. You may choose to delete or unshare these files after moving them to your desired location.\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nLog into\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nSelect\nTeams\n, and then\nUse the web app instead\n.\nSelect the team to which you need to upload files, and select the\nFiles\ntab.\nSelect\nUpload\n, and then select\nFiles\nor\nFolder\n, depending on whether you are uploading a single file or a folder.\nIn the File Explorer, navigate to the mapped drive corresponding to your\nGoogle My Drive\nor\nOneDrive\n, select the folder, and select\nUpload\n. If you are uploading institutional files to more than one team or uploading more than one folder, repeat this process as needed.\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\nImportant:\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\nare available only as a paid option for departments.\nStorage costs for paid\nGoogle Shared Drives\nare $48 per year, in 100 GB increments.\nUITS strongly recommends that you\nchoose an option other than\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\n; see\nOptions for storing files at IU\n.\nLog into\ngoogle.iu.edu\n.\nSelect\nDrive\n, and then\nGo to Drive\n.\nIf you are directed to an account other than your\nGoogle at IU\naccount, select the circle at the top right, and then select your\nGoogle at IU\naccount. For more, see\nManage multiple Google accounts at IU\n.\nSelect\nShared Drives\non the left to view your shared drives.\nSelect the Shared Drive to which you need to upload the files.\nSelect\nNew\n, and then\nFile Upload\nor\nFolder Upload\n, depending on whether you are uploading a single file or a folder.\nIn the File Explorer, navigate to the mapped drive corresponding to your\nGoogle My Drive\nor\nOneDrive\n, select the folder, and select\nUpload\n. If you are uploading institutional files to more than one Shared Drive or are uploading more than one folder, repeat this process as needed.\nIf your data is in\nGoogle My Drive\nbut contains Google documents (such as\nGoogle Docs\nor\nGoogle Sheets\n) that haven't been converted to\nMicrosoft 365\ndocuments\nIf your data is in\nGoogle My Drive\nbut contains Google documents (such as\nGoogle Docs\nor\nGoogle Sheets\n) that haven't been converted to\nMicrosoft 365\ndocuments, the method above may not work. Follow the alternate method below to move such files from\nGoogle My Drive\nto\nGoogle Shared Drives\n:\nLog into\ngoogle.iu.edu\n.\nSelect\nDrive\n, and then\nGo to Drive\n.\nIf you're directed to an account other than your\nGoogle at IU\naccount, select the circle at the top right, and select your\nGoogle at IU\naccount. For more, see\nManage multiple Google accounts at IU\n.\nSelect\nMy Drive\non the left if it is not already selected.\nNavigate to where the files are located. Select all of the Google documents.\nRight-click the files, and select\nMove to\n. Select the arrow to the left of "My Drive" in the window that appears. Select\nShared Drives\n, and then select the destination where you would like to move the files (the Shared Drive itself, or a folder within a Shared Drive). Select\nMove Here\n, and then select\nMove\n.\nNote:\nThis method allows moving multiple files at once, but does not work for moving folders at this time, so you'll need to recreate the folder structure in the new space.	KB0025297	KB0025297 v2.0	 institutional files in your individual Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU or Google at IU My Drive account a large amount of data (~100GB or more) from Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> to a Microsoft Teams at IU location using Note: The following steps will maintain copies of your files in your individual Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong>	6.0364		2026-01-31 16:52:27.469615	2026-01-31 16:52:27.469615	0	0bb9514147beba94d500b87b716d4388
10495	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d82030ff476456583d373803846d43a1	Use Microsoft or Google Forms to receive uploaded files	Table of contents\nOverview\nCreate an "Upload files" question in\nMicrosoft Forms\nCreate a "File upload" question in\nGoogle Forms\nOverview\nThese instructions describe how to use\nMicrosoft Forms\nor\nGoogle Forms\nto create questions that let respondents with IU\nMicrosoft 365\nor\nGoogle at IU\naccounts upload files to your\nOneDrive\nor\nGoogle My Drive\nspace.\nImportant:\nIf you are completing a non-IU Google form with a file upload component, you'll need to be signed in with a personal, non-IU Google account. This may often be the case with conference proposals, for example.\nThis is because of IU security settings. Further details are available at\nUse DLP for Drive to prevent data loss\n.\nCreate an "Upload files" question in\nMicrosoft Forms\nNote:\nIf you aren't signed into an IU\nMicrosoft 365\naccount, you won't be able to upload files through Microsoft Forms. See\nthis known issue\n.\nBe sure you're using a\nsupported web browser\n.\nUsing a supported web browser (Edge, Chrome, or Firefox), go to\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n; if necessary, choose\nLog in\n.\nIf you see a "Sign in" prompt, enter your\n@iu.edu\nemail address, and then, at the password prompt, enter your\nIU passphrase\n. (Do not select anything on the sign-in pages other than\nNext\nand\nSign in\n.) When prompted, confirm your identity with\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n(if you see a "Do you trust iu.edu" prompt, select\nContinue\n), and then choose whether to stay signed into your\nMicrosoft 365\naccount.\nSelect\nForms\n.\nOn the\nMy forms\ntab, select\nNew Form\nor select an existing form.\nIn your form, on the\nQuestions\ntab, select\nAdd new\n, and then select\nMore\n(the down arrow) to reveal more question options. Then, select\nFile upload\n.\nYou will be prompted with the following message:\nA new folder will be created in OneDrive. Files uploaded by responders will be captured in this folder. Note that responder names and file details will automatically be recorded in OneDrive.\nSelect\nOK\nto proceed.\nEnter your question text, use the "File number limit" drop-down to set the maximum number of files (1-10) that can be uploaded to the question, and use the "Single file size limit" drop-down to set the maximum size of a single file upload (\n10MB\n,\n100MB\n, or\n1GB\n).\nNote:\nThe\nUpload file\nbutton will be inactive (grayed out) for you as the form author, but it will be active for your form respondents.\nTo make the question required, select the\nRequired\ntoggle.\nTo the right of the\nRequired\ntoggle, select\n…\n(\nMore settings for question\n), and then:\nTo add a subtitle under your question, select\nSubtitle\n, and the enter your text in the\nEnter a subtitle\nfield.\nTo specify acceptable file types, select\nFile Type\n, and then select one or more the following:\nWord\n: Accept\n.doc\n,\n.dot\n,\n.wbk\n,\n.docx\n,\n.docm\n,\n.dotx\n,\n.dotm\n, and\n.docb\nfiles.\nExcel\n: Accept\n.xls\n,\n.xlt\n,\n.xlm\n,\n.xlsx\n,\n.xlsm\n,\n.xltx\n, and\n.xltm\nfiles.\nPPT\n: Accept\n.ppt\n,\n.pot\n,\n.pps\n,\n.pptx\n,\n.pptm\n,\n.potx\n,\n.potm\n,\n.ppam\n,\n.ppsx\n,\n.ppsm\n,\n.sldx\n, and\n.sldm\nfiles.\nPDF\n: Accept\n.pdf\nfiles.\nImage\n: Accept\n.jpg\n,\n.jpeg\n,\n.png\n,\n.gif\n,\n.bmp\n,\n.tiff\n,\n.psd\n,\n.thm\n,\n.yuv\n,\n.ai\n,\n.drw\n,\n.eps\n,\n.ps\n,\n.svg\n,\n.3dm\n, and\n.max\nfiles.\nVideo\n: Accept\n.avi\n,\n.mp4\n,\n.mov\n,\n.wmv\n,\n.asf\n,\n.3g2\n,\n.3gp\n,\n.asx\n,\n.flv\n,\n.mpg\n,\n.rm\n,\n.swf\n, and\n.vob\nfiles.\nAudio\n: Accept\n.mp3\n,\n.aif\n,\n.iff\n,\n.m3u\n,\n.m4a\n,\n.mid\n,\n.mpa\n,\n.ra\n,\n.wav\n, and\n.wma\nfiles.\nTo select branching options, select\nAdd Branching\n.\nTo view your question as respondents will see it, near the top, select\nPreview\n.\nFiles uploaded by respondents are saved to your\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nspace. In the\nMicrosoft Forms\nfolder, select the folder matching the name of your form, and then select the subfolder for the question to access the files.\nCreate a "File upload" question in\nGoogle Forms\nTo upload files, respondents will need to be logged into their\nGoogle at IU\naccounts. Files uploaded by respondents are stored in your\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n.\nGo to\ngoogle.iu.edu\nand select\nLog in to Google at IU\n.\nIf prompted, log in with your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nSelect\nDocs\n.\nFrom the menu on the left, select\nForms\n. If you don't see the menu, select\n(\nMain menu\n), and then select\nForms\n.\nIn\nForms\n, open an existing form or quiz, or create a new one.\nIn your form or quiz, select\nAdd question\n(the circle with a plus sign).\nIn the\nQuestion\nfield, enter the text of your upload request.\nIn the drop-down, to the right of the\nQuestion\nfield, select the down arrow next to the current selection, and then select\nFile upload\n.\nIf prompted, review the "Let respondents upload to files to Drive" information, and then select\nContinue\n.\nSet file upload options, as needed:\nAllow only specific file types:\nSelect the toggle to enable the option, and then check the checkboxes to select the file types respondents are permitted to upload.\nMaximum number of files:\nUse the drop-down to set a maximum of\n1\n,\n5\n, or\n10\n.\nMaximum file size:\nUse the drop-down to set a maximum of\n1 MB\n,\n10 MB\n,\n100 MB\n,\n1 GB\n, or\n10 GB\n.\nMaximum capacity:\nTo change the maximum capacity for the form, select\nChange\nto open the "Settings" screen, and then:\nOn the\nGeneral\ntab, under "Maximum size of all files uploaded", use the drop-down to select\n1 GB\n,\n10 GB\n,\n100 GB\n, or\n1 TB\n.\nSelect\nSave\n.\nThe form will stop collecting responses when the total size of all file uploads reaches the limit you select.\nTo access files uploaded by respondents, reopen your form and select\nResponses\n. The\nSummary\ntab lists all files uploaded by form respondents. Alternatively, on the left, select\nView Folder\nto access the files in\nGoogle My Drive\n. Your\nGoogle My Drive\nwill have a folder for each of your forms, with files from respondents stored by question in subfolders.	KB0025275	KB0025275 v1.0	 upload files to your <strong>OneDrive</strong> or Google My Drive space. Important: If you are completing a non-IU message: A new folder will be created in <strong>OneDrive</strong>. Files uploaded by responders will be captured in this folder. Note that responder names and file details will automatically be recorded in <strong>OneDrive</strong>	5.895		2026-01-31 16:47:25.808288	2026-01-31 16:52:34.742103	0	d82030ff476456583d373803846d43a1
10560	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c3df1fc74764f29cd500b87b716d436a	Accessibility and privacy statements for learning tools at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nAccessibility statements\nPrivacy policies\nOverview\nThis document contains all known accessibility statements and privacy policies for tools vetted by Indiana University. Link to this document to help students learn more about the accessibility features of the course tools at IU and how those tools help protect their privacy. Providing a link to this document also helps faculty meet Quality Matters standards 6.4 and 8.7.\nAccessibility statements\nAssessment\nCourse Questionnaire:\nExplorance Blue - VPAT v2.4 (Revised) Compliance Report WCAG Edition (PDF)\nGradescope:\nGradescope Accessibility Information and Resources\nHonorlock:\nAccessibility Statement\nQualtrics:\nCommitment to Accessible Products\nQuick Check:\nNo statement exists.\nRespondus LockDown Browser:\nIs LockDown Browser accessible to those with disabilities?\nLockDown Browser\nTop Hat:\nAccessibility at Top Hat\nTurnitin:\nAccessibility Standards Compliance\nCollaboration\nHypothes.is:\nAccessibility at Hypothesis\nInScribe 2.0:\nAccessibility and InScribe - 2024 VPAT\nPiazza:\nPiazza Supports Accessibility\nVoiceThread:\nAccessibility\nZoom:\nAccessibility Features\nContent creation, presentation, and management\nAdobe Creative Cloud:\nAdobe Creative Cloud accessibility\nAdobe Express:\nAdobe Express Accessibility Conformance Report\nCanvas:\nCanvas Voluntary Product Accessibility Template (VPAT)\nForClass:\nAccessibility Statement\nGoogle products:\nOur Approach to Accessibility\nKaltura:\nKaltura Accessibility Statement\nMicrosoft Excel:\nAccessibility tools for Excel\nMicrosoft\nOneDrive\n:\nAccessibility tools for OneDrive\nMicrosoft PowerPoint:\nAccessibility tools for PowerPoint\nMicrosoft Word:\nAccessibility tools for Word\nPlayPosit:\nAccessibility\nNote:\nOn December 29, 2025, PlayPosit will change its name to WeVideo.  For details, see\nAbout the change from PlayPosit to WeVideo\n.\nPressbooks:\nAccessibility\nUnizin Engage (IU eTexts):\nDisabled Student Services/Alt-Format\nYouTube:\nUse YouTube with a screen reader\nPrivacy policies\nAssessment\nCourse Questionnaire:\nPrivacy Policy\nGradescope:\nPrivacy Policy\nHonorlock:\nHonorlock Exam Taker Privacy Notice\nQualtrics:\nPrivacy Statement\nQuickCheck:\nNo statement exists.\nRespondus LockDown Browser:\nPrivacy Policy\nTop Hat:\nPrivacy Policy\nTurnitin:\nPrivacy and Security\nCollaboration\nHypothesis\nHypothesis Privacy Policy\nInScribe 2.0:\nInScribe Privacy Policy\nPiazza:\nPrivacy Policy\nVoiceThread:\nVoiceThread Privacy Policy\nZoom:\nPrivacy Policy\nContent creation, presentation, and management\nAdobe:\nAdobe Privacy Policy\nCanvas:\nInstructure Product Privacy Policy\nForClass:\nPrivacy Policy\nGoogle products:\nGoogle Cloud Security and Compliance\nKaltura:\nPrivacy Policy\nMicrosoft (including\nOneDrive\n):\nMicrosoft Privacy Statement\nPlayPosit:\nPlayPosit Privacy Policy\nNote:\nOn December 29, 2025, PlayPosit will change its name to WeVideo.  For details, see\nAbout the change from PlayPosit to WeVideo\n.\nPressbooks:\nPrivacy Policy\nUnizin Engage (IU eTexts):\nUnizin Privacy Policy for the Engage Platform\nYouTube:\nYouTube Privacy Guidelines	KB0023300	KB0023300 v5.0	 Microsoft Excel: Accessibility tools for Excel Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong>: Accessibility tools for <strong>OneDrive</strong> Cloud Security and Compliance Kaltura: Privacy Policy Microsoft (including <strong>OneDrive</strong>): Microsoft Privacy	5.786		2026-01-31 16:52:38.089937	2026-01-31 16:52:38.089937	0	c3df1fc74764f29cd500b87b716d436a
10567	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=db20343347a456583d373803846d43fe	File visibility in Microsoft Teams	Table of contents\nOverview\nShared sites, standard channels, and folders\nPrivate channels\nConsiderations\nOverview\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nand\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\n(which uses\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n) store files in\nSharePoint Online\n. However, folders in\nSharePoint Online\nare only accessible from the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication if they are associated with a channel. In some circumstances, it may be advantageous to access your files in\nSharePoint Online\ninstead. When creating folder structure and setting permissions in\nMicrosoft Teams\n, keep the following considerations in mind.\nShared sites, standard channels, and folders\nEach team has an associated shared file space, accessible through the\nOneDrive\n/\nSharePoint Online\nwebsite (see\nView shared content in Microsoft OneDrive at IU\n). Typically, each channel within a team will also have a folder with the same name in that shared site. Any files within those folders, and only the files within those folders, are accessible via the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication. Because of this:\nIf a folder is created within a shared site via the\nOneDrive\n/\nSharePoint Online\nwebsite that does not match the name of an existing channel, it will not show up in\nMicrosoft Teams\n. However, it will still be accessible in\nSharePoint Online\nand can be managed there. It is possible to link an existing folder to a channel after the fact by creating a standard channel with exactly the same name as a folder that is in the root directory of the shared site associated with a team. However, this will only work for standard channels (more on\nprivate channels\nbelow).\nIf a folder within a shared site associated with a channel is renamed or moved, then it will no longer show up in\nMicrosoft Teams\nunder that channel. The application will automatically create a new, empty folder with the same name as the channel.\nA renamed folder will exist separately within the root directory of the shared site.\nIf the folder is moved, whether it will show up in\nMicrosoft Teams\ndepends on the location to which it is moved. If it is moved inside another folder associated with a channel, then it will show up in\nMicrosoft Teams\nunder that channel; but, if it is in the root directory of a shared site or in a folder not associated with a channel, it will only be accessible via\nSharePoint Online\n.\nWhen a folder associated with a channel is renamed or moved,\nMicrosoft Teams\nwill generate a new empty folder with the same name as the channel.\nIf a channel is renamed after creation,\nMicrosoft Teams\nwill not rename the folder associated with that channel; that is, the folder associated with that channel will still have the original channel name. Renaming or moving that folder will result in the behavior described above, except that the new empty folder generated will have the channel's original name.\nPrivate channels\nPrivate channels behave a bit differently from standard channels in regards to how information is stored in\nSharePoint Online\n. Each private channel will have its own separate shared site associated with it. This shared site will have a subfolder inside it with the same name as the channel.\nSimilar to standard channels, only files within the folder with the same name as the channel will appear in the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication. If the folder is renamed or moved, a new empty folder with the same name as the channel will be generated.\nUnlike standard channels, the only way to get data stored within a private channel's associated shared site to appear in\nMicrosoft Teams\nis to place it inside the subfolder with the same name as the private channel. Creating a new channel with a name matching an existing folder will not work because a private channel has its own shared site separate from the team's primary shared site (in which all of the subfolders related to standard channels are stored).\nConsiderations\nWhether files should be kept associated with channels or simply in independent subdirectories within a shared site depends on the use case:\nWhen necessary permissioning can be managed entirely with team membership and private channel permissions, it can be beneficial to store all files within subfolders associated with channels. This way, you can use the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication to access them.\nWhen there is a folder structure with several levels and with varying permissions levels within subfolders of subfolders, it can be beneficial to leave the subfolders unassociated with a channel. The\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication does not support subfolder-level permissions, so keeping the entire folder structure accessible via\nSharePoint Online\navoids confusion.	KB0025548	KB0025548 v1.0	 the <strong>OneDrive</strong>/SharePoint Online website (see View shared content in Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU the Microsoft Teams application. Because of this: If a folder is created within a shared site via the <strong>OneDrive</strong>	5.7518		2026-01-31 16:52:41.469127	2026-01-31 16:52:41.469127	0	db20343347a456583d373803846d43fe
10568	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=0b964ee547c4fadc2fc6a55b416d4377	Advanced Diagnostics routed services	Table of contents\nOverview\nServices for Advanced Diagnostics\nExchange\nMicrosoft Teams\nSharePoint/OneDrive\nActive Directory/group policy/Azure\nAdobe\nDuo\nIUanyWare\nVPN\nMicrosoft 365 product issues\nCCI Tools\nDigital signatures (S/MIME)\nOther service owner escalations\nMCM/Jamf\nNetwork\nGoogle Drive\nIU List\nCanvas\nPrinting\nZoom\nOverview\nSupport tickets requiring assistance with the following services should be routed directly to the Advanced Diagnostics Team in ServiceNow by assigning the ticket to the\nAdvanced Diagnostics\nServiceNow assignment group. Be sure to include all relevant details, such as:\nA clear description of the issue and any relevant context\nTroubleshooting steps already taken\nSupporting documentation (screenshots, logs, error messages, etc.)\nNames of impacted users, teams, services, mailboxes, sites, or URLs\nComplete information helps ensure efficient and accurate resolution.\nServices for Advanced Diagnostics\nExchange\nAdvanced mail and calendar issues\nMail enabled AD groups\nFASA requests (group accounts and research rooms)\nIf additional users need access to a FASA enabled group, contact your on-site or Anchored IT support professional.\nIf a meeting organizer who has left IU needs to be removed, refer to\nExchange service policies at IU\n.\nMicrosoft Teams\nMulti-factor authentication resets\nDeleted team recovery (within 30 days)\nSoftware/hardware troubleshooting\nSharePoint/OneDrive\nAdvanced permissions troubleshooting\nDeleted storage recovery (up to 93 days, after which the data is not recoverable)\nActive Directory/group policy/Azure\nAdvanced troubleshooting and group policy review\nMail-enabled AD groups creation and troubleshooting\nHelp identifying OU administrators\nHelp identifying designated administrators authorized to modify group memberships and OU object moves\nAdobe\nVerify licensing\nDuo\nNew integrations (see\nIntegrate Duo with an application\n)\nTechnical issues with the Duo Mobile app installing, launching, or crashing\nTechnical/compatibility problems with the Configuring Duo interface\nIf you're experiencing any other issues with Duo, contact Account & Student Information Systems (ASIS) at\nsct2@iu.edu\n.\nIUanyWare\nGeneral troubleshooting\nIf initial troubleshooting does not work,\ncontact the Support Center\nto request an IUanyWare profile reset.\nVPN\nGeneral troubleshooting\nMicrosoft 365 product issues\nAny out-of-the-ordinary technical problems with Microsoft 365 products, including Yammer\nCCI Tools\nGeneral troubleshooting\nIf the site is down or non-functioning, contact the CCI Tools admins via the\nFeedback form\nor mail to\nads-admin@iu.edu\n.\nDigital signatures (S/MIME)\nRenewing certificates\nUsing S/MIME with group accounts\nOther service owner escalations\nRoute issues about the following services directly to the service owner, not to the Advanced Diagnostics team.\nMCM/Jamf\nIf you're experiencing any issues with Microsoft Configuration Manager (MCM, previously known as SCCM) or Jamf,\ncontact Endpoint Management Services\n.\nNetwork\nIf you are experiencing issues with the Campus Network Portal (CNP) or any large-scale network issues, contact the Global NOC at\nnoc@iu.edu\n.\nGoogle Drive\nDirect all issues to Mission Critical (\nscmc@iu.edu\n).\nIU List\nDirect all issues/troubleshooting to Mission Critical (\nscmc@iu.edu\n).\nCanvas\nDirect all issues/troubleshooting to Mission Critical (\nscmc@iu.edu\n).\nPrinting\nDirect all requests to IU Print (\nprinting@iu.edu\n).\nZoom\nDirect all issues to IU Learning Spaces (the\nLearning Spaces\nassignment group in ServiceNow).	KB0026783	KB0026783 v7.0	Table of contents Overview Services for Advanced Diagnostics Exchange Microsoft Teams SharePoint/<strong>OneDrive</strong> Active Directory/group policy/Azure Adobe Duo IUanyWare VPN Microsoft 365 product issues CCI SharePoint/<strong>OneDrive</strong> Advanced permissions troubleshooting Deleted storage recovery (up to 93 days, after	5.6619	km-aud-uits, km-aud-local-support	2026-01-31 16:52:44.886131	2026-01-31 16:52:44.886131	0	0b964ee547c4fadc2fc6a55b416d4377
11041	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ec3d4cfa47e57a942fc6a55b416d43d8	About IT People	Table of contents\nOverview\nAssign an individual's permissions for various tools\nUITS staff and local UITS support people:\nFind units and people\nEdit your profile\nInstructions for IT leaders to edit unit information\nStart editing\nAdd, edit, or delete people\nAssign an individual's permissions for various tools\nAdd parent-child relationships\nEdit supported departments and buildings\nArchive policy\nOverview\nImportant:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nIT People\nis the comprehensive directory of personnel working in Information Technology at Indiana University. Whether you work within a department, school, administrative unit, or UITS, IT People is an inclusive and comprehensive record of who is doing IT at IU, what they are doing, and where.\nIT People includes those classed as\nlocal UITS support people\nthrough Human Resources, and also faculty members and graduate students managing IT systems, processes, data, or assets. IT People lists the IT units in which they work, and the departments and buildings they or their units support.\nBuilt on the\nIU Web Framework\nusing\nRivet\n, IT People is tied to Human Resources Management System data through IMS. Once in IT People, local UITS support people are added or removed based on current HR data, with updates running hourly during normal business hours.\nWhile an initial population of local UITS support people and IT units in which they serve is in place,\nIT unit leaders now need to update their respective unit pages\nby adding staff, the tools they can access, and departments and buildings served.\nIT leadership and those to whom leaders assign administrative access for their respective unit(s) can add users and their tools permissions, as well as add member categories, notes, and controls. Individuals can edit their profiles (all editable fields within member profiles are editable by leadership as well).\nAssign an individual's permissions for various tools\nNote:\nTool assignment requires compliance with the\nFERPA tutorial\nand the\nAcceptable Use Agreement\n. Verify that users are compliant in\nAccess Control Management (ACM)\nbefore assigning tools (compliance status may take up to 48 hours to update after completing the required tutorials). Tool access for users who are not compliant is removed by an automated process that runs every evening. If a user loses tool access because of a lapse in compliance, all tools must be reassigned after the user is back in compliance and appears compliant in ACM.\nTo edit your unit information, select the pencil icon near the top of the unit page, below the search box. Then, to add or modify permissions for individuals, select the pencil icon next to the appropriate name and set access for the following tools.\nList of tools\nLocal UITS Support Mail\nIT-related mailings for those providing local UITS support; also grants access to internal content in this Knowledge Base\nClearPass\nTools for DNS and DHCP registration inquiries and mass-DHCP registration\nLocal UITS Support Web\nAccess to internal Knowledge Base documentation (login required)\nUIPO Unblock\nAbility to unblock blocked machines\nSuperpass\nChange passphrase of users in supported departments (see AMS Admin)\nIUware\nAccess to non-public software\nProduct Keys (Deprecated)\nThe ability to access support-related product keys on the IUware site is included with "IUware" permissions above.\nAMS Admin\nCollection of tools including user search (view accounts), unlock passphrase (self-service), Superpass, and guest account search\nAccount Management\nCCI Tools, including searches by username, last name, and phone number\nUIPO Unblock Viewer\nAbility to view blocked machines\nUITS staff and local UITS support people:\nFind units and people\nLog into\nIT People\n. On the introduction page, you can select\nUnits\nor\nPeople\n, or use the search box.\nUnits\nSelect\nUnits\nat the top of the page.\nYou'll see an index of all IT units across all campuses. Browse through units and select accordingly, or search for an individual, unit, or department name.\nThe majority of local UITS support people will be assigned to only one unit, but IT People maintains the flexibility to represent complexities of local UITS support people working across multiple departments.\nYour associated units are listed under "My Units". Select your IT unit to view its prepopulated member list.\nNote:\nUnit Leaders will be listed as such, with Owner permissions allowing full control of your unit, including the ability to add additional leaders or Subleads with various permissions.\nPeople\nTo do a Boolean search of local UITS support people by role, responsibility, campus, and/or area, select\nPeople\nat the top of the page.\nChoose your filters and select\nApply Filters\n. If you wish, you can export the resulting list to a CSV file, which will return fields containing name,\nUniversity ID\n,\nIU email address\n, IU phone number, campus, department, position, and interests.\nEdit your profile\nFrom your unit, select your name.\nSelect the pencil icon to add HR areas of Responsibilities. For your Professional interests, you can use the resulting dialog box to add tailored interests (such as systems design, leadership, project management, etc.).\nThe job title under your name is drawn from HR records, and isn't editable.\nInstructions for IT leaders to edit unit information\nStart editing\nTo edit your unit information, select the pencil icon\nnear the top of the unit page, below the search box.\nImportant:\nA trash icon next to the pencil may be visible to "superadmins", and will\ndelete your entire unit\n! Proceed with caution.\nAfter you've entered the editor, you'll be able to manage the following:\nUnit title\nUnit description\nWebsite\nMembers/Leader/Subleader/Related people\nParent/child (generally for units with divisional or tiered organizational complexity)\nDepartments supported\nBuildings supported\nContact Email\nAdd, delete, or edit people\nAdd:\nTo add an individual to your unit, select\nadd member\nunder the person's appropriate role, choosing one of the four available options:\nLeaders:\nUnit Managers or Directors within your unit\nSubleads:\nCo-directors or positions of authority who report to the Director\nMembers:\nLocal UITS support people outside leadership roles.\nRelated People:\nThose who are part of departments that you support who may be classed as local UITS support people (such as research or data specialists working in labs, or faculty who are running their own technology-driven labs); not part of your IT team, but working in the capacity of a local UITS support person\nYou'll see a dialog box where you can add the appropriate information for the individual.\nRole:\nChoose one of the four options.\nUnit permissions:\nViewer:\nRead acess only, other than changing profile information\nOwner:\nFull control of unit site\nManage Members:\nAdd/remove members only\nManage Tools:\nAdd/remove tools only\nTitle:\nAppears in your unit's landing page directory, but separate from the often cryptic HR abbreviated job title that appears in the member's profile\nPercentage:\nTime or work responsibility dedicated to your unit\nNotes:\nParticularly useful for the "Related" role, where IT leaders can describe kinds of work being done, systems managed, etc.\nSelect\nSave\n. If you've finished all unit editing, select\nSave\nagain on the upper left of your unit landing page.\nEdit:\nTo edit a user's information, select the pencil icon next to the appropriate name.\nIn the resulting dialog box, make your modifications as noted in the\nAdd section above\n.\nSelect\nSave\n. If you've finished all unit editing, select\nSave\nagain on the upper left of your unit landing page.\nDelete:\nTo delete an individual from your unit, select the trash icon next to the appropriate name.\nSelect\nSave\n. If you've finished all unit editing, select\nSave\nagain on the upper left of your unit landing page.\nAssign an individual's permissions for various tools\nSee the\ninstructions\nabove.\nAdd parent-child relationships\nIf you'd like to create child objects beneath your parent IT unit, email\nitcp@iu.edu\n. For example, if you want to create a subunit, such as technical services or Tier 2, ITCP will create the new units with a naming convention, if necessary, that reflectes the IT unit under which the new child resides ("COLLIT Support Services", for example, rather than "Support Services").\nImportant:\nIf you segment your staff within child objects, Superpass access will be determined by departments listed under that child unit, and will not be inherited from the parent.\nEdit supported departments and buildings\nTo add departments that your unit supports (particularly important for Superpass control) or buildings in which your supported faculty/staff work, choose\nSupported Departments/Buildings\n.\nSelect\nadd department\nor\nadd building\n.\nIn the search box, enter a building name or number or a department name or code, and make your selection from the results. Save your changes, and your selections will appear on your unit's landing page.\nIf you are adding a department, an additional drop-down menu will appear asking you to select the "Support Type". This is a descriptor to best describe the relationship between your IT unit and the department. The default option for support type is\nnone\n, and not describing the relationship will have no impact on tools or other features.\nArchive policy\nA unit will be moved from active status to archive status if all of the following are true:\nThe unit has not been updated in IT People in the past two years.\nITCP has not met with the unit, or a parent unit, in the last year.\nITCP has not been in contact with the unit leader.\nIf a unit is currently archived and would like to be re-activated, a representative willing to act as leader for the unit must reach out to ITCP (\nitcp@iu.edu\n). If an archived unit is not re-activated within a year of the date it was first archived, the unit will be deleted from IT People. Once a unit is deleted, all information about the unit is removed and is no longer recoverable. Should the unit need to be re-activated, a new IT unit will need to be created.	KB0026031	KB0026031 v4.0	 is an inclusive and comprehensive record of who is doing IT at <strong>IU</strong>, what they are doing, and where on the <strong>IU</strong> Web Framework using Rivet, IT People is tied to Human Resources Management System data	1.835		2026-01-31 16:58:42.958939	2026-01-31 16:58:42.958939	0	ec3d4cfa47e57a942fc6a55b416d43d8
11049	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5b20343347a456583d373803846d439f	Accommodations for students	There are various resources on campus available to students with disabilities. Accommodations will vary based on individual circumstances. Though not exhaustive, forms of assistance could include:\nTesting accommodations and proctoring\nComputerized assistance\nClassroom accommodations\nAdvisement and priority registration\nAccommodations are also available for pregnancy, childbirth, lactation, and other related medical conditions. Visit\npregnancy.iu.edu\nto learn how to request accommodations at your campus.\nAccessible Educational Services (AES) offices\nEach campus has an Accessible Educational Services (AES) office. The offices listed below are specified by campus and can address accessibility concerns in the form of accommodation and services. Procedures for requesting accommodations are listed in IU's\nAmericans with Disabilities Act Policy (UA-02)\n(see paragraph B of the "Procedures" section).\nIU Bloomington\nIU Indianapolis\nIU Columbus\nIU East\nIU Fort Wayne\nIU Kokomo\nIU Northwest\nIU South Bend\nIU Southeast\nIU Bloomington\nEigenmann Hall 001\n1900 E. Tenth Street\nBloomington, IN 47406\n812-855-7578\nEmail:\niubaes@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nIU Indianapolis\nJoseph T. Taylor Hall (UC) 100\n815 W. Michigan Street\nIndianapolis, IN 46202\n317-274-3241\n317-278-2052\nEmail:\naes@iu.edu\nFall/spring semester hours: Monday-Friday 8am-5pm\nSummer hours: Staffed Monday-Friday 8am-4pm.\nIU Columbus\nOffice of Academic and Student Affairs\nLearning Center (LC) 1620\n4555 Central Avenue\nColumbus, IN 47203\n812-375-7528\nEmail:\nmcrothro@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Thursday 9am-6pm; Friday 9am-3pm.\nIU East\nWhitewater Hall 116B\n2325 Chester Blvd.\nRichmond, IN 47374\n765-973-8675\n765-973-8237\nEmail:\nasiue@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nIU Fort Wayne\nJoseph T. Taylor Hall (UC) 100\n815 W. Michigan Street\nIndianapolis, IN 46202\n317-274-3241\n317-278-2052\nEmail:\naes@iu.edu\nFall/spring semester hours: Monday-Friday 8am-5pm\nSummer hours: Staffed Monday-Friday 8am-4pm.\nIU Kokomo\nKelley Student Center 139\n2300 S. Washington Street\nKokomo, IN 46904\n765-455-9301\nEmail:\naccessibility@iuk.edu\nWebsite:\nAccessible Educational Services\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nIU Northwest\nHawthorn 237\n3400 Broadway\nGary, Indiana 46408\n219-980-6941\nEmail:\nfjcoxhar@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nIU South Bend\nAdministration Building 175\n1700 Mishawaka Ave.\nSouth Bend, IN 46615\n574-520-4460\n574-520-4121\nEmail:\nsbdss@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Thursday 9am-5pm, Friday 9am-4pm.\nIU Southeast\nUniversity Center South 207\n812-941-2243\nEmail:\nseaes@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.	KB0025542	KB0025542 v1.0	There are various resources on campus available to students with disabilities. Accommodations will vary based on individual circumstances. Though not exhaustive, forms of assistance could include: Testing accommodations and proctoring Computerized assistance Classroom accommodations Advisement and priority registration Accommodations are also av	1.6137		2026-01-31 16:58:49.31215	2026-01-31 16:58:49.31215	0	5b20343347a456583d373803846d439f
10576	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9a46695647b89ed83d373803846d43f8	Recommendations for using ArcGIS Pro in IUanyWare	Table of contents\nLaunch ArcGIS Pro in IUanyWare\nWork with files\nGet help\nLaunch ArcGIS Pro in IUanyWare\nIn\nIUanyWare\n, you can launch ArcGIS Pro either as an app on the\nApps\ntab or via the\nSTC Desktop - General Use\nvirtual desktop on the\nDesktops\ntab. You should use the desktop version of the Citrix Workspace app rather than the light browser version when working with ArcGIS Pro; for more about Citrix Workspace, see\nSet up and use IUanyWare\n.\nWork with files\nThe best practice for file management when using ArcGIS Pro via IUanyWare is to access and save files locally to your device; for help, see\nWork with files when using an IUanyWare application\n. You can work with local files in Windows or macOS only if you are using the desktop version of the Citrix Workspace app; you cannot access files stored locally on your device if you are using the light browser version of Citrix Workspace.\nIf you launched ArcGIS Pro from the\nSTC Desktop - General Use\ndesktop, you can save data and your project file to the virtual desktop, and then, at the end of your session, save them to your local device or to one of the\nIU Cloud Storage\noptions. To ensure that you have all the files needed for your ArcGIS Pro project, see follow Esri's instructions for\nsharing a project package\nto save your project as a project package (\n.ppkx\nfile).\nYou cannot save ArcGIS project files to\nGoogle Drive\nor\nOneDrive\nwhile using ArcGIS Pro in IUanyWare.\nNote:\nWorking with files (datasets, shapefiles, and/or other types of layer files) stored in\nGoogle Drive\nor\nOneDrive\nwhile using ArcGIS Pro in IUanyWare adversely affects performance and should be avoided.\nGet help\nUITS Geographic Information System (GIS) Services provides\naccess to GIS software\n, and technical support to users. For technical support or questions about GIS software across all IU campuses,\ncomplete the GIS Help form\n.\nFor help with IUanyWare, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n, or search this Knowledge Base.	KB0026367	KB0026367 v1.0	 as a project package (.ppkx file). You cannot save ArcGIS project files to Google Drive or <strong>OneDrive</strong> types of layer files) stored in Google Drive or <strong>OneDrive</strong> while using ArcGIS Pro in IUanyWare adversely	5.5621		2026-01-31 16:52:48.805382	2026-01-31 16:52:48.805382	0	9a46695647b89ed83d373803846d43f8
10577	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=478a3b2b4758fe502fc6a55b416d431b	Store, share, and collaborate on institutional files at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nDetermine whether you're storing institutional files\nBasic features\nFile access\nCollaboration\nOther features\nIntegration\nOverview\nImportant:\nNot all storage services are listed here.\nThis information provides a comparison of storage solutions for\ninstitutional data\n. For similar details about\nindividual data\n, see\nStore and share your individual files at IU\n.\nAt Indiana University, you can use\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nor\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nto store institutional files, and to share and collaborate with others.\nImportant:\nShared storage in Google is available\nonly as an option for departments that want to\npay for continued storage in Google\n.\nDue to\nlimits to IU's storage footprint in the Google platform\n, all individual\nGoogle My Drive\ns now have a\n5 GB\nquota.\nThe services described here are administered by IU, and are distinct from any external accounts you may have with Google or Microsoft (for example, an account associated with a free, personal\nGmail\naccount).\nBoth\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nand\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nmay be used for\nCritical or Restricted data\n, with the exception of credit card and Payment Card Industry (PCI) data. For further details, see\nAbout dedicated file storage services and IT services with storage components appropriate for sensitive institutional data, including research data containing protected health information\n.\nFor quick, temporary file sharing, you can also use\nSecure Share\n.\nFor storage services that meet other types of needs, see\nOptions for storing files at IU\n.\nDetermine whether you're storing institutional files\nIf you're unsure whether you're storing individual or\ninstitutional files\n, ask yourself:\nIf you were to change positions or leave Indiana University tomorrow, would your department or colleagues need these files to continue their work? If so, they are\ninstitutional files\n.\nAre any of these files classified as\nPublic data\n,\nUniversity-Internal data\n,\nRestricted data\n(such as grades), or\nCritical data\n(such as PHI)? If so, they are\ninstitutional files\n.\nExamples of institutional files may include research data, course files (such as lesson plans, assignment outlines, syllabi, etc. that are not stored within Canvas), training manuals, or files associated with IU committees, labs, and student groups, etc. Most faculty and staff store institutional files.\nIndividual files, on the other hand, are personally created files, such as resumes, personal meeting notes, or presentations.\nImportant:\nIf you are storing institutional files, see the\nInstitutional storage request form\nunless your\nlocal UITS support person\nhas already set this up for you. You may request an institutional storage account that only you can access, if desired.\nFeatures and storage\nBasic features\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nSharePoint Online\nsites\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nBest for...\nStoring, sharing, and collaborating on institutional\nGoogle Docs\n,\nGoogle Sheets\n, and\nGoogle Slides\nStoring, sharing, and collaborating on Microsoft documents (Word, Excel, PowerPoint) when you desire integration with\nExchange\nemail, calendars, and chat\nInstitutional documents with advanced security requirements or web-/wiki-based access requirements\nStoring, sharing, and collaborating on sensitive institutional data (\nCritical or Restricted data\n) using\nGoogle Docs\n,\nGoogle Sheets\n, and\nGoogle Slides\nStoring, sharing, and collaborating on sensitive institutional data (\nCritical or Restricted data\n) using Microsoft Word, Excel, or PowerPoint\nMay be requested by\nDepartments only; see\nPaid storage in Google at IU Shared Drives\nEveryone at IU; see\nAbout Microsoft Teams at IU\nFaculty and staff; see\nRequest a SharePoint Online site for my team or department\nDepartments only; see\nPaid storage in Google at IU Shared Drives\nFaculty and staff via the\nInstitutional storage request form\nCost\nStorage costs for paid\nGoogle Shared Drives\nare $48 per year, in 100 GB increments.\nNo charge\nNo charge\nStorage costs for paid\nGoogle Shared Drives\nare $48 per year, in 100 GB increments.\nNo charge\nStorage allotment\nUnlimited overall size, but with\nlimits\non numbers of files (currently 400,000), daily uploads, etc.\n5 TB\n5 GB\nUnlimited overall size, but with\nlimits\non numbers of files (currently 400,000), daily uploads, etc.\n5 TB\nMaximum file size\n5 TB\n250 GB\n250 GB\n5 TB\n250 GB\nFile types\nAny\nMost with some exceptions; see\nInvalid or blocked file types\nMost with some exceptions; see\nInvalid or blocked file types\nAny\nMost with some exceptions; see\nInvalid or blocked file types\nOff-campus access\nYes\nAccessibility to individuals with disabilities\nWhile UITS endorses the use of\nGoogle at IU\nand\nMicrosoft 365\napps, they can pose accessibility issues, and can take considerable time for individuals with disabilities to master. Therefore, it is best to offer additional options for completing course requirements. For more, see:\nGoogle Accessibility\nAn inclusive, accessible\nMicrosoft 365\nFile access and storage\nFile access\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nSharePoint Online\nsites\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nUpload methods\nDrag and drop\nSync via\nDrive for desktop\nDrag and drop\nWeb widget\nSync\nWebDAV\nDrag and drop\nWeb widget\nSync\nWebDAV\nDrag and drop\nSync via\nDrive for desktop\nDrag and drop\nWeb widget\nSync\nWebDAV\nAccess methods\nFrom your web browser at google.iu.edu\nSync via\nDrive for desktop\non a secure device\nDevices must be secured according to IU's\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\n; contact your\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help determining whether your device is secure.\nFrom your web browser at\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\nDesktop client\nSync via\nOneDrive\napplications on a secure device; contact your\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help determining whether your device is secure.\nDevices must be secured according to IU's\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\n; contact your\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help determining whether your device is secure.\nFrom your web browser at\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\nSync via\nOneDrive\napplications on a secure device\nDevices must be secured according to IU's\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\n; contact your\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help determining whether your device is secure.\nFrom your web browser at google.iu.edu\nSync via\nDrive for desktop\non a secure device\nDevices must be secured according to IU's\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\n; contact your\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help determining whether your device is secure.\nFrom your web browser at\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\nDesktop client\nSync via\nOneDrive\napplications on a secure device\nDevices must be secured according to IU's\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\n; contact your\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help determining whether your device is secure.\nDownload local copy\nYes\nYes\nIt depends; some secure data should not be stored on your device.\nDevices must be secured according to IU's\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\n.\nLocal copies of\nCritical or Restricted data\nrequire appropriate technical safeguards (see\nIT-12 Policy\n,\nIT-12.1 Standard\n,\nDM-01 Standard\n),\nand\nprior formal written approval from the senior executive officer of the unit confirming a critical business need for such storage. Sector-specific\nCritical or Restricted data\n(\nHIPAA\n,\nPCI-DSS\n, etc.) may have additional requirements. Consult with the appropriate office.\nLocal copies of\nCritical or Restricted data\nrequire appropriate technical safeguards (see\nIT-12 Policy\n,\nIT-12.1 Standard\n,\nDM-01 Standard\n),\nand\nprior formal written approval from the senior executive officer of the unit confirming a critical business need for such storage. Sector-specific\nCritical or Restricted data\n(\nHIPAA\n,\nPCI-DSS\n, etc.) may have additional requirements. Consult with the appropriate office.\nPreview file content in browser\nMost common file types; see\nFiles you can store in\nGoogle Drive\n.\nMost common file types; see\nFile types supported for previewing files in OneDrive, SharePoint, and Teams\n.\nMost common file types; see\nFile types supported for previewing files in OneDrive, SharePoint, and Teams\n.\nMost common file types; see\nFiles you can store in\nGoogle Drive\n.\nMost common file types; see\nFile types supported for previewing files in OneDrive, SharePoint, and Teams\n.\nSharable web links\nYes, but UITS recommends\nadding those needing access to the content directly to the folder\n. Adding an individual directly to a folder keeps a record of everyone with access to the content. This helps you remember that others have access to the content, enabling you to remove or adjust their access when desired.\nDefault behavior: Specific individuals (only individuals you specify can use the link to access the file)\nYes, but UITS recommends\nadding those needing access to the content directly to the folder\n. Adding an individual directly to a folder keeps a record of everyone with access to the content. This helps you remember that others have access to the content, enabling you to remove or adjust their access when desired.\nDefault behavior: Specific individuals (only individuals you specify can use the link to access the file)\nNote:\nA unique share URL is generated each time the option to copy a shared link is selected in the\nOneDrive\nor\nMicrosoft Teams\ninterface.\nYes\nDefault behavior: Specific individuals (only individuals you specify can use the link to access the file)\nNo, unless specifically requested via the\nInstitutional storage request form\nNo, unless specifically requested via the\nInstitutional storage request form\nNote:\nA unique share URL is generated each time the option to copy a shared link is selected in the\nOneDrive\nor\nMicrosoft Teams\ninterface.\nEdit files in browser\nYes; edit\nGoogle Docs\n,\nGoogle Sheets\n, and\nGoogle Slides\n, or use\nOffice Compatibility Mode (OCM)\nto edit\nMicrosoft 365\ndocuments, spreadsheets, and presentations.\nYes; edit\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles with\nMicrosoft 365 on the web\n.\nYes; edit\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles with\nMicrosoft 365 on the web\n.\nYes; edit\nGoogle Docs\n,\nGoogle Sheets\n, and\nGoogle Slides\n, or use\nOffice Compatibility Mode (OCM)\nto edit\nMicrosoft 365\ndocuments, spreadsheets, and presentations.\nYes; edit\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles with\nMicrosoft 365 on the web\n.\nOffline access\nYes\nMobile access\nYes, if the device is secured.\nDevices must be secured according to IU's\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\n; contact your\nlocal UITS support person\nfor help determining whether your device is secure.\nEmail to folder\nNo\nYes\nNo\nNo\nYes\nCollaboration and storage\nCollaboration\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nSharePoint Online\nsites\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nAccess for IU group accounts\nNo\nNo\nGroup accounts can be used for administration of permissions (via PowerShell) and for API calls.\nNo\nGroup accounts can be used for administration of permissions (via PowerShell) and for API calls.\nNo\nNo\nGroup accounts can be used for administration of permissions (via PowerShell) and for API calls.\nGrant access to...\nAnyone\nAnyone\nAnyone\nAnyone, if requested in the\nInstitutional storage request form\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nfolders with external collaborators\nmust be identified in the folder names\n.\nAnyone, if requested in the\nInstitutional storage request form\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nexternal collaborators (guests)\nmust be identified in the folder names\n.\nCollaboration via ADS groups\nNo\nYes, with Entra ID (formerly Azure AD)\nYes\nNo\nYes, with Entra ID (formerly Azure AD)\nInvite collaborators\nYes; content manager or manager must approve\nYes; owner must approve\nYes; owner must approve\nYes; content manager or manager must approve\nYes; owner must approve\nPermission levels\nManager, content manager, contributor, commenter, viewer; see\nShared drives access levels\n.\nOwner, member, guest; see\nTeam owner, member, and guest capabilities in Microsoft Teams\n.\nView only, limited access, read, contribute, edit, design, full control; for additional permissions to set, see\nUser permissions and permission levels in SharePoint Server\n.\nManager, content manager, contributor, commenter, viewer; see\nProtect sensitive data in Google at IU Secure Storage\n.\nOwner, member, guest; see\nProtect sensitive data in Microsoft at IU Secure Storage\n.\nPermissions controlled by...\nManager, content manager\nOwner\nOwner\nManager, content manager\nOwner\nAccount the content is associated with\nNone; associated with the institution\n(This refers to technical ownership, not copyright ownership. For more about copyright ownership, see IU's\nIntellectual Property Policy (UA-05)\n.)\nCommenting\nYes; if you have permission to edit or comment on\nGoogle Docs\n,\nGoogle Sheets\n, and\nGoogle Slides\n, you can add comments, reply to comments, and close comments for discussions that are finished. You can also fully edit and comment on\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles natively in\nGoogle Drive\n; see\nWork with\nMicrosoft Office\nfiles\n.\nYes, using\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles; you can also use chat to comment on files you upload.\nYes, using\nMicrosoft Office\nfiles; you can also comment on files.\nYes; if you have permission to edit or comment on\nGoogle Docs\n,\nGoogle Sheets\n, and\nGoogle Slides\n, you can add comments, reply to comments, and close comments for discussions that are finished. You can also fully edit and comment on\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles natively in\nGoogle Drive\n; see\nWork with\nMicrosoft Office\nfiles\n.\nYes, using\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles; you can also use chat to comment on files you upload.\nVersion control\nYes, continuous history stream\nYes, for\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles\nYes, for\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles\nFor information about versioning in SharePoint lists or libraries, see\nEnable and configure versioning for a list or library\n.\nYes, continuous history stream\nYes, for\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles\nReal-time, simultaneous editing\nYes for\nGoogle Docs\n,\nGoogle Sheets\n, and\nGoogle Slides\nYes, using\nMicrosoft 365 on the web\n, in Microsoft Teams, or installed on the desktop to edit\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles\nYes, using\nMicrosoft 365 on the web\n, in Microsoft Teams, or installed on the desktop to edit\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles\nYes, for\nGoogle Docs\n,\nGoogle Sheets\n, and\nGoogle Slides\nYes, using\nMicrosoft 365 on the web\n, in Microsoft Teams, or installed on the desktop to edit\nMicrosoft 365\nfiles\nOther features and storage\nOther features\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nSharePoint Online\nsites\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nSearch\nYes; search file names, metadata, and file contents, filter results by file type, ownership, or application, or use advanced search to refine your search using specific phrases and commands (see\nFind files in\nGoogle Drive\n).\nYes; search for messages and more in\nMicrosoft Teams\n(see\nSearch for messages and more in Microsoft Teams\n).\nYes; search in SharePoint (see\nSearch in SharePoint\n).\nYes; search file names, metadata, and file contents, filter results by file type, ownership, or application, or use advanced search to refine your search using specific phrases and commands (see\nFind files in\nGoogle Drive\n).\nYes; search for messages and more in\nMicrosoft Teams\n(see\nSearch for messages and more in Microsoft Teams\n).\nVideo preview\nYes\nVirus scanning\nYes\nUsage statistics\nYes\nBookmarks\nYes\nCustom file metadata\nNo\nYes\nYes\nNo\nYes\nIntegration and storage\nIntegration\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nSharePoint Online\nsites\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nIntegration with Canvas\nYes\nNo, but forthcoming\nNo\nYes\nNo, but forthcoming\nPartner apps and add-ons\nYes, with IU-approved apps\nBy request; see\nAbout Microsoft 365 add-ins and Microsoft Store apps at IU\n.\nBy request; see\nAbout Microsoft 365 add-ins and Microsoft Store apps at IU\n.\nYes, with IU-approved apps\nBy request; see\nAbout Microsoft 365 add-ins and Microsoft Store apps at IU\n.	KB0025428	KB0025428 v3.0	 browser at Microsoft 365 at IU Desktop client Sync via <strong>OneDrive</strong> applications on a secure device; contact browser at Microsoft 365 at IU Sync via <strong>OneDrive</strong> applications on a secure device From your web browser at IU Desktop client Sync via <strong>OneDrive</strong> applications on a secure device Download local copy Yes Yes	5.5123		2026-01-31 16:52:52.679464	2026-01-31 16:52:52.679464	0	478a3b2b4758fe502fc6a55b416d431b
10580	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ae72c757473cfa18d500b87b716d43cf	Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory) and Microsoft 365 service policies at IU	Table of contents\nEntra ID general operational policies\nEntra ID general service policies\nMicrosoft 365\nlicensing policies\nEntra ID user accounts\nEntra ID guest accounts\nEntra ID computer accounts\nEntra ID groups\nEntra ID app registrations\nEntra ID naming convention\nEntra ID restoration\nEntra ID deletion\nEntra ID support model\nEntra ID tenants\nEntra ID service level\nLog requests\nEntra ID general operational policies\nMicrosoft Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory or AAD) provides a wide range of\nauthentication\nservices on all Indiana University campuses. Furthermore,\nExchange Online\n,\nMicrosoft OneDrive\n,\nMicrosoft Teams\n, and other Microsoft cloud-based tools require users to be synchronized to or created in Entra ID.\nUITS restricts departments or individuals from setting up tenants outside of the\nindiana.onmicrosoft.com\ntenant.\nEntra ID general service policies\nEntra ID service policies are bound by those at\nUniversity-wide IT policies\n.\nMicrosoft 365\nlicensing policies\nMicrosoft licenses allow users access to\nOffice\napps, such as\nOutlook\n, Word, and Excel. They also allow access to Microsoft services, such as SharePoint,\nOneDrive\n, Teams, Planner, Power BI, and more. At IU,\nMicrosoft 365\nlicenses are available and assigned to students, faculty, and staff based on the user's affiliation with the university. Any directly assigned licenses (a license applied directly by the user) will be automatically removed to avoid conflicts with the currently assigned licenses.\nIf you have questions about\nMicrosoft 365\nlicensing at IU,\ncontact Tier 2\n.\nEntra ID user accounts\nEvery student, faculty member, and staff member on all eight IU campuses is eligible for an Entra ID account. UITS creates a unique user account for each user on every IU campus via\nActive Directory\n, which is then replicated to Entra ID.\nTo ensure that unique usernames exist for all students, faculty, and staff, only accounts generated by UITS may be created directly in Entra ID; any Entra ID user accounts created outside of this process may be deleted or disabled without notice.\nRequests for Azure-only departmental or service accounts also go through the UITS Support Center.\nTier 2 Desktop, Server, and Mobility (DSM)\ncan help with the request for Entra ID user, resource room, and/or service accounts. For requests,\ncontact Tier 2\n.\nEntra ID guest accounts\nGuest account invites in Entra ID can be created through many of the Microsoft tools (\nMicrosoft Teams\n, SharePoint,\nOneDrive\n, Planner, etc.) but can also be created directly in the\nAzure Portal\n.\nUITS does not have an inactive guest account deletion policy. If a guest account needs to be removed,\ncontact Tier 2\n.\nGuest accounts will be required to use Microsoft Authenticator; see\nSet up Microsoft Authenticator for Microsoft multifactor authentication\n.\nEntra ID computer accounts\nComputers that join Active Directory at IU are automatically hybrid-joined to Entra ID, which allows for some policies and settings to be managed from either interface.\nComputers can also be joined directly to Entra ID without an ADS connection, and can be managed from the\nAzure Portal\nor Microsoft Intune. For questions about access to Intune, contact UITS\nEndpoint Management Services\n.\nEntra ID groups\nEntra ID groups created in the\nAzure Portal\nare prefixed with the "O365-" value, to prevent conflicts with any ADS groups replicated from on-premise. Other prefixes (such as "[Sec]" or "[Sec-E]") are used for services like Secure Storage. If a department requires a group without a prefix,\ncontact Tier 2\nfor help.\nIf you wish to use an on-premises group in Entra ID, it must be a Universal Security Group that is mail-enabled.\nUITS staff and local UITS support people:\nSee\nCreate and manage mail-enabled contacts, or security/distribution groups\n.\nGroups should adhere to the following practices:\nWhen using groups to assign permissions, follow the\nprinciple of least privilege\n.\nGroup membership should not contain the default groups\nEveryone\nor\nO365-AllDevices\n.\nUse group nesting where appropriate and supported.\nAvoid circular nested groups.\nUse the same\nstandard naming convention as ADS\n.\nGroup writeback\nEntra ID groups are replicated to\nads.iu.edu\nas part of the AD Connect synchronization process. This means that Entra ID groups also can be used for on-premises security and role assignment. Allow at least one hour for replication to occur.\nEntra ID app registrations\nEntra ID can have two kinds of applications:\nApp Registrations\nand\nEnterprise Applications\n. The process for creating and requesting access is the same.\nFollow the vendor guidelines for adding the registration, including requesting any permissions needed. If the application requires administrative permissions,\ncontact Tier 2\nwith the app name and Application ID, along with any supporting documentation and justification. Delegated permissions are preferred to Application.\nIU community:\nPermission requests may need to go through an\nSSSP\nreview before being evaluated;\n3PA\nreview may also be needed.\nRequests for Application/Delegated permissions to "Directory.Read.All" or "Users.Read.All" and the like are often not a problem. The "Sites.Read.All" or "User.ReadWrite" permissions will never be approved at the Application level.\nFor a list of permissions, see\nMicrosoft Permissions Reference\n.\nEntra ID naming convention\nA naming convention for all computers, groups,\nMicrosoft Teams\n, app registrations, Planners, SharePoint sites, and other Entra ID services is strictly enforced. This not only simplifies administrative tasks and prevents possible phishing or other attacks, but also is necessary to maintain a unique namespace in Entra ID. Before you add a computer, group, or Microsoft cloud service, see\nRecommended naming conventions for IU Windows computers and groups\n.\nNew institutional storage\nMicrosoft Teams\nshould be created through\nManage my Secure Storage\n, which will help to structure a\nMicrosoft Teams\nname properly. See\nRequest institutional storage in Microsoft shared storage\n.\nNew SharePoint sites can be created via the\nSharePoint Online\n(SPO) - Site Request Form\n.\nEntra ID restoration\nEntra ID resources, structures, and data will not be restored except in the event of catastrophic failure of the directory structure. Groups, computers,\nMicrosoft Teams\n, and other directory constructs should be maintained with great care and should be carefully documented and/or backed up, so that file restoration or settings can be restored.\nMany Microsoft products are tied to licenses. UITS does not have extended capability to restore deleted personal storage (such as\nMicrosoft OneDrive\n, Forms, or\nExchange\nmailboxes). For this reason, any institutional use of those products should rely on an institutional offering instead of something tied to the individual.\nFor\nExchange\n, that means a group account mailbox, and for\nMicrosoft OneDrive\n, Power Automate, or Forms, group accounts or\nMicrosoft Teams\nare appropriate places to store that data or resource.\nIf something institutional was incorrectly stored in individual accounts, and the department is no longer able to access that individual storage, an\nIT-07 Policy\nrequest can be filed, but there is an extremely limited restoration window; for details, see\nYour Microsoft 365 account when you leave IU\n.\nEntra ID deletion\nOwners of groups,\nMicrosoft Teams\n,\nSharePoint sites\n, Forms, Planners, and other\nMicrosoft 365\nofferings can delete their objects. If an orphaned object needs to be deleted,\ncontact Tier 2\n.\nEntra ID support model\nEntra ID follows the IU distributed support model for computing services. The\nlocal UITS support person\nmay serve as the first level, or may choose to escalate issues to the UITS Support Center. The local UITS support person or Support Center may escalate issues to\nTier 2 Support\n, which may in turn escalate issues to the various product service owners:\nEnterprise Microsoft Administration (EMA):\nIn UITS Enterprise Systems, serves as Global Admins and directly administrates\nMicrosoft Teams\ncreation,\nExchange Online\n,\nSharePoint Online\n, Enterprise and App Registrations, AD Connect, and other core properties of the suite.\nIdentity Management Services (IMS):\nIn UITS Enterprise Systems, serves as Licensing Admins and manages Grouper integration, as well as third-party contact syncing and other identity based changes.\nEnterprise Windows & Azure Administration\n(\nEWA\n):\nIn UITS Enterprise Systems, serves as Power Platform admins, manages secondary licensing for Select products, and supports Power Automate and other low code/no code solutions.\nEWA\nalso assists with\nAzure Cloud\nsolutions.\nIU community:\nAlso, see\nPurchase and activate licenses for Microsoft Select Plus titles\n.\nEndpoint Management Services\n(EMS):\nIn UITS\nEnterprise Support Services\n, serves as Microsoft Intune admins, and runs SCCM\nCo-Management\nconnections. EMS also runs the Defender ATP service for desktop antivirus.\nIncident Response (IT-Incident) and the broader University Information Security Office:\nServe as owners of the Safe Links/Safe Attachments policies, Security and Compliance Centers, Microsoft Information Protection, eDiscovery, and other related services and portals.\nIU community:\nSee\nAbout Microsoft 365 security\n.\nMicrosoft Teams\nTelephony support:\nEnhanced by the UITS Networks Campus Communications Infrastructure (CCI) and Communications Planning and Implementation (CPI) teams.\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom hardware, and\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom Video Conferencing is supported by the UITS Learning Technologies Collaboration Technologies and Classroom Support unit.\nEntra ID tenants\nThe IU Entra ID is a single tenant that handles all IU members on each campus. IU schools, departments, and affiliated units shall not install, upgrade to, or participate in an Entra ID tenant of their own.\nUITS also runs a test tenant at\nportal.azure.com\n. If developers or local UITS support people need access to IUTest to evaluate software, develop solutions, or related actions,\ncontact Tier 2\n.\nEntra ID service level\nThe Entra ID architecture was designed to provide continuous service delivery free from interruption or impact from maintenance or hardware failure. In the event of a service interruption or modification, EMA will implement the notification and resolution procedures set forth by\nUITS Change Management\n.\nLog requests\nTo request logs or activity reports from this system, contact the\nSupport Center\n.\nIf the request for information involves someone other than the requester, or if the log information will be used in support or defense of an investigation, the request must be sent to the\nUniversity Information Security Office\n(UISO) via\nit-incident@iu.edu\n. UISO staff will then determine the context of the request, as well as the authorization required; for more, see\nPrivacy of Electronic Information and Information Technology Resources (IT-07)\n.	KB0024296	KB0024296 v4.0	, Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong>, Microsoft Teams, and other Microsoft cloud-based tools require users to Microsoft services, such as SharePoint, <strong>OneDrive</strong>, Teams, Planner, Power BI, and more. At IU through many of the Microsoft tools (Microsoft Teams, SharePoint, <strong>OneDrive</strong>, Planner, etc	5.3474		2026-01-31 16:52:56.701662	2026-01-31 16:52:56.701662	0	ae72c757473cfa18d500b87b716d43cf
10593	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=de207cff476456583d373803846d43f3	Understand permissions in Microsoft Storage	In\nMicrosoft Storage\n, permissions can be granted in two ways:\nFolder-level permissions:\nWhen a user has access to a folder via folder-level permissions, those items will appear in the user's\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nunder "Shared".\nTeam-level permissions:\nWhen a user has access to a folder via team-level permissions, those items will appear in the team within\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n, and they may also appear in\nOneDrive\nunder "Quick access".\nMicrosoft uses additive permissions; as a result, if users are granted permissions at both the folder level and the team level, managing permissions becomes more difficult. For example:\nIf users have access to a folder because they are members of a team, and if they also have folder-level permissions to the same folder, removing access for those users at the folder level does\nnot\nremove their access to the folder, because they still have team-level access permissions.\nIf users have access to a folder because they are members of a team, and if they also have folder-level permissions to the same folder, removing for those users at the team level does\nnot\nremove their access to the folder, because they still have folder-level access permissions.\nTo remove access to a folder for a user who has both folder-level and team-level permissions, you must remove the permissions at both levels. It can be easy to forget that a user has access to a folder based on their permissions at both the folder level and the team level, especially since the management of teams and folder-level permissions are handled in different places.\nAs a result, it is easier to choose to use either folder-level permissions or team-level permissions for a user rather than using both types of permissions to manage that user's access.\nMicrosoft and\nUITS\nrecommend that permissions be managed at the team level whenever possible.\nWhen you grant users team-level permissions, those users have access to all the folders and files stored in that team's public channels. If certain members of the team need access to specific files, and others on the team should not have access, create a private channel and store the files in that private channel.\nNote:\nThere is a limit of 30 private channels per team.\nIf you need to limit file access to groups of people who are not part of a larger team, you can create multiple teams to manage your files.\nItems migrated from Box via SkySync have folder-level permissions. If items were migrated from Box manually, the team owner chose the permission settings when items were moved into\nMicrosoft Teams\n.\nFor more on managing permissions, see:\nManage folder-level permissions in Microsoft Storage\nManage team-level permissions in Microsoft Storage\nManage ownership of a team in Microsoft Teams at IU	KB0025451	KB0025451 v1.0	 Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU under "Shared". Team-level permissions: When a user has access to a folder via team also appear in <strong>OneDrive</strong> under "Quick access". Microsoft uses additive permissions; as a result	5.2995		2026-01-31 16:53:00.702727	2026-01-31 16:53:00.702727	0	de207cff476456583d373803846d43f3
2456	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0022482	Group accounts cannot connect to IU's VPN or campus wireless network	To ensure accountability of network communication, the\nUniversity Information Policy Office\nprohibits\ngroup accounts\nfrom connecting to Indiana University's\nSSL VPN\nand campus wireless network.\nTo make a VPN connection or connect to the campus wireless network, you must log in using your personal\nIU username\nand\npassphrase\n.\nUITS staff:\nIf your department needs wireless access for a group account, contact the UIPO at\nuipo@iu.edu\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:35:58.978185	2026-01-31 15:06:25.168179	1	KB0022482
10597	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=81ffec7b476456583d373803846d4333	Microsoft Teams Call Quality Dashboard	Table of contents\nOverview\nUse the Call Quality Dashboard in IUanyWare\nUse the Call Quality Dashboard in Windows\nOpen the report in Power BI Desktop (both IUanyWare and Windows)\nOverview\nThe\nMicrosoft Teams\nCall Quality Dashboard (CQD) is a diagnostic tool for monitoring audio, video, and network issues in\nMicrosoft Teams\n. Access to it is limited to UITS CPI, UITS Collab Tech, and UITS EMA via\nTeams admin roles\n.\nMicrosoft provides instructions in using the\nCQD\n, as well as a download link.  This requires the use of Power BI Desktop, which is only available on Windows.\nUse the Call Quality Dashboard in IUanyWare\nSince the Mac and Unix operating systems are incapable of using Power BI Desktop, here are some instructions to use the application via the\nIUanyWare\napplications:\nDownload the CQD from the link above, and unzip it into\nMicrosoft OneDrive\nor another location you can easily access from IUanyware (UITS recommends that you use\nhttps://cloudstorage.iu.edu\nto map\nOneDrive\nto the IUA desktop).\nFrom\nhttps://iuanyware.iu.edu\n, log in and select\nDesktops\nfrom the top menu, and then\nIUanyWare Desktop\n. If prompted, you can ignore the prompt to install a Citrix client, and instead choose to use the light version.\nOnce the desktop opens in your browser, use the\nMy Files\nlink to browse to "Documents".\nRight-click in the folder to create a new folder. Name it\nPower BI Desktop\n.\nDouble-click to enter the folder. Right-click to make a new folder, and name it\nCustom Connectors\n.\nCopy the\nMicrosoftCallQuality.pqx\nfile you downloaded earlier into this folder.\nYou can now log out of the IUanyWare desktop (\nStart Menu\n>\nSign Out\n).\nFrom IUanyWare, change the top of the page to\nApps\ninstead of\nDesktops\n.\nScroll or search for\nPower BI Desktop v2\n.\nYou can use the star icon in the top left to favorite it and find it faster in the future.\nUse the Call Quality Dashboard in Windows\nIf you don't have it installed,\nPower BI Desktop\nis available for free.\nOn your Windows desktop, use the My Files/File Explorer to browse to\nDocuments\n.\nRight-click in the folder to create a new folder. Name it\nPower BI Desktop\n.\nDouble-click to enter the folder. Right-click to make a new folder, and name it\nCustom Connectors\n.\nCopy the\nMicrosoftCallQuality.pqx\nyou downloaded earlier into this folder.\nOpen the report in Power BI Desktop (both IUanyWare and Windows)\nWhen the application opens, sign into PowerBI as\nusername@iu.edu\n, and Duo authenticate.\nThen, use the\nFile\nmenu to\nOpen Report\n.\nSelect the location you expanded the ZIP file into, and then change the file filter in the bottom right to\nPower BI template files (*.pbit)\n. Open the report you're interested in.	KB0024300	KB0024300 v1.0	 from the link above, and unzip it into Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> or another location you can easily access from IUanyware (UITS recommends that you use https://cloudstorage.iu.edu to map <strong>OneDrive</strong> to the IUA	5.2869	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 16:53:04.440572	2026-01-31 16:53:04.440572	0	81ffec7b476456583d373803846d4333
10598	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f5e084d91bcdd6d416c02fc4bd4bcb22	Migrate content manually from Google to Microsoft storage	Table of contents\nBefore you begin\nManual migration process\nMigrate using IUanyWare\nMigrate using your computer\nResolve "filename too long" errors\nBefore you begin\nNotes:\nBefore manually migrating data, determine whether you need to migrate it to institutional or individual storage; for help, see\nDetermine whether your files are individual (personal) or institutional\n.\nManually migrating allows you to start with a fresh set of collaboration permissions in Microsoft storage, but it is an intensive task.\nIf you choose to migrate your data manually, UITS recommends that you use the IUanyWare option below.\nManual migration process\nMigrate using IUanyWare\nNote:\nThis process does not convert native Google documents to Microsoft documents. You must download and save your native Google documents as Microsoft files.\nIf you haven't used IUanyWare before, first refer to\nSet up and use IUanyWare\n.\nLog into\nIUanyWare\n.\nIn the navigation at the top of the IUanyWare interface, select\nDESKTOPS\n.\nLaunch the\nIUanyWare Desktop\n.\nIn the IUanyWare Desktop, open a web browser and log into the Google account containing the files you want to move.\nDownload the folder you want to manually migrate.\nUnzip the folder to the desktop on IUanyWare.\nIn a different browser, log into the destination Microsoft storage or Google storage space.\nUpload the unzipped folder to the destination storage space.\nMigrate using your computer\nIf you haven't already, be sure you know how to use and access\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\n. Also, install and configure\nGoogle Drive for desktop\n.\nIf necessary, open\nMicrosoft Teams\nand sync your team's storage to your computer via the\nOneDrive\napp.\nLog into\nGoogle Drive\nwith the account containing the files you want to move.\nOpen a file browser window and navigate to the destination Microsoft storage space.\nOpen another browser window and navigate to\nGoogle Drive\n.\nTo manually migrate files to Microsoft storage, select and drag the files and folders you want to move from the\nGoogle Drive\nwindow to the Microsoft storage window.\nLocate native Google documents and download them locally as Microsoft Office files by selecting\nFile\n, then\nDownload\n, and then\nMicrosoft Word/Excel/PowerPoint\n. Drag these files from your\nDownloads\nfolder to your Microsoft storage in the browser.\nLocate any bookmarks in Google you want to migrate, and recreate them in Microsoft storage.\nResolve "filename too long" errors\nSometimes a folder path in Google exceeds your operating system's file path length limit. When this happens, you'll see an error when extracting a file, or when attempting to move from content\nGoogle Drive\ncontent to your new storage destination in Microsoft. You'll have to resolve this error to be able to unzip and upload files to Microsoft storage:\nIn Google, navigate to the folder you attempted to extract or move.\nTry to move or download a subfolder of the folder that caused the error.\nIf you downloaded the folder from Google, try to extract the folder.	KB0024009	KB0024009 v2.0	 your computer If you haven't already, be sure you know how to use and access Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> and sync your team's storage to your computer via the <strong>OneDrive</strong> app. Log into Google Drive with the account	5.2718		2026-01-31 16:53:07.93792	2026-01-31 16:53:07.93792	0	f5e084d91bcdd6d416c02fc4bd4bcb22
10601	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7d30707347a456583d373803846d4332	About the University Administrative (UA) Lifecycle form	Important:\nThe following process applies to all university administrative groups as of July 2023.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nSubmit a request\nSubmit a request for multiple users in a department or school\nRequest to keep an old computer\nTransfer data from an old computer to your new device\nOverview\nThe University Administrative (UA) Lifecycle Request form is primarily used to request a computer lifecycle, but the form can also be used for new computer requests and to request individual pieces of IT equipment. Devices for University Administrative groups are lifecycle replaced every four years. The computers in this form all have four years of warranty coverage. If you are not an employee in a University Administrative department, use the IT Equipment Order form to make your request; see\nAbout the IT Equipment Order form\n.\nSubmit a request\nGo to\nUA Lifecycle Request\n.\nIf prompted, log in with your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nYour name,\nIU username\n, and\nIU email address\nwill be automatically entered into "Submitter Information". Required fields are identified with a red asterisk (\n).\nFor "Are you placing this equipment request on behalf of someone else (for another staff or faculty)?", if you are placing the order for someone besides yourself, select\nYes\n.\nIf you select\nYes\n, a new field will appear. Enter the person's IU username, or their first name and last name. Select their name when it appears, and press\nEnter\n.\nFor "Does this staff/faculty work from home and need their equipment shipped to their home address?", choose\nYes\nor\nNo\n.\nIf you select\nYes\n, additional fields will display for you to enter the faculty or staff member's address and delivery details.\nIf you select\nNo\n, a different set of fields will display for you to enter the campus address for where the equipment will be deployed.\nNote:\nIf you enter the above information before you submit the form, the Endpoint Lifecycle team will ship the equipment without fee. If you submit the form, and then request that the Endpoint Lifecycle team ship the equipment, you will need to provide an account number to pay for shipping.\nFor "What is the asset tag for the device being Lifecycle replaced?", if this request is for a lifecycle computer, enter the asset tag of the computer being replaced. If you are not lifecycle replacing a device, or do not know the asset tag number, enter\nNo service tag\n.\nUnder "Recommended builds", choose one of the recommended builds, or select\nOther - none of the above options will work for me\n.\nIf you select\nOther - none of the above options will work for me\n, the "Non-Standard builds" section will display. All non-standard devices require business need and approval from leadership. The lead time for non-standard devices will be longer because of the wait for leadership approval.\nIf none of the recommended builds or non-standard builds meet your need, select\nCustom request\n. If you choose this option, you will need to go to the Dell or CDW B2B catalogs, and create a quote for the computer you need; see\nCreate a custom IT equipment quote from the B2B catalogs in Buy.IU\n.\nTo order computer accessories only, select\nPeripheral order only (dock, monitor, keyboard and mouse)\n, and then select\nYes\nfor any item you want to order, and leave the other items marked\nNo\n.\nNote:\nThe Endpoint Lifecycle team encourages staff who are replacing their computer with a lifecycle replacement to choose one of the recommended builds, as these machines have been confirmed to perform well in the IU environment. Choosing one of the recommended builds will also reduce the number of different computer models your IT department will need to support, and will help the Endpoint Lifecycle team and Indiana University leverage greater savings for those models.\nChoose a display. Depending on your device selection, you can choose from the following:\nDual displays\n: Two 24" UltraSharp monitors (U2422H); the Dell Slim Soundbar SB522A is the correct speakerbar for this display\nSingle display\n: One 27" UltraSharp monitor (U2722H); speakers are built into the monitor, so no external speaker is needed\nUltrawide\n: One 34" UltraSharp monitor (U3423WE); speakers are built into the monitor, so no external speaker is needed; this monitor is a USB-C hub monitor, and no docking station is required\nNote:\nIf you choose the standard MacBook Pro from "Recommended builds", dual monitors will not be an option, as the standard MacBook Pro does not support more than one external monitor. Instead, UITS recommends selecting the\nUltrawide\noption for the MacBook Pro.\nFor "IT Support provided by", choose your IT support provider from the drop-down menu; if you don't know your IT support provider, or are unsure if you are a UA client, choose\nDon't Know\n. The Endpoint Lifecycle team uses this information to route your request to the IT support provider that will deploy your equipment.\nUse the "Notes" field to let the Enterprise Lifecycle team know if you need antying that is not already listed on the form or to ask questions about the UA lifecycle process.\nNote:\nIf you choose a recommended or non-standard build, and enter a request to modify the build in "Notes", your request will not be processed. To request a custom build, follow the instructions in step 7\nabove\n.\nSubmit a request for multiple users in a department or school\nTo send a request for multiple users, send an email to\nELHELP@iu.edu\nwith the following information:\nThe name of the users that need a computer lifecycle replacement.\nThe service tags for the computers that will be lifecycle replaced.\nThe type of computer (laptop or desktop), as well as the model of the device as listed in "Recommended builds". Do not submit requests for non-standard devices as a bulk purchase request; instead submit them through the Lifecycle Request form.\nThe peripherals that are needed (docking station, monitors, wireless keyboard and mouse combo, speaker bar, webcam, etc.).\nThe account number to be used to place the order (if applicable).\nIf any of the users that need a lifecycle computer replacement work from home, the home address for each user that will need the equipment shipped.\nRequest to keep an old computer\nIf you are in a UA group, you will not be able to keep a computer that is being lifecycle replaced. One of the goals of this process is to remove computers from IU's inventory that are not being used. This helps facilitate better asset management and tracking, and reduces the risk of lost devices and data breaches. There will also be fewer computers for your IT support provider to track, update, and support.\nTransfer data from an old computer to your new device\nThe Endpoint Lifecycle team recommends moving your data and files to\nOneDrive\nin preparation for receiving your new computer.\nOneDrive\nis the recommended place to store your files, in general, and it allows you to immediately access your files when you log on to your new computer.\nIf you need additional time to confirm that all of your files are in\nOneDrive\n, you may ask the support technician who deployed your new computer to let you keep your old computer for a short period of time for retrieval at a later date (in a week or two). When the Endpoint Lifecycle team reaches out to you on that date, if you are unable to provide the computer, its recovery will be escalated to your departmental administrator or financial officer.	KB0025739	KB0025739 v1.0	 team recommends moving your data and files to <strong>OneDrive</strong> in preparation for receiving your new computer. <strong>OneDrive</strong> is the recommended place to store your files, in general, and it allows that all of your files are in <strong>OneDrive</strong>, you may ask the support technician who deployed your new	5.1749		2026-01-31 16:53:11.360355	2026-01-31 16:53:11.360355	0	7d30707347a456583d373803846d4332
10603	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=66c23b94332a2ad404e605570e5c7b25	Your mobile device at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nEmail\nWireless on campus\nSecurity\nApps to download\nCell service plans\nOther mobile services\nOverview\nSince configuring your device will require your IU passphrase, you must perform these steps yourself.\nDo not provide your passphrase to a sales or service representative.\nDoing so is a violation of your Acceptable Use Agreement; see\nYour responsibilities as a computer user at IU\n.\nFor help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nEmail\nFollow the appropriate instructions to\nread your IU email on your mobile device\n.\nWireless on campus\nNote:\neduroam\nis replacing\nIU Secure\nas the default wireless network for students, faculty, staff, and affiliates on all IU campuses. IU Secure is no longer available in the residence halls, and all new wireless connections should use eduroam; see\nConnect to eduroam\n.\nLocal UITS support people and UITS staff:\nMoving to eduroam affects devices that use single sign-on (SSO) through group policy on faculty and staff devices, and administrators are exploring alternative options to address this functionality. IT Community Partnerships will provide updates as more information becomes available about SSO options.\nSecurity\nBecause your device will have access to personal information, you should make an effort to secure it and the data it holds. If your device will be accessing IU institutional data, proper security is necessary. For guidelines and detailed instructions, see\nProtect data on your mobile device\n.\nApps to download\nUITS\nrecommends that students, faculty, and staff use the following apps with tablets and smartphones:\nIU Mobile:\nAbout IU Mobile\nCanvas:\nAccess Canvas at IU\nGoogle at IU:\nUse Google at IU on mobile devices\nOneDrive\n:\nAbout Microsoft OneDrive at IU\nIUanyWare via Citrix Receiver app:\nSet up and use IUanyWare\nOne.IU:\nAbout One.IU\nPharos Print:\nSubmit a print job using the Pharos Print mobile app\nTransLoc:\nIU Ride\nsafe ride service\nVPN:\nConnect to the VPN\nCell service plans\nAbout AT&T discounts\nSet up a new plan or move an existing one for discounted rates with AT&T\nAbout Verizon discounts for IU employees\nIU Corporate cellular device policy\nOther mobile services\nTo get the most out of your mobile device at IU, consider the following options:\nOne.IU\nprovides a variety of services designed for the IU community.\nConfigure your device for\nIUanyWare\n, IU's virtualization service, which lets you run a wide range of popular software. See\nAbout IUanyWare\n.	KB0023782	KB0023782 v2.0	 at IU: Use Google at IU on mobile devices <strong>OneDrive</strong>: About Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU IUanyWare via	5.107		2026-01-31 16:53:14.755241	2026-01-31 16:53:14.755241	0	66c23b94332a2ad404e605570e5c7b25
2363	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=fce8de5c47bb5a54d500b87b716d434a	Use MPI I/O on IU research supercomputers	MPI I/O is an API standard for parallel I/O that allows multiple processes of a parallel program to access data in a common file simultaneously. MPI I/O maps I/O reads and writes to message-passing sends and receives. Implementing parallel I/O can improve the performance of your parallel application.\nIdeally, MPI I/O should be used on a parallel file system (such as\nSlate\n,\nSlate-Project\n, or\nSlate-Scratch\n). Common systems (for example,\nNFS\n, EXT3FS) do not provide the MPI I/O API. However, a popular MPI I/O implementation, ROMIO, allows MPI I/O to work on NFS.\nThe following example uses MPI I/O functions to copy files. Short explanations for each step are provided below:\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <mpi.h>       /* Include the MPI definitions */\nvoid ErrorMessage(int error, int rank, char* string)\n{\nfprintf(stderr, "Process %d: Error %d in %s\\n", rank, error, string);\nMPI_Finalize();\nexit(-1);\n}\nmain(int argc, char *argv[])\n{\nint start, end;\nint length;\nint error;\nchar* buffer;\nint nprocs;\nint myrank;\nMPI_Status    status;\nMPI_File      fh;\nMPI_Offset    filesize;\nif (argc != 3)\n{\nfprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s FileToRead FileToWrite\\n", argv[0]);\nexit(-1);\n}\n/* Initialize MPI */\nMPI_Init(&argc, &argv);\nMPI_Comm_size(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &nprocs);\nMPI_Comm_rank(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &myrank);\n/* Open file to read */\nerror = MPI_File_open(MPI_COMM_WORLD, argv[1],\nMPI_MODE_RDONLY, MPI_INFO_NULL, &fh);\nif(error != MPI_SUCCESS) ErrorMessage(error, myrank, "MPI_File_open");\n/* Get the size of file */\nerror = MPI_File_get_size(fh, &filesize);\nif(error != MPI_SUCCESS) ErrorMessage(error, myrank, "MPI_File_get_size");\n/* calculate the range for each process to read */\nlength = filesize / nprocs;\nstart = length * myrank;\nif (myrank == nprocs-1)\nend = filesize;\nelse\nend = start + length;\nfprintf(stdout, "Proc %d: range = [%d, %d)\\n", myrank, start, end);\n/* Allocate space */\nbuffer = (char *)malloc((end - start) * sizeof(char));\nif (buffer == NULL) ErrorMessage(-1, myrank, "malloc");\n/* Each process read in data from the file */\nMPI_File_seek(fh, start, MPI_SEEK_SET);\nerror = MPI_File_read(fh, buffer, end-start, MPI_BYTE, &status);\nif(error != MPI_SUCCESS) ErrorMessage(error, myrank, "MPI_File_read");\n/* close the file */\nMPI_File_close(&fh);\n/* Open file to write */\nerror = MPI_File_open(MPI_COMM_WORLD, argv[2],\nMPI_MODE_WRONLY | MPI_MODE_CREATE, MPI_INFO_NULL, amp;fh);\nif(error != MPI_SUCCESS) ErrorMessage(error, myrank, "MPI_File_open");\nerror = MPI_File_write_at(fh, start, buffer, end-start, MPI_BYTE, amp;status);\nif(error != MPI_SUCCESS) ErrorMessage(error, myrank, "MPI_File_write");\n/* close the file */\nMPI_File_close(amp;fh);\n/* Finalize MPI */\nMPI_Finalize();\n}\nIn the above example:\nThe first step is to establish the MPI environment, so the\nMPI_Init(C version)\nis required, and must be the first call in every MPI program.\nThe\nMPI_File_open\nfunction opens a file on all processes. Several access modes are supported. The one used in the example (\nMPI_MODE_RDONLY\n) is for read only.\nThe\nMPI_File_get_size\nfunction gives the file size, which is used later to determine the offset for each process.\nThe\nMPI_File_seek\nfunction points to the position in the file where each process will start reading data.\nThe\nMPI_File_read\nfunction reads data into the buffer specified in the second parameter. The size to be read is defined in the third parameter.\nThe\nMPI_File_write_at\nfunction will write data from the buffer (the third parameter) into a specific position in the file given by the second parameter.\nThe\nMPI_File_close\nfunction closes the file opened by\nMPI_File_open\n.\nThe MPI environment in every process must be terminated by the\nMPI_Finalize\nfunction. No MPI calls may be made after\nMPI_Finalize\n.\nFortran examples\nFollowing are two Fortran examples:\nExample 1:\n!^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nprogram  create_file\n!**************************************************************************\n!  This is a Fortran 90 program to write data directly to a file by each\n!  member of an MPI group.  It is suitable for large jobs which will not\n!  fit into core memory (such as "out of core" solvers)\n!\n!  Copyright by the Trustees of Indiana University 2005\n***************************************************************************\nUSE MPI\ninteger, parameter :: kind_val = 4\ninteger, parameter  :: filesize = 40\ninteger :: realsize = 4\ninteger ::  rank, ierr, fh, nprocs, num_reals\ninteger ::  i, region\nreal (kind = kind_val) :: datum\ninteger, dimension (MPI_STATUS_SIZE) :: status\ninteger (kind = MPI_OFFSET_KIND) :: offset, empty\n!  Set filename to output datafile\ncharacter (len = *), parameter :: filename = "/u/ac/rays/new_data.dat"\nreal (kind = kind_val), dimension ( : ), allocatable  :: bucket\n!  Basic MPI set-up\ncall MPI_INIT(ierr)\ncall MPI_COMM_RANK(MPI_COMM_WORLD, rank, ierr)\ncall MPI_COMM_SIZE(MPI_COMM_WORLD, nprocs, ierr)\n!  Sanity print\nprint*, "myid is ", rank\n!  Carve out a piece of the output file and create a data bucket\nempty = 0\nregion = filesize / (nprocs )\noffset = ( region * rank )\nallocate (bucket(region))\n!  There is no guarantee that an old file will be clobbered,  so wipe out any previous output file\nif (rank .eq. 0) then\ncall MPI_File_delete(filename, MPI_INFO_NULL, ierr)\nendif\n!  Set the file handle to an initial value (this should not be required)\nfh = 0\n!  Open the output file\ncall MPI_FILE_OPEN(MPI_COMM_WORLD, filename, MPI_MODE_CREATE+MPI_MODE_RDWR, MPI_INFO_NULL, fh, ierr)\n!  Wait on everyone to catch up.\ncall MPI_BARRIER(MPI_COMM_WORLD, ierr)\n!  Do some work and fill up the data bucket\ncall random_seed()\ndo i = 1, region\ncall random_number(datum)\nbucket(i) = datum * 1000000. * (rank + 1)\nprint *, " bucket  ",i ,"= ", bucket(i)\nenddo\n!  Basic "belt and suspenders insurance that everyone's file pointer is at the beginning of the output file.\ncall MPI_FILE_SET_VIEW(fh, empty, MPI_REAL4, MPI_REAL4, 'native', MPI_INFO_NULL, ierr)\n!  Send the data bucket to the output file in the proper place\ncall MPI_FILE_WRITE_AT(fh, offset, bucket, region, MPI_REAL4, status, ierr)\n!  Wait on everyone to finish and close up shop\ncall MPI_BARRIER(MPI_COMM_WORLD, ierr)\ncall MPI_FILE_CLOSE(fh, ierr)\ncall MPI_FINALIZE(ierr)\nend  program  create_file\nExample 2:\n!^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nprogram  read_file\n!**************************************************************************\n!  This is a Fortran 90 program to read data directly from a file by each\n!  member of an MPI group.  It is suitable for large jobs which will not\n!  fit into core memory (such as "out of core" solvers)\n!\n!  Copyright by the Trustees of Indiana University 2005\n***************************************************************************\nUSE MPI\ninteger, parameter :: kind_val = 4\ninteger,  parameter  :: filesize = 40\ninteger :: realsize = 4\ninteger ::  rank, ierr, fh, nprocs, num_reals\ninteger ::  i, region\ninteger, dimension (MPI_STATUS_SIZE) :: status\ninteger (kind = MPI_OFFSET_KIND) :: offset, empty\n!  Set filename to output datafile\ncharacter (len = *), parameter :: filename = "/u/ac/rays/new_data.dat"\nreal (kind = kind_val), dimension ( : ), allocatable  :: bucket\n!  Basic MPI set-up\ncall MPI_INIT(ierr)\ncall MPI_COMM_RANK(MPI_COMM_WORLD, rank, ierr)\ncall MPI_COMM_SIZE(MPI_COMM_WORLD, nprocs, ierr)\n!  Carve out a piece of the output file and create a data bucket\nempty = 0\nregion = filesize / (nprocs )\noffset = (region * rank )\nallocate (bucket(region))\n!  Sanity print\nprint*, "myid is ", rank\n!  Set the file handle to an initial value (this should not be required)\nfh = 0\n!  Open the output file\ncall MPI_FILE_OPEN(MPI_COMM_WORLD, filename, MPI_MODE_RDONLY, MPI_INFO_NULL, fh, ierr)\n!  Wait on everyone to catch up.\ncall MPI_BARRIER(MPI_COMM_WORLD, ierr)\n!  Basic "belt and suspenders insurance that everyone's file pointer is at the beginning of the output file.\ncall MPI_FILE_SET_VIEW(fh, 0, MPI_REAL4, MPI_REAL4, 'native', MPI_INFO_NULL, ierr)\n!  Read only the section of the data file each process needs and put data in the data bucket.\ncall MPI_FILE_READ_AT(fh, offset, bucket, region, MPI_REAL4, status, ierr)\n!  We could check the values received in the bucket (debug hint)\n!\n!      do i = 1, region\n!         print *, "my id is ", rank, " and my ", i, "number is ", bucket(i)\n!      enddo\n!  Wait on everyone to finish and close up shop\ncall MPI_BARRIER(MPI_COMM_WORLD, ierr)\ncall MPI_FILE_CLOSE(fh, ierr)\ncall MPI_FINALIZE(ierr)\nend  program  read_file\nResearch computing support at IU is provided by the\nResearch Technologies\ndivision of\nUITS\n. To ask a question or get help regarding\nResearch Technologies\nservices, including IU's\nresearch supercomputers\nand research storage systems, and the scientific,  statistical, and mathematical applications available on those systems,\ncontact UITS\nResearch Technologies\n. For service-specific support contact information, see\nResearch computing support at IU\n.	KB0022536	KB0022536 v3.0	 application. Ideally, MPI I/O should be used on a parallel file system (such as <strong>Slate</strong>, <strong>Slate</strong>-Project, or <strong>Slate</strong>	5.2243		2026-01-31 14:35:38.96617	2026-01-31 15:55:30.825421	0	c9491645475ce250d500b87b716d4368
2301	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024279	IU internal server co-location agreement	This document represents an agreement between a\nclient\nand UITS for server co-location in Indiana University Data Centers. The responsibilities and services provided are outlined, which include equipment installations, data center access, and operational procedures.\nDefinitions\nData Centers:\nAdvanced Cyberinfrastructure Facility (the IU Data Centers at IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis)\nUITS:\nThe party providing service within the Data Centers\nClient\n:\nThe IU party receiving service through UITS\nOverview\nThis document describes the server co-location services that University Information Technology Services will provide. Departments can use physical space in the Data Centers for their servers as a transition strategy to standard options provided by UITS that improve efficiency and reduce environmental impact:\nVirtualization:\nIntelligent Infrastructure (II) is the recommended path for departmental web, application, and database servers. Moving to IU's virtual environment achieves benefits of hardware efficiencies, reduced environmental impact, and increased flexibility to respond quickly to changing service needs. For more, see\nAbout IU Intelligent Infrastructure\n.\nConsolidation:\nA\nco-location service provided by IU's\nResearch Technologies\nconsolidates physical servers and components into fewer physical entities, achieving economies of scale and allowing greater flexibility for shifting resources from one application area to another.\nEnvironmental\nMachine Room environmental services and security in the Data Centers will be provided by UITS as follows:\nPower backup:\nUPS service and generator backup are provided as a secondary source of power in the event of utility power failure. UPS service is designed to provide power for seamless transition to an emergency generator. In the event of a lengthy power failure, the generator can be refueled as necessary and run indefinitely until power is restored. UITS may determine that it is not in the university's best interest to run the generator for extended periods if power has not been restored, and every effort to inform the\nclient\nwill be made.\nFire protection:\nFire suppression is provided via dry pipe, pre-action sprinkler system, in accordance with university risk management policy.  All other fire protection is provided in accordance with university policy and procedure and Indiana state code.\nCooling:\nAir conditioning is provided via chilled water coils in computer room air conditioner (CRAC) units:\nIndianapolis Data Center:\nPrimary source of chilled water is campus (CTE) water, with a dedicated backup chiller in case of a loss of primary supply\nBloomington Data Center:\nDedicated chilled water plant with redundant chillers and cooling towers\nClient\nwill follow shipping/receiving and storage (equipment, supplies, boxes) guidelines set forth and provided by UITS.\nClient\nwill coordinate their installation activities, including any temporary need for secure storage, with UITS.\nClient\nis responsible for removing debris, supplies, etc., from the area after installation. Only the equipment can be stored in the rack.\nSecurity/access provisions\nSecurity system:\nVideo cameras digitally record activity at each machine room entrance door. Access to the Data Centers is provided to only those individuals who are authorized. For non-regular visitor access, the procedures outlined in the\nData Center access control policy\ndocument should be followed.\nClient\ns agree to operate within the security and access policies and procedures of the Data Centers.  This includes access limitations and sign-in procedures.  A copy of these procedures will be provided by UITS and updated on a periodic basis.\nClient\ns will provide UITS with a list of personnel who are authorized for access.\nUpon termination of this agreement or the resignation/termination of staff, it is the\nclient\n's responsibility to notify UITS of staff changes. UITS will immediately deactivate card access privileges.\nClient\ns that occupy an entire rack and/or require isolation from shared rack space may install additional physical rack security measures at their own cost, provided these measures have been reviewed and approved by UITS in advance. Examples of such measures include cameras, locked racks, and rack door sensors. If the\nclient\nchooses additional methods, UITS must also have access on-site (keys for locked racks in case of an emergency).\nData network provisions\nAll cabinets will have network switches installed at the top of the cabinet to provide 10/100/1000 Mbps Ethernet connections into the Data Center switching infrastructure (10000 Mbps available and priced on an individual request). All public and private Ethernet connections are provided by UITS unless very special circumstances are reviewed and approved by Network Engineering and Campus Network Infrastructure.\nClient\nwill not deploy switching gear or other networking devices in their rack unless some specific exception is requested, documented, and granted by UITS.\nAll servers will be place behind the Data Center firewall.\nEquipment\nServer rack space will be assigned and all servers will be installed in the standard cabinets provided by UITS unless the server manufacturer specifically dictates the equipment must be housed in their proprietary cabinets. Proof of vendor requirements for proprietary cabinets must be submitted to UITS. Such cabinets should have front, back, and side panels. Doors on the cabinet must be closed when no one is working in the unit. Server racks should not exceed 5 kW per rack (additional cost to be applied based on power consumption).\nClient\ns will provide a space plan to UITS.  The space plan will include the number of servers, equipment, storage devices, and other devices having space and power considerations. UITS will approve these plans before\nClient\ns begin installing equipment.\nClient\ns will be responsible for delivery arrangements of equipment to the Data Centers and notifying UITS of the delivery date.\nClient\ns are responsible for all costs associated with this process and are liable for any equipment damaged during transportation.\nClient\ns are responsible for the installation of the equipment in the racks, and retain responsibility for the equipment and liability for any damages to that equipment during the installation and through to the initial power-up process.  After installation, UITS assumes responsibilities as outlined in this agreement.\nClient\ns will adhere to the "\nData Center standards\n" document.\nAll servers in the Data Centers are expected to be administered in a secure manner using industry best practices (\nSecurity of Information Technology Resources (IT-12)\n), including employment of host based firewalls and to be operated behind the Data Center firewall.\nClient\ns agree to provide UITS with firewall rules that restrict incoming traffic to include only those protocols, ports, and properly sourced packets as needed for operation of each server.\nClient\ns will not store cardholder data on equipment without compliance to Payment Card Industry Data Security Standards (PCI DSS). The PCI DSS security requirements apply to all system components. In the context of PCI DSS, system components are defined as any network component, server, or application that is included in or connected to the cardholder data environment. System components also include any virtualization components, such as virtual machines, virtual switches/routers, virtual appliances, virtual applications/desktops, and hypervisors. The cardholder data environment is comprised of people, processes, and technology that store, process, or transmit cardholder data or sensitive authentication data.\nIf there is an exception to process cardholder data, you must be compliant with the PCI DSS. This will require review and approval from UISO and the IU Office of the Treasurer.\nDisaster recovery\nThe current UITS disaster recovery plan for the Data Centers does not include replacement of co-located systems. Systems in the Data Centers are not insured by UITS. In the unlikely event that the Data Centers are substantially damaged or destroyed, UITS will make a best effort to restore services starting with those systems critical to the business needs of the university. Co-located systems remain the responsibility of their owners. Should the Data Centers be disabled, but owners' systems remain undamaged, UITS will attempt to find an acceptable alternate site or return the systems temporarily to their owners. UITS may be able to assist owners with disaster recovery planning.\nUITS cannot be responsible for system owner's business continuity or any losses directly or indirectly associated with use of UITS co-location services.\nOperational support\nUITS will provide contact information to\nClient\ns for use in requesting "eyes and hands" assistance with\nClient\ns' equipment when problems arise.  System monitoring and failure notification will be provided by UITS operations staff on site 24x7x365. There are no service-level guarantees for the availability of such resources.\nClient\ns will provide contact information to UITS for use in notifications.\nUITS will provide timely notification to\nClient\ns for planned and unplanned outages of power and/or cooling.\nUITS will make a good faith effort to provide workstation space to\nClient\ns.  However, the amount of space available to\nClient\ns may be minimal.\nClient\ns should plan accordingly.\nClient\ns will provide timely notification to UITS for all activities and agrees to coordinate with UITS Data Center Operations for any rack space changes.\nClient\ns will not sublet rack space or resale the functional equivalent of the service to another project, department, or third party.\nClient\ns may incur one-time setup charges if their requirements are beyond standards for rack(s) and/or power.\nData Center Operations contact information\nVisit the\nDCOPS Request Form\nor email\ndcops@iu.edu\n.\nRack definitions\nRack space:\nThe rack space is assigned for the server co-location. The rack space may not be contiguous and could possibly be shared with other co-location customers.	KB0024279	KB0024279 v1.0	: A co-location service provided by IU's <strong>Research</strong> Technologies consolidates physical servers	1.9087	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 14:35:32.281267	2026-01-31 16:43:43.900049	0	bcffac7b476456583d373803846d434c
10519	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=8e203cff476456583d373803846d43ee	About storage locations and access for shared files in Microsoft Teams	Table of contents\nOverview\nFiles shared during channel conversations\nStorage location\nWays to get there\nEmail messages sent to channels\nStorage location\nWays to get there\nFiles shared in direct messages (not "Team conversations")\nStorage location\nWays to get there\nMeeting recordings\nLearn more\nFiles shared during meetings\nStorage location\nWays to get there\nNotes in\nMicrosoft OneNote\nStorage location\nWays to get there\nOverview\nWhen a file is shared in\nMicrosoft Teams\n, its storage location depends on how the file was uploaded and how it was shared with you. See below for information about where such files are stored, and how to access them.\nFiles shared during channel conversations\nStorage location\nA Microsoft\nSharePoint Online\nsite is created for each team, within which a document library is created for each channel. Files are stored in the appropriate document library.\nWays to get there\nTo view files shared during channel conversations:\nWithin the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication:\nNavigate to the\nFiles\ntab.\nWithin the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication:\nNavigate to the\nFiles\ntab.\nSelect\nOpen in SharePoint\n.\nIn a browser:\nLog into\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nSelect\nTeams\n.\nNavigate to the appropriate team.\nNavigate to the\nFiles\ntab.\nSelect\nOpen in SharePoint\n.\nIn a browser:\nLog into\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nSelect\nSharePoint\n.\nNavigate to the appropriate team.\nIn the navigation menu on the left, select\nDocuments\n.\nEmail messages sent to channels\nStorage location\nEmail messages sent to channels are stored in the team's\nSharePoint Online\nsite.\nWays to get there\nTo view email messages sent to channels:\nWithin the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication:\nNavigate to the\nFiles\ntab.\nOpen the\nEmail Messages\nfolder.\nWithin the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication:\nNavigate to the\nFiles\ntab.\nSelect\nOpen in SharePoint\n.\nOpen the\nEmail Messages\nfolder.\nIn a browser\n:\nLog into\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nSelect\nTeams\n.\nNavigate to the appropriate team.\nOpen the\nFiles\ntab.\nSelect\nOpen in SharePoint\n.\nSelect the\nEmail Messages\nfolder.\nIn a browser\n:\nLog into\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nSelect\nSharePoint\n.\nNavigate to the appropriate team.\nIn the navigation menu on the left, select\nDocuments\n.\nSelect the\nEmail Messages\nfolder.\nFiles shared in direct messages (not "Team conversations")\nStorage location\nDirect messages sent outside of a team (that is, not through a channel within a team) are not associated with a team, even if the sender and receiver are part of the same team. As a result, files shared in direct messages are stored in the user's\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\n, in the\nMicrosoft Teams\nChat\nFiles\nfolder (not in\nMicrosoft Teams\n).\nWays to get there\nTo view files shared in direct messages:\nIn a browser:\nLog into\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nSelect\nOneDrive\n.\nNavigate to the\nMicrosoft Teams Chat Files\nfolder.\nMeeting recordings\nFor details about meeting recordings, see\nGet started with Microsoft Stream (on SharePoint)\n.\nLearn more\nRecord a meeting in Teams\nFiles shared during meetings\nStorage location\nFiles shared during meetings are stored in the team's\nSharePoint Online\nsite.\nWays to get there\nTo view files shared during meetings:\nWithin the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication:\nNavigate to the\nFiles\ntab.\nWithin the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication:\nNavigate to the\nFiles\ntab.\nSelect\nOpen in SharePoint\n.\nIn a browser:\nLog into\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nSelect\nTeams\n, and navigate to the appropriate team.\nOpen the\nFiles\ntab, and then select\nOpen in SharePoint\n.\nIn a browser:\nLog into\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nSelect\nSharePoint\n, and navigate to the appropriate team.\nIn the navigation menu on the left, select\nDocuments\n.\nNotes in\nMicrosoft OneNote\nStorage location\nNotes in\nMicrosoft OneNote\nare stored in the team's\nSharePoint Online\nsite.\nWays to get there\nTo view\nMicrosoft OneNote\nnotes:\nWithin the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication:\nNavigate to the\nNotes\ntab.\nIn a browser:\nLog into\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nSelect\nTeams\n, and navigate to the appropriate team.\nOpen the\nNotes\ntab.\nIn a browser:\nLog into\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n.\nSelect\nSharePoint\n, and navigate to the appropriate team.\nIn the navigation menu on the left, select\nNotebook\n.	KB0025435	KB0025435 v1.0	 in the user's Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU, in the Microsoft Teams Chat Files folder (not in Microsoft Teams. Select <strong>OneDrive</strong>. Navigate to the Microsoft Teams Chat Files folder. Meeting recordings For details	4.7592		2026-01-31 16:51:23.369658	2026-01-31 16:53:21.556398	0	8e203cff476456583d373803846d43ee
11733	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3c2074ff476456583d373803846d4362	Configure an ACM mail-enabled group	While ACM doesn't have a way to create a mailing list based on a group membership directly, it is possible to have a mail-enabled Active Directory group based on an ACM group.\nFirst, you need to make your ACM group available for use as an ADS security group:\nGo to\nAccess Control Management (ACM)\n.\nSelect or create an ACM group. For help, see\nCreate, edit, or delete Access Control Management (ACM) groups\n.\nOn the "Group" page, under "Features", check\nActive Directory Services\n.\nThis will create an Active Directory security group that contains the same members as your ACM group. ACM will automatically generate an ADS group name based on the group name you specified with an\niu-entlmt-\nprefix added. Membership changes that you make to your ACM group will be automatically reflected in the associated ADS group. This includes removing people from the group if they leave the university or are not compliant with the relevant data use tutorials.\nOnce you have an ADS group associated with your ACM group, you will need to work with your\nlocal UITS support person\nto have it mail-enabled and determine an alias for it or a display name for the\nGlobal Address List\n(GAL). To find the ADS group name, so you can share it with your local UITS support person, look under the "Features" section when you edit your ACM group.\nUITS staff and local UITS support people:\nTo mail-enable the group or contact, email\nsct2@iu.edu\nwith the alias you want to use. Names should conform to IU naming conventions as much as possible; see\nRecommended naming conventions for IU Windows computers and groups\n.\nNote:\nThe security or distribution group display name (which appears in the GAL) can be different from the object name (which appears in Active Directory Users and Computers, or ADUC) and the alias, for example:\nADUC name:\nbl-uits-exchangetestgroup\nAlias:\nbl-uits-exchtestgrp\nGAL display name: UITS Exchange Testing Group\nIf you want a specific display name, include it in your request.	KB0025322	KB0025322 v1.0	While ACM doesn't have a way to create a mailing list based on a group membership directly, it is possible to have a mail-enabled Active Directory group based on an ACM group. First, you need to make your ACM group available for use as an ADS security group: Once you have an ADS group associated with your ACM group, you will need to work with yo	1.6151		2026-01-31 17:16:43.213644	2026-01-31 17:16:43.213644	0	3c2074ff476456583d373803846d4362
11735	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a9ea8962476fa6d02fc6a55b416d43cb	Executive and Internal Technology Support	Table of contents\nOverview\nContact EITS Internal Support (UITS staff)\nContact EITS Executive Support (IU administration)\nOverview\nExecutive and Internal Technology Support (EITS) manages multi-campus 24/7/365 IT support for IU executives and their immediate representatives, serving the President's and Vice Presidents' offices, the University Counsel, the Office of the Provost, and the Board of Trustees. EITS also provides technical support for UITS and UA group staff at IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis.\nEITS provides broad computing support, including desktop, laptop, mobile device, and infrastructure support, operating system and application installation and continued updates, IT support for events hosted by EITS-supported staff, and assistance with IU computing accounts and systems.\nContact EITS Internal Support (UITS and UA group staff)\nSupport hours: 8am-5pm, Monday-Friday; closed for campus holidays.\nContact information\nPhone\n812-856-9999\nEmail\neits@iu.edu\nIn-person\nBloomington:\nCyberinfrastructure Building\n005-25 through 005-32\nIndianapolis:\nInformatics & Communications Technology Complex\n129\nUITS staff:\nServices include, but are not limited to:\nWindows and Macintosh client support\nMobile device support\nLaptop and mobile device encryption\nUITS file server access for individuals and workgroups\nAntivirus scans of server data\nWindows common builds via Configuration Manager (SCCM)\nMac common builds via Jamf Pro\nDesktop/laptop builds with standard software\nSoftware installation/updates of standard software and any specialty software\nSecurity patches and updates\nAssistance with planning and purchasing of hardware and software\nA/V and conference room support\nComputer equipment relocation and setup\nRecycling used computer equipment\nSecure erase of data and processing of equipment for Surplus\nIf you have other requests, or don't know whom to contact regarding a technology question or problem, EITS staff will do their best to assist you or direct you to someone who can.\nContact EITS Executive Support (IU administration)\nBusiness hours: 8am-5pm, Monday-Friday; closed for campus holidays. Technician on call 24/7.\nContact information\nPhone\nBloomington (Bryan Hall): 812-856-0600\nBloomington (CIB): 812-856-9966\nIndianapolis (ICTC): 317-278-0600\nIndianapolis (University Hall): 317-278-9966\nEmail\nexecit@iu.edu\nIn-person\nBloomington:\nBryan Hall\n001\nCyberinfrastructure Building\n005-25 through 005-32\nIndianapolis:\nBy request only	KB0026335	KB0026335 v4.0	Table of contents Overview Contact EITS Internal Support (UITS staff) Contact EITS Executive Support (IU administration) Overview EITS provides broad <strong>computing</strong> support, including desktop, laptop updates, IT support for events hosted by EITS-supported staff, and assistance with IU <strong>computing</strong>	1.6031		2026-01-31 17:16:47.18693	2026-01-31 17:16:47.18693	0	a9ea8962476fa6d02fc6a55b416d43cb
11739	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=88628062472a7a18d500b87b716d4332	Apply the IU Common Build to an iPad	Table of contents\nOverview\nComputer naming convention\nDevice requirements\nEnroll a device\nEnroll a new device\nMigrate a device\nDevice security\nApplication support\nAvailable applications\nOverview\niPads can be enrolled in the IU Common Build via Apple School Manager, and are assigned to the same sites as computers.\nComputer naming convention\nComputers will use the naming convention\nIU-\nSerialNumber\n, to stay in alignment with our Windows counterparts.\nDevice requirements\nThe IU Common Build requires that any iPad enrolled in the service be able to run a currently supported version of iPadOS.\nEnroll a device\nEnroll a new device\nUpon receiving the iPad, ensure that the serial number has been enrolled in Apple School Manager.\nPower on the device and connect it to the network via an ethernet adapter or, if necessary, Wi-Fi.\nProceed through the setup steps until you reach the "Remote Management" slide. This will indicate that the device has been enrolled properly.\nIf the device was set up while connected to eduroam (or IU Secure, etc.), go to\nSystem Settings\n>\nWi-Fi\n>\neduroam\n>\nForget This Network\n.\nImportant:\nWhen building a single-user iPad, it is highly recommended to use a\nwired (ethernet) connection\n. If you must use Wi-Fi during initial setup, make sure to "Forget This Network" for any credentials (to eduroam, IU Secure, etc.) before deploying the iPad to the client. On iOS and iPadOS,\nanyone with a passcode\ncan read Wi-Fi credentials for networks that were joined manually.\niPads enrolled in the multi-user Common Build should receive a Wi-Fi certificate via Configuration Profile upon enrollment, avoiding the need to log into the IU network manually through eduroam or similar networks. When Wi-Fi credentials are deployed via Configuration Profile, Wi-Fi is joined automatically and credentials are not exposed, even to users with a passcode. If your multi-user iPads are still using manual credentials, contact EMS so they can apply a Wi-Fi Configuration Profile to those devices.\nMigrate a device\nReassign the device from its existing site in Apple School Manager to the new IU-ADM01 site designation.\nAfter transferring the device, reset it according to the instructions at\nReturn iPad settings to their defaults\n.\nOnce the device is reset, restart it and proceed through the build steps\nabove\n.\nDevice security\nAll device security is handled through configuration profiles to enforce security settings. These settings include the requirement of a six-digit PIN for unlocking the device. For a full list of settings, use the\nEMS General Inquiry FireForm\n.\nApplication support\nAll device applications must be installed through either the IU Self Service application or through the Apple Store using volume purchasing. All device applications are automatically updated through the App Store.\nNote:\nUpdate times are usually scheduled for midnight each day by default.\nAvailable applications\nAdobe Creative Cloud\nDuo Mobile\nIvanti Secure Access\nMicrosoft Excel\nMicrosoft Outlook\nMicrosoft PowerPoint\nMicrosoft Teams\nMicrosoft Word\nNumbers\nPages	KB0025783	KB0025783 v4.0	Table of contents Overview Computer naming convention Device requirements Enroll a device Enroll a new device Migrate a device Device security Application support Available applications Overview iPads can be enrolled in the IU Common Build via Apple School Manager, and are assigned to the same sites as computers. Computer naming convention Devic	1.5892	km-aud-uits, km-aud-local-support, km-aud-ems-admins	2026-01-31 17:16:51.333463	2026-01-31 17:16:51.333463	0	88628062472a7a18d500b87b716d4332
10614	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=541b9c9047aaf69c2fc6a55b416d4355	Known issues with Microsoft Teams	Teams Calls\nprovides office telephone service for faculty and staff at Indiana University.\nFollowing is continually updated information about the status of known issues with\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n, including\nTeams Calls\n.\nIssue\nDescription\nWorkaround\nLast updated\nTeams CCX500, MP54 and MP56 desk phones that were tied to group accounts might suddenly display the sign-in screen but after sign-in is attempted, the phone gets stuck in a loop and fails to fully sign in.\nDue to a type of security upgrade, Teams CCX500,  MP54 and MP56 desk phones which are tied to group accounts will display a sign-in screen but never fully finish signing in because Duo authentication for the group account may be missing, phone device updates may be needed, and the MS Authenticator app may be mistakenly offered as an alternative during sign in .\nMake sure Duo is set up for the group account, and then:\nSign any user (but no group accounts) into the phone.\nHave UITS Telecom remotely complete all updates to the phone. Sign the user out.\nSign in as the group account.\nWhen prompted, do not use MS Authenticator; instead, choose\nNext\n.\nChoose\nDifferent sign in Method\n.\nChoose\nExternal\n.\nFollow any prompts for Duo authentication.\nJanuary 13, 2026\nA single digit (number) may appear to be stuck on the Teams desk phone screen.\nA single digit (number) stuck on the phone screen interferes with the input of a new phone number to be dialed.\nTap the "Backspace" button on your phone screen to erase the stuck digit before dialing. This problem may be resolved in later updates.\nOctober 14, 2025\nWhen using the Microsoft Teams web app, full-featured calling is not available.\nDifficulty using the Teams web app with off-campus calls and calling\nInstall the Microsoft Teams desktop app on your local computer/laptop to enjoy the full features.\nMay 27, 2025\nSigning into a Teams CCX500, MP54, or MP56 desk phone\nSince the mandatory firmware update on May 18, 2025, the option to sign in from the Microsoft website using\nREFRESH CODE\n(as prompted on the phone screen) does not work.\nOn the phone screen, touch\nSign In On This Device\n; when prompted, enter the username and passphrase. Respond to the Duo prompt; when prompted touch\nRegister\n. Follow any other standard prompts.\nMay 20, 2025\nAccounts with an A1 license are unable to set meetings to automatically record\nThe\nRecord and Transcribe automatically\nbutton is not present for A1 accounts. A1 accounts will need to manually start meeting recordings and transcription. This is true for personal meetings or channel meetings.\nThis behavior is by design; accounts with an A5 license do not have this issue.\nJune 12, 2024\nNew Teams calls muted when selecting hyperlinks\nNew Teams will mute calls when a user selects a hyperlink. See\nNew Teams Mutes Mic When Clicking Hyperlinks\n.\nFix anticipated late February 2024.\nFebruary 8, 2024\nMP50 phone not fully compatible with new Teams client\nThe phone works as a USB device, but it displays the wrong date and time, and it shows PC Softphone instead of user.\nWhen trying to choose options on a phone menu, the dial pad of MP50 and touch display may not work; use dial pad on client instead. UITS is waiting on a firmware update from the vendor.\nUITS is checking with the vendor for a solution.\nApril 1, 2024\nInability to dial or end a call using the Yealink MP50 phone\nIf the\nSkype\nfor Business\napp is running on your computer, the phone may not respond when using the dial pad, when the handset is replaced, or when choosing the End Call screen option.\nCompletely exit the\nSkype\nfor Business\napp. You may also need to disable\nSkype\nfor Business\nin your list of startup apps to prevent it from launching automatically when Windows launches.\nMarch 29, 2023\nLinux client unsupported\nUsers are unable to download Microsoft's Teams client for Linux; previous downloads no longer work.\nLinux users can use the web version of Teams to receive incoming calls or make PC-to-PC calls. If you use Linux and need to dial outbound calls by number, use a mobile app, or request an IP phone from your department.\nMarch 3, 2023\nYealink MP50 missing user info, dial tone, dialing issues\nWindows users may not hear dial tone, or see or be able to use the dial pad. They may not see their name or number appear on the screen.\nWindows users should sign into the phone using\nHot Desking mode\n.\nMarch 3, 2023\nYealink MP50 does not work with macOS.\nYealink MP50 does not work with macOS; compatible only with Windows/PC.\nChoose another\nUSB phone or headset equipment\n.\nMarch 3, 2023\nLoud or persistent dial tone after\nSkype\nfor Business\ncall or meeting is completed.\nInteraction between Teams and Skype for Business may cause Skype for Business users to hear a persistent dial tone or off-hook sound even after their call or meeting is completed. Microsoft is working on a Teams update which will likely solve this problem.\nExit and then restart Skype for Business and/or the Teams app, or restart the computer. This issue should no longer occur when all call or meeting participants are converted to\nTeams Calls\n.\nFebruary 17, 2023\nFeatures missing in some web browsers\nWhen using the web version of\nMicrosoft Teams\n, not all web browsers are fully supported. See\nLimits and specifications for Microsoft Teams\n.\nUse the web app in a fully supported browser, or download the desktop app.\nFebruary 16, 2023\nNo dial tone and difficulties with call control on CX300 USB phones with\nTeams Calls\non Windows\nUsers on\nTeams Calls\nwith newer Dell Latitude laptops and CX300 phones may experience the following:\nNo dial tone\nDial pad on the CX300 phone does not work\nAnswering and ending calls with the phone does not work (these must be done from the Teams app)\nTo restore call control, disable the Intel Smart Sound Technology driver:\nRun Windows updates.\nIn the Device manager, choose\nSound, video and game controllers\n.\nRight-click\nIntell Smart Sound Technology/Intel SST\n, and choose\nDisable device\n.\nUnplug the phone, and restart the computer.\nPlug the phone back in.\nNote:\nFor some steps, admin permissions may be necessary.\nThis will allow you to use the Dial pad (dialing will start after a 4-5 second delay) and Speakerphone button, and to answer and end the call with the handset; however, you'll still hear no dial tone.\nAn alternative USB phone (Yealink MP50) is available; see\nAbout office telephone equipment at IU\n.\nFebruary 6, 2023\nNo dial tone on CX300 phones with\nTeams Calls\non macOS\nMac users may not hear a dial tone on CX300 USB phones; however, the phones will work to make and receive calls in most cases.\nFebruary 6, 2023\nDelayed audio when answering phone calls\nAfter answering an incoming phone call, some\nTeams Calls\nusers hear silence of several seconds before the call connects.\nJanuary 20, 2023\nPolls are unavailable in channel meetings.\nPolls are only available for meetings created outside of channels.\nNovember 9, 2022\nExternal users cannot upload files in reply to "file upload" questions in Microsoft Forms.\nYou cannot configure "file upload" questions in Microsoft Forms to allow file uploads from non-IU respondents.\nJune 29, 2022\nRenaming a channel in a team in\nMicrosoft Teams\nwill not rename the root folder associated with the channel.\nIt is not possible to rename the root folder associated with the channel while keeping it tied to the channel. For more about this behavior, see "Shared sites, standard channels, and folders" in\nFile visibility in Microsoft Teams\n.\nDecember 2, 2021\nCannot send meeting invitations with more than 750 recipients in\nOutlook\n(Win32)\nIn\nOutlook\n(Win32), if you try to schedule or reschedule a meeting with a number of attendees larger than the new 750 recipient limit, you'll see an error message.\nReduce the number of recipients or user distribution lists to send the meeting invitation.\nJune 15, 2021\nGuest users cannot sync files in some cases.\nExternal guest users in a Team may not be able to use the\nSync\nbutton to sync files to\nOneDrive\n. They may be able to if they are logging in with another institutional\nOneDrive\naccount at an institution that allows syncing files, with some limitations. If they are using a personal or free Microsoft account, sync will not work. For more, see\nB2B Sync\n.\nMarch 29, 2021\nSome invitees do not get meeting invitation email messages.\nSome invitees will not get a meeting invitation email message if the meeting is scheduled via\nMicrosoft Teams\n.\nInvitees who are missing the invitation can still join the meeting if they are a member of the Team and channel the meeting is for. Alternatively, another meeting attendee can forward the invitation to them.\nMarch 8, 2021\nUnable to join a\nMicrosoft Teams\nmeeting if the computer's time zone is not set correctly\nIf the computer's time zone is not set correctly based on location, the\nMicrosoft Teams\nmeeting's\nJoin\nbutton may not appear.\nTo resolve this, make sure the device connecting to\nMicrosoft Teams\nis configured to automatically set the time zone and is correctly detecting the appropriate time zone.\nSeptember 7, 2020\nUnable to recreate a\nMicrosoft Teams\nchannel with the same name as one that has been deleted\nAfter a channel has been deleted in\nMicrosoft Teams\n, a new channel with the same name cannot be created for 30 days.\nJuly 1, 2020\nThe\nCalendar\n/\nMeetings\nbutton in\nMicrosoft Teams\nrequires an Exchange Online (EXO) account.\nResolved; the IU EXO migration is complete.\nApril 19, 2020\nDifficulties changing team picture\nIn\nMicrosoft Teams\n, when attempting to change the team picture, team owners may receive the error, "We're having trouble updating your team. Please try again later."\nIf you make a change to the team name at the same time, the picture will update properly. If needed, you can change the team name back to its original value directly after.\nJanuary 30, 2020	KB0023574	KB0023574 v6.0	 not be able to use the Sync button to sync files to <strong>OneDrive</strong>. They may be able to if they are logging in with another institutional <strong>OneDrive</strong> account at an institution that allows syncing files	4.602		2026-01-31 16:53:35.092495	2026-01-31 16:53:35.092495	0	541b9c9047aaf69c2fc6a55b416d4355
11050	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=76246f4733feaa5004e605570e5c7b78	Campus accessibility resources at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nOffice of Civil Rights Compliance\nAccessible Educational Services (AES) offices by campus\nAdditional resources for employees\nIU Bloomington\nIU Indianapolis\nIU Columbus\nIU East\nIU Fort Wayne\nIU Kokomo\nIU Northwest\nIU South Bend\nIU Southeast\nAdditional resources for visitors\nOverview\nSeveral offices throughout Indiana University provide resources to students, faculty, staff, and visitors with disabilities. The following will help you determine which office can best assist you.\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nFor students, faculty, and staff at all campuses, the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) provide resources including a software and hardware loan program, accessible eTexts, captioning services, demonstrations of adaptive technologies, and more. For contact information, office hours, and a comprehensive list of services offered, see\nAssistive technology services at IU\n.\nOffice of Civil Rights Compliance\nThe Office of Civil Rights Compliance (OCRC) ensures that the university complies with federal, state, and local affirmative action and equal opportunity laws (such as the ADA) by providing training, education, and access to data. It also investigates complaints of discrimination or harassment. For contact information, see the\nOCRC website\n.\nAccessible Educational Services (AES) offices by campus\nWhile ATAC focuses more on information technology resources, students may need other services such as test proctors, classroom accommodations (for example, accessible seating), or advising. If you need these or similar services, contact the campus-appropriate AES office below.\nIU Bloomington\nEigenmann Hall 001\n1900 E. Tenth Street\nBloomington, IN 47406\n812-855-7578\nEmail:\niubaes@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nIU Indianapolis\nJoseph T. Taylor Hall (UC) 100\n815 W. Michigan Street\nIndianapolis, IN 46202\n317-274-3241\n317-278-2052\nEmail:\naes@iu.edu\nFall/spring semester hours: Monday-Friday 8am-5pm\nSummer hours: Staffed Monday-Friday 8am-4pm.\nIU Columbus\nOffice of Academic and Student Affairs\nLearning Center (LC) 1620\n4555 Central Avenue\nColumbus, IN 47203\n812-375-7528\nEmail:\nmcrothro@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Thursday 9am-6pm; Friday 9am-3pm.\nIU East\nWhitewater Hall 116B\n2325 Chester Blvd.\nRichmond, IN 47374\n765-973-8675\n765-973-8237\nEmail:\nasiue@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nIU Fort Wayne\nJoseph T. Taylor Hall (UC) 100\n815 W. Michigan Street\nIndianapolis, IN 46202\n317-274-3241\n317-278-2052\nEmail:\naes@iu.edu\nFall/spring semester hours: Monday-Friday 8am-5pm\nSummer hours: Staffed Monday-Friday 8am-4pm.\nIU Kokomo\nKelley Student Center 139\n2300 S. Washington Street\nKokomo, IN 46904\n765-455-9301\nEmail:\naccessibility@iuk.edu\nWebsite:\nAccessible Educational Services\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nIU Northwest\nHawthorn 237\n3400 Broadway\nGary, Indiana 46408\n219-980-6941\nEmail:\nfjcoxhar@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nIU South Bend\nAdministration Building 175\n1700 Mishawaka Ave.\nSouth Bend, IN 46615\n574-520-4460\n574-520-4121\nEmail:\nsbdss@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Thursday 9am-5pm, Friday 9am-4pm.\nIU Southeast\nUniversity Center South 207\n812-941-2243\nEmail:\nseaes@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nAdditional resources for employees\nIU Bloomington\nOffice of the Vice Provost for Faculty and Academic Affairs\nFaculty and prospective academic employees should contact the Office of the Vice Provost for Faculty and Academic Affairs if they require accommodations in the workplace or assistance with a job application as it applies to a disability.\nBryan Hall 111\n107 S. Indiana Ave.\nBloomington, IN 47405\n812-855-2809\nEmail:\nvpfaa@indiana.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nADA Accommodations Specialist\nThe ADA Accommodations Specialist works within University Human Resources to assist staff members and prospective staff members in obtaining accommodations, assistance with a job application as it applies to a disability, and more.\n812-855-7511\nEmail:\nfepperso@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nFacility Operations\nIf you have concerns about your department or building being inaccessible, contact Facility Operations.\n2901 E. Discovery Parkway\nBloomington, IN 47408\nPhone: 812-855-8728 (answered 24 hours a day, 365 days a year; however, only emergencies are responded to after work hours)\nEmail:\nfacops@indiana.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 7:30am-5pm.\nIU Indianapolis\nADA Accommodations Specialist\nCampus Human Resources provides reasonable accommodations for staff or applicants interested in staff positions with IU Indianapolis, IU Columbus, IU Fort Wayne, and the IU School of Medicine. If you need assistance with an accommodation for any IU Indianapolis campus as staff, email Jamie Greig (\njgreigii@iu.edu\n), the ADA Accommodations Specialist.\nOffice of Academic Affairs\nIU Indianapolis, IU Columbus, or IU Fort Wayne faculty in need of accommodations should email Willie Miller (\nwmmiller@iu.edu\n), the Associate Vice President for Faculty Affairs.\nExecutive Director, Faculty Affairs, IU School of Medicine\nFaculty from the IU School of Medicine in need of accommodations should email Terri Christopher (\nterchris@iu.edu\n), Executive Director of Faculty Affairs.\nIU Indianapolis Office of Civil Rights Compliance\nIf you have questions or concerns about ADA-related resources, accommodations, or accessibility barriers at IU Indianapolis, call the Office of Civil Rights Compliance at 317-274-2306, or email them at\nocrcindy@iu.edu\n.\nCampus Facility Services\nIf you have concerns about your department or building being inaccessible, contact Campus Facility Services.\n1220 Wishard Boulevard\nIndianapolis, IN 46202\n317-278-1900\nEmail:\ncallcfs@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nIU Columbus\nADA Accommodations Specialist\nCampus Human Resources provides reasonable accommodations for staff or applicants interested in staff positions with IU Indianapolis, IU Columbus, IU Fort Wayne, and the IU School of Medicine. If you need assistance with an accommodation for any IU Indianapolis campus as staff, email Jamie Greig (\njgreigii@iu.edu\n), the ADA Accommodations Specialist.\nOffice of Academic Affairs\nIU Indianapolis, IU Columbus, or IU Fort Wayne faculty in need of accommodations should email Willie Miller (\nwmmiller@iu.edu\n), the Associate Vice President for Faculty Affairs.\nIU East\nOffice of Human Resources\nStaff, faculty, and prospective employees should contact the IU East Office of Human Resources should they require accommodations in the workplace or assistance with a job application as it applies to a disability.\nWhitewater Hall 121\n2325 Chester Blvd.\nRichmond, IN 47374\n765-973-8402\nEmail:\niuehr@iu.edu\nPhysical Facilities\nIf you have concerns about your department or building being inaccessible, contact Physical Facilities.\nSpringwood Hall 004\n2325 Chester Blvd.\nRichmond, IN 47374\n765-973-8423\nEmail:\nphyp@iue.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 7:30am-4:30pm.\nIU Fort Wayne\nADA Accommodations Specialist\nCampus Human Resources provides reasonable accommodations for staff or applicants interested in staff positions with IU Indianapolis, IU Columbus, IU Fort Wayne, and the IU School of Medicine. If you need assistance with an accommodation for any IU Indianapolis campus as staff, email Jamie Greig (\njgreigii@iu.edu\n), the ADA Accommodations Specialist.\nOffice of Academic Affairs\nIU Indianapolis, IU Columbus, or IU Fort Wayne faculty in need of accommodations should email Willie Miller (\nwmmiller@iu.edu\n), the Associate Vice President for Faculty Affairs.\nIU Kokomo\nOffice of Human Resources\nStaff, faculty, and prospective employees should contact the Office of Human Resources if they require accommodations in the workplace or assistance with a job application as it applies to a disability.\nMain Building 275\n2300 S. Washington Street\nKokomo, IN 46904\n765-455-9226\nEmail:\naplevans@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nPhysical Facilities\nIf you have concerns about your department or building being inaccessible, contact IU Kokomo Physical Facilities.\nMain Building 036\n2300 S. Washington Street\nKokomo, IN 46904\n765-455-9273\nEmail:\npplant@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nIU Northwest\nOffice of Academic Affairs\nFaculty and prospective academic employees should contact the Office of Academic Affairs should they require accommodations in the workplace or assistance with a job application as it applies to a disability.\nHawthorn 217\n3400 Broadway\nGary, IN 46408\n219-980-6707\nEmail:\nacadaffs@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm EDT.\nHuman Resources\nStaff and prospective staff should contact Human Resources should they require accommodations in the workplace or assistance with a job application as it applies to a disability.\nMarram Hall 108\n3400 Broadway\nGary, IN 46408\n219-980-6620\nEmail:\nmdiming@iu.edu\nFacilities Services\nIf you have concerns about your department or building being inaccessible, contact IU Northwest Facilities Services.\nMain Building 036\n3400 Broadway\nGary, IN 46408\n219-980-6710\nEmail:\niunfs@iun.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 7:30am-4pm.\nIU South Bend\nOffice of Academic Affairs\nFaculty and prospective academic employees should contact the Office of Academic Affairs if they require accommodations in the workplace or assistance with a job application as it applies to a disability.\nKyle Roberts\nAcademic Affairs Specialist/HR Business Partner for Faculty\nAI 246E\n574-520-4838\nEmail:\nkylecolem@iu.edu\nHuman Resources\nStaff and prospective staff should contact Human Resources if they require accommodations in the workplace or assistance with a job application as it applies to a disability.\nAdministration Building\n1700 Mishawaka Avenue\nSouth Bend, IN 46634\n219-980-6620\nEmail:\nmdiming@iu.edu\nFacilities Management\nIf you have concerns about your department or building being inaccessible, contact Facilities Management.\nParkside Hall Building 150\n1733 Northside Blvd.\nSouth Bend, Indiana 46634\n574-520-4386\nEmail:\nworkorders@iusb.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-12pm and 1pm-5pm.\nIU Southeast\nOffice of Human Resources\nStaff, faculty, and prospective employees should contact the Office of Human Resources should they require accommodations in the workplace or assistance with a job application as it applies to a disability.\nUniversity Center South 244\n4201 Grant Line Road\nNew Albany, IN 47150\n812-941-2356\nEmail:\nwebhmres@iu.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm EDT.\nFacility Operations\nIf you have concerns about your department or building being inaccessible, contact IU Southeast Facility Operations.\nService Building\n4201 Grant Line Road\nNew Albany, IN 47150\n812-941-2330\nEmail:\nphpwkord@ius.edu\nStaffed Monday-Friday 8am-5pm.\nAdditional resources for visitors\nVisitors in need of accommodations are encouraged to contact the event sponsor or department in advance of the event, as some accommodations require up to 72 hours' notice.	KB0024700	KB0024700 v5.0	 <strong>IU</strong> Bloomington <strong>IU</strong> Indianapolis <strong>IU</strong> Columbus <strong>IU</strong> East <strong>IU</strong> Fort Wayne <strong>IU</strong> Kokomo <strong>IU</strong> Northwest <strong>IU</strong> South Bend <strong>IU</strong> Southeast Additional resources for visitors Overview Several offices throughout Indiana hours, and a comprehensive list of services offered, see Assistive technology services at <strong>IU</strong>. Office	0.8166		2026-01-31 16:58:56.06977	2026-01-31 16:58:56.06977	0	76246f4733feaa5004e605570e5c7b78
10623	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e1a5b2b8331ee2dc04e605570e5c7bb4	About logging into Microsoft 365 or Azure at IU	The\nMicrosoft 365\nand Microsoft Azure portals at Indiana University use Microsoft authentication, instead of the standard\nIU Login\nmethod. This allows for additional account security settings.\nSome desktop or mobile clients of Microsoft cloud tools (such as\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\n,\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n, and Whiteboard) may prompt you to re-enter your credentials. You'll also see the following login prompts when you access\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n,\nAzure\n, or\nSharePoint Online\ndirectly:\nYou might also see the following new prompt:\nIf you see a "Sign in" prompt, enter your\n@iu.edu\nemail address, and then, at the password prompt, enter your\nIU passphrase\n. (Do not select anything on the sign-in pages other than\nNext\nand\nSign in\n.) When prompted, confirm your identity with\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n(if you see a "Do you trust iu.edu" prompt, select\nContinue\n), and then choose whether to stay signed into your\nMicrosoft 365\naccount.\nFor help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0024753	KB0024753 v2.0	The Microsoft 365 and Microsoft Azure portals at Indiana University use Microsoft authentication, instead of the standard IU Login method. This allows for additional account security settings. Some desktop or mobile clients of Microsoft cloud tools (such as Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU, Microsoft Teams	4.5291		2026-01-31 16:53:39.046994	2026-01-31 16:53:39.046994	0	e1a5b2b8331ee2dc04e605570e5c7bb4
10626	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=4caa7483477bee94d500b87b716d4379	Access Esri maps and data at IU	The\nEsri Data\ncollection contains many types of map data at many scales of geography. The data are useful for general purpose basemaps and for more specific uses. For each geographic scale included, the significant basemap layers are boundaries, cities, rivers, and roads. Where possible, demographic data are provided for states, counties, and their equivalents. Vector datasets include the United States, Europe, Canada, Mexico, and the world. Image and elevation data are available for North America and the world.\nEsri also offers the\nArcGIS Data & Maps\ngroup via ArcGIS Online, providing access to data layers from the Esri Data & Maps DVDs.\nIU's license agreement with Esri allows students, faculty, and staff to access GIS datasets for educational use. Datasets include census and market data, and are updated annually. Some additional datasets are available through the UITS GIS OneDrive; to request access,\ncomplete the GIS Help form\n. For information about how you may use the data, read the documentation included with the files.	KB0022574	KB0022574 v2.0	, and are updated annually. Some additional datasets are available through the UITS GIS <strong>OneDrive</strong>	4.4852		2026-01-31 16:53:42.455745	2026-01-31 16:53:42.455745	0	4caa7483477bee94d500b87b716d4379
10628	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ca30f07347a456583d373803846d4388	About IU Self Service	Most university-owned, faculty and staff Apple devices at IU are managed via Jamf Pro. These devices include an application called IU Self Service, which allows you to install a variety of free or IU-licensed applications.\nFor macOS systems managed in the Common Build, we maintain a default catalog of apps in IU Self Service, listed below. For Macs not yet in the Common Build this same catalog is also available. Some departments use a combination of our default catalog plus additional applications specific to their department. Other departments manage the catalog of apps entirely. Currently, there is not a default catalog of apps for IU Self Service on iOS devices. Your department's\nlocal UITS support person\ncan answer questions about available apps in IU Self Service.\nFor assistance installing applications from IU Self Service, contact your department's local UITS support person. If you need an application installed that is not listed in IU Self Service, the local UITS support person will assist with the installation or can request that the application be made available in IU Self Service.\nDefault catalog of macOS apps in IU Self Service\nAdobe Acrobat\nAdobe Creative Cloud Desktop\nAdobe Photoshop\nAlertus Desktop Alerts\nApple Rosetta 2\nCitrix Workspace\nCyberduck\nEndNote\nFirefox\nGitHub Desktop\nGoogle Chrome\nHandBrake\nIU Brand Fonts\nIvanti Secure Access (VPN)\nKaltura Capture\nKramer VIA\nMicrosoft Edge\nMicrosoft Office 365\nExcel\nOneDrive\nOneNote\nOutlook\nPowerPoint\nTeams\nWord\nMicrosoft Remote Desktop\nNvivo\nR\nRStudio Desktop\nSlack\nThinLinc\nVisual Studio Code\nVLC\nZoom\nUITS staff and local UITS support people:\nBy default, Macs managed in the\nCommon Build\nsee the default catalog of apps. Macs managed in the Casper Jamf Pro service do not see the default catalog in IU Self Service (Global Catalog). If your department wants to participate in the default catalog or the Common Build, contact\nsct2@iu.edu\nwith a request for the Jamf Admins.\nTesting:\nAs a local UITS support person managing a site in Jamf Pro, you can test what the default catalog looks like on a Mac by selecting an individual machine. Then, edit the General settings and set the Global Catalog to\nTrue\n.	KB0025777	KB0025777 v1.0	 Kramer VIA Microsoft Edge Microsoft Office 365 Excel <strong>OneDrive</strong> OneNote Outlook PowerPoint Teams Word	4.328		2026-01-31 16:53:45.848035	2026-01-31 16:53:45.848035	0	ca30f07347a456583d373803846d4388
11743	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e4d3fc0e3b866ed0476d941864e45a4c	Get started with the IU Common Build for macOS	Table of contents\nOverview\nIU Common Build structure and naming\nComputer naming convention\nIU Common Build and Active Directory\nBuild process\nManage computers on the Common Build\nLocal UITS support person access to Jamf\nLocal UITS support person tools\nFileVault\nSoftware\nUpdates\nRetire assets\nGet help\nOverview\nFor Apple computers, the IU Common Build provides central management for a great number of endpoint management functions. This includes Global Configuration Profiles for common applications, automatic FileVault key escrow, macOS LAPS, and a high security standard based off of the CIS Benchmarks.\nIU Common Build structure and naming\nComputer naming convention\nComputers will use the naming convention\nIU-\nSerialNumber\n, to stay in alignment with our Windows counterparts.\nIU Common Build and Active Directory\nBinding is supported only for devices that will be on campus full time. UITS\nEndpoint Management Services\n(EMS) does not recommend attempting to bind any device that will be used off campus for any length of time. If the device cannot maintain an always-on connection to the IU ADS, the client's passphrase can become out of sync and prevent login to the device.\nTo set up the AD binding option, contact EMS via the\nTicket Submission form\n. Provide an AD admin group, and specify which systems will be bound.\nFor more, see\nAD binding macOS on the IU Common Build\n.\nBuild process\nFor instructions on the IU Common Build process, see\nBuild information for Apple computers under the IU Common Build\n.\nManage computers on the Common Build\nLocal UITS support person access to Jamf\nLocal UITS support people will retain\naudit-level\n(that is, read-only) access to their new site within the IU Common Build, which includes all policies and configuration profiles. The site url is\nadm01.iu.edu:8443\n. The access level is determined by\nACM\ngroup, which will be set up by EMS; if access needs to change, managers will be able to edit the members of the ACM group. EMS requires that any administrative accounts be group accounts.\nClients will retain the rights to view LAPS password rotations and FileVault encryption keys as well as the ability to create, edit, and save searches and groups.\nLocal UITS support person tools\nSome policies on the IU Common Build have been placed into the IU Self Service store for use by local UITS support people. All of these policies are hidden by default in IU Self Service and require local UITS support people to sign into IU Self Service using their IU Jamf admin group account. After login, local UITS support person-specific tools will be available under "Admin".\nAccess to these policies is granted by the iu-entlmt-iu-uems-apple-itpros AD group. Membership is nested from your IT unit's local UITS support admin group, via ACM. IT managers have access to edit their unit's ACM group, using their admin group account. Contact EMS if you need to change or check access.\nFileVault\nAs part of IT-12, all laptops in the IU Common Build receive a configuration profile on enrollment to enable FileVault. The keys are automatically escrowed to Jamf for the entry of the device. You can view these keys with audit access to the Jamf site.\nSoftware\nTo enable standard build software, use the Setup Your Mac policy in IU Self Service; see\nBuild information for Apple computers under the IU Common Build\n.\nSubmit all requests for new applications via the EMS\nTicket Submission form\n.\nUpdates\nTo ensure software stays as up to date as possible, application updates are automatically installed for all supported applications daily, outside of business hours.\nmacOS security updates will be deployed as soon as they become available, with the option to defer for up to one week. After one week, the device will be put into a new update group and notifications will be sent. A week after the notifications are sent, an update will be forcibly applied to the device outside of business hours.\nBy default, the EMS team will not restrict any major macOS or iPadOS releases. For macOS operating system changes, the upgrade will be blocked until an evaluation of the new operating system and security compliances can be updated.\nRetire assets\nEMS relies on on-site local UITS support people and the UITS Asset Management team in order to properly facilitate the retirement of university assets. Any time a device is being sent to Surplus, follow the instructions in\nDecommissioning procedure\nin\nAbout Apple ADE (Automated Device Enrollment) and decommissioning\nto ensure the device is properly removed from endpoint management. For more about disposing of unneeded equipment, see\nDepartmental disposal of equipment at IU\n.\nNote:\nOnce every six months, EMS will run an inventory of devices that have not been in communication with Jamf. Any that have not checked into the service in over six months may be subject to lockdown until the client can be contacted.\nGet help\nUse the\nTicket Submission form\nfor Common Build requests.	KB0025622	KB0025622 v4.0	Table of contents Overview IU Common Build structure and naming Computer naming convention IU Common Build and Active Directory Build process Manage computers on the Common Build Local UITS support person access to Jamf Local UITS support person tools FileVault Software Updates Retire assets Get help Overview For Apple computers, the IU Common B	1.5892	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits, km-aud-ems-admins	2026-01-31 17:16:55.423301	2026-01-31 17:16:55.423301	0	e4d3fc0e3b866ed0476d941864e45a4c
10630	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ee83aab247e8b65cd500b87b716d4329	About the Research and Academic LLM Services (REALLMS) at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nAccess\nCurrent models\nCurrent features\nCreate knowledge collections\nUpcoming features\nGet help\nOverview\nREALLMS\n(\nResearch and Academic LLM Services\n) is a large-language model (LLM)-powered chat service provided at no fee to the Indiana University community. All the hardware is on premise in the IU Data Center. The service uses the Open WebUI AI platform with a vLLM back end to serve models from.\nNote:\nThis tool\nhas been approved\nfor use with\nCritical research data\nand\nPHI\n. For more about data classification at IU, see IU's\nManagement of Institutional Data (DM-01)\npolicy. Researchers with questions about the sensitivity of their data should contact the Data Steward for Research Data (\ndatard@iu.edu\n) for guidance.\nAccess\nREALLMS is open to anyone with an IU account; you don't need a separate account to use it.\nCurrent models\nThere are several models available to choose from:\nDeepSeek R1\nRunning on 8 AMD MI300x GPUs\n128 K context length\nLlama 4 Scout\nRunning on 2 NVIDIA H100 GPUs\n128 K context length\ngpt-oss-120b\nRunning on 2 NVIDIA H100 GPUs\n128 K context length\ncode-llama\nRunning on 1 NVIDIA H100 GPU\n16 K context length\nCurrent features\nWith REALLMS, you can:\nSelect your preferred model.\nSave multiple chats.\nUpload documents to add context to conversations.\nCreate knowledge collections\nThis is similar to uploading content to conversations, but you can share collections with specified groups of people, and can also use them as knowledge sources within other chats. You can also connect Google Drive and OneDrive folders as information sources. For more, see\nhttps://docs.openwebui.com/features/workspace/knowledge/\n. If you are interested in using this functionality,\ncontact the UITS Research Applications and Deep Learning (RADL) team\n.\nUpcoming features\nRADL is working on the API portion of the service, which will allow users to hook up tools or other services to the available models. Once it's implemented, you will be able to request an allocation for it on\nRT Projects\n.\nGet help\nIf you have questions about using REALLMS,\ncontact the UITS Research Applications and Deep Learning (RADL) team\n.	KB0026671	KB0026671 v11.0	. You can also connect Google Drive and <strong>OneDrive</strong> folders as information sources. For more, see https	4.0065		2026-01-31 16:53:49.276445	2026-01-31 16:53:49.276445	0	ee83aab247e8b65cd500b87b716d4329
11294	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ea5a71d63b862e14476d941864e45a42	Access Genio Notes (formerly Glean) at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nAccess Genio Notes\nPersonal computer\nSTC computing labs\nIUanyWare\nLearn more\nOverview\nNote:\nIn June of 2025, Glean, a provider of study and note taking tools for improving student success, rebranded to Genio. Its flagship note-taking tool has been rebranded as Genio Notes.\nGenio Notes is a notetaking application that allows you to combine audio recording, text notes and slides to improve your learning and productivity. Below are different ways you can access Genio Notes at IU once your Genio Notes account is set up.\nNotes:\nAt Indiana University, access to Genio Notes is granted by your campus ADA services office. Contact the\nADA services office on your campus\nto learn about arranging a course or workplace accommodation for your needs.\nOnce your accommodation is in place, you will receive an invitation email to set up your Genio Notes account. If you do not receive the email from Genio Notes App, check the spam/junk folder in your IU email. If you still can't find your Genio Notes App invitation email, then contact your\ncampus Support Center\n.\nAccess Genio Notes\nPersonal computer\nYou can access Genio Notes is via a web browser on your Windows, Mac, Chromebook, smartphone, or tablet. Genio Notes supports the Google Chrome and Microsoft Edge web browsers. To see if your devices are compatible with Genio Notes, review\nSystem requirements to use Genio Notes for Web and Mobile\n.\nSTC computing labs\nGenio Notes is available in all of the STC computing labs across all of the IU campuses. You will access Genio Notes from using either the Google Chrome or Microsoft Edge web browser. To find STC computing labs for your IU campus, see\nAbout the IU STCs (computing labs)\n.\nIUanyWare\nTo use Genio Notes on an\nIUanyWare\nDesktop, you will need to access your account using Google Chrome or Microsoft Edge.\nLearn more\nAbout Genio Notes at IU\nSet up your Genio Notes account at IU\nIf you didn't receive your Genio Notes login credentials\nSystem requirements to use Genio Notes for Web and Mobile\nReset your Genio Notes mobile app password\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0024260	KB0024260 v3.0	Table of contents Overview Access Genio Notes Personal computer STC <strong>computing</strong> labs IUanyWare Learn to use Genio Notes for Web and Mobile. STC <strong>computing</strong> labs Genio Notes is available in all of the STC <strong>computing</strong> labs across all of the IU campuses. You will access Genio Notes from using either the Google	91.2021		2026-01-31 17:05:02.506619	2026-01-31 17:05:02.506619	0	ea5a71d63b862e14476d941864e45a42
10642	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=caafe837476456583d373803846d433c	Availability of Microsoft 365 at IU	Microsoft 365\nis a subscription-based service that allows IU students, faculty, and staff to use the newest versions of Microsoft software on their personal Windows and\nmacOS\ncomputers, on the web, and on smartphones and tablets with mobile apps. Your IU\nMicrosoft 365\nsubscription allows you to install the software on up to five personal computers (Windows and macOS), five tablets (Windows, iPad, and Android), and five phones. You can use the software as long as your IU computing accounts are active; you are not entitled to use the software after you leave IU.\nDesktop versions:\nDesktop versions for Windows and macOS are available:\nFor installation on personal computers, from\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n32-bit (recommended) and 64-bit versions are available for Windows. For installation instructions, see\nAbout Microsoft 365 at IU\n.\nFor use on computers in the IU Student Technology Centers (\nSTCs\n)\nFor use on your personal computer via\nIUanyWare\nIUanyWare lets you run\nMicrosoft 365\napplications virtually in a web browser without having to install the applications locally and provides access to your\nIU Cloud Computing\nfiles. For more, see\nSet up and use IUanyWare\n.\nWeb-based apps:\nWeb-based apps are available through\nMicrosoft 365\nand as integrated solutions within IU's\nSharePoint\nservice.\nThese apps are lightweight, browser-based versions of\nMicrosoft 365\napplications (including Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and OneNote) with features such as real-time co-authoring and auto-saving. For a list of supported browsers, see\nWhich browsers work with\nMicrosoft 365\nfor the web and\nMicrosoft 365\nAdd-ins\n.\nMobile apps:\nApps for smartphones and tablets are available also from\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n. These are scaled-down, touch-optimized versions of\nMicrosoft 365\napplications (including Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and OneNote) available for free from your device's app store.\nSign into the mobile apps using your IU\nMicrosoft 365\naccount to access your\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nstorage space. To sign in with your IU\nMicrosoft 365\naccount after installing a Microsoft app, open the app, and then, when prompted for an email address, enter your IU username followed by\n@iu.edu\n(for example,\nusername@iu.edu\n).	KB0022641	KB0022641 v1.0	 store. Sign into the mobile apps using your IU Microsoft 365 account to access your Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong>	3.9864		2026-01-31 16:53:52.681318	2026-01-31 16:53:52.681318	0	caafe837476456583d373803846d433c
10643	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=65efec3b476456583d373803846d4337	About Microsoft 365 security	Table of contents\nOverview\nSafe Links\nSafe Attachments\nMailbox impersonation tips\nOverview\nMicrosoft has created a strong suite of tools to better protect information and people. Indiana University continues to evaluate these new tools and, when appropriate, enables them for the IU community. Security features are available to everyone using\nExchange Online\nat IU.\nSafe Links\nThe Safe Links feature scans inbound email for malicious URLs, rewrites URLs, and provides time-of-click verification of links in email messages and other locations. Along with regular anti-spam and anti-malware protection for inbound email messages, Safe Links scanning helps protect the IU community from malicious links used in phishing and other attacks.\nSafe Links in\nMicrosoft Teams\nfunctions slightly differently than in Exchange Email. Instead of rewriting the URL, a real-time query is made at time-of-click to determine if the destination is safe. Malicious destinations present the same modal message as in email.\nFor more, see\nSafe Links in Microsoft Defender for Office 365\n.\nSafe Attachments\nUpon receipt of an email message, Safe Attachments automatically scans any attachments. The attachments may be temporarily inaccessible when scanning is underway, but you can preview them in Outlook during scanning. Safe Attachments also scans files saved to\nSharePoint Online\n,\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\n, and\nMicrosoft Teams\n.\nFor more, see\nSafe Attachments in Microsoft Defender for Office 365\n.\nFor more about security in Office 365, consult\nMicrosoft's documentation\n.\nMailbox impersonation tips\nUpon receiving an email message from a new sender, or a known sender that doesn't regularly send email, Outlook will display a small banner with the phrase, "You don't often get email from\nsender@domain.com\n. Learn why this is important". This tool serves as a timely reminder to help prevent email spoofing and phishing attempts.\nFor more, see\nFirst Contact Safety Tip in Microsoft Defender for Office 365\n.\nFor more about security in Office 365, consult\nMicrosoft's documentation\n.	KB0024001	KB0024001 v1.0	. Safe Attachments also scans files saved to SharePoint Online, Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU	3.886	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 16:53:56.225181	2026-01-31 16:53:56.225181	0	65efec3b476456583d373803846d4337
10331	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d746eb3a3b5b2654476d941864e45ad2	Best practices for Microsoft Teams at IU	UITS recommends these best practices for effective use of\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n:\nScope:\nRather than having a single team for an entire organization, it is usually better to have multiple teams, each with a more focused group of people. For example, if a group is working on a project, that could be its own team. Other examples include student clubs, research labs, internal or interdepartmental committees, and teammates within an organization. If a team becomes unwieldy, with too many members or tasks, create new ones to help maintain focus and security.\nInstitutional data:\nMicrosoft Teams\nis appropriate for institutional data of all data classifications. It is important for the team owner and requester of the storage space to provide accurate information including what type of data will be stored and data classification when completing the\nInstitutional storage request form\n. This ensures the appropriate safeguards and naming conventions are applied. If the data classification or types of data you specify during the creation process change during the life of the team, it is important to\nupdate this information\nto remain in compliance. For additional help in identifying the type of data that will be stored, see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n.\nChannels:\nEvery team within\nMicrosoft Teams\nbegins with a General channel that all members can access. In addition, you can create additional channels for more focused topics. Standard channels are available to all within the team, while private channels can be limited to a subset. You can't add members to a private channel who aren't already in the team itself, however. You may also have no more than\n30 private channels in a team\n; if you find yourself frequently expanding the membership of your team to accommodate channel topics, consider creating new teams instead.\nPermissions:\nThree permission levels are available:\nOwner:\nShould be restricted to those with organizational responsibilities for the team. Owners can add and remove users, and also have the ability to\ndelete the team\nitself. You are limited to\n100 owners\n, and UITS recommends that you have at least two in any team to maintain access and security.\nMember:\nThis would apply to most people in the team.\nGuest:\nGuest designation is for\nexternal individuals\n.\nAccess:\nFor full functionality, use the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication or the web app. If you'd like to access\nMicrosoft Teams\ndocuments from File Explorer or the Finder, you can also use the\nOneDrive\napplication.\nTraining:\nIT Training\noffers webinars on\nMicrosoft Teams\n.	KB0025475	KB0025475 v2.0	 the <strong>OneDrive</strong> application. Training: IT Training offers webinars on Microsoft Teams.	3.8564		2026-01-31 16:41:40.423793	2026-01-31 16:53:59.6577	0	d746eb3a3b5b2654476d941864e45ad2
10648	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=21ff60bb476456583d373803846d432a	Your Google at IU account when you leave IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nHow long you'll have access to your files\nPreserve your data\nIf you have institutional data\nOverview\nWhen you leave IU, all data stored in individual accounts, such as\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\nand\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\n, is subject to deletion.\nEmployees leaving the university should ensure ownership changes as appropriate to retain needed data.\nEmployees whose collaborator is leaving the university should review ownership of shared content and work with the departing employee to make appropriate changes.\nHow long you'll have access to your files\nAt Indiana University, eligibility for computing accounts depends on your status as a student or employee:\nStudents:\nAs with your other IU computing accounts, your\nGoogle at IU\naccount remains active as long as you are enrolled at any IU campus. After two consecutive terms (fall, spring, and summer are each considered one term) during which you are not enrolled through the IU registrar,\nUITS\nwill disable access to your IU computing accounts, including your\nGoogle at IU\naccount. For more, see\nStudent computing accounts, data, and software when you leave IU\n.\nFaculty and staff:\nAs an IU employee, your IU computing accounts remain active as long as you are employed at any IU campus. When your employment at IU ends, UITS will disable access to your IU computing accounts, including your\nGoogle at IU\naccount. For more, see\nEmployee computing accounts when you leave IU\n.\nRetired faculty and staff:\nOfficial IU retirees keep full access to their\nGoogle at IU\naccounts; for more, see\nAccess to IU resources for official IU retirees\n.\nNote:\nGoogle at IU\naccounts are deleted after you've been separated from IU for more than 30 days. For more about account lifecycles, see\nLifecycle of typical computing accounts from creation to deletion\n.\nPreserve your data\nWhen dealing with your\nGoogle at IU\naccount before you leave IU, take care to move your data to appropriate locations. You can relocate your individual files to a desktop or laptop computer, a mobile device, another cloud-based storage service, or some form of storage media (for example, DVDs, flash drives, or external hard drives).\nFor more, see\nImport and export Google data\n.\nIf you have institutional data\nIf\nany\nof your\nGoogle at IU\nfiles contain\ninstitutional data\n, you must move them to an IU resource that's approved for storing such data. See:\nOptions for storing files at IU\nMove files into Microsoft or Google institutional storage from an individual storage location	KB0024346	KB0024346 v1.0	Table of contents Overview How long you'll have access to your files Preserve your data If you have institutional data Overview When you leave IU, all data stored in individual accounts, such as Google at IU My Drive and Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU, is subject to deletion. Employees leaving	3.732		2026-01-31 16:54:03.016133	2026-01-31 16:54:03.016133	0	21ff60bb476456583d373803846d432a
10650	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=2cdfe8f7476456583d373803846d439c	About cloud computing	Table of contents\nOverview\nService models\nSoftware as a Service (SaaS)\nPlatform as a Service (PaaS)\nInfrastructure as a Service (IaaS)\nTypes of clouds\nPrivate clouds\nPublic clouds\nHybrid clouds\nOverview\nCloud computing is the delivery of shared, on-demand computing services over the internet ("the cloud") to offer faster innovation, flexible resources, and economies of scale.\nCloud services include servers, storage, databases, networking, software, analytics, intelligence, and more. You typically pay only for what you use, helping lower your operating costs, run your infrastructure more efficiently, and scale as your business needs change. This lets people access and share information at any time from multiple devices, rapidly deploy computing services without purchasing hardware, temporarily leverage massive computing power, and more.\nCloud computing allows both consumers and IT providers to use technology as a utility, which makes IT nimbler and more cost-effective. Cloud computing includes familiar examples of cloud storage, such as storing pictures on\nFlickr\n, videos on\nYouTube\n, or files on\nGoogle Drive at IU\n, but also encompasses services such as\nAmazon Web Services (AWS)\n,\nGoogle Cloud\n, and\nMicrosoft Azure\n, which allow online services (for example, servers delivering a website) to rapidly scale up or down based on sudden fluctuations in traffic.\nService models\nCloud computing has three main service models, commonly referred to as Software as a Service (SaaS), Platform as a Service (PaaS), and Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS).\nSoftware as a Service (SaaS)\nSoftware as a Service lets you access a provider's services running on the provider's infrastructure, typically from multiple devices. Examples of commonly used SaaS applications are\nOneDrive\n, Canvas,\nFacebook\n, and Snapchat.\nPlatform as a Service (PaaS)\nPlatform as a Service removes the need for organizations to manage the underlying infrastructure (usually servers and operating systems) and allows them to focus on the deployment and management of their applications. Examples of PaaS include AWS Elastic Beanstalk, Microsoft Azure Web Apps, Salesforce, and the Google App Engine.\nInfrastructure as a Service (IaaS)\nInfrastructure as a Service contains the basic building blocks for cloud IT and typically provides access to networking features, computers (virtual or on dedicated hardware), and data storage space. Some examples are Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\nTypes of clouds\nThere are three deployment models for cloud computing: private, public, and hybrid clouds.\nPrivate clouds\nPrivate clouds involve a distinct and secure cloud-based environment in which only the specified client can operate.\nPublic clouds\nPublic clouds are cloud-based computing environments provided by a commercial provider, accessed via the internet. Their infrastructure is built to leverage massive scale and is shared with multiple clients. IU currently has contracts with two public cloud computing vendors, Amazon Web Services (AWS) and Microsoft Azure. To get an account, see\nGet an AWS, Google Cloud, or Microsoft Azure account at IU\n.\nHybrid clouds\nHybrid clouds use elements from both private and public clouds. For example, a hybrid cloud may use a public cloud for all non-sensitive information and only use a private cloud for more sensitive or confidential data.	KB0023561	KB0023561 v1.0	 from multiple devices. Examples of commonly used SaaS applications are <strong>OneDrive</strong>, Canvas, Facebook	3.6923		2026-01-31 16:54:07.098637	2026-01-31 16:54:07.098637	0	2cdfe8f7476456583d373803846d439c
10654	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=93eb36b747de7e10d500b87b716d4342	About Varonis at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nVaronis at IU\nImpact on users\nGuidance and resources\nOverview\nVaronis is an enterprise data security and governance platform that helps Indiana University protect institutional data across supported storage and collaboration services. It provides visibility into where data resides, how it is accessed, and how it is shared, supporting alignment with university policy and information security expectations. Varonis helps safeguard institutional data while enabling appropriate collaboration and access for faculty, staff, students, and affiliates.\nVaronis at IU\nAt IU, University Information Technology Services (UITS) uses Varonis to proactively identify and mitigate data risks within IU-managed environments, including Microsoft Teams, SharePoint, OneDrive, and file servers. This includes:\nData classification and labeling:\nVaronis works in concert with\nMicrosoft Information Protection\n(MIP) sensitivity labels to help classify and label data according to IU's\ninstitutional data standards\n. This ensures shared files and folders are appropriately marked based on their sensitivity and regulatory requirements.\nRisk detection and exposure reduction:\nVaronis continuously scans IU-managed storage environments to identify sensitive or regulated data that may be at risk due to misconfigured sharing or permissions. The system flags files and folders that are accessible to the public or broadly within IU, and highlights instances of excessive or unnecessary access.\nRemediation of overexposed data:\nWhen Varonis detects that sensitive data has been over-shared (for example, via links that allow anonymous access to the public or with anyone who has an IU username), UITS Varonis administrators take action to mitigate risk. This may involve removing public or anonymous sharing links, tightening permissions, or contacting data owners to confirm appropriate access levels. Remediation focuses on correcting sharing and access configurations, not altering or deleting the underlying data.\nIncident response and compliance support:\nIf regulated data, such as Social Security Numbers, financial records, or health information, is found to be exposed, IU's incident response protocols are activated. Designated staff review findings, coordinate with data owners, and ensure that remediation aligns with university policies, including\nPrivacy of Electronic Information and Information Technology Resources (IT-07)\n. All actions are conducted in accordance with IU privacy policies, and access to data is limited to authorized personnel only as needed.\nImpact on users\nMost data owners will not notice Varonis in daily operations. If Varonis identifies a potential risk, such as over-shared sensitive data or misconfigured permissions, data owners may be contacted to help resolve the issue or clarify sharing needs. In some cases, automated system changes may occur to protect institutional data, after which data owners will be notified.\nRemediation actions focus on correcting access and sharing configurations, not modifying or deleting the data itself. All actions are performed with respect for user privacy in accordance with IU policies, and only authorized personnel are involved.\nGuidance and resources\nUse of Varonis at IU aligns with existing university policies and standards related to data privacy, classification, and information security.\nUsers storing and sharing institutional data are encouraged to follow IU guidance, including the data classification and handling resources provided by\nIU Data Management\n, and to consult relevant Knowledge Base articles for additional information. IU encourages all data owners to stay informed about institutional guidance for\nstoring and sharing data\n, and to regularly review access permissions for files and folders.\nIf you have questions about secure data practices and policies at IU, contact the\nUniversity Information Security Office\n(\nuiso@iu.edu\n).	KB0027227	KB0027227 v1.0	 data risks within IU-managed environments, including Microsoft Teams, SharePoint, <strong>OneDrive</strong>, and file	3.5327	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 16:54:10.581215	2026-01-31 16:54:10.581215	0	93eb36b747de7e10d500b87b716d4342
