16:54:43.774735	0	ea72ef4447d4b2142fc6a55b416d43c1
10720	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e820b0ff476456583d373803846d43a8	About Power BI at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nLicenses\nGet started\nVersion control\nKnown issue\nData connection tips\nGet help\nOverview\nMicrosoft Power BI is a suite of business analytics tools for analyzing data and sharing insights with reports and dashboards. At Indiana University, authorized users can access interactive reports and dashboards using a standard web browser.\nPower BI consists of several elements that work together, starting with these two basics:\nPower BI Desktop:\nA free Windows application for connecting, transforming, and visualizing data, and for building interactive reports\nPower BI Service:\nA cloud-based SaaS (Software as a Service) offering (\nhttps://app.powerbi.com/\n) for publishing and sharing reports and dashboards. While less common, you can also create Power BI reports in the Power BI service.\nPower BI mobile apps:\nThe native apps for both\niOS\nand\nAndroid\nprovide live, interactive, mobile access to your published reports and apps.\nAt IU, Power BI is approved for\nCritical data\nincluding protected health information (PHI).\nImportant:\nThis system or service meets certain requirements established in the\nHIPAA Security Rule\nthereby enabling its use for work involving data that contain protected health information (\nPHI\n). However, using this system or service does not fulfill your legal responsibilities for protecting the privacy and security of data that contain PHI. You may use this system or service for work involving data that contain PHI only if you institute additional\nadministrative\n,\nphysical\n, and\ntechnical\nsafeguards that complement those UITS already has in place.\nLicenses\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nAs part of IU's agreement with Microsoft, Power BI Pro may be assigned to certain individuals at IU. This license allows sharing of data, reports, and dashboards in an App workspace with other Pro users.\nIndividual licensing:\nPower BI Pro licenses are automatically assigned to all IU faculty and staff.\nCoursework licensing:\nPower BI Pro licenses may be pre-assigned to IU students for coursework. To sponsor access, faculty must\ncontact Tier 2\n. Send student lists along with the start and end dates of classes.\nPremium:\nVarious premium offerings are available, but each situation may be unique. For a discussion on your goals to help determine the best Premium licensing option, contact\newahelp@iu.edu\n.\nIU community:\nFor more detailed licensing information, including some information about Premium licenses, see\nAbout Microsoft Power Platform licenses\n.\nGet started\nDownload the Power BI Desktop application for Windows from the\nMicrosoft store\n. Users on non-Windows platforms can use the Power BI Desktop tool in\nIUanyWare\n.\nPower BI Pro users publishing content to share with other Pro users on campus should request the creation of an App workspace; see\nRequest a Power BI workspace\n.\nVersion control\nVersion control, also known as source control or revision control, is the practice of tracking and managing changes to software code, documents, or other collections of information.\nUITS strongly recommends that your source files (PBIX reports as well as templates and themes) be stored somewhere that implements version control. The most important reason for this is to be able to roll back to a previous version if some change produces unwanted results.\nThere is currently no standardized process at IU for how you should store these files, but here are some options that range from simple to complex:\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\n,\nSharePoint Online\n,\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n:\nThese three options are related, and if your department is already using one of them, it may be the easiest to choose to save your PBIX files in a folder inside of one of these systems. This is a very good, simple option, as the data are not hosted on premises, and you get version history by default every time a file is saved.\nFile servers:\nIf your department uses an on-premises file server, that may also be a good place to store your files\nif\nthat server has Shadow Copies (also known as Previous Versions) enabled. Check with your\nlocal UITS support person\nto determine if and how Shadow Copies are implemented on the file server. You may want to use one of the MS options above, since Shadow Copies usually makes one or two copies per day (for example, at noon and midnight). If you made multiple versions within a single day (for example, at 8am, 9am, 10am, and 11am), you would miss some of the versions that were created between the scheduled times.\nGitHub, Azure DevOps, etc.:\nWhile these are full-featured source control platforms, you're still a bit limited, since PBIX files are binary, not text. This means that while you do have version control, you still lack the ability to compare what changed between versions. Additionally, they will be much more complicated to configure. Configuration of these platforms is beyond the scope of this document; however, if your team already uses one of these tools, it may be worth investigating further. Additionally, when on Power BI Premium capacity, you can use Azure DevOps or GitHub to integrate with Power BI Deployment pipelines for additional automation.\nKnown issue\nIf you plan to connect to an Oracle Data Source and are using the Microsoft Store version of Power BI Desktop, you may get an "Object reference not set to an instance of an object" error. As a workaround, copy\noraons.dll\nfrom the\nclient_X\nfolder into the\nclient_X\\bin folder\n.\nThe most recent version of the Oracle client tested by UITS is 64-bit Oracle 19.3.1.\nFor more, see:\nPowerBI desktop 64Bit oracle connection error Object reference not set to an instance of an object\nConnect to an Oracle database with Power BI Desktop\nData connection tips\nDenodo\nImportant:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nNote:\nYou do not need to create Denodo ODBC connections inside the ODBC Control Panel.\nDenodo\nrequires an ODBC driver for its built-in connector. To download an ODBC driver, see\nthe list of drivers\non the Denodo Community website.\nIU community:\nThe Denodo connection string should look like the following when you use it in Power BI Desktop, or when you create connections on the On-Premises Data Gateway:\nPRD:\nSERVER=ebidvt.uits.iu.edu;PORT=9995;DATABASE=xxxxx;SSLmode=prefer\nSTG:\nSERVER=ebidvt-stg.uits.iu.edu;PORT=9995;DATABASE=xxxxx;SSLmode=prefer\nDEV:\nSERVER=ebidvt-dev.uits.iu.edu;PORT=9995;DATABASE=xxxxx;SSLmode=prefer\nNote:\nThe order and capitalizations in the Denodo connection string must be used consistently.\nFor more information on IU Denodo servers, including up-to-date URIs for DEV, STG, and PRD servers, see\nGet started with Denodo at IU\n.\nGet help\nThe Power BI Service is administered by\nEnterprise Windows & Azure Administration\n(\nEWA\n).\nFor help with licenses, workspace creation, use of the On-Premises Data Gateway, permission issues, Premium Capacity, and other Power BI workspace configuration tasks, contact\newahelp@iu.edu\n.\nCurrently, no formal support or training is available for help creating or using Power BI content, but you can connect with others in the IU Power BI users community via the\nO365-PowerPlatform\nteam in\nMicrosoft Teams\n. Also, you can consult the following online training resources:\nMicrosoft Power BI Learning Overview\nLinkedIn Learning Power BI courses\nMicrosoft Power BI documentation	KB0025305	KB0025305 v1.0	 from simple to complex: Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU, SharePoint Online, Microsoft Teams at IU: These three	2.7686		2026-01-31 16:54:51.288252	2026-01-31 16:54:51.288252	0	e820b0ff476456583d373803846d43a8
10724	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=0280b7d033e22ad404e605570e5c7b12	Known issues with IUanyWare	Following is a continually updated list of issues with\nIUanyWare\nand their workarounds, if any. If you have problems not addressed here, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nIssue description and workaround\nApplication\nIssue description\nWorkaround\nCitrix Workspace for Windows 11\nVersions of Citrix Workspace prior to 2019.1 may not be compatible with Windows 11.\nDownload the latest version of Citrix Workspace\nAll apps:\nWhen accessing files, the following error may appear.\nWindow title: Restriction\n"This operation has been cancelled due to restrictions in effect on this computer. Please contact your system administrator."\nThis is a known error message with a security policy in place to prevent users from accessing the local drive on the server or prevent saving files to places like the\nDownloads\nfolder. You should use a cloud storage option such as\nGoogle Drive\nor\nOneDrive\nin order to avoid losing works in progress on a virtual machine in IUanyWare.\nAfter selecting\nOK\n, the error disappears. You can continue saving your work.\nError message when trying to log into IUanyWare (primarily Chrome browsers)\nYou see the following message:\n"RelayState in Response does not match with rule in Action. Please contact your administrator."\nGo to\ncas.iu.edu/cas/logout\n, close the browser completely, and then reopen\nIUanyWare\n.\nYou can also try clearing your cache and cookies.\nLaunched application or desktop asks for a Windows login\nWhen launching an application or desktop, you may be prompted with wording like:\nStarting Windows\nOther user\nUsername\nPassword\nDue to increasing resources for IUanyWare, it may take longer than usual to send the IU login cert to the application or desktop you wish to launch.\nSelect\nOther user\n.\nType\nads\\username\n(replace\nusername\nwith your IU username).\nUse your IU login credentials to log in.\nReceiver and Workspace app for macOS Catalina\nAll versions of the Citrix Receiver are incompatible with macOS Catalina. Citrix Receiver is a 32-bit app.\nThe Citrix Workspace app 1906 and older is not supported on macOS Catalina.\nUse the Citrix Workspace app 1910.2 or later.\nTo download the latest version of the Citrix Workspace app, go to\nDownload Citrix Workspace app\n.\nPalisade tools\nThe Palisade Suite in IUanyWare has an expired license. This includes the following applications:\nAtRisk 7\nBigPicture 7\nEvolver 7\nNeuralTools 7\nPtree 7\nStatTools 7\nTopRank 7\nIUanyWare admins have contacted the application owners. The Palisade license may not be renewed for all users in IUanyWare. This issue is still being investigated.\nWorkspace app or Receiver with scaled or high resolution displays\nIn Windows, you may experience display issues when using multiple displays with different resolutions or scaling.\nUse the latest version of\nCitrix Workspace app\nto enable the app to scale the session for high resolution. If you are still using the Citrix Receiver for special use cases or for the LTSR client, this feature is available in Receiver 4.9 and above.\nIn the system tray, right-click the\nWorkspace\nicon and select\nAdvanced Preferences\n>\nHigh DPI\n>\nYes\n>\nSave\n. Disconnect from your sessions and reconnect to them for the changes to take effect.\nNote:\nThis setting locks the application to a single monitor if the scaling of any monitor is different (it cannot be stretched across multiple displays).\nWorkspace or Receiver problems\nIf you experience problems with IUanyWare which you have not encountered before, check to make sure you have the latest version of Citrix Workspace app.\nBefore you begin, be sure to uninstall your current Citrix Workspace app or Citrix Receiver.\nVisit\nDownload Citrix Workspace app\nfrom the device experiencing the problem. This site will automatically install the proper version of Citrix Workspace app. Otherwise, it may direct you to the app store for your particular device to search for the latest version of the app.\nInvalid root certificate\nYou see this message:\n"This server has an invalid root certificate, so this may be a malicious server. Connection dropped."\nStrictly speaking, this is not an issue with IUanyWare.\nSee\nFixing an invalid root certificate in IUanyWare\n.\nDuo\nCitrix Workspace app lacks a Duo interface to let you choose your preferred Duo option. The Workspace app sends Duo Push notifications to your mobile device by default.\nBest practice: From a browser, log into\nhttps://iuanyware.cloud.com\n. Use the Duo interface to choose your preferred Duo option. From the browser's store of apps, you can run applications and desktops from the browser or through the Workspace app on your client.\nIf you prefer to use the Workspace app directly, and you want to use a token key for your second authentication method, see\nLogging In With the Citrix Receiver Client\n.\nAudio\nAudio is enabled on all applications, but some applications (for example, Adobe Audition) will not work through a standard IUanyWare virtual machine.\nThere is no workaround for this issue.\nMathematica\nWhen you access Mathematica from the Receiver on an iPad, the\nReturn\nkey does not evaluate an expression the way pressing\nEnter\non a Windows keyboard or\nShift-Return\non a Mac keyboard does.\nTap and hold\nReturn\nto access the right-click menu, and select\nEvaluate\n.\nMATLAB\nStudents cannot set the path in MATLAB because they cannot write to the restricted server drive where the application is hosted.\nHave a faculty or staff member add the path by adding the path command to a\nstartup.m\nfile in\nc:\\users\\\nusername\n\\My Documents\\MATLAB\n.\nR\nPackages cannot be installed in R because you cannot write to the restricted server drive where the application is hosted.\nRequest any additional packages using the\nSoftware Request Form\n.\nCloud Storage\nFor best results, use a Cloud Storage File Share. Always use a cloud storage location when working in IUanyWare. To set up your File Shares for use with IUanyWare, use the\nCloud Storage utility\n.\nVirtual Private Network (VPN)\nIf while off campus you make a\nVPN\nconnection via the SSL VPN tool available on IUware, you may have difficulty connecting or reconnecting to IUanyWare.\nTo connect to VPN before starting IUanyWare, browse to\nhttps://vpn.iu.edu\nand follow the prompts.\nIf you are already in IUanyWare when you browse to\nhttps://vpn.iu.edu\n, you will temporarily lose your IUanyWare connection; reconnect to IUanyWare through the browser at\nhttps://iuanyware.cloud.com\n.\nNote:\nWhile off campus, VPN is not required to access IUanyWare.\nPassphrases longer than 48 characters\nUsers with passphrases longer than 48 characters will sometimes see their application launches fail. This has been reported to Citrix, and it is a Citrix issue. UITS has started a ticket asking them to increase this character limit to allow users to use IU's maximum of 127 characters.\nTo use IUanyWare, reduce passphrase to 48 characters or less.\nHTML5 Receiver\nWhen printing, the HTML5 Receiver will not inherit the default printer of the local machine.\nChoose a printer available in IUanyWare, or save the file to cloud storage or to the local machine to print elsewhere.\nWhen saving, the HTML5 Receiver will not establish read/write access to your local hard drive.\nUse a cloud storage or file share option to save your files.\nIn most cases, when you save a backup of the file, you'll also have an option in the "Save" dialog box to select\nSave to My Device\n. When you make this selection, the session will send a copy of the file to the default download folder used by the browser in which you're working.\nWhen using the HTML5 Receiver via Firefox on Windows 10, you will not be able to type the\n\\\n(backslash) character in applications or desktops.\nUse another browser to access the HTML5 Receiver if you need to be able to type\n\\\n. Alternatively, you can copy the\n\\\ncharacter from within the open IUanyWare application and paste it where desired.\nCitrix Workspace App for Linux\nUsers are experiencing issues configuring the Citrix Workspace App/Receiver with the URL\nhttps://iuanyware.cloud.com\n.\nThe IUanyWare Team is investigating the issue. All Linux users should access IUanyWare via the web; see the first two sections of\nUse IUanyWare in Linux\nand\nAccess and use IUanyWare with Citrix Workspace app for Web\n.\nUITS staff:\nFirst launch fails\nWe believe this is because items like the icon cache and some unnecessary files are actually being captured in the user profiles. This is causing the profile to take just long enough to load that it causes the application to fail on first launch.\nWe have done some preliminary testing, and so far it's positive and resolving the issue. After we perform a couple more tests, we hope to roll it out into production.\nError Message "The User Profile Service service failed the logon.  User profile cannot be loaded"\nThis error may result if the write cache of the XenApp server becomes full from user files, page files, etc. We are working to expand the write cache by an additional 20 GB. We were able to isolate a server, fill the write cache to almost full, and replicate the error message. In the IUanyWare environment, the cache is continually fluctuating as users log on and off, open large files, and engage in activities that increase the page file.\nWe are hoping the expansion of the drive and possibly setting a limit on the page file will help to resolve this issue.\nThis error has also occasionally appeared when a user selects an option multiple times, causing the service to attempt to log the user onto several servers at the same time. The profile is being downloaded by one server and then immediately requested by another server. We have not been able to consistently reproduce this.\nError message "The task you are trying to do can't be completed because Remote Desktop Services is currently busy. Please try again in a few minutes. Other users should still be able to log on."\nThis could be a Microsoft issue, but we need to make sure we are not using all our licenses.\nSee\nRemote Desktop Services Client Access License (RDS CAL) Availability\n.\nPrinter queues not populating in a timely manner\nThis is because we are mapping multiple printer queues. The mapping process can take 45 seconds to a minute. Users should be able to see their locally installed default printer immediately. The locally installed printers are not guaranteed to work with IUanyWare. Our universal print driver should work with about 90% of the printers out there, but there are going to be printers that will not work properly with IUanyWare.	KB0023781	KB0023781 v6.0	 the Downloads folder. You should use a cloud storage option such as Google Drive or <strong>OneDrive</strong> in order	2.5606		2026-01-31 16:54:56.315486	2026-01-31 16:54:56.315486	0	0280b7d033e22ad404e605570e5c7b12
10733	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=62c2b6a147291a54e06785e8536d4343	Troubleshoot Classroom Technology, Collaboration Technologies, and Learning Spaces	Table of contents\nClassroom technology\nPower-cycle the system\nNo display on preview monitor\nMicrophone batteries\nRoom equipment issues\nProject or display an image\nPlay a DVD\nSwitch between the extended and mirrored desktop display modes\nVideo adapters\nKeyboard and mouse issues\nNetwork issues\nAdditional resources\nAudio issues\nZoom\nIssues with SSO in Zoom\nZoom lockouts\nAdditional resources\nKaltura\nUpload issues\nAdditional resources\nEscalation\nClassroom technology\nPower-cycle the system\nTo power-cycle the whole system in most rooms, use the glowing red power switch on the rack. See the\nClassroom Database\nfor photos of the rack in each room.\nNote:\nThis will generally not restart the PC. Instruct the\nclient\nto power-cycle the PC separately.\nNo display on preview monitor\nIf the\nclient\ncannot see an image on the preview monitor (the monitor on the instructor's station), ensure the monitor is powered on and cables are seated.\nMicrophone batteries\nIf the installed microphone's batteries are dead or missing, spares should be available somewhere in the rack, often in the "accessory" drawer. Some rooms have multiple drawers, and other instructors may have moved the spares.\nRoom equipment issues\nReport issues with room lighting, projector screens, chalkboards, furniture, or cleanliness to\nFacility Operations\n.\nProject or display an image\nTo use the projector or display in most rooms, the\nclient\nmust use a "two-tap" process on the touch panel. The idea is "pick a source, then pick a destination". On the touch panel, the\nclient\nshould select the desired projection source on the left (usually\nPC\n), and then select\nDisplay\nor\nProjector\nin the middle. Many rooms have multiple projectors and displays, so the second selection is important. Photos of the touch panel are available on many\nClassroom Database\npages.\nNotes:\nIt may help to switch to another source, then back to the desired source.\nEnsure the desired source is powered on. PC power buttons should be easily accessible.\nIt may also be helpful to\nrestart the whole system\n.\nPlay a DVD\nThe location of DVD players varies by room. The physical player may be installed in the rack, built into the PC, or attached to the lectern. See the\nClassroom Database\nfor photos.\nDVD players built into the PC or attached to the lectern should appear in the computer's "This PC" window. Use VLC or Windows Media Player to launch and control the video.\nDVD players built into the rack generally have their own controls. These controls are generally replicated on the room's touch panel.\nSome, but not all, DVD players also play Blu-ray discs; the\nClassroom Database\nincludes this information.\nSwitch between the extended and mirrored desktop display modes\nIn some rooms it is possible to switch between the "extended" and "mirrored" desktop display modes. The Windows shortcut\nWin-P\nmay be useful in switching.\nVideo adapters\nWhen\nclient\ns bring their own laptops, they must provide their own video adapters. The\nClassroom Database\nindicates what connections are available in each room.\nKeyboard and mouse issues\nIf the installed keyboard or mouse fails to respond,\nrestart the PC and rack\n(peripherals are often connected with USB extenders, so a simple PC restart may not be adequate).\nNote:\nWindows Virtual Keyboard may serve as a temporary workaround.\nNetwork issues\nIf the installed PC is not connecting to the network,\nrestart the PC and rack\n(the rack often contains a network switch).\nLearning Spaces does not manage or troubleshoot wireless network issues.\nClient\ns may refer to\nTips for using wireless in the classroom at IU\n.\nAdditional resources\nRecommendations for using technology in IU classrooms with hybrid or virtual learning\nClassroom Database\nKeep Teaching\nAssistive listening in IU Bloomington classrooms\nClient\ns should direct questions about pedagogy, best practices, or instructional consulting to their campus\nteaching and learning center\n.\nAudio issues\nFor both input and output problems, check the Windows audio device selection. Select the speaker icon at the bottom right corner of the desktop near the time, and rotate devices until one works.\nNote:\nSome applications (including Zoom and Kaltura) may have their own device selector. You may need to check these separately from the Windows selections.\nZoom\nIssues with SSO in Zoom\nIn Zoom, if a\nclient\nsees the message "Something went wrong when trying to sign in with SSO", guide the\nclient\nthrough the steps described in "Log into the Zoom client or mobile app" at\nUse Zoom on a desktop or mobile device\n.\nZoom lockouts\nIf a\nclient\nsees a message like "Your account has been locked due to multiple failed login attempts", and they are then unable to log in via SSO, receiving a message like "Something went wrong when trying to sign in with SSO", the\nclient\nhas been locked out of their Zoom account. This occurs when an invalid login method (something other than SSO) is used too many times, and the\nclient\nwill be locked out of their Zoom account for 30 minutes.\nRe-enable process:\nIf a\nclient\nfrom IU Bloomington or IU Indianapolis is unable to wait 30 minutes for the lock to clear on its own, have the\nclient\ncontact the IU Bloomington or IU Indianapolis Collaboration Technology Support team via\nContact Learning Spaces Support\n.\nAdditional resources\nAccess and share Zoom recordings in Kaltura\nAccommodate international students who can't access Zoom\nList of countries and regions where Zoom is restricted\nUsing breakout rooms\nfor instructors\nZoom Status page\nKaltura\nUpload issues\nIf the\nclient\nhad an issue uploading to Kaltura, the easiest solution is to recover the original\n.mp4\nfrom the local computer and upload it again:\nLocate the recording:\nFrom a classroom PC:\nThis PC > Windows (C:) > Program Files > Kaltura > Classroom > Recordings > completed\nFrom the\nclient\n's PC:\nThis PC > Windows (C:) > Users > [username] > AppData > Local > Kaltura > Capture > Recordings\nFrom the\nclient\n's Mac:\nAt the top of the desktop, select\nGo\n>\nGo To Folder\n>\n~/Library/Preferences/Kaltura/Capture/Recordings\n.\nThe screen share and webcam will be separate files, though both will have audio. If the\nclient\nis satisfied with only having one or the other, upload the files to MediaSpace.\nIf the user wants them spliced together (as they would have been if successfully uploaded) then the two\n.mp4\nfiles will need to be uploaded to cloud storage (\nMicrosoft OneDrive\n,\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n, etc) and escalated to Nate Pairitz (the Kaltura admin).\nAdditional resources\nDownload videos and other media from Kaltura\nAbout the Kaltura REACH captioning service\nDownload and edit captions or transcripts in Kaltura\nControl media access in Kaltura\nUpload and share video in Canvas with Kaltura\nKaltura Status page\nEscalation\nDuring Learning Space Support's hours of operation (Monday-Thursday 7:30am-8pm, Friday 7:30am-5pm):\nEscalate urgent issues via\nContact Learning Spaces Support\n. Examples of urgent issues include:\nA\nclient\nhas an issue on a classroom PC that requires admin rights\nA\nclient\nhas a problem with Zoom during a live meeting\nA\nclient\nneeds help finding an open classroom to move to\nEscalate BeyondTrust chat contacts to the\nLearning Spaces\nteam.\nFor other issues, escalate via ServiceNow by assigning the incident to\nLearning Spaces\n. Be sure to include:\nFor issues with classroom technology:\nProblem location (building and room number)\nDescription of the problem, including the seat number if the room is a lab\nIf the\nclient\nexpects follow-up, name and contact info\nList of attempted troubleshooting steps\nFor issues with collaboration technologies:\nProblem location (personal or campus computer)\nDescription of the problem\nClient\nusername and contact info\nList of attempted troubleshooting steps\nIf available, the date and time of problems with Zoom and Kaltura are incredibly useful	KB0025372	KB0025372 v2.0	 .mp4 files will need to be uploaded to cloud storage (Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong>, Google at IU My Drive, etc	2.4795	km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 16:54:59.935858	2026-01-31 16:54:59.935858	0	62c2b6a147291a54e06785e8536d4343
10739	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=42240fbb47492e942fc6a55b416d43da	Security tips for international travel	Table of contents\nOverview\nPurpose\nConsiderations\nGetting assistance\nExport and import restrictions\nData security safeguards\nBefore your trip\nDuring your trip\nAfter your trip\nOverview\nFor members of the campus community, a trip to a foreign country presents unique data security challenges.  The nature of international travel requires you to use your device (laptop, tablet or smartphone) in various unfamiliar places that may expose your data and device to malicious people and software.\nStaying digitally connected often means connecting devices to public networks in hotels, airports, train stations, and conference halls, which may employ minimal security measures.  Public networks can harbor malware from cybercriminals looking to steal your data for identity fraud, as well as nation-state actors and government agencies targeting academic and business travelers for intellectual property and data theft.\nPurpose\nTo help you protect institutional data throughout your trip, add the following data security safeguards, presented below by phase of trip, to your travel checklists:\nBefore your trip\nDuring your trip\nAfter your trip\nThis guide attempts to make safeguarding institutional data more approachable for all members of the campus community. Supplemental links and contact information for supporting units are provided, where possible.\nConsiderations\nGetting assistance\nSecuring institutional data is a team sport. IT staff are available to assist you. If you are not comfortable performing any of the following security tips and considerations, or would like someone to assist you, contact IT support:\nYour unit’s or department’s local IT staff member\nUnit-specific contact information\nCampus IT Support Center\nExecutive and Internal Technology Support (EITS)\nClient Technology Support (Academic, Health, and UA Technology Support)\nExport and import restrictions\nIn addition to data security safeguards outlined below, international travelers need to consider export and import restrictions imposed by the United States and destination countries. IU’s Research Security Office (RSO) specializes in this area and can assist you if you are unsure about your responsibilities.\nResources for complying with U.S. export laws and regulations\nGeneral concerns, email\nrsohelp@iu.edu\nExport control laws may require you to obtain a license or license exception under the Export Administration Regulations (EAR) prior to traveling overseas. Additionally, IU's encryption software is subject to export control for certain countries.\nFor the most recent list of approved countries, see\nSupplement No. 3 To Part 740\nin the EAR document.\nFor help determining whether your university-issued electronic components, information, and software on your device can be safely and legally transported to another country, email IU Research Compliance (\norcinfo@iu.edu\n).\nData security safeguards\nEach international trip is unique, and not all safeguards may apply in your travel scenario. Be sure to review each data security safeguard and act, where appropriate. See\nGetting assistance\nif you are unsure.\nBefore your trip\nIn the weeks before your scheduled travel date, add the following data security safeguards to your travel checklist:\nLeave your data and/or device at home.  The best way to safeguard your data or device is to not bring them on the trip.  If you don't need to access data stored on your computer, leave your computer in a secure location at home.\nRegister your trip and sign up for travel alerts. We recommend you sign up for the free US Department of State\nSTEP program\nto register your travel and sign up for alerts on political unrest, natural disasters, and other health warnings.\nReview\ntravel advisories\n.  Travel advisories are official warnings from the U.S. Department of State that provide information about the safety of traveling to a specific destination.\nShould you need a device and traveling to a Travel Advisory Level 2 through Level 4 destination, consult your local IT Staff to see if there’s an option to borrow a computer (i.e. a loaner computer) for your trip.\nFor destination-level information, item #3 above.\nFor IT help, see\nGetting assistance\n.\nBack up your data.  Whether you are traveling with a borrowed computer (that is, a loaner), your regular computer, tablet, or smartphone, you should always back up your data. Availability and integrity of your data are the primary reasons to do so. In the event of device loss or compromise, your data can be recovered.\nNote:\nFor data stored on IU servers or in IU cloud storage services (for example, Teams, OneDrive), backup is not required.\nFor IT help, see\nGetting assistance\n.\nInstall and configure disk encryption software.  In the unfortunate scenario where your device is lost or stolen, disk encryption software can help encode your data such that only you and people you authorized can decode and read the encrypted data.  Full disk encryption software, which is freely bundled with recent Microsoft Windows (BitLocker) and Mac OS X (File Vault) operating systems, is easy to use and setup.\nFor assistance specific to full disk encryption, see:\nBitlocker\nFile Vault\nFor IT help, see\nGetting assistance\n.\nInstall and configure campus VPN software.  To protect against eavesdroppers on networks during your trip, install and configure VPN Software to secure all internet traffic.\nDownload and install Avanti Secure from\nIUware\n.\nFor IT help, see\nGetting assistance\n.\nImplement multifactor authentication (MFA). As a member of the IU community, your IU account is protected with Duo, and you already have an authenticator app, like Duo, installed. In some destinations, the use of Duo is restricted. If your destination is one of these, see\nAbout international Two-Step Login (Duo) access and restrictions to some services\n.\nNote:\nIf you will not have access to your personal cell number while traveling and you use SMS (that is, text messages) as a MFA form at sites such as banking institutions, reconfigure those accounts to use authentication apps (for example, Duo, Google Authenticator, etc.) instead of SMS, if possible.\nEnsure the following computing security state before departure:\nUpdate operating system and application software to the latest versions, where possible\nInstall and update anti-malware (virus) software\nStrong credentials (that is,, passphrases) established on user accounts\nEstablish at least two computing accounts\n; one privileged account (for example, root, administrator) that have elevated privileges, and one user account (for example, non-root, non-administrator) used for day-to-day computing.\nFor IT help, see\nGetting assistance\n.\nDuring your trip\nDo NOT leave your device unattended. Physically having control of your device is the easiest way for someone to access your data.  Do not leave your device unattended, lend it to untrusted individuals, or leave it in your checked bag on your flight. When you leave your computer, turn it off instead of putting it to sleep.\nDo NOT plug in untrusted accessories. Accessories that come from untrusted sources can be infected with malware (i.e., virus) intended to steal your data.  Avoid plugging in any questionable accessories (e.g., flash drive, charging cable, SD cards, USB drive/stick, etc.) to your device.  Take all the necessary accessories with you, but if you must purchase an accessory abroad, make sure it is from a reputable source.\nDo NOT enter your credentials into public computers.  Public computers such as hotel business center workstations and internet cafe computers are often poorly managed and provide minimal security protection for their users.  If you need to use a public computer during your travel, avoid entering your credentials in the computer.\nNote:\nIf you cannot avoid entering credentials in an untrusted computer (i.e., a travel emergency), consider changing your used password as soon as possible thereafter.\nConnect only to known, trusted wireless (WiFi) and wired networks. Anyone can create a network and give the network a legitimate sounding name, hoping to lure unsuspecting travelers to connect so they can capture personal information transmitted through the network. This is especially prevalent at public cafes, hotel lobbies and airports.  When connecting to a network, verify the correct network name with establishment staff.\nNote:\nEstablish a connection to the IU VPN service when connected to a public network.\nAllow only trusted Bluetooth hardware to connect to devices. Limiting Bluetooth connectivity to only trusted hardware will help limit the attack vectors for your devices. If possible, do not leave your device in "discoverable" mode; this prevents unsolicited nearby connections to your device.\nEnable security settings to protect data on your mobile device. Set a PIN or passcode, set the device to time out (and require a password) when idle, and depending on the data classification of stored data, consider setting the device to auto-wipe its contents after several incorrect login attempts.\nNote:\nAuto-wipe is a disruptive measure that should only be implemented with due care and under advisory of IT Staff to ensure appropriate recovery measures are in place.\nIf your\nmobile device\nis lost or stolen, immediately contact the\nCampus IT Support Center\n; see\nIf your computer or other device is stolen\n.\nConsider implementing privacy best practices such as:\nUse a webcam cover to protect against unknown monitoring\nInstall a privacy screen that prevent others from shoulder surfing\nComply with any request from a border official to log into your laptop or mobile device.\nIf your device is seized by a foreign agency:\nObtain a contact name, phone number, and written documentation that the device was seized.\nContact the local US embassy or consulate for advice on resolution in the local country.\nNote:\nIf the incident involves the US government, report it to your department’s IT staff and contact\nIU Compliance: Export Control\n.\nFor IT help, see\nGetting assistance\n.\nAfter your trip\nIf you have traveled to a Travel Advisory Level 2 through Level 4 destination, reset credentials (that is, passwords) you used during the trip.  As noted above, consider credentials you used during the journey to be compromised.  Use a trusted computer, whether it's your own or one provided by your IT support staff, to reset credentials that were used during the trip.\nIf you have traveled to a Travel Advisory Level 3 or Level 4 destination, consult your local IT Staff to have your computing devices' security state assessed.\nFor IT help, see\nGetting assistance\n.	KB0026514	KB0026514 v2.0	: For data stored on IU servers or in IU cloud storage services (for example, Teams, <strong>OneDrive</strong>	2.2084	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 16:55:03.364794	2026-01-31 16:55:03.364794	0	42240fbb47492e942fc6a55b416d43da
