nAvailable to all courses\nSyllabus Supplement\n:\nAdd a syllabus supplement to Canvas for your campus, school, or department\nAvailable to all courses\nUnlock Course\n:\nUnlock or relock course after the term or course end date has passed\nAvailable to all courses\nDigital courseware and learning tools\nImportant:\nAll tools in this section involve a student fee, and must be adopted through IU eTexts and added to your course by a Canvas administrator.\nALEKS\n:\nAccess adaptive, interactive online course materials from McGraw-Hill\nAvailable to all SIS-provisioned courses associated with any campus (including the IU School of Medicine)\nNot available to courses that are not associated with a campus, such as IU Expand courses, manually created courses, or IU Online template courses\nCengage MindLinks\n:\nAccess interactive online course materials from Cengage\nAvailable to all courses\nCODIO\n:\nAccess interactive course materials from CODIO\nAvailable to all SIS-provisioned courses associated with any campus (including the IU School of Medicine)\nNot available to courses that are not associated with a campus, such as IU Expand courses, manually created courses, or IU Online template courses\nEvolve Link\n:\nIncorporate interactive online course materials from Elsevier Education into your course\nLimited to courses in BL-NURS, CO-NURS, EA-NURS, FT-DAST, FT-DHYG, FT-NURS, FT-RADX, IN-DAST, IN-DHYG, IN-NURS, IN-OCTH, IN-PTHR, IN-PULM, IN-RADI, KO-NURS, KO-RADI, NW-DAST, NW-DHYG, NW-NURS, NW-RADI, SB-DHYG, SB-NURS, SB-RADI, and SE-NURS\nIU eTexts (Unizin Engage)\n:\nRead electronic textbooks (eTexts) ordered through the IU eTexts initiative\nAvailable to all courses\nKnowledge Matters\n:\nAccess interactive case simulations from Knowledge Matters\nAvailable to all SIS-provisioned courses associated with any campus (including the IU School of Medicine)\nNot available to courses that are not associated with a campus, such as IU Expand courses, manually created courses, or IU Online template courses\nMacmillan Higher Education\n:\nAccess interactive online course materials from Macmillan\nAvailable to all courses\nMcGraw-Hill Campus Connect\n:\nAccess interactive online course materials from McGraw-Hill\nAvailable to all courses\nPearson\n:\nAccess interactive online course materials from Pearson, including MyLab and Mastering\nAvailable to all courses\nSIMnet\n:\nAccess\nMicrosoft 365\ntraining materials from McGraw-Hill\nAvailable to all SIS-provisioned courses associated with any campus (including the IU School of Medicine)\nNot available to courses that are not associated with a campus, such as IU Expand courses, manually created courses, or IU Online template courses\nWileyPlus\n:\nAccess interactive online course materials from Wiley\nAvailable to all courses\nLibrary research\nEBSCO Library Resources\n:\nCreate and manage course reading lists from\nEBSCO\nlibrary databases\nAvailable to all SIS-provisioned courses associated with any campus, except at the IU School of Medicine\nNot available to courses that are not associated with a campus, such as IU Expand courses, manually created courses, or IU Online template courses\nIU Libraries\n:\nAccess the IU Bloomington main library page\nLimited to IU Bloomington courses\nIUN Library:\nFind digital library resources from the IU Northwest library\nLimited to IU Northwest courses\nLibrary Research Guide\n:\nAccess customized and general research guides with links to online content from the IU Indianapolis Library\nLimited to IU Indianapolis courses\nLibrary Resources\n:\nAccess the main library page for the campus with which the course is associated\nLimited to the Columbus, East, Kokomo, South Bend, and Southeast campuses\nProQuest Resources\n:\nCreate and manage course reading lists from\nProQuest\nlibrary databases\nAvailable to all SIS-provisioned courses associated with any campus, except at the IU School of Medicine\nNot available to courses that are not associated with a campus, such as IU Expand courses, manually created courses, or IU Online template courses\nResearch Help\n:\nAccess customized and general research guides with links to online content from the IU Bloomington Libraries\nLimited to IU Bloomington courses\nMiscellaneous\nCampus course policies:\nAccess campus-specific course policy information\nAvailable to all SIS-provisioned courses associated with any campus (including the IU School of Medicine)\nNot available to courses that are not associated with a campus, such as IU Expand courses, manually created courses, or IU Online template courses\nCORE ELMS\n:\nManage clinical experiences and other types of externships\nLimited to courses in SHRS at IU Indianapolis\nIUSM Resources:\nAccess online resources needed by IU School of Medicine students and faculty\nAvailable only to courses in the IU School of Medicine\nReports and Dashboards\n:\nView and access all course-level reports and analytics dashboards in Canvas\nStudent Profile Report\n:\nSee demographic and academic information about students in your class and department\nAvailable to certain roles in all courses\nUniversity and School Policies\n:\nLimited to courses in the School of Social Work at the Bloomington, Indianapolis, East, Northwest, and Southeast campuses\nWebDB\n:\nAccess hosted environment for teaching/learning SQL Server database concepts and SQL programming\nAvailable to all courses\nSIS integration\nExport grades to SIS\n:\nExport course grades in an SIS-compatible format\nAvailable to all courses\nFaculty Center Administrative Withdrawal Roster\n:\nAccess the Faculty Center Administrative Withdrawal Roster (redirects to the Faculty Center to select the desired course)\nAvailable only to courses at the Indianapolis, Columbus, and Fort Wayne campuses\nSIS Faculty Center\n:\nAccess the SIS Faculty Center\nAvailable to all courses, except in the IU School of Medicine\nSIS Grade Roster\n:\nAccess the SIS Grade Roster associated with the current course (redirects to the Faculty Center if the course has cross-listed sections)\nAvailable to all courses, except in the IU School of Medicine\nStudent success\nACE Tutoring\n:\nLearn about available tutoring services from the South Bend Academic Centers for Excellence\nAvailable only at the South Bend campus\nBrainfuse\n:\nGet live tutoring assistance for courses in specific disciplines\nLimited to courses in Anatomy, Biology, Business, Chemistry, and Math at IU Southeast\nStudent Engagement Roster\nStudent Engagement Roster (Faculty)\n:\nProvide timely class feedback to students to encourage them to engage in experiences that result in graduation\nStudent Engagement Roster (Student)\n:\nView engagement feedback from your instructors\nBoth available to all courses, except at the IU School of Medicine\nStudent support services information:\nLearn about resources and services available to students at the relevant campuses\nBloomington:\nIU Support Services\nIndianapolis:\nIU Indianapolis Student Support Services\n(limited to courses in schools with undergraduate programs)\nEast:\nStudent & Academic Success Hub\nSoutheast:\nSucceed at IU Southeast\nEach is available only to courses on the relevant campus.\nSuitable\n:\nMeasure student engagement, development, and retention\nLimited to courses in the College of Arts and Sciences at IU Bloomington (courses in the BL-COAD-GRP, BL-COGI-GRP, BL-COLL-GRP, and BL-COMS-GRP subaccounts)\nTimelyCare\n:\nAccess free virtual mental health support, with 24/7 access to virtual care, self-care, and well-being tools for IU students\nAvailable to all courses	KB0025591	KB0025591 v14.0	 in Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU Available to all courses Canvas Commons: Store, find, import, and share course	1.327		2026-01-31 16:55:14.56969	2026-01-31 16:55:14.56969	0	e90e64a24726be18d500b87b716d4348
10931	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6b31ba51475c6e542fc6a55b416d436d	Manage access for a Geode-Project allocation in Windows	On this page:\nOverview\nSet up AD groups for administrator and user roles\nSet up a sub-allocation\nOverview\nGeode-Project\nis a\nResearch Technologies\nservice providing disk-based persistent storage allocations to research projects using Indiana University's\nresearch supercomputers\n.\nGeode-Project\nallocations are hosted on\nGeode\n, which is co-located at the IU data centers in Bloomington and Indianapolis, and are managed through the\nRT Projects\nportal. Files stored in\nGeode-Project\nspace are replicated, by default, at each data center. Project space users can access\nGeode-Project\ndirectly from all IU\nresearch supercomputers\nand remotely from personal workstations connected to the IU campus network.\nTo request a\nGeode-Project\nallocation, you must complete a memo of understanding (MOU) with\nUITS\nResearch Technologies\n; see\nRequest a project space allocation on Geode-Project\n.\nOnce space on\nGeode-Project\nis allocated, UITS\nResearch Technologies\nwill work with the project owner's\nlocal UITS support person\nto set up\nADS\nsecurity groups for controlling access permissions for the project space (as explained in the next section).\nSet up AD groups for administrator and user access\nThe local UITS support person will create two security groups in IU\nADS\nfor controlling the administrator and user roles for the project space:\nThe "admin" group (for example,\nBL-BIO-GEODE-LAB1-ADMINS\n) should be populated with only the local UITS support people who are responsible for administering the project space. Users added to this group are granted full permissions to the project space.\nThe "user" group (for example,\nBL-BIO-GEODE-LAB1-USERS\n) should be populated with the individuals and groups using the project space. Users added to this group are granted modify permissions.\nNote:\nIf your IT unit provides support for many organizations, leveraging a single group for each\nGeode-Project\nallocation is acceptable and encouraged.\nThe local UITS support person will share the group names with UITS Research Storage, which uses them to populate corresponding AD groups for managing administrator and user roles in\nGeode-Project\n.\nOnce this step is complete, the local UITS support person may add users and groups to the allocation by adding them to the appropriate AD group. Changes take approximately 15 minutes before they become available for use on\nGeode-Project\n.\nResearch Storage staff will provide a share path or mount point for your allocation.\nMount your allocation. Allocation names should follow the IU ADS naming conventions; see\nMap or mount a drive to your home directory space or a Geode-Project space\n.\nSet up a sub-allocation within a project space\nThe local UITS support person may also create a sub-allocation and restrict it to a single AD group:\nCreate an AD group that contains the users you want to limit to the sub-allocation (for example,\nBL-BIO-GEODE-LAB1-PROJECT1-USERS\n), and then add it to the main "user" group set up for the project space (for example,\nBL-BIO-GEODE-LAB1-USERS\n). Changes take approximately 15 minutes before they become available for use on\nGeode-Project\n.\nIn Windows,\nmap a drive to the appropriate project space\n, and then, immediately below the root level of the project space, create a new directory for the sub-allocation.\nRight-click the new folder, select\nProperties\n, and then, on the\nSecurity\ntab, select\nEdit\n.\nSelect\nAdd\n, enter the sub-allocation group name (for example,\nBL-BIO-GEODE-LAB1-PROJECT1-USERS\n), and then select\nOK\n.\nSelect the appropriate "Allow" permission (typically,\nModify\n), select\nApply\n, and then select\nOK\ntwice.\nUsers may need to log out and then log back in again for this change to take effect.\nNote:\nTo set a quota on a sub-allocation to limit the amount of storage available to a group of users, the local UITS support person should email the UITS Research Storage team (\nstore-admin@iu.edu\n) for help.	KB0024817	KB0024817 v2.0	 Overview To request a <strong>Geode</strong>-Project allocation, you must complete a memo of understanding (MOU) with UITS Research Technologies; see Request a project space allocation on <strong>Geode</strong>-Project. Once space on <strong>Geode</strong>-Project is allocated, UITS Research Technologies will work with the project owner's local UITS	196.4307	km-aud-rt, km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits, km-aud-sc	2026-01-31 16:55:18.673931	2026-01-31 16:55:18.673931	0	6b31ba51475c6e542fc6a55b416d436d
10933	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=422795a133f5621804e605570e5c7be6	Manage your Geode-Project allocation within your RT Project	Table of contents\nOverview\nRequest a Geode-Project allocation\nRequest a change to a Geode-Project allocation\nRequest removal of a Geode-Project allocation\nManage user access for a Geode-Project allocation\nOverview\nRT Projects\nallows you to request new\nGeode-Project\nallocations, request modifications to allocations, and manage collaborators in each allocation. An RT Project can contain a single Geode-Project allocation per principal investigator (PI). Geode-Project allocations must be linked to a single RT Project. All Geode-Project allocations are subject to an annual renewal in order to keep the storage active.\nImportant:\nOnly the RT Project's principal investigator (PI) can make changes to an existing allocation.\nRequest a Geode-Project allocation\nWhen the PI is viewing the "Allocations" section of a project, selecting\nRequest Resource Allocation\nwill display a list of resources. At the bottom of this list, under "Storages", is a request form to create a new Geode-Project allocation. This form will ask for a secondary contact, the storage size needed,\nActive Directory security groups\nfor the users and admins of the project, the purpose of the storage allocation, and some general questions about project lifetime, generation and protection of the data, and how long the data will be computed against. The owner of the RT Project will be the owner of the Geode-Project allocation.\nRequest a change to a Geode-Project allocation\nWithin your RT Project, under "Allocations", in the "Actions" column, select the folder icon to view the details of the project.\nWithin the details of the project, you may view the Geode-Project allocation's attributes, and request a change or removal of a project.\nRequesting a change results in a form that allows you to request a new quota for the project. The quota change is subject to an approval process and other technical limits. For example, it would not be suitable to reduce an allocation from 4 TB to 1 TB if 3 TB of data still exists in the project. All quota increase requests must provide a demonstrated need for the increased quota.\nRequest removal of a Geode-Project allocation\nTo request removal of a Geode-Project allocation, use the\nRemoval\nbutton next to the\nChange\nbutton. Make sure you have selected the correct allocation before proceeding at the confirmation screen.\nManage user access for a Geode-Project allocation\nAnyone with an active IU account can be given access to the Geode-Project allocation's storage space. The RT Project's PI does this by adding usernames to either of the\nActive Directory security groups\nprovided when the allocation was requested. Granular file storage access is also managed by the project's PI via\nNFSv4 ACLs\nwithin the Geode-Project.\nTwo Active Directory security groups are used to manage access in a Geode-Project: Admins and Users.\nAdmins:\nUsers in the Admins group have read/write access to the top-level directory in the Geode-Project storage allocation and have access to edit the NFSv4 ACLs within the directory structure.\nUsers:\nUsers in the Users group have basic read/write access to all directories below the top-level directory or as permitted by the NFSv4 ACLs set by the project's PI.\nThese groups are set upon the creation of the Geode-Project, and only the members of the groups should be modified over time.	KB0026680	KB0026680 v4.0	Table of contents Overview Request a <strong>Geode</strong>-Project allocation Request a change to a <strong>Geode</strong>-Project allocation Request removal of a <strong>Geode</strong>-Project allocation Manage user access for a <strong>Geode</strong>-Project allocation Overview RT Projects allows you to request new <strong>Geode</strong>-Project allocations, request	192.5677		2026-01-31 16:55:23.883961	2026-01-31 16:55:23.883961	0	422795a133f5621804e605570e5c7be6
11750	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=28ef2c3b476456583d373803846d4364	Manage administrative access on faculty and staff computers	Local UITS support people and UITS staff:\nSee the\nsection below\nabout managing administrative access on faculty and staff computers that you support.\nBest practices for safe computing dictate that you should not log into a computer with an\nadministrator account\nwhen performing routine tasks. The easiest way to accomplish this, and maintain compliance with IU policies, is to restrict all administrative access to trained IT staff.\nWhile you should never perform day-to-day activities with an administrator account, as part of your job you may need administrative access to install software, modify network settings, or perform other similar functions. If you need such access:\nWork with unit IT staff to configure administrative access appropriately, including a special administrator account that is separate from your\nUniversity account\n.\nLearn about security best practices and training at\nInformation Security and Policy\nto ensure that you are adequately prepared to perform administrative tasks.\nRead and accept responsibility for relevant\nIT policies\n, including:\nAppropriate Use of Information Technology Resources (IT-01)\nMisuse and Abuse of Information Technology Resources (IT-02)\nEligibility to Use Information Technology Resources (IT-03)\nPrivacy of Electronic Information and Information Technology Resources (IT-07)\nSecurity of Information Technology Resources (IT-12)\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\nCyber Risk Mitigation Responsibilities (IT-28)\nManagement of Institutional Data (DM-01)\nLocal UITS support people and UITS staff:\nConsider these best practices when it becomes necessary to grant administrative access to faculty and staff you support:\nInquire about the need for administrative access. Common scenarios may include:\nFaculty members who teach during evenings or weekends, when IT support may not be immediately available\nStaff and faculty members remotely conducting research or other work\nRemind those with administrative access that they are required to comply with the relevant\nIT policies above\n.\nMaintain paper or electronic documentation, updated at least annually, that captures relevant data:\nStaff/faculty name\nReason for administrative access\nDevice(s) involved\nAn acceptance of responsibility for IT policy compliance\nCreate a special administrator account, separate from the individual's\nUniversity account\n.\nEnsure that the Dean or other unit leader understands and approves the unit practices governing end-user administrative access.\nMake sure that faculty and staff have adequate training and are aware of resources such as\nInformation Security and Policy\n.\nRemind users that they should\nnever\nperform day-to-day activities with an administrator account.	KB0023934	KB0023934 v1.0	Best practices for safe <strong>computing</strong> dictate that you should not log into a computer with an administrator account when performing routine tasks. The easiest way to accomplish this, and maintain compliance with IU policies, is to restrict all administrative access to trained IT staff. While you should	1.5635		2026-01-31 17:16:59.902904	2026-01-31 17:16:59.902904	0	28ef2c3b476456583d373803846d4364
10985	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d262b7373b25e654476d941864e45a66	At the IU Columbus Learning Center, use a projector as a second display for Presenter Mode on a VDI computer	To view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nIU community:\nTo set up a projector as a second display for Presenter Mode on a VDI machine:\nBefore logging into the computer, right-click anywhere on the blue background and select\nDisplay Configurator\n.\nOn the resulting Configurator screen, select the third option; this will have a "2" on the left and a "1" on the right. The screen will darken for a couple seconds, and then you can select\nAdvanced\non the lower right of the screen.\nA screen will appear showing both displays. Select the left-facing arrow to the right of the screen.\nFrom the options that appear, select the top drop-down.\nFrom the two resulting displays, select the one labeled\nOptiPlex\n.\nFrom the resulting drop-down that appears under that, select the one that says\nIndex\n, and then select\n1\n.\nAt the bottom of the window, select\nApply\n, and log into the system as usual.\nOn the projector, you now can display PowerPoint in Presenter Mode, or other information from the computer.\nWhen you're finished, power the computer off and then restart so it's ready for the next person to use it.	KB0026493	KB0026493 v3.0		5.1009		2026-01-31 16:57:50.85681	2026-01-31 16:57:50.85681	0	d262b7373b25e654476d941864e45a66
11753	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=0d20b4ff476456583d373803846d43e0	Configure Exchange Online with iOS (iPhone and iPad)	Table of contents\nOverview\nInstructions\nBefore you begin\nSetup\nOverview\nNote:\nMicrosoft recommends using the\nOutlook app on iOS and Android\nfor better integration with the Exchange Online environment.\nAt IU,\nExchange Online\nallows you to access email, calendar, contacts, and tasks from your personal computer, the web, and mobile devices.\nImportant:\nIU's\nExchange Online\nenvironment does not support\nIMAP\n. For email configuration instructions, including details for those using Linux and Thunderbird, see\nConfigure your email client for Exchange Online\n.\nInstructions\nBefore you begin\nBefore setting up Exchange Online on your device, you must delete any previously existing Exchange email account:\nGo to\nSettings\n. In iOS 14 or higher, choose\nMail\nand then\nAccounts\n. In iOS 13 and 12, tap\nPasswords & Accounts\n.\nSelect the account to delete.\nAt the bottom of the screen, select\nDelete Account\n.\nOn the dialog box, choose\nDelete from my iPhone/iPad\n.\nSetup\nTo set up your\nExchange Online\naccount on your iOS device:\nGo to\nSettings\n. In iOS 14 or higher, tap\nMail\nand then\nAccounts\n. In iOS 13 and 12, tap\nPasswords & Accounts\n.\nChoose\nAdd Account\n, and then\nMicrosoft Exchange\n.\nType your\n@iu.edu\nemail address. The "Description" field defaults to "Exchange", but you may change it if you wish. Select\nNext\n.\nChoose\nSign in\n; the "IU Login" screen will appear, with your\nusername@iu.edu\naddress prepopulated. Enter your\nIU passphrase\nand select\nSign in\n. You may also be prompted to authenticate with\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n.\nNote:\nIf you are not prompted to authenticate with\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n, and you see an error that says "We're sorry, access is not allowed because you are not enrolled. Please contact your organization's IT help desk for assistance", you'll need to enroll a device in Duo before you can proceed. For more, see\nGet started with Two-Step Login (Duo) at IU\n.\nSelect\nMail\n. If you'd like to sync any other items, select them, and then select\nSave\n.\nNote:\nThe first time you add an\nExchange Online\naccount to your iOS device, you'll see a "Permissions requested" screen showing a list of the data the app will access. Tap\nAccept\nto add the account.	KB0025335	KB0025335 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Instructions Before you begin Setup Overview Instructions Before you begin Before setting up Exchange Online on your device, you must delete any previously existing Exchange email account: Go to Settings. In iOS 14 or higher, choose Mail and then Accounts. In iOS 13 and 12, tap Passwords & Accounts. Select the account 	1.5544		2026-01-31 17:17:03.918002	2026-01-31 17:17:03.918002	0	0d20b4ff476456583d373803846d43e0
11757	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=12df603b476456583d373803846d43d9	If your scheduled IUIE report didn't run	There are two main reasons a report scheduled in the\nIUIE\nmight fail to run:\nThe schedule has been disabled. On the IUIE scheduling form, check to make sure that the schedule is enabled for the report object.\nThe IUIE database has been down. Check\nIUIE News\nto see if this is the case. At the bottom of the scheduling form, you have the option for a scheduled job not to run if the database is down for any reason. You can also choose the option to have the job run when the database becomes available again, if it happens to be down when a report is scheduled to run.	KB0023717	KB0023717 v1.0	 of the scheduling form, you have the option for a scheduled <strong>job</strong> not to run if the database is down for any reason. You can also choose the option to have the <strong>job</strong> run when the database becomes	565.454		2026-01-31 17:17:12.826672	2026-01-31 17:17:12.826672	0	12df603b476456583d373803846d43d9
10937	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d7af6077476456583d373803846d432a	Use MS Query to connect to your MySQL database at IU RDC	In Excel, Microsoft Query (MS Query) lets you connect to and query external data sources, import data into your worksheet, and refresh worksheet data automatically whenever those external data sources are updated with new information. For more about using MS Query, see\nUse Microsoft Query to retrieve external data\n.\nNote:\nYou can\nuse joins\nin MS Query to retrieve data from multiple tables.\nTo use MS Query to connect to your MySQL database on the Research Database Complex (\nRDC\n) at Indiana University, you first need to create a MySQL ODBC source on your computer; for instructions, see\nSet up an ODBC source in Windows to access your MySQL database at IU RDC\n. Also, before users, including the root user (you), can connect to your MySQL server instance, you must create MySQL accounts for them and grant them appropriate access privileges; for instructions, see\nCreate user accounts for your MySQL database at IU RDC\n.\nOnce those criteria are met, to connect to your MySQL database via MS Query:\nIn an Excel worksheet, on the\nData\ntab, select\nGet Data\n>\nFrom Other Sources\n>\nFrom Microsoft Query\n.\nIn the "Choose Data Source" window, on the\nDatabases\ntab, find the ODBC source you created, and then double-click its name.\nIf you need help using MS Query to connect to your MySQL database on the RDC, email the RDC Administration team (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n); UITS does not support MS Query beyond helping you connect to your MySQL database. For more about RDC database accounts, see the\nDatabases\nsection of\nAbout the Research Database Complex (RDC) at Indiana University\n.	KB0022759	KB0022759 v1.0	 MS Query to connect to your MySQL <strong>database</strong> on the Research <strong>Database</strong> <strong>Complex</strong> (RDC) at Indiana ODBC source in Windows to access your MySQL <strong>database</strong> at <strong>IU</strong> RDC. Also, before users, including MySQL <strong>database</strong> at <strong>IU</strong> RDC. Once those criteria are met, to connect to your MySQL <strong>database</strong> via MS Query	54.5338		2026-01-31 16:55:40.49695	2026-01-31 16:55:40.49695	0	d7af6077476456583d373803846d432a
11761	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5cafe037476456583d373803846d4311	About Quick View in the IUIE	Table of contents\nOverview\nGeneral layout\nOperation\nOverview\nThe\nQuick View\ntab in\nIUIE\nallows you to display the last output generated from any selected\nscheduled report\n. Under the\nQuick View\ntab, you can place a link to the most recent report for a scheduled job. Rather than having to navigate through My Catalog and Completed Reports, you can directly access scheduled reports through\nQuick View\n.\nGeneral layout\nSelecting\nQuick View\ndisplays report links on the subtab bar in alphabetical order by report title.\nEach link represents the most recent output from a scheduled job.\nReport output is displayed in the content area under the links.\nEach report display has a link to make it the default report. If this link has been used, the associated report appears in the content area whenever the\nQuick View\ntab is selected.\nIf no report has been selected as the default, the first report is displayed.\nOperation\nTo add an output link to\nQuick View\n:\nAccess a report object, and set the desired parameter values. Select\nSave Settings\n, and then select\nSchedule\n. Alternatively, view an existing scheduled job in My Catalog.\nCheck\nAdd Link to Output in Quick View\n.\nSchedule the job for the appropriate time.\nTo remove an output link from\nQuick View\n:\nView an existing scheduled job in My Catalog.\nEither uncheck\nAdd Link to Output in Quick View\n, or check\nDisable schedule for this report object\n.\nTo set the default output display:\nSelect the\nQuick View\nlink that is to be displayed by default.\nSelect\nSet as default Quick View page\nlink.	KB0022481	KB0022481 v1.0	, you can place a link to the most recent report for a scheduled <strong>job</strong>. Rather than having to navigate by report title. Each link represents the most recent output from a scheduled <strong>job</strong>. Report output scheduled <strong>job</strong> in My Catalog. Check Add Link to Output in Quick View. Schedule the <strong>job</strong> for the appropriate	403.664		2026-01-31 17:17:18.663032	2026-01-31 17:17:18.663032	0	5cafe037476456583d373803846d4311
10942	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=00bc55d73ba5e2d0476d941864e45a86	Optimize Denodo views	Table of contents\nOverview\nUse caching\nExamine the data lineage\nChange the execution plan\nOverview\nWhen using\nDenodo\nfor data virtualization and integration, you may be able to improve the performance of Denodo views. For potential options, see below.\nUse caching\nIn Denodo at IU, caching is not performed at the server level. Instead, each virtual database (VDB) is configured with an exclusive Oracle tablespace for caching. Developers have sufficient permissions to manage partial caching at the view level.\nNote:\nDenodo caching is used to enhance query performance. Caching is resource intensive, so you should implement it only after detecting query performance issues (not by default). Run cache jobs as infrequently as possible. Do not refresh your cache more than once an hour. Cache necessary data only, and do not cache excessively large database columns, such as CLOBs or LOBs.\nNote:\nUITS recommends setting TIMETOLIVEINCACHE to\nNOEXPIRE\n.\nExamine the data lineage\nYou can examine your data lineage for a derived view by using\nTree View\n. If you are combining data that you don't need, consider redesigning your derived view.\nChange the execution plan\nYou can change how Denodo executes complex joins by modifying the execution plan. For details, see the Denodo\nVirtual DataPort Administration Guide\n. If you wish to try\ndata movement\n, your cache tablespace is exclusive to your VDB and available for your use.	KB0022986	KB0022986 v2.0	 the performance of Denodo views. For potential options, see below. Use caching In Denodo at <strong>IU</strong>, caching is not performed at the server level. Instead, each virtual <strong>database</strong> (VDB) is configured with an exclusive how Denodo executes <strong>complex</strong> joins by modifying the execution plan. For details, see the Denodo	12.7524		2026-01-31 16:57:11.095279	2026-01-31 16:57:11.095279	0	00bc55d73ba5e2d0476d941864e45a86
10945	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d1cf6cb7476456583d373803846d4344	IU RDC usage policies	Table of contents\nAccounts\nDatabase security\nResponsibilities\nAcknowledging grant support\nDatabase backup and recovery\nScheduled downtime\nDisk space for data loading and other applications\nMail usage\nReporting problems\nQuestions and comments\nAccounts\nThe Indiana University\nResearch Database Complex\n(\nRDC\n) supports research-related MySQL and PostgreSQL databases and data-intensive applications that require databases. The\nRDC\nis strictly devoted to supporting research, and is not an instructional, classroom environment.\nAccess to the RDC is available to all IU graduate students, faculty, and staff. Undergraduate students and non-IU collaborators need IU faculty sponsors to get access.\nTo create a database on the RDC, submit the\nRDC Database Account Application\n.\nWhen your database login has been created, you will receive a confirmation message in email with your login and information about connecting to your database. You will need to renew your database account annually.\nBy submitting the RDC Database Account Application, you will be affirming that:\nYou understand the database is to be used only for research purposes.\nYou agree to not store\nCritical data\n(except for ePHI data) on RDC databases.\nYou will acknowledge use of IU's high performance computing systems in publications resulting from your research.\nYou will provide periodic listings of citations of those publications upon request.\nYou agree to annually renew your RDC account.\nDatabase group accounts are used on the RDC. Although you are not allowed to use a group account for regular data access, group accounts are often the best process for administering a database that is used by a project or team. The RDC Database Account Application lets you specify a database group account. When you request a database group account:\nYou must first have an\nIU username\nfor your group.\nYou will be considered the responsible party for the group database account.\nYou must create additional logins for all access to data, and you must ensure that the database group account is never used for data access. It should only be used to administer user logins, schemas, and permissions.\nFor more, see\nAbout group and departmental accounts\n.\nDatabase security\nHIPAA\nThis system or service meets certain requirements established in the\nHIPAA Security Rule\nthereby enabling its use for work involving data that contain protected health information (\nPHI\n). However, using this system or service does not fulfill your legal responsibilities for protecting the privacy and security of data that contain PHI. You may use this system or service for work involving data that contain PHI only if you institute additional\nadministrative\n,\nphysical\n, and\ntechnical\nsafeguards that complement those UITS already has in place.\nFor more, see\nYour legal responsibilities for protecting data containing protected health information (PHI) when using UITS Research Technologies systems and services\n.\nNote:\nIn accordance with standards for access control mandated by the\nHIPAA\nSecurity\nRule\n, you are not permitted to access data containing protected health information (\nPHI\n) using a group (or departmental) account. To ensure accountability and maintain appropriate levels of access control, all users must use an individual login for all work involving PHI.\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.\nPasswords\nOnce your RDC Database Account Application is processed, you will receive a confirmation email message that describes how to access your database. Your initial database password will be sent under separate cover as a second email message.\nFor security reasons, when using a command-line client to connect to your MySQL database, do not enter your database password on the MySQL command line. Follow the instructions in\nAccess your MySQL database at IU RDC\n.\nResponsibilities\nYou are responsible for:\nCreating and managing your schemas and schema objects (for example, tables, views, procedures, triggers, and schema privileges)\nCreating and managing user logins for your databases (see the previous\nDatabase security note\nabout maintaining appropriate access for sensitive data, including PHI)\nChanging datatypes\nAny data processes, such as data imports, deletes, modifications, transformations, and retrievals\nCreating and maintaining copies of scripts\nEmailing the RDC Administration team (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n) about changes in space or database administration, or if you no longer need access to the research database\nAdapting your schema and data as required during system and database upgrades\nAdapting client applications and tools to system and database versions\nRDC database administrators are responsible for:\nManaging space allocation\nMonitoring and reporting database performance\nMonitoring and reporting invalid schema objects\nInstalling database and system upgrades and\npatches\nIf you have questions or need help, email the RDC Administration team (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n).\nAcknowledging grant support\nThe Indiana University cyberinfrastructure, managed by the\nResearch Technologies\ndivision of\nUITS\n, is supported by funding from several grants, each of which requires you to acknowledge its support in all presentations and published works stemming from research it has helped to fund. Conscientious acknowledgment of support from past grants also enhances the chances of IU's research community securing funding from grants in the future. For the acknowledgment statement(s) required for scholarly printed works, web pages, talks, online publications, and other presentations that make use of this and/or other grant-funded systems at IU, see\nSources of funding to acknowledge in published work if you use IU's research cyberinfrastructure\n.\nDatabase backup and recovery\nBackups for MySQL database servers on the RDC are the responsibility of the user. UITS recommends using MySQL's\nmysqldump\ncommand; for more, see\nmysqldump - A Database Backup Program\nin the MySQL documentation.\nScheduled downtime\nThe RDC's maintenance window is the third Sunday of each month, 12am-6am. Make sure to plan for this downtime each month. Notice of any emergency downtime will be posted at\nStatus.IU\n.\nDisk space for data loading and other applications\nIf you need space on the RDC for staging data, or for data-related packages and applications, email the RDC Administration team (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n), which will evaluate requests on a case-by-case basis.\nMail usage\nProduction mail service is not provided on the RDC.\nReporting problems\nTo report a problem with the RDC, email the RDC Administration team (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n) with the following information:\nThe name of the database server to which you were connecting\nA description of what you were trying to do\nA description of the problem\nThe name and version of the tool you were using to connect to the database server\nYour computer's operating system\nThe error message number and text, if applicable\nThe time the problem occurred\nSupport staff are available from approximately 8am-5pm Monday-Friday.\nQuestions and comments\nUITS established the policies described in this document with the goal of providing a stable, powerful, and efficient research database environment for IU graduate students, faculty, and staff. If you have a question or comment about these policies, email the RDC Administration team (\nrdcadmin@iu.edu\n).	KB0023284	KB0023284 v1.0	. When you request a <strong>database</strong> group account: You must first have an <strong>IU</strong> username for your group on the MySQL command line. Follow the instructions in Access your MySQL <strong>database</strong> at <strong>IU</strong> RDCTable of contents Accounts <strong>Database</strong> security Responsibilities Acknowledging grant support <strong>Database</strong>	12.0475		2026-01-31 16:57:15.013862	2026-01-31 16:57:15.013862	0	d1cf6cb7476456583d373803846d4344
10946	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f9c5b8854732f694d500b87b716d4374	Why SiteKube and AppKube are separate	Table of contents\nOverview\nDifferent use cases have different security needs\nMinimizing security risk for sites\nProviding flexibility where needed\nProtecting IU's data and systems\nSupporting security compliance and auditing\nEnabling better resource optimization\nCommon questions\nOverview\nIU has intentionally separated hosting into two environments:\nSiteKube\n(for\nWCMS\nsites) and\nAppKube\n(for dynamic applications).\nThis approach improves security, simplifies management, and ensures each environment is optimized for its purpose.\nThis article summarizes why the environments are separated and what benefits this architecture provides.\nDifferent use cases have different security needs\nSiteKube\nis designed for WCMS-managed sites with minimal dynamic content or server-side code.\nAppKube\nis designed for custom applications that need databases, APIs, or dynamic server-side code.\nMinimizing security risk for sites\nStatic sites rely on fewer technologies (like databases), making them well-suited for a more restricted environment that maximizes security and stability.\nCentralized publishing through WCMS limits who can make changes on the server. It also helps ensure sites use IU's most secure, accessible content management tools (such as the IU Web Framework).\nProviding flexibility where needed\nAppKube allows more complex functionality (databases, APIs, cron jobs) that SiteKube sites often don't need.\nDevelopers can use server-side PHP, database connections, and custom logic.\nThis extra flexibility requires a hosting environment with stricter security controls, monitoring, and management.\nProtecting IU's data and systems\nSegregating environments reduces the risk of cross-site vulnerabilities.\nSiteKube content stays isolated from more complex, potentially risky code.\nSensitive data (for example, database credentials) can be better secured with environment-specific secrets management.\nSupporting security compliance and auditing\nHaving a tightly managed environment makes it easier to prove security compliance for most public-facing IU websites.\nDynamic applications and web services in AppKube follow approved workflows, security reviews, and DevOps practices.\nEnabling better resource optimization\nSiteKube can scale more efficiently for the management of thousands of WCMS sites.\nAppKube dedicates more advanced resources to applications that need them without compromising the performance and management of simpler sites.\nCommon questions\nWhy can't everything just go on one system?\nDifferent types of sites have different security and technical needs. Separating static sites from dynamic applications lets administrators optimize the security, performance, and management of each.\nIsn't the static environment too restrictive?\nThe restrictions are intentional. Static sites often don’t need server-side code or databases (or use them minimally), so keeping them tightly controlled reduces risk without limiting necessary features.\nWhy do I have to use the WCMS for my static site?\nRestricting publishing to the WCMS ensures that only authorized users can write to the server. It also helps ensure that content managers are using IU's most security and accessibility focused tools for content management, such as the IU Web Framework.\nWhy can't I just use PHP includes or database calls like before?\nAllowing cross-site includes, database calls, or filesystem code on all sites increases security risk. SiteKube is designed for safer, more static content, while AppKube allows common developer\ncustomization options\n.\nCan I move my old Sitehost site exactly as-is?\nStatic content can often be migrated with minimal changes. Dynamic functionality will need to be reviewed to see if it belongs in SiteKube or AppKube. For more, see\nMigrate from Sitehost to SiteKube.\nIf you have further questions, contact\nwebhelp@iu.edu\n.	KB0026760	KB0026760 v3.0	 compliance and auditing Enabling better resource optimization Common questions Overview <strong>IU</strong> has, accessible content management tools (such as the <strong>IU</strong> Web Framework). Providing flexibility where needed AppKube allows more <strong>complex</strong> functionality (databases, APIs, cron jobs) that SiteKube sites often	11.859		2026-01-31 16:57:18.507002	2026-01-31 16:57:18.507002	0	f9c5b8854732f694d500b87b716d4374
10951	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7710bcbf476456583d373803846d4319	SQL example statements for retrieving data from a table	Overview\nSELECT statements\nExamples\nLearning more about SQL\nOverview\nStructured Query Language (SQL) is a specialized language for updating, deleting, and requesting information from\ndatabases\n. SQL is an\nANSI\nand\nISO\nstandard, and is the de facto standard database query language. A variety of established database products support SQL, including products from Oracle and Microsoft SQL Server. It is widely used in both industry and academia, often for enormous, complex databases.\nIn a distributed database system, a program often referred to as the database's "back end" runs constantly on a server, interpreting data files on the server as a standard relational database. Programs on client computers allow users to manipulate that data, using tables, columns, rows, and fields. To do this, client programs send SQL statements to the server. The server then processes these statements and returns result sets to the client program.\nSELECT statements\nAn SQL\nSELECT\nstatement retrieves records from a database table according to clauses (for example,\nFROM\nand\nWHERE\n) that specify criteria. The syntax is:\nSELECT column1, column2 FROM table1, table2 WHERE column2='value';\nIn the above SQL statement:\nThe\nSELECT\nclause specifies one or more columns to be retrieved; to specify multiple columns, use a comma and a space between column names. To retrieve all columns, use the wild card\n*\n(an asterisk).\nThe\nFROM\nclause specifies one or more tables to be queried. Use a comma and space between table names when specifying multiple tables.\nThe\nWHERE\nclause selects only the rows in which the specified column contains the specified value. The value is enclosed in single quotes (for example,\nWHERE last_name='Vader'\n).\nThe semicolon (\n;\n) is the statement terminator. Technically, if you're sending only one statement to the back end, you don't need the statement terminator; if you're sending more than one, you need it. It's best practice to include it.\nNote:\nSQL is not case sensitive (for example,\nSELECT\nis the same as\nselect\n). For better readability, some programmers use uppercase for commands and clauses, and lowercase for everything else.\nExamples\nFollowing are examples of SQL\nSELECT\nstatements:\nTo select all columns from a table (\nCustomers\n) for rows where the\nLast_Name\ncolumn has\nSmith\nfor its value, you would send this\nSELECT\nstatement to the server back end:\nSELECT * FROM Customers WHERE Last_Name='Smith';\nThe server back end would reply with a result set similar to this:\n+---------+-----------+------------+\n| Cust_No | Last_Name | First_Name |\n+---------+-----------+------------+\n| 1001    | Smith     | John       |\n| 2039    | Smith     | David      |\n| 2098    | Smith     | Matthew    |\n+---------+-----------+------------+\n3 rows in set (0.05 sec)\nTo return only the\nCust_No\nand\nFirst_Name\ncolumns, based on the same criteria as above, use this statement:\nSELECT Cust_No, First_Name FROM Customers WHERE Last_Name='Smith';\nThe subsequent result set might look like:\n+---------+------------+\n| Cust_No | First_Name |\n+---------+------------+\n| 1001    | John       |\n| 2039    | David      |\n| 2098    | Matthew    |\n+---------+------------+\n3 rows in set (0.05 sec)\nTo make a\nWHERE\nclause find inexact matches, add the pattern-matching operator\nLIKE\n. The\nLIKE\noperator uses the\n%\n(percent symbol) wild card to match zero or more characters, and the underscore (\n_\n) wild card to match exactly one character. For example:\nTo select the\nFirst_Name\nand\nNickname\ncolumns from the\nFriends\ntable for rows in which the\nNickname\ncolumn contains the string "brain", use this statement:\nSELECT First_Name, Nickname FROM Friends WHERE Nickname LIKE '%brain%';\nThe subsequent result set might look like:\n+------------+------------+\n| First_Name | Nickname   |\n+------------+------------+\n| Ben        | Brainiac   |\n| Glen       | Peabrain   |\n| Steven     | Nobrainer  |\n+------------+------------+\n3 rows in set (0.03 sec)\nTo query the same table, retrieving all columns for rows in which the\nFirst_Name\ncolumn's value begins with any letter and ends with "en", use this statement:\nSELECT * FROM Friends WHERE First_Name LIKE '_en';\nThe result set might look like:\n+------------+------------+-----------+\n| First_Name | Last_Name  | Nickname  |\n+------------+------------+-----------+\n| Ben        | Smith      | Brainiac  |\n| Jen        | Peters     | Sweetpea  |\n+------------+------------+-----------+\n2 rows in set (0.03 sec)\nIf you used the\n%\nwild card instead (for example,\n'%en'\n) in the example above, the result set might look like:\n+------------+------------+-----------+\n| First_Name | Last_Name  | Nickname  |\n+------------+------------+-----------+\n| Ben        | Smith      | Brainiac  |\n| Glen       | Jones      | Peabrain  |\n| Jen        | Peters     | Sweetpea  |\n| Steven     | Griffin    | Nobrainer |\n+------------+------------+-----------+\n4 rows in set (0.05 sec)\nLearning more about SQL\nTo learn more about SQL programming, Indiana University students, faculty, and staff can\ndownload materials for self-study from IT Training.\nFor the general public, various online tutorials are available, such as the\nw3schools.com SQL Tutorial\n.	KB0025233	KB0025233 v1.0	 is an ANSI and ISO standard, and is the de facto standard <strong>database</strong> query language. A variety of established <strong>database</strong> products support SQL, including products from Oracle and Microsoft SQL Server. It is widely used in both industry and academia, often for enormous, <strong>complex</strong> databases. In a distributed	10.3546		2026-01-31 16:57:23.688676	2026-01-31 16:57:23.688676	0	7710bcbf476456583d373803846d4319
10953	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b2a2e19e475a3e942fc6a55b416d43f9	Migrate from Sitehost to SiteKube	Table of contents\nOverview\nPublish your site to SiteKube\nHandling remaining Sitehost content\nSitehost.iu.edu URL retirement\nSitehost vs. SiteKube feature comparison\nTroubleshoot your SiteKube migration\nWhat can I NOT do on SiteKube?\nCheck for broken images and links\nFix PHP include errors\nBlank or white pages\nRestore missing CSS or JavaScript\nExternal forms or integrations not working\nGeneral tips\nHow do I know if my site is ready for production?\nWhat do I do about old Sitehost URLs?\nCan I get my old files and data from Sitehost, MachForm, or MySQL?\nOverview\nThis article explains the process for moving from Sitehost to SiteKube. For more about SiteKube's capabilities, see\nAbout SiteKube\n.\nInitial migration to test environment\nUITS will publish WCMS content to the SiteKube test environment.\nTest sites will be accessible at:\nhttps://\n<accountname>\n.sites-test.iu.edu\nSites will need to be reviewed by site administrators and stakeholders.\nSite review and QA\nSite managers must review their test sites for functionality and completeness.\nMany WCMS sites currently have external dependencies and may not function fully.\nSite administrators must work within the WCMS to bring their sites to a production-ready state.\nApproval and publish to production\nUITS is finalizing the production SiteKube environment. Once it is available, you will be notified and can publish your site to production directly through the WCMS.\nIf a site uses only WCMS content, it may be ready for production after minimal QA.\nResources\nRequest a WCMS site\nTransition your Machform to enterprise tools\nPublish your site to SiteKube\nIn order to publish to SiteKube, you will need to update your\n.htaccess\nfile. For instructions, see\nUpdate .htaccess when moving from Sitehost to SiteKube\n(requires IU login).\nHandling remaining Sitehost content\nFor remaining content on Sitehost (apps, databases, files), you may either:\nTransfer to\nWCMS\nMove to\nAppKube\nSitehost.iu.edu URL retirement\nNote:\nIf your site previously used a production URL in the format\nusername\n.sitehost.iu.edu\n, it will need to be transitioned to a new URL.\nWe are in the process of identifying appropriate replacement domains, and site stewards will be contacted directly with a recommended new URL for their site.\nSitehost vs. SiteKube feature comparison\nCompare Sitehost and SiteKube features\nFeature\nSitehost compatibility\nSiteKube compatibility\nPHP version\nPHP 8.1, 8.2 (default), 8.3\n8.2, 8.3\nApache version\n2.4\n2.4\nVirtual host required\nNo\nYes\n.htaccess file management\nOn Sitehost server\nPublished from WCMS\nCMS compatibility\nNo limitations\nWCMS (Cascade) only\nWeb apps allowed\nYes\nNo\nServer-side integrations with other accounts, databases, and servers\nYes\nNo\nTroubleshoot your SiteKube migration\nWhen you review your test site on SiteKube, you might find that some pages or features aren't working the way they did on Sitehost. Below are some common issues you can check and fix yourself.\nWhat can I NOT do on SiteKube?\nSSH/SFTP into the server\nConnect to external databases or APIs directly from PHP\nInclude PHP files from other IU group accounts or sites\nUse symlinks to other sites' content\nRun cron jobs\nUse certain PHP functions\n(IU Login required to view article).\nCheck for broken images and links\nProblem:\nImages or links don't load or show errors.\nSteps to fix:\nMake sure all images and pages were published to SiteKube from the WCMS. Republish missing images and pages to SiteKube.\nCheck that links use relative paths (\n/images/my-image.webp\n) instead of hard-coded URLs (\nhttps://mysite.iu.edu/images/my-image.webp\n).\nIf you see old URLs (like\nusername.sitehost.iu.edu\n), update them to use relative paths (if linking to image or page on your site) or the virtual host (if linking to image or page on another IU site).\nCheck all external link assets in the WCMS to ensure URLs have been updated to the correct domains (or pointed to temporary alternative URLs if the original link hasn’t been migrated to SiteKube).\nIf links in a shared/attached section are pointing to the wrong URLs or leading to 404 pages, make sure that shared section’s link rewriting behavior is set to\nSite-relative\nunder the\nEdit\n>\nConfigure\ntab.\nFix PHP include errors\nProblem:\nYou see errors like\n"Failed to open stream"\nor blank areas where includes should load.\nWhy:\nThe PHP include path has changed in SiteKube. In addition, SiteKube blocks PHP includes from one account into another.\nSteps to fix:\nMake sure your\n.htaccess\nfile has been correctly updated; see\nUpdate .htaccess when moving from Sitehost to SiteKube\n(requires IU login).\nUpdate any includes that used\n/groups/accountname/web/\nto use\n/var/www/html/\n.\nIf you have PHP files that are shared across multiple sites, move copies of those PHP files into each individual site and include those instead.\nCheck that your included PHP files are present in your site's\n/_php\nfolder in WCMS. Be sure to publish those PHP files to SiteKube.\nCheck that your PHP files aren’t using any of the PHP functions that have been disabled on SiteKube; see\nPHP functions disabled on SiteKube\n(requires IU login).\nBlank or white pages\nProblem:\nEntire page is blank or partially missing content.\nWhy:\nPHP errors can stop pages from rendering.\nSteps to fix:\nCheck that all PHP includes are updated to use the correct paths (see previous section).\nRemove any includes that try to load files from other IU accounts or old systems—cross-site includes are blocked (see previous section).\nMake sure your page doesn't depend on any databases or external server connections (which SiteKube now blocks).\nIf the PHP script was fetching information from a database, consider replacing it with static content, a folder of page assets in the WCMS with an index block, or FireForm.\nFor complex integrations, plan to move the server-side code to AppKube (IU’s environment for more advanced apps).\nInformation about AppKube is forthcoming.\nRestore missing CSS or JavaScript\nProblem:\nStyles look broken or interactive elements (like menus, accordions) don't work.\nWhy:\nYour site might be linking to CSS or JS files that weren’t copied over.\nSteps to fix:\nOpen your browser’s Developer Tools (usually by right-clicking and selecting\nInspect\n) and go to the\nNetwork\ntab.\nRefresh the page and look for any 404 errors for CSS or JS files.\nUpload the missing CSS files to your site's\n/css\nfolder in the WCMS and publish them.\nUpload missing JS files to your\n/js\nfolder in the WCMS and publish them.\nUpdate your HTML or Velocity includes to use the local copies.\nMake sure "Brand Version" in the "Settings" block is set to\n3.3.x\n(Framework sites only).\nExternal forms or integrations not working\nProblem:\nForms or tools that used to connect to databases or other systems don’t work.\nWhy:\nSiteKube doesn’t allow server-side connections to external systems or databases.\nSteps to fix:\nIf the form or integration was pulling live data, consider replacing it with static content, a folder of page assets in the WCMS with an index block, or FireForm; see\nTransition your MachForm to enterprise tools\n.\nFor complex integrations, plan to move the server-side code to AppKube (IU's environment for more advanced apps).\nInformation about AppKube is forthcoming.\nGeneral tips\nMake sure your site is correctly set up for\npublishing to SiteKube for the first time\n.\nAlways republish your page or folder in WCMS after making changes.\nKeep all required assets (images, CSS, JS, PHP includes) in your site's WCMS folders.\nRemember that SiteKube only supports WCMS-managed sites—no direct server access or database connections.\nHow do I know if my site is ready for production?\nMake sure:\nAll images, CSS, JavaScript, and PHP includes are present in WCMS.\nPages display correctly in the SiteKube test URL.\nNavigation works with no broken links.\nForms or integrations are replaced or updated for SiteKube compatibility.\nIf you're not sure, involve your site's stakeholders to help with review and testing. You can also\ncontact the Support Center\n.\nWhat do I do about old Sitehost URLs?\nAny hard-coded links to\naccountname.sitehost.iu.edu\nneed to be updated. (In general, hard-coded links to sitehost.iu.edu were discouraged, so there should not be many of these links.)\nUse relative paths when possible so your site works in test and production. If you need to link to images or pages on another site, use their virtual host URL (the URL\nwithout\nsitehost in it).\nCan I get my old files and data from Sitehost, MachForm, or MySQL?\nYes—learn more about\nrequesting files and data from your old site or database\n.	KB0026740	KB0026740 v13.0	. For instructions, see Update .htaccess when moving from Sitehost to SiteKube (requires <strong>IU</strong> login). Handling Connect to external databases or APIs directly from PHP Include PHP files from other <strong>IU</strong> group accounts or sites Use symlinks to other sites' content Run cron jobs Use certain PHP functions (<strong>IU</strong> Login	9.105		2026-01-31 16:57:28.022437	2026-01-31 16:57:28.022437	0	b2a2e19e475a3e942fc6a55b416d43f9
10968	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ed20309747dab610d500b87b716d4381	Service agreement for the Windows Hosting Environment	Table of contents\nOverview and definitions\nChanges as of 2026\nImportant information at a glance\nAgreements\nGuidelines\nFees\nAccess control\nOverview and definitions\nThe\nWindows Hosting Environment\n(\nWHE\n) enables clients to use desired technologies not available in any other hosting environment provided by University Information Technology Services (UITS), for as low a cost as possible, while maintaining an acceptable level of performance, reliability, and security. Applications requiring other levels of performance, reliability, or security must be hosted elsewhere.\nThe unique features of this hosting environment are a Microsoft IIS Web Server with the ability to serve dynamic web pages written using Adobe ColdFusion, Microsoft .NET Framework 4.8+, and Microsoft .NET 8 programming languages.\nWHE\nalso provides a Microsoft SQL Server database environment. The web pages and database servers can be used with resources outside of this environment.\nThe\nWindows Hosting Environment\nis available to departments and affiliates on all campuses of Indiana University and student organizations with a full-time faculty or staff sponsor. The clients develop and maintain the applications, and University Information Technology Services provides the hardware, software, and systems administration.\nThis environment is provided by the UITS Enterprise Windows and Azure Administration group. The remainder of this document describes the environment, and the relationship between the service provider and the service clients.\nTerminology:\n"Client" refers to the consumer of the services provided by this environment. A client is the organizational unit and all associated individuals served.\n"Provider" or "Service Provider" refers to the University Information Technology Services groups and personnel responsible for providing some aspect of this environment.\n"Workspace" refers to the total scope of a client's use of this environment. A workspace is comprised of all file and database space provided to a client.\n"Project" refers to a sub-grouping of resources within a workspace. A "Workspace" can have one or more "Projects". File and database space are associated with a "Project". The primary reason for multiple "Projects" is security–to compartmentalize access to files and data to different groups of people.\nWHE\n:\nWindows Hosting Environment\nChanges as of 2026\nUpgrades and migrations\nColdFusion 2023 is available.\nMaintenance windows\nColdFusion and .NET environments:\nWindows security updates will be applied to non-production systems on the Wednesday prior to the third Sunday of the month between 6pm and midnight. For production server environments, updates will occur on the third Sunday between midnight and 8am.\nProduction environments:\nMaintenance windows will be scheduled on Thursdays between 7-9am or Sundays between midnight and 8am.\nNon-production environments:\nMaintenance windows may occur at any time; WebTech will post a notice on\nwebhost.iu.edu\nif these maintenance periods are likely to affect availability of the stage servers.\nImportant information at a glance\nFees\nThe\nWindows Hosting Environment\nis a baseline service. There are no fees for internal departments to use the shared hosting environment. However, there may be fees for additional services such as file restorations and resources such as disk space. For more information, see the\nFees section\n.\nSite and application requirements\nThe client is required to maintain a developer for each project. Code and databases that cannot be maintained to respond to environmental changes will be removed.\nWeb applications and sites cannot create or upload files to the project content directories. If a web application needs a location to create and write files, the client must request a Storage Share location from the\nWHE\nadministrators.\nAll websites should include a privacy notice. The notice needs to be updated or reviewed at least every two years. For more information, see\nPrivacy Notice Generator\n.\nUITS\nWHE\nreserves the right to end service to any application that:\nViolates any IU policy or this service agreement\nFails to match environmental upgrade deadlines\nAdversely affects the performance of other users\nCompliance and data requirements\nTo host\nCritical or Restricted data\nand protected data types in the\nWindows Hosting Environment\n, the owner of the data and UITS\nWHE\nneeds to grant approval.\nCritical data\nwill require the approval of the IU\nData Stewards\nbefore hosting in\nWHE\n. Owners of\nWHE\n-hosted sites and databases with\nCritical data\n,\nRestricted data\n, or protected data will be required to sign the service agreement annually.\nIf your site has HIPAA requirements, notify\nWHE\nadministrators and describe your use case. The\nWindows Hosting Environment\nsatisfies certain requirements of the HIPAA security rule, but can only be used with protected health information (PHI) if you have the complementary safeguards in place. For more information, review\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n. If the PHI is part of research and you have questions how best to secure it, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nWHE\ndoes\nnot\nmeet compliance for Payment Card Industry Data Security Standards (PCI DSS) 0 SAQ A-EP. Merchant questionnaires submitted with the assistance of\nWHE\nwill note areas where policy is not met.\nOutages\nIf you have a problem with the\nWHE\nthat is not covered by any of the notices on\nStatus.IU\n, refer to the\nEmergency communications section\n.\nAll system-wide\nWHE\noutages will be posted to the Webhost Admin Portal at\nhttps://webhost.iu.edu\n. We will follow up by sending email to all developers listed in the contacts section of your project. It is up to our clients to make certain this list is up to date for efficient communications. If the\nWebhost Admin Portal\nis not available, see\nStatus.IU\n.\nAgreements\nService provider agreements\nThe service provider agrees to:\nProvide a\nWindows Hosting Environment\n24 hours a day, 7 days a week, except for maintenance and emergencies. See the\nGuidelines\nsection for further information about scheduled and emergency maintenance.\nProvide a full range of support resources. The service provider responsibilities are divided between the Enterprise Web Technical Services (WebTech)\nWindows Hosting Environment\n(\nWHE\n) team, Enterprise Windows and Azure Administration Enterprise Server Administration (EWA), Storage and Virtualization (SAV), and Support Center (SC) groups within University Information Technology Services (UITS).\nAdditionally:\nUITS\nWHE\nis the service owner and primary contact for all clients. UITS\nWHE\nwill introduce new clients to the environment and provide general information to existing clients.\nThe EWA group is the database administrator for this environment. The EWA group will install, configure, and maintain the stage and production database software.\nThe SAV group will provide virtual servers and allocate additional virtual resources as needed.\nClient agreements\nThe client agrees to:\nSecure projects and workspace data:\nFrom the\nWHE\nAdmin portal, the client must ensure Contact and Active Directory membership lists are up to date.\nInform UITS\nWHE\nbefore you use the environment for\nCritical data\n,\nRestricted data\n, or protected data. For information about data classifications, see\nClassification levels of institutional data\nand\nAbout sensitive data at IU\n. Sites that access or store HIPAA data require additional security measures. PCI DSS compliance is not met on the\nWHE\n.\nMaintain project workspace in accordance with the terms of this agreement:\nThe\nWindows Hosting Environment\n(\nWHE\n) is shared by many clients. The configuration of all applications is standardized to provide a maintainable and acceptable level of performance, reliability, and security. The client may request deviations from the standard, but the provider reserves the right to deny any request for customization.\nAll client applications must not adversely affect other client applications.\nIf a client application adversely affects other client applications, the provider may remove the offending client application from the service.\nMaintain project workspace to be compatible with environment upgrades:\nThe client is responsible for implementing any changes necessary to make their application function in the patched, updated, or upgraded environment.\nThe client is required to maintain a developer for each project\n. Projects that cannot be maintained to respond to environmental changes will be removed.\nThe client is required to patch, modify, and edit code to bring it to a working state.\nIn some cases, environmental changes will make previously working code fail to execute or display properly. The\nWHE\nadministrators may be able to assist with additional logs, error codes, etc.\nIf an environmental upgrade or policy change forces a change of hostname, server operating system, database version, or programming language, the service provider will give a timeline for this change. The timeline will be reasonable but nonnegotiable.\nUse the following procedures to make changes:\nUITS\nWHE\nis composed of stage and production environments. Best practice is to develop and adequately test in the staging environments before moving changes to the production area. Only tested code should be applied to the production area.\nFor timely production deployments for web applications and databases,\nWHE\nadministrators must be given ample notice by sending email to\nwebhost@iu.edu\n. Failure to plan a new deployment could result in a delayed launch date.\nEach time a new site is deployed or code is updated, the client must perform a web application security scan in the stage environment before the application will be placed into production. For instructions on how to request a scan, see\nAbout vulnerability scanners\nSignificant changes to the production environment (database or file content) should be preempted by an additional web application vulnerability scan against modified test data.\nOther changes can be made by submitting a request to\nwebhost@iu.edu\n. Requests must clearly specify the changes desired. The actual completion date and time will be determined upon review of each individual request.\nThe client must notify the\nWHE\nadministrators of any anticipated "significant" changes in usage, such as a dramatic user base increase or the need for a dramatic increase in disk space, in order to ensure the environment has the resources to accommodate the changes.\nImportant:\nFailure to meet these expectations will result in actions necessary to eliminate the resulting problem or risk. In most cases this will result in a site or database being taken offline until security concerns are met, or until an alternative hosting location is identified.\nGuidelines\nInitiate service\nFor projects that include a web presence, you must submit the\nWebsite Request form\nbefore sending a request to WebTech. Submissions to this form will return a ServiceNow ticket number; include this number in your request to WebTech. For database-only projects, you don't need to submit this form.\nSubmit all new requests to\nWHE\nby sending mail to\nwebhost@iu.edu\nor filling out the\nNew Site Request form\n.\nInclude a proposed name for the client, project, and the Network ID and email address for the primary contact.\nAdditional information that you can share, such as your additional contacts, your programming language, a description of the project function, and the project's\ndata classification\nwill speed up the initial process.\nDevelopment workspaces are available within a week.\nEnd service\nThe client may end their use of the service at any time.\nUITS\nWHE\nreserves the right to end service to any application that violates any IU policy or this service agreement, fails to match environmental upgrade deadlines, or adversely affects the performance of other users.\nWhen a client ends their use of the service, the provider will archive all files on the production file share and a complete backup of the production database; a copy of the archive will be made available to the client upon request. The provider will keep a copy of the archived project for one year.\nEmergency communications\nThe current status of UITS systems and services is available at\nStatus.IU\n. If a client is having a problem with a\nWHE\napplication that is not covered by any notices on\nStatus.IU\nor the\nWHE\nAdmin Portal, follow the guidelines below:\nIn case of an emergency, the client must first consult their developer to resolve the problem.\nIf the problem cannot be resolved by the client's developer, then the client may contact\nwebhost@iu.edu\nfor assistance. The problem will be investigated during normal business hours; if the problem is reported outside of normal business hours, it will be investigated the next business day.\nIf a problem with production is identified outside of business hours, then clients or users may contact their campus\nSupport Center\n. If immediate elevation is required, developers may report the problem to\nDC Operations\n. The Support Center or Operations will report the problem to the system administrators, who will only be able to perform high level functions such as restarting a problematic server. Do not contact the Support Center or Operations for problems with a stage environment outside of business hours; instead, email\nwebhost@iu.edu\nand the problem will be addressed the next business day.\nWhen clients or users report a problem to\nwebhost@iu.edu\n, Operations, or the Support Center, the following information will be needed:\nThat the application is part of the\nWindows Hosting Environment\nThe name of the application\nThe name, email address, and phone number of whom to contact during troubleshooting\nA description of the problem, including details such as:\nThe complete URL to the problem page\nThe UNC path to the share containing the file with the problem\nThe server and database name (if the problem is a database connection)\nA description of the aberrant behaviors\nRefer your users to\nReport a problem in the Windows Hosting Environment\nfor instructions for user problem reporting for the\nWindows Hosting Environment\n.\nAll system-wide\nWHE\noutages will be posted to\nStatus.IU\n. UITS\nWHE\nwill follow up with an email to all developers listed in the contacts section of your project as soon as possible. It is up to our clients to make certain this list is up to date for efficient communications.\nVirus protection\nClients are responsible for ensuring their files do not contain viruses, malware, or other code insecurities.\nThe service provider can provide a log of file share access failures.\nThe service provider may periodically scan files for viruses.\nData backup\nUITS SAV's CommVault service is used for backups and restores.\nFiles are backed up as detailed in\nVirtual Server Hosting Services (Intelligent Infrastructure): Service Level Expectations\n). Content/storage file share backups are kept for 30 days.\n"AllDisk" backups will be performed on Virtual Machines (VMs) as defined in\nData Protection Services (DPS) backups and VM disk tagging\n.\nClients with backup needs beyond what SAV and EWA provide are responsible for ensuring those needs are met.\nDatabase backups\nA full backup of each SQL database is performed daily and written to a file server.\nA transaction log database backup of each SQL Server database is performed every 10 minutes for production databases and every hour for non-production databases. Log backups are written to a file server.\nProduction backups are kept for 30 days. Non-production backups are kept for 14 days. All backups are stored on an EWA file server. Additionally, two days' worth of backups are kept locally on the database server.\nClients with backup needs beyond what default SQL Server backups offer are responsible for ensuring those needs are met. Additional backup and retention accommodations may result in additional fees.\nDatabase requests\nTo restore a backup of a client database, send a request to\nwebhost@iu.edu\nwith the source database server name, the date of the backup to be used, and the target database server of the backup. A database restore will replace the target database.\nDue to the complexity of some database designs, it may not always be possible to move individual objects from one\nWHE\ndatabase to another (for example, if a table has multiple dependencies) as these moves are extremely time-consuming and can place the client's data at risk. In these cases, the client is required to provide a script for reassigning any special attributes the object may possess (most commonly identity, foreign keys, triggers, and/or primary keys).\nWHE\nadministrators will work with EWA to perform all backups and restores.\nData restoration\nSend requests to restore files and databases to\nwebhost@iu.edu\n. Provide the server name, database name, or file name and path. Include the last "known good" date.\nScheduled jobs\nAll client scheduled job requests in Windows and ColdFusion must be reviewed, accepted, and set up by the\nWHE\nadministrators. All jobs will need to be scheduled to provide optimal server performance. The client and\nWHE\nadministrators will agree on job times.\nThis also applies to SQL Server Integration Services (SSIS). If a resource-intensive SSIS package running on a weekday after 8am is negatively impacting the\nWHE\nenvironment, it may be terminated at the service provider's discretion. The service provider will make every effort to contact the affected client before or immediately after the SSIS package has been terminated.\nSQL jobs and SSIS packages in\nWHE\nmust adhere to a naming convention specific to the environment. To learn more about setting up SSIS packages and SQL jobs, contact\nwebhost@iu.edu\n.\nScheduled maintenance\nColdFusion and .NET environments: Windows security updates to non-production server environments will occur on the Wednesday prior to the third Sunday of the month between 6pm and midnight ET. For production server environments, updates will occur on the third Sunday between midnight and 8am. Exceptions in timing may occur if UISO identifies a vulnerability requiring expedited patching. The servers or affected services will be down for only the amount of time necessary for the updates.\nOther maintenance to production environments will occur on Thursdays between 7am and 9am ET, or Sundays between midnight and 8am ET.\nOther maintenance to non-production environments may occur at any time. Whenever possible,\nWHE\nwill announce these maintenance periods in advance if they are likely to affect availability of the stage servers.\nNon-Windows updates will be announced at least three days in advance to affected\nWHE\nclients. Any maintenance likely to affect the availability of production servers will be performed outside of normal business hours if possible. Whenever possible, these updates will be applied in advance to stage environments to allow time for testing.\nNon-scheduled production maintenance activity will be listed at\nStatus.IU\nand the Webhost Admin Portal, and in some instances will be presented at UITS Change Management.\nEmergency maintenance\nEmergency maintenance will occur as needed. The first priority is preventing service loss or restoring service. Consequently, emergency maintenance may be performed without advance notice to clients. Clients will be notified of the emergency maintenance as soon as possible, before or after as the situation allows.\nA system administrator is on call 24 hours a day, 7 days a week for production environments. During an emergency, the system administrator will give their best effort to restore service. However, there is no guaranteed response or recovery time during an emergency.\nProblems and emergency maintenance activity will be listed at\nStatus.IU\n.\nVirtual host names and data encryption\nVirtual host names and SSL certificates are available upon request. The client is responsible for obtaining approval for domain names. For the DNS policies, see\nDNS policies at IU\n.\nBy default, all volumes at rest and network communications between servers are encrypted.\nWeb application vulnerability scans\nWindows Hosting Environment\nwebsites must be scanned by the UISO web vulnerability scanner. A scan must also be done to any new sites or major revisions, or annually. Clients will be responsible for requesting the scan and working with UISO to resolve any vulnerabilities. For instructions to request a scan, see\nAbout vulnerability scanners\n.\nImportant:\nUITS recommends doing all security scans in a stage environment outside of business hours and pre-coordinating with UITS\nWHE\n. If a database refresh is needed before or after a scan, send the request to\nwebhost@iu.edu\n.\nFees\nThere is no fee for shared web hosting.\nAdditional disk space fees:\nAll workspaces include 10 GB of disk space. The space includes:\nScript, image, and data files on file share\nDatabase data and log files\nDevelopment, test, and production\nFor a list of costs for additional disk space, see the "Disk Storage" and "Data Backup" sections in\nUITS Rates for Direct-Bill Services\n.\nCharges for additional file spaces are based on average monthly usage during the previous fiscal year.\nAccess control\nContacts and client access to the environment\nThe client must designate at least one individual as a contact for all matters related to use of this service. The provider will only act on requests made or approved by a designated contact.\nThe client must provide a developer contact for all projects.\nThe client is completely responsible for controlling access to their workspace.\nThe client is responsible for ensuring anyone with access to their workspace understands their responsibilities stated in this service agreement.\nThe client is responsible for developing and maintaining their content for the entire application lifecycle.\nThe client is responsible for adapting their application to changes to the environment, such as software and hardware updates and upgrades.\nNTFS and database access is controlled by Active Directory (ADS) domain groups. The groups have the following naming convention:\nADS\\IU-WH-Clientname-ProjectName_Developers\nThe Project ADS group has modify permission on the file content and storage (NTFS) directories.\nWeb applications and sites cannot create or upload files to the code content directories. If a web application needs a location to create and write files, the client must request a Storage Share location from\nWHE\nadministrators. Space requirements will be discussed at the time of the request.\nCritical data\nmay not be stored on the\nWHE\nStorage Shares without prior approval from the provider. This is nonnegotiable; shares that violate this rule will be terminated.\nThe Project ADS group has\ndb_owner\nrole permissions on development and non-critical production database environments.\nThe Project ADS group has\ndb_datareader\nand\ndb_datawriter\nroles on the critical production database servers. Production databases with a\nCritical data\nclassification can only have permissions elevated for four hours.\nThe project\n-Owner\nlocal SQL login will have\ndb_datareader\nand\ndb_datawriter\npermissions only in all environments.\nThe client may request other groups, permissions, or database server logins as needed.\nWebsite and virtual directory settings\nEach web application will have its own application pool running as a Group Managed Service Account (gMSA) managed by UITS\nWHE\n.\nYou may request other website and virtual directory settings as needed.\nIU Login\nUITS\nWHE\nsupports IU Login and CAS or SAML protocols for limiting access to web pages. See\nIntegrate IU Login with a web application\n.\nUITS\nWHE\nmay be able to provide code samples for popular security procedures upon request.	KB0025891	KB0025891 v4.0	. WHE also provides a Microsoft SQL Server <strong>database</strong> environment. The web pages and <strong>database</strong> servers of a client's use of this environment. A workspace is comprised of all file and <strong>database</strong> space provided" can have one or more "Projects". File and <strong>database</strong> space are associated with a "Project". The primary	7.8705	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 16:57:31.486827	2026-01-31 16:57:31.486827	0	ed20309747dab610d500b87b716d4381
2311	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024572	About Tableau at IU	Table of contents\nTableau at IU\nPurchase a desktop license for IU\nRenew a desktop license\nDownload Tableau Desktop\nUpdate Tableau Desktop\nLearn more and get help\nTableau at IU\nOn June 28, 2025, Indiana University upgraded to Tableau version 2025.1.\nTableau is visualization software that helps you view, understand, and analyze your data, no matter how complex. Using Tableau, you can create interactive dashboards and share them securely with others in seconds. IU uses Tableau Desktop for creating reports and Tableau Server for publishing the reports.\nTableau Server is available for production use at IU, providing the publishing platform to store reports and the web layer to view them. Reports security is managed by the Apply Security site where Access Control Management (ACM) groups can be assigned to reports for end-user access.\nUITS\nhas committed to supporting the server as a general data asset analytics and visualization tool. The value of the desktop tool to a unit is greatly enhanced by having a central Tableau Server instance in which to publish work. Once work is published from Tableau Desktop into the server environment, authorized users can access the reports and interactive dashboards built using a standard web browser.\nPurchase a desktop license for IU\nEach Tableau Desktop user is required to have a unique license, which allows them to install the desktop client on at most two devices. You purchase this license as part of Tableau Creator from Salesforce via BUY.IU, and then renew it annually to continue using it. As long as you continue to use Tableau Desktop as part of a Tableau Creator license, you must renew the subscription annually. If you choose to stop paying the subscription, you may continue using the software up to the version available when the subscription was terminated.\nTo purchase the Tableau Desktop client license:\nEmail Marc Drenning at Salesforce (\nmdrenning@salesforce.com\n) to request an order form for Tableau Desktop.\nGo to\nBUY.IU\n.\nIn the left pane of the main BUY.IU page, select\nShop\n, and then\nNon-Catalog Item\n.\nFor more on the non-catalog order process, see\nPlace a Non-Catalog Order\n.\nIn the "Supplier" field, enter\nSalesforce\n, and select the\nSALESFORCE.COM\nentry when it pops up.\nFill out the form. In the "Product Description" field, enter exactly what appears below:\nCreator License - Annual Subscription License\nIn the "Quantity" field, enter\n1\n.\nIn the "Price Estimate" field, enter the dollar amount from the form you received in\nstep 1\n.\nSelect\nSave and Close\n.\nWhen you\nCheckout\nyour shopping cart items, select "External Notes and Attachments". You will now see an "External Note" under the list of products in the requisition.\nTo the right of the "External Note" label, select\nEdit\n. Add the contact name and email address for this license and select\nSave\n.\nUnder "Internal Notes and Attachments", select\nAdd\n. Attach the quote/order form and select\nSave\n.\nOnce the requisition is processed, Purchasing will sign the order form via DocuSign on behalf of the requester.\nTo check the status of your Purchase Order at any time,\ncontact purchasing support\n.\nNote:\nIf you want to try out Tableau Desktop before purchasing a license, Tableau currently offers a 14-day free trial; see\nStart a Free Tableau Trial\n.\nRenew a desktop license\nTo renew a Tableau Desktop Creator license, follow the same steps as purchasing a new license, except email Amanda Falk at Salesforce (\nafalk@salesforce.com\n) to request the order form.\nDownload Tableau Desktop\nTo download the latest maintenance release of Tableau Desktop 2025.1 go to\nTableau Desktop Product Downloads and Release Notes\n, expand\n2025.1\n, select the latest release, and then, under "Download Files", select the Windows or Mac version.\nUpdate Tableau Desktop\nIf you are not using the latest maintenance release of Tableau Desktop 2025.1, you'll be prompted to install it. The maintenance release is denoted by the number after the second decimal point; for example, for version 2025.1.1, the maintenance release is 1. You may install new maintenance releases of the Tableau Desktop client, as long as you remain on a 2025.1.x version of the software.\nLearn more and get help\nImportant:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nIU community:\nFor information about publishing Tableau workbooks to IU's Tableau server, see\nPublish in Tableau\n.\nFor more about using Tableau, see\nTableau Desktop\n. For IT Training materials about Tableau, see\nTableau Training Resources\n.\nTo request help involving Tableau Server at IU, complete the\nEDSD Support Form\n.\nFor technical help with Tableau Desktop or with Tableau licenses,\ncontact Tableau Support\n.	KB0024572	KB0024572 v6.0	Table of contents Tableau at <strong>IU</strong> Purchase a desktop license for <strong>IU</strong> Renew a desktop license Download Tableau Desktop Update Tableau Desktop Learn more and get help Tableau at <strong>IU</strong> On June 28, 2025 and interactive dashboards built using a standard web browser. Purchase a desktop license for <strong>IU</strong> Each	5.5681		2026-01-31 14:35:32.281267	2026-01-31 16:57:39.298581	0	567e9ce03bab6658476d941864e45a37
10981	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ef40b0f347a456583d373803846d4341	About available alternative formats for textbooks at IU	The\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at Indiana University provide a wide range of services for qualifying students, faculty, and staff with disabilities, as well as comprehensive course accessibility evaluation, consulting, presentations, and training for the IU community.\nThe\nATAC\nspecializes in sourcing and creating alternative formatted media that provide the highest levels of accessibility and usability for students with authorized alternative media accommodations.\nInformation about requesting alternative formatted media is available to the IU community below.\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nIU community:\nThe\nATAC\nwill produce alternative formats of instructional materials for students authorized for alternate media accommodations. Alternative media options include but are not limited to:\nAccessible eTexts for interactive reading software/screen-reading software\nBraille and tactile graphics\nLarge print\nTo request alternative formatted textbooks, submit the\nAlternative Format Textbook\nrequest form in the\nIT Accessibility Service Catalog\n. When requesting textbooks, we may request a proof of purchase or ownership. Due to copyright laws, proof of access to the print material must be provided prior to the distribution of books.\nAvailable textbook formats and estimated production times are outlined in the table below. If you need alternative formatted media for coursework, make sure to submit your request early enough to allow for the estimated production times listed. If you don't, the\nATAC\nmay not be able to provide you with materials by the start of the semester.\nNote:\nAll timeframes are estimates. Material complexity, high volumes, and publisher responsiveness can result in longer production times. Also, the\nATAC\nreserves the right to substitute a requested format with a format that is equal to or more accessible than the original requested if readily available.\nTypes of alternative format textbooks and their associated estimated production times\nFormat\nFormat description\nEstimated production time\nEPUB: Simple\nText reflows when enlarged\nFont sizes can be adjusted within most EPUB readers\nText reading order is verified and optimized\nPage numbers are retained for easier navigation\n4 weeks\nLarge print\n18-point font standard; custom options available (increases lead time)\nRetains original textbook form\nPortrait page printed in landscape over two pages top to bottom\n4 weeks\nEPUB: Complex\nText reflows when enlarged\nFont sizes can be adjusted within most EPUB readers\nText reading order is verified and optimized\nFull alternate text descriptions are provided for non-decorative images and diagrams\nMath markup provided using MathML\n8 weeks\nBraille\nThe\nATAC\nonly recommends braille for highly technical materials due to the volume of materials that are produced. Those technical areas include:\nScience\nTechnology\nEngineering\nMath\nForeign Language\nMusic\nBraille materials can also include tactile graphics. Contact your campus\nAccessible Educational Services (AES)\noffice for more details on how braille materials are requested and produced.\nVaries (minimum 10 weeks or longer)\nIf you have any questions about the formats available, or need help requesting them, contact the\nATAC\nat 812-856-4112 or\natac@iu.edu\n.	KB0026237	KB0026237 v1.0	The ATAC specializes in sourcing and creating alternative formatted media that provide the highest levels of accessibility and usability for students with authorized alternative media accommodations. Information about requesting alternative formatted media is available to the <strong>IU</strong> community below.	5.4365		2026-01-31 16:57:43.288368	2026-01-31 16:57:43.288368	0	ef40b0f347a456583d373803846d4341
10982	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=024baa8c4761b2902fc6a55b416d4363	Denodo and REST APIs	Table of contents\nOverview\nPotential use cases for Denodo APIs\nDefault RESTful web service\nCreate an API with Denodo\nConsume Denodo APIs\nLearn more\nOverview\nYou can use Denodo to create simple APIs based off nearly any data source. Instead of architecting an application around a difficult data source, you can build a Denodo API to suit an application. See below for potential use cases and details about creating an API with Denodo.\nPotential use cases for Denodo APIs\nDenodo APIs can be useful if:\nAn app needs a simpler RESTful interface to data, instead of a JDBC or ODBC connection.\nAn app needs to combine data from multiple sources.\nAn app needs data from sources that are difficult to access or parse, such as cloud sources (for example, AWS or Azure), cubes, Hadoop, LDAP, SFTP, Excel files, flat files, or other APIs.\nExisting application APIs do not meet application specifications because of inflexible output format, inability to accept filters or criteria, missing data, or data format issues.\nFollowing are suggested use cases for an API with Denodo:\nUse an API to circumvent native APIs, so data are provided with the proper filter and in the required format.\nUse an API to expose tables as a RESTful API, cutting down on the overhead and complexity of direct database calls while providing a filter and output that meet specifications.\nUse an API to seamlessly union or join an Excel file on NPM or GitHub with relational databases.\nUse an API to query the ADS through LDAP, and return the members of a group or an individual's groups.\nUse an API as a proxy for SFTP transactions.\nDefault RESTful web service\nAll Denodo views are automatically and securely exposed through a default API. Before creating a new RESTful web service, access the default Denodo web service to check whether it is adequate.\nIU community:\nTo access it, use a URL in this format:\nhttps://ebidvt<-environment>.uits.iu.edu/denodo-restfulws/<your_VDB_name>/views/<view_name>\nIn the above example URL:\nReplace\n<-environment>\nwith\n-dev\nfor the DEV environment or\n-stg\nfor the STG environment, or omit\n<-environment>\nfor the PRD environment.\nReplace\n<your_VDB_name>\nwith the appropriate VDB name.\nReplace\n<your_view_name>\nwith the appropriate view name.\nFor example, to access the\nims_translate_i\nview in the\niu_edss\nVDB in the DEV environment, use this URL:\nhttps://ebidvt-dev.uits.iu.edu/denodo-restfulws/iu_edss/views/ims_translate_i\nAlternatively, to access the same view on PRD, use this URL:\nhttps://ebidvt.uits.iu.edu/denodo-restfulws/iu_edss/views/ims_translate_i\nTo retrieve the information in a different format, without pagination, you can append strings (such as\n?$format=XML\nor\n?$format=JSON\n) to the URL. You also can query the data through the default web service.\nCreate an API with Denodo\nTo create a REST web service in the Development environment using\nDesign Studio\n:\nOn the\nExporer\ntab, hover over the name of the view you want to publish, select the vertical ellipsis (\n), and then select\nNew\n>\nData service\n>\nREST Web service\n:\nThe "Create REST Web service" dialog box will open, and you'll see the following tabs:\nResources\n: Lists the view published by the web service. This tab will be automatically populated with the attributes of the view you selected to create your REST web service.\nSettings\n: Manages the configuration of the REST web service. On this tab, from the "Authentication" drop-down, select\nHTTP Basic with VDP\n, and leave the default configurations for all other fields:\nAdvanced\n: Manages the parameters of the connection between the web service and the Virtual DataPort Server. Accept the default settings on this tab.\nMetadata\n: Accept the default settings on this tab.\nSelect\nSave\n(located above the four tabs).\nThe new web service will not be available until it has been deployed. To deploy it, hover over the name of the web service in the Explorer tab, select the vertical ellipsis (\n), and then select\nDeploy\n:\nAfter you've successfully deployed the service, at the top of the page, use the\nTools\ndrop-down to select\nWeb services container\n:\nIn the "Web Services Container Status" window, locate the name of your web service using the unlabeled drop-down that lists the VDBs that are accessible and the "Name" search box, and then select the associated link in the "Context Path" column:\nNote:\nIf you use the Web Services Container in Production and select the Context Path link, the URL generated will not work; you must remove the colon and port number 9443 from URL for it to work. Also, in Production developers will not be able to use commands in the ellipsis, such as\nDeploy\nor\nUndeploy\n.\nIf prompted, log in with your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nIf HTML is the default format for your service, you'll see a page like the one below.\nTo display the data, select the link under "View name".\nConsume Denodo APIs\nWhen you're presenting data in HTML format, Denodo will paginate the output. Formats other than HTML will not be paginated.\nTo set the format of the data to XML, append the string\n$format=XML\nto the URL:\nhttps://ebidvt-dev.uits.iu.edu/server/iu_edss/ims_translate_rws/views/ims_translate_i?$format=XML\nLearn more\nTo learn more about using Denodo APIs, see:\nHow to create REST Web Services using Virtual DataPort\n, a video from the Denodo Community\nVirtual DataPort Administration Guide\n, particularly\nPublishing REST Web Services\n, from the Denodo Community	KB0022779	KB0022779 v2.0	 tables as a RESTful API, cutting down on the overhead and complexity of direct <strong>database</strong> calls while	5.3594		2026-01-31 16:57:47.431073	2026-01-31 16:57:47.431073	0	024baa8c4761b2902fc6a55b416d4363
10987	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6f00f07f476456583d373803846d4315	Avoid phishing scams	Note:\nTo learn how to spot fake email messages and social engineering scams, visit\nPhishing Education & Training\n. For online courses on phishing and email security, see\nEmail Security Fundamentals\n.\nTable of contents\nPhishing explained\nSpecific types of phishing\nAvoid scams\nWarnings\nReport phishing attempts\nIf you've fallen for a phishing scam\nExample of a phishing scam\nPhishing explained\nPhishing scams are typically fraudulent email messages appearing to come from legitimate enterprises (for example, your university, your\ninternet service provider\n, your bank). These messages usually direct you to a spoofed website or otherwise get you to divulge private information (for example, passphrase, credit card, or other account updates). The perpetrators then use this private information to commit identity theft.\nOne type of phishing attempt is an email message stating that you are receiving it due to fraudulent activity on your account, and asking you to "click here" to verify your information.\nSee an example below.\nPhishing scams are crude social engineering tools designed to induce panic in the reader. These scams attempt to trick recipients into responding or clicking immediately, by claiming they will lose something (for example, email, bank account). Such a claim is always indicative of a phishing scam, as responsible companies and organizations will never take these types of actions via email.\nSpecific types of phishing\nPhishing scams vary widely in terms of their complexity, the quality of the forgery, and the attacker's objective. Several distinct types of phishing have emerged.\nSpear phishing\nPhishing attacks directed at specific individuals, roles, or organizations are referred to as "spear phishing". Since these attacks are so pointed, attackers may go to great lengths to gather specific personal or institutional information in the hope of making the attack more believable and increasing the likelihood of its success.\nThe best defense against spear phishing is to carefully, securely discard information (that is, using a cross-cut shredder) that could be used in such an attack. Further, be aware of data that may be relatively easily obtainable (for example, your title at work, your favorite places, or where you bank), and think before acting on seemingly random requests via email or phone.\nWhaling\nThe term "whaling" is used to describe phishing attacks (usually spear phishing) directed specifically at executive officers or other high-profile targets within a business, government, or other organization.\nAvoid scams\nTo guard against phishing scams, consider the following:\nIndiana University and other reputable organizations will never use email to request that you reply with your password, full Social Security number, or confidential personal information.\nBe suspicious of any email message that asks you to enter or verify personal information, through a website or by replying to the message itself.   Never reply to or click the links in such a message. If you think the message may be legitimate, go directly to the company's website (that is, type the real\nURL\ninto your\nbrowser\n) or contact the company to see if you really do need to take the action described in the email message.\nRead your email as plain text.\nPhishing messages often contain clickable images that look legitimate; by reading messages in plain text, you can see the URLs that any images point to. Additionally, when you allow your\nmail client\nto read\nHTML\nor other non-text-only formatting, attackers can take advantage of your mail client's ability to execute code, which leaves your computer vulnerable to\nviruses\n, worms, and Trojans.\nIf you choose to read your email in HTML format:\nHover your mouse over the links in each email message to display the actual URL. Check whether the hover-text link matches what's in the text, and whether the link looks like a site with which you would normally do business.\nOn an iOS device, tap and hold your finger over a link to display the URL. Unfortunately, Android does not currently support this.\nWhen you recognize a phishing message, first\nreport it as noted below\n, and then delete the email message from your Inbox, and then empty it from the deleted items folder to avoid accidentally accessing the websites it points to.\nTo learn more about guarding against phishing scams, see:\nPhishing Education & Training\nTips to Protect Yourself Against Phishing Scams\n(video)\nWarnings\nReading email as plain text is a general best practice that, while avoiding some phishing attempts, won't avoid them all. Some legitimate sites use redirect scripts that don't check the redirects. Consequently, phishing perpetrators can use these scripts to redirect from legitimate sites to their fake sites.\nAnother tactic is to use a homograph attack, which, due to International Domain Name (IDN) support in modern browsers, allows attackers to use different language\ncharacter sets\nto produce URLs that look remarkably like the authentic ones. See\nDon't Trust Your Eyes or URLs\n.\nReport phishing attempts\nIf the phishing attempt targets IU in any way (for example, it asks for those using IU\nExchange\nto "verify their accounts", includes a malicious PDF directed to university human resources, or impersonates IU or UITS), you have two options for reporting it:\nUse the\nOutlook Report Message add-in\n.\nForward it with\nfull headers\nto\nphishing@iu.edu\n.\nFor instructions on displaying and sending full headers, see\nDisplay and send the full headers of an email message\n.\nNote:\nThe\nUISO\n(University Information Security Office) can take action only if the message asks for IU account credentials or originated from within IU. All other spam should be reported to the appropriate authority below. If the message did originate from within IU, see\nIf you receive spam\n.\nYou can report a phishing scam attempt to the company that is being spoofed.\nYou can also\nsend reports to the Federal Trade Commission (FTC)\n.\nDepending on where you live, some local authorities also accept phishing scam reports.\nFinally, you can send details to the\nAnti-Phishing Working Group\n, which is building a database of common scams to which people can refer.\nFor more about phishing scams, see\nEmail and Phishing Scams\n.\nIf you've fallen for a phishing scam\nFor specifics about what to do if you're a victim of a phishing scam, see\nEmail and Phishing Scams\n.\nExample of a phishing scam\nThe following phishing scam was targeted at the IU community:\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom: "INDIANA.EDU SUPPORT TEAM" <supportteam01@indiana.edu>\nReply-To: "INDIANA.EDU SUPPORT TEAM" <spupportteam@info.lt>\nDate: Sat, 12 Jul 2008 17:42:05 -0400\nTo: <"Undisclosed-Recipient:;"@iocaine.uits.indiana.edu>\nSubject: CONFIRM YOUR ACCOUNT\nDear INDIANA.EDU email Subscriber\nThis mail is to inform all our {INDIANA.EDU} users that we\nwill be maintaining and upgrading our website in a couple of days from\nnow.As a Subscriber you are required to send us your Email account\ndetails to enable us know if you are still making use of your\nmailbox. Be informed that we will be deleting all mail account that is\nnot functioning to enable us create more space for new students and\nstaffs of the school, You are to send your mail account details which\nare as follows:\n*User Name:\n*Password:\n*Date of birth:\nFailure to do this will immediately render your email address deactivated from our database.\nThank you for using INDIANA.EDU\nFROM THE INDIANA.EDU SUPPORT TEAM\n------------------------------------------------------------------	KB0024868	KB0024868 v1.0	. See Don't Trust Your Eyes or URLs. Report phishing attempts If the phishing attempt targets <strong>IU</strong> in any way (for example, it asks for those using <strong>IU</strong> Exchange to "verify their accounts", includes a malicious PDF directed to university human resources, or impersonates <strong>IU</strong> or UITS), you have two options	4.5842		2026-01-31 16:58:17.845912	2026-01-31 16:58:17.845912	0	6f00f07f476456583d373803846d4315
10996	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=84adbd0b3b4f56102b40571864e45a69	About Microsoft Power Platform licenses	Overview\nThere are many licensing options for Microsoft Power Platform products. The information here can help you choose the best option for your needs. To purchase licenses or learn more, see\nPurchase and activate licenses for Microsoft Select Plus titles\n.\nIf you have questions, contact\nEnterprise Windows & Azure Administration\n(\nEWA\n) at\newahelp@iu.edu\n.\nPower BI\nFor Power BI, you may already have the license you need. Below are the licenses for students, faculty, and staff, as well as other licensing options.\nPower BI Free license for students\nStudents have the Power BI Free license, which allows them to publish (but not share) content in "My Workspace". To view content that is published to a workspace, students can use the\nPremium Capacity license\n.\nPower BI Pro license for faculty and staff\nFaculty and staff have a Power BI Pro license that grants access to most features, including publishing to a workspace and sharing content.\nOther Power BI license options\nIf the Power BI licenses for students, faculty, or staff will not meet your needs, other licensing options are available:\nNK5-00001 - Power BI Pro:\nStudents may want this license if they need to publish content to a workspace or view content in a non-premium workspace.\nB1B-00007 - Power BI Premium Per User (PPU):\nYou might use the PPU license if you have a small group of people who need to access premium features (such as paginated reports, XMLA endpoints, and AI capabilities), and you don't want to use a\nPremium Capacity license\n. Note that anyone who needs to\nview\nthese reports also needs a PPU license. A Premium Capacity license is generally better than a PPU license.\nPower BI Premium Capacity:\nThis license has all the premium features and allows large datasets.\nMany SKUs\nhave various RAM and CPU offerings, two of which are:\nGTT-00001 Premium Capacity EM2\nGST-00001 Premium Capacity P1\nPower Platform\nPower Automate:\nFaculty and staff already have the Power Automate license, which lets them connect to basic Microsoft apps such as\nOutlook\n(\nMicrosoft 365\nExchange\n) and SharePoint.\nPower Automate licensing can be complex, but here are some notes to help:\nSFQ-00001 - Per User license:\nThis license allows "cloud flows" to use premium connections, such as connections to databases (for example, SQL and Oracle) and Microsoft Dataverse; it also enables more functionality.\n1SM-00001 - Per User license with RPA (attended):\nThis license is the same as the\nSFQ-00001 license\n, except it does robotic process automation (RPA), also known as a Desktop Flow. You only need RPA if you have a line-of-business app that requires UI automation (that is, you have to automate the app through actions such as clicking a mouse button or typing in text boxes). These flows are "attended", which means they require a human to trigger them.\n1SO-00001 - Unattended RPA:\nThis license is the same as the\n1SM-00001 attended license\n, except it allows an app to run from an agent on its own server that can be fully automated based on other triggers (for example, a schedule, an incoming email, an update to a SharePoint list, etc.). This instance will need to run on a server in the Data Center, and you will also need an attended license while you're developing the solution.\nSFK-00001 - Power Automate Per Flow plan:\nThis license is for cloud flows, not RPA, and it allows specific flows for everyone at IU. If you have a couple of flows with premium connectors, and you need a large number of people to execute them, it may make more sense to buy this license instead of a license for each person.\nCopilot Studio:\nFormerly known as Power Virtual Agents, this is a low-code tool for creating bots. A limited version is included with Microsoft 365 Copilot app licenses, but to take full advantage of this product and publish them for public use, you need the following two SKUs:\nYGB-00001 - Copilot Studio Messages:\nThis subscription gives you 25,000 messages per month. This can be split up between multiple Power Platform Environments; for example, you may want 5,000 assigned to your development environment and 20,000 assigned to your production environment. A billable message is a message sent to the bot triggering an action and/or response. A response using Generative AI consumes two messages, while all other responses consume one.\nSYT-00001 - Copilot Studio User:\nThis license is a $0 license that is required for all developers. When you order the Messages license, you'll need to make sure each developer also has one of these licenses to be able to use Copilot Studio to create your bot.\nPower Apps:\nI21-00001 - Power Apps Per User license:\nThis license allows you to create or execute a Power App that uses premium connections, such as connections to databases (for example, SQL and Oracle) and Microsoft Dataverse. (This special SKU replaced SEK-00001.)\nSER-00001 - Power Apps Per App plan:\nThis license has the same features as the Per User license, but it gives everyone at IU access to the specific app. Generally, it makes more sense to license the\nuser\n, but there may be times when the Per App plan is beneficial.\nNotes:\nA Power Apps license allows execution of a Power Automate cloud flow, if it was called from within the Power App. Often some flows need to happen outside of an app, so you would need both the\nPower Apps Per User license\nand the\nPower Automate Per User license\n.\nBecause the "owner" of a Power App cannot be changed, you should always create Power Apps using a group account. A group account should also have a license to prevent problems in the future.\nTo prevent problems if someone leaves the university, a Power Automate flow should likely be executed as a group account.\nYou should create Power Apps and Power Automate flows for business purposes in their own environment (that is, not the default environment). For more, contact\nEWA\nat\newahelp@iu.edu\n.	KB0024242	KB0024242 v3.0	 can be <strong>complex</strong>, but here are some notes to help: SFQ-00001 - Per User license: This license allows is for cloud flows, not RPA, and it allows specific flows for everyone at <strong>IU</strong>. If you have a couple features as the Per User license, but it gives everyone at <strong>IU</strong> access to the specific app. Generally	4.4738	km-aud-iu	2026-01-31 16:58:21.920351	2026-01-31 16:58:21.920351	0	84adbd0b3b4f56102b40571864e45a69
11055	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=23103cbf476456583d373803846d43a0	Student computing accounts when you transfer campuses, study abroad, or have active military service	Table of contents\nOverview\nTransfer to another IU campus\nStudy abroad or off campus\nActive military service\nOverview\nUse of Indiana University technology resources (such as computers, networks, phones) is restricted to purposes related to the university's mission of education, research, and public service. Access to IU computing resources is granted to all admitted IU students and current or\nretired\nuniversity faculty and staff, in support of their studies, instruction, duties as employees, official business with the university, and other university-sanctioned activities. Under certain circumstances, guests and affiliates of IU are also eligible.\nThe information in this document is intended for IU students who are transferring to another IU campus, leaving IU temporarily to study abroad, or entering active military service.\nFor a summary of different scenarios, see\nOverview of IU computing accounts when students, faculty, or staff leave IU\n.\nTransfer to another IU campus\nIf you have been a student at one IU campus and transfer to another:\nYou will be able to use your\nIU username and passphrase\nto log into IU\nStudent Technology Center\n(STC) computers, and into\nCanvas\n,\nOne.IU\nand other campus websites.\nYou can continue to use your existing email account to send and receive mail.\nStudy abroad or off campus\nIf you will study abroad through the Office of Overseas Study, you do not need to do anything to maintain your accounts.\nIf you have made other arrangements to study abroad or off campus for more than two consecutive semesters, a full-time faculty or staff sponsor could\nsponsor an affiliate account\nfor you.\nActive military service\nIf you are unexpectedly called to active military service, you may be eligible for a military leave of absence, in which case you can retain your active Indiana University computing accounts.\nFor help requesting a military leave of absence, contact your campus Veteran Support Services or Office of the Registrar:\nIU Bloomington\nIU Indianapolis\nIU East\nIU Kokomo\nIU Northwest\nIU South Bend\nIU Southeast\nFor more about military leave at IU Bloomington, see the Office of the Registrar's\nReserves Called to Active Duty Policy\n.	KB0025206	KB0025206 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Transfer to another <strong>IU</strong> campus Study abroad or off campus Active military service Overview The information in this document is intended for <strong>IU</strong> students who are transferring to another <strong>IU</strong> campus, leaving <strong>IU</strong> temporarily to study abroad, or entering active military service	0.8069		2026-01-31 16:58:59.49815	2026-01-31 16:58:59.49815	0	23103cbf476456583d373803846d43a0
11056	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=dcc0f2c747b99a909286dfbd436d43d7	Employee computing accounts when you leave IU	Table of contents\nPrepare\nIf you are called to active military service\nIf you return\nArchival and recovery of\nExchange Online\ndata\nPrepare\nNote:\nThis information does not apply to\nofficial retirees\n.\nFor a summary of different scenarios, see\nOverview of IU computing accounts when students, faculty, or staff leave IU\n.\nAs an Indiana University employee, your computing accounts remain active as long as you remain employed at any IU campus. When your employment at IU ends,\nUITS\nwill disable access to IT resources, although you will retain limited access to\nOne.IU\n.\nIf you also have former student or alumni status at IU, you may\nretain limited access to some student resources\n.\nBe sure to back up all valuable data before your accounts expire. While your accounts are still active, you may want to collect your data from a variety of sources, such as:\nDepartmental storage drives\nCanvas\nEmail:\nSee how to\naccess your Exchange email messages and transfer them to another email mailbox\n. Archival and recovery of\nExchange\ndata must be done within 30 days of an account becoming ineligible for Microsoft 365 licensing, or the data will be permanently lost. For more on account eligibility, see\nIU account types and eligibility\n.\nGoogle at IU\n:\nSee\ndetails about your Google at IU account\nMicrosoft 365\nat IU:\nSee\ndetails about your Microsoft 365 at IU account\n.\nAdobe:\nTo preserve personal Adobe assets you created while at IU,\ntransfer your content to a personal Adobe account\nor download and store them in a personal storage location. If any of your files are\ninstitutional data\nor may be needed by your colleagues, be sure to transfer them to an appropriate storage location before you leave. See\nOptions for storing files at IU\n.\nTo continue using Adobe products that require a license after you leave IU, you must purchase your own retail license. You will receive an academic discount if you purchase your license before leaving the university.\nNote:\nThe Adobe transfer tool is called the "Student File Transfer service", but is also available for use by faculty, staff, and part-time employees.\nImportant:\nOnce your employment at IU ends, you will have a seven-day grace period before you lose access to your Adobe at IU account. After the grace period ends, you will have 30 days to request a temporary re-enable before your account is permanently deleted. To request a temporary re-enable, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nNote:\nEligibility for access to IT resources is based on status information provided by university offices such as the Office of the Registrar and the Human Resources Management System (HRMS); see\nEligibility to Use Information Technology Resources (IT-03)\n.\nAccounts that have been disabled but not yet deleted may be eligible for temporary re-enabling within the first 30 days to allow account owners to retrieve needed personal files or make final changes to their accounts.\nIf you have questions or need help preserving your data, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nIf you are called to active military service\nIf you are unexpectedly called to active military service, you may be eligible for a military leave of absence, in which case you can retain your active Indiana University computing accounts.\nFor help requesting a military leave of absence, contact your campus Veteran Support Services or Office of the Registrar:\nIU Bloomington\nIU Indianapolis\nIU East\nIU Kokomo\nIU Northwest\nIU South Bend\nIU Southeast\nFor more about military leave at IU Bloomington, see the Office of the Registrar's\nReserves Called to Active Duty Policy\n.\nIf you return\nIf you return to active status with IU after your accounts have been disabled, your username and most of your accounts will be re-enabled upon your return. However, some data may have been purged while you were away (and, of course, any systems retired in your absence will be unavailable). If you have moved to a new position, but your accounts are disabled, contact the person who processes your employment records. If you are an academic appointee, you can also contact your\ncampus Academic Affairs Office\n.\nArchival and recovery of\nExchange Online\ndata\nFor assistance with accessing\nExchange Online\nemail messages and transferring them to another email mailbox, see\nKeep your Exchange account email messages and calendar appointments when leaving IU\n. Archival and recovery of Exchange data must be done within 30 days of an account becoming ineligible for Microsoft 365 licensing or the data will be permanently lost.	KB0024781	KB0024781 v2.0	 employed at any <strong>IU</strong> campus. When your employment at <strong>IU</strong> ends, UITS will disable access to IT resources at <strong>IU</strong>, you may retain limited access to some student resources. Be sure to back up all valuable data, or the data will be permanently lost. For more on account eligibility, see <strong>IU</strong> account types	0.8069		2026-01-31 16:59:03.877512	2026-01-31 16:59:03.877512	0	dcc0f2c747b99a909286dfbd436d43d7
11059	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=23c5334447b8fe102fc6a55b416d433b	Stellic quick start guide for students	Table of contents\nOverview\nLanding dashboard\nTrack progress\nProgress symbol overview\nPlan your path\nSchedule term\nOverview\nImportant:\nIU Columbus, IU East, IU Fort Wayne, IU Kokomo, IU Northwest, IU South Bend, and IU Southeast:\nUse\nStellic\nto register for spring and summer 2026 classes.\nIU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis:\nContinue to\nuse the SIS Student Center\nto register for your spring and summer 2026 classes.\nStellic\nprovides a fast, easy way to see your full academic plan for your remaining terms, organize your upcoming courses, and arrange your schedule. This article serves as a quick guide to some of the primary features; a\nmore detailed student guide\nis also available.\nYou will still want to work closely with your advisor. Preparing with Stellic beforehand will make reviewing your plan with your advisor quicker and more effective.\nLanding dashboard\nAfter logging into Stellic, you will land on a dashboard that provides a quick view of your current programs, schedule, and academic progress. It also shows recent messages and a quick view of your academic calendar.\nTrack progress\nIn the\nTrack Progress\ntab, hover over your degree progress bar to see the number of courses you have taken, the number of courses remaining, the number of courses for which you are registered, and the number of courses that do not count toward your degree.\nTo view the list of requirements for your program, scroll down and select\nProgress\n, and then select either\nOfficial\n(to see classes you have taken or officially registered for) or\nPlanned\n(to see everything you have planned out with Stellic, including any unofficial programs you have added to your plan). Additionally, choose\nFull Audit\nto see your full audit,\nCompact\nto see a compact version of your audit, or\nRemaining\nto see only the requirements you have remaining.\nProgress symbol overview\nThe following symbols are used in the audit to indicate whether or not you have completed a requirement, are currently taking it, or have yet to complete it:\nSymbol\nDescription\nGreen checkmark:\nThe course/requirement has been completed. These are already taken and passed classes, or requirements that are completely fulfilled. These will remain the same in the official and planned audit.\nRed box:\nThe course/requirement still needs to be completed. In your official audit, any section not taken or registered will show as red; in your planned audit, any section that is not yet in your plan will be red.\nYellow single checkmark:\nThe course/requirement is in the plan and will be satisfied once completed. In your official audit, only classes registered for the current semester will be yellow; in your planned audit, all the classes you already have planned out with Stellic, even years ahead, will be yellow.\nYellow double checkmarks:\nThe course is currently being taken.\nRed number in a red box:\nThis is the number of remaining courses for a requirement. Non-course requirements are not included in this total.\nGreen chain link:\nThe course is double counting with a requirement in another program. Hover over the link symbol for details on where the class is double counting.\nGreen circular arrow:\nThe course has been taken more than once. This appears only if the course has been taken more than once and cannot be counted for more than one requirement.\nRed circle with an exclamation point:\nThe course is taken/registered but not counting for the requirement (for example, a course with a grade too low to fill the requirement, or a course that is counting for a different requirement). These courses are listed at the bottom of the audit, under "Unmatched Courses".\nDouble-headed arrow:\nThe requirement is being filled by a course that your institution has designated as equivalent with the course noted in the audit. (Not all schools use this, so you may not see it in your audit.)\nPlan your path\nIn the\nPlan Your Path\ntab, you can view and plan out each of your terms individually. You can easily see only the courses you have left to plan out and add them to the term you choose. Stellic will let you know if you need a prerequisite for a course or if a course is not available in that term.\nOn the right, the\nTentative List\nshows only courses that still need to be planned. Select a course from the\nRemaining Requirements\ncolumn and drag it to the chosen semester.\nSearch for specific classes by selecting\nAdd to [Term]\nin the planner, or select\nSearch Courses\non the right.\nEnter a course subject keyword in the search box and filter the results by desired qualifiers. When you find a course, drag it to the planner. Classes linked as pre-requisites or co-requisites will be highlighted.\nAlternatively, on the right, select\nProgress\n, and then drag and drop classes between terms to find your ideal schedule. Any transfer courses and advanced placement credits are listed at the bottom of this view.\nTo add additional programs to your plan, select\n+ Add program to plan\nnear the top of the page.\nSchedule term\nIn the\nSchedule Term\ntab, you can plan course sections and times for the upcoming term (as long as the course schedule has been released for that term).\nIn the\nSchedule Term\ntab, select a time slot in the weekly calendar view to see courses available for that time slot that are still needed for your plan. Select a course and hover over the\n+\n(plus sign) to see how each section fits into your weekly plan. Select the\n+\n(plus sign) to add the section to your plan.\nTo change or modify sections already showing in your weekly view, in the\nSchedule Term\ntab, on the sidebar, select the drop-down arrow for a course to see its alternate sections. Hover over each section to see it on your weekly view, and then select the desired new section.	KB0026369	KB0026369 v17.0	Table of contents Overview Landing dashboard Track progress Progress symbol overview Plan your path Schedule term Overview Stellic provides a fast, easy way to see your full academic plan for your remaining terms, organize your upcoming courses, and arrange your schedule. This article serves as a quick guide to some of the primary features; a mo	0.7979		2026-01-31 16:59:23.526706	2026-01-31 16:59:23.526706	0	23c5334447b8fe102fc6a55b416d433b
11073	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=48c2a2b447f032d0d500b87b716d43f7	About Stellic at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nHow to customize your settings\nAdd or remove programs\nHow individual courses count towards specific requirements\nAbout courses counting double\nCourses you've taken that don't count\nView a list of courses that will fufill a requirement\nTrack Progress tab\nPlan your Path tab\nCourse search\nIf you thought a course would count and it doesn't\nWhat-if scenario: Remove a registered course from your planner\nConfirm that your requirements are met and you're ready to graduate\nOverview\nImportant:\nIU Columbus, IU East, IU Fort Wayne, IU Kokomo, IU Northwest, IU South Bend, and IU Southeast:\nUse\nStellic\nto register for spring and summer 2026 classes.\nIU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis:\nContinue to\nuse the SIS Student Center\nto register for your spring and summer 2026 classes.\nIndiana University is partnering with Stellic to launch a comprehensive student success tool across all campuses. This new platform will streamline the process for IU students to plan their path and track their progress toward graduation. Students can access Stellic through\nOne.IU\nand at\nstellic.iu.edu\n.\nTo learn more about Stellic, see:\nStellic degree planner quick start guide for students\nProgress symbol overview\nStellic videos\nStellic Advisor/Admin Guide\n(accessible version available soon)\nFor frequently asked questions and more information about Stellic at IU, see\nStudent Success: Stellic\n.\nHow to customize your settings\nRefer to\nCustomize your settings in Stellic\n.\nAdd or remove programs\nTo add a program, navigate to the top of your profile in the\nPlan your Path\ntab. Select\n+ Add program to plan\n, and then search for and select other programs to add.\nTo remove a program, select\nx\nnext to the program name at the top of your profile. You will see a "not declared" tag until you've officially declared a new program with the university; if you remove a declared program, the program name will still appear, but it will have a "not in plan" tag. You can add the program again by selecting\nadd to plan\n.\nHow individual courses count towards specific requirements\nStudents don't have the ability to choose where a course will populate within an audit. Generally, a class will always fill in the highest-level category first. Classes that are already completed (with grades) are prioritized first, then registered classes, and finally planned classes last.\nRules (constraints) about which class can fill a category, grade requirements, and double counting are set by your school.\nAbout courses counting double\nDouble counting rules are set for each degree program by your school. Reach out to your advisor if you think there's an issue with a class double counting or not, but it isn't something that Stellic support can change or update.\nCourses you've taken that don't count\nWhen a course you've taken doesn't count toward a requirement, a red exclamation point will display next to it. Reasons for this can vary. More information will be available soon.\nView a list of courses that will fufill a requirement\nYou have several options for viewing courses that will fulfill a requirement.\nTrack Progress tab\nIn the\nTrack Progress\ntab, find the requirement/category.\nHover over the requirement, and select\nSearch Qualifying Courses\n(the magnifying glass icon).\nA right-side panel will open. To more easily see courses you can take right away, refine your list further by selecting\nOnly with cleared prerequisites\n.\nPlan your Path tab\nIn the\nPlan your Path\ntab, the default view shows the "Tentative List" in the right-side panel, which lists all the requirements that you haven't taken or added to your plan. Any course that appears with a dotted outline is a placeholder, which is a requirement that has several course options.\nTo bring up a list of courses that will count for that requirement, select the placeholder either directly from the tentative list, or add it to your plan to narrow the search by term.\nCourse search\nFrom the left navigation menu's\nExplore Courses\ntab, select the\nCounts for\nfilter to search by requirement. You can search for requirements for any program that you have in your Stellic plan, including any majors, minors, or other programs that you have added to your planner.\nFrom the search results, select a course. In the right-side panel, to add the course to a specific term, select\nAdd to Another Semester\n; alternatively, select\nAdd to Tentative List\n.\nIf you thought a course would count and it doesn't\nStellic gets information for degree plans and audits from IU. Stellic staff cannot manually change course information or audits. Reach out to your advisor or instructor if something doesn't seem right.\nWhat-if scenario: Remove a registered course from your planner\nNote:\nThis process is hypothetical. Dropping a course in Stellic does not remove it from your IU registration.\nTo see what happens if you drop a registered course from your Stellic planner, from the\nPlan Your Path\ntab, hover over the course in your plan, and select the\nx\nthat appears in the course's upper right corner.\nA warning message will appear at the top of the screen reminding you that this will not drop the course in the Student Information System (SIS) until it is removed there also. To dismiss the message, select\nOK\n.\nTwo versions of your progress view are available. The Official view shows all courses you have taken and for which you've registered. The Planned view incorporates future planned courses from your planner. Select either option to toggle between them.\nIn the Official view, the course you removed appears in the requirements since it is still registered in SIS. In the Planned view, the course no longer appears in the requirement, but instead appears in "Unmatched courses". In certain scenarios, you may see that another course will count for the requirement if you drop this one.\nConfirm that your requirements are met and you're ready to graduate\nTo see how many classes you've taken or have left, check the top bar in your profile on the\nPlan your Path\ntab.\nYou'll want to see 0 (zero) remaining in "Unplanned" to know that you have all of your audit requirements in your plan.\nYou also can check the\nTrack Progress\ntab (the\nOfficial\noption shows only classes officially taken or registered with the university) to see what classes are remaining. A red box next to any requirement means there are classes remaining to fill that category.\nStellic support cannot give you an official all-clear for graduation. Always work with your advisor who knows your program well and can warn you of any pitfalls.	KB0026368	KB0026368 v16.0	 across all campuses. This new platform will streamline the process for <strong>IU</strong> students to plan their path soon) For frequently asked questions and more information about Stellic at <strong>IU</strong>, see Student Success and audits from <strong>IU</strong>. Stellic staff cannot manually change course information or audits. Reach out to your	0.7979		2026-01-31 16:59:26.964698	2026-01-31 16:59:26.964698	0	48c2a2b447f032d0d500b87b716d43f7
11089	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7334e7cc4730be102fc6a55b416d4326	Student guide: Use Stellic to register for IU courses	Table of contents\nOverview\nGet started\nLanding dashboard\nPlan your Path tab\nSchedule Term tab\nRegister for your courses\nOverview\nImportant:\nIU Columbus, IU East, IU Fort Wayne, IU Kokomo, IU Northwest, IU South Bend, and IU Southeast:\nUse\nStellic\nto register for spring and summer 2026 classes.\nIU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis:\nContinue to\nuse the SIS Student Center\nto register for your spring and summer 2026 classes.\nStudents will not be able register for classes in\nStellic\nuntil the enrollment period for the term is open. You will know if an enrollment period is open if you see the "Registration Open" message displayed at the top of your Home page or dashboard view. You will also see a "Registration Opens" message displayed under "Quick Access" in the "Schedule Your Courses" area on the Home page.\nIf the enrollment period for your selected term is not open, you will see a "Registration hasn't yet been enabled for this term" message.\nGet started\nLanding dashboard\nAfter logging into Stellic, you will land on your default Home page or dashboard view. This page gives you a quick glance at your current programs, schedule, and academic progress. It also includes any recent messages and a quick view of your academic calendar.\nFrom the menu on the left, select\nPlan your Path\n.\nPlan your Path tab\nOn the\nPlan your Path\ntab, you can view and plan out each of your terms individually. If you do not see the term for which you are planning course sections and times, you can add the term to your plan; from the\nSelect Semester To Add\ndrop-down, select the semester you wish to add.\nThe selected semester will appear in your plan.\nSelecting\nTentative List\non the right side of the page will display courses that still need to be planned. Select a required course from the "Remaining Requirements" column.\nCourses that fulfill the selected requirement will be listed at the bottom of the right sidebar. These courses cover all semesters.\nUnder "Filters", select\nSemesters\n, and then use the "Planning for" drop-down or the "Semester" selections to choose the semester you are currently planning.\nThe list of courses is filtered to show only those available during your chosen semester. To select the course you wish to add to your plan for this semester, select the course name.\nReview the course information and select the semester you would like to complete the course.\nScroll down to display the class sections available during your chosen semester.\nChoose your desired class section by selecting the blue plus sign (\n+\n) to the left of the class section number.\nThe blue plus sign (\n+\n) changes to a green checkmark to indicate your choice. The class is added to your plan for this semester.\nContinue adding your desired classes to the plan for this semester. When you have finished, you will be ready to schedule your classes.\nSchedule Term tab\nOn the\nPlan your Path\ntab, select\nGo to schedule\nin the "Registration" information block located in your plan for this semester.\nOn the\nSchedule Term\ntab, a class you selected appears in the weekly calendar view if a day and time period is assigned to it (such as an in-person class).\nIf the class you selected is an online class, it will not appear in the calendar view. You will need to check the semester on the\nPlan your Path\ntab to verify that you are enrolled in the class.\nRegister for your courses\nWhen you are ready to register your courses for the semester, select\nConfirm Courses For Registration\nat the top of the calendar view on the\nSchedule Term\ntab.\nThe Registration Cart pop-up window should display. Check the entries in the cart to determine that you are cleared to register for your selected courses.\nSelect\nComplete Registration\nat the bottom of the Registration Cart window.\nThe registration process may take several minutes to complete. If you receive a "Failed to register" message, make note the failure messages and course information. Common reasons that registration might fail include incomplete prerequisites for a class, instructor permission is needed to enroll in a class, and a hold exists on your account. Select\nBack to schedule\n. Contact your advisor if you require assistance.\nOnce the issue has been resolved, return to the\nSchedule Term\ntab and select\nConfirm Courses for Registration\n.\nSelect\nComplete Registration\nat the bottom of the Registration Cart window.\nA "Registered Courses" pop-up window should display for the courses that previously failed registration; the courses should now display the "Enrolled" tag. Select\nBack to schedule\n.\nYou have successfully completed your registration for the selected semester. Note that the "Enrolled" tag is displayed for all selected courses on the\nSchedule Term\ntab.\nYou should receive an email from the Stellic system indicating that you have successfully registered for classes, and that you are required to process and pay in your Student Information System (SIS) to complete your registration.	KB0026612	KB0026612 v10.0	Table of contents Overview Get started Landing dashboard Plan your Path tab Schedule Term tab Register for your courses Overview Students will not be able register for classes in Stellic until the enrollment period for the term is open. You will know if an enrollment period is open if you see the "Registration Open" message displayed at the top 	0.7979		2026-01-31 16:59:30.62225	2026-01-31 16:59:30.62225	0	7334e7cc4730be102fc6a55b416d4326
11094	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5cccf574474efed02fc6a55b416d4372	KB contributor style guide	Table of contents\nAbout this style guide\nContent\nStructure\nTitles and headings\nTables of contents\nLists\nOrder of information\nVoice and tone\nGrammar\nStyle specifics\nElements of technology\nCapitalization\nPunctuation\nEmphasis\nAcronyms\nPronouns\nLinks\nNumbers\nCampus locations\nImages\nTables\nAbout this style guide\nThis style guide is intended to guide those who contribute new content or edits to the Knowledge Base as part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.  KCS contributors should adhere as closely as possible to these guidelines.  However, everyone who sends content to the Knowledge Management team using any method is encouraged to use this style guide to make rough drafts match KB style.  The more closely your draft follows KB style, the more quickly it can be published.\nDuring the first stage of the KCS rollout, the Knowledge Management team will take care of editing anything that requires working directly in markup; thus, those style guidelines are not covered here.  These items include:\nPanels (that is, “Note:” or “Important:” panels or blocks with visibility that's more restricted than the surrounding article)\nBlockquotes\nIn a future iteration of the KCS model, we may make changes to make it easier to edit from the rich-text editor in ServiceNow. Check back for updates. For now, you can simply include the text you want to include for blockquotes or panels, and we'll mark it up. (If you are comfortable working in markup, you are welcome to do so.  Ask the KM team for guidance on how to use markup options to match KB style.)\nUITS style generally follows conventions outlined in the\nChicago Manual of Style\n, with exceptions specific to\nIU Brand Guidelines\n(which includes the\nEditorial Style Guide\n).  This style guide covers situations specific to technical writing for the KB.\nNote:\nThe KM team has made some style changes with the migration to ServiceNow Knowledge and subsequent adoption of KCS; thus, existing articles will often not match the style listed here. Use this style for changes and new content going forward.\nContent\nOnly create content that's needed\nDon't create a KB article unless there's a clear need for it.  The KCS philosophy encourages creating documentation in response to support needs, rather than creating documentation in advance just in case it's needed.\nGive clients all the information they need to solve or avoid common problems, but don’t include details most people won’t need to know.  Stick to the minimum.\nDon’t include background information unless it’s necessary to prevent confusion, or to help people understand whether they need to take action or not.\nDon't duplicate information\nBe sure to check whether the KB already includes the information, or similar information that could be edited to meet the need.\nIf the information exists and is publicly available elsewhere, link to it rather than duplicating it.  Don’t reproduce vendor instructions unless there is a clear need to do so, such as:\nVendor instructions that will cause client confusion because of IU-specific details\nVendor instructions that aren’t accessible to people with disabilities\nMake content stand alone\nSome KB content may appear in other websites and applications, and instructions may be shared in email messages to clients or used in other ways.  Make the content clear no matter how clients access it.\nMake the scope clear\nBe sure that the information is as general as the way you're presenting it. For example, if something works only on the Windows version or the Mac version of a program, make that clear.\nInformation should apply to all IU campuses unless you specify otherwise.\nStructure\nIn general, make the organization of new content as similar to existing content as possible.  However, don’t include a section that’s not needed just because it’s in a template, or in a similar document.  Avoiding unnecessary content is more important than following a pattern.\nTitles and headings\nIf you're editing in ServiceNow Knowledge directly, be sure to designate headings with the appropriate selection from the\nParagraph\ndrop-down menu (or the equivalent tag in markup).  Use\nHeading 2\nfor the top level within the body; level 1 is reserved for the title.\nHeading 3\nand (sometimes)\nHeading 4\nare appropriate for sub-sections, but if you need more than that, you should probably rethink the organization of your article.  Do\nnot\nsimply use bold font to mark a heading.\nTitles and headings should be clear, concise, and descriptive.\nCapitalize only the first word and proper nouns.\nIf the content gives instructions for a task, the title or heading should be what you would say if you were telling a person to do that task.  Don’t use gerunds (-ing verbs used as nouns) in titles or headings.  For example:\nUse:\nChange your passphrase\nAvoid:\nChanging your passphrase\nAvoid:\nHow do I change my passphrase?\nAvoid:\nHow to change your passphrase\nAvoid:\nPassphrase change procedures\nWhere applicable, use the following standardized headings:\nOverview:\nThe intro about a topic; not always needed\nBefore you begin:\nIf you need to do things before doing the actual task\nTroubleshoot:\nFor troubleshooting info or tips\nLearn more:\nInformation such as resources and other documentation\nGet help:\nSupport resources and/or contact information\nSometimes we combine the last two into "Learn more and get help".\nNote:\nKB style does not typically use an FAQ format; the industry experts we’ve read suggest that FAQs are usually not the most efficient way for people to get the information they need. They often include redundant or unneeded information conveyed in more words than necessary. Instead of using questions as section headings, use the shortest, clearest way to indicate the content of each section, and make sure all the information people need is present (only once) in a clear logical format. If there is some specific reason why an article should include real questions that are actually being frequently asked, consult with the KM team about whether an FAQ would make sense for a particular circumstance.\nTables of contents\nIf an article has any headings, it should have a matching table of contents.  ServiceNow Knowledge has an option you can select that will automatically generate a table of contents based on the headings present in an article.  If you change the headings at all (whether adding, removing, or rewording), be sure to revise the table of contents to match; you can do this with a single button, as well.\nWith rare exceptions, if there is a table of contents, don’t include any text outside of a relevant heading.  Add an “Overview” section if necessary.\nMake sure everything under a heading belongs in that section.  For example, a sentence at the end of the document that says “Contact someunit@iu.edu with questions” should only be in the last section of the document if it is specific to that section.  Otherwise, it should be under a separate heading such as “Get help”.\nLists\nFor all but the briefest instructions, use numbered lists. Use numbers only for steps one must follow in order.\nUse lowercase letters for sub-lists of ordered steps, and lowercase Roman numerals for third-level ordered lists, if applicable.\nAs a general rule, each step in an ordered list should be an action you take.  If you need to include a description of something that isn’t an active step (such as “A dialog box will open”), you can usually combine that either with the step before or the step after, whichever it’s more closely related to.  It’s typically not necessary to include a step such as “This process is now complete,” but if there is important information about the completion state that readers need to know, it may make more sense as a paragraph following the end of the ordered list.\nUse unordered (bulleted) lists for everything other than steps one must follow in order, such as:\nPeople, places, or things that might create differences in user experience (for example, university roles, types of accounts, operating systems, campuses, etc.)\nOptions or form fields that are all presented in the same page or window\nDifferent paths the user could follow\nTo introduce lists:\nIf you have a paragraph introducing the list, you’ll usually end it with a colon.  If the point of the list is obvious without an introduction, you don’t need to include one.\nOmit needless words.  For example:\nInstead of:\nTo convert the database, follow these steps:\nUse:\nTo convert the database:\nInstead of:\nFor more information, consult the following KB articles:\nUse:\nFor more, see: (unless there’s a specific reason to highlight the kind of information)\nBegin list items with a capital letter (unless the word is a proper name that is correctly spelled with a lowercase letter, such as “macOS”).\nIf a list consists of items followed by a colon and information about the items, use bold text for the introductory words and the colon, and capitalize the word after the colon, thus:\nWindows:\nDownload and install WinSCP.\nmacOS:\nDownload and install Cyberduck.\nPut a period at the end of a list item if it’s a complete sentence.  Omit the period if it isn’t.\nDo not use commas, semi-colons, or conjunctions such as “and” or “or” at the end of a list item.  Make the relationship between the list items clear from the introduction to the list, if it isn’t already obvious based on context.  For example, you can say “do one of the following:” to communicate an “or” relationship between the bullets of a sub-list.\nAs much as possible, try to make items within the same list logically and grammatically parallel. For example:\nInstead of:\nThere are several requirements for using Unicorn Maker:\nStart with a horse.\nTwo pints of pixy dust\nYou should already have passed your magical qualifying exams and obtained your license before you begin.\nTry:\nTo use Unicorn Maker, you will need:\nA current, valid magical license\nOne horse\nTwo pints of pixy dust\nOrder of information\nPut software versions in order from newest to oldest.  This applies to the organization of sections within an article, bullets in a list, and mentions in running text.\nWhen possible, keep the language generic, such as “In Windows 10 and earlier . . .”\nWhen dealing with a slight variation between versions, combine them if possible; for example, use "Select\nSave\nor\nSave file\n" rather than "In Windows 8.x, select\nSave\n; in Windows 7, select\nSave file\n".\nWhen giving instructions, present information in the order users will need it.  For example, "From the\nToppings\nmenu, select\nAnchovies\n" helps the user know what to look for first.\nSee below for the order for IU campuses.\nAlways list "students, faculty, and staff" or "students, faculty, staff, and affiliates" in that order.\nVoice and tone\nUse a style that’s simple, natural, practical, and concise\nAvoid being wordy or overly descriptive.\nBe as clear and simple as possible, and no more technical-sounding than necessary for the audience. For public articles, use the words most people will be familiar with. For example, refer to a person's computer, not their machine.\nDon't use a fancier word if a simpler one will work. Consider replacements such as:\nInstead of\nTry\nAssist, assistance\nAid, help\nFinalize\nComplete, finish\nImplement\nCarry out, finish\nRequire\nNeed\nUtilize\nUse\nIt's fine to use contractions, and will typically sound more natural than avoiding them (for example, “Log out when you’re finished” rather than “Log out when you are finished”).\nYou can usually omit words like “the . . . button” or “the . . . link”; instead, say "Select\nOK"\nor "Select\nTurn on maximum security\n" without extra words.\nDon't be too casual. Use standard language, without slang or jargon. Readers shouldn’t notice the style; let them focus on the meaning.\nAvoid using words such as "simply" or "just" (for example, "Simply complete and submit the form" or "Just download the software"); in addition to being wordier, this trivializes any difficulties readers may have.\nIn most cases, omit “please” in KB articles. Focus on telling people how to do things; don’t use extra words to try to persuade them. (In most cases, it’s something they’re trying to do anyway!)\nAddress readers directly\nAddress readers as "you" in most cases (whether the "you" is stated or implied). For example:\nRather than:\nThe user should clear their browser's cache.\nSay:\nClear your browser's cache.\nYou may need to clarify who should take action in some articles. For example, if both students and instructors will be using an app, you may sometimes need to say something like "The instructor will need to enable the\nDirect submission\noption to allow students to submit their papers." However, if there's no risk of confusion, address the reader directly.\nPrefer active voice\nMost instructions should state directly, clearly, and concisely who does what with verbs in the active voice, thus:\nRather than:\nSecurity settings will need to be configured to block automatic application changes.\nUse one of these:\nConfigure the security settings to block automatic application changes.\nAn administrator will need to configure the security settings to block automatic application changes. (Use if, for example, the intended reader doesn't have permission to do this themselves.)\nPassive voice may be better when saying who does something calls attention to a detail that doesn't matter. For example:\nUITS will disable your accounts after two semesters of non-enrollment. (This focuses on UITS unnecessarily.)\nYour accounts will be disabled after two semesters of non-enrollment. (This focuses on the impact on the student, which is more important if they're worried about keeping their stuff.)\nGrammar\nAvoid common grammatical errors, such as:\nUsing a comma to join two sentences.\nInstead of:\nOpen a new document, start composing your proposed article.\nUse one of these:\nOpen a new document.  Start composing your proposed article.\nOpen a new document; start composing your proposed article.\nOpen a new document, and start composing your proposed article.\nUsing an apostrophe to make a plural.\nInstead of:\nStudents use smartphone’s to access Top Hat.\nUse:\nStudents use smartphones to access Top Hat.\nNote:\nOmit the apostrophe for plurals of abbreviations and dates as well. For example:\nCPUs\n2020s\nStyle specifics\nFor specific words and phrases to use or avoid, and how to spell them, consult the\nKB standard terms list\n.\nDo\nnot\nchange the font, size, or color of text manually. For consistency, let the KB's style sheet control these details. When pasting in content from Word or Google Docs, choose\nRemove formatting\nto prevent font information from being pasted into ServiceNow Knowledge. (This will remove details like font size and color, but keep basic formatting such as lists.)\nElements of technology\nUse the following conventions for designating certain parts of technical instructions:\nThings people select:\nUse bold font for anything a user will select, such as:\nNames of buttons, tabs, icons, and radio buttons, for example:\nAt the top of the page, select\nPublish\n.\nNote that informal descriptions of an icon's appearance don't get tags.  Make sure you have the official name by which a screenreader will announce the icon somewhere as well; for example:\nSelect the\nSettings\n(gear) icon.\nSelect the\nEdit\nicon (looks like a pencil).\nNames of menus and the options within them, for example:\nFrom the\nMonth\ndrop-down menu, select\nMay\n.\nTypographic representations of selectable items (for example, a plus sign when it means a control used to expand a list, or the letter X when it means a control that closes a window)\nNames of ServiceNow assignment groups\nNames of folders or directories only when most users in the target audience will select them (for example, folders within Microsoft Outlook).  If users will navigate to a directory or folder at the command line, treat the name as text users type, as described below.\nYou can designate a path of options people select with greater-than signs, particularly in articles aimed at more technically savvy users, like so:\nNavigate to\nControl Panel\n>\nSystem\n>\nDisplay\n.\nHowever, in articles aimed at beginning users, or anytime things are less obvious, it may be better to spell things out:\nChoose\nControl Panel\n, select\nSystem\n, and then, in the left pane, choose\nDisplay\n.\nShort commands, keystrokes, and text users type:\nUse\n<code>\ntags for commands, keystrokes, or any kind of text that users enter, when it makes sense to keep the text inline. You can apply this formatting by first selecting the text in the rich-text editor, and then choosing the\nInsert/edit code sample\nicon (\n) from the editing bar.  (Don't choose the icon before typing the text; doing so will open an editing window for a code block, as described below.)\nNote:\nAvoid using inline\n<code>\ntags immediately before a punctuation mark. Because the pink background may not be visible to everyone, doing so may create confusion about where the command ends. When in doubt between inline\n<code>\ntags and a code block, it's generally better to use a code block.\nUse\n<code>\ntags for items such as:\nCommands, including flags or options\nNamed keys (such as\nCtrl\nor\nShift\n), except for the Spacebar and arrow keys (which are untagged)\nKeystroke combinations; use hyphens to connect these:\nCtrl-x\nRepresentations of prompts of any kind\nNames of Unix programs/commands that go by their filenames, as well as Unix\nshell\nnames, for example:\n<code>less</code>\n<code>more</code>\n<code>cat</code>\nExceptions:\nvi gets no <code> tags.\nDo not use <code> tags around names that are capped, such as Python or Emacs.\nDo not use <code> tags around environment variables, such as PATH, PYTHONPATH, X509_USER_PROXY, etc.\nFilenames and file extensions\nException:\nFor common file types, you may use all caps, no tags, and no dot ("a ZIP file", "a PDF file", "a CSV file"). When referencing a specific filename, then use code tags, thus:\nfilename.zip\n.\nCertain elements of internet addresses:\nUsernames, when used alone\nExample email addresses, such as\ndvader@iu.edu\n; for real, active email addresses, instead use a mailto: link.\nFully qualified machine names\nHTML tags and attributes\nLonger commands or code blocks:\nUse the\nInsert/edit code sample\nbutton (\n) in the ServiceNow rich-text editor for commands and blocks of code. This will set the text off in a gray box with a fixed-width font and all line breaks and whitespace preserved, allowing you to represent a block of code where line breaks or spacing matters, like this:\nage = 20\nif age >= 18:\nprint("You are an adult.")\nelse:\nprint("You are a minor.")\nSelecting the button will open a dialog with a drop-down menu and a text box for the code. From the drop-down, you must select one of the following language options:\nHTML/XML\nJavaScript\nCSS\nPHP\nRuby\nPython\nJava\nC\nC#\nC++\nIf your code block is not one of these, consult with the KM team.  Although the language you select will not appear visibly in the KB portal, there is a class specifying the language within the markup, so it would be semantically incorrect to choose a language that's inaccurate.\nNote:\nAlthough the\nPreformatted\noption from the\nParagraph\ndrop-down menu renders the same visually in the KB portal view, it doesn't insert\n<code>\ntags within the\n<pre>\ntags, so it's better to use\nInsert/edit code sample\ninstead.\nThings people read:\nUse quotation marks for text that appears on the screen that people don't directly interact with, such as headings, names of fields, and names of dialog boxes, windows, or pages within apps such as Canvas when using the exact name as it appears on the screen.  (Don't use quotation marks if you're simply indicating the general purpose rather than quoting the name.) See below for examples.\nKB style uses British quotes, in which ending punctuation marks are placed\noutside\nquotation marks. This allows our documentation to represent the text on the screen clearly and accurately; if a punctuation mark appears within the user interface, put it inside the quotes, and if it doesn't, put it outside the quotation marks.\nFor example:\nIn the "Search domains:" field, enter your desired search domains.\nUnder "Quick actions", select\nEdit your quick actions\n.\nOn the "Gradebook Items" page, choose\n+ New Gradebook Item\n.\nLonger quotations:\nUse quotation marks for short snippets of text presented within a sentence, such as in the examples above.  For longer segments of text, such as error messages (except very short ones), exact text of automated mailings, and postal addresses, use a blockquote. Since this option is currently unavailable from the editing bar, you can leave it to the KM team to apply the markup.  Don't use quotation marks within blockquote tags (unless they appear on the screen as part of the message), and don't resume a sentence after a blockquote.  For example:\nIn the "Destroy planet?" dialog box, choose\nNo\n. If you accidentally select\nYes\n, you will see the following message:\nYou have chosen to destroy your planet to make room for a hyperspace expressway. Your cooperation is appreciated. A Vogon fleet will arrive shortly to fulfill your request. Allow up to two business days for planetary destruction.\nIf you see this message, contact your campus Support Center to avert global catastrophe.\nCapitalization\nCapitalize proper names using the correct form for each product or service.\nFor vended products, follow the vendor’s usage.  This includes spacing.  Check the vendor’s website if you’re not sure.\nFor example:\nmacOS, Honorlock, PowerPoint, Top Hat\nFor IU systems, refer to the standard terms list.  (The standard terms list also includes some vended products with unusual capitalization, but it isn’t comprehensive.)\nFor example:\nIUware, IUanyWare, IU eTexts\nFollow the capitalization on the screen exactly when referring to part of the user interface.\nFor example:\nUnder "User Settings", select\nShow All Courses\n.\nAvoid over-capitalization. Other than at the beginning of a sentence, title, list item, etc., only capitalize proper names and as needed to match the user interface.\nCapitalize titles only when they're specific.\nFor example:\n"Call the Support Center Manager" is correct, as is "All managers must fill out the following form."\nPunctuation\nCommas:\nUse a comma before a conjunction that joins the last two elements in a series:\nThe Knowledge Base contains information for IU students, faculty, staff, and affiliates.\nYou'll need to clear your browser’s cache, change your passphrase, and restart your computer.\nTo help guide readers through the sentence, put a comma at the end of an introductory phrase that gives context, for example:\nInstead of:\nIn the "Snacks" window that appears select the checkbox for\nTwinkies\n.\nUse:\nIn the "Snacks" window that appears, select the checkbox for\nTwinkies\n.\nQuotation marks:\nWe use British quotes, wherein ending punctuation marks are placed outside quotation marks. This also applies to commas or any other punctuation mark within the sentence: only the text and punctuation marks that appear on the screen go within the quotation marks (or tags); any that belong to the sentence rather than the quoted material go outside the quotation marks.\nHyphens:\nWe mostly follow\nThe Chicago Manual of Style\n, which tends to dehyphenate. Generally,\nA compound noun gets compressed into one word (checkbox).\nHyphenate verb-preposition combinations (pull-down).\nHyphenate pairs of words that work together to modify a noun when they come before the noun; don’t hyphenate them when they’re not immediately before a noun.  Thus:\n. . . a third-party vendor\nDo not share your IU passphrase with a third party.\nMany common hyphenation issues are covered in the\nKB standard terms list\n.\nSlashes:\nAs a general rule, avoid using the slash symbol (/) as a substitute for “and” or “or”.\nEmphasis\nUse bold text to emphasize important words or phrases.\nDo not use italics or all caps for emphasis. Italics have several specific purposes in the KB, which the KM team will apply with specific tags. You should never use all caps except for acronyms or as needed to match the user interface.\nDon't overuse bold text. Most of the time, bolding only the most important words (such as "not") should be enough.  Putting a longer phrase or an entire sentence in bold may be appropriate in some cases, but use this judiciously.\nAcronyms\nTo keep titles concise, use only the acronym in the title, unless the acronym is not commonly known.\nThe first time an acronym (including "Indiana University") is used in the body of an article, spell it out; follow it with the acronym in parentheses (except in the case of IU). In later instances, use only the acronym. Follow this style whether or not you spell out the acronym in the title.\nDon't use acronyms for IU campuses (see\nbelow\n).\nPronouns\nWhen a pronoun refers to a non-specific person, use "they", "them", or "their".\nOften, it sounds better to recast the sentence in the plural, thus:\nInstead of:\nAn employee should lock their workstation when leaving.\nTry:\nAll employees should lock their workstations when leaving.\nHowever, if the plural version of the sentence will cause confusion, use the plural pronoun with a singular noun rather than using "his or her", etc.\nDon't use "we" in public Knowledge Base documents. Readers may not know who that is. In most cases, say "UITS recommends . . .", etc., but referring to a more specific team is okay if that will be clearer.\nLinks\nWhen quoting information that may change, list a URL for the original document so that readers can find the most recent copy. It is not adequate to just provide the information without an up-to-date link.\nFor most links, use one of the following as the link text:\nThe title of the page; match the spelling and capitalization of the site you’re linking to\nA short phrase indicating the purpose of the link, for example:\nIf your computer is compromised, you’ll need to\nchange your passphrase\n.\nMake sure that the link text indicates what readers will get, even if they don’t read the surrounding context.  Do not use general phrases like “click here” for link text.  For details, see\nWrite link text for accessibility\n.\nYou may use the URL for the link text in rare cases when it is short and memorable, so that you might reasonably expect that users will type it directly into their browser’s address bar in the future without looking up the instructions again (for example,\nstatus.iu.edu\n).\nIf a sentence ends in a link that includes a question mark as part of the link text, the question mark serves as the final punctuation for the sentence; don’t add a period after the question mark.  For example:\nTo add a hidden tool to your course navigation menu, see\nHow do I manage Course Navigation links?\nUse as few words as possible to introduce links, for example:\nFor more, see\nWireless networks for IU departments\n.\nUITS recommends using a separate course site; see\nCreate a new course site in Canvas\n.\nFor links to other KB articles, use this format for the URL:\n/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0012345\nDo not include a link to a more-restricted article within public or less-restricted content.  If you need to include a link to more-restricted content, it should typically be within a knowledge block that is restricted to the same Can Read criteria as the link, so that readers don't see a link they can’t use.  There will be\nno\nvisual indication of links to restricted content, so you must pay careful attention to the Can Read criteria of articles you link to.\nAlternatively, if you're linking from a public article to an article in the iu-all audience, you may simply put "(requires IU Knowledge Base login)" after the link text to alert readers that they will be prompted to log in, since all of the primary audience for the IU KB will be able to access the content if they log in.\nFor email addresses, use links with\nmailto:username@iu.edu\nas the URL and the email address as the link text.\nNumbers\nSpell out zero through nine; use numerals for 10 and up.\nWe generally say "24 hours a day, 7 days a week"; "24/7" or "24/7/365" are acceptable in internal docs, and in a few public docs under special circumstances.\nWhen referring to a step in an ordered list, use a numeral rather than spelling out the number ("If your widget is already installed, skip to step 5 below").\nAny other time the number is serving as a designator rather than indicating how many of a thing there are, use a numeral (for example, "roll 3" to indicate a specific roll of paper on a plotter).\nUnits of measurement\nFile sizes, speeds, etc. should appear in the format "300 MB" (with a space between the number and the unit of measure).\nMoney\nDon’t use decimals unless the amount includes cents.\nPhone numbers\nUse the format 812-123-4567.\nDates and times\nUse "am" and "pm" without periods, and without spaces between them and the time they modify (for example, 9am).\nOmit the colon and zeroes for on-the-hour times (9am and 9:30am).\nFor ranges of time, use a single hyphen.\nWhen listing a range of days and times, use the format "Day-day time-time", with no comma between (Saturday-Sunday 8am-5pm).\nInstead of "12pm" and "12am", use "noon" and "midnight".\nIf you need to specify a time zone, use “Eastern Time” or “Central Time”; do not use “Eastern Standard Time” or “EST”.  Eastern Standard Time refers to the time most of Indiana follows in the winter months (as opposed to Eastern Daylight Time).\nDon't capitalize the names of semesters (for example, spring semester and fall semester).\nSpell out months and days of the week.\nDo not use ordinal suffixes (May 2, not May 2nd).\nUse numerals for years.\nInclude the year in any reference to a specific (rather than recurring) date, except for short-term modifications that you know will be removed promptly (for example, temporary hours for spring break)\nDo\nnot\nuse relative time references such as "this year" or "next month".  For example, use “spring 2026” rather than “next spring”.\nWhen listing the time, day, and date together, place the time at the beginning or end of the sequence. For example:\nChanges are scheduled to go live 6am Sunday, July 17, 2022.\nThe office will close Monday, July 25 at 4pm.\nThus, proper KB style would be as follows:\nThe office is open Monday-Thursday 8am-midnight, Friday 8am-9pm, Saturday noon-5pm, and Sunday noon-midnight. However, on August 30, 2030, it is closed.\nCampus locations\nCampuses\nRefer to Indiana University campuses as follows; spell out the full name the first time in the body of the article (at least in public content), and use the shorter version with just "IU" for later references.  It's also fine in some contexts to say things like "the Bloomington campus", etc.  Always use the order below when naming more than one campus.\nIndiana University Bloomington, IU Bloomington\nIndiana University Indianapolis, IU Indianapolis\nIndiana University Columbus, IU Columbus\nIndiana University East, IU East\nIndiana University Fort Wayne, IU Fort Wayne\nIndiana University Kokomo, IU Kokomo\nIndiana University Northwest, IU Northwest\nIndiana University South Bend, IU South Bend\nIndiana University Southeast, IU Southeast\nBuildings and rooms\nUse full building names rather than the two-letter abbreviations used on campus maps (although the abbreviations may be added in parentheses if needed for clarity).\nUse abbreviations when the full name is already present (for example, in a column of a table with room numbers when the full name of the building is elsewhere in the table)\nFor the Indiana Memorial Union on the Bloomington campus, use the full name for the first appearance, and if necessary add "(IMU)" after the first appearance; you may then abbreviate to IMU thereafter.\nAfter the first spelled-out appearance, abbreviate "Learning Commons" as "LC".\nFor rooms in the ICTC, use "IT". Since it is commonly known as "the IT Building" on campus, refer to it that way when giving directions.\nPut a space between the building name or abbreviation and the room number, thus:\nHayes Hall 109\nIT 126\nImages\nImages often help users follow a task, but they add to the work needed to maintain an article (for example, if a software update changes the look of a button, but not the name or the way it works).  Only include images in KB articles where they add value.\nDon’t rely on images alone to convey information; this is critical for accessibility.  Put the information people need to do the task in the text of the article, and use images if they help people follow the directions more easily.  For example:\nUse a screenshot with an arrow or circle to help users locate something in a dense user interface.\nUse a small image of an icon when the link between its name and its appearance isn’t obvious.\nUse a screenshot with sample data to help users picture how a step will work.\nAll images need alt text that makes sense in context. For general guidelines, see\nAlternative text for images\n.\nWhen using screenshots, redact sensitive or personal information, such as the names or usernames of real people.\nFor more, see\nImages in the IU Knowledge Base\n.\nTables\nDon’t use tables unless the data fits a table format logically.  (Don’t use a table just for layout, for example.)\nMake sure tables aren’t too wide to render properly.  If they are, the table should be broken up or presented in nested list format, or something of that sort.\nAll tables need a caption.\nFor more, see\nTables in the IU Knowledge Base\n.	KB0026645	KB0026645 v7.0	 conventions outlined in the Chicago Manual of Style, with exceptions specific to <strong>IU</strong> Brand Guidelines (which that will cause client confusion because of <strong>IU</strong>-specific details Vendor instructions that aren’t accessible version or the Mac version of a program, make that clear. Information should apply to all <strong>IU</strong>	0.7943	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 16:59:33.900082	2026-01-31 16:59:33.900082	0	5cccf574474efed02fc6a55b416d4372
11105	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=29d61f8747c8eed82fc6a55b416d436b	Make course changes during the first week of classes	Important:\nIU Columbus, IU East, and IU Fort Wayne, IU Kokomo, IU Northwest, IU South Bend, and IU Southeast:\nFor spring and summer 2026 registration, use\nStellic\nrather than the instructions here. Stellic is a new easy-to-use tool to help students plan their path, track their progress toward graduation, and register in one location. See\nStellic quick start guide for students\n.\nIU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis:\nFor spring and summer 2026 registration, continue to use the SIS Student Center to register for your classes.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nAdd a new course to your schedule\nSwap courses in your schedule\nOverview\nContinuous drop/add is available from the beginning of early registration through the Sunday following the first week of classes; check your campus academic calendar for the exact timing. If you drop a class during this time, the class won't appear on your transcript or grade reports, and you will not be billed for it. If you need to adjust your class schedule and want to avoid a W ("Withdrawn") grade, make sure to drop by this deadline.\nYou can make changes to your schedule either directly from your iGPS plan, or with the\nSchedule Builder\n. For help making changes from your iGPS plan, follow the instructions below.\nNote:\nThe examples below are from the fall 2020 semester, but the process is the same for each semester.\nAdd a new course to your schedule\nTo add a new course to your schedule:\nLog into your iGPS Plan from\nOne.IU\n.\nOnce your full plan is displayed, find the semester tile for the term for which you would like to make changes. You will see the courses for which you are currently registered, as well as any courses you may have left in your shopping cart or that you considered for enrollment but did not use (planned items). At the top, select\nContinue planning\nto begin adjusting your schedule:\nFrom the single-term view of iGPS, check your weekly calendar to see available slots for scheduling. Use the search tools to find all the classes in which you want to enroll and add them to your shopping cart; start by selecting\nAdd from Course Search\n.\nIn the new window that opens, you can search for courses by entering a keyword. You can also choose a subject area, level (for example, 100, 200, 300, etc.), or number of units. In the example below, courses are filtered by the subject "Integrated New Media Studies".\nFor more filters, select\nClass Filters\n. You can select\nHide Closed Classes\n, specify the days and times you want, or choose a particular mode of instruction. The filters will help limit your search results.\nIf you find a course that looks interesting, select the link below to view available sections (in the example above,\nView FA20 classes/add to Shopping Cart\n). Check your calendar to confirm it will fit into your schedule, and then select\nAdd to cart\n:\nReturn to the single-term view and select\nEnroll\n. You will be returned to the enrollment shopping cart.\nSelect the class, select\nValidate\n, and then\nEnroll\n:\nSwap courses in your schedule\nTo swap courses in your schedule:\nFollow steps 1-5\nabove\nto identify the course you'd like to swap with one for which you are already registered.\nIf you are swapping classes, don't worry if you have already registered for a course that conflicts with the new course's time; you can still add it to your shopping cart.\nFrom either the Full Plan view or within the Single Term view, select\nEnroll\n. You will be returned to your enrollment shopping cart.\nFrom the menu on the left, under "Enrollment", select\nSwap Classes\n.\nAll classes you are currently enrolled in will appear in the list with a\nSwap This Class\nbutton to the right. Select\nSwap This Class\nfor the course you wish to swap out of.\nYou will see the following swap options:\nWith class from Search\nWith class from shopping cart\nwith class from Class Number\nSince you would have put your new class into your shopping cart when in iGPS Plan, select that option.\nThe window that appears will show you all classes in your shopping car. Select the class you want by selecting\nSwap\n.\nReview your swap confirmation information and select\nConfirm\n.	KB0025455	KB0025455 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Add a new course to your schedule Swap courses in your schedule Overview Continuous drop/add is available from the beginning of early registration through the Sunday following the first week of classes; check your campus academic calendar for the exact timing. If you drop a class during this time, the class won't appea	0.7936		2026-01-31 16:59:37.361332	2026-01-31 16:59:37.361332	0	29d61f8747c8eed82fc6a55b416d436b
11127	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=89ad1bcf4700221c2fc6a55b416d4306	About the Student Center Dashboard	Important:\nIU Columbus, IU East, and IU Fort Wayne, IU Kokomo, IU Northwest, IU South Bend, and IU Southeast:\nFor spring and summer 2026 registration, use\nStellic\nrather than the instructions here. Stellic is a new easy-to-use tool to help students plan their path, track their progress toward graduation, and register in one location. See\nStellic quick start guide for students\n.\nIU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis:\nFor spring and summer 2026 registration, continue to use the SIS Student Center to register for your classes.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nDashboard navigation and layout\nChange your campus/institution\nLearn more or submit feedback\nEdit your widgets\nOverview\nAs part of the Student Information System (SIS), the Student Center applications provide access to many services for students. In the Student Center, students can view course offerings, register for classes, view class schedules and grades, change contact information, view financial aid information, and other such tasks.\nWhen you navigate to the\nStudent Center\n, you'll be on the\nDashboard\n, and your preferred name will be displayed. In the navigation on the left side, you'll see the following sections:\nDashboard\n,\nChange Campus/Institution\n,\nClass Search\n,\nSchedule\n,\nEnrollment\n,\nAcademics\n,\nFinancial\n,\nMy Information\n, and\nMy Profile\n.\nThe Dashboard includes a\nGeneral\nsection and an\nAcademics\nsection.\nDashboard navigation and layout\nGeneral section\nWhen you arrive on the Dashboard, the\nGeneral\nsection is displayed, showing holds on your record, to do items, shopping cart information, and waitlisted classes.\nTo expand the navigation on the left, select the three stacked horizontal lines at the top of the blue bar.\nAcademics section\nAt the top of the Dashboard, select\nAcademics\nto change to the Academics section, where you can view your class schedule and grades.\nChange your campus/institution\nAfter you've expanded the navigation on the left, your campus's logo will display above the menu. By default, the campus displayed is the campus at which you are admitted, currently enrolled, or last enrolled.\nTo view information for another IU campus:\nAt the top left, in the navigation menu, select\nChange Campus/Institution\n(or select the gear icon):\nSelect the campus or institution you'd like to view. Note that the campus you are currently viewing will be selected.\nSelect\nChange Campus/Institution\n.\nThe logo for the campus you selected will now be displayed above the navigation menu on the left, and you'll see your information for the chosen campus.\nNote:\nIf you do not see the full menu with the campus logo, expand the menu using the three horizontal lines.\nWatch an instructional video\nHow to Change the Campus (Institution) You are Viewing\nLearn more or submit feedback\nAt the bottom of the\nPreferences\n(gear) menu, you'll see information about whom to contact with questions or feedback about the Student Center.\nEdit your widgets\nYou may select which widgets of information you'd like to appear in the Student Center Dashboard.\nTo edit the widgets that appear in your Dashboard:\nAt the top right, select\nEdit My Widgets\n:\nUncheck any widgets you'd like to hide:\nSelect\nSave Changes\n.\nReturn to the Dashboard; your updated widgets will be displayed.	KB0025350	KB0025350 v3.0	, currently enrolled, or last enrolled. To view information for another <strong>IU</strong> campus: At the top left	0.7936		2026-01-31 16:59:48.461491	2026-01-31 16:59:48.461491	0	89ad1bcf4700221c2fc6a55b416d4306
11113	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=95aedb4f4740221c2fc6a55b416d4336	Use Degree Maps to plan your academic path	Important:\nIU Columbus, IU East, and IU Fort Wayne, IU Kokomo, IU Northwest, IU South Bend, and IU Southeast:\nFor spring and summer 2026 registration, use\nStellic\nrather than the instructions here. Stellic is a new easy-to-use tool to help students plan their path, track their progress toward graduation, and register in one location. See\nStellic quick start guide for students\n.\nIU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis:\nFor spring and summer 2026 registration, continue to use the SIS Student Center to register for your classes.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nSearch for a degree map\nView a degree map\nPrint a degree map\nMove terms to your plan\nOverview\nAt Indiana University, Degree Maps are guides for outlining a path to degree completion, following departmental pathways through a particular major. Many of the maps are flexible, representing one way (but not the only way) to complete a degree.\nSearch for a degree map\nTo search for a degree map:\nGo to\nDegree Map Search\n.\nNote:\nDegree Maps are part of iGPS (the Interactive Graduation Planning System). For easy access to all iGPS tools, search for\nigps\nin\nOne.IU\n, and select\niGPS Tasks\nunder "Search Results: Task Centers". This is a collection of all the tools you'll need for academic planning.\nSelect the campus where you are a degree-seeking student.\nOptions will display for finding the degree map you wish to explore.\nTo see all the degree options for a particular school on your campus, select the school and select the magnifying glass icon.\nSearch on words, or parts of words, to display all possible degree options that include those letters.\nWhen you find a map you'd like to explore, identify the term in which you began as a degree-seeking student at your campus, and then the appropriate range of start terms for that degree plan.\nView a degree map\nThe first part of the map details the campus where it's offered, a description of the major, and links for information about financial aid and careers. You can also see an explanation of what critical courses and milestones are in the maps.\nNext, you can see a listing of courses by semester and year. A map may list specific courses that should be taken, or "placeholders" (descriptive labels that describe categories of courses).\nTo display further details about each item, select the arrow to its left.\nTo add a map to your iGPS Plan so you can easily return to it, at the top of a degree map, select\nAdd to My Maps\n. The map will then appear in your plan so you can add courses from it without having to search for it again.\nTo go back to your search results, select\nReturn to Search\nat the top of the page.\nPrint a degree map\nAt the top of a degree map, select\nPrint Degree Map\nto either print a paper copy or save a digital copy as a PDF. In your browser's print preview screen, you'll see that all of the information at the top of the map displays in the printed version, along with side-by-side semesters so you can easily see the suggested courses for an academic year. The course details and required semester details for critical courses are expanded for each applicable requirement.\nMove terms to your plan\nWhen you've decided on a Degree Map to use in planning, you can customize a plan to guide your future semesters of enrollment.\nTo add the Map to your plan, at the top of the page, select\nAdd to My Maps\n.\nTo customize a plan:\nSelect the checkbox at the top of each semester's course list, which selects that complete list of courses and placeholders to add your plan.\nAlternatively, if you only need to add a few courses, you can either check only those courses you want or check the box at the top of the term and then uncheck the courses you do not need.\nAt the bottom of the term, select the plan term to which you want to add those courses from the drop-down menu, and then select\nAdd\n.\nYou can modify and rearrange specific required courses in your plan, if you wish.\nNote:\nIf you move a placeholder into your plan, you will need to choose a course from the placeholder in your iGPS Plan before you can register for it.\nWhen you're finished customizing your plan, and if the schedule of classes for that term is available, you can\nUse Schedule Builder to plan your term of enrollment\nor register for classes from your iGPS Plan.\nFor more, see a\nplaylist of introductory videos\n.	KB0022713	KB0022713 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Search for a degree map View a degree map Print a degree map Move terms to your plan Overview At Indiana University, Degree Maps are guides for outlining a path to degree completion, following departmental pathways through a particular major. Many of the maps are flexible, representing one way (but not the only way) to	0.7936		2026-01-31 16:59:41.573403	2026-01-31 16:59:52.596779	0	95aedb4f4740221c2fc6a55b416d4336
11312	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=bd20b8ff476456583d373803846d43dd	Access VoiceOver in the STC computing labs	Overview\nThe steps below explain how to access VoiceOver, a screen reader software available in the macOS\nSTC computing labs\n.\nAccess VoiceOver\nBecause no screen reading software is running by default when you start working on macOS computers in the STC labs, you will need to launch VoiceOver by pressing the\nCommand\n+\nF5\nkeys.\nOnce VoiceOver is running at the login screen:\nPress\nCtrl\n+\nAlt\n+\nDelete\nto get to the login screen.\nEnter your username and then press\nTab\n.\nEnter your password and then press\nEnter\n.\nA long pause will occur while your information is loaded, which can take between one to two minutes.\nYou should arrive at the desktop after logging into the computer, but VoiceOver will not be on.\nPress the\nCommand\n+\nF5\nkeys to turn on VoiceOver.\nYou will be greeted by the "Welcome to VoiceOver" window. VoiceOver will provide audio information about how to proceed, but you can also:\nPress\nV\nif you want to start using VoiceOver.\nPress Spacebar to launch the tutorial for learning VoiceOver.\nTo make sure you have full keyboard accessibility and additional information on VoiceOver, refer to the\nVoiceOver basics\nguide.\nAt this point you can start using VoiceOver on the macOS computer in the STC lab.\nLog off the computer\nLeave VoiceOver running and close all other open applications.\nPress\nControl\n+\nfn\n+\nF2\nkeys to put focus on the Apple option in the menu bar.\nIf the command doesn't work, press\nControl\n+\nfn\n+\nF3\nto put focus on the Dock, and then press\nControl\n+\nfn\n+\nF2\nto get focus on the Apple option in the menu bar.\nPress\nControl\n+\nOption\n-Spacebar to open the Apple menu.\nPress the down arrow until you get to the\nLog Out [Username]…\noption, and then press Spacebar.\nA window will appear asking if you want to quit all applications.\nThere is a checkbox named "Reopen windows when logging back in". If you leave VoiceOver running and the checkbox is checked when you log off, VoiceOver will start back up the next time you log into the same computer.\nPress the\nTab\nkey until you get to the\nLogout\nbutton, and then press Spacebar to log off the computer.\nLearn more\nFor a full listing of keyboard commands for VoiceOver, see\nVoiceOver Commands and Gestures\n.\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0025399	KB0025399 v1.0	Overview The steps below explain how to access VoiceOver, a screen reader software available in the macOS STC <strong>computing</strong> labs. Access VoiceOver Because no screen reading software is running by default when you start working on macOS computers in the STC labs, you will need to launch VoiceOver	53.109		2026-01-31 17:05:39.224689	2026-01-31 17:05:39.224689	0	bd20b8ff476456583d373803846d43dd
10613	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=aa30b47347a456583d373803846d4330	Read an OME-encrypted message	Table of contents\nOverview\nScenarios\nIU Exchange to IU Exchange - same tenant\nIU Exchange to non-IU Exchange - different tenant\nIU Exchange to non-Exchange - self-validating provider (Gmail or Yahoo)\nIU Exchange to non-Exchange - other providers\nOpen an attachment\nMailing lists\nForwarding\nOverview\nUITS\nhas enabled\nOffice Message Encryption\n(OME) as a replacement for CSESS (Cisco Secure Email Encryption Service) formerly CRES (Cisco Registered Envelope Service). The method for reading an OME message depends on the recipient email system; for the highest compatibility, the sender and recipient should both use the "click to run" version of\nOutlook\n(often referred to as the "Microsoft 365 version of Office"), or use\nOutlook on the web\n(OWA). However, other versions of\nOutlook\n2016 or higher should perform similarly.\nTo check your version of\nOutlook\n, select\nFile\n, then choose\nOffice Account\n, and look under "About Outlook".\nNote:\nTo learn how to force the encryption of a message, see\nEnsure that mail sent from your Exchange account to an outside address is encrypted\n.\nEven if the sender does not force encryption, all outbound messages from IU mail servers to external recipients are scanned for certain potentially sensitive information; if the scan determines that the message is likely to contain sensitive data, it is encrypted.\nScenarios\nIn any of the scenarios below, if you select\nRead the message\nand get the following error message, "User account\nuser@external.com\nfrom identity provider "mail" does not exist in tenant "Indiana University" and cannot access the application...", copy the link and open it in a browser that is not already authenticated to your credentials.\nIU Exchange to IU Exchange - same tenant\nIn this scenario, there should be no difference from any other standard send/receive scenario. You may see some text saying that the email is encrypted, but the encryption will be seamless and should not impact message operations.\nIU Exchange to non-IU Exchange - different tenant\nIf the external tenant's settings are configured in the same way as IU's tenant (usually they are not), there should be no difference from any other standard send/receive scenario. The\n.rpmsg\nattachment is not intended to be opened, and should be ignored.\nIf the external tenant's settings are not configured in the same way as IU's tenant:\nOpen the notification email, and select\nRead the message\n.\nA dialog box may prompt you for the intended recipient's email address (this might not be you), if it isn't automatically applied from the link.\nRequest a temporary code that will be sent to the intended recipient's email address, and then use it to access the message.\nIU Exchange to non-Exchange - self-validating provider (Gmail or Yahoo)\nThe\n.rpmsg\nattachment is not intended to be opened, and should be ignored.\nNote:\nOther high volume email vendors may offer account self-validation, but Gmail and Yahoo are the only ones known currently.\nOpen the notification email, and select\nRead the message\n.\nA web page will open with the option to either access the message with the intended recipient's Gmail login, or to receive an access code.\nIf you choose to use the intended recipient's Gmail login,  enter the intended recipient's Gmail credentials to access the message.\nIf you choose to receive an access code, it will be sent to the intended recipient's Gmail account; use it to access the message.\nIU Exchange to non-Exchange - other providers\nThe\n.rpmsg\nattachment is not intended to be opened, and should be ignored.\nOpen the notification email, and select\nRead the message\n.\nA dialog box may prompt you for the intended recipient's email address (this might not be you), if it isn't automatically applied from the link.\nA web page will open with the option to receive an access code.\nThe code will be sent to the intended recipient's email address; use it to access the message.\nOpen an attachment\nIf the attachment is a file that was sent with the email, then you can open it like a standard attachment. If the attachment is a\nOneDrive\nlink, when you attempt to open it you may be prompted to receive and then enter a temporary code that is specific to\nOneDrive\n. The code will be sent to the intended recipient's email address; use it to access the attachment.\nNote:\nAs a Data Loss Prevention function, automatic encryption cannot be disabled. If you believe your message was incorrectly encrypted, contact\nit-incident@iu.edu\n; if possible send the message as an attachment, or include the text of the message in your email. Make sure to include the following:\nSender email address\nRecipient email address\nDate and time of the message\nSubject of the message\nMailing lists\nAs a best practice sensitive data should not be sent via listserv. If you send data that could be considered sensitive, you may need to use a different service, or send the message from the\nIU List interface\n.\nForwarding\nSince encrypted messages are intended for a specific recipient, forwarding an encrypted message is not considered best practice.	KB0025817	KB0025817 v1.0	 it like a standard attachment. If the attachment is a <strong>OneDrive</strong> link, when you attempt to open it you may be prompted to receive and then enter a temporary code that is specific to <strong>OneDrive</strong>. The code	4.7349		2026-01-31 16:53:24.94198	2026-01-31 16:53:24.94198	0	aa30b47347a456583d373803846d4330
11131	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9f8c57834700221c2fc6a55b416d4313	About the Student Center	Important:\nIU Columbus, IU East, and IU Fort Wayne, IU Kokomo, IU Northwest, IU South Bend, and IU Southeast:\nFor spring and summer 2026 registration, use\nStellic\nrather than the instructions here. Stellic is a new easy-to-use tool to help students plan their path, track their progress toward graduation, and register in one location. See\nStellic quick start guide for students\n.\nIU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis:\nFor spring and summer 2026 registration, continue to use the SIS Student Center to register for your classes.\nAs part of the Student Information System (SIS), the Student Center applications provide access to many services for students. In the Student Center, students can view course offerings, register for classes, view class schedules and grades, change contact information, view financial aid information, and other such tasks.\nThe Student Center uses multiple university servers, and thus you must enable cookies and your web browser security settings must be set to accept third-party cookies. Otherwise, you may be unable to initiate a Student Center session, or you could be logged out of your transaction.	KB0022786	KB0022786 v2.0	The Student Center uses multiple university servers, and thus you must enable cookies and your web browser security settings must be set to accept third-party cookies. Otherwise, you may be unable to initiate a Student Center session, or you could be logged out of your transaction.	0.7936		2026-01-31 16:59:56.516329	2026-01-31 16:59:56.516329	0	9f8c57834700221c2fc6a55b416d4313
11758	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=4edf203b476456583d373803846d43c0	Run or schedule reports in the IUIE	Run a report\nTo run a report in the\nIUIE\n, select a report object from either the Master Catalog or your personal My Catalog index of report objects, and then fill in any parameters you wish. To submit the report object, select\nRun\nat the bottom of the page.\nSchedule a report\nIUIE\nuser scheduling allows you to schedule the execution of a report object at a specified date and time, or on a specified recurring basis during IUIE online hours. You can schedule, run, and update the execution of any report object within the IUIE application to which you have access.\nScheduling occurs within My Catalog and is available from report objects whose parameters you have saved using the\nSave\nSettings\nbutton. Following are the specific steps for the stages of the scheduling process.\nPrepare a report for scheduling\nIn the Master Catalog, select a report object from the list.\nSelect the desired report parameters.\nTo save the report parameters, select\nSave Settings\n. This step creates a shortcut with these parameters in the\nNew\nShortcuts\nfolder of My Catalog. When you open a shortcut with saved parameters, a\nSchedule\nbutton appears near the bottom of the form.\nSchedule a report to run\nIn My Catalog, open the saved report from the\nNew\nShortcuts\nfolder (or from another folder, if you've moved the saved report).\nIf you change any report parameters, save the report by selecting\nSave Settings\n.\nTo open the schedule form, select\nSchedule\n. Select the desired scheduling parameters, and then save the schedule. This places a shortcut in your\nScheduled\nJobs\nfolder.\nEdit a report schedule\nIn My Catalog, open the\nScheduled Jobs\nfolder.\nFrom the list of scheduled jobs, select the job name.\nEdit the scheduling parameters as needed, and then save the form.	KB0023694	KB0023694 v1.0	, open the Scheduled Jobs folder. From the list of scheduled jobs, select the <strong>job</strong> name. Edit	157.0602		2026-01-31 17:17:12.826672	2026-01-31 17:17:31.693245	0	4edf203b476456583d373803846d43c0
11137	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7bcfa4f7476456583d373803846d4367	If your Two-Step Login (Duo) device is not with you, or is lost or stolen	Table of contents\nIf your device isn't with you\nIf your device has been lost or stolen\nIf your device isn't with you\nIf you are temporarily without your\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\ndevice or token (for example, you left it at home), contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nUITS staff:\nIf you've lost access to all of your Duo devices, you can recover access to your account by contacting:\nIU Bloomington:\nCall\nEITS\nat 812-856-9999.\nIU Indianapolis:\nCall EITS at 317-278-9999.\nIU Columbus, IU East, IU Kokomo, IU Northwest, IU South Bend, IU Southeast:\nCall\nTier 2 Support\nat 317-278-7282.\nIf your device has been lost or stolen\nIf your device enrolled in\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\nis lost or stolen:\nFor immediate help accessing what you need\n, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nIf your device is not a smartphone or tablet\n, contact your campus\nSupport Center\nfor help.\nIf your device is a smartphone or tablet\nand it's possible to remotely wipe it (that is, erase all data), do so; refer to\nIf your computer or other device is stolen\n.\nImportant:\nIf you use the Duo Mobile app on an Android or iOS device, you may be able to use the Duo Restore feature to recover your accounts from a backup. For more, see\nRestore Two-Step Login accounts in the Duo Mobile app on a new device\n.\nIf your device is a smartphone:\nReplace the device as soon as possible, keeping the same phone number.\nFollow the steps to\nEnroll a new smartphone in Two-Step Login (Duo) using your existing phone number\n.\nFor help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0023489	KB0023489 v1.0	Table of contents If your device isn't with you If your device has been lost or stolen If your device isn't with you If you are temporarily without your Two-Step Login (Duo) device or token (for example, you left it at home), contact 