11139	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6758ce163b8a6e14476d941864e45a43	Scan removable media for viruses at IU	Computers in the Indiana University\nStudent Technology Centers\n(STCs) are continually updated with the latest\nvirus\nprofiles, so you can scan removable media from any STC computer.\nOccasionally, a virus can damage an infected file. Also, sometimes it is not possible to remove a virus, due to file corruption. In these cases, it may or may not be possible to recover some or all of the file, depending on the situation and the extent of the damage.\nNote:\nFor recommendations about antivirus software, see\nRecommended antivirus software at IU\n.\nFor information about or help finding STCs/computing labs on each campus, see:\nSTC locations at IU Bloomington\nSTC locations at IU Indianapolis\nSTC locations at IU Columbus\nSTC locations at IU East\nIU Fort Wayne and Purdue University Fort Wayne\n(not managed by UITS)\nSTC locations at IU Kokomo\nSTC locations at IU Northwest\nSTC locations at IU South Bend\nSTC locations at IU Southeast	KB0025239	KB0025239 v2.0	Computers in the Indiana University Student Technology Centers (STCs) are continually updated with the latest virus profiles, so you can scan removable media from any STC computer. Occasionally, a virus can damage an infected file. Also, sometimes it is not possible to remove a virus, due to file corruption. In these cases, it may or may not be 	0.7799		2026-01-31 17:00:05.919155	2026-01-31 17:00:05.919155	0	6758ce163b8a6e14476d941864e45a43
11154	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ad45adc447127218d500b87b716d43ea	Computing labs at IU	Table of contents\nComputing lab locations and hours\nCampus housing computing\nAdditional lab information\nFor instructors and departments\nComputing lab locations and hours\nAbout the IU STCs (computing labs)\nAssistive technology services at IU\nFor information about or help finding STCs/computing labs on each campus, see:\nSTC locations at IU Bloomington\nSTC locations at IU Indianapolis\nSTC locations at IU Columbus\nSTC locations at IU East\nIU Fort Wayne and Purdue University Fort Wayne\n(not managed by UITS)\nSTC locations at IU Kokomo\nSTC locations at IU Northwest\nSTC locations at IU South Bend\nSTC locations at IU Southeast\nCampus housing computing\nAbout the IU RTCs\nConnect to the network in IU Bloomington or IU Indianapolis campus housing\nRegister your wired device on the IU network\nAdditional lab information\nGuidelines for use of the STCs\nAssistive technology services at IU\nMultimedia devices at IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis\nAbout the STC maintenance window\nFor instructors and departments\nReserve an STC for a class\nAbout STC Partnership Agreements\nAbout STC Software Sponsorship Agreements\nAbout conference use of the STCs	KB0023996	KB0023996 v4.0	 information For instructors and departments Computing lab locations and hours About the <strong>IU</strong> STCs (computing labs) Assistive technology services at <strong>IU</strong> Campus housing computing About the <strong>IU</strong> RTCs Connect to the network in <strong>IU</strong> Bloomington or <strong>IU</strong> Indianapolis campus housing Register your wired device on the <strong>IU</strong>	0.7799		2026-01-31 17:00:12.972796	2026-01-31 17:00:12.972796	0	ad45adc447127218d500b87b716d43ea
11165	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=79afa837476456583d373803846d430f	About IU Blogs (Edublogs)	Table of contents\nOverview\nAlternatives to IU Blogs\nPlug-ins\nOverview\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nIU Blogs provides a user-friendly platform for faculty and staff who want to create their own individual or departmental blogs. Through IU's subscription to the\nCampusPress\nEdublogs service, IU Blogs is available for use by all IU faculty, staff, and faculty-sponsored student projects.\nTo create your own blog, go to\nIU Blogs\n, and select\nRequest an IU Blogs site\n.\nAlternatives to IU Blogs\nThe IU Blogs platform is for blogging and may not be used for course websites, news distribution, or websites whose primary purpose is not blogging.\nUse\nCanvas\nfor course websites and materials.\nUse\nLiveWhale Storyteller\nfor news and event distribution.\nUse\nbeINvolved\nfor student organizations.\nUse the\nWeb Framework\nin the\nWeb Content Management System\n(WCMS) for websites whose primary purpose is not blogging.  For more, see\nAbout Sitehost accounts\n.\nPlug-ins\nCampusPress has tested, customized, and included several WordPress plug-ins that you can activate for use in IU Blogs. However, many WordPress plug-ins designed for self-hosted WordPress implementations (not multisite implementations, such as the CampusPress Edublogs service) will not work in IU Blogs.\nIU community:\nIf a WordPress plug-in you need is not available in IU Blogs,\nsend a request to the IU Blogs support team\nwith the name of the plug-in and details about your desired goals.\nIf you have questions about the IU Blogs service, or need help,\nemail the IU Blogs support team\n.	KB0022612	KB0022612 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Alternatives to <strong>IU</strong> Blogs Plug-ins Overview <strong>IU</strong> Blogs provides a user. Through IU's subscription to the CampusPress Edublogs service, <strong>IU</strong> Blogs is available for use by all <strong>IU</strong> faculty, staff, and faculty-sponsored student projects. To create your own blog, go to <strong>IU</strong> Blogs	0.7764		2026-01-31 17:00:24.728788	2026-01-31 17:00:24.728788	0	79afa837476456583d373803846d430f
11174	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=4fb9dde5475e9614e06785e8536d4399	About the annual HIPAA training requirement for OVPIT staff	To ensure the privacy and security of individually identifiable health information protected by the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (\nHIPAA\n), and meet annual training requirements set by the HIPAA\nSecurity Rule\n, OVPIT personnel who manage systems or services that create, receive, maintain, or transfer data containing protected health information (\nPHI\n) must complete HIPAA training every year.\nLog into the IU Knowledge Base to see information on this page (below) about enrolling in and fulfilling the required training.\nOVPIT community:\nAnnual HIPAA training\nOVPIT personnel must complete the following E Training course:\nHIPAA Privacy & Security:\nThis course is an annual requirement for all IU personnel handling PHI. It provides a general overview of HIPAA privacy and security requirements, outlines your responsibilities regarding the proper access, use, and disclosure of PHI, introduces privacy and security safeguards, and explains how to report privacy and security incidents.\nIf you have questions about the annual HIPAA training requirement for OVPIT staff members, email\nuitshipa@iu.edu\n.\nEnroll in the E Training course\nTo enroll in the required E Training course:\nIn a web browser, navigate to to browse or search for courses, or access it directly:\nHIPAA Privacy and Security Spring 2025\nTo enroll, on the course page, select\nLOG IN\n.\nChoose\nEnroll Now\n, and then choose\nEnroll in Course\n.\nTo access your course immediately, select\nGo to Canvas Course\n.\nFulfill your training requirement\nTo fulfill your training requirement, you must complete the required course and print the course completion certificate.\nYou must print your course certificate for the system to acknowledge that you fulfilled your training requirement. You do not need to send the printed certificate anywhere; keep it for your records. You can always access your transcript from your IU Expand dashboard.\nFor more, see the following sections of\nAbout IU E Training courses in IU Expand\n:\nAccess your courses after enrolling\nView your transcript or certificates\nTo determine your current training status, see\nView your IU compliance assignments and request support\n.	KB0023941	KB0023941 v4.0	 HIPAA training every year. Log into the <strong>IU</strong> Knowledge Base to see information on this page (below) about	0.7562		2026-01-31 17:00:59.832563	2026-01-31 17:00:59.832563	0	4fb9dde5475e9614e06785e8536d4399
11180	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=221074bf476456583d373803846d43d3	About expired certificates for digitally signed mail	To view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nNote:\nDue to enhanced security features in\nExchange Online\n, you should no longer use digital signatures at IU. Follow the\nappropriate instructions to remove your certificates\n.\nIU community:\nFor more about security features in\nExchange Online\nat IU, see\nAbout Microsoft 365 security\n.\nOnce your certificate expires, all old sent mail will display a message saying, "There is a problem with this digital certificate" to indicate the certificate is expired (some mail clients may also display a visual indicator, such as a red line); however, all old mail will still be readable by all parties.\nIU community:\nAbout expiration notifications\nThe Sectigo (formerly Comodo) Certificate Manager will automatically notify you when your S/MIME email certificate is about to expire.  These notifications are sent at intervals of 15, 7, and 2 days. You can delete these notifications, and no further action is required. A sample of the notification message follows:\nFrom: Certificate Services Manager <support@cert-manager.com>\nDate: 4/25/17 9:14 AM (GMT-04:00)\nTo: "Vader, Darth" <dvader@iu.edu>\nSubject: WARNING: Expiration Notice: Client certificate for Darth\nVader<dvader@iu.edu>.\nGreetings,\nThis automated email is being sent to you by Sectigo on behalf of Indiana University.  Sectigo is the preferred contracted partner for digital certificate services for members of the InCommon Federation and Internet2. As such they are authorized to send this email on IU's behalf.\nThe client certificate for Darth Vader<dvader@iu.edu> will expire at\n04/30/2017 00:59 GMT.  To renew this certificate, please visit the IU\nKnowledge Base, search for "renewing smime", and follow the instructions in the document titled "Renewing a certificate for digitally signed email".\nYou may continue to receive these messages even if you renew or obtain a new certificate, please disregard those messages.\nRegards,\nUniversity Information Security Office\niuca@iu.edu\nGet help\nIf you need help, contact your\nlocal UITS support person\nor your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0025124	KB0025124 v1.0	Once your certificate expires, all old sent mail will display a message saying, "There is a problem with this digital certificate" to indicate the certificate is expired (some mail clients may also display a visual indicator, such as a red line); however, all old mail will still be readable by all parties.	0.7556		2026-01-31 17:01:03.314863	2026-01-31 17:01:03.314863	0	221074bf476456583d373803846d43d3
11183	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3a007c3f476456583d373803846d43d6	Change the structure of your data collection instruments in IU REDCap	In\nIU REDCap\n, you can change your data collection instruments using either the online Form Editor or the Data Dictionary.\nAt Indiana University,\nUITS\nrecommends using the Data Dictionary, particularly if your data collection instruments have many variables or are using advanced functionalities, such as branching logic and calculated fields. For more, see\nUse the Data Dictionary to change the structure of your data collection instruments in IU REDCap\n.\nIf you have questions about IU\nREDCap\n, or need help setting up or managing an IU\nREDCap\nproject, email IU\nREDCap\nSupport at\nredcap@iu.edu\n.	KB0024798	KB0024798 v1.0	In <strong>IU</strong> REDCap, you can change your data collection instruments using either the online Form Editor or the Data Dictionary. At Indiana University, UITS recommends using the Data Dictionary the structure of your data collection instruments in <strong>IU</strong> REDCap.	0.7554		2026-01-31 17:01:23.985076	2026-01-31 17:01:23.985076	0	3a007c3f476456583d373803846d43d6
11789	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=cd00b03f476456583d373803846d43ab	Change your home page in the OnBase Unity client	When you've logged into the Unity client, the startup screen may look like the screenshot below:\nTo change the home page to the Retrieval screen:\nSelect the\nRetrieval\n(magnifying glass) button to go to the Retrieval screen.\nSelect the little arrow under the\nHome\nbutton and choose\nMake this layout my Home Page\n.\nYou can use the above instructions to set your home page as Custom Queries, File Cabinets, Batch Scanning, Batch Processing, or Workflow.	KB0024657	KB0024657 v1.0	 Cabinets, <strong>Batch</strong> Scanning, <strong>Batch</strong> <strong>Processing</strong>, or Workflow.	234.0049	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:18:40.926225	2026-01-31 17:18:40.926225	0	cd00b03f476456583d373803846d43ab
11410	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1aa55ddd3b726210476d941864e45a9a	SEO best practices for IU websites	Table of contents\nDigital Strategy Initiative and search engine optimization (SEO)\nSite structure and SEO\nSubfolders vs. subdomains\nOther resources\nDigital Strategy Initiative and search engine optimization (SEO)\nIndiana University has launched the\nDigital Strategy Initiative\n(DSI), working towards a more user-friendly web experience to improve IU's reputation and brand recognition as well as aid in student recruitment and retention.\nA site audit in the summer of 2023 found nearly one-third of IU's web presence to be unmaintained or dead, with issues that have a direct impact on web traffic and visibility on search engine results pages.\nWebsite owners can check their sites in\nSiteimprove\n, a tool that identifies issues to address to improve search engine optimization (SEO). Some of the most important issues for SEO include:\nBroken links:\nOnce found, these should be removed or replaced quickly because they negatively affect user experience and search authority.\nCanonical URLs:\nSearch engines like Google consider a page's canonical URL the "one true source" of information about the content. The canonical version of a page's URL should include the full filename (that is,\nbloomington.iu.edu/research/index.html\ninstead of\nbloomington.iu.edu/research/\n).\nRedirects:\nRedirects should link to the final canonical version of the new page. Uncorrected redirect chains slow page loading and create a negative user experience, resulting in reduced search authority.\nImage file size:\nImages over 1 MB should be replaced with something smaller (see the IU Web Style Guide for information about\ncompressing images\n); when replacing images, use the\nWebP\nformat for better performance.\nAutoplay videos:\nVideos should not be set to autoplay or appear in the hero section of a webpage; the large file size creates slower page load speed.\nSitemaps:\nsitemap.xml\nfiles should update nightly (WCMS sites update sitemaps automatically). Make sure to update sitemaps when large sections of sites have changes in folder structure, content changes, or redirects; search engines use sitemaps as guideposts for indexing pages. Large sites should break the sitemap into multiple sitemaps, linked together; for more, see\nGoogle Search Central Documentation on sitemap management\n.\nVirtual tours:\nSearch engines will read virtual tours on higher education websites, so be sure to include equivalent text descriptions and present that version first, before any interactive or 3D tour. Well-crafted text within the tour, along with alt text and text transitions, can be effective SEO indicators; the\nIU Brand\nwebsite has more\nbest practices for creating virtual tours\n.\nAccordions:\nWhile content in closed accordion panels is readable by search engines, it may not be considered as important as top-level content; for best results, use a maximum of 5-7 accordions on a page, be sure headers clearly summarize the body content, and avoid including critical content in closed panels.\nSite structure and SEO\nIn order to improve IU's search engine results, custom subdomains will only be approved in special circumstances. Subfolders (or subdirectories) are the recommended practice for IU websites.\nA website's structure has a significant impact on search performance, so it's important to create a structure that drives traffic in a way that brings the best possible results.\nSubfolders vs. subdomains\nSearch engines consider subdomains separate from the main domain (\npresident.iu.edu\nis considered a subdomain of iu.edu), so they rank the content and assets within each subdomain separately, leading to reduced traffic.\nBecause subfolders organize content and assets within an individual site's domain (as in\niu.edu/president/\n), search engines consider them part of the main site. This boosts the site's domain ranking and can drive more traffic. When building sites, it's recommended to use subfolders for better SEO performance.\nOther resources\nDNS policies at IU\nDomain and virtual host request form	KB0024415	KB0024415 v2.0	Table of contents Digital Strategy <strong>Initiative</strong> and search engine optimization (SEO) Site structure and SEO Subfolders vs. subdomains Other resources Digital Strategy <strong>Initiative</strong> and search engine optimization (SEO) Indiana University has launched the Digital Strategy <strong>Initiative</strong> (DSI), working towards	10.7765	km-aud-iu	2026-01-31 17:08:58.16449	2026-01-31 17:08:58.16449	0	1aa55ddd3b726210476d941864e45a9a
2396	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024747	Differences between development and production projects in IU REDCap	In\nIU REDCap\n, your new project has "Development" status while you're building and testing it. When you're ready to enter real data, you change the project status to "Production" from the "Control Panel" page (accessed via the menu on the left). When you're finished collecting data, or if you wish to stop collection, you can set the status to "Inactive". You can change the status back to "Production" whenever you're ready to resume data collection.\nChanges you make to a project with "Development" status take effect immediately. Once your project's status has changed to "Production", you can no longer edit its database fields in real time. To modify a project with "Production" status, you must enter Draft Mode to make your changes, and then pass them for approval to a REDCap administrator. For more, see\nMake changes to an IU REDCap project that is currently in production\n.\nIf you have questions about IU\nREDCap\n, or need help setting up or managing an IU\nREDCap\nproject, email IU\nREDCap\nSupport at\nredcap@iu.edu\n.	KB0024747	KB0024747 v1.0	In <strong>IU</strong> REDCap, your new project has "Development" status while you're building and testing", you can no longer edit its <strong>database</strong> fields in real time. To modify a project with "Production" status. For more, see Make changes to an <strong>IU</strong> REDCap project that is currently in production.	0.7554		2026-01-31 14:35:49.029376	2026-01-31 17:01:28.533385	0	4e00b83f476456583d373803846d4350
11775	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=51a1e0a347757a502fc6a55b416d43b6	Use cron jobs in AppKube	Note:\nThe following information is technical and intended for web application developers.\nTable of contents\nAbout cron jobs\nEnable cron job functionality in AppKube\nDefine cron jobs\nCron job name character limits\nHow the naming works\nExamples\nReference information\nGet help\nAbout cron jobs\nA cron job is a command that runs on a fixed schedule or interval. These are typically used to automate maintenance tasks or other background processes that support the operation of an application. Within\nAppKube\n, you can schedule one or more cron jobs that will have access to your application's code, your secrets, and any persistent storage that you may be using. They are defined within your application's code and are deployed alongside your application.\nEnable cron job functionality in AppKube\nTo enable cron job functionality in AppKube, you need to ensure that the\nRequires Cronjob\noption is selected for your application. If you did not enable cron jobs when you originally requested your application, you can go to\nhttps://webapps.iu.edu\nand request to add the feature. Once the option is enabled, you can define and deploy cron jobs through your application's deployment process as outlined below.\nDefine cron jobs\nTo define one or more cron jobs in AppKube, you must first create a directory named\n.appkube\nin the root directory of your application's source code in GitHub. Then, within the\n.appkube\ndirectory, create a file named\ncronjobs.yaml\n. This file can define zero or more cron jobs for your application.\nFollowing is an example\ncronjobs.yaml\nfile:\ncronjobs:\n- name: daily-task\nschedule: "0 21 * * *"\ntimeZone: "US/Eastern"\ncommand: "php"\nscript: "scripts/my-daily-task.php"\narguments: [ "$STORAGE_DIR/my-output-file.txt" ]\nenvironments: [ "test", "prod" ]\nenabled: true\n- name: monthly-task\nschedule: "30 2 L * *"\ncommand: "php"\nscript: "scripts/my-monthly-task.php"\nThe above example schedules two cron jobs:\nThe first cron job runs daily at 9pm (ET) and executes the PHP script in the\nscripts\ndirectory of the project named\nmy-daily-task.php\n. It passes two arguments to the script. The first argument is a command line option named\n--output\n; the second argument references the $STORAGE_DIR environment variable, which points to the location of persistent storage (for apps that are using it). This cron job is configured to execute in the test and production environments.\nThe second cron job executes a script on the last day of each month at 2:30am (ET) in the test and production environments. It takes no arguments.\nWhen your application is deployed to test and/or production, the\ncronjobs.yaml\nspecification will be read, and the requested cron jobs will be installed within AppKube's infrastructure. You can update these later by changing the file and redeploying. If you remove any cron jobs from the list, those cron jobs will be shut down and removed; if you add any new ones, they will be newly installed for you.\nImportant:\nIf you have existing cron jobs configured for your application and want to completely remove them from all environments, modify your\ncronjobs.yaml\nfile to look like the following, and then deploy it to your test and production environments:\ncronjobs: []\nIf you only remove the\n.appkube/cronjobs.yaml\nfile from your project, the AppKube build process will skip the cron job deployment workflow step, and as a result will not remove any existing cron jobs. If you want to remove the file completely from your project, deploy a file with an empty set of cron jobs (as shown above) before removing the\ncronjobs.yaml\nfile.\nCron job name character limits\nAppKube cron jobs are subject to Kubernetes' 52-character limit for cron job names. The system automatically maximizes the available characters for your cron job names and validates this limit during deployment.\nHow the naming works\nFull CronJob name format: {group-account}-{app-code}-{cronjob-name}\nTotal name length limit: 52 characters\nAvailable characters for cronjob name = 52 - (group-account length) - (app-code length) - 2 (for hyphens)\nExamples\nExample 1\nGroup account:\ninfotern\n(8 characters)\nApp code:\ninternship-app\n(14 characters)\nCron name:\nvery-long-cronjob-name-that-exceeds-limit\n(41 characters)\nFull name:\ninfotern-internship-app-very-long-cronjob-name-that-exceeds-limit\n(65 characters)\nToo long (exceeds 52-character limit)\nExample 2\nGroup account:\ninfotern\n(8 characters)\nApp code:\ninternship-app\n(14 characters)\nCron name:\ndaily-cleanup\n(13 characters)\nFull name:\ninfotern-internship-app-daily-cleanup\n(37 characters)\nOkay (within 52-character limit)\nThe system will automatically validate cron job names during deployment and provide clear error messages if the limit is exceeded, preventing deployment failures.\nReference information\nReference for\ncronjobs.yaml\nfields\nField\nRequired?\nDescription\nname\nYes\nThis is a unique, human-readable identifier for the job. It is limited in size and may be truncated if too long; see\nCron job name character limits\nfor details. Each of your application's cron jobs\nmust\nhave a unique name.\nschedule\nYes\nStandard 5-field cron schedule; for example,\n"*/5****"\n(see note below)\ntimeZone\nNo\nIANA time zone (for example,\n"America/New_York"\n); if omitted,\n"US/Eastern"\nis the default\ncommand\nYes\nExecution command; currently restricted to\nphp\n(see note below)\nscript\nYes\nThis is the path to the script you want to execute. If you provide a relative path, it will be relative to the root of your application's deployment directory.\narguments\nNo\nZero or more command-line arguments passed to the script\nenvironments\nNo\nThis is used to specifiy the environments where the job should run. The only options are\n"test"\nand\n"prod"\n(if not present, it defaults to both\n"test"\nand\n"prod"\n).\nenabled\nNo\nToggles job activation; enabled by default\nNote:\nFor more about the\nschedule\nfield, see the\nFreeBSD crontab manual page\nand the\ngo.dev cron manual page\n.\nCurrently, only\n"php"\nis supported as the execution command for cron jobs. The majority of cron functionality can be implemented using PHP code within your application project. The system is configured this way to avoid arbitrary code execution within the container in which your application runs. This is partially for security reasons and partially to avoid dependencies on the binaries installed within AppKube that could lead to compatibility issues as the environment continues to evolve. To share feedback about any limitations you may experience, email\napphelp@iu.edu\n.\nGet help\nFor help using your AppKube cron jobs, email\napphelp@iu.edu\n.	KB0026805	KB0026805 v6.0	Table of contents About cron jobs Enable cron <strong>job</strong> functionality in AppKube Define cron jobs Cron <strong>job</strong> name character limits How the naming works Examples Reference information Get help About cron jobs A cron <strong>job</strong> is a command that runs on a fixed schedule or interval. These are typically used	140.2465	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:17:37.177607	2026-01-31 17:17:37.177607	0	51a1e0a347757a502fc6a55b416d43b6
11779	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a4cfe8b7476456583d373803846d4322	Set appointment availability in the Student Appointment Scheduler	Table of contents\nOverview\nSet up your calendar\nView and manage your appointments\nScheduling notice to redirect students\nEmail messages to students\nOverview\nThe Student Appointment Scheduler (SAS) lets staff, faculty, tutors, and other users create and manage blocks of available time in an online calendar. Students may access this calendar to view, schedule, and cancel appointment slots.\nSet up your calendar\nWhen setting up your calendar, you can view an individual day or week by using the\nDay\nand\nWeek\nbuttons. Note that appointment blocks cannot be set from the month view.\nTo navigate between months, use the arrows in the upper corners of the inset calendar or the arrows to the left of the\nDay\n,\nWeek\n, and\nMonth\nbuttons.\nAppointment restrictions\nAppointment restrictions let you select parameters for which students can view and schedule appointment slots restricted to student populations you specify. To create a restriction:\nFrom your main calendar view, select\nManage Restrictions\n, and then\nCreate New Slot Restriction\n.\nEnter a name for your restriction, and then select one of the options below:\nInclude assigned advisees\n, along with any additional criteria from the drop-down menus. If you are interested in having only your assigned advisees able to see your appointment slots, check this box only and save with the name of the group; any other criteria could create a much smaller number of students who could see your availability.\nAny/all of the parameters you would like associated with that restriction from the drop-down menus\nOne of your Caseload Groups from\nAdRx\nIf you are creating a campaign for a group of your students, select the name of the campaign as the only criteria and save it along with a restriction name.\nNote:\nWithin a restriction, all criteria are set up with "AND" logic; that is, students have to meet\nall\nselected criteria in order to schedule an appointment at the time you specify. The best practice is to make each restriction as clear and simple as possible.\nAs you save restrictions, they will display on the left. Select a restriction to view its details, as well as to access the\nDelete\nbutton to remove it. You cannot edit a saved restriction. If there is a restriction that does not work for what you need, you can delete the existing restriction and create a new one with adjusted details.\nCreate appointment slots\nTo set an appointment slot:\nImportant:\nMake sure you can see your calendar in full (that is, the left, center, and right panels). The following appointment options will appear in the right panel.\nChoose a date and time:\nSelect any 30-minute interval on the calendar. The details for the appointment will appear in the right sidebar:\nAppointment Date:\nConfirm the correct date of the appointment slot.\nTime:\nAdjust an appointment's start and/or end times. Remember to:\nPut a zero (\n0\n) before all single digits\nLeave no spaces before am/pm (for example, use\n09:00am\n, not\n9:00 am\n)\nSelect a meeting type:\nFrom the\nMeeting Type\nmenu, select\nAppointment\n,\nGroup\n, or\nDrop-in\n. For each meeting type, select one to three meeting modes:\nin-person\n,\nonline\n, or\nphone\n. When you select a meeting mode, the appointment details will expand to include specific information for the students about each mode. This information will be included in the confirmation and reminder emails to the student.\nDrop-In\nshows students that they may drop in to see you, but they cannot reserve that time.\nGroup appointments require that you enter both the number of students who may meet with you during that appointment slot and a name for the group slot. The group slot name is displayed in SAS for students searching your calendar. Students will see the group meeting only if spots are still available.\nIf the appointment is recurring, selecting\nYes\nwill display the frequency of the appointment slots and the start and end dates for the reoccurrence.\nImportant:\nTo account for server load issues and improve performance, the maximum length for which you can set a recurring series is 31 calendar days, regardless of how frequently the series repeats (weekly, every weekday, every day). You can set up recurring series multiple months in advance, but you must make a new recurring series for each month. OCSS recommends setting up appointments only three to four months into the future.\nChoose a profile:\nIf you have more than one profile, select the appropriate profile for the appointment slot. If you have only one profile, your default will be this profile.\nSet restrictions\n:\nApply any restrictions by checking the boxes next to them. If you add more than one restriction within an appointment slot, the limitation is an "OR".  For example, students who meet\neither\nrestriction A or restriction B could view and schedule this appointment slot.\nSet location details:\nFor in-person, select the building and enter the room number. The default selection is from your profile, in your user settings managed by your\nAdRx\nadministrator. If your building is not listed, there is a\nBuilding Not Listed\noption at the bottom of the "Building" list; you would then enter both your building and room location in the specified field.\nFor online, you can enter your Zoom link, but you are required to provide details on how to connect with you online.\nFor phone, you can enter your office phone, but you are required to describe who initiates the call and how. Remember that if the student has a US-based telephone number, they will enter it when scheduling an appointment and that is the number you would call.\nFinalize the appointment:\nTo finish creating the appointment slot, select\nCreate Slot\nor\nSchedule\nfor a specific student.\nView and manage your appointments\nYou can view appointments by day, week, or month. With day and week views, you see times allotted for each appointment, an icon showing the type of appointment, and restrictions on the appointment block.\nView student details\nIf a student has scheduled to meet with you during an appointment block, the student's name will appear in the box and will change to a black font color. Select the student's name to see more details, including the student ID and purpose for the appointment.\nYou can also view student details from the "Appointments" area in\nAdRx\n.\nChange an appointment\nFrom the calendar, you can delete an appointment, change the appointment type (for example, change from an in-person to a phone appointment), update the reasons for the appointment, or change the profile assigned to the appointment.\nTo change the appointment time, you must delete the appointment or appointment series and re-create it where desired.\nImportant:\nUsers cannot add new modes to existing flexible appointments. If a new mode is needed, you will have to delete the appointment and make a new appointment. For instance, if you have an in-person or Zoom appointment and a student scheduled for Zoom, but would like to change to in-person, you will be able to edit the existing appointment for the alternate mode.\nIf you have an in-person or Zoom appointment and a student scheduled for Zoom, but would like to change to phone, you will need to delete the old appointment and make a new appointment with phone as the mode.\nBest practice:\nOffer as many modes as are reasonable when you first make appointments to minimize difficulty in editing. (If you are working in your office, for instance, we recommend offering all three modes on any appointment.)\nView all appointment blocks\nIn the month view, you will see the calendar for the month with a listing of how many open appointment slots you have and how many scheduled appointments you have for each day. Selecting a specific day will take you to the daily view.\nDuring peak advising times and course registration times, it's possible that all appointment slots are filled and new students will have no times to select unless you add more appointment slots, or make more slots available based on your maximum future time setting in "\nAdRx\n- My Profile - SAS Student Appointment Scheduler - Calendar Settings".\nNote:\nBecause SAS is integrated into Outlook, student names will display in the subject of the Outlook calendar item. Any related scheduling history (for example, students who canceled that spot, making it available) will be in the notes area.\nScheduling notice to redirect students\nAdvisors may sometimes have no available appointments for weeks or months due to employment status, extended leave, or job requirements such as orientation programming. A scheduling notice gives students this information in SAS and directs them to another advising source while their assigned advisor is unavailable.\nYou might use scheduling notices for the following cases:\nSummer New Student Orientation means you are largely meeting with new students; a scheduling notice informs continuing students and provides directions on how to get help and whom to contact.\nA faculty advisor is on a 10-month contract, so for two months the advisor will not be meeting with students; a scheduling notice lets students know whom they can contact for advising until the faculty advisor's return.\nAn advising office only takes drop-in appointments the week prior to a new semester and the first week of classes; a scheduling notice would inform students of that and direct them to the hours the office is taking drop-ins.\nAn advisor will be unavailable for an extended period (such as FMLA) and needs to remind students whom they can see for advising in the interim.\nNote:\nScheduling notices are not "out of office" messages or constant reminders ("this is a busy time of year") that replace calendar and caseload management. In most cases, such as PTO, training, or busy advising seasons, students can still make future appointments on your SAS, so you should not use a scheduling notice.\nExample scheduling notices\nScenario 1: Faculty who are not available over the summer need to refer students to an advising office for assistance\nSubject: No appointments until fall semester\nMessage: During the summer, from (date) to (date), I won't have advising appointments available; please contact the Undergrad Advising Center for advising questions  and help at 123-4567.\nScenario 2: An advisor is largely working in orientation for the summer and needs to refer students to others for assistance\nSubject: Summer Orientation Advising\nMessage: I will be working with New Student Orientation this summer and most or all of my appointments will be taken up with new student advising meetings. If you need advising help or an advising appointment, please contact the advising office at 123-4567 to meet with an academic advisor.\nScenario 3: An advisor is on FMLA for an extended leave and needs to refer students to colleagues for assistance\nSubject: On extended leave, contact Sally or Anne\nMessage: I will be away on extended leave from (date) to (date). Please contact Sally Supervisor at 456-7891 or Anne Advisor at 123-4567 for academic advising during this time. They have access to all of the notes, plans, and advising emails from our appointments and are happy to help you with registration.\nEmail messages to students\nExample confirmation email\nBelow is an example of an email message sent to students after they schedule an appointment. The appointment details are filled in automatically. The personalized message was created by the advisor in the "SAS Student Appointment Scheduler – Advisor Messaging – Appointment Confirmation Message to Students" setting in My Profile.\nAppointment Confirmation with Ima Advisor-Coach on Wed Feb 08 2020, 09:00 AM\nHello [Student Name]\nYour appointment is confirmed with Ima Advisor-Coach.\nAppointment Details\nDate : Wed Feb 08 2020\nTime : 09:00 AM- 09:30 AM\nReason : I need to discuss graduation\nLocation : Dunes Med/Prof Bldg 3104\nDetails: Please check in at the front reception desk so they can let me know you have arrived.\nHello! I am looking forward to meeting with you. Please prepare for our meeting by bringing your questions, possible course selections and your academic plan. Thank you for using the calendar.\nIma Advisor-Coach\nCancellation email\nIf either the advisor or the schedule manager cancels an existing appointment, the student is automatically notified from the advisor's email. The message includes the reason entered by whoever canceled the appointment.\nHow to direct students to SAS\nDirect students to visit\nOne.IU\nand search for\nStudent Appointment Scheduler\n.\nYou can also send basic directions, such as:\nYou can access my calendar online to find the time that best fits with your schedule:\nSAS One.IU direct URL:\nhttps://one.iu.edu/task/iu/student-appointment-scheduler	KB0023225	KB0023225 v1.0	 to redirect students Email messages to students Overview The Student Appointment <strong>Scheduler</strong> (SAS Appointment <strong>Scheduler</strong> - Calendar Settings". Note: Because SAS is integrated into Outlook, student due to employment status, extended leave, or <strong>job</strong> requirements such as orientation programming	80.6098		2026-01-31 17:17:43.058869	2026-01-31 17:17:43.058869	0	a4cfe8b7476456583d373803846d4322
11783	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7df4dfa2470e16509286dfbd436d4323	PDQ reports in the Data Delivery PDQ application	Table of contents\nOverview\nIUIE PDQ report migration to the Data Delivery PDQ system\nChanges to IUIE PDQ report terms and concepts\nRun reports\nView completed report output\nJoblog\nSaved parameters\nCreate saved parameters\nManage saved parameters\nShare saved parameters\nSchedule jobs using saved parameters\nOverview\nThe IUIE PDQ report system has been rewritten and moved out of the IUIE. PDQ reports that have been migrated from the IUIE are available in this system. Access it at\nhttps://pdq.dd.iu.edu\n.\nIUIE PDQ report migration to the Data Delivery PDQ system\nPDQ report developers will migrate reports from the IUIE to DD PDQ as they are ready. Until that happens, you may continue to use the report in the IUIE. After the report is migrated, you will still be able to look up the report in the IUIE, but it will be changed into a link pointing to the migrated report in the new system.\nNote:\nWhen a report is migrated, your shortcuts and scheduled jobs for that report will also be migrated.\nChanges to IUIE PDQ report terms and concepts\nSome terms and concepts have changed in the redesign:\nMaster and Test Catalogs have become main and develop contexts\nIn the IUIE, the "Test Catalog" was for reports that were being developed and the "Master Catalog" was for reports that were finished and ready for production use. The Data Delivery PDQ system has two contexts that fulfill the same roles as those catalogs, "develop" for reports that are still under revision, and "main" for reports that are ready for wide use. Each report appears on the home page\nhttps://pdq.dd.iu.edu\nwith each context it is published to.\nShortcuts have become saved parameters\nThe functionality of shortcuts (saving the values entered in a report form in order to rerun the report with the same values) is now saved parameters. Any report with saved parameters will show them in a selectable drop-down menu at the top of the form. For more, see\nSaved parameters\nbelow.\nRun reports\nTo run a report:\nOn\nhttps://pdq.dd.iu.edu\n, select the report name to open the report run form.\nEnter values for each of the form parameters.\nUnder "Output format", if the report developer has enabled multiple formats, select the format you want the output to be delivered in.\nUnder "Output destination", select\nWait\nto wait for the report to run and the output to be displayed in the browser, or\nSend to Completed Reports\nto have the report run in the background and be notified when it has finished. You will receive an email from the Central Notification Service when the report finishes.\nNote:\nIf you don't have the option to send the report to Completed Reports, it's because the report has been classified as sensitive. You may only run it and wait for output.\nIf you want to save the values you have entered for form parameters, select\nSave Parameters\n. For more, see\nSaved parameters\nbelow.\nChoose\nRun\n.\nNote:\nBy default, PDQ reports in the develop context will execute queries to DSS against DSS1TST, while reports in the main context will execute DSS queries against DSS1PRD. To override this and have the report run against the other DSS instance, instead of\nRun\n, select\nRun against Prod\nfor reports in the develop context, or\nRun against Test\nfor reports in the main context.\nView completed report output\nTo view reports you selected to go to completed reports, visit\nCompleted reports\n. Under the "Action" drop-down menu, choose\nDetails\nto view the joblog entry for that report run.\nJoblog\nThe joblog entry has links to the report output and to logs from the report execution, as well as metadata about the run.\nNote:\nReport output is retained for 30 days before deletion. You can choose\nremove now\nto delete output immediately, or extend the retention period to 60 days by choosing\nextend 30 days\n.\nWhile the report output is avilable, you can run the report again using the parameters from the run that generated this joblog; to do so, at the bottom of the page, choose\nReplicate\n. This will take you to the report run form with all form parameters that were entered for this run filled in. To save these parameters separately from the joblog, see\nCreate saved parameters\nbelow.\nSaved parameters\nCreate saved parameters\nAs you enter values into a PDQ report form, you may save them to be loaded back into the form later. To do so, after entering values for fields in a report form, at the bottom select\nSave Parameters\n. You will be prompted to enter a unique name to save them with.\nTo have your saved parameters populate the form fields, at the top of the form, select the name from the "Populate with saved parameters" selector.\nTo update existing saved parameters that you have loaded previously, when saving leave the name unchanged and check the "Overwrite existing saved parameters?" box before choosing\nSave\n.\nTo manage saved parameters, next to the "Populate with saved parameters" selector, choose\nManage\nto go to the\nSaved parameters page\n.\nManage saved parameters\nThe\nSaved parameters page\ndisplays the saved parameters you have access to in two sections. The top section shows saved parameters you have created. The bottom section shows saved parameters created by other users that they have shared with an ACM group you are a member of. For shared parameters you have created, the "Actions" drop-down allows you to take several actions:\nDetails\ntakes you to the detail page for the saved parameters where you can view their metadata, manage sharing them, and create scheduled jobs using them.\nrun\nallows you to run a report using the saved parameters.\ndelete\nallows you to delete the saved parameters.\nrename\nallows you to change the name under which the saved parameters are stored.\nShare saved parameters\nThe saved parameters that have been shared with you will appear in the saved parameters drop-down selector in the form of the report they were created for. You cannot modify saved parameters shared with you; you can only load them in report forms. If you would like to give saved parameters shared with you a personal name distinct from the name given by the creator, under the "Actions" drop-down, choose\nChange Personal Name\nand enter a name. This name will only appear for you.\nTo share saved parameters you have created, on the "Details" page, choose\nShare\n. In the "Select Sharing Group" popup, enter the name of an ACM group to share them with, and then choose\nSave\n. To stop sharing, on the "Details" page, select\nStop Sharing\n.\nSchedule jobs using saved parameters\nTo run a report on a schedule, create a scheduled job using saved parameters. To do so:\nOn the saved parameters "Details" page, choose\nSchedule Job\n.\nFill out the form to configure how you want the schedule to function.\nTo have the job run on the schedule you entered, choose\nSave\n. Alternatively, to keep a scheduled job but prevent it from running, select the\nDisable this schedule\ncheckbox and then choose\nSave\n.\nTo see all your scheduled jobs, go to\nScheduled jobs\n.	KB0026478	KB0026478 v2.0	 a scheduled <strong>job</strong> using saved parameters. To do so: On the saved parameters "Details" page, choose Schedule <strong>Job</strong>. Fill out the form to configure how you want the schedule to function. To have the <strong>job</strong> run on the schedule you entered, choose Save. Alternatively, to keep a scheduled <strong>job</strong> but prevent it from	60.4304		2026-01-31 17:17:47.43471	2026-01-31 17:17:47.43471	0	7df4dfa2470e16509286dfbd436d4323
11776	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9129e06f477d7a502fc6a55b416d43e7	Request a new AppKube application	Note:\nThe following information is technical and intended for web application developers. AppKube is still under active development and this information may change. Subscribe to this article to get updates.\nTable of contents\nRequest form\nAfter you submit your request\nWhile awaiting approval\nWhen your AppKube repository is ready\nSupport\nRequest form\nTo request a new AppKube application, go to\nhttps://webapps.iu.edu/\n, select\nRequest New Application\n, and then provide the following in the request form:\nGroup account name:\nMust be a valid IU group account\nYou'll need to\ncreate a new group account for use with your application\nif you don’t already have one.\nYour application will be owned by this group.\nApplication name:\nMust be lowercase, alphanumeric, with dashes only (for example,\nmy-iu-app\n)\nDetermines default test and production URLs on\n*.webapps-test.iu.edu\nand\n*.webapps.iu.edu\nFor example, if your application name is\nmy-iu-app\n, your default test and production URLs would be\nmy-iu-app.webapps-test.iu.edu\nand\nmy-iu-app.webapps.iu.edu\n, respectively.\nYou can point your\ncustom virtual hostname\nto the production URL later.\nDetermines the name of the GitHub repository that will be generated for you\nTry to limit application names to fewer than 15 characters, as many other features such as\ncron jobs\ncan also add characters that ultimately can exhaust Kubernetes' system-imposed character limit of 52.\nApplication type:\nChoose from these options:\nStatic\nAn application that only includes static files (no server-side PHP)\nPHP 8.2/8.3\nChoose the latest version of PHP unless your application needs to run on an earlier version for compatibility reasons.\nLaravel with PHP 8.2/8.3\nSame as PHP 8.2/8.3 but mounts in a writable storage​ folder which is required for Laravel to function\nNote:\nAppKube does not currently support other server-side programming languages (Python, PERL, etc.).\nDatabase requirement:\nChoose\nNo Database\nor select the database your application needs (Denodo, MySQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL, SQL Server).\nIf MySQL is selected:\nYou'll be asked in a follow-up communication to provide details about your MySQL database to be set up as\napplication secrets\n.\nYour application can\nconnect to MySQL via mysqli or PDO\n.\nNote:\nYou can always add a database to your application later.\nAuthentication method:\nChoose\nNo Authentication\nor\nIU Login\n.\nIf IU Login is selected:\nIf you are using a newly created application URL, you will need to register your app (both test and production URLs, as applicable to your needs) as a\nCAS service provider\n.\nUsers of your application will be required to sign in with their IU username.\nAppKube handles authentication setup for your container. To limit access to a specific directory, include the following code in a\n.htaccess\nfile within that directory:\nAuthGroupFile /dev/null\nAuthType CAS\nAuthName "IU Network ID"\n# Allow anyone with an IU username\nRequire valid-user\n# Or allow only specific IU users\nRequire user username1 username2 username3\nLDAP requirement:\nChoose\nNo LDAP\nor\nActive Directory\n.\nIf Active Directory is selected, you only need to specify Auth Name, AuthType, and Require statements in your\n.htaccess\nfile (see below):\nAuthType CAS\nAuthName "IU Login"\nRequire ldap-group CN=YourGroup,OU=Accounts,DC=ads,DC=iu,DC=edu\nNote:\nYou can always add IU Login or LDAP authentication to your application later.\nUses storage?\nCheck if you need persistent, writeable file storage (for example, uploads, caches).\nEnter the estimated amount of storage in gigabytes your application needs (subject to approval).\nRequires cron job?\nCheck if you need scheduled\n(\ncron\n) tasks\n.\nBusiness owner email:\nEmail address for the product or service owner\nIf possible, use a group account email address instead of a personal IU email address.\nThe business contact is not necessarily the technical contact (see below).\nTechnical contact email:\nEmail address for the application's technical contact\nIf possible, use a group account email address instead of a personal IU email address.\nACM group:\nThis drop-down will contain all\nACM groups\nof which you are a member.\nThe group controls who can access the GitHub repository and the AppKube management console. It does not control who can use your application on the web. Use IU Login or LDAP authentication to manage end-user access.\nThis ACM group must be\nset up to provision to Active Directory\n.\nCreate a new ACM group if you don't already have one for this purpose.\nDescription:\nDescribe your application's purpose and functionality.\nAfter you submit your request\nYour request will go to the AppKube administrators for review. Once approved:\nYour test environment will be set up on AppKube.\nA private GitHub repo will be created at\nhttps://github.com/iu-apps\nwith the following name:\n<group account>-<app name>\n.\nThe technical contact will receive an email with next steps.\nWhile awaiting approval\nCreate or update a GitHub.com account:\nTo maintain your code for AppKube, you will need to have an account on GitHub.com. You can use an existing one or\ncreate a new GitHub.com account\n. Once you have an account, you will need to do the following:\nIf your account uses an email address other than your iu.edu email address, you will need\nadd your IU email address to your account\n.\nSet up\ntwo-factor authentication\non GitHub. You must use a TOTP authenticator app such as Duo or 1Password (SMS will not work).\nReview and update your PHP code:\nReview your application's PHP code for compatibility with the AppKube environment (see\nUpdate PHP code for use on AppKube\n).\nWhen your AppKube repository is ready\nIf you already manage your application's code in GitHub, you will need to push code from your existing GitHub.com or GitHub@IU repositories into your new repository (see\nMigrate code from an existing GitHub repository to your AppKube repository\n).\nIf you do not already use GitHub to manage your application's code, you will need to\ncommit your code into your new repository\n.\nAfter you push your code to your new private repository, respond to the original email you received after your application was approved (or email\napphelp@iu.edu)\n. AppKube administrators will add the following to your repository:\nGitHub Actions workflows to deploy to test and production\nCODEOWNERS\nfile, which restricts editing of workflow files to AppKube administrators only\nAppKube administrators will also help you set up application secrets (database credentials, API keys, etc.) as environment variables for use in your PHP code. Learn more about\nmanaging secrets and configuration in AppKube\n.\nManage code and deployments using your AppKube GitHub repository\n.\nImportant:\nNever\ncommit application secrets to GitHub.\nSupport\nFor help requesting a new AppKube application, email\napphelp@iu.edu\n.	KB0026766	KB0026766 v11.0	Note: The following information is technical and intended for web application developers. AppKube is still under active development and this information may change. Subscribe to this article to get updates. Table of contents Request form After you submit your request While awaiting approval When your AppKube repository is ready Support Request f	0.0001	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:17:37.177607	2026-01-31 17:26:25.204702	0	9129e06f477d7a502fc6a55b416d43e7
11186	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=59dfacf7476456583d373803846d439b	Track changes to your project in IU REDCap	In\nIU REDCap\n, use the data logging tool to track changes to your project. The tool provides access to the data log or audit trail created by REDCap. In your project, access the data logging tool from the menu on the left.\nFor each event that changes data in the database, REDCap records the time and date, the username of the person logged in at the time, the type of event, and the changes made. The entire audit trail is stored during the lifecycle of the database.\nIf you have questions about IU\nREDCap\n, or need help setting up or managing an IU\nREDCap\nproject, email IU\nREDCap\nSupport at\nredcap@iu.edu\n.	KB0023638	KB0023638 v1.0	In <strong>IU</strong> REDCap, use the data logging tool to track changes to your project. The tool provides access the menu on the left. For each event that changes data in the <strong>database</strong>, REDCap records the time. The entire audit trail is stored during the lifecycle of the <strong>database</strong>.	0.7554		2026-01-31 17:01:32.485179	2026-01-31 17:01:32.485179	0	59dfacf7476456583d373803846d439b
11188	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=66003c3f476456583d373803846d43c6	Make changes to an IU REDCap project that is currently in production	To make changes to an\nIU REDCap\nproject that has "Production" status:\nIn the Online Form Editor, select\nEnter Draft Mode\n, and then apply your changes.\nWhen you're finished editing, in the Online Form Editor, select\nSubmit Changes for Review\n.\nThe REDCap administrator will review your changes to make sure they don't impact data you have already collected. If anything is questionable, you may receive an email from the administrator to confirm that you want to make the change.\nNote:\nAny changes to the structure of the project, such removing forms or modifying events in a longitudinal project, can be made only by the REDCap administrator.\nIf you have questions about IU\nREDCap\n, or need help setting up or managing an IU\nREDCap\nproject, email IU\nREDCap\nSupport at\nredcap@iu.edu\n.	KB0024777	KB0024777 v1.0	To make changes to an <strong>IU</strong> REDCap project that has "Production" status: In the Online Form Editor, select Enter Draft Mode, and then apply your changes. When you're finished editing, in the Online Form Editor, select Submit Changes for Review. The REDCap administrator will review your changes to make	0.7554		2026-01-31 17:01:36.572764	2026-01-31 17:01:36.572764	0	66003c3f476456583d373803846d43c6
11314	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=0ebf2c77476456583d373803846d4375	Find published printers in the IU Active Directory	If you add a printer to Indiana University's\nActive\nDirectory\nand then open the Active Directory Users and Computers snap-in to look for it, you may not be able to find the printer listed anywhere.\nTo view printers in the Active Directory:\nFrom the\nView\nmenu, select\nUsers, Groups, and\nComputers as containers\n.\nFind the appropriate computer in the Active Directory Users and Computers snap-in.\nDouble-click the computer. You will be able to view the printers attached to the computer if you selected them to be published in the Active Directory.\nNote:\nFor help with\nActive Directory\n, contact your\nlocal UITS support person\n.	KB0023001	KB0023001 v1.0	If you add a printer to Indiana University's Active Directory and then open the Active Directory Users and Computers snap-in to look for it, you may not be able to find the printer listed anywhere. To view printers in the Active Directory: From the View menu, select Users, Groups, and Computers as containers. Find the appropriate computer in the	44.5456	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:05:46.15823	2026-01-31 17:05:46.15823	0	0ebf2c77476456583d373803846d4375
11190	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6e007c3f476456583d373803846d437a	Use the Data Dictionary to change the structure of your data collection instruments in IU REDCap	In\nIU REDCap\n, the Data Dictionary is a spreadsheet in CSV format representing the structure of your database. Your Data Dictionary contains the metadata used to construct your data collection instruments. It captures the same information as the Online Form Editor, but displays it in a more concise format.\nEach row in a Data Dictionary spreadsheet corresponds to a field in your data entry form. The order of questions in a Data Dictionary file defines the order they appear in the database.\nYou can use the Data Dictionary to change the structure of your data collection instruments. If you have many variables, or are using advanced functionalities (branching logic and calculated fields), use the Data Dictionary to modify your project. Changes you make with the Data Dictionary are not propagated in real time to your project; changes are not committed until you upload the modified Data Dictionary file. Changes for development databases will take effect immediately, but changes to production databases will need to be reviewed and approved by the REDCap administrator before they take effect. The new structure will apply to both new and existing records.\nAfter the Data Dictionary has been uploaded to REDCap, a human-readable version is accessible by clicking the appropriate link at the top of the "Quick Tasks" section on the project home page. This version is called the "Codebook", and it serves as a quick reference that lets you view the attributes of any given field in the project without having to download and interpret the Data Dictionary.\nIf you have questions about IU\nREDCap\n, or need help setting up or managing an IU\nREDCap\nproject, email IU\nREDCap\nSupport at\nredcap@iu.edu\n.	KB0024790	KB0024790 v1.0	In <strong>IU</strong> REDCap, the Data Dictionary is a spreadsheet in CSV format representing the structure of your <strong>database</strong>. Your Data Dictionary contains the metadata used to construct your data collection. The order of questions in a Data Dictionary file defines the order they appear in the <strong>database</strong>	0.7554		2026-01-31 17:01:40.009635	2026-01-31 17:01:40.009635	0	6e007c3f476456583d373803846d437a
1422	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0025241	About UITS	University Information Technology Services\n(UITS) at Indiana University develops and maintains a modern information technology environment throughout the university in support of IU's vision for excellence in research, teaching, outreach, and lifelong learning.\nTech support:\nFor immediate help using technology at IU, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nBusiness issues:\nFor questions or help related to UITS business, contact the UITS receptionist at 812-855-9255.\nGeneral information:\nFor general university or campus information, contact\nAskIU\n.\nIf you would like to tell us about how we've done, or if you feel your query has not been addressed via the above channels, you can email\nitombuds@iu.edu\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:32:41.739544	2026-01-31 14:36:05.548314	1	KB0025241
11192	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=95df6cf7476456583d373803846d43f8	Longitudinal projects in IU REDCap	In\nIU REDCap\n, longitudinal projects are similar to traditional data collection projects in that multiple data entry forms are defined. However, unlike the traditional model, forms in a longitudinal project can be completed repeatedly for a single record. The longitudinal model allows any data entry page to be repeated any number of times across predefined time-points specified by the user before data is collected.\nTherefore, instead of repeating a data entry form multiple times in the Data Dictionary, you can have one longitudinal project that is repeated\nN\nnumber of times.\nA longitudinal project lets you define events that allow data collection forms to be used multiple times for any given database record. An event may be a temporal event in the course of your project, such as a participant visit, or a task to be performed. After you have defined events, you must designate the data entry forms for any or all events, thereby enabling the use of one form for multiple events in the same database record.\nYou can group your events into arms; your project may have one or more arms. Each arm can have as many events as you wish. You can use the table provided to create new events and/or arms, or modify existing ones. (One arm and one event is the initial default for all databases).\nLongitudinal projects are most commonly used for collecting clinical and research data.\nIf you have questions about IU\nREDCap\n, or need help setting up or managing an IU\nREDCap\nproject, email IU\nREDCap\nSupport at\nredcap@iu.edu\n.	KB0023620	KB0023620 v1.0	In <strong>IU</strong> REDCap, longitudinal projects are similar to traditional data collection projects <strong>database</strong> record. An event may be a temporal event in the course of your project <strong>database</strong> record. You can group your events into arms; your project may have one or more arms. Each	0.7554		2026-01-31 17:01:43.397921	2026-01-31 17:01:43.397921	0	95df6cf7476456583d373803846d43f8
11194	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c1df6cf7476456583d373803846d438b	Get help using IU REDCap	The\nIndiana Clinical and Translational Sciences Institute (\nIndiana CTSI\n)\nprovides technical support and consultations, education, and project development help for\nIU REDCap\nusers.\nIf you have questions about IU\nREDCap\n, or need help setting up or managing an IU\nREDCap\nproject, email IU\nREDCap\nSupport at\nredcap@iu.edu\n.	KB0023609	KB0023609 v1.0	The Indiana Clinical and Translational Sciences Institute (Indiana CTSI) provides technical support and consultations, education, and project development help for <strong>IU</strong> REDCap users.	0.7554		2026-01-31 17:01:47.561651	2026-01-31 17:01:47.561651	0	c1df6cf7476456583d373803846d438b
11196	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=cb00fc3f476456583d373803846d43dc	Import and export data in IU REDCap	Table of contents\nImport data\nExport data\nGet help\nImport data\nImportant:\nBefore storing data on any of Indiana University's research computing\nor storage systems, make sure you understand the information in\nTypes of sensitive institutional data appropriate for UITS Research Technologies services\n.\nMake sure you do not include sensitive institutional data as part of a file's filename or pathname.\nIn\nIU REDCap\n, use the Data Import tool to import data from a comma-delimited (CSV) file:\nFrom the Data Import tool, download your Data Import Template (click\nDownload your Data Import Template\n) and save it to your computer as a CSV file.\nOpen the template and enter the data you want to import. To prevent upload errors, reduce upload time, and ensure your database is processed correctly:\nDo not change the Variables/Field Names in the file.\nEnter only numerical values in the "dropdown" and "radio" cells.\nDelete any empty rows and columns before importing the file.\nWhen you are finished, save the file.\nFrom the Data Import tool, select\nBrowse\n, and then select the file on your computer.\nSelect\nUpload File\n. The Data Import Tool will display the number of records that were created or modified during the import to allow you to confirm that all records were imported.\nNote:\nWhen the file is uploaded, its data will not be imported immediately. Data will be displayed and checked for formatting errors before they are imported into the database.\nExport data\nImportant:\nIf the IU\nREDCap\nproject data you are accessing contain protected health information (\nPHI\n), you must treat the data as PHI, which means files must be encrypted when they are stored (at rest) and when they are transferred from one system to another (in transit). For more, see:\nRecommended tools for encrypting data containing HIPAA-regulated PHI\nYour legal responsibilities for protecting data containing protected health information (PHI) when using UITS Research Technologies systems and services\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.\nIn\nIU REDCap\n, use the Data Export tool, accessed from your project using the link in the menu on the left, to export data. The tool provides several options for exporting data, including a simple one-click option for a quick dump of all records for your project.\nFor granular control over data export, choose\nAdvanced Export\n, which lets you select individual fields or entire data collection instruments for export. If you have the necessary user privileges on your project, you can use the data de-identification options to limit the amount of sensitive information that is exported.\nOnce you've selected all fields and optional de-identification boxes, at the bottom of the page, select\nSubmit\n. Subsequently, a page will appear that allows you to save the files to your computer. The files are comma-delimited and may be read into SPSS, Excel, R, SAS, and other analysis packages. Project data can also be exported in CDISC ODM format. If any fields in the database are tagged as Identifiers, those particular fields will be displayed in red.\nAn entire REDCap project can now be exported as a single XML file (which happens to be in CDISC ODM format). The file includes events, arms, instruments, fields, project attributes, and descriptive field attachments. If the project contains data, then users can also optionally export the project data (including uploaded files) in the same XML file. This file can serve as a snapshot or backup copy of the project.\nAlternatively, you can export all project data to a single PDF file. The PDF will retain the page format from your data entry or survey page, and include all data for all records for all data collection instruments.\nAnother data export option allows users with data export privileges to download a ZIP file containing the uploaded files for all records in the project (files uploaded to "File Upload" fields on data entry forms and/or surveys). The ZIP file will contain a folder with all the files organized by record name and variable/field name, and it will also contain an\nindex.html\nfile that lists the following attributes for the files: record name, variable/field name, original filename, and the date/time the file was uploaded.\nNote:\nREDCap automatically places backup copies of all exported files in the\nFile Repository\n.\nGet help\nIf you have questions about IU\nREDCap\n, or need help setting up or managing an IU\nREDCap\nproject, email IU\nREDCap\nSupport at\nredcap@iu.edu\n.	KB0024818	KB0024818 v1.0	 into the <strong>database</strong>. Export data Important: In <strong>IU</strong> REDCap, use the Data Export tool, accessed from yourTable of contents Import data Export data Get help Import data In <strong>IU</strong> REDCap, use the Data Import time, and ensure your <strong>database</strong> is processed correctly: Do not change the Variables/Field Names	0.7554		2026-01-31 17:01:52.164272	2026-01-31 17:01:52.164272	0	cb00fc3f476456583d373803846d43dc
10385	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=4ddda3b54756fe142fc6a55b416d43d0	Support Center In-Person Support job opportunities at IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis	Table of contents\nConsultant position\nJob description\nQualifications\nWorking conditions\nConsultant shifts\nCompensation\nApply online\nPolicy notice\nEmployee handbook\nConsultant position\nSupport Center In-Person Support\n(SCIPS) offers employees the opportunity to gain marketable customer service and technical skills. Our operational structure requires staff who are motivated by growth and opportunity. Many former consultants have used their experience to obtain positions with reputable firms and organizations worldwide.\nApplicants will be considered based on:\nCustomer service skills and work experience\nTechnical skills and abilities\nApplicant availability\nYou may submit one application to SCIPS per year; make sure your application is complete, accurate, and representative of your skills, abilities, and work history. Once submitted, your application will be reviewed and you will be notified of your status via email. Please be patient, as the hiring process often takes 30 to 60 days.\nJob description\nAt Indiana University Bloomington and IU Indianapolis, under general supervision, SCIPS consultants:\nProvide technical assistance in the UITS Consulting Services Mission areas on a demand basis.\nDesktop and mobile computing device platforms (including Windows, macOS, iOS, Android, and others)\nCentral systems (email, learning management systems, storage platforms, etc.)\nCard services (card issuance, card-related financial activity, card suspension and reinstatement, door access, payroll deduction, and many others)\nProvide direct assistance to clients of the\nStudent Technology Centers\n(STCs) for technical assistance relating to software and hardware issues, printer problems, supplies, and the overall lab environment.\nProvide direct assistance to campus housing residents in acquiring and maintaining their network connection to the university network.\nProduce and issue the official photo ID card (CrimsonCard) to students, employees, and affiliates, including identity verification, confirmation of eligibility, and payment processing.\nIn addition, consultants are required to demonstrate exemplary interpersonal communication skills and professionalism at all times, and support and comply with all Indiana University,\nUITS\n, and SCIPS policies and procedures.\nQualifications\nProactive customer service attitude\nStrong organizational and interpersonal skills\nProfessional attitude and demeanor\nExcellent written and verbal communication skills\nAt least one year of related customer service experience preferred\nComputing skills across the Windows and Mac platforms, including operating systems and applications\nCommitment to working a minimum of 12 hours per week\nWorking conditions\nConsultants will work in a variety of locations on campus, depending on staffing needs. The role requires the ability to effectively communicate and to operate computers and printers. The position involves sedentary work, as well as periods of time moving around campus. Ability to move up to 45 pounds. The person in this role must be able to perform the essential functions with or without an accommodation.\nConsultant shifts\nAll SCIPS consultants are required to work a minimum of 12 hours per week. Schedules are made for each fall, spring, and summer semester. SCIPS is open to clients Monday-Friday.\nCompensation\nSCIPS consultants are paid $15 per hour and are expected to complete the probationary training period within six weeks.\nIU part-time staff are paid on an hourly, biweekly basis, with no remission of academic fees and no commitment to continuous employment. Positions include work-study opportunities; for more, see the Student Central Work-Study pages for\nIU Bloomington\nor\nIU Indianapolis\n.\nPositions include work-study opportunities.  For more, see the Student Central Work-Study pages for\nIU Bloomington\nor\nIU Indianapolis\n.\nApply online\nTo apply for a SCIPS position, complete the\nApplication for Consultant Position\n.\nApplicants not affiliated with Indiana University should select\nContinue as Guest\n. Use the dropdown to select the appropriate application for your campus, IU Bloomington or IU Indianapolis.\nFor employment information, contact\nsciphr@iu.edu\n.\nPolicy notice\nNotice:\nIndiana University is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access/Affirmative Action institution. IU intends to maintain an alcohol- and drug-free workplace and to comply with the Drug Free Workplace Act of 1988 and its amendments. To that end, all employees must comply with the university's Substance Free Workplace policy. Policy statements and crime and fire statistics for Indiana University campuses are available at\nAnnual Security Reports\n.\nEmployee handbook\nFor more, see the hourly consultant\nHandbook\n.	KB0026114	KB0026114 v4.0	Table of contents Consultant position <strong>Job</strong> description Qualifications Working conditions Consultant shifts Compensation Apply online Policy notice Employee handbook Consultant position Support Center of your status via email. Please be patient, as the hiring process often takes 30 to 60 days. <strong>Job</strong>	44.077		2026-01-31 16:42:18.644436	2026-01-31 17:17:52.53297	0	4ddda3b54756fe142fc6a55b416d43d0
1431	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0022971	Your IU username	Your IU username is the name with which you log into your accounts and access other network services. For current Indiana University students, faculty, and staff, your username is assigned automatically based on your last name, first name, and possibly middle name.\nYour username is the section of your\nIU email address\nbefore the\n@\n(for example,\nusername\n@iu.edu\n), and is also part of your\nUniversity account\n.\nAll usernames at IU (for personal as well as group or departmental accounts) are from three to eight characters long.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:32:41.739544	2026-01-31 14:36:37.18607	1	KB0022971
11198	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e766ac5247a872182fc6a55b416d4306	New features available in IU REDCap	The UITS\nResearch Technologies\nBiomedical and Health Research Services (BHRS) regularly upgrades Indiana University's implementation of\nREDCap\nas new versions are released.\nThe latest release of IU REDCap, version 15.5, entered production on October 26, 2025. For a description of features and improvements included in the latest release, see the\nIU REDCap 15.5 Release Notes\n.\nIU's REDCap implementation is one of several research-related collaboration tools offered through the\nIndiana Clinical and Translational Sciences Institute (\nIndiana CTSI\n)\n.\nIf you have questions about IU\nREDCap\n, or need help setting up or managing an IU\nREDCap\nproject, email IU\nREDCap\nSupport at\nredcap@iu.edu\n.	KB0024486	KB0024486 v5.0	The UITS Research Technologies Biomedical and Health Research Services (BHRS) regularly upgrades Indiana University's implementation of REDCap as new versions are released. The latest release of <strong>IU</strong> and improvements included in the latest release, see the <strong>IU</strong> REDCap 15.5 Release Notes. IU's REDCap	0.7554		2026-01-31 17:01:56.16863	2026-01-31 17:01:56.16863	0	e766ac5247a872182fc6a55b416d4306
11205	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c8d335581bc9169416c02fc4bd4bcb28	Forward your IU email to another address using Microsoft Outlook or Outlook on the web	Important:\nBefore setting your IU email address to forward to a non-IU service (for example, personal Gmail, Hotmail, Yahoo!), note:\nUITS\ncannot guarantee email delivery to non-IU accounts\n, delays in delivery can occur, and the UITS Support Center may not be able to help with some problems concerning non-IU accounts. If your email forwarding fails for any reason,\nyou may\nmiss important official communications sent to your IU address for\nwhich you are still responsible.\nFor more, see\nIf you forward your IU email address and aren't receiving external email messages\n.\nFaculty, staff, residents, and students in\nHIPAA Affected Areas\nwhose roles may require sharing\nprotected health information\n(\nPHI\n) via email (such as the Schools of Medicine, Dentistry, or Optometry) are\nnot\npermitted to set up mail forwarding to outside accounts, as this could result in the violation of federal and state laws.  You\nare\npermitted to forward your IU email to your IU Health (\nusername\n@iuhealth.org\n) email account. For more, see\nHIPAA Privacy and Security Compliance\n.\nTable of Contents\nClassic Outlook for Windows\nNew Outlook and Outlook on the web\nOutlook for Mac\nClassic Outlook\nfor Windows\nNote:\nThese instructions apply to\nOutlook\ninstallations that are configured to access an Indiana University Microsoft\nExchange\nServer mailbox.\nIn\nOutlook\nfor Windows, you can use\nOutlook\nInbox rules to handle all incoming mail so you can easily forward, filter, or move it. To do so in\nOutlook 2016\nor earlier:\nFrom the\nHome\ntab, select\nRules\n, and then\nManage Rules & Alerts...\n.\nFrom the\nRules\ntab, choose\nNew Rule...\n.\nIn the Rules Wizard that opens, select\nApply rule on messages I receive\n.\nDo\nnot\ncheck any boxes under "Which condition(s) do you want to check?" Choose\nNext\n.\nWhen asked, "This rule will be applied to every message you receive. Is this correct?", choose\nYes\n.\nIf you do not want a copy left in your Inbox, check\ndelete it\n.\nCheck\nforward it to people or public group\n, and then, in the lower pane, select\npeople or public group\n. The "Rule Address" window will open along with the address book.\nNote:\nUse the forward option instead of the redirect option, because the redirect option will cause messages to fail DMARC; for more, see\nDomain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance (DMARC)\n.\nUnder "Specify to whom to forward", in the right pane, enter the email address to which you wish to forward your mail.\nYou may also enter the full internet address for a non-IU account where you want your mail to be forwarded.\nChoose\nOK\n, and then\nNext\n.\nApply exceptions only if you do not want the rule to forward all messages. Choose\nNext\n.\nUnder "Please specify a name for this rule:", type something descriptive; for example,\nForward all messages to Yahoo\n.\nIf you want all of the messages currently in your Inbox to be forwarded according to this rule, check\nRun this rule now on messages already in "Inbox"\n. (Leaving it unchecked will forward all messages that arrive after you finish creating this rule.) If you want this rule to start working immediately, leave\nTurn this rule on\nchecked.\nSelect\nFinish\n, and then select\nOK\nto close the Rules Wizard.\nAny mail sent to your\nExchange\nmailbox will be forwarded to the address you listed in the above procedure. You may return to the Rules Wizard at any time to toggle this rule on or off.\nNew Outlook and Outlook on the web\nImportant:\nAt this time, UITS does not recommend using Microsoft's new Outlook for shared mailboxes.  For details and other known issues, see\nKnown issues with new Outlook\n. For feature comparisons with the new application, see\nGetting started with the new Outlook for Windows\n.\nNote:\nYou can't add or edit rules in\nOutlook on the web\nLight.\nLog into\nOutlook on the web\nwith your\nusername\n@iu.edu\naddress.\nSelect the\nSettings\nicon (round gear) at the top right.\nIn the first column, choose\nMail\n. Then, in the second column, choose\nRules\n.\nUnder\nRules\n, choose the plus icon ("Add new rule").\nGive your new rule a name that you can easily recognize, such as "Mail forwarding".\nUnder "Add a condition", use the drop-down menu to select\nApply to all messages\n.\nUnder "Add an action", use the drop-down menu to select\nForward to\n, and enter the desired email address in the box that appears to the right.\nWhen finished, choose\nSave\n.\nNote:\nUse the forward option instead of the redirect option, because the redirect option will cause messages to fail DMARC; for more, see\nDomain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance (DMARC)\n.\nOutlook\nfor Mac\nTo forward your incoming mail in\nOutlook\nfor Mac:\nIn\nOutlook 2019\nor\nOutlook 365\non Mac, at the top left, choose\nOutlook\n, and then select\nPreferences...\n.\nUnder "Email", select\nRules\n.\nAt the bottom of the window, select\n+\n(the plus sign).\nNext to "Rule name:", enter a descriptive name for the rule.\nUnder "When a message arrives that meets all these conditions:", from the drop-down menu, select\nAll Messages\n.\nUnder "Do the following:", select\nForward To:\n, and then enter the address to which you want to forward your email in the text box.\nNote:\nUse the forward option instead of the redirect option, because the redirect option will cause messages to fail DMARC; for more, see\nDomain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance (DMARC)\n.\nChoose\nOK\n. Close the "Rules" window, and then, when prompted to save your changes, choose\nSave\n.	KB0022717	KB0022717 v2.0	 address for a non-<strong>IU</strong> account where you want your mail to be forwarded. Choose OK, and then Next	0.7553		2026-01-31 17:02:12.641435	2026-01-31 17:02:12.641435	0	c8d335581bc9169416c02fc4bd4bcb28
11351	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5900343f476456583d373803846d4367	Report problems with the IMU Campus Link computers	Report any problems with the IMU Campus Link computers to\nimutech@indiana.edu\n. Though these computers are running the UITS STC PC build, the IMU maintains them. Neal Heidler is the contact person.\nThe kiosk machines by the IMU Tree Suites on the Mezzanine Level are managed by the IMU as well.	KB0024674	KB0024674 v1.0	Report any problems with the IMU Campus Link computers to imutech@indiana.edu. Though these computers are running the UITS STC PC build, the IMU maintains them. Neal Heidler is the contact person. The kiosk machines by the IMU Tree Suites on the Mezzanine Level are managed by the IMU as well.	30.3323	km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:07:22.726856	2026-01-31 17:07:22.726856	0	5900343f476456583d373803846d4367
11209	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=28cfa8b7476456583d373803846d43b4	Data storage in Adobe Creative Cloud	Important:\nIndividuals who work with institutional data are responsible for ensuring that those data are stored in approved locations. In addition, regulated data such as health information or personally identifiable information are often subject to federal and state laws (for example,\nHIPAA\nand FERPA) that require users to apply additional security measures.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nStorage capacity\nInstitutional data\nGet help\nOverview\nAdobe Creative Cloud has two storage options: Creative Cloud storage and Document Cloud storage.\nCreative Cloud storage is appropriate for personal files and creative work. All Adobe Creative Cloud desktop applications and services, including Adobe mobile applications, can access Creative Cloud storage.\nDocument Cloud is a storage option for Adobe Acrobat and is appropriate for storing personal documents.\nStorage capacity\nAdobe Creative Cloud accounts have the following data storage capacities:\nCreative Cloud storage:\n100 GB\nDocument Cloud storage:\n100 GB\nStorage capacities cannot be changed at this time.\nInstitutional data\nAdobe Creative Cloud storage and Document Cloud storage are both approved for\ninstitutional data\nclassified as Public or\nUniversity-internal\n.\nOfficial classifications for institutional data at IU are defined in\nManagement of Institutional Data (DM-01)\n. If you have questions about the classifications of institutional data at IU, see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n, use the\nData Sharing and Handling (DSH) tool\n, or contact the appropriate\nData Stewards\n. To determine the most sensitive classification of institutional data you can store on any given UITS service, see\nChoose an appropriate storage solution\n.\nGet help\nAdobe provides online\nSupport\nand\nContact\ninformation.\nTroubleshoot Creative Cloud download and installation issues\n.\nContact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0023212	KB0023212 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Storage capacity Institutional data Get help Overview Adobe Creative Cloud has two storage options: Creative Cloud storage and Document Cloud storage. Creative Cloud storage is appropriate for personal files and creative work. All Adobe Creative Cloud desktop applications and services, including Adobe mobile applicatio	0.7551		2026-01-31 17:02:16.19635	2026-01-31 17:02:16.19635	0	28cfa8b7476456583d373803846d43b4
11210	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=47ef247b476456583d373803846d43ae	FireForm compliance	Table of contents\nOverview\nConfigure data classification and category\nConfigure compliance requirements\nCompliance checks options\nResolve compliance requirement restrictions\nGet help\nOverview\nFireForm is a business process management tool.  As an administrative user of FireForm, you are responsible for adherence to Indiana University data management policies and standards.  For information about these policies, visit\nInformation Security and Policy\n.\nIU is subject to laws and regulations that safeguard information privacy. Becoming familiar with privacy requirements will help you develop forms that meet organizational goals.\nFireForm is approved to store all classifications of institutional data. You are responsible for selecting the appropriate classification and data category on form elements.\nOfficial classifications for institutional data at IU are defined in\nManagement of Institutional Data (DM-01)\n. If you have questions about the classifications of institutional data at IU, see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n, use the\nData Sharing and Handling (DSH) tool\n, or contact the appropriate\nData Stewards\n. To determine the most sensitive classification of institutional data you can store on any given UITS service, see\nChoose an appropriate storage solution\n.\nConfigure data classification and category\nTo apply the data classification and data domain on a form element, make the following selections when creating the element:\nData Classification:\nA data classification must be selected based on the content the user will enter; see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n.\nElement Domain:\nDesignate the type of data that will be collected in this form field. This is required for\nRestricted data\n,\nCritical data\n, and\nPHI\n.\nConfigure compliance requirements\nA FireForm administrator may apply compliance requirements on forms. A compliance requirement on a form will prevent users from viewing or accessing the form’s entries until they have completed the compliance tutorial associated with the requirement.\nTo apply compliance requirements on a form, complete the following steps:\nNavigate to and select the form from\nTools\n>\nForms\n.\nOn the form, select\nEdit\n.\nOpen the\nForm Permissions\n.\nSelect any or all the requirements listed under "Compliance Checks".\nCompliance checks options\nThe available compliance requirements and associated tutorials are listed below:\nAcceptable Use Agreement\nFERPA tutorial\nHIPAA Mobile\nHIPAA Privacy\nHIPAA Security (designed for OVPIT)\nHRMS Data Use tutorial\nResolve compliance restrictions\nTo view the compliance status for a user, visit\nEnterprise Application Compliance\n. This website links to the tutorials required for completing the requirements. A user must complete the appropriate compliance tutorial to remove the filtering restriction from their entries' lists.\nGet help\nIf you have questions about compliance policies at IU, visit\nUniversity Compliance\n.\nIf you have questions about information security polices at IU, visit\nInformation Security and Policy\n.\nIf you have general inquires or currently use FireForm, your questions may be answered by searching the IU Knowledge Base or reviewing the\nFireForm at IU help menu\n.\nIf you have additional questions, need to report a bug, or would like to make enhancement requests for the FireForm system, submit a\nFireForm Support Request\n.	KB0024118	KB0024118 v1.0	 these policies, visit Information Security and Policy. <strong>IU</strong> is subject to laws and regulations policies at <strong>IU</strong>, visit University Compliance. If you have questions about information security polices at <strong>IU</strong>, visit Information Security and Policy. If you have general inquires or currently use FireForm	0.7551		2026-01-31 17:02:20.23074	2026-01-31 17:02:20.23074	0	47ef247b476456583d373803846d43ae
4080	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a40a5c2d3335ead404e605570e5c7b35	About the IU VPN	Table of contents\nOverview\nVPN at IU\nConnect to the VPN\nOverview\nA virtual private network (VPN) allows you to temporarily create or join a private network across an existing public network by creating an encrypted tunnel between two hosts. The encryption protects the data your computer or mobile device transmits and receives over the internet, enabling secure remote access to restricted online resources.\nMany public wireless networks (such as those provided by hotels and restaurants) are not encrypted, leaving transmitted data unprotected and vulnerable to electronic surveillance. Even data originating from a secure network (for example, one provided by your employer or school) may be vulnerable if they are being transmitted to another secure network over the public internet. To prevent data from being intercepted (especially confidential financial data, or data protected by federal laws, such as\nFERPA\nand\nHIPAA\ndata), businesses, government offices, universities, and other institutions use VPN systems to encrypt data transmitted between their secure networks and remote users.\nVPN at IU\nAt Indiana University,\nUITS\ndelivers a centrally managed, fault-tolerant SSL VPN service for use by all IU campuses.\nEstablishing an IU VPN connection creates a secure tunnel between your off-campus computer or mobile device and the IU network, allowing you to access online services and resources that are configured to refuse connections from outside the IU network (such as\nIU Print\nqueues, certain library databases, and most network storage devices). Also, you can use an IU VPN connection or\nIUanyWare\nto make a\nremote desktop connection\nfrom off campus.\nNote:\nWhenever you are off campus and cannot connect to an IU service, connect to the IU VPN to check whether that fixes the problem.\nFor more about what traffic is encrypted with IU VPN connections, see\nVPN split tunneling at IU\n.\nConnect to the VPN\nImportant:\nAs of February 19, 2025, the process for connecting to the IU SSL VPN has changed:\nOpen Ivanti Secure Access:\nWindows:\nFrom the task bar, right-click the\nIvanti Secure Access\nicon, and then choose\nConnect\n.\nmacOS:\nFrom the menu bar, select the\nIvanti Secure Access\nicon, choose the SSL VPN profile you created, and then\nConnect\n.\nWhen you connect, you will be presented with the IU Login prompt to enter your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nAfter successfully authenticating, you will see a prompt to authenticate via Duo and should proceed as normal.\nYou will then see a prompt for a secondary password.\nImportant:\nThe behavior of this field has changed. This prompt will require you to enter their IU passphrase again to finalize the authentication, rather than entering\npush\nor any other command or code.\nFor the greatest reliability across the widest range of platforms and network configurations, UITS recommends using the\nIvanti Secure Access (formerly Pulse Secure)\nclient, where available, to connect to IU's SSL VPN.\nFor instructions for connecting to IU's SSL VPN, see:\nWindows or macOS:\nConnect to IU's SSL VPN using Ivanti Secure Access (formerly Pulse Secure) in Windows or macOS\niOS:\nConnect to IU's SSL VPN with Ivanti Secure Access (formerly Pulse Secure) on an iOS device\nAndroid mobile:\nConnect to IU's SSL VPN with Ivanti Secure Access (formerly Pulse Secure) from an Android mobile device\nChromebook:\nConnect to IU's SSL VPN with Ivanti Secure Access (formerly Pulse Secure) from a Chromebook\nLinux:\nConnect to IU's SSL VPN using Ivanti Secure Access (formerly Pulse Secure) on a 32- or 64-bit Linux workstation\nConnect to IU's SSL VPN using OpenConnect on a 64-bit Linux workstation\nImportant:\nUse the IU VPN only under one of these conditions:\nIf you are trying to access a service you can't get to another way.\nIf your local UITS support person has told you that you need to use it.\nGenerally, you won't need to use the VPN if you are a student. For example, you don't need a VPN connection to:\nUse learning tools, such as Canvas, Zoom, or Kaltura.\nRead your IU email over the web.\nWork with your files in\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nor\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n.\nIf you have difficulty with the VPN:\nTry using\nIUanyWare\n(doesn't require a VPN connection).\nFor example, you can use IUanyWare's Remote Desktop Connection app to remote into a device on campus. Additionally, IUanyWare's Chrome browser allows you to access sites as if you were on campus.\nIn IUanyWare, search the available apps to find what may be helpful.\nOn the IU network:\nYou cannot connect to the VPN, as it is for off-campus use only.\nIf you receive a 1329 error when attempting to connect to the VPN, try connecting using a non-eduroam or non-IU Secure connection.\nNote:\nTo ensure accountability of network communication, the\nUniversity Information Policy Office\nprohibits\ngroup accounts\nfrom connecting to IU's SSL VPN. To make a VPN connection, you must log in using your personal\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nUITS staff and local UITS support people:\nAlthough group accounts cannot access the SSL VPN by default, exceptions are allowed for areas using group accounts to fulfill separation of duty requirements in the groups or two-factor realms. For more, see\nGroup accounts and the SSL VPN\n.\nIf you have questions or need help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:43:34.007473	2026-01-31 15:07:27.377911	1	a40a5c2d3335ead404e605570e5c7b35
11212	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9d456a0547023a142fc6a55b416d4310	File storage in Canvas	Table of contents\nOverview\nStorage quotas\nWork with institutional data\nGet help\nOverview\nThe Files tool serves as the file repository in\nCanvas\ncourses. It's commonly used to store course materials such as readings, the course syllabus, images embedded in content elsewhere in Canvas (for example, Pages, Discussions, Assignments, Quizzes), and other course-related information. Upload files one at a time, or in bulk using drag-and-drop or the file picker built into your operating system. You can use Files to organize course files in folders, and set visibility and access restrictions on files and folders. Use the built-in preview tool to open files without downloading them. Students are not permitted to upload files to the course repository, but each student group gets its own repository, and every Canvas user has a personal file repository.\nIn course and group spaces, files uploaded as attachments or assignment submissions are placed in the personal file spaces of the individuals who uploaded them.\nNote:\nStorage in Canvas is intended only for purposes directly related to course and group work. For general-purpose storage needs, refer to\nOptions for storing files at IU\n.\nStorage quotas\nDefault storage quotas\nCourses\nGroups\nIndividuals\n4096 MB\n314.6 MB\n1.1 GB\nTo determine how much of your site's storage quota is currently in use, open the Files tool, and in the lower left, look for the "Storage Used" information.\nNot all files in Canvas count against your site's storage quota:\nCopied files:\nWhen you copy a file in Canvas, or upload a copy of an existing Canvas file, Canvas stores only one instance of that file. Only the original file counts against your site's storage quota; copies don't affect your site's quota. For example, if you copy several files from one course to a new course, the storage quota for the new course will not be affected, regardless of how many files you copied.\nWebcam recordings created in Canvas:\nWebcam recordings created directly in Canvas are stored on a separate media server and do not count toward your site's storage quota.\nGraded assignment submissions:\nFiles uploaded for a graded assignment are uploaded to user files but don't count against the 1.1 GB quota; see\nHow do I view my user files as a student?\nWork with institutional data\nThe Files tool in Canvas is appropriate for storing\ninstitutional data\nelements classified as Public, University-internal, or Restricted. Do\nnot\nuse Canvas for storing or sharing institutional data classified as Critical, such as Social Security and driver's license numbers, passwords and passphrases, and protected health information (\nPHI\n) regulated by the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (\nHIPAA\n).\nOfficial classifications for institutional data at IU are defined in\nManagement of Institutional Data (DM-01)\n. If you have questions about the classifications of institutional data at IU, see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n, use the\nData Sharing and Handling (DSH) tool\n, or contact the appropriate\nData Stewards\n. To determine the most sensitive classification of institutional data you can store on any given UITS service, see\nChoose an appropriate storage solution\n.\nGet help\nFor more about the Files tool, see\nHow do I use Files?\nFor help from a UITS Support Center consultant, fill out the "Ask for Help" form found in the\nCanvas\nHelp\nmenu. You can also contact your campus\nSupport Center\ndirectly.\nInstructors can get help using technology in their teaching at the\nteaching and learning centers\non each campus.\nFor other support options, see\nAbout Canvas at IU\n.	KB0023112	KB0023112 v3.0	 and group work. For general-purpose storage needs, refer to Options for storing files at <strong>IU</strong>. Storage support options, see About Canvas at <strong>IU</strong>.	