11215	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e900b43f476456583d373803846d4304	About standard EITS workstation builds	Table of contents\nOverview\nAvailability\nMaintenance windows\nmacOS\nWindows\nStandard builds\nmacOS\nWindows\nUITS staff:\nUITS-internal: Common concerns\nI won't have administrative rights to my computer\nEITS builds take a long time to deploy (and I can't afford to lose access to my workstation)\nI'll have issues using my computer off campus\nUITS-internal: Custom team builds\nOverview\nEITS uses Unified Device Management (UDM) services to deploy and manage standardized operating system and software configurations (builds) for Windows and macOS workstations. For any questions or concerns not addressed in this document,\ncontact EITS\n.\nAvailability\nThe standard EITS workstation builds are provided as a service to full-time staff members under EITS support (for use on laptops and desktops) and to teams (for use on shared workstations). EITS builds are not available for the following use cases: servers, virtual machines, test systems, and workstations used by staff who are not supported by EITS. For more information on service availability,\ncontact EITS\n.\nMaintenance windows\nEITS updates EITS-managed systems weekly. Maintenance windows ensure that devices are kept up to date. During the maintenance window, EITS requests that your device be powered on with an active network connection. Devices will only automatically install updates during the maintenance window.\nNote:\nUpdates to address actively exploited zero-day vulnerabilities may be installed without notice.\nmacOS\nThe general maintenance window for macOS devices is from 8pm Thursday to 4am Friday, and from noon to midnight Saturday and Sunday. EITS uses Jamf Pro to keep EITS installed applications up to date. Operating system updates will be served through the Software Update utility built into macOS.\nNote:\nWhile macOS updates will be pushed during the maintenance window, a manual restart may be required to complete installation. Be sure to restart your computer occasionally to ensure updates are fully applied.\nWindows\nThe general maintenance window for Windows devices is from 8pm Thursday to 4am Friday, and from noon to midnight Saturday and Sunday. EITS uses the Global Application Update Service to provide operating system and application updates.\nNote:\nIf prompted by the Software Center to restart or snooze, note that the snooze option will only close the window. A restart will still be required when the time expires. EITS is unable to change the default time.\nImportant:\nIf a device has not been updated within 42 days, it will be placed into an extended maintenance window, where it will attempt to install updates until it is up to date. A restart may be required.\nStandard builds\nThe following standard EITS builds are currently available.\nmacOS\nThe standard EITS build for Mac systems is based on macOS 14 (Sonoma).  New major version updates typically are adopted within one to three months of their release.\nEITS has packaged the following software for automated\ndeployment. Additionally, almost any application can be manually\ninstalled on request or is available within the Self Service application installed on our standard build.\nNote:\nEITS does not support the installation of Windows via Bootcamp. If you have a business need for running Windows alongside macOS, consider using virtualization software.\nStandard software:\nAdobe Acrobat DC\nBeyondTrust Jump Client\nGoogle Chrome\nIU Fonts\nMacPass\nMicrosoft Defender Advance Threat Protection (ATP)\nMicrosoft 365\nMicrosoft Teams\nMozilla Firefox\nIvanti Secure Access (formerly Pulse Secure)\nSlack\nVLC\nZoom\nImportant:\nShared storage in Google is available\nonly as an option for departments that want to\npay for continued storage in Google\n.\nDue to\nlimits to IU's storage footprint in the Google platform\n, all individual\nGoogle My Drive\ns now have a\n5 GB\nquota.\nWindows\nThe default Windows build is based on\nWindows 11\n, version\n22H2. New major version updates typically are adopted within one to three months of their release.\nEITS has packaged the following software for automated\ndeployment. Additionally, software is available in the Software Center catalog installed on your machine. Let EITS know if you have specific requests not available in the catalog.\nStandard software:\n7-Zip\nAdobe Acrobat DC\nBeyondTrust Jump Client\nGoogle Chrome\nIU Fonts\nKeePass\nMicrosoft 365\nMicrosoft Teams\nMozilla Firefox\nNotepad++\nIvanti Secure Access (formerly Pulse Secure)\nVLC\nZoom Client\nUITS staff:\nCommon concerns\nThe following information is intended to address a few common concerns potential\nclient\ns commonly have about switching to a standard EITS workstation build:\nI won't have administrative rights to my computer:\nBy default, as the primary user on an EITS workstation, you will not be given a local\nadministrator account\nthat allows you to install software and make other changes to your workstation. However, you can request that an administrator account be provisioned for your Windows machine by first\ncreating an IU group account\n(if you do not currently own one), and then signing the\nEITS Administrator Account Agreement\n. For Mac devices, after the EITS Administrator Account Agreement is signed, a local administrator account will be provisioned on the device by EITS staff.\nImportant:\nYou are responsible for using your administrator account in a secure manner. You should not enter your administrator credentials unless you are certain you are being prompted by a trusted source. If you are unsure whether a source is trusted,\ncontact EITS\n.\nThe administrator account is separate from your primary, everyday-use account, and has its own username (typically\nadm\nabc\n,  where\nabc\nare your initials) and passphrase. You should practice the\nprinciple of least privilege\nand avoid logging into your workstation using your administrator account credentials. Instead, log in using your primary (non-privileged) account, and then enter your administrator credentials only when necessary (that is, to perform tasks that require administrator access).\nEITS builds take a long time to deploy (and I can't afford to\nlose access to my workstation):\nOn most modern hardware, a typical rebuild takes about one business day. Usually, loaner systems are available for use during this period. Rebuilds on older, slower hardware, and ones involving large data backups may take longer than one business day. To inquire about your system,\ncontact EITS\n.\nI'll have issues using my computer off campus:\nUsing an EITS-built system outside the IU network will not affect usability or security. However, some minor complications may arise in the following instances, but in each case, the issue can be resolved using existing practices:\nActivating Windows or\nMicrosoft 365\n:\nBecause IU uses Microsoft's Key Management System for maintaining license information, your computer may display activation errors while off campus. To resolve this issue, connect to the IU VPN. Alternatively, by request, EITS can activate your software using a different method.\nLogging into domain accounts:\nBy default, all EITS-built\nsystems are joined to IU's ADS environment, and you will use your\nIU username and passphrase\nto log into your workstation even when you are off campus. This is possible because EITS-built systems allow you to log in indefinitely using cached credentials.\nLogging in after changing your passphrase:\nWhile your system is outside the IU network (that is, off campus), it cannot access IU's Active Directory; consequently, passphrase changes cannot automatically propagate to your system. To resolve this issue, connect to the IU VPN, and then lock and unlock your workstation. For more, see\nPrecautions when changing your passphrase\n.\nLogging in for the first time:\nIf you are unable\nto visit an EITS office to pick up your workstation, or have arranged\nwith your team to have it sent to you, special accommodations will\nbe necessary to allow you to log in for the first time. EITS will make\nthese arrangements with you directly.\nGroup policy management:\nSome settings on EITS-built systems are managed via Group Policy, and any changes to Group\nPolicy will not reach off-campus systems. However, policies that were\nin place when the system was built will still be enforced.\nFurthermore, Group Policy will be updated whenever you connect to the IU VPN.\nApplication updates:\nWhen warranted by a security\nvulnerability, EITS will occasionally use Unified Device Management to\ndeploy updates for installed applications. These updates will be\ndelivered to any internet-connected computer, even those outside of\nthe IU network.\nCustom team builds\nIf your team consistently uses applications that are\nnot included in the standard EITS builds, EITS can set up\na custom team build. Doing this ensures platform consistency\nacross your team's systems, and reduces downtime when rebuilds are needed. To request a custom team build,\ncontact EITS\n.	KB0024684	KB0024684 v1.0	 Chrome <strong>IU</strong> Fonts MacPass Microsoft Defender Advance Threat Protection (ATP) Microsoft 365 Microsoft: 7-Zip Adobe Acrobat DC BeyondTrust Jump Client Google Chrome <strong>IU</strong> Fonts KeePass Microsoft 365	0.7542		2026-01-31 17:02:27.559368	2026-01-31 17:02:27.559368	0	e900b43f476456583d373803846d4304
11216	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3320b43347a456583d373803846d43c1	View your UITS computing accounts	UITS computing accounts can be owned by personal or group accounts. To see a list of current IU computing accounts associated with a personal or group account:\nGo to\nYour Accounts\n.\nSign into\nIU Login\nusing the username and passphrase of the account that maintains ownership of the computing accounts.	KB0025583	KB0025583 v1.0	UITS <strong>computing</strong> accounts can be owned by personal or group accounts. To see a list of current IU <strong>computing</strong> accounts associated with a personal or group account: Sign into IU Login using the username and passphrase of the account that maintains ownership of the <strong>computing</strong> accounts.	151.3102		2026-01-31 17:02:31.977013	2026-01-31 17:02:31.977013	0	3320b43347a456583d373803846d43c1
11217	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=8bafec37476456583d373803846d4369	Restrictions on use of IU computing accounts	According to\nAppropriate Use of Information Technology Resources (IT-01)\n:\nUse of Indiana University information technology resources is restricted to purposes related to the university's mission of research and creative activity, teaching and learning, and civic engagement.\nThe policy continues:\nComputing resources and network capacity may not be used for illegal purposes. Examples of illegal purposes include:\nIntentional harassment of other users\nIntentional destruction of or damage to equipment, software, or data belonging to IU or other users\nIntentional disruption or unauthorized monitoring of electronic communications\nUnauthorized copying and/or distribution of copyrighted material\nUse of IU computing resources and networks must adhere to all IU policies. For a list of current information technology policies, see\nInformation and IT policies\n.	KB0022733	KB0022733 v1.0	: <strong>Computing</strong> resources and network capacity may not be used for illegal purposes. Examples of illegal of IU <strong>computing</strong> resources and networks must adhere to all IU policies. For a list of current	143.2899		2026-01-31 17:02:43.414316	2026-01-31 17:02:43.414316	0	8bafec37476456583d373803846d4369
11221	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5abfa41a471676942fc6a55b416d436b	Cloud computing at IU	About cloud computing\nPublic cloud accounts for students\nPublic cloud resources for teaching\nPublic cloud resources for research\nPublic cloud resources for staff at IU\nData classes for use on public cloud computing platforms at IU\nAbout Amazon Web Services (AWS) at IU\nGet an AWS, Google Cloud, or Microsoft Azure account at IU\nGet help with Amazon Web Services, Google Cloud, and Microsoft Azure at IU	KB0024768	KB0024768 v2.0	About cloud <strong>computing</strong> Public cloud accounts for students Public cloud resources for teaching Public cloud resources for research Public cloud resources for staff at IU Data classes for use on public cloud <strong>computing</strong> platforms at IU About Amazon Web Services (AWS) at IU Get an AWS, Google Cloud	138.7652		2026-01-31 17:02:49.705331	2026-01-31 17:02:49.705331	0	5abfa41a471676942fc6a55b416d436b
10976	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=bd5db851479a36902fc6a55b416d438e	About the Reconciler app at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nAccess the Reconciler app\nSelect a match set\nReview unmatched records\nUnderstanding matches\nUnderstanding the Matched screen\nReconciliation periods\nPerform automated matches\nPerform manual matches\nOverview\nThe Reconciler app is designed to streamline and modernize the financial reconciliation process for finance teams across Indiana University by replacing manual spreadsheet work with a streamlined, automated interface. Whether you're reconciling monthly invoices, comparing transactions between systems like KFS, MMS, or IUIE, or preparing audit-ready exports, Reconciler offers a powerful and user-friendly interface tailored to your workflow.\nFinance professionals often spend hours manually comparing spreadsheets, identifying mismatches, and tracking down discrepancies. Reconciler replaces these manual processes with a centralized, automated system that:\nConnects directly to your data sources (via Denodo)\n, including databases, data warehouses, and spreadsheets\nAutomates matching logic\nusing configurable rules for exact and near matches\nSupports manual matching\nwith annotation and comment features for collaboration\nProvides audit-ready exports\nof matched and unmatched records, including notes and error resolutions\nHandles large data volumes\n(from 20 to more than 700 records per reconciliation) with ease\nThis article outlines the initial steps to begin using the app effectively. It is intended to help finance team members who:\nPerform regular reconciliations between financial systems.\nReview matched and unmatched records for accuracy.\nPrepare documentation for audits or internal reviews.\nCollaborate with teammates to resolve discrepancies.\nYou do not need technical expertise to use Reconciler effectively. The app is designed to shield end users from complex setup tasks, which are handled by app administrators. Your focus will be on reviewing, matching, annotating, and exporting financial data.\nAccess the Reconciler app\nYou will receive a link to the Reconciler app from your team lead or administrator. Once you open the app:\nYou'll land on the "Home" screen, which shows a summary of your current reconciliation period.\nYou'll see tiles with information on each match set that you have access to.\nIf you encounter access issues, such as missing match sets or permissions issues, notify your admin contact. These issues are typically resolved quickly.\nSelect a match set\nA match set is a pair of data sources (for example, KFS and MMS) that you'll reconcile. You'll typically work with one match set at a time:\nTo choose a match set, select the arrow next to its name.\nEach match set is pre-configured to include fields from each data source that are relevant to your work.\nReview unmatched records\nUnmatched records are transactions that don't yet have a corresponding match in the other data set. To begin:\nOn the "Unmatched" screen, you will see a scrollable grid of unmatched records from the two different data sources.\nSelect a record for matching, or select again to deselect.\nIn the bottom left, you will see a summary of all selected records; in the bottom right, you will see options to select a match type. The available types are enabled or disabled based on the records that have been selected.\nUnderstanding matches\nA match is made up of:\nTwo or more selected records\nA match type, which categorizes why it was matched\nAn optional note to provide more information about the match (in some cases, these may be required)\nMatch types are configurable in cooperation with your application admin. They can be set up to allow different types of matches. Some may only allow one record on the left and one on the right (one-to-one). Others may allow multiples on the left or right or both, or may be single-sided.\nThe match types allowed for the records you have selected will be enabled or disabled based on your selection.\nIf notes are required for a particular match type, the "Notes" field will be outlined in red.\nUnderstanding the Matched screen\nFor each match, the "Matched" screen shows :\nMatched records from the left data source\nMatched records from the right data source\nA button to un-match records if they were matched by mistake\nDetail about the match, including match type, who did the match and when, and any notes; this may be optionally shown or hidden to save screen space using the "Show Detail" checkbox)\nOn this screen, you have the option to filter based on the reconciliation period for the left or right table, or to particular match types. You can also filter based on text found in the data or match detail.\nYou also have the option to export an Excel spreadsheet of matched data.\nReconciliation periods\nReconciliation periods may be set up for some match sets. These are configured by your application admin on the creation of the match set.\nThey automatically classify records into time periods based on dates found in the data. This can be by month, quarter, or other date groupings. The left and right side can be filtered independently and can have their periods defined differently.\nUse the period filters on the "Unmatched" or "Matched" screen to filter.\nPerform automated matches\nFrom the "Unmatched" screen, select\nStart Auto Matching\n. The process will occur in the background and will let you know when it is done.\nYou cannot perform other matches or data refreshes while auto matching is going on. An alert bar will indicate that there is an active matching session going on and will alert you when it is finished.\nIf the auto match does not complete after a significant period of time, contact your application admin to check whether there is an issue and to clear the lock.\nPerform manual matches\nOn the "Unmatched" screen:\nTo find records, use the search box in the left or right data grids to filter your view. This will filter based on any text or numeric field in the data.\nSelect the records you want to match.\nTo de-select matches, select the record again in the data grid, and then select\nClear\nin the left or right data grid; alternatively, select the\nX\nnext to the record in the "Selected" listing in the lower right side of your screen.\nSelect the match type, and then select\nMark as Matched\n.\nThe records will be removed from your screen, and you can proceed with additional matches.	KB0027213	KB0027213 v3.0	 from <strong>complex</strong> setup tasks, which are handled by app administrators. Your focus will be on reviewing	7.6087		2026-01-31 16:57:35.365804	2026-01-31 16:57:35.365804	0	bd5db851479a36902fc6a55b416d438e
11230	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=eeaf2c37476456583d373803846d43d6	Avoid allowing someone to use your IU computing account	Under no circumstances should you ever allow anyone else to use your Indiana University computing accounts; don't even give technical support providers your passphrase for temporary use. IU/UITS support personnel will\nnever\nneed or ask for your passphrase, in person, over the phone, via postal mail, or via email/chat. The following is an official policy statement from the\nUniversity\nInformation Policy Office\n(UIPO):\nYour\nUniversity account\nand computing accounts are provided for your use as a member of the university community. Network IDs provide access to a wide range of services that are restricted for use by you personally (such as grades, address information, bursar bill, salary, benefits) or are restricted for use by members of the university community (VPN, wireless access, library services, and\nIUware\n). If you share your usernames and passphrases with spouses, family members, friends, or roommates, then you are giving them access to services they are not authorized to use.\nFor more about proper use of your computing account, see\nAppropriate Use of Information Technology Resources (IT-01)\n.	KB0022671	KB0022671 v1.0	Under no circumstances should you ever allow anyone else to use your Indiana University <strong>computing</strong> Policy Office (UIPO): Your University account and <strong>computing</strong> accounts are provided for your use. For more about proper use of your <strong>computing</strong> account, see Appropriate Use of Information Technology	137.9305		2026-01-31 17:02:53.678719	2026-01-31 17:02:53.678719	0	eeaf2c37476456583d373803846d43d6
11232	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3aaf6c37476456583d373803846d4396	About use of IU computing resources for political campaigns	You may use your IU\nUITS\ncomputer accounts only for purposes related to the university's mission of research and creative activity, teaching and learning, and civic engagement. In nearly all cases, political campaigns not related to the university fall outside the university's mission.\nIf you wish to arrange to use IU computing resources for political purposes, contact the\nUniversity Information Policy Office\n(UIPO) via email at\nuipo@iu.edu\n. However, you may find it easier and less expensive to host your campaign website on an account from a commercial internet service provider (ISP).\nNote:\nOfficial IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis student organizations\nmay\nuse their organizational accounts to post campaign-related materials. See\nComputing accounts for IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis student organizations\n.\nInstructors and departments are allowed to post campaign-related information on IU computing resources in the course of their educational activities. For example, political science instructors may install links to Republican and Democratic presidential candidates' campaign home pages from a class home page. This is clearly an appropriate use of IU computing resources for instruction, according to the policy on Appropriate Use of IT Resources:\nUse of Indiana University information technology resources is restricted to purposes related to the university's mission of research and creative activity, teaching and learning, and civic engagement. Eligible individuals are provided access in order to support their studies, instruction, duties as employees, official business with the university, and other university-sanctioned activities.\nFor more, see\nAppropriate Use of Information Technology Resources (IT-01)\n.	KB0022696	KB0022696 v1.0	 to arrange to use IU <strong>computing</strong> resources for political purposes, contact the University Information accounts to post campaign-related materials. See <strong>Computing</strong> accounts for IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis on IU <strong>computing</strong> resources in the course of their educational activities. For example, political	131.1086		2026-01-31 17:03:01.385036	2026-01-31 17:03:01.385036	0	3aaf6c37476456583d373803846d4396
11237	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=64efe83b476456583d373803846d43d2	View UITS news and information online	A wealth of news and information about upcoming IT events and computing at Indiana University is available at\nIT News & Events\n.	KB0023918	KB0023918 v1.0	A wealth of news and information about upcoming IT events and <strong>computing</strong> at Indiana University is available at IT News & Events.	116.3351		2026-01-31 17:03:09.099184	2026-01-31 17:03:09.099184	0	64efe83b476456583d373803846d43d2
4958	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6ebf20b7476456583d373803846d43a8	Your responsibilities as a computer user at IU	By receiving and using your\nIU username and passphrase\n, you accept certain responsibilities and standards for computer use, which apply to all IU computing resources.\nThe university deals seriously with failure to use IU computing resources as described. Violators are subject to the normal disciplinary procedures of the university, and the loss of computing privileges may result. Illegal acts involving IU computing resources may also be subject to prosecution by state and federal authorities.\nBy accepting your IU computing accounts, you agree to observe the responsibilities and standards outlined in the\nAcceptable Use Agreement for access to technology and information resources at IU\n.\nAll students, faculty, staff, and affiliates must complete the Acceptable Use Agreement every two years.  In most cases, an\nIU Login\ninterrupt will notify you when your previous agreement expires.\nYou are also responsible for following all applicable IU policies. Additionally, university units such as colleges and departments may have in place more specific policies related to the use of information technology.\nFor policy statements on information technology-related activities, see\nInformation and IT policies\n.	KB0023051	KB0023051 v1.0	 and standards for computer use, which apply to all IU <strong>computing</strong> resources. The university deals seriously with failure to use IU <strong>computing</strong> resources as described. Violators are subject to the normal disciplinary procedures of the university, and the loss of <strong>computing</strong> privileges may result. Illegal acts	114.1746		2026-01-31 14:48:01.601426	2026-01-31 17:03:20.916995	0	6ebf20b7476456583d373803846d43a8
11241	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ceafe837476456583d373803846d4331	Retrieve files accidentally deleted on a Linux workstation in Luddy Hall	The\nLuddy School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering\nat Indiana University Bloomington owns and maintains the\nLinux\nworkstations in Luddy Hall, and is responsible for nightly backups. To request a file retrieval, post to the\nLuddy School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering Help Desk\nspecifying the directory path and filename to be recovered, the date the file was created or last modified, and the time the file was deleted.	KB0022639	KB0022639 v1.0	The Luddy School of Informatics, <strong>Computing</strong>, and Engineering at Indiana University Bloomington owns and maintains the Linux workstations in Luddy Hall, and is responsible for nightly backups. To request a file retrieval, post to the Luddy School of Informatics, <strong>Computing</strong>, and Engineering Help Desk	113.7034		2026-01-31 17:03:27.434152	2026-01-31 17:03:27.434152	0	ceafe837476456583d373803846d4331
