11313	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=70ffac7b476456583d373803846d4396	Report issues accessing NVDA on computers in the STC computing labs	Note:\nTo verify that the location with the access issue is an STC computing lab, see\nAbout the IU STCs (computing labs)\nand pick your campus.\nReport issues accessing NVDA on computers in STC labs to your\ncampus Support Center\n.\nLearn more\nTo get started learning NVDA, see\nNVDA basics\n.\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0024288	KB0024288 v1.0	Report issues accessing NVDA on computers in STC labs to your campus Support Center. Learn more To get started learning NVDA, see NVDA basics.	48.591		2026-01-31 17:05:42.755591	2026-01-31 17:05:42.755591	0	70ffac7b476456583d373803846d4396
11151	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d0bdc846330aa21804e605570e5c7b21	Guidelines for use of the STCs	Use of the\nStudent Technology Centers\n(STCs) is intended for purposes related to Indiana University's mission of education, research, and public service. Students and faculty may use these computing resources, funded entirely by the student technology fee, only for purposes related to their studies, their instruction, and other university-sanctioned activities.\nMute your cell phone.\nUse headphones when listening to music or audible software while working in an STC.\nAlways log out before leaving a workstation.\nDon't leave a logged-in workstation unattended for more than 15 minutes. If you take a break and are gone longer than 15 minutes, even if your workstation is locked, the computer will be restarted and unsaved data will be lost.\nAny of the following practices may result in the loss of computing privileges or other disciplinary action:\nThe use of a username other than your own to gain access to an STC workstation\nAny attempt to disconnect a workstation, any of its components, or any other computing hardware in an STC\nAny attempt to circumvent IU network security\nAny attempt to circumvent security in an STC\nAny unauthorized attempt to attach a device or cable of any kind to the back of a workstation. On most STC workstations, front panel connections for headphones and USB-compliant devices are readily available for the convenience of students.\nAnyone disturbing the academic environment of an STC may be asked to leave.	KB0022433	KB0022433 v3.0	 University's mission of education, research, and public service. Students and faculty may use these <strong>computing</strong> of <strong>computing</strong> privileges or other disciplinary action: The use of a username other than your own, or any other <strong>computing</strong> hardware in an STC Any attempt to circumvent IU network security Any attempt	103.6056		2026-01-31 17:00:09.408318	2026-01-31 17:04:26.950071	0	d0bdc846330aa21804e605570e5c7b21
11268	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=21efec3b476456583d373803846d4366	Access NVDA at IU	Overview\nNVDA (NonVisual Desktop Access), a free Windows-based screen reading application, is available at IU. Listed below are the different ways you can access NVDA while at IU.\nAccess NVDA\nPersonal computer\nDownload page for the NVDA installer\nNVDA User Guide\nSTC computing labs\nNVDA is available in the Windows-based STC computing labs across all of the IU campuses. To find STC computing labs for your IU campus, see\nAbout the IU STCs (computing labs)\n.\nFor instructions on accessing NVDA in the STCs, see\nAccess NVDA in the STC computing labs\n.\nIUanyWare\nIUanyWare\nis a client virtualization (CV) service available to Indiana University students, faculty, and staff. You can access NVDA from the STC Lab Desktops offered through the IUanyWare service.\nFor instructions on accessing NVDA on IUanyWare, see\nAccess NVDA on an STC public desktop with IUanyWare\n.\nLearn more\nNVDA basics\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0024005	KB0024005 v1.0	 Personal computer Download page for the NVDA installer NVDA User Guide STC <strong>computing</strong> labs NVDA is available in the Windows-based STC <strong>computing</strong> labs across all of the IU campuses. To find STC <strong>computing</strong> labs for your IU campus, see About the IU STCs (<strong>computing</strong> labs). For instructions on accessing NVDA	102.7638	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:04:30.722622	2026-01-31 17:04:30.722622	0	21efec3b476456583d373803846d4366
11270	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a3efe47b476456583d373803846d432d	Access Fusion at IU	Overview\nFusion, a software program consisting of both Zoomtext and JAWS is available at IU. The different ways you can access Fusion at IU are listed below.\nAccess Fusion\nPersonal computer\nFusion isn't available for use on personal computers. However, IU students, staff, and faculty have access to home editions of JAWS and Zoomtext through IUware for personal use only.\nGet the Home Use edition of JAWS\nGet the Home edition of ZoomText\nNote:\nYou will be granted access for only one license of either JAWS or Zoomtext, which allows you to install the program on your computer.\nSTC computing labs\nFusion is available in the Windows-based STC computing labs across all of the IU campuses. To find STC computing labs for your IU campus, see\nAbout the IU STCs (computing labs)\n.\nFor instructions on accessing Fusion in the STCs, see\nAccess Fusion in the STC computing labs\n.\nIUanyWare\nIUanyWare is a client virtualization (CV) service available to Indiana University students, faculty, and staff. You can access Fusion from the STC Lab Desktops offered through the\nIUanyWare\nservice.\nFor instructions on accessing Fusion on IUanyWare, see\nAccess Fusion on an STC Desktop in IUanyWare\n.\nGet help\nReport issues accessing Fusion on computers in the STC computing labs\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0024161	KB0024161 v1.0	 of either JAWS or Zoomtext, which allows you to install the program on your computer. STC <strong>computing</strong> labs Fusion is available in the Windows-based STC <strong>computing</strong> labs across all of the IU campuses. To find STC <strong>computing</strong> labs for your IU campus, see About the IU STCs (<strong>computing</strong> labs). For instructions	101.8273		2026-01-31 17:04:34.150762	2026-01-31 17:04:34.150762	0	a3efe47b476456583d373803846d432d
10983	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=2edfe03b476456583d373803846d43bf	Migrate resources in Denodo at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nMigrate resources\nPrepare to migrate\nAccess the Scheduler\nMigrate to STG\nMigrate to PRD\nHandle environment-specific settings\nMigrate web services\nGet help\nOverview\nIn\nDenodo\nat Indiana University, developers can migrate their own resources from DEV to STG to PRD.\nDevelopment occurs in the IU Denodo Dev environments at the following URI:\nDEV:\n//ebidvt-dev.uits.iu.edu:9999/<your_VDB_name>\nAfter migration, you can access Denodo views in Stage and Production at the following URIs:\nSTG:\n//ebidvt-stg.uits.iu.edu:9999/<your_VDB_name>\nPRD:\n//ebidvt.uits.iu.edu:9999/<your_VDB_name>\nYou can read, write, and create new objects only in the DEV environment. You can view objects in STG and PRD, but you cannot alter them in those environments. The only way to get objects into STG and PRD is through the migration process.\nThe only way to get objects into STG and PRD is through the full VDB migration process or the selected items migration process.\nThis document describes full VDB process migration process. For a description of the selected items migration process, see\nMigrate selected objects in Denodo at IU\n.\nSTG access is for testers, and PRD access is for consumers. To request access for additional users or groups to STG and/or PRD, see\nUse Data Delivery Apply Security to assign security to Denodo views\n.\nMigrate resources\nPrepare to migrate:\nBefore migrating your resources, prepare all of your data sources and views in the virtual database (VDB) without the\n_dev\nsuffix (this is the VDB that will migrate). All the objects in this VDB will be migrated, except the data sources. To migrate data sources, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\n; under "Application", select\nDenodo\n, and under "Question Type", select\nRequest datasource migration\n. Include proof of data manager approval for all data sources that contain any enterprise data; for details about data manager approval, see\nData Manager approval for Denodo and Tableau\n. Migrate any new data sources before running the migration script in\nstep 3\nbelow. Until the data sources are migrated, all objects that rely on those data sources will fail due to a missing dependency.\nIf you have a significant amount of work in progress, you should keep that in your other VDB. If you have work in progress that only you need to access, you may request a personal VDB.\nAccess the scheduler:\nNavigate to the appropriate version of the scheduler:\nGo to\nhttps://denodo-dev.dvt.iu.edu/solution-manager-web-tool/\n.\nLog in with your\nIU username\nand\npassphrase\n, and then confirm your identity with\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n.\nUse the "Environment" drop-down to select\nStg\nfor the Stage scheduler.\nOn the\nScheduler\ntile, select\nOpen\n.\nMigrate your resources to STG:\nIn the Stage scheduler:\nSelect\nJobs\nin the navigation bar at the top of the display. Use the “Job” and/or “Project name” filters to find the\nimport <your_vdb> Dev to Stage\njob. Use one of the following methods to start the job:\nSelect the checkbox next to the name of the job, and then select a start option from the\nStart\ndrop-down menu in the line of icons above the results grid.\nHover at the extreme right of the row containing the name of the job (in the "Processed (Tuples/Errors)" column) until the vertical ellipses appear. Select the ellipses and choose a start option.\nYou should see your changes in the STG VDB within minutes.\nNote:\nYour job may be named\nMigrate <your_vdb> Dev to Stage\n, or\nMigrate <your_vdb> Stage to Prod\n.\nMigrate to PRD:\nMake sure to\ntest everything thoroughly on STG\nbefore migrating your VDB to PRD. When you're ready, access the\nstage scheduler\n(using the instructions from\nstep 2\n, above), and then:\nSelect\nJob\nin the navigation bar at the top of the display.\nIn the list of jobs, find your\nimport <your_vdb> Stage to Prod\njob, and start it as described in\nstep 3\n.\nImportant:\nConsumer access privileges do not migrate between environments. If consumers need access to views in STG and/or PRD, see\nUse Data Delivery Apply Security to assign security to Denodo views\n.\nHandle environment-specific settings\nIn some cases, you may need to have different data source connections in different environments (for example, you may want the STG Denodo VDB to point to DSS1STG and PRD point to DSS1PRD).\nIf environment-specific settings are part of your requirements, and you need to migrate data sources, make note of the environment-specific settings in the "Comments" section when you complete the\nEDSD Support Form\n. Also include proof of data manager approval for all data sources that contain any enterprise data; for details about data manager approval, see\nData Manager approval for Denodo and Tableau\n. Any subsequent modification of data sources in STG and PRD will also be handled via the\nEDSD Support Form\n.\nNote:\nCurrently, Excel data sources and custom data sources cannot be managed with environment-specific configurations.\nMigrate web services\nIf you migrate web services to production or staging and the web service needs to be deployed, create a request using the\nEDSD Support Form\n.\nGet help\nIf you need help or have questions about the migration process, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\n.	KB0023743	KB0023743 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Migrate resources Prepare to migrate Access the <strong>Scheduler</strong> Migrate to STG, you may request a personal VDB. Access the <strong>scheduler</strong>: Navigate to the appropriate version of the <strong>scheduler</strong>: Go to https://denodo-dev.dvt.iu.edu/solution-manager-web-tool/. Use the "Environment" drop	16.9169	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 16:57:47.431073	2026-01-31 17:17:58.232072	0	2edfe03b476456583d373803846d43bf
11276	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b420b4ff476456583d373803846d4340	Public cloud resources for staff at IU	IU has contracts with the three main public cloud platforms: Amazon Web Services (AWS), Google Cloud, and Microsoft Azure. They offer hyper-scale computing resources on demand for a wide variety of technology projects, from software development to AI/ML and from IoT to natural language processing. The discounts vary by platform, but all are below the commercial rates.\nIU staff can use these IU-sanctioned computing environments for institutional workloads and application development. The IU Cloud Technology Support team provides guidance on platforms and tooling, and regularly arranges for direct consultations with software engineers from the cloud vendors, all at no cost to departments. To get started, see\nGet an AWS, Google Cloud, or Microsoft Azure account at IU\n.  For help,\nsubmit the\nCloud Support form\n.\nA variety of training and certification prep resources are also available at low or no cost to IU employees. For a list of current training and support resources, see\nGet help with Amazon Web Services, Google Cloud, and Microsoft Azure at IU\n.\nImportant:\nUse of these public cloud platforms by IU staff is governed by IU's Public Cloud Acceptable Usage Agreement. For details, see\nGet an AWS, Google Cloud, or Microsoft Azure account at IU\n.\nFor guidelines on using\ninstitutional data\nwith these platforms, see\nData classes for use on public cloud computing platforms at IU\n.	KB0025328	KB0025328 v1.0	 Cloud, and Microsoft Azure. They offer hyper-scale <strong>computing</strong> resources on demand for a wide variety-sanctioned <strong>computing</strong> environments for institutional workloads and application development. The IU with these platforms, see Data classes for use on public cloud <strong>computing</strong> platforms at IU.	99.545		2026-01-31 17:04:37.616162	2026-01-31 17:04:37.616162	0	b420b4ff476456583d373803846d4340
11277	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b810f87f476456583d373803846d436f	Access former student bills, transcripts, and other university information	When you graduate from or leave Indiana University,\nUITS\ndisables your computing accounts. This will happen roughly two semesters after your last semester at IU; see\nStudent computing accounts, data, and software when you leave IU\n.\nAfter your computing accounts have been disabled, your\nADS domain\naccount may remain active for up to five years. You will be able to use this account to access\nOne.IU\nto get to your bursar bill, unofficial transcripts, and financial aid information. However, you will\nnot\nbe able to use this account to access other university services (for example,\nSTC\ncomputers,\nVPN\n, email).\nIf it's been over five years since you were last enrolled in classes at IU, you will no longer be able to use your ADS domain account to access IU resources. To order a transcript after your ADS domain account has been disabled, see\nAccess your IU transcript\n.\nIf you have an IU username but you have problems logging in, you might need to\nreset your passphrase\n.\nFor information about your student email account once you have left IU, see\nAbout IU Alumni Association email accounts\n.	KB0024975	KB0024975 v1.0	When you graduate from or leave Indiana University, UITS disables your <strong>computing</strong> accounts. This will happen roughly two semesters after your last semester at IU; see Student <strong>computing</strong> accounts, data, and software when you leave IU. After your <strong>computing</strong> accounts have been disabled, your ADS domain	99.5038		2026-01-31 17:04:41.008547	2026-01-31 17:04:41.008547	0	b810f87f476456583d373803846d436f
11281	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=90ff2c7b476456583d373803846d43fb	Access ZoomText at IU	Overview\nZoomText is a Windows-based screen magnification program. The different ways you can access ZoomText at IU are listed below.\nAccess ZoomText\nPersonal computer\nThe Home edition of ZoomText is available without cost to Indiana University students, faculty, and staff through\nIUware\n.\nNotes:\nDo not use this version for school or departmental work as well as testing. You can access ZoomText in the STC computing labs or on the STC Desktops in\nIUanyWare\n.\nIf you need access to ZoomText as part of a workplace accommodation, contact the\nADA services office on your campus\nto learn about arranging accommodations for your needs.\nFor instructions on getting ZoomText, see\nGet the Home edition of ZoomText\n.\nSTC computing labs\nZoomText is available in the Windows-based STC computing labs across all of the IU campuses. To find STC computing labs for your IU campus, see\nAbout the IU STCs (computing labs)\n.\nFor instructions on accessing Zoomtext in the STCs, see\nAccess ZoomText in the STC computing labs\n.\nIUanyWare\nIUanyWare is a client virtualization (CV) service available to Indiana University students, faculty, and staff. You can access ZoomText from the STC Lab Desktops offered through the IUanyWare service.\nFor instructions, see\nAccess ZoomText on an STC Desktop in IUanyWare\n.\nLearn more\nAbout ZoomText\nFor video tutorials and training on ZoomText, visit\nZoomText Training\n.\nFor ZoomText User guides, visit\nZoomText User Guides\n.\nFor recorded webinars on ZoomText features and tools, see\nWebinars on Demand\n.\nFor additional ZoomText support, contact your\ncampus Support Center\n.\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0024245	KB0024245 v1.0	 in the STC <strong>computing</strong> labs or on the STC Desktops in IUanyWare. If you need access to ZoomText as part of ZoomText. STC <strong>computing</strong> labs ZoomText is available in the Windows-based STC <strong>computing</strong> labs across all of the IU campuses. To find STC <strong>computing</strong> labs for your IU campus, see About the IU STCs (<strong>computing</strong>	99.2344	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:04:44.791987	2026-01-31 17:04:44.791987	0	90ff2c7b476456583d373803846d43fb
