11288	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f3efa87b476456583d373803846d4329	Access JAWS at IU	Overview\nJAWS, a Windows-based screen reading software that provides braille and speech access to the information on a screen, is available at IU. The different ways you can access JAWS at IU are listed below.\nAccess JAWS\nPersonal computer\nThe Home Use edition is available for you to download on your Windows-based personal computer. The Home Use edition is for personal use only.\nNotes:\nDo not use this version for school or departmental work or testing. You can\naccess JAWS in the STC computing labs\nor\naccess JAWS on the STC desktops in IUanyWare\n.\nIf you need access to JAWS as part of a workplace accommodation, contact the\nADA services office on your campus\nto learn about arranging accommodations for your needs.\nFor setup instructions, see\nGet the Home Use edition of JAWS\n.\nSTC computing labs\nJAWS is available in the Windows-based STC computing labs across all of the IU campuses. To find STC computing labs for your IU campus, see\nAbout the IU STCs (computing labs)\n.\nFor instructions on accessing JAWS in the STCs, see\nAccess Fusion in the STC computing labs\n.\nIUanyWare\nIUanyWare is a client virtualization (CV) service available to Indiana University students, faculty, and staff. You can access JAWS from the STC Lab Desktops offered through the\nIUanyWare\nservice.\nNote:\nThe Home Use Edition of JAWS that is available through IU for download does not include the remote access add-on, and can't be used to communicate with JAWS on the STC Lab Desktop in IUanyWare.\nFor instructions on accessing JAWS on IUanyWare, see\nAccess Fusion on an STC Desktop in IUanyWare\n.\nLearn more\nAbout JAWS\nFor training, documentation, podcasts, webinars, and other JAWS resources, visit\nJAWS Headquarters\n.\nGet help\nFor additional JAWS support, contact your\ncampus Support Center\n.\nReport issues accessing JAWS on computers in the STC computing labs\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0024184	KB0024184 v1.0	 this version for school or departmental work or testing. You can access JAWS in the STC <strong>computing</strong> accommodations for your needs. For setup instructions, see Get the Home Use edition of JAWS. STC <strong>computing</strong> labs JAWS is available in the Windows-based STC <strong>computing</strong> labs across all of the IU campuses. To find	93.6842	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:04:55.37204	2026-01-31 17:04:55.37204	0	f3efa87b476456583d373803846d4329
11293	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=16dfa03b476456583d373803846d430a	Your status with IU	Your access to UITS accounts and services is dependent upon your status with Indiana University. Most services are restricted to IU students and employees (faculty and staff), while others are available to affiliates and other guests of the university. For a full list of UITS accounts and services (including IUware) and standards for eligibility, see\nComputing accounts at IU\n.\nStudent:\nOnce you have been admitted to IU, you are eligible to create the full suite of student computing accounts, even if you have not yet paid your enrollment fee or registered for classes. If you do not register for classes at IU, your accounts will be disabled approximately six weeks into the semester.\nStudent status is determined by your presence in the Student Information System (SIS), which receives its information from the registrar on each campus. If you have questions about your standing as a student, contact your\ncampus registrar\n.\nFaculty or staff:\nOnce your employment as a faculty or staff member has been finalized by\nIU Human Resources\n, you will be eligible for the full suite of employee computing accounts.\nNote that you must be employed by IU, as defined by IU HR. At IU, you are considered an employee if your hiring was completed through the Human Resources Management System (HRMS). This includes:\nFormally appointed staff members (paid or non-paid)\nAppointed adjunct faculty. Instructors who teach credit or non-credit courses are considered adjunct faculty.\nInstructors contracted to teach a single class. Even instructors who teach only one class for one semester, if they were hired through the official HRMS system, are eligible for full IU computing accounts and services, including downloads from IUware.\nFor more about accounts for official IU retirees, see\nAccess to IU resources for official IU retirees\n.\nIU affiliate:\nIU faculty or staff may sponsor affiliate accounts for those who need them. Affiliates are eligible for the full suite of computing accounts for a predetermined period of time. For more, see\nSponsor a computing account for an IU affiliate\n.\nGuest:\nAnyone may request a\nGuest\naccount\n, which provides limited access to certain online applications and services of the university. For more, see\nAbout IU Guest accounts\n.	KB0023724	KB0023724 v1.0	 (including IUware) and standards for eligibility, see <strong>Computing</strong> accounts at IU. Student: Once you have been admitted to IU, you are eligible to create the full suite of student <strong>computing</strong> accounts <strong>computing</strong> accounts. Note that you must be employed by IU, as defined by IU HR. At IU, you are considered an	93.1954		2026-01-31 17:04:59.104434	2026-01-31 17:04:59.104434	0	16dfa03b476456583d373803846d430a
11389	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ca30b07347a456583d373803846d4387	Microsoft Teams Room overview and deployment guide	Table of contents\nOverview\nMTR components\nPlan for deployment\nConsultation/site survey\nPurchase equipment\nTypical recommended hardware\nPurchase a Teams Room Pro license\nCreate a MTR resource account\nPrepare for installation\nChoose an AD security group name to manage the MTR PC\nPrepare the MTR PC to connect to the internet\nAt time of installation\nManage the MTR's resource account calendar permissions\nOverview\nThe\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom (MTR) system is a specific hardware and software package developed by Microsoft as an AV integration solution for conference rooms. The chief benefit of the MTR is its ability to connect with a single tap to\nMicrosoft Teams\nor Zoom meetings from its tabletop touchscreen console.  This means no more long conference numbers to remember and no complicated logins required.\nMTR components\nEach MTR system includes the following components:\nMTR PC:\nThe heart of the MTR is a Windows PC running Windows 10 IoT Enterprise OS onto which is installed a purpose-built\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom app.  The\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom app auto-launches at startup and runs over the top of (hides) the normal Windows desktop from users.\nTouchscreen console:\nThis small tablet sits on the tabletop and connects to the MTR PC. The touchscreen console is used to control the system: place calls, mute microphone, etc.\nAV peripherals:\nFront-of-room TV, USB camera, microphone(s) and loudspeaker.\nMTR Resource account:\nThis is a dedicated and unique Active Directory account that the MTR uses to log in to the O365 cloud.  It is also the account name that users include in a Microsoft Outlook meeting request to reserve the\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom.  When the Outlook meeting request also includes a\nMicrosoft Teams\nor Zoom meeting URL, the MTR PC reads the meeting information from the cloud and displays it on the Touchscreen console as a tap-to-join meeting event.\nNote:\nIn some circumstances, there may already be an existing Outlook resource calendar in place that is used to schedule the conference room.  This existing resource calendar account can be converted into a MTR resource account, so that users can continue to use the familiar calendar account name to reserve the conference room/\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom.\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom Pro license:\nEach MTR installation requires an annual license fee (approx. $175/yr/MTR).\nOptional:\nAt IU, UITS strongly recommends an additional AV peripheral called the Logitech Swytch, which is a device that supports Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) conferencing mode.  The Swytch features a USB cable that appears at the tabletop.  When a user plugs the Swytch USB cable into their laptop, the laptop will connect to the room TV, camera, microphones and loudspeaker, effectively allowing the user’s laptop to serve as the conferencing device while plugged in.  When the user unplugs the cable, those peripheral devices revert to the MTR PC.\nPlan for deployment\nConsultation/site survey\nTo determine the hardware that's best suited to your specific conference room, UITS recommends an onsite consultation with the UITS Learning Space Design group.\nRequest a consultation\n.\nPurchase equipment\nTypical recommended hardware\nMost MTR equipment packages will include the following UITS-recommended hardware. This list does not include certain AV integration items that will be specific to a particular room such as equipment rack or credenza, TV wall-mounting hardware, surface-mount cable raceway, miscellaneous cables, etc. Note too that UITS Learning Spaces may wish to place the Buy.iu order for this and other recommended hardware not listed here on the customer's behalf.\nLogitech BASE\nMicrosoft Teams\nRooms (no AV) with Tap + Intel NUC (Mfg. part: TAPMSTBASEINT)\nHeckler Stand for Logitech Tap (Mfg. part: H660-BG)\nLogitech Rally Bar (for larger rooms, typically 7 or more seats)  (Mfg. part: 960-001308)\nor\nRally Bar Mini (for smaller rooms, typically six or fewer seats) (Mfg. part: 960-001336)\nLogitech Rally Mic Pods: Table or ceiling mount.  In general, one mic pod per two people, for example, rectangular table with four seats on a side needs two mic pods. A table with five or six seats on a side needs three mic pods. (Mfg. part: 989-000430)\nLogitech Rally Mic Pod Extension Cable (Mfg. part: 952-000047) (Note that only one cable is needed per room, because the mic pods connect in daisy-chain.)\nLogitech Rally Mic Pod Mount - microphone desktop mount (Mfg. part: 952-000002) (one for each mic pod)\nLogitech Swytch (Mfg. part: 952-000009)\nLogitech Strong - USB-C cable - USB Type A to 24 pin USB-C - 33 ft (Mfg. part: 939-001799) (one to extend the Tap Touchscreen console, and another to extend the Swytch hub)\nSony Bravia HDR Professional Display TV; specific model and size will vary depending on room, availability, and pricing.  (Typical models as of June 2023: Mfg. part: FW-85BZ40H, FW-75BZ40H, FW-65BZ40H, or FW-55BZ40H)\nPurchase a\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom Pro license\nTo purchase a\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom Pro license, email Learning Spaces Support (\ncthelp@iu.edu\n) with subject line "Teams Room Pro License Request".  Indicate the campus-building-room number that the MTR will be installed in, and the IU account number to be used for the purchase.  Learning Spaces Support will submit a purchase requisition in Buy.iu from Dell Inc. on your behalf and will reply to indicate the Buy.iu requisition number for your records.\nNote:\nIf the license is purchased in any month except June, the first year's license cost will be pro-rated to expire on June 30 (end of fiscal year). The IU Purchasing office uses an internal mechanism called Project Code (MTR project code:\nTEAMSROOM\n), which will automatically draw the annual ~$175 from that account to renew the license each July 1 so that you don't need to remember to submit a new requisition each year.\nCreate a MTR resource account\nTo create a MTR resource account, email Learning Spaces Support (\ncthelp@iu.edu\n) with subject line "MTR Resource Account Request".\nYour email should contain the campus/building/room where the MTR will be installed.\nIf there is\nno existing\nOutlook resource calendar used to reserve your conference room\nDecide what the MTR resource account name should be.  UITS recommends the name format\n<Campus code>-<Building code>-<Room#>@iu.edu\n(for example,\nIN-IT-219@iu.edu\n), but you may wish to name the system to conform to some pattern that other resource calendars in your building or department follow.\nIndicate the IU username of the person that should be assigned as the owner of the MTR resource account.\nIndicate the mail-enabled AD group account (also known as the FASA or Full Access Send-As permissions group account) that should be assigned as manager of the MTR resource account (for setting calendar permissions etc.)\nOnce you provide the above information, Learning Spaces Support will submit a request on your behalf with IU's Enterprise Microsoft Administration team (EMA) and will cc you on that request.\nFor more information about the FASA or Full Access Send-As permissions group account, see\nRequest Full Access Send-As permissions for an Exchange Online departmental/group account mailbox\n.\nFor more information about setting calendar permissions, see\nManage Exchange resource rooms using the resource scheduling settings\n.\nIf there is\nalready an existing\nOutlook resource calendar used to reserve your conference room\nIndicate the display name and/or alias of the existing Outlook resource calendar.\nIf known, indicate the IU username of the person that has been assigned as the owner of the existing resource calendar.\nIf known, indicate the mail-enabled AD group account (also known as the FASA or Full Access Send-As permissions group account) that is assigned as manager of that resource calendar mailbox.\nIndicate a preferred date and time that you'd like the conversion of your existing resource calendar to an MTR resource calendar to occur.\nOnce you provide this information, Learning Spaces Support will submit a request on your behalf with IU's Enterprise Microsoft Administration team (EMA) and cc you on that request.\nTo prepare your local users (those who reserve the conference room using that resource calendar) for this change:\nThe owner or managing FASA group member should log into the existing resource calendar and export existing appointments to a backup file as described in\nHow to import and export calendar in resource mailbox?\nAfter the conversion, the manager can re-import that appointments backup file.\nContact your local users and indicate that the existing resource mailbox will be converted on\nXX\ndate to support the new\nMicrosoft Teams\nRoom system in your conference room, and that after the change is completed, they may need to:\nReschedule existing appointments with this room (in the unlikely event the FASA group manager is unable to re-import the existing appointments backup file)\nDelete and re-add the resource calendar within their Outlook desktop client's\nCalendar\n> "Shared Calendars" list.\nNotes:\nThe actual process that EMA employs to convert an existing resource calendar account to an MTR resource account is to delete the existing resource calendar account entirely, and then immediately re-create it as a MTR resource account using the exact same Outlook resource calendar account display name and alias.  This minimizes friction for end users who are accustomed to reserving the conference room using that familiar Outlook display name/alias.  After the conversion, when users reserve the conference room by creating a meeting request using the familiar calendar name, and their meeting request contains a\nMicrosoft Teams\nor Zoom meeting URL, their meeting will now also display as a tap-to-join meeting event on the MTR tabletop console.\nUnlike a traditional resource mailbox that has only a name, an MTR resource account has both a username and its own unique passphrase.  The EMA Team will share this username and passphrase securely with Learning Spaces Support.  When installing the MTR hardware in your room, the (Learning Spaces) technician will type the MTR resource account username and passphrase into the MTR PC's\nMicrosoft Teams\nsign-in menu. This is a one-time operation that allows the MTR PC to sign into the Office 365 cloud. MTR resource accounts use an MS-designed method called Modern Authentication for sign-in, meaning that two-factor (Duo) authentication is not used for this account type.\nPrepare for installation\nChoose an AD security group name to manage the MTR PC\nAn individual or group must assume ongoing responsibility for managing the MTR PC itself. Ideally, an AD security group name should be designated as the administration group for the PC. You should also know this AD group name before hardware installation, so that it can be entered during the on-site MTR PC bootup and config process; see\nCreate, edit, or delete Access Control Management (ACM) groups\n.\nPrepare the MTR PC to connect to the internet\nNote the MTR PC's Ethernet MAC address.\nImportant:\nIf you've purchased the Logitech BASE\nMicrosoft Teams\nRooms (no AV) with Tap + Intel NUC (Mfg. part:\nTAPMSTBASEINT\n), the MAC address of the MTR PC should be listed on a sticker on the outside of the MTR PC's cardboard shipping box. Do\nnot\ndiscard the MTR PC's shipping box before noting the MAC. If you do not have the box, contact Learning Spaces Support for guidance on determining the MTR PC's MAC address.\nMake note of the VLAN that the MTR will reside in if known, or: note the campus, building and data jack id that is listed on a sticker that is affixed to the data jack on the wall that the MTR PC will plug into.\nDetermine a machine FQDN hostname.  Typically, the hostname will closely or exactly match the MTR device account name (for example, if a device account name is\nIN-IT-219@iu.edu\n, the hostname can be\nIN-IT-219.uits.iu.edu\n.\nOnce you've gathered the information above, submit a DHCP Reservation request for the MTR PC; see\nRequest a static IP address at IU\nfor information and the link to the DHCP reservation request form. In the form, for "Address Type:" select\nPublic (not private)\n.\nAt time of installation\nIf using Logitech AV peripherals (Rally Bar + SWYTCH, and Tap), connect them into the MTR PC prior to powering up the MTR PC. For illustration, see page 7 of the\nSwytch Setup Guide\n(PDF).\nConnect the MTR PC to the network using an Ethernet cable and power on the MTR PC.\nOn the "Logitech Tap" screen, a "Setup" menu wizard will appear.\nAccept the EULA agreement. Tap\nNext\n.\nIn the "Account" screen, enter the MTR resource account email address and passphrase.  In the "Supported meeting mode" dropdown, select\nMicrosoft Teams only\n.  Modern Auth should be set to\nenabled\n.  If you do not have the MTR resource account information, just tap\nNext\n.\nIn the "Cortana" menu, enable\nVoice Activation\n.  Tap\nNext\n.\nIn the "Advanced" menu, tap\nNext\n.\nTap\nFinish\nto exit the "Setup" menu wizard.  A new page appears.  Tap the ellipsis (\n…\n) to enter the "More" sidebar menu.\nIn the "More" sidebar menu, tap\nSettings\n.  The local administrator sign-in dialog box appears.  Enter the local administrator password, which when shipped from factory is\nsfb\n.  (You will change this default local administrator password in a subsequent step below).\nThe "Settings" menu appears. Tap\nWindows Settings\n.  Once again, the local administrator sign-in dialog box appears.  Enter the local administrator password, which when shipped from factory is\nsfb\n.\nThe normal windows desktop appears.  Tap to open a web browser and type in this URL:\ngo.iu.edu/4NPz\nDownload the file named\nbomgar-jump-client-mtr.exe\nand double-click to install it.  If successfully installed, a new orange shield icon should appear in the lower right of the Windows taskbar.\nOnce the jump client is installed, the remaining steps can be performed either by the local tech or by a remote representative using the BeyondTrust (Bomgar) representative console:\nDownload the remaining three files:\nLogisyncProvisionToken.json\n,\nLogiSyncProvisionStub.exe\n, and\nMTR_Config.ps1\n.\nDouble-click to install\nLogiSyncProvisionStub.exe\n.  This process registers the MTR PC and its Logitech AV peripherals with the Logitech Sync cloud management portal.  The installation takes approximately five minutes to complete.  If successful, users with access to\nhttps://sync.logitech.com/\nwill see the MTR PC and its connected Logitech peripherals appear in the "Unassigned" section.\nFrom Windows Search, search for\nWindows PowerShell\n, then select\nRun as Administrator\n.  In the PowerShell window, type or copy/paste:\nSet-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy Unrestricted\n, press\nEnter\n, and then type\nY\nfor "Yes".\nType\nSet-Location -Path C:\\users\\Admin\\Downloads\nThe prompt should now read "PS C:\\users\\Admin\\Downloads>"\nType\n.\\MTR_Config.ps1\nand press\nEnter\n.\nAt the "Security" warning prompt, type\nR\nfor "Run Once".\nAt the "Enter your IU username" prompt, type your IU username (does not really matter whose IU username is used here).\nA Windows PowerShell credential request dialog box appears.  Enter your IU passphrase.\nAt the prompt to add "Another group", enter the AD group name that you'd like to grant admin permissions to the MTR PC.  If you have no name in hand, just press\nEnter\n.\nAt the prompt to "Type a password for the Administrator account", type a new local password to replace the factory-default\nsfb\npassword. Be sure to record this new local administrator password in a secure location.\nClose the PowerShell window, and then restart the PC.\nAfter restart, a banner at the top of the Logitech Tap should briefly display a message: "Signing in".  When the signing-in banner disappears, the MTR PC is properly signed into the O365 cloud.\nThe MTR PC is now configured.\nTest the unit by sending an Outlook meeting request with embedded\nMicrosoft Teams\nmeeting information, including the MTR's resource account name as a required participant.  Within two minutes, your meeting request should appear on the "Tap" screen as a tap-to-join meeting event.\nManage the MTR's resource account calendar permissions\nAny individual who belongs to the FASA group can manage the MTR resource account's calendar permissions as per the instructions in\nManage Exchange resource rooms using the resource scheduling settings\n.	KB0025761	KB0025761 v1.0	 a consultation. Purchase equipment Typical recommended hardware Most MTR equipment <strong>packages</strong> will include	4.0419	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:08:46.933651	2026-01-31 17:08:46.933651	0	ca30b07347a456583d373803846d4387
9163	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024673	If you can't log into Canvas	Important:\nIf you just attempted to log into Canvas and were redirected to this page, first check for a Canvas outage at\nStatus.IU\n.  If there is a Canvas alert, wait until it is removed and try to log in again.\nIf no Canvas alert is posted, or you still can't log in, select the option below that corresponds to your situation and follow the instructions to remedy the problem:\nI recently created my first IU computing accounts.\nI am an established faculty member, staff member, or student with an active IU account.\nI am trying to log into Canvas with a group or departmental account.\nNone of the above\nRecently created first IU computing accounts\nTypically, your Canvas account should be available within 24 hours of\ncreating your first IU computing accounts\n. However, if you did not complete the entire account creation process, a Canvas account may not have been created for you.\nTo correct this problem:\nGo to\nCreate My First IU Account\n.\nComplete all steps until you reach the "Summary" page, which looks like this:\nWait three hours and try to log into\nCanvas\nagain. If you are unsuccessful, contact your campus\nSupport Center\nfor assistance.\nFor more, see\nCreate your first IU computing accounts (admitted students, faculty, staff, affiliates, and those returning to IU)\n.\nEstablished employee or student with an active IU account\nIf you already have active IU accounts but your email settings are incomplete or out of date, a Canvas account will not be created for you until you update your settings. This issue commonly affects students, faculty, and staff returning to IU after a prolonged absence, but can affect others as well.\nTo correct this problem:\nFollow the instructions for new students, faculty, and staff\nabove\n.\nIf you're still unable to access Canvas after following the instructions above, or if you're unable to complete the account creation process due to an error, follow the instructions in\nChange your primary email address\n.\nWait approximately three hours after selecting your primary email address and email forwarding settings and try to log into\nCanvas\nagain. If you are unsuccessful, contact your campus\nSupport Center\nfor assistance.\nGroup or departmental accounts\nCanvas accounts are not automatically created for\ngroup or departmental accounts\n. To establish a Canvas account for a group or departmental account,\nsend a request to the Canvas at IU administrators\n. Once the Canvas administrators confirm the creation of the account, you can use your IU group account username and passphrase to log into Canvas.\nNone of the above\nIf none of the scenarios above apply to you, contact your campus\nSupport Center\nfor assistance.	KB0024673	KB0024673 v1.0	 first IU <strong>computing</strong> accounts. I am an established faculty member, staff member, or student with an of the above Recently created first IU <strong>computing</strong> accounts Typically, your Canvas account should be available within 24 hours of creating your first IU <strong>computing</strong> accounts. However, if you did not complete	89.9902		2026-01-31 15:05:59.125389	2026-01-31 17:05:05.963582	0	9900343f476456583d373803846d4362
11302	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6cbfa477476456583d373803846d4349	Find information about previous PEARC and XSEDE conferences	Note:\nThe XSEDE project ended August 31, 2022, making way for the Advanced Cyberinfrastructure Coordination Ecosystem: Services & Support (\nACCESS\n) project, a new NSF project that began September 1, 2022. For more, see the\nACCESS\nwebsite.\nThe annual Practice & Experience in Advanced Research Computing (\nPEARC\n) conference series continues the success of past Extreme Science and Engineering Discovery Environment (XSEDE) conferences in fostering the development of a dynamic, connected community of innovative research computing professionals from academia, government, industry, and other organizations who are significantly advancing the effective operation and use of campus and national research cyberinfrastructure. PEARC conference organizers place particular emphasis on engaging under-represented minorities, under-represented domains, women, and people with disabilities.\nProceedings for previous PEARC and XSEDE conferences are archived in the Association for Computing Machinery (\nACM\n) Digital Library:\nPEARC23:\nThe 2023 conference was held July 23-27, 2023, in Portland, OR. For the proceedings, see\nPractice and Experience in Advanced Research Computing\n2023: Computing for the Common Good\n.\nPEARC22:\nThe 2022 conference was held July 10-14, 2022, in Boston, MA. For the proceedings, see\nPractice and Experience in Advanced Research Computing 2022: Revolutionary - Computing, Connections, You\n.\nPEARC21:\nThe 2021 conference was held July 19-22, 2021. For health and safety reasons, PEARC21 was a virtual conference. For the proceedings, see\nPractice and Experience in Advanced Research Computing 2021: Evolution Across All Dimensions\n.\nPEARC20:\nThe 2020 conference was held July 26-30, 2020. For health and safety reasons, PEARC20 was a virtual conference. For the proceedings, see\nPractice and Experience in Advanced Research Computing 2020: Catch the Wave\n.\nPEARC19:\nThe 2019 conference was held July 28 - August 1, 2019, at the Hyatt Regency Hotel in Chicago, IL. For for the proceedings, see\nPractice and Experience in Advanced Research Computing 2019: Rise of the Machines (learning)\n.\nPEARC18:\nThe 2018 conference was held July 22-27, 2018, at the Wyndham Grand Hotel in Pittsburgh, PA. For the proceedings, see\nPractice and Experience in Advanced Research Computing 2018: Seamless Creativity\n.\nPEARC17:\nThe inaugural PEARC conference was held July 9-13, 2017, at the Hyatt Regency Hotel in New Orleans, LA. For the proceedings, see\nPractice and Experience in Advanced Research Computing 2017: Sustainability, Success and Impact\n.\nXSEDE16:\nThe fifth annual XSEDE conference (XSEDE16) was held July 17-21, 2016, at the InterContinental Miami Hotel in Miami, FL. For the proceedings, see\nProceedings of the XSEDE16 Conference on Diversity, Big Data, and Science at Scale\n.\nXSEDE15:\nThe 2015 XSEDE conference (XSEDE15) was held July 26-30, 2015, in St. Louis, MO, at the Marriott Renaissance St. Louis Grand Hotel. For the proceedings, see\nProceedings of the 2015 XSEDE Conference: Scientific Advancements Enabled by Enhanced Cyberinfrastructure\n.\nXSEDE14:\nThe 2014 XSEDE conference (XSEDE14) was held July 13-18, 2014, at the Marriott Marquis Hotel in Atlanta, GA. For the proceedings, see\nProceedings of the 2014 Annual Conference on Extreme Science and Engineering Discovery Environment\n.\nXSEDE13:\nThe 2013 XSEDE conference (XSEDE13) was\nheld July 22-25, 2013, at the Marriott Marquis Hotel & Marina in San\nDiego, CA. For the proceedings, see\nProceedings of the Conference on Extreme Science and Engineering Discovery Environment: Gateway to Discovery\n.\nXSEDE12:\nThe 2012 XSEDE conference (XSEDE12) was held July 16-19, 2012, at the InterContinental Hotel in Chicago, IL. For the proceedings, see\nProceedings of the 1st Conference of the Extreme Science and Engineering Discovery Environment: Bridging from the eXtreme to the Campus and Beyond\n.	KB0022868	KB0022868 v1.0	 <strong>Computing</strong> (PEARC) conference series continues the success of past Extreme Science and Engineering Discovery of innovative research <strong>computing</strong> professionals from academia, government, industry, and other for previous PEARC and XSEDE conferences are archived in the Association for <strong>Computing</strong> Machinery (ACM	80.5961		2026-01-31 17:05:14.538617	2026-01-31 17:05:14.538617	0	6cbfa477476456583d373803846d4349
11303	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b600bc3f476456583d373803846d43cf	Public cloud resources for research	Table of contents\nOverview\nAmazon Web Services (AWS)\nGoogle Cloud\nMicrosoft Azure\nOverview\nIU has contracts with the three main public cloud platforms, Amazon Web Services (AWS), Google Cloud, and Microsoft Azure. They offer hyper-scale computing resources on demand. While those services are not free beyond limited trial usage, each vendor provides support for research in the form of credits and, in some cases, free consulting. The information here outlines what is available to IU faculty, how to take advantage of it through IU-sanctioned environments, and the kind of support available to help you choose what works best for your use case.\nEach vendor has a slightly different process and guidelines for applying for research credits. The links below point to vendor documentation. If you have additional questions,\nsubmit the\nCloud Support form\n.\nResearch credits are time-limited and can generally be requested only once per project. To ensure that you have the best opportunity to use your award before it expires, pay special attention to questions about when you will start your work when completing your application.\nImportant:\nSome resources linked here may take you into the account interface for that cloud platform. Be sure to work in an account on IU's cloud contracts to  take advantage of the listed support and benefits.\nAmazon Web Services (AWS)\nAWS is a generous supporter of academic research and the advancement of science. This support takes the form of data sets, tools, consultation, and credits to pay for the use of the AWS computing platform.\nResearch and Technical Computing Portal\n: The\nResearch and Technical Computing Portal\nis a gateway to information about research computing on AWS. Find case studies, request letters of support, and learn about AWS collaborations with NSF, NIH, NASA, and others.\nPublic Datasets:\nThe\nAWS Public Dataset Program\noffers support for preparing and publicly hosting high-value datasets.\nVisit the registry of currently hosted datasets at the\nRegistry of Open Data on AWS\n.\nEarth on AWS\nis a program for working with geospatial workloads and applications.\nCloud Credits for Research:\nAWS Machine Learning Research Awards\nprovides both cash and credit awards to support eligible programs and researchers in this rapidly emerging computing field.\nAWS Cloud Credits for Research\nsupports researchers doing and sharing their work in a variety of areas.\nResearch Consulting:\nThis dedicated team of scientific experts focuses on scientific computing and research workloads. To get connected,\nsubmit the\nCloud Support form\n.\nResearch-focused training:\nTo help researchers learn about cloud computing, Amazon has curated a\nlist of no-cost, on-demand online courses tailored to researchers\n.\nGoogle Cloud\nGoogle Cloud is generous in its support of research and teaching. The IU Google Cloud environment has safeguards in place to protect research workloads. For fast-track assistance with account setup,\nsubmit the\nCloud Support form\n.\nDatasets:\nGoogle Cloud Public Datasets are available to query directly, to use for developing machine learning models, for application development, or other uses.\nFor an overview, see\nGoogle Cloud Public Datasets\n.\nFor Public Dataset program details, see\nGoogle Cloud Public Datasets\n.\nCloud Credits for Research:\nGoogle Cloud research grants start at $5,000, and higher grant amounts are possible with written justification. See\nBreak through with research credits\n.\nResearch Consulting:\nTo get connected to technical subject matter experts on Google Cloud to help with needs gathering, best practices, solutions architecture, and skill building,\nsubmit the\nCloud Support form\n.\nMicrosoft Azure\nMicrosoft's Azure cloud platform has a deep and flexible platform suitable for use in a wide variety of research use cases; see\nMicrosoft Azure for research\n.\nDatasets:\nMicrosoft has a number of public datasets for a variety of uses, including the two below, and others are available through web searches.\nAzure Open Datasets\nPublic data sets for testing and prototyping\nCloud Credits for Research:\nFor details about Azure research credits,\nsubmit the\nCloud Support form\n. Staff will put you in touch with the appropriate Microsoft representative.\nResearch Consulting:\nTo be connected to an Azure solutions architect or research associate, depending on your use case and area of research,\nsubmit the\nCloud Support form\n.	KB0024805	KB0024805 v1.0	, and Microsoft Azure. They offer hyper-scale <strong>computing</strong> resources on demand. While those services, consultation, and credits to pay for the use of the AWS <strong>computing</strong> platform. Research and Technical <strong>Computing</strong> Portal: The Research and Technical <strong>Computing</strong> Portal is a gateway to information about research	74.5509		2026-01-31 17:05:19.921666	2026-01-31 17:05:19.921666	0	b600bc3f476456583d373803846d43cf
