11326	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d3362d8d47323e94d500b87b716d433a	Delete an IU computing account	To delete a computing account at Indiana University,\ncontact Tier 2\n.\nNote:\nA request to delete a group, departmental, or student organization account must come from the account's current owner. For auditing purposes, the group account username will remain associated with your University ID after the account is deleted.\nIf possible, send your message from an IU email account. In the message, include:\nYour username\nYour name\nYour\nUniversity ID\nnumber (if any)\nIf you are deleting a group account, the name of the account you wish to delete\nThe system(s) from which you need to have the account deleted (for example, Exchange).\nIf you need accounts deleted from all systems, specify "All".	KB0025246	KB0025246 v3.0	To delete a <strong>computing</strong> account at Indiana University, contact Tier 2. Note: A request to delete a group, departmental, or student organization account must come from the account's current owner. For auditing purposes, the group account username will remain associated with your University ID after	38.206		2026-01-31 17:06:28.247708	2026-01-31 17:06:28.247708	0	d3362d8d47323e94d500b87b716d433a
11394	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=31c13a78477872d0d500b87b716d4366	Get started with the IU Common Build for Windows	Table of contents\nOverview\nIU Common Build structure and naming\nComputer locations\nComputer naming convention\nActive Directory Organizational Units\nGroup policy\nBuild process\nPost-build scripts\nManage Common Build computers\nLocal UITS support people access to AD and SCCM\nSoftware\nUpdates\nGet help\nOverview\nThe IU Common Build for Windows provides central management for a great number of endpoint management functions. This includes AD group policy, BitLocker, LAPS, and all SCCM settings and software. The IU Common Build adheres to a high standard of security, based on CIS Benchmarks.\nTo use the Global Windows OSD with Data Transfer, see\nTechnician instructions: IU Common Build Windows Wipe and Rebuild with Data Transfer\n.\nTo install a clean IU Common Build, see\nTechnician instructions: Clean IU Common Build for Windows\n.\nFor common windows management tasks, see\nIU Common Build: Windows management tasks\n.\nIf your unit or department does not yet have an OU within the Windows Common Build structure, fill out the\nOn-Boarding Questionnaire\n.\nIU Common Build structure and naming\nComputer locations\nFollowing the adoption of the IU Common Build, your department's computers will reside in the following locations:\nIn Active Directory:\nads.iu.edu\\IU\\IU-UEMS\\Production\\\nCampus\n\\\nDept-Prefix\n\\Workstations\nIn Microsoft Configuration Manager (SCCM):\nAssets and Compliance\\Overview\\Device Collections\\IU-UEMS\\Departments\\\nDept-Prefix\nComputer naming convention\nComputers will use the naming convention\nIU-\nSerialNumber\n. This universal naming convention is necessary to allow IU to transition to the Autopilot adoption system, in which machines come from Dell pre-built; thus, department prefixes cannot be included in the system name.\nActive Directory Organizational Units\nAll legacy unit OUs will continue to exist with the same structure in Active Directory, with all servers and AD groups intact.\nEMS will create sub-OUs under the Common Build departmental OU for specific functional needs, but will not create them solely for visual organization.\nGroup policy\nThe IU Common Build for Windows applies the same baseline security GPOs to all workstations. You may request an up-to-date report of these policies; to do so, use the\nEMS FireForm\n.\nEMS will set up printer and drive map policies for each unit. Local UITS Support People cannot write GPOs directly. If you have additional group policies that provide a documented functional need, submit them to EMS in advance via the\nEMS FireForm\n. EMS will vet all such requests.\nBuild process\nTo move workstations to the IU Common Build, local UITS support people will need to rebuild every existing workstation. The timeline for rebuilding workstations is at the discretion of the unit. For standard workstations, you can use one of two methods:\nTechnician instructions: IU Common Build Windows Wipe and Rebuild with Data Transfer\n. Submit an SCCM collection to EMS to specify which devices will receive the Global Windows OSD. Note that since all users can see the upgrade within Software Center, you may wish to populate the collection only as you are ready to rebuild devices. If the logged-in user is not a local UITS support person, the rebuild will be visible but will not run.\nStandard build from a thumb drive\n; the build media is at\n\\\\iu-sccm-cmfs1\\uems$\nNote:\nDuring the build, the system will be automatically joined to the ADS domain using the\nSCCMJOIN\ngroup account.\nAfter rebuild, legacy computer objects will continue to exist in your old SCCM tenant and AD OU until automatic cleanup removes them. You may delete them manually if you wish, but it is not necessary.\nPost-build scripts\nPost-build scripts will not be supported on the Common Build; however, where applicable, functionality will be built into the GPO and SCCM infrastructure. Send EMS the names and content of any post-build scripts you are running against your current workstations.\nManage Common Build computers\nLocal UITS support people access to AD and SCCM\nLocal UITS support people will have full read access to all system details. In AD, this includes LAPS and BitLocker information. For SCCM, this includes\nreports\n, full read access to the tenant, and basic device actions. For more, see\nIU Common Build: Windows management tasks\n.\nFor full details on setting up and editing members of your team's Local UITS Support People admin group for the Common Build, see\nLocal UITS Support People admin accounts for the IU Common Build\n.\nLocal UITS support people will be able to see computers in AD and SCCM, but not delete them. However, deletion from SCCM or AD is not necessary to rebuild a system. For workstations that will no longer be used (sent to Surplus), send a request for EMS to delete the system via the\nEMS FireForm\n.\nSoftware\nAll standard software will still be available at build. All software in the\nGlobal Software Catalog\nwill be available in Software Center.\nMost software is optional and can be selected at build or from Software Center after build. Required software (automatically installed) includes only a few basics, such as asset inventory software and basic security prerequisites.\nAdditional software will not be deployed as required to Common Build devices without a functional need.\nIf you need any custom software or settings, fill out the\nTicket Submission form\n. EMS can package and deploy any software for you, including licensed and specialty applications. local UITS support people cannot deploy software or settings via SCCM directly.\nUpdates\nStandard Windows and third-party updates are deployed during the weekly maintenance window from 11pm Thursday to 5am Friday. For more, see\nGlobal Software Updates Workflow, Processing, and End-user Experience\n. This maintenance window is the only SCCM setting applied to all computers.\nGet help\nUse the\nTicket Submission form\nfor Common Build requests.	KB0025669	KB0025669 v12.0	Table of contents Overview IU Common Build structure and naming Computer locations Computer naming convention Active Directory Organizational Units Group policy Build process Post-build scripts Manage Common Build computers Local UITS support people access to AD and SCCM Software Updates Get help Overview The IU Common Build for Windows provides	3.2662	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits, km-aud-ems-admins	2026-01-31 17:08:50.983476	2026-01-31 17:08:50.983476	0	31c13a78477872d0d500b87b716d4366
11329	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6320743347a456583d373803846d4358	Access JAWS in the STC computing labs	This guide is intended to walk you through the process of accessing JAWS, a screen reader software available in the\nSTC computing labs\n.\nAccess JAWS\nBecause no screen reading software is running by default when you start working on computers in the STC labs, you will need to open Windows Narrator with\nWindows\n-\nCtrl\n-\nEnter\n.\nOnce Narrator is running, do the following to log in:\nPress\nCtrl\n-\nAlt\n-\nDelete\nto get to the login screen.\nEnter your username and then press\nTab\n.\nEnter your password and then press\nEnter\n.\nA long pause will occur while your information is loaded, which can take up to 1 minute.\nNarrator will not be running from the desktop screen, so press\nWindows\n-\nCtrl\n-\nEnter\nto launch Narrator again.\nPress the\nWindows\nkey and type\nJAWS\n.\nWhen you hear Narrator announce JAWS, press\nEnter\n.\nOnce JAWS has loaded successfully, you will hear both screen readers running simultaneously. To shut off Microsoft Narrator, press\nWindows\n-\nCtrl\n-\nEnter\nand then the right arrow key until you get to the Exit Narrator button.\nPress\nEnter\nto exit Windows Narrator.\nJAWS is now running, and you will be able to use it until logging off the computer.\nLearn more\nAbout JAWS\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0025555	KB0025555 v1.0	This guide is intended to walk you through the process of accessing JAWS, a screen reader software available in the STC <strong>computing</strong> labs. Access JAWS Because no screen reading software is running by default when you start working on computers in the STC labs, you will need to open Windows Narrator	37.682		2026-01-31 17:06:33.660941	2026-01-31 17:06:33.660941	0	6320743347a456583d373803846d4358
11330	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=2eaf6c37476456583d373803846d435b	If someone else is using your Unix computing accounts	Note:\nIf you authenticate to the Unix system with your IU passphrase, you should change that.\nIn\nUnix\n, if you find that someone else is using your computing accounts:\nChange your password immediately.\nYour local security office or system administrator may want to know that your accounts have been compromised. Check with your system administrator to see what process you should follow. At Indiana University, email the\nUniversity Information Security Office\nat\nuiso@iu.edu\n. In the message, explain in detail what has happened.\nMake sure you don't have a\n.rhosts\nfile in your account, as the intruder might have created a file that allows access to your account without a password. If the file isn't there, the attempt to delete it won't hurt. At the\nshell\nprompt, enter:\nrm .rhosts\nTo be on the safe side, consider doing this with all of your accounts.\nAt Indiana University, for personal or departmental Linux or Unix systems support, see\nGet help for Linux or Unix at IU\n.	KB0022687	KB0022687 v1.0	Note: If you authenticate to the Unix system with your IU passphrase, you should change that. In Unix, if you find that someone else is using your <strong>computing</strong> accounts: Change your password immediately. Your local security office or system administrator may want to know that your accounts have been	37.3216		2026-01-31 17:06:38.360761	2026-01-31 17:06:38.360761	0	2eaf6c37476456583d373803846d435b
11332	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=4920f4ff476456583d373803846d4317	Access NVDA in the STC computing labs	Overview\nThe steps below explain how to access NVDA, a screen reader software available in the\nSTC computing labs\n.\nAccess NVDA\nBecause no screen reading software is running by default when you start working on computers in the STC labs, you will need to open Windows Narrator with\nWin\n-Ctrl-Enter\n.\nOnce Narrator is running, do the following to log in:\nPress\nCtrl\n+\nAlt\n+\nDelete\nto get to the login screen.\nEnter your username and then press\nTab\n.\nEnter your password and then press\nEnter\n.\nA long pause will occur while your information is loaded, which can take up to one minute.\nNarrator will not be running from the login screen, so press\nWin\n+\nCtrl\n+\nEnter\nto launch Narrator again.\nPress the\nWin\nkey and type\nNVDA\n.\nWhen you hear Narrator announce NVDA, press\nEnter\n.\nOnce NVDA has loaded successfully, you will hear both screen readers running simultaneously. To shut off Microsoft Narrator, press\nWin\n+\nCtrl\n+\nEnter\n. If Narrator doesn't announce that it is exiting by default, press the right arrow until you get to the\nExit Narrator\nbutton.\nPress\nEnter\nto exit Windows Narrator.\nNVDA is now running, and you will be able to use it until you log off the computer.\nLearn more\nNVDA basics\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0025336	KB0025336 v1.0	Overview The steps below explain how to access NVDA, a screen reader software available in the STC <strong>computing</strong> labs. Access NVDA Because no screen reading software is running by default when you start working on computers in the STC labs, you will need to open Windows Narrator with Win-Ctrl-Enter	37.2322		2026-01-31 17:06:41.922021	2026-01-31 17:06:41.922021	0	4920f4ff476456583d373803846d4317
10999	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a95c42f2476466942fc6a55b416d435f	Add a VDB Cache Refresh job to the Denodo Scheduler	To add a VDB Cache Refresh job to the Denodo Scheduler:\nAccess the Denodo Scheduler:\nIU community:\nGo to\nhttps://denodo-dev.dvt.iu.edu/solution-manager-web-tool/\n.\nLog in with your\nIU username\nand\npassphrase\n, and then confirm your identity with\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n.\nUse the "Environment" drop-down to select\nDev\nfor the Development scheduler.\nOn the\nScheduler\ntile, select\nOpen\n.\nFrom the\nAdd Job\ndrop-down, choose\nVDPCache\n:\nNote:\nDenodo caching is used to enhance query performance. Caching is resource intensive, so you should implement it only after detecting query performance issues (not by default). Run cache jobs as infrequently as possible. Do not refresh your cache more than once an hour. Cache necessary data only, and do not cache excessively large database columns, such as CLOBs or LOBs.\nSelect your project from the "Project name" drop-down menu.\nNote:\nIf the\nADD JOB\nbutton is grayed out, verify that you are connected to the development scheduler; permission to create new jobs is only available in dev. If the button is grayed out in the development scheduler, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\nto contact EDSS.\nFor "Job name:", enter a name for your job; for "Job description:", enter a job description.\nOn the\nExtraction section\ntab:\nChoose your data source. Data sources will be named\nxx_xxxx VDB\n, in which\nxx_xxxx\nis the name of your virtual database (VDB).\nNote:\nIf you don't see your data source listed, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\nto contact EDSS.\nNext to "Database", select\nCycle\n. Choose your VDB.\nNext to "View:", select\nCycle\n, and then select the appropriate view from the drop-down.\nAdd criteria as needed, and then select\nSave\n.\nOn the\nHandlers section\ntab, set mail notifications:\nSelect\nAdd Handler\n, and then, from the drop-down, choose\nMail\n.\nFill in the options as appropriate, and then select\nSave\n.\nOn the\nTriggers section\ntab:\nSelect\nAdd Trigger\n.\nIn the "Cron expression:" field, enter the cron expression; alternatively, use the cron-like UI to specify when the extract should run. For instance, you can specify a particular minute and hour of the day, which will schedule the extract to run daily at that time.\nNote:\nDon't schedule your cache refresh job to run more than once per hour, as such jobs will be re-scheduled to run hourly without notice.\nSelect\nSave\n.\nNote:\nWhen you are ready to migrate the VDB Cache Refresh job from dev to stage and/or production, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\nto contact EDSS.\nTo learn more, see the\nDenodo Scheduler Administration Guide\n. For assistance with the Denodo Scheduler, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\n.	KB0024078	KB0024078 v2.0	 virtual <strong>database</strong> (VDB). Note: If you don't see your data source listed, fill out the EDSD Support Form to contact EDSS. Next to "<strong>Database</strong>", select Cycle. Choose your VDB. Next to "View:", select Cycle	4.1005		2026-01-31 16:58:25.448765	2026-01-31 16:58:25.448765	0	a95c42f2476466942fc6a55b416d435f
10651	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3100383f476456583d373803846d43e6	Data classes for use on public cloud computing platforms at IU	Public cloud providers\nAmazon Web Services (AWS)\n,\nGoogle Cloud\n, and\nMicrosoft Azure\nare approved for use by Indiana University faculty and staff through central contracts. (Student accounts on these services are not covered.)\nPrior to obtaining an account or developing using AWS, Google Cloud, or Azure at IU, you must follow the steps in\nGet an AWS, Google Cloud, or Microsoft Azure account at IU\n.\nAWS, Google Cloud, and Azure are approved for data classified as\nPublic or University-Internal data\n. Use of these platforms to store, process, or transmit\nCritical or Restricted data\n(including, but not limited to, health data regulated by\nHIPAA\nand\npayment card data\n) must be specifically approved by the appropriate\nData Stewards\nor responsible office (for example, the Office of the Treasurer for PCI data).\nImportant:\nAlthough public cloud vendors apply their own security protocols, you are ultimately responsible for securely managing the data stored, processed, or transmitted by the services you build or use on the approved cloud platforms.\nReview Protect IU's\nUse of Cloud Computing\nfor risks and recommendations while using cloud computing through IU.	KB0024719	KB0024719 v1.0	 you build or use on the approved cloud platforms. Review Protect IU's Use of Cloud <strong>Computing</strong> for risks and recommendations while using cloud <strong>computing</strong> through IU.	35.8402		2026-01-31 16:54:07.098637	2026-01-31 17:06:53.257977	0	3100383f476456583d373803846d43e6
11334	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=2620303347a456583d373803846d43c2	Access ZoomText in the STC computing labs	Overview\nThe steps below explain how to access ZoomText, a screen magnification software available in the\nSTC computing labs\n.\nAccess ZoomText\nNote:\nIf magnification is required, you can launch Windows Magnifier from the STC login screen by pressing\nWin\n- +\n(plus sign).\nPress\nCtrl\n+\nAlt\n+\nDelete\nto bring up the login screen.\nLog in using your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nPress\nWin\n, type\nZoomText 2020\n, and then press\nEnter\n.\nThe ZoomText splash screen will appear.\nIf you launched Magnifier, press\nWindows\n+\nEsc\nto exit at this time.\nNext you should see a "Welcome to ZoomText" dialog box with some introductory information. When you are finished reading it, select the\nOK\nbutton.\nNote:\nIf the ZoomText "Updates Available" dialog box appears, select the\nNo\nbutton.\nThe ZoomText startup wizard window will appear next:\nLeave "Language" set to\nEnglish\n.\nSelect the "Synthesizer" from the dropdown box or leave the default.\nSelect the "Voice" from the dropdown box or leave the default.\nSelect the\nNext\nbutton.\nLeave "Enable ZoomText in the Windows logon prompt" unchecked.\nLeave "Start ZoomText automatically when Windows starts" unchecked.\nSelect the\nNext\nbutton.\nLeave "Enable ZoomText Error Reporting" unchecked.\nSelect the\nFinish\nbutton.\nYou can now begin using ZoomText.\nLearn more\nFor video tutorials and training on ZoomText, visit\nZoomText Training\n.\nFor ZoomText User guides, visit\nZoomText User Guides\n.\nFor recorded webinars on ZoomText features and tools, see\nWebinars on Demand\n.\nFor additional ZoomText support, contact your\ncampus Support Center\n.\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0025481	KB0025481 v1.0	Overview The steps below explain how to access ZoomText, a screen magnification software available in the STC <strong>computing</strong> labs. Access ZoomText Note: If magnification is required, you can launch Windows Magnifier from the STC login screen by pressing Win - + (plus sign). Press Ctrl+Alt+Delete	35.6703		2026-01-31 17:06:57.425051	2026-01-31 17:06:57.425051	0	2620303347a456583d373803846d43c2
3180	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=45ef6c3b476456583d373803846d43c7	Computing accounts for IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis student organizations	Student organization accounts must be sponsored by a faculty or staff member who is ultimately responsible for the account and its use. To obtain and maintain UITS computing accounts:\nAt IU Bloomington, organizations must be registered with the Student Involvement and Leadership Center. For more, see the\nregistration instructions\n.\nAt IU Indianapolis, organizations must be registered with the\nDivision of Student Affairs\n.\nIU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis student organizations may possess one\nUniversity account\nand the\nUITS\ncomputing accounts to which it is entitled. The organizational representatives may suggest an account name which reflects the official name of the organization.\nOrganizational accounts are renewed each year to verify that the organization still exists, that it remains an officially recognized IU Bloomington or IU Indianapolis student organization, and that the faculty member agrees to continued sponsorship of the account. Normally, organizational accounts are automatically renewed, unless the faculty sponsor or the account owner leaves the university. In this case, UITS consults with the Student Involvement and Leadership Center or the SLDP office to decide which accounts should be renewed and which accounts should be deleted. Therefore, owners or sponsors of student organization computer accounts need not do anything to renew the account, unless they will be leaving the university. In this case, the student group must find another faculty sponsor or another student to act as the account owner.\nThe Student Involvement and Leadership Center at IU Bloomington and the SLDP office at IU Indianapolis handle changes of faculty sponsorship. UITS\nTier 2 Accounts and Student Information Systems (ASIS)\nis responsible for changes of computer account ownership. To request a transfer of a group account, see\nTransfer ownership of a group or departmental account\n.\nImportant:\nUse of UITS computing accounts by IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis student organizations is subject to the rights and responsibilities accepted at initial account creation. IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis student organization computing accounts are for the sole and exclusive use of the IU Bloomington or IU Indianapolis student organization and its designated members, and may not be used for or by other organizations or individuals not affiliated with the university.	KB0023973	KB0023973 v1.0	 responsible for the account and its use. To obtain and maintain UITS <strong>computing</strong> accounts: At IU University account and the UITS <strong>computing</strong> accounts to which it is entitled. The organizational. Important: Use of UITS <strong>computing</strong> accounts by IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis student	35.6473		2026-01-31 14:39:02.523288	2026-01-31 17:07:01.107486	0	45ef6c3b476456583d373803846d43c7
11337	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=0fafec37476456583d373803846d4364	Give yourself or others login privileges on your Windows computer at IU	At Indiana University, to give yourself or others login privileges on your Windows computer, first set up your computer to\njoin the IU ADS domain\n.\nYou can then add anyone who belongs either to that computer's domain or to a domain that has a trust relationship with your computer's domain.\nTo add a domain user as a user on your computer:\nLog in as an\nadministrator\nor as a user in the local Administrators group.\nFrom the Control Panel, open\nUser Accounts\n. Select\nManage User Accounts\n, select\nChange account type\n, and then select\nAdd\n.\nTo locate the account, select\nBrowse\nand select the account when you find it. If you know the account you want to add, type the username, and enter\nADS\nas the domain.\nThe account receives Standard user rights by default.\nSelect\nFinish\n. ADS accounts added in this manner will appear in the list of user accounts. Users in the list of user accounts will be able to log into your computer using their IU ADS username and password.	KB0022732	KB0022732 v1.0	At Indiana University, to give yourself or others login privileges on your Windows computer, first set up your computer to join the IU ADS domain. You can then add anyone who belongs either to that computer's domain or to a domain that has a trust relationship with your computer's domain. To add a domain user as a user on your computer: Log in a	34.3779		2026-01-31 17:07:04.577187	2026-01-31 17:07:04.577187	0	0fafec37476456583d373803846d4364
11338	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a9f6b15247bf1a902fc6a55b416d4358	About costs for IU research computing services	At Indiana University,\nUITS\nResearch Technologies\nprovides a broad range of research computing services that are available without fee to IU students, faculty, and staff. Baseline\nResearch Technologies\nservices include accounts on IU's\nresearch supercomputers\n, access to allocated and short-term storage space on\nResearch Technologies\nsystems, use of advanced media and interactive visualization technologies, widely used scientific, analytical, and bioinformatics applications, and proven professional training, consultation, and support services. For more, see:\nSupercomputers for academic research at IU\nAvailable access to allocated and short-term storage capacity on IU's research systems\nAbout the UITS Advanced Visualization Lab (AVL)\nResearch software available at IU\nVisualization and graphics software at IU\nResearch computing support at IU\nTraining and outreach for advanced computing and research\nSchools, departments, labs, and individual investigators with needs beyond those met by baseline (no fee) services can arrange direct-bill, fee-based agreements with UITS\nResearch Technologies\n. For more about fee-based and/or chargeback services offered by\nResearch Technologies\n, see:\nResearch Technologies chargeback services policy\nPublic cloud resources for research\nColocation services: Departmentally owned or UITS-managed research IT systems and system components\nFee-based research storage\nTo find fee information for\nResearch Technologies\nservices, see the "IU Internal\nResearch Technologies\nServices" section of\nUITS Rates for Direct-Bill Services\n. If you are interested in fee-based storage services,\ncontact UITS\nResearch Technologies\nto request a meeting.	KB0022561	KB0022561 v3.0	At Indiana University, UITS Research Technologies provides a broad range of research <strong>computing</strong> services that are available without fee to IU students, faculty, and staff. Baseline Research and graphics software at IU Research <strong>computing</strong> support at IU Training and outreach for advanced <strong>computing</strong>	34.3627		2026-01-31 17:07:08.029967	2026-01-31 17:07:08.029967	0	a9f6b15247bf1a902fc6a55b416d4358
11348	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e1bfa877476456583d373803846d43f3	Change the name of your Windows computer	Table of contents\nOverview\nWindows 11\nWindows 10\nor\nWindows 8.x\nOverview\nYour computer's name is the unique name that identifies it on a network.\nUITS\nrecommends that you use your\nUniversity account\nusername to name your computer on the IU network.\nNotes:\nFor computers in campus housing, UITS recommends you enter either\nbl-rh-username\n(at IU Bloomington) or\nin-rh-username\n(at IU Indianapolis), replacing\nusername\nwith your\nUniversity account\nusername. If you have more than one computer connected on campus, you can place a single-digit number after the username. For example, at IU Bloomington, if your username were\ndvader\n, your first computer name would be\nbl-rh-dvader\nand the second would be\nbl-rh-dvader2\n.\nFor computer naming conventions in IU departments, see\nRecommended naming conventions for IU Windows computers and groups\n.\nWindows 11\nTo change your computer's name in\nWindows 11\n:\nPress\nWin\n+\nx\nand select\nSystem\n.\nNext to the computer model at the top, select\nRename this PC\n.\nIf prompted, enter an\nadministrator account username and password\n.\nEnter a new name for your computer.\nChoose\nRestart now\nor\nRestart later\n. Your computer's name will change.\nWindows 10\nor\nWindows 8.x\nTo change your computer's name in\nWindows 10\nor\nWindows 8.x\n:\nLog into your computer with\nadministrative rights\n.\nNavigate to the Control Panel.\nSelect the\nSystem\nicon. If you do not see the\nSystem\nicon, in the upper right corner, switch the view to\nLarge icons\nor\nSmall icons\n.\nIn the "System" window that appears, under the "Computer name, domain and workgroup settings" section, on the right, select\nChange settings\n.\nYou will see the "System Properties" window. At the top of the window, select the\nComputer Name\ntab.\nSelect\nChange...\n. You will see your computer's name listed.\nIn the space marked "Computer Name:", delete the text and enter a new name for your computer.\nSelect\nOK\n, then\nOK\nagain, and then close the "System" window.	KB0022959	KB0022959 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Windows 11 Windows 10 or Windows 8.x Overview Your computer's name is the unique name that identifies it on a network. UITS recommends that you use your University account username to name your computer on the IU network. Notes: For computers in campus housing, UITS recommends you enter either bl-rh-username (at IU Blo	32.6316		2026-01-31 17:07:15.772212	2026-01-31 17:07:15.772212	0	e1bfa877476456583d373803846d43f3
11349	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c2bf2c77476456583d373803846d43c7	Restore your Windows computer to a previous configuration	For help restoring your Windows computer to a previous working configuration, see\nRecovery options in Windows\n.	KB0023007	KB0023007 v1.0	For help restoring your Windows computer to a previous working configuration, see Recovery options in Windows.	30.4385		2026-01-31 17:07:19.167941	2026-01-31 17:07:19.167941	0	c2bf2c77476456583d373803846d43c7
10526	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=83ef647b476456583d373803846d43f5	Paid storage in Google at IU Shared Drives	Table of contents\nOverview\nMigrate\nPay for your storage\nConfirm that your paid storage is in place\n(KB login required)\nShare folders\nMore on\nGoogle Shared Drives\nand\nGoogle Secure Storage\nOverview\nDue to\nchanges to Google storage at IU\n, faculty and staff who wish to pay for additional storage in Google must use\nGoogle at IU\nShared Drives\n(GSD).\nStorage costs for paid\nGoogle Shared Drives\nare $48 per year, in 100 GB increments.\nEnsure that you have a sustainable source of funding before moving forward with the process described here; consult your\nlocal UITS support person\nor financial officers as appropriate.\nNote:\nGoogle Shared Drives\nare approved for higher data classification levels than\nGoogle My Drive\nin some data domains. In addition,\nGoogle Shared Drives\nare an appropriate location for\ninstitutional data\n, but\nGoogle My Drive\nis not.\nSee\nIU Data Management\nfor more on data privacy, security, and storage.\nMigrate your data\nTo prepare and migrate your data for the paid GSD option in\nGoogle at IU\n:\nIf you don't already have a GSD you'd like to use as your paid option,\ncomplete the form\nto create one for that purpose. Under "Choose your storage service", select\nGoogle Shared Drive\nand\nPaid GSD\n.\nNote:\nIf you need to move data from a\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n, be sure to request the ability to\nmove whole folders\n(not individual files) from\nGoogle My Drive\nvia the custom\nGoogle at IU Shared Drive Folder Movement Request form\n. Requests are processed once weekly, on Fridays.\nMove the content from your My Drive (group or personal account) into the new Google Shared Drive; see\nMigrate files and folders from Google My Drive to a Google Shared Drive\n.\nPay for your storage\nNotes:\nAll new GSDs created in\nGoogle at IU\nhave a zero byte quota until the purchase order has been processed.\nGSDs are renewed annually and provide access from February 1 of the current year until January 31 of the following year.\nPurchases initiated after February 1 are prorated to end by January 31 of the following year.\nThere is no limit to the number of licenses you can purchase for individual GSDs.\nTo set up any GSD with paid storage or to renew your existing GSD:\nBe sure that you have a Google Shared Drive prepared, as noted in the section above.\nAs part of the purchasing process, be ready to provide:\nEmail address of a secondary contact for your license; this could be the co-owner of your GSD, your local UITS support person, or perhaps an individual from your finance office\nName of your GSD\nNumber of licenses you wish to purchase\nTo check your GSD usage:\nGo to\ndrive.google.com\nand select\nShared Drives\non the left.\nSelect your desired shared drive; when you do so, a blue bar will appear over it.\nSelect the information icon (lower case letter "i" with a circle around it), and your current GSD storage will be displayed.\nWhen you have the above information, complete and submit the\nGoogle Paid Storage Request Form\n.\nImportant:\nYou'll receive email from Qualtrics with instructions for completing your purchase.\nLog into this Knowledge Base\nto view those purchase instructions below.\nIU community:\nAfter you've submitted the form, you'll receive email notification that your request as been submitted to\nCDWG\n. Once CDWG has processed your request, you will receive a quote via email.  With that quote, you can start the purchase process in Buy.IU.\nYou may find it helpful to view the\nvideo infoshare on Google Shared Drive purchasing\n, which walks through this process.\nPrepare your cart\n:\nSelect the\nNon-Catalog Item\nshortcut in the "Shop" section of the buy.iu screen.\nFor supplier, type\nCDW Government LLC\n.\nFor "Product Description", type\nGoogle Teaching and Learning Licenses\n.\nLeave the "Catalog No," field blank.\nIn the "Quantity" field, type the number of licenses you will be purchasing  from the quote you received from CDWG (1 license for each 100 GB of storage).\nFor "Price Estimate",  type the price for each individual license (this will be in the quote from CDWG).\nFor "Packaging", select\nEA'-Each\n.\nSelect\nSave and Close\n.\nReview your Cart\nUnder the shopping cart logo in the top right corner of the screen, choose\nView My Cart\n.\nUpdate the "Cart Name" to\nGoogle Teaching and Learning Licenses\n.\nUpdate the "Description/Business Purpose" to\nIndividual Paid Google Storage\n.\nSelect\nProceed to checkout\n.\nReview and Submit the Requisition\nYour cart has become a Requisition. In "Accounting Codes", choose\nEdit\nto add:\nThe account that will be used to fund the purchase order\nThe Obj Code 4616 (for "COMPUTER SOFTWARE PURCHASES")\nYou must provide your quote from CDWG to IU Purchasing. Select\nAdd Comment\nto add your quote as an attachment.\nDo not upload critical data, as this will be visible to all Buy.IU users.\nReview your requisition to make sure all is accurate. To submit your Requisition, select\nPlace Order\n.\nYou will receive a "Requisition Submitted" notification with next steps and your requisition.\nWhen processing is complete, IU Purchasing will send your purchase order to the reseller, who will then submit an invoice for payment. At that time, CDWG will also email the Google administrators so that they can allocate your paid storage to your Google Shared Drive.\nImportant:\nTo see if your Shared Drive allocation has been finalized, follow the steps above to\ncheck your GSD usage\n. If your storage is noted as anything other than 60 TB, your paid storage is in place.\nShare folders\nTo better manage access to data, users with the Content Manager role can share folders from a GSD; see\nNew setting for content managers to modify shared drives now on by default\n.\nMore on\nGoogle Shared Drives\nand\nGoogle Secure Storage\nCollaborate in Google at IU Shared Drives with external contacts\nProtect sensitive data in Google at IU Secure Storage	KB0024137	KB0024137 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Migrate Pay for your storage Confirm that your paid storage is in place (KB login required) Share folders More on Google Shared Drives and Google Secure Storage Overview Due to changes to Google storage at IU, faculty and staff who wish to pay for additional storage in Google must use Google at IU Shared Drives (GSD). 	1.7245		2026-01-31 16:51:23.369658	2026-01-31 17:08:54.434542	0	83ef647b476456583d373803846d43f5
11352	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5fcf64f7476456583d373803846d432a	About computing certifications	If you need help deciding which professional certification program best suits your career objectives, UITS recommends the ThoughtCo.com\nCertifications\npage as a resource for information about the benefits and requirements of certifications in a variety of IT fields (for example, programming, networks, cybersecurity, and system administration).\nAdditionally, at Indiana University, you can\nexplore certification and credentialing options\navailable through UITS IT Training and other IU educational partners.\nFollowing are some of the most popular IT certifications, their intended audiences, and additional links to help you get started:\nMicrosoft software certifications:\nMicrosoft Certified Systems Engineer (MCSE)\n: This is the most sought-after certification on the market. It tests your knowledge of the setup and maintenance of\nWindows Server\nnetworking environments. You must pass nine tests to earn this certification. Since the MCSE is so popular, a large amount of information about it is available online.\nMicrosoft Certified Solution Developer (MCSD)\n: Often confused with the MCSE, this certification is intended primarily for experienced programmers. You must pass four tests from a multitude of options to earn this certifications. Visual Basic, C++, and FoxPro are the supported languages.\nMicrosoft Certified Professional (MCP)\n: This certification validates your expertise on a range of Microsoft products, technologies, and solutions. You earn certification when you pass your first qualifying exam.\nMicrosoft Office\nSpecialist (MOS)\n: This certification validates your proficiency with one or more\nMicrosoft 365\napplications and is a good entry point for anyone planning to pursue higher-level certifications.\nNote:\nFor a comprehensive overview of Microsoft certifications, see Microsoft Learning's\nOnline Certificate Programs\n.\nHardware certifications:\nCompTIAA+ certification\n: This certification is a standard for entry-level service technicians. It covers the hardware and software technologies most commonly used in business and validates the skills needed for supporting complex IT infrastructures.	KB0023452	KB0023452 v1.0	If you need help deciding which professional certification program best suits your career objectives, UITS recommends the ThoughtCo.com Certifications page as a resource for information about the benefits and requirements of certifications in a variety of IT fields (for example, programming, networks, cybersecurity, and system administration). A	30.2079		2026-01-31 17:07:26.145761	2026-01-31 17:07:26.145761	0	5fcf64f7476456583d373803846d432a
11357	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a020b0ff476456583d373803846d4368	Access Fusion in the STC computing labs	Table of contents\nOverview\nBefore you begin\nAccess Fusion (screen reader)\nAccess Fusion (magnification)\nLearn more\nOverview\nThe steps below explain how to access Fusion in the\nSTC computing labs\n.\nBefore you begin\nSince Fusion offers access to both a screen reader and magnification, it supports a broad range of people. Below are instructions for accessing Fusion if the primary focus is either using a screen reader or for magnification.\nAccess Fusion (screen reader)\nInitially you will need access to a screen reader until you can start Fusion. On the Windows login screen, press\nCtrl\n-\nWindows\n-\nEnter\nto launch Narrator.\nPress\nCtrl\n-\nAlt\n-\nDelete\nto bring up the username and password fields.\nPut in your IU username, and then press\nTab\n.\nPut in your IU passphrase, and press\nEnter\n.\nWait 30 seconds to 1 minute for logging into the workstation.\nPress\nCtrl\n-\nWindows\n-\nEnter\nto launch Narrator.\nPress the Windows key, type\nFusion 2020\n, and then press\nEnter\n.\nPress\nCtrl\n-\nWindows\n-\nEnter\nto close Narrator.\nNext you should see a "Welcome to Fusion: screen with some introductory information. Read it, and then select\nOK\n.\nIf a "Fusion Updates Available" windows appears, select\nNo\n, and go to the next step.\nThe Fusion startup wizard window will appear next:\nLeave "Enable Fusion in the Windows logon prompt" unchecked.\nLeave "Start Fusion automatically when Windows starts" unchecked.\nSelect\nNext\n.\nLeave "Enable Fusion Error Reporting" unchecked.\nSelect\nFinish\n.\nThe JAWS Fusion Startup wizard will appear after that:\nOn the first screen, leave "Participate" unchecked, and then select\nNext\n.\nOn the speech settings screen, pick your options, and then select\nNext\n.\nOn the "Common Options" screen, pick your settings, and then select\nNext\n.\nOn the "Braille Translation Settings" screen, pick your options, and then select\nNext\n.\nOn the "Braille Settings" screen, pick your options, and then select\nFinish\n.\nFusion is now running on the STC workstation.\nAccess Fusion (magnification)\nInitially you may need access to magnification until you can start Fusion. On the Windows login screen, press\nWindows\n-\n+\n(plus sign) to launch Magnifier.\nNote:\nOnce Magnifier is running, use\nWindows\n-\n+\n(plus sign) to increase magnification and\nWindows\n-\n-\n(minus sign) to decrease magnification.\nPress\nCtrl\n-\nAlt\n-\nDelete\nto bring up the username and password fields.\nPut in your IU username, and then press\nTab\n.\nPut in your IU passphrase, and press\nEnter\n.\nOnce you are logged into the workstation, press\nWindows\n-\n+\n(plus sign) to launch Magnifier.\nPress the\nWindows\nkey, type\nFusion 2020\n, and press\nEnter\n.\nOnce you see the Fusion splash screen, press\nWindows\n-\nEsc\nto close Magnifier.\nNext you should see a "Welcome to Fusion: screen with some introductory information. Read it, and then select\nOK\n.\nIf a "Fusion Updates Available" windows appears, select\nNo\nand go to the next step.\nThe Fusion startup wizard window will appear next:\nLeave "Enable Fusion in the Windows logon prompt" unchecked.\nLeave "Start Fusion automatically when Windows starts" unchecked.\nSelect\nNext\n.\nLeave "Enable Fusion Error Reporting" unchecked.\nSelect\nFinish\n.\nThe JAWS Fusion Startup wizard will appear after that:\nOn the first screen, leave "Participate" unchecked, and then select\nNext\n.\nOn the speech settings screen, pick your options, and then select\nNext\n.\nOn the "Common Options" screen, pick your settings, and then select\nNext\n.\nOn the "Braille Translation Settings" screen, pick your options, and then select\nNext\n.\nOn the "Braille Settings" screen, pick your options, and then select\nFinish\n.\nFusion is now running on the STC workstation.\nIf you experience issues using Fusion, contact your\ncampus Support Center\n.\nLearn more\nFor some short video tutorials, see the\nFusion Videos playlist on YouTube\n.\nUITS staff and local UITS support:\nWhen a\nclient\nrequires additional support with this application:\nTransfer emergency requests to the\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at 812-856-4112.\nFor non-emergency requests (or if the\nclient\ncannot speak to the\nATAC\n), gather the following information, and then escalate the issue by assigning it to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow:\nName\nIU username\nContact email address and/or phone number\nA description of the issue (make sure that you\ndo not\ninclude any of the\nclient\n's medical- or condition-related information in the ticket)\nThe\nATAC\n's hours of operation are Monday-Friday 8am-5pm. Assign any support issues received after hours to\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\nin ServiceNow.	KB0025300	KB0025300 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Before you begin Access Fusion (screen reader) Access Fusion (magnification) Learn more Overview The steps below explain how to access Fusion in the STC <strong>computing</strong> labs. Before you begin Since Fusion offers access to both a screen reader and magnification, it supports	29.2275		2026-01-31 17:07:29.527989	2026-01-31 17:07:29.527989	0	a020b0ff476456583d373803846d4368
11358	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c9afa437476456583d373803846d43e7	If you receive an "Access Denied" error when you attempt to join your new computer to the IU ADS domain	For the Indiana University\nADS domain\n, all new computers are added in the default\nOU\n(organizational unit). All\ndomain\nusers have rights to create new computer accounts in this default OU. However, IU has a script that moves computers named with the syntax of "campus-department" from the default OU to the name-appropriate OU. An example of this naming syntax would be "bl-uits" for "Bloomington-University Information Technology Services (UITS)".\nOnce the computer is moved to the OU corresponding to the name, it will have restricted access, and only people who have been granted access to that OU or the original creator of the computer account will be able to join the computer to the domain.\nIf you receive the "Access Denied" error, try the original user account that joined the computer to the domain. If this does not work, contact your departmental\nlocal UITS support person\n.\nNote:\nTo learn the name of your department's local UITS support person, consult your supervisor, your departmental website, or\nIT People\n. If you still cannot identify your local UITS support person, call the\nUITS\nSupport Center at 812-855-6789 (Bloomington) or 317-274-4357 (Indianapolis).	KB0022542	KB0022542 v1.0	For the Indiana University ADS domain, all new computers are added in the default OU (organizational unit). All domain users have rights to create new computer accounts in this default OU. However, IU has a script that moves computers named with the syntax of "campus-department" from the default OU to the name-appropriate OU. An example of this 	28.5778		2026-01-31 17:07:32.948703	2026-01-31 17:07:32.948703	0	c9afa437476456583d373803846d43e7
11356	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b720b43347a456583d373803846d4398	About EdCert classes at IU	EdCert\n, or Education Certification, is a UITS initiative aimed at elevating the level of expertise in departmental computing support and building a solid technical infrastructure at Indiana University. The EdCert Program offers advanced technical training to\nlocal UITS support people\n, currently enrolled IU students, and faculty who teach advanced technical skills in their courses. Topics include Microsoft Windows, Unix system administration, and Windows and Unix Security, among others.\nMicrosoft and CompTIA EdCert classes are designed for computing support providers registered with\nUITS\nas\nlocal UITS support people\n. The Windows classes use Microsoft Official Curriculum (MOC) and are taught by\nMicrosoft Certified Trainers\n(MCTs). CompTIA classes are taught using CompTIA Approved Courseware by CompTIA Certified Trainers.\nMost EdCert classes require a prerequisite course or fundamental knowledge of core basic computing skills. EdCert classes have different fees depending on course length; most EdCert courses are five days long.	KB0025580	KB0025580 v1.0	Microsoft and CompTIA EdCert classes are designed for computing support providers registered with UITS as local UITS support people. The Windows classes use Microsoft Official Curriculum (MOC) and are taught by Microsoft Certified Trainers (MCTs). CompTIA classes are taught using CompTIA Approved Courseware by CompTIA Certified Trainers. Most Ed	7.0324		2026-01-31 17:07:26.145761	2026-01-31 17:09:43.574122	0	b720b43347a456583d373803846d4398
11361	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ff13669047b79a54d500b87b716d439f	Open a data set stored on your computer using SPSS on IUanyWare	Note:\nAccess to locally stored files from IUanyWare applications is available only when using the\nCitrix Workspace app\n(formerly known as Citrix Receiver) for Windows or macOS. If you are using the web Receiver (the "light version"), switch to the Workspace app; see\nSwitch between Citrix Workspace app and web Receiver\n.\nIn SPSS on\nIUanyWare\n, to open a data set stored on your computer:\nFrom the\nFile\nmenu, select\nOpen\n, and then choose\nData\n.\nIn the "Open Data" dialog box, you will see that SPSS is looking in the\nMy Documents\nfolder. This is a virtual folder, not a folder on your computer.\nTo find files on your local computer, in the dialog box, browse the directory structure under the\nComputer\nicon until you find an entry stating, for example,\nLocal Disk (C on ...)\n. Select the\nLocal Disk\nentry to access the directories on your computer.\nNote:\nIf you want to open a data file that is not in SPSS format, you must change the "Files of type:" drop-down menu.\nFor more, see\nWork with files when using an IUanyWare application\n.\nIf you have questions about using research software at Indiana University,\ncontact the UITS\nResearch Applications and Deep Learning\nteam\n.	KB0022892	KB0022892 v2.0	In SPSS on IUanyWare, to open a data set stored on your computer: From the File menu, select Open, and then choose Data. In the "Open Data" dialog box, you will see that SPSS is looking in the My Documents folder. This is a virtual folder, not a folder on your computer. To find files on your local computer, in the dialog box, browse the director	28.3332		2026-01-31 17:07:36.829414	2026-01-31 17:07:36.829414	0	ff13669047b79a54d500b87b716d439f
11364	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=caffe0bb476456583d373803846d4399	Configure your computer to use DHCP (obtain IP address and DNS server automatically) at IU	To properly communicate on Indiana University networks, personal computers need to be configured to use DHCP. Unless the\nSupport Center\nor your\nlocal UITS support person\nadvises otherwise, use the following instructions to configure your computer.\nWindows:\nTo ensure that your\nWindows 11\ncomputer is set to use DHCP:\nOpen\nSettings\n.\nGo to\nNetwork & internet\n.\nGo to\nAdvanced network settings\n.\nDepending on how you are connecting, select\nEthernet\nor\nWi-Fi\n.\nGo to\nView additional properties\n.\nNext to "IP assignment", select\nEdit\n.\nSelect\nAutomatic (DHCP)\nand select\nSave\n.\nNext to "DNS server assignment", select\nEdit\n.\nSelect\nAutomatic (DHCP)\nand select\nSave\n.\nTo ensure that your\nWindows 10\nor\nWindows 8.x\ncomputer is set to use DHCP, select the\nObtain an IP address automatically\nand\nObtain DNS server address automatically\noptions.\nMac:\nTo ensure that your Mac computer is set to use DHCP, open the "Network" pane of\nSystem Preferences\nand select\nUsing DHCP\non the active network adapter:\nOpen\nSystem Preferences\nand select\nNetwork\n.\nDepending on how you are connecting, select\nEthernet\nor\nWifi\nin the left pane.\nIn the "Configure IPv4" drop-down box, select\nUsing DHCP\n.\nSelect\nApply\nin the lower right of the "Network" window.	KB0024381	KB0024381 v1.0	To properly communicate on Indiana University networks, personal computers need to be configured to use DHCP. Unless the Support Center or your local UITS support person advises otherwise, use the following instructions to configure your computer. Windows: To ensure that your Windows 11 computer is set to use DHCP: Open Settings. Go to Network &	27.9125		2026-01-31 17:07:40.507447	2026-01-31 17:07:40.507447	0	caffe0bb476456583d373803846d4399
11365	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=79bfe877476456583d373803846d43ca	Search for users, groups, computers, and other ADS objects in the ADS domain	At Indiana University, when you try to list all the users, computers, and groups in the\nads.iu.edu\ndomain, you may receive the following error message:\nThis location contains more than 10000 objects. Only the first 10000 objects will be shown.\nQuerying for large numbers of records is not the best strategy for locating specific\nADS\nobjects. Doing so is network\nbandwidth\nintensive, and slows network response by consuming domain controller resources. Also, objects are not returned in any order; what you are looking for may or may not be listed in the first 10,000 returned objects.\nInstead of attempting to list every object in the domain, try searching through\nActive Directory\nUsers and Computers by following these instructions:\nOpen the Microsoft Management Console snap-in Active Directory Users and Computers. The snap-in comes with\nWindows\nRemote Server Administration Tools\n.\nFrom the menu at the top of the window, select\nAction\n, and then\nFind...\n. The "Find:" box should list what you are searching for ("Users, Contacts, and Groups"). From the "In:" box, select\nEntire Directory\n.\nIn the "Name:" field, type part or all of the first name, last name, username, or computer name for which you wish to search.\nOnce you find the name that you are looking for, enter it into the box where you were receiving the 10,000 objects limit error message. Even though that name may not be included in the 10,000 objects listed, all usernames and computer names are available for your use.\nNote:\nFor help with\nActive Directory\n, contact your\nlocal UITS support person\n.	KB0022983	KB0022983 v1.0	At Indiana University, when you try to list all the users, computers, and groups in the ads.iu.edu domain, you may receive the following error message: This location contains more than 10000 objects. Only the first 10000 objects will be shown. Querying for large numbers of records is not the best strategy for locating specific ADS objects. Doing	27.092	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:07:47.584688	2026-01-31 17:07:47.584688	0	79bfe877476456583d373803846d43ca
11366	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=06bf2c77476456583d373803846d433c	Log into your Windows computer with administrative rights using your ADS domain account	Note:\nSecurity of Information Technology Resources (IT-12)\nrequires that you normally refrain from running your Windows computer as an administrator. UITS strongly discourages making an ADS account an administrator.\nAvoid using an ADS account for administrative access when a local account will reasonably suffice. If the number of computers to be managed makes using local administrative accounts burdensome, local UITS support people should consider using a group ADS account with administrative privileges before making an individual's ADS account an administrator. For more, see\nAbout the principle of least privilege\n.\nThese instructions assume you have created an\nADS domain\naccount\nfor your Windows computer at Indiana University. For more, see\nConfigure your Windows computer to connect to IU's ADS domain\n.\nIf you need to make your ADS domain account an\nadministrator\non your local computer:\nLog in as an administrator or as a user in the local Administrators group.\nNavigate to the Control Panel.\nOpen\nUser Accounts\n, select\nManage User\nAccounts\n, and then select\nAdd\n.\nTo locate the account, select\nBrowse\n, and double-click the account when you find it. If you know the account you want to add, enter the username, and enter\nADS\nas the domain. Choose\nAdministrator\n.\nSelect\nFinish\nor\nOK\n. ADS accounts added in this manner will appear in the list of user accounts. Users in the list of user accounts will be able to log into your computer using their IU ADS username and password.	KB0022997	KB0022997 v1.0	Note: Security of Information Technology Resources (IT-12) requires that you normally refrain from running your Windows computer as an administrator. UITS strongly discourages making an ADS account an administrator. Avoid using an ADS account for administrative access when a local account will reasonably suffice. If the number of computers to be	26.8457		2026-01-31 17:07:51.770437	2026-01-31 17:07:51.770437	0	06bf2c77476456583d373803846d433c
11367	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=faff24fb476456583d373803846d43fd	Access IU labs and office computers during campus closures	Through\nIUanyWare\n, UITS provides an alternative method for remotely accessing departmentally managed Windows computers during campus closures. This method does not involve the use of RDP nor require an IU VPN connection.\nWith Citrix Virtual Delivery Agent (VDA) installed, departmentally managed Windows computers on campus can be registered with the IUanyWare service. Remote users can then log into IUanyWare, click a published desktop (as directed by their local UITS support people), and access a random pool of their department's computers or their personally assigned computers.\nIf your computer lab is a Windows Student Technology Center (\nSTC\n), UITS recommends using the\nSTC Lab Desktop\nin IUanyWare. For more, see\nUse IUanyWare to access IU STC labs\n. Some applications may be pushed to the pool of computers available for the STC Desktop. If you have a highly customized lab, you may request a pool of STC lab machines to be published separately.\nTo request that your department or classroom computer lab be registered with the IUanyWare service,\ncontact your campus Support Center\n.\nWhen your request is approved, you'll be able to access this\nSharePoint folder containing the installers and additional instructions\n. The VDA installer is also packaged in UITS ConfigMgr.\nFollowing are firewall rules that will be required to support this method:\nFor Receiver: 1494 TCP/UDP - All\nFor Receiver with session reliability: 2598 TCP/UDP - All\nFor HTML5 Receiver: 8008 TCP - All - HTML5\nTo use real-time audio: 16500-16509 UDP - All\nIUanyWare VLAN restriction (communication back to the IUanyWare environment): 80 - TCP - IUAW VLAN	KB0024460	KB0024460 v1.0	Through IUanyWare, UITS provides an alternative method for remotely accessing departmentally managed Windows computers during campus closures. This method does not involve the use of RDP nor require an IU VPN connection. With Citrix Virtual Delivery Agent (VDA) installed, departmentally managed Windows computers on campus can be registered with 	26.3934	km-aud-uits, km-aud-local-support	2026-01-31 17:07:55.228651	2026-01-31 17:07:55.228651	0	faff24fb476456583d373803846d43fd
11368	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=254038b347a456583d373803846d4383	Ensure interoperability between Samba and Windows computers at IU	UITS\ndoes not support the LAN Manager (LM) and\nNTLMv1\nauthentication\nprotocols on the Indiana University\nActive Directory\n. Nearly all Windows computers on the IU network are configured to use only NTLMv2. Windows computers on\nADS\nreceived a policy configuring them to accept only NTLMv2; owners of Windows computers not joined to ADS can change their settings on their own, either with an executable available on\nIUware\n, or manually through certain console or\nregistry\nsettings.\nThis affects how Windows computers on the IU network access\nSamba\nfile or printer shares on\nUnix\n,\nLinux\n, and\nBSD\nservers. Only recent versions of Samba can understand the NTLMv2 protocol, and by default that ability is disabled in those versions. Therefore, if you administer a server running Samba, you may see a problem with Windows clients unless you take action to avoid it.\nWhile it's impossible to cover every configuration across every version of Unix, Linux, or BSD, the way to avoid the problem can be generalized as follows:\nThe server administrator must be running a version of Samba capable of handling NTLMv2 securely. UITS recommends versions\ncurrently supported\nby the Samba team.\nThe\nsmb.conf\nmust include a line in the Global Settings section, labeled\n[global]\n, that reads:\nclient ntlmv2 auth = yes\nAt Indiana University, for personal or departmental Linux or Unix systems support, see\nGet help for Linux or Unix at IU\n.	KB0026051	KB0026051 v1.0	UITS does not support the LAN Manager (LM) and NTLMv1 authentication protocols on the Indiana University Active Directory. Nearly all Windows computers on the IU network are configured to use only NTLMv2. Windows computers on ADS received a policy configuring them to accept only NTLMv2; owners of Windows computers not joined to ADS can change th	26.2926		2026-01-31 17:07:59.507577	2026-01-31 17:07:59.507577	0	254038b347a456583d373803846d4383
11371	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d1afe437476456583d373803846d43e5	If your computer is infected with an instant messaging (IM) Trojan	Important:\nTo avoid viruses spread via\ninstant messaging\n(IM), think before you click; if you receive a message out of the blue, with nothing more than a link and/or general text, do not click it. For more, see\nTips for staying safe online\n.\nIf your computer is infected with an instant messaging (IM) Trojan, the remote attacker can, among other things, control chat sessions, send the Trojan to people on your buddy list, and perform Denial of Service (DoS) attacks using your computer.\nIf you're notified that your computer is sending a virus or participating in DoS attacks, immediately unplug the network cable and reformat and reinstall your operating system. Additionally, if you were running IM software while logged into your computer with\nadministrative rights\n, any infection contracted through that software will have administrative rights also, and you will have to reformat and reinstall the operating system. It is not sufficient to simply remove the infection, because doing so will not remove any programs that may have been installed with administrative rights while the computer was compromised. For help reformatting and reinstalling your system, see:\nSafely rebuild your Windows computer\nWhy you should reformat your hard drive and reinstall Windows after your computer is infected with a virus\nIt is safest to run IM software without administrative rights, as outlined in\nAbout the principle of least privilege\n. If you were following the\nprinciple of least privilege\nat the time of infection,\nUniversity Information Security Office\n(UISO) at Indiana University may consider using a cleaning tool or deleting your profile instead of wiping your computer.\nNote:\nFor recommendations about antivirus software, see\nRecommended antivirus software at IU\n.	KB0022559	KB0022559 v1.0	Important: To avoid viruses spread via instant messaging (IM), think before you click; if you receive a message out of the blue, with nothing more than a link and/or general text, do not click it. For more, see Tips for staying safe online. If your computer is infected with an instant messaging (IM) Trojan, the remote attacker can, among other t	25.6597		2026-01-31 17:08:10.864607	2026-01-31 17:08:10.864607	0	d1afe437476456583d373803846d43e5
11373	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1010387f476456583d373803846d4394	Driver packages in Microsoft SCCM	Table of contents\nOverview\nCommon and currently available packages\nDefault driver support in\nWindows 10\nNew driver requests\nUnusual and currently unavailable packages\nStandard process for building devices with fewer than 10 similar devices\nNew driver requests when the manufacturer does not supply a driver pack or CAB file\nOverview\nCommon and currently available driver packages are available for deployment and installation to managed devices in the UITS Microsoft SCCM environment. Drivers that are common and currently available are typically requested by one or more service members and then packaged, tested, and made available to the entire customer community.\nHowever, if the driver packages are unusual and currently unavailable, local UITS support people may be responsible to collect additional information. Not all unusual drivers requested will be made available.\nFor a complete list of driver packages, see\nUDM Current Supported Drivers\n.  This is a multi-page list, and is only available to user accounts that have access to the SCCM console.\nCommon and currently available packages\nDefault driver support in\nWindows 10\nManufacturers will often provide default driver support for\nWindows 10\n-compatible devices.\nNew driver requests\nDriver packages are updated on a per-request basis.  To request new driver packages, email your collection name and a request for support to\nsct2@iu.edu\n, including:\nDevice manufacturer, make, and model\nService architecture (x86/x64)\nOperating system (\nWindows 10\n)\nExpected number of supported devices\nManufacturer URL for the driver pack or CAB file, if available\nFrom PowerShell, the results of the command:\nGet-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_ComputerSystem | Select-Object -Property Model,Manufacturer\nUnusual and currently unavailable packages\nThe driver package is considered unusual if one or more of the following is true:\nThe manufacturer does not provide default support for\nWindows 10\n.\nThe driver package will be used for fewer than 10 similar devices.\nThe manufacturer does not supply a driver pack or CAB file.\nStandard process for building devices with fewer than 10 similar devices\nBuild devices using a\nWindows 10\nimage from IUware, and then either manually install the SCCM agent, or push the SCCM agent to these devices via the SCCM console.\nNew driver requests when the manufacturer does not supply a driver pack or CAB file\nIf the device cannot be built using the standard process and does not have an existing driver pack or CAB file:\nDownload all necessary drivers from the site.\nExtract all the relevant files from the executables.\nSave these files into a folder, zip the folder, and send it and a support request to\nsct2@iu.edu\n.	KB0024926	KB0024926 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Common and currently available <strong>packages</strong> Default driver support in Windows 10 New driver requests Unusual and currently unavailable <strong>packages</strong> Standard process for building a driver pack or CAB file Overview Common and currently available driver <strong>packages</strong> are available	6.7891	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits, km-aud-hts	2026-01-31 17:08:14.849245	2026-01-31 17:08:14.849245	0	1010387f476456583d373803846d4394
4067	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024347	About IUware	IUware\nis a software distribution service for Indiana University students, faculty, and staff, offering a wide variety of software packages. The university pays for the relevant licenses through agreements with vendors; this allows students, faculty, and staff to use the programs available through IUware, most without fee. The IUware server is regularly updated, so patches and upgrades for IU-supported software are consistently available.\nEligible individuals with a valid\nUniversity account\nand\ncompatible web browsers\ncan download, install, and, in some cases, execute applications from the IUware website. For eligibility information, see\nAbout software at IU\n. Some applications distributed via IUware are reserved for use by individuals in specific courses, departments, or roles.\nTechnically, you do not own the IUware applications installed on your computer. As long as you meet the\neligibility\nrequirements\n, you are entitled to use an installed IUware application, subject to the terms and conditions specified by IU and the software publisher. When you are no longer eligible, you are required to uninstall any IUware applications stored on your personal computer.\nFor help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0024347	KB0024347 v1.0	Eligible individuals with a valid University account and compatible web browsers can download, install, and, in some cases, execute applications from the IUware website. For eligibility information, see About software at IU. Some applications distributed via IUware are reserved for use by individuals in specific courses, departments, or roles. T	6.7166		2026-01-31 14:43:34.007473	2026-01-31 17:08:26.111597	0	65ff60bb476456583d373803846d432f
11381	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f45408a1338b6ad404e605570e5c7be8	About Sibelius at IU Bloomington	Important:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nAvailability at IU\nOverview\nSibelius is music notation and education software for creating, editing, printing, and playing musical scores. Sibelius is distributed by Avid Technology, Inc. For more, see Avid's\nSibelius product page\n.\nAvailability at IU\nAt Indiana University, the Jacobs School of Music makes Sibelius available for use by IU Bloomington students, faculty, and staff:\nAt IU Bloomington, you can access Sibelius from the Mac computers in the\nStudent Technology Centers\n(STCs) located in the William & Gayle Cook Music Library.\nFor a list of STCs offering Sibelius, see\nSoftware Published to STC - Mac\n.\nIU Bloomington students, faculty, and staff can download and install Sibelius from\nIUware\n:\nSibelius for Windows\nSibelius for Mac\nJacobs School of Music faculty who want Sibelius installed on an office workstation should email Music IT Services at\nmusicits@iu.edu\n.\nNote:\nAlthough Sibelius is available for use by all IU Bloomington students, faculty, and staff, support for the application by Music IT Services is limited to those currently enrolled or employed at the Jacobs School of Music.\nFor additional instructions, including how to connect to IU's Sibelius license server, you will need to be logged in.\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nIU community:\nConnect to IU's Sibelius license server\nTo use Sibelius at IU, you must connect to the Sibelius Network License Server. If you are located off campus, you will need to\nconnect to the IU VPN\n.\nWhen you launch Sibelius, choose the\nSpecify License Server\noption, and then enter:\n129.79.184.42\nOnly a limited number of licenses are available, so the server will assign a license to your computer each time you launch Sibelius. When you close the program, the license will return to the pool of available licenses and become available for someone else to use. When the number of concurrent users reaches the total number of available licenses, you will be unable to use Sibelius until someone else closes the program. If you leave Sibelius open and idle for more than one hour, the system may automatically disconnect you from the server.\nIf you receive the error message "Unable to connect to license server", make sure you are connected to the appropriate network, as listed above. If you are properly connected and still receive the error, email Music IT Services at\nmusicits@iu.edu\nfor help (include your computer's\nIP address\n, and the date and time you received the message).\nResolve permission issues in macOS\nA permission issue on some Mac computers prevents Sibelius Ultimate 2018 from creating its initial application support folder structure at runtime. If you try to open Sibelius and continue to see the box that prompts for a license server, follow the commands below to manually fix it:\nOn a client computer, open a Terminal window: From the Macintosh HD, select\nApplications\n, then\nUtilities\n, and then\nTerminal\n.\nEnter the following commands:\ncd /Library/Application\\ Support/Avid\nsudo mkdir Sibelius\\\nsudo chmod -R 775 Sibelius\\\ncd Sibelius\\\nsudo mkdir _manuscript\ncd _manuscript\nsudo touch LicenceServerInfo\nsudo chmod -R 775 LicenceServerInfo\nvi LicenceServerInfo\nThe Terminal window will change. To insert text, press\ni\n.\nEnter your license server's IP address.\nPress\nEsc\n.\nEnter\n:wq\n.\nClose the Terminal and then start Sibelius.\nSibelius should now see the license server on the IP you provided and launch as a network client.	KB0023775	KB0023775 v2.0	Important: Table of contents Overview Availability at IU Overview Sibelius is music notation and education software for creating, editing, printing, and playing musical scores. Sibelius is distributed by Avid Technology, Inc. For more, see Avid's Sibelius product page. Availability at IU At Indiana University, the Jacobs School of Music makes Si	5.872		2026-01-31 17:08:33.329895	2026-01-31 17:08:33.329895	0	f45408a1338b6ad404e605570e5c7be8
11383	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ca99921847fb5a54d500b87b716d43ac	Download, install, or update MATLAB	Table of contents\nOverview\nDownload and install MATLAB\nUpdate your MATLAB license\nOverview\nIndiana University provides access to a campus-wide Total Academic Headcount (TAH) license for students, faculty, and staff. New users can download, install, and activate MATLAB from\nMathWorks\n.\nIf you have questions about using research software at Indiana University,\ncontact the UITS\nResearch Applications and Deep Learning\nteam\n.\nDownload and install MATLAB\nWhether you are a new or existing MATLAB user, when you are ready to download and install MATLAB, follow the steps below.\nGo to\nMathWorks\nand create an account using your IU email address.\nGo to the\nMathWorks download page\n.\nSelect\nDownload\nfor the current release; the release name typically follows the format\nR<year>a\nor\nR<year>b\n.\nChoose the appropriate installer for your operating system.\nLocate the downloaded installer file. It should be in the default download folder, unless you specified another location. The name of the installer file depends on your operating system:\nWindows:\nmatlab_<release>_win64.exe\nmacOS:\nmatlab_<release>_maci64.zip\nLinux:\nmatlab_linux_<release>.tgz\nIn the above examples,\n<release>\nrepresents the release number.\nStart the installer:\nWindows:\nDouble-click the installer file you downloaded to run the Windows Self-Extractor.\nmacOS:\nDouble-click the installer file you downloaded. This will extract the files and create another folder called\nmatlab_<release>_maci64\n, where\n<release>\nrepresents the release number. Inside this folder, double-click\nInstallForMacOSX\nto start the installer.\nLinux:\nDepending on what Linux distribution you are using, move the file to either\n/usr/local/src\nor\n/opt\n(it does not matter which you choose), and then do the following steps in a terminal window to start the installation process:\ncd /usr/local/src (or cd /opt)\nsudo tar xf matlab_linux_<release>.tgz\ncd MATHWORKS_<release>\nsudo ./install\nIn the MathWorks installer, select\nLog in with a MathWorks Account\nand follow the online instructions.\nWhen prompted, select the license you want to use.\nSelect the products you want to download and install.\nAfter downloading and installing your products, keep\nActivate MATLAB\nselected and select\nNext\n.\nUpdate your MATLAB license\nThe university renews the MATLAB TAH license each July; around this time your MATLAB application will begin displaying expiration warnings. To continue using MATLAB, you can either download and install the newest version (follow\nsteps 5-12 above\n), or use the MATLAB activation client to update your license for the installed version.\nTo update your license:\nLaunch the activation client:\nIf MATLAB is currently activated, open the application, go to the\nHome\ntab, select\nHelp\n(in the "Resources" section), mouse-over\nLicensing\n, and then select\nActivate Software\n.\nIf you can't open MATLAB (for example, because your license is expired or otherwise unusable), launch the activation client manually; its location varies by operating system:\nWindows:\nThe activation client is located here:\nC:\\Program Files\\Matlab\\R20XXx\\bin\\winXX\\activate_Matlab.exe\nmacOS:\nIn the Finder, go to\nApplications\n, right-click or\nCmd\n-click the MATLAB icon (for example,\nMATLAB_R2015b\n), select\nShow Package Contents\n, and then open\nActivate.app\n.\nLinux:\nThe activation client is located here:\n/usr/local/Matlab/R20XXx/bin/activate_Matlab.sh\nIn the activation client, select\nActivate automatically using the Internet\n.\nLog into your MathWorks account.\nFrom the list of licenses, select the license you want to activate.\nConfirm the activation information.\nTo complete the activation, select\nFinish\n.	KB0023015	KB0023015 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Download and install MATLAB Update your MATLAB license Overview Indiana University provides access to a campus-wide Total Academic Headcount (TAH) license for students, faculty, and staff. New users can download, install, and activate MATLAB from MathWorks. Download and install MATLAB Whether you are a new or existing 	5.5866		2026-01-31 17:08:36.708121	2026-01-31 17:08:36.708121	0	ca99921847fb5a54d500b87b716d43ac
11385	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=65a90a174730da5c3d373803846d4394	About the Jamf Application Catalog	Table of contents\nOverview\nApplication updates\nCritical security updates (typically addressing Zero Day issues)\nCritical and out-of-band patching\nOverview\nThe Jamf Global Application Catalog is a list of applications available for deployment and installation to managed macOS devices in the Jamf Pro environment. Applications in the application catalog are typically IU enterprise licensed or requested by one or more service members.\nApplication updates\nApplication updates typically become available on the test server every Tuesday. Those updates will then be available on the production server the following week.\nCritical security updates (typically addressing Zero Day issues)\nApplication update is released for testing in CTE (Casper Test Environment) within three days after release.\nApplication update is released to production in CPE (Casper Production Environment) 24 hours after testing is complete in CTE.\nCritical and out-of-band patching\nIncidents may arise where the cyber threat or cyber risk to Indiana University is such that decisions to patch systems out of band, patch systems while ignoring maintenance windows, or patch systems which force a reboot on the endpoint are necessary actions. The decision to implement these methods does not reside with UITS\nEndpoint Management Services\n, but with UISO and UIPO.\nEndpoint Management Services\nwill work collaboratively with the UISO and UIPO to ensure that the least amount of\nclient\nimpact occurs in these instances, and that communication is distributed to all relevant parties.\nEndpoint Management Services\nwill communicate service-specific information as soon as possible after UISO and UIPO announce the exploit to the university community.\nList of applications\nApplication name\nCore application\nSelf-service\nUpdate notes\nOS 10.11.6\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nOS 10.12.6\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nAdium\nNo\nNo\nNo\nAdobe Captivate\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nAdobe Photoshop Elements\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nAdobe Premiere Elements\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nAdobe Flash Player\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nAleks PlugIn\nNo\nNo\nNo\nAlertus\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nAudacity\nNo\nNo\nNo\nAudacity w/Lame\nNo\nNo\nNo\nBlender\nNo\nNo\nNo\nBrackets\nNo\nNo\nNo\nCanon Scan Gear\nNo\nNo\nNo\nCC Acrobat DC\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC After Effects\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Animate Mobile Device Packaging\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Audition\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Bridge\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Dreamweaver\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Edge Animate\nNo\nNo\nNo\nCC Edge Code Preview\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Edge Reflow Preview\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Extendscript Toolkit\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Extension Manager\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Fireworks\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Flash Builder Premium\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Fuse\nNo\nNo\nNo\nCC Gaming SDK\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Illustrator\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC In Copy\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC In Design\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC License Update\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Lightroom\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Media Encoder\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Muse\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Photoshop\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Prelude\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Premiere Pro\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Project Felix\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Remote Update Manager\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Scout\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCC Speed Grade\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCitrix Receiver\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nCommand Line Tools\nNo\nNo\nNo\nCyberduck\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nDell Drivers\nNo\nNo\nNo\nDockutil\nNo\nNo\nNo\nDropbox\nNo\nNo\nNo\nFinale\nNo\nNo\nNo\nFirefox\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nFirefox ESR\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nFMOD Studio\nNo\nNo\nNo\nFMOD Studio with Programmers API\nNo\nNo\nNo\nGame Salad\nNo\nNo\nNo\nGephi\nNo\nNo\nNo\nGitHub Desktop\nNo\nNo\nNo\nGitHub Desktop Deer Types\nNo\nNo\nNo\nGoogle Chrome\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nGoogle Earth\nNo\nNo\nNo\nHand Brake\nNo\nNo\nNo\nHewlett Packard Printer Drivers\nNo\nNo\nNo\nHP Printer Essentials\nNo\nNo\nNo\nImage\nNo\nNo\nNo\nOS Sierra\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nOS X El Capitan\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\niOS Simulator\nNo\nNo\nNo\nIU Brand Fonts\nNo\nNo\nNo\nJava6 for OS\nNo\n✔\nYes\nNo\nJava 8 Update\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nJava Bars\nNo\nNo\nNo\nJunos Pulse\nNo\nNo\nNo\nMacTeX\nNo\nNo\nNo\nMEGA606\nNo\nNo\nNo\nMicrosoft Auto Update\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nMicrosoft Excel\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nMicrosoft Office\n(Excel, OneNote, Outlook, PowerPoint, Word, MAU)\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nMicrosoft Office\nVL Serializer\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nMicrosoft OneNote\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nMicrosoft Outlook\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nMicrosoft PowerPoint\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nMicrosoft Word\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nNet Beans\nNo\nNo\nNo\nNetwork Workbench\nNo\nNo\nNo\nOffline Image Viewer\nNo\nNo\nNo\nOpen Sesame\nNo\nNo\nNo\nPraat\nNo\nNo\nNo\nIvanti Secure Access (formerly Pulse Secure)\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nPython\nNo\nNo\nNo\nQGIS\nNo\nNo\nNo\nR\nNo\nNo\nNo\nR Studio\nNo\nNo\nNo\nRicoh PS Printers EXPLIO Driver\n✔\nYes\nNo\nNo\nSetregproptool\nNo\nNo\nNo\nSibelius\nNo\nNo\nNo\nSibelius Scorch\nNo\nNo\nNo\nSilverlight\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nSketch Up\nNo\nNo\nNo\nSkype\nNo\nNo\nNo\nSource Tree\nNo\nNo\nNo\nStellarium\nNo\nNo\nNo\nSystem Center Endpoint Protection\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nNo\nUgeeM\nNo\nNo\nNo\nUnity Web Player\nNo\nNo\nNo\nVirtualBox\nNo\nNo\nNo\nVLC\nNo\nNo\nNo\nWacom\nNo\nNo\nNo\nWacom Bamboo\nNo\nNo\nNo\nWacom Intuos\nNo\nNo\nNo\nWacom Pen Tablet\nNo\nNo\nNo\nXcode\nNo\nNo\nNo\nXerox Printer Drivers\nNo\nNo\nNo\nXQuartz\nNo\nNo\nNo	KB0024927	KB0024927 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Application updates Critical security updates (typically addressing Zero Day issues) Critical and out-of-band patching Overview The Jamf Global Application Catalog is a list of applications available for deployment and installation to managed macOS devices in the Jamf Pro environment. Applications in the application ca	5.471	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:08:40.050301	2026-01-31 17:08:40.050301	0	65a90a174730da5c3d373803846d4394
11387	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f1cfecb7476456583d373803846d4338	Install RSAT features in Windows 10 version 1809 or Windows Server 2019 and later	Table of contents\nOverview\nLegacy build instructions\nInstall RSAT features\nOverview\nStarting with\nWindows 10\nversion 1809 and\nWindows Server 2019\n, Microsoft no longer provides Remote Server Administration Tools (RSAT) as a downloadable package; instead RSAT is available as a set of Features on Demand (FODs). For workstations running earlier versions of\nWindows 10\nor\nWindows Server\n, you can still\ndownload the RSAT installer from the Microsoft Download Center\n.\nLegacy build instructions\nAt Indiana University, workstations built with Microsoft's System Center Configuration Manager (SCCM) that are not on the\nWindows Common Build\nwon't be able to find the RSAT features because they are not available from the IU server. To get RSAT features, you first must change your system settings to specify Microsoft Windows Update as the source for downloading features:\nPress\nWindows\n+\nr\n, enter\ngpedit.msc\n, and then select\nOK\n.\nUnder\nComputer Configuration\n, expand\nAdministrative Templates\n, and then select\nSystem\n.\nIn the list of\nSystem\nsettings, right-click\nSpecify settings for optional component installation and component repair\n, and then select\nEdit\n.\nSelect\nEnabled\n, and then, under "Options", select\nDownload repair content and optional features directly from Windows Update instead of Windows Server Update Services (WSUS)\n.\nNote:\nUITS recommends having this policy enabled only while you are downloading components that are not offered by IU's SCCM service.\nSelect\nOK\n, and then close Local Group Policy Editor.\nInstall RSAT features\nTo install RSAT features, launch PowerShell as an administrator, and then use Deployment Image Servicing and Management (\nDISM.exe\n) with the\n/add-capability\noption; for example (replace\n<capability_name>\nwith the capability name associated with the feature you want to install; to find the appropriate capability name, refer to Microsoft's\nlist of RSAT features\n):\nDISM.exe /Online /add-capability /CapabilityName:<capability_name>\nAlternatively, you can add multiple features with one command; for example:\nDISM.exe /Online /add-capability /CapabilityName:<capability_name1> /CapabilityName:<capability_name2>\nWhen you install a feature that has one or more dependencies, the dependent feature(s) will be installed automatically, as well. For example, the following command installs Active Directory Domain Services and Lightweight Directory Services Tools (ADUC), Group Policy Management Tools (GPMC), and BitLocker Drive Encryption Administration Utilities (the\nBitlocker Viewer\ntab in ADUC), as well as the Server Manager feature, which is a dependency for ADUC:\nDISM.exe /Online /add-capability /CapabilityName:Rsat.ActiveDirectory.DS-LDS.Tools~~~~0.0.1.0 /CapabilityName:Rsat.GroupPolicy.Management.Tools~~~~0.0.1.0 /CapabilityName:Rsat.BitLocker.Recovery.Tools~~~~0.0.1.0	KB0023333	KB0023333 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Legacy build instructions Install RSAT features Overview Starting with Windows 10 version 1809 and Windows Server 2019, Microsoft no longer provides Remote Server Administration Tools (RSAT) as a downloadable package; instead RSAT is available as a set of Features on Demand (FODs). For workstations running earlier vers	4.4139		2026-01-31 17:08:43.460344	2026-01-31 17:08:43.460344	0	f1cfecb7476456583d373803846d4338
1600	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024785	About local UITS support at IU	At Indiana University, local UITS support people and teams provide IT services or support to faculty, staff, students, and other members of the IU community in departments and units across IU.\nNote:\nTo learn the name of your department's local UITS support person, consult your supervisor, your departmental website, or\nIT People\n. If you still cannot identify your local UITS support person, call the\nUITS\nSupport Center at 812-855-6789 (Bloomington) or 317-274-4357 (Indianapolis).				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:33:21.638798	2026-01-31 14:43:58.509503	1	KB0024785
12454	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=bf86735147d4b6942fc6a55b416d43b5	Apply and customize the IU Course Template for your Canvas course	Table of contents\nOverview\nApply the template to your course\nAdapt the template to your course\nCreate additional modules\nDelete the template from your course\nOverview\nThe IU Course Template is a collection of instructional assets to help you get started with developing your face-to-face, hybrid, or fully online course in Canvas. Created by the UITS Teaching and Learning Technologies team, this fully accessible template includes a home page, a syllabus, a "Getting Started" module for students, and a sample module with a module overview page, interactive lecture placeholder, discussion topic, and an assignment. To review the template structure and content before copying it to your course, visit the\npreview course in Canvas\n.\nApply the template to your course\nImportant:\nWhen you apply a template to your Canvas course, the settings and navigation in your course will be replaced with those in the template. Apply the template\nbefore\nyou start creating or importing course content. If you apply it afterward:\nYou may overwrite your course syllabus or other content in your course. If you already created content in your course site, consider making a copy of the course before applying the template.\nIf you made customizations to the settings or navigation of the course, you may need to reset them.\nIf your course uses an\neText\n, you will need to re-add the IU eTexts (Unizin Engage) tool to your course navigation. For help with course navigation, see\nHow do I manage Course Navigation links?\nTo apply the IU Course Template to your Canvas course:\nNavigate to your course and select\nSettings\n.\nIn the settings sidebar at the right or bottom, choose\nApply Course Template\n. You'll see a pop-up with information and instructions.\nNote:\nThe\nApply Course Template\nbutton is only visible in unpublished courses.\nSelect\nIndiana University\nto expose the templates available to the entire institution.\nLook for the template named "IU Course Template". To preview the template, select the template name. To apply the template to your course, select\nApply Template\n. An informational pop-up will appear. Choose\nApply\nto proceed with applying the template.\nThe template will be copied to your course immediately, but it may take a few minutes for the template to be fully imported. To check the import status, open the Settings tool. In the sidebar, choose\nImport Course Content\n, and review the status of the first item under "Current Jobs".\nAdapt the template to your course\nThe template is a starting point for your course. You can keep the elements you like and remove or modify the ones that don't quite fit. You can also add tools, materials, and activities as needed.\nBegin by reading and following the instructions on the course home page to customize it. Carefully review and customize each element in the template to reflect the particulars of your course. As your review each item, look for and follow the instructions that appear in boldface surrounded by brackets. For example:\n[Note to instructors: Replace the bullet points below with the information that pertains to this assignment.]\nIf you plan to use the Canvas Modules tool for organizing the materials and activities in your course, continue with the\nnext section\n. If not, delete the "Week 1" module from the Modules tool, but leave the tool enabled so students can access the "Getting Started" module.\nCreate additional modules\nThere are two options for creating modules from the sample module and content in the template:\nOption 1: Duplicate modules manually\nThe template includes one sample module. If you'd like to create additional modules, you can duplicate the sample. Duplicating a module also creates copies of the module's items, that is, the pages, assignments, and discussions that make up the module.\nBefore duplicating the sample module, review the structure and content to make sure it works for your class. Add, remove, or modify the module items as needed. Once you are satisfied with the content and structure of the module, begin making copies.\nImportant:\nModules containing one or more quizzes cannot be duplicated unless the quizzes were created with the New Quizzes tool. If you want to include classic quizzes in your modules, you'll need to add them to your modules after duplication.\nFrom your course navigation, select\nModules\n.\nAt the upper right of the "Week 1" module, open the\nOptions\nmenu (\n...\n) and select\nDuplicate\n. A copy of the module and its items will be added to the Modules tool. The word "copy" (or "copy 1", "copy 2", etc.) will be added to the name of the module and each item.\nRepeat the previous step to create as many modules as you'll need for your course.\nAfter your modules are created, customize the title and instructions or instructional content in each module item with material appropriate for your course.\nOption 2: Duplicate modules with the Multi-Tool\nThe Module Builder utility in the DesignPlus Multi-Tool, an external app from Cidi Labs, allows you to quickly create a complete set of modules for your course. Also, unlike the manual method described above, the Multi-Tool offers support for duplicating classic quizzes. To use the Multi-Tool to generate course modules from the IU Course Template:\nOpen the Settings tool in your course and from the settings sidebar, select\nMulti-Tool\n.\nOn the Multi-Tool home page, select\nModule Builder\n. Under the "Existing Modules" heading, Module Builder displays the two modules that are part of the template.\nScroll down to the "New Modules" heading.\nUse the "Module Prefix" dropdown to select the prefix that should be applied to the beginning of each module and item name (for example,\nModule\n,\nLesson\n,\nWeek\n, etc.). You can even designate your own custom prefix.\nFor "Number of Modules to Create", enter the number of modules that will be needed to cover all of your instructional content in the course.\nFor "Starting Number", leave the value at\n1\n. You will eventually delete the sample module that was included in the template.\nNext, you'll create the Module Pattern. The module pattern is the sequence of content and activities that should appear in most or all mod ules.\nSince each module will begin with an overview page, under "Add Items" select\nPage\nto add a page to the pattern as the first item. A page row is added to the module pattern:\nUse the "[Blank Page]" dropdown to the select the sample module overview page,\nWeek 1: Overview\n, as the page template.\nIn the "Page Name [Optional]" box, enter\nOverview\n,\nIntroduction\n, or something similar. When Module Builder creates your module, the page name will be preceded by the prefix and module number. For example, "Lesson 1: Overview", "Lesson 2: Overview", and so on.\nUse the item options under "Add Items" to add the other items to your module pattern. The number, type, and order of items in the module pattern is up to you. For each item, you will enter a name, and select the template in the template dropdown. Where appropriate, use the items provided in the sample module as template files. For quizzes, select\n[Blank Quiz]\nsince the sample module does not include a sample quiz. Additional considerations:\nFor assignments added to the pattern, enter the submission type, the point value, and the "[Display grade as]" value.\nFor graded discussion topics added to the pattern, enter the point value and the "[Display grade as]" value.\nTo move any item in the pattern, select the handle to the left of its icon and drag to the desired location.\nReview the module pattern to make sure you've added all the necessary items and the requested parameters for each. Your module pattern should look similar to the example below:\nAfter you are satisfied with the module pattern, at the lower left of the "New Module Pattern" panel, select\nGenerate Module List\n. A preliminary, editable list of the modules and their content items is generated beneath the heading "Customize Modules".\nUnder "Customize Modules", review the module list and make changes as needed. You can add, modify, or delete individual items in the module list, or you can return to the module pattern to regenerate the list from scratch.\nWhen you are ready to create the module content according to your specifications in the module list, select the\nAdd Modules to Course\nbutton located at the bottom of the "New Module Pattern" panel as well as at the bottom of the page. It can take several minutes to generate the complete set of modules.\nOnce the job is complete, the new modules and their content will have been added to your course. You can review them in the Canvas Modules tool, or under the "Existing Modules" heading in the Module Builder. You can also use the list under "Existing Modules", to add, remove, or modify items in each module. If you make changes to a module, be sure to select\nUpdate This Module\nto save your changes.\nAfter your modules are created, customize the instructions and instructional content in each module item with material appropriate for your course.\nTip:\nTo add or update available, due, until, and feedback dates for assignments, discussions, quizzes, and pages, try the\nMulti-Tool Due Date Modifier\n. This utility provides an efficient interface for setting item dates individually or in bulk.\nFinally, unpublish or delete the sample module from the IU Course Template so students won't see it.\nTo unpublish the module and its content:\nNavigate to the Modules tool.\nLocate the module named "Week 1" immediately following the "Getting Started" module.\nOpen the module status menu and select\nUnpublish module and all items\n.\nTo delete the same module:\nIf you are not already in the Multi-Tool, open the Settings tool in your course and from the settings sidebar, select\nMulti-Tool\nand then select\nModule Builder\n.\nUnder the "Existing Modules" heading, select the module named "Week 1" immediately following the "Getting Started" module to display the module's content.\nTo delete the module without deleting the items it contains, select\nDelete Module\n. To delete the module and its contents, select\nDelete Module and Contents\n. After you confirm your selection, the content will be deleted.\nDelete the template from your course\nIf, after applying the IU Course Template, you decide you don't want to use it, follow the instructions at\nReset your Canvas course content and settings\n.	KB0023155	KB0023155 v2.0	: For assignments added to the pattern, enter the <strong>submission</strong> type, the point value, and the "[Display the <strong>job</strong> is complete, the new modules and their content will have been added to your course	4.9731		2026-01-31 17:36:02.829468	2026-01-31 17:36:02.829468	0	bf86735147d4b6942fc6a55b416d43b5
11413	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ff2c51be47b03ad42fc6a55b416d434b	Build information for Linux computers on the IU Common Build	Important:\nRead the documentation and installation guide in their entirety before attempting to build the device. While the new installation and configuration processes are automated, they require the builder to take a fresh approach and discard any previous experience with Ubuntu.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nIU Common Build notable standards\nComputer naming convention\nEntra login\nBuild process\nPrepare a device\nLUKS and full-disk encryption\nBuild experience\nBuild a device\nPost-installation and configuration notes\nLUKS recovery key\nSoftware\nSystem updates\nGet help\nOverview\nFor Linux computers, the IU Common Build provides a widely used, enterprise-friendly desktop operating system and central management solution. This includes automated full-disk encryption, system configuration, and a high security standard based on CIS Benchmarks. Device sign-in is managed with Microsoft Entra. The goal isn't to enforce a "one size fits all" solution, but to provide a "one size fits most" option.\nEndpoint Management Services (EMS) selected Ubuntu Linux for the IU Common Build based in large part on Ubuntu's popularity with university constituents and enterprise organizations.\nIU Common Build notable standards\nComputer naming convention\nTo align with the IU Common Build naming standard for macOS and Windows, Linux computers use the naming convention\nIU-\nSerialNumber\n.\nEntra login\nDue to technical limitations and potential security risks, the UITS recommends logging into your Linux system using the format\nusername\n@iu.edu\n.\nImportant:\nThe system expects authorized users to log in by defining the actual\nIU email address\n. Windows and macOS mask the\n@iu.edu\nportion of the domain name, but Linux requires the whole name.\nFailing to explicitly include\n@iu.edu\nin your login as recommended above will result in degraded performance.\nBuild process\nMake sure to review the PDF installation guide before proceeding. The installation guide is comprehensive and provides a large amount of detail.\nSee the\nBuild media\nsection below for download links to each ISO and its respective installation and configuration guide(s). These items can be used to build a virtual or bare-metal machine.\nIf you are building a virtual machine, make sure to assign a serial number to your VM's BIOS before installing the operating system. For example, if you are building a VM in VirtualBox:\nTo set the serial number for the PC BIOS, use the command:\n$ VBoxManage setextradata "My VM 1" "VBoxInternal/Devices/pcbios/0/Config/DmiSystemSerial" "string:VM\n[your_initials]\n001"\nTo set the serial number for the EFI firmware, use the command:\n$ VBoxManage setextradata "My VM 2" "VBoxInternal/Devices/efi/0/Config/DmiSystemSerial" "string:VM\n[your_initials]\n002"\nIn the above examples, replace\n[your_initials]\nwith your initials; for example,\nvmjqp001\nor\nvmjqp002\n.\nPrepare a device\nThere are currently no data preservation options; the operating system installation is fully automated. Make sure you have a full backup of the system's data before proceeding with the build process. Any pre-existing data will be lost.\nIf you encounter any issues booting the ISO or installed system, first try disabling Secure Boot for your Boot Device within your UEFI firmware settings. Due to technical limitations, TPM devices and the Secure Boot setting currently are not fully supported.\nLUKS and full-disk encryption\nFull-disk encryption (FDE) is enforced and cannot be disabled. Be aware that LUKS (Linux Unified Key Setup) requires the encryption key to be provided at boot time. The system will fail to boot if the disk cannot be unlocked. Local UITS support people may use the\nuems.py\napplication to cycle through encryption keys, after initially re-keying the device.\nBuild experience\nMake sure to fully review the PDF installation guide before attempting to build a device. The build experience can be categorized as follows:\nPhase 1\nOperating system installation\nPhase 2\nSystem configuration\nPhase 3\nDuring system configuration, you'll be asked to provide the IU network username of the client receiving the computer. This doesn't require knowledge of their passphrase, but the client needs to be present when the device is delivered because their login is required to complete the re-keying process (as the default key should not be left as the active key, and the default installation credentials and keys should not be provided to clients).\nBuild a device\nMake sure to read the build documentation before starting to build a device.\nISO:\nubuntu-24.04.2-IU-LCBv2-GA1-desktop-amd64.iso\nMD5:\nubuntu-24.04.2-IU-LCBv2-GA1-desktop-amd64.md5\nInstallation guide:\nUEMS Ubuntu 24.04 Install Guide - LCBv2-GA1.pdf\nPost-installation and configuration notes\nLUKS recovery key\nPer\nSecurity of Information Technology Resources (IT-12)\n, FDE is automatically enabled for all IU Common Build systems. Due to current technical limitations, the system owner is responsible for remembering the encryption key. If you forget your encryption key, you will have no way to access or recover data from the device.\nSoftware\nWhile\napt\nis available for installing software packages, UITS recommends using\nsnap\ninstead. These packages are designed to automatically update and do not conflict with system software or configurations. Installing software with\napt\nmay result in unintended negative effects.\nSystem updates\nSecurity patches are automatically installed. You should only update the system under advisement, as critical configurations could be overwritten, resulting in degraded performance.\nGet help\nUse the\nLinux Support Request\nfor Linux Common Build requests.	KB0023863	KB0023863 v6.0	Important: Read the documentation and installation guide in their entirety before attempting to build the device. While the new installation and configuration processes are automated, they require the builder to take a fresh approach and discard any previous experience with Ubuntu. Table of contents Overview IU Common Build notable standards Com	10.6929	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:09:03.215962	2026-01-31 17:09:03.215962	0	ff2c51be47b03ad42fc6a55b416d434b
11420	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=2c44baeb3b4a26d4476d941864e45a8b	Portal vs. agent view in the IU Knowledge Base	Table of contents\nOverview\nPortal view\nAgent (editing) view\nOverview\nNote:\nThis information is intended for contributors to the Knowledge Base who are part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.\nThe IU Knowledge Base is hosted in the ServiceNow platform, which offers different ways to view articles depending on your permissions and whether you're logged in.\nPortal view\nThe portal is the public-facing view of the IU Knowledge Base, available at\nhttps://servicenow.iu.edu/kb\n. You can also view restricted content available to you by logging in.\nWhen editing KB articles, the Knowledge Management team recommends using two browser windows side by side: one for making the actual changes, and another for previewing the changes in the portal before they're published.\nAlthough ServiceNow gives you preview links from the editing screen, these will not accurately reflect how the article will look in the live KB. When previewing your edits in a separate window, make sure the URL looks like the following to sure you're looking at the article permalink:\nhttps://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0026692\nAgent (editing) view\nAn agent view of an article is what you see when accessing it directly from the\nServiceNow environment\n. See an\nexample of an article in an agent view\n. Note that it looks quite different from how the same article appears in the\nportal view\n. This is why the KM team recommends previewing your changes in the portal.\nThere are multiple ways to view articles in ServiceNow, but when we refer to the "agent view", most often we mean the editing screen.	KB0026697	KB0026697 v3.0	Table of contents Overview Portal view Agent (editing) view Overview The IU Knowledge Base is hosted in the ServiceNow platform, which offers different ways to view articles depending on your permissions and whether you're logged in. Portal view The portal is the public-facing view of the IU Knowledge Base, available at https://servicenow.iu.edu	7.3	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:09:06.589024	2026-01-31 17:09:06.589024	0	2c44baeb3b4a26d4476d941864e45a8b
11422	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=2cb22b5d3b4ea290476d941864e45af2	Images in the IU Knowledge Base	Note:\nThis information is intended for contributors to the Knowledge Base who are part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.\nTable of contents\nBefore you begin\nAdd a new image to an article\nRemove an image from an article\nBefore you begin\nBefore adding an image to a Knowledge Base article, consider the following:\nIs the image needed?\nIf a written step is easy to understand, the image may not be needed. For example, a step that says to select\nOK\nrarely needs an image. However, if a step tells you to find a drop-down menu in a busy or confusing interface, an image may be helpful.\nImportant:\nFor accessibility reasons, images should always support rather than replace written instructions.\nIs the image clear and easy to read?\nIf the image is so blurry or small that the text is unreadable, it won't add value.\nDoes the image focus on the right details?\nCrop large images to show only what's needed, or add annotation (arrows, circles, etc.) to highlight buttons, menus, or other details.\nHas alt text been prepared for the image?\nFor accessibility reasons, all images must have meaningful alt text. To learn more, see\nAlternative text for images\n.\nIs the image the right size?\nSmall images used within sentences should measure somewhere between 14 x 14 pixels and 22 x 22 pixels.\nLarge images should be cropped or resized as necessary.\nDoes the image contain information that should be redacted, such as a username or personal phone number?\nIf so, either edit the image to remove this information, or create a new image.\nDoes the image only show text on a screen?\nFor accessibility reasons, error messages and code snippets should be actual text. Text in images can't be read by assistive technology.\nAdd a new image to an article\nCheck out an article for editing.\nPlace your cursor where you want the image to be added.\nSelect the\nInsert/edit image\nicon (\n).\nSelect the\nUpload\ntab, and then choose\nBrowse for an image\n. Select the image you want to upload. Alternatively, you can drag and drop the image to where it says "Drop an image here".\nIn the "Alternative text" field, enter meaningful alt text to describe the image. Alt text conveys meaning to screen readers. For help, see\nAlternative text for images\n.\nMake sure there is no text in the "Image title" field.\nSelect\nSave\n.\nRemove an image from an article\nCheck out an article for editing.\nSelect the image and press the\nBackspace\nor\nDelete\nkey to remove it.\nThe image will still be attached to the article. To finish removing it, select the\nManage Attachments\nicon (\n), choose the image, and select\nRemove\n.	KB0025593	KB0025593 v8.0	Table of contents Before you begin Add a new image to an article Remove an image from an article Before you begin Before adding an image to a Knowledge Base article, consider the following: Is the image needed? If a written step is easy to understand, the image may not be needed. For example, a step that says to select OK rarely needs an image. 	7.3	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:09:10.638503	2026-01-31 17:09:10.638503	0	2cb22b5d3b4ea290476d941864e45af2
11427	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=58d64fb43b5ee698476d941864e45a28	About KB workflow	Note:\nThis information is intended for contributors to the Knowledge Base who are part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.\nKnowledge Base articles may move through any of the following workflow states:\nDraft:\nAn article is in the Draft state whenever there is an unpublished version of it, whether it's the initial draft (0.01) or a revision that someone with editing privileges is working on (1.01, 2.01, etc.) As a KCS contributor, you'll be able to see in-progress drafts in the KB portal, regardless of who initiated them.\nDraft revisions (version 1.01, etc.) have "(Draft)" immediately after the article number in the portal.\nInitial drafts (version 0.01) do not have a visible draft designation in the portal.\nFor either type of draft, if someone else began it, you won't see the\nEdit Article\noption from the\nActions\ndrop-down menu in the portal.\nInitial drafts won't be returned in KB searches. You won't see them in the portal unless you view them from a direct link using the article number.\nOnly people with editing privileges in ServiceNow Knowledge can see drafts.\nReview:\nAfter someone chooses\nPublish\nfrom the editing screen, an article moves to the Review state, and stays there until a member of the Knowledge Management team approves it. The version number (visible within ServiceNow) will change from\nN\n.01 to\nN\n.02.\nFor revisions (version 1.02, etc.), you'll see "(Review)" after the article number in the portal.\nLike version 0.01, version 0.02 lacks the indication of the state in parentheses.\nFor either type of revision in a Review state, you won't see the\nEdit Article\noption from the\nActions\ndrop-down menu in the portal.\nVersion 0.02 won't be returned in KB searches.\nScheduled for publish:\nThe KM team can schedule revisions or drafts for future publication, but we don't do this extensively, because the live version of the article can't be updated in the meantime, since only one draft of a given article can exist at any given moment. If you'd like to submit a revision to go live at a future date, consult with the KM team, and avoid beginning your edit too far in advance of the publication date.\nPublished:\nWhen an article is in a Published state (that is, there is no current draft of it), you won't see anything following the article number in the portal,\nand\nyou'll have the\nEdit Article\noption from the\nActions\ndrop-down menu.  (However, this may also be true of an initial draft that you began.)\nPending retirement:\nAs contributors, you won't see an option to retire KB articles; the KM team will still do that.  Send us a ticket requesting the retirement of an article by the usual methods.  As we move through the process of approving our own actions in the system, it's possible (though not very likely) that you may see an article marked "(Pending retirement)".\nRetired:\nWhen an article is retired, no one except the KM team can view it.  Anyone else will see the same message that appears when you try to view an internal article that you don't have permission to see.\nWhen updating articles, be sure not to link to anything that's retired. This is unlikely, of course, if you're looking at the current live content in the KB portal when working on content, which is always the best practice. Avoid working from old drafts, as content may have changed in the meantime.	KB0026712	KB0026712 v1.0	Knowledge Base articles may move through any of the following workflow states: Draft: An article is in the Draft state whenever there is an unpublished version of it, whether it's the initial draft (0.01) or a revision that someone with editing privileges is working on (1.01, 2.01, etc.) As a KCS contributor, you'll be able to see in-progress dr	7.3	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:09:14.092135	2026-01-31 17:09:14.092135	0	58d64fb43b5ee698476d941864e45a28
11428	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=62f3cba33bc2a6d4476d941864e45a8e	Boost articles in Knowledge Base portal search results using meta tags	Note:\nThis information is intended for contributors to the Knowledge Base who are part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.\nTo boost an article in IU Knowledge Base search results for specific terms:\nBegin\nediting the article\n.\nAdd the terms to the "Meta" field. Make sure each term is separated by a comma (for example,\none fish,two fish,red fish,blue fish\n, etc.).\nIf adjustments need to be made to how all parts of an article are weighted for search, the Knowledge Management team should make the request using the\nKM - Text Index Search Weights catalog request item\n.	KB0026568	KB0026568 v6.0	To boost an article in IU Knowledge Base search results for specific terms: Begin editing the article. Add the terms to the "Meta" field. Make sure each term is separated by a comma (for example, one fish,two fish,red fish,blue fish, etc.). If adjustments need to be made to how all parts of an article are weighted for search, the Knowledge Manag	7.3	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:09:17.738018	2026-01-31 17:09:17.738018	0	62f3cba33bc2a6d4476d941864e45a8e
1534	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0025958	Determine whether high performance computing can help your research	Those new to high performance computing (HPC) often have a difficult time understanding if or when HPC can help their research. The information here offers a starting point, though it does not answer the question comprehensively. The staff of Indiana University's\nResearch Technologies\ndivision of University Information Technology Services are happy to answer any additional questions. See\nResearch and high performance computing\nfor more information.\nHPC systems provided by\nResearch Technologies\noffer significant advantages over personal computers for unique or demanding computational tasks. However, for many day-to-day tasks, running programs on local personal computers may be more convenient and/or faster than using an HPC system. For example, depending on the specific situation, an HPC system may take longer to load a given application than a personal computer, but provides an advantage in terms of huge throughput for processing a large job.\nYou may benefit from HPC systems if you are working with one of the following:\nYou have computationally intensive programs that can be divided into multiple tasks, taking advantage of parallel processing with multiple processors. For example, if your program takes only an hour to run but you need to run it a thousand times, or your program can be scaled up to run across multiple processors, HPC systems may be beneficial.\nYour programs require more CPUs, GPUs, or memory than your personal computer can handle.\nYour projects involve working with massive data sets that exceed the storage capacity of your personal computer.\nYou estimate that you cannot complete your research tasks in a reasonable amount of time with your personal computing resources.\nHowever, HPC systems may not provide an advantage if:\nYour tasks are simple or small in scale; for smaller tasks that cannot be scaled up, personal computing devices will likely perform better (for example, office-type tasks, spreadsheets, word processing, etc.).\nYou are working with smaller data sets that can be processed effectively on your personal computer.\nThe increased parallel processing, expanded memory capacity, ample disk space, and efficient file system access offered by HPC systems make them indispensable for computationally demanding projects. On the other hand, do not use HPC systems for simple CPU jobs and smaller data sets, as these systems might not perform any better than your personal computing devices.\nIf you need help evaluating whether HPC is right for you,\ncontact the UITS\nResearch Applications and Deep Learning\nteam\n.	KB0025958	KB0025958 v3.0	Those new to high performance computing (<strong>HPC</strong>) often have a difficult time understanding if or when <strong>HPC</strong> can help their research. The information here offers a starting point, though it does not answer and high performance computing for more information. <strong>HPC</strong> systems provided by Research Technologies	176.6051		2026-01-31 14:33:03.11173	2026-01-31 16:39:12.746279	0	abe3d29233a22e5004e605570e5c7b82
9805	sys_kb_id:9c81eb9833839a1004e605570e5c7b13	Indiana University - ServiceNow	The page you are looking for could not be found.\nGo				0	Public	2026-01-31 15:44:04.872297	2026-01-31 15:44:18.563495	0	\N
1834	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023892	About Microsoft OneDrive at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nAccess Microsoft OneDrive at IU\nSync OneDrive files\nOverview\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nis a cloud storage service included with\nMicrosoft 365\nwhere you can store, share, and sync your files.\nOneDrive\nstorage is approved for\nPublic or University-Internal data\n, and certain types of\nRestricted data\n. For details about which data are allowed, see\nAbout dedicated file storage services and IT services with storage components appropriate for sensitive institutional data, including research data containing protected health information\n.\nFor the security of data stored within Microsoft OneDrive at IU, IU may employ automated tools to detect sensitive information and potential misconfigurations. These tools are crucial for maintaining a secure environment. In cases where a detection occurs, designated IT staff may evaluate the findings to assist people using the service in resolving the issue, consistent with IU’s IT privacy obligations outlined in\nPrivacy of Electronic Information and Information Technology Resources (IT-07)\n.\nImportant:\nFor changes to OneDrive storage allotments in the 2025-26 academic year, see\nChanges to Microsoft 365 storage\n.\nAccess\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nThere is no setup to access\nOneDrive\n, unless you wish to sync files to your computer or mobile device (see the following section for syncing instructions).\nUsing a supported web browser (Edge, Chrome, or Firefox), go to\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n; if necessary, choose\nLog in\n.\nIf you see a "Sign in" prompt, enter your\n@iu.edu\nemail address, and then, at the password prompt, enter your\nIU passphrase\n. (Do not select anything on the sign-in pages other than\nNext\nand\nSign in\n.) When prompted, confirm your identity with\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n(if you see a "Do you trust iu.edu" prompt, select\nContinue\n), and then choose whether to stay signed into your\nMicrosoft 365\naccount.\nFrom the left-hand navigation, choose\nApps\nand then select\nOneDrive\n. This should allow you to access files in both\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nand in shared sites associated with\nMicrosoft Teams\nand\nMicrosoft Secure Storage\n. (For more, see\nOptions for storing files at IU\n.)\nNote:\nIf your list under "Quick access" seems incomplete, see\nView shared content in Microsoft OneDrive at IU\n.\nSync\nOneDrive\nfiles\nNote:\nOneDrive sync is an appropriate way to work on all Teams and OneDrive data from a secured, managed workstation.\nCritical or Restricted data\nshould not be synced to unmanaged devices (for example, personal laptops, smartphones, tablets, personal digital assistants, thumb drives) without prior formal written approval and appropriate technical safeguards. If you have questions about whether your workstation is an appropriate location for your data, contact your local UITS support person.\nIf you're using a managed university workstation, it may already be configured to sync your OneDrive files; if not, follow the appropriate instructions for the device you wish to sync:\nSync files with OneDrive in Windows\nSync files with OneDrive on macOS\nUse OneDrive on Android and iOS devices\nNote:\nWord, Excel, and PowerPoint apps are available as free downloads from the App Store to those with a Microsoft 365 A5 license, see\nAbout A5 and A1 licenses in Microsoft 365 at IU\n. To access your\nMicrosoft 365\naccount from these apps:\nAfter installing Word, Excel or PowerPoint from the App Store, open the app.\nIf you see a "Sign in" prompt, enter your\n@iu.edu\nemail address, and then, at the password prompt, enter your\nIU passphrase\n. (Do not select anything on the sign-in pages other than\nNext\nand\nSign in\n.) When prompted, confirm your identity with\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n(if you see a "Do you trust iu.edu" prompt, select\nContinue\n), and then choose whether to stay signed into your\nMicrosoft 365\naccount.	KB0023892	KB0023892 v5.0	Table of contents Overview Access Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU Sync <strong>OneDrive</strong> files Overview Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU is a cloud storage service included with Microsoft 365 where you can store, share, and sync your files. <strong>OneDrive</strong> storage is approved for Public or University-Internal data, and certain	207.992		2026-01-31 14:34:06.379991	2026-01-31 16:46:42.966636	0	ddefe8a6477dba9c2fc6a55b416d4359
11430	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7048704a3bce6ed0476d941864e45aa2	Work with audience restrictions as a KB contributor	Table of contents\nOverview\nIdentify internal content\nLink to restricted content\nContent that should be restricted\nOverview\nNote:\nThis information is intended for contributors to the Knowledge Base who are part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.\nMuch of the content in the IU Knowledge Base is available publicly, but a significant amount is restricted to one or more internal audiences.  Some of the most important audiences are:\niu-all:\nAll IU students, faculty, staff, and affiliates\nuits:\nUITS staff\nlocal-support:\nLocal UITS support people; content in this audience is usually in the UITS audience as well\nIn ServiceNow Knowledge, the audience metadata is set by Can Read criteria.  These appear with "km-aud" before the specific audience name, such as "km-aud-iu-all".\nWhen working with any KB content, it's very important to keep in mind who can access it.\nNote:\nFor Phase 1 of the KCS initiative, please don't modify Can Read criteria. If you believe Can Read criteria need to be set, removed, or modified, let the KM team know. In future phases, this guidance may change; consult with the KM team if you have questions or concerns.\nIdentify internal content\nYou can see the Can Read criteria of a given article in the following places:\nIn the portal view of the article:\nBelow the\nCopy Permalink\nbutton, next to the indicator of when the article was last updated\nIn the agent (reading) view of the article:\nBelow the article number, between the number of views and the\nCopy Permalink\nbutton\nIn the editing view of the article:\nNear the bottom of the metadata fields, above the Meta Description field\nIn search results:\nAt the bottom of the entry for each article\nLink to restricted content\nTake care when linking to other KB articles; don't link to an article that readers can't access, at least not without warning them.  There are two ways to handle links from less-restricted content to more-restricted articles:\nPut the link inside a\nknowledge block\nwith Can Read criteria that are at least as restrictive as the article you're linking to.  This is typically the best way to handle internal processes, such as links to Support Center documentation.  For Phase 1 of the KCS initiative, contact the KM team for help with this process.\nAdd text indicating that a link is internal, like this:\nFor more, see\nKB contributor style guide\n(requires IU Knowledge Base login).\nThis option is appropriate when linking to an IU-internal article from a public article, but may not be appropriate for anything more internal than iu-all.  Consult the KM team if you have questions about this.\nImportant:\nIf you link to an internal article from a less-restricted article, you won't see any indication of the problem from within the article you're editing, either in the portal view or the editing page. Be sure to check your links.\nContent that should be restricted\nThe KM team has been asked to restrict content of many types in the past, including some of the following. If you plan to include information like this in a public article, it may be worth a conversation to determine whether that is appropriate.\nIP addresses\nExact text of automated mailings IU users may receive\nDetails about security features in the IU IT environment\nNegotiated prices for certain vended products	KB0026695	KB0026695 v1.0	 important to keep in mind who can access it. Note: For Phase 1 of the KCS <strong>initiative</strong>, please don't modify, such as links to Support Center documentation. For Phase 1 of the KCS <strong>initiative</strong>, contact the KM team for help	7.3	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:09:21.353057	2026-01-31 17:09:21.353057	0	7048704a3bce6ed0476d941864e45aa2
11433	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=72840ba73b06a6d4476d941864e45aec	Article metadata in the IU Knowledge Base	Table of contents\nOverview\nMetadata fields\nCustom fields\nDefault fields\nOverview\nNote:\nThis information is intended for contributors to the Knowledge Base who are part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.\nFollowing is a list of metadata fields for articles in the IU Knowledge Base. Metadata refers to structured information about the article, which includes creation date, who last updated the article, etc.\nAll articles in the KB use the KM_T1 template, which includes custom fields meant to preserve information about content migrated from the old system.\nImportant:\nWith the exception of the "Meta" and "Meta Description" fields,\ndo not\nchange the fields listed below. Consult the KM team first.\nMetadata fields\nCustom fields\nDocid:\nFor the four-letter docids of migrated content. These are used for old URLs that redirect to ServiceNow (for example, kb.iu.edu/d/abxl).\nStakeholder:\nThe stakeholder/owner group responsible for the content. Plain text field.\nOriginal Author:\nFor the original creator of migrated content. Leave blank in new articles.\nBirth Date:\nFor the original creation date of migrated content. Leave blank in new articles.\nLast Verified:\nThe date when the article was last approved by the stakeholder/content owner. We update this date manually.\nDefault fields\nNumber:\nServiceNow generates numbers for articles automatically.\nVersion:\nServiceNow generates version numbers for articles automatically.\nCreated:\nThe date when the article was created in ServiceNow; not the same as the custom "Birth Date" field, which refers to a creation date in the old system.\nHelpful count:\nNot currently used.\nPublished:\nThe date when the article was published.\nKnowledge base:\nMake sure this field shows "Indiana University Knowledge Base".\nCategory:\nNot currently used; leave blank for now.\nOwnership group:\nNot currently used; leave blank for now.\nUpdated by:\nThe person who last updated the article, including any field.\nRevised By:\nThe person who last checked out the article and published.\nMeta:\nA place to enter keywords to improve the article's searchability. For more, see\nBoost articles in Knowledge Base portal search results using meta tags\n.\nMeta Description:\nThis field is what Google and other search engines crawl; the text in this field should appear in Google search results. To edit this field, deselect\nCustom Meta Description\nif it's selected, add your text, select the custom checkbox again, and save your changes to the article.\nScheduled publish date:\nThis is where we can select a date and time to publish an article in the future. There can only be one scheduled version of an article at a time, and we cannot publish other changes to the article in the meantime; thus, we'd use this sparingly and only when something will go live in the very near future.\nValid to:\nThe date when the article will no longer be valid and will be unavailable to users. To prevent this from happening, all KB articles are automatically created with a "valid to" date of 2100-01-01. Avoid changing this field.\nCan Read/Cannot Read:\nThese fields determine which groups can and can't view articles. If an article is to be public, leave the "Can Read" field blank. If it's to be internal, use the "Can Read" drop-down to choose the audience(s)s in the form km-aud-kbstaff, for example. Currently we don't use the "Cannot Read" field.\nDisplay attachments\nand\nAttachment link:\nWe aren't using these.	KB0025774	KB0025774 v13.0	Table of contents Overview Metadata fields Custom fields Default fields Overview Following is a list of metadata fields for articles in the IU Knowledge Base. Metadata refers to structured information about the article, which includes creation date, who last updated the article, etc. All articles in the KB use the KM_T1 template, which includes 	7.3	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:09:24.833675	2026-01-31 17:09:24.833675	0	72840ba73b06a6d4476d941864e45aec
11435	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9b7de6b03352e2dc04e605570e5c7bbd	Edit an article in the IU Knowledge Base	Note:\nThis information is intended for contributors to the Knowledge Base who are part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.\nTable of contents\nBefore you begin\nEdit an article\nGet help\nBefore you begin\nThese instructions assume that you already know the KB article number and that you have permissions to edit content in the IU Knowledge Base.\nWhen editing an article, keep in mind the following:\nConsult the\nKB style guide\nand\nKB content checklist\n.\nThese resources offer guidance on KB style, accessibility, and more.\nSave your work often.\nIt's a good idea to select\nSave\noften as you make edits. This will help you avoid losing work in case your browser crashes or your connection to ServiceNow times out. It will also enable you to refresh your preview of the content, so that you can catch errors and correct them.\nDon't keep a draft checked out for long.\nOther people may need to make edits to the same content.\nEdits will not be published immediately.\nWhen you select\nPublish\n, your changes will be routed to the Knowledge Management team for final review. The KM team may make final edits or contact subject matter experts for additional input as needed.\nEdit an article\nNote:\nWhen editing KB articles, the Knowledge Management team recommends using two browser windows side by side: one for making the actual changes, and another for previewing the changes before they're published. For more, see\nPortal vs. agent view in the IU Knowledge Base\n.\nNavigate to the article and check it out for editing.\nFrom the portal view:\nGo to\nhttps://servicenow.iu.edu/kb\nand search for the article number. It should be the first search result.\nYou can also set up special keywords in your browser that let you quickly look up a given article from your browser's address bar. For help, see\nCreate browser shortcuts to view Knowledge Base content by number.\nSelect\nActions\n>\nEdit Article\n.\nSelect\nCheckout\n.\nFrom the agent view:\nGo to\nhttps://servicenow.iu.edu\nand, in the global search box, enter the article number.\nSelect the first result that appears, and then select\nEdit\n.\nSelect\nCheckout\n.\nBegin making your changes. For help with the interface or instructions for adding specific elements, see:\nUsing the embedded text editor (TinyMCE)\nAdd a table of contents to an article in the IU Knowledge Base\nImages in the IU Knowledge Base\nTables in the IU Knowledge Base\nArticle metadata in the IU Knowledge Base\nImportant:\nIf you see the\nInsert\nand\nInsert and Stay\nbuttons, do\nnot\nuse them. They will create a copy of the article, which will lead to confusion about which version is the most correct and up to date.\nSelect\nSave\noften, and be sure to preview your work to see how it will look once it's published.\nWhen you're finished, select\nPublish\n. This will route your edits to the KM team for final review.\nGet help\nFor questions, contact the Knowledge Management team at\nkb@iu.eu\n.	KB0026692	KB0026692 v10.0	Table of contents Before you begin Edit an article Get help Before you begin These instructions assume that you already know the KB article number and that you have permissions to edit content in the IU Knowledge Base. When editing an article, keep in mind the following: Consult the KB style guide and KB content checklist. These resources offer 	7.3	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:09:29.072969	2026-01-31 17:09:29.072969	0	9b7de6b03352e2dc04e605570e5c7bbd
11442	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9df04fe33b0e66d4476d941864e45aa3	Add a table of contents to an article in the IU Knowledge Base	Note:\nThis information is intended for contributors to the Knowledge Base who are part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.\nIn ServiceNow Knowledge, the text editor can automatically create a table of contents for you.\nBegin editing an article. For help, see\nEdit an article in the IU Knowledge Base\n.\nMake sure there are multiple sections with\n<h2>\nheadings.\nPlace your cursor where you want the table of contents to appear (usually at the top), and select the\nTable of contents\nicon (\n).\nChange the text "Table of Contents" to "Table of contents" to match the\nKB contributor style guide\n.\nIf you change the headings, be sure to update the table of contents to reflect your edits. Select the table of contents section, and then select the\nRefresh\nicon (\n) that appears.	KB0026693	KB0026693 v2.0	In ServiceNow Knowledge, the text editor can automatically create a table of contents for you. Begin editing an article. For help, see Edit an article in the IU Knowledge Base. Make sure there are multiple sections with h2 headings. Place your cursor where you want the table of contents to appear (usually at the top), and select the Table of con	7.3	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:09:32.475613	2026-01-31 17:09:32.475613	0	9df04fe33b0e66d4476d941864e45aa3
11343	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=2610b4bf476456583d373803846d43ac	Reasons your network connection may have been blocked	Individual computers (not you as the user) may be prevented from accessing the Indiana University network for a variety of reasons:\nViruses:\nCertain viruses reside on the computers they infect and use those computers to attack other users' computers over the network. The user of the infected machine will usually not be aware of the attacks leaving the computer. In these cases, the computer is blocked to protect other computers on the IU network and on the internet.\nFor more about viruses and protecting your computer, see:\nAbout viruses, worms, and Trojan horses\nTips for staying safe online\nWhy you should reformat your hard drive and reinstall Windows after your computer is infected with a virus\nBots:\nOften put in place on the blocked computer by a virus, Internet Relay Chat (\nIRC\n) bots are scripts or independent programs that perform special functions on IRC. Bots can be used to launch denial of service attacks against other computers, to log and transmit keystrokes (including passwords and credit card numbers), and to facilitate transmission of copyrighted material. In these cases, the infected computer is blocked both to protect other computers on the network, and to prevent the loss of users' sensitive personal information.\nUnauthorized device or service on network:\nThe blocked computer is connected to the IU network through a router or other network-extending device, or is running as a server without authorization and/or of a possibly dangerous type on the IU network. In these cases, the computer or unauthorized device can be used as a less-secure point of entry for threats to the IU network, or in some cases can cause direct disruption to the operation of other computers on the network.\nFor more about routers and servers, see:\nAbout rogue DHCP servers on the IU network\nAbout wireless access points in IU campus housing, classrooms, or offices\nIllegal file sharing:\nThe computer has been observed running file sharing services online that degrade network services on the IU network, or, more often, IU has received a complaint from the copyright holder or his or her appointed agent. In these cases, according to law, IU must take certain actions to remove the material from the IU network.\nFor more about file sharing and protecting yourself, see:\nAbout the Digital Millennium Copyright Act\nPeer-to-peer (P2P) file sharing and copyright safety\nSpamming:\nMultiple reports indicate that the computer is sending unsolicited email, or spam. The cause of spam is most often a compromised computer. An attacker sends spam from a compromised computer (rather than from his or her own computer) in the hopes that the spam will more easily get through mail filters.\nFTP servers on non-standard ports:\nFile Transfer Protocol (FTP) servers are file depositories. Users can connect to an FTP client and download or upload files. FTP servers are typically on TCP port 21; when an FTP server is found on a non-standard port, it usually means that an attacker set it up and is trying to hide it. An attacker may use this server to store his own files (often unauthorized copyrighted material) or to copy off files that were present on the machine.\nFor more about FTP, including how to use it safely, see\nUse FTP to transfer files\n.\nSSH brute-forcing:\nThe computer has been observed attempting to make a large number of SSH connections. This activity typically means that someone is trying to guess account names and passwords. It also usually means that the computer is compromised, and that this activity is being performed by an attacker.\nCheck your email for a block message from the University Information Security Office (UISO).	KB0025139	KB0025139 v1.0	Individual computers (not you as the user) may be prevented from accessing the Indiana University network for a variety of reasons: Viruses: Certain viruses reside on the computers they infect and use those computers to attack other users' computers over the network. The user of the infected machine will usually not be aware of the attacks leavi	33.4395		2026-01-31 17:07:11.64132	2026-01-31 17:07:11.64132	0	2610b4bf476456583d373803846d43ac
11443	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c3d4314e330e2e1804e605570e5c7b5f	Tables in the IU Knowledge Base	Note:\nThis information is intended for contributors to the Knowledge Base who are part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.\nTable of contents\nBefore you begin\nAdd a table to an article\nBefore you begin\nBefore adding a table to a Knowledge Base article, consider the following:\nEvery table must have a caption.\nA table heading that appears within\n<caption>\ntags is required for accessibility.\nWide tables will not fit the page.\nIf a table has many columns, it will be too wide for the page and will result in a horizontal scrollbar.\nLists are better for mobile devices.\nConsider whether the data can be converted to a list. Large tables are hard to view on devices with small screens.\nAdd a table to an article\nCheck out an article for editing.\nPlace your cursor where you want the table to be added.\nSelect the\nTable\nicon (\n), and then the select the desired number of columns and rows.\nWith your cursor in the newly created table, select the\nTable\nicon again and choose\nTable properties\n.\nAdd a table caption either directly in the markup or using the WYSIWYG editor:\nIn the WYSIWYG editor:\nCheck\nShow caption\nand select\nSave\n.\nReplace the "Caption" text with an appropriate heading for the table.\nIn the markup:\nSelect the\nSource code\nicon (\n).\nAfter the\n<table>\ntag, enter a caption:\n<caption>My table heading</caption>\nFill out the table as needed.	KB0026690	KB0026690 v2.0	Table of contents Before you begin Add a table to an article Before you begin Before adding a table to a Knowledge Base article, consider the following: Every table must have a caption. A table heading that appears within caption tags is required for accessibility. Wide tables will not fit the page. If a table has many columns, it will be too wi	7.3	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:09:36.600957	2026-01-31 17:09:36.600957	0	c3d4314e330e2e1804e605570e5c7b5f
11444	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c7d8f7a847413ad82fc6a55b416d43d3	Use knowledge blocks as a KB contributor	Table of contents\nOverview\nCommon use cases for blocks\nLimitations of blocks\nComponents of a knowledge block\nView a knowledge block\nEdit an existing knowledge block\nAdd a knowledge block to an article\nCreate a knowledge block\nOverview\nNote:\nThis information is intended for contributors to the Knowledge Base who are part of the Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) initiative.\nIn ServiceNow Knowledge, a knowledge block is a smaller unit of content intended to appear within one or more Knowledge Base articles.  Knowledge blocks serve two purposes:\nReuse:\nWhen the same content should appear in multiple articles, a knowledge block allows you to update it in one place and have the change automatically appear everywhere the block is used.  In IU's legacy system, this type of block was called a "boiler" (short for "boilerplate").  Reusing (in other words, single-sourcing) content can help keep information consistent; when the same information lives in multiple places, it tends to get out of sync as changes occur.\nAudience restrictions:\nA knowledge block can have Can Read criteria, which display the content to only those who belong to the relevant group and are logged into the Knowledge Base.  You can thus include more restricted information (for example, a note about how support staff should address issues related to what the article describes) within a public or less restricted article.\nFor Phase 1 of the KCS initiative, contact the Knowledge Management team if you need an internal block, or if you believe the Can Read criteria of an existing block should be changed.\nA block can serve both purposes (that is, internal as well as public blocks can appear in multiple articles).\nCommon use cases for blocks\nBlocks are useful when you can reasonably expect a piece of information to apply equally to a set of articles for its useful lifespan.  For example:\nTo alert users to an upcoming retirement of or significant change to a service\nTo provide support information for a given service or unit\nTo warn users of about something they should or should not do, whether because of policy or technical risk\nTo present a common set of instructions that applies to multiple tasks or contexts\nHowever, in some cases, even if you need the same wording in more than one article, it may be more trouble than it's worth to use a block.  You should be reasonably confident that if the information changes in the future, the change will apply to every article in which the block is used, so be cautious about using the same block to apply to different services or systems.  Also, too much repetition can clutter articles and dilute search results.  In some cases, it may be better to link to an existing article rather than including reused information in more than one place.\nWhen in doubt about whether using a block makes sense, consult the Knowledge Management team.\nLimitations of blocks\nNote the following restrictions:\nBlocks cannot be renamed.\nKnowledge blocks display on a block level only and can't be used inline. For example, a knowledge block that consists of a name or a single word can't be used in a sentence. By default, blocks have paragraph tags around them that can't be modified.\nBlocks can't be nested.  This also means that a block created to restrict content to a smaller group can't contain a block of reused content, and vice versa.\nComponents of a knowledge block\nA knowledge block consists of the following:\nMetadata:\nBlocks have some of the same metadata as articles, but they do not have the stakeholder field or "Last verified:" field. The most relevant metadata fields are:\nNumber:\nBlock numbers begin with "KBB" rather than "KB", for example: KBB0014392.\nVersion:\nVersion numbers work the same for blocks and articles.\nCan Read:\nBlocks without "Can Read" criteria will be visible to anyone who can see the articles in which they appear. If a block should not be visible to the public, be sure to set "Can Read" criteria on the block itself, so that it isn't accidentally placed in a public article.\nWorkflow:\nBlocks can have the same workflow values as articles. When a block is no longer used in any live articles, the KM team recommends retiring it to reduce clutter, unless it's likely to be used again in the future.  Contact the KM team for help with this process.\nShort description:\nThe title for the block will not appear in the articles in which the block is used, which means that end users of the KB will never see it. Think of it as a code to help you and other people who work on the KB find the content and know what it's about. The Knowledge Management team has historically used a series of words separated by hyphens for block names. For example:\nkcs-contributor-documentation\nis the name of the block that appears in the overview section of this article. Notice that it's fine to use shorthand like acronyms in block titles.\nems-linux-support\nis the name of KBB0014391, which contains a link to Endpoint Management Services' request form for Linux users.\nhonorlock-tech-reqs\nis the name of KBB0014370, which contains a list of technical requirements for Honorlock.\nFor boilers and internal notes migrated from the legacy Knowledge Management System (KMS), you'll see short descriptions that begin with "PUB" (for public reused boilers), "INT" (for internal reused boilers), and "WHO" (for internal notes that were previously maintained directly in one document).  In the case of "WHO" blocks, which didn't have boiler names in the KMS, the migration process automatically generated short descriptions based on the (legacy) document ID. Since Can Read criteria is visible in a block when you view it, the Knowledge Management team does not intend to maintain the "PUB", "INT", and "WHO" naming convention going forward.\nBody:\nThe body of a block can contain any combination of elements that can stand alone within the body of an article, such as paragraphs, lists, or tables.\nBlocks can also consist of, or contain, panels such as those headed by "Note:" or "Important:". Since you need to work in the markup of an article to create a panel, for KCS Phase 1, the KM team will take care of formatting panels; just include the content in some way that makes your intention clear.\nFor internal sections, the KM team has historically used the following style in order to alert those who can see the information that it is internal:\nAudience:\nThe body of the internal content goes here. Note that the designations we use for the audience are not the technical names of the "Can Read" criteria, but a human-friendly description such as\nIU community:\nor\nUITS staff and local support:\n, etc.\nSince you need to work in the markup of an article to edit a panel, the KM team may be evaluating alternatives to simplify our style in the future. For now, ask the KM team for help with editing or creating an internal block.\nView a knowledge block\nWhen you're editing an article in ServiceNow, you won't see the content of knowledge blocks within the article.  Instead, you'll see a rectangle with the number and short description (name) of the block, like this:\nYou can view the content of this block in any of the following ways:\nView the block in the context of the article:\nView the entire article in the KB portal using the permalink for the article number; the KM team recommends having the portal view of an article you're editing open in another tab or window so that you can see the content of blocks in context.\nBelow the editing window, under "Related Links", select\nView Article\n.  This takes you out of editing mode to the agent view of the article.  Note that the formatting of some content will be different from the way it will appear in the live KB.  To resume editing from this view, at the top of the page, choose\nEdit\n.\nView the block alone:\nView the content of the block itself in the KB portal using a URL of the same format as an article permalink.  For example, if you replace the article number\nKB\nXXXXXXX\nin the permalink of any KB page with\nKBB0014405\n, you can see the sn-next-experience block alone at\nhttps://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KBB0014405\n.  The KM team recommends previewing knowledge blocks in the portal when you're editing them, just as you do for articles.  Note, however, that you can't find knowledge blocks by searching in the KB portal; you can only get to one by the direct URL.\nTip:\nIn Chrome and Firefox, you can set up a shortcut to go directly to any block or article by its number.  For instructions, see\nCreate browser shortcuts to view Knowledge Base content by number\n.\nView the block in the editing view in ServiceNow.  From the\nAll\nmenu in ServiceNow, type\nblock\n, and then, under "Knowledge Blocks", select\nPublished\nto see a list of all published blocks.  (If you don't see the\nAll\nmenu, turn on the Next Experience, as described in the knowledge block under discussion.)  You can then filter by any field visible on the screen.  When you find the block, select its number to view it.\nEdit an existing knowledge block\nIf the content you want to edit is within a knowledge block, the block needs to be checked out and edited separately.\nNote:\nIf a block is used in more than one article, it's important to determine whether the changes will make sense and be accurate in all the contexts where the block is used before proceeding. If you're reasonably confident that your changes will apply everywhere (for example, the information in the block is specific to a service you own, it's true in all contexts without exception, etc.), go ahead and submit the changes according to the process described below. If you're not sure whether the changes will make any other articles inaccurate, contact the KM team for help with analyzing the impact of the change.\nTip:\nTake care if you're adding a link to a KB article within a block; if the block appears in that article, you'll create a circular reference that goes nowhere.  Watch out also for adding a block to an article that it links to.\nFor KCS Phase 1, although you may submit edits to blocks with a relatively limited impact (as indicated above), feel free to ask the KM team for help with edits to blocks.\nView the block using one of the methods listed above under "View the block alone".\nIf you're starting from the KB portal, from the\nActions\nmenu, select\nEdit Article\n.\nAt the top of the editing view of the block, choose\nCheckout\n.\nEdit the block just as you would edit an article.\nWhen you're done, choose\nPublish\nto route it to the Knowledge Management team for action.  The revised language will appear in all the articles containing the block when the KM team publishes it.  If it's important that the changes to the block and other changes to your article go live at the same time, contact the KM to specify that.\nAdd a knowledge block to an article\nTo add an existing knowledge block to an article:\nWhile editing an article in the agent view of ServiceNow, make sure your cursor is positioned where you want to add a knowledge block. Either at the top of the editing window or just below the editing pane, select\nAdd Blocks\n.\nImportant:\nDo\nnot\nselect the\nInsert\nor\nInsert and Stay\nbutton.  Doing so will create a copy of the article.\nIn the "Add Blocks" sidebar, search for the knowledge block, or enter the block's ID number if you have it, and then select\nInsert\n.\nIf the basic search doesn't work, select\nAdvanced search\n. Select the checkbox for the block you want and then, at the top, select\nInsert to article\n.\nImportant:\nIf you open a knowledge block to view it,\ndo not try to insert it into the article from that window\n. When you're viewing a block, the\nInsert\nand\nInsert and Stay\nbuttons above and below the body are the same as those above and below an article in editing view, meaning that they create a\ncopy\nof the block instead of inserting it into an article.\nCreate a knowledge block\nFor KCS Phase 1, the KM team recommends that contributors not create new knowledge blocks.  This guidance may change in future phases; contact the KM team if you have questions or concerns.	KB0026683	KB0026683 v4.0	 the article describes) within a public or less restricted article. For Phase 1 of the KCS <strong>initiative</strong>, contact	7.3	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:09:40.091057	2026-01-31 17:09:40.091057	0	c7d8f7a847413ad82fc6a55b416d43d3
11448	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=0610f0bf476456583d373803846d4308	Data Cookbook definition workflow cheat sheet	Table of contents\nAbout workflows\nIU standard definition approval\nUnderlying assumptions\nStages of standard definition approval\nRejected definitions\nAbout workflows\nWorkflow definition\nIn Data Cookbook, a workflow is a predefined process for approving an object or a request for information. Data Cookbook includes a customizable workflow engine. Custom workflows specific to Indiana University were created and tested for adoption by the IU Data Cookbook community during the first quarter of 2017.\nWorkflow types\nData Cookbook includes three basic workflow types: definition approval, specification approval, and information request resolution. At IU, Data Cookbook only uses workflow for definitions and specifications.\nWorkflow stages and steps\nStages\nEach workflow has several stages that represent a point in the process that requires specified action from one or more users or groups of users.\nHappy path\nIn Data Cookbook, "happy path" is a term that describes the most common approval process. In project management terminology, this would be the critical path. Steps with green-colored transition action buttons are the happy path for the stage.\nSteps\nIn a stage, steps represent available tasks and/or groups of actions. Steps can be required or optional and may be assigned to specific users or user groups based on the workflow object's attributes.\nTransition actions\nTransition actions are associated with steps within stages and represent what a user does to move an object from one stage to another. These actions appear as buttons on the definition or specification. The transition actions available to a user will depend on both the user's role and the workflow stage that contains the object.\nIn the following example, two transition actions are available; you can send the definition to QA or reject it:\nConditions\nConditions function as "where" clauses for workflows. Conditions let you specify appropriate workflows or steps based on criteria such as functional area, data system, or classification code.\nNote:\nIU Data Cookbook currently doesn't use conditional criteria. For ease of understanding and maintenance, the current implementation defines a flexible standard approach to workflow. Direct experience and business requirements will dictate future revisions to workflows or the creation of conditional workflows.\nWorkflow permissions\nWorkflow stages, steps, and transition actions are governed by permissions handled by interactions between user groups, assignment mappings, and named roles.\nUser groups\nUser groups are the primary means for granting permissions within Data Cookbook, especially in the absence of workflow integration. You can grant user groups permissions for definitions, specifications, and technical information based on functional area. For specifications and definitions, these permissions are manager, editor, and viewer. For technical information, these permissions are editor, view, and hidden. Any user assigned to the group will have the permissions associated with the group.\nNote:\nWorkflow stages and steps can override user group permissions both positively and negatively. For example, during the quality assurance stages of workflows, only staff associated with QA functions are allowed to make modifications. Conversely, data stewards or data managers may sometimes delegate authority to user groups to edit or approve definitions.\nUser groups of moderators and editors are associated with definitions; user groups of specification managers and specification developers are associated with specifications.\nAssignment mappings\nAssignment mappings are a way to create "super groups" of users for assigning steps in a workflow. They allow workflow standardization and let you create correlations between attributes such as functional areas, data systems, or specification types so that you can assign user groups to specific steps in workflow stages.\nAs mentioned in the\nConditions\nsection, the current implementation of Data Cookbook defines a standardized workflow that uses assignment mappings based on functional area. Direct experience and business requirements will dictate future revisions to this approach.\nNamed roles\nNamed roles allow you to define a "pool" of users who may be added to a workflow step when the step is initiated. These users are assigned by another defined "pool" of users who receive notification to make an assignment. If you are tasked with managing an assignment, named roles let you assign an individual user to a step in a workflow stage.\nNote:\nAt this time, named roles can't be mapped to functional areas and are not being used. Instead, IU Data Cookbook uses assignment mappings and user groups to associate specification managers and developers to workflow stages and steps in the appropriate functional areas.\nIU standard definition approval\nUnderlying assumptions\nThe stages of the IU standard definition approval workflow were created using the following assumptions:\nThe fewer the variations that exist, the more understandable and maintainable the workflows will be.\nData managers and data stewards should be involved as appropriate for reviewing, editing, and applying classification codes.\nData managers should not be solely responsible for the creation of all definitions. Initial creation should take place as gaps are identified during the creation of specifications/reports by developers and functional experts.\nQuality assurance is necessary for good governance to ensure a solid body of consistent standardized information; however, QA should not adversely affect the workflow.\nAll stages will have explicit steps that allow designated workflow administrative users to intervene and "unstick" the workflow as needed. This step will not be included in subsequent documentation.\nStages of standard definition approval\nAt IU, approval of a standard definition proceeds through the following stages; see\nbelow\nfor a visual representation of these stages:\nCreate/Revise\nQA Initial Review\nDM Review/Edit\nDM Apply Classification (optional)\nQA Final Review\nApproved\n1. Create/Revise\nDuring this stage, definitions are created and revised. This stage is made up of two primary steps, "Create/Revise Definition" and "Edit Draft as a Moderator".\na. Create/Revise Definition\nAccess to the "Create/Revise Definition" step is available to definition editors associated with each functional area. These editors can create definitions and submit them for QA initial review, or they can reject definitions. To take a transition action on the definition, under "Step(s): Create/Revise Definition", select\nQA Initial Review\nor\nReject\n:\nb. Edit Draft as Moderator\nAccess to the "Edit Draft as a Moderator" step is available to definition moderators; "moderator" is Data Cookbook's term for data managers or their delegates. Moderators fulfill an oversight role with regard to definition content, and in many cases also with regard to definition classification. Moderators have the ability not only to create definitions but also to submit them to multiple stages. In this stage, moderators can choose to approve definitions and submit them for final QA, if they believe the definitions are both accurate and complete. To take a transition action on the item, under "Step(s): Edit Draft as a Moderator", select\nQA Initial Review\n,\nApprove/QA\n,\nReject\n, or\nRequest Classification\n:\n2. QA Initial Review\nDuring the QA initial review stage, definitions are checked for compliance with naming conventions and adherence to IU's Data Cookbook standards. Only administrators are able to edit a definition in the QA initial review stage, to ensure that QA is not being done on a moving target. In general, standards and formatting are overseen by the QA role, while the actual definition content and classification of the data are overseen by the moderator or data manager role. The EBI team within UITS is the service owner for Data Cookbook, and staff from the EBI team are currently responsible for QA. A definition that passes QA is submitted for DM review; if it does not pass QA, it will be returned to the previous stage for further revision.\nThe QA initial review stage is made up of the primary "QA Initial Review" step and an optional "QA Return for Revision" step.\na. QA Initial Review\nQA occurs during the "QA Initial Review" step. To take a transition action on the definition, under "Step(s): QA Initial Review", select\nDM Review\nor\nReturn for Revision\n:\n3. DM Review/Edit\nIn the DM review/edit stage, the moderator reviews, edits, or rewrites the definition. This stage is made up of two steps, the primary "DM Review/Edit" step and the optional "Revise Definition" step.\na. DM Review/Edit\nIn the "DM Review/Edit" step, the moderators, who are the designated primary subject matter experts for the corresponding functional area, review the content of the definition. Moderators can choose to approve, modify, or rewrite the definition; return the definition to the initial stage for revision; or request that the classification be assigned. This step is at the heart of the data governance process. To take a transition action on the definition, under "Step(s): DM Review/Edit", select\nApprove/QA\n,\nReject\n,\nRequest Classification\n, or\nReturn for Revision\n:\nRevise Definition:\nThe optional "Revise Definition" step allows the initial definition creator or editor group to retrieve a definition from data manager review for further editing or revision. The creator or editor can retrieve the definition without intervention on the part of either the moderator or administrators. To take a transition action on the definition, under "Step(s): Revise Definition", select\nReject\nor\nReturn for Revision\n:\n4. DM Apply Classification (optional)\nApply classification is an optional stage where you can apply the security classification to the definition. In most cases, it is expected that the group responsible for the DM review/edit stage will supply the classification, but there are functional areas in which this will not be the case (for example, in student records, the Data Steward has delegated responsibility for the DM review/edit stage to UIRR, but the Student Data Management & Access group supplies the classification); for that reason, this step is optional. The original "Moderators by Functional Area" assignment mapping is used for the DM review/edit stage, while a new "Data Managers by Functional Area" assignment mapping is used for the apply classification stage.\nThe apply classification stage is made up of the primary "Apply Classification" step, and the optional "Request DM Revision" step.\na. Apply Classification\nDuring the "Apply Classification" step, if there is a separation of duties for managing definition content and managing security, the classification is supplied. To take a transition action on the definition, under "Step(s): DM Apply Classification", select\nApprove/QA\n,\nReject\n,\nReturn for DM Revision\n, or\nReturn for Revision\n:\nRequest DM Revision:\nIn the "Request DM Revision" step, the data manager associated with the previous stage can retrieve a definition from the current stage for further editing. To take a transition action on the definition, under "Step(s): Request DM Revision", select\nReject\n,\nReturn for DM Revision\n, or\nReturn for Revision\n:\n5. QA Final Review\nDuring the QA final review stage, the definition gets a final check for completeness and adherence to IU's Data Cookbook standards. Only administrators are able to edit a definition in the QA final review stage, to ensure that QA is not being done on a moving target. The EBI team within UITS is the service owner for Data Cookbook, and staff from the EBI team are currently responsible for QA. A definition that passes final QA is approved and becomes the current official version.\nThe QA final review stage is made up of the primary "QA Final Approval" step and an optional "Request DM Revision" step.\na. QA Final Approval\nFinal QA occurs during the QA final approval step. To take a transition action on the definition, under "Step(s): QA Final Approval", select\nQA Final Approval\n,\nReturn for DM Revision\n, or\nReturn for Revision\n:\nRequest DM Revision:\nIn the "Request DM Revision" step, the data manager associated with the DM review/edit stage can retrieve a definition from the current stage for further editing. To take a transition action on the definition, under "Step(s): Request DM Revision", select\nReject\n,\nReturn for DM Revision\n, or\nReturn for Revision\n:\n6. Approved\nThe approved stage is the final stage of the definition workflow. A definition that reaches this point has received approval for both content and format, and is the current official version for the IU Data Cookbook community. Any subsequent revision to an approved definition requires a new version of the definition to be created, and those new versions must go through the above workflow and be approved in their turn.\nRejected definitions\nAt various stages and steps in the workflow, appropriate users can choose to reject a definition for any reason (for example, duplication of definitions, discovery that the definition is not required).	KB0025084	KB0025084 v1.0	Table of contents About workflows IU standard definition approval Underlying assumptions Stages of standard definition approval Rejected definitions About workflows IU standard definition approval Underlying assumptions The stages of the IU standard definition approval workflow were created using the following assumptions: The fewer the variatio	2.718		2026-01-31 17:09:47.084702	2026-01-31 17:09:47.084702	0	0610f0bf476456583d373803846d4308
11449	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e210b4bf476456583d373803846d4352	Data Cookbook specification workflow cheat sheet	Table of contents\nAbout workflows\nIU standard specification approval\nUnderlying assumptions\nStages of standard specification approval\nCanceled specifications\nAbout workflows\nWorkflow definition\nIn Data Cookbook, a workflow is a predefined process for approving an object or a request for information. Data Cookbook includes a customizable workflow engine. Custom workflows specific to Indiana University were created and tested for adoption by the IU Data Cookbook community during the first quarter of 2017.\nWorkflow types\nData Cookbook includes three basic workflow types: definition approval, specification approval, and information request resolution. At IU, Data Cookbook only uses workflow for definitions and specifications.\nWorkflow stages and steps\nStages\nEach workflow has several stages that represent a point in the process that requires specified action from one or more users or groups of users.\nHappy path\nIn Data Cookbook, "happy path" is a term that describes the most common approval process. In project management terminology, this would be the critical path. Steps with green-colored transition action buttons are the happy path for the stage.\nSteps\nIn a stage, steps represent available tasks and/or groups of actions. Steps can be required or optional and may be assigned to specific users or user groups based on the workflow object's attributes.\nTransition actions\nTransition actions are associated with steps within stages and represent what a user does to move an object from one stage to another. These actions appear as buttons on the definition or specification. The transition actions available to a user will depend on both the user's role and the workflow stage that contains the object.\nIn the following example, two transition actions are available; you can send the definition to QA or reject it:\nConditions\nConditions function as "where" clauses for workflows. Conditions let you specify appropriate workflows or steps based on criteria such as functional area, data system, or classification code.\nNote:\nIU Data Cookbook currently doesn't use conditional criteria. For ease of understanding and maintenance, the current implementation defines a flexible standard approach to workflow. Direct experience and business requirements will dictate future revisions to workflows or the creation of conditional workflows.\nWorkflow permissions\nWorkflow stages, steps, and transition actions are governed by permissions handled by interactions between user groups, assignment mappings, and named roles.\nUser groups\nUser groups are the primary means for granting permissions within Data Cookbook, especially in the absence of workflow integration. You can grant user groups permissions for definitions, specifications, and technical information based on functional area. For specifications and definitions, these permissions are manager, editor, and viewer. For technical information, these permissions are editor, view, and hidden. Any user assigned to the group will have the permissions associated with the group.\nNote:\nWorkflow stages and steps can override user group permissions both positively and negatively. For example, during the quality assurance stages of workflows, only staff associated with QA functions are allowed to make modifications. Conversely, data stewards or data managers may sometimes delegate authority to user groups to edit or approve definitions.\nUser groups of moderators and editors are associated with definitions; user groups of specification managers and specification developers are associated with specifications.\nAssignment mappings\nAssignment mappings are a way to create "super groups" of users for assigning steps in a workflow. They allow workflow standardization and let you create correlations between attributes such as functional areas, data systems, or specification types so that you can assign user groups to specific steps in workflow stages.\nAs mentioned in the\nConditions\nsection, the current implementation of Data Cookbook defines a standardized workflow that uses assignment mappings based on functional area. Direct experience and business requirements will dictate future revisions to this approach.\nNamed roles\nNamed roles allow you to define a "pool" of users who may be added to a workflow step when the step is initiated. These users are assigned by another defined "pool" of users who receive notification to make an assignment. If you are tasked with managing an assignment, named roles let you assign an individual user to a step in a workflow stage.\nNote:\nAt this time, named roles can't be mapped to functional areas and are not being used. Instead, IU Data Cookbook uses assignment mappings and user groups to associate specification managers and developers to workflow stages and steps in the appropriate functional areas.\nIU standard specification approval\nUnderlying assumptions\nThe stages of the IU standard specification approval workflow were created using the following assumptions:\nThe fewer the variations that exist, the more understandable and maintainable the workflows will be.\nData managers and data stewards are not necessarily directly involved in the workflow unless they have requested or are overseeing the specification under development.\nProduct owners, functional managers, and business analysts will be responsible for initial drafts and/or approval as appropriate.\nQuality assurance is necessary for good governance to ensure a solid body of consistent standardized information; however, QA should not adversely affect the workflow.\nAll stages will have explicit steps that allow designated workflow administrative users to intervene and "unstick" the workflow as needed. This step will not be included in subsequent documentation.\nData stewards grant approval of security classification at the specification level outside Data Cookbook via existing systems and channels (for example, ACM).\nStages of standard specification approval\nAt IU, approval of a standard specification proceeds through the following stages; see\nbelow\nfor a visual representation of these stages:\nInitial Draft\nDevelopment\nQA Initial Review\nApproval Requested\nQA Final Review\nApproved\nNote:\nTo see the transition action buttons for a particular specification, to the far right and near the top of the specification, select the\nWorkflow\ntab:\n1. Initial Draft\nSpecifications are created and revised during the initial draft stage. In this stage, you'll create a request for a report and flesh out its business requirements. This stage's primary step is draft specification.\na. Draft Specification\nAccess to the "Draft Specification" step is available to members of the "Spec Drafters by Functional Area" assignment mapping. By default, this step is mapped to the moderator user groups; note, however, that it is not assumed that this step is the responsibility of the moderator or data manager. In areas where EBI has ongoing or anticipated future development, the EBI business analysts and the EBI AM360 product owner are also mapped to this step. Additionally, for the financial and budget areas, the EBI KPI product owner is mapped to this step. Mapping of user groups to this step will be updated appropriately based on adoption and need. As user groups for specification managers by functional area are incrementally built, they will also be mapped to this step.\nAfter you've completed the initial draft stage, move the report to the development stage for potential assignment to a specific person for development. To take a transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): Draft Specification", select\nSubmit\nor\nCancel\n:\n2. Development\nOnce you've made your request and added the initial requirements to the specification, the development stage begins. The development stage is made up of the primary "Initial Development" step, as well as two optional steps, "Edit Specification - Manager" and "Edit Specification - Drafter".\na. Initial Development\nAccess to the "Initial Development" step is available to members of the "Specification Developers by Functional Area" assignment mapping. Once the specification is under development, any developer in this group can edit the specification to reflect the iterative development and refinement of requirements. When complete, select\nRequest QA\nto submit the specification for QA:\nDuring the development stage, the manager and the specification drafter have the option to edit the specification:\nEdit Specification - Manager:\nAccess to the "Edit Specification - Manager" step is available to members of the "Specification Managers by Functional Area" assignment mapping. The specification manager can edit and take various transition actions on the specification in this step. To take a transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): Edit Specification - Manager", select\nRequest QA\n,\nBack to drafting\n,\nCancel\n, or\nQA Final Review\n:\nEdit Specification - Drafter:\nAccess to the "Edit Specification - Drafter" step is available to members of the "Specification Drafters by Functional Area" assignment mapping. The user group that drafted the specification can edit and take various transition actions on the specification in this step. To take a transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): Edit Specification - Drafter", select\nRequest QA\n,\nBack to drafting\n, or\nCancel\n:\n3. QA Initial Review\nDuring the QA initial review stage, specifications are checked for compliance with naming conventions and adherence to IU's Data Cookbook standards. Only administrators are able to edit a specification in the QA initial review stage, to ensure that QA is not being done on a moving target. In general, standards and formatting are overseen by the QA role, while the actual specification content is overseen by the product owner or specification manager role. The EBI team within UITS is the service owner for Data Cookbook, and staff from the EBI team are currently responsible for QA. A specification that passes QA is submitted for approval; if it does not pass QA, it will be returned to the previous stage for further revision.\nThe QA initial review stage is made up of the primary "QA Initial Review" step, with an optional "QA Return for Revision" step.\na. QA Initial Review\nQA occurs during the "QA Initial Review" step. To take a transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): Request Approval", select\nRequest Approval\nor\nReturn for Revision\n:\nQA Return for Revision:\nThe "QA Return for Revision" step is an optional step allowing members of the "Specification Managers by Functional Area" assignment mapping to intervene and return the specification for further development or refinement. To take this transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): QA Return for Revision", select\nReturn for Revision\n:\n4. Approval Requested\nDuring the approval requested stage, members of the "Specification Manager by Functional Area" assignment mapping review the specification and then either approve it or return it (and, by extension, return the associated report/object) to development. Note that this step represents approval of the specification itself, not data manager security approval for the report/object, which is handled via regular processes.\nThe approval requested stage is made up of the primary "Approve Specification" step (associated with the specification manager), and the optional "AC Return for Revision", "Spec Drafter Delegation" and "Cancel" steps. Only the original report requester has the option to cancel at this point.\na. Approve Specification Step\nIn the "Approve Specification" step, the specification manager reviews work to date on the specification and the associated report/object, and then either approves it or sends it back to development for modifications. For DSI-related specifications, this approval is associated with the product owner. To take a transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): Approve Specification", select\nApprove\n,\nCancel\n, or\nReturn for Revision\n:\nAC Return for Revision:\nIn the "AC Return for Revision" step, agile coaches can return the specification to the development stage, allowing them to correct issues they or the product owner have identified without the PO having to take action. To take this transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): AC Return for Revision", select\nReturn for Revision\n.\nSpec Drafter Delegation:\nIn the "Spec Drafter Delegation" step, the user group associated with drafting specifications for the associated functional area can choose to return the specification for revision or to cancel it. To take a transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): Spec Drafter Delegation", select\nCancel\nor\nReturn for Revision\n:\n5. QA Final Review\nDuring the QA final review stage, the specification gets a final check for compliance with naming conventions, adherence to IU's Data Cookbook standards, and completeness. Only administrators are able to edit a specification in the QA final review stage, to ensure that QA is not being done on a moving target. In general, standards and formatting are overseen by the QA role, while the actual specification content is overseen by the product owner or specification manager role. The EBI team within UITS is the service owner for Data Cookbook, and staff from the EBI team are currently responsible for QA. A specification that passes final QA is approved and becomes the current official version.\nThe QA final review stage is made up of the primary "QA Final Approval" step, with optional "Return to Spec Manager" and "QA Return for Revision" steps.\na. QA Final Approval\nFinal QA occurs during the QA final approval step. To take a transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): QA Final Approval", select\nQA Final Approval\n,\nReturn for Revision\n, or\nReturn to Approval\n:\nReturn to Spec Manager:\nIn the "Return to Spec Manager" step, the product owner can return the specification to the previous stage, in order to correct any identified issues or perform any other actions available in the "Approve Specification" step. Note that the user in this step may be a member of both the specification managers and the specification drafters groups; this will not be uncommon. To take a transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): Return to Spec Manager, Return for Revision", select\nReturn for Revision\nor\nReturn to Approval\n:\nReturn for Revision:\nIn the "Return for Revision" step, the user group associated with managing specifications for the associated functional area can choose to return the specification for revision or, where there is an assigned product owner, to act on behalf of the PO. To take a transition action on the specification, under "Step(s): Return for Revision", select\nReturn for Revision\nor\nReturn to Approval\n:\n6. Approved\nThe approved stage is the final stage of the specification workflow. A specification that reaches this point has received approval for both content and format, and is the current official version for the IU Data Cookbook community. Any subsequent revision to an approved specification requires a new version of the specification to be created, and those new versions must go through the above workflow and be approved in their turn.\nCanceled specifications\nAt various stages and steps in the workflow, appropriate users can choose to cancel a specification for any reason (for example, duplication of specifications, discovery that the specification is not required).	KB0025134	KB0025134 v1.0	Table of contents About workflows IU standard specification approval Underlying assumptions Stages of standard specification approval Canceled specifications About workflows IU standard specification approval Underlying assumptions The stages of the IU standard specification approval workflow were created using the following assumptions: The few	2.718		2026-01-31 17:09:50.593643	2026-01-31 17:09:50.593643	0	e210b4bf476456583d373803846d4352
10523	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=da8d33fa3b97e654476d941864e45a65	Request institutional storage in Microsoft shared storage	Table of contents\nOverview\nSubmit a request for\nMicrosoft Teams\nstorage\nOverview\nImportant:\nShared storage in Google is available\nonly as an option for departments that want to\npay for continued storage in Google\n.\nDue to\nlimits to IU's storage footprint in the Google platform\n, all individual\nGoogle My Drive\ns now have a\n5 GB\nquota.\nAt Indiana University, employees must use the\nInstitutional storage request form\nto request shared storage in\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n.\nNote:\nAny\nMicrosoft\nteam at IU (except for a Canvas course) must be created using IU's\nInstitutional storage request form\n. To access the form in the Teams client, in the left navigation pane, select\nNew Team\n. This process ensures the team is created as private, where specific members are assigned access, and it provides additional safeguards, naming conventions, and tools that will be valuable for storage owners and the IU community.\nTo create a team for your Canvas course, see\nAbout the Microsoft Education app for Canvas\n.\nIf you need to create a team with an alternative template,\ncontact your campus IT Support Center\n.\nManually created teams (created without using the form above) need to be registered on the\nInstitutional storage request form\nwithin seven days, or they will be deleted.  Team owners will be notified via email for three days after creation with this requirement.\nIf you are an employee who will be storing content that is classified as\nUniversity-Internal data\nor greater and that includes information that terminated employees and the public should not have access to, you are required to use the institutional storage request form.  If you have questions about data classification or safe handling of sensitive data, contact\niudata@iu.edu\n.\nIt's important to use the institutional storage request form rather than the\nJoin or Create Team\nbutton (\n) in Microsoft Teams because requesting through the form:\nApplies security configurations and controls based on the data selected\nEnsures the appropriate naming conventions are in place\nProvides storage owner access to the "Manage your storage" tool, which provides an interface to update the storage location (for example, adding external collaborators, updating data classification, updating team description and metadata, etc.). The management tool also provides valuable audit information.\nProvides the owner with a list of their responsibilities as well as training resources for maintaining user access in the team, in any channels created, and within\nSharePoint Online\n.\nSubmit a request for\nMicrosoft Teams\nstorage\nIf you have questions about your department's storage options, consult with your unit's\nlocal UITS support person\nbefore submitting your request for storage.\nTo complete the\nInstitutional storage request form\nform, you must:\nIdentify two owners for the storage location. Owners must agree to follow the\nusage standards\nand accept responsibility for preserving the security and confidentiality of information that you store, post, access, or share before you are provided access to the environments.\nDetermine the highest data classification to be managed in your storage location. If your storage location will be managing\nCritical or Restricted data\n, such as personal health information (PHI), personally identifiable information (PII), and/or FERPA, additional controls will be applied to help storage owners fulfill their responsibilities.\nDetermine whether you will need to share with collaborators who are external to IU (and have no IU accounts). Storage locations created for use with external collaborators will have an "E" appended to the name prefix and will allow access to external collaborators that you approve.\nOnce you have submitted the form, the new storage site will be created immediately.\nIf your team in\nMicrosoft Teams\nwas initially requested without external sharing enabled, and you later need to enable it, see\nEnable external sharing in Microsoft Teams at IU\n.	KB0025401	KB0025401 v3.0	Table of contents Overview Submit a request for Microsoft Teams storage Overview At Indiana University, employees must use the Institutional storage request form to request shared storage in Microsoft Teams at IU. Note: If you are an employee who will be storing content that is classified as University-Internal data or greater and that includes 	2.4897		2026-01-31 16:51:23.369658	2026-01-31 17:09:54.071206	0	da8d33fa3b97e654476d941864e45a65
11450	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f9909a933b831e102b40571864e45a04	Service agreement for Microsoft and Google storage owners	Table of contents\nOverview\nUsage responsibilities\nSanctions\nAssent\nLog requests\nOverview\nImportant:\nShared storage in Google is available\nonly as an option for departments that want to\npay for continued storage in Google\n.\nDue to\nlimits to IU's storage footprint in the Google platform\n, all individual\nGoogle My Drive\ns now have a\n5 GB\nquota.\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nand\nGoogle at IU Shared Drives\nare designed to provide you with better and more secure methods of fostering collaboration. As a Microsoft or Google storage owner (\nMicrosoft Teams\nowner and/or\nGoogle Shared Drives\nmanager), you must agree to follow these standards and accept responsibility to preserve the security and confidentiality of information that you store, post, access, or share before you are provided access to the environments.\nUITS\nreserves the right to audit the\nMicrosoft Teams\nor\nGoogle Shared Drives\nstorage location for compliance and share the information with the team owners and shared drive managers for maintenance. Audits and log files may also be shared with service administrators and local UITS support people for service management, incident response, and site/drive compliance.\nUsage responsibilities\nImportant:\nMicrosoft Teams\nand\nGoogle Shared Drives\nare approved for all\nClassification levels of institutional data\nas well as non-institutional data. Critical data that is\nnot\napproved for these environments includes: Credit card and Payment Card Industry (PCI) data, export-controlled research, controlled unclassified information (CUI), and advancement donor gift agreements and wealth information.\nIf your storage location will be managing\nCritical or Restricted data\n, additional controls are applied to aid you in fulfilling your responsibilities as a Microsoft or Google storage owner.\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\nand\nGoogle at IU\nSecure Storage\n, or [Sec] and [Sec-E] locations, are\napproved\nfor storing and sharing most types of data, including critical data and restricted data.\nProtected Health Information\n(PHI) is included in the approved data for these services.\nFor more, see\nTypes of institutional data appropriate for Microsoft 365 at IU and Google at IU\n.\nYou agree to:\nAbide by the responsibilities outlined in the\nAcceptable Use Agreement for access to technology and information resources at IU\n.\nUnderstand the\nClassification levels of institutional data\nand maintain the appropriate safeguards to protect the information. See\nRules and Policies of Data Management\n.\nUnderstand that if new\nMicrosoft Teams\nor\nGoogle Shared Drives\nare needed, they must be created via the\nInstitutional storage request form\n.\nUnderstand your responsibilities in assigning roles and permissions in\nMicrosoft Teams\n(co-owner, member, guest) or\nGoogle Shared Drives\n(co-manager, content collaborator, contributor, commenter, viewer) to grant permissions appropriately.\nApply the\nprinciple of least privilege\n, ensuring the confidentiality of personally identifiable information (PII) and sensitive data stored.\nPeriodically review your security and sharing settings, ensuring that university information is shared only with intended audiences. This includes review of any audit reports provided to you to aid in your compliance responsibilities.\nEnsure external (non-IU) collaborators are not provided access to a\nMicrosoft Teams\nor a\nGoogle Shared Drives\nsite that has not been requested and designated for external sharing.\nIf your team in\nMicrosoft Teams\nwas initially requested without external sharing enabled, and you later need to enable it, see\nEnable external sharing in Microsoft Teams at IU\n.\nIf you choose to assign a co-owner, it is your responsibility to ensure they are informed of their usage responsibilities outlined here.\nReport any breach or data exposure as soon as you are made aware to\nit-incident@iu.edu\n.\nNo Microsoft at IU or Google at IU storage location (for example,\nMicrosoft Teams\n) that includes personal health information (PHI), student information (FERPA), or any other personally identifiable information (PII) should add a marketplace app (add-on or add-in) without first ensuring the product has been vetted through the Software and Services Selection Process (SSSP) to ensure the appropriate safeguards and contract language are in place. As the owner, it is your responsibility to comply with all applicable\nInformation and IT policies\nand departmental policies and guidelines, including\nDisclosing Institutional Information to Third Parties (DM-02)\n, which states that you must consult with a Data Steward before sharing institutional data with a third-party product or service.\nEnforce naming conventions for\nMicrosoft Teams\nand\nGoogle Shared Drives\n:\nFor\nMicrosoft Secure Storage\nand\nGoogle Secure Storage\n, names must start with the prefixes\n[Sec]\n. If collaborators external to IU are permitted, then the prefix must instead be\n[Sec-E]\n.\nMicrosoft Teams\nand\nGoogle Shared Drives\nsites not meant for sensitive data must not use either of these prefixes.\nThe rest of the name should use the format\nCampus-Department-Name\n:\nCampus:\nCan either be an individual campus, or\nIU\nfor cross-campus activities.\nDepartment:\nFour or fewer letters; normally should be set to a departmental code. Student organizations, multi-departmental committees, ad hoc groups, and others that aren't affiliated with a specific department may set this as they prefer.\nName:\nA brief description of the site itself (entered as the "Short Name" in the institutional storage request form). It may be up to 24 characters long. Spaces and hyphens are permitted.\nThe name must be unique. These characters are not permitted:\n~ " # % & * : < > ? / \\ { | } .\nExample names\nSecure storage sites for internal collaborators\n[Sec] IU-UIPO-UDMC\nSecure storage sites that allow external collaborators\n[Sec-E] IU-ORA-Research Standards\nMicrosoft Teams\nor\nGoogle Shared Drives\nBL-SPEA-projectZ\nSanctions\nFailure to comply with Indiana University information technology policies may result in sanctions relating to the individual's use of information technology resources (such as suspension or termination of access, or removal of online material); the individual's employment (up to and including immediate termination of employment in accordance with applicable university policy); the individual's studies within the university (such as student discipline in accordance with applicable university policy); civil or criminal liability; or any combination of these.\nAssent\nAlthough UITS applies technical controls to the\nMicrosoft Storage\nand\nGoogle Storage\nenvironments, you are responsible for the data you access and share in this environment. As a Microsoft or Google storage owner, you have additional responsibilities to maintain access and permissions in accordance with the policies and regulations described above. By accepting these terms, you agree to follow these rules in your interactions with the service and accept the additional responsibilities as the storage owner. If you choose not to accept these standards of behavior, you could be denied access to the service.\nOn the service request form, you'll be asked to agree to the following statement by checking the box and typing your initials:\nI understand as storage owner (\nMicrosoft Teams\nowner and/or\nGoogle Shared Drives\nmanager) of these services, I must agree to follow these usage standards as highlighted in\nService agreement for Microsoft and Google storage owners\n.\nLog requests\nTo request logs or activity reports from this system, contact the\nSupport Center\n.\nIf the request for information involves someone other than the requester, or if the log information will be used in support or defense of an investigation, the request must be sent to the\nUniversity Information Security Office\n(UISO) via\nit-incident@iu.edu\n. UISO staff will then determine the context of the request, as well as the authorization required; for more, see\nPrivacy of Electronic Information and Information Technology Resources (IT-07)\n.	KB0025327	KB0025327 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Usage responsibilities Sanctions Assent Log requests Overview Microsoft Teams at IU and Google at IU Shared Drives are designed to provide you with better and more secure methods of fostering collaboration. As a Microsoft or Google storage owner (Microsoft Teams owner and/or Google Shared Drives manager), you must agre	2.4897		2026-01-31 17:09:57.517485	2026-01-31 17:09:57.517485	0	f9909a933b831e102b40571864e45a04
1527	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023133	About Two-Step Login (Duo) at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nFirst steps\nUse\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\nto log in\nDuo Verified Push\nDuo Universal Prompt\nOverview\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\nprovides an additional layer of security when you log in at IU. Also known as two-factor\nauthentication\n, this helps protect\nsensitive data\nand guard against increasingly sophisticated email and online scams (for example,\nphishing attacks\n) that can leave you vulnerable to\nidentity theft\n. IU has partnered with Duo to provide this service.\nImportant:\nLogins for all IU students, faculty, staff, retirees, affiliates, and those with Academic (ACNP) status require\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n, including group or departmental accounts (but not IU Guest accounts).\nAs of March 15, 2024, students, faculty, staff, and affiliates use Duo Verified Push.  Retirees, group accounts, and anyone with an exception will not need to use Duo Verified Push.  See\nbelow\nfor details.\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\ndoes not protect your\npassphrase\nfrom being hacked, phished, or otherwise compromised. Think of it as a safety net that protects your accounts on IU services if your passphrase is compromised.\nEven though\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\ncan help protect your account, you should still choose a strong passphrase and\ntake steps to protect it\n.  If there is reason to believe your passphrase has been compromised, UITS will scramble it as a precaution.\nFirst steps\nBefore you can log in, you'll need to enroll a device.  UITS recommends using the Duo Mobile app on a smartphone or tablet, but if you don't have such a device, devices with Touch ID, and\nUSB security keys\nare also supported.\nSingle-button hardware tokens\nare also available.\nFor step-by-step instructions for enrolling all supported types of devices, see\nGet started with Two-Step Login (Duo) at IU\n.\nEnroll multiple devices\nto ensure alternative methods of two-step login, in case you are separated from your main device. For more, see\nIf your Two-Step Login (Duo) device is not with you, or is lost or stolen\n.\nUse\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\nto log in\nWith\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n, you log in by:\nEntering your username and passphrase.\nConfirming your login with an enrolled device.\nNote:\nYou can reduce the number of times you need to complete a login with Duo if you're logging in on a device that isn't shared with others.  You may see any of the following:\nA\nRemember me\ncheckbox on the initial Duo login screen\nYes, trust browser\nYes, this is my device\nChoosing any of these will temporarily suspend Duo prompts from the browser you're using.\nLog in with Duo Verified Push\nFor applications that use the\nuniversal prompt\n, users who are part of the Duo Verified Push group will receive a prompt to log in using Duo Verified Push, which can reduce the chance that a push sent by an unauthorized person will accidentally be approved. To see how this works, visit this\ninteractive demonstration to try Duo Verified Push\n, or see\nsample screenshots\nbelow.\nWith Duo Verified Push, to log in:\nEnter your username and passphrase.\nEnter a code shown on the screen of the device you are logging in from into the Duo Mobile app on your authentication device (phone, tablet, or compatible smartwatch).\nNote:\nIf you are logging in with an Apple Watch, you may enter numbers by drawing them on the face of the watch, or you can press the prompt button and speak the numbers aloud.\nTechnical requirements for Duo Verified Push\nTo log in with Duo Verified Push, you need the Duo Mobile app on your device. To download the app, you need to be running:\nAndroid 10 or later; for details, see\nWhich versions of Android does Duo Mobile support?\niOS 14 or later; for details, see\nWhich versions of iOS does Duo Mobile support?\nIf you don't have a device that meets those requirements, you can select\nOther options\nat the bottom of the prompt and log in with a\nsecurity key\n,\nhardware token\n,\nDuo Mobile passcode\n, or Touch ID.  If you currently have a special exception to use text messages or phone calls to log into Duo, you can continue using those methods instead.\nAccessibility concerns\nSome members of the initial group using this feature have found accessibility issues with Duo Verified Push.  If you have concerns about accessibility, contact the\nSupport Center\nor your\nlocal UITS support person\nto request assistance or an exception, if necessary.\nSample login\nThe following screenshot shows a sample of what you might see in the system you're logging into; the actual Duo Verified Push prompts will have a different three-digit code each time you log in.  The second image below shows what you would see on a smartphone; you'll type the three-digit code into the three boxes.\nDuo Universal Prompt\nMost applications at IU use the Duo Universal Prompt for anyone who is not required to use Duo Verified Push (that is, retirees, group accounts, and anyone with an exception).  Duo Universal Prompt automatically sends a push as the default login option, and displays the following screen:	KB0023133	KB0023133 v1.0	Table of contents Overview First steps Use Two-Step Login (Duo) to log in Duo Verified Push Duo Universal Prompt Overview Two-Step Login (Duo) provides an additional layer of security when you log in at IU. Also known as two-factor authentication, this helps protect sensitive data and guard against increasingly sophisticated email and online sca	2.4648		2026-01-31 14:33:03.11173	2026-01-31 17:10:04.381316	0	e3bf24b7476456583d373803846d4339
11454	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=12d920684795e6502fc6a55b416d434b	Use automated tools to review accessibility	Table of contents\nOverview\nRecommended tools\nTypes of content issues that automated tools can find\nGet help\nOverview\nImportant:\nThis information is intended for web developers, providing a holistic process for performing an initial pass of evaluating your website for accessibility. This is not a substitute for performing a complete\nWeb Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1\nAA site evaluation, but following this full process will capture most of the WCAG 2.1 AA Guidelines more efficiently.\nIt is impossible to meet Indiana University's digital accessibility standard and related accessibility policies using only automated tools.\nAutomated tools can be useful for identifying certain types of web accessibility issues. There are many tools you can use, each with different strengths and weaknesses. Be aware that automated tools can find only about 30% of accessibility problems; the rest must be located using manual methods.\nAutomated tools can detect programmatic accessibility issues based on the code. For this reason, the tools will produce more accurate results if the code is valid. If you have not already validated your code, running the\nHTML Nu\nchecker before using automated accessibility tools will improve the quality of the results.\nThere are many automated tools available for use.\nRecommended tools\naXe\naXe\nis an accessibility rules library from\nDeque\nthat is also available as a browser extension in Chrome or Firefox. The aXe library will only flag as errors the things that can be reliably programmatically checked. aXe strives to report zero false positives. The aXe extension is run on each web page individually, and exists in the browser's Developer tools.\nAdd aXe to Chrome\nAdd aXe to Firefox\nAdd aXe to Edge\naXe is a very useful tool for developers.\nAccessibility Insights for Web\nAccessibility Insights for Web\nfrom Microsoft is available as a browser extension in Chrome or Microsoft Edge. Powered by Deque Systems' aXe library, Accessibility Insights for Web offers a quick FastPass check to get you started and a full Assessment that includes step-by-step instructions for manually performing tests. Accessibility Insights for Web is run on each web page individually.\nAdd Accessibility Insights for Web in Chrome\nAdd Accessibility Insights for Web in Microsoft Edge\nA Windows application version,\nAccessibility Insights for Windows\n, is available to test Windows applications.\nAccessibility Insights is useful for developers.\nWAVE\nWAVE\n, from\nWebAIM\n, is available as either a browser extension or as a web service, which requires web pages to be public, rather than behind authentication. WAVE will flag errors which can be programmatically checked, and will also flag issues for further manual review. WAVE is run on each web page individually.\nAdd WAVE in Chrome\nAdd WAVE in Firefox\nWAVE web service\nWAVE assesses the whole page after it has been rendered in the browser, which means that it will look at objects that are visually on the screen and objects that are hidden.\nWAVE is a good tool for checking color contrast and headings automatically. Because WAVE checks the hidden content, some headings that are normally hidden may be shown.\nWAVE is easy for anyone to use.\nFor tips on using this tool, see\nWAVE Help\n.\nARC Toolkit\nARC Toolkit\n, available as a browser extension in Chrome, will flag errors that can be programmatically checked, and will also flag potential errors for further manual review. ARC Toolkit is run on each web page individually and is accessed in the Chrome Developer tools.\nAdd ARC Toolkit in Chrome\nARC Toolkit is useful for developers.\nARC Toolkit also includes easy tests for focus order and new WCAG 2.1 success criteria Reflow and Text spacing as well as buttons to validate the code through the W3C Nu HTML validator.\nAInspector\nAInspector is a Firefox browser extension developed by the\nUniversity of Illinois Division of Disability Resources & Educational Services\n. AInspector is run on each web page individually.\nAdd AInspector in Firefox\nAInspector is most useful for developers. It will go deeper into explaining the issues and label a lot more manual checks than any other tool.\nHelpful user guides are available in the\nAInspector Sidebar documentation\n.\nSiteimprove\nSiteimprove\nis a cloud-based web governance tool enabling IU's web professionals to continuously and efficiently improve their unit's web presence. Siteimprove will scan, analyze, and suggest ways to grow engagement; boost search engine rankings; and improve the accessibility, performance, and usability of your website.\nIU units can learn more at\nAbout the Siteimprove service at IU\n.\nBookmarklets\nMany bookmarklets are available that will highlight roles, states, and properties of various elements on a web page. Bookmarklets are JavaScript programs stored as bookmarks in your browser. They are easy to use: select the bookmark and the JavaScript program will run on the current page.\nTo install a bookmarklet, open one of the bookmarklet resource links below. Choose the bookmarklet you want to install. Drag and drop the bookmarklet to your bookmarks bar.\nIn some browsers, you may need to manually add the bookmarklet. Copy the link address of the bookmarklet. Open the bookmark manager in your browser. Add a new bookmark. Enter a name for the bookmark and paste the link address as the URL.\nThe\nJavascript Bookmarklets for Accessibility Testing\nfrom Paul J. Adam are easy to use and highlight many important features of a web page.\nDisability Resources and Educational Services (DRES) at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign and Pixo have also created easy to use,\nopen source accessibility bookmarklets\n.\nA11y audit bookmarklets\nincludes several useful tools, such as Are Ya Hidden? and List images.\nBookmarklets are useful for developers and designers.\nTypes of content issues that automated tools can find\nLinks\nMost tools will identify common link issues such as:\nLinks with no link text\nLinks with the same link text but different\nhref\nattributes\nLinks with different link text but the same\nhref\nattributes\nTools may or may not identify:\nAnchors with missing\nhref\nattributes\nLinks with the wrong\nhref\nattribute\nLinks with invalid\nhref\nattributes\nSuspicious link text such as "click here", "here", "more", "more details", or "click the red button"\nLinks to PDFs and other file types\nThat links are uniquely styled to differentiate from page text\nHeadings\nTools usually identify the programmatic\nheading structure\nof a web page. Some tools, such as WAVE, will present the headings as an outline of the page. Tools will catch:\nSkipped heading levels\nMissing\nh1\nheadings\nHeadings of the same level with the same text\nHeadings with no text content\nCompare the HTML headings to the visual headings:\nVisual headings should match HTML headings.\nThings that appear to be headings should be HTML headings.\nThings that are HTML headings should appear to be headings.\nHeadings should follow a hierarchical order, creating an outline for the page.\nHeadings should not skip heading levels.\nThe headings should describe the topic of the section.\nImages\nTools will identify:\nImages missing\nalt\nattributes\nImages where the alt text is similar to nearby text\nInstances where multiple images have the same alt text\nA manual check is still necessary to verify that the alt text for each image is appropriate. For a more in-depth discussion of alternative text, see\nAlternative text for images\n.\nTables\nAutomated tools will find the most common table issues:\nData tables need accessible names. An accessible name may not be visible onscreen but is used by assistive technology to identify the table.\nData tables should have header rows and/or columns.\nLayout tables should have the ARIA attribute\nrole="presentation"\n.\nTables should not have empty rows, columns, or cells.\nLandmarks\nTools are useful for identifying\nlandmarks\nand checking that the landmarks follow landmark rules:\nAll content should be contained in a landmark, including skip-links.\nEvery web page should have at least\nmain\nand\nnavigation\nlandmarks.\nEach page should have only one\nmain\n,\nbanner\n, and\ncontentinfo\nlandmark.\nbanner\n,\nmain\n,\ncontentinfo\n, and\ncomplementary\nlandmarks must be top-level, not nested in other landmarks.\nLandmarks of the same type, such as two\nnavigation\nlandmarks, must have unique names so they may be easily differentiated from each other.\nARIA\nAutomated tools will help identify common problems with\nAccessible Rich Internet Applications (ARIA)\n. Many tools will identify:\nIncorrect ARIA roles\nIncorrect ARIA labeling\nInvalid ARIA properties\nInvalid ARIA attributes\nInvalid ARIA values\nForms\nAutomated tools excel at checking certain aspects of forms. They will identify if:\nForm elements are correctly labeled\nForm labels are unique\nRadio buttons are grouped in fieldsets\nFieldsets have legends\nID attributes are unique\nSome automated tools may also verify that the\naccessible name\nmatches or starts with the visible label.\nBeyond the programmatic checks that automated tools carry out, forms will always require additional manual checking with keyboard and screen readers, and to explore their error handling.\nPage language\nAutomated tools will check if the human language used on the page (for example, English) has been set using the\nlang\nattribute on the\n<html>\nelement. Automated tools are not good at finding language changes within a page. If the language used on the page changes, you will need to manually check that the\nlang\nattribute has been used correctly.\nLanguage of Page: Understanding Success Criterion 3.1.1\nLanguage of Parts: Understanding Success Criterion 3.1.2\nNext step:\nUse a screen reader to evaluate a website\nPrevious step:\nCheck keyboard access and visual focus on web pages\nReturn to index:\nPerform an accessibility review on your website\nGet help\nFor questions or consultations, email the Enterprise Experience\naccessibility team\n.	KB0023968	KB0023968 v3.0	Table of contents Overview Recommended tools Types of content issues that automated tools can find Get help Overview Automated tools can be useful for identifying certain types of web accessibility issues. There are many tools you can use, each with different strengths and weaknesses. Be aware that automated tools can find only about 30% of acce	2.3673		2026-01-31 17:10:08.335656	2026-01-31 17:10:08.335656	0	12d920684795e6502fc6a55b416d434b
11460	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1fb48e4747315a909286dfbd436d43ff	Check zoom and magnification for web accessibility	Table of contents\nOverview\nFont size\nZooming\nText spacing\nGet help\nOverview\nImportant:\nThis information is intended for web developers, providing a holistic process for performing an initial pass of evaluating your website for accessibility. This is not a substitute for performing a complete\nWeb Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1\nAA site evaluation, but following this full process will capture most of the WCAG 2.1 AA Guidelines more efficiently.\nIt is impossible to meet Indiana University's digital accessibility standard and related accessibility policies using only automated tools.\nPeople with low vision may use any of several methods to enlarge content. They may use a screen magnifier such as Windows Magnifier or Mac Zoom, increase the default font size in their browser, zoom their browser, or change the default text spacing on a website. Different strategies for enlarging content behave differently, and you need to verify that important content is not lost if any of them are being used.\nThree methods of enlarging content need to be checked because they can affect the website design: font size, zooming, and text spacing.\nFont size\nMost browsers have a way to change the default minimum font size. How font size changes will differ depending on the browser. Checking font size in more than one browser is recommended.\nChange the font size:\nChrome:\nOpen\nSettings\nor\nPreferences\nand change the font size to\nVery large\nor\nVery small\n.\nFirefox:\nOpen\nPreferences\n, select\nContent\nin the side menu, and then in the "Fonts & Colors" section, select\nAdvanced\n. In the pop-up box, change the "Minimum font size" to at least\n24 point\n. Select\nOK\n.\nSafari:\nOpen\nPreferences\n. In the pop-up box, select\nAdvanced\n. Check\nNever use font sizes smaller than\n, and in the drop-down box, select\n24 point\n.\nEdge:\nOpen\nSettings\n, and then select\nAppearance\n. Under "Fonts", change the font size to\nVery large\n.\nReload the page.\nLook for any text that does not change size and any content that is obscured, lost, hidden, or otherwise problematic.\nCheck that all functionality is available. Can each control be reached and activated with a keyboard?\nChange back to your preferred settings.\nZooming\nZooming changes the size of all content proportionally, including images. Depending on screen size, zooming may trigger the "mobile" or small screen view of the web page. Changing zoom in more than one browser is recommended.\nZoom the browser to at least 200%:\nMac:\nCommand\nplus\n+\nto increase zoom\nWindows:\nCtrl\nplus\n+\n(or mouse wheel up) to increase zoom\nReload the page.\nLook for any content that is obscured, lost, hidden, or otherwise problematic.\nCheck that all functionality is available. Can each control be reached and activated with a keyboard?\nSet your browser width to 1280 px and zoom to 400%, and then repeat steps 2 and 3.\nReset the browser zoom to 100% or preferred settings:\nMac:\nCommand\nplus\n0\nto reset to 100%, or\nCommand\nplus\n-\nto reduce the zoom\nWindows:\nCtrl\nplus\n0\nto reset to 100%, or\nCtrl\nplus\n-\n(or mouse wheel down) to reduce the zoom\nView a one minute video demonstration on\ncontent resizing using browser zoom\n.\nText spacing\nText spacing includes line height, letter spacing, word spacing, and paragraph spacing.\nThe website should have no loss of content or functionality if changed to any of the following:\nLine height to at least 1.5 times the font size\nSpacing following paragraphs to at least 2 times the font size\nLetter spacing to at least 0.12 times the font size\nWord spacing to at least 0.16 times the font size\nThe easiest and quickest way to check text spacing is by using the\ntext spacing bookmarklet\nin any browser:\nActivate the bookmarklet.\nLook for any text that does not adapt to the new settings and any content that is obscured, lost, hidden, or otherwise problematic.\nCheck that all functionality is available. Can each control be reached and activated with a keyboard?\nRefresh the page to deactivate the bookmarklet.\nNext step:\nInspect website code for accessibility\nPrevious step:\nCheck color contrast on a website\nReturn to index:\nPerform an accessibility review on your website\nGet help\nFor questions or consultations, email the Enterprise Experience\naccessibility team\n.	KB0026079	KB0026079 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Font size Zooming Text spacing Get help Overview People with low vision may use any of several methods to enlarge content. They may use a screen magnifier such as Windows Magnifier or Mac Zoom, increase the default font size in their browser, zoom their browser, or change the default text spacing on a website. Differen	2.3673		2026-01-31 17:10:12.620522	2026-01-31 17:10:12.620522	0	1fb48e4747315a909286dfbd436d43ff
11499	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=760bfc9f1b70d25416c02fc4bd4bcb9b	About Slack for UITS	Notes:\nUITS staff, to view additional information below, log into both\nIU Login\nand\nthe Knowledge Base (see\nLog into the IU Knowledge Base (KB)\n).\nAutomatic group management and permissioning (for instance, by ADS group) is not currently available, but is being actively pursued and should be available in the future.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nEtiquette guidelines\nAvailability\nAccess for non-UITS staff\nAccess for group accounts\nInstall and configure Slack for UITS\nDesktop app\nIn your browser\nMobile apps\nNotification settings\nDisplay settings\nComplete your profile and set your status\nCreate and join channels in Slack for UITS\nChannel names\nShared channel creation\nGet help\nUITS staff:\nAccess for UITS employees\nMigrate existing Slack instances into Slack for UITS\nMigration options\nRequest a migration\nThird-party integrations in Slack for UITS\nIntegrations available in Slack for UITS\nRequest an integration\nOverview\nSlack for\nUITS\nincludes a number of channels for work-related discussions and casual conversation. There is no fee to UITS teams to join Slack for UITS, and teams who migrate from their existing Slack instances into Slack for UITS may be eligible for an ERC credit.\nTo access Slack for UITS, go to\niu-uits.slack.com\n. For additional installation and configuration instructions, see\nInstall and configure Slack for UITS\n.\nFor help or to submit requests relevant to Slack for UITS, join\n#slack-requests\n. The following are examples of questions appropriate to submit to the\n#slack-requests\nchannel:\nUITS staff:\nWorkspace migration\nApp and integration additions\nPublic channel creation\nShared channel creation\nAccess for non-UITS staff\nAdditions to the exceptions group (in other words, requests to add new Slack users\nwho are eligible for access\nbut do not currently have accounts)\nRequests to share data classified beyond University-Internal in Slack\nImportant:\nSlack for UITS is approved for\nUniversity-Internal data\nin channels that contain only UITS employees. If you wish to share\nRestricted data\nin Slack, contact the Slack admins in the\n#slack-requests\nchannel for further instructions.\nEtiquette guidelines\nBe professional:\nKeep your comments work appropriate, because everything you post will be publicly visible to all channel members.\nStay on topic. Posts should be relevant to their channel.\nUse work-appropriate profile photos and custom emojis.\nBe civil. Slack is a great platform for offering praise but a terrible platform for offering criticism.\nBefore you create a channel, consider the institutional data sensitivity classification needs of that channel, and whether Slack is an appropriate platform. In addition, consider the data classification of any information you post in Slack.\nBe respectful:\nBe welcoming. Many members are new to Slack and will make mistakes, and you can help them learn.\nBe considerate. Slack encourages debate and discussion, but you should avoid controversial issues, politics, religion, and gossip. Avoid spamming channels and members. If a member or group is unresponsive, consider sending email instead.\nKnow the difference between\n@here\n,\n@channel\n, and mentions (in other words,\n@username\n):\n@here\nsends a notification to all active channel members. This is best used for non-urgent announcements.\n@channel\nsends a notification and email to all channel members. This is best used for emergencies or channel-wide announcements.\n@username\nsends a notification to the specified user. This is best used for getting someone's attention.\nWhen creating a new channel, consider who actually needs membership. Inviting more people than necessary leads to channel clutter and spam.\nAvailability\nUITS staff:\nAccess for UITS employees\nAll UITS employees, including hourly/temporary staff, partial UITS staff with split reporting lines, and full-time consultants with IU Affiliate accounts may access Slack for UITS via any means noted in the\nInstall\nsection below. If you believe you should have access but do not, contact your supervisor; they can post in the\n#slack-requests\nchannel to request access for you.\nFormer employees aren't yet automatically removed from the UITS Slack instance (that will soon be the case). For now, post such requests in the\n#slack-requests\nchannel.\nAccess for non-UITS staff\nIndividuals and groups that are not part of UITS or Indiana University can share channels with Slack for UITS. To do so, interested groups need pre-existing access to a paid Slack instance. For other requirements, see\nSlack Connect guide: work with external organizations\n. To request the creation of a shared channel in Slack for UITS, post in the\n#slack-requests\nchannel. If you don't have access to Slack for UITS, an existing Slack for UITS member must submit the request on your behalf.\nAccess for group accounts\nBy default, only personal accounts are granted access to Slack for UITS. Each active account has a fee, so group accounts (or "service accounts") are authorized for access only when there is a clear need or institutional benefit. If you wish to request access to Slack for UITS on behalf of a group or service account, post your request in the\n#slack-requests\nchannel.\nInstall and configure Slack for UITS\nYou can access Slack via a web browser or a number of applications. When prompted, enter\niu-uits\nfor the workspace, and then log in with your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nDesktop app\nSlack for Windows\nSlack for Mac\nSlack for Linux\nIn your browser\nTo access Slack for UITS in your browser, navigate to\nhttps://iu-uits.slack.com\n.\nMobile apps\nSlack for Android\nSlack for iOS\nNotification settings\nBy default, Slack applications will send you notifications when you receive a direct message, when someone mentions you, when someone notifies a channel you're in, and more. You may wish to adjust your settings to minimize the notifications you receive to only those you consider priority. For more about desktop notifications, see\nConfigure your Slack notifications\nPay special attention to the "Channel-specific & group DM notifications" section\nMobile notifications\n.\nUITS recommends that you consider the following notification options:\nMute channels and direct messages\nSet up keyword notifications\nDisplay settings\nSlack provides a number of options for adjusting your display settings. For help, see:\nChange your Slack theme\nAdjust your sidebar preferences\nUse dark mode in Slack\nChange how messages are displayed\nComplete your profile and set your status\nUpdate your profile in Slack to let your colleagues know who you are and when you're available. For instructions, see:\nEdit your profile\nUpload a profile photo\nSet your Slack status and availability\nCreate and join channels in Slack for UITS\nMembers of Slack for UITS may create private channels as needed. Before you create a private channel, consider visiting and searching\n#slack-news\nto determine whether a similar public channel already exists.\nIf you would like to request the creation of a public channel, post your request in\n#slack-requests\n. Include the preferred channel name using one of the prefixes\nbelow\n, as well as the channel's purpose. A Slack for UITS admin will review your request and, if there is no other channel with that name or purpose, create the public channel.\nChannel names\nWhen you request a new public channel, use one of the prefixes described below in your proposed channel name. If you are creating a new private channel, UITS recommends using one of the prefixes below so that like channels are kept together when viewed alphabetically, but this is not required.\nPrefix\nPurpose\n#shared-\nFor channels that are shared with other workspaces, or with non-UITS Slack members\n#topic-\nFor channels dedicated to discussion of a topic, typically broadly applicable across the department, such as diversity and inclusion or professional development\n#offtopic-\nFor channels dedicated to discussion of a non-work topic\n#club-\nFor channels related to formal or informal common-interest groups, such as the Living Green Team (formal group) or UITS Frisbee (informal club)\n#proj-\nFor channels related to a specific project or effort. This prefix is a good option when members from different teams need a place to collaborate.\n#service-\nFor channels related to a specific IT service. This prefix is a good way to scope a channel for discussion among adopters, service owners, and support.\n#team-\nFor collaboration within a team (in other words, a group with a common manager). Many teams will have a private "general" channel using this convention.\n#unit-\nFor discussion within a unit (in other words, a group with a common director)\n#reg-\nFor discussion within a regional campus\n#div-\nFor discussion within a division (in other words, a group with a common AVP)\nFor more about Slack channels, see:\nWhat is a channel?\nCreate a channel\nJoin a channel\nShared channel creation\nChannels may be shared to Slack for UITS from existing paid workspaces. For requirements, see\nSlack Connect guide: work with external organizations\n. To request the creation of a shared channel in Slack for UITS, post in the\n#slack-requests\nchannel.\nUITS staff:\nMigrate existing Slack instances into Slack for UITS\nNote:\nBefore you export a Slack workspace, all the email addresses of all users in that workspace who are part of UITS should be set to\nusername\n@iu.edu\n. Non-UITS Slack members' email addresses do not need to be changed, as they will need to access Slack for UITS via bridged channels. For more, see\nAccess for non-UITS staff\n.\nMigration options\nTo initiate a migration, you'll first need to export the contents of your existing Slack workspace.\nPublic channels only\nAny workspace owner can perform a Standard Export, which includes all content from public channels only. For more, see\nUse Standard Export\n.\nPublic channels, private channels, and direct (private) messages\nIf you wish to migrate the contents of private channels and/or direct messages, you'll need to perform a Corporate Export. This option is only available if your workspace is on the Slack Plus plan, and is made available only by request from a workspace owner to Slack support. For more, see\nUse Corporate Export\n.\nRequest a migration\nTo request a Slack workspace migration, post your request in the\n#slack-requests\nchannel in Slack for UITS.\nUITS staff:\nThird-party integrations in Slack for UITS\nIntegrations available in Slack for UITS\nThe following third-party integrations are currently available in Slack for UITS:\nGiphy\nGitHub Enterprise Server\nGoogle Drive\nIFTTT\nincoming-webhook\nJira-Integration+\nMicrosoft Entra ID Provisioning\nRSS\nSimple Poll\nZoom\nRequest an integration\nTo request an integration, post your request in\n#slack-requests\n. The Slack for UITS admins will review your request and reply to you as soon as possible.\nGet help\nIf you wish to report harassment or abuse that has occurred in Slack, email UITS HR at\nuitshr@iu.edu\n.\nTo report a security or privacy violation that has occurred in Slack, follow IU guidelines to\nReport an IT incident\n.\nFor general questions about Slack, visit the\nSlack Help Center\n, or see the\n#slack-discussion\nchannel. This channel is intended for questions don't require intervention by admins, such as how to use Slack, how to customize your profile and settings, how to manage notifications, and how to get the most value out of the tool. Be sure to search the channel history before asking a question, as someone may have already answered it.\nIf your issue requires assistance from Slack admins, post in\n#slack-requests\n. Members of the Slack Team (volunteers who help maintain Slack for UITS) will respond to your request as soon as possible.	KB0026274	KB0026274 v2.0	Notes: UITS staff, to view additional information below, log into both IU Login and the Knowledge Base (see Log into the IU Knowledge Base (KB)). Automatic group management and permissioning (for instance, by ADS group) is not currently available, but is being actively pursued and should be available in the future. Table of contents Overview Eti	2.0663		2026-01-31 17:11:06.182625	2026-01-31 17:11:06.182625	0	760bfc9f1b70d25416c02fc4bd4bcb9b
11501	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f5b01d2f4756f6982fc6a55b416d432c	Use the ServiceNow IU Knowledge Base	Table of contents\nOverview\nKnown issues\nWork with the KM team\nQuestions and comments\nThe KB interface\nLogging in and internal content\nSee who has access to an article (audiences)\nSearch\nURLs\nLegacy KB URLs\nAlways use article permalinks\nArchived content\nMore\nGoogle search\nDark mode\nInformation for the IU community\nImportant:\nOverview\nAs of September 26, 2024, the IU Knowledge Base is using the ServiceNow Knowledge platform.\nKnown issues\nFor details about current known issues, see\nKnown issues with the Knowledge Base\n.\nWork with the KM team\nIf your group owns content in the Knowledge Base, you'll still work with the UITS Knowledge Management (KM) team to maintain your documentation; see\nContribute to the IU Knowledge Base\n.\nTools that were part of the legacy system are retired. Contact the KM team with any questions about managing your content.\nQuestions and comments\nIf you have questions or comments, contact the KM team at\nkb@iu.edu\n.\nThe KB interface\nLogging in and internal content\nAlways log into the KB to see all content available to you!\nSelecting\nLog in to find more articles\ninitiates an\nIU Login\nor passes your credentials if you are already logged in:\nOnce you're logged in, your name will appear in the upper right.\nMany pages contain information that doesn't appear unless you complete a login; this page, in fact, contains details for the IU community\nbelow\n.\nRestricting content to internal audiences\nis still supported. The legacy system used ADS security groups, while ServiceNow supports ACM groups.\nNote:\nFor some groups/teams, access to internal content may need to be updated. If you or someone on your team doesn't have appropriate access to internal content, email\nkb@iu.edu\nand the KM team will assist.\nIf you have an appropriate public ACM group ready for the system to use, this will speed the process.\nWhile viewing an article, related articles appear on the right on wider screens and drop below the article in a narrow view.\nSee who has access to an article (audiences)\nWhile viewing internal articles, the ServiceNow "Can Read" criteria (audience) is displayed, prepended by "km-aud" (for "Knowledge Management audience"). This article below is internal to the Tier 2 accounts team, for example. Public articles do not display "Can Read".\nSearch\nSearches will return both public and internal articles\nif you're logged in.\nIn search results, "Can Read" (audience), article numbers, last updated metric, and relevancy score are displayed, for example:\nServiceNow's search engine computes relevancy score based on frequency, sequence, and weight of the search terms in the article.\nIf you know an article number (in the form KBxxxxxxx), enter it in the search bar and it will be the first article listed in your search return.\nURLs\nLegacy KB URLs\nLegacy KB URLs no longer function. For more, see\nAbout redirecting URLs for Knowledge Base articles\n(requires IU Login).\nAlways use article permalinks\nWhen linking to articles in the IU Knowledge Base,\ndo not use the URL in an article's address bar\n, since each address bar URL is version-specific. If the article is revised after you copy that URL and you then visit it, you'll view that same older version of the article (not the latest version), and it will display with a selectable message at the top saying that an updated version of the article is available.\nEach KB article has a permalink, which will always take you to the article's latest version.\nAlways copy the permalink rather than the URL in your browser's address bar:\nArchived content\nArchived content is no longer available via KB search; however, the KM team has access to archived documents. If you have questions about a page that was archived or retired, email the KM team at\nkb@iu.edu\n.\nMore\nGoogle search\nThe process for Google to crawl the new KB has been set.\nDark mode\nControl dark mode through your browser's settings, rather than in ServiceNow.\nInformation for the IU community\nImportant:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nIU community:\nAbout KB articles for those working in ServiceNow\nThe article view for ServiceNow agents:\nWhile working with incidents in\nServiceNow\n, articles are displayed in the ServiceNow default view for agents:\nIn agent view,\narticles have different URLs than they do in the KB at kb.iu.edu\n.\nAdditionally, if you use the feature described below to "Attach" an article to an incident, the\nagent-view version-specific URL\nwill be listed in the "Additional comments" field by default. You'll want to change that to the article\npermalink URL\nfrom kb.iu.edu.\nInitially after the transition, articles did not indicate who can view them, but as of October 10, 2024, the "Can Read" criteria (audience) is listed at the top left of each article page.\nWhen viewing an article, related articles aren't listed.\nLinks to sites outside of the Knowledge Base will not load. To open a link to an external site, open the link in a new tab or window; see\nKnown issues with the IU Knowledge Base\n.\nImportant:\nThe project team plans to address the agent view, but in the meantime, strongly recommends that you always have a window open to kb.iu.edu while working in ServiceNow, and share the\nkb.iu.edu URLs\nby copying the permalink; avoid sharing agent view URLs.\nSee and share articles while working in ServiceNow:\nTo see potentially related KB articles while viewing a ticket, and share them with a\nclient\nin the ticket's "Additional comments" field:\nWhile viewing a ticket, select the\nRelated Search Results\nbutton, usually under the "Description" field.\nA list of KB articles, potentially related to the ticket and including\nboth public and internal\n, will appear below that button. (You may need to select\nKnowledge Articles\nfrom the drop-down menu.)\nTo view an article, select its title. Remember that this is the default agent view, described above.\nYou can share any article with the\nclient\nin the "Additional comments" field:\nImportant:\nIf you use this feature, be sure to follow these instructions.\nBefore proceeding, check the "Can Read" criteria (audience) any article you wish to share with the client, so you avoid sharing internal articles the\nclient\ncan't see. This criteria is displayed in articles both in the agent view and in the live KB.\nTo place a link to an article in the "Additional comments" field, while viewing the article list, select\nAttach\nfor the article you'd like to share. You may select to attach more than one article.\nIn the refreshed screen, scroll down to the "Additional comments" field, where you'll see a string of code. This URL is the agent-view, version-specific URL for the article you've chosen.\nChange the article URL to the the article's permalink URL from kb.iu.edu.\nThen select\nPost\n.\nPaste article links in the email tool:\nIn the email tool, enter the text you want hyperlinked.\nCopy the KB article URL.\nIn the email tool, select the text you want hyperlinked, and then choose the\nInsert/edit link\nicon.\nIn the "URL" box, paste in the URL, and then select\nSave\n.	KB0025901	KB0025901 v20.0	Table of contents Overview Known issues Work with the KM team Questions and comments The KB interface Logging in and internal content See who has access to an article (audiences) Search URLs Legacy KB URLs Always use article permalinks Archived content More Google search Dark mode Information for the IU community Important: Overview As of Septem	1.6687		2026-01-31 17:11:10.204683	2026-01-31 17:11:10.204683	0	f5b01d2f4756f6982fc6a55b416d432c
11463	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3e407cb347a456583d373803846d43e4	Perform an accessibility review on your website	Important:\nThis information is intended for web developers, providing a holistic process for performing an initial pass of evaluating your website for accessibility. This is not a substitute for performing a complete\nWeb Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1\nAA site evaluation, but following this full process will capture most of the WCAG 2.1 AA Guidelines more efficiently.\nIt is impossible to meet Indiana University's digital accessibility standard and related accessibility policies using only automated tools.\nThis review process consists of seven stages, which should be followed in order:\nVisually inspect web pages for accessibility\nCheck keyboard access and visual focus on web pages\nUse automated tools to review accessibility\nUse a screen reader to evaluate a website\nCheck color contrast on a website\nCheck zoom and magnification for web accessibility\nInspect website code for accessibility\nFor questions or consultations, email the Enterprise Experience\naccessibility team\n.	KB0026158	KB0026158 v1.0	This review process consists of seven stages, which should be followed in order: Visually inspect web pages for accessibility Check keyboard access and visual focus on web pages Use automated tools to review accessibility Use a screen reader to evaluate a website Check color contrast on a website Check zoom and magnification for web accessibilit	2.3673		2026-01-31 17:10:16.824726	2026-01-31 17:10:16.824726	0	3e407cb347a456583d373803846d43e4
11467	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=8bdbbe1d47c5be5c2fc6a55b416d4374	Check keyboard access and visual focus on web pages	Table of contents\nOverview\nCheck keyboard navigation and visual focus\nGuidelines relevant to keyboard accessibility\nTools\nSpecific things to pay attention to\nSkip links\nFocus indicators\nVideos\nCarousels and slide shows\nAccordions\nForms\nLinks and buttons\nPDFs and other documents\nWidgets\nGet help\nOverview\nImportant:\nThis information is intended for web developers, providing a holistic process for performing an initial pass of evaluating your website for accessibility. This is not a substitute for performing a complete\nWeb Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1\nAA site evaluation, but following this full process will capture most of the WCAG 2.1 AA Guidelines more efficiently.\nIt is impossible to meet Indiana University's digital accessibility standard and related accessibility policies using only automated tools.\nSome people use a keyboard or keyboard emulator to navigate websites. Many assistive technologies rely on keyboard-only navigation. All interactive elements, such as links, buttons, and form fields, need to be usable with only the keyboard and should clearly indicate when they can be activated with the keyboard.\nFor a basic introduction to keyboard access and visual focus, see\nAbout keyboard access and visual focus\n.\nCheck keyboard navigation and visual focus\nMake sure that all interactive content is operable using only a keyboard and follows a logical order. Pressing the\nTab\nkey repeatedly should move you smoothly through an expected order, based on the onscreen content. At the same time, make sure you can tell where you are on the page. There should be a clear visible indicator of which element has focus and will receive input.\nLook for the following:\nVisible skip links\nLogical tabbing order\nExcessive tabbing\nInteractive elements that are not accessible by keyboard\nNon-interactive elements that receive keyboard focus\nLost focus, where you can't tell which element has keyboard focus\nLost focus, where the item you are focused on is partially or entirely hidden by other content\nAny keyboard traps where a person can't navigate away using only the keyboard\nPlaces where new content appears when an item has keyboard focus\nFor a brief demonstration, view this\none minute YouTube video on testing keyboard support\n.\nNote:\nIf you are using a Mac, you may need to update your keyboard settings first.\nIn the System Preferences, select\nKeyboard\n. Depending on the MacOS version, you may need to select\nShortcuts\n, then either toggle "Keyboard Navigation" to\non\n, check\nUse keyboard navigation to move focus between controls\n, or select\nAll controls\nunder "Full Keyboard Access: In windows and dialogs, press Tab to move keyboard focus between:". Do not enable "Full Keyboard Access" in the accessibility keyboard settings (\nSystem Preferences\n>\nAccessibility\n>\nKeyboard\n).\nTo enable full keyboard accessibility in the Safari web browser, open the Safari\nPreferences\n. Select\nAdvanced\n. In "Accessibility:", check the box next to "Press Tab to highlight each item on a webpage".\nGuidelines relevant to keyboard accessibility\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.13: Content on Hover or Focus\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.1.1: Keyboard\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.1.2: No Keyboard Trap\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.4.1: Bypass Blocks\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.4.3: Focus Order\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.4.7: Focus Visible\nWCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.11: Focus Not Obscured (Minimum)\nWCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.12: Focus Not Obscured (Enhanced)\nTools\nIf you find that you can't tell which element has keyboard focus, you can use a focus enhancer tool to help discover problem areas. Focus enhancer tools will indicate where the focus is, helping to identify any focusable elements that lack a visible focus indicator.\nNerdeFocus Chrome Developer Tools Extension\nFocus Finder plugin for Firefox\nSpecific things to pay attention to\nCertain keyboard interactions beyond using\nTab\n,\nEnter\n, and the Spacebar are expected and should be tested. Native semantic HTML elements have built-in keyboard interactions. Complex widgets and modified HTML elements often require added keyboard support.\nThe following resources may be helpful in determining the expected keyboard interactions:\nWebAIM's guide to Keyboard Testing\nWAI-ARIA Authoring Practices: Developing a Keyboard Interface\nWAI-ARIA Authoring Practices: Design Patterns and Widgets\nSkip links\nSkip links should be the first interactive items you find when tabbing into a page. Skip links allow someone using a keyboard to skip repetitive content like navigation menus. At minimum, there should be a link to skip to the main content.\nVerify that skip links are present and visible. Also activate the skip links to verify the destination matches expectations. For example, "Skip to content" should move focus to the start of the main content.\nFor a brief demonstration, view this\nshort YouTube video on checking skip links\n.\nFocus indicators\nMake sure you can tell which item you are focused on.\nThe focus indicator should be vivid. Does it contrast with both the background and the item in focus? If the focus indicator is a change in color, such as inverting the colors on a button, can you tell that this change has happened?\nWhen you tab through the page, do you focus on any items that are hidden behind other content, such as sticky headers or chat widgets? Make sure to check both moving forward and backward through the page. At minimum, enough of the focus indicator and focused item should be visible that you can tell what item you are on and what it will do.\nVideos\nVideos should not play automatically.\nExamine the video controls. Make sure you can play and pause the video. Check that you can reach and interact with all the controls: volume, speed, closed captions, etc.\nCarousels and slide shows\nNote:\nIndiana University does not recommend use of slide carousels.\nCarousels should not rotate automatically.\nExamine the carousel controls. There should be previous and next buttons, navigation buttons that indicate which slide is currently displayed, and play or stop buttons. The selected item should have visible focus. The carousel should pause when it has keyboard focus or when the mouse hovers over it. Otherwise, there may not be enough time to read the content if the slides automatically advance.\nCarousels can cause keyboard traps in many ways, including if they advance faster than a person can navigate with a keyboard.\nResources for accessible carousels:\nShould I use a Carousel?\nWeb Accessibility Tutorials: Carousel Concepts\nAccordions\nMake sure accordion panels can be opened and closed with either the Spacebar or the\nEnter\nkey and that they are navigable using the arrow keys on the keyboard. Check that the accordion is not a keyboard trap.\nResources for accessible accordions:\nWAI-ARIA Authoring Practices--Accordions\nAccessible accordion example\nForms\nMake sure that all inputs, buttons, date selections by calendar, and other controls are operable by keyboard. Also check the\nerror messaging\n,\nrequired form fields\n, and non-form elements.\nError messages must clearly identify specifics about an error and the error location. There should be some suggestion on how to correct the error. For example, was a required field left blank? Or was an email address incorrectly formatted?\nFor a single error, focus should move immediately to the location of the error. For multiple errors, a list of links at the top of the form listing all error locations makes it easy to navigate directly to each error. Each field with an error should always include an inline error message.\nRequired form fields need to be clearly identified. If an asterisk (\n*\n) is used to identify required fields, the asterisk should be defined at the start of the form. Including\n(required)\nin the label instead of using an asterisk is clearer and more robust. Alternatively, a statement that all fields are required except those marked as "optional" is acceptable.\nPay close attention to submission and error handling if a mistake on the form would cause serious consequences, such as a form that involves a legal commitment, financial transaction, or modifies or deletes data that would be unrecoverable. Make sure there is a way to prevent errors:\nCan the submission be reversed, such as with an "undo" button?\nIs the data displayed for review and correction before submission?\nIs there a confirmation before the form is submitted?\nNon-form elements in forms can cause problems for assistive technologies. For example, screen readers enter forms mode in forms. When in forms mode, non-form elements, such as a paragraph of text, are ignored by the screen reader. Note any non-form elements in the form. These are often found in instructions, disclaimers and other fine print within forms.\nResources for accessible forms:\nCreating accessible forms\nG83: Providing text descriptions to identify required fields that were not completed\nG139: Creating a mechanism that allows users to jump to errors\nH90: Indicating required form controls using label or legend\nWAI-ARIA Authoring Practices: Design Patterns and Widgets\nError Suggestion: Understanding SC 3.3.3\nError Identification: Understanding SC 3.3.1\nError Prevention (Legal, Financial, Data): Understanding SC 3.3.4\nLinks and buttons\nLinks and buttons have different semantic functions. Make sure the correct element,\nlink or button\n, has been used.\nOpen links and verify that the link goes to the correct location. The\nlink text\nshould accurately describe the link destination.\nMake sure any buttons do what they are expected to do. The button text should accurately describe the button action.\nLinks that go to different places and buttons that perform different actions should have different text. If the same button or link appears on more than one page, it should be consistently identified and have the same name.\nCheck for any places where new content appears when you focus on an item, such as sub-navigation appearing when you focus on a navigation item. Does the new content disappear when you tab away?\nIf so, check that the following are true:\nDoes the new content go away when you press the\nESC\nkey without tabbing away?\nDoes the new content stay visible until tab away or press\nESC\n?\nResources for accessible links and buttons:\nChrome Developers A11ycasts: Just use a button (YouTube video)\nLinks vs. Buttons in Modern Web Applications\nContent on Hover or Focus: Understanding SC 1.4.13\nLink Purpose (In Context): Understanding SC 2.4.4\nHeadings and Labels: Understanding SC 2.4.6\nConsistent Identification: Understanding SC 3.2.4\nQuick accessibility test: Link text (YouTube video)\nQuick accessibility test: Visual presentation of links (YouTube video)\nPDFs and other documents\nMake note of any PDFs, Word files, and other documents discovered while checking links. Once all possible PDFs and other documents are found, they will need to be checked for accessibility.\nChecking a PDF:\nStart checking PDF accessibility with\nAdobe Acrobat DC's built-in accessibility check\n.\nChecking a Word document:\nUse the built-in accessibility checking tool on Windows computers.\nResources for accessible PDFs and other documents:\nPDF Accessibility\nUnderstanding Success Criterion 2.4.9: Link Purpose (Link Only)\nMicrosoft, Adobe, and other cheatsheets\nWidgets\nMake sure all widget controls can be operated with the keyboard. Types of widgets include maps, calendars, chats, social media, and video and audio players.\nResources for accessible widgets:\nGuidelines for accessible maps\nDeque University accessible datepicker example\nWhatSock accessible datepicker example\nNext step:\nUse automated tools to review accessibility\nPrevious step:\nVisually inspect web pages for accessibility\nReturn to index:\nPerform an accessibility review on your website\nGet help\nFor questions or consultations, email the Enterprise Experience\naccessibility team\n.	KB0026061	KB0026061 v4.0	Table of contents Overview Check keyboard navigation and visual focus Guidelines relevant to keyboard accessibility Tools Specific things to pay attention to Skip links Focus indicators Videos Carousels and slide shows Accordions Forms Links and buttons PDFs and other documents Widgets Get help Overview Some people use a keyboard or keyboard emu	2.3673		2026-01-31 17:10:20.299822	2026-01-31 17:10:20.299822	0	8bdbbe1d47c5be5c2fc6a55b416d4374
11471	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a14038b347a456583d373803846d43a3	Visually inspect web pages for accessibility	Table of contents\nOverview\nIndex the website\nPage titles\nThings to check now\nColor and sensory characteristics\nVideo and audio files\nImages and alt text\nResponsiveness\nNavigation and menus\nFlashing and moving content\nTimed content\nLanguage changes\nNo styles\nThings to check later\nGet help\nOverview\nImportant:\nThis information is intended for web developers, providing a holistic process for performing an initial pass of evaluating your website for accessibility. This is not a substitute for performing a complete\nWeb Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1\nAA site evaluation, but following this full process will capture most of the WCAG 2.1 AA Guidelines more efficiently.\nIt is impossible to meet Indiana University's digital accessibility standard and related accessibility policies using only automated tools.\nLooking at each web page before running any tools or doing any other checking can reveal many possible accessibility issues and identify areas that need a closer look.\nIndex the website\nStart by creating a list of the URLs and page titles of each page in the website. This index is helpful for identifying specific pages and tracking which pages have been evaluated.\nPage titles\nPay attention to page titles. The page titles help identify individual pages if you are indexing the website.\nThe page title should be unique within a website and indicate what the page is about. If a website has multiple pages with the same title, it can be confusing to identify an individual page, especially when using a screen reader or bookmarking the page.\nWell-written page titles should be unique and descriptive. The current subsection and website name can be included, with a preferred order of page title, section, then website name.\nLearn about\nchecking page titles [one minute video]\n.\nGuidelines relevant to page titles\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.4.2: Page Titled\nThings to check now\nRemember the goal of the visual check at this step is to survey the entire site, including the content structure, check some content immediately, and identify those areas which may require closer examination in the next steps. Learn more about\nchecking content structure\nin this short video.\nWe have listed the most relevant guidelines for each area below, but realize other guidelines in the WCAG 2.X AA standards may also apply.\nColor and sensory characteristics\nNote any use of color to convey meaning and instructions that rely on sensory characteristics. Sensory characteristics include shape, color, size, visual location, orientation, and sound.\nSome examples of using sensory characteristics include:\nA calendar where available times are shaded green and unavailable times are shaded red\nInstructions such as "click the button on the left to continue"\nA list with color-coded items\nGuidelines relevant to color and sensory characteristics\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.3.3: Sensory Characteristics\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.1: Use of color\nVideo and audio files\nNote any video or audio files that play automatically when the page loads. Video and audio should not play automatically.\nAutomatically playing video lasting more than five seconds and audio lasting more than three seconds should:\nHave the ability to be paused\nBe operable with a keyboard and have keyboard focus\nCheck any videos and audio files for the following:\nTranscripts: Documents including correctly sequenced text descriptions of time-based visual and auditory information\nAudio description: Narration added to the soundtrack to describe important visual details that cannot be understood from the main soundtrack alone\nClosed captions: Synchronized visual or text alternative for both speech and non-speech audio information\nThe closed captions, transcripts, and audio description must accurately reflect the video or audio content.\nFor a brief overview, view this\none minute video about captions\n.\nGuidelines relevant to video and audio files\nAudio-only must have a transcript:\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.2.1: Audio-only and Video-only (Prerecorded)\nPre-recorded video-only must have audio description or a text alternative that presents the information in the video:\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.2.1: Audio-only and Video-only (Prerecorded)\nPre-recorded videos with audio must have the following to meet IU ADA policy:\nAccurate closed captions:\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.2.2: Captions (Prerecorded)\nSynchronized audio description:\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.2.5: Audio Description (Prerecorded)\nNote:\nIf the video has accurate closed captions, synchronized audio description, and a detailed transcript, it meets AAA standards.\nLive video must have closed captions:\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.2.4: Captions (Live)\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.2: Audio Control\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.2.2: Pause, Stop, Hide\nFor more about video and audio, see\nMake video and audio accessible\n.\nImages and alt text\nNote images, icons, and any other graphics. While many automated tools will find images missing alt attributes, a manual check is necessary to verify that the alternative text is appropriate.\nPay attention to image links. The alt text for image links should indicate where the link will lead rather than describe the image; for example, "mail" instead of "envelope."\nPay special attention to any images of text, which should rarely be used. Images of text do not respond when resizing text and may become blurry when magnified. If the image of text is necessary, such as in a logo or as part of a picture such as a graph, screenshot, or diagram, the alt text should provide equivalent text.\nImage elements (\n<img>\n) should always have an alternative (alt) text attribute.\nIf the image is considered decorative or the description is provided in surrounding text, the alt should be null. Null alt is empty alt text, where alt equals double quotation mark followed by a double quotation mark, with no space between (\nalt=""\n).\nOtherwise, the alt text should describe the image.\nSVGs that are in an image element (\n<img src="filename.svg">\n) will have alt text. SVGs that are not in an image element (\n<svg>\n) do not have alt text but should use a\n<title>\nas the first child to provide a text alternative.\nTurn off images in your browser and reload the page. This will help you discover informative images that do not have properly descriptive alt text as well as any layout problems that may occur if images fail to load.\nTo turn off images in Chrome, open\nSettings\n. At the bottom of the page, select\nAdvanced\n. Select\nSite Settings\n, and then\nImages\n. Toggle the switch from\nShow all (recommended)\nto\nDo not show any images\n. Reload your web page to view it without images.\nFor more about alt text, see\nAlternative text for images\n.\nGuidelines relevant to images and alternative text\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.1.1: Non-text Content\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.5: Images of Text\nResponsiveness\nCheck different screen sizes, including mobile. Also check both portrait and landscape orientation. This can be done by changing the size of the browser window, using a tool such as the device toolbar in Chrome's developer tools, or by opening the web page on a mobile device. A responsive web site should not have content that is overlapping or obstructing other content. Content and functionality should not be lost in mobile.\nGuidelines relevant to responsiveness\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.3.4: Orientation\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.10: Reflow\nNavigation and menus\nNavigation should be consistent throughout the website. The names and order of items within a navigation area should be the same across pages, and navigation areas should be consistently located. Look at the navigation areas in both desktop and mobile on the website.\nHover over navigation items to check is sub-navigation appears. Does the sub-navigation disappear when you move your mouse pointer away? If so, check that the following are true:\nDoes the sub-navigation go away when you press the\nESC\nkey without moving your mouse?\nCan you move your mouse pointer over the sub-navigation without it disappearing?\nDoes the sub-navigation stay visible until you move the mouse pointer away or press\nESC\n?\nDo the same checks on any other non-navigation items where content appears on mouse hover. These items will also need to be checked with keyboard.\nGuidelines relevant to navigation and menus\nWCAG 2.1 SC 3.2.3: Consistent Navigation\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.13: Content on Hover or Focus\nFlashing and moving content\nNote any flashing or moving content. Moving content will require additional checking with keyboard and screen reader.\nFlashing should be three flashes per second or less to prevent seizures.\nMoving content lasting more than five seconds, such as animated GIFs, auto-playing videos, and auto-advancing carousels should:\nHave the ability to be paused\nBe operable with a keyboard and have keyboard focus\nCommunicate informative changes to all website visitors\nNote:\nFlashing and moving content should be used sparingly. Learn more about\nmotion and parallax\n, including potential physical harm.\nGuidelines relevant to flashing and moving content\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.2.2: Pause, Stop, Hide\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.3.1: Three Flashes or Below Threshold\nTimed content\nMost often, non-moving timed content is caught when pages time out. If you have pages that time out, you will need to note these.\nOther examples of timed content include content that automatically advances or updates, including page refreshes and the moving content identified under\nFlashing and moving content\n.\nVerify that time limits are adjustable to at least ten times the default, allow extension of the time limit at least ten times, or allow the time limit to be turned off.\nExceptions:\nReal-time events like auctions\nTime limit is essential and extending time limits would invalidate the activity\nTimed content that extends beyond 20 hours\nGuidelines relevant to timed content\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.2.1: Timing Adjustable\nWCAG 2.1 SC 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide\nLanguage changes\nNote any changes of language. For example, on a page written in English, there may be a paragraph in Mandarin or Spanish. You should verify that the language change has been indicated in the code.\nGuidelines relevant to language changes\nWCAG 2.1 SC 3.1.2: Language of Parts\nNo styles\nView each page with styles turned off. This will allow you to quickly check the reading order and spot potential problems that appear if the style sheet fails to load.\nPotential problems include instructions or information that rely on color (for example, overdue items marked in red) or style (for example, unavailable dates marked with a strikethrough). Turning off styles also reveals places where styles were used instead of meaningful HTML elements, such as increased text size rather than a semantic heading element.\nTo turn off styles in Firefox, open the\nView\nmenu. Select\nPage Style\nand then\nNo Style\n.\nGuidelines relevant to reading order\nWCAG 2.1 SC 1.3.2: Meaningful Sequence\nThings to check later\nLook for the existence of the following, which will require in-depth checking with keyboard, screen reader, and automated tools:\nForms and form elements\nCarousels and slide shows\nAccordions\nTables\nWidgets: maps, calendars, social media, chats\nPDFs and other documents\nDetails about how to check these are in the next steps.\nNext step:\nCheck keyboard access and visual focus on web pages\nReturn to the index:\nPerform an accessibility review on your website\nGet help\nFor questions or consultations, email the Enterprise Experience\naccessibility team\n.	KB0026057	KB0026057 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Index the website Page titles Things to check now Color and sensory characteristics Video and audio files Images and alt text Responsiveness Navigation and menus Flashing and moving content Timed content Language changes No styles Things to check later Get help Overview Looking at each web page before running any tools	2.3673		2026-01-31 17:10:24.581776	2026-01-31 17:10:24.581776	0	a14038b347a456583d373803846d43a3
11472	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ace3c6cf47fd1a909286dfbd436d4320	Use a screen reader to evaluate a website	Table of contents\nOverview\nChoose a screen reader\nEvaluate a website\nNormal screen reader behavior\nAreas requiring extra attention\nForm elements\nInteractive elements\nTables\nAccordions\nCarousels and slide shows\nImages\nAlerts, errors, and pop-ups\nGet help\nOverview\nImportant:\nThis information is intended for web developers, providing a holistic process for performing an initial pass of evaluating your website for accessibility. This is not a substitute for performing a complete\nWeb Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1\nAA site evaluation, but following this full process will capture most of the WCAG 2.1 AA Guidelines more efficiently.\nIt is impossible to meet Indiana University's digital accessibility standard and related accessibility policies using only automated tools.\nUsing a screen reader to check a web page or website can reveal many accessibility issues. To ensure that your website has not been created in a way that takes advantage of bugs or features of any one particular screen reader, you will need to check the site with more than one screen reader.\nChoose a screen reader\nDifferent screen readers behave in slightly different ways. Each has its own key combinations. The way content is announced may differ from screen reader to screen reader. For example, some screen readers may announce "link" before the name of the link, while others might announce "link" after the name of the link.\nCommon screen readers include:\nVoiceOver\nComes with all MacOS and iOS devices\nMost often paired with Safari\nNVDA\nWindows-only\nMost often paired with Firefox\nFree, open source\nJAWS\nWindows-only\nMost often paired with Chrome or Firefox\nPrice depends on license type\nNarrator\nComes with all Windows devices\nOnly compatible with Edge, at this date\nTalkBack\nAvailable on some Android products\nThe screen reader and browser combinations above are widely accepted as the best and most consistent combinations, although many other combinations are possible. For example, Firefox can be used with VoiceOver on both desktop and mobile environments, but most people who use VoiceOver use it with Safari. While Chrome can also be used across multiple platforms and screen readers, its inconsistency makes its viability as a testing tool limited.\nAt the minimum, you should test with the following combinations:\nNVDA with Firefox\nVoiceOver with Safari\nVoiceOver on iOS\nTo get started with these screen readers, including keyboard commands or touch gestures, see:\nNVDA basics\nVoiceOver basics\nVoiceOver for iOS basics\nEvaluate a website\nWhen evaluating a website with a screen reader, listen for clarity and the logical order of page elements. Each web page should make sense without the visual context. First explore and listen to the page with the monitor turned off or the screen covered to measure the page's comprehensiveness and make note of problem areas. Then turn the screen back on and navigate the page with the screen reader key commands, verifying visually that all screen elements are accessible through the navigation and that any information communicated visually, such as the current page in the navigation, is also conveyed by the screen reader.\nListen to the full page from start to finish to verify that the order of elements on the page follow a logical progression, such as left to right, top to bottom.\nUse the\nTab\nkey to navigate through all the interactive items. Make sure they can all be accessed and function when using the screen reader.\nUse the arrow keys to navigate through the page. The arrow keys will read line by line, reading everything on the page at your navigation pace.\nNavigate through landmarks, links, and headings only. This can be done through a list, such as in the VoiceOver rotor or NVDA elements list, or by using special keys that navigate by item type.\nLandmarks should be identifiable. For example, if there are two navigation landmarks, they should be labeled, such as "Main navigation" and "Section navigation".\nLinks should make sense out of context. The link destination should be clear from the link text. The announced link text should match or start with the visible link text. For a brief demonstration, see\nQuick accessibility test: Link text (YouTube video)\n.\nHeadings should create an outline and indicate the type of content on the page.\nNormal screen reader behavior\nScreen readers may mispronounce words, including acronyms and abbreviations. This is normal and depends on the particular screen reader; see\nDon't Override Screen Reader Pronunciation\n. Screen reader users become familiar with the behavior of their particular screen reader, and if they choose to, can customize certain pronunciations.\nOdd pronunciations should usually be ignored, including:\nAbbreviations:\nScreen readers may try to fill in the full word, if the abbreviation is in the screen reader's dictionary. Other times, the abbreviation will be announced letter by letter. Occasionally, the screen reader will select the wrong word. For example, "W" may be announced as "w", "west" or "watts".\nAcronyms:\nScreen readers may announce an acronym letter by letter or try to pronounce it. Words that appear to be acronyms, such as words in all capital letters, may also be treated as acronyms.\nEmail:\nScreen readers may pronounce this as it is seen on screen. For example, "someone@somewhere.com" may be read as "someone at somewhere dot com". Keep in mind that some screen readers could read it differently.\nAddresses:\nScreen readers may announce the number portion of the address such as "9998 West Rd" as "nine thousand nine hundred and ninety eight West Road."\nPhone numbers:\nScreen readers may state these as single numbers. For example, 812-999-9999 may be announced as "eight hundred and twelve dash nine hundred and ninety nine dash nine thousand nine hundred and ninety nine".\nDates:\nScreen readers may try to pronounce dates such as 8/20/2018 as "eight slash twenty slash two thousand and eighteen".\nTimes:\nScreen readers may try to pronounce a timespan such as 9:00 - 5:00 as "nine zero zero five zero zero".\nPronunciations of acronyms, abbreviations, and such are defined within each screen reader's default speech settings. These settings can then be altered to fit each user's specific preferences. These settings are accessed within the screen reader's preferences menu.\nAreas requiring extra attention\nForm elements\nPay careful attention to forms.\nAll form elements should be announced by the screen reader.\nVerify that the type of form element, for example, checkbox or text input, is announced upon entering each form field.\nVerify that the form label is announced upon entering each form field. The\nform labels\nshould be clear, describing the purpose of the field and any expected formatting. The announced label (\naccessible name\n) should also match or start with the visible label.\nVerify that any states, properties, or values are announced. Examples include checked versus unchecked, expanded versus collapsed, or a pre-populated value.\nVerify that each form field can be completed with the screen reader.\nVerify that changes to states, properties, and values are announced.\nRequired form fields should be announced. If an asterisk (*) is the only method used to indicate required form fields, the symbol must be included in the label and defined at the start of the form.\nMany screen readers switch to forms mode when encountering forms. Non-form elements are ignored by screen readers in forms mode and will not be announced. Pay attention for any text on the form that is not announced.\nEnter erroneous data in some form fields, including leaving required fields blank. Submit the form and pay careful attention to the\nerror messages\n. The screen reader should immediately announce the presence of errors. Error messages should be clear, indicate the number of errors, and indicate how to correct the errors.\nIt should be easy to navigate to the fields in error, and field-specific error messages should be announced when entering the field.\nInteractive elements\nThere are three key considerations for interactive elements:\nIdentity:\nDoes the screen reader announce a name for the item? The announced name should match or start with the visible text.\nOperation:\nDoes the screen reader indicate what type of interactive element it is? Is it a button, a link, a combo box? The type of element or widget provides information on how to interact with it.\nState:\nDoes the screen reader announce the current state (such as checked/unchecked or expanded/collapsed) or the selected value? If you change the state or value, is that announced?\nTables\nData tables must have names. The table name identifies the table.\nHeader cells must be present. When used correctly, the corresponding header(s) will be announced with each data cell.\nTables should be simple. Headers may be announced incorrectly for nested tables and complex tables with headers spanning multiple cells.\nThere should be no empty rows, columns, or data cells.\nAccordions\nCheck any accordions. Make sure each accordion panel can be opened and that panel content can be accessed by the screen reader. Some accordions may not work with a keyboard and may not be announced correctly by screen readers.\nAccordion resources:\nCreate an expandable details widget in Canvas\nAccordion (Sections With Show/Hide Functionality)\nAccessible accordion example\nCarousels and slide shows\nCarousels should not rotate automatically. Carousels and slide shows that automatically advance may cause problems for screen readers.\nNote any interruptions, such as:\nThe screen reader announcing a slide change even when focus is on another part of the page\nThe slide advancing before the screen reader finishes announcing the current slide\nCheck the carousel controls; they should work with the screen reader. Best practices for carousel functionality include:\nPrevious\nand\nNext\nbuttons\nNavigation buttons that indicate which slide is currently displayed\nFocus on selected item\nPlay\nand\nStop\nbutton if the carousel advances automatically\nPause on mouse hover and keyboard focus\nCarousel resources:\nShould I use a Carousel?\nWeb Accessibility Tutorials: Carousel Concepts\nImages\nImage alt text should be announced, unless the image is decorative. The alt text should describe the image's content and function on the page.\nFor more, see\nAlternative text for images\n.\nAlerts, errors, pop-ups\nAny new content that appears on screen must be announced by the screen reader. This includes pop-ups, error messages, alerts, and any other new content.\nCheck any new interactive elements to be sure they work with the screen reader. Check that any pop-ups can be closed with the keyboard.\nIn many cases, such as a pop-up, focus should move to the new content. In other cases, such as status messages, the message should be announced, but focus should remain on the current element.\nNext step:\nCheck color contrast on a website\nPrevious step:\nUse automated tools to review accessibility\nReturn to index:\nPerform an accessibility review on your website\nGet help\nFor questions or consultations, email the Enterprise Experience\naccessibility team\n.	KB0026175	KB0026175 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Choose a screen reader Evaluate a website Normal screen reader behavior Areas requiring extra attention Form elements Interactive elements Tables Accordions Carousels and slide shows Images Alerts, errors, and pop-ups Get help Overview Using a screen reader to check a web page or website can reveal many accessibility i	2.3673		2026-01-31 17:10:29.656557	2026-01-31 17:10:29.656557	0	ace3c6cf47fd1a909286dfbd436d4320
11478	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b640bcb347a456583d373803846d430a	Inspect website code for accessibility	Table of contents\nOverview\nInspection methods\nValidators\nManual inspection\nWhat to check\nGet help\nOverview\nImportant:\nThis information is intended for web developers, providing a holistic process for performing an initial pass of evaluating your website for accessibility. This is not a substitute for performing a complete\nWeb Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1\nAA site evaluation, but following this full process will capture most of the WCAG 2.1 AA Guidelines more efficiently.\nIt is impossible to meet Indiana University's digital accessibility standard and related accessibility policies using only automated tools.\nInspecting the HTML can reveal non-conforming code that can cause accessibility problems. For example, some assistive technologies have trouble performing as expected when the HTML markup is invalid.\nInspection methods\nMany automated tools, such as SortSite, aXe, AInspector and FAE, will identify code that may cause accessibility problems. Additional review, either through manual inspection or with a validator, can identify other problem areas.\nValidators\nValidators are easy to use because they automatically check code based on specifications. You can find reliable validators through the W3C.\nRecommended validators\nW3C Nu HTML Checker\n(checks HTML and ARIA)\nW3C CSS Validation Service\nBookmarklets\nAn easy way to find HTML parsing errors that may affect accessibility is with two\nbookmarklets\nthat work with the W3C Nu HTML Checker:\nWhile on the page to be inspected, use the\nCheck serialized DOM of current page\nbookmarklet to send the page to the Nu HTML Checker.\nFrom the Nu HTML Checker results page, use the\nCheck for WCAG 2.0 parsing compliance\nbookmarklet to filter the results.\nThe results will show errors and warnings relevant to WCAG 2.1.\nManual inspection\nManually reviewing the HTML markup is more difficult, but useful when looking for specific types of non-conforming code.\nTo check the code in a browser, right-click on a web page and choose either:\nView Page Source\nInspect\nor\nInspect Element\nBecause web browsers often try to correct non-conforming code, inspecting the code may show markup that differs from the page source.\nView Page Source\nwill show the code as written by the developer;\nInspect\nor\nInspect Element\nwill show the code as interpreted by the browser.\nNote:\nTo open the developer toolbox by keyboard to the last tool accessed, use the following shortcuts:\nMac:\nCommand-Option-I\nWindows:\nCtrl-Shift-I\nTo open the code inspector by keyboard, use the following shortcuts:\nMac:\nCommand-Option-C\nWindows:\nCtrl-Shift-C\nTo open the source code by keyboard, use the following shortcuts:\nMac:\nCommand-Option-U\nWindows:\nCtrl-U\nMore keyboard shortcuts are available for the developer toolbox:\nFirefox\nChrome\nSafari\nWhat to check\nCheck the code for any problem areas you have identified in previous steps. Pay attention to non-semantic elements and ARIA used in those areas.\nRecognizing non-semantic elements can help identify potential issues. Using a\n<div>\nor\n<span>\ninstead of a button, for example, requires adding the full set of accessibility features that come standard with the semantic element.\nExamples of common non-conforming code that may cause accessibility problems include:\nIncomplete start and end tags\nDeprecated code\nno longer used in HTML\nEmpty or duplicate IDs\nAnchors (\n<a>\nelements) missing\nhref\nattributes\nInvalid ARIA attributes or values\nHTML elements with non-standard attributes\n<meta>\ntags placed in the\n<body>\nof the HTML code\nMissing semicolons (";") at the end of CSS rules\nCheck that appropriate\nautocomplete values\nare used on form fields that collect information about the person completing the form.\nPrevious step:\nCheck zoom and magnification for web accessibility\nReturn to index:\nPerform an accessibility review on your website\nGet help\nFor questions or consultations, email the Enterprise Experience\naccessibility team\n.	KB0026163	KB0026163 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Inspection methods Validators Manual inspection What to check Get help Overview Inspecting the HTML can reveal non-conforming code that can cause accessibility problems. For example, some assistive technologies have trouble performing as expected when the HTML markup is invalid. Inspection methods Many automated tools,	2.3673		2026-01-31 17:10:33.438468	2026-01-31 17:10:33.438468	0	b640bcb347a456583d373803846d430a
11479	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ca40f8b347a456583d373803846d436e	Check color contrast on a website	On this page:\nOverview\nRecommended contrast ratios\nColor contrast evaluation\nText over images\nRecommended tools\nHigh contrast display settings\nGet help\nOverview\nImportant:\nThis information is intended for web developers, providing a holistic process for performing an initial pass of evaluating your website for accessibility. This is not a substitute for performing a complete\nWeb Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1\nAA site evaluation, but following this full process will capture most of the WCAG 2.1 AA Guidelines more efficiently.\nIt is impossible to meet Indiana University's digital accessibility standard and related accessibility policies using only automated tools.\nTo accommodate people with low vision or color vision deficits, web pages should be designed with adequate contrast between the text and its background. Buttons, links, and other user interface components also should have adequate contrast compared to their backgrounds.\nRecommended contrast ratios\nThe W3C Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (\nWCAG 2.1\n) provide the following contrast ratios as success criteria for helping people distinguish foreground content, such as text and buttons, from background elements:\nFor people with moderately low visual acuity (20/40), the contrast ratio between text and its background should be at least 4.5:1. For large-scale text (at least 18-point normal or 14-point bold), a contrast ratio of at least 3:1 between text and its background is recommended. For more, see\nSuccess Criterion 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum)\n.\nFor people with low visual acuity (20/80), the contrast ratio between text and its background should be at least 7:1. For large-scale text (at least 18-point normal or 14-point bold), a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 between text and its background is recommended. For more, see\nSuccess Criterion 1.4.6 Contrast (Enhanced)\n.\nLink text embedded in paragraphs should have a contrast ratio of at least 3:1 compared to the paragraph text. For more, see\nSuccess Criterion 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast\n.\nUser interface components, such as buttons, and graphical objects (parts of graphics that are required to understand the content) should have a contrast ratio of at least 3:1 against adjacent colors (\nSuccess Criterion 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast\n).\nColor contrast evaluation\nSome color contrast problems are easily identified when looking at a web page. These include text and background color combinations that hurt your eyes or make the text difficult to read, and user interface components, such as buttons, that blend into the background.\nAutomated tools are available for checking color contrast. You should do a visual check to confirm any issues found by automated tools. Many tools do not reliably check images of text, text over images, or gradients for color contrast.\nTo properly evaluate the color contrast of your web page:\nIdentify areas of potential low color contrast either visually or with an automated tool.\nDetermine the RGB or hexadecimal value for the color of the text, button, or graphical object.\nDetermine the RGB or hexadecimal value for the color of the background.\nCheck the contrast ratio using a color contrast tool.\nText over images\nManually check any text that displays over an image. Depending on screen width and responsive design, the location of your text may vary in relation to the underlying image. Consequently, you should check the text against background colors from more than one area of the image.\nConfirm that a default background color has been set using CSS, and make sure it provides enough contrast with the text if images are turned off or fail to load. For example, white text may have sufficient contrast when displayed over your background image, but it will be invisible when displayed over a default white background.\nRecommended tools\nFollowing are recommended tools for checking color contrast ratios:\nContrast Finder\nContrast Ratio\nWebAIM Color Contrast Checker\nWebAIM Link Contrast Checker\nColour Contrast Analyzer\nfrom The Paciello Group\nNote:\nUse the\nContrast Ratio\ntool to check a color with transparency.\nWhile not part of a standard evaluation, simulating vision impairments can be helpful during the design process or for demonstrations. Following are recommended tools for simulating vision impairments:\nA11y – Color blindness empathy test\nis a Chrome browser extension that can display a web page through a variety of filters, including nine types of color vision deficits.\nToptal.com Colorblind Web Page Filter\nis a web-based tool with filters for four types of color vision deficits; it provides a side-by-side comparison of the page with and without the chosen filter.\nHigh contrast display settings\nAlong with checking color contrast, you should also check how your web pages appear when high-contrast display settings are used. In Windows, you can do this using high-contrast themes:\nSelect\nSettings\n>\nEase of Access\n>\nHigh contrast\n.\nUnder "Use high contrast", select the "Turn on high contrast" toggle switch.\nUnder "Choose a theme", use the drop-down list to select a high contrast theme.\nNote:\nYou can use the\nAlt\n+\nleft Shift\n+\nPrint Screen\nkeyboard shortcut to switch between high contrast and default settings in Windows.\nThe macOS\nInvert colors\naccessibility system preference is not functionally equivalent to Windows high contrast themes.\nChecking your web page with high contrast settings helps ensure that no visual content will be lost. Some specific issues to check include:\nInformative CSS background images, including icons, that may disappear\nBackground images with text\nTransparent backgrounds on PNGs or GIFs\nColors used to convey meaning, such as differentiating heading levels\nButtons without borders\nText fields and other form elements\nAny other lost content\nView the short\nQuick accessibility test: High Contrast mode video\nfor a demonstration.\nNext step:\nCheck zoom and magnification for web accessibility\nPrevious step:\nUse a screen reader to evaluate a website\nReturn to index:\nPerform an accessibility review on your website\nGet help\nFor questions or consultations, email the Enterprise Experience\naccessibility team\n.	KB0026107	KB0026107 v1.0	On this page: Overview Recommended contrast ratios Color contrast evaluation Text over images Recommended tools High contrast display settings Get help Overview To accommodate people with low vision or color vision deficits, web pages should be designed with adequate contrast between the text and its background. Buttons, links, and other user in	2.3673		2026-01-31 17:10:37.079974	2026-01-31 17:10:37.079974	0	ca40f8b347a456583d373803846d436e
11528	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=30ef2c3b476456583d373803846d43e5	Check the CPU usage of a job in Unix	Note:\nIf you are concerned about slowing the system down, you can use the\nnice\ncommand to lower your program's priority. For more information about the\nnice\ncommand, at the Unix prompt, enter:\nman nice\nIn\nUnix\n, you can see CPU usage on a job that is running in a number of ways, as described below:\nThe\ntime\ncommand\nIf you want to see a grand total of CPU time for a program when it finishes running, you can use the\ntime\ncommand. At the Unix prompt, enter:\ntime myprog\nReplace\nmyprog\nwith the name of the program you are running. The following is an output example for users in the\ncsh\nor\ntcsh\nshells\n:\n1.406u 0.042s 0:04.96 29.0% 2+5k 0+1io 0pf+0w\nThe program\nmyprog\nused 1.406 seconds of user time, 0.042 seconds of system time, and 4.96 seconds of real time. The sum of the user and system times is the total CPU time of the process. The percentage (29.0%) indicates the percentage of the CPU's time that the process used while it ran. The output will appear in a slightly different format when using\nsh\n,\nksh\n, or\nbash\n, since the\ntime\ncommand is not built into those shells.\nThe\nps\ncommand\nYou can also use the Unix command\nps\n. At the Unix prompt, enter:\nps -u username\nReplace\nusername\nwith your username. You will see something like the following:\nPID TTY TIME COMMAND 10005 ? 3:03 a.out 15852 rb 0:01 -tcsh (tcsh) 24980 rb 0:00 sh -c /usr/local/bin/emacs /usr1/mmouse/snd.18106 24981 rb 0:02 /usr/local/bin/emacs /usr1/mmouse/snd.18106 22311 rf 0:22 elm\nIn this example, the "TIME" column shows that the process running Elm has used 22 CPU seconds.\nThe\ntop\ncommand\nYou may also use the\ntop\ncommand. At the Unix prompt, enter:\ntop\nYou will see something similar to the following:\nPID USERNAME PRI NICE SIZE RES STATE TIME WCPU CPU COMMAND 28000 mmouse 96 4 276K 144K run 292:20 16.80% 16.80% desert.exe 27999 mmouse 96 4 276K 144K run 292:42 16.02% 16.02% denver.exe 19004 goofy 96 4 428K 160K run 357:11 15.63% 15.63% diskr\nUser\nmmouse\nis at the top of the list, and the "TIME" column shows that the program\ndesert.exe\nhas used 292 minutes and 20 seconds of CPU time. This is the most interactive way to see CPU usage.\nAt Indiana University, for personal or departmental Linux or Unix systems support, see\nGet help for Linux or Unix at IU\n.	KB0023947	KB0023947 v1.0	 nice In Unix, you can see <strong>CPU</strong> usage on a job that is running in a number of ways, as described below: The time command If you want to see a grand total of <strong>CPU</strong> time for a program when it finishes times is the total <strong>CPU</strong> time of the process. The percentage (29.0%) indicates the percentage	205.9981		2026-01-31 17:11:14.342049	2026-01-31 17:11:14.342049	0	30ef2c3b476456583d373803846d43e5
11480	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=26e2a57e47aa3298d500b87b716d438f	Load final grades from Canvas into the SIS Grade Roster	Table of contents\nBefore you begin\nSet or confirm the grading scheme for your course\nReview and update missing grades in Canvas\nExport a copy of your gradebook\nLoad final grades to the SIS Grade Roster\nBefore you begin\nYou can use the Canvas SIS Grade Roster tool to navigate to the SIS Grade Roster, where you can load final course grades directly from\nCanvas\n. However, it is critical that you review the grades transferred from Canvas to SIS before submitting final grades. Review the instructions below carefully before attempting to load final grades from Canvas for the first time.\nNotes:\nYou can override calculated course grades with any valid grade in the course grading scheme from within Canvas. When grades are loaded into SIS, override grades take precedence over calculated grades. For more, see\nOverride the final course grade in Canvas\n.\nYou can't set grades for special circumstances (\nAcademic Misconduct\n,\nI\n,\nFN\n,\nFNN\n,\nR\n, etc.) in Canvas. These grades must be entered directly in the SIS Grade Roster before final submission.\nYou can't set requirements designation (RD) grades (for RISE designations, intensive writing requirements, etc.) in Canvas. If your course has a requirements designation, you must set the RD grade directly in the SIS Grade Roster.\nIf you need to make changes to assignment grades in Canvas\nafter\nyou have loaded grades into the SIS Grade Roster, load the grades into SIS again before submitting them. If your changes were made after submitting the final grade roster and they resulted in discrepancies between calculated course grades in Canvas and submitted grades in SIS,\ninitiate an eGrade Change Request\nfor each affected student.\nYou can't submit final grades until the final grade roster is\navailable for each term; the Office of the Registrar will email\ninstructors when rosters are available. For questions about roster\navailability, grade submission deadlines, or submitting final grades\nafter the deadline, contact your campus\nOffice of the Registrar\n.\nSet or confirm the grading scheme for your course\nIn Canvas, the course grading scheme determines the mapping of percentage grades to letter grades. When Canvas courses are provisioned, a default grading scheme is enabled based on the grading basis assigned to the course in SIS.\nCourses with a grading basis of GR1, GR2, and NC have a default scheme named "Letter Grades with +/-" in Canvas.\nCourses with a grading basis of SF or SF2 have a default scheme named "Satisfactory/Fail" in Canvas.\nYou can use the initial default scheme, choose a different pre-existing scheme (for example, a scheme published by your department), or create your own custom scheme. You will not be able to transfer final course grades to SIS unless a grading scheme has been enabled.\nWhen selecting a grading scheme in Canvas, be sure to choose a scheme that is compatible with the grades allowed for your course in SIS. For example, if your course allows only\nS\nand\nF\ngrades, use the predefined Satisfactory/Fail grading scheme in Canvas, or create your own scheme for which the only possible grades are\nS\nand\nF\n. To determine which grades are allowed for your course:\nFollow the first three steps in the\nLoad final grades to the SIS Grade Roster\nsection below.\nSelect the\nValid grades\nlink in any row except the top row.\nYour Canvas grading scheme should include only grades that can be calculated based on points earned in your course. If you create your own grading scheme, do not include grades used for special circumstances (\nAcademic Misconduct\n,\nFN\n,\nFNN\n,\nI\n,\nR\n, etc); these grades must be entered directly into the SIS Grade Roster.\nFor more on working with grading schemes, see\nHow do I add a grading scheme in a course?\nand\nHow do I enable a grading scheme for a course?\n.\nReview and update missing grades in Canvas\nIn Canvas, missing or empty grades do not count toward the calculated final grade. If you want to penalize a student for not completing a graded activity, you must enter a grade of\n0\nfor that activity. To excuse a student from a graded activity, enter a grade of\nEX\n(which stands for "excused"). Before submitting your final grades, review all empty grades in your gradebook and zero out missing grades if necessary.\nTo set all missing grades for a given assignment to 0, see\nHow do I set a default grade for an assignment in the Gradebook?\n.\nExport a copy of your gradebook\nIt's a good idea to make an offline copy of your Canvas gradebook right before you transfer grades to SIS. Occasionally, instructors or TAs make changes to the gradebook after final grade submission that result in discrepancies between grades in the LMS and in SIS; having an offline copy allows you to address any questions or concerns that might arise.\nTo create an offline copy, see\nHow do I export grades in the Gradebook?\n.\nImportant:\nIf you plan to export and maintain local offline copies of\nany\nstudent information (including but not limited to names,\nUniversity ID\nnumbers, or grades), you are responsible for taking adequate security measures to handle and safeguard such data, as it remains sensitive university data.\nFor more, see\nProtecting Data & Privacy\n.\nFor more on handling sensitive data, see:\nAbout sensitive data at IU\nAbout institutional data at IU\nLoad final grades to the SIS Grade Roster\nTo load grades from your Canvas course to the final grade roster in SIS:\nIn Canvas, navigate to your course.\nFrom the course navigation menu, select\nGrades\n.\nTo open the SIS Grade Roster tool:\nOpen the\nIU Grading Tools\nmenu and select\nSIS Grade Roster\n.\nNote:\nYou can also add the SIS Grade Roster tool to your course navigation menu. For instructions, see\nHow do I manage Course Navigation links?\nIf your internet pop-up blocker is set to\nblock all pop-ups, you'll need to turn it off or change the setting to\nallow them; refer to your browser help for instructions.  For further\nassistance, contact your local\nSupport Center\n.\nIf your course only has one section, a new tab will open displaying the SIS grade roster for that section.\nIf your course contains cross-listed sections:\nThe SIS Faculty Center will open in a new tab. In the "Class" column (noted by a 1 in the image below), locate the course section for which you would like to enter grades.\nTo open the grade roster for that section, select\nGrade Roster\n(2 in the image below) in the course section's row.\nScreenshot of term information in SIS Faculty Center\nIn the Grade Roster, select\nLoad Grades from Canvas\n. After a few moments, the calculated final grades from the Canvas gradebook will appear in the "Grade Input" column.\nSelect\nSave\nto save the imported grades.\nCompare the grades in the SIS Grade Roster with the calculated course grades in the Canvas gradebook to make sure they are identical.\nFor students who have undergone an academic misconduct investigation and for whom the academic misconduct designation was deemed appropriate, check the box in the "Academic Misconduct" column.\nIn the "Grade Input" column, review the grades and adjust the values as necessary. You can assign special grades such as\nFN\n(for students who stopped attending),\nFNN\n(for students who never attended),\nI\n(for incomplete),\nR\n(for deferred), etc. Select\nSave\nperiodically to save your changes.\nNote:\nIf you assign a grade of\nFN\n, you must also provide the date of last attendance. The "Last Date Attended" field will appear when you select\nSave\n.\nIf your grade roster has a "Requirement Designation (RD) Grade" column (for RISE designations, intensive writing requirements, etc.), set the RD grade for each student. Select\nSave\nperiodically to save your changes.\nWhen you are ready to submit your final grades, select\nApprove and Submit to Registrar\n.\nTo load grades for additional sections in a course with cross-listed sections, select\nReturn\nto go back to the Faculty Center, and repeat steps 3 through 9 for each additional section.	KB0024570	KB0024570 v5.0	Table of contents Before you begin Set or confirm the grading scheme for your course Review and update missing grades in Canvas Export a copy of your gradebook Load final grades to the SIS Grade Roster Before you begin You can use the Canvas SIS Grade Roster tool to navigate to the SIS Grade Roster, where you can load final course grades directl	2.3545		2026-01-31 17:10:44.608283	2026-01-31 17:10:44.608283	0	26e2a57e47aa3298d500b87b716d438f
11488	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c3aae5f247aa7298d500b87b716d4334	Submit final grades from Canvas via a file upload	Table of contents\nBefore you begin\nExport calculated grades from Canvas\nImport final grades into SIS\nBefore you begin\nUITS\nhas developed a custom tool that exports a\n.csv\nfile compatible with the SIS Grade Roster file upload option. To export grades from\nCanvas\nand upload them to your midterm or final grade roster, follow the instructions below.\nNotes:\nWhile it is possible to submit final grades via a file upload, it is much easier to transfer grades directly from Canvas to the SIS Grade Roster. For details and instructions, see\nLoad final grades from Canvas into the SIS Grade Roster\n.\nYou can't submit final grades until the final grade roster is\navailable for each term; the Office of the Registrar will email\ninstructors when rosters are available. For questions about roster\navailability, grade submission deadlines, or submitting final grades\nafter the deadline, contact your campus\nOffice of the Registrar\n.\nIf you need to make changes to assignment grades in Canvas\nafter\nyou have loaded grades into the SIS Grade Roster, load the grades into SIS again before submitting them. If your changes were made after submitting the final grade roster and they resulted in discrepancies between calculated course grades in Canvas and submitted grades in SIS,\ninitiate an eGrade Change Request\nfor each affected student.\nExport calculated grades from Canvas\nTo export final grades from Canvas:\nIn Canvas, missing or empty grades do not count toward the calculated final grade. If you want to penalize a student for not completing a graded activity, you must enter a grade of\n0\nfor that activity. To excuse a student from a graded activity, leave the grade blank. Review all empty grades in your gradebook and zero out missing grades if necessary.\nIf you haven't already done so, confirm or edit the grading scheme for your course gradebook. See\nHow do I add a grading scheme in a course?\nand\nHow do I enable a grading scheme for a course?\n.\nFrom the course navigation menu, select\nGrades\n.\nIn the Canvas Gradebook, select\nIU Grading Tools\nand select\nExport Grades for SIS\n.\nYou will be prompted to open or save the file. Save the file to your local storage device.\nNote:\nDo not open and save the file in Microsoft Excel. Excel removes the leading zeroes from student ID numbers, which will prevent the SIS import from working.\nImport final grades into SIS\nTo import final grades into SIS:\nIn Canvas, navigate to your course. From the course navigation menu, select\nSIS Grade Roster\n. This tool is visible only to instructors.\nThe Faculty Center will initially display information for the current term and campus. To select a different term or campus, select\nChange Term or Campus\nand select the appropriate term or campus.\nIf your course only has one section, a new tab will open displaying the SIS grade roster for that section.\nIf your course contains cross-listed sections:\nThe SIS Faculty Center will open in a new tab. In the "Class" column, locate the course section for which you would like to enter grades.\nTo open the grade roster for that section, select\nGrade Roster\nin the course section's row.\nOn the "Grade Roster" screen, select\nLoad Grades from File to Roster\n.\nSelect\nBrowse\nto locate and select your final grades file.\nOnce the filename displays in the text box, select\nUpload\n. The system will attempt to load the file. You should see the following message, where "Y" is the number of records in the file and "X" is the number of records loaded:\nLoading X Records out of Y Read. Would you like to continue?\nSelect\nOK\n.\nVerify that all grades loaded, and select\nSave\n.\nCompare the grades in the SIS Grade Roster with the calculated course grades in the Canvas gradebook to make sure they are identical.\nIn the "Grade Input" column, review the grades and adjust the values as necessary. You can assign special grades such as\nFN\n(for students who stopped attending),\nFNN\n(for students who never attended),\nI\n(for incomplete),\nR\n(for deferred),\nAcademic Misconduct\n, and so on. Select\nSave\nperiodically to save your changes.\nNote:\nIf you assign a grade of\nFN\n, you must also provide the date of last attendance. The "Last Date Attended" field will appear when you select\nSave\n.\nIf your grade roster has a "Requirement Designation (RD) Grade" column (for RISE designations, intensive writing requirements, etc.), set the RD grade for each student. Select\nSave\nperiodically to save your changes.\nIf you selected one or more grades for academic misconduct, you'll see a pop-up window to confirm that you intend to assign academic misconduct for the student(s) in question. Select\nOK\nto submit the grades to the registrar or\nCancel\nto return to the roster to make adjustments.\nWhen all required fields are completed and you are satisfied that all grades are correct, select\nApprove and Submit to Registrar\n.\nIf your Canvas course has cross-listed sections, select\nReturn\nto go back to the Faculty Center. Repeat steps 2 through 10 for each section.	KB0023069	KB0023069 v2.0	Table of contents Before you begin Export calculated grades from Canvas Import final grades into SIS Before you begin UITS has developed a custom tool that exports a .csv file compatible with the SIS Grade Roster file upload option. To export grades from Canvas and upload them to your midterm or final grade roster, follow the instructions below.	2.3545		2026-01-31 17:10:48.57013	2026-01-31 17:10:48.57013	0	c3aae5f247aa7298d500b87b716d4334
11493	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=06203cff476456583d373803846d43cf	Connect FireForm to Salesforce CRM with the Convergence Cloud Form Handler	Table of contents\nBefore you begin\nSend data to the Form Handler from FireForm\nReceive a response from the Form Handler after submission\nTroubleshoot issues with the Form Handler\nBefore you begin\nBefore you import data using the Convergence Cloud Form Handler:\nBack up your form data.\nIf Convergence Cloud fails while processing the data in your form, there is no way to recover that data. Many form builders automatically store this data and allow you to export it into a spreadsheet. Make sure this is the case, or have another backup process in place.\nAlways test your form in the IU CRM Test environment.\nThis helps to minimize the chances of lost or incorrectly mapped data.\nThe instructions below assume you have already created your form and have followed the steps in\nUse the Convergence Cloud Form Handler\nto add your form URL to the Form Handler within Salesforce CRM.\nSend data to the Form Handler from FireForm\nAfter you have created your form in\nFireForm\n:\nWhile viewing the form settings and configuration, at the top of the screen, select\nIntegration\n.\nSelect\nAdd Integration\n, and then make the appropriate selections for "State" and "Move to routing step".\nSelect\nAdd Integration Rule\n, and fill out the following fields:\nName:\nSalesforce Form Handler\n(or anything descriptive)\nRule Order:\nThe appropriate choice for your form (default is fine if no other integrations are being used for your form)\nStatus:\nActive\nHTTP Method:\nPOST\nEnd Point URL:\nSalesforce CRM Test:\nhttps://iu-convergence-cloud-test.herokuapp.com/api/form\nSalesforce CRM Production:\nhttps://iu-convergence-cloud.herokuapp.com/api/form\nUse Custom Header/Proceed on Failure:\nUnchecked\nAuthorization Type:\nBasic Auth\nUsername:\nThe FireForm URL or custom username established in the Form Handler\nPassword:\nThe password/encryption key generated or set in the Form Handler\nData Format:\nSend Key-Value Pairs\nAdd Key Value Pairs:\nAppropriate form fields (they should be under "Source" in the format\n{Element_1}1\n)\nTarget:\nSalesforce field in the format\nContact.firstname\nNote:\nWhen sending your data to Salesforce CRM, you will need to know the API names of the values you are sending. For a catalog of the API values, go to Convergence Cloud's\nUpload Template\ntab and search for\nForm Handler Users\nin all templates. The file attached to the record will contain all of the field names that can be populated via the Form Handler.\nWhen creating contacts, your form must contain\neither\n:\nFirst name, last name, and either email, phone number, or address\nA unique identifier, such as a\nUniversity ID\n, ADS username, or an existing Salesforce ID\nWhen creating contact records with the Form Handler, Convergence Cloud will attempt to match and update any contacts before creating a new record.\nThe "Name" column should contain Salesforce field names (for example,\nContact.firstname\n).\nThe "Value" column should be either a static value or the field from the form that you want to map. To map a form field, select\nmerge tags\nat the bottom of the page, select the desired field, and copy the tag, including the curved brackets.\nReceive a response from the Form Handler after submission\nA response will be sent back to your form after submission. The response will include an initial processing success/failure message.\nSubmission failure:\nDepending on the\nnotification frequency you chose when setting up your URL in Salesforce\n, you will either receive an email message immediately upon submission failure, or receive such a message once daily.\nAuthentication failure:\nNo matter what notification frequency you selected, you will receive immediate notification of authentication failures.\nDepending on the volume of data Convergence Cloud is currently processing, it could take some time before you see the submitted data in Salesforce CRM.\nTroubleshoot issues with the Form Handler\nIf your form is not working properly:\nMake sure you have added the form's username/URL to the correct instance.\nMake sure the API names for the submitted data are spelled correctly and that you have entered all the required fields for the object for which you are submitting data.	KB0025434	KB0025434 v1.0	Table of contents Before you begin Send data to the Form Handler from FireForm Receive a response from the Form Handler after submission Troubleshoot issues with the Form Handler Before you begin Send data to the Form Handler from FireForm After you have created your form in FireForm: While viewing the form settings and configuration, at the top	2.3201	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:10:52.780893	2026-01-31 17:10:52.780893	0	06203cff476456583d373803846d43cf
11495	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=95df6cf7476456583d373803846d43f3	Connect MachForm to Salesforce with the Convergence Cloud Form Handler	Table of contents\nBefore you begin\nSend data to the Form Handler from MachForm\nReceive a response from the Form Handler after submission\nTroubleshoot issues with the Form Handler\nBefore you begin\nBefore you import data using the Convergence Cloud Form Handler:\nBack up your form data.\nIf Convergence Cloud fails while processing the data in your form, there is no way to recover that data. Many form builders automatically store this data and allow you to export it into a spreadsheet. Make sure this is the case, or have another backup process in place.\nAlways test your form in the IU CRM Test environment.\nThis helps to minimize the chances of lost or incorrectly mapped data.\nThe instructions below assume you have already created your form and have followed the steps in\nUse the Convergence Cloud Form Handler\nto add your form URL to the Form Handler within Salesforce CRM.\nSend data to the Form Handler from MachForm\nAfter you have created your form in\nMachForm\n:\nIn the ribbon under the form you want to connect to Salesforce, select\nLogic\n.\nCheck\nEnable Rules to Send Form Data to Another Website\n.\nSet a rule to determine when MachForm will send the data to Salesforce. To ensure all form entries are sent, choose\n--Form is submitted--\nfrom the drop-down menu.\nFill out the following fields:\nSend form data to URL:\nPaste the appropriate API Endpoint URL from Salesforce (listed at the top of the Form Handler page):\nSalesforce CRM Test:\nhttps://iu-convergence-cloud-test.herokuapp.com/api/form\nSalesforce CRM Production:\nhttps://iu-convergence-cloud.herokuapp.com/api/form\nSetting Configuration:\nHTTP Method:\nHTTP POST\n(recommended)\nUse HTTP Authentication:\nChecked\nHTTP User Name:\nYour form's URL (the same URL you entered in Salesforce)\nHTTP Password:\nThe password/encryption key generated in Salesforce\nUse Custom HTTP Headers:\nUnchecked\nParameters (Field Mapping):\nNote:\nWhen sending your data to Salesforce CRM, you will need to know the API names of the values you are sending. For a catalog of the API values, go to Convergence Cloud's\nUpload Template\ntab and search for\nForm Handler Users\nin all templates. The file attached to the record will contain all of the field names that can be populated via the Form Handler.\nWhen creating contacts, your form must contain\neither\n:\nFirst name, last name, and either email, phone number, or address\nA unique identifier, such as a\nUniversity ID\n, ADS username, or an existing Salesforce ID\nWhen creating contact records with the Form Handler, Convergence Cloud will attempt to match and update any contacts before creating a new record.\nThe "Name" column should contain Salesforce field names (for example,\nContact.firstname\n).\nThe "Value" column should be either a static value or the field from the form that you want to map. To map a form field, select\nmerge tags\nat the bottom of the page, select the desired field, and copy the tag, including the curved brackets.\nReceive a response from the Form Handler after submission\nA response will be sent back to your form after submission. The response will include an initial processing success/failure message.\nSubmission failure:\nDepending on the\nnotification frequency you chose when setting up your URL in Salesforce\n, you will either receive an email message immediately upon submission failure, or receive such a message once daily.\nAuthentication failure:\nNo matter what notification frequency you selected, you will receive immediate notification of authentication failures.\nDepending on the volume of data Convergence Cloud is currently processing, it could take some time before you see the submitted data in Salesforce CRM.\nTroubleshoot issues with the Form Handler\nIf your form is not working properly:\nMake sure you have added the form's username/URL to the correct instance.\nMake sure the API names for the submitted data are spelled correctly and that you have entered all the required fields for the object for which you are submitting data.	KB0023619	KB0023619 v1.0	Table of contents Before you begin Send data to the Form Handler from MachForm Receive a response from the Form Handler after submission Troubleshoot issues with the Form Handler Before you begin Send data to the Form Handler from MachForm After you have created your form in MachForm: In the ribbon under the form you want to connect to Salesforc	2.3201	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:10:57.954383	2026-01-31 17:10:57.954383	0	95df6cf7476456583d373803846d43f3
11497	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ae20fcff476456583d373803846d4381	Connect a custom-built form to Salesforce with the Convergence Cloud Form Handler	Table of contents\nBefore you begin\nSend data to the Form Handler\nReceive a response from the Form Handler after submission\nTroubleshoot issues with the Form Handler\nBefore you begin\nBefore you import data using the Convergence Cloud Form Handler:\nBack up your form data.\nIf Convergence Cloud fails while processing the data in your form, there is no way to recover that data. Many form builders automatically store this data and allow you to export it into a spreadsheet. Make sure this is the case, or have another backup process in place.\nAlways test your form in the IU CRM Test environment.\nThis helps to minimize the chances of lost or incorrectly mapped data.\nThe instructions below assume you have already created your form and have followed the steps in\nUse the Convergence Cloud Form Handler\nto add your form URL to the Form Handler within Salesforce CRM.\nSend data to the Form Handler\nWithin your form, make sure you include the following fields so that your form will authenticate and submit successfully:\nAPI Endpoint:\nThe URL to which you are submitting the form\nMethod:\nPOST\nAuthentication:\nThe Form Handler uses\nHTTP Basic Authentication\n. Use the username and password that you set up in the previous step.\nNote:\nBe sure to use\nHTTPS (SSL)\nwhen transmitting this data.\nWhen sending your data to Salesforce, you'll need to know the API names of the value you are sending. For a catalog of the API values, go to Convergence Cloud's\nUpload Template\ntab.\nThe data needs to be submitted in one of two ways:\nIn the body of your post request in JSON Key:\nValue pair format:  {"Contact.firstname":"John", "Contact.lastname":"Doe"}\nIn the query string:\n?Contact.firstname=John&Contact.lastname=Doe\nReceive a response from the Form Handler after submission\nA response will be sent back to your form after submission. The response will include an initial processing success/failure message.\nSubmission failure:\nDepending on the\nnotification frequency you chose when setting up your URL in Salesforce\n, you will either receive an email message immediately upon submission failure, or receive such a message once daily.\nAuthentication failure:\nNo matter what notification frequency you selected, you will receive immediate notification of authentication failures.\nDepending on the volume of data Convergence Cloud is currently processing, it could take some time before you see the submitted data in Salesforce CRM.\nTroubleshoot issues with the Form Handler\nIf your form is not working properly:\nMake sure you have added the form's username/URL to the correct instance.\nMake sure the API names for the submitted data are spelled correctly and that you have entered all the required fields for the object for which you are submitting data.	KB0025470	KB0025470 v1.0	Table of contents Before you begin Send data to the Form Handler Receive a response from the Form Handler after submission Troubleshoot issues with the Form Handler Before you begin Send data to the Form Handler Within your form, make sure you include the following fields so that your form will authenticate and submit successfully: API Endpoint:	2.3201	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:11:02.197196	2026-01-31 17:11:02.197196	0	ae20fcff476456583d373803846d4381
11531	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=360f008a333a2a1c04e605570e5c7b5b	Interact with a VM in the Intelligent Infrastructure (II) self-service portal	Log into the II self-service portal; see\nAccess the Intelligent Infrastructure self-service portal\n.\nIf prompted, select\nService Broker\n.\nFrom the\nConsume\ntab, select\nVirtual Machines\n.\nIn the list, find the name of the virtual machine (VM) you want to interact with. Use the search field to narrow down the list to the specific VM.\nSelect the vertical ellipsis icon (\n) next to the VM name, and then select the action you want to perform on the VM.\nNote:\nThe required fields for each action will differ.\nChange CPU-Memory\nis outlined in the example below.\nFollow the prompts to enter any required values.\nIn this example, we are changing the CPUs from 1 to 2 and memory from 1 GB to 4 GB.\nSince this action lets you change the amount of pool resources, a line showing current and maximum usage for CPU, memory (in GB), and disk (in GB) displays on the form for reference.\nThis action requires powering off the VM, or else the values won't change successfully. Select\nAuto PowerOff\nto have the self-service portal power down the VM.\nImportant:\nIf a pool is over quota on any resource (for example, memory), requests to alter any resource (for example, CPU) will fail until you expand the available resources.\nYou should periodically\ndetermine remaining resources in an Intelligent Infrastructure (II) resource pool\n.\nSelect\nSubmit\nto immediately initiate the request. You will see the request working.\nTo see the status of the request, select\nDeployments\n.\nIf the task succeeded, the status bar will disappear.\nIf the request failed, you will see a failed message. In the following example, the VM was not powered down before attempting to change CPU/memory amounts.	KB0025216	KB0025216 v3.0	. Change <strong>CPU</strong>-Memory is outlined in the example below. Follow the prompts to enter any required this action lets you change the amount of pool resources, a line showing current and maximum usage for <strong>CPU</strong>, memory), requests to alter any resource (for example, <strong>CPU</strong>) will fail until you expand the available	165.495		2026-01-31 17:11:20.125214	2026-01-31 17:11:20.125214	0	360f008a333a2a1c04e605570e5c7b5b
11537	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=2d9b34d31bb0d25416c02fc4bd4bcb60	About the output fields of the ps command in Unix	The\nps\ncommand varies significantly among\nUnix\nimplementations. Each vendor incorporates its own flags and outputs the results differently.\nMost\nps\nvariants are rooted enough in either the System V or BSD syntax that entering\nps -elf\n(System V) or\nps alx\n(BSD) will produce something like the following:\nF\nS\nUID\nID\nPPID\nC\nPRI\nNI\nADDR\nSZ\nWCHAN\nSTIME\nTTY\nTIME\nCOMD\n1\nR\nobiwan\n792\n779\n22\n183\n20\n10ec5f80\n29\n-\n12:52:24\npts/2\n0:00\nps -elf\n1\nS\nroot\n24621\n560\n0\n154\n20\n13603f80\n11\n4697c0\nJun 16\nttyp2\n0:00\ntelnetd\n1\nS\ndvader\n1162\n1153\n0\n154\n20\n110a1f80\n77\n452be4\n11:25:41\npts/3\n0:00\nssh deathstar\nThis particular example is from HP-UX, whose output is basically vanilla System V.\nThe following table describes the meanings of the columns that commonly appear in\nps\noutputs. No version of\nps\nwill display all of these fields, however.\nColumn Header\nContents\n%CPU\nHow much of the CPU the process is using\n%MEM\nHow much memory the process is using\nADDR\nMemory address of the process\nC\nor\nCP\nCPU usage and scheduling information\nCOMMAND\n*\nName of the process, including arguments, if any\nNI\nnice\nvalue\nF\nFlags\nPID\nProcess ID number\nPPID\nID number of the process's parent process\nPRI\nPriority of the process\nRSS\nReal memory usage\nS\nor\nSTAT\nProcess status code\nSTART\nor\nSTIME\nTime when the process started\nSZ\nVirtual memory usage\nTIME\nTotal CPU usage\nTT\nor\nTTY\nTerminal associated with the process\nUID\nor\nUSER\nUsername of the process's owner\nWCHAN\nMemory address of the event the process is waiting for\n*\n= Often abbreviated\nFor information specific to your Unix implementation, consult the\nps\nman\npage.\nAt Indiana University, for personal or departmental Linux or Unix systems support, see\nGet help for Linux or Unix at IU\n.	KB0026042	KB0026042 v2.0	 all of these fields, however. Column Header Contents %<strong>CPU</strong> How much of the <strong>CPU</strong> the process is using %MEM How much memory the process is using ADDR Memory address of the process C or CP <strong>CPU</strong> usage Virtual memory usage TIME Total <strong>CPU</strong> usage TT or TTY Terminal associated with the process UID or USER	165.3616		2026-01-31 17:11:24.65244	2026-01-31 17:11:24.65244	0	2d9b34d31bb0d25416c02fc4bd4bcb60
11698	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=23cf64f7476456583d373803846d43d7	Why you can't telnet to the UITS research supercomputers	To maximize security,\ntelnet\naccess to the UITS\nresearch supercomputers\nat Indiana University is not available. To connect to your account on an IU research supercomputer, you must use an SSH client that supports\nSSH2\n.\nTo request an account on an Indiana University research system, see\nGet additional IU computing accounts\n. Account availability depends on your\neligibility\n.	KB0023473	KB0023473 v1.0	To maximize security, telnet access to the UITS research supercomputers at Indiana University is not available. To connect to your account on an IU research supercomputer, you must use an SSH client that supports SSH2.	4.4823		2026-01-31 17:15:40.168788	2026-01-31 17:15:40.168788	0	23cf64f7476456583d373803846d43d7
11539	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=04d6dc4633b26a1c04e605570e5c7b04	Use the Deployments view to execute Day2 actions in the Intelligent Infrastructure (II) self-service portal	Log into the II self-service portal; see\nAccess the Intelligent Infrastructure self-service portal\n.\nIf prompted, select\nService Broker\n.\nFrom the\nConsume\ntab, select\nDeployments\n.\nIn the list, find the desired VM deployment. It should follow the naming convention\nDeployment for\nVM name\n. Use the search field to narrow the list down to the specific VM deployment.\nSelect the deployment name.\nBy default, you will be presented with a topology map of the VM.\nMake sure the right side shows either the VM name or "VirtualMachine_1". If it does not, select the VM object in the middle of the topology map.\nOn the far right (not the top), use the\nActions\nmenu to select the action you want to perform on the VM.\nNote:\nThe required fields for each action will differ.\nChange CPU-Memory\nis outlined in the example below.\nFollow the prompts to enter any required values.\nIn this example, we are changing the CPUs from 1 to 2 and memory from 1 GB to 4 GB.\nSince this action lets you change the amount of pool resources, a line showing current and maximum usage for CPU, memory (in GB), and disk (in GB) displays on the form for reference.\nThis action requires powering off the VM, or else the values won't change successfully. Select\nAuto PowerOff\nto have the self-service portal power down the VM.\nImportant:\nIf a pool is over quota on any resource (for example, memory), requests to alter any resource (for example, CPU) will fail until you expand the available resources.\nYou should periodically\ndetermine remaining resources in an Intelligent Infrastructure (II) resource pool\n.\nSelect\nSubmit\nto immediately initiate the request. You will see the request working.\nTo see the status of the request, select\nDeployments\n.\nIf the task succeeded, the status bar will disappear.\nIf the request failed, you will see a failed message. In the following example, the VM was not powered down before attempting to change CPU/memory amounts.	KB0023850	KB0023850 v2.0	 on the VM. Note: The required fields for each action will differ. Change <strong>CPU</strong>-Memory is outlined resources, a line showing current and maximum usage for <strong>CPU</strong>, memory (in GB), and disk (in GB (for example, <strong>CPU</strong>) will fail until you expand the available resources. You should periodically determine	162.0354		2026-01-31 17:11:28.950477	2026-01-31 17:11:28.950477	0	04d6dc4633b26a1c04e605570e5c7b04
11541	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=aecf60f7476456583d373803846d43a8	Modify or deactivate a resource pool with the Resource Allocation Center (RAC)	Table of contents\nModify resource pool settings\nFiscal approval\nDeactivate a resource pool\nModify resource pool settings\nLog into the\nResource Allocation Center\n(RAC) using your normal, non-administrative user account.\nNote:\nUse your standard IU account to submit changes. If you use a group account or administrative account, attempts to submit changes will fail.\nSelect\nResource Pool\n.\nSelect the resource pool you wish to modify.\nYou will see a summary of the resource pool settings; this includes the cost summary, which contains the maximum potential expenditure over a year.\nNote:\n"Maximum Annual Cost" represents only a potential.  The Intelligent Infrastructure service does not charge for unused resources, only for resources actually allocated to a VM. The account associated with a pool would only be charged the maximum amount if every CPU, every GB of memory, and every GB of storage in the quota were to be actually allocated to VMs.\nFor more, see\nSelf-service provisioning in Intelligent Infrastructure (II)\nTo edit the settings of the pool, select the\nSettings\ntab.\nModify values as you wish.\nAs you change CPU, memory, storage, or data protection values, the table of maximum costs will update, so you have an idea of potential costs of such changes.\nFiscal approval\nSubmitting changes will require fiscal approval changes are made to any of the following fields:\nCPU\nMemory\nStorage\nData Protection\nIf instead, only other fields (for example, the label or VLANs) were to be changed, then no fiscal approval is required.\nDeactivate a resource pool\nIf you no longer require a resource pool, you can deactivate the pool to remove it from your list and from the II Self-Service portal:\nSelect your resource pool and view the\nSettings\ntab as directed above.\nFind and expand the\nDanger Zone\nblock at the bottom of the page.\nSelect\nDeactivate\nto request that the pool be removed from the II Self-Service portal and from the list of RAC resource pools.\nImportant:\nMake sure there are no VMs in the pool before you request to deactivate the pool. If there are VMs in the pool, they will not get deleted, but the pool will be unable to be removed.\nVerify that the pool is no longer in use; see\nDetermine remaining resources in an Intelligent Infrastructure (II) resource pool\n.	KB0023383	KB0023383 v1.0	 would only be charged the maximum amount if every <strong>CPU</strong>, every GB of memory, and every GB of storage as you wish. As you change <strong>CPU</strong>, memory, storage, or data protection values, the table of maximum costs changes will require fiscal approval changes are made to any of the following fields: <strong>CPU</strong> Memory Storage	159.5673		2026-01-31 17:12:34.866666	2026-01-31 17:12:34.866666	0	aecf60f7476456583d373803846d43a8
11544	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ed5c4e1c4772f6d0d500b87b716d4306	Configure container CPU and memory resources in a namespace with quotas enabled	There are four resource types that should be set on all of your containers (see\nQuota for infrastructure resources\n).\nLimits and requests\nmust\nbe set on all of your containers. Limits define the maximum amount of CPU or memory that a container may use. Requests reserve a certain amount of CPU or memory and factor into the\nscheduling\nof pods. Unless you have a specific need, set both\nrequests.cpu\nand\nrequests.memory\nto\n0\n. Both limits and requests have a quota, and the sums across all of your containers count toward those quotas.\nBelow is an example of how to set resources on a container of a deployment:\napiVersion\n: apps/v1\nkind\n: Deployment\nspec\n:\ntemplate\n:\nspec\n:\ncontainers\n:\n-\nname\n: sample-container\nresources\n:\nrequests\n:\ncpu\n:\n0\nmemory\n:\n0\nlimits\n:\ncpu\n:\n0.25\nmemory\n:\n4\nGi\nTo calculate resources when\nrequesting access\n, sum all of your\nlimits.cpu\nand\nlimits.memory\nacross all of your containers. When you need more resources, follow the same instructions for requesting access to Kubernetes. Create a pull request in the\nKube-RBAC project\nor email\ndocker@iu.edu\nwith your request.\nIf you're submitting a pull request, define something similar to the following in your cluster values file (for example,\nvalues/ua-vpit/enterprise-systems/esimw/es-sandbox.yaml\n):\nnamespace:\nname:\nua-vpit--enterprise-systems--esimw\nquota:\ncreate:\ntrue\ncpu:\nlimits:\n1\nmemory:\nlimits:\n16\nGi	KB0027178	KB0027178 v3.0	 the maximum amount of <strong>CPU</strong> or memory that a container may use. Requests reserve a certain amount of <strong>CPU</strong>:\r\n      - name: sample-container\r\n        resources:\r\n          requests:\r\n            <strong>cpu</strong>: 0\r\n            memory: 0\r\n          limits:\r\n            <strong>cpu</strong>: 0.25\r\n            memory: 4Gi\r\n To calculate	62.3233	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:12:42.531832	2026-01-31 17:12:42.531832	0	ed5c4e1c4772f6d0d500b87b716d4306
11534	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f94078b347a456583d373803846d43e2	Determine remaining resources in an Intelligent Infrastructure (II) resource pool	Table of contents\nOverview\nVM Information report\nPool Information report\nUse the reports\nOverview\nTo determine whether you have sufficient resources in your\nIntelligent Infrastructure\n(II) VM's resource pool to expand resources on one or more of your VMs, you can generate one or both of the following reports in the II Self-Service environment.\nVM Information report\nInitiating a VM Information report creates a CSV document containing all VMs that belong to a particular organization. It will include various data about each VM such as number of CPUs, amount of memory and storage allocated, type of backups configured, OS, IP(s), and some additional metadata. To generate the report:\nLog into the self-service portal; see\nAccess the Intelligent Infrastructure self-service portal\n.\nIf prompted, select\nService Broker\n, then the\nConsume\ntab, and then\nCatalog\n.\nYou'll see tiles representing different actions. Look for one labeled "Generate VM Information", and select\nRequest\n.\nIf you are a member of more than one organization (labeled "Business Group" in the application), you will be prompted to select the desired organization to run this report for. The resulting report will reflect the VMs belonging to the organization specified.\nWhen the form loads, if desired, specify an email address to receive the file; the default is the contact address of the organization.\nChoose\nSubmit\n. The system will generate the report and email it to the address you specified.\nPool Information report\nInitiating a Pool Information report creates a CSV document containing all resource pools that belong to a particular organization. It will include data about each pool such as\nCPU_Maximum_Quota\n(total available CPUs for the resource pool) and\nCPU_Current_Usage\n(number of CPUs actually allocated to active VMs). It will include similar data for memory and storage, and some additional metadata. To generate the report:\nLog into the self-service portal; see\nAccess the Intelligent Infrastructure self-service portal\n.\nIf prompted, select\nService Broker\n, then the\nConsume\ntab, and then\nCatalog\n.\nYou'll see tiles representing different actions. Look for one labeled "Generate Pool Information", and select\nRequest\n.\nIf you are a member of more than one organization (labeled "Business Group" in the application), you will be prompted to select the desired organization to run this report for. The resulting report will reflect the pools belonging to the organization specified.\nWhen the form loads, if desired, specify an email address to receive the file; the default is the contact address of the organization.\nChoose\nSubmit\n. The system will generate the report and email it to the address you specified.\nUse the reports\nThe Pool Information report alone will help you identify any resource constraints. It can aid in general resource expansion planning on an annual, semi-annual, or monthly basis.\nHowever, by combining the two together, you can do more specific resource planning. For example, using the VM report, you can identify the resource pool to which a VM belongs. Then, by using the Pool report, you can determine whether there is sufficient remaining capacity for a specific needed resource expansion on the VM.	KB0026082	KB0026082 v1.0	Table of contents Overview VM Information report Pool Information report Use the reports Overview To determine whether you have sufficient resources in your Intelligent Infrastructure (II) VM's resource pool to expand resources on one or more of your VMs, you can generate one or both of the following reports in the II Self-Service environment. V	36.9873		2026-01-31 17:11:20.125214	2026-01-31 17:12:47.06381	0	f94078b347a456583d373803846d43e2
11549	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5a71af703356e6dc04e605570e5c7b6a	Self-service provisioning in Intelligent Infrastructure (II)	Table of contents\nAbout the fiscal model\nResource pools\nRequests\nCost summary calculations\nExample\nApproval\nBilling\nAccount changes\nPeriodic notices\nManage VMs\nQuestions\nAbout the fiscal model\nTo enable self-service provisioning within the Intelligent Infrastructure (II) environment, CPU, RAM, and disk resources will be associated with resource pools rather than individual virtual machines (VMs). This model will allow II VM administrators to create, modify, expand, clone, and delete VMs at their own discretion.\nThis resource pool model will require fiscal approvers to approve resource pools rather than individual VMs. Teams can then manage additions, deletions and other changes to their VMs on their own, as long as they do not exceed the approved resource limits.\nPricing\n2025-2026 rates:\nAllocated CPU:\n$38/vCPU/year\nAllocated RAM:\n$9.75/GB/year\nAllocated fiber channel attached disk storage:\n$0.28/GB/year\nConsumed DPS space:\n$0.15/GB/year\nPools\nMoving forward, II will treat VMs as belonging to resource pools. A resource pool will represent an allocation of resources that a team can use across as many VMs as needed, enabling VM administrators to create VMs, allocate resources to them, adjust resources as needed to meet performance demands, and delete VMs. The capacity of a resource pool enables self-service within a known maximum expenditure.\nNote:\nYou will be charged only for the resources you use. Your costs will fluctuate based on actual usage each month (with an absolute maximum of the approved pool size).\nResource pools\nResource pools let you group virtual machines (VMs) together and allocate a fixed amount of resources (such as CPUs, memory, and storage) for them to share. For example, a resource pool with 8 CPUs, 32 GB of memory, and 400 GB of storage could support:\n8 VMs with 1 CPU, 4 GB of memory, and 50 GB of storage each, or\n2 VMs with 4 CPUs, 16 GB of memory, and 200 GB of storage each, or\n1 large VM using the entire pool, or\nAny combination that fits within the pool's total resources\nEach resource pool is tied to a specific billing account. This means you will need a separate pool for each account you want to bill. A VM can belong to only one resource pool, and therefore only one account, at a time. If you need to split billing across multiple accounts, you must create separate resource pools and assign different VMs to each.\nThere is some flexibility. If an account runs out of funds, you can update a resource pool to charge a different account; however, keep in mind that billing occurs on the first of each month. You also can move a VM to a different resource pool if needed.\nNote:\nOnce you reach the allocated limit for a particular resource (such as CPU, RAM, or disk), additional resources will not be available to any of the VMs in that pool until you either reduce or retire resources on another VM in the pool, or request that the pool's allocation for that resource be increased.\nRequests\nA member of a particular organization will request resource pools be created and modified; see more about organizations in the\nResource Allocation Center\n(RAC) system. Requesters will specify their estimate of the maximum resource use for the year in terms of number of CPUs, GB of memory, and TB of storage. Requesters should anticipate potential growth over the course of the year in order to avoid having to expand the capacity of the pool during the year. Expansion will require a new request and fiscal approval. Within the RAC system, fiscal approvers will be provided the maximum cost for that pool assuming all resources are consumed; however,\nyou will only be charged for the resources that are actually consumed.\nFor example, if you approve a pool for 10 CPUs and only actually use two, you will only be charged for two.\nRequests will consist of the following:\nLabel:\nThis is a name that will be easily identifiable by your group. At a minimum, we recommend that it contain the data center on which this pool will reside and the funding source (the account) to which VMs in this pool will be billed.\nData Center:\nA pool must be tied to a specific data center. This allows you to control where the VM is hosted.\nCPUs:\nMaximum number of CPUs that should be available for various VMs in this pool to use.\nMemory:\nMaximum amount of memory in gigabytes (GB) that should be available for various VMs in this pool to use.\nStorage:\nMaximum amount of storage in terabytes (TB) that should be available for various VMs in this pool to use.\nVLAN IDs:\nComma-separated list of VLAN IDs that your organization manages that should be available for VMs in this pool to use.\nData Protection Services (DPS):\nIndicate whether the VMs in this pool are eligible for CommVault AllDisk backup services; see\nIU's Data Protection Service (DPS)\nand\nDetermine the type of data protection used for your Intelligent Infrastructure (II) virtual machines\n.\nCost summary calculations\nOn the request form and the fiscal approval page, there is a section that presents a cost summary for the request. This "Maximum Annual Cost Summary" represents the maximum annual potential cost that this pool could represent.\nCPUs:\nCPU cost is calculated by taking the number of CPUs requested * the current annual rate per CPU.\nMemory:\nMemory cost is calculated by taking the number of GB of memory requested * the current annual rate per GB.\nStorage:\nStorage cost is calculated by taking the number of GB of storage requested * the current annual rate per GB.\nData protection (DPS):\nA DPS cost would appear only if AllDisks backups are requested to be available for a particular Resource Pool. The cost is calculated by taking the number of GB of storage requested * the current annual rate per GB for DPS backups. Also note that this total would apply only if all VMs in this resource pool used AllDisks backups. VMs that do not use AllDisks backups would not incur a DPS charge.\nSee\nExample\n, below, for a demonstration of how these calculations would be carried out with actual numbers. Costs will fluctuate based upon actual usage each month, with an absolute maximum of the approved pool size.\nExample\nThe following example uses 2025-26 charge rates. A request is submitted for a pool consisting of 20 CPUs, 50 GB of memory, and 1 TB (1024 GB) of storage, with DPS enabled. The resulting Maximum Annual Cost Summary would look like this.\nItem\nSubtotal\nCPUs\n$760.00\nMemory\n$487.50\nStorage\n$286.72\nData protection\n$153.60\nEstimated total\n$1,687.82\nCPUs:\n20 CPUs * $38/CPU = $760\nMemory:\n50 GB memory * $9.75/GB = $487.50\nStorage:\n1 TB (1024 GB) storage * $0.28/GB = $286.62\nDPS:\n1 TB (1024 GB) storage * $0.15/GB = $153.60\nThe estimate reflects the annual maximum cost if all requested resources are fully utilized within the pool. This estimate also assumes the entire storage capacity is using DPS. This estimate should be treated as an absolute maximum, where costs will fluctuate based on actual usage each month.\nApproval\nAll new resource pool requests as well as changes to existing pools must be approved by the appropriate fiscal officer prior to the pool being created or modified. Fiscal officers will be notified by the Kuali Enterprise Workflow system that they have action items to approve. An approval page similar to the RAC resource pool will be displayed, listing all the resource maximums as well as maximum annual cost summary, but will contain\nApprove\nand\nDisapprove\nbuttons at the bottom. The cost summary reflects the maximum cost if all requested resources are fully utilized within the pool. This estimate also assumes the entire storage capacity is using DPS. As such, the estimate should be treated as an absolute maximum where costs will fluctuate below that maximum based on actual usage each month.\nBilling\nEvery day, the resources allocated to each VM (as well as any storage used by AllDisks VM backups) in Intelligent Infrastructure are counted and recorded with the Resource Pool the VM is associated with that day. In addition, a charge is calculated based on a 1/365th of the annual costs reflected above. Then, on the first of each month, that data for the previous month is tallied for each Resource Pool and calculated as a total amount to charge for the month. This resulting charge will be billed against the account associated with the Resource Pool. The sum of 12 months worth of resource allocations should never represent a sum greater than the maximum annual cost estimate.\nAccount changes\nIn the past, account changes could take place by requiring fiscal officers to instruct the business office to modify the intended billing account. However, resource pools dictate the billing configuration for the VMs associated with them. This means VM admins are able to control how VMs are billed. In order to update billing, either the resource pool needs to be updated to reflect the new account, or the desired VMs need to be moved to a different resource pool with the correct account. Thus, if the billing account for a VM needs to change, fiscal officers should work with VM admins to make this happen.\nPeriodic notices\nAnnual re-attestation\nIn an effort to keep fiscal officers informed about the charges hitting their accounts, II resource pools will undergo an annual re-attestation. This involves the RAC system automatically generating a new workflow acknowledgment action. This process will take place at the beginning of the spring semester, prior to budget construction.\nFY 25-26: February 24, 2025\nFY 24-25: February 5, 2024\nFY 23-24: February 6, 2023\nFiscal officers for accounts associated with II resource pools will receive a Kuali workflow action item for each associated resource pool. For example, if five resource pools use the same account, the fiscal officer will receive five action items. Each action item will take the fiscal officer to an RAC document that displays the projected costs of the given resource pool.\nNote:\nAny resource pools created or modified within 90 days of the re-attestation date will\nnot\nbe included, as the creation/update was recent enough to warrant bypassing this reminder.\nThese re-attestation acknowledgments will not block new modifications should VM admins need to expand the limits of a resource pool. This process gives both VM admins and fiscal officers the opportunity to verify that all VMs and resources are still necessary with appropriate funding sources.\nExpiring accounts\nEach resource pool is associated with a funding account. Most accounts have an expiration date associated – whether due to the end of a grant, the end of revenue source (project or event), or merely as a forced review point.\nSixty days prior to the expiration date of a funding account, any resource pools associated with that account will notified about the upcoming expiration. This notification will go to the email address associated with the owning organization. It will note the upcoming date of the expiration of the account, indicate that this will impede billing to process, and will encourage them to contact the fiscal manager of the relevant account to either postpone the expiration date, or to find a new funding source account to use - including instructions on how to update the resource pool to use the new account.\nIf the admins of the resource pool do not make any changes, it will continue to send reminder notices every seven days until the pool is updated or until the account expires.\nNote:\nSince billing is performed in arrears, deactivating a pool within a month of the expiration date does not eliminate the billing that will take place on the 1st of the next month. So if, for example, an account expires on August 31, deactivating the pool on August 3 will still incur a bill on September 1 (assuming there were any VMs in existence during that period of August 1 - August 31). So if the account is not updated, billing will still be impeded.\nManage VMs\nThe RAC system is primarily used to manage resource pools. Once you have requested one or more resource pools and they have been approved and created, you can manage VMs with the self-service portal. See the following:\nAccess the Intelligent Infrastructure self-service portal\nRequest a new VM in Intelligent Infrastructure (II) self-service portal\nInteract with a VM in the Intelligent Infrastructure (II) self-service portal\nDetermine remaining resources in an Intelligent Infrastructure (II) resource pool\nQuestions\nIf you have additional questions or concerns, contact the Storage and Virtualization team at\nsav-request@iu.edu\n.	KB0023220	KB0023220 v5.0	 To enable self-service provisioning within the Intelligent Infrastructure (II) environment, <strong>CPU</strong>, RAM: Allocated <strong>CPU</strong>: $38/vCPU/year Allocated RAM: $9.75/GB/year Allocated fiber channel attached disk support: 8 VMs with 1 <strong>CPU</strong>, 4 GB of memory, and 50 GB of storage each, or 2 VMs with 4 CPUs, 16 GB	25.239		2026-01-31 17:12:47.06381	2026-01-31 17:12:51.2053	0	5a71af703356e6dc04e605570e5c7b6a
11558	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6668615b1bb0565416c02fc4bd4bcb6c	If your computer beeps and fails to boot	Table of Contents\nOverview\nAward\nAMI (American Megatrends)\nAST BIOS\nCompaq\nPhoenix\nIBM Desktop\nIBM ThinkPad\nMylex\nQuadtel BIOS\nOverview\nOn startup, computers perform a Power-on Self Test, commonly referred to as a POST. If problems are found while booting, you can usually diagnose them using the error codes displayed on the screen. However, if nothing displays on the screen, the computer might use sounds (that is, beep codes) to tell you what is wrong. Many of these errors indicate hardware problems that a service technician should fix. However, some errors are easily fixed. For example, keyboard controller errors often mean that your keyboard isn't plugged in.\nFollowing are beep codes for some common brands of BIOS for PCs. For information about Mac beep codes or startup tones, see Apple Support's\nAbout Mac computer startup tones\n.\nNote:\nMany BIOSes will have different beep codes even if they appear to be from the same manufacturer. This is because motherboard manufacturers reprogram codes to reflect their own customizations. You can often go to the website for the motherboard's manufacturer to find the updated codes, but you may find cases where the manufacturer simply does not document the changes.\nFor more about beeps and error codes, see ComputerHope.com's\nComputer POST and beep codes\n.\nAward\nAward BIOSes typically use more screen errors than beep codes.\nFor Award BIOSes manufactured by Phoenix Technologies, note the following from the Phoenix Technologies AwardBIOS Error Messages page:\n"The only AwardBIOS beep code indicates that a video error has occurred and the BIOS cannot initialize the video screen to display any additional information. This beep code consists of a single long beep followed by two short beeps. Any other beeps are probably RAM (Random Access Memory) problems."\nAward BIOSes not manufactured under Phoenix Technologies use the following beeps; this information is from the BIOS Central Award BIOS Beep Codes page:\nBeeps\nMeaning\n1 long, 2 short\nVideo adapter error: Bad or improperly seated video card\nRepeating beeps\nMemory error: Bad or improperly seated RAM\n1 long, 3 short\nBad video RAM or video card not present\nHigh-frequency beeps\nOverheated CPU: Check fans\nRepeating high/low beeps\nCPU: Improperly seated or defective CPU\nAMI (American Megatrends)\nThe information below is from the BIOS Central AMI BIOS Beep Codes page:\nBeeps\nMeaning\n1 short\nDRAM refresh failure\n2 short\nParity circuit failure in the first 64 KB of RAM: Likely a bad RAM IC, or possibly one of the hardware tests has failed\n3 short\nMemory failure in the first 64 KB of RAM: Likely a bad RAM IC\n4 short\nSystem timer failure: Bad clock IC or bad RAM in the first bank of memory\n5 short\nProcessor failure: The CPU has failed.\n6 short\nKeyboard controller Gate A20 error: The keyboard controller has failed. Due to its integration into the system board, you usually must replace the motherboard.\n7 short\nVirtual mode exception error: CPU has generated an exception error; bad CPU or motherboard\n8 short\nDisplay memory Read/Write test failure: Bad video card\n9 short\nROM BIOS checksum failure: BIOS is defective\n10 short\nCMOS shutdown Read/Write error\n11 short\nCache Memory error: Specifically, the L2 cache is bad.\n1 long, 2 short\nVideo system failure: Error in video card BIOS, or a horizontal retrace has failed\n1 long, 3 short\nConventional/Extended memory failure: RAM is bad\n1 long, 8 short\nDisplay/Retrace test failed: Video adapter is defective or missing\nAST BIOS\nThe following information is from the BIOS Central AST BIOS Beep Codes page:\nBeeps\nMeaning\n1 short\nCPU register test failure: The CPU has failed.\n2 short\nKeyboard controller buffer failure: The keyboard controller has failed.\n3 short\nKeyboard controller reset failure: The keyboard controller has failed or the motherboard circuitry is faulty.\n4 short\nKeyboard communication failure: Bad keyboard controller or keyboard\n5 short\nKeyboard input failure: The keyboard controller IC has failed.\n6 short\nSystem board chipset failure: The chipset on the motherboard has failed. Replace the motherboard.\n9 short\nBIOS ROM checksum error: The BIOS ROM has failed. If possible, replace the BIOS on the motherboard.\n10 short\nSystem timer test failure: The system clock IC has failed.\n11 short\nASIC failure: Motherboard circuitry has failed. Replace the motherboard.\n12 short\nCMOS RAM shutdown register failure: The real-time clock/CMOS IC failed. Replace the CMOS or motherboard.\n1 long\nDMA controller 0 failure: The DMA controller IC for channel 0 has failed. If possible, replace the IC.\n1 long, 1 short\nDMA controller 1 failure: The DMA controller IC for channel 1 has failed. If possible, replace the IC.\n1 long, 2 short\nVideo vertical retrace failure: The video adapter has probably failed. Replace the video adapter.\n1 long, 3 short\nVideo memory test failure: The video adapter's memory has failed. Replace the video adapter.\n1 long, 4 short\nVideo adapter failure: The video adapter has failed. Replace the video adapter.\n1 long, 5 short\n64 KB memory failure: A failure has occurred in the base 64 KB of memory. If possible, replace the RAM IC.\n1 long, 6 short\nUnable to load interrupt vectors: The BIOS was unable to load the interrupt vectors into memory.\n1 long, 7 short\nUnable to initialize video: This is a video problem. Replace the video adapter first. If the problem persists, replace the motherboard.\n1 long, 8 short\nVideo memory failure: There is a failure in the video memory. Replace the video adapter first. If the problem persists, replace the motherboard.\nCompaq\nThe following information is from the BIOS Central Compaq BIOS Beep Codes page:\nBeeps\nMeaning\n1 short\nNo error: The system is booting properly.\n1 long, 1 short\nBIOS ROM checksum error: The contents of the BIOS ROM do not match the expected contents. If possible, reload the BIOS from the PAQ.\n2 short\nGeneral error: No specifics available on what this code means\n1 long, 2 short\nVideo error: Check the video adapter and make sure it's seated properly. If possible, replace the video adapter.\n7 beeps (1 long, 1 short, 1 long, 1 short, pause, 1 long, 1 short, 1 short)\nAGP video: The AGP video card is faulty. Reseat the card or replace it outright. This beep pertains to Compaq Deskpro systems.\nContinuous beep\nMemory error: Bad RAM; replace and test\n1 short, 2 long\nBad RAM: Reseat RAM, then retest; replace RAM if failure continues.\nPhoenix\nThe following information applies to Phoenix BIOS Q3.07 or 4.x. This information is from the\nPhoenix BIOS beep codes section\nof the\nComputerHope.com Computer POST and beep codes page\n. Dashes indicate pauses between beeps:\nBeepsMeaning 1-1-1-3Verify real mode.1-1-2-1Get CPU type.1-1-2-3Initialize system hardware.1-1-3-1\nInitialize chipset registers with initial POST values.\n1-1-3-2Set in POST flag.1-1-3-3Initialize CPU registers.1-1-4-1\nInitialize cache to initial POST values.\n1-1-4-3Initialize I/O.1-2-1-1Initialize Power Management.1-2-1-2\nLoad alternate registers with initial POST values.\n1-2-1-3Jump to UserPatch0.1-2-2-1\nInitialize keyboard controller.\n1-2-2-3BIOS ROM checksum1-2-3-18254 timer initialization1-2-3-3\n8237 DMA controller initialization\n1-2-4-1\nReset Programmable Interrupt Controller.\n1-3-1-1Test DRAM refresh.1-3-1-3\nTest 8742 Keyboard Controller.\n1-3-2-1\nSet ES segment to register to 4 GB.\n1-3-3-128 Autosize DRAM.1-3-3-3Clear 512 KB base RAM.1-3-4-1\nTest 512 KB base address lines.\n1-3-4-3Test 512 KB base memory.1-4-1-3Test CPU bus-clock frequency.1-4-2-4Reinitialize the chipset.1-4-3-1Shadow system BIOS ROM.1-4-3-2Reinitialize the cache.1-4-3-3Autosize cache.1-4-4-1\nConfigure advanced chipset registers.\n1-4-4-2\nLoad alternate registers with CMOS values.\n2-1-1-1Set Initial CPU speed.2-1-1-3Initialize interrupt vectors.2-1-2-1Initialize BIOS interrupts.2-1-2-3Check ROM copyright notice.2-1-2-4\nInitialize manager for PCI Options ROMs.\n2-1-3-1\nCheck video configuration against CMOS.\n2-1-3-2\nInitialize PCI bus and devices.\n2-1-3-3\nInitialize all video adapters in system.\n2-1-4-1Shadow video BIOS ROM.2-1-4-3Display copyright notice.2-2-1-1Display CPU type and speed.2-2-1-3Test keyboard.2-2-2-1Set key click if enabled.2-2-2-356 Enable keyboard.2-2-3-1\nTest for unexpected interrupts.\n2-2-3-3\nDisplay message "Press F2 to enter SETUP".\n2-2-4-1\nTest RAM between 512 and 640 KB.\n2-3-1-1Test expanded memory.2-3-1-3\nTest extended memory address lines.\n2-3-2-1Jump to UserPatch1.2-3-2-3\nConfigure advanced cache registers.\n2-3-3-1\nEnable external and CPU caches.\n2-3-3-3Display external cache size.2-3-4-1Display shadow message.2-3-4-3\nDisplay non-disposable segments.\n2-4-1-1Display error messages.2-4-1-3\nCheck for configuration errors.\n2-4-2-1Test real-time clock.2-4-2-3Check for keyboard errors.2-4-4-1\nSet up hardware interrupts vectors.\n2-4-4-3Test coprocessor if present.3-1-1-1Disable onboard I/O ports.3-1-1-3\nDetect and install external RS232 ports.\n3-1-2-1\nDetect and install external parallel ports.\n3-1-2-3\nRe-initialize onboard I/O ports.\n3-1-3-1Initialize BIOS Data Area.3-1-3-3\nInitialize Extended BIOS Data Area.\n3-1-4-1Initialize floppy controller.3-2-1-1\nInitialize hard-disk controller.\n3-2-1-2\nInitialize local-bus hard-disk controller.\n3-2-1-3Jump to UserPatch2.3-2-2-1Disable A20 address line.3-2-2-3\nClear huge ES segment register.\n3-2-3-1Search for option ROMs.3-2-3-3Shadow option ROMs.3-2-4-1Set up Power Management.3-2-4-3Enable hardware interrupts.3-3-1-1Set time of day.3-3-1-3Check key lock.3-3-3-1Erase "F2" message.3-3-3-3Scan for\nF2\nkeystroke.3-3-4-1Enter SETUP.3-3-4-3Clear in-POST flag.3-4-1-1Check for errors.3-4-1-3\nPOST complete; prepare to boot operating system.\n3-4-2-1One beep.3-4-2-3Check password (optional).3-4-3-1\nClear global descriptor table.\n3-4-4-1Clear parity checkers.3-4-4-3Clear screen (optional).3-4-4-4\nCheck virus and backup reminders.\n4-1-1-1Try to boot with INT 19.4-2-1-1Interrupt handler error.4-2-1-3Unknown interrupt error.4-2-2-1Pending interrupt error.4-2-2-3Initialize option ROM error.4-2-3-1Shutdown error.4-2-3-3Extended Block Move.4-2-4-1Shutdown 10 error.4-3-1-3Initialize the chipset.4-3-1-4Initialize refresh counter.4-3-2-1Check for Forced Flash.4-3-2-2Check HW status of ROM.4-3-2-3BIOS ROM is okay.4-3-2-4Do a complete RAM test.4-3-3-1Do OEM initialization.4-3-3-2\nInitialize interrupt controller.\n4-3-3-3Read in bootstrap code.4-3-3-4Initialize all vectors.4-3-4-1Boot the Flash program.4-3-4-2Initialize the boot device.4-3-4-3Boot code was read okay.\nIBM Desktop\nIBM beeps and their meanings:\nBeeps\nMeaning\nNo beep\nNo power, loose expansion card (ISA, PCI, or AGP), a short, or an improperly grounded motherboard\n1 short\nSystem okay\n1 long\nVideo/display problem; video card incorrectly seated or defective\n2 short\nPOST Error displayed on monitor\n3 long\nProblem with 3270 keyboard card\n1 long, 1 short\nProblem with system board\n1 long, 2 short\nProblem with display adapter (MDA, CGA)\n1 long, 3 short\nProblem with EGA\nRepeating short beeps\nProblem with power supply or system board\nContinuous beep\nProblem with power supply or system board\nIBM ThinkPad\nThinkPad beeps and their meanings:\nBeeps\nMeaning\nContinuous beeping\nSystem board failure\n1 beep with blank display\nLCD connector problem, LCD backlight inverter failure, video adapter failure, or LCD assembly failure\n1 beep w/message "Unable to access boot source"\nBoot device failure or bad system board\n1 long, 2 short\nSystem board, video adapter, or LCD assembly failure\n1 long, 4 short\nLow battery voltage\n1 beep every second\nLow battery voltage\n2 short w/message\nRead the error message on the display\n2 short with blank display\nSystem board failure\nMylex\nMylex beeps and their meanings:\nBeeps\nMeaning\n1\nNormal boot: No problems\n2\nVideo adapter error: Video adapter is not seated or is faulty\n3\nKeyboard controller error: Keyboard controller IC bad\n4\nKeyboard error: Keyboard itself might be bad, or the controller IC on the motherboard\n5\nPIC 0 error: The programmable interrupt controller IC is bad\n6\nPIC 1 error: Same as above\n7\nDMA page register error: DMA controller IC is bad\n8\nRAM refresh error\n9\nRAM data error\n10\nRAM parity error\n11\nDMA controller 0 error: DMA controller IC for channel 0 has failed\n12\nCMOS RAM error: Bad CMOS RAM\n13\nDMA controller 1 error: DMA controller IC for channel 1 has failed\n14\nCMOS RAM battery error: Dead CMOS battery; can usually be replaced\n15\nCMOS RAM checksum error: CMOS RAM failed\n16\nBIOS ROM checksum error: BIOS RAM failed\nQuadtel BIOS\nThe following information is from the BIOS Central Quadtel BIOS Beep Codes page:\n1 short\nNormal boot: System is booting normally.\n2 short\nCMOS IC error: The CMOS RAM is faulty. Replace the IC if possible.\n1 long, 2 short\nVideo failure: The video adapter is faulty. Reseat the video adapter or replace the adapter if possible.\n1 long, 3 short\nPeripheral controller error: One or more of the system peripheral controllers is bad. Replace the controllers and retest.	KB0026026	KB0026026 v2.0	 Overheated <strong>CPU</strong>: Check fans Repeating high/low beeps <strong>CPU</strong>: Improperly seated or defective <strong>CPU</strong> AMI (American of memory 5 short Processor failure: The <strong>CPU</strong> has failed. 6 short Keyboard controller Gate A20 error replace the motherboard. 7 short Virtual mode exception error: <strong>CPU</strong> has generated an exception error; bad	8.3601		2026-01-31 17:12:54.677145	2026-01-31 17:12:54.677145	0	6668615b1bb0565416c02fc4bd4bcb6c
11560	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d2bfac77476456583d373803846d43d5	Build and deploy a Timekeeping kiosk	Table of contents\nOverview\nRecommended build\nSoftware (minimum)\nHardware (minimum)\nOverview\nIU departments are responsible for maintaining their own\nTimekeeping\nkiosk. This information outlines how to build and deploy a kiosk.\nRecommended build\nSoftware (minimum):\nOperating System:\nWindows 10 Enterprise for 64-bit/x64-based processor (or higher version); Windows 10 Enterprise for 32-bit/x86-based processor is also acceptable\nBrowser:\nMagTek Swipe Reader:\nYou must use the Microsoft Edge browser in compatibility mode.\nSchlage MT20 Proximity Smart Chip Reader:\nThe kiosk is browser-independent, as long as the system can be locked down by the GPO.\nHardware (minimum):\nProcessor:\nIntel Core i3-7100T CPU @ 3.40GHz, 3.41GHz (or better)\nInstalled RAM:\n8 GB; usable RAM 7.89 GB (or better)\nSystem:\nDell OptiPlex 3050 (or better), small form factor mounted to back of monitor\nMonitor:\nStandard Dell 15-inch; if touchscreen, make sure the GPO is configured correctly\nPeripherals:\nStandard keyboard and mouse, preferably wired to OptiPlex CPU (although you could deploy a wireless keyboard and mouse)\nCard reader options:\nUSB MagTek swipe reader\n:\nYou should no longer order the swipe reader; instead refer to the proximity reader below.\nSee\nConfigure USB card readers for Timekeeping kiosks\n.\nUSB Schlage MT20 proximity reader\n:\nConfigured with Indiana University's custom encryption key (set at vendor/integrator) and to read smart chip only (flash card-CE-050-818); see\nConfigure USB card readers for Timekeeping kiosks\n.\nThe card is 13.56 MHz ISO 14443A MIFARE®  DESFire EV1.\nFor assistance in purchasing proximity card readers,\nemail IU Public Safety\n.	KB0023032	KB0023032 v1.0	-7100T <strong>CPU</strong> @ 3.40GHz, 3.41GHz (or better) Installed RAM: 8 GB; usable RAM 7.89 GB (or better) System and mouse, preferably wired to OptiPlex <strong>CPU</strong> (although you could deploy a wireless keyboard and mouse	6.897	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits, km-aud-uco-payroll	2026-01-31 17:12:58.032363	2026-01-31 17:12:58.032363	0	d2bfac77476456583d373803846d43d5
11550	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=311070bf476456583d373803846d430d	Request a new VM in Intelligent Infrastructure (II) self-service portal	Log into the self-service portal; see\nAccess the Intelligent Infrastructure self-service portal\n.\nIf prompted, select\nService Broker\n,\nCatalog\n, and then\nRequest\nwithin the "Create New VM" dialog box.\nFill out the required fields for the VM request. Any field marked with a red "*" is required. Select\nSubmit\nwhen finished.\nNotes:\nDo not use hard returns on any fields on the request.\nUse custom fields 1-5 if you wish to record any additional information with this virtual machine. These fields will populate custom attributes on the VM in vCenter.\nThe "Pool Utilization" field will populate once you select the pool this VM is to bill against. Current and maximum CPU usage, memory (in GB), and Disk (in GB) are displayed on the form for reference.\nImportant:\nApplication tier:\nWithin II, the virtual server system administrator has the ability to align system criticality with a tier rating. University Information Technology Services (UITS) services delivered with II leverages the tier to prioritize recovery operations in the event of a disaster recovery. All system administration teams are encouraged to utilize the tier settings to align with service criticality. It is understood this tier rating could change based upon the University calendar, and the tier rating can be modified at any time based upon University business requirements. Office of Vice President for Information Technology (OVPIT) division leadership manages the assignment of tier for UITS services.\nApplication tier [0-3] definitions:\nTier 0 is the highest level of services, to be deployed geographically independently and able to deliver services without operational impact. Examples of these services would be Active Directory, IU Login, and Domain Name Services (DNS). If all computing services experience an interruption of services, ensuring Tier 0 services are operational will be prioritized over other service tiers.\nTier 1 represents services that are critically important to the end user community to deliver business functions to operate university. Example services Human Resource applications, Student Systems applications, and Finance applications. Priority of recovery order of these services would follow Tier 0.\nTier 2 represents services whose primary role is to assist in the support and management of Tier 0 and Tier 1 services. Priority of recovery would follow Tier 0 and Tier 1.\nTier 3 is the default tier rating when creating a new server within II. It represents the lowest priority service for recovery operations.\nYou may view the current status of your requests in the\nConsume\ntab under "Deployments".\nYou can also search VM requests by using the search field, or by selecting the blue filter icon and using various filters.\nOnce the VM has been created, it will appear in the\nConsume\ntab under "Virtual Machines".\nUse the search field toward the top to search for a VM.\nTo refresh the view, use the refresh icon in the upper right of the page.\nIf there are any errors, read and attempt to correct.\nFor questions or issues with your VM deployment,\ncontact the Storage and Virtualization team\n.	KB0025065	KB0025065 v1.0	 you select the pool this VM is to bill against. Current and maximum <strong>CPU</strong> usage, memory (in GB), and Disk	4.7849		2026-01-31 17:12:47.06381	2026-01-31 17:13:29.172012	0	311070bf476456583d373803846d430d
11562	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ef40b0f347a456583d373803846d4337	Prepare for a new virtual machine using Intelligent Infrastructure	Table of contents\nView existing organizations\nCreate a new organization\nView existing organizations\nThe first thing the department will need to decide is whether they have existing organizations that they wish to use to provide access to the VMs.\nNote:\nThis is\nonly\naccess to manage the VMs in II. In our experience, only some people need access to manage the VM itself (for example, initial install of the OS, modify CPU/Memory, etc.), but several/many more people need access to the OS (via SSH/RDP/etc.). Here, we're interested in the former, who will manage the VMs directly as administrators.\nTo see if any of the ADS groups listed fit the access you need:\nRAC Organizations\nCreate a new organization\nIf none of the existing options fit your needs, you will need to find or create a different ADS group that contains what you need. Then you can use the following form to create a new organization:\nCreate RAC Organization\nThis will require you specify a name for the organization, the ADS group, and a group email address (not a single person's account) in the event SAV needs to contact someone regarding the VMs.\nOnce the organization is created, you can start using the\nResource Allocation Center\nto create resource pools. For more, see\nAbout the Resource Allocation Center (RAC)\n.\nFor this, you just need to know:\nWhat datacenter (Indianapolis or Bloomington) these VMs should run in\nThe maximum CPU, memory, and storage you want the VMs to be able to use\nWhether the VMs should be able to use AllDisks backups or just the default OSDisk (see\nIU's Data Protection Service (DPS)\nfor more information)\nWhat VLANs the VMs will need to be able to access.  If your department or group does not have a dedicated data center VLAN, you can request one from the\nCampus Network Engineering group\n.\nWhat account information should be used to bill\nAfter the fiscal approval is given, SAV will create the resource pool.  To start creating VMs,\nuse the self-service portal\n.\nWhen you log in to the self-service portal, you can create a VM as described in\nRequest a new VM in Intelligent Infrastructure (II) self-service portal\n. You can interact with the VM as outlined in\nInteract with a VM in the Intelligent Infrastructure (II) self-service portal\n.\nFor questions or problems, email Storage and Virtualization (\nsav-request@iu.edu\n).	KB0026235	KB0026235 v1.0	 of the OS, modify <strong>CPU</strong>/Memory, etc.), but several/many more people need access to the OS (via SSH in The maximum <strong>CPU</strong>, memory, and storage you want the VMs to be able to use Whether the VMs should be able	6.7636		2026-01-31 17:13:01.454716	2026-01-31 17:13:01.454716	0	ef40b0f347a456583d373803846d4337
11563	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1baec25447f6f6d0d500b87b716d4378	Spring Boot quickstart guide	This article is intended as a quickstart guide to help you get a Spring Boot application up and running on Kubernetes. It is not a replacement for the other documentation on this site; it's intended to serve as a reminder of the steps involved in getting an application up quickly.\nDetermine which\ncluster\nyou want to host your application. If you're unsure start with\nes-sandbox\n.\nRequest access to IU's Kubernetes environment\n.\nInstall\nUseful tools for working with Kubernetes clusters\n. At minimum you'll want:\nDocker\nkubectl\nHelm\nkube-login\nGo to your preferred\nHarbor instance\nand\ncreate a robot account\nfor pulling images.\nPackage your application into a Docker image (see\nDocker container best practices\n) and push it to Harbor.\nCreate an image pull secret\ncontaining the robot account's credentials.\nAdd Harbor as a Helm chart repository\nusing the robot credentials.\nPull the\nk8s-boot\nchart (for example,\nhelm pull harbor/k8s-boot\n).\nCreate a Helm values file (\nfull details here\n); for example:\nnameOverride\n: demoapp\nnamespace\n: ua-vpit--enterprise-systems--esimw\nimage\n:\nrepository\n: registry.docker.iu.edu/esimw/demoapp\ntag\n: latest\nreplicaCount\n:\n2\nresources\n:\nrequests\n:\ncpu\n:\n"0"\nmemory\n:\n"0"\nlimits\n:\ncpu\n:\n"1"\nmemory\n:\n"1Gi"\nimagePullSecrets\n:\n-\nname\n: registry-prod\nbootstrapSecret\n:\n"demoapp-config"\nactiveProfiles\n:\n"snd"\ningress\n:\nenabled\n: true\nhosts\n:\n-\nhost\n:\n"demoapp.apps-snd.iu.edu"\nNote:\nThe configuration example above references a\nKubernetes secret\nnamed\ndemoapp-config\n. This would contain sensitive configuration information that shouldn't be placed in version control. Here's an example of what that might look like:\napiVersion:\nv1\nkind:\nSecret\nmetadata:\nnamespace:\nua-vpit--enterprise-systems--esimw\nname:\ndemoapp-config\ntype:\nOpaque\nstringData:\nbootstrap.yml: |-\nspring.cloud.consul.config.acl-token:\n<value here>\nencrypt.key:\n<value here>\nUse kube-login to authenticate to Kuberentes:\nkube-login es-sandbox\nThe secret from a previous step needs to be created in Kubernetes before deploying the app, as it is maintained separately from the rest of the stack:\nkubectl create -f demoapp-\nconfig\n.yaml\nDeploy the app using the Helm chart:\nhelm upgrade demoapp harbor/k8s-boot -f helm-values\n.yaml\n--install\nWatch the application come up:\nkubectl\nget\nall\nNext steps\nThis was a quick manual deployment that set up the bare minimum needed to get something working. You may find the next steps useful from here:\nCheck out the other\nuseful tools\nwhen you're comfortable with the basics.\nMake sure to put everything except your secrets in version control.\nManual deployments are error prone; consider\nconfiguring a deployment pipeline\n.	KB0027189	KB0027189 v4.0	:\r\n     <strong>cpu</strong>: "0"\r\n     memory: "0"\r\n   limits:\r\n     <strong>cpu</strong>: "1"\r\n     memory: "1Gi"\r\n\r\n imagePullSecrets	6.3778	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:13:04.862559	2026-01-31 17:13:04.862559	0	1baec25447f6f6d0d500b87b716d4378
11572	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1bf8014c47a0b694d500b87b716d43f1	Terms of use for the UITS Lifecycle Fund	Table of contents\nOverview\nComputer lifecycle\nNon-standard computer options and requirements\nBundled items\nComputer accessories\nShipping and return of computer equipment\nInformation required when using the UA-VPIT on-hand inventory\nOverview\nUITS has created a fund to pay for UA-VPIT FTE (employees whose salaries are funded out of a 19-XXX-XX account number) to lifecycle their computers over a four-year term. E\nligible people may have one primary computer at a time, unless a documented business or instructional need requires otherwise.\nThis article describes the terms and conditions for the use of these funds.\nComputer lifecycle\nThe lifecycle fund should be used primarily to purchase one of the following computers:\nDell Pro 14 Plus laptop with an Intel Core Ultra 5 processor, 16 GB memory, 512 GB SSD\nDell Pro 16 Plus laptop with an Intel Core Ultra 5 processor, 16 GB memory, 512 GB SSD\nDell Pro Plus Slim desktop with an Intel Core Ultra 5 processor, 16 GB memory, 512 GB SSD\nDell Pro 24 All-in-One desktop with an Intel Core Ultra 5 processor, 16 GB memory, 512 GB SSD\nMacBook Air 15" laptop with a 10‑core CPU, 10‑core GPU Apple M4 chip, 16 GB memory, 512 GB SSD\nNon-standard computer options and requirements\nA selection of non-standard computers is available to those who have computing needs beyond those of standard office applications, PDFs, and web browsing. For those with a legitimate job function (software development, business analytics, video editing, etc.), UITS provides the following computer options:\nDell Pro 14 Plus laptop with an Intel Core Ultra 7 processor, 32 GB memory, 1 TB SSD\nMacBook Air 15" laptop a 10‑core CPU, 10‑core GPU Apple M4 chip, 24 GB RAM, 1 TB SSD\nDell Latitude 7350 detachable (convertible to a tablet) with an i5 processor, 16 GB RAM, 512 GB SSD, and a stylus\nNote:\nIf a non-standard computer is selected, an automated approval process will be triggered and will require the approval from the director (or director level equivalent) of the person for whom this computer is being requested. A bona fide business need for the device will be required.\nBundled items\nLaptop bundles come with the following items:\nDell USB-C docking station\nNote:\nA dock is not included with the selection of a MacBook Air. The MacBook Air listed will not support more than one external monitor. Choose a single 27" or 34" monitor in the "Displays" section below if purchasing the standard Mac.\nDell keyboard/mouse combo\nDell webcam\nDesktops come with the following items:\nDell webcam\nWired keyboard and mouse\nChoice of the following monitors:\nTwo 24" Dell HD Ultrasharp monitors\n27" Dell Video Conferencing monitor\nDell 34" Ultrawide curved USB-C hub monitor\nNo monitor\nNote:\nThe appropriate soundbar will be purchased for the display you select.\nThe 34" and 27" monitors will not come with a docking station because they have built-in USB-C hubs.\nComputer accessories\nThe UITS Lifecycle Fund will pay for computer accessories at any time, except at the end of a computer's lifecycle. The following items are eligible for purchase with the UITS Lifecycle Fund. UITS Finance will periodically run reports/audits on lifecycle purchases and inquire for business purposes in cases of multiple replacement orders for the same individual(s).\nErgonomic mice and keyboards (when requested)\nPower adapters (if outside the one-year warranty replacement period)\nThe following items are not eligible for purchase with the UITS Lifecycle Fund; departmental funds will need to be used to purchase the items below:\nWrist rests and other ergonomic support items\nPrinters\nMonitor stands and brackets\nExtra cables\nPower strips\nUninterrupted power supplies (UPS)\nHeadsets\nShipping and return of computer equipment\nThe UITS Lifecycle Fund will not pay for the shipping of computers and related equipment to an employee working fully remote (from home). The department that employs a person working fully remote is responsible for the payment for shipping computers and computer equipment to the employee's home.\nThe UITS Lifecycle Fund does not pay for computers to be returned from fully remote employees when those employees separate from IU. Payment for shipping equipment back is the responsibility of the employee's department.\nInformation required when using the UA-VPIT on-hand inventory\nUA-VPIT has an inventory of the standard equipment listed above on hand. To use equipment from this inventory, the information below must be provided before the equipment can be provided from the on-hand inventory:\nEmployee's name (who is receiving this equipment)\nEmployee's IU ID number\nDepartment's four-letter code (minimum)\nTicket number of the equipment request\nQuestions regarding this policy may be directed to Alex Baldwin, Manager of Endpoint Lifecycle.	KB0026815	KB0026815 v5.0	 15" laptop with a 10‑core <strong>CPU</strong>, 10‑core GPU Apple M4 chip, 16 GB memory, 512 GB SSD Non-standard computer memory, 1 TB SSD MacBook Air 15" laptop a 10‑core <strong>CPU</strong>, 10‑core GPU Apple M4 chip, 24 GB RAM, 1 TB SSD	5.9667	km-aud-uits, km-aud-el	2026-01-31 17:13:08.296069	2026-01-31 17:13:08.296069	0	1bf8014c47a0b694d500b87b716d43f1
11577	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f159021c473ab6d0d500b87b716d4335	Request access to IU's Kubernetes environment	Table of contents\nRequest Kubernetes access\nAccess to Harbor\nBot access\nRequest Kubernetes access\nIndiana University's Enterprise Kubernetes Environment is a multi-tenant system where multiple teams' workloads run on shared infrastructure. These workloads are logically separated through a Kubernetes construct called a\nnamespace\n. To request a new namespace, submit the\nRequest Kubernetes Access\nform. On the form, you'll need to:\nProvide the name of the cluster on which you'd like your team created; see the list of\nAvailable Kubernetes clusters\n.\nProvide the department, division (optional), and name of your team. These are combined to form a unique identifier for your namespace.\nProvide an\nACM group\ncontaining the users who will be granted access to the resources in your namespace. The ACM group must be\nprovisioned as an ADS security group\nwith membership requiring at least the completion of the\nAcceptable Use Agreement\n. If your workloads require additional compliance requirements (FERPA and/or HRMS Data Use), those requirements should be enforced on the group also.\nIndicate whether you want a Kubernetes service account that can be used in your continuous delivery pipelines. See\nConfigure delivery pipelines\nfor information on how to use a deployer service account.\nIndicate the total amount of\nCPU and memory resources\nrequired to run your applications. See\nConfigure container CPU and memory resources in a namespace with quotas enabled\nfor help choosing the appropriate values.\nProvide a group email address for your team. This will be added to relevant mailing lists to keep you informed about updates and changes. This is necessary only when first requesting access.\nIndicate the highest\ndata classification\nyour workloads will be processing or storing. If you are working with\nRestricted data\nor\nCritical data\n, review the guidelines in\nGuidelines for Kubernetes workloads that interact with sensitive data\n.\nThe information above will be used to create a Kubernetes namespace in the specified cluster(s), and set up access to the Harbor image registry and the LogInsight logging appliance where all application logs are aggregated.\nAccess to Harbor\nHarbor\nis a Docker image registry and\nHelm chart\nrepository. Access to Harbor is required to use IU's Kubernetes environment, but you do not need to use Kubernetes to use the Harbor repository. When you request access to the Kubernetes environment, you will be set up with access to Harbor as well. To get access to Harbor without using Kubernetes, create a pull request in the\nhelm-rbac project\nand set the property\nregistryOnly: true\nin your values file; alternatively, email\ndocker@iu.edu\nwith the following details:\nAn identifier for your team (this must be globally unique; indicate what you want to use and UITS will inform you whether it is available)\nThe name of an\nACM group\ncontaining the users who will be granted access to your artifacts in Harbor. The ACM group must be\nprovisioned as an ADS security group\nwith membership requiring at least the completion of the\nAcceptable Use Agreement\n.\nBot access\nFor pushing images, IU's Harbor image registry supports\nrobot accounts\nthat serve the same purpose. This allows you to create one or more accounts (that are not directly tied to ADS) for managing resources in Harbor. Harbor requires mutli-factor authentication, so setting up a robot account is equivalent to using a Harbor CLI secret for a group account.\nFor accessing Kubernetes, UITS recommends using a set of dedicated\nservice accounts\n. These service accounts can interact with the Kubernetes API and are easier to manage compared to group accounts. Access to the Kubernetes API requires multi-factor authentication, so service accounts are a simpler solution for automated deployments.	KB0027177	KB0027177 v5.0	 amount of <strong>CPU</strong> and memory resources required to run your applications. See Configure container <strong>CPU</strong>	5.7984	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:13:11.70205	2026-01-31 17:13:11.70205	0	f159021c473ab6d0d500b87b716d4335
11585	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7b25d2e03beb6698476d941864e45a4e	Hardware and software requirements for School of Dentistry student laptops	To successfully run the axiUm application and the ExamSoft testing software, these minimum hardware and software requirements are based on the software vendors' recommendations.\nNote:\nTablet PCs, including the Microsoft Surface, will not run the exam software and thus are not recommended for use at this time. Also, Chromebooks are not allowed, since they cannot have full disk encryption and will also not run the exam software.\nMinimum hardware requirements:\nProcessor: 2 GHz Intel Core/Celeron or equivalent x64 processor\nDisplay resolution must be at least 1024 x 768 or higher\nWireless connectivity\nAt least 1 USB port\nAt least 8 GB RAM\nAt least 250 GB hard drive (solid-state drive recommended)\n2 GB free HDD space\nWebcam\nMicrophone\n2 Mbps upload speed\nCPU equivalent to Intel i3 2+ GHZ\nMinimum software requirements:\nMicrosoft Windows 10 Professional, Enterprise, or Education editions (Version 1809 or 1903)\nMacintosh OS X 10. 15 or higher (Catalina)\nAcrobat Reader 11 or DC\nMicrosoft Office 2016, 2019, or Office 365\nFull hard disk encryption software (BitLocker for Windows and FileVault for MAC)\nAnti-virus software\nHealth Technology Services (HTS) Phone Support\nwill provide guidance and assistance to any students who have questions regarding these minimum requirements, or need assistance with configuring the device for use in the school environment. HTS will be able to refer the students to additional resources such as other UITS units or the device manufacturer. For assistance,\ncontact Health Technology Services\n.\nStudents can purchase laptops for a discount through IU's personal purchasing program. Access vendor stores through\nComputer Guide: Deals by vendor, recommendations, and common questions\n. Download software via the\nIUware\nportal.	KB0025468	KB0025468 v2.0	 recommended) 2 GB free HDD space Webcam Microphone 2 Mbps upload speed <strong>CPU</strong> equivalent to Intel i3 2+ GHZ	5.2801		2026-01-31 17:13:15.046274	2026-01-31 17:13:15.046274	0	7b25d2e03beb6698476d941864e45a4e
2440	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024874	Request an IUIE account	To request an Indiana University Information Environment (\nIUIE\n) account, you need a valid\nUniversity account\n. To request an account:\nVisit the\nIUIE registration page\n.\nYou will be redirected to a login page.\nLog in with your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nIf you have no "Acceptable Use Agreement - Access to Technology and Information Resources - Employees" on file, you will be directed to it and will need to read and complete the form before proceeding.\nOnce your acceptance of the Acceptable Use Agreement has been verified, the resulting IUIE Registration page fields will automatically be populated with your current user information.\nNote:\nIf an IUIE account already exists for you, a message will appear in red indicating that you are already registered with an account. If you see this message, do not submit a new account request.\nSupply any missing information and select\nRegister\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:35:58.978185	2026-01-31 15:05:54.994355	1	KB0024874
11588	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d510bc7f476456583d373803846d431a	Funding for Intelligent Infrastructure resources used by UITS services	Due to technology advances that significantly reduced rates for\nIntelligent Infrastructure\n(II), UITS no longer bills UITS teams for use of II resources (exceptions noted below). This approach, in operational practice since January 2017, eliminates the administrative burden of budgeting and billing for II resources within UITS.\nAll II virtual machines (VMs) have a funding source, including those used by UITS services (there are no free VMs within II). Enterprise Systems provided existing funding from the ES budget. No additional funding was used.\nII resources used by UITS services are funded from existing Enterprise Systems budgets with the following exceptions:\nCosts billed to a third-party (external to UITS) client for II resources that support a service will continue to be charged. Example scenarios may include but are not limited to:\nUA Technology Support\nfunding II costs through revenue from client departments.\nII costs funded through grant money for a specific project.\nII costs funded through student technology fees.\nII resources funded from chargeback accounts (typically chargeback accounts start with a 6) will continue to be billed within UITS unless specific exceptions are evaluated.\nAdditional II resources for UITS services were allocated based on December 2016 resource levels, plus an additional 10% to enable growth. Growth rate maximums were calculated and maintained within each manager's portfolio, and allow for 10% growth in CPU, memory, and storage needs. Growth beyond 10% requires review by divisional leadership and a new funding source.\nSelf-service provisioning was introduced as part of this process; for more, see\nSelf-service provisioning in Intelligent Infrastructure (II)\n.\nSystem administrators can review available capacity by following the instructions in\nDetermine remaining resources in an Intelligent Infrastructure (II) resource pool\n.	KB0025015	KB0025015 v1.0	 and maintained within each manager's portfolio, and allow for 10% growth in <strong>CPU</strong>, memory, and storage needs	5.2171	km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:13:18.406147	2026-01-31 17:13:18.406147	0	d510bc7f476456583d373803846d431a
11589	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=56d475f647717a18d500b87b716d4379	OnBase scan station requirements	Table of contents\nWorkstation minimum requirements for scanners\nBasic scanner requirements\nRecommended scanners\nLight duty\nMedium duty\nWorkstation minimum requirements for scanners\nThe workstation should meet the following minimum requirements:\nOperating system (OS):\nWindows 11\nRAM:\n2 GB (32-bit OS) or 4 GB (64-bit OS)\nCPU:\n1.6 GHz, dual-core\nScreen resolution:\n1280 x 768\nFree hard disk space:\n2 GB\nNote:\nAll-in-one devices and tablets are not recommended for use as scanning workstations.\nBasic scanner requirements\nWhen purchasing a scanner, consider future capacity. The scanner purchased will last a long time. Even though a low volume might be scanned initially, plan for the future; more than likely, the scanner will eventually need to support a higher volume.\nFollowing are basic scanner requirements:\nDuplex (ability to scan front and back)\nAutomatic document feeder (ADF)\nISIS, TWAIN, Kofax, or Paperstream compliant\nPaperStream scanner software is recommended\nKofax VRS software for image enhancing (optional if your scanner has Paperstream)\n(VRS) VirtualReScan Prof for USB/FireWire workgroup scanners = (v.4.1) - complete package - 1 user (high speed USB) - MFG# VP-W004-00U1\nRicoh scanners may come bundled with PaperStream image enhancing software instead of Kofax VRS.\nRecommended scanners\nAny scanner that is TWAIN or PaperStream certified should work with OnBase. UITS recommends Ricoh scanners, as they come with PaperStream installed:\nLight duty\nRicoh fi-8170*\nDaily duty cycle: Less than 1,000 pages per day\nScanning mode: Simplex and duplex\nOutput: 60 ppm\nDocument feeding: Automatic document feeder\nADF capacity: 80 Sheets\nPaper size: Max. 8.5" x 14"\nDimensions: 6.4" H x 11.8" W x 6.7" D\nMedium duty\nRicoh fi-8190\nDaily duty cycle: 30,000 pages per day; 13,000 scans per day\nScanning mode: Duplex\nOutput: 90 ppm\nDocument feeding: Automatic document feeder\nADF capacity: 100 sheets\nPaper size: Max. 8.5" x 17"\nDimensions: 11.8" W x 6.7" D x 7.4" H\n*Manufacturer model numbers change frequently; if the model number listed here is not available, a comparable scanner will suffice.	KB0025161	KB0025161 v4.0	) or 4 GB (64-bit OS) <strong>CPU</strong>: 1.6 GHz, dual-core Screen resolution: 1280 x 768 Free hard disk space	5.0006	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:13:21.81864	2026-01-31 17:13:21.81864	0	56d475f647717a18d500b87b716d4379
11010	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=91680edc4776b6d0d500b87b716d438c	IU Kubernetes documentation	Table of contents\nGet started\nRun services\nResources\nGet started\nAvailable Kubernetes clusters\nRequest access to IU's Kubernetes environment\nConnect to IU's Enterprise Kubernetes Environment\nUseful tools for working with Kubernetes clusters\nRole-based access control (RBAC) in Kubernetes at IU\nHarbor image registry\nGet help with Kubernetes at IU\nRun services\nDocker container best practices\nKubernetes best practices\nUse Helm charts to deploy and manage Kubernetes applications\nRoute traffic to your services running in Kubernetes\nConfigure container CPU and memory resources in a namespace with quotas enabled\nConfigure delivery pipelines\nAttach persistent storage to your Kubernetes applications\nConfigure data center proxy\nLogging infrastructure for Kubernetes applications\nMonitoring configuration for Kubernetes applications\nOne-time and recurring batch jobs in Kubernetes\nGuidelines for Kubernetes workloads that interact with sensitive data\nManage security incidents with workloads running on Kubernetes\nResources\nMigrate to a new Harbor robot account\nSpring Boot quickstart guide\nLinks to official Kubernetes, Helm, and Docker glossaries\nLinks to Kubernetes tutorials\nExample Kubernetes projects	KB0027171	KB0027171 v17.0	 to your services running in Kubernetes Configure container <strong>CPU</strong> and memory resources in a namespace	4.7203	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 16:58:32.43374	2026-01-31 17:13:32.525852	0	91680edc4776b6d0d500b87b716d438c
11790	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c200783f476456583d373803846d4360	Perform document separation in the OnBase Unity Client	When you scan documents from a multi-function device (MFD) directly into OnBase, all the pages are stored as a single document. Document separation allows you to store a large batch of scanned pages as several separate documents. The following explains how to perform document separation within the OnBase Unity Client.\nLog into the OnBase Unity Client and scan your documents into OnBase from your MFD.\nIn the task ribbon, select\nBatch Processing\n. Under\nScan Queues\n>\nDocument Separation\n, a batch should appear in the\nAwaiting Document Separation\nqueue.\nSelect the\nAwaiting Document Separation\nqueue once to enter it, and select the batch you would like to work on. Then right-click and select\nPerform Document Separation\n.\nA screen showing an unindexed document will appear.\nCheck the document you want to separate. It will be easiest to separate each page into its own document and then append pages together (if necessary) during the indexing phase. To do so, from the ribbon, select\nSplit\n. Select\nBreak into Documents\n, and then\nEvery Page\n.\nThe "Document Separation" screen should now display a list of unindexed documents.\nWhen you are finished separating the document, select\nSave and Close All\n. In the confirmation box that appears, select\nYes\n.\nThe batch will move to the\nAwaiting Index\nqueue. From there, you can index the documents as normal.	KB0024728	KB0024728 v1.0	 MFD. In the task ribbon, select <strong>Batch</strong> <strong>Processing</strong>. Under Scan Queues  Document Separation, a <strong>batch</strong> are stored as a single document. Document separation allows you to store a large <strong>batch</strong> of scanned pages once to enter it, and select the <strong>batch</strong> you would like to work on. Then right-click and select	216.5322	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:18:55.377253	2026-01-31 17:18:55.377253	0	c200783f476456583d373803846d4360
11842	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b3b0550c479efed4d500b87b716d434b	Support and escalation processes for EMA services	Table of contents\nOverview\nActive Directory Services\nExchange\nEnterprise Certificate Authority\nTeams Calls\nOverview\nThese lists include common service requests for the services EMA (Enterprise Microsoft Administration) manages and the proper escalation contacts for each. The lists are not exhaustive, and are updated periodically.\nActive Directory Services\nA list of Active Directory service requests\nRequest\nProcess\nEscalations\nNotes\nADS account creation or deletion issues\nContact\nTier 2 ASIS\nIMS\nUpdate name, description, division, department, affiliation, status, primary email address, office, etc.\nContact\nTier 2 ASIS\nIMS\nADS group creation or deletion\nMust be performed by OU Admins\nDIAG\nADS group membership changes\nMust be performed by OU Admins\nDIAG\nCreate and delete computer objects\nMust be performed by OU Admins\nDIAG\nCreate and delete contacts\nMust be performed by OU Admins\nDIAG\nCreate and delete GPOs\nMust be performed by OU Admins\nDIAG\nChange GPO owner\nContact\nDIAG\nEMA\nUpdate OU Admins membership\nMust be performed by OU Admins\nEMA\nChange permissions on GPOs\nMust be performed by OU Admins\nEMA\nCreate DDNS registration in ads.iu.edu\nAutomatic process\nDIAG\nDelete DDNS registration in ads.iu.edu\nTier 2 Support\ncan perform via\nCCI Tools\nEMA\nStatic DNS registrations in ads.iu.edu\nContact\nDIAG\nwith justification\nEMA\nStatic DNS registrations in other zones\nContact\ndns-admin@iu.edu\nN/A\nDNS primer for Indiana University\nCreate Kerberos keytabs\nSubmit request via form\nEMA\nTier 2 Forms\nUpdate Display Name for accounts\nContact\nTier 2 ASIS\nIMS\nView BitLocker recovery keys\nMust be performed by OU Admins\nUISO\nTroubleshoot account lockouts\nRequest lockout report via\nCCI Tools\n; contact\nDIAG\nN/A\nRequest OU creation or deletion\nSubmit request via form\nEMA\nTier 2 Forms\nCreate or delete DFS Namespaces or Replication Groups\nSubmit request via form\nEMA\nTier 2 Forms\nEnable Kerberos constrained delegation for user accounts\nContact UISO for approval\nN/A\nIU Health\nclient\ns on IU tenant AzureAD 2FA/MFA reset\nContact\nDIAG\nIU Health\nclient\ns accessing IU tenant content (SharePoint, Teams, etc.) may need their "Guest User" multi-factor authentication reset for the IU tenant.\nExchange\nA list of Exchange service requests\nRequest\nProcess\nEscalations\nNotes\nCreate, delete, enable, or disable mailboxes\nContact\nTier 2 ASIS\nIMS\nCreate and delete resource mailboxes\nContact\nDIAG\nEMA\nCreate, delete, or change permissions on Public Folders\nContact\nDIAG\nEMA\nGrant full-mailbox and send-as access on user mailboxes\nNot permitted\nUISO\nGrant full-mailbox and send-as access on group mailboxes\nPerform in\nCCI Tools\n; contact\nDIAG\nEMA\nMail enable contacts or distribution groups\nCan be performed by OU Admins or\nDIAG\nDIAG\nChange mail enabled distribution group settings\nCan be performed by OU Admins or\nDIAG\nDIAG\nPerform message tracking\nContact\nDIAG\nEMA\nRecover deleted items\nSelf-service in Microsoft\nOutlook\n/\nOutlook on the web\nDIAG\nCreate mail alias\nContact\nDIAG\nMail Admin\nAdministrative set Out-of-Office for user mailbox\nContact UISO\nN/A\nAllowlist sender or IP address\nContact\nDIAG\nMail Admin\nIncrease mailbox rules quota\nContact\nDIAG\nEMA\nEnterprise Certificate Authority\nA list of Enterprise Certificate Authority service requests\nRequest\nProcess\nEscalations\nNotes\nRequest internal use certificates (only for FQDNs ending in ads.iu.edu)\nSelf-service\nDIAG\nCertificate templates available from the ECA\nRequest public certificates\nSelf-service\nDIAG\nGet an SSL certificate for your web server\nTeams Calls\nTeams Calls\nprovides office telephone service for faculty and staff at Indiana University.\nA list of Teams Calls service requests\nRequest\nProcess\nEscalations\nNotes\nPhone number assignment, changes, removals\nSubmit request via form/contact CPI\nN/A\nUITS Telecommunications Request form\nUpdate directory phone number for a user account\nSelf-service\nCPI\nView and modify your personal information (such as preferred name, home address, birthday, or emergency contacts) for IU employees, groups, or affiliates\nUpdate directory phone number for a group account\nContact CPI\nN/A	KB0025601	KB0025601 v3.0	Table of contents Overview Active Directory Services Exchange Enterprise Certificate Authority Teams Calls Overview These lists include common service requests for the services EMA (Enterprise Microsoft Administration) manages and the proper escalation contacts for each. The lists are not exhaustive, and are updated periodically. Active Director	1.6144	km-aud-uits, km-aud-local-support	2026-01-31 17:20:52.500983	2026-01-31 17:20:52.500983	0	b3b0550c479efed4d500b87b716d434b
11791	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=fc00303f476456583d373803846d43bb	Index documents using the OnBase Unity Client	To index documents in the OnBase Unity Client, follow the steps below.\nOpen and log into the OnBase Unity Client.\nScan your documents into OnBase; for help, see\nUse the OnBase Unity Client to scan a document\n.\nTo reach your scanned/swept documents on the OnBase "Batch Processing" page, do one of the following:\nSelect\nBatch Processing\n.\nAfter scanning/sweeping, select\nGo to Batch\n.\nOn the "Batch Processing" page, you will see a set of status queues marked with arrows. To find your batch of documents, select the Status Queue\nIndex\n, and then select\nAwaiting Index\n. Alternatively, go to your scan queue under "Scan Queues" and select it from there.\nAfter finding your batch, either right-click the batch and select\nIndex Documents\n, or select\nStart Processing\nat the top of the page.\nSelect the\nDocument Type Group\n(if applicable) and\nDocument Type\ncorresponding to the document in the open window to the right. Fill in the keywords.\nIn\nthe keywords section, select\nIndex\n.\nIn the keywords section, you can also do the following:\nSkip documents:\nSelect the right-pointing arrows.\nStop indexing:\nSelect the key with the red box.\nAfter you select\nIndex\nfor the first document or page, you have the following options:\nIf you have a new document, change the keywords and select\nIndex\n.\nIf it is part of the same document, select\nAppend\nto append that page to the previous one.\nTo scan the page again, select\nScan\n.\nAfter indexing all of the documents, the "Indexing Stopped or Completed" menu will load. Do one of the following:\nIf a secondary person\nwill not\ncheck the indexed documents, select\nSave and Transition\n. This will push the documents to "Awaiting Commit" and complete the indexing process.\nIf a secondary person\nwill\ncheck the indexed documents, select\nSave and Close\n.\nOnce all documents have been indexed, they will automatically move to the next step, "Awaiting Commit". This is an overnight automatic system process.	KB0024627	KB0024627 v1.0	 a document. To reach your scanned/swept documents on the OnBase "<strong>Batch</strong> <strong>Processing</strong>" page, do one of the following: Select <strong>Batch</strong> <strong>Processing</strong>. After scanning/sweeping, select Go to <strong>Batch</strong>. On the "<strong>Batch</strong> <strong>Processing</strong>" page, you will see a set of status queues marked with arrows. To find your <strong>batch</strong>	202.5185	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:18:58.806272	2026-01-31 17:18:58.806272	0	fc00303f476456583d373803846d43bb
11793	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=27048747478c7e10d500b87b716d4332	Services: Data Center Operations	Equipment monitoring\nData Center Operations uses WhatsUp Gold for constant monitoring of systems, servers, and infrastructure.\nTo initiate the process for Data Center Operations to monitor your equipment,\nsubmit an equipment monitoring request\n.\nFeatures of WhatsUp Gold include:\nHigh performance predictive monitoring of networks, systems, applications, and services, including wireless networks\nMore than 200 network and infrastructure reports providing real, actionable intelligence\nAn industrial-strength management architecture using the most advanced standards available (SNMP v1-3, TCP, ICMP, SSH)\nComprehensive out-of-the-box support for WMI, supporting complete Windows service monitoring\nAgentless monitoring for Unix and Linux systems using SSH\nIntegrated application (Microsoft\nExchange\n, NT Services, and Microsoft SQL) monitoring capabilities delivering critical process and performance analytics\nRich capability for synthetic transaction monitoring ensuring application performance and integrity\nThe industry's leading alert management platform, featuring easy-to-use alarms, alerts, and notification capabilities for real-time operations support\nSplit-second graphs and the InstantInfo feature supporting real-time troubleshooting activities\nA highly flexible, automated, and comprehensive network discovery and mapping platform\nQuick navigation with intuitive web and Windows consoles, which provide 360° visibility\nScheduling services\nList of scheduling duties:\nThe production migration of Enterprise System online and batch source code for IU written applications\nEmergency deployment of batch and online source code\nDefine Enterprise System batch schedules utilizing an automated batch scheduling product\nMonitor and review batch schedules for optimal design, perform runtime analysis\nProvide test batch scheduling options for pre-production testing of applications batch schedules\nProvide scheduling support for application releases and upgrades\nDefine and manage batch schedules utilized to provide database replication\nMaintain software updates for batch scheduling product: web client, server agents, manager databases, security patching and product licensing, and reporting options\nWork with system administrators for system upgrade for new install of automated batch scheduler product and patching to insure least impact to customers\nValidate and test automated batch scheduling product after subsequent changes\nTrain development staff on use of automated batch scheduler product\nManage batch and infrastructure batch documentation repositories\nProvide batch flow diagrams and version control for application and infrastructure batch inventory spreadsheets\nAssist with Disaster Recovery document storage and annual review process when needed\nMaintain contents of UITS DS.CALL, after hours contact list\nChange management\nSee\nChange Management process\n.	KB0024230	KB0024230 v2.0	 migration of Enterprise System online and <strong>batch</strong> source code for IU written applications Emergency deployment of <strong>batch</strong> and online source code Define Enterprise System <strong>batch</strong> schedules utilizing an automated <strong>batch</strong> scheduling product Monitor and review <strong>batch</strong> schedules for optimal design, perform runtime	177.387		2026-01-31 17:19:02.188883	2026-01-31 17:19:02.188883	0	27048747478c7e10d500b87b716d4332
11795	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5fd730d83b1aa258476d941864e45aa2	Configure WinSCP to transfer student records batch files to DTP.IU.EDU and DTT.IU.EDU for processing	Note:\nThe instructions in this document are intended for users authorized to access the\nDTP.IU.EDU\nproduction and\nDTT.IU.EDU\ntest data transfer servers:\nIf you believe you should be able to access these servers from your workstation, email\newahelp@iu.edu\n.\nThese instructions are based on the latest version of WinSCP. If you don't have the latest version installed, you can download the installation package for free from the\nWinSCP Downloads\npage.\nTo configure WinSCP to securely transfer student records batch files to\nDTP.IU.EDU\n(production) or\nDTT.IU.EDU\n(test) for processing:\nIn WinSCP, select\nNew Site\n.\nUnder "Session", use this information to set up a new session:\nFile protocol:\nSFTP\nHost name:\ndtp.iu.edu\nPort number:\n22\nUser name:\nYour\nIU username\nNote:\nDo not enter your\nIU passphrase\n.\nSelect\nSave\n.\nChange "Site name" to\ndtp.iu.edu (PRD)\n.\nSelect\nOK\n.\nRepeat steps 1-5 to set up your connection to\nDTT.IU.EDU\n, except:\nIn step 2, for "Host name", enter\ndtt.iu.edu\n.\nIn step 4, change "Site name" to\ndtt.iu.edu (TST)\n.\nOnce both sites are added, to avoid known issues, you must customize preferences in WinSCP:\nIn the bottom left corner, under the list of sites, choose\nTools\n, and then select\nPreferences\n.\nIn the "Preferences" window, from the menu on the left:\nSelect\nEnvironment\n, and then uncheck\nContinue on error\n.\nSelect\nTransfer\n, and then select\nEdit\n. Under "Upload options" check\nIgnore permissions errors\n.\nUnder\nTransfer\n, select\nEndurance\n. In the "Enable transfer resume/transfer to temporary filename for" section, select\nDisable\n.\nTo save your changes and exit "Preferences", select\nOK\n.\nAfter adding the servers and configuring the preferences in WinSCP, you should be able to log into\nDTP.IU.EDU\nand/or\nDTT.IU.EDU\n, and drag and drop student records batch files onto either server for processing.	KB0024464	KB0024464 v2.0	 records <strong>batch</strong> files to DTP.IU.EDU (production) or DTT.IU.EDU (test) for <strong>processing</strong>: In WinSCP, select into DTP.IU.EDU and/or DTT.IU.EDU, and drag and drop student records <strong>batch</strong> files onto either server for <strong>processing</strong>.	112.4756	km-aud-iu	2026-01-31 17:19:05.52444	2026-01-31 17:19:05.52444	0	5fd730d83b1aa258476d941864e45aa2
11797	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=2730f87347a456583d373803846d43b1	About advising teams in Advisor Assignment	Table of contents\nOverview\nLanding page\nCreate an advising team\nAdd team members\nSettings\nAdd a team administrator\nLeave a team as an administrator\nRename a team\nDelete a team\nReport an issue\nOverview\nAdministrators who make advising assignments can use advising teams to see assignments associated with specific advisors or advising offices, and to better organize those advisors and offices. An advising team is a group of advisors or offices that an administrator assigns students to.\nYou do\nnot\nneed to create an advising team to complete the batch load process. Advising teams provide a way for you to better see how many assignments are associated with specific individuals and offices, and to see who is assigned to them.\nAdvising teams only exist in the advisor assignment dashboard; they are not linked to any other applications.\nLanding page\nFrom the Advisor Assignment landing page, you can either create a new advising team, or if you have already prepared a spreadsheet to upload, you can\nupload an assignment\n.\nCreate an advising team\nTo create an advising team:\nFrom the landing page, select\nCreate new advising team\n.\nEnter a name into the "Team name" field, and then in the "Campus" drop-down list, select the campus that the advising team is associated with.\nSave your team, and your new team's dashboard will display.\nAdd team members\nTo add individual advisors or advising offices to your team:\nFrom the advising team's dashboard, select\nAdd team members\n.\nTo add a person:\nUnder "Team member type" select\nPerson\n.\nEnter the person's University ID or username into the "University ID or username" field.\nUse the "Advising role" drop-down menu to select the person's role (for example, academic advisor, faculty advisor, etc.).\nTo add an advising office:\nUnder "Team member type" select\nAdvising Office\n.\nEnter the person's University ID or username into the "University ID or username" field.\nUse the "Advising office" drop-down menu to select the office; only offices associated with the campus that was assigned to the advising team when\nit was created\nwill be available.\nSelect\nSave\n. The advising team's dashboard will display, and will show the person or office you added.\nIf there are students already assigned to the team member or office, you will see the number of associated assignments the person or office has in the "Assignments" column. To view the assigned students, select the number.\nSettings\nIf you need to add additional team administrators, leave a team as an administrator, rename an advising team, or delete an advising team, you can do so from the advising team's settings. To access settings, at the top of the screen, select\nSettings\n.\nAdd a team administrator\nThe person who created the team is automatically assigned as an administrator. If other people need to have access to the team and need the ability to upload assignments, you can add them as team administrators.\nTo add a team administrator:\nSelect\nSettings\n.\nSelect\nTeam administrators\n.\nAbove the list of current team administrators, select\nAdd team administrator\nLeave a team as an administrator\nIf you no longer need to be an advising team administrator, you can leave the team as an administrator. To do so:\nSelect\nSettings\n.\nSelect\nTeam administrators\n.\nNext to your name, under the "Action" column, select\nLeave\n.\nWhen you leave, you will have two options: you can either assign another team administrator, or delete the team.\nRename a team\nTo rename an advising team:\nSelect\nSettings\n.\nSelect\nRename advising team\n.\nIn the "Team name" field, enter the new name.\nSelect\nSave\n.\nDelete a team\nTo delete an advising team:\nSelect\nSettings\n.\nSelect\nDelete advising team\n.\nCheck\nDelete advising team\n.\nSelect\nDelete advising team\n.\nReport an issue\nTo report an issue, visit\nStudent Success\n.	KB0025902	KB0025902 v1.0	 to. You do not need to create an advising team to complete the <strong>batch</strong> load process. Advising teams provide a way	9.0729		2026-01-31 17:19:08.925295	2026-01-31 17:19:08.925295	0	2730f87347a456583d373803846d43b1
11799	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=07ef247b476456583d373803846d43cd	Ad hoc Caseload groups in AdRx	Table of contents\nOverview\nLog into\nAdRx\nand access Caseload\nCreate a new ad hoc Caseload group\nAdd students to an existing ad hoc Caseload group\nRename, delete, or remove all members from an ad hoc Caseload group\nRemove members of an ad hoc Caseload group\nSet Caseload filters\nNavigate to, from, and between student records\nExport Caseload lists\nSend messages or add an advising note via Caseload\nOverview\nAs an authorized user of\nAdvising Records\n(\nAdRx\n), you can access Caseload to identify and manage your assigned advisees as designated in SIS, or to create a custom ("ad hoc") group of students to manage and monitor. Caseload's filter options enable you to find students possessing common characteristics in your assigned advisees group or in an ad hoc group. You can generate and save a list of your assigned advisees or ad hoc group members for later viewing in Microsoft Excel. You can also use Caseload to send messages to individual students or groups of students.\nLog into\nAdRx\nand access Caseload\nGo to\nAdRx\n, select\nFaculty/Staff Login\n, and follow any login prompts.\nOn the\nAdRx\nlanding page, in the header, select\nCaseload\n:\nBy default, you'll go to the\nAssigned Advisees\ntab. Here, you'll see all of your assigned advisees in one group at the top of the list. All the other groups listed on the tab are organized by academic program plan. The expectation is that every student has at least one advising assignment (individual advisor or advising office) while they are active in an academic degree plan. Assigned advisees can be added individually or in a batch process within the Student Information System (SIS).\nNote:\nAdvisor assignments are visible to students in the Student Online Advising Record (SOAR) system, in the Student Appointment Scheduler (SAS), and in the Student Center. The advisor/office email address and phone number are displayed.\nIf you wish to create an ad hoc group, select the\nAd Hoc Caseload Group\ntab. Here, you'll see an alphabetical list of all of the ad hoc Caseload groups that you've created. Ad hoc Caseload groups are static lists of students created and maintained by individual users, and your groups will remain visible to you as long as you have permission to access this feature.\nCreate a new ad hoc Caseload group\nTo create a new ad hoc Caseload group:\nFrom the\nAdRx\nDashboard, select\nCaseload\n, and then select the\nAd Hoc Caseload Groups\ntab.\nAt the top of the list, select\nCreate New Caseload Group\n.\nIn the pop-up window, enter a name for the new group.\nUnder "Caseload Group Members", paste or type a list of student usernames or University IDs, with entries separated by a space, comma, or new line. If you want to create an empty ad hoc group, leave the "Caseload Group Members" box blank.\nSelect\nFind Students\nto import the student names and confirm your list has no errors.\nTo remove any student that should not be in the group, select the\nx\nto the right of the student's information.\nOnce you've established that the list is complete and does not include any students that don't belong, select\nCreate Group\nto add this group to your other ad hoc groups.\nNote:\nAd hoc Caseload groups have a size limit of 10,000 students in a single group. You can create an ad hoc Caseload group with over 10,000 students, but you won't be able to open it. In such cases, you'll need to either delete the group or remove all members from it; see\nRename, delete, or remove all members from an ad hoc Caseload group\n.\nAdd students to an existing ad hoc Caseload group\nThere are several methods to add students to an existing ad hoc Caseload group:\nOption 1:\nFrom the list view of an ad hoc group, at the top of the list, select\nAdd Students\n.\nIn the "Add Students" dialogue box, paste or type a list of student usernames or University IDs, with entries separated by a space, comma, or new line.\nSelect\nFind Students\n.\nVerify that the students listed are correct, and then select\nAdd Students\n.\nOption 2:\nFrom the list of students for a particular Caseload group, select at least one student, and select\nAdd to a Caseload Group\n.\nIn the dialog box that appears, you'll have the option to add the student(s) to a new ad hoc group, or to add the student(s) to an existing group selected from a drop-down list of your groups.\nRename, delete, or remove all members from an ad hoc Caseload group\nTo modify an ad hoc Caseload group, navigate to the\nAdhoc Caseload Groups\ntab. To the right of the group you want to change, select the three vertical dots, and then, as appropriate, select\nRename Group\n,\nRemove All Members\n, or\nDelete Group\n.\nRemove members of an ad hoc Caseload group\nTo remove specific members of an ad hoc Caseload group, navigate to the group, select the member(s) you want to remove, and select\nRemove Members\n. This button will become active once you've selected at least one student.\nSet Caseload filters\nYou can use Caseload's filter options to help you find students that have common characteristics within your assigned advisees group or an ad hoc group.\nThe filters pane is displayed to the left of any group, and always contains the same options to start:\nCampus\nCareer\nAction Items\nAdmit Term\nAlerts\nArea of Study\nClass Standing\nCourse Grades\nCourse Registration\nEnrollment\nGPA\nGraduation Review Status\nHours\nService Indicators\nStudent Engagement Roster\nStudent Groups\nTransfer Students\nTo apply filters to a group:\nSelect and set filter values as desired, either one at a time or all at once; a summary of all the filters you have selected will display as you go.\nIf you wish to delete a filter before it's applied, select the\nx\nnext to it. To remove all filters and start again, select\nClear All\n.\nOnce you've selected at least one filter value, it will be displayed above the selections. At that point, you can either apply that filter, or you can return to the larger list to add more. In other words, you can continuously filter the list one parameter at a time, or you can add multiple filters at once; the results will be the same.\nIf you are applying multiple filters at once, you will see limited options in some categories for filtering based on the values you've already chosen.\nOnce you've selected all the filters you want, select\nApply to Results\n.\nOnce a filter is applied, it will be displayed at the top of the page under "Selected Filters". All available filters will also continue to be displayed on the left side of the page.\nYour results will be displayed. The number of students in the filtered group as compared to the number in the entire group will appear next to the group name.\nNote:\nIf you choose to\nexport your list\n, the exported file will only include the students in the filtered list (as opposed to all students in the Caseload group). The file will display an addition column for each filtered value.\nNavigate to, from, and between student records\nWhen you select the name of a student in the list, you'll be taken to the "Student Summary" page, and a secondary header will display. The secondary header provides two navigation options:\nTo return to the Caseload group, select the group's name at the upper left of the secondary header; any filters you selected will still be applied when you return to the group.\nTo page through the list of students without returning to the overall Caseload group, use the arrows on the right side of the secondary header; these arrows work on entire Caseload groups or on filtered lists.\nExport Caseload lists\nYou can use Caseload to generate and export a comma-delimited (\n.csv\n) file containing a Caseload group page. Such files store data as plain text and are viewable in Excel.\nTo export a Caseload list:\nOn the Caseload landing page, select the group you want to export from either the\nAssigned Advisees\ntab or the\nAdhoc Caseload Groups\ntab.\nSelect\nExport\nin the upper right corner.\nNote:\nThe exported list will contain all members of the chosen group, regardless of whether any students were selected or not. For each student, the list will include a standard set of student data along with the values of any filters you may have used.\nIf you wish to save the list as an Excel file, open the\n.csv\nfile in Excel, select\nSave As\n, and save the file as an Excel file type (such as\n.xlsx\nor\n.xls\n).\nSend messages or add an advising note via Caseload\nFor details about sending messages and adding advising notes with Caseload, see\nEmail a group of students using Caseload in AdRx\n.	KB0024121	KB0024121 v1.0	 or in a <strong>batch</strong> process within the Student Information System (SIS). Note: Advisor assignments are visible	8.2378		2026-01-31 17:19:12.277229	2026-01-31 17:19:12.277229	0	07ef247b476456583d373803846d43cd
11846	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=83db8d1b474dfe502fc6a55b416d4318	Set up a Google Custom Search Engine for your IU website	On this page:\nOverview\nRequest a Google Custom Search Engine\nSet up your Custom Search Engine\nAdd your Custom Search Engine to your website\nEdit your Custom Search Engine\nDelete a Custom Search Engine\nOverview\nAt Indiana University, you can use Google Custom Search to help people find the information they need on your website(s). You can add a Google Custom Search Engine to your web page(s) by copying and pasting a few lines of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. You can configure your Custom Search Engine to search whatever site(s) you want, even sites you don't own. People who use the Google Custom Search box on your website will see search results from only the site(s) you choose.\nAt IU, you can use Google Custom Search on your website without fee as part of the\nGoogle at IU\nservice.\nRequest a Google Custom Search Engine\nNote:\nTo create and administer your department's Google Custom Search Engine(s), UITS recommends using one of your department's\ngroup accounts\nto create a shared\nGoogle at IU\naccount. Using a single shared\nGoogle at IU\naccount to create, own, and manage your department's Custom Search Engine(s) saves you the inconvenience of managing multiple Google accounts, and simplifies the reassignment of search engine duties whenever staff and roles change within your department.\nTo request a Google Custom Search Engine for use with your IU website, fill out and submit the\nGoogle@IU Custom Search Engine Request\nform.\nOn the form, you must provide the following information:\nName:\nEnter your full name (first and last name).\nEmail address:\nEnter the IU group account email address used to create your department's shared\nGoogle at IU\naccount.\nPhone number:\nEnter the phone number of a contact person whom the\nGoogle at IU\nadministrator can call if any questions or problems arise.\nUsername(s) of CSE administrator(s):\nEnter the\nIU username\n(or usernames, comma-separated) of the individual(s) to whom you want to grant administrative rights to your Custom Search Engine.\n"Friendly" name for CSE:\nEnter a name to identify this search engine (for example,\nDeathstar.org search\n) from others your department has created or may create in the future.\nURL of search site:\nEnter the URL of an initial website to add to the Custom Search Engine (you can add more websites later).\nWhen you're finished, enter your name in the\nSIGN HERE\nbox, and then select\nSubmit\n. The\nGoogle at IU\nadministrator will notify you when your Custom Search Engine is ready.\nSet up your Custom Search Engine\nGo to\nGoogle Custom Search Engine\n. If you're not already logged into\nGoogle at IU\nwith your department's shared Google account, select\nSign in To Custom Search Engine\n, and then log in through\nIU Login\nand\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\nusing the group account credentials associated with your department's shared Google account.\nUnder "Edit search engines", find the search engine you created, and select its name.\nOn the\nSetup\npage, on the\nBasics\ntab, you can change the name of your search engine, add a description and keywords, and list the URLs of the websites you want the search engine to search. When you're finished making changes, at the bottom of the page, select\nUpdate\n.\nFrom the menu on the left, choose\nLook and feel\nand use the provided tabs to customize your search engine's appearance:\nOn the\nThemes\ntab, you can select one of the provided design themes.\nOn the\nCustomize\ntab, you can choose the fonts and colors that are used in search engine elements (for example, search box, search button, and search results).\nOn the\nLayout\ntab, you can choose one of several layouts that display the search box and search results on the same web page, or choose the layout for displaying the search box and search results on separate pages.\nWhen you're finished with\nLook and feel\nchanges, select\nSave & Get Code\n.\nImportant:\nOnce you add your Custom Search Engine to your website, you can apply your site's CSS rules to customize it further. However, to comply with the university's agreement with Google, do not use CSS to remove/hide Google branding from your search results. The light gray "powered by Google Custom Search" text is a standard component that cannot be altered in the search engine's control panel. Although the text can be hidden using CSS, UITS requests that you honor its agreement with Google and refrain from doing so. Also, UITS recommends not using a gray background for your search pages (most IU-affiliated search pages have white backgrounds). The Google logo is an image, and its appearance cannot be changed.\nAdd your Custom Search Engine to your website\nThe process of embedding Custom Search components (your search box and search results) in your web page(s) involves copying the code you generated by selecting\nSave & Get Code\nat the end of the previous section, and pasting it into the HTML of your web page(s). The exact steps you take will depend on the layout you choose. Following are instructions for one of the one-page layouts and the two-page layout.\nOverlay:\nChoosing the\nOverlay\nlayout and selecting\nSave & Get Code\ngenerates code for displaying the search results as an overlay on the same page as the search box. The generated code will look similar to this (the value for\ncx\nwill be a unique ID specific to your search engine):\n<script>\n(function() {\nvar cx = 'your_search_engine_ID';\nvar gcse = document.createElement('script');\ngcse.type = 'text/javascript';\ngcse.async = true;\ngcse.src = 'https://cse.google.com/cse.js?cx=' + cx;\nvar s = document.getElementsByTagName('script')[0];\ns.parentNode.insertBefore(gcse, s);\n})();\n</script>\n<gcse:search></gcse:search>\nCopy the generated code, and then paste it into a single <div> element in the body of your web page(s).\nTwo page:\nChoosing the\nTwo page\nlayout and selecting\nSave & Get Code\ngenerates code for displaying your search box and search results on separate pages. The generated search box code will look similar to this (the value for\ncx\nwill be a unique ID specific to your search engine):\n<script>\n(function() {\nvar cx = 'your_search_engine_ID';\nvar gcse = document.createElement('script');\ngcse.type = 'text/javascript';\ngcse.async = true;\ngcse.src = 'https://cse.google.com/cse.js?cx=' + cx;\nvar s = document.getElementsByTagName('script')[0];\ns.parentNode.insertBefore(gcse, s);\n})();\n</script>\n<gcse:searchbox-only></gcse:searchbox-only>\nCopy the generated code and paste it into a <div> element in the body of the web page(s) where you want your search box to be displayed. To specify the URL of your search results page, add the resultsUrl argument to the <gcse:searchbox-only> element; for example:\n<gcse:searchbox-only resultsUrl="search_results_url"></gcse:searchbox-only>\nBack on the "Get code for search box" page, select\nNext: Get code for search results\n(beneath the text/code box). On the resulting "Get code for search results" page, copy the generated code and paste it into a <div> element in the body of your search results page.\nNote:\nAlternatively, you can specify the URL of your search results page without manually adding it to your search box code:\nOn either the "Get code for search box" or "Get code for search results" page, select\nSearch Results Details\n(above the text/code box).\nIn the resulting window, enter the complete URL of your search results page. (In the "Specify the query parameter name embedded in the URL" field, either leave the default\nq\nor, optionally, change it to\nquery\n.)\nSelect\nSave\n.\nFor more about Custom Search elements, see Google's\nCustom Search Element Control API\npage.\nNote:\nGoogle Custom Search also has a JSON/Atom API you can use to develop websites and applications that query your Custom Search Engine and retrieve search results in JSON or Atom format. Use of the JSON/Atom Custom Search API is free for up to 100 queries per day, but any usage beyond the free quota will result in charges being assessed. For this reason, UITS recommends using the JavaScript API.\nEdit your Custom Search Engine\nTo edit the name, description, websites to search, and/or the look and feel options of your Custom Search Engine, return to\nGoogle Custom Search Engine\nand follow the steps (as needed) from the\nSet up your Custom Search Engine\nsection, above.\nTo add or remove search engine administrators, email the Google at IU administrator at\ntlthelp@iu.edu\n.\nDelete a Custom Search Engine\nTo delete a Google Custom Search Engine, email the Google at IU administrator at\ntlthelp@iu.edu\n.	KB0023812	KB0023812 v3.0	On this page: Overview Request a Google Custom Search Engine Set up your Custom Search Engine Add your Custom Search Engine to your website Edit your Custom Search Engine Delete a Custom Search Engine Overview At Indiana University, you can use Google Custom Search to help people find the information they need on your website(s). You can add a G	1.2149		2026-01-31 17:20:55.89962	2026-01-31 17:20:55.89962	0	83db8d1b474dfe502fc6a55b416d4318
11854	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=819b96334712ba10d500b87b716d4307	About Google Course Tools	Table of contents\nOverview\nSet up and manage Google Course Tools (instructors only)\nEnable Google Course Tools\nSelect desired features and permissions\nSync the roster with Google Course Tools\nGrant or remove access to IU guests\nAccess and use course folders\nAccess course folders from Google Course Tools\nAccess course folders from\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\nAccess course folders as an IU guest\nShare and collaborate on content in course folders\nCourse folder permissions\nPermissions inheritance\nUpload files or folders\nCreate new Google content\nShare existing\nGoogle Drive\ncontent that you own\nUse the course mailing list/discussion forum in\nGoogle Groups\nOverview\nGoogle Course Tools is an external app that integrates Canvas with the\nGoogle at IU\nservice. Currently this tool facilitates sharing and collaboration among class members by creating a collection of folders for the course in\nGoogle Drive\nand\nGoogle Groups\nfor managing permissions on the folders. Instructors can also create a mailing list and web-based discussion board for the course in\nGoogle Groups\n.\nImportant:\nFiles that you add to folders created by Google Course Tools count toward your\n5 GB quota\n. To keep your\nGoogle Drive\naccount under the quota, you may need to remove files in folders for courses from prior terms. To retrieve a list of Google files that have been shared with other users or groups, including files shared in Google Course Tools folders, run the\nGoogle at IU "My Drive" Shared Files Report\n.\nSet up and manage Google Course Tools (instructors only)\nEnable Google Course Tools\nThe Google Course Tools app is hidden by default. To add a hidden tool to your course navigation menu, see\nHow do I manage Course Navigation links?\nSelect desired features and permissions\nUsing the "Google Course Tools setup" screen, you can decide which features to enable and determine whether TAs and Designers should have instructor-level access or not. To set up Google Course Tools:\nFrom the course navigation menu, select\nGoogle Course Tools\n. The first time you launch the tool, the setup screen will display immediately. After that, you can access the setup screen from the main menu by selecting\nSet up Google Course Tools\n.\nRead the folder/feature descriptions and check the boxes for the folders/features you wish to enable.\nBy default, TAs and Designers will have the same permissions to access the folder content as students. To give one or both roles instructor-level access, under "Which additional roles should have instructor-level access?", check the boxes for the desired roles.\nWhen you are satisfied with your selections, select\nSubmit\n. You can revisit the setup screen later to enable additional folders/features or change TA or Designer permissions.\nAfter a few seconds, the main menu will appear with a success message at the top of the page. You'll also receive a Canvas notification of the groups and folders created.\nThe setup program enables the folders/features you selected and changes TA and designer permissions if requested. It also creates two Google groups, one for instructors only and one for all members of the class.\nSync the roster with Google Course Tools\nAccess to the resources created by Google Course Tools is controlled by membership in two Google groups, one for instructors only and the other containing all members of the course. When course members launch the tool for the first time, they are automatically added to the appropriate groups. In addition, a nightly sync updates the membership of the groups based on the current course roster. You can also run the roster sync at any time by selecting\nSync course roster\nfrom the Google Course Tools main menu.\nThe roster sync job makes the following changes to the course groups and folders:\nRemoves users who have dropped or been removed from the course\nAdds users who are added to the course manually or via SIS enrollment updates\nCreates student drop boxes for students who were added after drop boxes were enabled\nRemoves drop box editor permissions for students who dropped or were removed\nAdds or removes TAs and/or Designers from the instructors-only group to reflect current permissions chosen on the setup screen\nGrant or remove access for IU guests\nCourse members with an IU Guest account do not have permission to use the Canvas Course Tools app. Guests who attempt to launch the app will receive an error. Instructors can, however, give guests access to the Google resources created by the app. You can do so by manually adding the guests in your course to the appropriate Google groups.\nImportant:\nIn order to add guests to your course groups, you'll need the Google username for each guest. Ask each guest to provide you with this information.\nThe roster sync option will not remove guests who have been removed from the course. You must remove them from the Google groups manually.\nPersonal drop box folders are not created for guests, and instructors do not have permission to create them manually.\nTo manually add guests:\nFrom the course navigation menu, select\nGoogle Course Tools\n.\nFrom the Google Course Tools main menu, select\nCourse information\n.\nThe two Google groups created for the course are listed at the top of the page. Select the group for the entire class. You will be taken directly to the selected Google group.\nIn the menu for the group at the lower left, under "People", select\nMembers\n.\nOn the "Members" page, select\nAdd members\n.\nIn the "Add members" pop-up, under "Group Members", enter the Google username for each guest into the text box.\nSelect\nAdd members\n. In the "Group Members" list, guests will be flagged as "external".\nTo also give a guest instructor-level access to course resources, repeat steps 1-7 above, but on step 3, select the link for the "Teachers only" group.\nSend the guest a message letting them know they've been added.\nTo remove a guest :\nRepeat steps 1 through 4 above.\nLocate the guest in the member list for the group and check the box for the guest's name.\nSelect the\nRemove Member\nicon (\n).\nIn the confirmation window, select\nOK\n.\nAccess and use course folders (all roles)\nYou can access the folders created by Google Course Tools from the main menu of the Canvas app or directly in Google. However, you must launch the app in Canvas at least once in your course before the folders will appear in your\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n.\nAccess course folders from Google Course Tools\nTo access a folder from the Google Course Tools app:\nFrom the course navigation menu, select\nGoogle Course Tools\n.\nLinks to the folders you have permission to access appear under "Course folders (open in\nGoogle Drive\n)". Select the name of the folder you wish to open. The folders are:\nCOURSE FILES\n: For course materials shared with the entire class\nINSTRUCTOR FILES\n: For files and folders shared by and accessible to instructors only\nDROP BOXES\n: Container for individual student drop boxes\nMY DROP BOX\n: Personal drop box for the current user\nFILE REPOSITORY\n: For files and folders shared by any class member\nGROUP FILES\n: Container for folders created for Canvas groups; a separate folder is created for each group\nIf you are not already logged into\nGoogle at IU\n, you will be prompted to do so. At the\nIU Login\nprompt, enter your username and password. You may also be prompted to complete a\nTwo-Step Login\n.\nYou will be taken directly to the selected folder in\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n.\nAccess course folders from\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\nThe folders created by Google Course Tools are owned by an administrative account and shared with the Google groups created for each class. The individually shared folders are listed under the\nShared with me\ntab in\nGoogle Drive\n.\nAll course folders are also organized under a folder shortcut named\nGoogle Course Tools (\nyour_username\n)\nin your\nGoogle My Drive\n. Beneath this folder is a separate folder for each course in which the Google Course Tools app has been enabled. Beneath the top-level folder for each course are the special purpose folders (\nCOURSE FILES\n,\nINSTRUCTOR FILES\n,\nDROP BOXES\n,\nFILE REPOSITORY\n,\nGROUP FILES\n) created for the course.\nMost folder names begin with the course title followed by the Canvas internal ID for the course in parentheses, for example,\nSP21: ECOTOURISM: 25256 (1559999)\n. The only exception are student drop box folders, which begin with the student's name and username followed by the course title and ID, for example:\nDoe, Jane (jdoe): SU15: ECOTOURISM: 25256 (1559999)\n.\nYour role in the corresponding Canvas course will determine your permissions for each folder. For more about the folder permissions, see\nCourse folder permissions\n.\nAccess course folders as an IU guest\nUsers with IU Guest accounts cannot use the Google Course Tools app in Canvas. In order to access the Google resources created by the app, you'll need to ask your instructor to manually add your Google account to the appropriate Google groups for the course as described above in\nGrant or remove access for IU guests\n. After the instructor has added you, launch Google Course Tools and select the link to the top-level course folder to access the folder for the first time. To add a shortcut to your\nGoogle My Drive\n, right-click the folder link in the\nGoogle Drive\nbreadcrumb trail and select\nAdd shortcut to Drive\n.\nImportant:\nDo not attempt to send a request for access in\nGoogle Drive\n. The folder owner will not receive your request and you will not be added.\nIf you want to be removed from the course groups and folders, ask the instructor to remove you. You cannot unsubscribe on your own.\nShare and collaborate on content in course folders\nYou can add content to the folders created by Google Course Tools in the following ways:\nUpload files and folders from your computer or mobile device.\nCreate new Google content (such as Docs, Sheets, Slides, Forms, Sites).\nShare existing content that you own in\nGoogle Drive\n.\nThis section covers all three options. However, before you start sharing content, it's important to understand how permissions work in the folders created by Google Course Tools.\nCourse folder permissions\nThe default permissions for each course folder determine which course roles can add, view, edit, or comment on content in the folder. Editors can open, view, add, delete, and edit the content in the folder. Viewers can open and view content, but cannot make changes. The table below gives the default permissions for each folder.\nFolder and permission\nFolder\nInstructors\n1\nEveryone else\nTOP-LEVEL COURSE FOLDER\nViewer\nViewer\nCOURSE FILES\nEditor\nViewer\nINSTRUCTOR FILES\nEditor\nNo access\nDROP BOXES\n2\n(container for individual student drop boxes)\nViewer\nViewer\nMY DROP BOX\n(individual student drop boxes)\nEditor\nStudents can see and edit their own drop box; otherwise no access.\nFILE REPOSITORY\nEditor\nEditor\nGROUP FILES\n(container for individual group folders)\nEditor\nViewer\n[\nCanvas Group Folders\n]\n3\n(container for Canvas group folders)\nEditor\nStudents can see and edit folders for their own Canvas groups; otherwise no access\nNotes:\n1\nTAs and/or Designers can be given instructor-level access via the Google Course Tools setup menu.\n2\nAll course members can see and open the\nDROP BOXES\ncontainer folder. However, student drop boxes are only accessible to instructors and the student for whom the folder was created.\n3\nAll course members can see and open the\nGROUP FILES\ncontainer folder. However, the folders created for each Canvas group are only accessible to instructors and the student members of the group.\nWhen Google Course Tools is initialized, two Google groups are created for each course, one for instructors only and one containing all course members. Access to the course folders is managed by sharing them with one or both groups. This keeps the "\nGoogle Drive\nsharing" screen uncluttered and makes it much easier to manage permissions on content added to each folder. The screenshot below shows the group permissions for a sample\nCOURSE FILES\nfolder.\nThe naming convention for the groups created by Google course tools is "Course Title - Canvas Course ID" (the number following the word "courses" in the Canvas course URL), followed by the word "All" (for the group with all course members) or "Teachers" (for the instructor-only group). For example:\nFA20: AMERICAN SUPERNATURAL: 35157 - 1942004 All\nFA20: AMERICAN SUPERNATURAL: 35157 - 1942004 Teachers\nTo see the names and Google ID for the groups in your course, from the course navigation menu, select\nGoogle Course Tools\n, and then, on the main menu, select\nCourse Information\n. When sharing individual files and folders via the\nGoogle Drive\ninterface, you can add specific course groups as collaborators just as you can add specific users.\nPermissions inheritance\nWhen files or folders are added to a course folder, they inherit the permissions of their parent folder. The owner of the added items can either leave the inherited permissions or modify them as desired.\nPermissions for course groups can be changed or removed entirely. The owner can also add specific course members by name in order to grant them greater access than the permissions granted to the groups to which they belong.\nFor more about sharing files and folders in\nGoogle Drive\n, see\nShare files from\nGoogle Drive\nand\nShare folders in\nGoogle Drive\n.\nUpload files or folders\nUsers with folder editor permissions can upload files or folders from their desktop or mobile device to a course folder using the drag-and-drop method, or by browsing and selecting the items:\nOpen the course folder where you want the files or folders to go.\nFollow the instructions in\nUpload files and folders to\nGoogle Drive\n.\nTo change the permissions on any of the uploaded items, see\nShare files from\nGoogle Drive\nand\nShare folders in\nGoogle Drive\n.\nCreate new Google content\nUsers with editor permissions can add new Google files and folders to a course folder. Most Google file formats support real-time and asynchronous editing and collaboration, which can be very powerful in teaching and learning contexts. Available file formats include:\nDocs (word processing documents)\nSheets (spreadsheets)\nSlides (presentations)\nForms (for surveys, data collection, self-assessments, etc.)\nSites (for building web sites)\nMaps\nDrawings\nA variety of third-party apps integrated with\nGoogle Drive\nTo add a Google file or folder to a course folder:\nNavigate to the course folder where you want to add the item.\nSelect\n+ New\nin your web browser, or select\n+\n>\nCreate new\nin the\nGoogle Drive\nmobile app.\nFrom the new item menu, select the item type you wish to create.\nIf you created a folder, you'll be prompted to enter the folder name. If you created a file, you'll be directed to a new file of the specified format. Give the item a file name before you attempt to share it.\nTo modify the permissions on the item, see\nShare files from\nGoogle Drive\nand\nShare folders in\nGoogle Drive\n.\nShare existing\nGoogle Drive\ncontent that you own\nAlthough there are several ways to share existing\nGoogle Drive\ncontent with members of your course, the "Share Google Files and Folders" option in the Google Course Tools main menu is safer and more flexible than manipulating items directly in Google. When you use this feature:\nThe shared file or folder remains in its original location. Rather than moving the file, a shortcut to the file is added to the destination course folder.\nThe original permissions on the file or folder (whether inherited from the parent folder or added manually) remain intact, ensuring that users who already have access to the items don't lose it. You'll be prompted to specify the permissions for the two Google groups for your course.\nThe same files or folders can be shared with multiple courses at the same time. You don't need to make copies in order to share content with multiple sections of the same class.\nTo use the "Share Google Files and Folders" feature:\nFrom the course navigation menu, select\nGoogle Course Tools\n.\nOn the Google Course Tools main menu, select\nShare Google files and folders\n. If a pop-up appears reminding you to use your\nGoogle at IU\naccount, follow the instructions. The pop-up only appears the first time you select an option that connects to Google.\nOn the "Share Google Files and Folders" page, choose\nSelect item(s) in Google My Drive\n.\nIf you are not already logged into\nGoogle at IU\n, you will be prompted to do so. On the "Google Sign in" screen, enter your Google username (\nyour_username\n@iu.edu\n). You may be prompted to complete a\nTwo-Step Login\n.\nIf this is the first time you have used Google Course Tools, you will be asked whether to allow the Google Course Tools app to interact with your Google account. Select\nAllow\n.\nOnce you have logged into Google, the Google File Picker will appear in a pop-up window. Use the picker to select the files or folders you want to share with the class. To select multiple items, hold down\nCtrl\n(Windows) or\nCommand\n(Mac) while making your selections.\nWhen you have finished selecting items, choose\nSelect\n.\nYou will be returned to the "Share Google file and folders" screen. Open the\nDestination folder\nmenu and select the folder where you want the items to appear.\nSelect\nContinue\nto advance to the "Sharing Permissions" screen.\nSet the preferred permissions for each item, and then select\nSubmit\n.\nA shortcut for each item will be added to the selected destination folder, and the permissions you set will be added to the original permissions.\nUse the course mailing list/discussion forum in Google Groups\nGoogle Course Tools includes an option to create a course mailing list and discussion forum in Google Groups. All posts and replies to the group will be accessible via the web and distributed to the IU email accounts of all course members. To enable this feature:\nFrom the course navigation menu, select\nGoogle Course Tools\n.\nSelect\nSet up Google Course Tools\n.\nCheck\nCourse Mailing List and Discussion Forum\n.\nSelect\nSubmit\n.\nOnce this option is saved, a new option named\nDiscuss in Google Groups\nwill appear in the main menu.\nManage the group settings\nThe default settings for your course group are set by Google Course tools, but many settings are editable. To view and edit the settings for the group:\nFrom the course navigation menu, select\nGoogle Course Tools\n.\nSelect\nSet up Google Course Tools\n.\nSelect\nCourse Information\n.\nUnder "Google Groups for course", select the display name of the group for the entire class.\nA new browser tab will open to the "Settings" page for the Google Group.\nAll settings appear on a single page. Scroll down to view the current settings and make changes if desired. For information on each setting and what it does, see the\nGoogle Groups Settings Reference\n.\nTo save your changes, select\nSave Changes\n.\nParticipate in discussion via the web\nTo view, post, or reply to course discussion via your web browser:\nFrom the course navigation menu, select\nGoogle Course Tools\n.\nSelect\nDiscuss in Google Groups\n. A new browser tab will open to the main discussion page for your course group. Each original topic/conversation is listed as a separate row on the main page.\nTo view the initial post and subsequent replies for a topic/conversation, select anywhere in the row.\nTo reply to a post or reply, select\nReply all\n, enter your message, and select\nPost message\n.\nTo start a new topic/conversation, at the top of the left menu, select\nNew Conversation\n. Enter your subject and message, and select\nPost Message\n.\nFor more about how to participate in Google Groups, see\nParticipate, post, and manage subscriptions\nin Google Groups Help.\nParticipate in discussion via email\nTo get the mailing list address for your Google group, from the main menu of Google Course tools, select\nCourse Information\n. If the course mailing list has been enabled, the address will display beneath the list of Google Groups created for the course.\nTo post a new topic/conversation from your email client, send your message to the mailing list address for the group.\nTo reply to a post from your email account, use the reply function in your email client	KB0025453	KB0025453 v4.0	Table of contents Overview Set up and manage Google Course Tools (instructors only) Enable Google Course Tools Select desired features and permissions Sync the roster with Google Course Tools Grant or remove access to IU guests Access and use course folders Access course folders from Google Course Tools Access course folders from Google at IU My	1.0143		2026-01-31 17:20:59.232149	2026-01-31 17:20:59.232149	0	819b96334712ba10d500b87b716d4307
11803	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d657f6994701be5c2fc6a55b416d43a8	Set up a scan format for a scanner in the OnBase Unity Client	To set up a scan format for a scanner:\nAfter installing the drivers, open and log into the OnBase Unity Client.\nFrom the ribbon, select\nBatch Scanning\n.\nNote:\nIf the\nBatch Scanning\nicon is grayed out:\nAt the top left, select the\nFile\ntab, and select\nUser Options\n.\nWhen the menu opens on the left, select\nImaging\n, and then select\nProduction Document Imaging (TWAIN)\n.\nSelect\nSave\nto close the menu. The\nBatch Scanning\nicon should now appear in color on the right side of the toolbar.\nSelect\nBatch Scanning\n.\nOn the "Batch Scanning" page, select the cog icon in the "Configuration" section of the ribbon.\nIn the "Manage Scan Formats" box, select\nNew\n.\nGive the scan format a name, and then, from the "Source Type" drop-down menu, select\nTwain\n. Select\nOK\n.\nSelect the PaperStream IP device option (\nPaperStream TWAIN\n) and select\nOK\n. The numbers will change based on your scanner model number.\nA scanner settings box will give you "Paper Source" options (\nFeeder (Both Sides)\n,\nFeeder (Front Side)\n, and\nFeeder (Back Side)\n). If your scanner has a flatbed, you will also get an option for the\nFlatbed\n.\nChoose the option that works best for your business practice. The EBPS team suggests\nFeeder (Both Sides)\n. Select\nOK\n.\nFor the "Document Setup" box, the EBPS team suggests using\nMultiple Documents\n. This will create a new document for every page. You can append them during the index process, which is usually simpler than having to separate them. Select\nOK\n.\nYou will be taken back to the "Manage Scan Formats" box. Select\nOK\n, and you are ready to scan.	KB0024646	KB0024646 v2.0	To set up a scan format for a scanner: After installing the drivers, open and log into the OnBase Unity Client. From the ribbon, select <strong>Batch</strong> Scanning. On the "<strong>Batch</strong> Scanning" page, select the cog icon in the "Configuration" section of the ribbon. In the "Manage Scan Formats" box, select New. Give	8.1678	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:19:17.730929	2026-01-31 17:19:17.730929	0	d657f6994701be5c2fc6a55b416d43a8
11806	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=439f6cf3476456583d373803846d4338	Handle problems with PDP or ID billing encumbrance transfer in WorkFlows for Library Acquisitions	A nightly batch PDP (pre-disbursement processor) interface allows acquisitions libraries to transfer payment requests from library invoices in Sirsi WorkFlows to university accounting and check-writing systems administered by\nOffice of the University Controller\n. In addition, encumbrance changes can be transferred from Library Fund screens to University Accounts weekly.\nIn WorkFlows, extended info fields are used to transfer pertinent information to\nOffice of the University Controller\n. Detailed documentation on the use of extended info fields is available to each acquisitions library. When data entry errors are made, normally the entire batch will be rejected and appropriate error messages will be sent by PDP or ID billing to Acquisitions staff. When the errors have been corrected, the invoices will be processed on the following nightly PDP run. There is no need to contact computing or financial staff for assistance in these cases.	KB0022377	KB0022377 v1.0	A nightly <strong>batch</strong> PDP (pre-disbursement processor) interface allows acquisitions libraries to transfer payment requests from library invoices in Sirsi WorkFlows to university accounting and check. When data entry errors are made, normally the entire <strong>batch</strong> will be rejected and appropriate error messages	7.4098		2026-01-31 17:19:22.092056	2026-01-31 17:19:22.092056	0	439f6cf3476456583d373803846d4338
11807	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1615b080475abed4d500b87b716d439b	About Google Gemini at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nLimitations on Gemini accounts at IU\nUse Google Gemini with your Google at IU account\nLearn more and get help\nOverview\nGoogle Gemini is a generative artificial intelligence chatbot tool available to all Indiana University students, faculty, and staff. Along with\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot Chat\n, Google Gemini is one of IU's recommended tools for interacting with generative AI. Like other generative AI platforms, Gemini can be used to create new content, including text and images, and to help brainstorm ideas, draft or summarize writing, explore complex topics, and assist with research and creative work.\nIU accounts provide access to the base Gemini chatbot, including its ability to create and use Gems, custom AI agents designed to perform specific tasks. Gemini is powered by Google's Gemini model family and offers enterprise-grade data protections, making it a flexible and creative tool for learning and productivity.\nLimitations on Gemini accounts at IU\nUnder IU's license agreement, Google sets daily quotas of some advanced-level Gemini features. Such feature add-ons may be called AI Pro, Ultra AI, or other names. As of summer 2025, there is no a pathway for IU users to upgrade their institutional Google accounts to increase these quotas or add capabilities. If the vendor offers such individual licenses in the future, the Knowledge Base will be updated.\nUse Google Gemini with your Google at IU account\nGoogle Gemini is approved to interact with\nRestricted data\nexcept for research data with contract restrictions related to AI (see\nAcceptable use of AI tools with IU research data\n); you must be logged in with your\n@iu.edu\nemail address and\nIU passphrase\nto ensure you're using\nGoogle at IU\nrather than the consumer-based Google service.\nThere are two ways to access Google Gemini:\nBrowser:\nNavigate directly to\nhttps://gemini.google.com\nin your preferred web browser (for example, Firefox, Chrome).\nApps:\nGoogle Gemini mobile apps\nare available for iOS and Android devices.\nImportant:\nAlways use your IU account for Indiana University business. Personal (free) non-IU Google accounts are not approved for institutional data. Gemini users' chats and uploaded files are not reviewed by human reviewers or otherwise used to improve generative AI models only if used with your\nGoogle at IU\naccount. Also, your interactions and generated content within Gemini in Google at IU remain in IU's domain and are not shared externally. Any public Google account will not have such protections.\nBe aware of how to\nnavigate between your IU Google account and non-IU Google accounts\n, or consider creating a\nGoogle Chrome profile\nto manage multiple Google accounts.\nThe following are general use cases for Google Gemini at IU:\nAnswer questions:\nIf you need a quick fact, a detailed explanation, or information on a complex topic, Gemini can provide answers and summaries across a vast range of subjects.\nGenerate images:\nYou can use Gemini to generate original images for general use, such as presentations.\nResearch and brainstorming:\nThe Gemini app can help you research and brainstorm new ideas (for example, for lesson plans or IT security policies), and can speed up time-consuming tasks.\nDouble-check feature:\nGemini includes a feature that uses Google Search to help you verify the accuracy of its responses.\nEven if you use generative AI tools for activities that do not share personal or institutional data, you should still check the tool's output for accuracy. Since these tools have been known to produce inaccurate content (sometimes called "hallucinations"), verify any factual information generated by an AI tool, and make sure to reference the tool as you would any other source.\nIf you have any privacy-related concerns related to Google Gemini, or if you have questions about the type of data that can be shared with it, email\nprivacy@iu.edu\n.\nLearn more and get help\nTo learn more about Google Gemini and discover ways it can help boost your productivity and accelerate learning, see\nGet Started with Google AI in Higher Education\n.\nFor technical assistance or concerns, contact the Google at IU Administration team at\ntlthelp@iu.edu\n.	KB0026654	KB0026654 v9.0	 license agreement, Google sets daily <strong>quotas</strong> of some advanced-level Gemini features. Such feature for IU users to upgrade their institutional Google accounts to increase these <strong>quotas</strong> or add capabilities	175.1472		2026-01-31 17:19:46.161797	2026-01-31 17:19:46.161797	0	1615b080475abed4d500b87b716d439b
11815	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=274070f347a456583d373803846d4360	Check your storage quota in SharePoint Online	At Indiana University, to check the quota of a\nSharePoint Online\nsite, you must be the site owner or a site collection\nadministrator.\nIn your site, select\nSite Contents\n, and then select\nSettings\n.\nOn the "Site Settings" page, under "Site Collection Administration", select\nStorage Metrics\n.\nYour site collection storage quota will appear in the top right corner of the "Storage Metrics" page, along with a breakdown of data usage.\nNote:\nFiles in your Recycle Bin count toward your storage quota. To free up that space, you must empty the Recycle Bin.\nFor more, see\nGet help with SharePoint Online\n.	KB0026221	KB0026221 v1.0	At Indiana University, to check the quota of a SharePoint Online site, you must be the site owner or a site collection administrator. In your site, select Site Contents, and then select Settings. On the "Site Settings" page, under "Site Collection Administration", select Storage Metrics. Your site collection storage quota will appear in the top 	114.5725		2026-01-31 17:20:01.467429	2026-01-31 17:20:01.467429	0	274070f347a456583d373803846d4360
11816	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7dafa837476456583d373803846d4369	About disk quota in Unix	A disk quota is the amount of space allotted to each user for file storage on a given computer.\nOn shared systems such as\nUnix\n, every user has a maximum disk quota. This prevents any individual from using more than his or her fair share of disk space. In order to keep from going over your quota, you must be sure to periodically clean out old, unused, and unneeded files.\nTwo commands are very helpful in bringing your disk space back under control:\nThe\nls -al\ncommand shows you the contents of your directory.\nThe\nrm\ncommand removes any unnecessary files.\nTo use the\nrm\ncommand (which stands for "remove"), enter:\nrm filename\nReplace\nfilename\nwith the name of the file you want to remove.\nYou can also enter:\nrm -i filename\nThe\n-i\nflag tells Unix to inquire for confirmation before removing.\nAlways be careful when you remove files, because there is no command to retrieve the file. You may create an alias that will move files to a trash can rather than removing them from the system completely, but this is generally not an accepted practice.\nIf you are unsure of the content of a file, enter:\ncat filename\nReplace\nfilename\nwith the name of your file. If it is a text file, the\ncat\ncommand will print the contents of the file to the screen. If it is a\nbinary file\n,\ncat\nwill print screen after screen of garbage characters. If you are unsure of the type of file, you may prefer to use the\nmore\ncommand, which will show only one screen of the file at a time and will allow you to cancel the command easily. To use the\nmore\ncommand, enter:\nmore filename\nReplace\nfilename\nwith the name of your file.\nThe following items can usually be discarded:\nAny file named\ncore\n, which is a file created when certain programs crash\nTemporary files which have names that begin and end with\n#\nBackup files which have names that end with\n~\nBackup files which have names that end with\n%\nAlso, discard any personal files that are obsolete or no longer needed.\nDon't forget to check all of your directories and subdirectories. Directories are the items that begin with "d" in the first column when you issue the\nls -al\ncommand.\nFor example, to check the subdirectory\ngalactica\n, enter the following commands:\ncd galactica ls -al rm filename1 filename2 filename3 cd\nAt Indiana University, for personal or departmental Linux or Unix systems support, see\nGet help for Linux or Unix at IU\n.	KB0022622	KB0022622 v1.0	A disk quota is the amount of space allotted to each user for file storage on a given computer. On shared systems such as Unix, every user has a maximum disk quota. This prevents any individual from using more than his or her fair share of disk space. In order to keep from going over your quota, you must be sure to periodically clean out old, un	92.6944		2026-01-31 17:20:05.131861	2026-01-31 17:20:05.131861	0	7dafa837476456583d373803846d4369
11818	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=20cfe8b7476456583d373803846d430c	Data Protection Services (DPS): Known limitations	Table of contents\nOverview\nBackup limitations\nRecovery limitations\nOverview\nKnown limitations within the\nData Protection Services (DPS)\nenvironment can cause issues when backing up or recovering a VM.\nBackup limitations\nLimitation:\nVMs with client snapshots that have existed for an extended period of time, or snapshots that have grown beyond the recommended safe size limit, or snapshots that were created with the "Snapshot the virtual machine's memory" (\nor equivalent\n) option selected, could present problems when performing a backup.\nResult:\nThe backup will fail and leave the VM in an unprotected state. This occurs due to fragmentation within the VM's\nvmdk\nfiles when any of the above conditions are true. The fragmentation means DPS will not be able to properly collect the client data in time, causing a timeout.\nWorkaround:\nEnsure VM snapshots are deleted in a timely fashion. VMware's\nBest practices for using snapshots in the vSphere environment\nsuggest that snapshots remain for a maximum of 72 hours.\nEnsure that snapshots do not grow beyond a safe limit. IU's ESXi Admins suggest that snapshots should be deleted once they have grown beyond 10 GB in size.\nEnsure that snapshots do not include memory:\nSnapshots created from vRA:\n"include memory?"\nRecovery limitations\nVM configurations\nLimitation:\nThere are certain VM configurations that limit the ability to perform self-service recovery operations within DPS. Depending on the configuration, this could mean that either no volumes or only some of the volumes will appear when browsing the backup history for your VM. These limitations are presented below:\nVolumes affected:\nAll VMs (regardless of OS):\nVolumes using full disk encryption (for example, BitLocker)\nVolumes that have been configured in a software raid or have data striping enabled within the operating system\nVolumes using deduplication software within the operating system\nWindows VMs:\nVolumes that are configured using Windows Storage Pools or Storage Spaces\nLinux VMs:\nVolumes formatted with the Oracle ASM file system type\nResult:\nWhen browsing from the web console, volumes for the VM will not be displayed properly. Granular file recovery is not available for these configurations.\nWorkaround:\nVMs should be built in a way that does not limit the use of granular file recovery.\nMax session file limit\nLimitation:\nFor VMs that do not have the\nData Protection Services (DPS) Restore Only Agent (ROA)\ninstalled, the maximum number of files that can be restored in a single session is one million. Once the session hits the max number of files, the session will stop and will report back that it completed successfully.\nResult:\nThe restore process reports that it has completed successfully, but not all client data was restored.\nWorkaround:\nInstall the Data Protection Services (DPS) Restore Only Agent (ROA)\non any VM that you want to be able to restore from and to without restrictions.\nFor VMs without the ROA installed, break up restores into multiple jobs to limit the number of files restored in any one session to under one million. If you cannot easily break up the restore into multiple jobs, the SAV team will need to perform an assisted restore to ensure that all data is recovered. SAV works with the application vendor to notify our team when this issue occurs.\nMax browse file limit\nLimitation:\nFor all VMs, regardless of OS, the maximum number of files that can be browsed in a single folder/directory is one million. Once the session hits the max number of files, the browse window will stop and report back that no files could be found.\nResult:\nNo files are available to view for restoration.\nWorkaround:\nLimit the maximum number of files for a single folder or directory to a value under one million files.\nDown level ROA versions\nLimitation:\nFor VMs with a down level version of the ROA installed, you may encounter issues when attempting to browse or restore operations.\nResult:\nNot all data is shown when attempting to browse the backup. You may be able to successfully browse the backup data for the VM, but not the file structure of the target destination. The restore process may also fail, reporting communication errors between the ROA and the DPS environment.\nWorkaround:\nUpdate the ROA version by downloading the current published version from\nIUware\n. See\nInstall the Data Protection Services (DPS) Restore Only Agent (ROA)\n.\nFilesystem Compression\nLimitation:\nFor VMs that have filesystem compression set, clients will encounter longer than normal windows for recovery processing.\nResult:\nThe window to complete the restoration will take on average, four times longer to process when compared to processing the same uncompressed data recovery.\nWorkaround:\nRefrain from enabling compression at the filesystem level on VMs that require Commvault Data Protection Services.	KB0023223	KB0023223 v1.0	 version from IUware. See Install the Data Protection Services (DPS) Restore Only Agent (ROA). <strong>Filesystem</strong> Compression Limitation: For VMs that have <strong>filesystem</strong> compression set, clients will encounter longer recovery. Workaround: Refrain from enabling compression at the <strong>filesystem</strong> level on VMs that require	11.082		2026-01-31 17:20:08.534781	2026-01-31 17:20:08.534781	0	20cfe8b7476456583d373803846d430c
11819	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a4134db93b472658476d941864e45a88	About the II Enterprise S3 Object Storage service at IU	On this page:\nOverview\nNamespaces and tenants\nData protection\nSize limits\nMulti-part uploads\nMulti-threading support\nObject versioning support\nBilling\nDifferences between II Enterprise S3 Object Storage and Amazon's S3 offering\nBucket-level IP address restrictions\nRequest a new tenant or additional namespaces\nConnect to a namespace\nOverview\nAt Indiana University, the Intelligent Infrastructure (II) Enterprise S3 Object Storage service is an S3-compatible, on-premise object storage service provided by the\nUITS\nStorage and Virtualization (SAV) team. Serviced from two distinct Hitachi Content Platform (HCP) clusters, the S3 Object Storage service provides high availability and durability through a combination of object replication and hash verification. The II Enterprise S3 Object Storage service is available to enterprise systems at UITS.\nThe list\nsav-hcp-l@list.iu.edu\nis used for communicating updates and patch schedules to HCP end users. If you're using the II Enterprise S3 Object Storage service, UITS highly recommends subscribing to this list.\nNote:\nAlthough the SAV team does not prohibit it, you should not use S3 for another application that resembles a file system. Overlaying a file system on an object store is not recommended, as the emulation of the file system is slow and does not scale well.\nNamespaces and tenants\nNamespaces are the term Hitachi uses to refer to a bucket. You will see both terms used interchangeably while working with the II Enterprise S3 Object Storage service.\nA tenant is a collection of namespaces, users, and permissions. Tenants are generally created for a department or a particular project that requires specific object isolation. Charge-back is calculated at the tenant level; objects within a tenant will be charged to a single account.\nData protection\nObjects are continuously replicated in near-realtime to the HCP cluster in the opposing data center.\nAs objects are ingested into the cluster, a SHA-256 hash is generated and stored as metadata with the object. Automated object health checks compare hashes, and healing is performed if needed.\nStorage quotas\nEach bucket (namespace) is assigned a quota for space consumption. Quotas are used to keep bucket consumption from growing unchecked. Quotas can be adjusted by the SAV team upon request (contact the SAV team at\nsav-request@iu.edu\n).\nAccess to the II Enterprise S3 Object Storage service is limited to addresses within the IU Data Center IP space. External access to objects must be provided by your application.\nSize limits\nThe maximum object size supported is 5 TB, using multi-part upload.\nMulti-part uploads\nMulti-part uploads are supported for objects with up to 10,000 parts. Attempts to upload objects with more than 10,000 parts will fail.\nMulti-threading support\nMulti-threading is supported and recommended to achieve high throughput.\nObject versioning support\nObject versioning can be configured on a per-bucket (per-namespace) basis and will be retained by the configured duration (0 days - several years). Each object version will count toward the bucket's quota. Expired versions are pruned from the object store periodically throughout the day.\nBilling\nBilling is calculated by the total amount stored at the end of the billing cycle. There is no charge for reads, writes, deletes, or network transfers.\nDifferences between II Enterprise S3 Object Storage and Amazon's S3 offering\nThe II Enterprise S3 Object Storage service does not have a "region" setting. Some applications are required to have this configuration. Using the tenant name as the region may be a possible workaround in some instances.\nExample:\nThe region name is the same as your tenant name. If your tenant name is ABC, you would also use ABC for region aligning with your URL:\nhttps://ABC.s3b.uits.iu.edu/bucket\nBucket-level IP address restrictions\nAll buckets (namespaces) are required to have IP access restrictions applied. These can be applied per IP address or over an IP range.\nRequest a new tenant or additional namespaces\nTo request a new tenant or additional namespaces, email\nsav-request@iu.edu\nwith the following information:\nTenant name\nContact email (group email only)\nBilling account number\nInitial space requirement\nInitial namespaces (buckets) to create\nQuota on each namespace\nIP ranges for access (CIDR notation preferred)\nWhether versioning is required for each bucket\nConnect to a namespace\nTo connect to a namespace, see\nTools for use with II Enterprise S3 Object Storage\n.	KB0023519	KB0023519 v3.0	 if needed. Storage <strong>quotas</strong> Each bucket (namespace) is assigned a quota for space consumption. <strong>Quotas</strong> are used to keep bucket consumption from growing unchecked. <strong>Quotas</strong> can be adjusted by the SAV team upon	10.5149	km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:20:11.895762	2026-01-31 17:20:11.895762	0	a4134db93b472658476d941864e45a88
11823	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=329a142f47f53a502fc6a55b416d4346	Use file systems and persistent storage in AppKube	Note:\nThe following information is technical and intended for web application developers.\nTable of contents\nAbout file systems in AppKube\nUse cases for writable storage\nTemporary storage\nPersistent storage\nPersistent storage outside the web root\nUse symlinks for persistent storage inside your application's deployment root or web root\nUpload files to persistent storage\nQuotas\nBackup\nOther storage options\nGet help\nAbout file systems in AppKube\nThe file system where AppKube deploys your code is loaded into your application’s execution environment in read-only mode. This means that once your application is deployed, the files within the deployment directory cannot be modified, and you cannot create new files or directories within the location that your application is deployed. In general, this is a best practice that improves the overall security of the AppKube environment. That said, there are still valid use cases where writable storage is required.\nUse cases for writable storage\nYour application might need writable persistent storage for certain use cases, such as:\nWriting compiled files or temporary caches\nStoring and serving files uploaded by a user of your application\nDynamic generation of web content or other files that will be served on the web\nTemporary storage\nYou can write files to temporary storage within your AppKube environment if you wish. The best way to do this is to reference the\nTMPDIR\nenvironment variable to determine where to write these files to.\nImportant:\nTemporary storage in AppKube is volatile and will disappear as soon as your application gets restarted, which can happen at any time. Use it only for things which are truly temporary and can be regenerated again, such as caches or compiled templates.\nAppKube runs multiple instances of your application (at least two) and balances load between them. Each of these instances has its own independent temporary storage.\nDo not use temporary storage for large files or large numbers of files; aim to use less than a few megabytes per app. Space in the AppKube environment for this type of storage is limited.\nDo not write to log files in the AppKube environment if you can avoid it. You will not have access to these files, so collecting log output there just consumes space. Instead, configure your application to log to\nstdout\nand\nstderr\n. These output streams will be automatically collected by AppKube’s logging sub-system. More information on logging and log retrieval in AppKube is coming soon.\nIf your use cases don't fit well into temporary storage given the caveats noted above, consider using persistent storage instead.\nPersistent storage\nAppKube can deliver persistent storage to your application's environment. Unlike temporary storage, persistent storage will be writable, readable, and consistent across all instances of your application which may be running at the same time.\nThis storage is mounted into your application's runtime environment\noutside\nof the path where your application's code is deployed. However, you can include persistent storage paths\nwithin\nyour application's deployment using symbolic links (symlinks), which will be described later in this document.\nWhen you\nrequest an application\n, you will be able to request your desired amount of persistent storage (in gigabytes) . You can request a maximum of 100 GB. If you have a valid use case which requires more storage than that, email\napphelp@iu.edu\nto submit your request and justification.\nPersistent storage outside the web root\nMaintaining persistent storage outside of your application's web root is preferred because it prevents anything unexpected from being exposed onto the web. You can still read and write from this storage inside of your application's code. You should use the\nSTORAGE_DIR\nenvironment variable to retrieve the location of your persistent storage. This environment variable will automatically be available within your application's environment. UITS recommends always using this environment variable instead of hardcoding the absolute path.\nThis type of persistent storage is ideal for storing and retrieving user-supplied file upload data, or any other file-based data you will manage from your application's code.\nUse symlinks for persistent storage inside your application's deployment root or web root\nYou can use symlinks in AppKube to create links from within your application's deployment directories to locations within your persistent storage. This includes creating symlinks within your application's web root, which is the\npublic\nsubdirectory by default. To use symlinks:\nWithin your application's source code in GitHub, create a folder named\n.appkube\n.\nIn this folder, create a file named\nstorage.yaml\n.\nIn this file, you will define one or more storage links that will define which symlinks are created when your application is deployed into the AppKube environment.\nBelow is an example\nstorage.yaml\nfile with comments:\nstorageLinks:\n- webPath: /public/example-webpath\nstoragePath: /example-storagepath\n- webPath: /storage\nstoragePath: /laravel-storage\nIn this example, we are creating two symlinks. The\nwebPath\nis relative to the root of your application’s deployment. The\nstoragePath\nis relative to the location of your\nSTORAGE_DIR\n.\nThe first entry will create a symlink named\nexample-webpath\ninside the\npublic\ndirectory of your deployed application code, pointing to a directory named\nexample-storagepath\nwithin the root of your\nSTORAGE_DIR\n. This means that whatever files are in\nexample-webpath\nwill be available on the web and served from your application.\nUse this very carefully!\nIt is crucial that you don't expose anything under your web root that you don't want to be available to those interacting with your web application. This approach is useful for larger sets of static or generated assets (files, images, etc.) that you want to serve directly to the web.\nThe second symlink is an example of how to create a storage location for Laravel. Laravel requires a special writable directory named\nstorage\nto be mounted in the same location as its application code. Since the file system that AppKube deploys is read-only, by default Laravel will be unable to write to this directory. Instead, any Laravel app should request storage and set up a symlink in this manner from the\nstorage\nlocation within the application directory to a location (like\nlaravel-storage\n) within your persistent storage.\nNote:\nThe\nstorage.yaml\nfile uses the YAML format. Within YAML, indentation is semantic and must be done correctly and consistently in order for the file to be successfully parsed. By convention, two spaces are used for indentation within YAML, and the indentation must be consistent throughout. Do\nnot\nuse tabs.\nUpload files to persistent storage\nSince your persistent file storage is made available in writable mode to your AppKube application, you can always interact with it from your application's code. If you need to interact with it through SFTP, the AppKube team can give you access to do this. Access will generally be granted on a temporary basis and is meant to be used for storage maintenance or initial loading or migration of files, but the AppKube team will consider use cases that require longer-term access when requested.\nTo request SFTP access to your persistent storage, email\napphelp@iu.edu\n.\nQuotas\nThe amount you select when requesting your storage will define your quota. The system will enforce this quota, and the technical contact for your application will be notified at certain points as your quota nears exhaustion. You can request additional quota when needed through\nhttps://webapps.iu.edu\n(up to the maximum of 100 GB), subject to review and approval by AppKube administrators.\nBackup\nYour application's storage is automatically backed up twice a day (at noon and midnight Eastern Time). If you need a restore, contact\napphelp@iu.edu\n.\nOther storage options\nDepending on your use case, consider the following alterative options:\nFor video files, use\nKaltura\n.\nFor large numbers of static files, consider\nEnterprise S3\n(subject to approval).\nFor assets that will be reused across multiple systems, consider a content delivery network from a\npublic cloud vendor\n, such as Amazon CloudFront (additional costs will apply)\nGet help\nFor help using your AppKube storage, email\napphelp@iu.edu\n.	KB0026789	KB0026789 v2.0	 your application's deployment root or web root Upload files to persistent storage <strong>Quotas</strong> Backup. To request SFTP access to your persistent storage, email apphelp@iu.edu. <strong>Quotas</strong> The amount you select	7.9273	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:20:15.170093	2026-01-31 17:20:15.170093	0	329a142f47f53a502fc6a55b416d4346
11817	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=90efe83b476456583d373803846d4323	Make better use of your disk space in Unix	On this page:\nDelete backup and temporary files\nStore temporary files elsewhere\nCompress files\nStore unused files elsewhere\nTo effectively manage your disk space at Indiana University, you need first to determine the size of your disk space and how much of it you have used. On a\nUnix\ncomputer, to see how much disk space you are using and how much you are allowed to use, from the Unix\nshell\nprompt, enter:\nquota\nTo see how much space each file on your account takes, enter:\nls -alR ~/\nThe command above shows a listing of all files in your account, including those in subdirectories. If you have many files and subdirectories, the output may be very long. To read it in page-sized chunks, use the\npipe\ncommand to format it:\nls -alR ~/ | more\nSee the following examples for ways to maximize storage on your Unix account without a space increase.\nDelete backup and temporary files\nDelete backup and temporary files of the form\nfilename~\nand\n#filename#\nby entering:\nrm *~ rm \\#*\nNote:\nWith some\nshells\n, you need to escape the\n#\n(pound sign) character with a\n\\\n(backslash) character, since otherwise the shell would treat the rest of the line as a comment.\nStore temporary files elsewhere\nPut temporary files in the directories\n/tmp\n,\n/scratch/username/\n, or\n/scr/username/\nwhile you are using them (where\nusername\nis your username).\nSome systems may not have\n/scr\nor\n/scratch\ndirectories, or you may have to create your own subdirectory in\n/scr\nor\n/scratch\n. Files in these directories are periodically erased and are not counted against your space. For information about scratch space on IU's research systems, see\nAvailable access to allocated and short-term storage capacity on IU's research systems\n.\nCompress files\nCompress files using one of several Unix compression programs. To compress a file named\nbigstuff\nwith the Unix\ngzip\ncommand, at the Unix prompt, enter:\ngzip bigstuff\nThe file will be replaced in your directory with a compressed file that has a\n.gz\nextension. You can't read files or run programs while they are in compressed format, so this command is most useful for storing things you want to keep but won't need to use soon. To expand the file back to its original state, enter:\ngzip -d bigstuff.gz\nFor more information about Unix compression utilities, see the online manual pages. At the Unix prompt, enter any of the following:\nman gzip man tar man zip man compress\nStore unused files elsewhere\nDownload some of the files you are not using to a Windows or Macintosh computer and save them on the hard drive or a removable disk.\nAt Indiana University, for personal or departmental Linux or Unix systems support, see\nGet help for Linux or Unix at IU\n.	KB0023896	KB0023896 v1.0	On this page: Delete backup and temporary files Store temporary files elsewhere Compress files Store unused files elsewhere To effectively manage your disk space at Indiana University, you need first to determine the size of your disk space and how much of it you have used. On a Unix computer, to see how much disk space you are using and how muc	2.0729		2026-01-31 17:20:05.131861	2026-01-31 17:20:41.822001	0	90efe83b476456583d373803846d4323
11827	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=0bbfa0b7476456583d373803846d43a4	About the ELA migration from Red Hat to CentOS	Table of contents\nOverview\nDifferences between Red Hat and CentOS\nChoice to upgrade or build new servers\nBuild process\nIssues ELA encountered during the migration\nDifferences between Red Hat 6 and 7\nHow ELA patches Red Hat and CentOS servers\nOverview\nThe following provides details on Enterprise Linux Administration's migration from Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) to CentOS.\nDifferences between Red Hat and CentOS\nELA encountered the following differences going from Red Hat to CentOS:\nMost CentOS package updates are available within a day or two of becoming available for Red Hat.\nCentOS point releases (for example, 7.2 to 7.3) are often a month or two after the same release in Red Hat.\nFor more, see Wikipedia's\nCentOS Version 7\n.\nChoice to upgrade or build new servers\nELA built new servers for the migration. Although an upgrade-in-place function is available, it wasn't compatible with ELA's standard build.\nRed Hat provides a pre-upgrade check application, but it doesn't check all necessary points; you need to pay careful attention to the notes.\nFor more, see\nRed Hat Migration Planning Guide\n.\nBuild process\nELA began with the CD-ROM images available in\nIntelligent Infrastructure\n.\nELA then implemented a variety of hardening recommendations (such as the\nCIS Benchmarks\n) and customizations/scripts as required by the ELA environments.\nFinally, ELA cloned remaining servers from the initial servers (the "Golden Masters").\nIssues ELA encountered during the migration\nEven though CentOS is basically identical to Red Hat, some vendors would not support their applications when running on CentOS.\nDifferences between Red Hat 6 and 7\nELA encountered the following differences between Red Hat 6 and 7:\ngrub2\nhas a different emergency/rescue boot process\nApache httpd 2.4\nsystemd\nversus\ninit\nFirewall management through\nfirewalld\nversus\niptables\nNetworkManager by default\nThe default filesystem is xfs rather than ext4\n/bin\nchanged to\n/usr/bin\n(\n/bin\nis now a link to\n/usr/bin\n)\nOther differences are listed in\nRed Hat's Migration Guide\n.\nHow ELA patches Red Hat and CentOS servers\nRed Hat:\nELA syncs local repositories (Red Hat 6 and 7) against the IU Red Hat Satellite server.\nIn the future, ELA will re-register servers, as described in an earlier CBRI presentation.\nCentOS:\nELA uses the CBRI Open Source Mirror. See:\nIndex of /linux/epel/7/x86_64\nIndex of /linux/centos/7\nELA updates production servers from a local repository that is synced to spout when test patches are applied. This prevents the application of any updates to production that haven't been verified in the test environment.	KB0023093	KB0023093 v1.0	 <strong>filesystem</strong> is xfs rather than ext4 /bin changed to /usr/bin (/bin is now a link to /usr/bin) Other	7.7988		2026-01-31 17:20:18.612723	2026-01-31 17:20:18.612723	0	0bbfa0b7476456583d373803846d43a4
11831	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=46afe837476456583d373803846d4360	Access scratch space in Unix	Many\nUnix\nsystems set aside a directory that makes temporary storage space available for anyone who needs it. It is public, shared space that does not count against your quota; it may be frequently cleared, possibly daily, or whenever the machine boots; and it is usually not backed up. It may be called\n/scratch\n, or\n/scr\n, or\n/tmp\n, for example. In order to use it, create a subdirectory (normally named by your username), and set the Unix file permissions as desired. See\nManage file permissions on Unix-like systems\n.\nFor information about scratch space on Indiana University's research systems, see\nAvailable access to allocated and short-term storage capacity on IU's research systems\n.\nAt Indiana University, for personal or departmental Linux or Unix systems support, see\nGet help for Linux or Unix at IU\n.	KB0022643	KB0022643 v1.0	Many Unix systems set aside a directory that makes temporary storage space available for anyone who needs it. It is public, shared space that does not count against your quota; it may be frequently cleared, possibly daily, or whenever the machine boots; and it is usually not backed up. It may be called /scratch, or /scr, or /tmp, for example. In	2.673		2026-01-31 17:20:31.606561	2026-01-31 17:20:31.606561	0	46afe837476456583d373803846d4360
1675	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b300f07f476456583d373803846d43fe	Exchange service policies at IU	To view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nTable of contents\nDependent policies\nAccount policies\nInstitutional data\nAddress book\nSystem maintenance\nExchange Online\nProtection\nLog requests\nDependent policies\nThe\nExchange\nservice at Indiana University is bound by all applicable existing\nInformation and IT policies\n.\nAccount policies\nAll IU students, faculty, staff, and affiliates are eligible for Exchange accounts.\nNote:\nAny mail sent to\nusername\n@exchange.\ncampus\n.edu\nfrom a system outside IU will be rejected, generating a bounce message to the sending system. Be sure your mail is sent to\nusername\n@iu.edu\n.\nAccount generation:\nCreate your Exchange account by following the instructions in\nAbout Exchange Online at IU\n.\nDisk space policies:\nPrimary user accounts in a paid status at IU are allotted 100 GB of storage capacity, while affiliates and group accounts are allotted 50 GB. There is no provision for expansion.\nTo determine how much data you have saved in your Exchange mailbox (that is, Inbox and folders), see\nCheck the storage space of your IU Exchange account\n.\nData restoration policies:\nUITS\nwill restore Exchange data\nonly\ndue to a system failure.\nUITS will not restore single messages or single mailboxes.\nTake care when deleting items.\nDeleted messages are moved to the\nDeleted Items\nfolder, where you may recover them.\nTo use the\nDeleted Items\nfolder as an effective recovery mechanism, turn off Microsoft Outlook's\nEmpty Deleted Items at exit\noption.\nFor a certain number of days, you can undo deletions after they have left the\nDeleted Items\nfolder. See\nRecover deleted items from Exchange at IU\n.\nObject removal policies:\nIf someone has left the university and the department requires an "Out of Office" notification to redirect mail to another account, contact\nit-incident@iu.edu\nto request one.\nIf a deactivated account owns a meeting series, participants should decline the invites, and someone else should recreate the meeting from another account.\nInstitutional data\nIU Exchange accounts are appropriate for storing\ninstitutional\ndata\nelements classified as\nUniversity-internal\n.\nOfficial classifications for institutional data at IU are defined in\nManagement of Institutional Data (DM-01)\n. If you have questions about the classifications of institutional data at IU, see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n, use the\nData Sharing and Handling (DSH) tool\n, or contact the appropriate\nData Stewards\n. To determine the most sensitive classification of institutional data you can store on any given UITS service, see\nChoose an appropriate storage solution\n.\nImportant:\nDo not send or receive\nCritical or Restricted data\nvia email unless it is required by your role within the university. Instead of email, use\nSecure Share\nto share any Restricted or Critical data. If you are sharing personally identifiable information (PII) classified as\nUniversity-Internal data\n(for example, names, University IDs, student addresses, etc.) via\nExchange\nmail, best practice is to limit the data set to 100 or fewer individuals; to share larger data sets, use Secure Share. If you are unsure whether email is appropriate for a particular situation, consult with the appropriate\nData Stewards\nand the\nUIPO\n. Always\nforce encryption for messages that contain Restricted or Critical data\n. This is especially important since any recipient may forward messages you send or have IU email configured to automatically forward all messages to an outside email account. IU mail servers use\nOffice Message Encryption\n(OME) to encrypt messages sent to external recipients. For more, see\nAbout confidential information in email\n.\nAddress book\nNaming conventions:\nAll address book objects (that is, entries in the IU Exchange Address Book) must adhere to the following formats:\nContacts:\nLastName, FirstName Initial\nResources:\nMust follow\nADS\nnaming convention (for example,\nIN-UITS-ETLarge\n)\nDistribution lists:\nMust follow ADS naming convention (for example,\nBL-UITS-ESST\n) or already exist in the\n@indiana\nnamespace\nDepartmental identifiers that adhere to the IU given code will take precedence over unofficial departmental or workgroup codes.\nSystem maintenance\nMaintenance is performed by the vendor (Microsoft), and is designed to not be impactful to clients.  For unplanned outages, see\nStatus.IU\nand\nMicrosoft 365 Service health status\n.\nExchange Online\nProtection\nMicrosoft\nExchange\nhas a series of spam, phishing, junk, and other mail security settings collectively called\nExchange Online\nProtection (EOP). IU deploys a variety of these settings, including Safe Links and Safe Attachments. There is no ability for\nIU\nExchange\naccount\ns to opt out of these security settings, individually or in total.\nIU community:\nFor information about IU's implementation of Safe Links and Safe Attachments, see\nAbout Microsoft 365 security\n.\nLog requests\nTo request logs or activity reports from this system, contact the\nSupport Center\n.\nIf the request for information involves someone other than the requester, or if the log information will be used in support or defense of an investigation, the request must be sent to the\nUniversity Information Security Office\n(UISO) via\nit-incident@iu.edu\n. UISO staff will then determine the context of the request, as well as the authorization required; for more, see\nPrivacy of Electronic Information and Information Technology Resources (IT-07)\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:33:38.978175	2026-01-31 14:46:15.801597	1	b300f07f476456583d373803846d43fe
11832	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=39ff60bb476456583d373803846d43ee	Display statistics on free disk space in Unix	To display how much disk space is free on mounted file systems in\nUnix\n, use the\ndf\ncommand. At the Unix prompt,\nenter:\ndf\nThis is\nnot\nthe same as your quota; rather, this\ndisplays how much space is left on the device or devices designated.\nThe output and capabilities of the\ndf\ncommand vary\ndepending on which variety of Unix you are using, so consult the\nman\npages for specifics. However, typically, you can see\ninformation about all mounted file systems, including total space,\nused space, and free space.\nTo output information on a specific device or file system node, you\nmay also specify the device or node, for example:\ndf /node_or_dirname\nReplace\n/node_or_dirname\nwith the name of your chosen\nfile system node or mounted directory name.\nAt Indiana University, for personal or departmental Linux or Unix systems support, see\nGet help for Linux or Unix at IU\n.	KB0024357	KB0024357 v1.0	To display how much disk space is free on mounted file systems in Unix, use the df command. At the Unix prompt, enter:   df This is not the same as your quota; rather, this displays how much space is left on the device or devices designated. The output and capabilities of the df command vary depending on which variety of Unix you are using, so c	2.6598		2026-01-31 17:20:34.922687	2026-01-31 17:20:34.922687	0	39ff60bb476456583d373803846d43ee
11833	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3577accf470db2502fc6a55b416d43bd	Manage Google data owned by those who've left IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nProcess to remove separated people\nIdentify data owned by those who've left IU\nTransfer ownership of data\nOverview\nWhen people leave IU, their\nGoogle at IU\naccounts are moved to a "separated" status. These accounts must be removed from our\nGoogle at IU\ninstance to get under the new storage limits set by Google; see\nAbout changes to storage in Google at IU\n. The information here outlines the process for removing these accounts, as well as the process for transferring ownership of data in Google to another person's account before deletion.\nProcess to remove separated people\nOn August 15, 2022, the quota for all separated accounts was set to zero bytes of data. After this date, any newly separated accounts continue to be transferred to separated status and also have their data quota set to zero. On November 1, 2022, all data from those who have been separated from IU for 30 days or longer were deleted.\nThis process will continue into the future;\nall separated accounts will be deleted from\nGoogle at IU\nafter 30 days of separation has passed\n.\nIdentify data owned by those who've left IU\nBelow are two possible ways to determine whether data you routinely use is owned by a separated person:\nYou may open a file in Google and see this banner:\nThis file is view-only. No edits can be made to this file. Contact file owner for details.\nThis means the owner has a quota set on their files in Google. Select\nShare\non the file to confirm who the owner is. If that person is separated from IU, follow the process below to transfer ownership and unlock the file.\nSome files may not be part of your regular work process, but are owned by a separated account. To ensure that files you haven't accessed recently are not deleted:\nLog into\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\nvia\nhttps://drive.google.com/drive/my-drive\n.\nHover over the toggle icon in the search bar that says "Search options" and select it.\nSelect the owner drop-down menu and choose\nSpecific person\n.\nNext to "Specific person", enter the name or email address of the separated person. On the lower right, select\nSearch\n.\nYou will then see a list of all files you have access to that are owned by that separated person.\nFor data that is shared with the internet or that is internal to everyone at IU, but not specific individuals, follow the non-collaboration process of transferring ownership outlined below.\nTransfer ownership of data\nNote:\nTaking ownership of a separated individual's files includes all of them. As owner, you can then handle them as seems best.\nIf you are a collaborator:\nAny current IU faculty or staff member who is a collaborator on Google files owned by a separated IU user account can request a transfer of ownership of those files. You must have direct collaboration access in Google to the file to follow this transfer process. To start the transfer process, email the Google at IU administrators at\ntlthelp@iu.edu\n, including:\nIU username or email address of the separated IU user\nIU username or email address of the user that need to take on ownership of orphaned Google data\nRequests will be processed at the end of business day every Friday on a weekly basis\nIf you are not a collaborator:\nCurrent IU faculty and staff who do not have collaborative access to orphaned files in Google should request a transfer of ownership by emailing\nit-incident@iu.edu\n, including:\nIU username or email address of the separated IU individual\nIU username or email address of the person who will take on ownership of the orphaned Google data\nGoogle links to the files and/or folders in Google for which you would like to transfer ownership\nJustification indicating why you need to transfer ownership of this data	KB0024021	KB0024021 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Process to remove separated people Identify data owned by those who've left IU Transfer ownership of data Overview When people leave IU, their Google at IU accounts are moved to a "separated" status. These accounts must be removed from our Google at IU instance to get under the new storage limits set by Google; see Abo	2.5504		2026-01-31 17:20:38.490802	2026-01-31 17:20:38.490802	0	3577accf470db2502fc6a55b416d43bd
11838	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7b7f82d347b4da5c3d373803846d4351	Data recovery guidance	On this page:\nBefore you begin\nUseful software and hardware\nMac data recovery\nSimple data recovery method\nAdvanced data recovery\nAccess the drive\nCreate a disk image\nMount the disk image\nRecover data\nAdditional options\nBefore you begin\nIn the event of catastrophic data loss (such as corruption of a file system or partition, accidental formatting, etc.), refer to these instructions for general guidelines and suggestions for using software tools in the data recovery process.\nImportant:\nIf the data you are seeking to recover is extremely important, consider consulting a professional drive recovery service. The methods described below use software for data recovery; in contrast, a professional service would have an engineer in a clean room disassemble your drive and use specialized equipment to get data off the drive platters.\nWhen a drive is physically damaged, the longer it is running, the more likely it is that the drive will stop working altogether.\nNo one method of data recovery works for every situation.\nRead this guide in its entirety before beginning a data recovery attempt.\nUseful software and hardware\nFree software\nSystemRescueCd\nFTK Imager\nTestDisk & PhotoRec\nPC Inspector\nNon-free software\nOntrack EasyRecovery\nData recovery for IU-owned equipment:\nUITS recommends that you\ncontact IU Purchasing\n, as discounts may be available with specific data recovery vendors.\nNote:\nThese are options that UITS has investigated. Other good, and potentially less expensive, non-free data recovery software options may well be available.\nHardware\nAn older (3-6 years) desktop computer will suffice, as long as it is capable of running Windows XP\nSATA drive to USB adapter; SATA is the same for laptop- and desktop-sized drives\nPATA drive to USB adapter\nPATA laptop to PATA desktop adapter\nEnough disk space to hold a disk image of the damaged drive, plus the recovered data\nMac data recovery\nmacOS optimizes data on the fly, which reduces your odds of data recovery over time. It is important to stop using the drive as soon as possible.\nMac recovery software\nDiskWarrior\nfrom Alsoft\nData Rescue\nfrom Prosoft\nSimple data recovery method\nOn a Windows installation, connect the hard drive in question and run the recovery software directly against the hard drive.\nMake sure you are recovering the data to a different drive, or to a network share. Do not recover data to the same drive from which you are attempting to recover data.\nOf the free software listed above, UITS suggests trying PC Inspector or TestDisk & PhotoRec with this data recovery method.\nThough this method is relatively simple, it may not recover as much data as the more advanced methods discussed below. It may also cause additional wear on the hard drive.\nRunning the recovery directly against the drive is not necessarily that risky if the drive isn't physically damaged. If the recovery does not go as expected, the drive should still be in good enough condition that additional recovery attempts should be possible.\nAdvanced data recovery methods\nAccess the drive\nIf a drive has physical issues, it's possible that if you connect it directly to the computer, the system will not get past the BIOS screen. Thus, UITS recommends that you use the USB adapter to attach the drive to the computer after the boot process has finished.\nCreate a disk image\nThe more you work directly with a drive, the more likely that the data it holds could be corrupted, overwritten, or otherwise damaged. UITS recommends that you make an image of the hard drive, particularly if the drive has physical issues. You can make a block-level image of the drive in\ndd\nformat (a type of raw disk format) using one of several different utilities. For more on\ndd\n, see\ndd (Unix)\nfrom Wikipedia.\nTo make a block-level image of a drive in\ndd\nformat, use one of the following tools:\nFTK Imager:\nIn Windows, the FTK Imager tool is capable of creating a\ndd\n-based image of a drive. If the drive has physical issues, or if FTK Imager hangs during the copy process, you can switch to using tools on the Linux-based bootable SystemRescueCd.\nSystemRescueCd:\nIn Linux, you can use the SystemRescueCd tool to recover data from hard disks, floppy disks, ZIP disks, CompactFlash devices, and others. These directions are current for SystemRescueCd 1.5.1. For other versions, the steps may vary.\nIt can take many hours to create a disk image, so consider allowing this process to run overnight.\nTo recover data using SystemRescueCd, do the following:\nBoot from the CD.\nAt the system prompt, to start the graphical environment, type\nwizard\n. Accept the default settings and then press\nEnter\n.\nRight-click anywhere on the desktop. Select\nSystem\n, and then click\nShow Filesystems\n.\nIn the resulting display, find the device name of the disk to which you will be recovering data (\nnot\nthe disk that has data that needs to be recovered); it should be something like\nsda1\nor\nsdb1\n.\nThe drive must be mounted before data can be recovered to it. If it is a NTFS drive, run the following command:\nntfs-3g /dev/sda1 /mnt/windows\nTo make sure the drive is mounted, run the command\ncd /mnt/windows\nand then\nls\n.\nIf you haven't already, plug in the USB adapter that is attached to the drive containing data that needs to be recovered.\nRight-click anywhere on the desktop. Select\nSystem\n, and then click\nShow Filesystems\n.\nUnder the "Disk" label, the attached USB drive should appear, possibly as\nsdb\n. Remember its name, and press any key to exit.\nTo start the creation of the\ndd\nimage, at the command prompt, type:\ndd_rhelp /dev/sdb /mnt/windows/recovery_image.dd\nA\ndd\nimage named\nrecovery_image.dd\nis now being created at the root of the recovery drive.\nMount the disk image\nA number of data recovery applications were designed to work directly on physical disks, not on disk images. For this reason, the disk image must be mounted before the data recovery software will recognize it.\nIn Windows, the Virtual Disk Driver 3 application mounts the disk image in such a way that data recovery applications can see the drive. However, some other mounting applications, such as ImDisk, do not properly present the disk image as a disk.\nRecover data\nTo begin recovering data, simply start running the recovery application of your choice.\nApplications used by Tier 2 Support\nPhotoRec\nData carving/recovery application\nRuns on Windows or Linux\nCan recover multiple file types\nDoes not recover folder structure or file names (for example,\ndocument.doc\nwould be recovered as\nFILE01.doc\n)\nCan run directly against\ndd\nimage, so there is no need to mount the image\nFree\nTestDisk\nTool for rebuilding file systems and/or MBR\nRuns on Windows or Linux\nIf successful, data may be accessible via the command line or file browser\nCan run directly against\ndd\nimage, so there is no need to mount the image\nFree\nPC Inspector\nCan recover directory structure and file names from a disk (this is not always possible)\nRuns on Windows only\nEasy-to-use GUI\nFree\nOntrack EasyRecovery Professional\nCan recover directory structure and file names from a disk (this is not always possible)\nRuns on Windows only, but can recover files from non-Windows file systems\nMicrosoft file repair tools (Outlook PST, Word, Access, Excel, PowerPoint)\nEasy-to-use GUI\nNot free\nAdditional options\nInstead of using SystemRescueCd, you could run the pre-built virtual machine\nSANS SIFT\n, which contains a number of additional tools, including Scalpel, dd_rhelp, Rapier, Foremost, PTK, and others.\nScalpel:\nScalpel is not installed by default, but you can install it from the package manager.\ndd_rhelp:\ndd_rhelp is not installed by default. To install it, download the latest version (currently 1.2), extract, and then copy\ndd_rhelp\nto your path (\n/usr/local/bin\n).\nRapier:\nTo run Rapier, from the command line, enter:\nsudo ln -s /usr/lib/libncurses.so.5 /usr/lib/libtinfo.so.5\nsudo ln -s /usr/lib/libtinfo.so.5 /usr/lib/libtinfo.so\nFor more, see\nRapier: A Different Data Carver\nfrom SANS's Computer Forensics and Incident Response blog.	KB0023598	KB0023598 v2.0	On this page: Before you begin Useful software and hardware Mac data recovery Simple data recovery method Advanced data recovery Access the drive Create a disk image Mount the disk image Recover data Additional options Before you begin In the event of catastrophic data loss (such as corruption of a file system or partition, accidental formatting	2.0691	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:20:45.825195	2026-01-31 17:20:45.825195	0	7b7f82d347b4da5c3d373803846d4351
2397	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023624	IU REDCap user agreement	Table of contents\nDocument scope\n1 Service level expectations\n1.1 Description of the service\n1.2 Service hours and availability\n1.3 Incidents and service requests\n1.4 Service upgrades and maintenance window\n1.5 Service governance and security\n2 User requirements\n2.1 User responsibilities and data governance\n2.2 Cost of service\nDocument scope\nFollowing are appropriate use policies for Indiana University's implementation of the IU Research Electronic Data Capture service (\nREDCap\n).\nThe\nResearch Technologies\ndivision of IU (Indiana University) University Information Technology Services (\nUITS-RT\n) and the\nIndiana Clinical and Translational Sciences Institute (\nIndiana CTSI\n)\nwork as partners to offer the IU Research Electronic Data Capture service (IU\nREDCap\n) service to the community of IU and\nIndiana CTSI\naffiliated researchers. This document describes the service level expectations and user requirements for IU\nREDCap\n. By using IU\nREDCap\n, a user agrees to the terms of this document.\n1 Service level expectations\n1.1 Description of the service\nThe URL for the service is\nhttps://redcap.uits.iu.edu/\n. An up-to-date description of the capabilities of the service is located at this URL. In supporting IU\nREDCap\n,\nUITS-RT\nand the\nIndiana CTSI\n:\nHost the\nREDCap\nservice in IU-approved infrastructure.\nMaintain regular backups of the database underlying\nREDCap\nto minimize the risk of data loss.\nProvide account management of users:\nCreation and deactivation of system-level accounts\nAutomatic suspension of user accounts after six months of inactivity\nUsers manage user rights in their projects\nProvide user support for questions about\nREDCap\nfeatures.\nProvide access to the\nREDCap\nAPI to allow software clients to interact directly with\nREDCap\n.\nProvide paid consulting services for in-depth user support and project builds (contact\nredcap@iu.edu\n).\n1.2 Service hours and availability\nThe intent is for the IU\nREDCap\nservice to be continuously available, except for scheduled maintenance periods.  However, the current funding and operating model requires "best effort" support.  By "best effort", we mean:\nService issues or questions about the use of IU\nREDCap\nare resolved during normal business hours, Monday-Friday 8am-5pm, or outside of business hours if a staff member is available to investigate a problem.\nIU\nREDCap\nhas no guaranteed percentage uptime, although empirically it has been operating well above 99% since 2015.\nIU\nREDCap\nhas no guaranteed response time for support tickets, although in practice\nUITS-RT\ntries to make an initial response to inquiries within 24 to 48 hours.\n1.3 Incidents and service requests\nMajor issues with IU\nREDCap\nwill be reported at\nStatus.IU\n. Users may report problems or questions concerning IU\nREDCap\nto\nredcap@iu.edu\n.\n1.4 Service upgrades and maintenance window\nThe maintenance window is every Sunday 12am-5pm. During maintenance, the service is typically only unavailable for about an hour, but we reserve the right to use the entire maintenance window for major upgrades.\n1.5 Service governance and security\nA consensus of representatives from various groups, including\nUITS-RT\n, the\nIndiana CTSI\n, the IU HIPAA officers, the IU Data Stewards, IU General Counsel, and the IU OVPIT HIPAA Liaison, are consulted to make policy decisions for IU\nREDCap\n.\nUITS-RT\ninstalls the Long-Term Support (LTS) version of\nREDCap\n, which has a major update twice a year, and minor updates more frequently for bug-fixes and security patches.\nThe timeframe for implementing non-essential changes to the IU\nREDCap\nservice depends on available staff resources.\nIndiana University ensures that IU\nREDCap\nhas the requisite HIPAA Security Rule safeguards in place for its infrastructure components, allowing data with electronic protected health information (ePHI) onto the system. However, the infrastructure safeguards alone do not fulfill a user's legal responsibilities for protecting the privacy and security of data containing ePHI.  A user must implement additional safeguards to satisfy HIPAA requirements. For more details, see\nYour legal responsibilities for protecting data containing protected health information (PHI) when using UITS Research Technologies systems and services\n.\n2 User requirements\n2.1 User responsibilities and data governance\nIU\nREDCap\nis for use only in support of academic research and for projects that do not involve sensitive institutional data except ePHI. However, IU\nREDCap\nmay not be used to directly support patient care in a clinical environment; case-by-case exceptions to this restriction may be made when the overarching purpose of\nREDCap\n's use in a project is research, and where\nREDCap\nis used in such a way that other systems (digital or paper-based) may be used to replace IU\nREDCap\nif it is not available. Regarding the IU classifications of data that may be stored in IU\nREDCap\n, see\nAbout dedicated file storage services and IT services with storage components appropriate for sensitive institutional data, including research data containing protected health information\n.\nWhen using IU\nREDCap\nwith ePHI, the user, and, if applicable, the Principal Investigator (PI), are responsible for the privacy and security of these data, and for compliance with any applicable federal and state laws and regulations, and institutional policies. To the extent applicable, this may include having the appropriate Institutional Review Board (IRB) approvals, patient authorization, and/or a Data Management Plan. In particular, the user, the PI, and the department are responsible for implementing administrative, physical, and technical safeguards that complement IU's\nREDCap\ninfrastructure safeguards. See\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\nfor what is expected.\nUITS-RT\nmaintains appropriate database backups to minimize the risk of data loss in the event of a system failure. Although\nUITS-RT\ncannot make any guarantee to the user about being able to recover a user's data in the event of data loss due to an error on the part of the user (for example, a user accidentally deletes data in a project),\nUITS-RT\ncan, for a consulting fee, try to recover the lost data via the\nREDCap\naudit log, and if the data is recovered, the project owner would be responsible for re-loading the data into\nREDCap\n; this is not a routine process, and should be considered a method of last resort.\nDue to the way IU\nREDCap\naudits user activity on the system,\nUITS-RT\nhas no convenient way of purging a project's data from the\nREDCap\nlogs, even after a project has been deleted.  If this presents an issue for your data management plan, you should not use IU\nREDCap\n.\n2.2 Cost of service\nIU\nREDCap\nservices are available without charge to IU and\nIndiana CTSI\naffiliated users.  Most data collection in\nREDCap\nis low-throughput and involves relatively small amounts of structured data collected through forms and surveys. Therefore, currently there are no quotas that limit the amount of data collected in\nREDCap\nby a given user. However,\nUITS-RT\nreserves the right to change this at any time for new projects.  In addition, a user who wishes to use\nREDCap\nin a way that uses a large amount of storage or compute resources (for example, collecting large digital media files, or high-throughput usage of the API) should contact\nredcap@iu.edu\nto discuss their usage and whether infrastructure cost-sharing would be appropriate;\nUITS-RT\nreserves the right to stop any current non-standard projects, since that could affect other users of the service.	KB0023624	KB0023624 v1.0	, currently there are no <strong>quotas</strong> that limit the amount of data collected in REDCap by a given user. However	4.5951		2026-01-31 14:35:49.029376	2026-01-31 17:20:28.210766	0	91dfacf7476456583d373803846d4336
1467	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b9af6837476456583d373803846d43cf	About Slate high performance storage for research computation at IU	On this page:\nOverview\nCreate an account\nQuota allotment\nWork with PHI\nGet help\nOverview\nSlate\nis a centralized, high performance Lustre file system designed for the persistent storage of scholarly data to meet the needs of data-intensive workflows and analytics running on Indiana University's\nresearch supercomputers\n. Space on\nSlate\nis not subject to a purge policy.\nData stored on\nSlate\nare not backed up in any way, shape, or form by\nResearch Technologies\nor any other entity in UITS.\nImportant:\nBefore storing data on any of Indiana University's research computing\nor storage systems, make sure you understand the information in\nTypes of sensitive institutional data appropriate for UITS Research Technologies services\n.\nMake sure you do not include sensitive institutional data as part of a file's filename or pathname.\nCreate an account\nSpace on\nSlate\nis available to all IU\nresearch supercomputer\nusers. To create a\nSlate\naccount, follow the instructions in\nGet additional IU computing accounts\n.\nWhen your account is created, your space on\nSlate\nwill be mounted on the IU\nresearch supercomputers\nat (replace\nusername\nwith your\nIU username\n):\n/N/slate/username\nQuota allotment\nThe default quota allotment is\n800 GiB\nper user. Upon request, your quota may be increased to a maximum of\n1.6 TiB\n. To request more space on\nSlate\n,\ncontact the UITS High Performance File Systems (HPFS) group\n. Additionally, an inode quota (sometimes called "file quota") limits the number of objects a single user can create to\n6.4 million\n.\nTo check your quota, use the\nquota\ncommand from the command line of any IU\nresearch supercomputer\n. If the\nquota\ncommand is not already loaded by default, use the\nmodule load quota\ncommand to add it to your environment. The\nquota\ncommand displays disk (data) quotas and usage for your\nhome directory space\non the\nresearch supercomputers\n, your space on\nSlate\n, and your space on the\nScholarly Data Archive\n(\nSDA\n), as applicable.\nTo check your inode usage (the number of items stored in your allotted space) on\nSlate\n, enter (replace\nusername\nwith your IU username):\nlfs quota -h -u username /N/slate\nTo check the size of a file stored on\nSlate\n, enter (replace\nusername\nwith your IU username and\nfilename\nwith the name of your file):\nls -sh /N/slate/username/filename\nNote:\nYour quota is associated with your\nUnix UID\n. If you exceed your quota, any data transfers to\nSlate\nthat you've initiated, or jobs belonging to you that write to\nSlate\n, will fail until you either get a quota increase or delete enough data to be below your present quota.\nIf you are granted a double allocation (\n1.6 TiB\n) and do not exceed the\n800 GiB\ndefault allocation for six months, your account will revert to the default allocation. (You may request the double allocation again, if necessary.)\nResearchers needing shared space or an allocation larger than\n1.6 TiB\nmay request an account on\nSlate-Project\n.\nWork with PHI\nThis system or service meets certain requirements established in the\nHIPAA Security Rule\nthereby enabling its use for work involving data that contain protected health information (\nPHI\n). However, using this system or service does not fulfill your legal responsibilities for protecting the privacy and security of data that contain PHI. You may use this system or service for work involving data that contain PHI only if you institute additional\nadministrative\n,\nphysical\n, and\ntechnical\nsafeguards that complement those UITS already has in place.\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.\nFor more, see:\nYour legal responsibilities for protecting data containing protected health information (PHI) when using UITS Research Technologies systems and services\nAbout protected health information (PHI) data elements in the classifications of institutional data\nSecure research data containing HIPAA-regulated PHI on high performance file systems at IU\nGet help\nSlate\nis managed by the UITS High Performance File Systems (HPFS) team. If you need help or have questions,\ncontact HPFS\n.	KB0022605	KB0022605 v2.0	) on <strong>Slate</strong>, enter (replace username with your IU username): lfs quota -h -u username /N/<strong>slate</strong> To check the size of a file stored on <strong>Slate</strong>, enter (replace username with your IU username and filename with the name of your file): ls -sh /N/<strong>slate</strong>/username/filename Note: Your quota is associated	466.0435		2026-01-31 14:32:55.519873	2026-01-31 15:52:55.050062	0	442fea1f3b6266d8476d941864e45a61
11840	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=342a33c51bc9d6d416c02fc4bd4bcb06	Use Google Takeout to move Google at IU data to a personal Google account	Table of Contents\nOverview\nTransfer Google at IU My Drive files to a personal Google account\nDownload all Google at IU account data\nTransfer Google Photos\nOverview\nGoogle Takeout gives IU students the ability to copy content from\nGoogle at IU\nto another Google account. You can directly download content, or copy it via the Google Takeout transfer service.\nImportant:\nIt is against IU policy to transfer\ninstitutional data\nfrom\nGoogle at IU\nto a personal Google account. To gain access to Google Takeout, you must attest to transferring only personal data to a personal Google account. See\nDetermine whether your files are individual (personal) or institutional\n.\nNotes:\nGoogle Takeout does not delete your data; you must do so after a transfer or download. In order to reduce your storage quota in your\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n, you must also\nempty your trash\nto complete the process.\nThis method will not migrate any\nsharing information\nfor your files or folders. After your migration is complete, you'll need to reshare files or folders with any users (internal or external to IU) that were shared in Google.\nYou are the owner of all copied files that move to your personal account.\nComments are copied, but version history is not.\nGoogle Takeout does not work with Google Shared Drives.\nTransfer\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\nfiles to a personal Google account\nOnce you have access to Google Takeout in your\nGoogle at IU\naccount, to transfer all of your\nGoogle My Drive\nfiles to a personal Google account:\nIn Google Takeout, select\nTransfer your content\n.\nSign in with your\nGoogle at IU\naccount (\nusername@iu.edu\n). .\nUnder "Enter a destination account", enter the email address associated with your separate Google account. Select\nSEND CODE\n. A code will be sent to the address you entered.\nTo retrieve the code, log into your separate account, open the message with the subject line "Verify your account", and select\nGet confirmation code\n. In the new tab that opens, copy your code and select\nCONTINUE\n.\nUnder "Verify your destination account", paste or enter the code from the previous step. Select\nVERIFY\n.\nUnder "Select content to copy and transfer", select to transfer your Google My Drive files.\nNote:\nYou'll see email as an option also, even though you have no Google email to move.\nWhen you're ready to begin the transfer, select\nSTART TRANSFER\n. When the transfer is complete, you should receive email notification at the address you listed in step 3.\nImportant:\nYour\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\ncontent will be migrated to your personal My Drive via a folder labeled with your\nGoogle at IU\naccount name and the date you started the transfer.\nThe more data you have in your\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n, the longer the migration will take. This could range from an hour to weeks.\nWhen the migration is complete, you'll receive a confirmation email at your personal Gmail account.\nDownload all\nGoogle at IU\naccount data\nGoogle Takeout also gives you the ability to download all data associated with your\nGoogle at IU\naccount. Follow the instructions in\nHow to download your Google data\nto manage the full download of your\nGoogle at IU\ndata.\nTransfer Google Photos\nRather than using Takeout to transfer photos, UITS strongly recommends the instructions in\nMove photos out of your Google at IU account\n.	KB0024225	KB0024225 v2.0	Table of Contents Overview Transfer Google at IU My Drive files to a personal Google account Download all Google at IU account data Transfer Google Photos Overview Google Takeout gives IU students the ability to copy content from Google at IU to another Google account. You can directly download content, or copy it via the Google Takeout transfer	1.9053		2026-01-31 17:20:49.171206	2026-01-31 17:20:49.171206	0	342a33c51bc9d6d416c02fc4bd4bcb06
11676	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=daefa07b476456583d373803846d4319	Migrate files and folders from Google My Drive to a Google Shared Drive	Important:\nShared storage in Google is available\nonly as an option for departments that want to\npay for continued storage in Google\n.\nDue to\nlimits to IU's storage footprint in the Google platform\n, all individual\nGoogle My Drive\ns now have a\n5 GB\nquota.\nImportant:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nIU community:\nTable of contents\nBefore you begin\nMove files to a Google Shared Drive\nMove folders to a Google Shared Drive\nBefore you begin\nIf you wish to keep data in\nGoogle Shared Drives\n, a paid option for departments, you can transfer ownership of both files and folders from\nGoogle My Drive\nto a Shared Drive.\nLearn more about\nmigration options for Google at IU My Drive data\n.\nBefore moving files from My Drive to a Shared Drive:\nYou must have Edit access to the file(s) you want to move.\nIf you are not the file owner, then the owner of the file must be a member of the destination Shared Drive.\nYou must be a Contributor, Content Manager, or Manager in the destination Shared Drive.\nIf moving files from one Shared Drive to another, you must be a Manager in the Shared Drive you are moving the file from and a Contributor, Content Manager, or Manager in the destination Shared Drive.\nMove files to a Google Shared Drive\nTo move a file to a Shared Drive:\nRight-click the file you would like to move, and select\nMove\n. If the file is already open, select\nFile\nand then\nMove\n.\nIn the "Move" dialog box that appears, you'll select the destination of the file move. Select the arrow in the upper left until it disappears and "Drive" appears at the top of the dialog box.\nSelect\nShared drives\n, and find your destination Shared Drive. If you wish, you can select the folder destination you would like the file to reside in after the move. When you're ready, select\nMove Here\n.\nYou'll be prompted with "Change ownership to a shared drive?" The system will note that everyone in the top level of that Shared Drive will gain access. To complete the move, select\nMOVE\n.\nMove folders to a Google Shared Drive\nBefore moving folders from\nGoogle My Drive\nto a Shared Drive, you must first\nrequest a special administrative role in\nGoogle at IU\nthat allows you to do this migration with full folders.\nAfter you have been granted permission to move folders:\nEnsure that you have View access or higher to any folder that you want to move. If you do not, you can request the folder owner to grant you this access.\nYou must also be granted Manager access to the destination Shared Drive for each folder you want to move.\nNavigate to\ndrive.google.com\nand sign in with\nusername@iu.edu\nand your IU passphrase, completing the authentication to sign in.\nIf you own the folder in your\nGoogle My Drive\n, right-click and choose\nMove to\nfor each folder you want to move. To move the folder to the designated Shared Drive, follow the same directions in the section above for files.\nIf you do not own the folder, open\nShared with me\nin the navigation on the left, and view the folders you want to move that you have access to but that are owned by others. In the "Shared Drive" section, select the down arrow to expand all the Shared Drives you have access to. Drag the folder you want to move from the "Shared with me" section to the Shared Drive where you want to move the folder. If desired, you can navigate to sub-folders in your team by selecting the down arrow on each Shared Drive. After you drag each folder, Google will review the files and if the transfer can occur, you can select the\nMove\nbutton to start the migration process.\nYou will be notified when the move is complete.\nNotes:\nAll file shares will remain active and shared with previous collaborators. Additionally, users that have access at the top level of the Shared Drive will now have access to each moved folder, even if they previously did not have access.\nGoogle will enforce the following folder move limits:\n25 unmovable items\n1% of items that are unmovable items\nThe move can have no more than 100,000 items total\nIf some items can't be moved due to permission or access issues, but the number of unmovable items is less than 25 items and less than 1% of the total number of items, moving folders from My Drive to a Shared Drive will create a shortcut in the Shared Drive to preserve the existing folder hierarchy.\nIf moving a large number of folders into a shared drive is going to exceed a limit, you will see a warning notice about this before attempting the move. See Google support for details on what might prevent you from moving a folder:\nKnown issues with shared drives\nShared drive limits	KB0024057	KB0024057 v1.0	Important: IU community: Table of contents Before you begin Move files to a Google Shared Drive Move folders to a Google Shared Drive Before you begin If you wish to keep data in Google Shared Drives, a paid option for departments, you can transfer ownership of both files and folders from Google My Drive to a Shared Drive. Learn more about migra	5.152		2026-01-31 17:14:56.724025	2026-01-31 17:14:56.724025	0	daefa07b476456583d373803846d4319
1649	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023508	About the Cyber Risk Mitigation Responsibilities policy (IT-28) at IU	Note:\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nTable of contents\nIT-28 basics\nProcess overview\nFrameworks help to prioritize controls\nFurther points for consideration\nResources\nGet help\nIT-28 basics\nStandards-based, proactive risk management:\nNew and recurring cyber threats constantly expose Indiana University to risks. To reduce the likelihood and impact of such threats, IU units must proactively manage risk and keep up with evolving industry standards.\nScope:\nCyber Risk Mitigation Responsibilities (IT-28)\napplies to all units and organizations on any IU campus that make use of the university's information technology infrastructure.\nGoals:\nThe goals of IT-28 are:\nConsolidation of IT assets into secured facilities\nReduction of duplicative services\nEnsuring proper data protections\nFocus on operational security\nIT strategies strongly encouraged:\nAccess control\nConfiguration management\nIterative risk/security assessment\nOngoing and continuous:\nAlthough the policy calls for a formal review of cyber risk mitigation efforts every two years, the cyclical process of assessing, documenting, and mitigating risk is ongoing. To learn more about this process, see\nInfoshare: Cyber Risk Mitigation - Continuous Engagement\n(recorded May 4, 2021).\nIU community:\nSee\nCyber Risk Engagement annual schedule\n.\nBalance:\nThe purpose of IT-28 is to ensure that the IU community minimizes, to the greatest extent practicable, the unnecessary creation of cyber risks, while also enabling the productive work of all units. This requires a balanced approach to (a) activities that create cyber risks, and (b) activities that can help mitigate them. Both enabling and mitigating are essential for the diverse IT services required for the university's research, education, and service mission.\nProcess overview\nThe risk mitigation process set out below should be conducted quarterly.\nDocumentation:\nSubmit and/or update IT security documentation in the context of an IT security framework.\nConsultation:\nAttend consultation(s) or otherwise engage with your UISO-assigned security liaison. The UISO may refer to these consultations as "continuous engagement".\nIU community:\nSee\nUISO security analysts and security engineers assigned to units\n.\nPrioritization:\nPrioritize and plan risk mitigation.\nMitigation:\nImplement risk mitigations.\nThe UISO will report on each unit's cyber risk mitigation efforts at the end of the calendar year.\nFrameworks help to prioritize controls\nUnits are encouraged to adopt an IT security framework.\nFrameworks differ from standard controls in that a framework merely helps to conceptualize and prioritize the implementation of standard controls.\nRecommended IT security frameworks include:\nTrusted CI Framework\nNIST Cyber Security Framework (CSF)\nThe 52 control objectives in the IIT were based on the NIST CSF.\nFurther points for consideration\nAn individual, department, or responsibility center may conclude that the current risk profile of a particular departmental service is acceptable (and gain relevant approval).\nEach unit also may decide to reduce risks by changing configurations on systems, relocating systems to a UITS-managed secure data facility, or migrating services from departmental servers to one of the array of service options offered by UITS.\nUITS provides many baseline services at no direct cost to the user(s) of those services. In the case of chargeback services offered to the IU community, IT-28 specifies that UITS will be cost-competitive with comparable commercial offerings. For more, see\nEnterprise services for units in support of the IT-28 policy\n.\nResources\nTools and documentation:\nInformation Security and Privacy program\nResources for local UITS support persons\nEnterprise services for units in support of the IT-28 policy\nUITS services directory\nNIST Cybersecurity Overview\nNIST Cybersecurity Framework overview video\nIU Data Management Tools and Resources\nData Sharing and Handling (DSH) tool\nAbout CIS SecureSuite at IU\nFactor Analysis of Information Risk (FAIR) methodology\nGet help\nIf you have questions concerning IT-28 or cyber risk mitigation responsibilities, email\nuiso@iu.edu\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:33:38.978175	2026-01-31 14:45:09.407292	1	KB0023508
11859	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=38079d2b472afa10d500b87b716d432b	Use Excel to create or modify outcomes in Canvas	Table of contents\nOverview\nCreate the outcomes framework in Canvas\nExport the outcomes\nEdit the CSV file in Excel\nEdit existing outcomes in Excel\nAdd new outcomes in Excel\nImport the CSV file into Canvas\nOverview\nCreating a large set of outcomes in the Canvas Outcomes tool for a department, school, campus, or program can be a very labor intensive process. If you are comfortable working in Microsoft Excel, you can speed things up by creating or updating the outcomes data in Excel and importing the file into Canvas. This article explains how to create a skeletal outcomes structure in Canvas, export it to Excel, modify the data, and import it into the Outcomes tool. It's also possible to create an entire outcomes set from scratch in Excel, but since minor errors can prevent the data from importing properly, this option is only recommended for advanced users. For more, see\nHow do I import outcomes for an account?\nand\nOutcomes Import Format Documentation\n.\nNote:\nCompleting the actions in this document requires administrative privileges in Canvas. For information on how to request administrative access, see\nAdministrative access to Canvas at IU\n.\nThe instructions for adding and editing outcomes in this document will result in all outcomes in the set sharing the same rating scale and calculation method, which is a common assessment practice. If your outcomes do not follow this practice, you will enter this data manually rather than copying and pasting as instructed below.\nCreate the outcomes framework in Canvas\nTo create the skeletal framework in Canvas:\nIn the Canvas subaccount for your school or department, open the Outcomes tool.\nLocate or create the parent outcome group and subgroups for the set of outcomes with which you want to work.\nCreate one outcome in the appropriate group or subgroup to serve as a model for the other outcomes in the set. An explanation of each field in the user interface is provided below:\nNote:\nThe "Description" field is formatted as rich text and cannot be easily edited in Excel. Add the outcome description manually after the Excel data is imported.\nName:\nA brief descriptive name for the outcome.\nFriendly Name (optional):\nA brief descriptive name for the outcome in language that can be easily understood by students. If populated, the "Friendly Name" will display instead of the "Name" in the student view of the Learning Mastery Gradebook.\nFriendly description (optional):\nA longer explanation of the outcome (or a rubric for scoring the outcome) in language that can be easily understood by students. If populated, the "Friendly Description" will display instead of the "Description" in the student view of Canvas Rubrics.\nProficiency Ratings:\nThe score and descriptive label for each value in the rating scale for the outcome.\nMastery at X points:\nThe lowest value in the rating scale that indicates mastery of the outcome; the scale may include higher values for work that exceeds mastery.\nCalculation Method:\nThe method for determining the score in the Learning Mastery Gradebook if the student has two or more ratings for the outcome. For an explanation of each calculation method, see\nSelect Calculation Method\nin\nHow do I manage outcome mastery calculations in an account?\n. Ignore the content at the beginning of the guide, as Account and Course Level Outcome Mastery Scales are not enabled at IU.\nIf the number of outcomes in the set is relatively small, you can create the remaining outcomes in the Outcomes tool. Enter text for "Name" and "Friendly Name (optional)", but do not change the defaults for the ratings scale, mastery level, or calculation method for these outcomes. That will be done later when you\nedit existing outcomes in Excel\n.\nAlternatively, you can create all of the remaining outcomes directly when you\nadd new outcomes in Microsoft Excel\n. The outcomes that you add to the CSV file will be created when you import the file into Canvas.\nExport the outcomes\nOnce the outcomes framework is complete, export it to a CSV file:\nLog into Canvas and navigate to the administrative account containing your outcomes set.\nIn the account navigation menu, select\nSettings\n.\nIn the Settings tool, select\nReports\nto view the reports available in the account.\nLocate the report named "Outcome Export", and select\nRun Report\n.\nRefresh the Reports page to look for the report (it may take a few minutes for the report to generate). When the report is available, you will see today's date and a download link for the file similar to the screenshot below:\nDownload link next to current date\nSelect the download icon to save the CSV file to a local drive or cloud storage service.\nEdit the CSV file in Excel\nNote:\nWhen editing an exported CSV file or creating an import file from scratch, note the following requirements:\nParent outcome groups must precede child groups.\nOutcome groups must precede outcomes.\nThe value in the "vendor_guid" column must be unique across Canvas; keep that in mind when you add new outcomes to the CSV.\nIn the "vendor_guid" column, only values created in Canvas via the user interface should begin with the prefix\ncanvas_outcome:\nor\ncanvas_outcome_group:\n. If you want to add a new outcome or group to the CSV file, the value of the "vendor_guid" column should not use the prefix.\nEdit existing outcomes in Excel\nIf you created all of your outcomes in the Outcomes tool, to update the rating scale and calculation method for those outcomes, see the following instructions:\nOpen the CSV file in Microsoft Excel.\nThe first row contains the column headings. Never change the values in this row. Descriptions of the purpose and requirements for each column are available in\nOutcomes Import Format Documentation\n.\nThe value in column B indicates whether the row contains data for an outcome or an outcome group. You will be primarily concerned with the rows containing outcomes, not outcome groups.\nLocate the row in the file for the model outcome you created - that is, the one with the correct rating scale, mastery level, and calculation method. If you did not change the defaults for method ("decaying average") and weight ("65"), skip to step 4. If you changed one or both default values, select the data in columns G ("calculation_method") and H ("calculation_int") and copy the data to the clipboard.\nCSV file with Columns g and h highlighted for the first outcome row\nThe remaining outcomes should have the default values in columns G and H; select all the cells in column G and paste the clipboard contents into these rows. Be sure not to paste into rows that represent groups and/or rows for outcomes that belong to other sets. All the outcomes in your set should now have the same values for "calculation_method" and "calculation_int".\nReturn to the row for the first outcome you created. Select the data in columns K ("mastery_points"), L ("ratings"), and all columns to the right of L containing rating scores and rating labels, and then copy this range to the clipboard.\nCSV file with columns k through s highlighted for the first outcome row\nThe remaining outcomes in the set should have the default values for the "master_points" and "ratings" columns. Select all the cells in column K for the rows of the remaining outcomes and paste the clipboard contents into these rows. If the pasted rating scale has fewer values than the original scale for those rows, delete the extra values and their labels. (They will appear to the right of the label for the smallest value in the pasted scale.)\nAll of the outcomes in the new outcomes set(s) should now have identical values for columns G, H, K, and columns to the right of K with the rating scale values and labels.\nSave the CSV file.\nAdd new outcomes in Excel\nIf your outcome set is large, and you would prefer to create all but the model outcome directly in the CSV, see the following instructions:\nOpen the CSV file in Microsoft Excel.\nThe first row contains the column headings; never change the values in this row. For descriptions of the purpose and requirements for each column, see the\nOutcomes Import Format Documentation\n.\nThe value in column B indicates whether the row contains data for an outcome or an outcome group. You will need to primarily focus on the rows that contain outcomes, not outcome groups.\nLocate the row in the file for the model outcome you created (the one with the correct rating scale, mastery level, and calculation method).\nBeneath the model row, insert new rows for the outcomes you want to add.\nCopy the entire model row to the clipboard. Select the new rows and paste the content in the clipboard into the rows.\nFor each new outcome, replace the data in the following columns with the appropriate data for the outcome:\nvendor_guid:\nA unique alphanumeric identifier for the outcome. It must be unique across IU's entire Canvas instance.\nNote:\nOne way to create a "vendor_guid" is to designate a descriptive prefix followed by a number (for example, undergrad_bio:1, undergrad_bio:2, etc.).\ntitle:\nA brief descriptive name for the outcome. This column corresponds with "Name" in the user interface.\ndisplay_name (optional):\nA brief descriptive name for the outcome in language that can be easily understood by students. This column corresponds with "Friendly Name" in the user interface.\ndescription:\nIf the model outcome contained a description, delete it from the new outcome. After the outcomes are imported, use the Rich Content Editor to add the descriptions.\nparent_guids:\nIf the new outcome is in a different outcome group than the model outcome, locate the "vendor_guid" for the desired group (all outcome group "vendor_guids" are at the top of the CSV), copy it to the clipboard, and paste it into the "parent_guids" column for the outcome.\nSave the CSV file.\nImport the CSV file into Canvas\nReturn to Canvas and navigate to the Outcomes tool in your unit's account.\nSelect\nImport\n. Then use drag and drop or browse to upload the CSV file into the Outcomes tool. When the import has completed, you'll be notified both on screen and via email. If any errors were encountered, the email notification will provide details.\nReview the imported outcomes, and if you want to, add a rich text description in the "Description" field for each of the new outcomes.	KB0023357	KB0023357 v2.0	 to create an entire outcomes set from <strong>scratch</strong> in Excel, but since minor errors can prevent the data from file to a local drive or cloud <strong>storage</strong> service. Edit the CSV file in Excel Note: When editing an exported CSV file or creating an import file from <strong>scratch</strong>, note the following requirements: Parent	5.8391		2026-01-31 17:21:02.575264	2026-01-31 17:21:02.575264	0	38079d2b472afa10d500b87b716d432b
11863	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d20f08eb478cfa502fc6a55b416d434e	About stretched clusters in Intelligent Infrastructure	Table of contents\nOverview\nRequirements\nAvailability\nDetermine whether your VMs are protected\nFailover events\nPlanned failovers\nUnplanned failovers\nBackups and data recovery\nSynchronous storage replication\nExample failover scenarios\nGet help\nOverview\nThe Intelligent Infrastructure (II) supports stretched clusters, an active Data Center deployment model in which a logical cluster contains two or more host servers in different locations. Stretched clusters use a failover process that significantly improves disaster preparedness. If one server fails, the virtual machines it hosts will automatically restart on another server either in the same location or at a secondary location, reducing downtime. The components required to enable stretched clusters include synchronously replicated storage, common network infrastructure, and sufficient compute resources.\nRequirements\nIn order to take advantage of the II stretched cluster Data Center failover capabilities, virtual machines (VMs) and services must meet certain requirements.\nInfrastructure requirements\nThe Data Center infrastructure must meet the following high-level requirements:\nNetwork infrastructure supports common (same) IP space between campus Data Centers\nCommon storage environment between campus Data Centers\nCommon compute infrastructure between campus Data Centers\nLogical configuration established to connect network, storage, and compute resources into a stretched cluster\nService requirements\nThe service environment's VMs must:\nBe hosted within the Intelligent Infrastructure (II) service\nUse replicated storage\nBe configured to use\nVirtual Private LAN Service (VPLS)\nVLANs\nBe defined to stretched clusters\nAvailability\nII stretched clusters are live for VMs reporting protected status in the\nVM information report\n. The II system algorithms manage the online location of VMs, homing VMs during normal operations to their configured campus Data Center.\nA small percentage of VMs are not configured to use stretched clusters. These include VMs not configured to use\nVPLS-enabled\nnetworks and VMs whose owners requested hosting on a single site only. Also, not all workloads require infrastructure failover; examples include services that have a geographically redundant infrastructure (DNS, DHCP, containers), and system architectures that are geographically dependent on a single site.\nDetermine whether your VMs are protected\nTo determine if your VMs are protected by II stretched clusters, generate a\nVM information report\n. The "Stretched_Cluster_Protected" column for each VM will denote if it is protected or not.\nFailover events\nA failover event occurs when one Data Center goes down, causing VMs to run in the remaining IU Data Center. Failover events are either planned or unplanned.\nPlanned failovers\nAn example of a planned failover event is a scheduled upgrade or a maintenance event at the Data Center level. There may be predictive failure resulting from a planned failover.\nCommunication:\nThe Storage and Virtualization (SAV) team uses the following channels to communicate planned failover events:\nUITS Change Management\nVMADMIN-L mailing list\n(for full site failovers)\nEmail messages sent directly to owners of affected VMs ("OwnerEmail" addresses)\nUnplanned failovers\nAn example of an unplanned failover event is a complete loss of Data Center power to racks for storage and compute resources, or a widespread compute resource failure in one Data Center with power loss to an individual rack.\nUnplanned failures cause affected VMs to reboot. Depending on boot dependencies, virtual server system administrators may need to restart application tiers. For example, if an application server starts before the database server, a service may need to be restarted to establish database connectivity.\nCommunication:\nOnce the Storage and Virtualization (SAV) team has determined the scope of the unplanned failover, the team will use the following channels to communicate the event:\nEmail messages sent directly to owners of affected VMs ("OwnerEmail" addresses)\nVMADMIN-L mailing list\n(if the failover is environment-wide)\nBackups and data recovery\nIn the event of an unplanned widespread failure in the Data Center, II stretched clusters improve service availability and ensure that VMs recover quickly in a crash-consistent state. Stretched clusters do\nnot\nreplace out-of-band backup solutions such as\nIU's Data Protection Service (DPS)\n.\nData Protection Services (DPS) have options for recovering a VM's files or operating system from a historical state. The DPS AllDisks backup option lets you recover a deleted file or corrupted operating system on a single VM.\nSynchronous storage replication\nIf synchronous storage replication is suspended, VMs will continue to operate normally, but Data Center failover will\nnot\nbe available.\nWhen synchronous storage replication resumes, VMs will continue to operate normally, replication will synchronize incremental changes that occurred, and stretched cluster capabilities will be restored.\nExample failover scenarios\nPlanned failover due to scheduled upgrade or maintenance event at a Data Center\nThe Storage and Virtualization (SAV) team will migrate VMs from the Data Center undergoing maintenance to the remote IU Data Center.\nIf possible, storage synchronization will stay enabled throughout the event.\nThe SAV team will communicate details of the event through the\nmailing list\n.\nVMs that are not protected by II stretched clusters will remain local and at risk during the maintenance event.\nComplete loss of Data Center power to racks for storage and compute resources\nFor VMs protected by II stretch clusters, storage and compute resources will automatically failover to the opposing campus.\nEvery VM on the impacted campus will experience a high availability (HA) event and restart on the remaining campus Data Center.\nVMs will continue running in the available Data Center until power is restored to the original campus, with storage and compute resources supporting the failback operation.\nOnce resources are available and storage is synchronized, VMs will automatically migrate back to the home Data Center.\nThe Storage and Virtualization (SAV) team will communicate details of the event through the\nmailing list\n.\nVMs that are not protected by II stretched clusters will remain offline during the event. Owners may need to do full disaster recovery for these VMs.\nWidespread compute resource failure in one Data Center power lost to an individual rack\nImpacted VMs will experience a high availability (HA) event.\nVMs will restart at the local Data Center as long as there are sufficient compute resources.\nIf compute resources are exhausted on the local Data Center remaining on the II stretched cluster, protected VMs will power on in the remote IU Data Center.\nWhen power is restored to the rack and servers are validated as operational, VMs will automatically migrate back to the home Data Center.\nThe Storage and Virtualization (SAV) team will communicate details of the event through the\nmailing list\n.\nVMs that are not protected by II stretched clusters will remain offline during the event. VM owners may need to do full disaster recovery for these VMs.\nNetwork connection severed between campus Data Centers\nVMs will continue to operate normally.\nStorage synchronization will automatically be suspended.\nWrite access to volumes will be available in the Data Center where the last write occurred.\nIf last write occurred in Bloomington:\nWrite access to that volume will only be available in Bloomington until the network is back online and the volume is resynchronized.\nUntil the network is restored, access to that volume will only be available in Bloomington.\nOnce the network is available, compute resources will have access to storage through a single Data Center (local access in Bloomington, remote access from Indianapolis).\nAfter volumes are resynchronized, compute resources will have local access to storage (local access in Bloomington, local access from Indianapolis).\nIf last write occurred in Indianapolis:\nWrite access to that volume will only be available in Indianapolis until the network is back online and the volume is resynchronized.\nUntil the network is restored, access to that volume will only be available in Indianapolis.\nOnce the network is available, compute resources will have access to storage through a single Data Center (local access in Indianapolis, remote access from Bloomington).\nAfter volumes are resynchronized, storage resources will have local access to storage (local access in Indianapolis, local access from Bloomington).\nThe Storage and Virtualization (SAV) team will communicate details of the event through the\nmailing list\n.\nGet help\nAfter a failover, validate that all services you support are responding as expected. If there are any issues, contact the Storage and Virtualization team at\nsav-request@iu.edu\n.	KB0024812	KB0024812 v2.0	 to the <strong>home</strong> Data Center. The <strong>Storage</strong> and Virtualization (SAV) team will communicate details of the event Failover events Planned failovers Unplanned failovers Backups and data recovery Synchronous <strong>storage</strong> Data Centers Common <strong>storage</strong> environment between campus Data Centers Common compute infrastructure	10.8992		2026-01-31 17:21:07.405074	2026-01-31 17:21:07.405074	0	d20f08eb478cfa502fc6a55b416d434e
2447	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=67cf64f7476456583d373803846d43aa	About IU accounts for Ivy Tech students associated with IU	If you are an Ivy Tech student living in Indiana University Bloomington and IU Indianapolis residence halls, or a New Castle Ivy Tech student taking classes in IU East buildings, you should be granted\nIU affiliate\nstatus during the admissions process. Once your affiliate status is processed, you can\ncreate the IU accounts available to you as an affiliate\n. The IU accounts available to you are in addition to those you have through Ivy Tech.\nIf you live on the IU Bloomington campus, you have a $4 print credit bonus in addition to the $6.50\nStudent Technology Center\n(STC) printing allotment. (As an affiliate, you cannot download software available at\nIUware\nor use\nIUanyWare\n.)				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:35:58.978185	2026-01-31 15:06:03.1723	1	67cf64f7476456583d373803846d43aa
11593	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f50fff5b337dee5004e605570e5c7ba0	Guidelines for installing software on IU central web accounts	It is understood that web application developers frequently obtain segments of sample program code, useful HTML or JavaScript segments, and CGI scripts in the course of the development of a web application. The guidelines below apply to complete, ready-to-install software products (commercial, open source, or otherwise) that account holders might obtain and wish to install within their accounts and use as part of their websites.\nImportant:\nIf you install software in your account, it is your responsibility to keep informed about updates and security issues surrounding the software. The\nUniversity Information Security Office\n(UISO) also offers a web application scanner to help discover vulnerabilities in your web site. For details or to request a scan, see\nAbout vulnerability scanners\n.\nSoftware that an account holder wishes to install on\nSitehost\nservers must not:\nRequire changes to the underlying operating system configuration of the web host server.\nRequire changes to the underlying host server's network configuration (other than perhaps the use of a virtual host).\nRequire changes to the HTTP (non-secure) and HTTPS (secure) web server configuration that cannot already be made by the account holder using\n.htaccess\nfiles containing appropriate directives in any web directory within the account.\nRequire changes to directory or file permissions (system or user) outside of the account holder's directory space.\nRequire additions or deletions of language (Perl, PHP, C, C++, etc.) extensions that will be specific to the account holder's product, and be of little use to other account holders who are developing applications. Requests made via the UITS Web team will be considered carefully to ascertain if they will have reasonably broad appeal across account holders. Perl language module extensions are a good example of this type of request.\nRequire specific versions of languages or their extension components that conflict with current versions or planned future versions.\nBe anticipated to consume or observed to consume storage, CPU, memory, input/output, or network bandwidth resources at a level significantly greater than the average usage by other account holders.\nRequire regular support services by UITS staff at the behest of the account holder above and beyond the level of support provided to all account holders generally.\nRequire root access during installation or during operation of the account holder's software product.\nRequire the use of a TCP/IP network port above 1023, such as in the case of a product that acts as a server and receives connections from the TCP/IP network. UITS will check for and monitor unauthorized use of network ports on any UITS central web hosting server, and take appropriate actions if needed.\nFor questions about these guidelines,\ncontact Tier 2\n.	KB0024534	KB0024534 v2.0	. Be anticipated to consume or observed to consume storage, <strong>CPU</strong>, memory, input/output, or network	4.9266		2026-01-31 17:13:25.732766	2026-01-31 17:13:25.732766	0	f50fff5b337dee5004e605570e5c7ba0
11866	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1adfa03b476456583d373803846d4304	About monitoring data stored in shared, common, or open locations for UITS employees	UITS\nemployees cannot monitor stored computer information in shared, common, or open storage areas. In consultation with University Counsel, the\nUniversity Information Policy Office\n(UIPO) has formulated the policy that UITS staff will not systematically review shared, common, or open file spaces for alleged infractions.\nPrivacy of Electronic Information and Information Technology Resources (IT-07)\noutlines IU's position that stored computer information may not be accessed by someone other than the person to whom the account or device has been assigned. This includes those who have job duties in technology areas; they also may not monitor or look at users' files stored in shared spaces, including but not limited to\nPages\nhome pages, IU-hosted web pages, and lab computer storage spaces.\nIf users report that they have found materials in shared storage areas that violate IU policy, are offensive, or are potentially illegal, ask them to send this information to the UIPO via email at\nuipo@iu.edu\n. Do\nnot\nlook at the materials to verify the claim, and do not ask any questions about the nature of the materials or how the user discovered them, as that may cause you to have information that may be confidential or legally protected as well. Instead, simply tell them that the proper place to report such information is to the UIPO.	KB0023723	KB0023723 v1.0	, including but not limited to Pages <strong>home</strong> pages, IU-hosted web pages, and lab computer <strong>storage</strong> spaces. If users report that they have found materials in shared <strong>storage</strong> areas that violate IU policyUITS employees cannot monitor stored computer information in shared, common, or open <strong>storage</strong> areas	8.3506	km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:21:12.571044	2026-01-31 17:21:12.571044	0	1adfa03b476456583d373803846d4304
11867	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=8abf2c77476456583d373803846d434d	Delete a SharePoint Online group site	To delete a group site in SharePoint Online at Indiana University, you should first save the files you want to keep; for storage options, see\nStore and share your individual files at IU\nand\nAbout dedicated file storage services and IT services with storage components appropriate for sensitive institutional data, including research data containing protected health information\n.\nThen, to proceed with deletion of the group site:\nLog into your group site by going to:\nhttps://indiana.sharepoint.com/site/group_site_name\nReplace\ngroup_site_name\nwith your group site name.\nWhen logging in, enter the appropriate email address when prompted, replacing\nusername\nwith your IU username:\nusername@iu.edu\nLog in with your\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nOn your group site's home page, select\nSite Content\n, and then select\nSettings\n.\nIn the "Site Actions" section, confirm that you want to delete your site by selecting\nDelete this site\n, and then\nOK\n.	KB0022998	KB0022998 v1.0	 you want to keep; for <strong>storage</strong> options, see Store and share your individual files at IU and About dedicated file <strong>storage</strong> services and IT services with <strong>storage</strong> components appropriate for sensitive On your group site's <strong>home</strong> page, select Site Content, and then select Settings. In the "Site Actions	7.1392		2026-01-31 17:21:16.485714	2026-01-31 17:21:16.485714	0	8abf2c77476456583d373803846d434d
1924	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c061b5863b02e214476d941864e45a07	Share Geode home directory or Slate-Project data with non-IU collaborators	Table of contents\nBefore you begin\nAbout work with sensitive data\nSet up and manage a guest collection\nCreate a guest collection\nAdd collaborators to a guest collection\nRemove collaborators from a guest collection\nGet help\nBefore you begin\nSlate-Project allocations are not automatically enabled for external sharing. Project owners must request external sharing to be enabled for their projects. To do so,\ncontact the UITS High Performance File Systems (HPFS) group\n. Project owners are the only individuals who can request and manage external sharing. Individuals accessing an external share must have a Globus account or a federated Globus login; anonymous sharing is not allowed. External sharing is limited to 30 days and provides only read access (downloading).\nAbout work with sensitive data\nThis system or service meets certain requirements established in the\nHIPAA Security Rule\nthereby enabling its use for work involving data that contain protected health information (\nPHI\n). However, using this system or service does not fulfill your legal responsibilities for protecting the privacy and security of data that contain PHI. You may use this system or service for work involving data that contain PHI only if you institute additional\nadministrative\n,\nphysical\n, and\ntechnical\nsafeguards that complement those UITS already has in place.\nYou are responsible for ensuring that users granted access to PHI or other protected information via your guest collection are authorized to access this protected data. The technical controls of Globus may be configured to enable re-sharing of data shared with them via guest collections. You are responsible for any unauthorized disclosure of protected information if you elect to grant other users the option to re-share your data. For more on your responsibility to protect PHI, see Y\nour legal responsibilities for protecting data containing protected health information (PHI) when using UITS Research Technologies systems and services\n.\nSet up and manage a guest collection\nUse the following instructions to create and manage a guest collection in the IU Globus Web App for sharing Geode home directory or Slate-Project data with non-IU collaborators.\nCreate a guest collection\nTo create a guest collection:\nMake sure you are logged into\nIU Login\nwith the\nIU username\nassociated with ownership of the Geode home directory or Slate-Project allocation.\nIn your web browser, go to the\nIU Globus Web App\n.\nUnder "Use your existing organizational login", use the drop-down list to find and select\nIndiana University\n.\nSelect\nContinue\n.\nIn the menu on the left, select\nCollections\n.\nNear the top, deselect all filters (\nRecent Tasks\n,\nAdministered By You\n,\nIn Use\n,\nShareable By You\n, and\nShared With You\n).\nIn the search field above the filters, enter\nIURT\n, and then select the magnifying glass icon.\nIn the list of search results, in the "COLLECTION" column, find the desired collection, and then select its name to activate it:\nFor a Geode home directory, select\nIURT - Geode\n.\nFor Slate-Project, select\nIURT - Slate\n.\nNear the top, select the\nCollections\ntab.\nIf prompted, select\nAllow\nto allow the IU Globus Web App to manage this collection.\nOn the right, select\nAdd Guest Collection\n.\nOn the "Create new Guest Collection" page:\nTo the right of the "Directory" field, select\nBrowse\n, and then find and select the directory you want to share.\nFill in the "Display Name" field. This is the name your collaborators will use; it is a required field.\nNote:\nAlthough it is possible to create multiple guest collections with the same name, you should choose a unique, meaningful name for each guest collection.\nOptionally, fill in any of the other available fields; they are not required fields.\nSelect\nCreate Collection\n.\nWhen your guest collection is created, you will be redirected to the\nPermissions\ntab of your new guest collection, where you can add the collaborators you want to share this collection with; for instructions, see\nAdd collaborators to a guest collection\nbelow.\nAdd collaborators to a guest collection\nTo add collaborators to a guest collection, navigate to your guest collection (from the menu on the left, select\nCollections\n, select the\nShareable By You\nfilter, then select your guest collection's name), and then:\nOn the\nPermissions\ntab, in the upper right corner, select\nAdd Permissions - Share With\n.\nOn the "Add Permissions - Share With" page:\nThe "Path" field typically should be set to\n/\n, which is relative to the directory you selected in step 12 above.\nFor "Share With", make sure\nuser - share with specific individuals\nis selected.\nIn the "User" field, start typing the Globus username (typically an email address) of the collaborator you want to share with. An automatic search will display results as you type; select the Globus user you want to share with. (Only one user can be selected.)\nTo send your collaborator an email notification, next to "Email Notification", select\nSend notification\n. You may add a customized message in the "Message" field. If you don't want to send a notification, uncheck the\nSend notification\ncheckbox.\nNext to "Permissions",\nread\nshould be selected by default. (The\nwrite\npermission is administratively disabled.)\nNext to "Permission Expiration", select a date and time when the share should expire. Alternatively, select a number of minutes, hours, or days that the share should remain active.\nNote:\nA policy limiting shared read permissions to a maximum of 30 days is administratively set.\nRead permissions will be removed automatically when the expiration time has passed.\nSelect\nAdd Permission\n.\nIn the "Permissions Added" pop-up:\nTo add another collaborator, select\nAdd another Permission\n, and then repeat step 2 above to fill out the "Add Permissions - Share With" page again.\nIf you are finished adding collaborators, select\nDone\n.\nRemove collaborators from a guest collection\nTo remove collaborators from a guest collection, navigate to your guest collection (from the menu on the left, select\nCollections\n, select the\nShareable By You\nfilter, then select your guest collection's name), and then:\nOn the\nPermissions\ntab, locate the collaborator you want to remove, and then, to the far right of their name, select\nrevoke access\n(the trash can icon).\nSelect\nRemove\nto confirm removing the permission.\nGet help\nResearch computing support at IU is provided by the\nResearch Technologies\ndivision of\nUITS\n. To ask a question or get help regarding\nResearch Technologies\nservices, including IU's\nresearch supercomputers\nand research storage systems, and the scientific,  statistical, and mathematical applications available on those systems,\ncontact UITS\nResearch Technologies\n. For service-specific support contact information, see\nResearch computing support at IU\n.\nGeode is operated by the Research Storage team within the\nResearch Technologies\ndivision of UITS. If you have questions about Geode home directories, email the Research Storage team (\nstore-admin@iu.edu\n).\nSlate-Project is managed by the UITS High Performance File Systems (HPFS) team. If you need help or have questions,\ncontact HPFS\n.	KB0026500	KB0026500 v10.0	 a guest collection Get help Before you begin <strong>Slate</strong>-Project allocations are not automatically enabled directory or <strong>Slate</strong>-Project data with non-IU collaborators. Create a guest collection To create a guest of the Geode home directory or <strong>Slate</strong>-Project allocation. In your web browser, go to the IU Globus Web	41.7172		2026-01-31 14:34:31.481641	2026-01-31 15:54:21.18671	0	1bb53e5f3bee66d8476d941864e45aa5
9931	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7dafa837476456583d373803846d435f	Create your first IU computing accounts (admitted students, faculty, staff, affiliates, and those returning to IU)	Table of contents\nEligibility\nNote for those returning to IU\nCreate your accounts\nVerify your accounts\nEligibility\nYou can create your first Indiana University computing accounts if you are:\nAn admitted, enrolled, or distance student:\nYou can create your accounts when the system has processed you appropriately; this typically is one day after receiving notice of your admittance.\nA faculty or staff member whose paperwork has been processed by IU Human Resources (HR):\nIf you are a new faculty or staff member, under normal circumstances you will not be able to create accounts until the morning after your employment record is entered into the university's human resources data system.\nA sponsored IU\naffiliate\nApplying to IU:\nAfter you apply to Indiana University, you will receive email from the Office of Admissions containing your\nuniversity ID number\nand instruction for creating your\nIU username and passphrase\n, which you can then use to log into\nOne.IU\nfor access to\nyour application status\nand financial aid information. If you have questions,\ncontact the admissions office\nfor the campus to which you applied.\nGraduate applicants receive their university IDs and instructions for creating computing accounts in email from their individual schools or departments after they are admitted.\nA student admitted to Purdue University in Indianapolis or a new Purdue University employee in Indianapolis:\nAs a Purdue student or employee in Indianapolis, you'll use your IU account to access services IU provides to the shared campus. If you don't already have an IU account, create one using the instructions below.\nNote:\nReturning to IU:\nIf you are returning to IU after being away, use the process on this page to set up your accounts, even if you still have access to some of the systems you used before leaving IU.\nIf you begin, and the system says you have active accounts, you are all set and do not need to continue.\nIf you try, but the system says you aren't eligible, most likely your status has not yet been completely processed; you should wait two days, and then try again. If you still can't complete the process, contact your\ncampus Support Center\n.\nIf the system says you don't have an\nExchange\naccount, follow the steps in\nGet additional IU computing accounts\nto create an\nExchange\naccount.\nCreate your accounts\nAllow yourself 10 to 15 minutes to complete the account creation process.  Your session will time out if left inactive for more than 15 consecutive minutes, requiring that you log in and begin the process again.\nImportant:\nYour accounts should be active within 20 minutes. Since the information must propagate through IU systems, however, it may take up to 24 hours before you can log into some services.\nIf you encounter a problem with the account creation process, copy the full text of any error message and contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nNote:\nMake sure you know your 10-digit\nUniversity ID number\nbefore proceeding.\nPurdue students and employees:\nYour 10-digit IU account number functions as your University ID number within IU systems; it is different from your 10-digit PUID number.  To find your IU University ID number, log into the\nmyPurdue portal\n.  Your IU ID and IU email is visible on the "Purdue Indianapolis Info" card.  If the card is not visible, choose\nDiscover More\n, or select\nDiscover\nfrom the menu and search for\nPurdue Indianapolis\n.\nGo to\nCreate My First IU Account\n.  This process is sometimes referred to as the Starter Kit.\nEnter your last name, date of birth, and University ID number in the fields provided. Choose\nNext\n.\nNote:\nIf you have multiple unhyphenated last names, enter all of your last names with no spaces between them.\nYou will see an Acceptable Use Agreement, which contains rules and policies for IT account use at IU. After you have read the Agreement, enter\nYes\nto agree to these policies, and then choose\nNext\n.\nAll students, faculty, staff, and affiliates must complete the Acceptable Use Agreement every two years.  In most cases, an\nIU Login\ninterrupt will notify you when your previous agreement expires.\nSelect a username from the available options, and then choose\nNext\n.\nEnter the passphrase you want to use in both fields, and then choose\nNext\n. For information about selecting a valid and secure passphrase, see\nYour IU passphrase\n.\nSelect at least three different passphrase questions from the drop-down list and type the correct answers into the fields provided. If you'd like more questions, choose\nAdd Another Question\n.\nBy default, IU provides everyone an email address with the\n@iu.edu\ndomain. To continue, choose\nNext\n.\nSelect a device to enroll in\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n, and then choose\nNext\n.\nFollowing the instructions, use the Duo Control Panel to enroll your device, and then choose\nNext\n.\nThe summary provides an overview of your account information. If you've been given an email address, you'll see\nExchange\nas your designated email service. To find out more about IU's IT benefits, choose\nGetting Started Guide\n.\nVerify your accounts\nAllow the system 20 minutes to process before continuing:\nGo to\nYour Accounts\n.\nThe following accounts should be listed as "Created":\nActive Directory Service\nThe email account(s) you created\nIf these are not listed after 24 hours, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 15:54:17.3744	2026-01-31 16:24:42.92513	1	7dafa837476456583d373803846d435f
9906	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=8dab870247e39690d500b87b716d4311	Install packages in a conda environment on IU's high performance computers	Table of contents\nOverview\nBefore you begin\nCreate a conda environment and install packages\nActivate a previously created conda environment\nModify the location of the conda packages directory\nWorkaround for the conda init command\nAdditional useful conda commands\nGet help\nOverview\nConda\nis an open source package manager similar to\npip\nthat makes installing packages and their dependencies easier. Unlike pip, conda is also an environment manager similar to\nvirtualenv\n.\nFollowing are instructions for creating and activating a conda environment, and installing packages in your\nSlate\ndirectory space from any of the\nresearch supercomputers\nat Indiana University.\nNote:\nPackage managers can be helpful because they allow users to install packages and their dependencies locally with just one command. However, UITS\nResearch Technologies\ninstalls and maintains many software packages on IU's\nresearch supercomputers\n, which users can access with\nmodule commands\n. Since\nResearch Technologies\ninstalls many commonly used Python packages in the python and\npython/gpu\nmodules, including numpy, pandas, tensorflow, pytorch, jupyter, and many others, check the Python and\npython/gpu\nmodules before you install a Python package.\nBefore you begin\nConda installs virtually all dependencies, even system libraries that are a part of\nResearch Technologies\noperating system installations. As a result, conda environments take up a large amount of disk space. Named environments (those created with the\n-n\noption) are placed in a subdirectory of the\n.conda\ndirectory in your\nhome directory\n. In order to avoid exceeding your home directory disk quota,\nResearch Technologies\nrecommends you use the\n-p\noption to build your environments in your\nSlate\ndirectory, rather than your home directory tree. For information on how to create a\nSlate\naccount, see\nAbout Slate high performance storage for research computation at IU\n.\nCreate a conda environment and install packages\nFollow the steps below to create a conda environment. Within this environment, you can install and delete as many conda packages as you like without making any changes to the system-wide miniconda module.\nConda will attempt to resolve any conflicting dependencies between software packages and install all dependencies in the environment. For this reason, conda environments can be large.\nCheck whether your user environment has a version of Python loaded already; on the command line, enter:\nmodule list\nThis displays the modules that are already loaded to your environment; for example:\n[\nusername\n@h1 ~]$\nmodule list\nCurrently Loaded Modules:\n1) gnu/9.3.0   2) quota/1.8   3) xalt/2.10.34   4) mvapich/2.3.5   5) StdEnv\nMiniconda uses Python but prefers its own installation; consequently, if your user environment already has Python added, you first must unload that Python module and then load a miniconda module:\nTo unload the Python module, on the command line, enter:\nmodule unload python\nTo load a miniconda module, on the command line, enter:\nmodule load miniconda\nCreate the environment in your\nSlate\ndirectory:\nResearch Technologies\nrecommends installing the environment in your\nSlate\nspace. Use the\n-p\noption to specify the file path; for example (replace\nusername\nwith your\nIU username\n):\nconda create -p /N/slate/\nusername\n/env_name pkg1 pkg2 pg3\nActivate your conda environment; on the command line, enter (replace\nenv_name\nwith the name and non-default path, if applicable, of the environment you created):\nconda activate /N/slate/\nusername\n/env_name\nUpon activation, the environment name (for example,\nenv_name\n) will be prepended to the command prompt; for example:\n(/N/slate/\nusername\n/env_name)[\nusername\n@h1 ~]$\nNote:\nIf you have installed your own local version of miniconda, issuing the\nconda activate\ncommand may prompt you to issue the\nconda init\ncommand. To avoid having to issue the\nconda init\ncommand, use the\nsource activate\ncommand instead. UITS\nResearch Technologies\nrecommends\nnot\nissuing the\nconda init\ncommand, because when it runs, it adds commands to your\n.bashrc\nfile that stop certain things from working on IU's\nresearch supercomputers\n; in particular, it may break the\nconda activate\ncommand itself. It also makes it impossible to log in to\nResearch Desktop\n(\nRED\n). For more about this issue and a workaround for local miniconda installations, see the\nWorkaround for the\nconda init\ncommand\nbelow.\nIn 2022, UITS\nResearch Technologies\nupdated the miniconda modules available on IU's\nresearch supercomputers\nto correctly manage conda initialization and base environment activation. Consequently, you can use the\nconda activate\ncommand when one of the UITS-installed miniconda modules is loaded.\nOnce your conda environment is activated, you can download and install additional packages. The exact command will depend on the programs you are installing (consult the program's documentation); for example:\nTo download and install the latest release of\nipyrad\n, enter:\nconda install -c ipyrad ipyrad\nTo download and install (\nspaCy\n), enter:\nconda install -c conda-forge spacy\nThe\n-c\nflag tells\nconda\nto install the package from the channel specified. Check your program's documentation to determine the appropriate channel to use. It is usually best if you know all of the software that you want to install in an environment and to list all the packages when you create the environment. This lets conda resolve dependencies for all packages to ensure there are no conflicts.\nIf you need a Python package that is not available through conda, once the conda environment is activated, provided Python was one of the dependencies installed into your environment (which is usually the case), you can use pip to install Python packages in your conda environment:\npip install python-package-name\nTo confirm that all of your conda packages are installed, enter:\nconda list\nThe packages you installed using conda and all their dependencies should be listed. Any packages installed with pip will not be included. To list packages installed with pip, enter:\npip list\nYou now should be able to run your program within your conda environment. Enter your program's commands on the conda environment's command line. Remember, you should see your conda environment's name prepended to the command prompt; for example:\n(env_name)[\nusername\n@h1 ~]$\nIf you don't see your conda environment's name, most likely you did not activate the environment (see\nstep 4\n, above).\nWhen you are finished running your program, deactivate your conda environment; enter:\nconda deactivate\nThe command prompt will no longer have your conda environment's name prepended; for example:\n[\nusername\n@h1 ~]$\nActivate a previously created conda environment\nTo run a program you installed in a previously created conda environment:\nActivate the conda environment (see\nstep 4\nunder "Create a conda environment and install packages", above).\nRun your program's commands. (You won't have to install the package each time you activate your environment; it should be installed already.)\nWhen you're finished, deactivate the environment; enter:\nconda deactivate\nAlternatively, you can add these commands to a job script and submit them as a batch job; for help writing and submitting job scripts, see\nUse Slurm to submit and manage jobs on IU's research computing systems\n.\nModify the location of the conda packages directory\nConda environments download many packages during the creation process. By default, Conda stores these packages in your home directory at\n~/.conda/pkgs\n(even if you create an environment outside your home directory). These packages will add to your home directory quota, so if you have large (or many) conda environments, you should set the default package directory to another location outside your home directory. To do so:\nFrom the command line, run\nconda config\nto ensure that a\n.condarc\nfile is stored in your home directory (at\n~/.condarc\n).\nUse an editor of your choice to modify the\n.condarc\nfile to include the following lines (replace\n/PATH/TO/package_directory\nwith the path to desired package directory location):\npkgs_dirs:\n- /PATH/TO/package_directory\nIf you have conda environments on more than one compute system, repeat the preceding steps on the other system to create a\n.condarc\nfile that points to a differently named package directory.\nNote:\nIf you have conda environments on Quartz and Big Red 200, UITS recommends that you create a package directory with subdirectories for each system in your space on\nSlate\n, and then modify each\n.condarc\nfile to point to the appropriate subdirectory; for example (replace\nusername\nwith your Slate account username):\nOn Quartz, modify\n~/.condarc\nto point to\n/N/slate/\nusername\n/conda-pkgs/quartz\n.\nOn Big Red 200, modify\n~/.condarc\nto point to\n/N/slate/\nusername\n/conda-pkgs/br200\n.\nFor more on this, see\nUsing Custom Locations for Environment and Package Cache\n.\nWorkaround for the conda init command\nThe\nconda init\ncommand places code in your\n.bashrc\nfile that modifies, among other things, the PATH environment variable by prepending to it the path of the base conda environment. The\n.bashrc\nfile is executed before the default system modules are loaded. When those modules (or any other modules that are loaded at login) are loaded, libraries can be loaded that hide miniconda's libraries. Subsequently, this can cause errors when you use the\nconda\ncommand. On\nRED\n, the base miniconda environment has commands or libraries that hide some of those needed to run the\nRED\nsession, usually causing a bus error when you try to log in after\nconda init\nmodifies your\n.bashrc\nfile. In general, it is rarely a good practice to modify PATH in your\n.bashrc\nfile.\nTo work around this in local miniconda installations:\nRun\nconda init\n, and then immediately open\n.bashrc\nwith a file editor.\nRemove the code that was added by\nconda init\nand place it in another script file (for example,\nconda_init.sh\n).\nAfter the login process completes, run the code in the script file:\nsource conda_init.sh\nYou should now be able to use\nconda activate\n.\nAdditional useful conda commands\nTo check which packages are available in a miniconda module, enter:\nconda list\nTo list all the conda environments you have created, enter:\nconda info --envs\nTo delete a conda environment, use (replace\nenv_name\nwith the name of the conda environment you want to delete):\nconda env remove --name\nenv_name\nTo remove all unused packages, tarballs, logs, and the index cache, enter:\nconda clean -a\nTo share your conda environment with collaborators:\nCreate and activate your conda environment, and install your package(s).\nIn your conda environment, run the following command:\nconda env export > environment.yml\nThis exports a list of your environment's dependencies to the file\nenvironment.yml\n.\nEmail the\nenvironment.yml\nfile to your collaborators, and direct them to upload the file and run the following command:\nconda env create -f environment.yml\nThis downloads the conda packages as a conda environment in their local directories. From there, they can activate the environment and start running their analyses.\nGet help\nFor more about conda, see the\nconda User Guide\n.\nIf you have questions or need help,\ncontact the UITS\nResearch Applications and Deep Learning\nteam\n.	KB0022379	KB0022379 v9.0		0	Public	2026-01-31 15:54:04.070219	2026-01-31 16:35:07.522504	1	8dab870247e39690d500b87b716d4311
11700	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=45cf2cb7476456583d373803846d4366	Troubleshoot Microsoft Outlook integration for OnBase	Table of contents\nOverview\nAdd-in shows as enabled but the OnBase task is not appearing\nIntegration for Microsoft Outlook is disabled on startup\nOverview\nSince the upgrade to OnBase 16, the Microsoft Outlook 2016 and 2019 add-in for OnBase sometimes becomes disabled. To resolve this, follow the steps below. You may need help from your local UITS support person.\nNote:\nThe Enterprise Business Process Solutions (EBPS) team cannot assist with Outlook integration issues beyond the instructions below, which were provided by Hyland (creators of OnBase) support team.\nAdd-in shows as enabled but the OnBase task is not appearing\nIn Microsoft Outlook, from the\nFile\nmenu, select\nManage COM Add-ins (Slow and Disabled COM Add-ins)\n.\nUnder "Hyland.Office.Outlook.Addin", select\nDisable this add-in\n.\nRestart Outlook. If the add-in is still disabled, follow steps 1-2 again, but choose\nAlways enable this add-in\n.\nIntegration for Microsoft Outlook is disabled on startup\nWarning:\nThis contains instructions for editing the\nregistry\n. If you make any error while editing the registry, you can potentially cause Windows to fail or be unable to boot, requiring you to reinstall Windows. Edit the registry at your own risk. Always back up the registry before making any changes. If you do not feel comfortable editing the registry, do not attempt these instructions. Instead, seek the help of a computing support provider.\nVerify the add-in is available\nIf the integration for Microsoft Outlook is disabled on startup, verify the add-in is available:\nIn Microsoft Outlook, from the\nFile\nmenu, select\nOptions\n, and then\nAdd-ins\n.\nFrom the\nManage\ndrop-down, select\nDisabled Items\n.\nHighlight\nHyland.Office20XX.Outlook.Addin\nand select\nGo\n.\nWhen prompted with the "Disabled Items" list, highlight\nHyland.Office20XX.Outlook.Addin\nand select\nEnable\n.\nRestart Outlook. If the add-in is available but will not enable, complete the steps in\nForce-enable Outlook integration\n.\nForce-enable Outlook integration\nIf you have\nverified that the add-in is available\n, but it will not enable, change the\nLoadBehavior\nentry in the registry to force-enable the integration.\nFrom the Start menu, search for\nregedit\n. Select it.\nNavigate to the appropriate registry entry:\nDefault:\nHKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Microsoft\\Office\\Outlook\\Addins\\Hyland.Office20XX.Outlook.Addin\nClick-Once location:\nHKEY_CURRENT_USER\\Software\\Microsoft\\Office\\Outlook\\Addins\\Hyland.Office20XX.Outlook.Addin\nNote:\nIf using the 32-bit Outlook integration, verify that you're under\nWow6432Node\n.\nSet\nLoadBehavior = 3\n.\nFor more about the meaning of LoadBehavior values, see the "LoadBehavior Values" section in Microsoft's\nRegistry Entries for VSTO Add-ins\n.\nRestart Outlook and verify the add-in is no longer disabled. If LoadBehavior is still not working, follow the steps in\nUse DoNotDisableAddinList in the registry\n.\nUse DoNotDisableAddinList in the registry\nIf you followed the steps in\nForce-enable Outlook integration\nand your\nLoadBehavior\nchange had no effect:\nFrom the Start menu, search for\nregedit\n. Select it.\nVerify the\nResiliency\nkey is created by navigating to:\nHKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Microsoft\\Office\\16.0\\Outlook\\Resiliency\\DoNotDisableAddinList\\Hyland.OfficeXXXX.Outlook.Addin\nNotes:\nYou can create this key manually if required.\nIf using the 32-bit Outlook integration, verify that you're under\nWow6432Node\n.\nOnce the location has been verified or created, set the value to\n1\n.\nTest Outlook to verify the add-in is no longer disabled.\nNotes:\nWhen OnBase is upgraded on your machine, you will need to complete these steps again. Uninstalling OnBase should remove the registry entries.\nIf Outlook is uninstalled or reinstalled, you will need to uninstall or reinstall the integration and edit the registry entries again. A patch update to Outlook shouldn't affect the integration.	KB0023261	KB0023261 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Add-in shows as enabled but the OnBase task is not appearing Integration for Microsoft Outlook is disabled on startup Overview Since the upgrade to OnBase 16, the Microsoft Outlook 2016 and 2019 add-in for OnBase sometimes becomes disabled. To resolve this, follow the steps below. You may need help from your local UITS	4.4727	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:15:43.787193	2026-01-31 17:15:43.787193	0	45cf2cb7476456583d373803846d4366
1697	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024814	About IU Enterprise Confluence	Table of contents\nOverview\nInstitutional data in IU Enterprise Confluence\nOverview\nIU Enterprise Confluence allows teams in support of the Indiana University community to easily publish, organize, and access team/department information.\nConfluence works in conjunction with JIRA.\nProject collaboration:\nCreate, share, and collaborate on projects in one place. Supports multimedia and dynamic content.\nTeam documentation:\nCapture, store, and add to your team documentation.\nKnowledge base:\nEasily publish, organize, and access your information in one central location. Share PDFs, images, and more. Automatic versioning, instant previews, full-text search, and pinned comments make it easy to manage files.\nTeam calendars:\nPlan Jira software releases, plan projects, or schedule team leave, travel, and events.\nFor information on how to use Confluence, including examples and links to documentation, visit\nTesting Tools Support\nand drill into Confluence, or see the\nConfluence description\n.\nInstitutional data in IU Enterprise Confluence\nYou\nmay not\nuse IU Enterprise Confluence to store institutional data classified as\nCritical or Restricted data\n. This includes, but is not limited to:\nSocial Security and driver's license numbers (even if considered PHI)\nPINs and credit/payment card information (even if considered PHI)\nBanking and student loan information\nStudent academic transcripts that contain student Social Security numbers\nPasswords and passphrases\nFinancial account information\nInformation regulated by Export Control Laws, such as certain types of research or information about restricted items, technology, or software (see\nCompliance: Export Control\n)\nFor more about classification levels for institutional data at IU, see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n. For more about working with sensitive institutional data at IU, see:\nCritical Data Guide\nAbout dedicated file storage services and IT services with storage components appropriate for sensitive institutional data, including research data containing protected health information	KB0024814	KB0024814 v2.0	 dedicated file <strong>storage</strong> services and IT services with <strong>storage</strong> components appropriate for sensitive	6.2284		2026-01-31 14:33:38.978175	2026-01-31 17:21:23.801744	0	77808ceb47ab5e182fc6a55b416d43da
1445	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023488	Sponsor a computing account for an IU affiliate	Table of contents\nOverview\nInformation you will need about the affiliate\nRequest affiliate sponsorship\nRenew sponsorship\nOverview\nIndividuals who are not students, faculty, or staff, but who are affiliated with IU (for example, contracted employees or consultants) may be eligible for an account as an IU affiliate. These accounts must be sponsored by a full-time IU faculty or staff member. An Affiliate account is active for up to one year and can be renewed annually. For details about account eligibility, see\nEligibility to Use Information Technology Resources (IT-03)\n.\nAffiliate accounts are\nnot\nintended for:\nThose who will later become IU students, faculty, or staff. These individuals must wait to create their accounts until they are admitted or their records are entered into the relevant IU system (such as the Human Resources office).\nThose who simply need parking, door access, and a picture ID while performing duties on campus. These individuals can be designated as having "Affiliate" status in the CrimsonCard system without having a sponsored IU Affiliate account.\nInformation you will need about the affiliate\nFull name (first, middle, and last)\nUniversity ID (If known)\nCampus: The specific IU campus where the affiliate needs accounts\nDate of birth\nExternal email\nDepartment with which the person is affiliated\nCampus address\nAffiliation with IU: If affiliation is not listed, choose\nOther - Please Explain\nDate affiliation with IU begins (Affiliate access to accounts is not available until this date.)\nDate affiliation with IU expires\nReason for affiliation with IU: A description of how the person is affiliated with IU. Also, if there is a grant or contract on file, include it here.\nRequest affiliate sponsorship\nGo to\nManage your affiliates\n.\nSelect\nrequest to add IU affiliate\n.\nEnter the requested information about you and about the affiliate. (The field for the department name has a 30-character limit.)\nSelect\nRequest Affiliate\n.\nWithin two business days, the sponsor will receive a response with further instructions.\nRenew sponsorship\nGo to\nManage your affiliates\n.\nSelect\nView affiliate requests\n.\nAt the bottom of your affiliate list, select\nUpdate Approved requests\n. This will change the "Action" field to "Update" from "View". You can now make the necessary changes to affiliate records: name, birth date, email, campus address, and date this affiliation with IU expires.\nWhen you have completed the changes, select the\nUpdate\nbutton at the bottom.\nTier 2 Accounts and Student Information Systems (ASIS)\nwill process your request within 24 hours, unless there are questions about the request.\nNote:\nUnder Indiana University's\nEligibility to Use Information Technology Resources (IT-03)\n:\nSponsors of accounts should weigh the risks of an affiliated user causing harm through inappropriate use of IU information technology resources against the benefits of the user having access. Sponsors are responsible for taking steps to reduce the likelihood of inappropriate use (including appropriate user training). Additionally, prior to requesting the account, the sponsor and the person being sponsored should have a written agreement defining the terms of the affiliation relationship. Any written agreement must comply with university policy\nSignature Authority and Delegation (FIN-TRE-100)\n. Sponsors should consult with their department or unit on how best to document the user's affiliation with and responsibilities to both IU and the sponsor's department or unit.\nFor more, see\nYour responsibilities as a computer user at IU\n.	KB0023488	KB0023488 v2.0	Table of contents Overview Information you will need about the affiliate Request affiliate sponsorship Renew sponsorship Overview Individuals who are not students, faculty, or staff, but who are affiliated with IU (for example, contracted employees or consultants) may be eligible for an account as an IU affiliate. These accounts must be sponsore	27.1018		2026-01-31 14:32:41.739544	2026-01-31 17:07:44.001651	0	aa98be2247186ed42fc6a55b416d43b3
11870	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=fb30bc7347a456583d373803846d430f	Privacy guidelines for managing event data at IU	Indiana University hosts many events that may include the collection of personal information during the registration process. Personal information that is collected for an IU-hosted event is classified as\nRestricted data\nand should not be shared with others without consent from the participants. Even if it doesn't fall under the university's definition of\ninstitutional data\n, it is important that there are safeguards in place to protect the information from unauthorized access.\nEvent data is classified as any information that is collected at the registration of a conference, webinar, or other activities such as an academic camp or sports clinic. Examples include:\nParticipant name\nParticipant email address\nParticipant home address\nParticipant home phone number\nUser affiliation\nEvent attended\nIP address\nIf you manage event data, it is important to follow the\nbasic privacy principles\nthat guide data privacy at IU. Remember to limit the collection, storage, and processing of data to only what is strictly necessary for business operations, and do not share without consent from the event participants.\nConsent to share personal information for an event does not permit event organizers to further share the data for unrelated reasons or follow-up communications for other events or services.  Event attendees have the expectation that they are sharing information to attend a specific event; they may not wish to receive follow-ups unrelated to the event or to share their information with other organizations and third parties. Participants should have the choice to opt in to have their information shared for these purposes, after they have been informed of the consent language on the registration form.\nIf you receive a request for the release of event data or have general questions about appropriate use and sharing of personal event information, consult with the appropriate\nData Steward\nor contact\nprivacy@iu.edu\n.	KB0025922	KB0025922 v1.0	 email address Participant <strong>home</strong> address Participant <strong>home</strong> phone number User affiliation Event that guide data privacy at IU. Remember to limit the collection, <strong>storage</strong>, and processing of data to only	6.211		2026-01-31 17:21:27.632358	2026-01-31 17:21:27.632358	0	fb30bc7347a456583d373803846d430f
11680	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=50ff2c7b476456583d373803846d4344	Determine whether your files are individual (personal) or institutional	If you're unsure whether you're storing individual or\ninstitutional files\n, ask yourself:\nIf you were to change positions or leave Indiana University tomorrow, would your department or colleagues need these files to continue their work? If so, they are\ninstitutional files\n.\nAre any of these files classified as\nPublic data\n,\nUniversity-Internal data\n,\nRestricted data\n(such as grades), or\nCritical data\n(such as PHI)? If so, they are\ninstitutional files\n.\nExamples of institutional files may include research data, course files (such as lesson plans, assignment outlines, syllabi, etc. that are not stored within Canvas), training manuals, or files associated with IU committees, labs, and student groups, etc. Most faculty and staff store institutional files.\nIndividual files, on the other hand, are personally created files, such as resumes, personal meeting notes, or presentations.\nImportant:\nIf you are storing institutional files, see the\nInstitutional storage request form\nunless your\nlocal UITS support person\nhas already set this up for you. You may request an institutional storage account that only you can access, if desired.	KB0024228	KB0024228 v1.0		4.7526		2026-01-31 17:15:03.387959	2026-01-31 17:15:03.387959	0	50ff2c7b476456583d373803846d4344
11874	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=16c34b9b477cfa18d500b87b716d4306	Collaborate in Microsoft Teams at IU with external contacts	Table of contents\nOverview\nAdd a contact to\nMicrosoft Teams\nAdd an external collaborator to a team\nAdd an external collaborator to a private channel\nOverview\nIn\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\n, you can add people who use\nMicrosoft Teams\nat other organizations as guest users. For more, see\nCommunicate with users from other organizations in\nMicrosoft Teams\n.\nNote:\nIf you're collaborating within\nMicrosoft at IU Secure Storage\n, ensure that external users are permitted before adding them. For more, see\nFolder naming conventions\nin\nProtect sensitive data in Microsoft at IU Secure Storage\n.\nAdd a contact to\nMicrosoft Teams\nTo add a contact to\nMicrosoft Teams\n:\nIn the side menu, select\nPeople\n. If you don't see\nPeople\nin the side menu, select the three dots (\n...\n, and then select\nPeople\n.\nOn the screen that opens, next to "People", select\nAdd contact\n(person with a plus sign icon).\nEnter the name, IU username, phone number, or email address of the contact, and then select\nAdd\n.\nFor more about managing contacts, see\nView or add contacts in\nMicrosoft Teams\n.\nAdd an external collaborator to a team\nTo add an external collaborator to a private channel within a Microsoft Teams team, the collaborator must first be a member of the main team\n:\nNavigate to the team, select the three dots (\n...\n) beside the team name, and then select\nAdd member\n.\nEnter the email address of the external collaborator. If you have the external collaborator added\nas a contact\n, select the result that appears. If not, select\nAdd [email address] as a guest\n, then\nAdd\n, and then\nClose\n.\nThe guest will receive an invitation at their email address with information to access the team.\nTo be able to successfully accept the invitation, the guest user will need to have a Microsoft account with access to\nMicrosoft Teams\n. The guest may also be required to set up Microsoft two-factor authentication when they first access the team (it will prompt them to enter a phone number for call/text), and, if the guest user's Microsoft account is a member of\nMicrosoft Teams\nat another institution, they may need to use the drop-down menu at the top right of their\nMicrosoft Teams\nclient to switch between views for their home institution, Indiana University, and any other institutions through which they have access to\nMicrosoft Teams\n.\nAdd an external collaborator to a private channel\nTo add an external collaborator to a private channel within a\nMicrosoft Teams\nteam:\nSelect the three dots (\n...\n) beside the channel, and then select\nAdd member\n.\nEnter the email address of the external collaborator. If you have the external collaborator added\nas a contact\n, select the result that appears. If not, select\nAdd [email address] as a guest\n, then\nAdd\n, and then\nClose\n.\nThe guest will receive an invitation at their email address with information to access the private channel.\nNote:\nIf a guest user without a Microsoft account enters their email address when trying to access a team, they may be redirected to an\nIU Login\npage rather than receiving an error message explaining the issue.	KB0025477	KB0025477 v3.0	 at IU Secure <strong>Storage</strong>, ensure that external users are permitted before adding them. For more, see Folder naming conventions in Protect sensitive data in Microsoft at IU Secure <strong>Storage</strong>. Add a contact right of their Microsoft Teams client to switch between views for their <strong>home</strong> institution, Indiana	5.6779		2026-01-31 17:21:53.705281	2026-01-31 17:21:53.705281	0	16c34b9b477cfa18d500b87b716d4306
11877	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5b00b07f476456583d373803846d432d	Manually add Windows devices to Microsoft SCCM	Table of contents\nOverview\nBegin the import\nImport more than one device from a CSV file\nImport a single device\nOverview\nTo manually create computer objects in Microsoft SCCM for devices that are currently unknown to the site, request an OU scan for a specific OU path(s) in Active Directory from\nsct2@iu.edu\nor follow the steps below to avoid USB network devices becoming bound to a device as part of the SCCM computer object.\nDevices manually added to SCCM will not immediately appear in your collection environment. In general, devices will join your "All Machines Without SCCM Agent" collection within four hours of being added to the site. To expedite this process, right-click your\nAll Machines Without SCCM Agent\ncollection and choose\nUpdate Membership\n. Allow at least 15 minutes between the manual addition of devices and your attempts to update the collection membership.\nBegin the import\nBefore adding devices, you will need:\nA list of device names\nEither the MAC addresses or the SMBIOS GUID from each device\nNote:\nIf you intend to create computer objects that let you use USB network devices to build multiple devices, use the SMBIOS GUID when manually importing a device. In all other cases, UITS\nEndpoint Management Services\nrecommends using the MAC address.\nTo import devices:\nIn the SCCM console, select\nAssets and Compliance\n>\nOverview\n>\nDevices\nto see a list of all devices that are scoped to your collection environment.\nIn the "Assets and Compliance" navigation pane, right-click\nDevices\nand choose\nImport Computer Information\n.\nAlternatively, under the\nHome\ntab, select\nImport Computer Information\n.\nProceed to\nimport more than one device from a CSV file\n, or\nimport a single device\n.\nImport more than one device from a CSV file\nNote:\nIf using a CSV file, each record should represent one device (that is, each row should have one device name and its relative MAC address or SMBIOS GUID).\nTo import more than one device:\nIn the "Select Source" window, choose\nImport computers using a file\n, and select\nNext\n.\nIn the "Choose Mapping" window:\nNext to "Import file:", select\nBrowse\n. Note that the CSV file must be saved in the departmental storage space on the SCCM file server for your department.\nExample:\n\\\\iu-uits-sccmfp1\\ROOT\\Test\\Departmental\\BL\\Demo\nIf you do not have departmental storage space in the file server, submit a request to\nsct2@iu.edu\n.\nUnder "File preview:", configure the source columns so that the sample data matches the correctly assigned value, and then select\nNext\n.\nIn the "Data Preview" window, confirm that the data to be imported from the CSV file are accurate, and then select\nNext\n.\nIn the "Choose Target Collection" window, choose\nAdd new computers only to the All Systems collection\n, and then select\nNext\n.\nReview the summary to confirm the details associated with the pending import, and then select\nNext\nto complete the process.\nIf the import is successful, you will see the "Confirmation" window. Select\nClose\nto exit the Import Computer Information Wizard.\nImport a single device\nTo import a single device:\nIn the "Select Source" window, choose\nImport single computer\nand select\nNext\n.\nIn the "Single Computer" window:\nEnter the computer name and the MAC address\nor\nthe SMBIOS GUID.\nSelect\nNext\n.\nIn the "Data Preview" window, confirm that the data that will be used to create the machine object are accurate, and then select\nNext\n.\nIn the "Choose Target Collection" window, choose\nAdd new computers only to the All Systems collection\n, and then select\nNext\n.\nReview the summary to confirm the details associated with the pending import, and then select\nNext\nto complete the process.\nIf the import is successful, you will see the "Confirmation" window. Select\nClose\nto exit the Import Computer Information Wizard.	KB0024853	KB0024853 v1.0	 Devices and choose Import Computer Information. Alternatively, under the <strong>Home</strong> tab, select Import that the CSV file must be saved in the departmental <strong>storage</strong> space on the SCCM file server for your <strong>storage</strong> space in the file server, submit a request to sct2@iu.edu. Under "File preview:", configure	5.3989	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:21:57.344949	2026-01-31 17:21:57.344949	0	5b00b07f476456583d373803846d432d
1446	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023441	About protected health information (PHI)	According to the US Department of Health and Human Services,\nprotected health information (PHI)\nis individually identifiable information (see below for definition) that is:\nexcept as provided in item 2 of this definition,\ntransmitted by electronic media;\nmaintained in electronic media; or\ntransmitted or maintained in any other form or medium (includes paper and oral communication).\nProtected health information excludes individually identifiable health information:\nin education records covered by the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act, as amended, 20 U.S.C. 1232g;\nin records described at 20 U.S.C. 1232g(a)(4)(B)(iv);\nin employment records held by a covered entity (see below for definition) in its role as employer; and\nregarding a person who has been deceased for more than 50 years.\nIndividually identifiable health information\nis information that is a subset of health information, including demographic information collected from an individual, and\nis created, or received by a health care provider, health plan, or health care clearing house; and\nrelates to past, present, or future physical or mental health conditions of an individual; the provision of health care to the individual; or past, present, or future payment for health care to an individual, and\nthat identifies the individual; or\nwith respect to which there is a reasonable basis to believe the information can be used to identify the individual.\nIndividually identifiable health information (PHI) is subject to state and federal privacy and security rules including, but not limited to, the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA).\nA\ncovered entity\nis any health plan, health care clearing house, or health care provider who transmits any health information in electronic form in connection with a qualified transaction and their business associates. Indiana University has designated itself as a Hybrid Covered Entity and all IU HIPAA Affected Areas are expected to adhere to the HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules as well as any other area or unit that creates, uses, or stores PHI from another HIPAA Affected Area or outside covered entity.\nData are "individually identifiable" if they include any of the 18 types of identifiers for an individual or for the individual's employer or family member, or if the provider or researcher is aware that the information could be used, either alone or in combination with other information, to identify an individual. These identifiers are:\nName\nAddress (all geographic subdivisions smaller than state, including street address, city, county, or ZIP code)\nAll elements (except years) of dates related to an individual (including birth date, admission date, discharge date, date of death, and exact age if over 89)\nTelephone numbers\nFAX number\nEmail address\nSocial Security number\nMedical record number\nHealth plan beneficiary number\nAccount number\nCertificate/license number\nVehicle identifiers and serial numbers, including license plate numbers\nDevice identifiers or serial numbers\nWeb URLs\nIP address\nBiometric identifiers, including finger or voice prints\nFull-face photographic images and any comparable images\nAny other unique identifying number, characteristic, or code\nAll protected health information is subject to federal Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (\nHIPAA\n) regulation.\nElectronic protected health information (ePHI)\nElectronic protected health information (ePHI) is any protected health information (PHI) that is created, stored, transmitted, or received electronically.\nElectronic protected health information includes any medium used to store, transmit, or receive PHI electronically. The following and any future technologies used for accessing, transmitting, or receiving PHI electronically are covered by the HIPAA Security Rule:\nMedia containing data at rest (storage)\nPersonal computers with internal hard drives used at work, home, or traveling\nExternal portable hard drives, including iPods and similar devices\nMagnetic tape\nRemovable storage devices, such as USB memory sticks, CDs, DVDs, and floppy disks\nPDAs and smartphones\nData in transit, via wireless,\nEthernet\n, modem,\nDSL\n, or cable network connections\nEmail\nFile transfer\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.	KB0023441	KB0023441 v1.0	 by the HIPAA Security Rule: Media containing data at rest (<strong>storage</strong>) Personal computers with internal hard drives used at work, <strong>home</strong>, or traveling External portable hard drives, including iPods and similar devices Magnetic tape Removable <strong>storage</strong> devices, such as USB memory sticks, CDs, DVDs, and floppy disks	6.1878		2026-01-31 14:32:41.739544	2026-01-31 17:21:30.919981	0	07cf24f7476456583d373803846d43d7
1653	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024978	About Google Chat at IU	On this page:\nOverview\nHow to access\nGroup chat vs. rooms\nManage rooms and groups for your department, organization, or school\nBots in\nGoogle Chat\nLearn more\nOverview\nGoogle Chat\nfacilitates direct messaging and collaboration within\nGoogle at IU\n. Faster and more interactive than email,\nGoogle Chat\nlets anyone with a\nGoogle at IU\naccount chat with individuals and groups.\nGoogle Chat\nallows seamless collaboration across many devices and systems, including the web, desktop clients, and smartphones.\nHow to access\nThere are two ways to access\nGoogle Chat\n:\nBrowser:\nNavigate directly to\nchat.google.com\nin your preferred web browser (for example, Firefox, Chrome).\nChat app:\nGoogle has developed a standalone app which provides a fast, reliable, and engaging way to use\nGoogle Chat\non any computer with the Google Chrome web browser. For more about the app, including installation instructions, see\nInstall the Chat standalone app\n.\nNote:\nTo use the standalone app, you must have the Google Chrome web browser installed on your computer.\nGroup chat vs. rooms\nGoogle Chat\noffers group chats and rooms for large-scale discussions. Group chats and rooms have different features and capabilities; to determine which chat option is best for your needs, see\nRooms vs. group messages\n.\nManage rooms and groups for your department, organization, or school\nBy default, you can add people to a room one at a time. However, if you would like to add users in bulk, you can\ncreate a Google Group\nand then invite said group to your room. To invite a group to an existing room:\nIn the\nFind people, rooms, bots\nsearch bar on the left of the page or chat window, enter the name of the desired room, and then select it.\nThe name of your room should now appear at the top of the chat window or page. To the right of the name, select the down arrow.\nSelect\nAdd people & bots\n.\nIn the "Enter name or email of person or group" search bar, enter the name of your group.\nSelect the name of your group (for example,\nTest Group\n).\nSelect\nSend\n.\nImportant:\nMake sure to invite the entire\nGoogle Group\nrather than the email address associated with your group. Groups display the number of members under their names (for example, "3 people"). If you select a group's email address (for example,\ntestgroup@iu.edu\n), you will not invite the entire group.\nOnce invited, individual members of the\nGoogle Group\ncan\njoin your room\n. Members will be notified in\nGoogle Chat\nthat they have been invited to your room. If you'd like, you can also send an email notification when initially inviting the group.\nNote:\nAlthough you can invite\nGoogle Groups\nto rooms, you cannot invite\nGoogle Groups\nto group chats.\nBots in\nGoogle Chat\nBots are a unique way for users to connect to services integrated with\nGoogle Chat\n. You can talk to bots and command them to perform various tasks. Bots do not interact with\nGoogle Chat\ndirectly, and they adhere to all permissions and configurations maintained in the service delivered by the bot.\nThe following tools are approved for use in\nGoogle Chat\nat IU:\nGoogle Drive\nbot in\nGoogle Chat\nGoogle Chat\nfor Confluence\nJira for\nGoogle Chat\nMeet bot for\nGoogle Chat\nZoom\nTo propose other applications to be approved for use in\nGoogle Chat\nat IU, contact the UITS\nSupport Center\n.\nLearn more\nTo learn more about\nGoogle Chat\n, see:\nWhat can you do with\nGoogle Chat\n?\nManage chat for an organization\nGoogle Chat\ncheat sheet\nTalk to people outside of your domain in\nGoogle Chat				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:33:38.978175	2026-01-31 14:45:25.529307	1	KB0024978
11884	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9e3f3c6747a2fa10d500b87b716d438d	Make content in Canvas available to the public	Note:\nGiven the default storage space for\nCanvas\nat IU, you may want to consider using another IU service for storing files and making them publicly available; for example, consider\nPages\nor\nSitehost\n.\nCurrently, Canvas does not provide a way to make most content, such as files and folders, publicly viewable (that is, available on the web). However, you can make an entire course available to the public, and use it to share content; see\nHow do I customize visibility options for a course or course content?\nIf you decide to use Canvas for this purpose, UITS recommends that you create a course separate from your regular instructional courses; see\nCreate a new course site in Canvas\n.\nFor this workaround to function properly, your course must be published; for instructions, see\nHow do I publish a course?\nTo prevent access to content other than files in your publicly visible course, hide all remaining tools (with the exception of\nHome\n, which cannot be hidden); for instructions, see\nHow do I manage Course Navigation links?\nAdditionally, if you want to hide your files but still permit access to them via link, you can either hide the\nFiles\ntool altogether, or selectively adjust lock setting on individual files; for instructions, see\nHow do I restrict files and folders?\nNote:\nA locked folder still displays the folder's name and the names of any subfolders it contains; only the files within the folder are hidden.\nTo learn how to perform common tasks in Canvas, consult the\nCanvas Guides\n.\nFor help from a UITS Support Center consultant, fill out the "Ask for Help" form found in the\nCanvas\nHelp\nmenu. You can also contact your campus\nSupport Center\ndirectly.\nInstructors can get help using technology in their teaching at the\nteaching and learning centers\non each campus.	KB0023028	KB0023028 v2.0	Note: Given the default <strong>storage</strong> space for Canvas at IU, you may want to consider using another IU service for storing files and making them publicly available; for example, consider Pages or Sitehost remaining tools (with the exception of <strong>Home</strong>, which cannot be hidden); for instructions, see How	5.258		2026-01-31 17:22:03.938154	2026-01-31 17:22:03.938154	0	9e3f3c6747a2fa10d500b87b716d438d
11889	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6270ce763312eed004e605570e5c7b76	About Enterprise Web Technical Services	Important:\nSiteKube is replacing Sitehost. For more, see\nAbout SiteKube\n.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nWebTech services\nLinux hosting\nSitehost\nPages\nWindows Hosting Environment (WHE)\nWeb content management\nWCMS\nIU Blogs\nPartnership with UITS Web team\nOverview\nAt Indiana University, the UITS Enterprise Web Technical Services (WebTech) team provides web hosting, content management, and other professional services.\nWebTech services\nLinux hosting\nSitehost\n:\nSitehost\nis the university's central web server solution for core and gateway websites and thousands of other websites related to official IU business. The\nSitehost\nservice is provided at no cost. For more, see\nAbout Sitehost\n.\nPages\n:\nThe\nPages\nservices lets IU faculty, staff, and students create individual web pages to allow colleagues, conference organizers, potential research partners, or potential employers to find you and learn more about your work. You might also use the home page account to post files for a class or provide information for a project. The\nPages\nservice is provided at no cost. For more, see\nAbout pages.iu.edu\n.\nWindows Hosting Environment (WHE)\nThe Windows Hosting Environment (WHE) works with multiple UITS teams to provide services for a wide variety of web applications and other information system needs. Services include a Microsoft IIS web server environment with the ability to support Adobe ColdFusion, Microsoft .NET Framework, and .NET Core 3.1 programming languages. WHE services are free up to 10 GB of storage at which point extra resources are billed. For more, see\nAbout the Windows Hosting Environment\n.\nWeb content management\nWeb Content Management System (WCMS):\nThe Web Content Management System (WCMS) is one application publishing many sites. The WCMS allows developers and designers to build and produce modern, feature-rich websites. Site managers can structure environments where content authors can create, maintain, and publish web content to their web hosting service. The WCMS is available at no cost. For more, see\nAbout IU's WCMS\n.\nIU Blogs:\nIU Blogs provides an easy-to-use platform for faculty, staff, and students who want a blogging presence at IU. The platform is a multi-site WordPress environment hosted by an external vendor (CampusPress) and supported by WebTech. The IU Blogs service is available at no cost. For more, see\nAbout IU Blogs (Edublogs)\n.\nPartnership with the UITS Web team\nWebTech partners with the UITS Web team to provide support for WebTech services, such as\nSitehost\n,\nPages\n, and the WCMS. For more, see\nAbout the UITS Web team\n.	KB0024017	KB0024017 v5.0	 to find you and learn more about your work. You might also use the <strong>home</strong> page account to post files are free up to 10 GB of <strong>storage</strong> at which point extra resources are billed. For more, see About	4.8281		2026-01-31 17:22:10.704876	2026-01-31 17:22:10.704876	0	6270ce763312eed004e605570e5c7b76
11900	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9928a9b8474abed02fc6a55b416d4386	Web conferencing tools and online proctoring at IU	When conducting assessments in courses taught online at Indiana University, it is important to only use services that have been vetted for proctoring at IU. While\nUITS\nunderstands that faculty may view\nZoom\n, Microsoft Teams, or other web conferencing tools as services that could be used to facilitate proctoring in their courses, these products have\nnot\nbeen approved and should not be used for this purpose.  Honorlock is the only tool approved for online proctoring at IU.\nIU recommends against using general purpose web conferencing software to monitor or proctor exams, or requiring students to be present via a web conferencing tool during exams for the following reasons.\nGeneral considerations:\nLimited access to the internet:\nSome students have low quality internet, and others are in households with multiple users who are either schooling or working from home; even those with reasonably good internet might face connectivity issues.\nLimited access to devices:\nNot every student is in a household with multiple devices. A household might have multiple devices, but not enough for the number of people who need simultaneous access. Additionally, some devices may not meet the standards required for use with online tools like Zoom and Teams, or even approved proctoring software like Honorlock.\nLimited access to other technologies:\nNot every household has access to a printer, scanner, or webcam. Webcams are an integral part of online proctoring for recording an exam. Additionally, students may not be able to print paper materials if they are required for an exam, or scan and upload final exam documents or scrap work after an exam.\nTime zone:\nMany IU students are outside the US or spread out across the country and may not be available for a specific testing window.\nIssues specific to web conferencing software:\nFairness:\nLimitations on an instructor's ability to monitor all students equally may exist. Remote proctoring requires specific training, and instructors have usually not been trained to serve as effective remote proctors.\nLimited monitoring:\nWhile web conferencing tools allow instructors to view test takers' webcams, they cannot show the screens of all test takers at once.  Software developed specifically for online proctoring, like Honorlock, allows proctors to see all activity on individual students' devices during the testing session, and allows instructors to view any student afterward using the recording.\nPrivacy:\nMany students are concerned about privacy. Web conferencing tools allow classmates to see their faces and testing environment during the exam.\nData storage:\nIU does not have an agreement in place for how testing session data is transmitted and stored in Zoom and Teams that meets the expectation of an enterprise proctoring service.\nIU continues to recommend\nalternative assessments\nto avoid the use of traditional proctored exams, which is often not the best form of assessment in online courses. However, proctored exams may be unavoidable in some cases. To ensure integrity, the services and technologies that have been approved for online assessment at IU include:\nHonorlock\n:\nHonorlock has been approved for use with online proctoring at IU, and integrates with Canvas and ExamSoft. For more, see\nAbout online proctoring at IU\n.\nCanvas Quizzes\nand\nNew Quizzes\n:\nEither Canvas quizzing tool can be used for both low-stakes assessments (for example, quizzes, practice quizzes, or surveys) and high-stakes assessments (tests or exams).\nQuick Check\n:\nQuick Check is a tool that makes it easy to create low-stakes quizzes that appear at the bottom of Canvas content.	KB0025445	KB0025445 v3.0	 are in households with multiple users who are either schooling or working from <strong>home</strong>; even those with reasonably. Data <strong>storage</strong>: IU does not have an agreement in place for how testing session data is transmitted	4.4803		2026-01-31 17:22:18.081685	2026-01-31 17:22:18.081685	0	9928a9b8474abed02fc6a55b416d4386
11906	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=87bc4e9847b2f6d0d500b87b716d431b	Configure delivery pipelines	Table of contents\nOverview\nPull images from a private repository\nCreate an image pull secret\nSpecify an image pull secret in a manifest\nAssign an image pull secret to a service account\nUse a deployer service account\nSpring Boot application deployments\nHow to use a Harbor robot account in Bamboo using a CLI\nOverview\nFollowing are instructions for automating deployments using a private registry, Kubernetes service accounts, and CD environments, such as Bamboo.\nPull images from a private repository\nIf you are deploying containers in Kubernetes from a private repository, you will need to configure your Kubernetes deployment with the credentials to access your private repository.\nAn image pull secret will allow a Kubernetes deployment to pull private images to deploy your containers. The following steps will help you configure an image pull secret for your Kubernetes deployment. You may also refer to the official\nKubernetes documentation\nfor further details.\nNote:\nThis guide uses the commonly used\nDeployment\nmanifest as an example but should apply for other manifests that support a\nPod\nspecification, such as\nStatefulSets\nand\nDaemonSets\n.\nCreate an image pull secret\nTo create an image pull secret in your namespace, run the following command:\nkubectl create secret docker-registry\nregcred\n\\\n-\n-docker-server=\nyour-registry-server\n\\\n# e.g: registry.docker.iu.edu\n-\n-docker-username=\nyour-username\n\\\n# e.g: groupacc (a group account with pull privilege only)\n-\n-docker-password=\nyour-password\n\\\n-\n-namespace=\nteam-namespace\n# e.g: ua-vpit--enterprise-systems--mydept\nThe command above creates an image pull secret with the name\nregcred\nthat you can later refer to from your\nDeployment\nmanifest. (You may choose any easily identifiable image pull secret name to replace\nregcred\nin this example.)\nSpecify an image pull secret in a manifest\nSpecify the name of the image pull secret (\nregcred\n) that created above in the\nDeployment\nmanifest, as shown in the following example:\napiVersion:\napps/v1\nkind:\nDeployment\nmetadata:\nname:\nnginx-deployment\nlabels:\napp:\nnginx\nspec:\nreplicas:\n3\nselector:\nmatchLabels:\napp:\nnginx\ntemplate:\nmetadata:\nlabels:\napp:\nnginx\nspec:\nimagePullSecrets:\n- name:\nregcred\n# Specify your Image Pull Secret name here\ncontainers:\n- name:\nnginx\nimage:\nregistry.docker.iu.edu/mydept/nginx:latest\nports:\n- containerPort:\n80\nAssign an image pull secret to a service account\nAlternatively, you can assign the image pull secret you created to the service account that runs your deployment, rather than specifying it in your\nDeployment\nmanifest. This could simplify your manifest if you have a lot of applications.\nFirst, determine the service account that runs your deployment. By default, Kubernetes has already created a service account with the name\ndefault\nto run all your deployments in your namespace. If you configure your\nDeployment\nto be run by another service account, use that service account instead of\ndefault\nin the following example:\nkubectl patch serviceaccount\ndefault\n\\\n-p '{\n"imagePullSecrets"\n: [{\n"name"\n:\n"\nregcred\n"\n}]}'\n# Image Pull Secret name you create earlier\nUse a deployer service account\nIf you requested a\ndeployer\nservice account, you should have received a Kubernetes configuration file. This file contains all of the details needed to connect to a specific cluster and perform actions as the\ndeployer\nservice account in your namespace. Generally, this file is placed in your home directory at\n~/.kube/config\n(for example,\nbind mounted\ninto the container used to perform deployments in your CD environment). The following\ndocker run\ncommand shows how to mount a\nmy.config\nfile on the host machine into a user's\n~/.kube\ndirectory within the container. Other CD tools, such as Bamboo, should have a volumes section to specify directories and files to mount.\ndocker\nrun\n-v $(\npwd\n)/my.config:/home/foobar/.kube/config\n$IMAGE\n$COMMAND\nOnce that's in place,\nkubectl\nwill use this file to authenticate with the cluster. You may also specify a config file using the\n--kubeconfig\nflag on\nkubectl\n; for example:\nkubectl --kubeconfig /path/to/my.\nconfig\nget\npods\nThe\ndeployer\naccount has a role binding on the\nrestricted-control\ncluster role. See\nRole-based access control (RBAC) in Kubernetes at IU\nfor all of the resources available. If any resources or verbs are missing that you require for your deployments, send a request to\ndocker@iu.edu\n.\nA Docker image is currently being developed to facilitate deployments. The current stable version is\nregistry.docker.iu.edu/library/kube-deployer:stable\nand contains a supported version of\nkubectl\n. To use this image, mount your config file at\n/root/.kube/config\n. It also contains a Helm 3 binary (\nhelm\n) if using Helm to perform deployments.\nSpring Boot application deployments\nFor teams deploying Spring Boot applications, a\nHelm chart\nis available to standardize deployments.\nWorkflow\nis an example project using this chart. Two values files are required:\nhelm-common.yaml\n, which contains common values for all of your application environments, and an environment specific values file, for example\nhelm-snd.yaml\n.\nIf using Bamboo to deploy this Spring Boot application, create a job that runs in a Docker container. Set the image to\nregistry.docker.iu.edu/library/helm-k8s-boot-deployer:stable\nand add a couple of tasks. The first task should check out your source code. The second should be a Script task; for this task set the following:\nInterpreter:\nShell\nScript location:\nFile\nScript file:\n/deploy.sh\nArgument:\nappcode envcode namespace dockerSecret\n(replace\nappcode\nwith your application code,\nenvcode\nwith your environment code,\nnamespace\nwith your Kubernetes namespace,\ndockerSecret\nwith registry credentials formatted as\nusername:password\n; for example,\nwrkflw snd ua-vpit--enterprise-systems--esimw foobar:mypassword\n).\nYou will also need to ensure some volumes are present. Use the default volumes for both Host and Container directories:\n${bamboo.working.directory}\nand\n${bamboo.tmp.directory}\n. Create a third volume that mounts the\ndeployer\nconfig file at\n/root/.kube/config\n.\nThe\nDockerfile\nfor the kube-deployer image is available in\nIU GitHub\n. The\ndeploy.sh\nscript baked into the image performs a\nhelm upgrade\non your project.\nNote:\nThere is a known issue when running tasks in a Docker container. The image is not pulled on every run, so if the\nstable\ntag is updated, you may still be running on an older image. One workaround is to add a job before your deployment that pulls the docker image on a particular agent. Your deployment job should also run on the same agent.\nUse a Harbor robot account in Bamboo using a CLI\nThis section assumes you've followed\nthese instructions for creating a robot account\n.\nBelow is an example showing how to sign in, build, and push an image to Harbor using Bamboo using the Agent environment (replace\nrobot$foobar\nwith your robot account name).\nCreate a plan and add the repository that contains your\nDockerfile\n.\nCreate a variable named\nregistryPassword\nto hold the password for\nrobot$foobar\n.\nCreate a stage and add a job with the following tasks:\nA Source Code Checkout task for the repository containing your\nDockerfile\nA Script task with the following Script body:\ndocker login --username=\n'robot$deployer'\n\\\n--password=\n"${bamboo.registryPassword}"\nregistry\n.docker\n.iu\n.edu\ndocker build --rm --pull \\\n-t registry\n.docker\n.iu\n.edu\n/your-project/your-image:some-tag .\ndocker push registry\n.docker\n.iu\n.edu\n/your-project/your-image:some-tag\nDue to escaping issues related to the dollar sign, if you choose to use a variable for the username (for example,\nregistryUsername\n), you will need to set it in the environment variables field like this,\nREGISTRY_USERNAME\n=\n"${bamboo.registryUsername}"\nThen, use it in the script body:\ndocker login --username=\n"${REGISTRY_USERNAME}"\n--password=\n"${bamboo.registryPassword}"\nregistry\n.docker\n.iu\n.edu\nIf you choose to build your images in a Docker container rather than the Bamboo agent environment, you will need to choose an image that has the\ndocker\ncommand line tool installed (for example,\ndocker:19.03.8\n).\nYou will also need to mount in the Docker socket that's available on the Bamboo agents. Under the Docker tab of a job, set the following volume where both the Host directory and the Container directory are\n/var/run/docker.sock\n. From that point forward, you can follow the steps above for the agent environment.	KB0027150	KB0027150 v5.0	 service account in your namespace. Generally, this file is placed in your <strong>home</strong> directory at ~/.kube section to specify directories and files to mount. docker run -v $(pwd)/my.config:/<strong>home</strong>/foobar/.kube	4.3178	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:22:21.474597	2026-01-31 17:22:21.474597	0	87bc4e9847b2f6d0d500b87b716d431b
11350	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b73f215b47f45a5c3d373803846d430d	Install Windows at IU	Table of contents\nIf Windows is not installed on your computer\nIf Windows is already installed on your computer\nIf Windows is not installed on your computer\nSteps to take for other operating systems\nOS currently installed on your computer\nWhat you want to do\nAction\nmacOS\nInstall Windows from IU in addition to the existing Mac OS\nInstall Boot Camp\nLinux\nInstall Windows from IU\nAdditional purchase required\nNone (blank hard drive)\nInstall Windows from IU\nAdditional purchase required\nIf Windows is already installed on your computer\nWarning:\nInstalling Windows via any of these methods, with the exception of refreshing, will permanently\ndelete all\ndata and desktop programs (including\nMicrosoft Office\nand all others) from your computer. Before proceeding, back up/copy all data files (such as documents, pictures, music, and videos) to a flash drive, external hard drive, or cloud storage. Additionally, you should locate the installation media for your software and your subscription information for third-party software.\nWhy reinstall Windows\nImportant:\nMicrosoft\nended support for\nWindows 7\non January 14, 2020. If you're using\nWindows 7\n, UITS strongly recommends that you upgrade to a new operating system.\nReinstall Windows if you have any of these issues:\nVirus or malware infection:\nIf your computer has been infected with a virus, malware or other type of malicious infection, the only way to fully clean it is to back up your files and reinstall Windows. Reinstalling Windows is not the same as the Windows System Restore utility, which does not help in the case of a virus or malware infection. If your IU network access has been blocked by the\nUniversity Information Security Office\n(UISO) due to a virus infection, you are required to reinstall.\nSlow to the point of being unusable:\nOver time (especially after a few years), any operating system can become sluggish due to changes that have occurred (such as installing/uninstalling programs). If your computer has become slow to the point of being unusable, reinstalling Windows may be the best and quickest solution. Extreme slowness may also be a symptom of infection.\nGeneral stability issues:\nIf you're having general stability issues such as "blue screens", unusual error messages, or other strange issues, or you've exhausted all other troubleshooting methods, it may be best to reinstall Windows.\nFor help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nRecycling, selling, or giving your PC away:\nIf your personal device will no longer be in your possession, you should reinstall Windows to remove your personal information. This does not guarantee that your personal files would not be recoverable, however. Thus, if you have sensitive data on your personal computer, UITS recommends that you\nsecurely wipe\nthe disk drive. Be aware that this removes the Windows operating system and license from the device. For departmental computers, see\nDepartmental disposal of equipment at IU\n.\nNetwork access blocked due to rootkit:\nIf your network access has been blocked due to a virus infection called a rootkit, nothing mentioned here will fix the problem; UISO will email you with specific instructions for getting rid of the infection.\nHow to install, reinstall, or perform an in-place upgrade of Windows on a PC\nPrepare for the installation process:\nFor security reasons, you should install Windows or rebuild your Windows computer offline (unplug your network cable), and then take certain measures to assure its security before putting it back on the network; see\nSafely rebuild your Windows computer\n.\nCheck your computer manufacturer's support site and manual (see\nAdditional Resources\n) for the following (for the most reliable information, enter the computer's service tag or serial number where available):\nDetermine the optimal method(s) that your manufacturer recommends for installing Windows (since Windows is increasingly tied to the specific hardware of your computer due to security enhancements, including secure boot).\nMake sure that upgrading Windows will not result in a voided warranty, loss of service and support, or lack of drivers (software that tells Windows how to manage the hardware components, such as the sound card, video card, touchpad, and others).\nMake sure that your computer is compatible with the version of Windows you wish to install; see\nmethods below\nfor a guide to the various options. This is especially critical if you're doing a clean install and upgrade, such as\nWindows 8\nto\nWindows 10\n.\nIf you haven't already done so, back up/copy all data files (documents, pictures, music, videos) to a flash drive, external hard drive, or cloud storage.\nBe aware of the settings you'll need to select during setup or immediately afterward:\nBe sure of the language you wish to choose, and your time zone.\nIU Northwest is on (GMT-06:00) Central Time (US & Canada). All other IU campuses are on (GMT-05:00) Eastern Time (US & Canada), not the Indiana East setting.\nDuring the installation process, you will be prompted to enter information such as a name for your computer and an account name and password. This is for the computer's\nadministrator\naccount, and for security purposes should not be the account you will generally use (you can set up that account later). Keep this in mind as you choose a username and password you'll be able to remember.\nConsider these descriptions to determine the best method for installing, reinstalling, or upgrading Windows, based on your needs and your manufacturer's recommendations:\nIn-place upgrade\nImportant notes:\nUITS does\nnot\nrecommend this method, since in-place installations of operating systems tend to cause problems, especially if the new version is very different from the previous one; see\nin-place upgrade\n. For example, if you're doing a major version upgrade from\nWindows 8\nto\nWindows 10\n, then UITS generally recommends a clean installation instead.\nIf your operating system is corrupted or infected, an in-place upgrade will most likely retain this infection/corruption. In those cases, UITS strongly recommends installing via a Reset/Reset with the Remove everything option, Factory Recovery, or Clean installation.\nApplies to:\nWindows 7\nand higher\nWhat happens:\nInstalls a newer version of an operating system without removing the older version first (\nWindows 8\nto\nWindows 10\n,\nWindows 10\nHome to\nWindows 10\nEducation, etc.); see\nin-place upgrade\n.\nGenerally, reinstalls Windows while preserving your programs, apps, files, and settings; however, only your personal files may be retained, depending on the version of Windows with which you begin and end (for example,\nWindows 7\nto\nWindows 8.1\n; see\nWindows 8\nand\nWindows 8.1\nUpgrade Paths\n).\nAdditional notes:\nWhile this option should preserve your files, UITS recommends backing up your files to be safe.\nCurrently installed programs may not be compatible with the upgraded operating system, and may not function at all. Be sure to check to see if you'll need a newer version of the program, and if one is available.\nIf you perform an in-place upgrade and have multiple, ongoing issues, then you will likely need to back up your files and perform a clean installation instead.\nRefresh/Reset this PC with the Keep my files option\nWarning:\nIf you're reinstalling due to an infection, UITS recommends installing via a Reset/Reset, Factory Recovery, or Clean installation instead, as this option may not remove the infection or all its effects.\nApplies to:\nWindows 8.1\nand higher\n(In\nWindows 8.x\n, this process is named "Refresh your PC", while in\nWindows 10\n, it is "Reset your PC with the Keep my files option".)\nWhat happens:\nReinstalls Windows while preserving your files and personalization settings\nRetains apps that came with your PC and that were installed from the Windows Store\nRestores PC settings back to default\nRemoves installed desktop programs (such as Office)\nAdditional notes:\nWhile this option should preserve your files, UITS recommends backing up your files to be safe.\nA list of removed apps that you will need to reinstall is saved to your desktop.\nIncludes manufacturer-supplied drivers.\nIf refreshing the computer does not resolve your issues, try resetting it instead.\nIf you upgraded your PC from\nWindows 8\nto\nWindows 8.1\nand your PC has a\nWindows 8\nrecovery partition, refreshing your PC will restore\nWindows 8\n. You'll need to upgrade to\nWindows 8.1\nvia the Store app after the refresh has finished.\nRefresh/Reset with the Remove everything option:\nApplies to:\nWindows 8.1\nand higher\n(In\nWindows 8.x\n, this process is named "Reset your PC". In\nWindows 10\n, this is "Reset your PC with the Remove everything option".)\nWhat happens:\nReinstalls Windows back to factory settings via a hidden recovery partition.\nUses Microsoft Windows to perform the install.\nDeletes files, settings, and applications you've installed (both Windows Store apps and desktop programs).\nRetains apps and desktop programs that came with the device.\nAdditional notes:\nIncludes manufacturer-supplied drivers.\nIf you upgraded your PC from\nWindows 8\nto\nWindows 8.1\nand your PC has a\nWindows 8\nrecovery partition, resetting your PC will restore\nWindows 8\n. You'll need to upgrade to\nWindows 8.1\nvia the Store app after the reset has finished.\nFactory Recovery:\nApplies to:\nWindows 7\nand higher\nWhat happens:\nReinstalls Windows back to factory settings via a hidden recovery partition.\nUses the computer manufacturer's software to perform the install.\nDeletes files, settings, and applications you've installed (both Windows Store apps and desktop programs).\nRetains apps and desktop programs that came with the device.\nAdditional notes:\nIncludes manufacturer-supplied drivers\nClean Install\nApplies to:\nWindows 7\nand higher\nWhat happens:\nInstalls Windows from installation media, such as a USB flash drive or DVD.\nDeletes all files, settings, applications, and desktop programs (for example, Office).\nAdditional notes:\nWarning about potential loss of drivers:\nA clean installation of Windows includes Microsoft-supplied drivers, which may not be as up-to-date as those supplied by the manufacturer. As a result, some devices may not be detected, and you will need to get drivers from the manufacturer's site. If you are upgrading to a newer version of Windows (for example, from\nWindows 7\nto\nWindows 8.1\n), your computer manufacturer may not supply drivers for the new version of Windows, especially if your computer is several years old. Keep in mind that determining the correct drivers for your computer can be a tedious process of trial and error, even for the technically savvy. If you are reinstalling due to an infection, doing so by resetting or via a factory recovery process is both quicker and easier.\nBy the terms of the IU/Microsoft Campus Agreement, each license key for Windows obtained through IU is capable of installing the software on only one computer. If you need a second license, contact your campus\nSupport Center\nfor help.\nAlso by the terms of the IU/Microsoft Campus Agreement, each license key for Windows obtained through IU can only be installed on a computer with an existing copy of Windows or via Boot Camp on a Mac.\nUse this option to perform a major version upgrade (such as\nWindows 7\nto\nWindows 8.x\nor\nWindows 10\n, or\nWindows 8.x\nto\nWindows 10\n) if you don't want to risk issues that can occur from an in-place upgrade.\nThis option is required if either of the following are applicable:\nYou are moving from a 32-bit to 64-bit version of Windows.\nYou're upgrading from\nWindows 7\nor earlier to\nWindows 8.x\nor higher.\nPlug your computer into a power source to proceed with installation.\nInstall Windows according to the method you selected above, following the appropriate instructions below:\nIn-place upgrade:\nWindows 7\nor\nWindows 8.x\nto\nWindows 10\n: Download the\nAcademic Media Creation Tool\n, and follow the instructions at\nUsing the tool to upgrade this PC to\nWindows 10\n, being sure to run the Academic Media Creation tool, and not the tool on the instructions page.\nWindows 7\nto\nWindows 8\nor\nWindows 8.1\n: Contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nRefresh/Reset This PC with the Keep My Files option\nWindows 11\n: Follow the "Reset this PC" instructions in\nRecovery options in\nWindows 11\n, choosing the "Keep my files" option.\nWindows 10\n: Follow the "Reset this PC" instructions in\nRecovery options in\nWindows 10\n, choosing the "Keep my files" option.\nWindows 8.1\n: Follow the "Refresh your PC without affecting your files" instructions in\nHow to refresh, reset, or restore your PC\n.\nRefresh/Reset with the Remove everything option\nWindows 11\n: Follow the "Reset this PC" instructions in\nRecovery options in\nWindows 11\n, choosing the "Remove everything" option.\nWindows 10\n: Follow the "Reset this PC" instructions in\nRecovery options in\nWindows 10\n, choosing the "Remove everything" option.\nWindows 8.1\n: Follow the "Remove everything and reinstall Windows" instructions in\nHow to refresh, reset, or restore your PC\n.\nFactory Recovery\nWindows 7\nand higher: Check your computer's user guide (see\nAdditional resources\n) for the factory recovery instructions.\nClean Install:\nNote:\nBe sure you don't format all partitions, but only the Windows partition.\nReview\nSafely rebuild your Windows computer\nObtain the appropriate Windows installation media:\nWindows 10\nEducation: See\nCreating a bootable installation flash drive for Windows 10 Education Edition\n.\nWindows 8.1\nPro: No longer available through IUware or disc via the bookstore. If you wish to install this version, seek alternate media.\nInstall Windows according to the instructions for the version you're installing:\nWindows 10\nEducation:\nInstall Windows 11 Education or Windows 10 Education Edition at IU\nWindows 8.1\nPro:\nHow to Clean Install\nWindows 8\nor\nWindows 8.1\n(PC Support)\nInstall Windows updates and additional software.\nFor security reasons and to improve performance, follow the steps in\nWhat you should do immediately after installing Windows\n.\nAdditional resources\nCommon manufacturer's support sites:\nAcer\nAsus\nCompaq (HP Customer Support)\nDell\neMachines\nFujitsu\nGateway\nHP\nLenovo\nSamsung\nSony\nToshiba\nFor additional instructions including screenshots, search the web for the version of Windows and type of install you're doing (for example, clean install), or search\nYouTube\nfor video walk-throughs.\nFor help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0023033	KB0023033 v2.0	 (such as documents, pictures, music, and videos) to a flash drive, external hard drive, or cloud <strong>storage</strong> files (documents, pictures, music, videos) to a flash drive, external hard drive, or cloud <strong>storage</strong> operating system without removing the older version first (Windows 8 to Windows 10, Windows 10 <strong>Home</strong>	3.6703		2026-01-31 17:07:19.167941	2026-01-31 17:22:24.861508	0	b73f215b47f45a5c3d373803846d430d
11918	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d1dbc2dc47feb6d0d500b87b716d43ae	Use Helm charts to deploy and manage Kubernetes applications	Table of contents\nOverview\nInstall Helm\nSet environment variables\nUse charts\nAdd a chart repository\nChart values\nChart versions and upgrades\nMaintain your own chart\nChart linting\nGitHub status checks\nHelm releases\nUse the kube-deployer image\nUse Harbor as a Helm repository\nRoll back a deployment\nHelm resources\nOverview\nHelm\nis a command line tool that can help you deploy and manage your Kubernetes applications. A Helm chart contains a set of\nGo templates\nused to produce all of the Kubernetes manifests, such as Deployments, Services, Ingresses, and ConfigMaps, required to run applications. Helm renders these templates and can deploy, upgrade, and rollback your applications. Maintaining a Helm chart provides a consistent method of deploying applications that can be shared across teams. The following information will help you get started with Helm by using existing charts (or maintaining your own), as well as publishing and using charts in IU's Harbor registry.\nInstall Helm\nTo get started, you first need to\ninstall Helm\n. UITS suggests using the latest release of version 3. You also may use a pre-built Docker image containing the\nhelm\nbinary. UITS maintains\nregistry.docker.iu.edu/library/kube-deployer\n, which is used for deploying applications to various IU Kubernetes clusters (for more, see\nConfigure delivery pipelines\n). For information on using the\nkube-deployer\nimage on your local machine, see the\nUse the kube-deployer image\nsection below.\nSet environment variables\nThere are several environment variables you can set to make working with Helm easier. For example,\nHELM_NAMESPACE\nlets you run\nhelm\nwithout passing your Helm release namespace every time you perform an action:\nexport HELM_NAMESPACE="ua-vpit--enterprise-systems--esimw--helm-release"\nFor a complete list of environment variables, see Helm's\nEnvironment Variables\npage. For more about the Helm release namespace, see the\nHelm releases\nsection below.\nUse charts\nYou can create charts using the\nhelm create\ncommand or by using a curated chart. For example, the\nlibrary/k8s-boot\nchart is recommended for deploying Spring Boot applications on IU's enterprise Kubernetes environment.\nArtifact Hub\n(previously Helm Hub) is another repository with a variety of Helm charts.\nFor more on creating a chart, see Helm's\nGetting Started\npage.\nFor common Helm commands, see\nUsing Helm charts with the Enterprise Docker Registry (Harbor)\n.\nAdd a chart repository\nUITS maintains a\ncollection of public Docker images and Helm charts\nin the production Harbor registry. Refer to the\nUse Harbor as a Helm repository\nsection below for information on how to use OCI charts with Harbor.\nFor more, see the official\nHelm Repo\ndocumentation or run\nhelm repo --help\n.\nChart values\nEach chart contains a\nvalues.yaml\nfile with some default values. You may override these values by creating a values file of your own and passing it to\nhelm\nusing the\n--values\nflag.\nThe values are replaced in the Go templates. To see the rendered templates, use the\nhelm template\ncommand, or perform a dry run on an install or upgrade. Below are some sample commands for rendering the\nk8s-boot\ntemplates:\n# Render library/k8s-boot templates using your custom values from `my-values.yaml`\nhelm template my-release oci://registry.docker.iu.edu/library/k8s-boot --values my-values.yaml\n# Perform a dry run install (replace install with upgrade if you\n# are upgrading to a new chart version)\nhelm install my-release oci://registry.docker.iu.edu/library/k8s-boot --values my-values.yaml --dry-run\nFor more, see the official\nValues Files\ndocumentation.\nChart versions and upgrades\nTo use a specific version of a chart, set the\n--version\nflag. For example, the\nlibrary/k8s-boot\nlatest version is 0.10.1, so the following could be used to upgrade an application deployment (note that this is the chart version, not your application version):\n# Upgrade a release to chart version 0.10.1\nhelm upgrade my-release oci://registry.docker.iu.edu/library/k8s-boot --values my-values.yaml --version 0.10.1\nIf this is the first time you're deploying the application, use\nhelm install\n. You also may add the\n--install\nflag to\nhelm upgrade\n, which will install if no release exists.\nFor more, see the official\nHelm Install\nand\nHelm Upgrade\npages.\nMaintain your own chart\nChart linting\nHelm provides a command for linting chart templates. To lint a chart, run the following:\nhelm lint\nCheck out\nhelm lint --help\nfor all the available flags.\nGitHub status checks\nGitHub provides an API for setting statuses on branches. By setting a status (for example, based on the outcome of\nhelm lint\n), you can block a PR from being merged. There is a set of scripts in\nthis repository\nto help facilitate GitHub status checks. There are also corresponding\nDockerfiles\nfor building an image that includes\nhelm\nand the status check scripts. To create a plan in Bamboo for automatically linting and setting a status on your branch in Github, do the following:\nCreate a branch in your project that won't change (the contents are not important; for this example, it will be\nbamboo\n):\ncd <chart repo>\ngit checkout --orphan bamboo\ngit rm -rf .                # leave the files not tracked by git\necho "bamboo" > index.html  # some content is required to commit and push\ngit add index.html\ngit ci -m "empty commit"\ngit push origin bamboo\nCreate a plan in Bamboo and open\nDefault plan configuration\n.\nAdd your repository under "Repositories" and set "Branch" to\nbamboo\n.\nAdd a trigger under "Triggers" (for example,\nRepository polling\n.\nUnder "Branches" > "Create plan branch", you may choose to watch feature branches; for example, select\nWhen new branch in repository is created and matches expression\nand set regular expression to\nfeature/.+\n.\nCreate a variable named\ngithubTokenSecret\nand set the value to your GitHub API token.\nCreate a new stage and add a Helm Lint job.\nOn the\nDocker\ntab, set "Run this job in" to\nDocker container\n.\nSet "Docker image" to\nregistry.docker.iu.edu/esimw/kube-github-status-check:stable\n. (You may also use a specific\nreleased version\ntag, such as\n1.1.1\n).\nOn the\nTasks\ntab, create the following tasks in this order:\nCreate a Source Code Checkout of your repository.\nCreate a Script task named\nSet pending status on commit\n.\nSet "Script file" to\n/usr/local/bin/mark-commit-pending\n.\nSet "Argument" to\n--context="\nHelm Linting\n"\n(or any description you like for the context flag).\nSet "Environment variables" to\nGITHUB_API_TOKEN="${bamboo.githubTokenSecret}"\n.\nCreate a Script task named\nHelm Lint\n. Set the inline Script Body to\nhelm lint -f values.yaml --strict\n.\nCreate a Script task named\nSet success status on commit\n.\nSet "Script file" to\n/usr/local/bin/mark-commit-successful\n.\nSet "Argument" to\n--context="\nHelm Linting\n"\n(or any description you like for the context flag).\nSet "Environment variables" to\nGITHUB_API_TOKEN="${bamboo.githubTokenSecret}"\n.\nCreate a Script task named\nSet failed status on commit if build failed\n.\nSet "Script file" to\n/usr/local/bin/mark-commit-failed\n.\nSet "Argument" to\n--context="\nHelm Linting\n"\n(or any description you like for the context flag).\nSet "Environment variables" to\nGITHUB_API_TOKEN="${bamboo.githubTokenSecret}"\n.\nSave this task and drag it into the "Final tasks" section.\nHelm releases\nHelm stores its release information in a Kubernetes secret. By default, the last 10 revisions are kept, but you can override this by passing the\n--history-max\nflag to\nhelm install\nand\nhelm upgrade\n. For this reason, application manifests and Helm releases use different namespaces at IU. The convention at IU is to append\n--helm-release\nto your team's namespace; for example, if your team's namespace is\nua-vpit--enterprise-systems--esimw\n, your Helm release namespace would be\nua-vpit--enterprise-systems--esimw--helm-release\n. This allows Helm to manage its secrets while keeping secrets in the team's namespace secure.\nYou will need to pass this namespace on every\nhelm\ncommand that interacts with a Kubernetes cluster. For example:\nhelm install my-release stable/chart -n ua-vpit--enterprise-systems--esimw--helm-release\nhelm list -n ua-vpit--enterprise-systems--esimw--helm-release\nUse the kube-deployer image\nThe\nConfigure delivery pipelines\npage contains information on deploying applications using an environment like Bamboo, but you may also use it locally if you don't have Helm installed and want to execute Helm commands in a Docker container.\nTo check the version of\nhelm\ninstalled, use:\ndocker run --rm registry.docker.iu.edu/library/kube-deployer:1.0.0 bash -c '\nhelm version\n'\nHere is an example of rendering the default\nbitnami/node\ntemplates to STDOUT using the default values and latest version of the chart:\ndocker run --rm \\\nregistry.docker.iu.edu/library/kube-deployer:1.0.0 bash -c '\nhelm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami\nhelm repo update\nhelm template my-release bitnami/node\n'\nIf you are developing a custom chart not found in a Helm repository, you can mount the source into the container. This result can be achieved in one of two ways:\nCreate a new Dockerfile FROM kube-deployer:\nBy default,\nkube-deployer\n's deploy script will look inside the\n/helm\ndirectory for a chart to use when deploying. Therefore, you can build your own image and copy your packaged Helm chart into the\n/helm\ndirectory:\nFROM registry.docker.iu.edu/library/kube-deployer:\n<most recent version of kube-deployer>\n# We want to ADD this file instead of COPYing so it automatically extracts it\nADD my-chart-*.tgz /\nRUN mv /my-chart /helm\nFrom your chart's working directory:\nhelm package .\ndocker build .\nMount the chart into a container:\nThis command will render your custom chart templates to STDOUT. It assumes your chart is checked out on your local machine at\n/path/to/your/chart\nand your application source is at\n/path/to/your/project\n. It also assumes your custom values file is in that project directory and named\nyour-project-values.yaml\n. Your chart is mounted at\n/chart\ninside the container and your project at\n/project\n. This command also sets your container working directory to your project directory\n/project\n. Replace these values as necessary.\ndocker run --rm \\\n-v\n/path/to/your/chart\n:/chart \\\n-v\n/path/to/your/project\n:/project \\\n-w /project\nregistry.docker.iu.edu/library/kube-deployer:1.0.0 bash -c '\nhelm template my-release /chart --values\nyour-project-values.yaml\n'\nTo interact with a Kubernetes cluster, you need to mount your Kubernetes configuration file; for example, the following command will mount your Kubernetes configuration file and list your Helm releases (replace\nyour-helm-release-namespace\nwith your Kubernetes namespace; see\nConnect to IU's Enterprise Kubernetes Environment\nfor more information if you don't have a config file.\ndocker run --rm \\\n-v $HOME/.kube/config:/root/.kube/config \\\nregistry.docker.iu.edu/library/kube-deployer:stable bash -c '\nhelm list -n your-helm-release-namespace\n'\nUse Harbor as a Helm repository\nHarbor\nprovides OCI-compliant storage for your Helm charts. Below are instructions for packaging and uploading your charts to Harbor using the OCI format.\nUpdate\nChart.yaml\nand set the\nversion\nproperty. Below is an example\nChart.yaml\napiVersion: v2\nname: my-chart\ndescription: A sample chart\ntype: application\nversion: 1.0.0\nPackage your chart.\ncd\n/path/to/your/my-chart\nhelm package .\nThis produces a tarball named\nmy-chart-1.0.0.tgz\n.\nAuthenticate with Harbor using the\nregistry login\ncommand (see\nGet credentials for your CI process and CLI usage\n).\necho "\n<password>\n" | helm registry login registry.docker.iu.edu -u\n<username>\n--password-stdin\nPush your packaged chart to Harbor using the OCI format. Replace\n<project>\nwith your Harbor project name (for example,\nesimw\n).\nhelm push my-chart-1.0.0.tgz oci://registry.docker.iu.edu/\n<project>\nPull charts from Harbor using the OCI format.\n# Pull a specific version of a chart\nhelm pull oci://registry.docker.iu.edu/<project>/my-chart --version 1.0.0\nDeploy using OCI charts. Below are sample commands showing how to work with your OCI charts in Harbor:\n# Install a release directly from the OCI registry\nhelm install my-release oci://registry.docker.iu.edu/<project>/my-chart --version 1.0.0\n# Upgrade a release using OCI format\nhelm upgrade my-release oci://registry.docker.iu.edu/<project>/my-chart --version 1.0.0\n# Render Helm templates from OCI registry\nhelm template my-release oci://registry.docker.iu.edu/<project>/my-chart --version 1.0.0\n# Show chart information from OCI registry\nhelm show chart oci://registry.docker.iu.edu/<project>/my-chart --version 1.0.0\nNote:\nHarbor no longer uses Chart Museum. Charts are now stored using the OCI format as described above.\nRoll back a deployment\nTo roll back a release, use the\nhelm rollback\ncommand. Make sure you have\nexported the\nHELM_NAMESPACE\nvariable\n.\nTo see the history of releases, use\nhelm history\n:\nhelm history <app>-<env>\nExample output:\nREVISION\tUPDATED                 \tSTATUS    \tCHART          \tAPP VERSION\tDESCRIPTION\n116     \tThu Apr 20 19:00:19 2023\tsuperseded\tk8s-boot-3.11.0\t           \tUpgrade complete\n117     \tWed Apr 26 20:01:14 2023\tsuperseded\tk8s-boot-3.11.0\t           \tUpgrade complete\n118     \tThu Apr 27 15:29:50 2023\tdeployed  \tk8s-boot-3.11.0\t           \tUpgrade complete\nYou can now run\nhelm-rollback <app>-<env>\nto roll back the previous version (117 from 118 above) or\nhelm rollback <app>-<env> <revision>\nto roll back to a specific revision.\nExample output:\nREVISION\tUPDATED                 \tSTATUS    \tCHART          \tAPP VERSION\tDESCRIPTION\n116     \tThu Apr 20 19:00:19 2023\tsuperseded\tk8s-boot-3.11.0\t           \tUpgrade complete\n117     \tWed Apr 26 20:01:14 2023\tsuperseded\tk8s-boot-3.11.0\t           \tUpgrade complete\n118     \tThu Apr 27 15:29:50 2023\tsuperseded\tk8s-boot-3.11.0\t           \tUpgrade complete\n119     \tFri Apr 28 10:18:10 2023\tdeployed  \tk8s-boot-3.11.0\t           \tRollback to 117\nHelm resources\nHelm Docs Home\nQuickstart Guide\nCharts\nChart Best Practices Guide\nChart Template Developer's Guide\nHelm Cheat Sheet\nFrequently Asked Questions	KB0027169	KB0027169 v5.0	 run --rm \\\r\n    -v $<strong>HOME</strong>/.kube/config:/root/.kube/config \\\r\n    registry.docker.iu.edu/library/kube repository Harbor provides OCI-compliant <strong>storage</strong> for your Helm charts. Below are instructions\tdeployed  \tk8s-boot-3.11.0\t           \tRollback to 117\r\n Helm resources Helm Docs <strong>Home</strong> Quickstart	2.8672	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:22:28.7137	2026-01-31 17:22:28.7137	0	d1dbc2dc47feb6d0d500b87b716d43ae
11922	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6dbfa877476456583d373803846d438c	Determine the version numbers and installation dates of existing Perl modules on the IU ES nodes	At Indiana University, on the Enterprise System (ES) nodes, the modules that come with the default Perl installation are listed in the perlmodlib perldoc. To access it, enter the following command:\nperldoc perlmodlib\nTo find modules added after the default installation, enter:\nperldoc perllocal\nTo check for a specific module (for example,\nCGI.pm\n), enter:\nperl -MCGI -e "CGI.pm"\nIf no errors are returned, the module was found successfully. If the module was not found, to request that it be added, see\nRequest new software on the IU ES nodes\n.\nTo check the version of an installed module, enter:\nperl -MNet::SSLeay -e "print Net::SSLeay->VERSION"	KB0022949	KB0022949 v1.0	At Indiana University, on the Enterprise System (ES) nodes, the <strong>modules</strong> that come with the default:   perldoc perlmodlib To find <strong>modules</strong> added after the default installation, enter:   perldoc perllocal that it be added, see Request new <strong>software</strong> on the IU ES nodes. To check the version of an installed module	303.627	km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:22:32.014933	2026-01-31 17:22:32.014933	0	6dbfa877476456583d373803846d438c
11924	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1d13b42347f1ba50d500b87b716d43b1	Use external modules and other advanced functionality in IU REDCap	Table of contents\nOverview\nREDCap API\nAccess from Tableau\nExternal modules\nREDCap-ETL\nAPI Sync, Copy Data on Save, and Cross-Project Piping\nFlight Tracker\nOther external modules available in IU REDCap\nRequest more external modules\nGet help\nOverview\nIU REDCap\nusers can use the\nAdvanced Functionality Request\nform to request access to certain advanced functionality, third-party external modules,\nREDCap-ETL\n(\nREDCap\nExtract, Transform, Load) and/or the\nREDCap API\n. The form aims to reduce the risk of security, privacy, and performance issues by warning you of possible concerns and providing guidance on how to avoid them.\nNote:\nExternal modules are add-on packages of software that can extend REDCap's current functionality, as well as provide customizations and enhancements for REDCap's existing behavior and appearance, either at the system level or project level. While IU REDCap vets third-party external modules for security and privacy, it does not maintain them.\nUse them at your own risk.\nREDCap-ETL is not a third-party external module; it is developed and maintained by IU REDCap.\nSome third-party external modules are available within your IU REDCap project without needing any additional approval, so they are not listed in the\nAdvanced Functionality Request\nform. Other third-party external modules could potentially be made available within IU REDCap, but doing so may require a lengthy vetting process.\nImportant:\nIf the IU\nREDCap\nproject data you are accessing contain protected health information (\nPHI\n), you must treat the data as PHI, which means files must be encrypted when they are stored (at rest) and when they are transferred from one system to another (in transit). For more, see:\nRecommended tools for encrypting data containing HIPAA-regulated PHI\nYour legal responsibilities for protecting data containing protected health information (PHI) when using UITS Research Technologies systems and services\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.\nREDCap API\nYou can use the\nAdvanced Functionality Request\nform to request access to the REDCap API if you are planning to use Tableau, create a custom API software client, or use an external module that requires the API (such as REDCap-ETL or API Sync).\nThe REDCap API (Application Programming Interface) is used to programmatically import/export data. See\nGet started with the REDCap API\n.\nAccess from Tableau\nYou can use the\nAdvanced Functionality Request\nform to request access to IU REDCap from Tableau. The retrieval of data from IU REDCap into Tableau also requires REDCap API access. See\nUse your IU REDCap project as a data source in Tableau\n.\nExternal modules\nYou can use the\nAdvanced Functionality Request\nform to request that the following external modules be enabled in your REDCap project(s).\nREDCap-ETL\nREDCap-ETL automates the transformation and export of data into relational form for use in relational databases, such as MySQL, SQL Server, and PostgreSQL. A REDCap API token is required. For more, see\nUse REDCap-ETL to transform and move data from IU REDCap to another service\n.\nAPI Sync, Copy Data on Save, and Cross-Project Piping\nAPI Sync:\nThe API Sync external module transfers data between projects. Data transfers can be scheduled or manual. A REDCap API token is required. API Sync automates exporting/importing to/from REDCap servers via the API. The Data Dictionaries for the local and remote projects are expected to be either identical or compatible.\nCopy Data on Save:\nThe Copy Data on Save external module transfers data to other REDCap projects. Data transfers are triggered by new data. Copy Data on Save copies data from one place to another when you save a form. You can set up multiple copy processes to different projects or to other fields in the same project.\nCross-Project Piping:\nThe Cross-Project Piping external module pulls data from other projects. Data pulls are performed manually or triggered by saves. Cross-Project Piping "pipes" from one field in one project into another field in another project during data entry (not surveys). The projects have to be matched on a third field in both projects. Enable this module in the destination project, and select appropriate fields in the configure panel.\nFlight Tracker\nThe Flight Tracker external module tracks scholarly career development. Flight Tracker tracks a scholarly group's career development by charting publications, grants, and patents over time. Training grants and other scholar communities can benefit from graphical, analytical, and reporting tools, including automatically produced drafts of NIH training tables. Only US data are supported.\nOther external modules available in IU REDCap\nThe following third-party external modules are available within your IU REDCap project without any additional approval, so they are not listed in the\nAdvanced Functionality Request\nform. These external modules can be enabled by selecting\nManage\nin the "External Modules" section on the left side of the REDCap project page and then selecting\nEnable a Module\n.\nAuto-Record Generation:\nThis module allows for a new record to be generated in another project (or the same project) on a flagging field being saved, and allows for data fields to be transferred to the new record, as well. Events and repeating instruments are not currently supported.\nDate Calculated Fields:\nThis module allows for "piping" (automatically copying) from date fields to other date fields, regardless of format. It can pipe data across events within a REDCap project.\nGeomarker:\nGeomarker generates a Google map with marker and information windows using geolocation data from specified fields in a REDCap project.\nHIDESUBMIT Action Tags:\nThis module allows users to conditionally hide various Submit/Save/Next instance buttons on surveys and/or data entry forms using action tags and branching logic.\nImage Map:\nThis module replaces an input, radio, or checkbox field with an interactive image where users can click on image regions to select one or more field options. For complete documentation and an example instrument, see\nhttps://github.com/ctsit/imagemap\n.\nInstance Table:\nUse the action tag\n@INSTANCETABLE=form_name\nin a descriptive text field to include a table showing data from repeat instances of that form. If your project is longitudinal, use\n@INSTANCETABLE=event_name:form_name\nto specify the event and form (a repeating form or a form in a repeating event). Other action tags are available for controlling certain properties of the tables and their contents.\nMatrix Question Randomization:\nThis module randomizes the order of the questions for specified matrices on survey pages and highlights rows in red that were missed on the first form submission.\nNo Copy/Paste:\nThis module prevents copying from or pasting into fields.\nRename File Upload:\nTag file fields with the action tag\n@RENAME-UPLOAD='name_pattern'\nto specify how files uploaded to the field should be renamed; piping of fields and smart variables into the file name is supported. Examples:\n@RENAME-UPLOAD='MyCustomFilenamePattern_rec-[record-name]'\n@RENAME-UPLOAD='MyCustomFilenamePattern_[project-id]-[record-name]-[filename_part]'\n@RENAME-UPLOAD='MyCustomFilenamePattern_[record-name]-[survey-time-completed]'\nDo not include any file extension within the pattern. Renamed files will keep the extension with which they were uploaded. For suggestions on characters to avoid in filenames, see\nProblematic characters\n.\nRequest more external modules\nOther third-party external modules may potentially be made available within IU REDCap, but doing so may require a lengthy vetting process. To learn what is available, see the\nREDCapRepo\n. To request vetting of an external module, email\nredcap@iu.edu\nspecifying the module of interest and how it will help meet your needs.\nGet help\nIf you have questions about the\nAdvanced Functionality Request\nform or external modules in IU REDCap, email IU REDCap Support at\nredcap@iu.edu\n.	KB0026544	KB0026544 v9.0	 guidance on how to avoid them. Note: External <strong>modules</strong> are add-on packages of <strong>software</strong> that can extendTable of contents Overview REDCap API Access from Tableau External <strong>modules</strong> REDCap-ETL API Sync, Copy Data on Save, and Cross-Project Piping Flight Tracker Other external <strong>modules</strong> available in IU	161.4799		2026-01-31 17:22:37.004322	2026-01-31 17:22:37.004322	0	1d13b42347f1ba50d500b87b716d43b1
2162	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024124	Use REDCap-ETL to transform and move data from IU REDCap to another service	Table of contents\nOverview\nRun\nREDCap\n-ETL\nwithin IU\nREDCap\nRun\nREDCap\n-ETL\nas standalone software\nGet started\nOverview\nREDCap\n-ETL\n(\nREDCap\nExtract, Transform, Load) extracts data from\nREDCap\n, transforms it into a relational format, and loads that data to a relational database or, if run as standalone software, to an SQLite file or a series of CSV files.\nREDCap\n-ETL\ncan be run within\nIU\nREDCap\nor as standalone software.\nImportant:\nIf the IU\nREDCap\nproject data you are accessing contain protected health information (\nPHI\n), you must treat the data as PHI, which means files must be encrypted when they are stored (at rest) and when they are transferred from one system to another (in transit). For more, see:\nRecommended tools for encrypting data containing HIPAA-regulated PHI\nYour legal responsibilities for protecting data containing protected health information (PHI) when using UITS Research Technologies systems and services\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.\nRun\nREDCap\n-ETL\nwithin IU\nREDCap\nA\nREDCap\n-ETL\ninterface is available within IU\nREDCap\nas an external module. The\nREDCap\n-ETL\nexternal module provides a graphical interface for configuring\nREDCap\n-ETL\n, enables\nREDCap\n-ETL\nto be scheduled, and can generate at the press of a button the transformation rules needed by\nREDCap\n-ETL\nto define how to transform\nREDCap\ndata into a relational format.\nThe\nREDCap\n-ETL\nexternal module allows data to be loaded into relational databases such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server, and to be downloaded interactively as a set of CSV files. When downloading CSV files, the\nREDCap\n-ETL\nexternal module cannot be scheduled.\nFor more about the\nREDCap\n-ETL\nexternal module, see\nREDCap\n-ETL\nExternal Module\n.\nRun\nREDCap\n-ETL\nas standalone software\nREDCap\n-ETL\ncan be run as standalone software. Doing so requires a server to host\nREDCap\n-ETL\n. The\nREDCap\n-ETL\nconfiguration, including the transformation rules that define how to transform\nREDCap\ndata into a relational format, must be manually created. Automating\nREDCap\n-ETL\nas standalone software requires using your server's scheduling functionality.\nThe standalone version of\nREDCap\n-ETL\nallows data to be loaded into relational databases and to files in SQLite or CSV format.\nFor more about running\nREDCap\n-ETL\nas a standalone application outside of\nREDCap\n, see\nREDCap\n-ETL\n.\nGet started\nFill out and submit the\nIU\nREDCap\n- Advanced Functionality Request\nform.\nWhen your pre-approval request is accepted, go to the IU\nREDCap\nproject for which you are requesting the API token; in the left column, select\nAPI\n, and then select\nRequest API token\n.	KB0024124	KB0024124 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Run REDCap-ETL within IU REDCap Run REDCap-ETL as standalone <strong>software</strong> <strong>software</strong>, to an SQLite file or a series of CSV files. REDCap-ETL can be run within IU REDCap or as standalone <strong>software</strong>. Important: Run REDCap-ETL within IU REDCap A REDCap-ETL interface is available	66.3526		2026-01-31 14:35:07.091651	2026-01-31 17:22:40.903902	0	c3ef247b476456583d373803846d43ef
11975	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=7dffa0bb476456583d373803846d4323	What to do before you contact the UITS Support Center for help	Table of contents\nCheck the Knowledge Base first\nContact the Support Center\nGet help for new computer hardware or software\nCheck the Knowledge Base first\nUITS\nSupport Center consultants initiate a great deal of Knowledge Base content in response to questions people in the IU community ask. Searching this Knowledge Base for help with your problem before calling or emailing the Support Center may give you a speedier answer (enter your search terms in the box at the top of this page).\nContact the Support Center\nIf the Knowledge Base does not address your problem or if you need further assistance, contact your\ncampus Support Center\n. Provide as much of the following information as possible. If you call, have the information available, be at your computer, and have the computer turned on.\nWhat operating system are you using (for example,\nWindows 11\n, macOS 10.12)?\nIf the problem has to do with software, what version of that software are you using (for example, Microsoft Outlook 2016)?\nIt often helps to provide some context, for example:\nWhat you were trying to do\nWhether you've ever been able to do this, and if so, if anything about your system has changed since then\nIf you've done anything to try to fix the problem\nHow long the problem has been occurring\nIf you received an error message, note its exact text. Some error messages have a\nDetails\nbutton. Click the\nDetails\nbutton, if available, to show a more complete error similar to this:\n"\nprogramname.exe\ncaused an invalid page fault / general protection fault in module XXXXX at xxxx:xxxxxxxx"\nThis is followed by a list of numbers called the register dump. Copy the complete error message for the Support Center, but ignore the long register dump.\nGet help for new computer hardware or software\nIf your computer is less than a year old and you have problems with its DVD drive, wireless card, network card, monitor, or any other hardware or software that came with the computer, contact the computer vendor. A technical support phone number should be available in the computer's user manual. For new computers, free technical support and warranty coverage are usually available for a limited time (usually one year). If you were sold a computer with faulty components, contact the vendor right away.\nIf you have problems with the software you purchased for your computer, contact the technical support number for that software package before contacting the Support Center. Software companies often provide either 30 to 90 days worth of free support, or free help for the first one or two questions.	KB0024362	KB0024362 v1.0	 computer hardware or <strong>software</strong> Check the Knowledge Base first UITS Support Center consultants initiate to do with <strong>software</strong>, what version of that <strong>software</strong> are you using (for example, Microsoft Outlook 2016 the long register dump. Get help for new computer hardware or <strong>software</strong> If your computer is less than a year	4.9462		2026-01-31 17:23:13.534748	2026-01-31 17:23:13.534748	0	7dffa0bb476456583d373803846d4323
11901	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5f3152e747b7ded0d500b87b716d43f7	Honorlock for IU students	Table of contents\nOverview\nCost of Honorlock\nTechnology requirements\nPrepare for a proctored assessment\nBefore your first assessment with Honorlock\nBefore every assessment with Honorlock\nLaunch and take an assessment with Honorlock\nCanvas Quizzes\nThird-party assessments\nLearn more\nGet help\nOverview\nHonorlock is an online proctoring service that allows online and distance learners to take exams without needing to schedule an on-campus visit or proctor. Honorlock uses a combination of AI and live human proctors to monitor your exam. The AI scans for unusual activity, such as unexpected movement or someone entering the room, and flags potential concerns. If a flag is triggered, a live proctor will check the exam session. They may briefly pause your exam and open a chat with you, offering assistance in returning to your exam. Once the exam is complete, your instructor has access to the recordings and any flags, and can review them.\nFor more about Honorlock's privacy policies, see:\nStudent Privacy Statement\nHonorlock Privacy Policy\nCost of Honorlock\nThe costs for using Honorlock are covered by IU Online or the school or department offering the course.  You should never be charged for the use of Honorlock in your Indiana University courses.\nTechnology requirements\nIn order to take an assessment that uses Honorlock, you will need:\nA computer running Windows 10 or 11, macOS 10.14 or higher, or ChromeOS (Chromebooks)\nThe Google Chrome browser version 120 or higher with the\nHonorlock Chrome Extension\nenabled\nA built-in or external webcam and microphone\nAn internet connection rated at 1.5 Mbps download/750 Kbps upload. To test your internet speed, visit\nhttp://www.speedtest.net\n.\nDual monitors are not permitted. If the software detects more than one monitor, see\nMultiple Monitors Detected\n.\nNotes:\nFor the most up-to-date requirements information, see\nMinimum System Requirements\n.\nCurrently, Honorlock is incompatible with:\nTablets and mobile devices\nWalmart-branded cameras (Onn/Surf Onn)\nAmcrest cameras\nNexigo cameras\nPrepare for a proctored assessment\nBefore your first assessment with Honorlock\nInstall\nthe Honorlock extension for the Chrome browser\n. This will save setup time before the exam. For step-by-step instructions, see\nInstall the Honorlock Extension\n.\nWatch the following videos to see what to expect before the exam starts and during the the exam:\nHonorlock Best Practices\nProctoring Overview\nStudent Guide: How to Use Honorlock\nYour experience with Honorlock may be slightly different than what’s shown in the videos because your instructor may have chosen different proctoring settings.\nBefore every assessment with Honorlock\nBefore you start an assessment with Honorlock, take the following steps to ensure you, your equipment, and your test-taking environment meet the requirements of the assessment and the Honorlock software.\nMake sure your test-taking equipment meets the\nHonorlock minimum system requirements\nand the Honorlock extension has been added to Chrome.\nTry to use the bathroom before you start the assessment.  You may not be permitted to leave the room after it starts.\nFind a well-lit room with a door to take the test so there are no interruptions during the assessment. Ideally, the room should be equipped with a desk (or table) and chair so you can sit upright while taking the exam.\nPlace your official student ID on the table (or desk) so you can locate it easily when prompted to take a photo of it.\nIf your instructor provides the assessment guidelines beforehand, remove all disallowed equipment and materials from the room.  Otherwise, review the test-taker guidelines when you start the assessment and make adjustments to bring your environment into compliance.\nMake sure your face is visible on the webcam throughout the exam.\nLaunch and take an assessment with Honorlock\nImportant:\nWhen you launch a proctored assessment, the Honorlock software will walk you through a series of pre-assessment checks before the assessment begins. Read the on-screen prompts carefully and follow the instructions.\nYou may be asked to perform an ID verification. Honorlock will prompt you to take a picture of an official ID (such as your CrimsonCard, driver's license, state ID, etc). During this process, the name on your ID will be compared to your name in Canvas (which uses your\npreferred name\nin the Student Center). They don't need to match exactly, but if there are significant differences, you'll be asked to try again. If you're unable to pass the ID verification after three tries, select the\nContact Support\nbutton at the bottom of the "ID Verification" screen.\nHonorlock will show you the test-taker guidelines selected by your instructor, which will tell you the behaviors and resources that are allowed and disallowed. Read them carefully and make sure you remain compliant throughout the exam.\nIf you encounter any problems during the assessment, select the chat icon at the lower right to contact Honorlock support. If you are unable to start the in-app chat, open a separate browser tab, go to\nhttps://honorlock.com/support\n, and select\nBegin Live Chat\n.\nYou should never need to enter the password for an assessment. When the exam password is requested, Honorlock will enter it for you.\nThe timer for the assessment will not begin until after the password has been entered and the first question in the assessment is displayed.\nCanvas Quizzes\nHonorlock is fully integrated with Canvas and Canvas Quizzes. If your instructor provided specific instructions on where to find and take your quiz, follow those instructions. Otherwise, for quizzes created in Canvas, you can navigate to the quiz wherever it is located in your course (Quizzes, Assignments, Modules, etc.) and select the title of the quiz to get started. All of your proctored quizzes will also be listed in the Honorlock tool (located in the course navigation). You can launch them from there as well.\nFor step-by-step instructions, see\nHow to Use Honorlock (Test Takers\n). If your experience doesn't match the Honorlock guide exactly, don't worry. Depending on the proctoring settings selected by your instructor, some of the steps may not apply to you.\nThird-party assessments\nHonorlock can also be used to proctor assessments created outside of Canvas, such as in a publisher courseware site. To start a third-party assessment:\nOpen the Honorlock tool and locate the assessment on the main Honorlock screen. Even if there's a link to the assessment elsewhere in your course, you must launch the assessment from within the Honorlock tool.\nTo start the assessment, choose\nLaunch\n, and follow the on-screen instructions.\nAfter completing the pre-assessment checks, Honorlock will prompt you to open the assessment in a new tab. It may not always be possible to deliver you directly to the assessment. If you are taken to the main page of the third-party tool, you'll need to navigate to the assessment on your own.\nOnce you reach the password prompt for the assessment, in the Honorlock window at the lower left, select\nI have found my assessment\n. Then select\nUnlock assessment\n. Honorlock will enter the password for you so you can begin the exam.\nFor more in-depth instructions, see\nHow to Use Honorlock with a Universal Exam (Test Takers)\n. If your experience doesn't match the Honorlock guide exactly, don't worry. Depending on the proctoring settings selected by your instructor, some of the steps may not apply to you.\nLearn more\nConsult the following Honorlock resources for students:\nTest Taker Resources\n(all Honorlock Guides for Students)\nLaunching Honorlock from Your Exam\nCompleting a Room Scan Using Honorlock\nHonorlock FAQs for Test Takers\nHonorlock Toolbar Overview for Test Takers\nHonorlock Best Practices for Test Takers\nStudent Privacy Statement\n<\nStudent Privacy FAQ\nGet help\nFor live assistance from a proctor, use the in-app chat tool, or go to\nhttps://honorlock.com/support\nand select\nBegin Live Chat\n.\nFor less urgent assistance, send an email message to\nsupport@honorlock.com\n.	KB0026531	KB0026531 v5.0	 <strong>software</strong>. Make sure your test-taking equipment meets the Honorlock minimum system requirements a proctored assessment, the Honorlock <strong>software</strong> will walk you through a series of pre-assessment it is located in your course (Quizzes, Assignments, <strong>Modules</strong>, etc.) and select the title of the quiz	4.9344		2026-01-31 17:22:18.081685	2026-01-31 17:23:16.865635	0	5f3152e747b7ded0d500b87b716d43f7
11935	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=cbcf24f7476456583d373803846d4388	Purchase IDRISI software at IU	IDRISI\nis an integrated\nGIS\nand image processing system that includes nearly 300 modules for the analysis and display of digital spatial information.\nThe\nDepartment of Geography\nmanages the Indiana University site license with Clark Labs for IDRISI and Cartalinx software. At IU Bloomington, IDRISI is available in all\nStudent Technology Centers\n.\nStudents:\nMay purchase IDRISI directly from Clark Labs at reduced prices. The Student Starter license is fully functional for a year after the purchase, and includes documentation and tutorial exercises. When\nordering student software\n, you will need to provide proof of student status, such as your current semester's schedule of classes.\nFaculty and staff:\nMay purchase IDRISI for $200 annually from the\nGeography Department\nstaff. The license year for IDRISI software extends from July 1 to June 30 of each year. If you have an existing license and would like to continue using the software, you will need to renew in July at a rate of $200 per license.\nIf you have questions about IDRISI software,\ncontact the Geography Department\n.	KB0023438	KB0023438 v1.0	IDRISI is an integrated GIS and image processing system that includes nearly 300 <strong>modules</strong> University site license with Clark Labs for IDRISI and Cartalinx <strong>software</strong>. At IU Bloomington, IDRISI, and includes documentation and tutorial exercises. When ordering student <strong>software</strong>, you will need	47.519		2026-01-31 17:22:44.8124	2026-01-31 17:22:44.8124	0	cbcf24f7476456583d373803846d4388
10336	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=66294f3947e0f698d500b87b716d4385	Improve and troubleshoot your Zoom experience	Table of contents\nOverview\nImprove your experience\nReview technical requirements\nCheck your network\nAdjust your computer's settings\nAdjust Zoom settings\nTroubleshoot\nI can't join my meeting\nI've joined my meeting, but no one else is here\nI can't hear or talk to people\nI want to turn off Zoom's automatic audio adjustments\nPeople can't see me\nMy video is poor quality\nI can't screenshare a video with sound\nI can't access my recordings, I accidentally deleted a recording, or I can't record\nMy recording won't convert\nError adding alternative hosts\nMy problem isn't listed here\nOverview\nImportant:\nDue to federal law enforcement concerns regarding foreign government interference and surveillance risks associated with Zoom, it is strongly recommended that individuals involved in research activities discontinue using Zoom. Instead, all virtual meetings should be conducted through\nMicrosoft Teams\n, which meets government requirements for secure and private communications.\nIU Research has chosen to prohibit the use of Zoom by its employees. Other IU divisions may enact similar prohibitions.\nTo learn more about using Microsoft Teams for virtual meetings, see\nUITS IT Training Microsoft resources\n.\nFor questions or further guidance,\ncontact your local UITS Support Center\n.\nIf Zoom appears to distort audio or video, or does not perform in a manner you expect, use the information here to improve your experience, or troubleshoot issues.\nImprove your experience\nReview technical requirements\nReview\nZoom's system requirements\nand ensure your system meets these recommendations.\nCheck your network\nCheck your network speeds using one of the free services available online. Zoom's system requirements recommend a minimum of 1.5 Mbps for both upload and download speed. If necessary, check with your internet service provider.\nPower cycle your modem/router, if possible.\nIf you're on a wireless connection:\nConfirm that you have a strong signal.\nConnect to a 5 GHz network (rather than 2.4 GHz), if possible.\nLimit the number of devices connected to your wireless router.\nConsider switching to a wired connection.\nEnsure that other devices on your network aren't consuming bandwidth; these could include cloud-connected security cameras, cloud backup solutions, smart TVs, streaming devices, smart hubs, gaming devices, or other cloud or internet-connected devices. You can turn them off, lower the bandwidth they consume by adjusting performance settings, or make other changes that may improve the amount of bandwidth available for Zoom.\nIf you're using a network-wide VPN, consider disabling it.\nImportant:\nDo not disable any necessary devices that help protect or otherwise secure your network.\nIf you can't improve or resolve your connection, check with your internet service provider.\nAdjust your computer's settings\nRestart your Zoom client.\nRestart your computer.\nUpdate system drivers, especially network drivers, if necessary.\nClose out of any unneeded programs, especially those that use the internet. This could include office applications, additional browser tabs, or anything that uses system or network resources. Closing out of these items will free more system resources for Zoom to use.\nStop all downloads, uploads, or other unnecessary processes that are using network resources.\nEnsure that your devices are up to date.\nEnsure that your application(s) are up to date, especially the Zoom client.\nDisconnect from any VPNs you may be using.\nAdjust Zoom settings\nDisable HD Video from your Zoom client:\nWhile not in a call, select the\nHome\ntab.\nSelect the gear icon (\nSettings\n).\nIn the left Navigation pane, select\nVideo\n.\nUncheck\nEnable HD\n.\nSelect\nX\nin the top right corner to close the Settings window.\nDisable "Touch up my appearance" from your Zoom client:\nWhile not in a call, select the\nHome\ntab.\nSelect the gear icon (\nSettings\n).\nIn the left Navigation pane, select\nVideo\n.\nDeselect\nTouch up my appearance\n.\nSelect the\nX\nin the top right corner to close the Settings window.\nMute your audio when not speaking.\nConsider disabling your webcam if it's not necessary.\nTroubleshoot\nI can't join my meeting\nIf you're unable to join a meeting, check these items:\nIf you're on a desktop or mobile device, be sure you've\ninstalled the Zoom client\nand that it's up to date; see\nViewing the Zoom version number\nIf you've received any of these messages:\nYou are on hold:\nThe host has enabled a waiting room; you'll remain on hold until the host admits you into the meeting room.\nRoom connector is not enabled for this meeting:\nIf you are using videoconferencing equipment to join a meeting hosted by a free Zoom account or an account that doesn't have the room connector enabled, you will receive this error. Connect to the meeting using a desktop or mobile device instead.\nPlease wait for the host to start this meeting:\nThe\nJoin before host\nfeature is not enabled, and the host has not yet joined the meeting. Hosts automatically receive email asking them to join. You will be prompted to join the meeting a short time after the host joins.\nPlease enter the meeting password:\nThe meeting requires a passcode; enter the passcode provided by the meeting host (in the Zoom meeting invitation). If you do not know the passcode, contact the meeting host for assistance.\nNote:\nIf you are connecting via telephone, and the IU Zoom meeting passcode contains alphabetic characters, check the meeting invitation for an all-numeric passcode (which Zoom generates automatically when the host sets a passcode containing alphabetic characters).\nThis meeting does not allow phone dial-in:\nThe host may only have allowed VoIP audio. In this case, if you dial into the general Zoom teleconferencing number and enter the meeting ID, you will hear the message above and the call will disconnect. You will need to\njoin the meeting\nwith a computer or a smartphone app.\nNot meeting host. (3,003):\nIf you received a link to a Zoom meeting in the format\nhttp://iu.zoom.us/meeting/123456789\n, change\n/meeting/\nin the URL to\n/j/\nand try to connect again. For example,\nhttp://iu.zoom.us/meeting/123456789\nshould instead be\nhttp://iu.zoom.us/j/123456789\n.\nI've joined my meeting, but no one else is here\nIf you've joined a meeting but don't see other participants:\nConfirm with the meeting host that the meeting has begun. If it has, ask for the meeting ID at the top of the host's Zoom window (for example, Zoom Meeting ID: 123-456-7890) and compare that to the meeting ID at the top of yours. If they don't match, you've joined a different meeting. Ask the host for the meeting ID, and\njoin that meeting\n.\nIf you're the host, check whether the\nwaiting room is enabled\n. If it is, you may need to manually admit your participants before they can join your meeting.\nI can't hear or talk to people\nIf you're unable to hear or talk to people in a meeting:\nCheck the attendee controls at the bottom of your meeting window. If you see the Join Audio icon\non the far left, select it. (If you've already joined your audio, you'll instead see the mute/unmute icon\n.)\nTo check whether the issue is related to the Zoom program, see:\nTesting computer or device audio\nMy audio is not working on iOS or Android\nAudio settings for iOS\n(iPhone, iPad)\nIf your audio isn't working in the Zoom client\noutside of the meeting\n, try troubleshooting the device itself. For instructions, see\nTroubleshoot audio and video issues on your computer or mobile device\n.\nThe host may have allowed only telephony audio. In this case, you will not see the message "join meeting with computer audio". You may still be able to join the meeting with video or\nvia phone\n.\nI want to turn off Zoom's automatic audio adjustments\nFor musical content, applied lessons, or other situations where preserving audio quality is important, you can change Zoom's audio settings to minimize automatic adjustments such as gain, extended frequency response, and echo cancellation.\nWhile turning off automatic adjustments can preserve the natural sounds of music, singing, or spoken word, it also requires meeting participants to position their microphones and speakers properly, and to manually adjust their microphone levels. Good conferencing etiquette is also important with these settings; participants should use headphones when possible and mute their microphones when not speaking or playing.\nNote:\nNetwork connection also impacts audio quality. Consider disabling video for a better audio experience.\nThese are advanced features; the instructions below assume you have basic knowledge of Zoom and your computer's audio and video settings.\nTo turn off automatic audio adjustments:\nLog into\nZoom\n.\nSelect\nSettings\nfrom the menu at the top or left side of the page.\nUnder "In Meeting (advanced)", make sure these settings are toggled on (blue):\nAllow users to select stereo audio in their client settings\nAllow users to select original sound in their client settings\nLaunch your meeting normally.\nEach\nmeeting host and participant must then:\nFrom the Zoom client, select the gear icon (\n) in the upper right.\nSelect\nAudio\nfrom the menu on the left.\nUnder the microphone settings, uncheck\nAutomatically adjust volume\n.\nSelect\nAdvanced\nin the lower right.\nCheck\nShow in-meeting option to "Enable Original Sound" from microphone\n.\nFrom the drop-down menus under "Audio Processing", set "Suppress Persistent Background Noise" and "Suppress Intermittent Background Noise" to\nDisable\n.\nClose the settings window.\nAt the top of the meeting window, a\nTurn [on/off] Original Sound\nbutton will now be visible. Turn original sound on to transmit more natural audio to other participants. Turn it off to restore default settings.\nRemember that\neach\nparticipant will need to adjust their settings to transmit natural sound. Be prepared to advise other participants on how to adjust their audio settings and speaker and microphone placement for best results.\nPeople can't see me\nIf you've joined a meeting successfully but others can't see you:\nMake sure your video isn't turned off by checking the Start Video icon\nin the attendee controls at the bottom of your meeting window.\nIf you're connecting from a Lenovo device and your video isn't working, see\nVideo not working on Lenovo devices\n.\nIf your video isn't working in the Zoom client\noutside of the meeting\n, try troubleshooting the device itself. For instructions, see\nTroubleshoot audio and video issues on your computer or mobile device\n.\nMy video is poor quality\nThe "Optimize for full screen video clip" setting greatly reduces video quality. Restart the screen share with that setting unchecked.\nIf you are experiencing a problem with the internet or the device itself, see\nTroubleshoot audio and video issues on your computer or mobile device\n.\nI can't screenshare a video with sound\nMake sure you've muted unneeded microphones (your device and/or the Zoom meeting microphone). After you select\nShare\nto share your screen, select\nShare computer sound\nin the lower left of the screenshare window.\nI can't access my recordings, I accidentally deleted a recording, or I can't record\nIf your meeting is missing from the recorded meetings list in the Zoom client (for example, as a result of pressing\nDelete\nin the Zoom client for a recorded meeting), you may be able to find the recording on your device in its Zoom recordings folder.\nTo see where your Zoom recordings are stored, see the "Change location for Recording" section of\nLocal recording\n. Recordings will be named in the format\ndouble_click_to_convert_01.zoom\n. When you find the\n.zoom\nfile you are looking for, double-click it.\nNote:\nPressing\nDelete\nin the Zoom client removes the recording from the recorded meetings list in Zoom, but not from the device itself.\nOnly current hosts can record a meeting. If you can't record, check to see whether you're the host by selecting the Participants icon\nin the attendee controls at the bottom of your meeting window; if you aren't the host, you can ask the host to record the meeting.\nFor more about recording Zoom meetings, see\nCreate and store recordings of Zoom meetings\n.\nMy recording won't convert\nYour device might be low on disk space. To check, see:\nWindows 11\nand\nWindows 10\n:\nFind out how much storage your PC has\nWindows 8.x:\nTips to free up drive space on your PC\nmacOS:\nHow to free up storage space on your Mac\nError adding alternative hosts\nTo add an alternative host, you must enter that person's full\n@iu.edu\nemail address, even if the person's primary email address uses another domain. Zoom recognizes\n@iu.edu\nemail addresses only.\nThe desired host must have logged into Zoom at least once before you can add that person to any meeting.\nPeople from outside IU cannot be added as hosts. This includes people with free or individual accounts, or accounts from other institutions.\nMy problem isn't listed here\nFor help or to learn more,\ncontact UITS Learning Spaces Support\n.	KB0025105	KB0025105 v4.0	: If you're on a <strong>desktop</strong> or mobile device, be sure you've installed the Zoom client and that it's up to date; see, you will receive this error. Connect to the meeting using a <strong>desktop</strong> or mobile device instead. Please	3.3486		2026-01-31 16:41:43.897309	2026-01-31 16:43:32.982296	1	66294f3947e0f698d500b87b716d4385
11936	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e830f83347a456583d373803846d432a	Software and Services Selection Process (SSSP) contract renewals	When renewing an information technology (IT) software or IT service contract, use the guidance below to determine whether a Software and Services Selection Process (\nSSSP\n) form submission or other action is required. Use this guidance for items on the Conditional Allow List, as well.\nDetermine whether your contract renewal requires an SSSP form submission:\nIf the use case for the existing information technology software product or IT service contract has changed from the prior contract term, submit the SSSP Contract Renewal form from the\nSSSP Service Catalog\nand provide detailed information on any use case changes.\nExamples of use case changes include:\nChange in data classification to include new Restricted or Critical data\nChange to primary audience, especially for students\nAdditional modules or functionality; changes to implementation\nChange from locally hosted service to cloud or third-party\nIf the use case for the software or IT service contract has not changed, proceed to the next step and determine if other action is required. If there is no use case change, you are not required to submit the SSSP Contract Renewal form.\nDetermine whether your renewal requires Third Party Assessment (3PA) review by the Data Stewards:\nNote:\nIf the existing information technology software product or IT service contract involves any of the following, Data Steward review is required at\nevery renewal\n, and you must submit a\nThird Party Assessment (3PA) form\n:\nCollection of customer financial information\nCollection of revenue (PCI) from the person using the software product or IT service (this does not refer to IU payment for IT software or IT services)\nHIPAA-regulated protected health information (\nPHI\n)\nIf none of the above circumstances apply, you are not required to submit a Third Party Assessment (3PA) form.\nIf you have questions about the Third Party Assessment process, email the University Information Policy Office (UIPO) at\niudata@iu.edu\n.\nIf you have questions about the Software and Services Selection Process (SSSP), email\nssspserv@iu.edu\n.	KB0025643	KB0025643 v1.0	When renewing an information technology (IT) <strong>software</strong> or IT service contract, use the guidance below to determine whether a <strong>Software</strong> and Services Selection Process (SSSP) form submission or other information technology <strong>software</strong> product or IT service contract has changed from the prior contract term	42.4158	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:22:48.152523	2026-01-31 17:22:48.152523	0	e830f83347a456583d373803846d432a
11939	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=dadd912f47aafa10d500b87b716d43fd	Effects of third-party cookies in Canvas	Table of contents\nChanges to Chrome\nIf you suspect third-party cookies are causing an error\nTemporary fix for instructors\nMore about cookies\nHow browsers use cookies\nAbout third-party (or "cross-domain") cookies\nHow browsers handle third-party cookies\nChanges to Chrome\nIn February 2020, Google released version 80 of its Chrome web browser, which changes the way third-party cookies are handled. By default, Chrome will block third-party cookies unless the embedded web application takes necessary steps to prevent the cookies from being blocked.\nIn Canvas, you may experience difficulties launching third-party tools from the course navigation menu, course modules, and elsewhere while UITS works with application providers to patch their software to comply with Chrome 80.\nNote:\nDo not alter your web browser's settings to force your browser to accept third-party cookies, as they can pose risks to privacy and/or security. UITS recommends that you leave your browser's default cookie settings in place.\nIf you suspect third-party cookies are causing an error\nTry a different web browser:\nThis is often the quickest troubleshooting step to try. If you have another browser (or two), try opening the problematic page in a different web browser to see if it functions properly.\nVisit the embedded tool directly to establish "first party trust" with it:\nIf you know the website URL for the embedded service that you suspect is failing due to a blocked third-party cookie, try visiting that website in its own browser tab. This may establish what's called "first party trust" with the external application, allowing the embedded application to then function properly in Canvas.\nContact the Support Center:\nYour campus\nSupport Center\nmay be able to help you troubleshoot the issue and determine if third-party cookies are in fact the problem.\nReport the problem to your instructor:\nIf you experience problems loading external tools in Canvas, alert your instructor. It may be possible to provide access to the external tool/resource in a way that does not involve third-party cookies.\nTemporary fix for instructors\nIf a third-party tool is broken in your course site due to a third-party cookie issue, try this workaround:\nCreate a new module in your course site called "Canvas Third-party Tools" or another name you choose. (If you aren't sure how to use Canvas Modules, see the Modules section of the\nCanvas Guides\n).\nIn the new module, click\n+\n(Add Item) to add a new item to the module.\nFrom the\nAdd\ndrop-down menu in the "Add Item" window, select\nExternal Tool\n.\nSelect the desired tool from the external tools list. Scroll to the bottom of the window and check\nLoad in a new tab\n. Loading the tool in a new tab will circumvent the third-party cookies problem. If you wish, you can also change the display name of the tool by editing the Page Name.\nTo finish adding the tool to the module, click\nAdd Item\n.\nRepeat steps 1-5 for each problem tool.\nPublish the module and items. Your students will now be able to launch the tools from Canvas Modules.\nIf you receive an "Access Denied" message:\nMake sure you're running the most recent version of Chrome; to update your browser, see\nUpdate Google Chrome\n.\nIn Chrome, visit\nchrome://flags\n.\nAt the top of the page, in the "Show flags" box, type\nsamesite\n.\nNext to "SameSite by default cookies", make sure the drop-down is set to\nDefault\n.\nMore about cookies\nHow browsers use cookies\nWhen a website needs to keep track of something about your interaction with the site (such as settings or preferences), it will often store a small text file called a cookie in your web browser's storage. Cookies allow websites to "remember" your settings in between browsing sessions, so that when you leave the site and come back later, the site will preserve your settings. This is a useful mechanism, and an extremely common part of how websites and web browsers work.\nAbout third-party (or "cross-domain") cookies\nWebsites often embed external resources and experiences from other web materials served from another location on the web. Canvas, for example, may embed a learning tool inside a frame in the Canvas environment. These external web applications will sometimes need to store their own cookies. Since the external web application is being served from a different domain, or URL, than the one you see in the browser's URL bar, this cookie is considered to be third-party (or cross-domain).\nHow browsers handle third-party cookies\nSince third-party cookies operate in a different domain than the main website that you may be visiting, some web browsers may block these cookies in some circumstances, depending on how the external application's cookies have been structured. Depending on how the third-party application is programmed, it may not function as expected, or it may fail to function entirely (with or without an error message). In short, the way an application behaves when the web browser blocks third-party cookies depends on the application's programming, and may vary.	KB0023518	KB0023518 v2.0	 launching third-party tools from the course navigation menu, course <strong>modules</strong>, and elsewhere while UITS works with application providers to patch their <strong>software</strong> to comply with Chrome 80. Note: Do not alter your" or another name you choose. (If you aren't sure how to use Canvas <strong>Modules</strong>, see the <strong>Modules</strong> section	10.191		2026-01-31 17:22:55.105735	2026-01-31 17:22:55.105735	0	dadd912f47aafa10d500b87b716d43fd
11903	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=633b8e81470eb6142fc6a55b416d43a0	Honorlock for IU instructors	Table of contents\nOverview\nEligibility\nAssessment types in Honorlock\nAssessment settings and test-taker guidelines\nSet up proctoring in Canvas\nEnable Honorlock in your course\nStudent access to your exams\nPractice exams\nEnable proctoring for a Quiz or New Quiz in Canvas\nEnable proctoring for third-party assessments\nModify or disable the Honorlock settings for an assessment\nBypass Honorlock for test centers and accommodations\nCreate and use exam profiles\nFind and remove public copies of your test questions\nReview Honorlock results\nSample syllabus language\nLearn more\nVendor documentation\nIU documentation\nGet help\nVendor support for instructors and students\nLocal support for instructors\nOverview\nHonorlock, an online proctoring service, is available at Indiana University to help mitigate the issues surrounding the administration of tests in qualifying online and distance education courses. Fully integrated with Canvas, Honorlock can be used for proctoring exams created in Canvas Quizzes (in both Classic and New Quizzes format), as well as with third-party exams from textbook publishers and other external sources.\nHonorlock's approach to proctoring combines real-time artificial intelligence with live proctors who monitor behind the scenes. Proctors interact with students only when necessary, resulting in a less intrusive test-taking experience than other proctoring services. Moreover, students can start a proctored session at any time without scheduling the session in advance, and proctors are trained in de-escalation techniques to reduce test anxiety. Students have access to live support throughout the exam, and instructors can access a complete recording of the student and all activity on the student's computer during the proctored session. Suspicious and inappropriate activity is flagged in the reports and recordings.\nEligibility\nHonorlock is a fee-based service. IU Online covers the fee for most distance education classes. Instructors teaching courses that do not meet IU Online's requirements must obtain relevant academic and fiscal approvals before using Honorlock in their course(s). For more, see the "Costs and eligibility" section in\nAbout online proctoring at IU\n.\nAssessment types in Honorlock\nHonorlock supports several different types of exams:\nCanvas Classic Quizzes and New Quizzes:\nAll assessments created with Canvas Quizzes (both Classic and New Quizzes format) are available to instructors in Honorlock for proctoring setup.\nThird-party assessments:\nExams from textbook publishers and other external web sites can be added to Honorlock by providing the assessment URL and a few additional details.\nPractice assessments:\nHonorlock can create a three-question practice quiz in Canvas Classic Quizzes format to give your students a chance to experience the proctoring process and ensure their technology is working properly before taking a graded assessment. Practice assessments are not proctored or recorded, and no fee is charged when students take them.\nSimulated - Not Proctored:\nAssessments with simulated proctoring enabled are not proctored or recorded (nor is there a per-student fee), but for students the experience is identical to that of a proctored Honorlock exam. Simulated proctoring can be an effective deterrent to academic misconduct for low-stakes assessments. The simulated option can also be used to construct custom practice assessments.\nAssessment settings and test-taker guidelines\nHonorlock offers a variety of settings and test-taker guidelines (rules that students must follow during an exam) that can be applied to assessments. As the instructor, you decide which proctoring settings and guidelines to enable for each assessment. For a list and explanation of the available options, see the "Proctoring Settings" and "Test Taker Guidelines" sections in the second half of\nUsing Honorlock with Canvas\n.\nIf no test-taker guidelines are specified in a proctored assessment, Honorlock proctors will abide by the\nHonorlock Standard Exam Guidelines\n.\nSet up proctoring in Canvas\nEnable Honorlock in your course\nThe Honorlock tool is designed to operate from your course navigation menu, but it is disabled (hidden) by default. Once enabled, it is visible to instructors and students alike (students need access to the tool to launch third-party assessments). To enable Honorlock in your course, see\nHow do I manage Course Navigation links?\nImportant:\nIf Honorlock is not visible in the list of disabled tools on your course's Settings > Navigation page, the course may not meet IU Online's eligibility requirements. Email\nonline@iu.edu\nto confirm this and determine next steps.\nStudent access to your exams\nQuizzes created in Canvas Quizzes and New Quizzes offer flexibility in terms of where students can start the proctored assessment. Depending on how your course is designed, you can have students launch the quiz from any location in Canvas, including Quizzes, Assignments, Modules, or a link to the quiz in a Canvas Page. Students can also launch the quiz from the Honorlock tool.\nFor third-party exams from publishers or other external sources, students must launch the assessment from the Honorlock tool. Launching from within Honorlock initiates the recording and proctoring before delivering the student to the external site with the assessment. For more, see\nHow to Use Honorlock with a Universal Exam (Test Takers)\n.\nPractice exams\nUITS\nstrongly recommends that you offer your students a practice exam so they can become familiar with Honorlock before any graded work is due.\nTip:\nYou can try out the Honorlock student experience by entering the student view in Canvas and launching a practice assessment created in Canvas Quizzes or New Quizzes. This will help you understand how Honorlock impacts the test-taking process.\nCanvas Quizzes:\nIf you primarily use Classic Quizzes for assessment, the Honorlock practice assessment can create a three-question practice quiz for you. For instructions, see\nCreate a Practice Exam in Canvas\n. Don't forget to set Due or Until dates if you want students to complete the practice exam within a specific timeframe.\nNew Quizzes:\nIf you primarily use New Quizzes, you will use the Honorlock simulated option to create a practice quiz for students. Follow these steps:\nCreate a new quiz with a few sample questions. Do not set a student access code in the quiz settings. Honorlock will automatically enable this setting and create the code.\nIn the assignment settings for the quiz, check the following two boxes:\nDo not count this assignment towards the final grade\nDo not display in gradebook or the student's grade page\nFollow the instructions in\nUsing Honorlock with Canvas\nto enable Honorlock with the proctoring settings and guidelines that you plan to use in your proctored assessments. Be sure to enable the\nSimulated - Not Proctored\nsetting to ensure that there will be no fee for the exam.\nChoose\nEnable\nor\nSave\nto save your Honorlock settings.\nThird-party/external assessment:\nFollow the instructions in\nEnable proctoring for third-party assessments\nto add your practice exam to Honorlock with the proctoring settings and guidelines that you plan to use in your proctored assessments. Be sure to enable the\nSimulated - Not Proctored\nsetting to ensure that there will be no fee for the exam.\nChoose\nEnable\nor\nSave\nto save your Honorlock settings.\nEnable proctoring for a Quiz or New Quiz in Canvas\nTo enable proctoring or simulated proctoring for a Canvas quiz:\nNotes:\nYou can\nmodify or disable the Honorlock settings\nfor an assessment at any time. Your updates will take effect as soon as you save them.\nFor a brief video overview of the exam enablement process, see\nHow to Use Honorlock\n.\nCreate your quiz in Canvas.\nAdjust the quiz settings (availability, accommodations, questions and feedback visibility, etc) as you normally would for an unproctored quiz, with one exception: do\nnot\nset an access code. Honorlock will set the access code when you enable the quiz.\nIf your course uses the Modules tool, add the quiz to the desired location in Modules.\nFrom your course navigation menu, launch Honorlock. You should see a listing of all your Canvas quizzes on the main Honorlock page.\nLocate the quiz you want to enable. Note that you can sort, search, and filter the quizzes in the list to more easily locate the one you want to work with.\nFollow the instructions in\nUsing Honorlock with Canvas\nto configure the quiz for proctoring or simulated proctoring. Explanations of all the proctoring settings and test-taker guidelines are provided in the second half of the document.\nChoose\nEnable\nto save the current proctoring settings for the quiz.\nImportant:\nOnce a Canvas quiz is enabled in Honorlock, students can launch the quiz from within Honorlock or wherever it appears in your course (for example, Modules, Assignments, or Quizzes). Regardless of where students begin, the Honorlock software will start up, guide students through the pre-assessment checks, and inject the password into the access code field for assessment. If they attempt to circumvent Honorlock by disabling the Honorlock extension or using a browser other than Chrome, they will not be able to advance past the password screen for the quiz.\nEnable proctoring for third-party assessments\nTo enable Honorlock for a quiz or exam in a third-party tool, such as publisher courseware ordered through the IU eTexts initiative:\nNavigate from Canvas to the publisher courseware or other third-party site.\nCreate the assessment in the publisher courseware.  In addition to selecting or creating the questions, complete the following steps.\nSet a password for the assessment.\nDo not give this password to your students.\nIn a later step you'll enter this password into Honorlock. When each student gets the password prompt for the assessment, Honorlock software will inject the password on behalf of the student.\nBe sure to use a different password for each exam and make them difficult to guess. If students can guess the password, they can bypass Honorlock.\nAdjust the other settings for the assessment as needed and save your changes.\nReturn to your Canvas course. If your publisher courseware allows you to create a direct link to the assessment in Canvas Assignments or Modules, create the direct link in the desired location in your course.\nFrom your course navigation menu, launch the Honorlock tool.\nAt the far right of the blue Honorlock banner, select the menu icon and, from the drop-down menu, choose\nRegister Third Party Assessment\n.\nFill out the "Register a Third Party Assessment" form as follows:\nIn the "Email Notifications" section, keep or update the list of email addresses that will receive notifications from Honorlock about the assessment.\nIn the "Admin Settings" section:\nAssessment Name:\nEnter a descriptive name for the assessment. This is the name that will be visible on the Honorlock dashboard for you and your students. Ideally it should match the name as it appears in the third-party tool as well as in Canvas (if you provided a direct link as described in step 3 above).\nAssessment Start and End:\nEnter the start and end date and time for the assessment. Here again, this information should match start and end dates entered into the assessment itself as well as in Canvas.\nAssessment Platform:\nRead the instructions below carefully and select the most appropriate option:\nAccess through the LMS\n: This is the most common scenario at IU. If the assessment is in publisher courseware ordered through the IU eTexts program or in\nany external tool with a Canvas integration at IU\n, select this option from the drop-down menu.\nImportant:\nDo not choose one of the other options in the\nAssessment Platform\ndrop-down menu, even if your publisher is listed.\nUse Another Platform\n: This is an unusual scenario at IU; select this\nonly\nif the assessment is in a platform that isn't integrated with Canvas and students have a separate username and password to access it.\nPlatform Name:\nEnter the familiar name that you and your students use to refer to the courseware or external tool.\nDirect URL:\nRead the instructions below carefully:\nIf you chose\nAccess through the LMS\nfor "Assessment Platform" above\nand\nyou created a Canvas module item or assignment with a direct link the assessment in step 3 above, open a new browser tab and locate the item on the main Assignments or Modules page. Then right-click the item and choose\nCopy Link Address\n(or the equivalent command in your browser). Finally, return to the original tab with Honorlock open, and paste the URL into the "Direct URL" field. Your link will look similar to one of examples below, but the numeric course ID and item or assignment ID will be different:\nhttps://iu.instructure.com/courses/\n2309331\n/modules/items/\n34279670\n(for a module item link)\nhttps://iu.instructure.com/courses/\n2309331\n/assignments/\n16984673\n(for an assignment link)\nIf you chose\nAccess through the LMS\nfor "Assessment Platform" above\nand\nyour course has only one link to the third-party tool (usually in the course navigation), open a new browser tab and locate the link to the third-party tool. Then right-click the link and choose\nCopy Link Address\n(or the equivalent command in your browser). Finally, return to the original tab and paste the URL into the "Direct URL" field. Your link will look similar to the following example, but the numeric course ID and external tool ID will be different:\nhttps://iu.instructure.com/courses/\n2309331\n/external_tools/\n146300\nIf you chose\nUse Another Platform\nabove, enter the direct URL to the assessment, or to the login page for the platform (in which case, your students will need to navigate to the assessment once they are logged in).\nPassword:\nEnter the same password you set in the assessment itself, as instructed in step 2a above.  It must match the original password exactly.\nIn the "Proctoring Settings" and "Test Taker Guidelines" sections, enable the settings that you want to be applied to the assessment.  Enabled settings have a white check icon within a blue circle.  Note that some settings are enabled by default, but you can disable them if you choose.  For an explanation of each option, see the "Proctoring Settings" section near the bottom of\nCreate a Third Party Exam\n.\nIn the "Assessment Accommodations" section, enter the names of students requiring accommodations and the specific allowances afforded to each. Honorlock cannot overrule settings controlled by the assessment software, but you can specify exceptions to the test-taker guidelines.\nAt the bottom of the "Register a Third Party Assessment" form, choose\nEnable\nto save the details and settings for your assessment.\nNote:\nYou can\nmodify or disable the Honorlock settings\nfor an assessment at any time. Your updates will take effect as soon as you save them.\nImportant:\nWhen taking a proctored third-party assessment, students must first open the Honorlock tool and launch the assessment from within the Honorlock software. After the pre-assessment checks, Honorlock will direct them to open the assessment in a new tab using the direct URL you provided in the Honorlock settings. Then, when they reach the screen with the password for the assessment, Honorlock will enter the password. Be sure to let your students know that Honorlock will be their starting point for the assessment. If they try to access the assessment from another link in your course, they will not be able to advance past the password screen.\nModify or disable the Honorlock settings for an assessment\nYou can modify or disable the Honorlock settings for an assessment at any time. Your updates will take effect as soon as you save them.\nTo edit the Honorlock settings for an assessment:\nOpen the Honorlock app and locate the quiz you want to modify.\nSelect\nSettings\n.\nTo modify the settings:\nMake the desired changes and choose\nSave\n.\nTo disable proctoring:\nSelect the red\nDisable\nbutton.\nBypass Honorlock for test centers and accommodations\nIf you have students who need to take their assessments in a testing center or with a local proctor, you can request a bypass pin for the assessment. Bypass pins are issued on a per-test basis, so you'll need to request one for each assessment that will involve a local proctor. To request a bypass pin:\nCreate a Canvas quiz or third-party assessment, and enable proctoring as described above.\nOpen Honorlock and contact support via the in-app chat tool, or send an email request to\nsupport@honorlock.com\n.\nIn your request, include the full course title and assessment title (as it appears in Honorlock).\nSend the bypass pin to the person who will be proctoring the exam.\nFor more about bypass pins, see\nBypass Pin Best Practices\n.\nCreate and use exam profiles\nAn exam profile is a reusable template of proctoring settings and test-taker guidelines.  Once you create one or more profiles, you can apply them to any Canvas or third-party assessment.  This can be a real time saver and ensure that your settings are consistent across all assessments that use the profile.   For information on how to create and apply exam profiles, see\nExam Profiles\nand\nProfile Management and Best Practices\n.\nFind and remove public copies of your test questions\nHonorlock's "Search and Destroy" feature scans the internet for compromised exam questions once an exam is enabled for proctoring.   If it finds copies of questions in your assessment, you can send DMCA takedown requests for compromised items and/or revise your questions. For more, see the Honorlock guides below.\nNotes:\nThe question search only finds exact matches to the question stem. Images and variable equations are not included.\nSearch and Destroy does not work with:\nRandom draw question groups linked to question banks in Classic Quizzes.\nRandom draw question groups or individual questions copied from item banks in New Quizzes.\nFor question/item bank questions and third-party assessments, use the question upload method.\nSearch & Destroy Guide\n(for Canvas Quizzes)\nSearch & Destroy Guide - Third Party Assessments\nSearch & Destroy FAQ\nReview Honorlock results\nThe Honorlock "Results" screen provides summary and detailed information for each test-taker session, including the number of human-verified rule violations, flags, and proctor pop-in sessions. Two separate reports are available:\nSummary Report:\nThe Summary Report provides summary data for each test taker, as well as the total number of test-taker sessions in each of the following categories: sessions with no issues, sessions with high flags, and sessions classified as critical. This report is available immediately and updated as test takers submit their assessments. For instructions on accessing and using this report, see\nReviewing a Summary Report\nand\nHow to Interpret Flags\n.\nFull Results Report:\nThe full results report provides both summary and detailed results for each test taker, including webcam and screen recordings, as well as the event log.  The report interface includes options for searching by student as well as filtering and sorting by a variety of criteria.  For instructions on accessing and using this report, see\nHow to View Your Honorlock Results\nand\nReviewing Session Results Best Practices\n.\nNotes:\nHonorlock recommends using the Chrome browser for reviewing the Honorlock results reports. Other browsers may require setting adjustments in order to work properly with this feature.\nHonorlock results reports are not available for practice or simulated assessments.\nSample syllabus language\nBe sure to let your students know that your course will be using Honorlock in your course syllabus.   Here is some sample language that you can use as is or adapt to suit your specific needs.\nWe will be using\nHonorlock\n, an online proctoring tool, for the exams in this course. Honorlock offers you flexibility to take your exams when and where it is convenient for you.\nTechnology requirements:\nA computer running Windows 10 or 11, macOS 10.14 or higher, or ChromeOS (Chromebooks)\nThe Google Chrome browser version 120 or higher with the\nHonorlock Chrome Extension\nenabled\nA built-in or external webcam and microphone\nAn internet connection rated at 1.5 Mbps download/750 Kbps upload. To test your internet speed, visit\nhttp://www.speedtest.net\n.\nDual monitors are not permitted. If the software detects more than one monitor, see\nMultiple Monitors Detected\n.\nTesting environment:\nDuring the course of an exam, Honorlock will record the testing environment; therefore, it's important to select a private space for the exam session where disruptions are unlikely and where recording devices can be enabled.\nRoom scans:\nYour instructor may choose to require a room scan. If so, the software will ask you to complete a 360° room pan and desk sweep with your webcam. This is to ensure your workspace is clear of any materials unauthorized by your instructor.\nAcademic integrity and academic misconduct:\nPlease note that while Honorlock proctors or software may flag suspected cheating during exams, these measures do not constitute a determination of academic misconduct. Any suspicion of misconduct will be referred to the Office of Student Conduct and evaluated through a fair and transparent process (see\nhttps://studentcode.iu.edu/responsibilities/academic-misconduct.html\n).\nIf you have concerns about any of the requirements above, or other concerns about using an online proctoring tool, please work with your instructor to find an equivalent alternative.\nLearn more\nVendor documentation\nAll topics\nInstructor topics\nTest taker (student topics)\nIU documentation\nAbout online proctoring at IU\nHonorlock for IU students\nGet help\nVendor support for instructors and students\nLive:\nFor live 24/7 technical assistance from the vendor, use the online chat option within the Honorlock app in Canvas, or go to the\nHonorlock support page\nand select\nBegin Live Chat\n.\nEmail:\nFor less urgent support needs, send a message to\nsupport@honorlock.com\n.\nPhone:\nFor toll-free phone support for instructors and test administrators only, call 844-243-2500.\nLocal support for instructors\nFor assistance with setting up proctored exams, contact your\ncampus teaching and learning center\n.	KB0026521	KB0026521 v9.0	 location in Canvas, including Quizzes, Assignments, <strong>Modules</strong>, or a link to the quiz in a Canvas Page course uses the <strong>Modules</strong> tool, add the quiz to the desired location in <strong>Modules</strong>. From your course in your course (for example, <strong>Modules</strong>, Assignments, or Quizzes). Regardless of where students begin	8.649		2026-01-31 17:22:18.081685	2026-01-31 17:22:58.844608	0	633b8e81470eb6142fc6a55b416d43a0
1647	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024419	About Google at IU My Drive	Table of contents\nOverview\nPrivate folders\nShared folders\nOverview\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n, available as part of\nGoogle at IU\n, provides a simple, secure way to share and store files online. The sharing settings allow secure collaboration on files and folders.\nGoogle at IU\naccounts are unrelated to personal Google accounts that are available free to the public; for more, see\nCompare Google at IU accounts and personal (free) Google accounts\n.\nImportant:\nDue to Google's Terms of Service, IU group accounts are not allowed to store data in\nGoogle My Drive\n.\nNotes:\nTo view your total\nGoogle at IU\nusage, go to\nhttps://drive.google.com/settings/storage\n. Be sure you are logged into\nyour\nGoogle at IU\naccount\n, and not a personal Google account. To be sure, you could use a\nprivate browser\n.\nItems in\nGoogle My Drive\nare automatically deleted once they have been in your trash for 30 days.\nFor more, see\nManage files and folders in Google at IU My Drive\n.\nPrivate folders\nWhen you create a new folder, it will be private by default. Private folders have a plain folder icon.\nShared folders\nThe shared folder icon displays a person symbol. You can share folders with specific people, generate share links, and/or\nshare folders with a Google Group\n. For content that should be restricted to a specific audience, UITS recommends sharing with specific people or with a Google Group rather than using a share link.\nTo share files and folders using\nGoogle My Drive\n, follow the steps at\nShare folders in\nGoogle Drive\n.\nWhen sharing with specific people, use\nusername@iu.edu\nto share with other\nGoogle at IU\nusers.\nWhen sharing with external users, you will receive a prompt indicating that you are sharing with an external user, and you will need to confirm to continue.\nGoogle My Drive\nhas three permissions settings:\nCan view\nCan organize, add, & edit\nIs owner\nTo access advanced sharing settings, right-click a file or folder, select\nShare\n, and then choose\nAdvanced\n.\nPrevent editors from changing access and adding new people\nwill make it so that only the owner can add, remove, and adjust permissions of collaborators. This is a good option to have selected from a security standpoint if a folder has many collaborators and contains data that should be restricted to a specific audience.\nIn the advanced settings, you can also change the permissions settings of collaborators on a folder, including making someone else an owner of the folder (this will remove you as an owner and change your permission to\nCan organize, add, & edit\n).	KB0024419	KB0024419 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Private folders Shared folders Overview Google at IU My Drive, available as part of Google at IU, provides a simple, secure way to share and store files online. The sharing settings allow secure collaboration on files and folders. Google at IU accounts are unrelated to personal Google accounts that are available free t	4.7212		2026-01-31 14:33:38.978175	2026-01-31 17:15:06.745626	0	96ffecbb476456583d373803846d432e
2296	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3cdf2cf7476456583d373803846d435d	Enterprise services for units in support of the IT-28 policy	Table of contents\nOverview\nBusiness functions\nGateways and portals\nCollaborative tools\nCommunication and publishing\nStorage\nApplication services\nResearch technology\nOverview\nThe following is a guide to IU enterprise services that may best meet the needs of your department or unit in support of\nCyber Risk Mitigation Responsibilities (IT-28)\n. For technical help with any of the services listed, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n. For assistance with IT-28 questions, email\nit28help@iu.edu\n. For details about IT-28 processes, objectives, and compliance, see\nAbout the Cyber Risk Mitigation Responsibilities policy (IT-28) at IU\n.\nBusiness functions\nThe following services enable day-to-day work for faculty and staff.\nIntended for:\nAll academic departments and administrative and support units\nEmail\nExchange Online\nVoice\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nCalendar\nExchange Online\nPrinting\nIU Print\nDesktop communication\n(instant messaging and sharing)\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nGateways and portals\nThese services allow users to find and use your application, tool, or service from a convenient, central location.\nIntended for:\nAny department or unit with an online service\nGateways and portals\nOne (One.IU)\nIU Mobile\nCollaborative tools\nThese services and tools facilitate collaborative work within units, across the university, and with partners outside IU.\nAsynchronous\ncollaboration is accomplished primarily through shared repositories, while\nsynchronous\ncollaboration is mainly done through live communication channels.\nIntended for:\nAny department or unit that needs virtual space for group projects, does collaborative or committee-based work, consults with other groups, etc.\nAsynchronous (storage-based)\nGitHub\nConfluence\nSecure Share\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nSynchronous ("face-to-face")\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nZoom\nCommunication and publishing\nThe following services allow efficient and reliable communication to both the university community and the public, through managed web publication and video and audio services.\nIntended for:\nAcademic departments with online or distance learning components; departments and units with a need for a public presence, including web pages\nDistance/online education\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nZoom\nClassroom recording\nKaltura Lecture Capture\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nMarketing and communications\nSalesforce and IU CRM\nDigital Signage\nContent management\nWeb Content Management System\nWeb hosting\nSiteKube\nWindows Hosting Environment\nImportant:\nSiteKube is replacing Sitehost. For more, see\nAbout SiteKube\n.\nStorage\nThe following services offer document, file, and data storage for individuals, groups, and departments at IU. Additionally, the university provides associated services for generating reports on relevant data and building workflows for workflow documents.\nIntended for:\nAny department or unit with file or data storage needs\nNote:\nMuch of the data handled by university departments, including (but not limited to) data regulated by the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (\nHIPAA\n) or the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (\nFERPA\n), are\nprotected by law\n. These and other\ninstitutional data\nmay be classified as Critical, Restricted, or University-internal; see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n. No storage solution is appropriate for every classification. Before choosing a storage solution, consult your\nData Managers\nor see\nAbout dedicated file storage services and IT services with storage components appropriate for sensitive institutional data, including research data containing protected health information\n.\nFile storage\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nUA Technology Support\nEnterprise data storage\nEnterprise Information Governance\nOptions for storing files at IU\nCritical data\nstorage\nCritical Data Infrastructure (CDI) through\nUA Technology Support\nEnterprise Data Warehouse\nMicrosoft Teams at IU\nDocument storage\nOnBase\nBusiness analysis and reporting\nTableau\nWorkflow\nOnBase\nKuali Enterprise Workflow\nApplication services\nThese services provide the infrastructure necessary for units to implement IT services and applications, from research and development to ongoing hosting and administration, with state-of-the-art tools to ensure security and support.\nIntended for:\nUnits that develop, research, or host IT services; departments or individuals performing computational research\nApplication hosting\nWindows Hosting Environment\nIntelligent Infrastructure Enterprise (II-E)\nServer rack space\nServer Co-location\nServer logging\nLog-Alert\nCode repository\nGitHub\nIssue tracking\nServiceNow\nJira\nSecurity\nVulnerability Scanning\nEnterprise Certificate Authority\nLog-Alert\nEnterprise Device Management\nResearch technology\nThe following services are advanced technology solutions for research and teaching at IU.\nIntended for:\nDepartments or individuals performing computational, data-driven research\nResearch database hosting\nResearch Database Complex (RDC)\nData capture and management\nIU REDCap\nData storage\nScholarly Data Archive (SDA)\nSlate\nSlate-Project\nSlate-Scratch\nGeode-Project\nCybersecurity and compliance consulting\nSecureMyResearch	KB0023581	KB0023581 v2.0	 storage Scholarly Data Archive (SDA) <strong>Slate</strong> <strong>Slate</strong>-Project <strong>Slate</strong>-Scratch Geode-Project Cybersecurity	5.2989		2026-01-31 14:35:32.281267	2026-01-31 15:55:24.065854	0	90cc753a3bd22610476d941864e45ac9
1656	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023800	About Canvas at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nLearn more\nGet help\nOverview\nDeveloped by\nInstructure, Inc.\n, Canvas is IU's learning management system (LMS). Canvas includes most of the standard tools found in open source and commercial management systems (assignments, discussions, announcements, quizzes, syllabus, etc.) as well as enhanced tools and features for communication, collaboration, and assessment, including real-time web conferencing, a multimedia recorder, a\nGoogle Docs\nintegration, outcomes and rubrics, and course analytics.\nIU students, faculty, and staff can log into Canvas using their personal\nIU username and passphrase\nor an IU Guest account. Access Canvas at IU from a web browser (on a computer, smartphone, or tablet) or via the mobile apps distributed by Instructure.\nCanvas accounts are not automatically created for\ngroup or departmental accounts\n. To establish a Canvas account for a group or departmental account,\nsend a request to the Canvas at IU administrators\n. Once the Canvas administrators confirm the creation of the account, you can use your IU group account username and passphrase to log into Canvas.\nFor more about accessing Canvas, see\nAccess Canvas at IU\n.\nLearn more\nIT Training resources\nIU students can access\nCanvas training & resources for students\nfrom\nIT Training\nfor help getting started with Canvas.\nCanvas Community\nVarious online support resources are available in Instructure's\nCanvas Community\n:\nDocumentation:\nTo get started using Canvas, see:\nInstructor Getting Started Resources\nStudent Getting Started Resources\nAdditional guides intended for students, instructors, instructional designers, and system administrators, as well as guides for using Canvas mobile apps, are available at\nCanvas Guides\n.\nFeature ideas:\nSubscribe to various discussion categories to keep abreast of work on new Canvas features. Canvas deploys a new release every three weeks; for more, see\nAbout Canvas releases\n.\nFor additional training on Canvas tools and features, check out the\nInstructure Live\nfree webinar series.\nAnswers:\nVisit the\nCanvas Question Forum\nto search questions that have already been asked and post new questions for the Canvas community to answer.\nGroups:\nA resource for posting questions to specific Canvas community groups, or posting solutions based on your experiences in forums such as\nHigher Education\n,\nK-12\n, and more.\nTraining videos and self-paced tutorials\nFor videos intended specifically for IU instructors, see the\nCanvas Training Series\n.\nYou can also view training videos and access self-paced tutorials about Canvas in the Instructure Training Services Portal. To access the portal:\nLog into\nCanvas\n.\nFrom the global navigation menu on the left, select\nHelp\n.\nFrom the help menu, select\nTraining Services Portal\n.\nIf prompted to allow the portal to access your account, choose\nAuthorize\n.\nYou will first be presented with a tutorial on how to use the portal. Choose\nStart Tutorial\nto view the tutorial, or choose the\nx\nat the upper right to view the training offerings.\nYou can browse the offerings by category (Learning Pathways, Courses, Videos, or Portfolium). To view or participate in an offering, choose\nEnroll\n.\nGet help\nInstructors can get help using technology in their teaching at the\nteaching and learning centers\non each campus.\nFor help from a UITS Support Center consultant, fill out the "Ask for Help" form found in the\nCanvas\nHelp\nmenu. You can also contact your campus\nSupport Center\ndirectly.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:33:38.978175	2026-01-31 14:45:30.083714	1	KB0023800
11949	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=4dbfe477476456583d373803846d43d1	Enterprise Linux Administration how-to guides and support information	Table of contents\nHow-to guides\nInformation and support\nHow-to guides\nTransfer data between the IU ES nodes\nStart a process on the IU ES nodes whenever the node is booted\nCreate a profile on the IU ES Linux systems if you don't have a home directory\nRestore a file or directory on the IU ES nodes\nRequest new software on the IU ES nodes\nDetermine the version numbers and installation dates of existing Perl modules on the IU ES nodes\nJ2EE deployment and re-deployment information\nUse the ELA scripts to clone a directory, such as your home directory, to another host on the IU ES nodes\nCreate a home directory on an ELA Linux server\nConfigure the WinSCP client to work with the Windows data transfer servers at IU\nInformation and support\nHelp creating a personal ES UNIX account\nWhen nodes maintained by ELA are unavailable\nHelp for BRTE at IU\nGet help transferring files to DTP.IU.EDU and DTT.IU.EDU\nAbout WebDAV on the ES nodes\nELA Linux server naming conventions	KB0022900	KB0022900 v1.0	 Request new <strong>software</strong> on the IU ES nodes Determine the version numbers and installation dates of existing Perl <strong>modules</strong> on the IU ES nodes J2EE deployment and re-deployment information Use the ELA scripts	6.506	km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:23:02.531072	2026-01-31 17:23:02.531072	0	4dbfe477476456583d373803846d43d1
11965	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=208dde9747f722982fc6a55b416d4351	History of the Knowledge Base	KB1\nThe\nUITS\nSupport Center\nhas been answering computing questions since the early 1980s. Even in those early days, constant change and repetitive answers were the modus operandi. These two conditions encouraged staff to maintain an official, correct source for frequently changing and frequently requested information. Consultants also needed a repository for results of difficult or lengthy research they had undertaken to resolve some problems.\nThe first online "Knowledge Base" (KB), established in about 1988, was a directory of files on a shared VMScluster account, known as the "M" directory (named for Marie Meyer, one of the originators). Consultants answering questions via email found it convenient to base their responses on these files rather than repeatedly writing the answer from scratch. Upon sending information to answer an original question, they also created a more general version of the answer to include in the M directory for reuse.\nThis directory quickly grew into over a hundred files, often with multiple versions. There were no standards for the format of the files, and answers were written, revised, and maintained ad hoc. In about 1989, Support Center staff attempted a more logical organization of these files in a VAX Notes conference, called the "Pat Answer Library", but because of the unwieldy character of VAX Notes, this never really caught on.\nBy 1990, the Support Center was determined to find software appropriate for storing, maintaining, and retrieving this Knowledge Base, and examined several text-oriented, LAN-based databases (LAN-based so that the files could be shared, yet independent of any central operating system). The staff settled on "IZE," a commercial product that ran only on DOS, a major limitation. Furthermore, budget constraints allowed only a site license for five concurrent users. Nonetheless, IZE served well for almost two years. It offered content-based outlining and keyword searches that made maintenance easy. The KB grew to several hundred files written by Support Center consultants or adapted for IU from content found elsewhere.\nThen came Gopher. In the early 1990s, a clever, ambitious young staffer, Scott Hutton, created a full-text searchable, world-accessible Gopher server that revolutionized the KB. Support Center staff stopped thinking of it as a tool for their own private use, and started orienting information for public use. Gopher allowed around-the-clock public access to the Support Center's question-and-answer repository.\nThe staff had been enchanted by Gopher for about six months when they first heard about the World Wide Web. Scott had the Knowledge Base on the web within a few months. Almost immediately, the Gopher version became an albatross. Scott wrote many hacks to make the Gopher version simulate the web (linking texts, for example), but the team was, in effect, maintaining two copies of the Knowledge Base. The simplicity of the Gopher version handicapped the web version. Staff could not write texts embedding links or internal references. In March 1995, the team disabled the KB's Gopher version, freeing them to exploit HTML features to their fullest.\nKB2 and KB3\nDuring the winter of 1995-96, the staff rewrote the KB software to provide easier maintenance, faster searching, more search options, and other features not possible when the original web version was written. KB2, written primarily by Matt Liggett, with additional modules written by Alan Meiss and John Nienart, replaced the original on March 6, 1996.\nAfter over three years of work by Mike MacKenzie, Ed Dragomer, David Jantzen, Mark Meiss, Mike Fragassi, Josh Reedy, Andrea Donderi, Jonathan Phillips, and others, a new iteration of the Knowledge Base code, KB3, went into production in July 2002.\nThe KMS\nIn 2009, the KM team was tasked to develop a modern KMS, to better realize the potential for knowledge management at IU.\nAfter a few frustrating false starts, in June 2013 the KM team began using Scrum/Agile development methods. By June 2014, they had delivered a new Knowledge Base front end; in October 2015, a new back-end work environment (kms.iu.edu) was released and the legacy KB (bell.ucs.indiana.edu) decommissioned.\nFor more, see\nAbout the IU Knowledge Base\n.\nAbout the Knowledge Base\nThe KB is regularly referenced by people all over the world, and Support Center consultants still use the system to capture solutions to problems customers experience. For more, see\nAbout the UITS Knowledge Management team\n.	KB0022637	KB0022637 v2.0	, the Support Center was determined to find <strong>software</strong> appropriate for storing, maintaining, and retrieving of 1995-96, the staff rewrote the KB <strong>software</strong> to provide easier maintenance, faster searching, more primarily by Matt Liggett, with additional <strong>modules</strong> written by Alan Meiss and John Nienart, replaced	6.1987		2026-01-31 17:23:06.175958	2026-01-31 17:23:06.175958	0	208dde9747f722982fc6a55b416d4351
11861	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1e3e1d7e47623298d500b87b716d4374	Use Canvas outcomes and rubrics for program assessment and improvement	Table of contents\nOverview\nOutcomes and the Canvas academic hierarchy\nPlan your outcomes assessment implementation\nEmbedded assessment vs. a centrally managed approach\nBefore you begin implementation\nRequest Canvas admin rights\nReview plans with colleagues and academic leadership\nCreate account-level outcomes and rubrics\nCreate outcomes at the account level\nCreate rubrics at the account level\nFacilitate embedded assessment activities\nImplement a centrally managed assessment process\nSet up a Canvas site for assessment\nTools for tracking and reporting\nLearning Mastery Gradebook (availability: course only)\nCanvas Gradebook (availability: course only)\nOutcomes Report for an individual student (availability: course and account)\nOutcome Export report (availability: account only)\nOutcome Results/Student Competency reports (availability: account only)\nOverview\nLearning outcomes in Canvas can be published at any level of the Canvas academic hierarchy--campus, school/college, department, or course. When outcomes at the department level or higher are incorporated into course rubrics to assess student learning, the results can be aggregated across all courses under the purview of the academic unit where the outcomes reside for purposes of longitudinal assessment, accountability, and program improvement. This document provides guidance on how to implement Canvas outcomes to support your assessment efforts.\nNote:\nUITS strongly recommends collaborating on your outcomes implementation with the instructional design and/or instructional technology consultants in your campus\nteaching and learning center\n. There are many technical and pedagogical decision points along the way, and they can help guide you through the process.\nOutcomes and the Canvas academic hierarchy\nCanvas outcomes make use of the academic hierarchy of accounts in Canvas, a branching organizational structure representing the university, its campuses, and their respective schools, colleges, and departments (see figure below).\nLevel 1:\nAt the top of the hierarchy is Indiana University.\nLevel 2:\nReserved for the nine IU campuses: BL, CO, EA, FT, IN, KO, NW, SB, and SE.\nLevel 3:\nBeneath each campus are the accounts representing the schools and colleges on the campus. These accounts begin with the two-letter campus code, followed by the SIS academic group code for the unit, and ending with a three letter "GRP" suffix (for example, BL-ADMIN-GRP, CO-BUS-GRP).\nLevel 4:\nThese accounts correspond to the academic subject codes used by the courses offered through the parent school or college.  Level 4 accounts begin with the two-letter campus abbreviation followed by the academic subject code (for example, BL-AERO, CO-EDUC). SIS courses are provisioned into the level 4 account that matches the campus and academic subject code in their course ID. For example, a course with the ID SP19-CO-EDUC-E201-18797 would be placed in the CO-EDUC account.\nTree diagram depicting sample units in the IU academic hierarchy\nAs soon as outcomes are created in a specific account within the hierarchy, they become available to any account or course that is a child or descendent of the account in which the outcomes were published. Outcomes published at the campus level, for example, can be incorporated into rubrics in any course offered on the campus, and the results can be aggregated at the department, school/college, and campus level. School or college-level outcomes, on the other hand, are only available to the courses and departments belonging to that school or college.\nWhen creating outcomes, it's always best to place them in the account at the highest level to which they apply. In other words, campus-level outcomes should be created in the corresponding campus account, school and college-level outcomes should live in the associated school/college account, and so on. This approach will ensure that student performance results can be aggregated and reported upon by the academic units responsible for those outcomes at all relevant levels of the academic hierarchy.\nPlan your outcomes assessment implementation\nBefore you begin to create outcomes for your unit, it's important to have a clear plan for how the outcomes will be organized, described, scored, promoted, and used to achieve the assessment goals of your unit as well as the academic unit(s) under your purview. This planning process often engages an assessment coordinator, assessment committee, or curriculum committee in consultation with the academic leadership of the unit. Topics and questions your planning group will need to consider are covered in the Canvas Outcomes Planning Worksheet. Use either of the links below to create a copy for your assessment team:\nCanvas Outcomes Planning Worksheet\n(\nGoogle Docs\n)\nCanvas Outcomes Planning Worksheet\n(Microsoft Word)\nIf you use the above worksheet to guide discussion and document your decisions, you'll have everything you need to start building your assessment framework in Canvas.\nEmbedded assessment vs. a centrally managed approach\nThe Canvas Outcomes feature was designed on an embedded assessment model. In this model, outcomes are published by a campus, school, or department. Faculty then incorporate them as criteria in rubrics, which are used to grade assignments in their classes. A key benefit is that no extra work is required on the part of the student or the instructional staff. Outcomes are assessed as part of the normal grading process. Under this distributed model, however, the academic unit has very little control in terms of when and how outcomes are used, the rigor with which they are scored, the nature of the student work being evaluated, etc.\nSchool and programs subject to specialized disciplinary accreditation often want greater control over the assessment process in order to meet the requirements of their accrediting body. Implementing a centrally managed assessment process requires considerably more planning and oversight and typically increases the workload of individuals responsible for evaluating student work. To decide which approach is the best fit for your unit's needs, use your completed worksheet and the lists of characteristics below.\nEmbedded assessment:\nInstructors assess their own students in their own courses.\nInstructors determine which artifacts and outcomes are assessed.\nAssessment takes place in regular course sites as part of the regular assignment submission and grading process.\nCentrally managed assessment:\nAssessment coordinator or committee determines who evaluates student work.\nAssessment coordinator or committee determines which artifacts and outcomes are assessed.\nAll assessment takes place in a dedicated assessment site.\nIn addition to outcomes assessment, can be used to track student GPA, standardized test scores, completion of program requirement or milestones, etc.\nBlind evaluation (student identity hidden) of student work can be enforced.\nTwo or more evaluators per student submission can be enforced.\nAssessment coordinator or committee can easily track individual and aggregate student performance over time.\nStudents can easily review and be asked to reflect on their own intellectual development over time.\nMulti-campus collaborative programs can be accommodated.\nAggregate assessment data is available in Canvas Gradebook and Learning Mastery Gradebook.\nBefore you begin implementation\nRequest Canvas admin rights\nSpecial permissions are needed in order to create outcomes and rubrics, as well as generate reports at the academic unit level in Canvas. IU has created the Outcomes Manager administrative role for assessment coordinators or others who will be responsible for overseeing the unit's outcomes implementation. See\nAdministrative access to Canvas at IU\nfor information about and instructions on how to request access as an Outcomes Manager.\nReview plans with colleagues and academic leadership\nOnce outcomes or rubrics are published at the account level, they are immediately visible and available for use by instructors in your unit. After they have been used to assess student work, Canvas outcomes are difficult to change and they\ncannot\nbe deleted. Therefore, it's important to get them right from the start. Be sure to share your plans with colleagues and seek the approval of the academic leadership for your unit before publishing anything in Canvas.\nCreate account-level outcomes and rubrics\nCreate outcomes at the account level\nGeneral instructions on how to create and manage outcomes and outcome groups at the account level are available in the\nOutcomes\nsection of the Canvas Admin Guide. The most labor-intensive aspect of creating outcomes is customizing the rating scale, mastery level, and mastery calculation method for each outcome. You can do this more quickly in Microsoft Excel; see\nUse Excel to create or modify outcomes in Canvas\n.\nCreate rubrics at the account level\nLike outcomes, rubrics published at the account level can be used in any course that is a child or descendent of the account in which the rubrics were published. Providing instructors with pre-defined rubrics containing the outcomes for your unit can save time on importing outcomes and creating rubrics.\nTo create an account-level rubric:\nLog into Canvas and navigate to the administrative account containing your outcomes set.\nIn the account navigation menu, select\nRubrics\n.\nCreate a new rubric and give it a meaningful name (such as "Undergraduate Spanish Outcomes").\nDelete the default criterion added to the rubric.\nUse the\nFind Outcome\noption to select and add the outcomes from the account-level set that you wish to add to the rubric. After selecting each outcome, be sure to uncheck the option\nUse this criterion for scoring\nbefore selecting\nImport\n.\nWhen you are finished adding outcomes, select\nCreate Rubric\nto save the rubric.\nNote:\nWhen an instructor attaches the rubric to an assignment in a course:\nThe outcomes referenced in the rubric will automatically be imported into the course.\nThe instructor can delete criteria from the rubric that are not applicable to the assignment.\nThe instructor can add assignment-specific criteria to the rubric.\nFacilitate embedded assessment activities\nOnce you've created your outcomes and rubrics, no additional setup in Canvas is necessary as long as your unit plans to use embedded assessment as the principal method of data collection. However, it's not enough to simply publish these assets in Canvas. If you want instructors to take advantage of them, someone (ideally your department head or assessment coordinator) needs to communicate clear expectations to faculty about how to locate them and when, where, and how they should be used. Otherwise, instructors who do happen to find them may use them in ways that aren't consistent with your assessment goals. You may also want to develop documentation for your faculty with instructions on how to find and make the best use of the outcomes and rubrics published for your unit.\nBelow is a list of Canvas Guides that can be shared with instructors who will be using account-level outcomes or rubrics to assess student work. You may also want to develop some custom documentation that provides specifics on how to locate and import the outcomes and rubrics for your unit.\nHow do I find an existing outcome to add to a course?\nHow do I align an outcome with a rubric in a course?\nHow do I add a rubric to an assignment?\nImplement a centrally managed assessment process\nAcademic units that want more control over the assessment process can conduct assessment activities in one or more Canvas sites designed for and dedicated to this purpose. This approach requires more time and effort on the part of students, evaluators, and program managers, but allows the unit to regulate the types of student evidence collected as well as how evidence is evaluated, and by whom. Another advantage is enhanced tracking and reporting afforded by both the Learning Mastery Gradebook as well as the standard Canvas Gradebook.\nSet up a Canvas site for assessment\nMany of the tools and features used for teaching in Canvas can be used for outcomes or program assessment. Unlike Canvas course sites, however, which are typically active for one term, your assessment site can remain open indefinitely, allowing you to collect and analyze assessment data over time. Since assessment requirements and practices vary widely from one discipline to the next, UITS recommends working closely with a consultant from your teaching center to design an assessment framework that best meets your needs. In general, to set up a Canvas site for assessment:\nCreate a new Canvas site:\nThis site will serve as the center of all assessment activity for your program. Set the expiration date for the site far out into the future (for example, January 1, 2030). For instructions, see\nCreate a new Canvas course site or change the expiration date for a course you created\n.\nEnable the Learning Mastery Gradebook feature option(s):\nThe Learning Mastery Gradebook provides an overview of how the entire class and individual students are performing in relation to learning outcomes. This feature is disabled by default. To turn it on in your assessment site:\nOpen the Settings tool in the site, and select\nFeature Options\n.\nOn the "Feature Options" page, toggle the "Learning Mastery Gradebook" option from\nOff\nto\nOn\n. This will enable the instructor view of the gradebook.\nIf you would like students to be able to track their own performance, toggle "Student Learning Mastery Gradebook" to\nOn\nas well.\nRemove unused tools from the course navigation:\nIn a dedicated assessment site, students will interact with a small subset of the tools typically available in Canvas course sites, and may only need access to the Modules tool. To keep students focused on the tasks required of them in the site, disable the tools they don't need. (Disabled tools are still visible to instructors in the course navigation.) For instructions, see\nHow do I manage Course Navigation links?\nFor the course navigation in Canvas assessment sites, UITS recommends some or all of the following:\nHome:\nUse the home page to provide a description of the purpose of the site and links to appropriate documentation if needed.\nAnnouncements\nYou can use Announcements to communicate to site members, if desired.\nModules:\nUse Modules in place of Assignments and Quizzes so that students will have one place and tool to view and participate in assessment activities.\nGrades:\nThis provides access to both the standard and the Learning Mastery Gradebook.\nOutcomes:\nYou can allow students to view the outcomes and their descriptions in the Outcomes tool, if desired; outcomes may also be visible in assignment rubrics and the Learning Mastery Gradebooks.\nAdd your account-level outcomes:\nIn order to use your account-level outcomes to assess student work, you'll first need to add them to the course site. For instructions, see\nHow do I find an existing outcome to add to a course?\nDecide what types of evidence you want to collect:\nWith your assessment committee, determine what types of artifacts you want to collect at what points in the program. Some programs are very prescriptive about the work that students submit (for example, signature assessments A,B, and C from courses X, Y, and Z), while others want students to make the selections in response to prompts that lay out the requirements.\nCreate a Canvas assignment, quiz, or survey for each submission or piece of evidence:\nBegin by selecting the Canvas tool and submission type best suited to the type of evidence that you intend to collect.\nExamples of Canvas tools suited to evidence and submission type\nEvidence\nCanvas tool\nSubmission type\nRepresentative artifacts (assignments, projects, etc.) uploaded by the student\nAssignments\nOnline > File Upload\nReflection\nAssignments\nOnline > File Upload or Text Entry\nWeb site or web resource created by the student\nAssignments\nWeb Site URL\n(Note that web content can be changed after assignment submission or evaluation.)\nVideo files uploaded directly to Canvas\nAssignments\nOnline > Media Recording\n(Maximum file size 500 MB)\nStudent videos stored in Kaltura\nAssignments\nText Entry. See\nCreate a video assignment in Canvas with Kaltura\n.\nObservations of student behavior (for example, student teaching observations)\nAssignments\nNo submission. Evaluate with grade, rubric, and/or comments.\nGPA, exams, standardized test scores\nAssignments\nNo submission\n(Can be hand entered or imported into the gradebook)\nSurvey or self-assessment\nQuizzes\nGraded or ungraded survey\n(With graded surveys, you can track submissions in the Canvas Gradebook)\nKnowledge or skills assessment\nQuizzes\nGraded quiz\nAs you set up each assignment, quiz, or survey for evidence collection and evaluation, also consider:\nAssignment/quiz/survey instructions:\nProvide students with detailed directions for what they are required to submit for the assignment and when it is due (for example, at the end of the sophomore year, after completing a certain course, etc.), as well as contact information in case they have questions. Also, if students are expected to submit work products created in specific classes, ask the instructors of those classes to remind students to make the submission.\nRubrics:\nFor evidence that will involve scoring students on learning outcomes, create or attach a rubric that incorporates your account-level outcomes as criteria. The rubric can also include criteria that are not outcomes, but the scores for those criteria will not be available in the student mastery gradebook or other reports. For information on how to work with rubrics, see:\nHow do I align an outcome with a rubric in a course?\nHow do I add a rubric to an assignment?\nHow do I add a rubric to a quiz?\nBlind evaluation:\nIf you don't want evaluators to know the identity of the person they're evaluating, enable the anonymous grading feature in the assignment as described in\nHow do I add an assignment that includes anonymous grading?\nIn the assignment instructions, ask students to remove their names and other identifying information from their work before they submit it in the assignment. This feature is not available for quizzes.\nMultiple evaluators per submission:\nIf your program requires two or more evaluators per submission, use the moderated grading feature in Canvas to set the number of graders and designate the person responsible for reconciling differences and posting the final scores. For instructions, see\nHow do I add a moderated assignment to be graded by multiple reviewers?\nThis feature is not available for quizzes.\nGrading setup for assignments:\nThe grading options in the assignment can be helpful for tracking how successful students are in meeting all of the outcomes associated with the task in the standard gradebook. You can either let the points in the rubric determine the total points for the assignment or ask evaluators to manually enter the score.\nTo display performance descriptors in the gradebook (meets expectations, does not meet expectations, etc.) instead of point values, create a custom grading scheme in the course; see\nHow do I add a grading scheme in a course?\nThen set the\nDisplay Grade as\noption in the assignment to\nLetter Grade\nand choose the custom scheme. Finally, since students won't be receiving a final grade in the assessment site, it's a good idea to check the box\nDo not count this assignment towards the final grade\n.\nRestricting or granting access to an assignment:\nFor requirements that only apply to a subset of the students in the site, you can use differentiation to restrict or grant access to specific individuals or sections. See\nHow do I assign an assignment to everyone, individual students, differentiated tags, or sections?\nHiding evaluation results from students:\nIf you don't want students to be able to see the evaluation results for all or specific assignments, mute them. See\nHow do I use posting policies in a course?\nOrganize assignments, quizzes, and surveys into Modules:\nUse the Canvas Modules tool to group the assignments in your site in a way that makes sense to both students and evaluators. Among the more common schemes for grouping assessment tasks evidence are:\nProgram year:\n(Year One, Year Two, etc.) Used to organize submissions chronologically, making it easier to monitor progress over time\nLearning outcome:\n(Critical Thinking, Written Communication, Team Work, etc.) Used when students are expected to submit representative work for each outcome at specific points in their program; students may be required to upload specific work products or make their own selections\nDecision point:\n(Decision Point 1, Decision Point 2, etc.) Commonly used in education programs. Performance in a given decision point module determines whether students are ready to advance to the next stage of the program.\nCourse:\nUsed when students are expected to submit specific artifacts or representative work from specific courses in the program\nIf desired,\nmodule prerequisites\nand\nrequirements\ncan be added to control how students progress through the modules.\nPublish all assignments, quizzes, surveys, and modules:\nBefore you start adding students, publish all of the elements in your assessment framework so they are available to students.\nCreate course sections:\nCanvas sections can be used to group students or students and evaluators for a variety of purposes, including:\nReporting:\nGrouping students by cohort, program year, concentration, etc. facilitates filtering by the characteristics in the standard and Learning Mastery Gradebook, as well as in unit level reports.\nAssigning evaluators:\nTo distribute the evaluation workload, evaluators can be assigned to specific students by creating a section for each evaluator and adding their students to it. To prevent evaluators from seeing and assessing students in their group, limit their section privileges as described in How do I limit a user to only interact with other users in the same course section? Evaluators who are permitted to see all students can use section filtering in SpeedGrader and the Gradebook to narrow the list to their assigned students.\nAssignment differentiation:\nStudent groups can also be used for assignment differentiation. For example, if students are grouped by entering cohort, the availability and due dates for assignment can be customized to reflect when students in each cohort are expected to complete the assignment.\nWhen setting up sections in your assessment site, keep in mind that any member of the course can belong to multiple sections. For instructions on how to create ad hoc sections, see\nHow do I add a section to a course as an instructor?\nCommunicate expectations to students, faculty, and evaluators:\nBefore you add users to your assessment site and at regular intervals thereafter, communicate with each audience (students, faculty who will be facilitating student submissions, evaluators, etc.) to let them know the purpose of the site and their role in supporting your unit's assessment strategy. Ideally, evaluators and faculty facilitators will already be aware of your assessment plans and have a clear understanding of what they need to do to ensure its success. Communications to students should explain when and how they are expected to interact with the assessment site. And of course, reminders are needed at specific points in your program. Student reminders can come from you or from faculty who have been tasked with facilitating the submission of specific artifacts produced in your curriculum.\nAdd the students and evaluators to the assessment site:\nYou'll need to maintain the roster for your assessment site manually. This will involve adding new students each term and adding/removing faculty evaluators as needed. Leave students who have graduated or left the university in the site if you want them to be included in reports as well as the standard and Learning Mastery gradebooks.\nTo add or move an individual user to a specific section, locate the user in the People tool, open the\nOptions\nmenu at the right, and choose\nEdit Sections\n.\nTo add users to a section in bulk, in the People tool, select\n+People\n. Enter or paste the list of email addresses or usernames into the text box, and then select the role and section for the group. This method can be used to add new users to a specific section in the site, or to add existing users to another section.\nSee the\nPeople\nsection of the Canvas Instructor Guide for instructions on other roster management features.\nTools for tracking and reporting\nTools for tracking and reporting on learning outcomes are available at the Canvas account level as well as in individual Canvas sites. Descriptions of these tools and links to documentation are provided below:\nLearning Mastery Gradebook (availability: course only)\nThe Learning Mastery Gradebook provides summary and detailed student performance data for the outcomes used in the course. Using the mastery calculation method defined for each outcome, the gradebook assigns a proficiency rating to each student using a customizable color-coded scale. As with the standard Canvas Gradebook, instructors have access to a grid view (which can be filtered by section) listing all students in the course as well as an individual student view. For more, see:\nHow do I use the Learning Mastery Gradebook to view outcome results in a course?\nHow do I view outcomes or student results individually in the Learning Mastery Gradebook?\nGrid view\nIndividual student view\nCanvas Gradebook (availability: course only)\nFor centrally managed assessment processes, the Canvas Gradebook shows the grade, score, or proficiency level earned by each student on each assignment or quiz in the assessment framework. The value in the gradebook represents the students' overall performance on each assessment task, whereas the Learning Mastery Gradebook gives performance information on individual outcomes. The gradebook can also be used to track evaluation progress since it indicated which assignments have been graded and which have not. For more, see the\nGrades section of the Canvas Instructor Guide\n.\nCanvas Gradebook with custom grading scales for proficiency levels, requirements, etc.\nOutcomes Report for an individual student (availability: course and account)\nThe Outcomes Report lists the outcomes that apply to the selected student along with the aligned artifacts, number of attempts, latest score, and average percentage score. Available at both the course and the account level, this report can be accessed by finding or selecting the user with the Canvas People tool and navigating to the User Details page. For more, see:\nHow do I view the outcomes results report for an individual student in an account?\nHow do I view the outcomes results report for an individual student in a course?\nOutcomes results for individual student\nOutcome Export report (availability: account only)\nThe Outcomes Export is a CSV file that lists the outcomes and outcomes groups in the account where the report is run. This report conforms to the\nOutcomes Import Format\nand is especially useful for editing large outcomes sets in Microsoft Excel and importing the changes back into Canvas.\nView a sample Outcomes Export report.\nTo generate the report:\nNavigate to your unit's account in Canvas.\nFrom the navigation menu, select\nSettings\nand then\nReports\nto open the "Reports" screen.\nLocate the report named "Outcomes Export" and select\nRun Report\n.\nIt may take some time for the report to be generated. Return to the "Reports" screen after a few minutes and look for a download link. Select the link to view and/or download the report.\nFor more, see\nHow do I view reports for an account?\nOutcome Results/Student Competency reports (availability: account only)\nThe Outcomes Results and Student Competency reports aggregate raw outcomes results data across all courses that use the outcomes published in the account. Whenever a student is assessed on an account-level outcome, a row with relevant information about the student, course, assignment, and outcome is added to the report. In addition to providing insights into student performance, the data in these reports can be used to generate a curriculum map showing which outcomes are emphasized and assessed in the courses that make up your program. Both the Outcomes Results and Student Competency reports are in CSV format and can be easily imported into Excel or statistical software for analysis.\nView a sample Outcomes Results report.\nView a sample Student Competency report.\nThe data elements available in each report are detailed below.\nData elements\nOutcomes Results\nStudent Competency\nstudent id\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nstudent sis id\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nassessment title\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nassessment id\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nassessment type\n✔\nYes\nNo\nsubmission date\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nsubmission score\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nlearning outcome name\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nlearning outcome id\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nattempt\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\noutcome score\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nassessment question\n✔\nYes\nNo\nassessment question id\n✔\nYes\nNo\ncourse name\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\ncourse id\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\ncourse sis id\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nsection name\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nsection id\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nsection sis id\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nassignment url\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nlearning outcome friendly name\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nlearning outcome points possible\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nlearning outcome mastery score\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nlearning outcome mastered\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nlearning outcome rating\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nlearning outcome rating points\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nlearning outcome group title\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nlearning outcome group id\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\naccount id\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\naccount name\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nenrollment state\n✔\nYes\n✔\nYes\nTo generate either of these reports:\nNavigate to your unit's account in Canvas.\nFrom the navigation menu, select\nSettings\nand then\nReports\nto open the "Reports" screen.\nLocate the report named "Outcomes Results" or "Student Competency", and select\nConfigure\n.\nSelect the desired term(s) and sort order, and then select\nRun Report\n.\nIt may take some time for the report to be generated. Return to the "Reports" screen after a few minutes and look for a download link. Select the link to view or download the report.\nFor more, see\nHow do I view reports for an account?	KB0023336	KB0023336 v5.0	 course sites, and may only need access to the <strong>Modules</strong> tool. To keep students focused on the tasks, if desired. <strong>Modules</strong>: Use <strong>Modules</strong> in place of Assignments and Quizzes so that students will have one, and surveys into <strong>Modules</strong>: Use the Canvas <strong>Modules</strong> tool to group the assignments in your site in a way	5.8899		2026-01-31 17:21:02.575264	2026-01-31 17:23:09.480072	0	1e3e1d7e47623298d500b87b716d4374
11980	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=135862ed3bd3d6902b40571864e45a5f	Connect to IU Login with the CAS protocol	Table of contents\nOverview\nCAS basics\nRequirements\nRecommendations\nIntegrate your service\nBefore you begin\nConnect your service\nHow CAS works at IU\nLog in\nValidate ticket\nLog out\nCAS URI extensions\n/login as credential requester and acceptor\n/serviceValidate\nOverview\nThe information in this document is intended for developers who want to integrate\nIU Login\ninto their web applications to provide\nsingle sign-on authentication\nusing the CAS protocol.\nCAS basics\nIU Login\nsupports the CAS 2.0 protocol; see\nCAS Protocol Specification\n.\nImportant:\nThe CAS software, which has been synonymous with authentication at IU, is distinct from the CAS protocol. Many existing applications have used the SAML protocol to integrate with the CAS software (via Shibboleth). If you have integrated with CAS in the past, do not assume that your application will need to connect with the CAS protocol. To learn more, see\nGuidelines for choosing a protocol\nin\nIntegrate IU Login with a web application\n.\nYou can access the official CAS clients at\nhttps://apereo.github.io/cas/6.6.x/integration/CAS-Clients.html\n.\nTo stay informed or provide feedback about\nIU Login\n, or to ask questions about developing with it, use the\nauth-discuss-l\nmailing list. To subscribe, email\nauth-discuss-l-subscribe@iu.edu\n.\nNote:\nIf you have comments or need help,\ncontact IMS\n.\nRequirements\nUITS\nrequires that you adhere to these practices when implementing CAS:\nApplications using CAS must operate entirely over TLS (that is, the service URL must be an HTTPS URL).\nUsers must be able to see\nhttps://idp.login.iu.edu/\nto verify that they are entering their IU\nUniversity account\ncredentials into IU Login, and not a phishing site. Therefore, applications must redirect to IU Login and\nnot\nrender the login page inside an iframe or use other similar techniques.\nImportant:\nThe infrastructure supporting connections to\nhttps://cas.iu.edu/\nwas retired on February 7, 2022. If you have applications still connected to the legacy infrastructure, begin the migration process immediately by completing the\nCAS Integration Request form\n.\nRecommendations\nUITS strongly encourages you to adhere to the following best practices when using CAS:\nEnabling HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) is highly recommended.\nAll of these requests take place through normal web transfers, either by use of redirects or through standard page requests. All communication with the CAS server is handled via\nSSL\n. Applications are strongly encouraged to use SSL on their web servers as well.\nApplications should maintain their own states with a user, so the login process with CAS should only happen once per session. After the initial login and validation, the application should not have to redirect the user back to CAS within the same session.\nCompiled modules are available. Make sure they meet your security needs before using them with your application.\nBe aware that the CAS ISAPI filter does not work on IIS 7.x, and will not be supported further.\nIntegrate your service\nBefore you begin\nYou'll need to understand your role as a Service Owner and the role of the Service Provider you will be sponsoring:\nService Owner:\nThe department and individuals within Indiana University that are sponsoring the CAS service integration being requested. Service Owners are responsible for facilitating all communications and updates from the Service Provider to the Identity Management Systems (IMS) team. Every Service Provider must have a sponsoring department and contacts.\nService Provider:\nThe organization that is providing a service to users who have identities established at IU. This organization can be inside or outside IU, but it must have an IU sponsor.\nUITS\nstrongly recommends\nintegrating your test systems with the pre-production\nIU Login\nenvironment. This environment is available to verify changes to the\nIU Login\ninfrastructure prior to release.\nConnect your service\nAfter familiarizing yourself with the roles above, follow the steps below to connect your service to IU Login 2.0:\nWorking with the Service Provider, complete the\nCAS Integration Request form\n.\nWhen you are notified that the integration has been set up, update your application to connect to IU Login 2.0. For details, see the example below.\nHow CAS works at IU\nNote:\nTo point your application to the pre-production environment, change the configured CAS URL to\nhttps://idp-stg.login.iu.edu/idp/profile\n. To verify that your application is pointing to the pre-production environment after the integration has been set up, log in. You will be directed to the\nhttps://idp-stg.login.iu.edu\nlogin page, rather than the production URL\nhttps://idp.login.iu.edu\n.\nUITS staff and local UITS support people:\nWhen using localhost, ensure that your service URL includes\nhttps://\n.\nAt IU, CAS authentication works as follows:\nYour web application redirects users to:\nhttps://idp.login.iu.edu/idp/profile/cas/login?service=https://yoursite.iu.edu\nAbove,\nservice\nis the URL of your service.\nFor details, see\nLog in\nbelow.\nUsers log in at\nidp.login.iu.edu\n, and are redirected to your web application at\nhttps://yoursite.iu.edu\nas follows:\nhttps://yoursite.iu.edu?ticket=ST-1234LSDFJMVAKFHK234HVNLJ2FM\nYour web application (\nyoursite.iu.edu\n) validates with CAS by sending a request to:\nhttps://idp.login.iu.edu/idp/profile/cas/serviceValidate?ticket= ST-1234LSDFJMVAKFHK234HVNLJ2FM&service=https://yoursite.iu.edu\nIf the validation is successful, CAS sends back a response with the "\nusername\n". If validation fails, CAS responds with an error message code.\nFor details, see\nValidate ticket\nbelow.\nWhen the user has finished with the web application, you may choose to log the user out of CAS. For details, see\nLogging out\nbelow.\nLog in\nThe login process begins when a user first requests a page from your CAS-integrated application. Your site will need to redirect the user to the\nIU Login\npage. You will need to pass the return URL, normally\nhttps://yoursite.iu.edu\n, so that\nIU Login\nwill know where to redirect users upon successful login. A properly formatted redirect URL using the above information would look like:\nhttps://idp.login.iu.edu/idp/profile/cas/login?service=https://yoursite.iu.edu\nIU Login\nmaintains its own state with each user, and can determine if the user has a logged-in session. If the user has not authenticated yet,\nIU Login\nrequires a valid username and passphrase combination before the user can proceed. If the user has already logged in, the process continues silently.\nThe following is some quasi-code to help show the process of logging in if the user is not authenticated:\nif (!$authenticated) { $_SESSION['CAS']= true;\nheader("Location:https://https://idp.login.iu.edu/idp/profile/cas/login?service=https://pathForYourApplication");\nIn either case, once the user has authenticated,\nIU Login\nredirects the user back to the original application specified by the\nservice\nvalue, generating and transmitting a unique string called a "CAS ticket"; a CAS ticket may look like this example:\nhttps://yoursite.iu.edu?ticket=ST-1234LSDFJMVAKFHK234HVNLJ2FMST\nNote:\nYour service URL should be URL encoded. Replace any non-ASCII characters with a percent sign (\n%\n) followed by the appropriate hexadecimal digits (for example, replace a space in your URL with\n%20\n). For a complete list of ASCII character codes, see\nASCII Code - The extended ASCII table\n. For more about URL encoding, see\nHTML URL Encoding Reference\nfrom w3schools.com.\nValidate ticket\nOnce CAS redirects the user back to your site, the validation phase begins. Your application must now take the newly provided CAS ticket and send a request to CAS to check if the ticket is valid. The validation request must be in the following format (using the service URL in the format of\nhttps://yoursite.iu.edu\n):\nhttps://idp.login.iu.edu/idp/profile/cas/serviceValidate?ticket=ST-1234LSDFJMVAKFHK234HVNLJ2FM&service=https://yoursite.iu.edu\nThis snippet of code provides instruction to validate the user, if authenticated, by getting the user's CAS ticket and seeing if it's good:\nif ($authenticated) {\n//validate since authenticated\nif (isset($_GET["ticket"])) {\nThe\nservice\nparameter passed here to the validation service must match the values passed in the login request.\nTo see a full example of simple validation code, see\n/serviceValidate\n.\nIf validation is successful, CAS sends back an XML response that contains the "\nusername\n".\nNote:\nThe ticket must be validated only once, and within two seconds.\nTLS 1.2 or higher is required for\nIU Login\n.\nLog out\nYou can provide a logout with the following URL:\nhttps://idp.login.iu.edu/idp/profile/cas/logout\nHowever, this is not reliable; the only secure way to log out from CAS is to close the browser. If secure logout is an issue with your service, direct your users to close their browsers when they have finished.\nCAS URI extensions\n/login as credential requester and acceptor\nThis HTTP request parameter may be passed to /login while it is acting as a credential requester. It is case sensitive, and\nmust\nbe handled by /login.\nservice\n(optional): The identifier of the application the client is trying to access. In almost all cases, this will be the URL of the application. As an HTTP request parameter, this URL value\nmust\nbe URL-encoded.\nSimple login example:\nhttps://idp.login.iu.edu/idp/profile/cas/login?service=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.example.org%2Fservice\nFor more about the /login URI, see\n/login as credential requester\n.\n/serviceValidate\n/serviceValidate checks the validity of a service ticket and returns an XML-fragment response. /serviceValidate\nmust\nalso generate and issue proxy-granting tickets when requested. /serviceValidate\nmust not\nreturn a successful authentication if it receives a proxy ticket. UITS\nrecommends\nthat if /serviceValidate receives a proxy ticket, the error message in the XML response\nshould\nexplain that validation failed because a proxy ticket was passed to /serviceValidate.\nThe following HTTP request parameters\nmay\nbe specified to /serviceValidate. They are case sensitive and\nmust\nall be handled by /serviceValidate.\nservice\n(required): The identifier of the service for which the ticket was issued, as discussed in\nsection 2.5.1\nof CAS Protocol 2.0 Specification. As an HTTP request parameter, the "service" value\nmust\nbe URL-encoded.\nticket\n(required): The service ticket issued by /login\npgtUrl\n(optional): The URL of the proxy callback. As an HTTP request parameter, the "pgtUrl" value\nmust\nbe URL-encoded.\nrenew\n(optional): If this parameter is set, ticket validation will only succeed if the service ticket was issued from the presentation of the user's primary credentials. It will fail if the ticket was issued from a single sign-on session.\nOn ticket validation success:\n<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>\n<cas:serviceResponse xmlns:cas="http://www.yale.edu/tp/cas">\n<cas:authenticationSuccess>\n<cas:user>username</cas:user>\n</cas:authenticationSuccess>\n</cas:serviceResponse>\nOne example of ticket validation failures:\n<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>\n<cas:serviceResponse xmlns:cas="http://www.yale.edu/tp/cas">\n<cas:authenticationFailure code='INVALID_TICKET'>\nE_TICKET_EXPIRED\n</cas:authenticationFailure>\n</cas:serviceResponse>\nSimple validation example:\nhttps://idp.login.iu.edu/idp/profile/cas/serviceValidate?service=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.example.org%2Fservice&ticket=ST-1234LSDFJMVAKFHK234HVNLJ2FM\nFor more about the /serviceValidate URI, see\n/serviceValidate [CAS 2.0]\n.	KB0024519	KB0024519 v2.0	 protocol; see CAS Protocol Specification. Important: The CAS <strong>software</strong>, which has been synonymous protocol to integrate with the CAS <strong>software</strong> (via Shibboleth). If you have integrated with CAS <strong>modules</strong> are available. Make sure they meet your security needs before using them with your application	4.0265		2026-01-31 17:23:21.181262	2026-01-31 17:23:21.181262	0	135862ed3bd3d6902b40571864e45a5f
11982	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=cc448cf947771a502fc6a55b416d4323	IU Common Build Windows servicing feature updates	Table of contents\nOverview\nDeployment phases\nUpgrade compatibility blocks\nDeployment and installation method\nNotes and troubleshooting\nOverview\nWindows servicing feature updates are critical to maintaining current support for Windows operating systems. The latest Windows servicing update will be configured as a required deployed to Common Build devices in 4 phases over the course of 12 months, the phases are outlined below.\nThe Windows servicing updates deployment process is automatic and perpetual with the yearly Windows servicing feature update releases being automatically deployed after being released by Microsoft.\nFor more on Windows as a service and Windows servicing feature updates, see\nOverview of Windows as a service\n.\nDeployment phases\nPhases are based on the Common Build departmental OU's three-letter or four-letter department code, excluding the campus designation. Progression to each subsequent phase is based primarily upon the success rate and duration of the deployment to the current phase. The phase configuration is automated; it is not possible to move units to a different phase.\nInitial "Available" deployment:\nDeployed as "Available" in the Software Center Updates tab\nThe update will be deployed as "Available" to all Common Build devices within three months of the latest Windows operating system release.\nThis will allow early update installation if desired.\nPhase 1:\nEMS and early adopters\nPhase 1 will begin no later than three months after release.\nEarly adopters can be individual devices by name or an entire departmental OU. Contact EMS to be included as an early adopter.\nPhase 2:\nDepartments whose department code begins with letters A-H\nPhase 2 will begin no later than four months after release.\nPhase 3:\nDepartments whose department code begins with letters I-P\nPhase 3 will begin no later than seven months after release.\nPhase 4:\nDepartments whose department code begins with letters Q-Z\nPhase 4 will begin no later than 10 months after release.\nUpgrade compatibility blocks\nDevices with an application compatibility block preventing upgrade to the latest Windows operating system must be rebuilt with Windows LTSC. Due to Windows operating systems reaching end-of-life and losing support, it is no longer possible to create long-term exceptions for devices with an upgrade compatibility block. Contact EMS for assistance rebuilding devices with Windows LTSC.\nDeployment and installation method\nWindows servicing feature updates are installed using the default built-in Windows update installation mechanism. Windows servicing feature updates are the same as regular Windows updates and use the same update mechanisms in SCCM and in Windows as do normal Windows updates and will obey the same reboot maintenance windows. The result is a more streamlined and reliable update process. Servicing updates are more readily available off campus and, unlike task sequences, are not reliant upon on-premises resources for the installation to succeed. If on-premises resources are not readily available, the servicing update can be configured to reach out to the Microsoft CDN to download the update content.\nNotes and troubleshooting\nServicing updates will deploy with a 14-day installation deadline. When the deadline arrives, the update will automatically install, and any required reboots are scheduled for the next available maintenance window. For more information about Common Build maintenance windows, see\nGet started with the IU Common Build fo Windows\n.\nServicing updates will appear in the Software Center under the\nUpdates\ntab. If the device is not compatible with the servicing update, the update will not appear in the Software Center.\nEMS is not currently offering a task sequence option for Windows upgrades.\nWindows servicing feature update failures should use the same troubleshooting steps as would be used for any normal Windows update and/or upgrade failure. Unlike with a task sequence, once the servicing update begins to install, SCCM does not impact or interact with the servicing update installation except to report the installation success or failure and reason code in the Software Center.\nSome common reasons for failure include:\nThe device is pending a reboot.\nThe device has another update pending install.\nAnother application is trying to install.\nThe device does not have the latest Windows monthly cumulative update installed. This can cause the servicing update to fail, especially if the latest monthly cumulative update includes a servicing stack update.\nWindows update log; contains process and installation information about Windows feature updates\nWindows Update Log Files\nGenerating WindowsUpdate.log\nfile PowerShell using\nGet-WindowsUpdateLog (WindowsUpdate)\nFeature update log locations:\nC:\\Windows\\Panther\nC:\\Windows\\Logs\\MoSetup\nC:\\Windows\\Logs\\CBS\nC:\\Windows\\inf\nC:\\$Windows.~BT\nC:\\$Windows.~WS\nFeature update logs:\nSetupact.log\nSetuperr.log\nPreGatherPnPList.log\nBlueBox.log\nHelpful links:\nTroubleshoot Windows 11 upgrade and Installation errors\nResolve Windows upgrade errors: Technical information for IT Pros\nWindows upgrade log files\nQuick Fixes\nVerify disk space\nWindows Upgrade Troubleshooting Logs (Non-Microsoft Site)\nTroubleshooting upgrade errors\nResolution Procedures	KB0023890	KB0023890 v4.0	 to a different phase. Initial "Available" deployment: Deployed as "Available" in the <strong>Software</strong> Center Updates Windows. Servicing updates will appear in the <strong>Software</strong> Center under the Updates tab. If the device is not compatible with the servicing update, the update will not appear in the <strong>Software</strong> Center. EMS	3.8222	km-aud-uits, km-aud-local-support, km-aud-ems-admins	2026-01-31 17:23:24.62278	2026-01-31 17:23:24.62278	0	cc448cf947771a502fc6a55b416d4323
11872	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e120b8ff476456583d373803846d4336	About data encryption	Table of contents\nOverview\nEncryption at rest\nEncryption in transit\nSymmetric key algorithms\nAsymmetric key algorithms\nOverview\nEncryption is the process of transforming information (referred to as plaintext) using an algorithm (called a cipher) to make it unreadable to anyone except those possessing special knowledge, usually referred to as a key.\nData can be encrypted in two ways: at rest and in transit.\nNote:\nEmploying these two types of encryption safeguards must occur in tandem; it's not automatic. Data encrypted at rest is not guaranteed to remain encrypted as it traverses a network. Conversely, the fact that data is encrypted in transit does not offer any guarantee that the data remains encrypted after it has reached its destination.\nEncryption at rest\nEncryption at rest refers to the encryption of data storage, whether in a database, on a disk, or on some other form of media.\nNote:\nIndiana law recognizes the value of disk encryption, such that a lost/stolen laptop or storage media is not considered a breach if that media was encrypted (and the encryption key was not available with the device).\nExamples of encryption at rest include:\nBitLocker disk encryption\nmacOS FileVault\nDatabase encryption\nMySQL\nPostgreSQL\nVeraCrypt\nEncryption in transit\nEncryption in transit refers to data that is encrypted as it traverses a network, including via web applications, smartphone apps, chats, and so on. Data is in transit from the point at which the data leaves the storage drive or database until it's re-saved or delivered to its destination. Protecting information in transit essentially ensures protection from others attempting to snoop or eavesdrop on information as it traverses the network.\nExamples of encryption in transit include:\nTLS/SSL\n(HTTPS)\nWPA2\nVPN\nIPsec\nSymmetric key algorithms\nSymmetric key algorithms use related, often identical keys to both encrypt and then decrypt information. In practice, this is known mostly as a shared secret between two or more parties.\nAsymmetric key algorithms\nAsymmetric key algorithms use different keys to encrypt and decrypt information; one key encrypts (or locks) while the other decrypts (or unlocks). In practice, this is known mostly as a public/private key; the public key can be shared openly, but the private key should not be. In most cryptographic systems, it is extremely difficult to determine the private key values based on the public key.\nUsing public/private keys, the lock/unlock algorithm can work in two ways. For example, Alice can encrypt a message with Bob's public key, and then send it to Bob. Only the holder of Bob's private key should be able to decrypt and read the message. Conversely, Alice could digitally sign a message with her own private key, and while anyone else in the world could read the message, they could use Alice's public key to verify the message must have come from Alice.\nCommon technologies that rely on public key cryptography include\nTLS/SSL\nand S/MIME.\nFor more, see\nPublic-key cryptography\n.	KB0025390	KB0025390 v1.0	 to the encryption of data <strong>storage</strong>, whether in a database, on a disk, or on some other form of media. Note: Indiana law recognizes the value of disk encryption, such that a lost/stolen laptop or <strong>storage</strong>, chats, and so on. Data is in transit from the point at which the data leaves the <strong>storage</strong> drive	6.1035		2026-01-31 17:21:34.746383	2026-01-31 17:21:34.746383	0	e120b8ff476456583d373803846d4336
11987	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5134af8c4730be102fc6a55b416d4352	About the GenAI 101 course in Canvas	The GenAI 101 course in\nCanvas\nfor IU students, faculty, and staff is a beginner-friendly introduction to the fundamentals of\ngenerative AI tools\n. Through hands-on examples and real-world use cases, participants learn what generative AI is, how it works, and how to use it responsibly and effectively in academic, professional, and creative contexts. No prior experience with AI is required. The course also features Crimson Jr., an AI assistant that can answer questions about topics covered in the course.\nAll students are automatically enrolled in the GenAI 101 course at no charge. Faculty, staff, and anyone else with an active\nUniversity account\nwho is not already enrolled can go to\nIU Expand\nto enroll at no charge. No special software is required to complete the course; all you need is an internet-connected computer.\nNote:\nAlthough some aspects of the course can be viewed from a mobile device, UITS recommends that you use a computer and web browser to access the course.\nYou cannot complete the course at the experience level needed using the Canvas mobile app; however, you can use a tablet device with a mobile browser.\nThe GenAI 101 course is not for credit and is not part of employee compliance training. Participants are not graded, but you can earn badges for AI Interaction and Prompting, AI Content and Verification, AI for Problem Solving, and AI for Communication and Creativity. You can share your badges on LinkedIn and include the certificate of completion on your resume. Although you are not graded, you will have the opportunity to answer questions during videos, test the skills you have learned, and reflect on what you have learned at the end of each module. There is no penalty for not completing the course, but you will miss out on information that could prove helpful in your academic coursework and/or professional development.\nNote:\nAlumni access:\nA special "Alumni and Friends" version of the GenAI 101 course is available to alumni with a\nMy IU account\n. Eligible alumni and friends will receive email with a link and instructions for accessing the course. For help creating a My IU account, see the\nMy IU help page\n. If you have issues creating or logging in with a My IU account, email\narlo@iu.edu\nor call 317-278-2756.\nIf you have issues with either version of the GenAI 101 course,\ncontact the UITS Support Center\n.	KB0026791	KB0026791 v4.0	 to enroll at no charge. No special <strong>software</strong> is required to complete the course; all you need is an	2.9869		2026-01-31 17:23:28.02158	2026-01-31 17:23:28.02158	0	5134af8c4730be102fc6a55b416d4352
11993	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=5c00bcfb476456583d373803846d4302	Install Windows 11 Education or Windows 10 Education Edition at IU	Before proceeding, refer to:\nInstall Windows at IU\nDepartmental Windows installations and the IU/Microsoft Campus Agreement\nWarning:\nFollowing the installation instructions below, specifically step 9, will involve completely wiping any existing data on your hard drive. Make sure you've backed up important files and have the installation disks for your software before you proceed past that point.\nNote:\nAny computer purchased since 2016 should be capable of installing\nWindows 11\n(as long as the computer has a TPM 2.0 chip) or\nWindows 10\n(TPM not required). If you to run into issues, check if your computer meets the minimum requirements:\nWindows 11 specifications\nWindows 10 specifications\nA TPM module is required to install\nWindows 11\n. A TPM module is compatible with but not required for\nWindows 10\n. For more about TPM, see\nTrusted Platform Module Technology Overview\n.\nThese instructions will guide you through installing the\nEducation Editions of Windows 11 and Windows 10\n:\nTo create a flash drive you can use for installation, follow the instructions in\nCreate a bootable flash drive for installing Windows\n.\nInsert the Windows DVD/USB into your computer.\nRestart your computer. You should see a message that says, "Press any key to boot from CD or DVD... ". If you see this message, press any key. If Windows starts normally instead, refer to\nBoot from something other than the hard drive in Windows\n.\nAfter Windows has finished loading files, you will see the installer screen. Choose your language, time and currency format, and keyboard or input method.\nSelect\nNext\n, and then\nInstall now\n.\nEnter the product key for Windows if prompted, and select\nNext\n. For instructions on getting the product key, see\nDownload Windows from Microsoft's Azure Education Portal\n.\nNote:\nYou are not required to enter the product key now, but if you don't, you'll have to select what version of Windows you are installing in a subsequent step. Make sure to choose\nWindows 11 Education\nor\nWindows 10 Education\n. You will still have to activate Windows after installation.\nRead the license agreement. To agree, check the box and select\nNext\n.\nChoose the\nCustom: Install Windows only (Advanced)\ninstallation option.\nYou will see a list of the partitions defined on your hard drive(s). If your computer has more than one physical hard drive, the drives will be displayed as separate disks (for example, Disk 0, Disk 1).\nNote:\nFollow these instructions only for the disk that contains your existing Windows installation, leaving any others alone. If you are unsure which disk contains your Windows installation, ask for assistance before continuing. Do not format all of the partitions in this menu, but only the partition for Windows.\nWith the desired partition selected, choose\nDelete\n. This will prompt you to erase everything on the partition. Select\nYes\n.\nAfter formatting, select the newly created\nUnallocated Space\nand select\nNext\n. The Windows installation will begin.\nAt this point, the process will be automatic for some time. Ensure that the computer is plugged into a power source while you wait.\nYour computer will restart after installation and begin configuring devices and settings. It may restart several times.\nWhen prompted, confirm your previous region and keyboard layout selections. You can add additional keyboard layouts if you wish.\nIf your computer does not have an active internet connection, you'll be presented with a page offering to connect you to a network. This is not required, but if you are able to connect to a network (either wired or wireless), do so.\nSelect\nNext\nand let the installation process continue.\nOn the "Choose how you'll connect" screen, choose\nJoin a\ndomain\n(or\nContinue with limited setup\n) and select\nContinue\n.\nNote:\nLimited setup simply means you are not using a Microsoft account to log into a machine; no features are affected.\nEnter a name and a password for your local user account.\nDo not use your IU passphrase.\nWhen prompted, set up three security questions to assist with recovering your password should you forget it.\nOn the next screens, you'll be able to customize various Windows functions. You can always enable/disable these features after installation, so the choices you make here are not final. You may be asked about:\nPersonal Assistant settings:\nCarefully read the description of the feature, and determine whether you are willing to share the information indicated with Microsoft in exchange for this feature. If so, select\nAccept\n; if not, select\nDecline\n.\nActivity history:\nSelect\nLearn More\nand read the description of the feature. Determine if you're willing to share that sort of information with Microsoft in exchange for this feature, and then select\nNo\nor\nYes\n, accordingly.\nPrivacy settings\nRead the description of each feature, and determine if you're willing to share the indicated information with Microsoft in exchange for the features. If you wish, select\nLearn More\nto get a more detailed description of each feature. Turn off any you don't want, and then select\nAccept\n.\nThe process will continue automatically until you have booted into Windows and are viewing the desktop.\nOnce you have booted into Windows, be sure to adjust the time zone by right-clicking the clock in the system tray. Select\nAdjust date/time\n, and change the time zone to your local time zone.\nTo increase security and performance, follow the steps in\nWhat you should do immediately after installing Windows\n.\nFor help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0024581	KB0024581 v1.0	 important files and have the installation disks for your <strong>software</strong> before you proceed past that point	2.9766		2026-01-31 17:24:06.051396	2026-01-31 17:24:06.051396	0	5c00bcfb476456583d373803846d4302
12001	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=4ca8962e4760b6582fc6a55b416d43f5	About the VoiceThread licensing change	Table of contents\nOverview\nExport VoiceThread content\nAlternatives to VoiceThread\nAlternatives for creating multimedia presentations\nAlternatives to "watch" assignments\nAlternatives to "commenting" assignments\nAlternatives to "create" assignments\nCommunications log\nOverview\nIndiana University's enterprise license for VoiceThread, a multimedia presentation and discussion app that plugs into Canvas, has changed as of January 1, 2025. As part of a regular review of actual tool usage, Teaching and Learning Technologies in UITS determined that the level of use did not justify maintaining a university-wide site license.\nInstructors who wish to continue to use VoiceThread in their Canvas courses can order it as a digital learning tool under the\nIU eTexts program\n. Under this program, the heavily discounted licensing costs are charged directly to students as part of regular bursar billing processes. To learn more about IU eTexts, see the\nIU eTexts: A Faculty and Staff Introduction\nCanvas resource site.\nBeginning January 1, 2025:\nThe VoiceThread tool will be available only in Canvas courses in which VoiceThread has been licensed through the IU eText program.\nFaculty and students who aren't enrolled in a course with VoiceThread will be able to access their accounts at\nhttps://voicethread.com/\n. For more, see\nAccess VoiceThread outside of Canvas\n.\nThe rest of this article is intended for instructors who aren't planning to use VoiceThread through IU eTexts.\nExport VoiceThread content\nYou don't need to export your content unless you want a video output of your threads to use in a different platform. Otherwise, all of your content is available in VoiceThread format to view and share from within your account.  You can export individual threads (with or without comments and annotations) and download them to a local hard drive or a cloud storage account. Exported threads are saved as\n.mov\nfiles, a common video format compatible with\nKaltura\n.\nTo export an entire VoiceThread, including all audio, video, text comments, and annotations, see the VoiceThread support article,\nHow to export\n. Then download the\n.mov\nfile as described in\nDownloading an exported VoiceThread\n.\nTo export a VoiceThread without the comments and annotations or with only your own comments, first create a copy and select the desired setting for copying comments as described in\nCopying a VoiceThread\n. Then follow the instructions above to export and download the copied VoiceThread.\nNotes:\nStudents with free accounts will be charged $2.99 for each export.\nShort VoiceThreads take just a few minutes to export, but longer threads can take over an hour. You’ll be notified by email when the export is complete. To view the status of any export, see\nViewing the status of an export\n.\nExported VoiceThreads will be available to download for 90 days.\nModerated (hidden) comments are never included in exports.\nBy default, anyone with whom you have shared a VoiceThread can export it. If you do not want others to export your threads,\nedit the settings\nfor each VoiceThread.\nAlternatives to VoiceThread\nNo other tool available at Indiana University offers the unique combination of presentation and interaction capabilities afforded by VoiceThread. If your courses rely heavily on VoiceThread to facilitate graded or ungraded activities in your course, your best option is ordering VoiceThread through IU eTexts.\nIf you don’t use VoiceThread intensively enough to justify the additional cost to your students, you can combine other supported technologies to acheive a similar result. Your technology choices will depend on how you use VoiceThread in your course. Below are some of the common ways in which VoiceThread is used and alternative technologies that can achieve a similar result. If you need advice on which options would work best for your classes, contact your\ncampus teaching and learning center\n.\nAlternatives for creating multimedia presentations\nThe focus and centerpiece of all discussion in VoiceThread is a multimedia presentation consisting of any combination of slides, photos, video and audio clips, and documents. At IU, there are many options for creating presentations that can easily be shared for comment and discussion or submitted for grading. The table below outlines the key differences among IU's most popular and accessible presentation tools.\nComparison between presentation tools\nTool\nMS PowerPoint\nKaltura Personal Capture\nZoom Recordings\nBest for\nNarrated presentations with static slides and/or slides with embedded media\nNarrated presentations of content that can't easily be placed on slides (website tours, software demos, media critiques, etc.)\nNarrated presentations of content that can't easily be placed on slides (website tours, software demos, media critiques, etc.)\nSupported media\nSlides with embedded audio, video, still images, charts, animations, etc.\nAnything that can be displayed on a computer screen.\nAnything that can be displayed on a computer screen.\nNarration recording options\nAudio or video\nAudio or video\nAudio or video\nEasily editable\nYes\nNo\nNo\nAnnotate while recording\nYes\nYes\nYes\nSpeaker notes\nYes\nNo\nNo\nCollaborative authoring\nYes\nNo\nYes\nSave or export to\n.mp4\nvideo\nYes\nYes\nYes\nSharing method in Canvas\nExport to\nmp4\n; then, share via Embed Kaltura Media or Kaltura Media Gallery\nShare via Embed Kaltura Media or Kaltura Media Gallery\nShare via Embed Kaltura Media or Kaltura Media Gallery\nDocumentation\nRecording an individual or group presentation with PowerPoint 365 or 2019\nRecord a slide show with narration and slide timings\nInstall and use IU's Kaltura Personal Capture\nCreate a recording\nGetting Started with Zoom Meetings\nAccess and share Zoom recordings in Kaltura\nAlternatives to "watch" assignments\nIn VoiceThread, a\nwatch assignment\nrequires the student to watch or listen to a presentation without making comments. The following technologies can facilitate a similar workflow:\nPlayPosit\n:\nCreate a self-paced PlayPosit bulb in Canvas\nwithout any interactions. If the bulb is graded, students will receive full points upon viewing the complete presentation. If the bulb is ungraded, use the bulb analytics to track completion.\nNote:\nOn December 29, 2025, PlayPosit will change its name to WeVideo.  For details, see\nAbout the change from PlayPosit to WeVideo\n.\nKaltura Media Gallery Analytics\n:\nUse the\nEmbed Kaltura Media\ntool in the Canvas rich content editor to add the presentation to a Canvas assignment, page, or module. To track which students have watched the presentation, open the video in Kaltura Media Gallery. Then open the\nActions\nmenu, select\nAnalytics\n, and choose\nView Engagement Per User\n.\nAlternatives to "commenting" assignments\nIn VoiceThread, a\ncommenting assignment\ninvites or requires students to comment on one or more slides in the presentation. Comments can be "private" (only viewable by the author and the instructor) or "public" to allow students to discuss the material with each other. The following technologies can facilitate a similar workflow:\nFor private "commenting" assignments\nCanvas Assignments:\nCreate an "online submission" assignment. Use the\nEmbed Kaltura Media\ntool to embed your presentation into the assignment instructions. Below the embedded video, add your assignment prompt.\nKaltura Interactive Video Quizzes\n:\nCreate an "external tool" assignment and select the\nKaltura Quiz\napp. Then, in the modal dialog, open the\nAdd New\nmenu and select\nVideo Quiz\n. Select or upload the video for your presentation and add one or more open-ended questions to the quiz. You can also include one or more autograded questions if desired.\nPlayPosit\n:\nCreate a graded or ungraded PlayPosit bulb for your presentation with one or more "free response" interactions for students to complete. You can also add a variety of autograded interactions if desired.\nNote:\nOn December 29, 2025, PlayPosit will change its name to WeVideo.  For details, see\nAbout the change from PlayPosit to WeVideo\n.\nFor "public" commenting assignments\nCanvas Discussions:\nUse the\nEmbed Kaltura Media\ntool to embed your presentation in the topic introduction. Below the embedded video, add your discussion prompt.\nPlayPosit\n:\nCreate a PlayPosit bulb for your presentation with one or more "discussion forum" interactions. Student replies will include timestamps linking them to the locations in the video where the replies were created.\nNote:\nOn December 29, 2025, PlayPosit will change its name to WeVideo.  For details, see\nAbout the change from PlayPosit to WeVideo\n.\nAlternatives to "create" assignments\nIn VoiceThread, a\ncreate assignment\nrequires the student to create a presentation and share it privately with the instructor or publicly so that the entire class can view and comment on it. The following technologies can facilitate a similar workflow:\nCanvas Assignments (for private sharing):\nCreate a\nvideo submission assignment\nin Canvas, explaining the type of presentation you'd like students to create. In your assignment instructions, be sure to include a link with instructions for students on\nhow to use the Embed Kaltura Media tool\n.\nCanvas Discussions (for public sharing and comment):\nCreate a discussion topic with a prompt that explains the type of presentation you'd like students to create and share. Instruct students to post their presentations by embedding them in their initial post to the topic. Be sure to include a link with instructions for students on\nhow to use the Embed Kaltura Media tool\n, as well as your expectations regarding commenting on each other's work.\nCommunications log\nOctober 2, 2024:\n"About the VoiceThread retirement" (this document) published in the IU Knowledge Base; boilerplate retirement notifications added to related documents in the KB\nOctober 3, 2024:\nDirect email notification sent to Canvas instructors with VoiceThread enabled in one or more of their courses\nOctober 3, 2024:\nRetirement notification added to\nVoiceThread entry\nin IU's\nTechnology Tool Finder\nOctober 9, 2024:\nRetirement article in the\nUITS Monitor\nOctober 24, 2024:\nDirect email notification sent to Canvas instructors with VoiceThread enabled in one or more of their courses announcing the availability of VoiceThread through the IU eTexts program.\nOctober 25, 2024:\n"About the VoiceThread retirement" (this document) and VoiceThread retirement boilerplate updated with information about ordering VoiceThread through the IU eTexts program.\nOctober 30, 2024:\nCanvas message with VoiceThread ordering information sent to members of the Canvas site\nIU eTexts: A Faculty and Staff Introduction\nOctober 30, 2024:\nMessage with VoiceThread ordering information sent to the IU teaching centers mailing list\nNovember 19, 2024:\nDirect email notification sent to Canvas instructors with VoiceThread enabled in one or more of their courses about VoiceThread ordering deadline.  Canvas message about ordering deadline sent to members of the Canvas site\nIU eTexts: A Faculty and Staff Introduction\nNovember 22, 2024:\n"About the VoiceThread retirement" (this document) and VoiceThread retirement notification in other documents updated with information about free VoiceThread accounts.	KB0026402	KB0026402 v16.0	 on slides (website tours, <strong>software</strong> demos, media critiques, etc.) Narrated presentations of content that can't easily be placed on slides (website tours, <strong>software</strong> demos, media critiques, etc	2.3311		2026-01-31 17:24:15.037802	2026-01-31 17:24:15.037802	0	4ca8962e4760b6582fc6a55b416d43f5
3020	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=0488fbe747ae3250d500b87b716d43ba	IT help options	Search the Knowledge Base\nOnline help available anytime\nCampus support centers\nTechnical support via phone, chat, email, and in person\nHelp in the Student Technology Centers\nConsultant availability in the campus STCs and RTCs at IU Bloomington and IU Indianapolis (follow the link and select\nContinue as Guest\n)\nContact Centers\nFaculty and staff telephone numbers, dialing instructions, building addresses and code explanations, after-hours telephone and cable repair, and more\nAskIU\nGet answers to your IU questions. For general information for all IU campuses,\nsend email\n.\nAssistive technology services at IU\nFull suite of services for students with disabilities\nIT Community Partnerships\nOperational leadership for developing, strengthening, and nurturing partnerships and collaboration among UITS and the schools and service units\nResearch computing help\nResearch computing support at IU is provided by the\nResearch Technologies\ndivision of\nUITS\n. To ask a question or get help regarding\nResearch Technologies\nservices, including IU's\nresearch supercomputers\nand research storage systems, and the scientific,  statistical, and mathematical applications available on those systems,\ncontact UITS\nResearch Technologies\n. For service-specific support contact information, see\nResearch computing support at IU\n.\nNote:\nComputing support resources may also be available through your school or department.	KB0022784	KB0022784 v3.0	 and the schools and service units Research <strong>computing</strong> help Note: <strong>Computing</strong> support resources may	39.9911		2026-01-31 14:38:45.585514	2026-01-31 17:06:09.816612	0	0488fbe747ae3250d500b87b716d43ba
12018	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=655213f33b462218476d941864e45aed	Guide to math formats, converters, and accessible speech	Table of contents\nOverview\nMath formats\nLaTeX\nAsciiMath\nMathML\nConverters\nMathJax\nSpeech Rule Engine (SRE)\nMathCAT\nMath speech grammars (styles)\nClearSpeak\nMathSpeak™\nSimpleSpeak\nChromeVox\nSpeech style comparison\nBraille codes\nNemeth Braille Code for Mathematics and Science Notation\nUnified English Braille (UEB)\nEuro Braille\nAdditional tools\nMathpix Snipping Tool\nOverview\nA variety of resources are available to help you understand the features of and libraries used by the\nmath converter tool\n. This document includes an overview of math formats, grammar rules (speech styles) for verbalizing math, format converters, and rendering tools.\nMath formats\nLaTeX\nLaTeX is a high-quality typesetting system commonly used for producing scientific and technical documents. Because of its exceptional and well-known support for mathematical notation and complex formulas, its math syntax is widely used in other contexts, such as web pages, markdown documents, and equation editors.\nFormat:\nExpressions start and end with delimiters such as\n$…$\n,\n\\(…\\)\n,\n$$…$$\n, and\n\\[…\\]\n. For example, the Canvas LMS uses\n\\(…\\)\nfor inline expressions and\n$$…$$\nfor displayed (block) expressions.\nExpressions then consist of math terms, operators, and commands, starting with backslashes and using braces for grouping; for example,\n\\[\\frac{a}{b+c}\\]\n.\nAdvantages:\nFamiliar to academics and authors, it's easier to author by hand than MathML. It's supported by MathJax, KaTeX, many markdown processors, equation editors, and to some extent by Microsoft Word.\nAccessibility notes:\nLaTeX needs to be rendered to MathML for full screen reader support. It's not inherently accessible on its own. Raw LaTeX is typically not meaningfully interpreted by assistive technologies unless first converted into MathML.\nDocumentation:\nOverleaf is an online collaborative writing and publishing platform designed for researchers, students, and professionals.\nThe Mathematical Expression section\noffers a variety of math expressions, templates and examples that are translated into LaTeX formatting.\nAsciiMath\nAsciiMath is a more human-readable alternative to LaTeX and was designed to be easier to type quickly.\nFormat:\nSome systems (for example, MathJax) use the backtick character as delimiters for AsciiMath expressions:\n`…`\n. Expressions are generally simpler and shorter than the equivalent LaTeX. Here is the equivalent AsciiMath for the expression used in the LaTeX example above:\n`a/(b+c)`\n.\nAdvantages:\nAsciiMath is designed to be easier to type quickly.\nAccessibility notes:\nAsciiMath needs to be rendered to MathML for full screen reader support. It's not inherently accessible on its own.\nDocumentation:\nSee\nasciimath.org\nfor details on the syntax and how to use it in web pages with MathJax or the AsciiMath JavaScript file.\nMathML\nMathematical Markup Language (MathML) is an XML-based markup language for describing mathematical and scientific content on the web. It's a W3C recommendation and an ISO standard.\nWhen authoring mathematical content for the web, EPUBs, and other digital formats, it's important to understand the distinction between the full MathML specification and\nMathML Core\n. MathML Core is a focused subset of the larger MathML specification specifically designed for web browser implementation. Unlike the complete MathML3 specification, which was extensive but lacked detailed rendering guidelines, MathML Core prioritizes consistent visual presentation across browsers through clear implementation rules and integration with modern web technologies, such as HTML, SVG, CSS, and DOM.\nLimit your mathematical markup to MathML Core features to ensure the widest compatibility for your content across web browsers, e-readers, and other digital platforms that process mathematical expressions.\nFormat:\nMathML is the internal format for representing math expressions in many systems. While MathML is powerful for an internal purpose, it can be difficult to create manually. It's rarely written by hand and most often is generated by conversion tools. It can be recognized by its beginning and ending\n<math>\ntags; for example:\n<math><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>\nAdvantages:\nMathML is designed for machine-readability and accessibility.\nAccessibility notes:\nMathML is directly understood by many assistive technologies, such as screen readers (when supported).\nMany libraries and tools exist for converting MathML into other formats such as rendered expressions, speech text, and braille.\nMathML is the preferred format for accessible math.\nDocumentation:\nSee\nwww.w3.org/Math\nfor a comprehensive overview of MathML, its standards, supporting software, and future implementations.\nSee the\nMathML Core\nspecification for details on authoring MathML that is suitable for browser implementation.\nConverters\nMathJax\nMathJax\nis an open-source JavaScript library that processes TeX, LaTeX, AsciiMath, and MathML to render mathematical notation using CommonHTML mode with web fonts, plain HTML-CSS, SVG, and native MathML.\nAccessibility notes:\nMathJax converts input formats to MathML behind the scenes and applies various heuristics to given MathML to correct for bad practices commonly found from MathML producers.\nMathJax can be configured to supply hidden "assistive" MathML for assistive technologies.\nMathJax can be configured to enlarge (zoom) math expressions upon a given trigger. Some versions (MathJax 4+) are "self-voicing" and can provide built-in speech access for the print-impaired.\nMathJax uses the Speech Rule Engine (SRE) for speech text, braille, and expression navigation features.\nThe MathJax menu varies across versions and can be difficult for a screen reader user to operate. When manually adding MathJax to a web page, make sure the MathJax configuration provides accessibility support by default.\nThe MathJax menu allows users to copy equations for use in other applications, such as Microsoft 365 applications, LaTeX, wikis, and other software.\nSpeech Rule Engine (SRE)\nThe\nSpeech Rule Engine\n(SRE) is a JavaScript/TypeScript library for generating speech and braille output for mathematical expressions. It can be used in the web browser, as a standalone tool, or as a node module for Node.js. SRE provides extensive browser support not only for translating mathematics into text or braille but also for voicing, navigating, highlighting, and semantically restructuring expressions.\nAccessibility notes:\nSRE is used internally by MathJax.\nSRE provides speech text in the MathSpeak™, ClearSpeak, and ChromeVox styles.\nSRE provides braille output using the Nemeth braille and Euro braille codes.\nMathCAT\nMath Capable Assistive Technology (\nMathCAT\n) is a library designed to convert MathML notation into speech text and braille, and to provide multiple ways to navigate math for use with assistive technologies. It is intended for use in screen readers, such as JAWS and NVDA, and is under active development.\nAccessibility notes:\nMathCAT was designed as an open-source replacement for Design Science's MathPlayer. It provides high-quality speech and/or braille output and intuitive yet powerful expression navigation features.\nMathCAT provides speech text in the ClearSpeak and SimpleSpeak styles.\nMathCAT provides braille output using the Nemeth and UEB braille codes.\nMathCAT can provide unambiguous intuitive math speech tailored to user needs.\nMathCAT works with content created in MathML, so ensuring that your content is available in MathML format (either directly or through conversion) will make it compatible.\nMathCAT provides some support for speaking chemical expressions for MathML generated using the LaTeX\nmhchem\npackage.\nMath speech grammars (styles)\nA math speech style is a standardized approach to verbalizing mathematical notation through synthesized speech. It's a set of rules and conventions that defines how mathematical expressions are translated from their written form (like MathML or other formats) into spoken words.\nEach speech style must address several fundamental challenges:\nStructure representation:\nHow to convey the hierarchical structure of expressions (such as fraction numerators and denominators, exponents, and nested functions)\nAmbiguity avoidance:\nHow to avoid ambiguities (such as announcing where fractions begin and end)\nVerbosity control:\nFinding the right balance between providing enough information and avoiding overwhelming the listener\nSpecialized notation:\nHow to handle specialized mathematical symbols and notations consistently\nProsody:\nHow pausing, timing, and speech emphasis can be used to aid understanding\nThe choice of math speech style for users depends on multiple factors, including the user's needs, background, and preferences.\nClearSpeak\nClearSpeak was originally developed through collaboration between Educational Testing Service (ETS) and Design Science (creators of MathPlayer). It aims to make mathematical expressions sound more natural and classroom-like. ClearSpeak was created because the MathSpeak™ speech style did not meet the requirement for classroom-like speech, a major goal being the creation of a new automated synthetic speech style that would better reflect how math is spoken in educational settings.\nAccessibility notes:\nThe implementation of ClearSpeak is not consistent between MathCAT and MathJax.\nClearSpeak does not always produce unambiguous speech for expressions.\nThe listener must understand how prosody (mainly pauses) is used to communicate structural information and be practiced in interpreting it.\nThe ClearSpeak style comes in three verbosity levels: Verbose, Medium, and Terse.\nThe least ambiguous option of the ClearSpeak style is the Verbose implementation provided in MathCAT.\nDocumentation:\nNo detailed treatment of the ClearSpeak speech style is available online.\nSee the MathCAT, MathJax, and Speech Rule Engine documentation for settings information.\nMathSpeak™\nMathSpeak™ was developed by Abraham Nemeth (creator of the Nemeth Braille Code) and is maintained by SeeWriteHear. It's designed as a standardized way to verbalize mathematical expressions with a focus on precision and unambiguity. It's particularly notable for its relationship to Nemeth Braille, as it was optimized for real-time Nemeth Code transcription by creating a one-to-one mapping of speech to Nemeth Code symbols.\nAccessibility notes:\nMathSpeak™ may be preferred by users who need precise structural information about complex expressions, especially those who are familiar with Nemeth Braille.\nMathSpeak™ is available in MathJax via its use of the Speech Rule Engine.\nMathSpeak™ is not currently available in MathCAT (but is planned).\nDocumentation:\nMathSpeak™ Grammar Rules\n(SeeWriteHear)\nQuick MathSpeak™ Tutorial\n(SeeWriteHear)\nMathSpeak™ Settings Tutorial\n(SeeWriteHear)\nSimpleSpeak\nSimpleSpeak follows the principle of speaking simple expressions as simply and briefly as possible while using begin/end markers to disambiguate more complicated expressions. It does not attempt to map directly to Nemeth Code or to any other braille system, nor does it attempt to replicate how math is spoken in the classroom.\nAccessibility notes:\nSimpleSpeak is not recommended for non-visual learners who are beginning to learn math or just learning how to listen to math.\nThis style is unambiguous for listeners who understand its rules and are competent in interpreting the speech combined with the rules.\nSimpleSpeak is available in MathCAT.\nThis style may be preferred by experienced users who want to listen to math spoken with the fewest words possible.\nThe listener benefits from understanding how prosody (mainly pauses) is used by SimpleSpeak to help communicate structural information.\nDocumentation:\nNo detailed treatment of the SimpleSpeak speech style is available online.\nSee the MathCAT documentation for settings information.\nChromeVox\nChromeVox refers to the speech rule set originally developed for the ChromeVox screen reader extension for Chrome. This style became the foundation for the Speech Rule Engine (SRE) that is now used with MathJax.\nDocumentation:\nNo detailed treatment of the ChromeVox speech style is available online.\nSee the MathJax and Speech Rule Engine documentation for settings information.\nSpeech style comparison\nSpeech style\nVerbosity\nTool\nBest for\nKey characteristics\nClearSpeak\nVerbose\nMathCAT\nNon-visual learners just learning to listen to and rely on speech for math access\nNatural, classroom-like phrasing\nExtensive disambiguation when set to Verbose\nMore intuitive for beginners\nBalances clarity with natural speech patterns\nSimpleSpeak\nAny\nMathCAT\nKnowledgeable, experienced non-visual users who rely on speech for math access and understand how SimpleSpeak disambiguates expressions\nMost concise expression of mathematics\nUses fewer words overall\nRequires understanding of the style's conventions\nEfficient for experienced listeners\nClearSpeak\nMedium or Terse\nMathCAT or MathJax\nSighted user wanting text-to-speech reinforcement\nLess verbose than needed for non-visual use\nNatural speech patterns that complement visual math\nReinforces visual understanding without excessive detail\nMathSpeak™\nAny\nMathJax\nNon-visual users who need precise structural information and may be familiar with Nemeth Braille; suitable for beginning learners who don't mind that it is very verbose and harsh sounding\nPrecise structural representation\nStrong connection to Nemeth Braille patterns\nExplicit begin/end markers for structures\nUnambiguous, but more verbose\nBraille codes\nNemeth Braille Code for Mathematics and Science Notation\nThe Nemeth Braille Code is a specialized 6-dot braille system developed by Dr. Abraham Nemeth in the 1950s for encoding mathematical and scientific notation. It's primarily used in the United States and other English-speaking countries. It provides a comprehensive system for representing complex mathematical expressions, including algebraic equations, geometric formulas, calculus notation, and scientific symbols, all while maintaining structural clarity. The code uses special indicators and context-sensitive symbols to represent different mathematical operations and structures, making it particularly effective for advanced mathematics.\nDocumentation:\nNemeth Tutorial\n(APH)\nNemeth Code\n(Braille Authority of North America)\nUnified English Braille (UEB)\nUnified English Braille (UEB) is an internationally adopted standard for English-speaking countries that unifies literary and technical braille within a single system. Unlike Nemeth, which is specialized for math and science, UEB handles both general text and technical material consistently. It uses a 6-dot system with specific symbols and indicators for mathematical expressions. UEB Technical outlines how to represent complex math, though its methods differ from Nemeth. UEB was developed to eliminate the need to switch between braille codes when reading text that includes math.\nDocumentation:\nUEB Math Tutorial\n(APH)\nUnified English Braille\n(Braille Authority of North America)\nTerminology - UEB Math/Science and UEB with Nemeth\n(Braille Authority of North America)\nEuro Braille\nThe Euro Braille output option in the MathJax 4.0+ beta uses a braille mode provided by the Speech Rule Engine (SRE) and is not yet fully documented. Euro Braille might not be a formal braille code standard recognized by international authorities, and instead is a naming convention used uniquely by MathJax and the SRE. Based on analysis of the Unicode braille strings MathJax produces, for Euro Braille it appears to encode parts of the LaTeX source directly into 8-dot braille using ASCII-style mappings. Using 8-dot braille allows for representation of a full ASCII character set, including symbols, and upper and lowercase letters, without ambiguity.\nAdditional tools\nMathpix Snipping Tool\nThe\nMathpix Snipping Tool\nis a Windows and macOS app that allows rubber-banding any content/image displayed on the screen. It uses AI-powered OCR to process handwritten or typeset math and chemistry. It can output various flavors of LaTeX and MathML that are compatible with the Math Converter tool, Microsoft Word, Canvas, and markdown environments.	KB0026701	KB0026701 v6.0	 overview of MathML, its standards, supporting <strong>software</strong>, and future implementations. See the MathML, such as Microsoft 365 applications, LaTeX, wikis, and other <strong>software</strong>. Speech Rule Engine (SRE	2.0779		2026-01-31 17:24:19.245237	2026-01-31 17:24:19.245237	0	655213f33b462218476d941864e45aed
12021	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=8050812a4785da109286dfbd436d436b	About BitLocker at IU	Table of Contents\nOverview\nWhen to encrypt\nTools needed\nSupported configuration\nTurn on a TPM\nWindows 11\nWindows 10/Windows 8.1\nWhen to use a PIN as two-factor authorization\nManage BitLocker using Group Policy (PIN optional)\nEnable BitLocker with a TPM\nRequiring a PIN (optional)\nWindows 11/Windows 10/Windows 8.1\nEnable BitLocker without a TPM\nEnable BitLocker (non-domain joined with TPM)\nBack up the key manually\nBackup key\nBack up BitLocker recovery information manually to Active Directory\nEncrypt hard disk and external drives\nView the recovery keys\nView the key in Active Directory\nVerify that a key has been escrowed\nView the key in Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory)\nSave recovery keys\nTips and troubleshooting\nOverview\nThe BitLocker Drive Encryption security feature in Windows protects files on your computer in the event that anyone tampers with the computer's startup process. BitLocker can leverage the Trusted Platform Module (TPM) security hardware to guard data and system files in the Windows operating system directory, and also protects early boot component integrity. Encrypting the entire Windows volume with BitLocker can help prevent others from seeing your files, even if your computer is stolen or a hard disk removed. At Indiana University, BitLocker may provide a useful tool for meeting the requirements for protecting confidential data described in\nProtecting Data & Privacy\n.\nFor more about BitLocker, see Microsoft's\nBitLocker Drive Encryption Overview\n.\nWhen to encrypt\nBitLocker Drive Encryption is a Windows security feature that protects your data. BitLocker works best when used with a Trusted Platform Module (TPM) version 1.2. A TPM is a chip built into most modern computers. For more about BitLocker, see Microsoft TechNet's\nBitLocker Overview\n.\nBefore you set up BitLocker in your department, test the processes you will use first.\nTier 2 Desktop, Server, and Mobility (DSM)\nsuggests you encrypt a few machines using the guidelines below. To help you become comfortable with BitLocker's processes and settings, the machines should have your most common build.\nIn all cases, you should follow the institutional, federal, or state guidelines governing the data that may be hosted on the systems or application. See\nClassification levels of institutional data\n.\nFor more data protection resources, see\nData Encryption\n.\nAccording to\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\n, any laptop or notebook computer used by faculty, staff, affiliates, or student employees to access, store, or manipulate institutional data must have full disk encryption.\nAlthough IT-12.1 does not specifically address desktop machines, you should encrypt the drives on desktops when possible.\nCyber Risk Mitigation Responsibilities (IT-28)\nand\nSecurity of Information Technology Resources (IT-12)\ncover topics specific to servers; for more, see\nProtecting Data & Privacy\n.\nNote:\nYou can use BitLocker on a bare metal server with a TPM version 1.2 or later. UITS is currently investigating BitLocker support in the II environment.\nTools needed\nTo effectively manage BitLocker in an Active Directory environment, you will need to download the Remote Server Administration Tools (RSAT) package from Microsoft. Specifically, you will need the Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC) and Group Policy Management (GPM) tools.\nBe sure to include the BitLocker Recovery Password Viewer after installing RSAT. To do so:\nOpen the Control Panel.\nChoose\nTurn Windows features on or off\n.\nExpand\nRemote Server Administration Tools\n, then\nFeature Administration Tools\n, and then\nBitLocker Drive Encryption Administration Utilities\n.\nSelect\nBitLocker Recovery Password Viewer\n.\nChoose\nOK\nor\nNext\n(depending on the OS) to start the installation.\nSupported configuration\nOperating Systems:\nWindows 10\n/\nWindows 8.1\nUltimate and Enterprise editions, /\nWindows Server 2012\nTrusted Platform Module (TPM):\n1.2 or higher\nBIOS/UEFI:\nMust be compatible with TPM\nPartitions:\nMust be two hard drive partitions; Windows desktop standard installation will automatically set this configuration.\nTo use BitLocker on the Windows Surface tablet, see\nEncrypt your Surface tablet using BitLocker\n.\nTurn on a TPM\nBefore you can manage BitLocker with a TPM, you must enable the TPM in the BIOS/UEFI:\nWindows 11\nThe TPM is enabled automatically and cannot be disabled. To clear the TPM:\nFrom the\nStart\nmenu, type\ntpm.msc\nand press\nEnter\n.\nIn the right pane, choose\nClear TPM...\nand follow the instructions to have it reset. This will result in a restart.\nWindows 10\n/\nWindows 8.1\nFrom the\nStart\nmenu, type\ntpm.msc\nand press\nEnter\n.\nIn the right pane, choose\nPrepare the TPM\n, which will require restarting and agreeing to modify the BIOS/UEFI settings during boot. If that option is unavailable, choose\nClear TPM...\nand follow the instructions to have it reset. This will result in more than one restart.\nAfter returning to the desktop, you will receive a message that the TPM is ready for use. If you wish, you can save the TPM owner password by selecting\nRemember my TPM owner password\nand saving the\n.tpm\nfile to a secure location; see the\nSave recovery keys\nsection below.\nFor cmdlets for enabling a TPM via PowerShell command line, see\nTrustedPlatformModule\n.\nTPM recovery information is different than the BitLocker recovery information. TPM recovery information can be backed up when you:\nSet the TPM owner password during TPM initialization\nChange the TPM owner password\nWhen to use a PIN as two-factor authorization\nAs a local UITS support person, you should follow the institutional, federal, or state guidelines governing the data that may be hosted on your systems or application. See\nClassification levels of institutional data\n.\nMachines storing any data above the IU data classification of Public should use a PIN with TPM in addition to encrypting the drive with BitLocker. This can provide a two-factor option for your system.\nYou may also use a PIN if you do not have TPM; see\nEnable BitLocker without a TPM\n.\nIf you are not using Folder Redirection, Offline Files, or running third-party backup software on your clients, consider investigating these options before encrypting your data. It is always wise to back up the data before you encrypt the drive.\nManage BitLocker using Group Policy (PIN optional)\nNote:\nTier 2 DSM\nrecommends\nenabling the TPM\nbefore you apply the GPO changes below.\nThese are the standard settings for domain-joined machines that are using a TPM. Also listed are optional settings to allow two-factor authentication or devices that do not support TPM.\nChanging the encryption method and cipher strength will not harm machines that are already encrypted or are in the process of encrypting.\nOpen the Group Policy Management Console tree and navigate to:\nComputer Configuration > Local Computer Policy > Administrative Templates >\nWindows Components > BitLocker Drive Encryption\nThe following options need to be enabled and modified in their respective folders:\nRoot folder of BitLocker Drive Encryption\nEnable\nChoose drive encryption method and cipher strength (Windows...)\nand select AES 256-bit.\nEnable\nChoose drive encryption method and cipher strength\nand select AES 256-bit.\nEnable\nStore BitLocker recovery information in Active Directory Domain Services (Windows Server 2008...)\n.\nEnable\nChoose how users can recover BitLocker-protected drives (Windows Server 2008..)\n.\nFixed data drives\nEnable\nEnforce drive encryption type on fixed data drives\nand set the encryption type to\nFull\n.\nEnable\nChoose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered\nand ensure\nSave BitLocker recovery information to AD DS\nis checked.\nOperating system drives\nEnable\nEnforce drive encryption type on operating system drives\nand set the encryption type to\nFull\n.\nEnable\nChoose how BitLocker-protected operating system drives can be recovered\nand ensure\nSave BitLocker recovery information to AD DS\nis checked.\nOptional:\nIf you wish to use two-factor authentication, or the device does not have a TPM, enable\nRequire additional authentication at startup\nfor both entries, and configure the settings as necessary.\nRemovable data drives (optional)\nIf you decide to use or allow BitLocker encrypted removable drives, it's a good idea to enable\nChoose how BitLocker-protected removable drives can be recovered\nso that the keys are also stored in Active Directory.\nFinally, select the\nScope\ntab on the GPO itself. Under\nSecurity Filtering\n, you will want to make sure that the device (or security group the device belongs to) is listed.\nImportant:\nIf you don't complete these steps before encrypting the laptop, the recovery key will not appear in the Active Directory.\nEnable BitLocker with a TPM\nRequiring a PIN (optional)\nIf Group Policy was configured to require a PIN, you will need to run a few commands on the client to finalize the process. These commands will work on\nWindows 11\n,\nWindows 10\n, and\nWindows 8.1\n.\nOpen an elevated command prompt.\nRun the following command:\nmanage-bde -protectors -add c: -TPMAndPIN.\nTo ensure the TPMAndPin protector has been added, run the following command:\nmanage-bde -status\nUnder\nKey Protectors\n, you will see\nTPM and PIN\n.\nWindows 11\n/\nWindows 10\n/\nWindows 8.1\nFrom the\nStart\nmenu, type\nManage BitLocker\nand select the top result.\nIn the new window, choose\nTurn on BitLocker\n. You can now determine if a PIN is necessary; otherwise, select\nLet BitLocker automatically unlock my drive\n.\nOn the following screen, you will want to print the recovery key, or store the\n.txt\nfile in a secure location; see the\nSaving recovery keys\nsection below.\nOn the next screen, UISO recommends encrypting the entire disk (not just the used space) for added security measures.\nOn the next screen, choose\nNew encryption mode\nunless\nthe drive is removable and may need to be accessed using\nWindows 8.1\nor\nWindows 10\nversion 1507. In that case, choose\nCompatible mode\n.\nOn the final screen, BitLocker will run a final system check. A restart will be required, and the encryption process will begin. This process could take several hours to complete, but it will run in the background so work can continue.\nTo enable BitLocker with TPM via Windows command line, see Microsoft TechNet's\nBitLocker: Use BitLocker Drive Encryption Tools to manage BitLocker\n(for\nWindows 11\n,\nWindows 10\n, and\nWindows 8.1\n).\nYou cannot enable BitLocker via Group Policy. The BitLocker settings you can apply to systems via GPO simply dictate the options BitLocker will use or allow when it is enabled.\nEnable BitLocker without a TPM\nIf your computer was not manufactured with TPM version 1.2 or later, you can create a BitLocker startup key using a USB flash drive to store the encryption and decryption keys. You will have to insert the flash drive each time you start the computer.\nYou also have the option of creating a startup personal identification number (PIN) of four to 20 digits. You can create either the startup key or the startup PIN, but not both. The PIN is stored on your computer. You will have to type the PIN each time you start the computer.\nTo enable:\nFrom the\nStart\nmenu, type\nManage BitLocker\nand select the top result.\nFrom the management window, select\nTurn On BitLocker\n. This will only appear with the operating system volume.\nOn the following screen, choose\nRequire Startup USB Key at every startup\n. This is the only option available for non-TPM configurations. This key must be inserted each time before you start the computer.\nInsert your USB flash drive in the computer, if it is not already there.\nChoose the location of your USB flash drive, and then select\nSave\n.\nOn the next screen, you will be able to save or print the key. Be sure to do so in a secure location (see the\nSaving recovery keys\nsection below).\nAfter continuing, you will be required to restart. Upon returning to the desktop, BitLocker will begin encrypting the drive.\nEnable BitLocker (non-domain joined with TPM)\nFor machines that are external to the IU network or cannot be joined to the Active Directory domain, you can still use BitLocker to encrypt the drive.\nBack up the key manually\nThe commands listed below are compatible with\nWindows 11\n,\nWindows 10\n, and\nWindows 8.1\n.\nBackup key\nTo generate a key file, which can be placed on a USB drive to bypass the manual entry of the key, run the following command in an administrative command prompt (replacing\nc\nwith your drive letter):\nmanage-bde -protectors -add c: -recoverykey c:\nThis command will generate a\n.bek\nfile to the root directory of the chosen driven (in the example it was\nc:\\\n).\nBack up BitLocker recovery information manually to Active Directory\nRun the following commands in an administrative command prompt to manually add the recovery information to the computer object in AD:\nmanage-bde -protectors -get c:\nmanage-bde -protectors -adbackup c: -id {numerical password ID from step 1}\nEncrypt hard disk and external drives\nEncryption of removable media is possible with BitLocker. The recovery key can be stored inside the computer object in Active Directory that was responsible for the encryption; see the\nManage BitLocker using Group Policy\nsection above.\nFrom the\nStart\nmenu, type\nManage BitLocker\nand select the top result.\nIf a removable drive is connected, it should appear under "BitLocker To Go", with a\nTurn On BitLocker\nlink. After selecting the link, you have two options (depending on any restrictions that may have been applied with Group Policy):\nUse a password to unlock the drive\nis the most commonly used (and recommended) method. After choosing a password, you can save the recovery key to a file, or print it.\nUse my smart card to unlock the drive\nrequires the PC smart card to decrypt the drive. This option requires an additional piece of hardware.\nView the recovery keys\nView the key in Active Directory\nIU's Active Directory allows the storage of BitLocker recovery information for your BitLocker encrypted devices, including the required schema updates.\nTier 2 DSM\nrecommends storing the keys in Active Directory as a disaster recovery option. The recovery password is stored with the computer object as a confidential attribute viewable only to the OU administrators, UISO, and the domain administrators.\nNote:\nFor this to work, you must first follow the directions in the\nManage BitLocker using Group Policy\nsection above.\nThe AD object containing the key that can recover a BitLocker-encrypted volume in Active Directory is\nm-RecoveryInformation\n; this is attached to the computer object. To view the AD object:\nOpen Active Directory Users and Computers.\nChoose\nView\nand select\nUsers, Groups, and Computers as containers\n.\nBrowse through the Active Directory tree until you find your computer object.\nSelect your computer object; you will see\nmsFVE-RecoveryInformation\n.\nIn the right pane, right-click the object type\nmsFVE-RecoveryInformation\n.\nSecurity groups with full control to\nmsFVE-RecoveryInformation\ncan see the recovery key in the BitLocker recovery tab in Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC). By default, this is the OU Admins group. You can delegate full control to the object on your sub-OU to a group to allow help desk access to the recovery passwords.\nIf the computer is moved to a new OU,\nmsFVE-RecoveryInformation\nis moved with the computer object.\nNote:\nIf you join the machine to ADS and forget to pre-create the machine account, the recovery key is not visible to others within the Computers OU. However, all local UITS support people can move the object to their OU, and they would receive the ability to view the key. Moving the machine to your departmental OU is best practice.\nTo view the key on the local machine, open an administrative command prompt and run the following command:\nmanage-bde -protectors c: -get\nReplace\nc:\nwith the encrypted drive letter.\nYou will see output in this format:\nBitLocker Drive Encryption: Configuration Tool version 6.2.9200\nCopyright (C) 2012 Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.\nVolume C: [OSDisk]\nAll Key Protectors\nTPM:      ID: {37CE71B7-8FE4-4CA9-9637-42516F599C02}\nNumerical Password:    ID: {31514A2F-147C-478C-B6A2-618CD6F66653}\nPassword:\n249238-002442-716694-646503-010879-234894-155485-185372\nNote:\nThere is a top-level BitLocker policy that is applied to all machines (unless Block Inheritance is enabled) that will allow UISO to potentially recover the drive data if no other option exists (for example, if no one in your department has the rights to see the BitLocker key). However, the BitLocker key must have been previously escrowed. That policy in and of itself does not escrow the BitLocker key. Drives encrypted before April 26, 2015, will not inherit the policy. For drives encrypted before this date, you'll need to back up the key manually.\nUITS staff and local UITS support people:\nSee\nBack up the key manually\n.\nVerify that a key has been escrowed\nEven if the account you're using doesn't have access to view the recovery key directly, you still can verify that the device's BitLocker key is escrowed.  In Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC), in the entry for the device, check the\nBitlocker Recovery\ntab.  You'll see one of the following results:\nKey not escrowed:\n"No Items in this view. To search for a recovery password, right-click on the domain object in tree view, and select 'Find Bitlocker Recovery Password...'"\nKey escrowed, but the viewer does not have rights to see the key:\n"Cannot retrieve recovery password information. Cannot get the password attribute of a recovery password record. Make sure you have sufficient permission to access the recovery password."\nKey escrowed and viewer has rights to see the key:\nThe date added and password ID will be visible, and the details section will be filled in, including the recovery password (typically eight sets of six digits).\nView the key in Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory)\nIU's Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory or AAD) syncs BitLocker keys from Active Directory as another recovery option. The recovery password is stored with the computer object as a confidential attribute.\nTo view the key in Entra ID:\nGo to\nEntra ID\n>\nDevices\n.\nIf you have the BitLocker key ID:\nChoose\nBitLocker keys (Preview)\n.\nEnter the BitLocker key ID.\nFind the machine and click\nShow Recovery Key\n.\nIf you have the device name:\nSearch for the device by name and select it.\nFind the BitLocker key ID.\nChoose\nShow Recovery Key\n.\nSave recovery keys\nRecovery keys are essentially hardware passwords. During the beginning of the encryption process, you are required to either generate a file containing the key, or print the key. UITS strongly recommends that you keep the keys in a secure location, such as a password vault. KeePass is a free utility that can meet this need.\nTips and troubleshooting\nSuspend BitLocker before making major system configuration changes. If you need to update the BIOS/UEFI, modify the boot order, or make any other major changes, suspend BitLocker first or you may be prompted for the 48-digit recovery key.\nBe sure to back up any critical information before encrypting or decrypting a drive. Encryption can be rough on physical media, especially older drives.	KB0022479	KB0022479 v2.0	. If you are not using Folder Redirection, Offline Files, or running third-party backup <strong>software</strong> on your	1.8002	km-aud-uits, km-aud-local-support	2026-01-31 17:24:22.596033	2026-01-31 17:24:22.596033	0	8050812a4785da109286dfbd436d436b
12025	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=63901d7647a6be58d500b87b716d4388	Fulfill common accommodations related to digital materials	Table of contents\nOverview\nAlternative format text\nTextbook conversions\nPDF content\nLarge print\nPrint-only\nBraille\nTactile graphics\nAudio description\nClosed captioning\nLive captioning (CART)\nNotetaking\nPermission to record\nAdditional resources\nOverview\nThe\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) at Indiana University provide a wide range of services for qualifying students, faculty, and staff with disabilities, as well as comprehensive course accessibility evaluation, consulting, presentations, and training for the IU community.\nThe\nAssistive Technology and Accessibility Centers\n(\nATAC\n) specializes in sourcing and creating alternative formatted media that provide the highest levels of accessibility and usability for students with authorized alternative media accommodations.\nWhile every instructor should attempt to meet\nminimum digital accessibility guidelines for course content\n, an instructor also may receive a course accommodation memo from their campus Accessible Educational Services (AES) office detailing a student's need for digital course materials to be made further accessible.\nThis page covers each type of accommodation related to digital material that an instructor might receive. For information related to pedagogy, group work, student presentations, or teaching strategies, consult your campus\nAES office\n. If you have received a notification with more than one of the accommodations listed on this page, you will need to look at each relevant entry. To receive a consultation on how to request the appropriate services, submit the\nAccessibility Consultation\nrequest form via the\nIT Accessibility Service Catalog\n. Also via the\nIT Accessibility Service Catalog\n, anyone at IU may submit the\nCanvas Course Accessibility Review\nrequest form.\nAlternative format text\nThe alternative format text accommodation indicates all classroom materials and exams must be provided to the student, if desired, in an alternative format prior to use. This can include print copies, ePub, braille, and tactile graphics.\nAll course documents must be provided in an accessible format. Instructors should use\nAlly\nto evaluate their course materials on Canvas and make any of the recommended changes to those documents.\nProviding alternative formats to some students with accommodations does not fulfill the need to ensure materials meet the\nminimum digital accessibility guidelines for all students\n. Handwritten materials (whether scanned, on a board, or on an overhead), materials shown via camera during a videoconference, and materials displayed with a projector in-person are all examples of material that do not meet the accessibility standard on their own. Ways to make these types of content accessible include giving thorough descriptions during a presentation, recording a presentation so that additional descriptions can be added later, and providing the same content in a Microsoft Word document, on a Canvas page, or in Pressbook format.\nTextbook conversions\nStudents with an alternative format text accommodation will automatically receive a Bookshare account and a link to approved readers. Bookshare is a collection site for accessible textbooks. Students also can submit the\nAlternative Format Textbook\nrequest form via the\nIT Accessibility Service Catalog\nfor any textbooks they cannot find as an ePub on Bookshare, or for textbooks they need in either physical large print or braille format.\nNote:\nAfter a student determines what textbooks are needed for their courses, it may take 10 weeks for them to get the first chapter of an alternative version. Because IU eTexts are not listed on the IU Bookstore website, instructors need to provide the textbook's title and ISBN number to the student listed in the accommodation memo.\nPDF content\nStudents with an alternative format text accommodation also may need PDF content remediated. If you have a PDF that is not available through the IU library, and you do not have access to the source document (Word file), you can either create or request an alternative format through Ally in Canvas.\nTo improve the accessibility of the PDF yourself, see\nImprove the accessibility of PDF documents in Canvas\n.\nTo request PDF remediation from the\nATAC\n:\nLocate the item in your Canvas course.\nSelect the dial icon in front of the item.\nSelect\nHow to tag a PDF\n.\nAnswer the series of questions presented.\nAt the end of the workflow, select\nHelp\n.\nIn the text box provided, indicate that you need an accessible copy of the item.\nSelect\nSend\n.\nThe request will be routed to the\nATAC\nfor processing. If the item is not available in the IU library system, proof of legal access may be requested to fulfill the request. Scanned documents submitted for remediation should follow the recommendations listed in\nCreate a high-quality document scan\n.\nPDF remediation can take up to four weeks to complete.\nLarge print\nThe large print accommodation indicates that instructors must provide classroom materials, if desired, in a large print format at the font size specified in the accommodation memo. If possible, send presentation files to the student so that they can be opened on a personal computer and magnified.\nStudents who have this accommodation are automatically added to the ZoomText reservation list. For assistance with printing large font, across pages, or at a magnified scale using\ntabloid printing\n, contact your campus\nSupport Center\nor\nteaching and learning center\n.\nPrint-only\nStudents who have a print-only accommodation are not able to endure prolonged screen use and will need to print out all digital materials. Since students with this accommodation can print materials on their own, instructors need only provide digital course materials to the student far enough ahead of time to give the student time to print them.\nBraille\nBraille is a system that conveys information using raised dots on specialized paper so it can be read by the fingers.\nAn alternative format text accommodation indicates a student is eligible for braille services, but it does not indicate whether a student will be able to, or want to, use braille for your exams or in-class materials. Check with the student and the\nAES office\nto clarify what will be needed in braille (for example, math, foreign language, chemistry, music, exams, and/or presentation printouts).\nIf braille services are needed, submit the\nAccessibility Consultation\nrequest form via the\nIT Accessibility Service Catalog\n), and follow the directions in\nRequest braille and tactile graphics for approved accommodations\n.\nNote:\nBraille requests require a 10-week lead time for in-class materials and a four-week lead time for exams. Submissions must already be digitally accessible. Canvas Quizzes (if requested in print format) must be converted to another document type before submission. In situations where circumstances, such as late enrollment, have made the lead time impossible to meet, make sure to discuss alternatives with the campus\nAES office\nwhile the braille versions of your materials and/or exams are being produced.\nTactile graphics\nTactile graphics make use of specialized paper to produce raised images that can be felt with the fingers; they often accompany braille or large print.\nAn alternative format text accommodation indicates a student is eligible for tactile graphics, but it does not indicate whether the student will want them for your class. If your course uses images that contain unique shapes (for example, graphs with unpredictable curves), images that will be heavily explored (for example, a flowchart or technical drawing), or images that are representative of other images they will come across in the class (for example, an introductory Venn diagram), you may need to submit those images for tactile graphic production.\nCheck with the student and\nAES office\nto determine whether tactile graphics will be useful to the student. If tactile graphic services are needed, submit the\nAccessibility Consultation\nrequest form via the\nIT Accessibility Service Catalog\n, and follow the directions in\nRequest braille and tactile graphics for approved accommodations\n. During your consultation with\nATAC\n, you will receive more information about how to submit an image for tactile graphic production.\nNote:\nTactile graphics requests require a 10-week lead time for in-class materials and a four-week lead time for exams. Submissions must include alt text or accompanying descriptions (and/or slide titles for PowerPoint presentations) so the student can have a way to connect the tactile references with the appropriate course materials. In situations where circumstances, such as late enrollment, have made the lead time impossible to meet, make sure to discuss alternatives with the AES office.\nAudio description\nThe audio description (AD) accommodation is also called "verbal description of visual content". It involves verbalizing visual content, such as videos, images, and whiteboard drawings, in a way that conveys the essential elements to the listener.\nThe amount of AD needed is dependent on the complexity of the visuals in a presentation or video. This may be provided by the instructor as an explanation during a presentation, by an in-class sighted assistant, or by a supplier service through the\nATAC\n. To request video description for videos that cannot be understood purely through listening to the audio, submit the\nAudio Description for Video\nrequest form via the\nIT Accessibility Service Catalog\n.\nNote:\nVideo description can take up to one week to produce.\nClosed captioning\nAll videos required for a course must contain completely accurate captioning. The automated captioning provided by services such as YouTube, Vimeo, and Kaltura are not sufficient to fulfill this requirement.\nIf you receive a closed captioning accommodation, submit the\nAccessibility Consultation\nrequest form via the\nIT Accessibility Service Catalog\nso that\nATAC\nstaff can review your video content and explain the process.\nNote:\nClosed captioning can take up to five business days to produce.\nLive captioning (CART)\nLive captioning, or Communication Access Realtime Translation (CART), is the real-time display of spoken dialog for a synchronous classroom setting (in-person or via a videoconferencing platform). The captioner is usually a trained stenographer who joins the class via video and provides the captions through the student's laptop or mobile device.\nThe live captioning accommodation indicates that virtual synchronous or in-person course lectures must have transcriptions available in real time. To request live captioning, contact your campus\nAES office\ndirectly for scheduling.\nNote:\nLive captioning can take a week or more to schedule for your course.\nNotetaking\nThe notetaking accommodation indicates that copies of class notes from a peer must be made available to the student unless the notes are posted by the instructor to Canvas.\nPermission to record\nThe permission to record accommodation indicates that the student is allowed to record class lectures and discussions using approved software (\nGenio Notes\n, smart pen, or OneNote). The student may not share the recordings with other students. Sharing recordings would result in the removal of this accommodation; using direct quotes would be considered plagiarism and subject to referral to the Office of Student Conduct. When students have this accommodation, they also are eligible for notetaking tools, such as Genio Notes, and technical training.\nAdditional resources\nIU Expand\ncourses:\nCreating an Accessible Syllabus using Microsoft Word\nCreating Accessible Documents at Work\nImproving the Accessibility of Your Canvas Course\nTeaching Online Series\n(Module 8 is about accessibility)\nAlly documentation:\nAlly instructor guide\nAlly alternative formats for students\nGeneral accessibility documentation:\nAccessibility resources at IU	KB0025516	KB0025516 v4.0	 to record class lectures and discussions using approved <strong>software</strong> (Genio Notes, smart pen, or OneNote	1.5646	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:24:25.930985	2026-01-31 17:24:25.930985	0	63901d7647a6be58d500b87b716d4388
12032	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=41cf2cb7476456583d373803846d433f	Options for implementing two-factor authentication for privileged access to servers, applications, and network infrastructure	Table of contents\nOverview\nOperating system and application integrations\nDuo Unix PAM module\nGroups VPN\nOverview\nSecurity of Information Technology Resources (IT-12)\nrequires use of two-factor authentication for privileged access (that is, root or system administrator access) to servers, applications, and network infrastructure where practicable.\nThis page lists some of the options available for implementing Duo two-factor authentication at IU.\nOperating system and application integrations\nDuo provides a number of native integrations with applications and operating systems. For a complete list, see the Duo\nSupport Applications\npage. If an integration is available for your product, you may request an integration key using the instructions in\nIntegrate Duo with an application\n.\nDuo Unix PAM module\nYou can add Duo two-factor authentication to a Linux-based bastion server by deploying the\npam_duo\nPAM module. For installation instructions, see\nDuo Unix - Two-Factor Authentication for SSH with PAM Support (pam_duo)\n.\nTo complete the configuration, the system administrator will need to request a Duo integration key using the instructions in\nIntegrate Duo with an application\n.\nGroups VPN\nIU's Groups VPN uses\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\nauthentication to restrict access to administrative environments. For more, see\nUse the IU Groups VPN to restrict remote access to your team's administrative resources\n.	KB0023256	KB0023256 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Operating system and application integrations Duo Unix PAM module Groups VPN Overview Security of Information Technology Resources (IT-12) requires use of two-factor authentication for privileged access (that is, root or system administrator access) to servers, applications, and network infrastructure where practicable	66.6832		2026-01-31 17:24:29.407424	2026-01-31 17:24:29.407424	0	41cf2cb7476456583d373803846d433f
12034	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c4dfe4f7476456583d373803846d4346	About the port group upgrade in IU's Intelligent Infrastructure environment	During the first quarter of 2020, as a follow-up to the\nIntelligent Infrastructure stretched cluster\nproject, the UITS Storage and Virtualization (SAV) team will enable additional security enhancements to the distributed port groups to align with current VMware best practices.\nThe specific change depends on the original port group configuration; the majority of the changes involve disabling\nMAC Address Changes\n,\nForged Transmits\n, and\nPromiscuous Mode\n. These features historically were required for certain older load balancers and clustered workloads. Most modern load balancers and clustered workloads do not require these features.\nTo minimize potential operational impact of the change:\nOn December 30, 2019, the SAV team created new port groups that use the new settings and denoted pre-existing port groups with settings that don't meet the updated standard by appending\n-Legacy\nto their names. For example, an existing port group named\nDPortGroup-VLAN-123\nwould be relabeled\nDPortGroup-VLAN-123-Legacy\n(with all of its settings remaining the same), and a new\nDPortGroup-VLAN-123\nport group would be created with the new, hardened security settings.\nTargeted email notification was sent to VM owners of the deprecated port groups. The message included directions to self-service port group migrations during their scheduled maintenance windows.\nThe SAV team is providing a self-service tool to enable bulk updates to multiple VMs at a time. To help VM admins with multiple VMs, the SAV team has published a PowerShell script that lets you identify and control the VM network adapter port group changes. It creates a\nCSV\nfile containing the VM network adapters that need port group updates. VM admins can edit the CSV file, and the script will programmatically update the indicated VM network adapters instead of requiring VM admins to manually log into the GUI to make each change.\nDownload the bulk update PowerShell script from GitHub:IU\n(log in with your\nIU username\nand\npassphrase\n).\nNote:\nTo run the script, your workstation must be connected to the IU network, and have the\nVMware.VimAutomation.Core\nPowerCLI module and the PowervRA PowerShell module installed.\nBefore March 1, 2020, organizations should update each NIC on any of their VMs that are attached to a legacy port group:\nTo check whether your organization has any VMs attached to a legacy port group,\nlog into vRealize Automation (vRA)\nand\nrequest a VM information report\n. This creates a CSV file containing all the VMs belonging to your organization. Check the "VLANs" column for any port groups marked with\n-Legacy\n.\nFor any VMs attached to a legacy port group, use the\nChange NIC (Add, Change, Remove)\naction in vRA (see\nInteract with a VM in the Intelligent Infrastructure (II) self-service portal\n) to update each NIC to the corresponding new port group. For example, if your VM is using\nDPortGroup-VLAN-9999-Legacy\n, switch to\nDPortGroup-VLAN-9999\n. This may occur with the VM powered on.\nNote:\nSAV recommends that you test the migration with pre-production systems first, and then perform the modifications during a scheduled maintenance window.\nA small percentage of workloads (particularly, some load balancers) may require non-standard port group configurations to function as expected. If you encounter issues after migrating to a new port group, use the\nChange NIC (Add, Change, Remove)\naction to reconfigure the NIC(s) back to the legacy port groups and restore functionality, and then email the SAV team at\nsav-request@iu.edu\nwith the VM name, the service(s) running on the VM, a description of the issues you are experiencing, and the name of VLAN that requires the exception. SAV will work with you to create a purpose-built exception port group for your workload.\nAt 12:01am on March 1, 2020, any VMs that have not been migrated off legacy port groups will be migrated automatically by the system. On March 4, 2020, all legacy port groups will be removed from the II environment.	KB0023514	KB0023514 v1.0	 4, 2020, all legacy port groups will be removed from the II <strong>environment</strong>.	33.0064		2026-01-31 17:24:34.884401	2026-01-31 17:24:34.884401	0	c4dfe4f7476456583d373803846d4346
12036	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=64cfa8b7476456583d373803846d43af	About the IUIE (Indiana University Information Environment)	Table of contents\nOverview\nGeneral information and support\nIUIE access and login information\nNavigating and searching the IUIE\nIUIE catalogs\nReporting in the IUIE\nMailing functions\nIUIE publishers\nData managers\nOverview\nThe Indiana University Information Environment (IUIE) is a centrally maintained, enterprise-wide, web-based operational reporting environment. It provides a collection of reports, tools, and information to assist the university in providing access to\ninstitutional data\n. In the IUIE, a developer creates a report object, and then it becomes available to all authorized IUIE users.\nNote:\nAll IUIE users can see all the report objects in the Master Catalog, but not all users have access to all objects. For more, see\nRequest access to a specific report in the IUIE\nThe IUIE contains institutional data from a variety of categories, including financial, human resource, and student data. The IUIE's goal is to provide the university with a single repository for institutional data and reporting tools.\nDevelopers from various application areas create and publish report objects based on a department's specific reporting needs. The\nEnterprise Decision Support Services\n(EDSS) team makes this data available and maintains the IUIE. From the Master Catalog or My Catalog, IUIE users can view or execute reports using report objects.\nGeneral information and support\nAbout IUIE training\nAbout IUIE News\nGet help for the IUIE\nChange personalized settings in the IUIE\nIUIE access and login information\nHours IUIE data is available for reports\nLog into the IUIE\nRequest an IUIE account\nRequest an IUIE account for an affiliate\nChange your DSS1PRD password in the IUIE\nRequest access to a specific report in the IUIE\nNavigating and searching the IUIE\nFunctions of persistent IUIE icons\nPerform a basic search for a report object in the IUIE\nAbout advanced searches in the IUIE\nIUIE catalogs\nAbout catalogs in the IUIE\nIf your IUIE Master Catalog won't load\nAbout indices of report objects in the IUIE\nReporting in the IUIE\nRun or schedule reports in the IUIE\nReport types in the IUIE\nIf your IUIE report times out\nGet help publishing IUIE reports\nSecure IUIE reports\nMailing functions\nMailing functions in the IUIE\nAccess IUIE mailing functions\nUse mailing functions in a data extract in the IUIE\nComponents of a mailing request file in the IUIE\nPublish a PDQ with mailing functions in the IUIE\nAbout PDQ mailing functions in the IUIE\nIUIE publishers\nGet help publishing IUIE reports\nAvailable parameters for a PDQ in the IUIE\nEdit a parameter in the IUIE\nIUIE display format for a datagroup Date column\nDatagroup column inheritance in the IUIE\nAssign user groups to report objects in the IUIE\nAbout the Sensitive Data Indicator in the IUIE\nIndicate data as sensitive in the IUIE\nAssign row-level security to manage IUIE user access\nSecure IUIE reports\nUpload and manage frozen files in the IUIE\nParameters available for an IUIE data extract\nAbout Information Providers in the IUIE\nData managers\nDisable email notifications to end users in the IUIE\nAssign row-level security to manage IUIE user access\nCreate a restricted row-level user group in the IUIE\nDetermine report security from the IUIE Master Catalog\nSearch the IUIE from the Collections Security Catalog\nLook up Data Managers for IUIE and Data Delivery Apply Security\nRevoke all row security for an individual user within a module in the IUIE\nAbout the IUIE Backdoor Userid function for Data Managers\nManage users in the IUIE as a Data Manager	KB0023211	KB0023211 v1.0	 Data managers Overview The Indiana University Information <strong>Environment</strong> (IUIE) is a centrally maintained, enterprise-wide, web-based operational reporting <strong>environment</strong>. It provides a collection of reports	27.8348		2026-01-31 17:24:39.160815	2026-01-31 17:24:39.160815	0	64cfa8b7476456583d373803846d43af
12079	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=dd946c18476e3a9c2fc6a55b416d43c7	About the Siteimprove service at IU	Important:\nSiteKube is replacing Sitehost. For more, see\nAbout SiteKube\n.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nAccess Siteimprove\nAdd a website to Siteimprove\nAdd users to Siteimprove\nWeb accessibility using Siteimprove\nWeb analytics using Siteimprove\nWCMS-Siteimprove integration\nHelp and support\nSiteimprove request form\nUITS Web team\nWeb Community of Practice\nOverview\nSiteimprove\nis a cloud-based web governance tool enabling IU’s web professionals to continuously and efficiently improve their unit’s web presence. Siteimprove will scan, analyze, and suggest ways to grow engagement; boost search engine rankings; and improve the accessibility, performance, and usability of your website.\nNote:\nThis page provides supplemental documentation on Siteimprove, a cloud-based accessibility tool. Vendor documentation is available at the\nSiteimprove Help Center\n.\nAs a web professional at IU, you can use Siteimprove to:\nEliminate misspellings and broken links\nCheck content freshness and readability\nVerify if a PDF is accessible\nVerify the accessibility of some web content\nOptimize for SEO\nImprove usability\nMeasure the overall quality of your website over time\nIU units can learn more and request access from the following sections.\nAccess Siteimprove\nSiteimprove is a cloud-based tool accessible via a web browser with your IU credentials. Access Siteimprove by logging in with your\n@iu.edu\nemail address.\nGo to\nSiteimprove\n.\nFrom the top right menu, select\nLogin\n. You will be redirected to a Siteimprove login page.\nSelect\nSign in with SSO\n.\nOn the "Sign in with SSO" page, under "Email or username", enter your\n@iu.edu\nemail address, and then select\nContinue sign in with SSO\n.\nIf prompted, log in with your IU username and passphrase.\nYou will be directed to the dashboard. If you have more than one website in Siteimprove, select the desired website from the drop-down list at the top of the dashboard.\nAdd a website to Siteimprove\nTo request adding one or more of your university websites to Siteimprove:\nGo to the\nSiteimprove Requests form\n.\nSelect\nAdd site to Siteimprove\n.\nIf you have a long list of websites to add, download and fill out the\nMultiple Websites Upload Spreadsheet\n, and then upload it to the form.\nComplete and submit the form.\nAdd users to Siteimprove\nTo request adding users to Siteimprove:\nGo to the\nSiteimprove Requests form\n.\nSelect\nGrant access to Siteimprove\n.\nComplete and submit the form.\nGroup accounts may not be users in Siteimprove.\nThe following Siteimprove roles are available:\nIU User:\nDefault recommended role\nIU Power User:\nFor daily users who need to create departmental dashboards and reports using APIs\nIU SEO Power User:\nIU Power User permissions with access to advanced Analytics and SEO modules\nWeb accessibility using Siteimprove\nWeb accessibility means ensuring that everyone, regardless of their abilities or disabilities, can use websites and web apps. Website owners and content creators are required to maintain the accessibility of their websites and content. For help, see\ninformation about website remediation\n.\nWeb analytics using Siteimprove\nWeb analytics is the collection of data, such as visitor traffic, to give website developers and content creators insights into optimizing user experiences.\nAbout Siteimprove Analytics\ndemonstrates how to use Siteimprove to gather and analyze website data.\nWCMS-Siteimprove integration\nIndiana University's Web Content Management System (WCMS) integrates with Siteimprove in two ways: CMS reporting and CMS deeplinking. For more about these integrations and how to enable them, see\ninformation about integrating Siteimprove with WCMS\n.\nHelp and support\nFor help, see the following resources.\nSiteimprove request form\nYou can reach theSiteimprove team by submitting a request form:\nAdd or remove a website from Siteimprove\nRequest a website URL change\nGrant an individual access to a website in Siteimprove\nRequest a website rescan\nRequest Siteimprove reports\nReport a false positive issue on my site report\nComplete the request form\n.\nUITS Web team\nThe UITS Web team is a team dedicated to assisting web developers, administrators, and IT Professionals with the WCMS, Web Framework, and Sitehost web hosting environment.\nContact the UITS Web team\n.\nWeb Community of Practice\nThe Web Community of Practice (WebCoP) brings together staff from every campus who work on IU's digital campus. WebCOP has monthly meetings and a Slack team.\nJoin WebCoP\n.	KB0025483	KB0025483 v11.0	 permissions with access to advanced Analytics and SEO <strong>modules</strong> Web accessibility using Siteimprove Web, and Sitehost web hosting <strong>environment</strong>. Contact the UITS Web team. Web Community of Practice The Web	5.4788		2026-01-31 17:24:42.934134	2026-01-31 17:24:42.934134	0	dd946c18476e3a9c2fc6a55b416d43c7
12092	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f104c6763bde2610476d941864e45a30	Developer tools: Web Framework hosted brand and search assets	Important:\nSiteKube is replacing Sitehost. For more, see\nAbout SiteKube\n.\nTable of contents\nOverview\nBranding requirements\nBranding header\nFooter\nSearch\nUse the branding header, footer, and search\nAsset types\nCascading Style Sheet (CSS) assets\nJavaScript assets\nStatic HTML assets\nAsset location\nHTML sites\nAsset implementation\nMaking alterations to the brand assets\nPage boilerplate\nFooter\nImplementation examples\nHosted assets\nGlobal CSS\nGlobal JS\nGitHub repositories\nOverview\nDevelopers must follow all\nbranding requirements\nwhen building and maintaining IU sites. Developers can use a predefined set of\nCSS components and Javascript functionality\nby pointing to global assets. Anything you see in the\npage parts\nis included in these global assets.\nNote:\nThis article is about assets that developers can use to create IU-branded content outside of the WCMS. Using these assets is no longer recommended, and they will be retired in summer 2027. Instead, use the\nRivet Design System\nfor creating websites and web applications if they're outside of the WCMS. If you need the assets described below, such as for an existing site, continue reading.\nBranding requirements\nIf you have a school, program, department, affiliated unit, or other official IU site, you must have the branding header (also called the branding bar) and footer on each page. Search is an optional component.\nAll branding bars must extend across the page, and the name of the university or campus should function as a link to its home page.\nNo other graphics, text, or features can be added to the branding bars without approval from University Communications and Marketing (UCM).\nBranding header\nThe header branding bar features the Block IU in a red bar and goes at the top of every page of your website. Each campus has its own branding bar, but only the name and link change. These centrally hosted static HTML elements can be\naccessed by using a specific URL\n.\nFooter\nThe branding footer should feature the IU trident with links to accessibility information, the College Scorecard, the site's privacy notice, and copyright.\nIf you have an IU Web Framework site or your site points to the global brand footer, your site should automatically update.\nIf you have a Web Framework site that does not utilize server-side includes, you may need to republish your site to receive the update. To see if your Web Framework site uses includes, check the\nSettings block\n.\nSearch\nThe IU search function appears as a small white magnifying glass within the branding header. When users click on it, the Google Programmable Search box opens.\nThe search box assets are also centrally hosted. However, you may need additional configuration to correctly implement the search function on your site.\nUse the branding header, footer, and search\nTo learn more about the assets that make up the branding header, view the\nasset types\nsection. For more information about where these branding assets are located, view the\nasset location\nsection. For instructions on how to implement these assets within your website, view the\nimplementation\nsection.\nIf you would like to use the global IU Framework CSS and JS assets within your website, check out those instructions in the\nglobal CSS and JS\nsection.\nAsset types\nThe branding assets found in the branding package are divided into the following types:\nCascading Style Sheets:\nThese contain Cascading Style Sheet (CSS) code to describe the look and format of the branding elements.\nJavaScript file:\nThis file contains JavaScript code that enables search interaction and Google search implementation for the site.\nStatic HTML elements:\nThese elements contain HTML markup code for rendering branding elements on the page.\nCascading Style Sheet (CSS) assets\nThe branding package includes the following pre-compiled Cascading Style Sheets:\nbrand.css:\nThis CSS file contains styles to format the header and footer elements on the HTML page.\nsearch.css:\nThis CSS file contains styles to format the search box and results on the search results page of the site.\nJavaScript assets\nThe branding package contains only one JavaScript file:\nsearch.js\n. This file contains JavaScript functions to implement search toggle and Google Custom Search implementation for the site.\nStatic HTML assets\nThe static HTML elements provide the markup code to render the header and footer assets on the page. The static HTML files available in the branding package are listed below.\nHeader\nThe header element contains the HTML markup that renders the trident and the campus name for your site. The branding package includes header elements to support all the IU campuses. The naming convention followed is the linking of the word header with the campus name.\nFor example, the Indiana University Bloomington header is the file named\nheader-iub.html\n. The table below describes the list of headers that have been built and are available through the branding package.\nBranding header assets\nFile name\nCampus\nDomain name\nheader-iu.html\nIndiana University\niu.edu\nheader-iub.html\nIU Bloomington\nbloomington.iu.edu\nheader-iue.html\nIU East\neast.iu.edu\nheader-iuk.html\nIU Kokomo\nkokomo.iu.edu\nheader-iun.html\nIU Northwest\nnorthwest.iu.edu\nheader-iui.html\nIU Indianapolis\nindianapolis.iu.edu\nheader-ius.html\nIU Southeast\nsoutheast.iu.edu\nheader-iusb.html\nIU South Bend\nsouthbend.iu.edu\nheader-iufw.html\nIU Fort Wayne\nfortwayne.iu.edu\nheader-iuc.html\nIU Columbus\ncolumbus.iu.edu\nFooter\nThe footer element contains the HTML markup code required to render the footer on your site. The footer element consists of the tagline, copyright information, and privacy link.\nSearch\nThe search element contains the HTML markup code that describes a search box. The search box is used to accept user input for search implementation.\nRefer to the\nAsset location\nsection of this article to learn how to find the assets you need. To learn how to implement these assets, refer to the\nImplementation\nsection.\nAsset location\nIU branding assets are centrally hosted and are available through specific URLs. The URLs needed to brand your website depend on your campus and your version of HTML.\nHTML sites\nThese assets support sites that are written using HTML5 syntax specifications. You will need to include six URLs to appropriately brand your content. In the header URL below, "*" represents the campus value for your site.\nBranding assets for HTML5 sites\nAsset type\nURL\nCascading Style Sheet\nhttps://assets.iu.edu/brand/3.3.x/brand.css\nhttps://assets.iu.edu/search/3.3.x/search.css\nJavaScript\nhttps://assets.iu.edu/search/3.3.x/search.js\nStatic HTML elements\nhttps://assets.iu.edu/brand/3.3.x/header-*.html\nhttps://assets.iu.edu/brand/3.3.x/footer.html\nhttps://assets.iu.edu/search/3.3.x/search.html\nVersioning of the assets:\nThe assets are versioned following the convention of\nmajor.minor\nversion depending upon the bug fixes or improvements. To avoid confusion related to the usage of the latest versions, URLs are numbered as\nmajor version.\nx\n. The version is symbolically linked to the latest version on the server.\nSite widths\nThe branding header and footer elements are set to 1020 pixels.\nIf you have a site that is of a different width, you can still use the header and footer elements. They will just be a little narrower or wider than your content.\nIf you need to match the width of your site, you can add the following CSS rules within your site’s CSS. (Be sure to update with the correct value.)\n#branding-bar-v1\n.wrapper,\n#footer-v1 .wrapper\n{ max-width: 965px !important; }\nAsset implementation\nTo implement the header and footer branding assets on your site, you will need to do two things:\nPoint to the\nbrand.css\nstylesheet\nInclude the\nheader-*.html\nand\nfooter.html\nmarkup elements on your site's pages\nYou\nmay not\ninclude tools, social media icons, other links, or anything else in the header, footer, and search branding assets.\nMaking alterations to the brand assets\nThe header, footer, and search brand assets must be implemented as they are unless search is a primary function of your site. If that is the case on your site, you may make limited alterations. No other alterations are allowed.\nSearch\nThe search in the branding bar is optional. For example, if your site doesn't need search, or if your site includes the Google Programmable Search function within the primary content area of your site, you can remove the search functionality by simply not including the search asset and associated script.\nImplement Google Programmable Search on your site\nTo implement the Google Programmable Search assets on your site, you will need to include a placeholder for rendering the search form in the site header section and initialization for Google Programmable Search. To learn how to customize search results for your website, see\nSet up a Custom Google Search Engine for your IU website\n.\nThe elements needed for search implementation are:\nsearch.css\nsearch.js\nsearch.html\nSearch initialization\nThe search module can be initialized using following code.\nThis code can be included in a local file, inline inside the\n$(document).ready\nblock, or as a standalone script inside the document without being wrapped inside the\n$(document).ready\nblock.\n/*!\n* Site Specific IU Search Init */\nvar IUSearch = window.IUSearch || {};\nvar searchOptions = {\nCX: {\nsite: 'xxxx:xxxx', // Replace this with a site CX value\nall: 'xxxx:xxxx' // Replace this with campus CX value },\nwrapClass: 'row pad',\nsearchBoxIDs: ['search', 'off-canvas-search']\n}\n/* Initialize global search */ IUSearch.init && IUSearch.init( searchOptions );\nThe\nsearch.js\nfile should be included in the document prior to initialization of search code shown above.\nSearch functions in the\nsearch.js\nfile implement the Google Programmable Search Engine functions.\nAs shown below, CX values for both the site and campus can be passed during the search initialization. If your site doesn't have a CX ID, refer to the\nGoogle search engine documentation\nto find it.\nGoogle Search CX values for a few IU campuses\nCampus names and corresponding CX values\nCampus name\nCX\nIU\n016278320858612601979:ddl1l9og1w8\nIU Bloomington\n016278320858612601979:w_w23ggyftu\nIU Indianapolis\n016278320858612601979:pwcza8pis6k\nIU Fort Wayne\n014162612540646926707:vjfmkkqguyo\nSearch results page\nCreate a search results page and include the following markup. Remember to replace\n*\nwith your campus name. This will allow you to toggle between search results for your site and your campus.\n<form class="search-toggle reset">\n<label>Expand your search</label> <input checked="checked" name="searchToggle" type="radio" value="site" />\n<label>This Site</label> <input name="searchToggle" type="radio" value="all" /> <label>All of Indiana University</label>\n</form>\n<div id="inline-search" class="search-box search-results"></div>\n<noscript>\n<h4>Message will be updated</h4>\n<div class="search-box no-script">\n<form class="gsc-search-box gsc-search-box-tools" accept-charset="utf-8" action="https://www.google.com/cse/publicurl" target="_blank">\n<table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" class="gsc-search-box">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class="gsc-input">\n<div class="gsc-input-box">\n<table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class="gsib_a">\n<input autocomplete="off" type="text" size="10" class="gsc-input" name="q"\ntitle="search" dir="ltr" spellcheck="false">\n<input type="hidden" name="cx" value="014109358301568672738:sj37ltqceas">\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td class="gsc-search-button">\n<input type="submit" class="gsc-search-button gsc-search-button-v2"\ntitle="search" value="Go">\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</form>\n</div>\n<!-- /.search-box -->\n</noscript>\nPage boilerplate\nAll the elements described above should be included on your page as described in this HTML boilerplate:\n<html>\n<head>\n<!-- stylesheet assets - brand.css, search.css -->\n<!-- site css -->\n</head>\n<body>\n<header id="header">\n<!-- include campus header element - header-*.html-->\n<!-- include search element - search.html-->\n</header>\n<main>\n<!-- site content -->\n</main>\n<!-- include footer element - footer.html-->\n<!-- javascript assets - search.js-->\n<!-- site javascript files -->\n<!-- search initialization -->\n</body>\n</html>\nFooter\nThe branding footer should feature the IU trident with links to copyright and accessibility information, as well as a link to the site’s privacy notice.\nIf your site does not use the IU Web Framework, update your footer’s markup to use the latest\nfooter markup\n. Then, update your CSS to include the latest footer CSS. The styles required in this case are all of the selectors which include #footer .tagline (such as #footer .tagline.bicentennial), and they can be found within\nthis CSS file\n. Please note that the second set of styles with these selectors are within a media query.\nUpdate the privacy link in the footer\nThe footer branding element also includes a link to the privacy policy for your site. The link defaults to an index page of the root-level ‘privacy’ folder of the site; virtualhostname/privacy/. To modify this link, use the following javascript code:\nvar\nnewPrivacyURL = 'http://site.iu.edu/privacy.html';\n(oldPrivacyURL = document.getElementById('privacy-policy-link')) ?\n(oldPrivacyURL.href = newPrivacyURL) : '';\nSocial media icons and tools\nIf you currently house social media channels, quick links, or tools in your header or footer, you must find another place for them after installing the new brand assets.\nIU Studios has created a solution for sites using a mobile-first framework. We developed a “skirt” area above the footer where these types of links can be housed.\nYou can see it in action on the\nIU Bloomington home page\n.\nImplementation examples\nThe following section describes a few methods that can be used for including IU branding assets on your site.\nThe branding implementation method that will work best for you will depend on your hosting environment, site development technology, and other factors.\nExamples for sites residing on IU Linux web hosting environment (Sitehost)\nFor sites residing in the IU Linux web hosting environment (Sitehost), the branding assets can be symbolically linked to the\ngwassets\nfolder and implemented using the following steps.\nCreate a symbolic link to the branding assets folder that is under the account "gwassets" by running the following command:\nln\n-s /groups/gwassets/web gwassets\nUse a PHP include script or server side include script to include the HTML markup elements that are inside the gwassets folder.\nTo use the PHP include method:\n<?php include\n"gwassets/brand/3.3.x/header-iu.html";>\nIn order to avoid using the dot patterns to match the filenames created in the include path, the following lines should be added to the php.ini file.\n<?php\ninclude_path=".:/groups/accountname/web";\n?>\nTo use the SSI (Server Side Includes):\nThe Apache server allows you to place SSI directives that enable including elements on a HTML page. Here is an example of a Server Side Include statement.\n<!--#include virtual="../header-iu.html" -->\nExamples for sites requiring a local copy of the branding markup\nFor sites requiring a local copy of the branding markup:\nRefer to the\nasset location\nsection to determine the URLs that are appropriate for your site.\nCreate local copies of the contents of the asset files.\nCopy the source markup and include it in your page as indicated above.\nHosted assets\nThis section describes implementing Global CSS and JS.\nGlobal CSS\nYou can use the current version of global CSS by including the following in the\n<head>\nof your page.\n<link type="text/css" href="//assets.iu.edu/web/3.3.x/css/iu-framework.min.css">\nGlobal JS\nThe current version of global JavaScript depends on a custom implementation of Modernizr and jQuery > 2.0. You should include Modernizr in the\n<head>\nportion of your page.\nUsing the\n<script></script>\ntags, add the following to the opening tag:\nsrc="http://assets.iu.edu/web/1.5/libs/modernizr.min.js"\nOnce you have included Modernizr, you can use the current version of global JavaScript by including the following script. Since the global JavaScript depends on jQuery, you must include this file\nafter\nyour implementation of jQuery.\nUsing the\n<script></script>\ntags, add the following to the opening tag:\nsrc="//assets.iu.edu/web/3.3.x/js/iu-framework.min.js"\nFinally, for global JavaScript to be in initialized, use the following after the global JavaScript file:\n/*! *\nInitialize IU JS\n*/\n(function($, window, document, undefined) {\n$(document).ready(function() {\nFoundation.OffCanvas.defaults.transitionTime = 500;\nFoundation.OffCanvas.defaults.forceTop = false;\nFoundation.OffCanvas.defaults.positiong = 'right';\nFoundation.Accordion.defaults.multiExpand = true;\nFoundation.Accordion.defaults.allowAllClosed = true;\n// Initialize Foundation $(document).foundation();\nvar\nIU = window.IU || {};\n// Delete modules if necessary\n// delete IU.uiModules['accordion'];\nIU.init && IU.init();\n});\n})(jQuery, window, window.document);\nGitHub repositories\nTo see the GitHub repositories for the Global CSS and JS, contact the UITS Web team (\nwebhelp@iu.edu\n).	KB0026567	KB0026567 v4.0	 method that will work best for you will depend on your hosting <strong>environment</strong>, site development technology, and other factors. Examples for sites residing on IU Linux web hosting <strong>environment</strong> (Sitehost) For sites residing in the IU Linux web hosting <strong>environment</strong> (Sitehost), the branding assets	4.3675	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:24:47.30146	2026-01-31 17:24:47.30146	0	f104c6763bde2610476d941864e45a30
12097	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d52038ff476456583d373803846d438f	Edit distribution groups	Table of contents\nOverview\nEdit an on-premises Active Directory distribution group\nOverview\nBecause Indiana University's\nActive Directory\nresides on-premises, and\nExchange\naccounts reside in\nMicrosoft 365\n, it is not possible to use\nOutlook\nand\nOutlook on the web\nto edit an existing on-premises Active Directory group (see\nOwners of an on-premises distribution group synced to O365 can't manage the distribution group in Exchange Online\n). To edit membership of a distribution group created using an on-premises AD group, you will need to use a different method. The manager of the AD group or a\nlocal UITS support person\ncan use the instructions below.\nDistribution groups created directly in Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory or AAD) can be managed using\nOutlook\nand\nOutlook on the web\n. The Enterprise Microsoft Administration team (formerly CCI) is developing a method to allow local UITS support people to create groups directly in the Entra ID environment.\nFor more on methods of sending mail to multiple recipients at IU, see\nEmail large groups of people at IU\n.\nEdit an on-premises Active Directory distribution group\nNote:\nSomeone with edit access to the Active Directory group (either the listed manager of the group or a local UITS support person) will need to complete these steps to edit the Active Directory group membership as needed.\nTo access Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC), you can use IUanyWare (see\nSet up and use IUanyWare\n), or you can install Remote Server Administration Tools on a Windows computer (see\nInstall RSAT features in Windows 10 version 1809 or Windows Server 2019 and later\n).\nTo edit a group with ADUC:\nOpen ADUC. If your edit access to AD groups has been granted through a service account, launch ADUC as the service account.\nGo to\nAction\n, then\nFind...\n, and then use the search dialog to search for the AD group.\nDouble-click the group in the search results.\nGo to the\nmembers\ntab. Use the\nAdd...\nand\nRemove\nbuttons and associated dialog boxes to add and remove users as necessary.\nYou can also edit Active Directory groups with PowerShell. For more, see\nAdd-ADGroupMember\nand\nRemove-ADGroupMember\n.	KB0025363	KB0025363 v1.0	 support people to create groups directly in the Entra ID <strong>environment</strong>. For more on methods of sending	3.6764		2026-01-31 17:24:52.451387	2026-01-31 17:24:52.451387	0	d52038ff476456583d373803846d438f
11896	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f930c7af47d5dad49286dfbd436d4383	About 1Password for IU departments	Table of contents\nOverview\nRequest a 1Password vault\nSet up 1Password\nAccess 1Password\nImport passwords\nGet help\nOverview\n1Password\nis an enterprise password solution available to Indiana University departments. This solution allows departments to securely share passwords and other secrets.\nNote:\nThis document summarizes how departments can leverage 1Password for shared storage. IU students, faculty, and staff are also entitled to free access to 1Password for personal use. For more, see\nAbout 1Password for personal use\n.\nRequest a 1Password vault\nTo request a shared 1Password vault for your IU department, fill out the\n1Password Vault Creation\nform. On the form, you will need to specify an ACM group with\nAcceptable Use Agreement\ncompliance enabled for the vault's Edit group (the people who will have the ability to read, create, edit, and delete passwords in this 1Password vault). Optionally, you can specify an additional ACM group for people who should have read-only access. If different groups need access to separate sets of passwords, you will need to create more than one vault.\nSet up 1Password\nWhen group members are added to a 1Password vault, they receive an invitation via email. They then have five days to follow the link in the email to set up their 1Password account.\nImportant:\nTo prevent account lockout and the need for account recovery, it is crucial that members sign into 1Password using the desktop application or mobile app in addition to the web browser. This ensures that they can access their accounts if their web sessions expire. Due to 1Password's built-in two-factor authentication, using at least two of the provided options (web browser, desktop application, and/or mobile app) is highly recommended.\nAccess 1Password\nOnce your 1Password account is set up, you can access it in a web browser:\nGo to\nhttps://indianauniversity.1password.com\n.\nUnder "Sign in to 1Password", enter your\nIU email address\n, and then select\nContinue\n.\nSelect\nMicrosoft Login\n; you will be redirected to an IU Microsoft login prompt.\nIf you are signing into a browser, desktop, or mobile application for the first time, you will be prompted to enter a verification code. You can get the code from any other browser, desktop, or mobile application. You should see a prompt like this:\nImport passwords\nIf you have an existing password manager, you can import those passwords into a 1Password vault. If you have an instance of Passwordstate EAS Vault, an administrator can export your password lists to a CSV file. Make sure to delete this file and any backups when you are finished.\nLog into\nhttps://indianauniversity.1password.com\n.\nOn the home page, in the top right, select your profile, and then select\nImport\n.\nFollow the on-screen instructions. Make sure to select the correct vault for the import.\nNotes:\nYou must select from three password types:\nCredit Card\n,\nNote\n, or\nLogin\n. In most cases, selecting\nLogin\nwill be the best choice. This type requires username, password, and website URL. You must have one password with the website URL listed; the rest can be blank if a website URL doesn't apply to them.\nCheck your CSV file to ensure that none of the passwords have been turned into formulas or had leading zeros removed.\nYou can add nested tags by separating them with forward slashes.\nGet help\nFor help using 1Password, see\n1Password Support\n. If you have questions about using 1Password at IU, email Enterprise Infrastructure Help (\neihelp@iu.edu\n).	KB0025806	KB0025806 v2.0	: This document summarizes how departments can leverage 1Password for shared <strong>storage</strong>. IU students into https://indianauniversity.1password.com. On the <strong>home</strong> page, in the top right, select your	4.5294	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:22:14.699569	2026-01-31 17:22:14.69