12252	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=65a9e8cf4774de1c3d373803846d433c	Update IUIE News	On this page:\nOverview\nLocking\nAccess\nInitial page\nButtons\nNews message editing:\nEdit fields\nActions\nOverview\nThe process of updating\nIUIE News\nin the\nIUIE\nconsists of the following steps:\nAdd, edit, delete, and/or move messages, and then preview the changes.\nIf the preview looks okay, select the\nUpdate IUIE News\nlink and save the changes.\nLocking\nNews messages are locked during editing to prevent one user from overwriting changes made by another. If the news messages are locked, you will receive the following message, where\nUSERNAME\nis the owner of the lock and\nN\nis the number of minutes the lock will be maintained:\n"The news is locked by USERNAME, who must save their changes within the next N minute(s)."\nIf you are granted a lock, it will be held for 10 minutes. After 10 minutes have elapsed, your lock will be cancelled and other users may acquire the lock. Your lock will also be released if you save your changes by selecting\nSave\n.\nWhenever you use an action or button, you'll see a message telling you how much time is left on your lock, for example:\n"You have 1 minute(s) left to Save your changes."\nIf you don't save your changes before your lock expires, they will be lost.\nAccess\nOnly users in the News Editor group (\nIE_NEWS_EDITORS_R\n) can update news.\nYou must be a member of an application publishing group to edit news messages for an application.\nYour access to messages is restricted to the list of applications in the\nAdd an Application Message Area\ndrop-down menu at the bottom of the page. If an application has an existing message, it will not appear in the drop-down menu.\nInitial page\n"Update IUIE News" starts with a display of all the active messages to which you have access. If there are no messages, you will see the following:\n"There are no News messages."\nYou can use the drop-down menu at the bottom of the page to create a message area for all applications you can access.\nThe title of the first message of each section (the message numbered 1) is on a blue background, while subsequent messages within a section are on white. This gives a visual cue to the section breaks when there are multiple messages in one section.\nButtons\nButton descriptions\nButton\nDescription\nAdd an Application Message Area\nThis drop-down contains a list of all applications for which you are a member of the associated publishing group. If messages for an application are already present in the news, that application is not listed in the drop-down menu.\nPreview\nSelecting this button saves changes on the page and invokes IUIE News to display how these changes will appear when saved. Select the\nUpdate IUIE News\nlink, and then select\nSave\nif the changes look okay, or make further changes if not.\nSave\nThe\nSave\nbutton makes changes permanent. IUIE News is updated every five minutes, so any saved changes will not appear in the news until the next run of IUIE News completes.\nCancel\nThe\nCancel\nbutton returns you to the IUIE Utilities menu.\nNews message editing\nEdit fields\nEdit Field descriptions\nEdit field\nDescription\nStarting Date\nEnding Date\nSelect the calendar icon to open a calendar in which you can set the text string date and time.\nYou must select a day to close the calendar. If you don't need to change the date, select the highlighted day. Otherwise, select the desired day.\nChange the month using the drop-down menu at the upper left.\nChange the hour and minute by editing the hour and minute text fields at the bottom.\nWhen a new message is inserted, the starting date defaults to the current date and time. The ending date defaults to the same time on the following day.\nNews Message\nThe news message is plain text containing fewer than 4,000 characters. Embedded HTML will not work.\nActions\nAction descriptions\nAction\nDescription\nThe current message will be moved above the preceding message.\nThe first message in an application message area has no preceding message, and thus cannot be moved up.\nThe current message will be moved below the following message.\nThe last message in an application message area has no following message, and thus cannot be moved down.\nInsert a new blank message, at the current date and time, above the current message.\nInsert a new blank message, at the current date and time, below the current message.\nDelete the current message.	KB0022455	KB0022455 v2.0	On this page: Overview Locking Access Initial page Buttons News message editing: Edit fields Actions Overview The process of updating IUIE News in the IUIE consists of the following steps: Add, edit, delete, and/or move messages, and then preview the changes. If the preview looks okay, select the Update IUIE News link and save the changes. Locki	2.8975		2026-01-31 17:32:21.064456	2026-01-31 17:32:21.064456	0	65a9e8cf4774de1c3d373803846d433c
12255	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=85b765171bb0565416c02fc4bd4bcbd9	About Nano	Table of contents\nIntroduction\nCreating or editing a file\nBasic operations\nOther features\nCommand overview\nIntroduction\nBased on the Pico message composition editor, Nano is a simple, display-oriented text editor for\nUnix\n. Although less powerful than programs such as\nvi\nand Emacs, Nano's basic feature set makes it easier for beginners to learn and use. To add text, just type it in, and it immediately becomes part of the document. You can also use the arrow keys to move about the document. Following is a brief overview of working with files in Nano.\nCreating or editing a file\nTo create a new file or edit an existing one, at your Unix\nshell\nprompt, type:\nNano filename\nReplace\nfilename\nwith the name of the file you want to create or edit. For example, to create a file and name it\nindiana.txt\n, enter:\nNano indiana.txt\nIf the file already exists, Nano opens it for you to edit. If it doesn't exist yet, Nano creates it and places you in an editing buffer.\nBasic operations\nNano displays a menu bar of commonly used commands at the bottom of the screen. Nano accepts commands from your keyboard, but not from your mouse. The following are some of Nano's basic operations:\nInserting text:\nTo insert text into your Nano editing screen at the cursor, just begin typing. Nano inserts the text to the left of the cursor, moving any existing text along to the right. Each time the cursor reaches the end of a line, Nano's word wrap feature automatically moves it to the beginning of the next line. (Also see\nJustifying text\nbelow.)\nCursor movement:\nTo move the cursor, use the arrow keys or use the following commands:\nCtrl-f\nMoves the cursor forward one character\nCtrl-b\nMoves the cursor backward one character\nCtrl-n\nMoves the cursor down to the next line\nCtrl-p\nMoves the cursor up to the previous line\nNote:\nSee the\nCommand overview\nfor more cursor movement commands.\nDeleting text:\nTo delete the character to the left of the cursor, press\nBackspace\n,\nDelete\n, or\nCtrl\n+\nh\n. To delete the character highlighted by the cursor, press\nCtrl\n+\nd\n. To delete the current line, press\nCtrl\n+\nk\n.\nSaving your work:\nTo save your edited file to disk, press\nCtrl\n+\no\n. Nano displays the current filename. (To save the file under a different name, delete the filename that Nano displays and type a new one.) Press\nEnter\n.\nExiting Nano:\nTo exit Nano, press\nCtrl\n+\nx\n. If you made any changes since the last save, Nano will ask whether or not to save them. Type\ny\n(for yes) or\nn\n(for no). If you type\ny\n, Nano displays the filename. (To save the edited file under a different name, delete the filename and type a new one.) Press\nEnter\n.\nOther features\nSearching for text:\nNano lets you search forward from the current cursor position for any text string you specify. Press\nCtrl\n+\nw\n(for whereis) to invoke the search. Nano will prompt you for a search term. Type the text you're looking for and press\nEnter\n. Nano will move the cursor to the first instance of the text string you entered. You can find additional occurrences by pressing\nCtrl\n+\nw\nagain.\nJustifying text:\nAs you type, Nano's word wrap automatically begins a new line when needed. However, when you edit existing text, you may create text lines that are either too short or too long. To re-wrap (that is, justify) a paragraph, move the cursor to that paragraph and press\nCtrl\n+\nj\n. To undo this action and restore the paragraph to its original condition, press\nCtrl\n+\nu\n.\nCut and paste functions:\nTo cut and paste text lines with Nano, first place the cursor on the text line you wish to cut and press\nCtrl\n+\nk\nto remove it. To cut and paste two or more consecutive text lines, press\nCtrl\n+\nk\nuntil all the text lines are removed. Then move the cursor to the location where you want to paste the text and press\nCtrl\n+\nu\n. Nano will paste the text back into the file at the new cursor position.\nYou can also cut and paste text blocks. To do so:\nMove the cursor over the first character of the text you want to remove, and then press\nCtrl\n+\n^\n(the caret,\nCtrl-Shift-6\n) to set the mark.\nUse the arrow keys to highlight the text you wish to cut, and then press\nCtrl\n+\nk\nto cut the text. Be sure you got all of the text you wanted, including the last character.\nMove the cursor to the place where you want to insert the text, and then press\nCtrl\n+\nu\nto paste the text into the new position.\nInserting an existing text file:\nTo insert the contents of an existing file at the cursor location, press\nCtrl\n+\nr\n. Nano will prompt you for a filename. Either type the filename and press\nEnter\n, or press\nCtrl\n+\nt\nto select from a list of available files. Nano will enter the File Browser, which displays a list of files in your current working directory. Use the arrow keys to highlight the file you wish to insert and press\nEnter\n. Select the parent directory (..) to move up the directory tree. Select a subdirectory to move down the directory tree.\nBesides inserting text, you can use the File Browser to rename, delete, or copy any file, even a file in another directory. To exit the File Browser, press\ne\n.\nSpell checking:\nTo use the spell checker, press\nCtrl\n+\nt\n. When Nano discovers a word it does not recognize, it will highlight the word and prompt you to enter a replacement. You can type a replacement or press\nEnter\nto keep the original word. Nano then continues to the next misspelled word. When Nano has checked your entire document, it will return the cursor to its original position.\nRecovering your work:\nIf your Nano session crashes, Nano will attempt to save a copy of the file you were working on. Look in your working directory for a filename with the extension\n.save\n.\nCommand overview\nThe following list contains most of the commands you'll need to work with Nano:\nCtrl-f\nMove forward one character.\nCtrl-b\nMove backward one character.\nCtrl-p\nMove to the previous line.\nCtrl-n\nMove to the next line.\nCtrl-a\nMove to the beginning of the line.\nCtrl-e\nMove to the end of the line.\nCtrl-v\nMove forward one page.\nCtrl-y\nMove backward one page.\nCtrl-d\nDelete the character at the cursor position.\nCtrl-i\nInsert a tab at the cursor position.\nCtrl-^\nMark or unmark the beginning of a block of text.\nCtrl-k\nIf a block of text has been selected with a mark, cuts the entire block. Otherwise, cuts the current line.\nCtrl-u\nPaste at the cursor position the last text cut.\nCtrl-l\nRedraw the screen.\nCtrl-w\nSearch for text.\nCtrl-j\nJustify the current paragraph.\nCtrl-t\nStart the spell checker.\nCtrl-c\nReport the cursor position.\nCtrl-r\nRead a file into the document at the cursor position.\nCtrl-o\nSave the file (without exiting Nano).\nCtrl-x\nExit Nano, giving you the option to save the file.\nCtrl-g\nGet help.\nFor more information, read Nano's\nman\npage. To do so, at the Unix prompt, enter:\nman Nano\nAt Indiana University, for personal or departmental Linux or Unix systems support, see\nGet help for Linux or Unix at IU\n.	KB0023923	KB0023923 v2.0	Table of contents Introduction Creating or editing a file Basic operations Other features Command overview Introduction Based on the Pico message composition editor, Nano is a simple, display-oriented text editor for Unix. Although less powerful than programs such as vi and Emacs, Nano's basic feature set makes it easier for beginners to learn a	2.6529		2026-01-31 17:32:28.590203	2026-01-31 17:32:28.590203	0	85b765171bb0565416c02fc4bd4bcbd9
12257	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=23ad867747def610d500b87b716d4319	Resources for 3D printing	Table of contents\nOverview\nObtain 3D models\nCreate 3D models\nPaid software\nTutorials\nLab locations and access\nIU Bloomington\nIU Indianapolis\nIU Columbus\nIU East\nIU South Bend\nIU Southeast\nOverview\nThis page lists resources to help you get started with 3D printing at IU. For further details, see\nUITS 3D Print Labs\n.\nObtain 3D models\nThingiverse\nis a community-based Makerbot website where you can download free models that are ready for printing, and find instructions and print settings for most models.\nMy Mini Factory\nis also a community based website where you can download all sorts of different models, primarily props from games, movies, and other sources of entertainment.\nShapeways\nis a professional 3D print service that also has a vast 3D model repository. The models aren't free, but they're guaranteed to be high quality.\nCreate 3D models\nBeginner\nTinkercad\nIntermediate\nPhotoshop, available from\nIUware\nAdvanced\nBlender\nAutodesk products\n(Free through student or educational versions)\nFusion 360\nMaya\n3DS Max\nAutoCAD\nInventor\nPaid software\nAdvanced/professional\nZBrush\nCinema 4D\nRhino\nTutorials\nBeginner\nTinkercad\nModeling for 3D printing: A guide for beginners\n(uses Tinkercad)\nAdvanced\nThe following resources assume that you are already fluent in the program of choice; they are\nnot\ntutorials for the actual software.\nHow to Prepare Your Render/Animation Model for 3D Printing\n(Maya)\n3D Printing your ZBrush Sculpts\nLab locations and access\nList of labs at IU campuses\nCampus\nLab name\nLocation\nContact\nAudience\nIU Bloomington\nCreator Commons Makerspace\nWells Library West Tower, 1st floor, W142\nlibmaker@iu.edu\nAll students, faculty, and staff\nProtolab I: Open Access Lab\nMyles Brand Hall E120\nmakehelp@iu.edu\nLuddy School; open to all IU students, faculty, and staff\nLuddy Fab Lab: Curricular Lab\nLuddy Hall 4010\nmakehelp@iu.edu\nLuddy School\nInnovative Lab: Teter Living-Learning Center\nTeter Quadrangle, Luddy Living-Learning Community\nmakehelp@iu.edu\nLuddy LLC students\nMake, Innovate, Learn Lab (MILL)\nWright Education Building 2260\nmillsoe@iu.edu\nSchool of Education; open to all IU students, faculty, and staff\nFine Arts Fabrication Lab\nFine Arts 141\nsoadfab@iu.edu\nEskenazi School; open to all IU students, faculty, and staff\nWood and Metal Shop\n822 E. 12th Street\nnmo@iu.edu\nEskenazi School; open to all IU students, faculty, and staff\nKirkwood Hall Fabrication Lab\nKirkwood 014\nsoadfab@iu.edu\nEskenazi School; open to all IU students, faculty, and staff\nIU Makerspace\nIndiana Memorial Union 150\nclubs@iu.edu\nStudent organizations\nIU Indianapolis\nIdea Garden\nHine Hall IP106\nigarden@iu.edu\nAll students\nThink It Make It Lab\nEskenazi Hall 183 and 111A\nherrtimi@iu.edu\nThink It Make It Lab\nAll students\nUniversity Library 4135G\nulems@iu.edu\nAll students\nInformatics and Communications Complex 255\ninform1@iu.edu\nSOIC students\nEngineering Technology Building 101\net3dprt@iu.edu\nEngineering and Technology students\nIU School of Medicine Makerspace\nMedical Education and Research Building HA 2061\nrlmltech@iu.edu\nIUSM Makerspace\nMedical students\nIU Columbus\nColumbus Center 233\nmendezja@iu.edu\nAll students\nIU East\nHayes Hall 127\ntiscales@iu.edu\nAll students, faculty, and staff\nIU South Bend\nEducation and Arts Building 2010\nryweber@iu.edu\nWide Format and 3D Printing\nAll students, faculty, and staff (also open to the public)\nIU Southeast\nLife Science Building 104\nAll students, faculty, and staff (by appointment only)\nMany of these spaces have other equipment and hardware aside from 3D printing. Reach out to the individual lab for equipment specifics, including what types of printers and materials are available.	KB0025603	KB0025603 v4.0	Table of contents Overview Obtain 3D models Create 3D models Paid software Tutorials Lab locations and access IU Bloomington IU Indianapolis IU Columbus IU East IU South Bend IU Southeast Overview This page lists resources to help you get started with 3D printing at IU. For further details, see UITS 3D Print Labs. Obtain 3D models Thingiverse is	2.6474		2026-01-31 17:32:32.023682	2026-01-31 17:32:32.023682	0	23ad867747def610d500b87b716d4319
12261	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c3eeb71a4774b6942fc6a55b416d43d4	Linux IU Common Build administrative tasks	Table of contents\nOverview\nUbuntu 24.04 GA1\nProxy settings\nAccount management\nGet help\nOverview\nFollowing are post-installation configuration tasks and guidance on authorizing and deauthorizing additional user accounts.\nUbuntu 24.04 GA1\nProxy settings\nLog into the system, open a Terminal, and then run the following command:\n/usr/local/uems/python/uems.py --system config-service-proxy\nImportant:\nDo not use\nsudo\nto run this script; you will be prompted for credentials.\nFollow the on-screen prompts to set the necessary proxy settings for system updates and applicable services. If you are behind a proxy, failure to do this will inhibit update reception and security protection.\nAccount management\nAt some point, you may want to add/remove accounts and/or\nsudo\nprivileges:\nIn a Terminal, enter the following:\n/usr/local/uems/python/uems.py --accounts manage-users\nImportant:\nDo not use\nsudo\n.\nEnter your password (an account with\nsudo\nprivileges is required).\nUse the Account Management tool provided to control which users can sign in. (See the installation guide for instructions and information about the\nRemove Account\nbutton.\nTo add a\nsudo\naccount, enter the account name (without\n@iu.edu\n), and then select\nAdd sudo Account\n. This gives login and\nsudo\nprivileges.\nTo add a login-only account, enter the account name, and then select\nAdd Account\n. This gives login access without\nsudo\nprivileges.\nAccounts are displayed in two lists;\nsudo\naccounts are listed on the right, and login-only accounts are listed on the left.\nReview and apply your changes:\nTo apply changes, select\nConfirm\n. The application will close and display the results in the Terminal.\nNote:\nChanges take effect immediately once confirmed. Select\nConfirm\nonly after you are satisfied.\nTo exit without saving, select the\nX\nin the upper right corner.\nGet help\nUse the\nLinux Support Request\nfor Linux Common Build requests.	KB0023845	KB0023845 v4.0	, and then run the following command: /usr/local/uems/<strong>python</strong>/uems.py --system config-service-proxy Important the following: /usr/local/uems/<strong>python</strong>/uems.py --accounts manage-users Important: Do not use sudo	7.7414	km-aud-uits, km-aud-local-support	2026-01-31 17:32:35.394905	2026-01-31 17:32:35.394905	0	c3eeb71a4774b6942fc6a55b416d43d4
12265	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=c1df6cf7476456583d373803846d4354	Tools for use with II Enterprise S3 Object Storage	On this page:\nOverview\nClusters\nS3CMD\nS4CMD\nS3CURL\nCyberduck\nWinSCP\nOverview\nUse these command-line Linux tools to interface with Indiana University's\nII Enterprise S3 Object Storage\nservice.\nClusters\nProduction clusters:\nIU Bloomington:\ns3b\nIU Indianapolis:\ns3i\nTest clusters:\nIU Bloomington:\ns3b-tst\nIU Indianapolis:\ns3i-tst\nS3CMD\nThe Amazon S3 command-line utility\ns3cmd\nsupports multi-part uploading but not multi-threading. It will accept input piped from other commands. Configuring access for one namespace in a tenant allows access to other namespaces in the tenant that use the same access key and secret key (username and password).\nTo set up\ns3cmd\nin CentOS:\nInstall\ns3cmd\n; run:\nyum install s3cmd\nTo create the\n~/.s3cfg\nfile, run:\ns3cmd --configure\nDo the following for each prompt:\n"Access Key": Enter the base64 hash of the username for the namespace.\n"Secret Key": Enter the base64 hash of the password for the namespace.\n"Default Region [US]": Press\nEnter\n.\n"S3 Endpoint [s3.amazonaws.com]": Enter\nexample-tenantname\n.s3b.uits.iu.edu\n.\n"[%(bucket)s.s3.amazonaws.com]": Enter\nexample-tenantname\n.s3b.uits.iu.edu/\nexample-namespace\n.\n"Encryption password": Enter a password.\n"Path to GPG program [/usr/bin/gpg]": Press\nEnter\n.\n"Use HTTPS protocol [Yes]": Press\nEnter\n.\n"HTTP Proxy server name": Press\nEnter\n.\n"Test access with supplied credentials? [Y/n]": Press\nn\n.\n"Save settings? [y/N]": Press\ny\n.\nEdit\n~/.s3cfg\nand make sure that each of the following is correct; you'll need to\nadd\nthe URL of the tenant to the\nwebsite_endpoint\nline, as this is not done in the initial configuration:\naccess_key\n: The base64 hash of the username for the namespace\nhost_base\n: The path to the tenant without protocol\nhost_bucket\n: The path to the namespace (without the\nhttps://\nprotocol)\nsecret_key\n: MD5 hash of the password\nwebsite_endpoint\n: URL of the tenant (including the\nhttps://\nprotocol)\nFollowing are some example commands:\nTo list files in a namespace, run:\ns3cmd ls s3://<namespace>\nTo put a file in a namespace, run:\ns3cmd put <local_file> s3://<namespace>/<remote_file>\nTo put a file in a namespace with a 500 MB chunk size, run:\ns3cmd --multipart-chunk-size-mb=500 put <local_file> s3://<namespace>/<remote_file>\nTo zip the output of another command (for example,\ntar\nor an SQL dump) and uploaded it, run:\n<command> | gzip -1 | s3cmd --multipart-chunk-size-mb=500 put - s3://<namespace>/<remote_file>\nTo get a file from a namespace, run:\ns3cmd get s3://<namespace>/<remote_file>\nS4CMD\nThe Python package\ns4cmd\nsupports multi-part and multi-threaded uploads, but it doesn't accept piped input. You can configure authentication for\ns4cmd\nusing the configuration files from\ns3cmd\nor by passing the access key and secret key as environment variables.\nTo set up\ns4cmd\n:\nMake sure Python is installed; run:\npython version\nInstall\nsc4cmd\n; run:\npip install s4cmd\nTo set up authentication, either install and configure\ns3cmd\n, or set the S3_ACCESS_KEY and S3_SECRET_KEY environment variables.\nFollowing are some example commands:\nTo list files in a namespace, run:\ns4cmd --endpoint-url https://<tenant>.<cluster>.uits.iu.edu ls s3://<namespace>\nTo get a file from a namespace, run:\ns4cmd --endpoint-url https://<tenant>.<cluster>.uits.iu.edu get s3://<namespace>/<remote_file>  <local_file>\nTo put a file in a namespace, run:\ns4cmd --endpoint-url https://<tenant>.<cluster>.uits.iu.edu put <local_file> s3://<namespace>/<remote_file>\nTo put a file in a namespace with a 500 MB chunk size, run:\ns4cmd --multipart-split-size=524288000 --endpoint-url https://<tenant>.<cluster>.uits.iu.edu put <local_file> s3://<namespace>/<remote_file>\nTo zip the output of another command (for example,\ntar\nor an SQL dump) and uploaded it, run:\n<command> | gzip -1 | s3cmd --multipart-chunk-size-mb=500 put - s3://<namespace>/<remote_file>\nTo get a file from a namespace, run:\ns3cmd get s3://<namespace>/<remote_file>\nS3CURL\ns3curl.pl\nis a Perl wrapper script for cURL. You can add multiple credentials to the configuration file and select them using the\n--id\nparameter.\nTo set up\ns3curl\n:\nDownload the\ns3curl\narchive; run:\nwget https://github.com/rtdp/s3curl/archive/master.zip\nUnzip the download, open\ns3curl.pl\nin an editor, and then edit the\n@endpoints\narray so that it has a single entry for the required tenant on the preferred cluster.\nCreate a\n~/.s3curl\nfile with the credentials for the tenant/namespace; for example:\n%awsSecretAccessKeys = (\nhcp=> {\nid => '<access_key>',\nkey => '<secret_key>',\n},\n);\nFollowing are some sample commands:\nTo list files in a namespace, run:\ns3curl --id hcp -- https://<tenant>.<cluster>.uits.iu.edu/<namespace>\nTo put a file in a namespace, run:\ns3curl --id hcp --put <local_file> -- -k "https://<tenant>.<cluster>.uits.iu.edu/<namespace>/<remote_file>"\nTo get a file from a namespace, run:\ns3curl --id hcp -- https://<tenant>.<cluster>.uits.iu.edu/<namespace>/<remote_file> > <local_file>\nCyberduck\nWhen using the II Enterprise S3 Object Storage at IU, you will need to set your preferences manually. To do so, follow the\ninstructions on modifying the Hidden Configuration Options\nand add the following options:\ns3.bucket.virtualhost.disable=true\nhttp.compression.enable=false\nAfter configuring the preference, you’ll need to open a new Cyberduck session.\nIU-specific connection settings:\nSelect\nAmazon S3\n.\nEnter the server name in the following format:\ntenantname.\ns3b\n.uits.iu.edu\nS3b\n(Bloomington) can be interchanged with\ns3i\n(Indianapolis).\nEnter the access key ID and secret access key for the specific namespace.\nFor the path, enter:\n/\nnamespace\n(replace\nnamespace\nwith the name of your namespace directory)\nAfter making these configurations, you can connect to your namespace in the S3 environment.\nWinSCP\nWinSCP can easily upload, download, and manage files in the II Enterprise S3 Object Storage environment. See\nWinSCP's instructions for connecting to Amazon S3\n.\nIU-specific connection settings in WinSCP:\nSelect the\nAmazon S3\nfile protocol.\nEnter the host name in the following format:\ntenantname.\ns3b\n.uits.iu.edu\nS3b\n(Bloomington) can be interchanged with\ns3i\n(Indianapolis).\nEnter the access key ID and secret access key for the specific namespace.\nSelect\nAdvanced\n>\nDirectories\n. For the remote directory, enter:\n/\nnamespace\n(replace\nnamespace\nwith the name of your namespace directory)\nAfter making these configurations, you can connect to your namespace in the S3 environment.	KB0023606	KB0023606 v1.0	 a namespace, run: s3cmd get s3://namespace/remote_file S4CMD The <strong>Python</strong> package s4cmd supports as environment variables. To set up s4cmd: Make sure <strong>Python</strong> is installed; run: <strong>python</strong> version Install	7.001	km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:32:43.709851	2026-01-31 17:32:43.709851	0	c1df6cf7476456583d373803846d4354
12266	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b473e68e47f026942fc6a55b416d433a	About the Canvas and Expand Provisioning API	Table of contents\nOverview\nRequest access\nGenerate the data files\nSet up a REST client or application to access the API\nSet up OAUTH2\nIssue the API call\nBase URLs\nEndpoints\nFormat the body of the API call\nResults messages\nExample\nCoding resources\nOverview\nThe Canvas and Expand Provisioning API lets schools and departments automate the process of provisioning IU\nGuest accounts\nand/or non-credit courses, sections, and enrollments in\nCanvas\nor Expand. Following are instructions for authenticating to the API and uploading provisioning data (as separate files or compressed into a single ZIP archive).\nRequest access\nAccess to the Canvas and Expand provisioning API is secured via OAUTH2. To request access,\ncomplete the request form\n. You will receive an immediate confirmation when you submit the request, but processing your request may take several days.\nWhen a request is approved, a client ID and secret for the Canvas test environment is issued to the individual designated in the form, and a request for production credentials is initiated. Both sets of credentials will be transmitted via\nSecure Share\n. Keep these credentials in a secure location, and share them only with individuals who need them for developing code.\nGenerate the data files\nWhether you are building your unit's provisioning files manually or via a custom application, file formats should conform to the guidelines in\nData specification for provisioning IU guests and non-credit courses, sections, and enrollments in Canvas and Expand\n.\nIf you want to generate custom notifications to the new Guest accounts, prepare the\nmessage.properties\nfile according to the guidelines in\nSend custom notifications to Canvas Guest account recipients\n. The API accepts individual CSV and\nmessage.properties\nfiles, or a single ZIP archive. (Unlike in the legacy DTP file drop service, you do not need to include a\n*.done\nfile corresponding to each data file.)\nSet up a REST client or application to access the API\nThe Canvas and Expand Provisioning API can be accessed via REST clients, such as Postman or Insomnia, or via a custom application, such as one your unit might develop. To make sure everything is working properly before you begin development, use a REST client to test your credentials and the file uploads. Information for setting up OAUTH2 and using the API POST call to upload files is provided below.\nSet up OAUTH2\nWhen configuring OAUTH2 in your REST client or application, use the following values.\nList of OATH2 field names and values\nField name\nValue\nGrant type\nClient credentials\nAccess token URL\n(OAUTH test environment)\nhttps://apps-test.iu.edu/uaa-stg/oauth/token\n(Use this URL with your test client secret to authenticate to the API for the Canvas test and beta environments.)\nAccess token URL\n(OAUTH production environment)\nhttps://apps.iu.edu/uaa-prd/oauth/token\n(Use this URL with your production client secret to authenticate to the API for the Canvas production environment.)\nClient ID\nYour unit's client ID\nClient secret\nYour unit's test or production client secret\nIssue the API call\nThe provisioning URLs and file upload endpoints are given in the tables below. To upload provisioning files with the API, issue a POST command using the base URL for the desired environment (test or production) and the endpoint that matches your upload type (separate CSV files or a single ZIP file).\nFor example, to upload a ZIP file to the Canvas test environment, issue the following command (replace\ngroup_name\nwith the group name assigned to your unit in the LMS provisioning database):\nhttps://lms-lti-3rdpartyprovisioning-stg.apps-test.iu.edu/rest/upload/group_name/zip\nBase URLs\nList of environments and base URLs\nEnvironment\nBase URL\nCanvas beta (\niu.beta.instructure.com\n)\nhttps://lms-lti-3rdpartyprovisioning-reg.apps-test.iu.edu\nCanvas test (\niu.test.instructure.com\n)\nhttps://lms-lti-3rdpartyprovisioning-stg.apps-test.iu.edu\nCanvas production (\niu.instructure.com\n)\nhttps://lms-lti-3rdpartyprovisioning.apps.iu.edu\nEndpoints\nFor the endpoints in the table below, replace\nbase_url\nwith the appropriate base URL (\nfrom the "Base URLs" table above\n) and\ngroup_name\nwith the group name assigned to your unit in the LMS provisioning database.\nList of endpoints and upload types\nUpload type\nEndpoint\nIndividual CSV files\nbase_url/rest/upload/group_name\nSingle ZIP file\nbase_url/rest/upload/group_name/zip\nFormat the body of the API call\nFormat the body of the API call as a multipart form. The following name/value pairs are supported.\nList of API names, types, and values\nName\nType\nValue\nNotes\ncustomUsersNotification\nText\ntrue\nTo send a custom notification along with your\nusers.csv\nfile, include\ncustomUsersNotification\nand\ntrue\nin the multipart form. Your upload must include a\nmessage.properties\nfile.\ndeptFileUpload\nFile\nFile name\nWhen uploading individual files, include a separate\ndeptFileUpload\nentry for each file name. ZIP files require only one entry.\nResults messages\nA successful transmission will return the following status code and message:\nHTTP 200 OK\n{\n"message": "The files are being processed. Summary emails will be sent at a later time.",\n"fileErrors": null\n}\nA failed transmission will return the following status code and message:\nHTTP 400 Bad Request\n{\n"message": "No files to process",\n"fileErrors": null\n}\nExample\nThe following screenshot illustrates the multipart form configuration for individual files in the Insomnia REST client:\nCoding resources\nSample .NET application to interact with LMS Provisioning REST API\nFor general information on coding API POST requests with a multipart form body, see:\nPython:\nRequests - File Upload (Python)\nHow to send a "multipart/form-data" with requests in python?\nPHP:\nUsing Form Data Objects (PHP)\nHow to send a multipart request with php\nJava:\nHow can I make a multipart/form-data POST request using Java?\nJava upload files by sending multipart request programmatically\nNodeJS:\nNodeJS Request how to send multipart/form-data POST request\nWindows ASP.NET\nPOST a File via HTTP Request | The Ultimate Guide	KB0025519	KB0025519 v3.0	 requests with a multipart form body, see: <strong>Python</strong>: Requests - File Upload (<strong>Python</strong>) How to send a "multipart/form-data" with requests in <strong>python</strong>? PHP: Using Form Data Objects (PHP) How to send	6.762		2026-01-31 17:32:47.430698	2026-01-31 17:32:47.430698	0	b473e68e47f026942fc6a55b416d433a
6029	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=8cdfe4f7476456583d373803846d43c5	Access Microsoft OneDrive at IU using Linux	Using a supported web browser (Edge, Chrome, or Firefox), go to\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\n; if necessary, choose\nLog in\n.\nIf you see a "Sign in" prompt, enter your\n@iu.edu\nemail address, and then, at the password prompt, enter your\nIU passphrase\n. (Do not select anything on the sign-in pages other than\nNext\nand\nSign in\n.) When prompted, confirm your identity with\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n(if you see a "Do you trust iu.edu" prompt, select\nContinue\n), and then choose whether to stay signed into your\nMicrosoft 365\naccount.\nFrom the left-hand navigation, choose\nApps\nand then select\nOneDrive\n. This should allow you to access files in both\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nand in shared sites associated with\nMicrosoft Teams\nand\nMicrosoft Secure Storage\n. (For more, see\nOptions for storing files at IU\n.)\nMicrosoft hasn't developed an app to sync\nOneDrive\nwith Linux operating systems, so you will need to use the website.	KB0023525	KB0023525 v1.0	Microsoft hasn't developed an app to sync <strong>OneDrive</strong> with Linux operating systems, so you will need to use the website.	301.3096		2026-01-31 14:50:55.549176	2026-01-31 16:46:36.710129	0	8cdfe4f7476456583d373803846d43c5
6033	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1b20f03347a456583d373803846d43e3	About A5 and A1 licenses in Microsoft 365 at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nDifferences between online apps and desktop applications\nGet help\nOverview\nMicrosoft 365\nEducation licensing allows those affiliated with Indiana University to access\nMicrosoft 365 at IU\nsoftware according to the following license levels:\nA5 (employee):\nThe A5 employee license provides full use of the IU\nMicrosoft 365\nservice, including the ability to download and install applications on personal devices.\nA5 (student):\nStudents are eligible for A5 licenses that provide full use of most features of\nMicrosoft 365\n, including the ability to download and install applications on personal devices.  However, a few features, such as the ability to dial by number with\nTeams Calls\nand access to some specialty applications like Power BI Pro, are excluded from the A5 student license.  Exceptions are available when appropriate; see\nbelow\n.\nA1:\nThe A1 license provides use of the\nMicrosoft 365\nservice, including web-based access to the online versions of\nMicrosoft 365\n, but does not include the ability to install these applications on personal devices.  This means that A1 license holders cannot dial by number with\nTeams Calls\n(see\nbelow\n). In addition, the A1 license has smaller\ndefault storage allotments\n.\nFor more on the differences between A5 and A1 licenses, see\nMicrosoft 365 Education license comparison\n.\nEligibility for the A5 and A1 plans depends on your status with IU; see\nIU account types and eligibility\n.\nDifferences between online apps and desktop applications\nNot all of the applications featured in desktop versions of\nMicrosoft 365\nare available online. Additionally, the web apps lack some functionality compared to the desktop versions. Differences in some of the most commonly used features include:\nThe online version of Word does not support mail merge, VBA and Forms scripts, SmartArt, or captions.\nThe online version of Excel does not offer VBA and Forms scripts, "What if" analysis tools, external references, workbook protection, or slicer and Timeline filters, and offers only basic formulas.\nThe online version of OneNote does not offer clipping experiences, drag-and-drop section groups, editing of embedded files, inking tools, or template support.\nWhen working with web apps, your files are stored in\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\n, not locally on your personal computer.\nYou can use the\nMicrosoft Teams\nweb app in a\nfully supported browser\n(Chrome or Edge) for most Teams functions, including PC-to-PC calls (campus calling only), but the dial pad will not appear in the web app for IU employees with office phone numbers. If you want to dial off campus or by phone number, you'll need to install the app.\nFor a full list of functionality differences, see\nOffice for the web service description\n.\nGet help\nIf you have an IU-related need for Microsoft applications that are not included in your license, or if you believe that you have been placed in a given licensing group in error,\ncontact your campus Support Center\nto ask for the appropriate license.\nNote:\nWhen student employees or others who are not otherwise eligible for an A5 employee license are assigned to an IU office phone number, they will be placed into an exception group allowing them full use of\nTeams Calls\n.  If you experience difficulties with this process,\ncontact your campus Support Center\n.\nIf you are not eligible for an A5 license through IU, and you would like to purchase a license that allows desktop app access for yourself, see\nBuy Microsoft 365 Personal\nand\nBuy Microsoft 365 Family\n. Note that these are personal\nMicrosoft 365\nlicenses; IU does not purchase licenses through these channels.	KB0025532	KB0025532 v1.0	 support. When working with web apps, your files are stored in Microsoft <strong>OneDrive</strong> at IU, not locally	3.4949		2026-01-31 14:50:55.549176	2026-01-31 16:54:14.241926	0	1b20f03347a456583d373803846d43e3
