7983	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d5ffec7b476456583d373803846d43a1	Access media equipment in a classroom at IU Bloomington	In most buildings at Indiana University Bloomington, media equipment no longer requires a password or code.\nSome classrooms in the Business building do require codes. For details, visit the Kelley School of Business Information Technology (KSBIT) office in the Graduate & Executive Education building, CG 0013, call 812-855-4247, or email KSBIT at\nksbit@iu.edu\n.\nFor information on equipment installed in classrooms at any Indiana\nUniversity campus, use the\nCTS Classroom Database\n.	KB0024313	KB0024313 v1.0	In most buildings at Indiana University Bloomington, media equipment no longer requires a password or code. Some classrooms in the Business building do require codes. For details, visit the Kelley School of Business Information Technology (KSBIT) office in the Graduate & Executive Education building, CG 0013, call 812-855-4247, or email KSBIT at	4.8259		2026-01-31 14:58:52.559002	2026-01-31 16:58:10.170475	0	d5ffec7b476456583d373803846d43a1
8106	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=256b165c477f5a54d500b87b716d43ec	Compile programs on Quartz at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nGNU Compiler Collection\nIntel Compiler Suite\nNVIDIA HPC compilers\nMVAPICH compilers\nGet help\nOverview\nFollowing is information about compiling serial and parallel C, C++, and Fortran programs using the GNU Compiler Collection, the Intel Compiler Suite, the NVIDIA HPC compilers, and the MVAPICH compilers available on\nQuartz\n.\nQuartz\nuses the Lmod module management system to allow users to customize their software environments. For more about Lmod, see\nUse modules to manage your software environment on IU research supercomputers\n.\nGNU Compiler Collection\nSet up your user environment\nThe GNU Compiler Collection (the\ngnu\nmodule) is added by default as part of the standard user environment. It includes compilers for C, C++, and Fortran codes:\nGNU Compiler Collection\nLanguage\nCompile command\nManual page\nC\ngcc\nman gcc\nC++\ng++\nman g++\nFortran\ngfortran\nman gfortran\nWith the\ngnu\nmodule loaded, the GNU Scientific Library (GSL) numerical library for C and C++ programs is available. To add it to your user environment, load the\ngsl\nmodule:\nmodule load gsl\nFFTW compiled with GNU and MVAPICH is available if you also load the\nopenmpi\nmodule. To add both to your user environment, enter:\nmodule load openmpi fftw\nOptimize your code\nAdding the\n-O\noption to your compile command enables various optimization flags that tell the compiler to try to improve your code's performance. A higher\n-O\nlevel indicates higher optimization, but can add compilation time and potentially alter the semantics of your program:\nUITS\nrecommends using\n-O2\nor\n-03\nfor production runs:\nOptimization level options\nOptimization level\nDescription\n-O0\nNo optimization (this is the default)\n-O\nor\n-O1\nThese levels are identical; the compiler tries to reduce code size and execution time, but doesn't perform optimizations that take a lot of compilation time.\n-O2\nThis level turns on all\n-O1\noptimization flags plus others that increase both performance and compilation time.\n-O3\nThis turns on additional optimization flags along with those specified by\n-O2\n.\nExamples\nUITS recommends using the\nmtune=native\nand\nmarch=native\noptions with the GNU compilers to generate instructions for the machine and CPU type. Compile by default in 64-bit. Add the\n-m32\nswitch to your compile command to compile in 32-bit; add the\n-m64\nswitch to return to 64-bit.\nFollowing are example commands for compiling optimized serial programs with the GNU compilers:\nTo compile a serial C program (for example,\nmy_program.c\n) and create an optimized executable (for example,\nmy_program.exe\n), on the command line, enter:\ngcc -O2 -mtune=native -march=native my_program.c -o my_program.exe\nTo compile a serial C++ program (for example,\nmy_program.cpp\n) and create an optimized executable (for example,\nmy_program.exe\n), on the command line, enter:\ng++ -O2 -mtune=native -march=native my_program.cpp -o my_program.exe\nTo compile a serial Fortran program (for example,\nmy_program.f\n) and create an optimized executable (for example,\nmy_program.exe\n), on the command line, enter:\ngfortran -O2 -mtune=native -march=native program.f -o my_program.exe\nTo enable support for shared-memory multi-threaded (OpenMP) parallel programs, you must pass the\n-fopenmp\nflag to the compiler; for example:\ngcc -fopenmp my_omp_program.c -o my_omp_program.exe\nFor distributed-memory examples, see the\nMVAPICH compiler\nsection below.\nIntel Compiler Suite\nSet up your user environment\nThe Intel Compiler Suite is available on\nQuartz\n; it includes compilers for C, C++, and Fortran codes:\nIntel Compiler Suite\nLanguage\nCompile command\nManual page\nC\nicc\nman icc\nC++\nicpc\nman icpc\nFortran\nifort\nman ifort\nOn\nQuartz\n, to switch your user environment to the Intel compilers, load the\nintel\nmodule; on the command line, enter:\nmodule load intel\nLmod automatically replaces your current compiler environment with the new one, activates and/or deactivates any dependent modules, and sets environment variables allowing you to link applications to Intel performance libraries, including Integrated Performance Primitives (IPP), Math Kernel Library (MKL), and Threaded Building Blocks (TBB).\nOptimize your code\nAdding the\n-O\noption to your compile command enables various optimization flags that tell the compiler to try to improve your code's performance. A higher\n-O\nlevel indicates higher optimization, but can add compilation time and potentially alter the semantics of your program:\nOptimization level options\nOptimization level\nDescription\n-O1\nOptimize for maximum speed, but disable some optimizations that increase code size for a small speed benefit.\n-O\nor\n-O2\nThese levels are identical. Optimize for maximum speed. This is the default.\n-O3\nOptimize for maximum speed and enable more aggressive optimizations that may not improve performance on some programs.\n-O0\nDisable optimizations.\nUITS recommends using\n-O2\nfor production runs. You can also try the\n-fast\nswitch to see if this improves your code's performance.\nExamples\nFollowing are example commands for compiling optimized serial programs with the Intel compilers (compile by default in 64-bit):\nTo compile a serial C program (for example,\nmy_program.c\n) and create an optimized executable (for example,\nmy_program.exe\n), on the command line, enter:\nicc -O my_program.c -o my_program.exe\nTo compile a serial C++ program (for example,\nmy_program.cpp\n) and create an optimized executable (for example,\nmy_program.exe\n), on the command line, enter:\nicpc -O my_program.cpp -o my_program.exe\nTo compile a serial Fortran program (for example,\nmy_program.f\n) and create an optimized executable (for example,\nmy_program.exe\n), on the command line, enter:\nifort -O my_program.f -o my_program.exe\nTo enable support for shared-memory multi-threaded (OpenMP) parallel programs, pass the\n-openmp\nflag to the compiler; for example:\nicc -O2 -openmp my_omp_program.c -o my_omp_program.exe\nFor distributed-memory examples, see the\nMVAPICH compiler\nsection.\nNVIDIA HPC compilers\nSet up your user environment\nThe NVIDIA HPC compilers are available on\nQuartz\n. The NVIDIA HPC package includes compilers for C, C++, and Fortran codes:\nNVIDIA HPC compilers\nLanguage\nCompile command\nManual page\nC\nnvc\nman nvc\nC++\nnvc++\nman nvc++\nFortran\nnvfortran\nman nvfortran\nOn\nQuartz\n, to switch your user environment to the NVIDIA HPC compilers, on the command line, enter:\nmodule load nvhpc\nLmod automatically replaces your current compiler environment with the new one, and activates and/or deactivates any dependent modules accordingly.\nFFTW compiled with NVHPC and Open MPI is available if you also load the\nopenmpi\nmodule. To add both to your user environment, enter:\nmodule load openmpi fftw\nOptimize your code\nUse the NVIDIA HPC compiler commands with the\n-O\noption to enable various local and global optimizations.\nOptimization level options\nOptimization level\nDescription\n-O0\nNo optimization; the compiler generates a basic block for each statement; no scheduling or global optimizations are performed\n-O1\nLocal optimization; scheduling of basic blocks and register allocation are performed\n-O\nLevel\n-O2\nglobal optimizations are performed, including traditional scalar optimizations, induction recognition, and loop invariant motion; no SIMD vectorization is enabled\n-O2\nGlobal optimization; all level\n-O1\nlocal optimizations, plus the global optimizations described in\n-O\n; more advanced optimizations, such as SIMD code generation, cache alignment, and partial redundancy elimination, are enabled\nExamples\nFollowing are example commands for compiling optimized serial programs with the NVIDA HPC compilers:\nTo compile a serial C program (for example,\nmy_program.c\n) and create an optimized executable (for example,\nmy_program.exe\n), on the command line, enter:\nnvc -O my_program.c -o my_prog.exe\nTo compile a serial C++ program (for example,\nmy_program.cpp\n) and create an optimized executable (for example,\nmy_program.exe\n), on the command line, enter:\nnvc++ -O my_program.cpp -o my_program.exe\nTo compile a serial Fortran program (for example,\nmy_program.f\n) and create an optimized executable (for example,\nmy_program.exe\n), on the command line, enter:\nnvfortran -O my_program.f -o my_program.exe\nMVAPICH compilers\nSet up your user environment\nMVAPICH compilers are added to your user environment on\nQuartz\nby default. MVAPICH compiler commands are available for C, C++, and Fortran codes. The compiler commands add the necessary flags for compiling and linking MVAPICH programs, and then invoke the respective back-end compilers to perform the actual commands:\nMVAPICH compilers\nLanguages\nCompiler commands\nManual pages\nC\nmpicc\nman mpicc\nC++\nmpicxx\nman mpicxx\nFortran\nmpif77\nmpifort\nman mpif77\nman mpifort\nTo optimize your code, use the optimization flags that correspond to the base compiler module (\nGNU\nor\nIntel\n) that you have loaded.\nExamples\nTo compile and link an MVAPICH program written in C (for example,\nmy_mvapich_program.c\n), on the command line, enter:\nmpicc my_mvapich_program.c -o my_mvapich_program.exe\nTo compile and link an MVAPICH program written in C++ (for example,\nmy_mvapich_program.cxx\n), on the command line, enter:\nmpicxx my_mvapich_program.cxx -o my_mvapich_program.exe\nTo compile and link an MVAPICH program written in Fortran 90 (for example,\nmy_mvapich_program.f\n), on the command line, enter:\nmpifortran my_mvapich_program.f -o my_mvapich_program.exe\nGet help\nIf you have questions about compilers on the IU\nresearch supercomputers\nor need help,\ncontact the UITS\nResearch Applications and Deep Learning\nteam\n.	KB0025421	KB0025421 v5.0	 compilers, and the MVAPICH compilers available on <strong>Quartz</strong>. <strong>Quartz</strong> uses the Lmod module management system up your user environment The Intel Compiler Suite is available on <strong>Quartz</strong>; it includes compilers C++ icpc man icpc Fortran ifort man ifort On <strong>Quartz</strong>, to switch your user environment to the Intel	35.5658		2026-01-31 15:00:49.768054	2026-01-31 16:11:36.632263	0	256b165c477f5a54d500b87b716d43ec
7997	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=572c906347eb22d4d500b87b716d430a	About Top Hat Guest Mode	Table of contents\nOverview\nBefore you begin\nUse Guest Mode\nGuest Mode vs. anonymous access\nLearn more\nGet help\nOverview\nTop Hat Guest Mode allows instructors to conduct polls with participants who are not registered Top Hat students, making it ideal for use in conferences, meetings, training sessions, and similar events. Previously, polling non-Top Hat users required special arrangements with the vendor. However, starting in May 2025, instructors can enable Guest Mode in any Top Hat course without enrolled students, with no special setup needed.\nBefore you begin\nBefore attempting to use Guest Mode, keep the following limitations, requirements, and recommendations in mind:\nYou must have a Top Hat instructor account in order to administer polls with Top Hat Guest Mode. For instructions on creating an account, see\nIndiana University - Single Sign-On Account Setup\n. IU students are not permitted to create instructor accounts.\nYou can't use Guest Mode to take attendance or to assign asynchronous content and questions to participants.\nYou should never use Guest Mode in regular academic courses with enrolled students nor in any course synced with the Canvas Gradebook.\nIf you use the same Top Hat course and questions at multiple separate sessions, the stored and live on-screen results will be cumulative rather than broken out by session. To avoid this problem, UITS recommends creating a new Top Hat course for each session or event in which you plan to use Guest Mode. This will ensure that the on-screen question results will include only the respondents at the current session.\nAlternatively, if you'd prefer to keep all related content in one Top Hat course, store your content in a folder, and create a separate copy of the folder each time you need to present it. As long as you use copies instead of reusing the content in the original folder, the data for each session will be kept separate.\nPoints are not recorded in the Top Hat gradebook or student manager for guests. However, it is possible to export individual responses by navigating to the desired question in the Gradebook and selecting\nExport Responses\n. The exported results include the names entered by guests when joining the session.\nFaculty participants who are already logged into Top Hat with an instructor account are not able to respond to Guest Mode polls. To participate in the Guest Mode session, they can either:\nGo to\napp.tophat.com/logout\nin their default browser and choose\nYes\n. After that, they should be able to access the guest poll via the URL or the QR code.\nOpen a private/incognito browser tab and go to\napp.tophat.com/guest\n, then enter the join code.\nTo view all the content available to you here,\nlog into the Knowledge Base\nnear the top of the page.\nIU community:\nContrary to the information in the\nGuest Mode\narticle on the Top Hat support site, there are no limits on sessions or enrolled students for Guest Mode at Indiana University. Guest Mode will continue to function regardless of the number of enrolled students or the number of Guest Mode sessions initiated in any given Top Hat course.\nUse Guest Mode\nFor step-by-step instructions for both presenters and participants, refer to the article\nGuest Mode\nin the\nTop Hat Support Center\n.\nGuest Mode vs. anonymous access\nGuest Mode is now the preferred method for conducting Top Hat polls at events and public settings.  If you are accustomed to using the legacy method, known as\nAnonymous Access Courses\n, it’s important to understand the differences, which are summarized in the table below:\nDifferences between anonymous access and Guest Mode\nFeature\nAnonymous access (legacy)\nGuest Mode (new)\nGuest access setup\nRequires Top Hat Support to enable per course\nTop Hat instructors can enable on their own\nParticipant access\nUnique course URL\nQR code or short URL (app.tophat.com/guest)\nAnonymity\nGuests are completely anonymous\nParticipants enter a real or fake name, which is tracked during the session.\nContent access (guest)\nCan see polls and discussion prompts only\nCan see polls and discussion prompts only\nData recording\nFully anonymous responses; no individual identification\nResponses are linked to names entered by guests. Names visible in results exports only.\nGradebook\nGuest users not visible\nGuest users not visible\nResults export\nAggregated results only\nCan export individual responses with entered names\nLearn more\nGet started with Top Hat at IU\nGetting Started with Top Hat\nGuest Mode\nAnonymous Access Courses\nGet help\nGetting started and pedagogical concerns\nInstructors can get help using technology in their teaching at the\nteaching and learning centers\non each campus.\nImmediate technical help in the classroom\nAt IU Bloomington or IU Indianapolis, contact\nClassroom Technology Services\n. At other campuses, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0026807	KB0026807 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Before you begin Use Guest Mode Guest Mode vs. anonymous access Learn more Get help Overview Top Hat Guest Mode allows instructors to conduct polls with participants who are not registered Top Hat students, making it ideal for use in conferences, meetings, training sessions, and similar events. Previously, polling non-	2.3641		2026-01-31 14:59:06.583382	2026-01-31 17:10:41.060138	0	572c906347eb22d4d500b87b716d430a
12308	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=205038f347a456583d373803846d437d	General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) departmental checklist	Find out if your processing activities are likely within the scope of the GDPR:\nFor each new activity, either:\nComplete the\nshort\nGDPR Scope Assessment\n; or\nDiscuss the activity with the IU GDPR Working Group to determine whether or not it is in scope. Your responses to the Scope Assessment can help the working group provide relevant guidance.\nIf your processing activities are within scope of the GDPR:\nImportant:\nSchedule a consultation with the\nIU GDPR Working Group\n(\ngdpr@iu.edu\n).\nAfter the conclusion of your scheduled consultation, complete the following steps (for\neach\nin-scope processing activity):\nEstablish technical and operational processes to honor data subject\nrights and protections\n(based on the guidance provided during your consultation).\nEnsure you have a\nlawful basis\nof processing.\nRegister\neach\nin-scope processing scenario\nseparately\nin the\nGDPR intake form\n.\nUpdate/implement privacy notice(s) where applicable based on your GDPR consultation.\n(A\nGDPR privacy notice template\nis available in the\nGDPR template kit\n.)\nSee also:\nWeb Site, Web Application, and Web Services Privacy Notices (ISPP-24)\nand\nStandards for Web Site, Web Application and Web Service Privacy Notice Content (ISPP-24-S\n);\nPrivacy notice generator\n(not tailored for GDPR).\nImplement\ndata transfer agreements\nwhen transferring EU personal data to/from:\nThird parties in other countries\nInternational organizations\nIt may be necessary to amend already existing agreements. (\nPurchasing\nmay facilitate implementation of amendments for contracts they manage. A\ndata transfer agreement template\nis available in the\nGDPR template kit\n.)\nObtain written consent when (or prior to) collecting EU personal data if consent is the lawful basis for processing. (A\nconsent declaration template\nis available in the\nGDPR template kit\n.)\nProvide a separate\ninformation notice\nbefore or at the time of\nin-person\ndata collection. (An\ninformation notice template\nis available in the\nGDPR template kit\n.)\nTo learn more about the EU GDPR, visit\nhttps://informationsecurity.iu.edu/protect-data/gdpr.html\n.	KB0026316	KB0026316 v1.0	 Content (ISPP-24-S); Privacy notice generator (not tailored for GDPR). Implement <strong>data</strong> <strong>transfer</strong> implementation of amendments for contracts they manage. A <strong>data</strong> <strong>transfer</strong> agreement template is available activity): Establish technical and operational processes to honor <strong>data</strong> subject rights and protections	36.1247	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:33:56.836337	2026-01-31 17:33:56.836337	0	205038f347a456583d373803846d437d
12309	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a8ff6c7b476456583d373803846d43b4	Use Microsoft's on-premises data gateway	Table of contents\nOverview\nDatasource types\nGet started\nModify or delete datasources\nOverview\nMicrosoft's on-premises data gateway acts as a bridge, providing secure data transfer between several Microsoft cloud services and your on-campus data.\nThese services include\nPower BI\n, Power Apps,\nPower Automate\n, Azure Analysis Services, and Azure Logic Apps.\nThe Power Platform and related components, including the on-premises data gateway, are administered by\nEnterprise Windows & Azure Administration\n(\nEWA\n). Only\nEWA\nstaff can create an on-premises data gateway, so they host a centralized data gateway that anyone may use.\nDatasource types\nThe central data gateway currently supports Microsoft SQL Server, Oracle, and File datasource types. More drivers are planned, but if you need additional capabilities, contact\newahelp@iu.edu\n.\nGet started\nPower BI, Power Apps and Power Automate, and Azure Analysis Services and Azure Logic Apps all have their own unique configurations.\nTo get started using the data gateway for Power BI, complete the\nCreate gateway datasource for Power BI form\n.\nTo get started using the data gateway for Power Apps/Power Automate, complete the\nPower Platform Gateway Access Request form\n.\nFor any other use of the data gateway, contact\newahelp@iu.edu\n.\nIf you don't think you can use the centrally managed, on-premises data gateway in your situation, contact\newahelp@iu.edu\n.\nModify or delete datasources\nPower BI:\nTo access datasources on the gateway or delete the datasource entirely, use the\nModify Power BI Gateway Datasource\nform.\nPower Platform:\nTo make changes, contact\newahelp@iu.edu\n.	KB0024267	KB0024267 v1.0	 Microsoft's on-premises <strong>data</strong> gateway acts as a bridge, providing secure <strong>data</strong> <strong>transfer</strong> between several Microsoft cloud services and your on-campus <strong>data</strong>. These services include Power BI, Power Apps, Power, including the on-premises <strong>data</strong> gateway, are administered by Enterprise Windows & Azure Administration	35.0775		2026-01-31 17:34:00.152655	2026-01-31 17:34:00.152655	0	a8ff6c7b476456583d373803846d43b4
12312	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6700b07f476456583d373803846d43cc	Use your IU REDCap project as a data source in Tableau	To use your\nIU REDCap\nproject as a data source in\nTableau\n:\nRequest an IU REDCap API token\n.\nFind the web data connector (WDC) URL for your REDCap project:\nNavigate to your IU REDCap project, and then, in the left navigation column, select\nData Exports, Reports, and Stats\n.\nOn the resulting page, select the\nOther Export options\ntab. In the "Tableau Export: Extract all records into Tableau via the REDCap API" section, select\nView export instructions\n. You should see a pop-up displaying a URL that you will use in the following steps to set up a WDC in Tableau.\nIn Tableau, under "To a Server", select\nMore\n, and then select\nWeb Data Connector\n.\nIn the "Web Data Connector" dialog box, enter the WDC URL for your IU REDCap project, and then press\nEnter\n.\nIn the "Tableau Web Data Connector for REDCap" dialog box, enter your IU REDCap API token, choose whether you want to receive data as raw variables or as labels, and then select\nSubmit\n.\nImportant:\nIf the IU REDCap project data you are accessing contain protected health information (\nPHI\n), you must treat your Tableau workbook file as PHI, which means the file must be encrypted when it is stored (that is, at rest) and when it is transferred from one computer to another (that is, in transit). For more, see:\nRecommended tools for encrypting data containing HIPAA-regulated PHI\nYour legal responsibilities for protecting data containing protected health information (PHI) when using UITS Research Technologies systems and services\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.	KB0024862	KB0024862 v1.0	To use your IU REDCap project as a <strong>data</strong> source in Tableau: Request an IU REDCap API token. Find the web <strong>data</strong> connector (WDC) URL for your REDCap project: Navigate to your IU REDCap project, and then, in the left navigation column, select <strong>Data</strong> Exports, Reports, and Stats. On the resulting page	28.0146		2026-01-31 17:34:03.540652	2026-01-31 17:34:03.540652	0	6700b07f476456583d373803846d43cc
12316	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=53b9c0a647b51a509286dfbd436d4389	Protect data in copiers and multifunction devices	Table of contents\nOverview\nPurchase or lease new equipment\nMaintain and protect existing equipment\nDetermine whether your equipment retains digital data\nInquire about a replacement\nPurchase add-on equipment and/or software\nHarden the device and develop departmental cleanup policies\nDispose of, transfer, or retire old equipment\nLearn more\nOverview\nUse the information below to help you make appropriate and informed risk-based decisions for handling information contained on office, unit, or departmental copiers and multifunction office devices.\nPurchase or lease new equipment\nWhen beginning the search for new equipment, engage and work closely with\nIU Purchasing\n. They have negotiated contracts with vendors to ensure the university receives the best prices on equipment, as well as additional protection for data processed by the machine, whether by keeping the cache encrypted, securely deleting the cache periodically, or (ideally) not keeping a cache at all.\nIf you have specific questions about purchasing this type of equipment,\ncontact IU Purchasing\n.\nMaintain and protect existing equipment\nIf your existing copier or multifunction device does not yet need to be replaced, you have several options for maintaining and protecting its data.\nUISO\nrecommends you carefully evaluate the options and choose one commensurate with your perceived risk.\nDetermine whether your equipment retains digital data\nBefore you do anything else, determine whether your equipment is actually retaining digital copies on a hard drive. To do this, you may want to contact your sales/leasing vendor, review the manufacturer's website, or consult\nIU Purchasing\n.\nInquire about a replacement\nContact either\nIU Purchasing\nor your vendor directly to inquire about replacement equipment. All equipment contracts are different, and you may be able to replace your equipment at no cost to you.\nPurchase add-on equipment and/or software\nSome vendors or manufacturers produce add-ons to their equipment for additional purchase that either regularly destroy or encrypt the cache copies stored on the equipment's hard drive. This option may be viable if you have a large, expensive piece of equipment that can't be replaced at this time.\nNote:\nIt has been speculated that several manufacturers have contributed to the media frenzy on this subject in an attempt to sell these add-ons. Though discussing options with a vendor is not discouraged, you should consult with\nIU Purchasing\nbefore making any final purchasing decisions.\nHarden the device and develop departmental cleanup policies\nYour best option may be to keep your current equipment and attempt to secure the data within the devices as you go. UISO recommends you take the following steps:\nReview all the functionality of the device, decide how it is to be used (or receive that information from others), and harden the configurations. Disable every service and feature except those identified as required on an everyday basis, including how accessible the machine is via the network. Revisit the requirements of the device as often as necessary, because offices are often organic environments with changing needs.\nDetermine if your make and model of equipment offers a "disable" option with respect to maintaining digital cache copies. If so, disable that option.\nIf you can't disable the option to maintain digital cache copies, determine whether your equipment allows periodic deletion of this data. Automatic deletion at specific intervals is preferable, but manual deletion will suffice.\nIf your equipment only allows for manual deletion, determine which person in your office will be responsible for this task. Copiers and related devices have not traditionally been considered IT equipment, so an office manager or other administrative personnel may oversee your equipment.\nIf a department head wants to learn more before assigning staff hours to this task, see:\nProtecting Data & Privacy\nAbout secure data removal\nDispose of, transfer, or retire old equipment\nSince it has become public knowledge that copiers and multifunction office devices may contain sensitive personal information, you must handle their disposal carefully. IU already has the following existing resources related to the disposal of hard drives and the secure removal of data, and you should apply the same standards to this type of equipment:\nDisposal and Redistribution of University Property (FIN-PURCH-11)\nAbout secure data removal\nIU Surplus: Data Security and Computer Disposal or Resale\n(IU Bloomington)\nSurplus Hard Drive/Data Shredding Program\n(IU Indianapolis and regional campuses)\nLearn more\nFor more, see\nAuditing and Securing Multifunction Devices\nfrom the SANS Institute Information Security Reading Room.	KB0025302	KB0025302 v2.0	 to this task, see: Protecting <strong>Data</strong> & Privacy About secure <strong>data</strong> removal Dispose of, <strong>transfer</strong>, or retire old Determine whether your equipment retains digital <strong>data</strong> Inquire about a replacement Purchase add of, <strong>transfer</strong>, or retire old equipment Learn more Overview Use the information below to help you make	24.6439		2026-01-31 17:34:07.32108	2026-01-31 17:34:07.32108	0	53b9c0a647b51a509286dfbd436d4389
12319	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=681fad131bb4565416c02fc4bd4bcb3b	About secure data removal	Table of Contents\nIU policy\nSimple deletion of data is not enough\nMethods of secure deletion\nWiping utilities\nDestruction\nDegaussing\nDestruction of data on paper\nExamples\nLearn more\nIU policy\nBefore a department may redistribute, sell, or dispose of computing equipment to another entity, all data must be removed from the storage device(s) to comply with\nDisposal and Redistribution of University Property (FIN-PURCH-11)\n.\nDepartments must choose and correctly use a tool that performs at least a one-pass\nwipe\nof the storage device. If a storage device is inoperable or cannot be wiped using an approved tool, your remaining options include degaussing and drive destruction.\nAdditionally, if a computer or mobile device is viable for reuse or resale, you should follow all guidelines at\nDepartmental disposal of equipment at IU\nbefore it leaves your department.\nSimple deletion of data is not enough\nWhen a file is deleted using the usual methods inherent in an operating system, these methods typically only remove the pointers to the files,\nnot\nthe actual files themselves. The data remains on the hard drive as unallocated space and can easily be recovered with readily available tools.\nA common misconception is that using system utilities like\nfdisk\nand reformatting a hard drive will securely delete all data on the hard drive. Like\nrm\nand\ndel\n, these utilities modify file system attributes but do not remove the actual data.\nCD-ROMs, since they are read-only, present a different challenge in that there is no way to programmatically and securely delete the contents of the CD. Inoperable hard drives are also a challenge since they cannot be connected to a system and accessed through software.\nMethods of secure deletion\nWiping utilities\nDisk wiping is a term used to describe a method that writes a series of ones and/or zeros over the disk to securely remove the data. Since tools that do this have to overwrite the entire disk, this process can be time-consuming.\nUISO\nhas verified that DBAN and the macOS Disk Utility,\nif used correctly\n, can be successfully used to securely remove data from a disk; for details, see\nSecurely wipe disk drives\n.\nNote:\nFor devices with a solid-state drive (SSD), instead see\nSecurely erase a solid-state drive\n.\nDestruction\nFor media that has contained highly sensitive data or for media that cannot be wiped (for example, inoperable hard drives or DVDs) or degaussed (for example, CD-ROMs), destruction of the media is the most effective means of ensuring that the data cannot be recovered.\nIn order to be effective, the destruction has to be thorough. For example, a simple whack with a hammer leaves the majority of data on the media readable.\nFor detailed guidelines for inoperable devices, see\nAbout disposing of failed devices and media containing data\n.\nDegaussing\nDegaussing is a process by which magnetic storage media is subjected to a powerful magnetic field to remove data on the media. It is appropriate for magnetic tape, but is ineffective with optical media such as DVDs and CDs. For guidelines, see\nAbout disposing of failed devices and media containing data\n.\nDestruction of data on paper\nInformation classified as\nCritical data\n, when stored in paper form, must be properly destroyed by shredding. For low-volume paper document destruction, units may consider purchasing a small cross-cut paper shredder. For high volume needs, a document destruction vendor may be the best solution. IU Purchasing currently lists the following contracted vendors for document destruction:\nIU Bloomington:\nShredding & Storage Unlimited\nEmail:\nChrisy Gornall (\nchrisy@shreddingunlimited.com)\nWebsite:\nShredding and Storage Unlimited\nIU Indianapolis:\nGRM Document Management\nEmail:\nCustomer service (\nindorders@grmdocument.com)\nWebsite:\nGRM Customer Service & Sales\nExamples\nScenarios and recommendations\nScenario\nRecommended action\nYou have an inoperable hard drive that contains sensitive data.\nSince disk wiping can't be used on inoperable drives, physical\ndestruction\nis the best alternative.\nYou are transferring a computer, which has been used to store FERPA-protected student records, to another user.\nDisk\nwiping\nis the best alternative.\nYou are transferring a computer to another department on campus. The computer was bought new and used as a public access terminal; it has never maintained sensitive data, but has applications installed on it that were licensed from a software vendor.\nSince data storage is not an issue, you can\nfdisk\nthe system and reformat the hard drive, ensuring that any individually licensed software is unusable.\nYou are transferring a computer, which has been used to store sensitive data, to another department on campus.\nSecure disk\nwiping\nis the best alternative.\nYour computer has reached the end of its life and no other IU department wants it.\nSee:\nDisposal and Redistribution of University Property (FIN-PURCH-11)\nSale of Computing Equipment (FIN-PUR-14.1)\nDepartmental disposal of equipment at IU\nYour hard drive containing sensitive data has a mechanical failure, and the manufacturer requests that the drive be returned for replacement under warranty.\nInform the manufacturer that the drive contains sensitive data and you'd prefer not to return it. If the manufacturer insists on return of the damaged drive before sending a replacement, request a formal letter from the manufacturer stating that they will ensure all data is securely removed from the hard drive. If the vendor refuses to comply, purchase a replacement drive and destroy the damaged disk using one of IU's destruction services (see\nAbout disposing of failed devices and media containing data\n).\nYou have a very large volume of media to be retired that contains sensitive data.\nIU Purchasing\ncan contract with a professional shredder company to come to campus and shred the media. When finished, they will provide a certificate of destruction.\nYou are replacing an old smartphone.\nRemove all data from smartphones, tablets, and other mobile devices that have been used to access, store, or manipulate institutional data. See:\nSecure your Android OS device\nSecure your iPhone, iPad, or iPod touch\nYou may have sensitive data cached in your office copier or multifunction device.\nSee\nProtect data in copiers and multifunction devices\n.\nLearn more\nTo learn more, see the following policies and documents:\nDisposal and Redistribution of University Property (FIN-PURCH-11)\nSale of Computing Equipment (FIN-PUR-14.1)\nNIST Special Publication 800-88: Guidelines for Media Sanitization\nEffectively Erasing Files\n, US-CERT National Cyber Awareness System Security Tip (ST05-011)	KB0025426	KB0025426 v2.0	Table of Contents IU policy Simple deletion of <strong>data</strong> is not enough Methods of secure deletion Wiping utilities Destruction Degaussing Destruction of <strong>data</strong> on paper Examples Learn more IU policy Before a department may redistribute, sell, or dispose of computing equipment to another entity, all <strong>data</strong>	18.3235		2026-01-31 17:34:10.748988	2026-01-31 17:34:10.748988	0	681fad131bb4565416c02fc4bd4bcb3b
12326	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=dfefa47b476456583d373803846d4388	IU Data Center Access Control Policy	Table of contents\nOverview\n1.0 Scope\n2.0 Purpose\n3.0 Responsibility\n4.0 Communication of policy\n5.0 Categories of access\n6.0 Faculty, staff, and student access\n7.0 IU affiliate access\n8.0 Daily access\n9.0 Visitor/tour access\n10.0 Card visibility\n11.0 Conduct of authorized users\n12.0 Data Center Access Review Board\nOverview\nDue to the sensitive nature of the data and information systems maintained within its facilities, security and access are important aspects of the Office of Vice President for Information Technology/University Information Technology Services (OVPIT/UITS) environment. In most cases, Indiana University is contractually and legally obligated to limit access to only those who have IT responsibilities requiring frequent access.\nSecurity cameras are located throughout OVPIT/UITS buildings. These cameras record footage for follow-up in the case of a security incident. They also provide an effective deterrence function in the safe operation of the building. IU Data Centers use two layer authentication smart cards and biometric hand scanners to grant access to the facilities.\nThese increased security protocols follow guidelines from NIST 800-53, PCI DSS, SOC 2 and other sources, and are modeled on best practices by other universities such as the BTAA partners.\n1.0 Scope\n1.0.1\nDocument the policy and procedures for requesting, reviewing, authorizing, assigning, and maintaining access rights for those who need to perform services or visit University Information Technology Services (UITS)-managed data centers at Indiana University.\n2.0 Purpose\n2.0.1\nIn support of IU data centers, data center access and physical security, these policies and procedures provide a strong security strategy that protects IU employees, data, and resources entrusted to UITS by IU and its customers. These procedures are intended to clarify access requirements for all UITS-managed data centers.\n3.0 Responsibility\n3.0.1\nUITS Data Center Operations is responsible for assigning access rights to individuals for secured areas under its control based on management-approved requests and adherence to IU Data Center Access Policy. Public Safety and Institutional Assurance is the owner of the card access system and is responsible for its system administration. Data Center Operations manages and provides operational support for the IU data centers.\n4.0 Communication of policy\n4.0.1\nAll sponsors of individuals with authorized access to IU data centers are responsible for ensuring those individuals are aware of and comply with the policies and procedures identified in this document.\n4.0.2\nAll personnel who are authorized to access IU data centers must read, understand, and comply with the policies and procedures identified in this document.\n4.0.3\nAll individuals applying for access to IU data centers must comply, understand and acknowledge that they have read the IU Data Center Access Policy and the\nIU Data Center Standards\n.\n5.0 Categories of access\nThere are four categories of access to IU data centers: faculty, staff, and student access, IU affiliate access, daily access, and visitor/tour access.\n5.1 IU faculty, staff, and student access\nFor IU employees with a business need to provide services in IU data centers\nRequires a valid Indiana University ID card (CrimsonCard)\nRequires a IU supervisor as a sponsor\nNo escort required\nA review will take place verifying the data center's access list every 60 days (refer to\nsection 12.0\nfor details).\nAccess will expire on an annual basis and must be renewed by completing the form and obtaining the appropriate approvals (refer to\nsection 12.0\nfor details).\nRefer to\nsection 6.0\nfor details regarding the acquisition of faculty, staff, and student access.\n5.2 IU affiliate  access\nAffiliates are individuals who require an ID card to access services or facilities but are not registered students or employees of Indiana University.\nMust be sponsored by a department\nFor contractors/vendors who have long-term support agreements to provide services for equipment in IU data centers. Includes external co-location clients\nNo escort required\nA review will take place verifying the data center's access list every 60 days (refer to\nsection 12.0\nfor details).\nAccess will expire on an annual basis and must be renewed by completing the form and obtaining the appropriate approvals (see\nsection 12.0\nfor details).\nRefer to\nsection 7.0\nfor details regarding the acquisition of IU affiliate access.\n5.3 Daily access\nFor external co-location customers or contractors without IU affiliate access\nFor those with limited-term engagements to provide a defined service\nFor individuals who are familiar with IU data center standards\nRequires Data Center Operations Manager sponsorship\nVisitor badges are issued at the Data Center Operations Center at the time of access.\nRequires escort by individual with IU faculty/staff/student access\nAccess expires in 24 hours.\nRefer to\nsection 8.0\nfor details regarding the acquisition of daily access.\n5.4 Visitor/tour access\nFor individuals with no primary business need to access IU data centers other than for education or demonstration purposes\nTour appointments must be scheduled at least 24 hours in advance.\nVisitor badges are issued at the DC Operations Center.\nRequires escort by individual with IU faculty/staff/student access at all times while in IU data centers\nRefer to\nsection 9.0\nfor details regarding the acquisition of Visitor/Tour access.\n6.0 Faculty, staff, and student access\n6.0.1\nFaculty, staff, and student access is generally approved for IU staff when job duties require access to IU data centers.\n6.1 Obtaining faculty, staff, and student access\n6.1.1\nIn order to be granted faculty, staff, and student access to IU data centers, the applicant must:\n6.1.1.1\nComplete the required faculty, staff, and student data center access request form and submit it.\n6.1.1.2\nAccess form will be routed for approval from the requester's supervisor, the DCOPS manager, the Enterprise Infrastructure Director and Data Center Access Control.\n6.1.1.3\nMust have a valid Indiana University ID (CrimsonCard).\n6.1.1.4\nThe applicant must visit DCOPS with card to register in the hand geometry scanner system and have approved access areas assigned.\n6.2 Maintaining faculty, staff, and student access\n6.2.1\nCards must not be altered or defaced in any way; cards must not be bent, written on, have anything affixed to, or have holes punched in them.\n6.2.2\nThe individual's supervisor must immediately report any change in job duties or employment status that would eliminate the need for data center access to DC Access Control (\ndcaccess@iu.edu\n).\n6.2.3\nThe individual must retain sole possession of the card for the duration of their approved use. The individual is responsible for card use. Card use is not transferable and cannot be shared. Anyone caught allowing another individual to use their card will have their data center access immediately deactivated.\n6.2.4\nFaculty, Staff, and Student access must be renewed on an annual basis.\n6.3 Replacing Faculty, Staff, and Student access cards\n6.3.1\nLost or stolen cards must be immediately reported to IU Data Center Access Control via email (\ndcaccess@iu.edu\n) or call 812-855-9910.\n6.3.2\nFor damaged, lost, or stolen cards, a replacement card will be assigned with the previously approved access areas.\n6.4 Returning Faculty, Staff and Student access cards\n6.4.1\nUpon termination of employment or change in job responsibilities, it's the supervisor/sponsors responsibility to return access cards to DC Access Control and access will be removed.\n7.0 IU Affiliate access\n7.0.1\nIU Affiliate access is generally granted to vendors who have annual support contracts to perform routine and emergency support of hardware and software used in IU data centers.\n7.1 Obtaining IU Affiliate access\n7.1.1\nRequests for IU Affiliate access must be initiated by an IU sponsor using the IU Affiliate access request form.\n7.1.2\nThe cost of an affiliate card is $25. To receive a card, present a completed Affiliate Card Request Form along with a valid, government-issued photo ID, at any CrimsonCard office location.\n7.1.3\nDC Access Control will process each request.\n7.1.4\nThe applicant must visit DCOPS with card to register in the Biometrics hand scanner system and have approved access areas assigned.\n7.2 Maintaining IU affiliate access\n7.2.1\nCards must not be altered or defaced in any way; cards must not be bent, written on, have anything affixed to, or have holes punched in them.\n7.2.2\nThe individual's IU sponsor must immediately report any change in job duties or employment status that would eliminate the need for data center access to DC Access Control (\ndcaccess@iu.edu\n).\n7.2.3\nThe individual must retain sole possession of the card for the duration of their approved use. The individual is responsible for card use. Card use is not transferable and cannot be shared. Anyone caught allowing another individual to use their card will have their data center access immediately deactivated.\n7.2.4\nIU affiliate access must be renewed on an annual basis.\n7.3 Replacing IU affiliate access cards\n7.3.1\nLost or stolen cards must be immediately reported to IU Data Center Access Control; email\ndcaccess@IU.edu\nor call 812-855-9910.\n7.3.2\nIf a card is damaged, lost, or stolen, a replacement card will be assigned with the previously approved access areas.\n7.4 Returning IU affiliate access cards\n7.4.1\nUpon termination of employment or change in job responsibilities, it's the individual's responsibility to return access cards to DC Access Control and access will be removed.\n8.0 Daily access\n8.0.1\nDaily access is generally assigned to those who only require data center access for short-term project work.\n8.1 Obtaining daily access\n8.1.1\nRequests for short-term cards must be initiated at the direction of the DCOPS manager using the data center sign-in form available from DCOPS.\n8.1.2\nDCOPS will process each request.\n8.1.3\nDCOPS will issue approved short-term cards.\n8.1.4\nThe applicant must visit DCOPS to obtain the card. The applicant will have to present government-issued identification.\n8.2 Maintaining daily access\n8.2.1\nCards must not be altered or defaced in any way; cards must not be bent, written on, have anything affixed to, or have holes punched in them.\n8.2.2\nThe individual must retain sole possession of the card for the duration of their approved use. The individual is responsible for card use. Card use is not transferable and cannot be shared. Anyone caught allowing another individual to use their card will have their data center access immediately deactivated.\n8.2.3\nDaily access cards expire in 24 hours.\n8.3 Replacing daily access cards\n8.3.1\nLost or stolen cards must be immediately reported to IU Data Center Access Control via email (\ndcaccess@iu.edu\n) or call 812-855-9910.\n8.3.2\nIf a card is damaged, lost, or stolen, it must be reported immediately to DC Access Control. A replacement will be issued by going to DCOPS. Refer to\nsection 8.0.1\n.\n8.4 Returning Daily Access Cards\n8.4.1\nSurrender the card to DCOPS upon signing out and leaving the DC.\n9.0 Visitor/tour access\n9.0.1\nVisitor/Tour Access for an IU data center is granted under limited circumstances. Tours are for educational purposes and are for viewing only.\n9.1 Obtaining Tour Access\n9.1.1\nRequests for tours must be arranged with DCOPS in person, by phone, or via email. Include the purpose of the tour, names of those attending, and preferable dates and times.\n9.1.2\nTours must be approved by the DCOPS manager (or designee).\n9.1.3\nTours must be requested at least 24 hours in advance.\n9.1.4\nA data center team tour guide will coordinate the tour.\n9.1.5\nApproved tour groups will meet their tour guide at DCOPS, sign in, and be issued their visitor badge(s). Individuals in the tour group will be required to present government-issued photo identification.\n9.1.6\nThe tour will be escorted at all times when in IU data centers.\n9.2 Returning visitor badges\n9.2.1\nWhen the tour is finished, the individuals must return their visitor badge(s) and sign out at DCOPS.\n10.0 Card visibility\n10.0.1\nWhile in IU data centers or related secured areas, ID badges must be worn and visible at all times. Acceptable card display areas are on the chest or front hip.\n11.0 Conduct of authorized users\n11.0.1\nNo food or drink is allowed within IU data centers.\n11.0.2\nVisitors may not tamper or interact with equipment that is not theirs.\n11.0.3\nIndividuals must comply with all Data Center Operations team instructions while in IU data centers.\n11.0.4\nCards are non-transferable and may not be used by anyone other than the person to whom the card was assigned.\n11.0.5\nIndividuals must present their access credentials at each access control point to ensure a valid access event is registered (that is, no tailgating).\n12.0 Data Center Access Review Board\n12.0.1\nThe Data Center Access Review Board is an authoritative governing body that reviews and approves all data center access requests.\n12.0.2\nUITS Data Center Access Control assigns and maintains access to IU data centers.\n12.0.3\nA review will take place verifying the ICTC and IU Bloomington data centers access list every 60 days. Data center access will be deactivated after 60 days of inactivity.\n12.0.4\nThe Data Center Access Review Board will review any justifications for renewing expired access. If it is determined that Data Center access is no longer needed the access will not be renewed.\n12.0.5\nAn audit log of all data center access provisioning and deprovisioning will be kept for at least 90 days.\n12.0.6\nUpon termination of employment or in the event of a disciplinary action, data center access will be immediately deactivated and suspended. In the case of disciplinary action, a justification and review process will be required for future access.\n12.0.7\nData Center Access Review Board can be reached by phone at 812-855-9910 or via email at\ndcaccess@iu.edu\n.	KB0024150	KB0024150 v1.0	 access 9.0 Visitor/tour access 10.0 Card visibility 11.0 Conduct of authorized users 12.0 <strong>Data</strong> Center Access Review Board Overview Due to the sensitive nature of the <strong>data</strong> and information systems maintained. They also provide an effective deterrence function in the safe operation of the building. IU <strong>Data</strong> Centers	13.506		2026-01-31 17:34:14.762772	2026-01-31 17:34:14.762772	0	dfefa47b476456583d373803846d4388
12327	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d90b4ea43ba72698476d941864e45a76	Migrate from a personal ChatGPT account to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace	Table of contents\nOverview\nWhat to expect when you request access to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace\nAccess the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace\nMigration prompt for iu.edu personal accounts\nMigration options\nRecommendations\nFrequently asked questions\nGet help\nOverview\nNote:\nAccess to the Indiana University ChatGPT Edu workspace is currently limited to selected users participating in a pilot program. At present, users not participating in the pilot who are using a personal ChatGPT associated with their IU email accounts can continue doing so and should continue following\nPrecautions about using ChatGPT at IU\n.\nIf you previously created a personal ChatGPT account using your\n@iu.edu\nemail address and want to be added to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace, you will be prompted to either migrate your existing ChatGPT data to IU’s environment or download your existing data for storage. Once you join the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace, your personal account associated with your\n@iu.edu\nemail address will be deleted and replaced by the IU-managed version.\nChatGPT does not provide options for changing the email address of an existing personal account or for transferring data from a personal account associated with your IU email address to a personal account associated with a non-IU email address.\nBefore requesting access and migrating your chat data to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace, UITS strongly recommends reviewing your chat history and deleting any sensitive or personal conversations that you do not want to be associated with your IU account.\nThis article outlines what to expect when joining the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace and how to decide between the two migration options.\nNote:\nThis migration process does not apply to personal OpenAI accounts created with non-IU email addresses. Those accounts remain unaffected.\nWhat to expect when you request access to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace\nBefore accessing the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace, you should understand what will happen behind the scenes and what decisions you may need to make ahead of time. This applies especially if you already use ChatGPT with your IU email address.\nWhen your\n@iu.edu\nemail address is added to IU's ChatGPT Edu workspace:\nOpenAI will check whether your IU email address is already associated with a personal ChatGPT account.\nIf it is, you will be prompted to choose what to do with your existing account before you can proceed.\nYou will need to either migrate your existing content (for example, chats and saved GPTs) into the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace, or export and delete your content to start fresh.\nImportant:\nYour IU email address can belong to only one workspace at a time. If it is already used in a personal account, you will be required to resolve that before joining the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace.\nOnce you are added to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace, your IU ChatGPT usage will be managed by the university and subject to institutional policies. Any personal OpenAI activity linked to your IU email account will become part of that managed workspace. Your personal account associated with your\niu.edu\nemail address will be deleted.\nUITS recommends reviewing your account beforehand so you can decide what action is best for your needs when prompted.\nAccess the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace\nOnce you are added to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace, follow these steps to access the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace:\nOn the\nChatGPT\nwebsite, in the top right corner, select\nLog in\n.\nIn the "Email address" field, enter your IU email address (for example,\nusername@iu.edu\n), and then select\nContinue\n.\nSelect\nIndiana University\n.\nOn the\nIU Login\npage, enter your\nIU username and passphrase\n, and then complete a\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\nauthentication.\nAfter authenticating, you will be placed into the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace.\nMigration prompt for iu.edu personal accounts\nWhen your IU email address is added to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace, OpenAI will detect that your IU email address is already associated with a personal account. As a result, you will see a prompt asking what you would like to do with your exiting data. This prompt appears only if your IU email address is already linked to a personal OpenAI account. To complete enrollment in the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace, you must choose between the two migration options outlined in the next section of this document.\nMigration options\nWhen you join the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace with an IU email address that is already associated with a personal OpenAI account, you will be prompted to resolve that conflict before proceeding. You will be presented with the following options for handling your existing personal account:\nOption 1: Transfer\nThis option allows you to merge your personal account data into the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace. It lets you retain your chat history, saved custom GPTs, and other activity by migrating them to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace.\nCertain personalized features, such as plug-ins and custom instructions, may be removed during this process.\nIf you have an active ChatGPT Plus subscription, it will be canceled and refunded once the account is transferred.\nAfter migration, ChatGPT data associated with your IU email address will be fully managed by IU.\nOption 2: Export and Delete\nThis option deletes your personal OpenAI workspace tied to your IU email and clears the way for your IU email address to be used in the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace; however, it does not let you transfer your personal workspace data to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace.\nYou will be offered the ability to export your chat history before it is deleted.\nIf you have an active ChatGPT Plus subscription, it will be canceled and refunded once the account is transferred.\nImportant:\nOnce you finalize your selection, the action is permanent and cannot be reversed. Make sure to use the\nSettings\nmenu in the ChatGPT interface to export anything you wish to retain before proceeding. For more on exporting data, see\nExporting ChatGPT Data\n.\nRecommendations\nIf you want to retain past chats or custom GPTs, choose Option 1.\nIf you prefer to start fresh or already use another email address for personal use, choose Option 2.\nYou cannot change the email address associated with your personal ChatGPT account. You must either transfer or delete your data tied to your\n@iu.edu\nemail address.\nBefore requesting access to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace, UTITS strongly recommends reviewing your chat history and deleting any sensitive or personal conversations that you do not want to be associated with your account in the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace.\nIf you are unsure, use the\nSettings\nmenu in the ChatGPT interface to export anything you wish to retain before proceeding. For more on exporting data, see\nExporting ChatGPT Data\n.\nFrequently asked questions\nWill I lose access to my chat history after migrating my data?\nNo, unless you're removed from the IU workspace. As long as you're active, your migrated history remains available. If your account is later deactivated (for example, if you leave the university or the workspace is removed by IU), your history will be deleted.\nCan I keep or create a personal OpenAI account?\nYes, as long as it is associated with a different (non-IU) email address.\nCan I reverse the migration?\nNo. Once you choose to transfer or delete your personal workspace, the action is irreversible.\nGet help\nIf you have questions or concerns about accessing the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace or migrating your personal ChatGPT account,\ncontact your campus Support Center\n.	KB0026698	KB0026698 v5.0	, it does not let you <strong>transfer</strong> your personal workspace <strong>data</strong> to the IU ChatGPT Edu workspace ChatGPT account. You must either <strong>transfer</strong> or delete your <strong>data</strong> tied to your @iu.edu email address ChatGPT <strong>data</strong> to IU’s environment or download your existing <strong>data</strong> for storage. Once you join the IU	11.7427		2026-01-31 17:34:18.857501	2026-01-31 17:34:18.857501	0	d90b4ea43ba72698476d941864e45a76
12334	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=b821be78473872d0d500b87b716d43d0	Technician instructions: Clean IU Common Build for Windows	Table of contents\nBefore you begin\nCreate boot media\nBuild the device\nBefore you begin\nTo place a fresh build on a Windows computer that will be part of the IU Common Build, follow the instructions\nbelow\n. Legacy UDM tenants must use\ndifferent boot media\n.\nTo use the in-place rebuild with data transfer, see\nTechnician instructions: IU Common Build Windows Wipe and Rebuild with Data Transfer\n.\nFor an overview of the IU Common Build process, see\nGet started with the IU Common Build for Windows\n.\nDo not pre-create the Active Directory (AD) computer object. It will be automatically created by the build process in the correct OU under\nads.iu.edu/IU/IU-UEMS\n. For first time builds, you do not need to delete the old AD object since the new object will be created using the new naming scheme (\nIU-\nSerialNumber\n). Rebuilds already on the IU Common Build also do not need to be deleted from AD. If the computer needs to be moved to a different OU under\nads.iu.edu/IU/IU-UEMS\n,\ncontact EMS\nor use the move tool at\n\\\\iu-sccm-cmfs1\\UEMS$\\Tools\\Common Build Device OU Move Tool.exe\n.\nIf you are going to use a USB network adapter, contact the EMS team to register the USB network adapter in SCCM. If you build a device with USB network adapters that have not been registered in the SCCM console, duplicate devices can appear in the console.\nCreate boot media\nGo to the SMB share:\n\\\\iu-sccm-cmfs1\\uems$\\OSD\\Build Media\nNote:\nYou will need to use your personal admin group account to access the "UEMS$" share. If you have tried to connect with your username, you may need to reboot before Windows will prompt you to enter a different user account.\nRun the Rufus executable as an administrator.\nNext to "Boot selection", choose\nSelect\n.\nSelect the\n.iso\nfile (\nIU-SCCM-UDM-UEMSx64.iso\n).\nUnder "Partition scheme", ensure\nGPT\nis selected.\nSelect\nStart\n. Once USB media creation is complete, close Rufus and eject the drive.\nBuild the device\nNotes:\nThe clean build does\nnot\nperform any automated user data backup. Make sure you manually back up any data as needed.\nInsert the prepared USB drive into the device that you intend to build. Follow the computer manufacturer's instructions for booting from the USB device.\nWhen the Task Sequence Wizard opens, select\nNext\n. You may have to wait a few minutes while policies and information on available task sequences are retrieved.\nNotes:\nIf the device is not registered on the IU network, a browser called Pale Moon will automatically open to the DHCP registration page. If Pale Moon does not open, you can press\nF8\nto open a\ncmd.exe\nwindow, and use command\nipconfig -all\nto get the physical address (also known as the MAC address). Use the physical address to register the device from a different device; see\nRegister your wired device on the IU network\n.\nOnce the Task Sequence Wizard says it has found the "Global Windows OSD", select\nNext\n.\nThe OS Deployment Wizard will open. As you proceed, note that most of the options are set by default.\nOn the "New Computer Details" screen, select the relevant Organizational Unit (OU). Under "Domain Join Credentials", enter an IU username. Make sure you include\nads\\\nor\nads.iu.edu\\\nat the beginning.\nNotes:\nFor the IU Common Build, this username is used for identification rather than to join the domain. During the build, the system will be automatically joined to the ADS domain using the\nSCCMJOIN\ngroup account; this negates the need to delete the AD object before any future rebuilds.\nOn the "BitLocker" screen, you have the option to uncheck "Enable BitLocker". This can be useful in rare cases in which hard drive encryption during the build causes issues.\nNotes:\nBitLocker will be automatically enabled after the build process, regardless of the option you select.\nOn the "Install Programs" screen, you can uncheck default applications and/or check optional software to choose which software is installed at build. All Global Catalog software can be installed later from the Software Center; for more, see\nGlobal UDM Software Catalog Overview\n.\nReview the "Summary" screen, and select\nFinish\n.\nNote that if you select Windows 11, there will be extra Deployment Readiness checks after you select\nFinish\n. These account for the extra Windows 11 requirements of TPM 2.0 and a minimum of 4GB of memory. Select\nFinish\nagain.\nWait for the installation to finish, which usually takes a couple of hours.	KB0025854	KB0025854 v9.0	 below. Legacy UDM tenants must use different boot media. To use the in-place rebuild with <strong>data</strong> <strong>transfer</strong>, see Technician instructions: IU Common Build Windows Wipe and Rebuild with <strong>Data</strong> <strong>Transfer</strong>. For an the drive. Build the device Notes: The clean build does not perform any automated user <strong>data</strong> backup. Make	11.7175	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits, km-aud-ems-admins	2026-01-31 17:34:23.031137	2026-01-31 17:34:23.031137	0	b821be78473872d0d500b87b716d43d0
8359	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=90cfa8b7476456583d373803846d430a	Manually configure an IU Print queue	Table of contents\nOverview\nPublic student queues\nWindows\nmacOS 13.3 (Ventura)\nLinux\nGet help\nOverview\nMost departmental printers and copiers are managed by\nUITS\n, and require you to use a specific queue. Queue paths managed by IU Print begin with\n\\\\EWA-PRNP00\\\nXX\n_\nDeptCode\n, where\nXX\nis the campus code and\nDeptCode\nis the three- or four-letter department code. To get your department's queue path, contact your\nlocal UITS support person\n. Once you have the queue path, follow your platform's instructions below.\nPublic student queues\nThe instructions below also apply to the public student queues. Note that for the public student queues you will need to be connected to an IU campus network.\nBlack & White:\n\\\\EWA-PRNP01.ads.iu.edu\\IU_BW\nColor:\n\\\\EWA-PRNP01.ads.iu.edu\\IU_Color\nWindows\nWindows 11\nPress\nWin\n+\nr\nto open Run Command.\nEnter\n\\\\EWA-PRNP\nXX\n\\\n, where\nXX\nis the print server number.\nPress\nEnter\n.\nWindows 10\nSelect the\nStart\nbutton.\nEnter\n\\\\EWA-PRNP\nXX\n\\\n, where\nXX\nis the print server number.\nPress\nEnter\n.\nIf prompted for your credentials, enter\nADS\\\ndvader\n, where\ndvader\nis your\nIU username\n.\nThe list of print queues available on the server will display. Double-click the queue name to open the print queue.\nOnce the job list window appears, print to the queue from your desired program.\nNote:\nIf you get an access denied error, your local UITS support person may need to use a local administrator account to install the queues for you.\nmacOS 13.3 (Ventura)\nSelect\nSystem Settings\n.\nIn the sidebar, select\nPrinters & Scanners\n.\nSelect\nAdd Printer, Scanner, or Fax…\n.\nSelect the\nIP\nicon (looks like a globe).\nIn the "Address" field, enter\nyour_username\n@EWA-PRNP\nXX\n.ads.iu.edu\n, where\nyour_username\nis your IU username and\nXX\nis the print server number.\nIn the "Protocol" field, select\nLine Printer Daemon – LPD\n.\nIn the "Queue" field, enter the queue name. You can leave all the other options as the default.\nSelect\nAdd\n.\nLinux\nThe process for adding a printer in Linux varies by distribution. See the vendor’s documentation for specific instructions. All installations should require the same basic information:\nProtocol:\nLPD/LPR\nPrint Server:\nEWA-PRNP\nXX\n, where\nXX\nis the print server number\nPrinter Name:\nThe name of the print queue provided by your local UITS support person\nIf you are prompted to enter a device URI, you can adapt the following example:\nlpd://dvader@EWA-PRNP00.ads.iu.edu/BL_DEPT_BW1\nGet help\nFor questions, contact\nprinting@iu.edu\n.	KB0023200	KB0023200 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Public student queues Windows macOS 13.3 (Ventura) Linux Get help Overview Most departmental printers and copiers are managed by UITS, and require you to use a specific queue. Queue paths managed by IU Print begin with \\\\EWA-PRNP00\\XX_DeptCode, where XX is the campus code and DeptCode is the three- or four-letter depar	3.5428		2026-01-31 15:01:58.984831	2026-01-31 17:31:56.986646	0	90cfa8b7476456583d373803846d430a
12343	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=cbdfe43b476456583d373803846d436a	Use a cable longer than 15 feet in campus housing requiring the IU-specific Ethernet cable	If you wish to use a cable longer than 15 feet in campus housing with proprietary Ethernet cables, you have two options:\nPurchase a straight-through cable and an RJ45 coupler from a computer or electronics store. Attach one side of the coupler to the end of the pre-installed IU cable, and plug the straight-through cable into the other side of the coupler and into your computer.\nIf your IU cable can be removed from the wall (not all can), you can purchase an IU proprietary network cable of the desired length from a local computer or electronics store. Ask specifically for an IU wall cable; if you are not specific, you may receive a straight-through cable, which will not work. Once you have your longer cable, unplug the provided IU cable from the jack and plug in your new cable. Leave the pre-installed cable attached to the wall.\nNote:\nUITS calculates its networking needs based on industry-standard specifications, and so will support cables of only 15 feet or less connected to a university data jack. Installing a longer cable on a data jack may exceed the specifications and cause data transfer problems if the total length of the cable from your computer to the switch port exceeds the capability of the electrical signal. (In some areas, you may experience no problems with a longer cable if the distance to the switch port does not yet violate the standard.)	KB0023797	KB0023797 v1.0	 to a university <strong>data</strong> jack. Installing a longer cable on a <strong>data</strong> jack may exceed the specifications and cause <strong>data</strong> <strong>transfer</strong> problems if the total length of the cable from your computer to the switch port exceeds	11.3932		2026-01-31 17:34:26.43255	2026-01-31 17:34:26.43255	0	cbdfe43b476456583d373803846d436a
12351	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=943d2bba47175a502fc6a55b416d432c	Request supplier assistance for electronic document remediation	Table of contents\nOverview\nSupplier information\nHow to choose a supplier\nTransfer documents\nOverview of the purchasing process\nOverview\nIndiana University requires electronic documents to be accessible to people with disabilities and now has contracts with two suppliers for document remediation services.\nElectronic documents include, but are not limited to, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, InDesign, and PDF files.\nElectronic document remediation is the process of ensuring that documents are accessible to individuals with disabilities. This includes providing appropriate alt text for images and graphics, verifying the tag structure of PDF documents, and checking color contrast, among other tasks.\nSupplier information\nIU has service-level agreements with two suppliers that can remediate electronic documents. These agreements lock in system-wide pricing. Both suppliers provide document remediation services, but exact services may vary. They are also rated for different data classification levels.\nAllyant T-Base Communications:\nThis supplier specializes in converting documents to accessible PDFs. The standard turn-around time is five business days. The pricing listed below is based on remediating an existing PDF into an accessible PDF. There are additional costs for rush service.\nContact: Kim Gilmer (\nkgilmer@allyant.com\n)\nAllyant services at a glance:\n$5 per page for standard pages\n$7 per page requiring Optical Character Recognition (OCR)\nFillable forms start at $50 per page\nBarrierBreak Solutions 247 Accessible Documents:\nThis supplier works with a variety of document types. The standard turn-around time is five to seven working days. There are additional costs for rush service.\nContact: Shashank Kapur (\nshashank@barrierbreak.com\n, +91 9920076289)\nBarrierBreak services at a glance:\n$7 per page for PDFs\n$4.50 per page for Word and PowerPoint files\n$4.50 per page for Excel files\n$27.50 per page for fillable PDF/Word forms\nHow to choose a supplier\nImportant:\nBefore choosing a document remediation supplier, you must determine the\ndata classification\nlevel of your documents. If your documents contain\nRestricted\nor\nCritical\ndata, you will need to use Allyant T-Base Communications and take additional steps to securely transfer documents. Documents containing only\nPublic\nand/or\nUniversity-internal\ndata can be remediated by either supplier.\nTo choose the best supplier for your documents, consider the following:\nWhat type(s) of documents do you need remediated?\nAllyant specializes in remediating PDFs but can work with other document types. BarrierBreak can remediate a variety of document types.\nIn what format do you want the remediated document(s)?\nAllyant specializes in creating accessible PDFs. BarrierBreak can remediate documents in the same format they were created in or convert other document types to PDF.\nDo your documents include languages other than English?\nIf your documents include languages other than English, there may be additional costs for remediation. You may need to provide any alt text in other languages. BarrierBreak may use Google translate for any translations.\nDo your documents include STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering, Math) images and graphics?\nIf your documents include STEM images and graphics, you should plan to provide alt text and image descriptions that require subject matter expertise.\nTransfer documents\nIU Secure Share\nis the recommended method for transferring documents to suppliers. Your supplier contact will provide any additional information needed for transferring your documents to them.\nOverview of the purchasing process\nEach department-driven request for services will require a formal quote from the supplier. Use the contact information above to request a quote from your chosen supplier. Each quote must include the following:\nDeclaration that "This Quote is subject to the Framework Services Agreement between the parties."\nReference to the effective date of the agreement\nBegin and end dates of the work to be completed\nItemized pricing\nDescription of services\nOnce a formal quote is obtained, the department must submit a non-catalog requisition in\nBUY.IU\n. The department must attach the quote to the non-catalog requisition for supporting documentation of the cost and services. The quote number should be referenced in the line item description.\nNote:\nWork should not begin until an IU purchase order is received by the supplier for the services requested.	KB0026554	KB0026554 v6.0	Table of contents Overview Supplier information How to choose a supplier <strong>Transfer</strong> documents services, but exact services may vary. They are also rated for different <strong>data</strong> classification levels a document remediation supplier, you must determine the <strong>data</strong> classification level of your documents	9.26	km-aud-iu	2026-01-31 17:34:38.51273	2026-01-31 17:34:38.51273	0	943d2bba47175a502fc6a55b416d432c
12352	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=e398f3004766b29c2fc6a55b416d43ff	About the IU Common Build	Table of contents\nOverview\nWindows\nmacOS\nLinux\nOverview\nThe IU Common Build is a service offered by\nEndpoint Management Services\n(EMS) at Indiana University. The service provides operating system updates, application updates, and baseline security for Windows, macOS, and iOS devices.\nTo learn more about the IU Common Build and how to enroll, see the documentation below and\nIU Common Build: Service Level Expectations\n.\nFor common requests or changes, contact EMS through the\nIU Endpoint Management Services form\n.\nWindows\nGet started with the IU Common Build for Windows\nTechnician instructions: Clean IU Common Build for Windows\nSCCM Global Software Catalog application listing\nGlobal UDM OSD overview\nTechnician instructions: IU Common Windows Wipe and Rebuild with Data Transfer\nThe IU Common Build and MBAM (Microsoft BitLocker Administration and Monitoring)\nIU Common Build: Windows management tasks\nIU Common Build Windows servicing feature updates\nLocal UITS Support People admin accounts for the IU Common Build\nAbout the Interim print servers\nmacOS\nGet started with the IU Common Build for macOS\nBuild information for Apple computers under the IU Common Build\nAD binding macOS on the IU Common Build\nApply the IU Common Build to an iPad\nIU Common Build macOS updates\nADM MUB adding a location name for smart group collections\nAbout the Jamf Endpoint Management ADM multi-user site admin build process\nLocal UITS Support People admin accounts for the IU Common Build\nAbout Apple ADE (Automated Device Enrollment) and decommissioning\nAbout device retirement notices for macOS Common Build devices\nMulti-user build on ADM: Application bundles explained\nUse the MDM Migration feature in macOS, iOS, and iPad 26 (and above)\nLinux\nBuild information for Linux computers on the IU Common Build\nLinux IU Common Build administrative tasks\nLinux IU Common Build: Install Microsoft Outlook and Teams	KB0025959	KB0025959 v18.0	 overview Technician instructions: IU Common Windows Wipe and Rebuild with <strong>Data</strong> <strong>Transfer</strong> The IU Common	8.2051	km-aud-uits, km-aud-local-support, km-aud-ems-admins	2026-01-31 17:34:44.733682	2026-01-31 17:34:44.733682	0	e398f3004766b29c2fc6a55b416d43ff
12386	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=27386a5247657a542fc6a55b416d430f	IU Common Build: Service Level Expectations	Table of contents\nOverview\nTerms and definitions\nGeneral\nDocumentation\nActive Directory computer accounts\nLSP and departmental expectations\nSupport requests\nAccess to IU Common Build IT resources\nEMS expectations\nOperating system build and functionality\nWindows OSD build\nDrivers/firmware/BIOS\nPost-OSD build\nSoftware installation\nApple and Jamf functionality\nOverview\nThe IU Common Build provides a standardized platform for operating system installation and configuration on IU devices. This includes Windows, Apple, and Linux devices. The goal is to improve client experiences by simplifying user and device setup, optimizing performance, ensuring security compliance, eliminating duplicate management tools, and enhancing supportability across all IU workstation devices.\nTerms and definitions\nOSD:\nOperating system deployment\nLegacy:\nThe device configurations (for example, OSD, group policy, etc.) that were previously used by a department prior to migrating to the IU Common Build\nAD/ADS:\nActive Directory\nGPO:\nGroup policy\nMBAM:\nMicrosoft BitLocker Administration and Monitoring\nLAPS:\nLocal Administrator Password Solution\nLSP:\nLocal (on-site) departmental CTS IT support provider (CTS personnel that supports a specific department)\nCTS:\nClient Technology Support (more general term for an LSP)\nPolicies:\nAny policy or configuration applied to a device for the purpose of device management. This includes but is not limited to: GPOs, SCCM configurations, Jamf Pro policies and profiles, and other device management functions.\nGeneral\nDocumentation\nAll Common Build articles\nGet started with the IU Common Build for Windows\nTechnician instructions: Clean IU Common Build for Windows\nTechnician instructions: IU Common Build Windows Wipe and Rebuild with Data Transfer\nThe IU Common Build and MBAM (Microsoft BitLocker Administration and Monitoring)\nGet started with the IU Common Build for macOS\nBuild information for Apple computers under the IU Common Build\nBuild information for Linux computers on the IU Common Build\nLinux IU Common Build administrative tasks\nActive Directory computer accounts\nIU Common Build device naming scheme is\nIU-\nSerialNumber\n.\nIncorrectly named devices may be renamed without notice.\nDevices must be (re)built into the Common Build (IU-UEMS) OU structure.\nDevices still on a legacy build should never be moved into the IU-UEMS OU structure as it can cause problems with the legacy build.\nDevices must always remain managed and joined to Active Directory (AD) with the OCS agent and SCCM client installed and functional.\nDevices' AD computer objects must remain under the IU-UEMS OU structure.\nIU Common Build devices found outside of the IU-UEMS OU structure may be moved back into the correct OU without notice.\nIU Common Build AD computer objects should never be added to AD computer groups.\nAD computer objects found to be a member of an AD computer group may be removed from that AD group without notice.\nDo not disjoin, rename, and rejoin IU Common Build devices outside of the IU-UEMS OU structure.\nLSP and departmental expectations\nAll group policy printer and drive map policies submitted to EMS for migration must be configured for AD user group targeting.\nPrinter policies for IU Print secure release printers may be granted exceptions.\nWindows OSD build and rebuild process should only be performed by IU LSPs.\nInstitutional data should not be stored on Common Build workstations or mobile devices; see\nAbout institutional data at IU\n.\nSupport requests\nSubmit support tickets through the\nEMS Ticket Submission form\nNote:\nTickets submitted to Tier 2 (\nsct2@iu.edu\n) may be delayed reaching EMS as they are routed through Tier 2 support.\nSubmit support tickets for the Linux Common Build through the\nEMS Linux Support Request form\n.\nUser-level functionality tickets should be triaged by the originating departmental on-site CTS support and, if necessary, a secondary ticket submitted to EMS for communication with the LSP to track and troubleshoot the larger issue.\nEMS does not support and does not have the ability to support users at the user level; that is, EMS does not have access to Bomgar or any other screen share utility to see what the user is seeing. This type of troubleshooting should be performed by on-site CTS personnel and, when requested, communicated back to EMS.\nTickets regarding IU Common Build support issues must always be submitted by an IU LSP.\nTickets submitted to EMS with a non-IU LSP as the caller may have the ticket reassigned back to the originating assignment group.\nAccess to IU Common Build IT resources\nIndividual LSP access is maintained via ACM groups set up by EMS and maintained by IT managers; see\nLocal UITS Support People admin accounts for the IU Common Build\n.\nACM admin groups for device administration should not contain primary user accounts.\nOnly secondary LSP admin group accounts may be used to access IU Common Build resources.\nEMS expectations\nChanges to global policies (top-level GPOs and Jamf Pro policies applying to all IU Common Build devices) will be submitted to and tracked through UITS Change Management.\nOperating system build and functionality\nThe IU Common Build does not restrict local user logon without a documented functional need.\nAll university owned devices must be managed.\nWindows devices must be domain joined and managed by SCCM or managed by Intune.\nApple devices will be managed under Jamf Pro.\nLinux devices must be domain-joined and managed by Landscape.\nApple devices must be assigned to Mobile Device Management (MDM) via Apple School Manager (ASM) prior to delivery to a user or setup by department IT staff.\nLocal administrative accounts should not be created on Windows, macOS, or Linux ADS-joined devices.\nAll Windows secondary administrative accounts are applied to devices exclusively through group policy.\nLinux devices operate on a "deny all, allow some" methodology. As such, accounts must be individually authorized on each system.\nFollow the "Account management" steps in\nLinux IU Common Build administrative tasks\nto authorize and de-authorize accounts and to assign and restrict\nsudo\nprivileges.\nSecondary administrative accounts must be ADS group accounts.\nRequests for a standard user (non-LSP) secondary administrative account policy must be submitted to EMS by an LSP.\nLocally created admin accounts will be removed from Administrators by policy on Windows and ADS joined macOS devices.\nApplication of the admin account is based on the computer name, so LSPs should update EMS if a computer is ever repurposed for a different user.\nDisabled and deleted group accounts will be removed from policy at the discretion of EMS.\nWindows LAPS is used to manage the local administrator account password on all IU Common Build Windows devices.\nWindows LAPS Overview\nWindows Laps vs legacy Microsoft LAPS\nJamf LAPS is used to manage the local administrator passwords for accounts created by pre-stage enrollment on all macOS Common Build computers.\nJamf LAPS Overview\nWindows, macOS, and Linux devices must always remain encrypted with BitLocker, FileVault, or LUKS.\nBitLocker is enforced using a combination of SCCM Microsoft BitLocker Administration and Monitoring (MBAM) and group policy.\nInformation about BitLocker on the IU Common Build\nMulti-user macOS devices will not use FileVault unless requested by the department. All Apple devices are encrypted at rest regardless of FileVault status.\nPrinter and SMB file shares should never be created on workstation devices.\nWindows updates and third-party updates will be deployed as they are released with a maximum two-day installation deadline.\nUpdate reboot maintenance windows occur weekly on Thursday nights from 11pm to 5am\nZero-day remediations may be applied outside of the standard installation deadlines and reboot maintenance windows.\nWindows servicing updates will be maintained with the latest version of the Windows operating system with servicing updates occurring approximately every 6 months.\nDue to the Microsoft design of Windows servicing, exceptions to Windows servicing updates cannot be granted. Devices that cannot be upgraded to the latest Windows operating system will need to be rebuilt to a Windows LTSC version.\nLinux and third-party security updates will be deployed every day at 5am. If necessary, the system will be rebooted after updates are installed.\nLinux and third-party feature and bugfix updates will be deployed once per week. The first attempt will take place Saturday at 10pm. If the system is offline or updates are unsuccessful, a second attempt will be made on Sunday at 10pm. If necessary, the system will be rebooted after updates are installed.\nmacOS system updates will be pushed after a week delay (see\nIU Common Build macOS updates\n).\nCommon Build Macs check for needed application updates daily from 5pm to 8am.\nZero-day remediations will be applied as soon as they become available.\nWindows OSD build\nOnly Microsoft Surface and Dell business line models, primarily the standard models offered on Buy.IU, are supported for the IU Common Build OSD process.\nNon-standard device models are not supported for OSD, and drivers cannot be added.\nIf necessary, EMS will assist directly with ensuring non-standard device models receive IU Common Build policies and configurations.\nDevices must be (re)built using the custom IU Common Build ISO\nThe device name is automatically set to "IU-SerialNumber" when using the IU Common Build ISO.\nSerial numbers longer than 12 characters will automatically have any non-alphanumeric characters removed and will be truncated to use the last 12 characters of the serial.\nManually configuring the device name\nwill not work\nto make a device use the IU Common Build OSD process.\nAll IU Common Build devices should be built with Windows 11 or higher operating system.\nDevices requiring Windows 10 will need to demonstrate a documented functional need, such as application computability restrictions, to be granted an exception to use Windows 10. In such cases, Windows 10 LTSC will be used to prevent future servicing updates.\nDrivers/firmware/BIOS\nEMS does not actively update OSD drivers with current versions unless there is a known issue; EMS may update drivers for a specific device model upon request. Only supported device models with drivers included in the Dell Enterprise OSD Driver Packs will be added to the OSD process. All post-build driver installations and/or updates must be completed by the on-site technician; drivers for external devices, such as monitors and docking stations, will not be added to the OSD process. Any post-build firmware and/or BIOS updates and configurations also must be performed by the on-site technician.\nFor Dell devices, use the Dell Command Update tool available in Software Center (or in the\nIntune Company Portal app\n). For Microsoft Surface devices, use the Microsoft Surface app (available in the Windows Store) to open Windows Update; use the drop-down next to the\nCheck for updates\nbutton, and then select\nCheck online for updates through Microsoft Update\n.\nPost-OSD build\nPost-build scripts are not supported on the IU Common Build.\nPost-build scripts created for legacy builds can cause problems and create conflicts with IU Common Build policies.\nThe Windows [Easy] Files and Settings Transfer Wizard should never be used.\nIt is not supported by Microsoft on Windows 10 or Windows 11.\n"Cleaner" applications, such as CCleaner, should never be used on the IU Common Build.\nThese applications commonly remove cached MSI files that are often required to update and uninstall applications. This could cause third-party application updates to fail resulting in a device rebuild.\nWindows LAPS passwords can be accessed on the LAPS tab of the Active Directory computer object device properties.\nManage and monitor Windows LAPS\nThe legacy Microsoft LAPS UI executable will not work to retrieve the Windows LAPS password.\nThe legacy version of Microsoft LAPS (LAPS UI and LAPS GPO extension) that required a manual install has been deprecated and is no longer supported on any current version of Windows.\nDeprecation of legacy Microsoft LAPS\nWindows LAPS is built-in to Windows and does not require any manual installation. Legacy Microsoft LAPS should never be installed on Common Build devices.\nManually installing legacy Microsoft LAPS will cause problems with Windows LAPS and should not be installed on Common Build devices.\nJamf LAPS passwords can be access in the Jamf console.\nUsing LAPS in the Jamf Pro Interface\nSoftware installation\nThe Software Center and Jamf Self Service should be used for software installation whenever possible.\nFrequently used software is packaged, and current versions are maintained as available in the Software Center and Jamf Self Service.\nGlobal applications that are available in Jamf Self Service, the Software Center, and/or OSD will not be deployed as required without a documented functional need.\nWhile\napt\nis available for installing software packages on Linux devices, UITS highly recommends using\nsnap\n. These packages are designed to automatically update and do not conflict with system software or configurations. Installing software with\napt\nmay result in unintended negative effects.\nApple and Jamf functionality\nmacOS computers residing on campus full time should bound to ADS.\nmacOS computers not residing on campus full time will not be bound to ADS.\nThis is to mitigate known issues with AD authentication using cached credentials including FileVault authorization errors.\nUniversity-owned macOS, iOS, and iPadOS devices not deployed as a university owned personal device such as one to one deployment or lifecycle replacements will be managed in Jamf Pro under the direction of EMS Apple Team and in coordination of the department.\nLegacy Casper Jamf Pro managed devices must be reassigned to the Common Build Jamf Pro service and rebuilt; they cannot be migrated.\nmacOS computers must have Microsoft Defender installed.\nmacOS laptop users must have access to privilege escalation options. Departments must vet and account for this configuration.\nOne to one deployments of iOS or iPadOS devices will require that user of the device do the initial setup. Technicians should not do the setup for the user.\nDepartment IT staff are responsible for printer and network share setup on devices.\nWhere possible EMS can make IU Print printer configurations available to departments for devices assignments.	KB0023841	KB0023841 v11.0	 Technician instructions: IU Common Build Windows Wipe and Rebuild with <strong>Data</strong> <strong>Transfer</strong> The IU Common Build build and rebuild process should only be performed by IU LSPs. Institutional <strong>data</strong> should not be stored on Common Build workstations or mobile devices; see About institutional <strong>data</strong> at IU. Support requests	6.2471	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits, km-aud-ems-admins	2026-01-31 17:34:49.502437	2026-01-31 17:34:49.502437	0	27386a5247657a542fc6a55b416d430f
1946	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0024586	About the IU Science DMZ	The Indiana University Science DMZ is a dedicated computer network optimized to accommodate the vast amounts of research data produced by applications\ncritical to scientific discovery and innovation. Unlike networks optimized for general-purpose, enterprise IT services, the IU Science DMZ is engineered specifically to support high performance, data-intensive science applications.\nThe IU Science DMZ's high performance network hardware is used exclusively for transferring research data between IU systems, and between IU and external networks. Its custom security policies are tailored to ensure maximum performance and baseline security. Real-time monitoring and testing systems are used to closely monitor traffic, performance, and security.\nThe IU Science DMZ is based on the\nScience DMZ network model introduced by the Energy Sciences Network (ESnet)\n.	KB0024586	KB0024586 v1.0	 the vast amounts of research <strong>data</strong> produced by applications critical to scientific discovery and innovation is engineered specifically to support high performance, <strong>data</strong>-intensive science applications. The IU Science DMZ's high performance network hardware is used exclusively for transferring research <strong>data</strong> between IU	5.6542		2026-01-31 14:34:35.560715	2026-01-31 17:34:53.36561	0	9c00bcfb476456583d373803846d4335
4065	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0026699	Connect to IU's SSL VPN with Cisco Secure Client (AnyConnect) on a mobile device	Important:\nAs of June 23, 2025, Ivanti Secure has been replaced with Cisco Secure Client (AnyConnect). If you are searching for information regarding Ivanti, update your client via the instructions below.\nTable of contents\nOverview\niOS\nInstall the app\nConfigure the connection\nConnect and disconnect\nAndroid\nInstall the app\nConfigure the connection\nConnect and disconnect\nGet help\nOverview\nTo connect to Indiana University's\nSSL VPN\non an iOS device, use the Cisco Secure Client (AnyConnect) app available in the app store.\nImportant:\nUse the IU VPN only under one of these conditions:\nIf you are trying to access a service you can't get to another way.\nIf your local UITS support person has told you that you need to use it.\nGenerally, you won't need to use the VPN if you are a student. For example, you don't need a VPN connection to:\nUse learning tools, such as Canvas, Zoom, or Kaltura.\nRead your IU email over the web.\nWork with your files in\nMicrosoft OneDrive at IU\nor\nGoogle at IU\nMy Drive\n.\nIf you have difficulty with the VPN:\nTry using\nIUanyWare\n(doesn't require a VPN connection).\nFor example, you can use IUanyWare's Remote Desktop Connection app to remote into a device on campus. Additionally, IUanyWare's Chrome browser allows you to access sites as if you were on campus.\nIn IUanyWare, search the available apps to find what may be helpful.\nNote:\nTo ensure accountability of network communication, the\nUniversity Information Policy Office\nprohibits\ngroup accounts\nfrom connecting to IU's SSL VPN. To make a VPN connection, you must log in using your personal\nIU username and passphrase\n.\nUITS staff and local UITS support people:\nAlthough group accounts cannot access the SSL VPN by default, exceptions are allowed for areas using group accounts to fulfill separation of duty requirements in the groups or two-factor realms. For more, see\nGroup accounts and the SSL VPN\n.\niOS\nInstall the app\nCisco Secure Client for iOS is available from the App Store. Look for the Cisco Secure Client icon:\nNote:\nCisco Secure Client does not support\nUSB security keys\nfor authentication using\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n. For alternative device options, see\nTwo-Step Login (Duo) device recommendations\n.\nConfigure the connection\nThe first time you open Cisco Secure Client, follow the instructions on the screen. It may ask you to enable the app, or to accept a license agreement.\nSelect\nConnections\n.\nSelect\nAdd VPN Connection\n.\nEnter an optional description.\nIn the "Server Address" field, enter the following:\nhttps://vpn.uits.iu.edu\nSelect\nSave\nto exit the setup menu.\nAllow Cisco to add a VPN profile when prompted. Enter your phone PIN to authorize this change.\nReturn to the Cisco Secure Client app and select\nHome\n. Slide to enable the AnyConnect VPN.\nLog in with your IU email address and passphrase.\nApprove the Duo prompts.\nFor other settings, use the defaults.\nConnect and disconnect\nTo connect:\nOpen Cisco Secure Client.\nSlide to enable the AnyConnect VPN.\nLog in with your IU email address and passphrase if prompted.\nApprove the Duo prompts.\nTo disconnect, slide the slider to the off position.\nAndroid\nInstall the app\nOpen the Google Play store.\nIn the search bar, enter\nCisco Secure Client\n. Look for the Cisco Secure Client logo:\nSelect and install the app.\nNote:\nCisco Secure Client does not support\nUSB security keys\nfor authentication using\nTwo-Step Login (Duo)\n. For alternative device options, see\nTwo-Step Login (Duo) device recommendations\n.\nConfigure the connection\nOpen the app, and, if prompted, accept the End User License Agreement.\nSelect\nConnections\n.\nIn the bottom right, select the\n+\nsymbol.\nEnter an optional description.\nIn the "Server Address" field, enter\nhttps://vpn.uits.iu.edu\nand then select\nDone\n.\nConnect and disconnect\nThe first time you try to connect, you may see a prompt asking if you trust this application. Check the box, and then select\nOK\n.\nTo connect:\nThe first time you try to connect, you may see a security warning asking you about a certificate. Accept the certificate.\nFlip the switch next to "AnyConnect VPN" into the "on" position.\nSign in with your IU email address and passphrase.\nApprove any Duo prompts.\nTo disconnect, flip the switch next to "AnyConnect VPN" into the off position.\nGet help\nFor help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0026699	KB0026699 v1.0	Important: As of June 23, 2025, Ivanti Secure has been replaced with Cisco Secure Client (AnyConnect). If you are searching for information regarding Ivanti, update your client via the instructions below. Table of contents Overview iOS Install the app Configure the connection Connect and disconnect Android Install the app Configure the connectio	4.7337		2026-01-31 14:43:34.007473	2026-01-31 16:53:28.332034	0	f07915523386a25804e605570e5c7bb4
12397	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=a09ddf64333ea21804e605570e5c7b58	WCMS Terms of Use	Table of contents\nScope\nRelated policies\nPurpose of Terms of Use\nTerms of Use\nData storage\nGroup name change\nSite names\nWeb standards\nWeb domains\nWeb Services\nEnforcement\nScope\nThese Terms of Use apply to all contributors, approvers, publishers, and managers of the Indiana University Web Content Management System (WCMS) service.\nRelated policies\nAppropriate Use of Information Technology Resources (IT-01)\nMisuse and Abuse of Information Technology Resources (IT-02)\nPurpose of Terms of Use\nIU expects the WCMS and related information technology resources will be used in a lawful manner and in support of the university's mission of research and creative activity, teaching and learning, and civic engagement.\nTerms of Use\nUnless otherwise specified in these Terms of Use or other university policies, use of the WCMS is restricted to purposes related to the university's mission of research and creative activity, teaching and learning, and civic engagement. Eligible individuals are provided access in order to support their studies, instruction, duties as employees, official business with the university, and other university-sanctioned activities.\nWCMS accounts are provided to individuals with university accounts that authenticate through\nIU Login\n. The WCMS Administrators require unique user identification when accessing the WCMS and its related applications. Group accounts may not be used for authentication and access. Unique user identification is required for auditing, security, and HIPAA requirements. Individuals may not share with or transfer their university accounts to others including network IDs, passwords, or other access codes that allow them to gain access to the WCMS.\nThe WCMS may not be used in a manner that violates the law, for private commercial activities that are not approved by the university, for personal private gain, or for political campaigning and similar activities that are inconsistent with the university's tax-exempt status.\nData storage\nOnly Public and University-Internal data are suitable to be stored in the WCMS. Restricted and Critical data cannot be stored in the WCMS. For definitions of Public, University-Internal, Restricted, and Critical data, see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n. In addition, federally and state-protected data, human subjects research data, passwords, and protected health information cannot be stored in the WCMS. For more about protected health information, see\nAbout protected health information (PHI)\n. For more about data classifications, see\nAbout sensitive data at IU\n.\nGroup name change\nThe Web Content Management System, Cascade CMS, does not allow users to change group names. Site Managers can create a new group with the desired name and delete the old group. To request the deletion of manager groups, email the UITS Web team at\nwebhelp@iu.edu\n.\nSite names\nSite names exist for the WCMS Administrators to locate and organize sites. Site names follow a naming convention of\nCampus-Organization-Department.siteName\n(for example,\nBL-COLLEGE-PSY.tomlab\n). Site Managers may request a site name change. However, if a site name is changed, access rights for all assets will be reset to the default state. It is the responsibility of the Site Manager to recreate groups and assign them to the appropriate assets.\nWeb standards\nSites within the WCMS must meet accessibility, visual design, content, and navigation guidelines outlined by the\nIU Brand Guidelines\nfor electronic media.\nWeb domains\nRequesting a\ndepartmental domain, subdomain, or designer domain name\nis the responsibility of the school, department, division, or unit.\nWeb Services\nWeb Services should not be used for publishing. Possible exceptions should be evaluated by WCMS Administrators. The WCMS team does not provide development support for custom Web Services applications. Use of Web Services API Keys instead of a Web Services user is preferred whenever possible.\nEnforcement\nIU will handle reports of misuse or abuse of information and the WCMS in accordance with existing policies and procedures issued by appropriate authorities. Depending on the individual and circumstances involved, this could include the offices of Human Resources, Vice Provost or Vice Chancellor of Faculties (or campus equivalent), Dean of Students (or campus equivalent), Office of the General Counsel, and/or appropriate law enforcement agencies. For more, see\nMisuse and Abuse of Information Technology Resources (IT-02)\n.\nFailure to comply with the WCMS Terms of Use may result in sanctions relating to the individual's use of the WCMS (such as suspension or termination of access, removal of online material, or disabling of the individual's WCMS site). WCMS Administrators are authorized to define violations of and enforce the Terms of Use. For more, see\nAppropriate Use of Information Technology Resources (IT-01)\n.	KB0026312	KB0026312 v4.0	Table of contents Scope Related policies Purpose of Terms of Use Terms of Use <strong>Data</strong> storage Group, and HIPAA requirements. Individuals may not share with or <strong>transfer</strong> their university accounts to others and similar activities that are inconsistent with the university's tax-exempt status. <strong>Data</strong> storage Only	5.3989		2026-01-31 17:34:56.821925	2026-01-31 17:34:56.821925	0	a09ddf64333ea21804e605570e5c7b58
12402	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=02a141cd472032942fc6a55b416d4339	Best practices for Microsoft Teams meetings in PC-based conference rooms	Instead of a dedicated videoconferencing (Cisco or Polycom) unit, many Indiana University conference rooms use a standard PC for collaboration, sometimes referred to as PCVC equipment (Personal Computer for Video Conferencing). These rooms provide flexibility by allowing participants to choose the app needed for each meeting. However, this flexibility requires the user who logs into the conference room PC to take steps to maintain privacy and to ensure a quality meeting experience.\nConsider the following tips for a successful meeting when using\nMicrosoft Teams\nin a PCVC room.\nTo adjust your privacy settings for the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication, choose one of the following options:\nNote:\nThe lobby function in\nMicrosoft Teams\nis turned off by default for future and recurring meetings. If you want to modify that setting, see\nAbout the lobby in Microsoft Teams meetings at IU\n.\nSet your\nMicrosoft Teams\nstatus to\nDo not disturb\n; for instructions, see\nChange your status in Microsoft Teams\n. This will prevent desktop notifications (pop-up messages at the lower right) from appearing during your meeting.\nTurn off pop-up chat notifications for the\nMicrosoft Teams\napp; for instructions, see\nOnly get notifications in the app\n.\nLog into the conference room PC with an administrative group account (if available) instead of your personal credentials; this will ensure that personal account information is not displayed. The group account will need to be invited to the meeting or the meeting will need to be joinable by any IU account.\nClose unneeded applications such as email, chat, or any documents that may contain private or sensitive information or that may require restricted access. For more about data classification at IU, see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n.\nTo reserve the physical room, include the Outlook account for the room in the meeting invitation; for more, see\nSchedule meetings and reserve rooms at IU\n.\nIf you know that a conference room with a Cisco or Polycom unit may be joining the\nMicrosoft Teams\nmeeting, verify that the invitation includes "Join with a video conferencing device" information within the\nMicrosoft Teams\nmeeting dialing instructions. These instructions only appear in\nMicrosoft Teams\nmeeting invitations that were created on or after July 11, 2021.\nOther participants in the physical conference room can share their screen wirelessly by joining the meeting from the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication on their personal device. When these in-room participants join, have them choose the video and audio option\nDon't use audio\nto prevent audio feedback in the conference room.\nIf you wish, you may add a second device to the\nMicrosoft Teams\nmeeting. When you are logged into the\nMicrosoft Teams\napplication on the conference room PC, and you join from the\nMicrosoft Teams\napp on your personal device to wirelessly share that device's screen, you'll see a prompt to either\nAdd this device\nor\nTransfer to this device\n; choose\nAdd this device\n. For more, see\nJoin a Microsoft Teams meeting on a second device\n.	KB0024218	KB0024218 v2.0	. For more about <strong>data</strong> classification at IU, see Classification levels of institutional <strong>data</strong>. To reserve, you'll see a prompt to either Add this device or <strong>Transfer</strong> to this device; choose Add this device	5.3616		2026-01-31 17:35:00.930425	2026-01-31 17:35:00.930425	0	02a141cd472032942fc6a55b416d4339
12405	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=ac7b42f5472bd650d500b87b716d43de	Migrate your servers to EWA	Table of contents\nOverview\nFor all servers\nFor all Active Directory OUs\nFor the Campus Network Portal\nMigration of physical servers to EWA\nOverview\nEnterprise Windows and Azure Administration (EWA) can assume management of a pre-existing server, or deploy a new one to replace it. Migrating an existing server may be preferable in cases where deploying a new one would be prohibitively complex or expensive. To migrate a server to EWA, submit a ticket to\newahelp@iu.edu\n.\nCompleting the steps below will help expedite this process; pre-migration tasks cannot begin until EWA has access to the server and related resources.\nFor all servers\nAdd\nads\\iu-ewa-admins\nto the local administrator group.\nWork up an inventory listing the following:\nServer name\nWhat the server does\nTest/production? EWA will migrate test workloads first, if available.\nLocal contact(s) for the server. Who can answer questions EWA might have and test before/after EWA migrates it?\nStored data classification (public, university-internal, restricted, critical). If restricted or critical, describe the type of data being stored.\nAny special considerations EWA might need to be aware of related to scheduling of maintenance. By default, all production servers are patched on the third Sunday of the month between 12am-8am; non-production servers patch the evening of the prior Wednesday. Decisions about patching at other times are case-by-case depending on business requirements and EWA staff overhead.\nIf the server hosts vended software, include the name of the software and any information you have about the vendor. All software deployed on EWA-managed servers needs to be actively maintained and patched at regular intervals. The EWA AppAdmin team will help with scheduling and implementing these upgrades. If vendor technicians need remote access to your servers, EWA has tools to facilitate that.\nWhat users or groups will need local admin permissions on the servers once migrated? EWA will ask you to submit a FireForm post-migration to formalize this and recertify on a yearly basis, but it's helpful to know in advance.\nYour preferred window for the actual migration to occur. The server will be intermittently available while EWA cut it over; the server will be rebooted, and it EWA may need to troubleshoot if services fail to start or if there are other obvious problems.\nIs the server located in II?\nIf so, provide the billing account(s) associated with your current resource pool(s). EWA will create new pools billed to these same accounts for the migrated servers and move the VMs at migration time. You may decommission the old pools if they are empty post-migration.\nIf it's a physical machine, provide management addresses for any iDRAC or similar out-of-band management interfaces; EWA will also need login credentials or permissions assigned to\nads\\iu-ewa-admins\n. Also provide the rack and U location of the server.\nIf this is a fileserver, how do your users access its shares? If you have the shares behind DFS-N, provide the details on the namespace: is it hosted locally on the server(s), or in AD? Do you have any client devices that are not standard Windows or Mac workstations (for example, scanners) that might have problems with newer SMB features like encryption?\nFor all Active Directory OUs\nGrant full control to\nads\\iu-ewa-admins\n.\nFor the Campus Network Portal\nEither add all members of\nads\\ewa-cnp\nto your CNP AD group(s), or grant EWA control over the AD group so EWA can do that for you.\nMigration of physical servers to EWA\nCustomers who wish to transfer existing bare-metal servers to EWA management may do so; EWA's ability to support the server may be limited by factors such as:\nAbsence of a hardware maintenance contract with the vendor\nAbsence of an out-of-band management interface (for example, iDRAC)\nAge of the server\nCustomers transferring out-of-support/end-of-life hardware to EWA assume all risks related to system failure. EWA strongly recommends prioritizing virtualization of these workloads to mitigate these risks. II supports "P2V" (physical-to-virtual) migrations of Windows servers, which may allow for expedited migrations when compared to a full rebuild. New hardware is subject to the terms of the EWA standards and practices related to physical servers.	KB0026524	KB0026524 v2.0	 EWA might have and test before/after EWA migrates it? Stored <strong>data</strong> classification (public, university-internal, restricted, critical). If restricted or critical, describe the type of <strong>data</strong> being stored can do that for you. Migration of physical servers to EWA Customers who wish to <strong>transfer</strong> existing bare-metal servers to EWA	5.24	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:35:04.264419	2026-01-31 17:35:04.264419	0	ac7b42f5472bd650d500b87b716d43de
12407	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d110bc7f476456583d373803846d43a3	About the Records Retention Schedule Database (RRSD)	Table of contents\nOverview\nAccess\nNavigation\nDatabase fields and values\nType\nArea/Sub-Area Scope\nRecords Retention Area\nRecords Retention Sub-Area\nArea/Sub-Area Description\nRecord Series Code\nRecord Series\nRecord Description\nVital\nEvent Code\nRetention Period\nDisposition\nBased On\nGoverning Statutes\nGoverning Regulations\nGoverning IU Policies\nReason for Retention Period\nOfficial Record Medium\nOffice of Record\nCan Units Retain Copies\nRequires Certificate of Destruction\nRecords Coordinator\nData Steward Domain\nCreation Date\nOverview\nThe Records Retention Schedule Database (RRSD) is the official repository of records retention and disposition schedules for all Indiana University campuses, schools, and departments. It organizes retention and disposition instructions for materials by record series, a group of logically related records with the same retention and disposition instructions that support a specific business or administrative activity (for example, a personnel file consisting of an application, reference letters, etc.).\nAll units, university-wide, are to follow the records retention and disposition instructions in the RRSD.\nTo suggest a change or addition to the records retention schedules in the RRSD, email\narchives@iu.edu\n.\nAccess\nAccess the database through\nOne.IU\n, or directly at\nhttps://rrs.iu.edu.\n.\nAnyone with an\nIU username and passphrase\ncan log in.\nIf you're on campus, you must be connected to the wired campus network,\neduroam\n, or\nIU Secure\n, and if off campus, you must use the\nIU VPN\n.\nNavigation\nAfter login, the default view is the Simple view, which displays a limited set of fields. To display all fields, check\nAdvanced view\n.\nThe default sort order of the entire database is reverse creation date.\nTo search in a field, use the funnel, choose\ncontains\nor another limiter, and enter the search term.\nTo sort a field alphabetically or numerically, select the field name.\nDatabase fields and values\nType:\nRecords Series that are common to more than one unit are called a General Schedule (G); Unit-Specific Schedules (U) are for records that are only handled/held by one unit.\nGeneral\nUnit-specific\nArea/Sub-Area Scope:\nUniversity-wide\nIU Bloomington\nIU Indianapolis\nIU East\nIU Kokomo\nIU Northwest\nIU Southeast\nIU South Bend\nRecords Retention Area:\nThese are standard values kept in the list of records coordinators by area linked from\nRecords Coordinators\n.\nExample: Financial, Intercollegiate Athletics, etc.\nRecords Retention Sub-Area:\nThese are standard values determined by the Records Coordinator for this area, kept in the University-Wide Records Areas and Records Coordinators spreadsheet.\nYou may leave this blank if none.\nArea/Sub-Area Description:\nThe Records Coordinator for this area may choose to describe or clarify the scope of the area and/or sub-area here.\nRecord Series Code:\nThis is a unique alphanumeric identifier for each record series, similar to IU Policy codes. This can be helpful when referring to a particular record or record series on other documents (for example, a destruction certificate), or if the name of the record series changes but still refers to the same documents. Use letters from the Office of Record's Org Code, which is helpful for sorting and exporting by Office of Record.\nExamples:\nUA-HUMM -001 = University Human Resource Services\nUA-TREA-001 = Office of the Treasurer\nThe first two digits represent the campus scope.\nThe second set of digits represents the functional or organizational area.\nThe third set of digits represents the chronological listing of record series in the schedule a particular scope and area.\nRecord Series:\nThis is a prose identifier for each record series, which can be helpful to clearly identify a particular record series.\nExample: Prospective Student Athlete Forms, or Tenure Packet\nRecord Description:\nProvides a definition/description of the record series, such as what documents it includes, etc.\nVital:\nThis is a Business Continuity term, and indicates records very important to the function of the unit.\nYes\nNo\nEvent Code:\nStandard codes for IU that indicate an event that starts the disposition, such as\nFYE\nfor Fiscal Year End. (If a record should be retained for seven years after the current fiscal year, the retention policy would be\nFYE + 7\n.)\nRetention Period:\nStandard time period that indicates how long the record should be retained after the event indicated by the event code.\nTo determine how long to keep the record, note when the event must occur (reach end of fiscal year, for example), and then add the time period here to that time. You would keep the records for the fiscal year that just ended, PLUS seven years more (add the time period to the event).\nIf kept indefinitely, this field will indicate\nPermanent\n.\nDisposition:\nDirections for how to dispose of the records. Disposition may include destruction or transfer to another office, including the campus archives.\nIf the official record medium is paper, and the records\ndo not\ncontain sensitive data, the value will be\nRecycle\n.\nIf the official record medium is paper, and the records\ndo\ncontain sensitive data, the value will be\nSecurely Shred\n.\nIf the official record medium is electronic, and the records\ndo not\ncontain sensitive data, the value will be\nDelete\n.\nIf the official record medium is electronic, and the records\ndo\ncontain sensitive data, the value will be\nSecurely Delete\n.\nIf the record is to be kept indefinitely, the value will be\nNot Applicable\n.\nThis field may also indicate if records are to be transferred to Archives or to another location.\nBased On:\nDescribes the source of information that assisted IU in choosing the retention period and disposition.\nGoverning Statutes:\nDescribes which overarching law on which the retention period is based.\nGoverning Regulations:\nDescribes the specific citation of the regulation, where the retention information is communicated.\nGoverning IU Policies:\nDescribes the IU policy where the retention information is communicated.\nReason for Retention Period:\nTo record any information about why this retention period was chosen and disposition instructions, especially if it is based on business need.\nOfficial Record Medium:\nThe format the official copy of the records is kept in.\nPaper\nElectronic (if you scan a paper version to store and shred the paper, then choose electronic)\nPaper and electronic\nOffice of Record:\nThe office/person ultimately responsible for decisions regarding the retention/disposition instructions for this record series for IU.\nCan Units Retain Copies?:\nProvide instructions to units as to whether they may retain copies, or if they need to retain originals, and for how long if this differs from the official copy retention period.\nExample: Yes, up to two years post termination\nRequires Certificate of Destruction:\nUsed only where a statute or regulation\nrequires\nit.\nYes\nNo\nRecords Coordinator:\nEmail address of the IU Records Coordinator relevant to this record series.\nData Steward Domain:\nIf the record series can be mapped to a Data Steward Domain, it is entered here.\nCreation Date:\nThis field is populated by the system when the record is created.	KB0025026	KB0025026 v1.0	 Office of Record Can Units Retain Copies Requires Certificate of Destruction Records Coordinator <strong>Data</strong> of the records. Disposition may include destruction or <strong>transfer</strong> to another office, including the campus archives. If the official record medium is paper, and the records do not contain sensitive <strong>data</strong>	5.1758		2026-01-31 17:35:08.236459	2026-01-31 17:35:08.236459	0	d110bc7f476456583d373803846d43a3
12449	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=99966a184714a2502fc6a55b416d433a	SMTP servers for external email services	Table of contents\nOverview\nExemptions\nInternal email\nServer applications that do not support authenticated SMTP\nSMTP authentication account\nAlternative authentication methods\nSMTP server protocols and ports\n"Too many recipients" error message\nOverview\nIndiana University requires\nSSL\n(Secure Sockets Layer) encryption and\nauthentication\nfor the university\nSMTP servers\n(\nmail-relay.iu.edu\n) to increase security and stem the flow of email messages propagating\nspam\n,\nviruses\n, and worms. SSL protects outbound email by encrypting it before sending it to an SMTP server. Authentication ensures that only legitimate IU users are able to send email messages through the SMTP server to email addresses outside IU.\nIf your group's service needs to send email outside the Indiana University network, you generally must set up SMTP authentication.\nAll SMTP authentication to\nmail-relay.iu.edu\nis restricted and requires prior authorization before being able to send authenticated email. To submit an authorization request, fill out the\nMail Relay Authorization form\n. After your submission is processed, you will be authorized to send authenticated email.\nExemptions\nInternal email\nEmail messages sent from a server on the IU network to addresses within the IU system do not require authentication. Authentication is required only for messages sent through the SMTP server to email addresses outside IU.\nServer applications that do not support authenticated SMTP\nIf your server does not support authenticated SMTP, you can request an exemption for it by filling out the\nMail Relay Authorization form\n.\nSMTP authentication account\nUITS\nrecommends requesting a separate service account for authenticating against the SMTP server. This way, if your server is compromised, the attackers may get the\npassphrase\nfor the service account, but will not gain access to an account that has administrative privileges in your environment.\nAlternative authentication methods\nThe IU SMTP server supports GSSAPI (Generic Security Services Application Program Interface) authentication in addition to authentication using a username and password. However,\nTier 2 Support\ndoes not currently support this method, meaning you must implement it yourself.\nSMTP server protocols and ports\nTCP ports 25/587\nTLS ports 25/587\nNote:\nDo not use Port 465. This port will be disabled as of March 27, 2025. It was a Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) port originally used for SMTPS (SMTP over SSL). Port 465 was deprecated after STARTTLS was introduced.\n"Too many recipients" error message\nIf you see the error message "4.5.3 Too many recipients", your email message originated from an off-campus IP address and included more recipients than\nmail-relay.iu.edu\npermits. The maximum number of recipients per email message is 30. This limit does not affect email messages sent from on-campus connections or messages sent within Exchange. If you need to email more than 30 recipients, you will need to compose additional messages for the remaining recipients.\nNote:\nIf an IU List address is a recipient, it counts as one recipient, regardless of how many subscribers are included on the IU List.	KB0023613	KB0023613 v3.0	Table of contents Overview Exemptions Internal email Server applications that do not support authenticated SMTP SMTP authentication account Alternative authentication methods SMTP server protocols and ports "Too many recipients" error message Overview Indiana University requires SSL (Secure Sockets Layer) encryption and authentication for the un	5.6118	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:35:59.399971	2026-01-31 17:35:59.399971	0	99966a184714a2502fc6a55b416d433a
12408	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=01df2cf7476456583d373803846d43e0	IU's policy on mobile device security	At Indiana University, any faculty or staff member, affiliate, or student-employee who uses a mobile device to access, store, or manipulate institutional data\nmust\n:\nApply appropriate safeguards to the device to mitigate the risk of information exposure due to loss or theft.\nReport any device that is lost, stolen, or otherwise compromised to\nit-incident@iu.edu\n.\nWipe (erase) all data stored on any device before transferring ownership (for example, by sale or trade-in).\nFor details, including specific safeguards required for mobile devices, see\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\n.\nImportant:\nIU's\nMobile Device Security Standard (IT-12.1)\nrequires the use of data encryption on handheld devices used to access, store, or manipulate\nCritical data\nat IU. Such use also requires "written approval from the senior executive of the unit involved or the Institutional Review Board confirming a critical business need".\nFor information on applying appropriate safeguards, see\nProtect data on your mobile device\nor contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.	KB0023596	KB0023596 v1.0	For information on applying appropriate safeguards, see Protect <strong>data</strong> on your mobile device or contact your campus Support Center.	5.1536		2026-01-31 17:35:11.997158	2026-01-31 17:35:11.997158	0	01df2cf7476456583d373803846d43e0
12411	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=3400303f476456583d373803846d4379	Manage users for your department's OnBase solution	On this page:\nOverview\nUser group permissions and membership\nAdd, change, or remove user access\nOverview\nTo add or remove users from your department's OnBase solution, you need to be an approved Point of Contact (POC) for your department. If you don't know who your POC is, ask representatives within your department.\nAll new users must be in compliance with:\nAcceptable Use Agreement\nFERPA tutorial\nHRMS Data Use tutorial\nTo check compliance, use\nEnterprise Application Compliance\n. Alternatively, you can view a user's compliance status using\nAccess Control Management (ACM)\n; for more about ACM, see\nAdd, change, or remove user access\n(below).\nUser group permissions and membership\nBefore adding or removing users from an OnBase user group, use the OnBase Reporting Dashboards to review the permissions and current membership for each group. You may see usernames listed in your Reporting Dashboard that are not listed in the ACM group; this is due to a delay in removal from OnBase. However, as long as usernames are not displayed within the ACM group (see below), they will not be able to log in to access data stored in OnBase. For help running reports, see:\nReporting Dashboards in OnBase\nRun a User Group Permissions report and a User Membership report in OnBase\nFor data security purposes, all POCs should routinely (at least monthly) review group membership. When OnBase users transfer between departments or terminate employment with IU, their OnBase access privileges need to be reviewed and removed as appropriate.\nAdd, change, or remove user access\nPOCs can add and remove OnBase user access via\nAccess Control Management (ACM)\n. If you are not a POC, you will not see any OnBase groups in ACM. If you are a POC but are not seeing the appropriate OnBase groups, submit an\nOnBase Support Request\n. If a user requires more extensive OnBase functionality (for example, add/remove from notifications, drop-down lists, reviewer assignments, access to specific queues, etc.), add the user to ACM, and then submit an\nOnBase Support Request\nasking for the additional user privileges.\nFor help adding, changing, and removing users from ACM groups, see\nEdit the membership of an Access Control Management (ACM) group\n.\nImportant:\nIn ACM, the\nCreate Group\ntool creates new groups in ACM only.\nIt does not create groups in OnBase\n. To request a new OnBase group, submit an\nOnBase Support Request\n.\nIf you have questions about using ACM to manage OnBase access, submit an\nOnBase Support Request\n.	KB0024624	KB0024624 v1.0	 below), they will not be able to log in to access <strong>data</strong> stored in OnBase. For help running reports, see in OnBase For <strong>data</strong> security purposes, all POCs should routinely (at least monthly) review group membership. When OnBase users <strong>transfer</strong> between departments or terminate employment with IU, their OnBase	5.0335	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:35:20.171374	2026-01-31 17:35:20.171374	0	3400303f476456583d373803846d4379
12414	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=08dfe4f7476456583d373803846d43ee	DFS management at IU	On this page:\nSet up a DFS namespace\nAdd redundant roots to an existing namespace\nDFS folders and folder targets\nDFS replication\nSet up DFS replication\nReplicate folders with data\nSet up a DFS namespace\nThese instructions assume you are running Server 2008 R2 or higher.\nIn\nServer Manager\n, add the\nFile Services\nrole. Enable the\nFile Server\nand\nDistributed File\nSystem\nrole services, and select the option to create a namespace later.\nNote:\nThis will open several DFS-related items in the firewall (including\nFile and Printer Sharing\n). To restrict access, include the IP addresses and domain controllers of your servers that will be participating in DFS;\nFile and Printer Sharing\nshould remain open.\nAssign a name to your DFS namespace (typically your campus-dept code, for example,\nIU-ITPS\n; see\nRecommended naming conventions for IU Windows computers and groups\n).\nVerify that the firewall rules created in step 1 are still open and domain admins are administrators on your machine. Make sure the name you want for your DFS namespace (for example,\n\\\\server\\IU-ITPS\n) has not already been assigned to a share on the server.\nIncluding the name of your namespace, the FQDN of the server initially hosting the namespace, and the group to be assigned as the namespace administrator (typically it's your OU Admin group),\ncontact Tier 2\n.\nAfter the ADS admins have created the domain-based namespace on your server:\nThe local path of your namespace will be\nC:\\DFSRoots\\{namespace}\n(for example,\nC:\\DFSRoots\\IU-ITPS\n).\nThis folder will be shared as\n\\\\server\\{namespace}\n(for example,\n\\\\server\\IU-ITPS\n).\nYou'll be able to access this namespace using the following path:\n\\\\ads.iu.edu\\{namespace}\n(for example,\n\\\\ads.iu.edu\\IU-ITPS\n).\nAdd redundant roots to an existing namespace\nWith redundant roots, clients will be redirected seamlessly to another DFS namespace server in the event of a server outage.\nNote:\nThese instructions assume you have set up a domain-based namespace.\nIn\nServer Manager\n, add the\nFile Services\nrole. Enable the\nFile Server\nand\nDistributed File\nSystem\nrole services, and select the option to create a namespace later.\nNote:\nThis will open several DFS-related items in the firewall (including\nFile and Printer Sharing\n). To restrict access, include the IP addresses and domain controllers of your servers that will be participating in DFS;\nFile and Printer Sharing\nshould remain open.\nMake sure the\nDFS Management snap-in\nis installed on the management server. This does not have to be the server you're using for DFS.\nRun the\nDFS Management\nsnap-in\n. Right-click\nNamespaces\n, and add your namespace to the display.\nRight-click your namespace, select\nAdd Namespace Server...\n, and enter the name of the server you configured above. The local path will automatically be created, along with the proper sharing permissions.\nSelect\nEdit Settings\n. Select\nAdministrators have full\naccess; other users have read-only permissions\n, and then select\nOK\ntwice. The wizard will finish, creating the folders on your server, along with the share.\nDFS folders and folder targets\nAfter you've set up your DFS namespace, you can create DFS folders that point to existing file shares on servers (folder targets). The folder targets do not have to be on the same server as the DFS namespace roots.\nA DFS folder can have folder targets to more than one server. To set which folder target is the preferred server, select the\nFolder\nTarget\ntab, and select\nProperties\n. Then select the\nAdvanced\ntab, and select\nOverride referral\nordering\n.\nNote:\nSpecifying a preferred server doesn't guarantee that all client referrals will be to that server.\nDFS replication\nAt IU, in order to allow for the most flexibility with DFS replication, the standard Microsoft recommendation isn't followed. Instead, SCT2 will create a single replication group for a department. Within that replication group, you may have multiple sets of replicated folders. You may add additional servers if desired.\nNotes:\nAlthough they often go hand in hand, DFS replication does not require the use of DFS namespaces.\nStandard DFS replication does not work well for files that can be opened and written to by multiple people or locations at the same time. Frequently, people want to use DFS to "automatically back up" user/group directories; however, this process is not as simple as putting two servers in a DFS link, enabling replication, and telling users to access it via the DFS link. For more, contact SCT2 or see\nDFS Step-by-Step Guide for\nWindows Server 2008\n.\nSet up DFS replication\nChoose two initial servers that will be in the replication group; you can change the servers later.\nCreate a folder on each server (for example,\nC:\\Test_Folder\n) that will be the initial folder to be replicated. Although not required, giving the folder the same name on each server is easier to manage.\nIf you haven't already done so, go to\nServer Manager\n, and add the\nFile Services\nrole. Enable the\nFile Server\nand\nDistributed File System\nrole services, and select the option to create a namespace later.\nAssign a name to your DFS namespace (typically your campus-dept code, for example,\nIU-ITPS\n).\nVerify that the firewall rules created in step 1 are still open and domain admins are administrators on your machine; your firewall needs to allow connections on all ports from the ADS domain controllers.\nEmail\nsct2@iu.edu\nwith the following:\nYour replication group name\nThe name of the AD group that will administer the replication group\nYou can add a new server to your replication group, or even add two new servers that will replicate data between each other, without affecting your initial two servers. When adding new servers, you will also need to create new\nConnections\nbetween the servers for replication to occur.\nReplicate folders with data\nGo to the\nDFS Management Console\nand add your replication group.\nRight-click your group, and select\nNew Replicated Folders\n.\nChoose the primary member (server). This will be the authoritative server for the initial transfer.\nAdd the folder on the primary server that is to be replicated.\nSelect the secondary server, select\nEdit\n, add the local path on that server, and then select\nFinish\n.\nNote:\nYou can also change NTFS permissions or make one partner "read only" from within the wizard.\nTo save time, you can pre-stage the existing data on the second server; see\nUse Robocopy to pre-seed files for DFS Replication\n.	KB0023530	KB0023530 v1.0	 and folder targets DFS replication Set up DFS replication Replicate folders with <strong>data</strong> Set up a DFS to your replication group, or even add two new servers that will replicate <strong>data</strong> between each other new Connections between the servers for replication to occur. Replicate folders with <strong>data</strong> Go	4.7093	km-aud-local-support, km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:35:24.341309	2026-01-31 17:35:24.341309	0	08dfe4f7476456583d373803846d43ee
12415	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d6669855476dfe902fc6a55b416d43da	Retire your IU website	Table of contents\nOverview\nOption 1 (preferred): UITS-managed website retirement\nOption 2: Manual website retirement (self-service)\nAdditional recommendations\nOverview\nImportant:\nSiteKube is replacing Sitehost. For more, see\nAbout SiteKube\n.\nWhen your Indiana University website is no longer needed, it is best practice to retire it from the web and archive any necessary content. This retirement process removes the site and associated services, and ensures compliance with IU policy.\nAs the Site Steward (the person responsible for the website), you can either ask UITS to handle the website retirement process for you or retire the site yourself.\nOption 1 (preferred): UITS-managed website retirement\nYou can ask UITS to retire your website and associated services on your behalf. This is the preferred method for retiring an IU website.\nYou may request a snapshot of your website to be stored on archive-it.org, however submitting a request does not guarantee your site will be archived.\nBefore submitting a retirement request, make sure to download and save any files or data you want to keep, as it is your responsibility to preserve this content locally.\nEmail the UITS Web team (\nwebhelp@iu.edu\n) with the following information:\nThe URL of the website you want to retire\nWhether the group account should also be retired\nWhether you need a redirect from the old URL\nWhether you want an archival copy of the site\nOption 2: Manual website retirement (self-service)\nTo retire your IU website on your own:\nBack up your website:\nSave a copy of your site files to an IU-provided file storage or another location recommended by your department's IT staff. If you want an official archive, complete the\nRequest to Archive Site\nform.\nNote:\nSubmitting an archival request does not guarantee that the website will be archived.\nRetire associated services:\nDepending on how your site is set up, complete some or all of the following:\nVirtual host:\nDelete or transfer the virtual host. To request a redirect (recommended for six months), email\nwebhelp@iu.edu\nwith the URL being retired and the destination URL for the redirect.\nWCMS:\nEmail\nwebhelp@iu.edu\nwith the WCMS site name and a request to archive the site.\nSiteimprove:\nGo to the\nSiteimprove Request Form\nand select\nOther\nto notify the Siteimprove team your site has been retired.\nFinalize website retirement:\nAfter completing all applicable tasks, email\nwebhelp@iu.edu\nto confirm that your website has been retired. The UITS Web team will finalize the process by confirming that all associated services are retired and marking the site as non-operational in the\nIU Website Directory\n.\nAdditional recommendations\nUse a\nbacklink checker\nto find other sites that link to yours, and then let them know your site will be taken offline.\nUse a 301 redirect to redirect content to a relevant site for at least six months.	KB0023410	KB0023410 v6.0	 or <strong>data</strong> you want to keep, as it is your responsibility to preserve this content locally. Email site is set up, complete some or all of the following: Virtual host: Delete or <strong>transfer</strong> the virtual	4.5082		2026-01-31 17:35:28.506949	2026-01-31 17:35:28.506949	0	d6669855476dfe902fc6a55b416d43da
12422	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=d4bf6477476456583d373803846d4347	Manage Salesforce CRM Event Campaigns	Table of contents\nClone an event\nMaster Event\nRecurring and Sub-Events\nClone from a Master Event\nManage an Event Campaign Member\nView or edit details for an individual Campaign Member\nClone an event\nCloning an event allows event managers to create copies of an Event Campaign to be used for other events so they won't have to re-create a new campaign from scratch each time. To do so:\nOpen an Event Campaign.\nSelect\nClone\n.\nUpdate all fields as appropriate for the new event (for example, Event Campaign name, time, date, location).\nSelect\nSave\n.\nMaster Event\nA Master Event is an umbrella event record used to organize a series of related events or Sub-Events.\nCreate a Master Event Campaign\nFollow the steps to create a new event; see\nSalesforce CRM Campaigns\n.\nSet "Type" to\nMaster\n.\nSelect\nSave\n.\nRecurring and Sub-Events\nA Recurring event is a series of the same type of event that repeats multiple times and is associated with a Master Event (for example, daily campus tours).\nSub-Events are components of a Master Event that are unique and have their own registration, such as individual sessions, which could occur on a single day or span multiple days.\nCreate a new Recurring or Sub-Event\nFollow the steps to create a new event; see\nSalesforce CRM Campaigns\n.\nIn the "Parent Campaign" field, select the lookup icon and enter or choose the parent (Master) campaign name from the drop-down menu.\nSet "Type" to\nRecurrence\nor\nSub-Event\n.\nUpdate any "Recurrence" or "Sub-Event" fields as appropriate.\nSelect\nSave\n.\nClone from a Master Event\nFollow the\nClone an event\nsteps above.\nIn the "Parent Campaign" field, select the lookup icon and enter or choose the parent (Master) campaign from the drop-down menu.\nSet "Type" to\nRecurrence\nor\nSub-Event\n.\nUpdate any "Recurrence" or "Sub-Event" fields as appropriate.\nSelect\nSave\n.\nCreate all Recurring or Sub-Events.\nClone the child event as many times as needed for the number of Recurring or Sub-Events.\nMake sure to update the appropriate fields on each cloned child event (for example, Event Campaign name, time, date, location).\nSelect\nSave\n.\nNote:\nMembers will not be cloned when you close a campaign. A Clone becomes a brand new empty campaign.\nManage an Event Campaign Member\nData on a campaign member can be updated or deleted at any time during a campaign.\nThe "Campaign Member Detail" page includes the following seven sections:\nCampaign Member Information\nCampaign\nContact\nLead\nSelf-Reported IUID\nStatus\nResponded\nWaitlist Position\nEvent Participant Detail\nUpdate details about the event as necessary:\nAttendee Type\nSecondary Attendee Title\nRegistration Date\nNumber of Guests\nSpecial Needs\nDietary Restrictions\nMeal Choice\nShirt Size\nEarly Arrival\nLodging Required\nLodging Selection\nIU Parking Pass Number\nIU Parking Garage Code\nPurpose of the Visit\nAdditional Attendee Detail\nChaperone Name\nEmergency Contact Name\nEmergency Contact Phone\nGroup Registration Name\nNumber of People\nCall Campaign Details\nAssigned User\nCall Date/Time\nCall Outcome\nCall Notes\nAdmissions (you can ignore this section if you are not in Admissions)\nStudent Type\nMainly used for reporting and tracking of event attendees (for example, High School Senior, Transfer Student, First Year Student)\nInterest Categories\nEvent attendee interests\nDegree\nAssociated Department\nAssociated University\nAcademic Program\nCustom fields\nCustom fields provide flexibility when you need to store information that does not have a preconfigured field in Salesforce. You can use up to 30 custom fields on a Campaign-by-Campaign basis.\nImported from form fill\nSome departments require event attendees to provide contact information to ensure the data in Salesforce CRM is up to date. Attendee-provided contact information is stored in these fields, which are part of the Campaign Member record. Having separate event fields that store contact information ensures that source system (SIS) populated contact information stored in the records is not overwritten.\nForm: Mobile\nForm: Phone\nForm: Department Involved\nForm: Program Code\nForm: Term Code\nForm: Email (will display email address entered on form)\nForm: Primary Street\nForm: Primary City\nForm: primary State/Province\nForm: Primary ZIP/Postal Code\nForm: Primary Country\nView or edit details for an individual Campaign Member\nOpen a campaign.\nSelect the\nMembers\ntab, and then search for the Member you want to edit.\nEither select the Member Name or the drop-down arrow next to the member you want to edit, and select\nEdit\n.\nMake any necessary changes to the sections.\nSelect\nSave\n.	KB0022846	KB0022846 v1.0	 Event Campaign Member <strong>Data</strong> on a campaign member can be updated or deleted at any time during for reporting and tracking of event attendees (for example, High School Senior, <strong>Transfer</strong> Student attendees to provide contact information to ensure the <strong>data</strong> in Salesforce CRM is up to date. Attendee	4.4976	km-aud-iu-all	2026-01-31 17:35:32.597681	2026-01-31 17:35:32.597681	0	d4bf6477476456583d373803846d4347
12424	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=26d666a94756fad02fc6a55b416d4343	Medium-level review of a VPAT, stage 2	Table of contents\nOverview\nCriteria tables\nAdditional resources\nOverview\nThis document will help you perform final checks and reviews of the Accessibility Compliance Report (ACR) portion of the VPAT for a small number of finalist products that remain under your consideration after performing\nstage 1 of the medium-level review\n. It contains the success criteria not addressed in stage 1.\nAll Level A and AA criteria are covered in\nPerform a full review of a VPAT\n. You may want to skip to a full review if you have only one or two products to compare and have not already performed a medium-level review.\nIf you are comparing many potential options and can perform a hands-on review of the products or services, you should use the\nquick review of the VPAT\nas an initial filter. You can then perform a\nmedium-level review of the VPAT\nwith software or applications that pass the quick review (If a hands-on review is not possible, skip straight to the medium-level review). The quick and medium-level reviews are intended to help you establish the reliability of each vendor's accessibility documentation and gain a general sense of the overall accessibility of their product.\nCriteria tables\nThe tables may represent information about three standards:\nWCAG\nRevised Section 508\nEN 301 549\nFor the purposes of this document, we are only looking at the WCAG standards and accompanying tables. The WCAG standard contains three conformance levels, A, AA, and AAA, which each have an individual table. There may be references to the other standards within these tables as there are matching criteria from all three standards.\nThe tables include three columns:\nCriteria\nConformance Level\nRemarks and Explanations\nCriteria\nwill list the specific standard targeted by the evaluation.\nConformance Level\ngives you the opportunity to gauge the possible accuracy of the submission. The conformance level column can have one of four different values:\nSupports\nPartially Supports\nDoes Not Support\nNot Applicable (N/A)\nNote:\nThere has been a language change with the release of VPAT 2.3 where "Supports with Exceptions" was replaced with "Partially Supports". This change took place in December of 2018. So, make note of the language and the date, which can tell you whether the vendor's submission is based on recent testing or may have been copied and pasted from an older version.\nRemarks and Explanations\nshould ideally be as detailed as possible about the specific problems encountered. Look for:\nSpecific examples of how the product supports, partially supports, or doesn't support the criterion\nRemarks and explanations for all criteria\nResponses that should concern you:\nExplanations that duplicate the wording of the criteria\nAll criteria marked as "Supports"\nLanguage like "this product was designed to...", which doesn't indicate if it actually does\nTable 1: Success Criteria, Level A\n2.1 - Keyboard Accessible\nThese guidelines should be considered the starting point in the evaluation process and the most critical within the standard.\nThis doesn't preclude an application that has "partially supports" from being considered. However, they should be flagged and then sent for more in-depth testing before purchase. View a\n1-minute video on testing keyboard support\n.\n2.1.4 - Character Key Shortcuts (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: If custom keyboard shortcuts have been implemented, they shouldn’t use a single character (letter, number, punctuation, or symbol) without modifier keys, such as Ctrl or Alt, or there should be a way for the user to change the shortcut.\nPriority: High\n2.2 - Enough Time\n2.2.1 - Timing Adjustable\nSummary: If there is a time limit of less than 20 hours, the user needs to be able to turn it off, adjust it, or extend it. For example, if a session times out after a period of inactivity, there should be a way for the user to indicate they are still active and stop the timeout. There is an exception for real-time events, like an auction.\nPriority: High\n2.4 - Navigable\n2.4.3 - Focus Order\nSummary: Someone using a keyboard should be able to navigate through the content in an order that is logical and meaningful.\nPriority: High\n2.4.4 - Link Purpose (In Context)\nSummary: The text of a link should give users an idea of where the link will take them.\nPriority: High\n2.5 - Input Modalities\nComplementary to the guidelines for keyboard users, these guidelines address barriers for people who use a pointer (mouse, touch) or their voice (voice control).\n2.5.1 - Pointer Gestures (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: This success criterion requires a single pointer alternative for any path-based or multipoint gestures. An example is zoom in and out buttons as an alternative to the multipoint pinch zoom gesture.\nPriority: High\n2.5.2 - Pointer Cancellation (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: This success criterion is about preventing accidental pointer input. If an action happens with a single pointer (mouse or touch), it can be cancelled or reversed.\nPriority: High\n2.5.3 - Label in Name (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: If there is visual text for a link or control, the accessible name contains that text.\nPriority: High\n2.5.4 - Motion Actuation (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: For functions activated by moving a device, such as shaking or tilting it, or by gesturing towards the device, there is another way to perform the action, for example, a button as an alternative to shaking the device to undo. It also requires a way to turn off these functions, such as by supporting system settings.\nPriority: High\n3.2 - Predictable\n3.2.6 - Consistent Help (2.2 only)\nSummary: If help is provided in the form of contact information, self-help, or a fully automated help mechanism, it is consistent across pages within a set. For example, a link to a help page is always in the same relative location.\nPriority: High\n3.3 - Input Assistance\n3.3.7 - Redundant Entry (2.2 only)\nSummary: This is about making it easy for users to complete processes. If the same information is needed again within the same session, the user shouldn't have to enter it again. There is an exception for security information, such as re-entering a new password.\nPriority: High\n4.1 -  Compatible\n4.1.1 - Parsing\nSummary: This requires a web-based application's markup language (generally HTML) to be appropriately structured. These can include properly opening and closing tags, appropriate specifications for nested list elements, and input fields with associated attributes. Essentially, this requirement assures the proper valid syntax (format) of the markup language.\nPriority: High\nTable 2: Success Criteria, Level AA\n1.2 - Time-based Media\n1.2.4 - Captions (Live)\nSummary: There must be\ncaptions\nprovided for any live video content. This is critical for a hearing-impaired user.\nPriority: High\n1.2.5 - Audio Description (Prerecorded)\nSummary: Video content is given additional\naudio descriptions\nduring pauses in the audio. Audio descriptions describe visual content for people who are blind or have low vision.\nPriority: High\n1.3 - Adaptable\n1.3.4 - Orientation (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: Devices can be used in any orientation. Content shouldn't be locked in landscape or portrait orientation.\nPriority: High\n1.3.5 - Identify Input Purpose (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: Autocomplete is used on certain form fields to identify what information goes in the field, such as a person's name or address.\nPriority: High\n1.4 - Distinguishable\n1.4.3 - Contrast (Minimum)\nSummary: Text has enough contrast so it can be seen. There are different standards for regular text and for large or bold text.\nPriority: High\n1.4.4 - Resize Text\nSummary: The user should be able to make the text larger, up to double the default size, without content disappearing or overlapping.\nPriority: High\n1.4.5 - Images of Text\nSummary: Real text should be used instead of images of text. This is related to the previous success criterion on resizing text because images of text become blurry when made larger.\nPriority: High\n1.4.10 - Reflow (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: Content doesn't require both horizontal and vertical scrolling. This is related to the previous success criterion on resizing text. As text is resized or the content zoomed, it should reflow, usually into a single column. There is an exception for content like a map or a table where the two-dimensional presentation is needed for usage and meaning.\nPriority: High\n1.4.11 - Non-text Contrast (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: Just as text should have enough contrast to be seen, user interface components and graphics should have enough contrast to be seen. User interface components are things like buttons and text input boxes. The graphics referred to here include icons without text and the parts of a complex graphic needed to understand the content, such as the lines on a graph.\nPriority: High\n1.4.12 - Text Spacing (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: The user can override the spacing of text to make it easier to read.\nPriority: High\n2.4 - Navigable\n2.4.5 - Multiple Ways\nSummary: This is about making sure there is more than one way to get to a page. It might be hard for some people to find a particular page in a large navigation list, for example, but they can find the page using a site search.\nPriority: High.\n2.4.6 - Headings and Labels\nSummary: This success criterion requires headings or labels to be descriptive. It does not require headings or labels, which are covered by other guidelines.\nPriority: High\n2.4.11 - Focus Not Obscured (Minimum) (2.2 only)\nSummary: Keyboard focus indicators should be at least partially visible. At least some portion of the focused item should be visible on screen and not hidden behind other content. This builds on the previous success criterion on visible focus.\nPriority: High\n2.5 - Input Modalities\nComplementary to the guidelines for keyboard users, these guidelines address barriers for people who use a pointer (mouse, touch) or their voice (voice control).\n2.5.7 - Dragging Movements (2.2 only)\nSummary: This requires an alternative in addition to dragging movements. For example, buttons move an item in a list up or down instead of having to press, hold, move and release with a mouse.\nPriority: High\n2.5.8 - Target Size (Minimum) (2.2 only)\nSummary: This is about making it easier for people to tap or click the correct item. It requires interactive items to be big enough or have enough space around them so that someone would not accidentally use the wrong one.\nPriority: High\n3.1 - Readable\n3.1.2 - Language of Parts\nSummary: If parts of a web page are in a different language than the main language, the language for those parts is programmatically defined for assistive technologies to access.\nPriority: High\n3.2 - Predictable\n3.2.3 - Consistent Navigation\nSummary: If there is navigation used throughout a website or application, the items should be in the same order on each page.\nPriority: High\n3.2.4 - Consistent Identification\nSummary: This is about making it easy for someone to understand the interface by making sure things are named consistently. For example, if a magnifying glass is named "search" in one location, it should be "search" in other locations, not "find".\nPriority: High\n3.3 - Input Assistance\n3.3.3 - Error Suggestion\nSummary: If there is an error, an error message should suggest how to correct the error.\nPriority: High\n3.3.4 - Error Prevention (Legal, Financial, Data)\nSummary: This is about helping people avoid serious consequences of making a mistake when an action involves legal or financial commitments or could cause the permanent loss of data.\nPriority: High\n3.3.8 - Accessible Authentication (Minimum) (2.2 only)\nSummary: This is about making it easier to authenticate or log in without needing to remember a password or solve a puzzle.\nPriority: High\n4.1 - Compatible\n4.1.3 - Status Messages (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: This success criterion is about making sure important changes are shared with assistive technology users without moving their focus.\nPriority: High\nAdditional resources\nTo learn more about reviewing a VPAT/ACR, see:\nA quick guide to reviewing a VPAT ACR\nby Karl Groves\nHow I Evaluate an ACR (VPAT)\nby Adrian Roselli\nMaking VPATs and ACRs more effective in procurement\nfrom WebAIM\nTo learn why a VPAT completed by a reputable third party is more trustworthy than one completed by the vendor, see\nWhy a third party should prepare your VPAT\nby Karl Groves.	KB0026632	KB0026632 v5.0	Table of contents Overview Criteria tables Additional resources Overview This document will help you perform final checks and reviews of the Accessibility Compliance Report (ACR) portion of the VPAT for a small number of finalist products that remain under your consideration after performing stage 1 of the medium-level review. It contains the su	6.2329		2026-01-31 17:35:36.477271	2026-01-31 17:35:36.477271	0	26d666a94756fad02fc6a55b416d4343
12428	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=8c8726294796fad02fc6a55b416d439d	Perform a full review of a VPAT	Table of contents\nOverview\nCompare a VPAT and an ACR\nOverview of an Accessibility Compliance Report (ACR)\nAdditional resources\nOverview\nThis document will help you perform a review of vendor provided accessibility documentation. It is most useful to perform a full review on a small number of finalist products.\nIf you are comparing many potential products, you should start with a\nquick review of the VPAT\nas an initial filter. You can perform a\nmedium-level review of the VPAT\nwith software or applications that pass the quick review. The quick review and medium-level reviews are intended to help you establish the reliability of each vendor's accessibility documentation and gain a general sense of the overall accessibility of their product.\nCompare a VPAT and an ACR\nBefore starting, you should understand the difference between a VPAT (Voluntary Product Accessibility Template) and the accompanying ACR (Accessibility Compliance Report).\nVPAT\nThe VPAT is the entire template with all the pages of instructions and resources to help gain a comprehensive understanding of the document.\nACR\nThe ACR is the report portion of the VPAT after the instructions and resources are removed. The ACR contains only the introduction form and the details of the criteria tables.\nOverview of an Accessibility Compliance Report (ACR)\nThe vendor may send an entire VPAT or just the ACR portion of it. The ACR contains the important parts to review. You need to examine the introduction form and the criteria tables.\nIntroduction form\nThe introduction form contains general information about the product and the evaluation.\nPay attention to the version of the VPAT.  Version 2.4rev or newer indicates the vendor follows more current standards. Version 2.5rev is the current version, released in April 2025.\nVerify the product and version. Make sure the VPAT is for the version you are interested in purchasing, especially if there are multiple versions of the product. There may also be multiple reports for a single product. If the product is complex, separate reports may exist, for example, for different interfaces (user versus administrator).\nFour specific fields in this introduction help establish the reliability of the form as a whole:\nEvaluation methods used:\nThe evaluation methods are perhaps the most critical aspect to the introduction. Look for:\nAssistive technology used during the testing process\nMultiple methods, including both manual and automated testing\nThird party evaluators that can be vetted to certify their value\nSpecific methodology references\nAnswers that should concern you:\nSimilar to another evaluated product\nUse of a vendor-proprietary testing method, unless the method can be vetted appropriately\nAutomated testing with no manual testing\nGeneral product knowledge\nOther similar responses\nApplicable standards and guidelines:\nWhat standards were used to create this report? This field requires at least one standard. However, it is better if multiple standards were used and listed. Look for:\nWCAG 2.1 or 2.2, A and AA conformance\nWCAG 2.0, A and AA conformance, is older and does not cover as much as WCAG 2.1 or 2.2\nAdditional standards that show more detailed knowledge and commitment by the vendor:\nRevised Section 508\nEN 301 549\nNote:\nWCAG 2.1, released in December 2018, is the standard required by the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) Title II. WCAG 2.2, released in October 2023, indicates the vendor follows more current standards. The level of conformance is also important, which exists as Level A, AA, and AAA. This document focuses on level AA, which is a common standard and should be the minimum goal.\nContact information:\nThe name of the tester, their position within the organization, and specific contact information should be provided.\nThe tester's position is usually more reliable if they are:\nOn the vendor's accessibility team\nFrom a third-party accessibility firm\nBe more cautious if the tester's position is from the vendor's:\nMarketing team\nSales team\nMarketing and sales people may have little knowledge of the product and thus any details of any testing that may have been done. They are also likely to have a biased purpose.\nDate:\nThis field should contain the date the VPAT was issued. Look for a date within the last six to nine months.\nBe cautious of anything over one year. You may need to request an updated report.\nNote:\nThis is highly dependent on the type of application it is and how frequently the vendor releases regular updates. Therefore, this is a general guideline, not a gold standard.\nCriteria tables\nThe tables may represent information about three standards:\nWCAG\nRevised Section 508\nEN 301 549\nFor the purposes of this document, we are only looking at the WCAG standards and accompanying tables. The WCAG standard contains three conformance levels, A, AA, and AAA, which each have an individual table. There may be references to the other standards within these tables as there are matching criteria from all three standards.\nThe tables include three columns:\nCriteria\nConformance Level\nRemarks and Explanations\nCriteria\nwill list the specific standard targeted by the evaluation.\nConformance Level\ngives you the opportunity to gauge the possible accuracy of the submission. The conformance level column can have one of four different values:\nSupports\nPartially Supports\nDoes Not Support\nNot Applicable (N/A)\nNote:\nThere has been a language change with the release of VPAT 2.3 where "Supports with Exceptions" was replaced with "Partially Supports". This change took place in December of 2018. So, make note of the language and the date, which can tell you whether the vendor's submission is based on recent testing or may have been copied and pasted from an older version.\nRemarks and Explanations\nshould ideally be as detailed as possible about the specific problems encountered. Look for:\nSpecific examples of how the product supports, partially supports, or doesn't support the criterion\nRemarks and explanations for all criteria\nResponses that should concern you:\nExplanations that duplicate the wording of the criteria\nAll criteria marked as "Supports"\nLanguage like "this product was designed to...", which doesn't indicate if it actually does\nTable 1: Success Criteria, Level A\n1.1 - Text Alternatives\n1.1.1 - Non-Text Content\nSummary: This is mostly about\nalternative text for images\n, charts, tables, and so on. Most products should support this. The use of CAPTCHA elevates this success criterion to critical. CAPTCHAs are an element of controversy in the accessibility community because of their potential barrier to access. If CAPTCHAs are included within a vendor's submission, there must be more than one option (for example, visual and auditory) as a minimum.\nPriority: Critical\n1.2 - Time-based Media\n1.2.1 - Audio Only & Video Only (Pre-recorded)\nSummary: There must be text alternatives to pre-recorded audio or video content, usually in the form of\ntext-based transcripts\n. This is critical for accessibility as these text transcripts can then easily be translated into different modes for different user needs.\nPriority: Critical\n1.2.2 - Captions (Pre-recorded)\nSummary: There must be\ncaptions\nprovided for any pre-recorded audio/video content. This is critical for people who are D/deaf or hard of hearing. "Does not support" or "Partially supports" indicate the product should be flagged for further testing. View a\n1-minute video overview about captions\n.\nPriority: Critical\n1.2.3 - Audio Description or Media Alternative (Pre-recorded)\nSummary: Video content is either given additional\naudio descriptions\nwithin the confines of the audio track or in an entirely separate audio track that contains the entirety of the video content described in audio format. Alternatively, there is a full text version of all visual and audio content. Exceptions here could be expected as full conformance seems a rarity in this area. However, it should still be viewed as a critical component.\nPriority: Critical\n1.3 - Adaptable\n1.3.1, 1.3.2, 1.3.3 - Info and Relationships, Meaningful Sequence, Sensory Characteristics\nSummary: These three success criteria are related to meaning and how the structures of applications can be read, and thus interpreted, with assistive technology. For this reason, they should be viewed more as a whole than as individual sub-sections. This area is as critical to accessibility as any on this list. However, because these are design guidelines, it is also the one with the largest room for interpretation. The good news is most modern web-based applications generally conform to these concepts during their initial design phase. Answers here should generally list "Supports". Possible exceptions should be very detailed if they exist.\nPriority: Critical\n1.4 - Distinguishable\n1.4.1 - Use of Color\nSummary: This deals with the\nuse of color\nwithin an application.\nIf color is used in a defining way (for example, click the red button), there must be alternative ways to identify that element or color identification.\nThis success criterion is critical as it has the potential for significant barriers to access.\nPriority: Critical\n1.4.2 - Audio Control\nSummary: If audio content is to be automatically played, there must be a way for the content to be stopped or paused, and the volume must be operated independently from the system volume.\nPriority: Critical\n2.1 - Keyboard Accessible\nThese guidelines should be considered the starting point in the evaluation process and the most critical within the standard.\nThis doesn't preclude an application that has "partially supports" from being considered. However, they should be flagged and then sent for more in-depth testing before purchase. View a\n1-minute video on testing keyboard support\n.\n2.1.1 - Keyboard\nSummary: This success criterion deals solely with the ability to\nnavigate the application with keyboard controls\n, which is crucial to the use of assistive technologies.\nPriority: Critical\n2.1.2 - No Keyboard Trap\nSummary: This success criterion deals with the process of navigating to a specific element and then becoming "trapped" on the element and not being able to navigate away from it.\nPriority: Critical\n2.1.4 - Character Key Shortcuts (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: If custom keyboard shortcuts have been implemented, they shouldn’t use a single character (letter, number, punctuation, or symbol) without modifier keys, such as Ctrl or Alt, or there should be a way for the user to change the shortcut.\nPriority: High\n2.2 - Enough Time\n2.2.1 - Timing Adjustable\nSummary: If there is a time limit of less than 20 hours, the user needs to be able to turn it off, adjust it, or extend it. For example, if a session times out after a period of inactivity, there should be a way for the user to indicate they are still active and stop the timeout. There is an exception for real-time events, like an auction.\nPriority: High\n2.2.2 - Pause, Stop, Hide\nSummary: The user needs to be able to pause, stop, or hide any content that moves, blinks, or scrolls automatically. This applies if the movement, blinking, or scrolling lasts longer than 5 seconds and there is other content on the screen at the same time.\nPriority: Critical\n2.3 - Seizures and Physical Barriers\n2.3.1 - Three Flashes or Below Threshold\nSummary: This success criterion restricts the flashing of content to no more than three flashes per second within an application. Considering its impact, its critical importance is clear.\nPriority: Critical\n2.4 - Navigable\n2.4.1 - Bypass Blocks\nSummary: This success criterion deals with "skip to" links at the top of web pages which allow quicker navigation to those critical areas. View a\nshort video on checking skip links\n.\nThis is a very basic function that is included in most web-based applications today. If unsupported, this should be a red flag.\nPriority: Critical\n2.4.2 - Page Titled\nSummary: This success criterion simply requires an appropriate and descriptive title for each web page. View a\nshort video on checking page titles\n.\nThis is a very basic function that is included in most web-based applications today. If unsupported, this should be a red flag.\nPriority: Critical\n2.4.3 - Focus Order\nSummary: Someone using a keyboard should be able to navigate through the content in an order that is logical and meaningful.\nPriority: High\n2.4.4 - Link Purpose (In Context)\nSummary: The text of a link should give users an idea of where the link will take them.\nPriority: High\n2.5 - Input Modalities\nComplementary to the guidelines for keyboard users, these guidelines address barriers for people who use a pointer (mouse, touch) or their voice (voice control).\n2.5.1 - Pointer Gestures (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: This success criterion requires a single pointer alternative for any path-based or multipoint gestures. An example is zoom in and out buttons as an alternative to the multipoint pinch zoom gesture.\nPriority: High\n2.5.2 - Pointer Cancellation (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: This success criterion is about preventing accidental pointer input. If an action happens with a single pointer (mouse or touch), it can be cancelled or reversed.\nPriority: High\n2.5.3 - Label in Name (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: If there is visual text for a link or control, the accessible name contains that text.\nPriority: High\n2.5.4 - Motion Actuation (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: For functions activated by moving a device, such as shaking or tilting it, or by gesturing towards the device, there is another way to perform the action, for example, a button as an alternative to shaking the device to undo. It also requires a way to turn off these functions, such as by supporting system settings.\nPriority: High\n3.1 - Readable\n3.1.1 - Language of Page\nSummary: This ensures that a web page's language is programmatically defined for assistive technologies to access. Exceptions should be rare.\nPriority: Critical\n3.2 - Predictable\n3.2.1 - On Focus\nSummary: This success criterion essentially prohibits the context change of a web element when it receives system focus through standard navigation. An example could be the automatic submission of a web form when the button receives focus, or the automatic launch of a new browser tab when a link gets focus.\nPriority: Critical\n3.2.2 - On Input\nSummary: This success criterion addresses changes in context when a user changes the state of a component, and the change will persist after interaction stops. An example of a state change would be checking a checkbox or entering text in a text box. These are elements where a user must be alerted to a context change to validate that element's status.\nPriority: Critical\n3.2.6 - Consistent Help (2.2 only)\nSummary: If help is provided in the form of contact information, self-help, or a fully automated help mechanism, it is consistent across pages within a set. For example, a link to a help page is always in the same relative location.\nPriority: High\n3.3 - Input Assistance\n3.3.1 - Error Identification\nSummary: This ensures that input entry\nerrors are identified in a clear and descriptive manner\nto all users. While slightly ambiguous, the intent here is that any error message must be immediately identifiable and accessible to any user using any type of assistive technology. The error should also be described in a way that the user can figure out what is wrong.\nPriority: Critical\n3.3.2 - Labels or Instructions\nSummary: There must be proper labeling and instructions for any application input fields. Examples include search fields or a web form. This is a barrier to access, so exceptions should be viewed cautiously.\nPriority: Critical\n3.3.7 - Redundant Entry (2.2 only)\nSummary: This is about making it easy for users to complete processes. If the same information is needed again within the same session, the user shouldn't have to enter it again. There is an exception for security information, such as re-entering a new password.\nPriority: High\n4.1 - Compatible\n4.1.1 - Parsing\nSummary: This requires a web-based application's markup language (generally HTML) to be appropriately structured. This success criterion does not apply in WCAG 2.2, where it is obsolete and removed. It is always supported in WCAG 2.0 and 2.1, per the September 2023 errata.\nPriority: High\n4.1.2 - Name, Role, Value\nSummary: This success criterion targets custom user interface elements created with scripting components instead of the standard markup language elements. It requires that these custom components' name, role, attributes, and any change of status be identifiable by assistive technologies. An example would be a custom checkbox being identified as "checkbox checked" after it has been marked by a user.\nPriority: Critical\nTable 2: Success Criteria, Level AA\n1.2 - Time-based Media\n1.2.4 - Captions (Live)\nSummary: There must be\ncaptions\nprovided for any live video content. This is critical for a hearing-impaired user.\nPriority: High\n1.2.5 - Audio Description (Prerecorded)\nSummary: Video content is given additional\naudio descriptions\nduring pauses in the audio. Audio descriptions describe visual content for people who are blind or have low vision.\nPriority: High\n1.3 - Adaptable\n1.3.4 - Orientation (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: Devices can be used in any orientation. Content shouldn't be locked in landscape or portrait orientation.\nPriority: High\n1.3.5 - Identify Input Purpose (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: Autocomplete is used on certain form fields to identify what information goes in the field, such as a person's name or address.\nPriority: High\n1.4 - Distinguishable\n1.4.3 - Contrast (Minimum)\nSummary: Text has enough contrast so it can be seen. There are different standards for regular text and for large or bold text.\nPriority: High\n1.4.4 - Resize Text\nSummary: The user should be able to make the text larger, up to double the default size, without content disappearing or overlapping.\nPriority: High\n1.4.5 - Images of Text\nSummary: Real text should be used instead of images of text. This is related to the previous success criterion on resizing text because images of text become blurry when made larger.\nPriority: High\n1.4.10 - Reflow (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: Content doesn't require both horizontal and vertical scrolling. This is related to the previous success criterion on resizing text. As text is resized or the content zoomed, it should reflow, usually into a single column. There is an exception for content like a map or a table where the two-dimensional presentation is needed for usage and meaning.\nPriority: High\n1.4.11 - Non-text Contrast (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: Just as text should have enough contrast to be seen, user interface components and graphics should have enough contrast to be seen. User interface components are things like buttons and text input boxes. The graphics referred to here include icons without text and the parts of a complex graphic needed to understand the content, such as the lines on a graph.\nPriority: High\n1.4.12 - Text Spacing (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: The user can override the spacing of text to make it easier to read.\nPriority: High\n1.4.13 - Content on Hover or Focus (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: This deals with hovering over or focusing on an item to activate extra content. Hover action elements are notorious for accessibility problems.\nPriority: Critical\n2.4 - Navigable\n2.4.5 - Multiple Ways\nSummary: This is about making sure there is more than one way to get to a page. It might be hard for some people to find a particular page in a large navigation list, for example, but they can find the page using a site search.\nPriority: High.\n2.4.6 - Headings and Labels\nSummary: This success criterion requires headings or labels to be descriptive. It does not require headings or labels, which are covered by other guidelines.\nPriority: High\n2.4.7 - Focus Visible\nSummary: There must be an indicator for keyboard focus within a web-based application. This is critical for multiple reasons. This focus indicator is the primary way testers evaluate the keyboard access and tabbing order of an application. So, this can not only act as evidence of that testing process, it will also be critical for future testing of the application during update cycles.\nPriority: Critical\n2.4.11 - Focus Not Obscured (Minimum) (2.2 only)\nSummary: Keyboard focus indicators should be at least partially visible. At least some portion of the focused item should be visible on screen and not hidden behind other content. This builds on the previous success criterion on visible focus.\nPriority: High\n2.5 - Input Modalities\n2.5.7 - Dragging Movements (2.2 only)\nSummary: This requires an alternative in addition to dragging movements. For example, buttons move an item in a list up or down instead of having to press, hold, move and release with a mouse.\nPriority: High\n2.5.8 - Target Size (Minimum) (2.2 only)\nSummary: This is about making it easier for people to tap or click the correct item. It requires interactive items to be big enough or have enough space around them that someone won't accidentally use the wrong one.\nPriority: High\n3.1 - Readable\n3.1.2 - Language of Parts\nSummary: If parts of a web page are in a different language than the main language, the language for those parts is programmatically defined for assistive technologies to access.\nPriority: High\n3.2 - Predictable\n3.2.3 - Consistent Navigation\nSummary: If there is navigation used throughout a website or application, the items should be in the same order on each page.\nPriority: High\n3.2.4 - Consistent Identification\nSummary: This is about making it easy for someone to understand the interface by making sure things are named consistently. For example, if a magnifying glass is named "search" in one location, it should be "search" in other locations, not "find".\nPriority: High\n3.3 - Input Assistance\n3.3.3 - Error Suggestion\nSummary: If there is an error, an error message should suggest how to correct the error.\nPriority: High\n3.3.4 - Error Prevention (Legal, Financial, Data)\nSummary: This is about helping people avoid serious consequences of making a mistake when an action involves legal or financial commitments or could cause the permanent loss of data.\nPriority: High\n3.3.8 - Accessible Authentication (Minimum) (2.2 only)\nSummary: This is about making it easier to authenticate or log in without needing to remember a password or solve a puzzle.\nPriority: High\n4.1 - Compatible\n4.1.3 - Status Messages (2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: This success criterion is about making sure important changes are shared with assistive technology users without moving their focus.\nPriority: High\nAdditional resources\nTo learn more about reviewing a VPAT/ACR, see:\nA quick guide to reviewing a VPAT ACR\nby Karl Groves\nHow I Evaluate an ACR (VPAT)\nby Adrian Roselli\nMaking VPATs and ACRs more effective in procurement\nfrom WebAIM\nTo learn why a VPAT completed by a reputable third party is more trustworthy than one completed by the vendor, see\nWhy a third party should prepare your VPAT\nby Karl Groves.	KB0026633	KB0026633 v7.0	 a vendor's <strong>submission</strong>, there must be more than one option (for example, visual and auditory) as a minimum example could be the automatic <strong>submission</strong> of a web form when the button receives focus	6.2329		2026-01-31 17:35:36.477271	2026-01-31 17:35:41.544516	0	8c8726294796fad02fc6a55b416d439d
2417	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0022631	Install Python packages on the research supercomputers at IU	Table of contents\nOverview\nInstall Python packages for personal use\nSet up your user environment\nInstall a package using\npip\nInstall a package using its\nsetup.py\nscript\nUnderstand the module search order\nSupported packages\nSupported packages in the Python modules\nSupported packages in the\npython/gpu\nmodules\nOverview\nPython packages are collections of modules (reusable code) that extend and enhance the functionality of the core Python language. Python developers contribute to the official Python Package Index (\nPyPI\n) repository, making their packages available to the Python community under open source license terms. The Python Packaging Authority (\nPyPA\n) manages the repository, and maintains a standard set of tools for building, distributing, and installing Python packages.\nOn Indiana University's\nresearch supercomputers\n, many third-party packages already are installed to supplement commonly used Python builds. For the most commonly used packages that are supported at IU, see the\nSupported packages\nsection; for a complete list, type\npip freeze\nafter loading the Python module to see what packages are installed. If you have a unique need for a third-party Python package that is not already installed, you can use\npip\nor\nsetup.py\nto install the package in your home directory or in your\nSlate\nstorage space.\nNote:\nSpace on\nSlate\nis available to all IU\nresearch supercomputer\nusers. To create a\nSlate\naccount, follow the instructions in\nGet additional IU computing accounts\n.\nIf you know several researchers are interested in using a Python package that is not already installed, you can request to have it installed as a system-wide site package.\nInstall Python packages for personal use\nSet up your user environment\nThe IU\nresearch supercomputers\nuse module-based environment management systems that provide a convenient method for dynamically customizing your software environment.\nTo install Python packages, you must have Python added to your user environment. To check which modules are currently loaded; on the command line, enter:\nmodule list\nIf Python is not among the list of currently loaded modules, use the\nmodule load\ncommand to add it; for example:\nTo add the default version, on the command line, enter:\nmodule load python\nTo add a non-default version:\nCheck which versions are available; on the command line, enter:\nmodule avail python\nLoad the preferred version; on the command line, enter (replace\nversion_number\nwith the preferred version number):\nmodule load python/\nversion_number\nIf Python is listed among the currently loaded modules, but you prefer or need to use another version, you must remove the currently loaded module before loading the other version. To do this with one command, use\nmodule switch\n; for example, on the command line, enter (replace\ncurrent_version\nwith the version number of the currently loaded\npython\nmodule and\nnew_version\nwith the preferred version number):\nmodule switch python/\ncurrent_version\npython/\nnew_version\nYou can save your customized user environment so that it loads every time you start a new session; for instructions, see\nUse modules to manage your software environment on IU research supercomputers\n.\nInstall a package using\npip\nThe\npip\npackage management tool, one of the standard tools maintained by the Python Package Authority (PyPA), is the recommended tool for installing packages from the Python Package Index (PyPI) repository.\nTo install a package from the PyPI repository (for example,\nfoo\n), use the\npip install\ncommand with the\n--user\nflag; for example:\nPython package installation commands\nTo install:\nUse the command:\nThe latest version\npip install foo --user\nA particular version (for example,\nfoo 1.0.3\n)\npip install foo==1.0.3 --user\nA minimum version (for example,\nfoo 2.0\n)\npip install 'foo>=2.0' --user\nThe\n--user\noption directs\npip\nto download and unpack the source distribution for your package (for example,\nfoo\n) in the user\nsite-packages\ndirectory for the running Python (for example,\n~/.local/lib/python3.6/site-packages/foo\n). Python automatically searches this directory for modules, so prepending this path to the PYTHONPATH environmental variable is not necessary. If you omit the\n--user\noption,\npip\nwill try to install your package in the global\nsite-packages\ndirectory (where you do not have the necessary permissions); as a result, the installation will fail.\nAlternatively, you can use the\n--prefix\noption to install your package in your\nSlate\nstorage space; for example, to install your package (\npackage_name\n) in an existing subdirectory (\npython-pkgs\n) in your\nSlate\nspace, enter:\npip install --prefix=/N/slate/$USER/python-pkgs package_name\nIf you install your package to a location other than the user\nsite-packages\ndirectory, you will need to prepend the path to that directory to your PYTHONPATH environment variable; for example:\nexport PYTHONPATH=$PYTHONPATH:/N/slate/$USER/python-pkgs/lib/python3.x/site-packages\nNote:\nIn the above example,\n3.x\nrepresents the first two elements of the Python version number you are using. For example, if your Python version is 3.10.5, replace\n3.x\nwith\n3.10\n.\nThis line can be added to your\n.bashrc\nfile to avoid having to add it every time you log in.\nFor more about using\npip\n, see the\npip install\npage in the\npip User Guide\n.\nInstall a package using its\nsetup.py\nscript\nTo install a Python package from a source other than the PyPI repository, you can download and unpack the source distribution yourself, and then use its\nsetup.py\nscript to install the package in the user\nsite-packages\ndirectory:\nSet up your user environment (as described in the\nprevious section\n).\nUse the\nwget\ncommand to download the distribution archive (for example,\nfoo-1.0.3.gz\n) from the source (for example, http://pythonfoo.org); for example:\nwget http://pythonfoo.org/foo-1.0.3.gz\nUse\ntar\nto unpack the archive (for example,\nfoo-1.0.3.gz\n); for example:\ntar -xzf foo-1.0.3.gz\nThe distribution should unpack into a similarly-named directory in your home directory (for example,\n~/foo-1.0.3\n).\nChange (\ncd\n) to the new directory, and then, on the command line, enter:\npython setup.py install --user\nThe\n--user\noption directs\nsetup.py\nto install the package (for example,\nfoo\n) in the user\nsite-packages\ndirectory for the running Python (for example,\n~/.local/lib/pythonX.Y/site-packages/foo\n).\nPython automatically searches this directory for modules, so prepending this path to the PYTHONPATH environmental variable is not necessary.\nIf you omit the\n--user\noption,\nsetup.py\nwill try to install the package in the global\nsite-packages\ndirectory (where you do not have the necessary permissions); as a result, the installation will fail.\nAlternatively, you can use the\n--home\nor\n--prefix\noption to install your package in a different location (where you have the necessary permissions); for example, to install your package in a subdirectory (for example,\npython-pkgs\n):\nWithin your home directory, enter:\npython setup.py install --home=~/python-pkgs\nIn your\nSlate\nstorage space, enter:\npython setup.py install --prefix=/N/slate/$USER/python-pkgs\nNote:\nIf you install your package to a location other than the user\nsite-packages\ndirectory, you will need to prepend the path to that directory to your PYTHONPATH environment variable. For more about PYTHONPATH, see\nUnderstand the module search order\nbelow.\nUnderstand the module search order\nKnowing how the Python interpreter responds to\nimport\nstatements can help you determine why a particular module or package isn't loading, or why an unexpected version of a package is loading, even though the correct version is installed and the path to its location is listed in your PYTHONPATH environment variable.\nWhen Python launches, it searches the paths found in\nsys.path\n, a list of directories that determines the interpreter's search path for modules. The\nsys.path\nvariable is initialized from the following locations, in this order:\nThe directory containing the script used to invoke the Python interpreter (if the interpreter is invoked interactively or the script is read from standard input, this first item,\npath[0]\n, remains an empty string, which directs Python to search modules in the current working directory first)\nThe directories listed in PYTHONPATH\nThe version-specific\nsite-packages\ndirectory for the running Python installation; for example\n<sys.prefix>/lib/pythonX.Y/site-packages\n, in which\n<sys.prefix>\nrepresents the path to the running Python installation and\nX.Y\nrepresents the version number of the running Python installation\nBy default, Python also imports the\nsite.py\nmodule upon initialization, which adds site-specific paths to the module search path (\nsys.path\n), including the path to your user\nsite-packages\ndirectory within in your home directory (for example,\n~/.local/lib/pythonX.Y/site-packages\n).\nAs\nsite.py\nadds paths to\nsys.path\n, it scans them for path configuration (\n.pth\n) files, which contain additional directories that are added to\nsys.path\n. If a directory contains multiple\n.pth\nfiles,\nsite.py\nprocesses them in alphabetical order.\nHowever, some\n.pth\nfiles contain embedded commands that insert directory entries at the beginning of the module search path (ahead of the standard library path). As a result, a module from one of the inserted directories will load instead of the module of the same name from the standard library directory. This can be undesired and confusing behavior unless such a replacement is intended.\nNote:\nIf your\nimport\nrequests are consistently disrupted by\nsite.py\nand\n.pth\nfiles, try invoking the Python interpreter with the\n-S\n(uppercase "S"):\npython -S\nThis disables the automatic import of\nsite.py\nand, as a result, prevents it from manipulating\nsys.path\n. However, it also prevents\nsite.py\nfrom adding your user\nsite-packages\ndirectory to\nsys.path\n. To import\nsite.py\nwithout adding your user\nsite-packages\ndirectory to\nsys.path\n, invoke Python with the\n-s\n(lowercase "s") option:\npython -s\nTo see which directories Python scans when you issue\nimport\ncommands, on the command line, enter:\npython -c "import sys; print ('\\n'.join(sys.path))"\nAlternatively, launch Python in interactive mode, and then invoke the same commands in this order (\n>>>\nis the Python primary prompt):\n>>>import sys\n>>>print ('\\n'.join(sys.path))\nNote:\nThe\nsys.path\nvariable is only an editable list of strings that you can edit like any other Python list. Avoid editing the first item in the list (\npath[0]\n), because many packages assume it refers to the directory containing the script used to invoke the Python interpreter.\nSupported packages\nSupported packages in the Python modules\nFollowing are the most commonly used packages available in the Python modules. To see a full list of available packages, after loading a Python module, type:\npip list\nor\npip freeze\nastropy\nbiopython\ncryptography\ncutadapt\ncycler\nCython\ndask\ndeeptools\nh5py\nidna\nigraph\njupyterlab\nkiwisolver\nMACS2\nmatplotlib\nmpi4py\nnose\nnotebook\nnumba\nnumpy\npandas\nPillow\nplotly\npycosat\npysam\nPySocks\npytz\nscikit-learn\nscipy\nseaborn\ntensorflow\ntornado\nSupported packages in the\npython/gpu\nmodules\nFollowing are the most commonly used packages available in the Python modules. To see a full list of available packages, after loading a\npython/gpu\nmodule, type:\npip list\nor\npip freeze\naccelerate\nbs4\ncausality\ncupy-cuda117\ndask\neditdistance\nGraphViz\nguppy3\nh5py\nimbalanced_learn\nimutils\njax\nJinja2\nkeras\nlightgbm\nMarkov\nmatplotlib\nmxnet-cu112\n*\nnibabel\nnltk\nnumba\nnumpy\npandas\nPillow\nsasa\nscikit-learn\nsds\nseaborn\ntensorflow\n*\ntorch\n*\nvaderSentiment\nWerkzeug\n* Specifically GPU-capable packages	KB0022631	KB0022631 v4.0	 <strong>Slate</strong> storage space. Note: If you know several researchers are interested in using a Python package package in your <strong>Slate</strong> storage space; for example, to install your package (package_name) in an existing subdirectory (python-pkgs) in your <strong>Slate</strong> space, enter: pip install --prefix=/N/<strong>slate</strong>/$USER	71.9108		2026-01-31 14:35:55.622052	2026-01-31 15:54:14.10291	0	7611db5a3b4f26d8476d941864e45ada
12426	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=f335e66547d2fad02fc6a55b416d43bc	Medium-level review of a VPAT, stage 1	Table of contents\nOverview\nCompare a VPAT and an ACR\nVPAT\nACR\nOverview of an Accessibility Compliance Report (ACR)\nIntroduction form\nCriteria tables\nTable 1: Success Criteria, Level A\nTable 2: Success Criteria, Level AA\nAdditional resources\nOverview\nThis document will help you perform a rapid review of vendor provided accessibility documentation, to see if a deeper review is necessary or desired. This is not a thorough review. It is intended to:\nEstablish the reliability of the vendor's accessibility documentation\nGive a general sense of the overall accessibility of the product\nEstimated time to perform task: Under 10 minutes.\nAfter you have completed this initial review, you can finish reviewing the VPATs for a few finalist products with\nstage 2\n. All Level A and AA criteria are covered in\nPerform a full review of a VPAT\n. You may want to skip to a full review if you have only one or two products to compare and have not already performed a medium-level review.\nCompare a VPAT and an ACR\nBefore starting, you should understand the difference between a VPAT (Voluntary Product Accessibility Template) and the accompanying ACR (Accessibility Compliance Report).\nVPAT\nThe VPAT is the entire template with all the pages of instructions and resources to help gain a comprehensive understanding of the document.\nACR\nThe ACR is the report portion of the VPAT after the instructions and resources are removed. The ACR contains only the introduction form and the details of the criteria tables.\nOverview of an Accessibility Compliance Report (ACR)\nThe vendor may send an entire VPAT or just the ACR portion of it. The ACR contains the important parts to review. At minimum, you need to examine the introduction form and the criteria tables.\nIntroduction form\nThe introduction form contains general information about the product and the evaluation. Verify the product and version. Four specific fields in this introduction help establish the reliability of the form as a whole.\nEvaluation methods used:\nThe evaluation methods are perhaps the most critical aspect to the introduction. Look for:\nAssistive technology used during the testing process\nMultiple methods, including both manual and automated testing\nThird party evaluators that can be vetted to certify their value\nSpecific methodology references\nAnswers that should concern you:\nSimilar to another evaluated product\nUse of a vendor-proprietary testing method, unless the method can be vetted appropriately\nAutomated testing with no manual testing\nGeneral product knowledge\nOther similar responses\nApplicable standards and guidelines:\nWhat standards were used to create this report? This field requires at least one standard. However, it is better if multiple standards were used and listed. Look for:\nWCAG 2.0, 2.1, or 2.2, A and AA conformance\nAdditional standards that show more detailed knowledge and commitment by the vendor:\nRevised Section 508\nEN 301 549\nNote:\nWCAG 2.1, released in December 2018, or WCAG 2.2, released in October 2023, indicates the vendor follows more current standards. The level of conformance is also important, which exists as Level A, AA, and AAA. This document focuses on level AA, which is a common standard and should be the minimum goal.\nContact information:\nThe name of the tester, their position within the organization, and specific contact information should be provided.\nThe tester's position is usually more reliable if they are:\nOn the vendor's accessibility team\nFrom a third-party accessibility firm\nBe more cautious if the tester's position is from the vendor's:\nMarketing team\nSales team\nMarketing and sales people may have little knowledge of the product and thus any details of any testing that may have been done. They are also likely to have a biased purpose.\nDate:\nThis field should contain the date the VPAT was issued. Look for a date within the last six to nine months.\nBe cautious of anything over one year. You may need to request an updated report.\nNote:\nThis is highly dependent on the type of application it is and how frequently the vendor releases regular updates. Therefore, this is a general guideline, not a gold standard.\nCriteria tables\nThe tables may represent information about three standards:\nWCAG\nRevised Section 508\nEN 301 549\nFor the purposes of this document, we are only looking at the WCAG standards and accompanying tables. The WCAG standard contains three conformance levels, A, AA, and AAA, which each have an individual table. There may be references to the other standards within these tables as there are matching criteria from all three standards.\nThe tables include three columns:\nCriteria\nConformance Level\nRemarks and Explanations\nCriteria\nwill list the specific standard targeted by the evaluation.\nConformance Level\ngives you the opportunity to gauge the possible accuracy of the submission. The conformance level column can have one of four different values:\nSupports\nPartially Supports\nDoes Not Support\nNot Applicable (N/A)\nNote:\nThere has been a language change with the release of VPAT 2.3 where "Supports with Exceptions" was replaced with "Partially Supports". This change took place in December of 2018. So, make note of the language and the date, which can tell you whether the vendor's submission is based on recent testing or may have been copied and pasted from an older version.\nRemarks and Explanations\nshould ideally be as detailed as possible about the specific problems encountered. Look for:\nSpecific examples of how the product supports, partially supports, or doesn't support the criterion\nRemarks and explanations for all criteria\nResponses that should concern you:\nExplanations that duplicate the wording of the criteria\nAll criteria marked as "Supports"\nLanguage like "this product was designed to...", which doesn't indicate if it actually does\nTable 1: Success Criteria, Level A\nHere are the most critical WCAG guidelines in Table 1 to concentrate on in your initial review.\n1.1 - Text Alternatives\n1.1.1 - Non-Text Content (Level A)\nSummary: This is mostly about\nalternative text for images\n, charts, tables, and so on. Most products should support this guideline. The use of CAPTCHA elevates this guideline to critical. CAPTCHAs are an element of controversy in the accessibility community because of their potential barrier to access. If CAPTCHAs are included within a vendor's submission, there must be more than one option (for example, visual and auditory) as a minimum.\nPriority: Critical\n1.2 - Time Based Media\n1.2.1 - Audio Only & Video Only (Pre-recorded Level A)\nSummary: There must be text alternatives to pre-recorded audio or video content, usually in the form of\ntext-based transcripts\n. This is critical for accessibility as these text transcripts can then easily be translated into different modes for different user needs.\nPriority: Critical\n1.2.2 - Captions (Pre-recorded Level A)\nSummary: There must be\ncaptions\nprovided for any pre-recorded audio/video content. This is critical for a hearing-impaired user. "Does not support" or "Partially supports" indicate the product should be flagged for further testing. View a\n1-minute video overview about captions\n.\nPriority: Critical\n1.2.3 - Audio Description or Media Alternative (Pre-recorded level A)\nSummary: Video content is either given additional\naudio descriptions\nwithin the confines of the audio track or there is an entirely separate audio track that contains the entirety of the video content described in audio format. Exceptions here could be expected as full conformance seems a rarity in this area. However, it should still be viewed as a critical component.\nPriority: Critical\n1.3 - Adaptable\n1.3.1, 1.3.2, 1.3.3 - Info and Relationships, Meaningful Sequence, Sensory Characteristics (Level A)\nSummary: These three guidelines are related to meaning and how the structures of applications can be read, and thus interpreted, with assistive technology. For this reason, they should be viewed more as a whole than as individual sub-sections. This area is as critical to accessibility as any on this list. However, because these are design guidelines, it is also the one with the largest room for interpretation. The good news is most modern web-based applications generally conform to these concepts during their initial design phase. Answers here should generally list "Supports". Possible exceptions should be very detailed if they exist.\nPriority: Critical\n1.4 - Distinguishable\n1.4.1, 1.4.2 - Use of Color, Audio Control (Level A)\nSummary: These deal with the\nuse of color\nand the ability to control audio playback within an application.\nIf color is used in a defining way (for example, click the red button), there must be alternative ways to identify that element or color identification.\nIf audio content is to be automatically played, there must be a way for the content to be stopped or paused, and the volume must be operated independently from the system volume.\nThese guidelines are critical as both have the potential for significant barriers to access.\nPriority: Critical\n2.1 - Keyboard Accessible\n2.1.1, 2.1.2 - Keyboard, No Keyboard Trap (Level A)\nSummary: These guidelines should be considered the starting point in the evaluation process and the most critical within the standard.\nGuideline 2.1.1 deals solely with the ability to\nnavigate the application with keyboard controls\n, which is crucial to the use of assistive technologies.\nGuideline 2.1.2 deals with the process of navigating to a specific element and then becoming "trapped" on the element and not being able to navigate away from it.\nThis doesn't preclude an application that has "partially supports" from being considered. However, they should be flagged and then sent for more in-depth testing before purchase. View a\n1-minute video on testing keyboard support\n.\nPriority: Critical\n2.1.3 - Keyboard (No Exceptions) (Level AAA)\nSummary: The guideline requires that an entire application be keyboard navigable. Even though AAA conformance guidelines are not included in the rest of this document, it is included here to highlight the importance of keyboard navigation. If this guideline is supported within a submission, it should be given top priority.\nPriority: n/a\n2.2 Enough Time\n2.2.2 - Pause, Stop, Hide (Level A)\nSummary: The user needs to be able to pause, stop, or hide any content that moves, blinks, or scrolls automatically. This applies if the movement, blinking, or scrolling lasts longer than 5 seconds and there is other content on the screen at the same time.\nPriority: Critical\n2.3 - Seizures and Physical Interactions\n2.3.1 - Three Flashes or Below Threshold (Level A)\nSummary: This guideline restricts the flashing of content to no more than three flashes per second within an application. Considering its impact, its critical importance is clear.\nPriority: Critical\n2.4 - Navigable\n2.4.1, 2.4.2 - Bypass Blocks, Page Titled (Level A)\nSummary: These guidelines are very basic functions that are included on most web-based applications today.\nThe first guideline deals with "skip to" links at the top of web pages which allow quicker navigation to those critical areas. View a\nshort video on checking skip links\n.\nThe second simply requires an appropriate and descriptive title for each web page. View a\nshort video on checking page titles\n.\nIf they are unsupported, this should be a red flag.\nPriority: Critical\n3.1 - Readable\n3.1.1 - Language of Page (Level A)\nSummary: This guideline ensures that a web page's language is programmatically defined for assistive technologies to access. Exceptions should be rare.\nPriority: Critical\n3.2 - Predictable\n3.2.1 - On Focus (Level A)\nSummary: This guideline essentially prohibits the context change of a web element when it receives system focus through standard navigation. An example could be the automatic submission of a web form when the button receives focus, or the automatic launch of a new browser tab when a link gets focus.\nPriority: Critical\n3.2.2 - On Input (Level A)\nSummary: This guideline addresses changes in context when a user changes the state of a component, and the change will persist after interaction stops. An example of a state change would be checking a checkbox or entering text in a text box. These are elements where a user must be alerted to a context change to validate that element's status.\nPriority: Critical\n3.3 - Input Assistance\n3.3.1 - Error Identification (Level A)\nSummary: This ensures that input entry\nerrors are identified in a clear and descriptive manner\nto all users. While slightly ambiguous, the intent here is that any error message must be immediately identifiable and accessible to any user using any type of assistive technology. The error should also be described in a way that the user can figure out what is wrong.\nPriority: Critical\n3.3.2 - Labels or Instructions (Level A)\nSummary: There must be proper labeling and instructions for any application input fields. Examples include search fields or a web form. This is a barrier to access, so exceptions should be viewed cautiously.\nPriority: Critical\n4.1 - Compatible\n4.1.2 - Name, Role, Value (Level A)\nSummary: This guideline targets custom user interface elements created with scripting components instead of the standard markup language elements. It requires that these custom components' name, role, attributes, and any change of status be identifiable by assistive technologies. An example would be a custom checkbox being identified as "checkbox checked" after it has been marked by a user.\nPriority: Critical\nTable 2: Success Criteria, Level AA\nHere are the most critical WCAG guidelines in Table 2 to concentrate on in your initial review.\n1.4 - Distinguishable\n1.4.13 - Content on Hover or Focus (Level AA WCAG 2.1 and 2.2)\nSummary: This guideline deals with hovering over or focusing on an item to activate extra content. Hover action elements are notorious for accessibility problems.\nPriority: Critical\n2.4 - Navigable\n2.4.7 - Focus Visible (Level AA)\nSummary: There must be an indicator for keyboard focus within a web-based application. This is critical for multiple reasons. This focus indicator is the primary way testers evaluate the keyboard access and tabbing order of an application. So, this can not only act as evidence of that testing process, it will also be critical for future testing of the application during update cycles.\nPriority: Critical\nAdditional resources\nTo learn more about reviewing a VPAT/ACR, see:\nA quick guide to reviewing a VPAT ACR\nby Karl Groves\nHow I Evaluate an ACR (VPAT)\nby Adrian Roselli\nMaking VPATs and ACRs more effective in procurement\nfrom WebAIM\nTo learn why a VPAT completed by a reputable third party is more trustworthy than one completed by the vendor, see\nWhy a third party should prepare your VPAT\nby Karl Groves.	KB0024199	KB0024199 v7.0	 <strong>submission</strong>, there must be more than one option (for example, visual and auditory) as a minimum to highlight the importance of keyboard navigation. If this guideline is supported within a <strong>submission</strong> navigation. An example could be the automatic <strong>submission</strong> of a web form when the button receives	6.2329		2026-01-31 17:35:36.477271	2026-01-31 17:35:45.335839	0	f335e66547d2fad02fc6a55b416d43bc
11487	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=6410787f476456583d373803846d43e3	Use the Faculty Center to enter grades into your grade roster	Table of contents\nOverview\nEnter grades individually\nEnter grades from a prepared file\nPrepare the file\nUse Load from File to enter a prepared file\nOverview\nAs an instructor at Indiana University, you can enter grades into your class rosters in the Faculty Center, as described below, or in\nCanvas\n.\nUITS\nrecommends that for a single course you use only one method to enter grades. To enter grades from Canvas, see:\nLoad final grades from Canvas into the SIS Grade Roster\nSubmit final grades from Canvas via a file upload\nYou can't submit final grades until the final grade roster is\navailable for each term; the Office of the Registrar will email\ninstructors when rosters are available. For questions about roster\navailability, grade submission deadlines, or submitting final grades\nafter the deadline, contact your campus\nOffice of the Registrar\n.\nNote:\nIf grades are submitted by 8pm, students can view their grades in the Faculty Center the following day after 7am Monday-Saturday, or after 8am on Sunday.\nEnter grades individually\nTo enter grades into your roster:\nGo to\nFaculty Center\n.\nThe Faculty Center will initially display information for the current term. To select a different term or campus, select\nChange Term or Campus\nand select your term and/or desired campus.\nIn the "Class" column, locate the course section for which you would like to enter grades. Select the\nGrade Roster\nlink in the "Grade Roster" column for that section. If no grade roster column or link appears for the class, the grade roster for the class has not yet been generated.\nVerify the term and roster type (for example,\nFinal Grade\n).\nEnter grades in the "Grade Input" fields. Alternatively, you can select\nValid Grades\nnext to the field to display a list of valid grade values, and then select an appropriate grade.\nNote:\nSave your work often; the\nSave\nbutton is at the bottom of the page. If your session is inactive for more than thirty minutes, it will time out and any grades not saved will be lost. If you have limited time, save your work and return to it later.\nTo assign a grade of\nFN\n, enter a value in the "Last Date Attended" field (this field will not appear until you have selected\nSave\n). Estimate the date if you do not know it. If the student never attended, enter a grade of\nFNN\n, which does not require a date to go with it. The system will not let you enter a date in this field that is not within the term-begin and term-end dates.\nFor students who have undergone an academic misconduct investigation and for whom the academic misconduct designation was deemed appropriate, check the box in the "Academic Misconduct" column.\nStudents who dropped during the 100% refund period will not appear on the roster.\nFor students taking IU classes through another university, you will see a placeholder grade of\nZZ\n. No further grade is necessary for these students.\nSelect\nSave\n. Verify that you have correctly entered all grades.\nSelect\nApprove and Submit to Registrar\n. All students must be assigned a grade before you select this button. If you select\nSave\nwithout selecting\nApprove and Submit to Registrar\n, you have not submitted your grades.\nIf you selected one or more grades for academic misconduct, you'll see a pop-up window to confirm that you intend to assign academic misconduct for the student(s) in question. Select\nOK\nto submit the grades to the registrar or\nCancel\nto return to the roster to make adjustments.\nIf a roster is submitted with\nF\ngrades, the instructor will receive a reminder for IU's grading policy for compliance of\nF\ngrades and financial aid.\nNote:\nIf you are acting as a proxy, you won't have access to this button unless you have been authorized to both enter and approve grades. You'll need to notify the faculty of record that you have finished entering grades. The faculty of record must then access the Faculty Center, navigate to the grade roster, and select\nApprove and Submit to Registrar\n.\nOnce you have approved the grades, you can no longer edit them. To make changes to grades after they have been submitted, contact the\nOffice of the Registrar\n.\nYou can tell whether your grades have been successfully submitted by checking for a message at the top of the roster that says "Grades have been submitted to the Registrar". Approved rosters are posted overnight.\nEnter grades from a prepared file\nTo enter midterm and final grade rosters from a prepared file, use the Load from File tool:\nNote:\nLoad from File may not be available at all IU campuses.\nPrepare the file\nBefore loading, prepare your grade file as follows:\nThe file must be comma delimited (CSV); if you use a Mac, save your CSV file in Windows comma-separated format.\nOn the first line, indicate whether the grade roster is for midterm grades (MID) or final grades (FIN).\nAll additional lines must be formatted with\nUniversity ID\nand course grade. Optionally, for final grades, if it applies to the student's enrollment, you can include a Requirement Designation Grade (such as S for Satisfied, or N for Not Satisfied).\nNotes:\nEvery University ID in the comma-delimited file must match a University ID in the grade roster; grades without matching University IDs will be ignored.\nThe University IDs must contain leading zeroes in order to match the grade roster.\nYou do not need to enter grades for students for whom a grade is already assigned (for example, a grade of W for withdrawn or a grade of ZZ). Attempts to replace the existing grade with a different one will be ignored.\nDo not enter a Last Date of Attendance when applying an FN grade. Enter the date after the upload, when validating the grades. If you try to add a date, the record will not be loaded.\nDo not attempt to designate academic misconduct on the file. Check the academic misconduct box after the upload, when validating the grades.\nFollowing is an example of a load file:\nFIN\n0000011111,A-\n0000022222,C+,S\n0000033333,FN,N\n0000044444,B\n0000055555,I\nIf the file does not contain "FIN" or "MID" on the first line, or if the value does not match the roster type, the following error message will appear:\nInvalid File. First Line of File must be MID for Mid-Term Grade Rosters or FIN for Final Grade Rosters.\nDepending on whether the file is for midterm or final grades, you may receive one of the following error messages:\nInvalid file. Grade lines must be as follows: EMPLID,GRADE[,S/N].\nInvalid file. Grade lines must be as follows: EMPLID,GRADE.\nUse Load from File to enter a prepared file\nTo use Load from File to enter your prepared grades file:\nComplete steps 1 through 4 from the\nEnter grades individually\nsection above.\nNote:\nIf you navigate to a roster in the Faculty Center before rosters are generated, the link to the roster will be inactive.\nSelect\nLoad Grades from File to Roster\n.\nSelect\nBrowse\nto locate your grade file.\nOnce the filename displays in the text box, select\nUpload\n. The system will attempt to load the file. The following message should appear, where "Y" is the number of records in the file and "X" is the number of records loaded:\nLoading X Records out of Y Read. Would you like to continue?\nSelect\nOK\nor\nCancel\n.\nSelect\nSave\n. Verify that all grades are loaded, edit as necessary, and then select\nApprove and Submit to Registrar\n.\nNote:\nIf you are acting as a proxy, you won't have access to this button unless you have been authorized to both enter and approve grades. You'll need to notify the faculty of record that you have finished entering grades. The faculty of record must then access the Faculty Center, navigate to the grade roster, and select\nApprove and Submit to Registrar\n.	KB0024949	KB0024949 v1.0	Table of contents Overview Enter grades individually Enter grades from a prepared file Prepare the file Use Load from File to enter a prepared file Overview As an instructor at Indiana University, you can enter grades into your class rosters in the Faculty Center, as described below, or in Canvas. UITS recommends that for a single course you use	5.6479		2026-01-31 17:10:44.608283	2026-01-31 17:35:49.331692	0	6410787f476456583d373803846d43e3
12446	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=1e30347347a456583d373803846d4340	Request SMTP authentication authorization for mail-relay.iu.edu	All SMTP authentication to\nmail-relay.iu.edu\nis restricted and requires prior authorization before being able to send authenticated email. To submit an authorization request, fill out the\nMail Relay Authorization form\n. After your submission is processed, you will be authorized to send authenticated email.	KB0025790	KB0025790 v1.0		5.6118		2026-01-31 17:35:52.690521	2026-01-31 17:35:52.690521	0	1e30347347a456583d373803846d4340
12447	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=49103c7f476456583d373803846d43ef	Error messages when sending email to IU addresses	When sending email to Indiana University email addresses, you may receive the following error messages:\nTable of contents\nUndeliverable message\nNot authorized\nToo many recipients\n(to view this section, you need to be affiliated with IU and\nlogged into the KB\n)\nUndeliverable message\nIf you send email from your IU\nExchange\naccount to another IU Exchange user, an undeliverable message will look like the following:\nSubject: Undeliverable:\n[Your subject line]\nBody:\nDelivery has failed to these recipients or groups:\nusername\n@iu.edu\nThe email address you entered couldn't be found. Please check the recipient's email address and try to resend the message. If the problem continues, please contact your helpdesk.\nThe following organization rejected your message: mssg-relay.indiana.edu.\nIf you send email from another type of email address (for example, a personal Gmail account) to any IU email account, an undeliverable message may look like this:\nSubject: Delivery Status Notification (Failure)\nBody:\nAddress not found\nYour message wasn't delivered to\nusername\n@iu.edu\nbecause the address couldn't be found, or is unable to receive mail.\nThese error messages may be caused by any of the following situations:\nYou've entered the person's email address incorrectly.  Search the\nIU Directory\nfor contact information for IU students, faculty, and staff.\nThe person's IU email account has been disabled. As students, faculty, and staff leave IU, their computing accounts are eventually retired. The first step in this process is to disable them before they are actually purged.  Check to see if the person appears in the\nIU Directory\n; if they do not, and you know the email address is correct, their account may be disabled.  If you need to contact someone in the person's IU department, use the departmental listings in the IU Directory.\nThere is an outage or other issue with an IU email system.  Check\nStatus.IU\nfor alerts.\nFor help, contact your campus\nSupport Center\n.\nNot authorized\nAll SMTP authentication to\nmail-relay.iu.edu\nis restricted and requires prior authorization before being able to send authenticated email. To submit an authorization request, fill out the\nMail Relay Authorization form\n. After your submission is processed, you will be authorized to send authenticated email.\nIU community:\nToo many recipients\nSee\nSMTP servers for external email services\n.	KB0024999	KB0024999 v1.0	When sending email to Indiana University email addresses, you may receive the following error messages: Table of contents Undeliverable message Not authorized Too many recipients (to view this section, you need to be affiliated with IU and logged into the KB) Undeliverable message If you send email from your IU Exchange account to another IU Exc	5.6118		2026-01-31 17:35:55.990022	2026-01-31 17:35:55.990022	0	49103c7f476456583d373803846d43ef
12462	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=9715c65b1bf0965416c02fc4bd4bcb22	About Log-Alert audit configurations	Table of contents\nOverview\nUnix- and Linux-based servers\nWindows servers\nSetting audit policy with GPO\nPolicies applied under the template GPO\nModify SACL entries to trigger file access events\nWindows event IDs collected\nOverview\nBefore\nLog-Alert\ndashboards can report your data for\nSecurity of Information Technology Resources (IT-12)\n, the events need to be generated on the servers for collection. Auditing for IT-12 is not enabled by default. To enable auditing, follow the appropriate instructions below.\nNote:\nThis is a bare-bones audit policy template. It is the responsibility of the departmental local UITS support person and IT management to determine if this meets IT-12 requirements. Depending on the data classification (see\nClassification levels of institutional data\n), additional logging may be required. UITS\nEndpoint Management Services\ndoes not make audit log or policy recommendations.\nUnix- and Linux-based servers\nThe following settings should be verified or added in the\n/etc/audit/rules.d/audit.rules\nfile:\nFailed file access through the audit daemon:\n-a exit,always -F arch=b64 -S open -F exit=-EACCES\n-a exit,always -F arch=b64 -S open -F exit=-EPERM\n-a exit,always -F arch=b32 -S open -F exit=-EACCES\n-a exit,always -F arch=b32 -S open -F exit=-EPERM\nSuccessful file access through the audit daemon:\n-w "directory OR full path to file" -p wrxa\nSuccessful/failed login attempts: The audit daemon generates all login success and failures by default.\nWindows servers\nSetting audit policy with GPO\nUsing Group Policy Objects (GPOs) is the best way to push down audit configurations to Windows servers. By default, several of the logging Event IDs required by IT-12 are not generated.\nWe have created a group policy object that can be used as a template, or linked to directly:\nIU-LS-LogAlertAudit\n.\nLegacy GPOs might be pushing local policy for audit. Setting both and creating a mixed-mode environment of settings on your servers may lead to potential conflict. If you encounter unusual or unexpected results in the Resultant Set of Policy (RSOP), check to see if you have multiple policy configurations in multiple GPOs applied to your organizational units (OUs).\nYou can use the following tools for troubleshooting:\nGroup Policy Management to investigate which GPOs are applied to particular OUs.\nAuditpol.exe\nto show actual results on the servers. Policies in the local security policy viewer are not accurate when using GPOs.\ngpresult -h gpresult.html\nfrom the command line will generate an interactive HTML page showcasing applied policies on a particular machine.\nPolicies applied under the template GPO\nBelow is a complete list of the policies applied by the\nIU-LS-LogAlertAudit\nGPO. If these settings need to be applied locally (and not via a GPO), they can be recreated as such.\nUnder\nPolicies/Windows Settings/Security Settings/Advanced\nAudit Configuration/\n, change the following:\nValues for policy settings in four categories: Account Logon, Account Management, Logon/Logoff, and Object Access\nPolicy setting\nValue\nAccount Logon\nAudit Credential Validation\nSuccess/Failure\nAudit Kerberos Authentication Service\nSuccess\nAudit Kerberos Service Ticket Operations\nSuccess\nAudit Other Account Logon Events\nSuccess\nAccount Management\nAudit Computer Account Management\nSuccess/Failure\nAudit Security Group Management\nSuccess/Failure\nAudit User Account Management\nSuccess/Failure\nLogon/Logoff\nAudit Account Lockout\nSuccess\nAudit Logon\nSuccess/Failure\nAudit Network Policy Server\nSuccess/Failure\nAudit Other Logon/Logoff Events\nSuccess/Failure\nAudit Special Logon\nSuccess/Failure\nObject Access\nAudit File System\nFor sensitive data:\nSuccess/Failure\nFor non-sensitive date:\nFailure\nAudit Other Object Access Events\nSuccess/Failure\nAudit Handle Manipulation\nSuccess/Failure\nOnce you have enabled auditing, the logon/logoff settings will immediately begin logging events. However, to ensure that events are generated, you must enable SACL-level instructions to tell the system whom to audit against.\nFor more about auditing, see Microsoft's\nAudit Policy Recommendations\n.\nModify SACL entries to trigger file access events\nRight-click the drive or directory that you wish to enable for auditing. Select\nProperties\n, then\nSecurity\n, and then\nAdvanced\n.\nSelect\nAuditing\n. To modify the audit entry, select\nAdd\n.\nSelect a principal; UITS recommends using\nEveryone\n.\nWindows event IDs collected\nLog-Alert\ngathers the following Windows event IDs\nWindows event ID\nDescription\n4624\nSuccessful logon\n4648\nSuccessful logon\n4625\nFailed logon\n4825\nFailed logon (other logon events)\n4656\nFailed file access\n4663\nSuccessful file access\n4985\nSuccessful file access\n4672\nSpecial privileges assigned to new logon (usually service logon or account used to run a batch job)	KB0024440	KB0024440 v2.0	 to new logon (usually service logon or account used to run a <strong>batch</strong> <strong>job</strong>)	12.5071	km-aud-iu	2026-01-31 17:36:15.710686	2026-01-31 17:36:15.710686	0	9715c65b1bf0965416c02fc4bd4bcb22
12464	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=fcfd10e347087e502fc6a55b416d430e	J2EE deployment and re-deployment information	Table of contents\nDeployments\nRestart individual servers\nTomcat and Java versions\nLibraries\nDebug\nProduction deployments\nRe-run production deployments\nOff-cycle deployments\n32-bit vs. 64-bit servers\nURLs\n/opt/ears permissions issues\nClean out log directories\nArchitecture\nRemote IP address\nSession affinity\nDeployments\nFor production deployments, Enterprise Linux Administration (ELA) schedules a shutdown of the Tomcat servers, runs a script to clean out the\nwork\nand\nwebapps\ndirectories, does a deployment of the new\nwar\nfile, and then starts the Tomcat instances again. ELA recommends following these steps in the test environments as well. This will prevent occasional problems with multiple deployments consuming all available Perm space and issues with hot-undeploying the old application. You can perform these same steps in\ntest\nwith these commands:\nsudo /usr/local/bin/j2ee_stop_server_cluster [app server name]\nsudo /usr/local/bin/j2ee_clean_server [app server name]\nsudo -u j2eemgr /usr/local/bin/appdeploy [app] [env]\nsudo /usr/local/bin/j2ee_start_server_cluster [app server name]\nThese commands should be run from the lead host in each cluster (for example,\nesappj01\n). Enter the following to list the\nsudo\ncommands you're allowed to run:\nsudo -l\nRestart individual servers\nTo restart your test servers without stopping both at the same time, you can log into each server individually and run the following commands, which will restart only the server on that host:\nsudo /usr/local/bin/j2ee_stop_server [app server name]\nsudo /usr/local/bin/j2ee_start_server [app server name]\nTomcat and Java versions\nWhen setting up a new Tomcat cluster, ELA will ask what version you'd like (that is, what version you've been running in your development environment). If you don't have a preference, ELA will generally install the latest in the ESI managed versions. Upgrades to Tomcat need to be requested by the application area, as later releases of Tomcat should be tested first in your development environment before being applied in the test and production environments.\nFor most applications, you will want to use the common ESI managed standard JDK, which is approved at J2EE Developer meetings, and announced via ESIT and Change Management for upgrades. If you wish to opt out of using this version (for example, if you require more control over the JDK release, or need to run an earlier version), you will be responsible for coordinating JDK updates with ELA for your test and production environments.\nLibraries\nIf you need additional\n.jar\nfiles installed to the\n/lib\ndirectory or other server-level locations within Tomcat, upload the files to your drop spot (\n/opt/java/deploy/<app>/<env>\ndirectory on\nescbstst\nor\nescbs03\n), and send an installation request using the\nEnterprise Platforms Support\nrequest form. Common\n.jar\nfiles you may need installed include Oracle and other database drivers.\nDebug\nYou can initiate a thread dump on any of your Tomcat instances (test or production) by logging into that host and using the following command:\nsudo /usr/local/bin/j2ee_thread_dump [app server name]\nThe thread dump will be in the\ncatalina.out\nlog file and begin with a line like the following:\nFull thread dump Java HotSpot(TM) 64-Bit Server VM (14.2-b01 mixed mode):\nBesides your application logs (usually in\n/opt/logs/[env]/[app]\n), the\ncatalina.out\nlog file is a good place to look for errors. This file is in the\n/usr/local/tomcat/[server name]/logs/\ndirectory and can be especially useful if the application isn't initializing.\nTo monitor the trend of the application server's thread, use the\nELA Grafana monitoring service\n.\nGrafana samples in five minute intervals. For real-time thread usage information on an individual server, run:\nsudo /usr/local/bin/j2ee_server_status [app server name]\nRun the following command on the lead host (for example,\nesappj01\n,\nesjava01\n,\nesoncprd01\n, etc.) for the status of all hosts in your cluster:\nsudo /usr/local/bin/j2ee_server_status_cluster [app server name]\nProduction deployments\nThe standard deployment window for J2EE applications is 5-6am Thursday. To request a production deployment, complete the\nEnterprise Platforms Support\nrequest form by\n11am Wednesday\n. Indicate any special needs, such as plug-in deployments, security file changes, or changes to the Tomcat servers (for example, JDK upgrades). Status email messages will be sent to the team mailing list at the conclusion of the deployment or after critical steps. You can also monitor the releases in real time via one of the following methods:\nLog into the production Central Batch Server (CBS) (\nescbs03.uits.iu.edu\n) and run\n/home/j2eemgr/bin/summary\n. You will get a list of actions completed today or currently running, including the status (Fail, Success, In Progress). This is the same data format emailed by the\nj2ee_email_status.sh\nCBS job.\nFrom the lead deployment host (for example,\nesjava01\n), you can tail the\nbuild.log\nfile to watch the full results of a\nj2ee_deploy_[app].sh\nCBS job (same as the\nappdeploy\ncommand). For example:\ntail -f /opt/ears/prd/[app code]/build.log\nOn the hosts on which your Tomcat instances reside, you can check the timestamp of the\n.war\nfile in the\nwebapps\ndirectory to verify the new file was copied into place. For example:\nls -l /usr/local/tomcat/[app server name]/webapps/\nAlso, on the hosts on which your Tomcat instances reside, you can watch the\ncatalina.out\nfile for all the normal start-up messages.\nTomcat messages and definitions\nMessage\nMeaning\nINFO: Deploying web application archive kr-unt.war\nTomcat is now trying to initialize this particular web application (watch the following lines closely for errors).\nINFO: JK: ajp13 listening on /192.168.220.1:8009\nThe Tomcat instance is now listening on its AJP connector and will start taking requests from the web servers.\nINFO: Server startup in 226029 ms\nThe Tomcat instance has completely finished initialization.\nRe-run production deployments\nIf you discover an error (for example, a CVS tag not set properly, or a small bug quickly identified and patched) and need to redeploy your production application on the morning of the release window, call DCOPS (812-855-9910) and ask for the first job in your block of commands to be re-run. Usually, that will be a\nj2ee_stop_[application server name].sh\njob, and the subsequent jobs will automatically be re-run as well (that is, the clean, deploy, start, and email scripts).\nOff-cycle deployments\nOff-cycle deployments require manager approval; director-level approval is required during change freezes. ELA schedules the deployment tasks through Production Services, which will not set the schedules until manager approval can be shown.\n32-bit vs. 64-bit servers\nJ2EE systems are 64-bit Linux Red Hat Linux 5 or 6 systems. 64-bit systems can address more memory, so they handle larger JVM heaps. Under the old 32-bit systems, servers were limited to 2 G heaps. With the 64-bit systems, ELA has also tried to exercise disaster recovery (DR) plans by housing the test systems (\nesappj*\n) in the IU Indianapolis Data Center. This helps ensure that, in the event of a major disaster, ELA can recover production systems to a different campus, since there are already resources and a completely tested architecture in Indianapolis.\nURLs\nURLs to access your application follow this standard:\nhttps://hostname[:port]/[application code]-[environment]/\nHostnames for the standard J2EE environments\nHostname\nEnvironment\nuisapp2.iu.edu\nProduction (\nesjava*\nservers)\ntrain.uisapp2.iu.edu\nTraining environment (\njtrain*\nservers)\ntest.uisapp2.iu.edu\n64-bit test servers (\nesappj*\nservers)\n/opt/ears\npermissions issues\nIf you're having trouble editing a file in your\n/opt/ears/[env]/[appcode]/\ndirectory due to a coworker having edited it with a restrictive\numask\n, run the following command, which should return group write permissions on all the files:\nsudo /usr/local/bin/j2ee_fix_perms [app] [environment]\nClean out log directories\nIf you'd like to remove all your existing log files, run the following on each test server on which your Tomcat instances reside:\nsudo /usr/local/bin/j2ee_clean_logs [app] [environment]\nArchitecture\nRemote IP address\nSince the load balancer works like a proxy server, the standard ways of getting the remote IP address (that is, the client's IP) for a request will instead return the IP address of the load balancer. If you need the remote IP address for logging or other purposes, you'll want instead to grab the\nX-Cluster-Client-Ip\nHTTP header's value. This additional HTTP header is set by the load balancer to contain the client's real IP address.\nSession affinity\nFor most applications, session affinity is controlled by a browser session cookie (which is cleared out when your browser is restarted) called\nJSESSIONID\n. The end portion of the content in the cookie will have a string like\nEspd_1Engine\n; this is what determines the back-end server to which your request is passed.\nIf you need a request to be handled by a specific instance for testing, you can edit the cookie to change the\n1Engine\nportion. Most Tomcat servers are configured so that the first instance is\n1Engine\nand the second is\n2Engine\n. Instances with more than two Tomcat servers will have increasing numbers (\nx\n) for\nxEngine\n; the actual order of those may not map well with the number of the hosts (for example,\nesjava03\n,\n04\n, etc.). If in doubt about the\nEngine\nnumber of your Tomcat instances, contact\nEnterprise Platforms Support\nfor assistance.	KB0022855	KB0022855 v4.0	 Central <strong>Batch</strong> Server (CBS) ( escbs03.uits.iu.edu) and run /home/j2eemgr/bin/summary. You will get a list). This is the same data format emailed by the j2ee_email_status.sh CBS <strong>job</strong>. From the lead deployment of a j2ee_deploy_[app].sh CBS <strong>job</strong> (same as the appdeploy command). For example: tail -f /opt/ears/prd/[app code	7.4716	km-aud-uits	2026-01-31 17:36:19.500965	2026-01-31 17:36:19.500965	0	fcfd10e347087e502fc6a55b416d430e
12468	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=bf317b3247ac7e5cd500b87b716d43e9	Data specification for provisioning IU guests and non-credit courses, sections, and enrollments in Canvas and Expand	Table of contents\nBefore you begin\nCreate users\nCreate or update courses\nCreate or update sections\nCreate or update enrollments\nCreate enrollments for listings in IU Expand\nBefore you begin\nCampus units that offer non-credit courses or maintain their own course student information systems can make arrangements with the UITS LMS Development team to automate the provisioning of courses, enrollments, and guest users in\nCanvas\nor\nIU Expand\n. To request access to this service, complete the\nCanvas/Expand Provisioning App and API Request form\n.\nThere are two options for transmitting your Canvas data to UITS:\nUpload files via a secure API. This method works best for automated batch jobs that run on a regular schedule. It can also be used for manual uploads with a properly configured REST client. For details, see\nAbout the Canvas and Expand Provisioning API\n.\nUpload files with the Canvas and Expand Provisioning app in Canvas. This method is recommended for campus units and external partners who plan to upload their data manually. For details, see\nAbout the Canvas and Expand Provisioning app\n.\nTo use the Canvas and Expand provisioning services, your department's provisioning data must conform to the formatting requirements outlined below. These standards are based on Canvas's\nSIS Import Format Documentation\n, with modifications to accommodate specific use cases at IU.\nCreate users\nCanvas accounts are automatically provisioned for all users within 24 hours of creation of their\nUniversity account\n, so you should not need to include IU students, faculty, or staff in the\nusers.csv\nfile. Campus units can, however, use the\nusers.csv\nfile to provision accounts for individuals without a\nUniversity account\n. If the user does not yet have an IU\nGuest account\n, when the file is processed, both an IU Guest account and a Canvas account will be created. The optional\nservice\ncolumn can be used to send users to Canvas or\nIU Expand\nafter they log in.\nNote:\nWhen running provisioning jobs in the Canvas test (\niu.test.instructure.com\n) or beta (\niu.beta.instructure.com\n) environments, do not upload a\nusers.csv\nfile with information for real external users. Doing so will send guest account invitations to each user for the\nIU Login\ntest environment, which they should not be using. You can use your own personal email accounts for testing guest account creation in the test environment.\nFilename:\nusers.\nunique_id\n.csv\nFile format:\nComma-separated values.\nCreate users with the following column headings and values\nColumn heading\nValue\nuser_id\n(required)\nLeave this column blank.\nWhen this field is processed, it will be populated with the IU Guest account sequence ID, a unique ID assigned to each IU Guest account.\nlogin_id\n(required)\nEmail address of the guest account to add or create\nfirst_name\n(required)\nFirst name of guest. This field is restricted to the\nISO/IEC 8859-1 character set\n. Note that apostrophes must be straight (\n'\n), not curly (\n’\n).\nlast_name\n(required)\nLast name of guest This field is restricted to the\nISO/IEC 8859-1 character set\n. Note that apostrophes must be straight (\n'\n), not curly (\n’\n).\nemail\n(required)\nEmail address of guest; this should be identical to\nlogin_id\n.\nstatus\n(required)\nLeave this column blank.\nEvery Canvas account will be created with "active" status.\nservice\n(optional)\nOptionally, to control where the user is sent after creating the IU Guest account:\nUse\nc\nor\nC\nfor Canvas.\nUse\ne\nor\nE\nfor IU Expand.\nIf you omit the column or leave it blank, users are sent to Canvas by default.\nExample\nSee the example below; you can also\ndownload the template\n.\nuser_id,login_id,first_name,last_name,email,status,service\n,joeschmoe1234@gmail.com,Joe,Schmoe,joeschmoe1234@gmail.com,,e\n,tomjones4321@gmail.com,Tom,Jones,tomjones4321@gmail.com,,c\nNotification messages\nWhen each new Guest account is created, a generic notification is sent to the recipient with instructions for how to activate the account. You can also send an optional custom email message to each new guest Canvas user by uploading a text file named\nmessage.properties\nalong with your\nusers.csv\nfile. For instructions on how to format the\nmessage.properties\nfile and preview the custom email message before sending, see\nSend custom notifications to Canvas Guest account recipients\n.\nCreate or update courses\nDepartments can provision non-credit courses and credit courses that are not part of the standard SIS provisioning process.\nFilename:\ncourses.\nunique_id\n.csv\nFile format:\nComma-separated values.\nCreate or update courses with the following column headings and values\nColumn heading\nValue\ncourse_id\n(required)\nA unique identifier used to reference courses in the enrollments data. This identifier must be globally unique. In the Canvas user interface, this is called the SIS ID for the course. Only Canvas superadmins can view or edit SIS IDs.\nAt IU, course_ids for SIS-provisioned courses take the form\nSEMESTER-CAMPUS-SUBJECT-COURSE_NUMBER-SECTION_NUMBER\n(for example,\nFA15-IN-ENG-W131-99999\n). Departments that create their own course_ids are responsible for ensuring that they do not conflict with other course_ids in the system by developing a naming scheme that incorporates the department name or abbreviation into the section number portion of the ID.\nTo view, set, or edit the Canvas course_id or section_id for non-credit courses, use the\nCourse Attribute Editor\napp.\nshort_name\n(required)\nA short descriptive name for the course\nlong_name\n(required)\nA long descriptive name for the course enclosed in double quotation marks (for example, "long name for course"). This can be the same as the short name, but if both are available, users will have a better experience when you provide both.\naccount_id\n(required)\nThe account identifier for the node in the institutional hierarchy in which the course will be placed (for example, BL-BUKD, IUSM-IN).\nThis field is case sensitive and must match the node name in Canvas exactly.\nterm_id\nA unique four-digit identifier for the term during which the course will be offered; for standard SIS Term IDs, see\nCurrent Term Processing Dates\n(downloadable Excel file).\nNote:\nThe term must be available in Canvas before it can be referenced in the\ncourses.csv\nfile.\nstatus\n(required)\nStatus of the course; accepted values are "active", "deleted", or "completed". Use "active" unless you want to delete or conclude the course.\nstart_date\nThe course start date; if the start_date is set, it will override the term start date. The date should be in ISO 8601 format (\nYYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ\n).\nend_date\nThe course end date; if the end_date is set, it will override the term end date. The date should be in ISO 8601 format (\nYYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ\n).\nblueprint_course_id\nThe SIS ID of a pre-existing Blueprint course; when provided, the current course will be set up to receive updates from the Blueprint course. The Blueprint Courses feature is required. To remove the Blueprint course link, pass\ndissociate\nin place of the ID. If the Blueprint course does not have an SIS ID, a Canvas superadmin can add one for you.\nExample\nSee the example below; you can also\ndownload the template\n.\ncourse_id,short_name,long_name,account_id,term_id,status,start_date,end_date,blueprint_course_id\n1516-IN-IUSM-G730-154778,1516-IN-IUSM-G730-154778,OB/GYN CLERKSHIP,IUSM-Clerkships,1516,active,,,obgyn_bp\n1516-IN-IUSM-K710-154046,1516-IN-IUSM-K710-154046,PEDIATRICS CLERKSHIP,IUSM-Clerkships,1516,active,,,\n1516-IN-IUSM-M720-154304,1516-IN-IUSM-M720-154304,INTMED CLERKSHIP,IUSM-Clerkships,1516,active,,,\nCreate or update sections\nFor users to be enrolled into a course, the course must have at least one section. By default, when you attempt to enroll a user into a course with no sections, Canvas automatically generates a section with the same name as the course. Sections generated in this manner do not have a unique identifier that allows them to be called and manipulated via the Canvas API. For this reason, even though the\nsections.csv\nfile is optional, generating at least one section for each course is recommended. A\nsections.csv\nfile is required for you to add multiple sections to a single course shell.\nFilename:\nsections.\nunique_id\n.csv\nFile format:\nComma-separated values.\nCreate or update sections with the following column headings and values\nColumn heading\nValue\nsection_id\n(required)\nA unique identifier used to reference sections in the enrollments data. Each section ID must be globally unique, but can be identical to the corresponding course ID. In the Canvas user interface, this is called the SIS ID for the section. Only Canvas superadmins can view or edit SIS IDs.\nAt IU, section_ids for SIS-provisioned sections are identical to course_ids, taking the form\nSEMESTER-CAMPUS-SUBJECT-COURSE_NUMBER-SECTION_NUMBER\n(for example,\nFA15-IN-ENG-W131-99999\n). Departments that create their own section_ids are responsible for ensuring that they do not conflict with other section_ids in the system by developing a naming scheme that incorporates the department name or abbreviation.\nTo view, set, or edit the Canvas course_id or section_id for non-credit courses, use the\nCourse Attribute Editor\napp.\ncourse_id\n(required)\nThe course identifier (from\ncourses.csv\n) with which the section is associated. In the Canvas user interface, this is called the SIS ID for the course.\nTo view, set, or edit the Canvas course_id or section_id for non-credit courses, use the\nCourse Attribute Editor\napp.\nname\n(required)\nThe name of the section\nstatus\n(required)\nStatus of the section; accepted values are "active" and "deleted". Use "active" unless you want to delete the section.\nstart_date\nThe section start date; if the start_date is set, it will override the course and term start dates. The date should be in ISO 8601 format (\nYYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ\n).\nend_date\nThe section end date; if the end_date is set, it will override the course and term end dates. The date should be in ISO 8601 format (\nYYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ\n).\nExample\nSee the example below; you can also\ndownload the template\n.\nsection_id,course_id,name,status,start_date,end_date\n1516-MU-IUSM-N720-154932,1516-IN-IUSM-N720-154219,MU 04-25,active,,\n1516-MU-IUSM-N720-154931,1516-IN-IUSM-N720-154219,MU 03-29,active,,\n1516-MU-IUSM-N720-154930,1516-IN-IUSM-N720-154219,MU 02-29,active,,\nCreate or update enrollments\nThe\nenrollments.csv\nfile associates users with specific courses or sections. The courses and sections referenced by the\nenrollments.csv\nfile must have assigned SIS IDs. If you need assistance determining the SIS ID or assigning an SIS IDs to a Canvas course or section, email the UITS LMS Development team at\nlmsreq@iu.edu\n.\nFilename:\nenrollments.\nunique_id\n.csv\nFile format:\nComma-separated values.\nCreate or update enrollments with the following column headings and values\nColumn heading\nValue\ncourse_id\n(required)\nThe course identifier from\ncourses.csv\nor the SIS ID for an existing course.\nTo view, set, or edit the Canvas course_id or section_id for non-credit courses, use the\nCourse Attribute Editor\napp.\nuser_id\n(required)\nFor users with a\nUniversity account\n, accepted values are the user's 10-digit University ID (also known as the student ID or emplID) or IU username. For IU Guest account holders, enter the email address associated with the user's guest account.\nrole\n(required)\nUser role in the course or section; accepted values are "student", "teacher", "ta", "observer", "designer", "Librarian", or a custom role defined by the account.\nsection_id\n(required)\nThe section identifier from\nsections.csv\nor the SIS ID for an existing section.\nTo view, set, or edit the Canvas course_id or section_id for non-credit courses, use the\nCourse Attribute Editor\napp.\nstatus\n(required)\nStatus of the enrollment; accepted values are "active", "deleted", or "completed". Use "active" to add or keep an enrollment, "deleted" to remove an enrollment, or "completed" to conclude an enrollment and leave the user in the course.\nlimit_section_privileges\n(optional)\nThis column controls a user's ability to interact with one or all sections in the course. If the value is set to "true", the user will only be able to access other users in this section of the course; if the value is "false" or empty, the user will be able to interact with everyone in all sections of this course. While this can be an extremely useful setting for large classes with multiple sections, it has no impact on single-section courses. Review\nStudent privacy in Canvas courses with cross-listed sections\nbefore using this column for managing student enrollments. Setting the wrong value could open your course to FERPA violations.\nExample\nSee the example below; you can also\ndownload the template\n.\ncourse_id,user_id,role,section_id,status,limit_section_privileges\n1516-IN-IUSM-N720-154219,0009999999,teacher,1516-IN-IUSM-N720-154219,active,true\n1516-IN-IUSM-N720-154219,astudent,student,1516-IN-IUSM-N720-154226,active\n1516-IN-IUSM-N720-154219,joeschmoe1234@gmail.com,student,1516-IN-IUSM-N720-154226,deleted\n1516-IN-IUSM-X720-154693,tomjones4321@gmail.com,student,1516-TH-IUSM-X720-154740,active\nCreate enrollments for listings in IU Expand\nYou can also use this process to enroll users in Canvas courses offered through\nIU Expand\n. To enroll users in an IU Expand listing, do not upload the standard\nenrollments.csv\nfile described above. Instead format the file as described in this section.\nFilename:\nexpand.\nunique_id\n.csv\nFile format:\nComma-separated values.\nCreate enrollments for listing in IU Expand with the following column headings and values\nColumn heading\nValue\nuser_id\n(required)\nFor users with a\nUniversity account\n, accepted values are the user's 10-digit University ID (also known as the student ID or emplID) or IU username. For IU Guest account holders, provide the user's\nIU Login\nname, which should be the same as the user's email address.\nlisting_id\n(required)\nThis is the internal numeric identifier for the listing in IU Expand. To determine the ID for an Expand listing:\nAs an Expand Admin, navigate to the "Listings" page.\nLocate and open the listing of the course into which you want to enroll users. The URL for the listing should look something like this:\nhttps://expand.iu.edu/admin#/products/99999/details\nThe number following "products/" and preceding "/details" in the URL is the listing ID.\nsend_email\n(optional)\nThis column controls if an email is sent to the student for an Expand course, notifying them that they have been enrolled in the course.\nSetting the value to "true" sends a notification to the student. If the value is "false" or empty, then no email notification is sent.\nExample\nSee the example below; you can also\ndownload the template\n.\nuser_id,listing_id,send_email\n0009999999,27475,false\nastudent,24954,true\njoeschmoe1234@gmail.com,27475\ntomjones4321@gmail.com,27475	KB0024552	KB0024552 v3.0	 to UITS: Upload files via a secure API. This method works best for automated <strong>batch</strong> jobs that run	5.494		2026-01-31 17:36:23.505787	2026-01-31 17:36:23.505787	0	bf317b3247ac7e5cd500b87b716d43e9
11772	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=175ee71347f4fe18d500b87b716d4352	Hours IUIE data is available for reports	Table of contents\nIntroduction\nHours of availability and support\nFrozen files\nScheduled jobs\nIntroduction\nThe\nIUIE\ndatabase (\nDSS1PRD\n) is locked at 10pm daily to allow overnight batch scripts to run. The database is copied, and the copy made available overnight. (To request access to this overnight copy, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\n.) New data is again available for reporting the next morning.\nFor current EDSS data refresh information, first check\nIUIE News\n. Since not all data is refreshed nightly, also check the refresh date on the top right of the report object form for refresh frequency. EDSS data is updated daily except Sunday morning.\nHours of availability and support\nDay\nDaytime online hours (IUIE fully available)\nOvernight online hours (IUIE fully available)*\nIUIE support available\nMonday-Thursday\n5am-10pm\n11pm-5am (the next day)\n8am-5pm\nFriday\n5am-10pm\n11pm-8am (Saturday)\n8am-5pm\nSaturday\n7am-10pm\n11pm-10am (Sunday)\nN/A\nSunday\n10am-10pm\n11pm-5am (Monday)\nN/A\n*During overnight hours, data available will be from the previous day using a copy of the database, named\nDSS2PRD\n. To request access to this overnight copy, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\n.\nThe time of your initial login determines your access to report objects in the IUIE. This initial determination remains in effect for the duration of your IUIE session. Before online hours, the database is locked for updates, and you cannot access any report objects. Therefore, if you log in before online hours, you will still be unable to access report objects and the database when online hours begin. The error message you receive may indicate that\nDSS1PRD\nis inaccessible or that the system could not log you into the database.\nTo restore access, exit the IUIE using the\nLogout\nicon (to clear the cache) and log back in during online hours. If you continue to receive an error, use the\nFeedback\nbutton to report the error to the IUIE Help Desk. IUIE administrators will restore your access.\nWhen you access certain report objects (for example, data extracts) outside the online hours, a message will display in the "Report Object" frame notifying you that data may not be available. If you are able to retrieve data from such report objects, pay attention to the refresh date of the report object in order to determine the age of the data against which you are reporting.\nFrozen files\nFor frozen files (which are always available), the date in the filename indicates the age of the data contained in the report object.\nScheduled jobs\nIUIE scheduled jobs will be released at 5am Monday-Friday and 10:30am Saturday and Sunday.	KB0023700	KB0023700 v3.0	Table of contents Introduction Hours of availability and support Frozen files Scheduled jobs Introduction The IUIE database (DSS1PRD) is locked at 10pm daily to allow overnight <strong>batch</strong> scripts to run. The database is copied, and the copy made available overnight. (To request access to this overnight	5.194		2026-01-31 17:17:31.693245	2026-01-31 17:36:27.984359	0	175ee71347f4fe18d500b87b716d4352
4081	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023102	VPN split tunneling at IU	Indiana University's\nSSL VPN\nemploys split tunneling for most users in order to lessen the load on the VPN service and allow for more concurrent users.\nSplit tunneling means that only network traffic to resources residing at IU is routed through the encrypted VPN. Traffic to cloud-based applications does not use your IU VPN connection; that is, it is the same as not being on the IU VPN service.\nFor example, these services do\nnot\ntravel over the IU VPN with split tunneling:\nCanvas\nZoom\nMicrosoft Teams\nKaltura\nServices that do use the IU VPN with split tunneling include:\nFile shares hosted on IU servers\nOne.IU\nResearch systems (for example,\nBig Red 200\n,\nSDA\n)\nThe IU\nSecurity Center\nIUanyWare\n(VPN use not required)\nNotes:\nImplementation of VPN split tunneling may affect your off-campus access to non-IU scholarly databases and journals, such as JSTOR. Because split tunneling routes external (non-IU) traffic outside the VPN, your connection will appear to the provider as coming from a device outside the IU network; consequently, you will be denied access to resources that are reserved for IU users.\nWhether you're connected to the VPN or not\n, you can access IU databases and journals via your\ncampus library's website\n.\nWith split tunneling, some streaming services and/or web content may not be supported/available when using the VPN outside of the US.	KB0023102	KB0023102 v4.0	 on IU servers One.IU Research systems (for example, <strong>Big</strong> <strong>Red</strong> <strong>200</strong>, SDA) The IU Security Center IUanyWare	24.3913		2026-01-31 14:43:34.007473	2026-01-31 16:36:19.772831	0	2e5e82cc33c6aed004e605570e5c7bec
2314	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023380	About Salesforce and IU CRM	In university settings, CRM refers to constituent relationship management (in industry parlance, "customer" relationship management).\nFor information about the CRM initiative at Indiana University, see\nIU CRM\n.\nThe IU CRM platform is made up of two components: Salesforce CRM and Marketing Cloud. For help with the IU CRM or Marketing Cloud implementations, email\ncrmhelp@iu.edu\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:35:32.281267	2026-01-31 15:02:41.269998	1	KB0023380
1904	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?sys_kb_id=584070b347a456583d373803846d432e&id=kb_article_view&sysparm_rank=25&sysparm_tsqueryId=4bdaee914776f610d500b87b716d4332	Use ACLs to provide additional access to Slate-Project files and directories	UITS\nResearch Technologies\nhigh performance file systems support POSIX Access Control Lists (ACLs). The use of ACLs provides granular control for sharing data with other IU research supercomputer users, allowing you to grant individual users or groups access to specific files and directories.\nUsing ACLs on\nSlate-Project\n, you can allow additional access to certain paths within a project. This advanced feature is recommend only for those who are familiar with ACLs and willing to micromanage access to certain paths on a per-user basis.\nNote:\nThese access settings must be managed on the command line. They are not reflected in\nRT Projects\nor the IU Rights Summary report in each project, or within other automated tools.\nOn\nSlate-Project\n, ACLs are used to allow read-only access to a project space; read-write access is controlled by membership in a specific group. The following instructions apply only to read-write or read-only collaborators on a project. There is no way to allow access to those who are not members of either group.\nFollowing are ways that access can be managed on a path basis; enter commands on the command line of a login node on an IU research HPC resource, such as\nBig Red 200\n,\nQuartz\n, or\nResearch Desktop\n(\nRED\n).\nNote:\nIf the collaborator's login name is not known on the high performance resource you are using, substitute the numerical UID instead.\nAdd access for one read-only collaborator to also write to a specific path:\nThis will set access for files that you own (not files that are owned by others). If another collaborator also has existing files in this path, they will also need to run these commands.\nsetfacl -R -m u:\ncollaborator-login-name\n:rwx /N/project/\nproject-name\n/\nsubdirectory-name\nsetfacl -R -m d:u:\ncollaborator-login-name\n:rwx /N/project/\nproject-name\n/\nsubdirectory-name\nThe first command above recursively (\n-R\n) modifies (\n-m\n) permissions for username\ncollaborator-login-name\nto allow the collaborator to read and execute all directories within\n/N/project/project-name/subdirectory-name\n.\nThe second command, which includes the\nd:\nbefore the access stanza, is necessary because it sets the default ACL for new files and directories beneath that path. The first command only sets parameters for existing files and directories.\nRemove access for a read-write collaborator from a specific path within your\nSlate-Project\nallocation:\nThis will set access for files that you own (not files that are owned by others). If another collaborator also has existing files in this path, they also will need to run these commands.\nsetfacl -R -m u:\ncollaborator-login-name\n:- /N/project/\nproject-name\n/\nsubdirectory-name\nsetfacl -R -m d:u:\ncollaborator-login-name\n:- /N/project/\nproject-name\n/\nsubdirectory-name\nThe first command above recursively (\n-R\n) modifies (\n-m\n) permissions for username\ncollaborator-login-name\nto prevent the collaborator from accessing all directories within\n/N/project/project-name/subdirectory-name\n.\nThe second command, which includes the\nd:\nbefore the access stanza, is necessary because it sets the default ACL for new files and directories beneath that path. The first command only sets parameters for existing files and directories.\nRestore original access for a collaborator to a path:\nThis set of commands uses the\n-x\nflag instead of\n-m\nto undo the previous configuration settings. The collaborator will get back the original permissions they had to the files affected.\nsetfacl -R -x u:\ncollaborator-login-name\n/N/project/\nproject-name\n/\nsubdirectory-name\nsetfacl -R -x d:u:\ncollaborator-login-name\n/N/project/\nproject-name\n/\nsubdirectory-name\nList ACLs for a particular directory or file:\nThe following command will list the user and group permissions, as well as the default user and group permissions:\n$ getfacl\nfile-or-directory-name\nACLs you have added with this method will be listed as\nuser:collaborator-id:rwx\n.\nFor more about using ACLs on\nSlate-Project\n, see\nSecure research data containing HIPAA-regulated PHI on high performance file systems at IU\n, or use the command\nman acl\nfrom a login node on\nBig Red 200\nor\nQuartz\n.	KB0025963	KB0025963 v1.0	Using ACLs on <strong>Slate</strong>-Project, you can allow additional access to certain paths within a project, or within other automated tools. On <strong>Slate</strong>-Project, ACLs are used to allow read-only access for a read-write collaborator from a specific path within your <strong>Slate</strong>-Project allocation: This will set access	45.8194		2026-01-31 14:34:27.534734	2026-01-31 14:34:27.534734	0	584070b347a456583d373803846d432e
1438	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sysparm_article=KB0023375	About HIPAA	The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (HIPAA) is a set of statutes designed to improve the efficiency and effectiveness of the US health care system:\nTitle I:\nHIPAA's Title I establishes rules to "improve the portability and continuity of health insurance coverage" for workers when they change employers.\nTitle II:\nHIPAA's Title II establishes rules to prevent health care fraud and abuse. Its "Administrative Simplification" section sets standards for enabling the electronic exchange of health information, and includes provisions establishing rules for protecting the privacy and security of personal health information:\nPrivacy Rule:\nThe\nHIPAA Privacy Rule\nprotects the privacy of individually identifiable personal health information.\nSecurity Rule:\nThe\nHIPAA Security Rule\ndefines national security standards for protecting electronic data that contain protected health information (\nPHI\n).\nEnforcement Rule:\nThe\nHIPAA Enforcement Rule\nempowers the Secretary of the US Department of Health and Human Services (\nHHS\n) to impose civil money penalties on entities that violate HIPAA rules.\nBreach Notification Rule:\nThe\nHIPAA Breach Notification Rule\nrequires HIPAA covered entities and their business associates to notify affected individuals, the\nHHS Secretary\n, and (in certain circumstances) the media following any breaches of unsecured PHI.\nThe HHS Office for Civil Rights (\nOCR\n) administers and enforces compliance with the HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules.\nIn 2009, the Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical Health (HITECH) Act was enacted to strengthen HIPAA enforcement rules. Subtitle D of the HITECH Act improved privacy and security provisions found in the original HIPAA privacy and security rules. In 2013, relevant provisions of HITECH and the Genetic Information Non-discrimination Act (GINA) of 2008 were combined to create the HIPAA "Final Rule".\nFor more about HIPAA and the HITECH Act, see these HHS pages:\nHIPAA for Professionals\nSummary of the HIPAA Privacy Rule\nSummary of the HIPAA Security Rule\nFinal Rule\n(in PDF format)\nAdditionally, see the US Department of Labor's\nHIPAA fact sheet\nand the US Government Printing Office's\nonline copy of the HIPAA statute\n.\nAt Indiana University, compliance with HIPAA's privacy and security rules is coordinated through the Office of the Chief Privacy Officer. For policies, procedures, and additional details about HIPAA compliance at IU, see\nHIPAA Privacy and Security Compliance\n, or email\nhipaa@iu.edu\n.\nIf you have questions about securing HIPAA-regulated research data at IU, email\nsecuremyresearch@iu.edu\n.\nSecureMyResearch\nprovides self-service resources and one-on-one consulting to help IU researchers, faculty, and staff meet cybersecurity and compliance requirements for processing, storing, and sharing regulated and unregulated research data; for more, see\nAbout SecureMyResearch\n. To learn more about properly ensuring the safe handling of\nPHI\non UITS systems, see the UITS IT Training video\nSecuring HIPAA Workflows on UITS Systems\n. To learn about division of responsibilities for securing PHI, see\nShared responsibility model for securing PHI on UITS systems\n.				0	Public	2026-01-31 14:32:41.739544	2026-01-31 14:37:01.711876	1	KB0023375
9694	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=dabf6c77476456583d373803846d4328	Have your IU-networked computer scanned for security vulnerabilities	Network security vulnerabilities may be unrelated to\nviruses\n, and are usually the result of a flaw in the operating system or other software. You should perform a scan of your Indiana University-owned computer when you install a new operating system, or when you install or upgrade networking or other system software.\nThe\nUniversity Information Security Office\n(UISO) provides access to software that can perform an external scan of your computer. UISO requires computers on Office of the Vice President for Information Technology (OVPIT) and\nUITS\nnetworks to be scanned for network security vulnerabilities every 30 days. Computers on other IU networks may also use the scanning service if they are directly connected to the IU network; you cannot have your computer scanned if you connect via a wireless connection or\nVPN\n.\nIf you are currently listed with UISO as a scanning administrator for your computer, you can request a scan at any time. If your computer has never been scanned, you need to request a scan from the UISO. For more, see\nAbout vulnerability scanners\n.\nAfter the\nUniversity Information Security Office\n(UISO) security scan of your Indiana University computer is complete, you will receive a summary of the results via email. If the scan reports vulnerabilities for devices assigned to you, visit\nAbout vulnerability scanners\nto evaluate the issues that need to be addressed. The UISO categorizes vulnerabilities as high, medium, and low risks, and urges you to correct high risks within one day, medium risks within one week, and low risks within one month.\nIf you don't understand the information in the message or the instructions to correct the vulnerabilities, or if you would like information about evaluating your system in detail, email\nscanner-admin@iu.edu\n.\nNote:\nUISO proactively scans computers in the UITS Data Centers. To request scanning of any other devices, email\nscanner-admin@iu.edu\n.	KB0023014	KB0023014 v1.0	Network security vulnerabilities may be unrelated to viruses, and are usually the result of a flaw in the operating system or other software. You should perform a scan of your Indiana University-owned computer when you install a new operating system, or when you install or upgrade networking or other system software. The University Information S	26.1409		2026-01-31 15:07:34.318439	2026-01-31 17:08:07.427636	0	dabf6c77476456583d373803846d4328
12476	https://servicenow.iu.edu/kb?id=kb_article_view&sys_kb_id=0c78f6c03bcbd2102b40571864e45ad3	About IU departmental Data Warehouse accounts	Table of contents\nOverview\nSponsorship\nUsernames\nRequest a departmental account\nData Warehouse hours of accessibility\nOverview\nIndiana University departments can use departmental Data Warehouse accounts to connect to the Data Warehouse to perform automated extracts of data. This data may then be fed into a departmental application or presented in a reporting tool, such as\nTableau\n. Using a departmental account rather than an individual account to access the Data Warehouse avoids loss of access to data should staff members change jobs or leave the university.\nSponsorship\nEvery departmental Data Warehouse account must have a sponsor who is an IU employee and who has completed the\nAcceptable Use Agreement\n. Only an account sponsor can request a password change for the departmental account. If an account sponsor changes jobs or leaves IU, the sponsor should identify a new sponsor and request that the account sponsorship information be updated. When account sponsorship changes, UITS recommends the account password be changed.\nUsernames\nUsernames for departmental Data Warehouse accounts are six to eight characters long, and are usually made up of an abbreviation for the department's campus and department followed by a two- or three-digit number (for example,\nBLVPFA01\nfor the Bloomington Vice Provost for Faculty Affairs). Alternatively, if a departmental application is known by an acronym, the username may be that acronym followed by a two- or three-digit number (for example, for an application known as ARMS, the username might be\nARMS001\n).\nRequest a departmental account\nTo request a departmental Data Warehouse account, fill out the\nEDSD Support Form\n:\nSelect\nIUIE & PDQ Support Request\n.\nUnder "Type of Request", select\nAccount Questions\n.\nUnder "Type of Account Request", choose\nDepartmental Account Access Request\n.\nIn the "Account name" box, enter the desired username for your departmental account; in the "Sponsor" box, enter the IU username of the account's sponsor. (If you already have a departmental account, you may select\nNo\nfor "I Need a Departmental Account".)\nIf your requested username is already taken, the Data Warehouse team will contact you.\nFill in any relevant comments and select\nSubmit\n.\nAfter your account has been processed, UITS\nTier 2 Accounts and Student Information Systems (ASIS)\nwill provide you with the password for your departmental account.\nSend requests for access to data for the new account to the data manager for the relevant area of responsibility.\nData Warehouse hours of accessibility\nDepartmental Data Warehouse accounts are meant for direct data access only, not for IUIE application use. Accounts can only access the DSS1PRD database during\nonline hours\n. A copy of DSS1PRD is available during the nightly batch schedule (10pm-5am) for special cases; separate authorization is required.	KB0023657	KB0023657 v3.0	 during online hours. A copy of DSS1PRD is available during the nightly <strong>batch</strong> schedule (10pm-5am	5.0861		2026-01-31 17:36:36.877079	2026-01-31 17:36:36.877079	0	0c78f6c03bcbd2102b40571864e45ad3
